\par }{\fs20\lang1024\langfe1024\noproof {\shp{\*\shpinst\shpleft1440\shptop2070\shpright10800\shpbottom2070\shpfhdr1\shpbxpage\shpbxignore\shpbypage\shpbyignore\shpwr3\shpwrk0\shpfblwtxt0\shpz2\shplockanchor\shplid2049{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 20}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn shapePath}{\sv 4}}{\sp{\sn fFillOK}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFilled}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fArrowheadsOK}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelh}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn posrelv}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}{\sp{\sn fLayoutInCell}{\sv 1}}}{\shprslt{\*\do\dobxpage\dobypage\dodhgt8194\dpline\dpptx0\dppty0\dpptx9920\dppty0\dpx1190\dpy2070\dpxsize9920\dpysize0\dplinew15\dplinecor0\dplinecog0\dplinecob0}}}}{\f0\fs16  \par }}{\footerf \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }{\footer \pard\plain \s22\ql \b\f354\fs18  Page {\field{\*\fldinst PAGE}{\fldrslt 1}} of {\field{\*\fldinst NUMPAGES}{\fldrslt 1}}{     }\'a9 2026 \uc2 Factiva, Inc.  All rights reserved.\par }\pard\plain \ql \li0\ri0\nowidctlpar\faauto\rin0\lin0\itap0 {\fs20 {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for July 19, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Catherine Herridge, Janet Shamlian, Lilia  Luciano, Scott MacFarlane, Justin Michaels, Chris Warren, Jonathan  Vigliotti, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 3,676 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 July 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, widespread damage and severe weather after a tornado hits the South, the ongoing threat, with millions coast to coast facing oppressive heat.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The urgent rescues in Kentucky and a state of emergency after flash flooding inundates homes and cars, plus the devastation after a tornado forms in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Former President Trump is staying defiant after receiving a target letter in the January 6 investigation.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Obstruction of an official proceeding could be one of the charges.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I didn`t know practically what a subpoena was and grand juries and all of this. Now I`m, like, becoming an expert.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: I didn`t have any option.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Emotional testimony for women suing Texas who believe the state`s abortion ban puts their lives at risk.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BRANDT (Plaintiff): I don`t feel safe to have children in Texas anymore.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: CBS News exclusive: our interviewer with an \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower who claims the Department of Justice limited his \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  investigation.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH ZIEGLER (IRS Whistle-Blower): I was asking to do these certain things, and roadblock after roadblock was put up in front of me.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The mystery deepens in the case of an Alabama woman who went missing for two days.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS DERZIS (Hoover, Alabama, Police Chief): The day of her disappearance, the term "how to take money from a register without being caught" was searched.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Big news from \uc2 Netflix\uc2  about one of its subscription plans. We have the details.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: The cash keeps piling up, and it is all up for grabs. The Powerball jackpot is now up to $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: One in about 292 million, those are your odds to win tonight`s Powerball jackpot.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Show me the money, baby.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 We begin with the dangerous weather that is gripping the nation, from brutally hot temperatures to life-threatening flooding and even tornadoes. In fact, this afternoon, there was a large twister that touched down in Rocky Mountain, North Carolina, causing significant damage. That is about 50 miles east of Raleigh.\par \par \uc2 Roofs were ripped off homes and trees and power lines were scattered across roadways. Several people were transported to the hospital. And then, from coast to coast, from Pasadena to Palm Beach, we`re talking about more than 120 million Americans who are now under heat alerts. And torrential rains have flooded entire towns in Western Kentucky, turning roads into rivers and forcing residents from their homes.\par \par \uc2 We have team coverage of today`s extreme weather and a forecast of what is ahead.\par \par \uc2 But, first, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Mark Strassmann will start us off tonight as emergency crews spring into action.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Is it coming this way?\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voiceover): It was.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Yes, look at it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: A gathering storm approaching Rocky Mountain, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Oh, my God, dude!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: It`s noontime fury leaving a debris trail, by some estimates, stretching 2.5-miles-wide. The tornado ripped into this \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  manufacturing plant, shearing off the roof.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: It was real fast, yes. I ain`t never seen nothing like this in person.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In Dortches, North Carolina, residential and ruined in stretches.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE VICK (Mayor of Dortches, North Carolina): It was bad for maybe a minute, and it was over.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Neighboring Kentucky`s weather worry, flooding.\par \par \uc2 Here`s Justin Michaels with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS (National Correspondent, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): Here in Western Kentucky, the water came in fast and furious, just outside the city of Mayfield, what is expected to be a new 24 hour rainfall record for this state with over 11 inches.\par \par \uc2 Residents here are still coping from the devastating and deadly tornado that ripped through the region less than two years ago.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: So it is a known flood risk area. But, like I said, it came so quickly.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN MICHAELS: And what that nearly a foot of rain did here in Mayfield, Kentucky, over the course of 24 hours was flood out cars and garages, even a home through those trees.\par \par \uc2 And, looking over here, the Mayfield Elementary School now surrounded by water, and there is more rain in the forecast.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Back in Dortches, North Carolina, the message, resilience.\par \par \uc2 MAN #4: Life is precious. Material things come and go. We will -- we will rebuild.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The \uc2 National Weather Service\uc2  says it was an EF-3 tornado with winds up to 150 miles per hour.\par \par \uc2 And, Norah, that`s very rare for North Carolina this time of year.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to the brutal heat, with triple-digit temperatures in nearly a dozen states, the daytime high near 120 degrees in Phoenix, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonathan Vigliotti reports that health officials across the Southwest are on alert.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voiceover): Here in Phoenix, first responders are on the move.\par \par \uc2 In just 24 hours, heat-related emergency calls jumped 35 percent, from close calls like this one to treating this patient showing signs of heat stress.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. TODD KELLER (Phoenix, Arizona, Fire Department): I can tell you that a majority of our calls that we are going on are going to be medical calls.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The priority for the responding crew is bringing a patient`s body temperature down fast using ice cold intravenous fluids.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. JASON HARDIN (Phoenix, Arizona, Fire Department): So, what you can see here is, we have I.V. bags that we throw in ice, right? So, we have several of them. I mean, this, we could use two of these for one call.\par \par \uc2 We also have these tools, which are very nice. We will put these around the necks. We will put these in the groin. We will put them underneath the armpits as well.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Signs of heat-related illness include dizziness, nausea, and headache. Heat stroke is the most dangerous, with warning signs including loss of sweat, a rapid pulse, and a body temperature of 103 degrees or higher. Officials say you should call 911 immediately.\par \par \uc2 At the Tumbleweed Day Camp in Los Angeles, they`re taking extra precautions to keep kids safe, making sure the equipment and the campers stay cool.\par \par \uc2 LIZ KIMMELMAN (Owner, Tumbleweed Day Camp): We make it a party. So it`s sunscreen party. Everyone`s putting on sunscreen together. We`re doing water breaks. We`re doing, you know, hydration challenges, and so we`re doing a lot of preemptive work on that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And back here in Maricopa County, officials have confirmed at least 12 deaths linked to this heat wave.\par \par \uc2 But perhaps most troubling about all of this, a third of those victims were actually inside at the time and may not have noticed the symptoms until it was too late, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That is really disturbing.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The extreme weather of heat and heavy rains is far from over, so we have got a look ahead.\par \par \uc2 So let`s bring in meteorologist Chris Warren from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS WARREN (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 A rare flash flood emergency this morning in Western Kentucky, with rainfall estimates more than a foot. And as Justin showed us, the rain was relentless overnight into the morning hours. The threat for flooding continues overnight tonight into the morning hours.\par \par \uc2 If it seems like these flash flood emergencies are common, they aren`t. But there has been 11 since July 1 in the Eastern part of the United States. What else is not common is this heat, more record heat tomorrow again, up to 118 in Phoenix. And, unfortunately, as we look at the pattern, we look at the weather, Norah, in the West, no significant cooling at all for the next several days.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Chris Warren, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re getting new details tonight about the possible charges that former President Trump faces after receiving a target letter from special counsel Jack Smith.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Scott MacFarlane spoke with a former prosecutor about what it all means.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We have prosecutors that are evil people. These are evil people.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voiceover): Former President Trump lashed out at special counsel Jack Smith and posted a video rallying supporters to join him.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @RealDonaldTrump/Truth Social)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: We will throw off the sick political class that hates our country.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: With a potential criminal indictment looming, CBS News has learned the target letter Smith sent to Trump cites several possible charges, including conspiracy to defraud the U.S., deprivation of rights under color of law, which applies when someone uses power to deny constitutional rights, and witness tampering and obstruction of an official proceeding, the January 6 electoral count certification.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT FREDERICKSEN (Former Federal Prosecutor): This goes to the top of the most serious charges that the former president will face. This is of a constitutional dimension. We have never seen anything like this in the history of our country.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The obstruction count was used against more than 300 defendants in the Capitol riot.\par \par \uc2 The D.C. grand jury probing Trump`s actions is still at work. And the special counsel recently contacted Arizona`s former Governor \uc2 Doug Ducey\uc2 , who Trump allegedly called in the aftermath of the election to pressure him to overturn the results.\par \par \uc2 The former president`s allies said he faces an uphill climb if charged in Washington.\par \par \uc2 You think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can`t get a fair trial in the District of Columbia. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-South Carolina): Well, he got 5 percent of the vote. You can indict a Republican for almost anything and get convicted in this city.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: You don`t think a jury can put the politics aside and to hear the facts?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 : No. No.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Meanwhile, a loss in court in New York for Trump today, where a judge has rejected Trump`s request for a new trial in the civil case for which he was found liable for sex abuse of the writer E. Jean Carroll -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Scott MacFarlane with all those details, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`re learning more about the U.S. soldier being detained in North Korea after bolting across the border on Tuesday. U.S. officials tell CBS News that 23-year-old Private Travis King served 47 days of hard labor after being charged last fall with assaulting two South Koreans and kicking a police car. He was supposed to return to the U.S. for separation from the Army before he crossed into North Korea.\par \par \uc2 There are new questions tonight about the mysterious disappearance of a 25- year-old Alabama woman. Police said today that Carlee Russell gave a harrowing account of being abducted by a man with orange hair, but that her Internet searches before she vanished included a Hollywood kidnapping thriller movie.\par \par \uc2 We get more now from \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Lilia Luciano.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO (voiceover): Tonight, police provide some answers to the many questions that followed a bizarre story of abduction in Birmingham, when Carlee Russell disappeared after making an alarming call to 911.\par \par \uc2 CARLEE RUSSELL (Alabama): I am on Interstate 459, and there is a kid just walking by theirself.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Her family says she was on the phone with her sister-in- law, who says they heard her scream.\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS DERZIS (Hoover, Alabama, Police Chief): We pretty much know exactly what took place from the time she left work until she got on the 911 call. And we can see getting out of that -- getting out of the car on the interstate from -- from that footage.\par \par \uc2 And, after that, I think she only knows.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: She told police that the orange-haired man had kept her in the back of an 18-wheeler.\par \par \uc2 Police say, during the investigation, it was revealed that she had made questionable Internet searches days and hours before vanishing, including about the Hollywood kidnapping thriller "Taken."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Taken, 2008, 20th Century Fox)\par \par \uc2 NICHOLAS DERZIS: On July 11th at 7:30 a.m., the term "You have to pay for an Amber Alert" was searched.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Russell`s disappearance prompted a statewide two-day search. Police say Russell eventually walked home, was treated at a hospital and released.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Hoover P.D. are still investigating, and they do want to ask her more questions.\par \par \uc2 But, today, police said that they have yet to find any evidence of either a wandering toddler or a kidnapper -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: So many questions.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, police are investigating a shooting inside a Florida City Walmart that left one person dead and two others injured. The suspected gunman is in custody. Investigators for the Miami-Dade police said it`s not clear if the shooting was a targeted attack. Multiple law enforcement agencies are on the scene investigating, along with emergency personnel.\par \par \uc2 Now to Capitol Hill, where two agents from the \uc2 Internal Revenue Service\uc2  testified about their role in the federal investigation of \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 . The president`s son is expected to plead guilty to two misdemeanor tax crimes next week.\par \par \uc2 But in an exclusive interview with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Catherine Herridge, the lead case agent said the evidence supported more serious charges.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMES COMER (R-Kentucky): Do you solemnly swear?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE (voiceover): Special Agent Joseph Ziegler is a 13-year veteran of the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , a registered Democrat and, until now, a confidential whistle-blower.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH ZIEGLER (IRS Whistle-Blower): I do.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Today, he publicly testified about his role investigating the president`s son Hunter for tax crimes.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH ZIEGLER: I`m the main case agent. I worked 95 percent of this tax investigation.\par \par \uc2 So nice to meet you.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: In an exclusive interview with CBS News, Ziegler told us he believed the evidence he gathered during the five-year probe supported multiple felony and misdemeanor counts, including allegations \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  wrote off personal expenses as business payments.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH ZIEGLER: Hotels he was blacklisted from, payments that were made to escorts, payments that are made to no-show employees.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Ziegler said he drafted this memo recommending Justice Department prosecutors charge \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  accordingly. But he said the Trump-appointed U.S. attorney, David Weiss, told him there was resistance to pursuing some of these charges by other officials at the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH ZIEGLER: It`s a matter of, are we treating all taxpayers the same?\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: And in this case?\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH ZIEGLER: And, in this case, no, I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Today, Democrats pushed back on claims of preferential treatment.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMIE RASKIN (D-Maryland): There is no evidence that \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  has received any kind of official favoritism in this prosecution for being Joe Biden`s son.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: But Ziegler described to CBS News a series of roadblocks that included his efforts to interview Hunter Biden`s adult children about the legitimacy of his tax deductions.\par \par \uc2 What did the assistant U.S. attorney tell you?\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH ZIEGLER: That that`s going to get us into hot water.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Ziegler also told us prosecutors would not sign off on pursuing leads that might involve President Biden.\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH ZIEGLER: Any time we potentially wanted to go down the road of asking questions related to the president, it was, that`s going to take too much approvals. We can`t ask those questions.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: It`s a politically sensitive case. Wouldn`t it require additional approvals?\par \par \uc2 JOSEPH ZIEGLER: Yes, I do understand that. But it would be like, well, let`s think about it. Let`s put that on the back burner. And it would now move down to item number 50.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: This probe was ongoing during the 2020 election, when Justice Department policy limited some investigative steps.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. attorney in Delaware has told Congress twice he had the ultimate decision-making authority. President Biden denies involvement in his son`s business deals.\par \par \uc2 And, Norah, Hunter Biden`s legal team said any claim the probe wasn`t thorough is -- quote -- "preposterous."\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Interesting new details.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Herridge, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to what`s being called a landmark lawsuit by abortion rights advocate.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian tonight reports that several women say they were denied an abortion despite facing serious pregnancy complications.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ZURAWSKI (Lead Plaintiff, Texas Abortion Ban Lawsuit): The state doesn`t care about the lives of their constituents.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): Emotional testimony from women in a Texas courtroom today, saying the state`s abortion ban put their lives in danger.\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ZURAWSKI: I had just been given the worst news of my life, and I was terrified.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Amanda Zurawski said she was denied an abortion when her water broke at 18 weeks. She developed sepsis and nearly died.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Was Willow alive when you delivered her?\par \par \uc2 AMANDA ZURAWSKI: No.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  filed the suit on behalf of 13 women who claimed they were denied emergency care due to the state`s abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BRANDT (Plaintiff): The law does not take cases like mine into consideration.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Ashley Brandt was pregnant with twins. When one was diagnosed with a fatal birth defect, she says she had to go out of state to terminate the pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BRANDT: I don`t feel safe to have children in Texas anymore. I know that it was very clear that my health didn`t really matter, but my daughter`s health didn`t really matter, and that is heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Texas is among 15 states that don`t allow abortions for a fatal fetal anomaly.\par \par \uc2 Rebecca Weaver is with Texas Right to Life.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA WEAVER (Legislative Director, Texas Right to Life): Certainly, these stories from these women are heartbreaking and tragic. But our view is that the law is fine as it is and doesn`t need to be changed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The state wants this case dismissed. It says the plaintiffs are unhappy with the medical care they received and oppose the Texas abortion law.\par \par \uc2 This hearing is scheduled to continue through tomorrow -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Janet Shamlian, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, explosive allegations of hazing and sexual abuse on the Northwestern football team. Our exclusive television interview is next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, in an exclusive TV interview, former \uc2 Northwestern University\uc2  quarterback Lloyd Yates is opening up to CBS News about the hazing and sexual abuse he says he endured.\par \par \uc2 Yates, who graduated from Northwestern in 2018, is among the first players to speak out after football coach Pat Fitzgerald was fired earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 Yates tells \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Roxana Saberi that the abuse included having to perform acts nude in front of teammates. And he says it left him with anxiety, nightmares, and unable to ever watch football.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LLOYD YATES (Former Northwestern University Quarterback): We really want to bring that to light to make sure this stuff --this type of stuff is not normalized. I think the biggest thing for us was being brainwashed that this is normal.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Yates is among several former Northwestern athletes represented by civil rights attorney \uc2 Benjamin Crump\uc2  who are now planning a lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 The university sent us a statement tonight, saying in part that hazing has no place at Northwestern and further steps are being taken to eliminate it.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, more about \uc2 Netflix\uc2  as it makes a big announcement about one of its subscription plans. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Big changes tonight at \uc2 Netflix\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The streaming giant has pulled the plug on its $10-per-month commercial- free plan in the U.S. Now, those already on the plan can continue to use it, but \uc2 Netflix\uc2  is now trying to boost its subscriber numbers and revenue by steering more users toward its $7-per-month option, which does include commercials.\par \par \uc2 One of the largest jackpots in Powerball history is up for grabs. We will have your odds of winning next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally, tonight, you could say there are a billion reasons to be excited about tonight`s Powerball drawing.\par \par \uc2 It`s just the third time in Powerball history that the jackpot has topped the billion-dollar mark. Long lines and high hopes can be spotted across the country. And if you take the lump sum, you could buy 369 Ferraris, more than 8,000 luxury cruises around the world, or about 88,000 diamond rings.\par \par \uc2 We should note though, that your odds of winning are about 292 million to one. But $2 will get you a ticket, but your dreams of winning, of course, are free.\par \par \uc2 Good luck to all the buyers out there. There were a lot in the office today.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230725ej7j000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Heat Dome Blankets 14 States; Wildfire Smoke Returns; Heat Wave Rages On; Global Heatwave; Europe Facing Record Heat; Biden Extends Invitation to Netanyahu; DeSantis Campaigns in South Carolina; Third Party Push; Iowa`s Six-Week Abortion Ban Blocked; Oregon Serial Killer Fears; New Details in Gilgo Beach Murder Case; Hidden Dangers for Ukraine`s Soldiers; American Detained in North Korea; \uc2 FDA\uc2  Approves Infant RSV Shots; Medial Uses of Artificial Intelligence; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; Actors Out on Strike; Colson Whitehead`s Novel Ideas, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Michelle Miller, Jonathan Vigliotti,  Jordan Steele, Chris Livesay, Nancy Cordes, Ed O`Keefe, Errol Barnett,  Lilia Luciano, Charlie D`Agata, Celine Gounder, Nancy Chen, Jon LaPook \par \uc2 13,705 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 July 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Tuesday, July 18. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And I`m Michelle Miller.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off, but we`re in good hands.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A brutal heatwave across the Southwest is fueling wildfires, while parts of the country`s electrical grid struggle to keep up with demand.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is hanging in here. The heat dome, not just entrenched, but expanding in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Europe is suffering through its own dangerous heatwave, as temperatures in Italy approach the highest ever recorded.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s hot. We expected heat, but not this hot.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Georgia`s Supreme Court dismissed Donald Trump`s bid to shut down a criminal probe into his attempts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Police say a search of the home of the accused Gilgo Beach serial killer uncovered a vault in his basement filled with weapons.\par \par \uc2 RODNEY HARRISON (Suffolk County, New York Police Commissioner): It is over 200 guns. He had an arsenal.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A judge in Iowa has put on hold the state`s new six-week abortion ban just days after it became law.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Cavs champions of the Summer League.\par \par \uc2 MAN: For the first time in its franchise`s history.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Country singer Miranda Lambert stopped singing mid concert after spotting fans who were busy with their phones.\par \par \uc2 MIRANDA LAMBERT (Singer): I`m going to stop right now with the singing. I am sorry. These girls are worried about their selfie, and not listening to the son. song, it`s pissing me off a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Reds and Giants went into a rain delay in the eighth inning.\par \par \uc2 MAN: So instead of a final score, enjoyed the announcer`s commentating on the Reds` field crew putting on the tarp.\par \par \uc2 MAN: High stepping.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, boy. There we go.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, he got eaten by the tarp.\par \par \uc2 MAN: He is under there.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They are not even stopping. No pause. He is under there somewhere.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is like basic training.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We lost him. Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 MAN Don`t -- you can`t leave a man behind.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They`ll pick it up, tied two in the eighth. Hopefully, he`ll be out by then, I`m pretty sure.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 His friends saw him go under.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They were like, should we get him? No, we are going to keep going.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nah.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You know, that is every mom`s worst nightmare.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Being eaten by a tarp monster.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Or your son being bad at his job?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes. There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ll tell you what --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m joking, man. It happens to everybody.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It does. It does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: High knees next time. High knees.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got to stay up. Stay upright.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`ve got a fascinating show for you today.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to CBS MORNINGS, including a look at how artificial intelligence may end up in your doctor`s office. But do you want it there? There are some folks who think it should play a big role in Medicine and well, others say it cannot be trusted in matters of life or death. Dr. Jon LaPook, he of the natural intelligence puts two new tools to the test for our series "Age of AI."\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Plus, two-time Pulitzer Prize winning author, Colson Whitehead will be here with us. He is going to share his brand new novel, "Crook Manifesto." It`s a sequel to his previous book, "Harlem Shuffle," and multiple reviewers have already called it dazzling. And he is a pretty dazzling guests, too, I hear.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that. We`re looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 But we begin today with the unrelenting heat nationwide. Roughly 68 million Americans are under some type of heat alert, and there is a heat dome right now over at least 14 states.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking triple digit temperatures for weeks. Making matters worse, the air quality is bad for millions in the East and the Midwest, due to the smoke from those Canadian wildfires that are still burning out of control.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti is in Los Angeles, where the heat is straining the power grid.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Hi, Nate. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Residents here in California are being urged to conserve their air conditioning use and to keep their thermostat set at 78 degrees to help avoid a potential blackout. This all comes as the state battles multiple wildfires under this extreme heat.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: California firefighters are working around the clock battling multiple brush fires throughout the state as it faces its hottest weather of the year.\par \par \uc2 Crews navigated difficult terrain to contain the Ladoga Fire in Northern California, which appears to have started by lightning.\par \par \uc2 California`s Death Valley continues to be stifled by triple digit heat. Temperatures are above 110 degrees at night. Jeff Smith is with \uc2 Pacific Gas and Electric\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JEFF SMITH (\uc2 Pacific Gas and Electric\uc2 ): When you`ve got really hot temperatures that don`t cool down at night, that can be really taxing on the electrical equipment.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Arizona`s power grid is meeting demand even as residents in the Phoenix area set a new record for the amount of electricity used. The city also broke a heat record today after experiencing 19 consecutive days of 110 degrees or higher. So far, 12 people have died in Maricopa County this year because of the heat.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I cry all the time. I like yell at the heat to like, go away.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Half of those deaths come from the most vulnerable, those without housing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Extreme heat is Arizona`s natural disaster, some ice cold water, a hat, some sunscreen, anything like that is a lifeline for people in need.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: In Las Vegas, Nevada, where it has been 110 degrees or above for more than a week --\par \par \uc2 JAMES LANGLEY (We Care Air Owner): We had probably a 20 or 30 percent increase in calls, if not more.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: James Langley has been working overtime all week after a power surge left residents in the neighborhood of Providence without air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 JAMES LANGLEY: We went from running two trucks eight hours a day to running three trucks about thirteen, fourteen hours a day. So quite, quite a bit. I mean, everybody really wasn`t ready for this heat.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And this heatwave is also shifting things for California, which has had a slow start to its fire season because of the record snowpack we received.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters are concerned that a combination of dry vegetation and all of this extreme heat could create dangerous wildfires -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to meteorologist, Jordan Steele from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 . He is tracking the heat and the return of this poor air quality.\par \par \uc2 Jordan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN STEELE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Yes, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 This summer has been so fickle -- the heat or the big rains, the flooding or like you mentioned, we`re choking on the smoke and the fires to our north are making our skies hazy once again, unfortunately, downright dangerous for so many of us.\par \par \uc2 Burlington, Philadelphia, Atlanta has some of the worst air quality today and we can show you how much of that smoke is impacting us. I`m talking over 1,500 miles of smoke along I-95. No, thank you. Now the rain moving in should clear this out in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 We go back West now, we`ve got a huge headline here. Phoenix has tied the record from June of 1974, eighteen straight days of 110 degrees or higher. And how about this little nugget, Phoenix has also seen eight straight days of overnight lows in the 90s. That`s also a new record.\par \par \uc2 The Southwest will bake yet again, with the hope of a monsoon kicking in to cool us down. Temperatures are going to be well in the triple digits.\par \par \uc2 But it`s not just the deserts of our Southwest, we`ve got to show you what is going on overseas. Some of the hottest temperatures ever recorded in Europe. Rome could get to 109.\par \par \uc2 Tony, limiting time spent outdoors is a good way to protect yourself for both the heat and the poor air quality.\par \par \uc2 Back to you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Jordan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on the extreme situation in Europe, including some devastating wildfires in Greece, Chris Livesay reports now from Rome, Italy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Fires seer through the Greek seaside forcing tourists to evacuate from at least six different towns. Once a vacation oasis, now reduced to ashes.\par \par \uc2 In Italy, temperatures could surpass 120 degrees, breaking the European heat record the country set just two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Rome is setting up 28 heat help points, like this one, as tourists have been collapsing under the sun.\par \par \uc2 The latest heat wave is called Charon named after the Greek deity who ferried souls acros