 such a slow grind.\par \par \uc2 And even though this is strictly an exercise for our benefit, and these real mines have been diffused, it`s still terrifying.\par \par \uc2 A forward team cuts a path only about two yards wide gradually widening it so troops and equipment can cut through the minefield. But Russians have been known to fire rockets containing smaller mines like these sometimes called petal mines or butterfly mines, re-mining an area that had already been cleared.\par \par \uc2 Not just the sheer number of mines, but mines of every description. Anti- tank, anti-personnel, cluster bombs, IEDs, callsign Mr. Brown shows us. One mine springs out of the ground four feet high and sprays 2,500 fragments in a 50-yard radius.\par \par \uc2 (CALLSIGN MR. BROWN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Which one scares you most?\par \par \uc2 (CALLSIGN MR. BROWN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "This one, which is rare," he says. "If the tripwire is activated, you can die on the spot. But those are the most scary ones. Six of our sappers have lost their legs to it."\par \par \uc2 Using metal detectors is not only dangerous, it is painstakingly slow, which is why, they like more of these.\par \par \uc2 American mine clearing line charges or MICLICs that can clear a 100-yard path in one spectacular blow. Troops say equipment like this US supplied Bradley fighting vehicle have saved lives, able to withstand anti-tank mines until they step outside. One unlucky soldier trips a mine he lost a leg, but he survived.\par \par \uc2 Callsign Odesa lost most of his foot and a few fingers to a mine. Yet, he is back on the job.\par \par \uc2 What does it take to be a sapper?\par \par \uc2 (CALLSIGN ODESA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "A lot of training because one step left or right can always be the last one," he says. "It`s very hard, but fun at the same time."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \u