s a shot, but it`s not a vaccine. It`s what we call a monoclonal antibody. People may be familiar with that from COVID, where we had monoclonal antibodies that we used for treatment. In this case, it is being given for prevention, and in clinical trials, reduce the risk of hospitalization among these very young infants by over 80 percent.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You have to do something special to get it or will it be recommended or brought up by your doctor at a regular checkup?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, the CDC`s ACIP which is the group that makes recommendations about the use of vaccines, they have yet to make those recommendations. But I think you will see those rolling out fairly soon. That will likely be in conjunction with what we predict will be the approval of an RSV vaccine for the protection of infants actually administered to pregnant women. We can get into that a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 But I think likely the ABRYSVO vaccine will be first line with the Beyfortus being given, say to high-risk infants whose moms just didn`t get a chance to be vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what are the parallels between the two, the one that`s given to infants and the one that might be coming out for women that are pregnant?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Right. So ABRYSVO is an RSV vaccine for pregnant women. You`d be giving it second or third trimester. The idea is the mom makes the antibodies, and she gives her baby antibodies through the placenta, and it is actually how women protect their babies against a whole host of things.\par \par \uc2 For the first six months or so of a baby`s life, it is mom`s antibodies that are protecting them.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: So many viruses out there. I`m sure folks out in TV land are confused by, you know, what might --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scared quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And they are scared, but what should people be looking out for and in particular, 