 Baseball, baseball, baseball.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s this move this thing along.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Oh, yes, we are.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this morning with the debate over whether artificial intelligence has a place in the field of Medicine. Like it or not, it`s already being used in a lot of ways like detecting rare genetic disorders, helping ALS patients speak, and even diagnosing some potentially deadly diseases.\par \par \uc2 Doctors hope AI can help save lives and companies like \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  say it can save time, but cannot be relied on?\par \par \uc2 As part of our continuing "Age of AI" series, Dr. Jon LaPook visited the Microsoft Technology Center to sit down with Peter Lee, one of the company`s top researchers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Trek: Voyager.")\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: There was a time when holographic doctors --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Trek: Voyager.")\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Were purely the stuff of science fiction.\par \par \uc2 In 2017, I met with actor Bob Picardo, who played the emergency medical hologram in "Star Trek" to talk about how Medicine might one day meet science fiction.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Trek: Voyager.")\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Do you think that eventually in the future, a computer algorithm could entirely replace a physician?\par \par \uc2 BOB PICARDO (Actor): That artificial intelligence physician will be created from the personal experiences of a large group of doctors. So yes, I believe the day will come when you will be obsolete.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Star Trek: Voyager.")\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Six years ago, Picardo`s prediction seemed far-fetched. But how about now in this age of AI?\par \par \uc2 I asked Peter Lee, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 `s Vice President of Research and Incubations.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  has said that ChatGPT 4 has scored something like the 90th percentile on the bar. Should we be concerned in health care that these computers are going to replace us in some way?\par \par \uc2 PETER LEE (\uc2 Microsoft\uc2 `s vice president of Research and Incubations): Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, GPT 4 also gets about 90 percent on the US medical licensing exam.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: But can it hold a hand?\par \par \uc2 PETER LEE: That`s the key.\par \par \uc2 This is a new type of tool. It`s not a human being, but it is not a computer like we`re used to.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Lee considers artificial intelligence to be a powerful tool that could dramatically improve patient care and cut down on one of the biggest complaints in health care, paperwork.\par \par \uc2 PETER LEE: A primary physician will spend about 40 to 45 percent of their working day writing clinical encounter notes and filling out prior authorization forms.\par \par \uc2 The hope is that it will allow doctors and nurses to again make eye contact and be present with their patients.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "2001: A Space Odyssey.")\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: But judging by its portrayal in movies and TV, AI has some convincing to do.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "2001: A Space Odyssey.")\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Is it developing some sort of machine consciousness?\par \par \uc2 PETER LEE: They don`t have a consciousness, but they are learning from all of us.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: I wondered if AI had already learned what a human practitioner has to do all the time. Figure out what`s important, and what`s not.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: How old would you like to be for today`s visit?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: So let`s say I`m 50 years old, mildly elevated blood sugar.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA BARRETT (Physician assistant): Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: I have a bellyache, it comes and goes.\par \par \uc2 Physician assistant, Andrea Barrett demoed for us the DAX Express, \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 `s artificial intelligence technology designed to capture and summarize a patient encounter.\par \par \uc2 My toes are numb every now and then. Yes, I`ve had that for years.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA BARRETT: All right, is one leg worse than the other? Are they both the same?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Both. Ever since I was in Australia and I was in a whitewater rafting accident and I ended up tearing my ACL in my knee when I hit a rock that was submerged.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA BARRETT: Okay, did you have it replaced?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: No. No, the rock stayed the same. It`s really hard to get it out of the stream.\par \par \uc2 As it turned out, it did recognize that my knee injury had nothing to do with my bellyache, and placed that piece of information in a separate part of the summary.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA BARRETT: As you see, it did take out that joke that you said about the rock.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: So it figured out that when I bashed my knee whitewater rafting in Australia, it had nothing to do with the abdominal pain.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA BARRETT: Correct.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: That`s impressive that it was able to filter out a lot of the sort of extraneous banter back and forth.\par \par \uc2 ANDREA BARRETT: Yep. As the clinician, you can decide if you want that included.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: But there are also concerns about what the technology might leave in.\par \par \uc2 There`s an expression that AI machines can hallucinate. What does that mean? What does it refer to?\par \par \uc2 PETER LEE: So it refers to the possibility that the AI system might provide answers that aren`t completely correct.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Lee says scientists are working on the equivalent of a lie detector for machines, based on finding unusual patterns of computer signals.\par \par \uc2 Sometimes they are defensive, these computers, right? They say -- they just double down on the mistake.\par \par \uc2 PETER LEE: Once the thing thinks a certain line of reasoning is the right one, it can get stuck in that line of reasoning.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Wait. Computers can be stubborn?\par \par \uc2 PETER LEE: They can remind us as human beings of some of our own foibles, but these are just machines. They`re just tools.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, that`s what they always say. Dr. Jon LaPook joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Fascinating what this AI can do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was good.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: But I mean, what are the potential risks? And most importantly, were you seriously concerned about it?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: How much time you got? You know, of course there is. Any new technology can be used for good, it can be used for bad. It is a tool.\par \par \uc2 So, I worry about privacy issues. Big discussion there. I mentioned hallucinations. It sometimes just definitively says two plus two is five, and has a reference for that, okay.\par \par \uc2 And then one of the things I`m really concerned about is bias. You know, there is -- we all have implicit bias. There is implicit bias in Medicine. You interject that into a computer program, and bias in, bias out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is an incredible report. I mean, the great thing about a real human doctor, of course, is they`re human, they can hold a hand, they`re intuitive and interesting. And all of these ways that are fantastic, that can`t be matched by a machine.\par \par \uc2 But there is all this research around how doctors make mistakes when they`re busy. There was a study recently about surgery, fatalities on the doctor`s birthday, so thinking maybe they`re distracted.\par \par \uc2 So could like the best of AI be married with what makes a human doctor great and we get the best outcome.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And I think that`s what we`re all hoping for, right? It`s already being used for imaging, helping with diagnosis, helping to figure out what new drugs might be used for what diseases or what old drugs might be applicable to other diseases.\par \par \uc2 One of the things -- you know, I`m involved with this empathy project.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Empathy project.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Right, and here is my fantasy about it. It could help teach empathy and here`s my fantasy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How so?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Okay, last summer --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The AI teaches it.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: AI, it hasn`t happened yet, but here is my fantasy for all of you, computer programmers out there, make this happen.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, a woman who I`ve known for 30 years came into my office, and she said, "Hi." I said, what`s the matter? She burst into tears. Her life is falling apart, and when she stopped crying, she said, how did you know? I said, it was too big of a high, too big of a high that something was off.\par \par \uc2 And first I thought, oh, wasn`t that great to pick that up? And then I thought, what am I missing? Can you imagine in the future, you`re having an encounter with a patient and by the way, now, I`m able to look the person right in the eye because ChatGPT or some equivalent is listening and making my note for me and in the middle of the encounter, up comes a little, little warning, a little prompt saying, you know, based on the voice patterns for the last 10 years with this patient, you might consider that perhaps there`s a little increase in anxiety today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Geez.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: So, that`s my fantasy, or looking at body language. You know, you asked a question, and the person closed their arms and moved backwards, maybe that`s -- maybe there is a little defensiveness there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But, Do, if you`re talking about artificial intelligence being able to identify emotion, it has to understand emotion. And if we`re talking about artificial intelligence going from logic, to now figuring out its own emotion, that`s where we start getting into the murky waters of movies.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what do you say?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And what does understanding mean, right? So one of the things that has been tough for me, is, you know, Dr. Paul Friedman is out in the Mayo Clinic, he has -- he told me, he said, you know, you can look at an EKG and somehow with AI, it can predict whether years from now, you`re going to be in a heart failure. I said, how? He goes, well, we don`t really know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: And so this -- I want to know if you`re telling me something, I want to know how do you get to that logic? And you know, they do some mumbo jumbo, well it is backward propagation into a neural network using variable weighting of nodes. And it`s like, wait, like what?\par \par \uc2 So it is hard for doctors like me to be able to say, all right, I`m going to trust this, if I don`t really totally understand how it reached the conclusion. Sometimes it can explain it and sometimes it really can`t.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, you`ve got to come back. We`ve got to talk more about this. This is so fascinating.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So interesting.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It really is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Jon LaPook, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up on \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, we`ve got something that could help you become a better golfer. All right, swing them things, you get better at it. We`ll show you it at an exclusive discount, but first, it is 8:11, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Ahead, what will it take to end the Hollywood actors strike? "Guardians of the Galaxy" star, Sean Gunn will share his thoughts on how to break the deadlock.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s time for \uc2 CBS\