let`s move this thing along though because someone is joining us who wants to help bring Hollywood back to life.\par \par \uc2 Now turning to some of those big issues behind the dual strikes that have shut down most of Hollywood, actors and writers are now on the picket lines including "Guardians of the Galaxy" star, Sean Gunn, who you may also remember from the "Gilmore Girls" and other projects.\par \par \uc2 Both the actors and writers say the shift to streaming has gutted their ability to make a living and both want better protections against the use of AI, which they say cannot replace human creativity.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the dispute, that \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2  claim they`ve -- they offered " historic pay and residual increases including protections." A quick note, some of CBS News` staff are \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  members, but we work under a different contract than actors and are not affected by the strike.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS, Sean Gunn joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Sean. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN (Actor): Hi, good morning. It`s good to be here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So let`s jump right into it. You and many actors are asking for fair residuals and that is from the streaming platform specifically. Why is that aspect important to you?\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN: Well, you know, there was an old bargaining agreement where the -- you know, you would get paid for every time that if you did a television show, every time it aired, you would get paid, based on the old agreement.\par \par \uc2 Well, streaming came along and changed everything. And all of a sudden, they started paying a licensing fee to own the product that you made in perpetuity, and they don`t pay based on how many times they will watch it. They don`t pay based on anything.\par \par \uc2 They just pay based on, you know, they make some kind of a deal with the production company. They buy the product for whatever amount of money and then they can run it as many times as they want, and this is absolutely unfair.\par \par \uc2 You know, we need to know, if we`re participating in the success of a program and if we are, we should participate in that. It`s just -- it is common sense.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, so Sean, I mean, what you bring up is a good point. It has been reported that, for example, the actors on "Friends" over the years with all of the replays on TV, because of their deals, they made millions and millions and millions of dollars each, whereas there are stories of actors on streaming shows like "Orange is the New Black," they`re in lots of episodes, it gets viewed millions of times, but they`re making pennies, if anything for those additional views. It happens to writers as well, there are stories there.\par \par \uc2 And so the question becomes, though, from the streamers perspective, if we had an executive on, I`m sure, they would say, hey, look, we`re not making any money either, so we can`t pay you if we`re not making any money. What`s your response to that?\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN: Well, first of all, we`re asking for a percentage of the profits. So, if you`re not making any money, okay, we`ll participate in that. But that is disingenuous on their part if they`re saying, hey, we`re not making any money because they won`t reveal their numbers. Why not?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN: Why won`t \uc2 Netflix\uc2  tell us how much money that they`re making you know, on their shows? Why won`t they let us know how much each show streams in a given day? They won`t tell you any of that stuff.\par \par \uc2 I can promise you that when a streamer like \uc2 Netflix\uc2  says we`re not going to release our numbers, it is because they`re screwing someone over somewhere. That`s why they do it. That`s why they tell you .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By the way, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  is making money, yes.\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN: . what the numbers are. Yes, yes, exactly. And we just want transparency. I mean, you know, tell us -- tell us what you`re making, and if you`re making less, okay, we`ll share in your pain, just like me sharing your profit, but they`re not -- they are not offering any of that.\par \par \uc2 They`re not offering us to share in their profits. They`re saying, hey, we`re going to be -- we`re going to shut you out, not tell you anything about how much money we`re making. And then we`ll, we`ll say, hey, we`re poor. You know, we`re not making enough money. It`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: We should note that we reached out to \uc2 Netflix\uc2  for comment. They declined to comment. Same thing with \uc2 Warner Bros\uc2 ., the makers of the "Gilmore Girls."\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN: Shocker.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And we haven`t heard back.\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN: Shocker that they don`t respond to comments. They don`t want it, like you guys are journalists. Ask them. If you ever get them on the phone, ask them. Why won`t you release your numbers? Why won`t you let people know how much money you`re making?\par \par \uc2 They won`t do it. It`s because they`re trying to screw people over and I`d be very worried if I was a shareholder in \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , like, oh, wait a minute, this company won`t tell me how much money they`re making. Why?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Sean, there`s another concern here and it deals with AI and certainly likeness, and the work that you`re putting out. What`s most concerning about the ability for, you know, essentially what a lot of actors, \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  members are saying could be stolen from them?\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN: Yes. You know, AI is a big, massive can of worms that everyone has just opened up, and we`re all looking at these worms.\par \par \uc2 First of all, I would say, it seems very, very clear that nobody should own your likeness in perpetuity. That seems obvious, like anybody else. You don`t have to be a Hollywood insider to know that. It`s not fair for somebody to own your likeness forever. So we should make sure that that`s not okay.\par \par \uc2 But also I think AI changes all the time and I think that -- let`s look at it every year. Let`s not sign an agreement, where we -- you know, where we decide how actors are going to be related to AI for five years plus into the future, because we don`t know where that technology is going. So, I think it would be better much better to -- yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sean, I am sorry to --\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN: To keep that short, yes, yes, go ahead. Go ahead. I know, I can talk all day. Sorry, guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say, tell us what you really think, but you really have and I appreciate your candor this morning. I did want to mention to our viewers before we say goodbye, just that, if any executive from any of the big streamers wants to come on, we`d be happy to have that interview and ask some of these questions.\par \par \uc2 And they are publicly traded companies, in many cases, I think all actually, so the overall earnings are known, but your point about the breakout for streaming is well taken and the number of views, individual shows gets that is often shrouded.\par \par \uc2 Sean Gunn, thank you for your candor, once again. Appreciate you being on.\par \par \uc2 SEAN GUNN: Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, now take a look who`s in our progressive greenroom with an outfit that matches the cover of his book. That is two- time Pulitzer Prize winning author, Colson Whitehead. He`s here to talk about that new novel. It is called "Crook Manifesto," second in a trilogy, but first time for a check if your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our next guest has been called America`s storyteller and the title fits. Colson Whitehead is his name. He is the author of 11 books now including "The Underground Railroad" and "The Nickel Boys," both of which won a prize called the Pulitzer Prize for Fiction. It`s a big one.\par \par \uc2 Whitehead is also the only writer to win that prize for consecutive novels, only ever. His latest work is "Crook Manifesto." It`s a sequel to his 2022 book, "Harlem Shuffle" and it follows a former crook, Ray Carney through 1970s Harlem, as he tries to keep his legitimate furniture store business thriving and his family happy.\par \par \uc2 Colson Whitehead joins us now, but then he`s got to get his daughter some concert tickets and things go haywire.\par \par \uc2 This is -- you grew up in Harlem, so you know this part of New York and you`re a child of the 70s. So it must have been interesting to go back in this time.\par \par \uc2 But beyond those two points, what drew you to turning "Harlem Shuffle," which was going to be one book into what`s now going to be a trilogy with this being the second?\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD ("Crook Manifesto" author): Yes, it was going to be a novel about a heist, the first book and then I kept coming up with more capers for Ray Carney, my main character, so it became three stories.\par \par \uc2 Then halfway through the first book, I was coming up with more, so maybe two books. And pretty much, if you do two, you might as well do three. That`s just the way it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: So it`s a trilogy in the 60s, 70s, and 80s following him 30 years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This book perfectly paints the duality of a couple of things. One, Harlem, and then also this character.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In the night and day.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, the night and day of Harlem, what you called a secret New York, but then also this character, him being a legit businessman, but then also a really good con man.\par \par \uc2 Was it difficult to walk that fine line between both day and night Harlem and also this character?\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: Well, it`s fun. I mean, I think all of us have our compartmentalized selves.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We do.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: A person that work, at home with our family and friends. Carney takes it to an extreme. You know, he wants to be an upstanding businessman, ascend the social economic ladder, but he has a voice in the back of his head saying, you know, let`s do some crimes.\par \par \uc2 So when we see him in this book, he`s retired from criminal activities, but wants to get tickets for -- Jackson Five tickets for his daughter, and he has to do some shenanigans to get them.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I totally love that. I was a huge Jackson Five fan in the 70s, but when you think about like this -- the theme and certainly the plotline of this book, you seem to really enjoy the heist plotline. And I`m curious, like what is it about making some bad guys so good that really feeds your soul?\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: Well, think of a heist story, you know, they are criminals, but we ally ourselves with them through the power of perspective. We see the world through their eyes, and suddenly, Tony Soprano is not so bad. Michael Corleone is not so bad, even as they`re killing people.\par \par \uc2 For me the heist -- there are two kinds of heist stories. You can pull it off like "Oceans 11" and become rich and famous or everything goes wrong. And that sort of -- you can defeat out with fate your destiny or not. I like that sort of dilemma of the heist, and it has a lot of permutations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re such a great observer of New York, not only because you grew up here, but because you`re a novelist, a Pulitzer Prize winning novelist, Genius Award, you name it.\par \par \uc2 What do you think in New York today? The state of the city. People are talking about it a lot.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: Writing about it, I mean, I love it more than ever. You know, a lot of my research was walking around in Harlem, just different streets I`ve never been on where can -- where is Ray Carney living? Where is his office? Where do you dump a body or keep a hostage for a couple of days?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You spent time thinking about that.\par \par \uc2 COLSON WHITEHEAD: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That`s a lot to think about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just for fiction.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: All right. Well, Colson, we appreciate you coming by. Great book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Looking forward to it. We`ve got to say goodbye.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Crook Manifesto" put it on the screen before we say goodbye.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It goes on sale today.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is on sale today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Get it.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That does it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230718ej7i00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For July 16, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Paul Goodloe, Chris Livesay, Danya Bacchus, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,041 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 July 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Millions around the world experience firsthand the effects of a warming planet. And most of Hollywood goes on strike, yet another blow to our economy.\par \par \uc2 A brutal and persistent heat wave has nearly a third of Americans living under a heat alert, many of them confronting temperatures that are downright dangerous. We will examine the impact on the U.S. and the rest of the world also suffering from the devastating temperatures.\par \par \uc2 Mesa, Arizona, has been particularly hard-hit. Mayor John Giles will tell us how his city is coping.\par \par \uc2 Then: President Biden is back from a trip to Eastern Europe, where he rallied America`s \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies to stand together against \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): We will not waver. We will not waver.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But he`s back home and faced with new developments from other foreign adversaries, another provocative missile test in North Korea, new tensions with Iran, and more cyberattacks out of China.\par \par \uc2 We will sort through those challenges with White House National Security Adviser Jake Sullivan and check in with the chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, Michael McCaul.\par \par \uc2 And, finally, one of America`s marquee industries, entertainment, is at a virtual standstill, as actors join writers on strike, hitting the picket line together for the first time in 60 years to demand higher pay and increased job security at a time of profound technological and social change.\par \par \uc2 We will ask media executive \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2  about the ripple effects on the broader economy. And we will hear from tech reporter Kara Swisher on the upheaval in the entertainment world, the tech industry and beyond.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot to get to this morning, but we want to begin in with some of the extreme weather we`re seeing at home and abroad. There are climate shocks across the country with flooding in some parts and a record-breaking heat wave in others.\par \par \uc2 CBS News correspondent Danya Bacchus is in Los Angeles with more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS (voice-over): In the Southwest, a heat dome is intensifying.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s like I`m sitting in a baker, and I feel like I`m going to get toasted.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Phoenix, which has been above 110 degrees for 16 days, hit a record high Saturday of 118. Las Vegas could see 117 today. And Death Valley, one of the hottest places on Earth, is on track to hit 130, the blistering heat attracting visitors.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I have never experienced this kind of heat.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: The extreme heat and dry conditions sparked multiple fires in Southern California, burning thousands of acres and prompting evacuation orders.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, beachgoers are finding it hard to cool off. Water temperatures there have been reaching above 90 degrees. Wildfires in Canada are causing smoke to once again blanket portions of the Midwest, bringing air quality alerts to Minnesota and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 On the East Coast, Vermont is in a state of emergency following flooding. Flash flooding also took the lives of three people in Bucks County, Pennsylvania, Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Southern California fire crews worked throughout the night.\par \par \uc2 And, Margaret, they remain on high alert, as the peak of this Southwest heat wave is expected to hit today.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Danya Bacchus for us from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 For a look at how the rest of the world is coping, we turn to CBS News foreign correspondent Chris Livesay, who filed this report from Rome.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Well, the heat wave is named after the three-headed dog that guards the underworld in Greek mythology. And with temperatures set to rise even further, it has its jaws firmly locked on Southern Europe.\par \par \uc2 (voice-over): People have been collapsing under the weight of the heat wave Cerberus.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: For two days, Greek officials were forced to close the ancient Acropolis during the most scorching hours to prevent further injuries.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We put on 60 SPF, so we`re good. We have our water. No complaints.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: It`s really hard. I have got asthma, so this heat is terrible for it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: In Italy, 16 cities have been placed under a red alert for the extreme heat. Some regions in the South may even exceed 120 degrees.\par \par \uc2 Surface temperatures are sizzling even hotter. In Spain, thermal imaging resembles the sun, as ground temperatures reach a blistering 140 degrees. Forest fires ripped through the Spanish island of La Palma, destroying homes and displacing hundreds, all this while East Asia is being inundated. Mudslides and torrential rain in South Korea have left dozens dead and a climbing death toll.\par \par \uc2 In one incident, a freeway tunnel was flooded, leaving many drivers trapped.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Indeed, temperatures have been rising steadily for years.\par \par \uc2 The highest temperature ever recorded in Europe was right here in Italy, just a hair under 120 degrees, only two years ago. The fear is that this year and years to come could be even worse -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Chris Livesay from Rome.\par \par \uc2 We now want to go to \uc2 Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist Paul Goodloe from Atlanta, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Paul, are we near the end of this heat streak?\par \par \uc2 PAUL GOODLOE (Meteorologist, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): Not at all, not even in Europe, also not in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 Yes, this afternoon, temperatures in Europe already upper 90s, even some temperatures above 100 degrees, and then, in the U.S., the Southeast sultry, the East sultry, stormy in the Northeast. The Southwest is broiling here, and the heat building in the Pacific Northwest, the interior as well.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s Phoenix, yet another day at or above 110 degrees. This streak started way back in June, but it really continues in earnest this month. In fact, our streak is at 16 days. The old record is 18 days. We`re at least going to have 21 days or more. And in terms of the end, it`s nowhere in sight, the forecast from this month into September likely above average, well above average in the Southwest for -- yes, at least for the rest of this summer -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re seeing reports of some kind of unusual things, like, in New England today, a warning of a tornado watch. I grew up there. I don`t remember that ever happening really before.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing the federal government and \uc2 NOAA\uc2  say that June was a record heat level, with sea temperatures the highest for any month on record. What is the global impact of all these changes?\par \par \uc2 PAUL GOODLOE: Well, it`s all about the extra energy we`re having here and changing and impacting our weather, which also is impacting our climate.\par \par \uc2 You mentioned temperatures. The waters around Florida, the sea surface waters, we`re talking not only bathwater, but hot tub water-type temperatures here. You don`t really get much relief in the water, it is so warm. And that plays into what happens in our atmosphere. The water is warming, so it`s adjusting the temperature above that water, adding more humidity.\par \par \uc2 For every one degree temperature increase, we can see 4 percent more water vapor, heavier snow events, but also heavier rain events. It doesn`t mean we still can`t have long, persistent droughts. It`s the long-term adding energy or heat in our atmosphere which is fueling wilder extremes.\par \par \uc2 And we`re seeing this also with rainfall, not only today in the Northeast, but also last week. A one in 100-year flood events doesn`t mean you only see one in 100 years. It`s the probability of any given year of 1 percent seeing that type of rainfall. But guess what? We`re seeing more of that.\par \par \uc2 In fact, more of -- days seeing two inches or more of rain have steadily increased since 1950. We`re seeing that right now with flooding in the Northeast. And, by the way, it`s not just here. Every region of the country has seen an increase in our heavy rain events, leading to more wilder flood events.\par \par \uc2 We have the concern for severe weather, including flash flooding going on, widespread flood alerts going on across the entire region, because their energy is just that supercharged. And, by the way, the two things we talked about this morning, heat and flooding, those are the two biggest weather killers.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I know things like tornadoes, even hurricanes, they have a lot of headlines, but the day-to-day occurrences of heavy rain and now the super long heat events we`re dealing with across the country and the globe are killing a lot more people -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Paul, wow. That is sobering. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to John Giles. He`s the Republican mayor of Mesa, Arizona, a city about 20 miles east of Phoenix at the epicenter of this heat wave.\par \par \uc2 Mayor Giles, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 I imagine it`s always challenging to manage a city in the middle of a desert. But with this record streak of heat, what -- what is the impact on people`s health and the community?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES (R-Mayor of Mesa, Arizona): Well, good -- good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 We absolutely are used to high temperatures in the Phoenix area and in Mesa, but -- but this is unusual. Due to some of the, I guess, global conditions that you just talked about earlier, we aren`t experiencing the normal rainstorms that we usually see this time of July in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re used to several days over 110, even -- even days in the high teens. But this is unusual because of the weeks-long duration of it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And so what is the short-term or future impact that you are planning for?\par \par \uc2 You mentioned rain.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What does that mean for your water supply?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: Well, water is actually always an issue in the desert, obviously.\par \par \uc2 And then so we -- Arizona, believe it or not, is -- really leads the nation when it comes to water conservation. This is the only place in the country where you have to prove that you have a 100-year guaranteed water supply before you can build anything. So that is always on our minds.\par \par \uc2 And we are continuing to see challenges with the Colorado River. Thankfully, we`ve got other water sources that we rely on. So we feel good about our water future, although that`s something that we`re very -- still working very hard on.\par \par \uc2 Temperatures, we do have a long-term plan for that as well. We have short- term plans. We have a lot of cooling stations. Every year, we do a big hydration donation campaign in Mesa, where we collect about several hundred thousand of these, so that we`re prepared for helping those who are outside, don`t have the resources to be inside during the hot temperatures.\par \par \uc2 And then, long term, we, of course, have climate action plans, but any climate action plan in a place like Mesa has got to take into account heat mitigation to be something that is taken seriously. So, for example, in Mesa, we have launched a campaign earlier this year to plant a million new trees in our community...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: ... to create more shade. And, also, we`re investing in transportation infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you going to have to slow development -- I mean, do you know at this point -- in terms of more construction because of the strain on infrastructure and resources? And how is the electrical grid faring?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: Electricity is good.\par \par \uc2 We have two major electric companies in the Phoenix area, \uc2 Salt River Project\uc2 , \uc2 Arizona Public Service\uc2 , Tucson Power down in Tucson. And we -- so far, we have not seen any brownouts or any hint that -- that the grid is going -- is going to be an issue or the wholesale electricity production is a problem.\par \par \uc2 And, believe it or not, the irony here is that the Phoenix area is really in a -- in a -- in an economic boom right now. Places like Mesa, we`ve got \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Google\uc2 , \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Meta\uc2  all coming in making multibillion-dollar investments, bringing a lot of great jobs. So the heat and the -- the weather, frankly, is the reason these companies are coming to Mesa and Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Hurricanes, blizzards, tornadoes, mudslides, we just don`t do that here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: So you do have to tough out, you know, some -- some -- some high temperatures in the middle of July. But, other than that, the weather is actually a bonus for us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, when you were here in Washington with us last on Face the Nation, we talked a bit about your concerns related to migration.\par \par \uc2 And you said at the time that -- that the city does get overwhelmed. You`re not right on the border, but you deal with the impact. I know Border Patrol says there has been a -- that the number of apprehensions has gone down in the past few months. Are you seeing that? And how is the extreme heat impacting people`s movement?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN GILES: Well, the heat absolutely is an issue for people who are attempting to cross the border, particularly those that are migrating from Central America, so that is something we`re very concerned about.\par \par \uc2 And the Border Patrol and humanitarian groups along the border are constantly, every day, rescuing people that are in life-threatening situations. So -- so that`s very concerning. The last time I talked with you, we were very nervous about the impacts of Title 42 and what would happen with that transition.\par \par \uc2 In the past, we`ve -- we`ve been -- every time there`s a surge at the border, communities like Mesa are heavily impacted, because our resources are called upon when the federal government`s resources are not enough. We have been so far so good, though, when it comes to the Title 42 situation. But, again, we remain very concerned and are looking for a long-term solution there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Mr. Mayor, good luck to you faring in this heat. Thanks for sharing your insights.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a minute, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The gears of America`s entertainment industry ground to a near-complete halt last week when the union that represents actors, \uc2 SAG- AFTRA\uc2 , joined writers on the picket line.\par \par \uc2 A disclosure: Some CBS News staff are \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  members, but work under a different contract than the actors and are not affected by this strike.\par \par \uc2 But to understand who is impacted here, we are joined by \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2 , a former movie studio head who`s currently the chairman of \uc2 IAC\uc2  and \uc2 Expedia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2  (Chairman and Senior Executive, \uc2 IAC\uc2  and \uc2 Expedia Group\uc2 ): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, we were trying to gauge the economic impact of this.\par \par \uc2 And, according to the \uc2 Milken Institute\uc2 , it could cause $4 billion in economic damage. What do you think the impact will be? And how long will these strikes last?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Well, the problem with this particular -- all strikes get settled. The issue for this one is -- is when, because you have almost a perfect storm here, which is, you had COVID, which sent people home to watch streaming and television and killed theaters.\par \par \uc2 You -- you`ve had the results of huge investments in streaming, which have produced all these losses for all these companies who are now kind of retrenching. So, at this moment, this kind of perfect storm, it`s OK if it gets settled in the next month.\par \par \uc2 But I will posit what happens if it doesn`t. And there doesn`t seem to be enough trust and energy to get it settled soon. What will happen is, if in fact, it doesn`t get settled until Christmas or so, then, next year, there`s not going to be many programs for anybody to watch. So, you`re going to see subscriptions get pulled, which is going to reduce the revenue of all these movie companies, television companies, the result of which is that there will be no programs.\par \par \uc2 And at just the time, strike is settled, that you want to get back up, there won`t be enough money. So this actually will have devastating effects, if it is not settled soon. And the problem with settlement in this case is, there`s no trust between the parties. There are existential issues, obviously, A.I., which...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : ... I think is just overhyped to death in terms of the worries that actors and writers are going to be replaced, rather than assisted, which is what I think will happen.\par \par \uc2 So -- but there`s no trust. You have the actors union, saying, how dare these 10 people who run these companies earn all this money...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : ... and won`t pay us, while, if you look at it on the other side, the top 10 actors get paid more than the top 10 executives.\par \par \uc2 I`m not saying either is right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Actually, everybody`s probably overpaid at the top end.\par \par \uc2 The one idea I had is to say, as a good-faith measure, both the executives and the most-paid actors should take a 25 percent pay cut to try and narrow, narrow the difference between those who get highly paid and those that don`t.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, let...\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : But I...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to talk about what you just said in terms of...\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : No, no, the other...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : The only other thing I would do, I would call for a September 1 deadline.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : There`s a strike deadline. I think there should be a settlement deadline, because unless it happens by September 1, the actions -- and, you know, of course, who cares about Hollywood? Who cares about it?\par \par \uc2 But the truth is, this is a huge business...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : ... both domestically and for world ex -- for world export.\par \par \uc2 And if it -- if these conditions...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : I`m -- like, don`t sound like I`m crying to the skies, but these conditions will potentially produce an absolute collapse of an entire industry.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, and, also, they`re all the people who are paid by the hour who work on these sets who aren`t receiving pay when things are shut down. It`s not just people in front of the camera here.\par \par \uc2 But on your point about A.I. and existential threats here, Fran Drescher, the head of the union, said: We`re all going to be in jeopardy of being replaced by machines.\par \par \uc2 That`s not the only industry worried about that. If you were running a studio right now, what restrictions would you put on generative A.I.?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Right now, overly hyped, as all revolutions that are at the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 You know, the downward consequences of the beginning of a revolution, you know, it can be the Romanovs getting shot and Marie Antoinette losing her head. But, in this case, I think the one-to-three-year period, not much is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But post that, there are, of course, all these issues. Now, by the way, these issues are not, I believe -- relative to some of the worries about replacement, I do not think you`re going to replace -- A.I.-generated actors. I don`t think you`re going to replace writers. Yes, you can ingest all this stuff and spit out something that sounds like Shakespeare, but guess what? It is not original Shakespeare.\par \par \uc2 And writers will get assisted, not replaced. Most of these actual performing crafts, I don`t think in danger -- are in danger of artificial intelligence.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the union wants some say in, I guess, crafting some guidelines on this.\par \par \uc2 I know A.I. impacts the publishing industry, because you have said that published content should be -- and somehow compensated for if it`s plugged into A.I. to program it. Are you going to seek to sue?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Yes. Well, actually, we are. A group of us, I think, are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : And it`s not that -- a group of us are.\par \par \uc2 It`s not that either \uc2 Google\uc2  or \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  that are the two real leaders of this, in terms of -- certainly, \uc2 Google\uc2  with having a monopoly on advertising. They too want to find a solution for publishers. The problem is, they also say that the fair use doctrine of the copyright law allows them to suck up all this stuff.\par \par \uc2 We, in the publishing side, do not agree with that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : It is -- it will be, long term, catastrophic if there is not a business model that allows people professionally to produce content.\par \par \uc2 That would be, I think everybody agrees, a catastrophe. The only way to get there is either legislation or litigation. I think litigation is not going to end up in some courtroom. But I think litigation will hopefully lead to sensible legislation here.\par \par \uc2 What has to happen is, copyright has to be -- copy -- unless you protect copyright, all is lost.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 So are you thinking of what -- like, the AP just came to an agreement with ChatGPT where they have to license AP`s archives of new stories in order to plug them into their model. Is that the future for publishers?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Yes. Yes, it`s probably -- yes, but that`s probably meaningless.\par \par \uc2 I mean, that is not going to solve anything. And, of course, it`s -- I understand AP and Chat -- look, ChatGPI -- what do -- GPT -- what do they want? They want to continue to suck all this stuff up. They want to, of course, say, we`re open to commercial agreements.\par \par \uc2 But, on the side of those people who are dependent upon advertising, \uc2 Google\uc2 , for instance, they say, yes, we`ll give you a revenue share. Right now, the revenue share is zero. So what percent of zero would you like today? I mean, that`s rational, but it`s not the point. The only way you get to the point is protect fair use, in other words, protect the copyright.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 Are you going to launch litigation soon? And who`s this group you referenced you`re with?\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : Well, it`s not fair for me to actually specify the group, other than -- other than it`s the leading -- it`s leading publishers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : And -- and, yes, we have to.\par \par \uc2 It -- it -- it`s not antagonistic. It`s to stake a firm place in the ground to say that you cannot ingest our material...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : ... without figuring out a business model for the future.\par \par \uc2 I will just give you one quick reference point. Twenty years ago, when the Internet began, the Internet was deemed free.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : And so everybody, all publishers said, OK, let`s put it all up for free. Everything`s great. Take it.\par \par \uc2 OK, it almost killed publishers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have got to wrap you here.\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : All right? It took 15 -- it took 15 years to get back paywalls that protected publishers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, we`re...\par \par \uc2 BARRY DILLER\uc2 : I don`t think that same thing is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2 , I love having you.\par \par \uc2 Hope to have you back soon. Got to go.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 And we are continuing a theme with podcast host and tech watcher Kara Swisher of talking about how technology is really changing so many industries.\par \par \uc2 And you, of course, you`ve watched this.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER (Host, "On With Kara Swisher" Podcast And Co-Host, "Pivot" Podcast): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you`ve also interviewed \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2  before.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, many times.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Many, many times.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Dozens of times, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I was reading an interview you did with him back in 2019 and it almost forecast some of this -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because he said Hollywood`s irrelevant.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: He did. He said -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the rise of tech companies.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, I think he said, if their - if their children had teeth - their children wouldn`t have teeth because they`re so inbred and they don`t understand what`s happening. He had some sort of colorful \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2  metaphor about that.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, he was -- he`s been very prescient (ph) and honest. And that`s the reason I`ve known him for so long because he was one of the first people to contact me from Hollywood, and one of the only. There were just two, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2  and him, about the internet back in the early days. And everyone else thought it would go away. And I kept saying, it`s not going away and it`s going to keep changing. And that`s what`s happening now. And it continues. And so Hollywood continues to struggle with the implications of technology, even at this advanced stage.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that`s the streaming and digital ways of --\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: That`s the latest.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Using the pipes to get it out to consumers. But it`s also the content makers.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Right. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s - it`s that \uc2 Apple\uc2  makes content and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: That`s right. They`re competing with people with enormous amounts of resources and money that - and that Hollywood doesn`t compare to.\par \par \uc2 Last year I interviewed \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , and he even said \uc2 Disney\uc2  was too small. Think about that. Think about the idea that \uc2 Disney\uc2 , with its parks and its other - its characters, it`s IP, has - has a hard time competing with these tech companies that are getting better and better. Whether it`s \uc2 Apple\uc2  TV or its \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . You know, obviously, \uc2 Netflix\uc2  is really, you know, really plowed the field here and it`s now back again in a very strong position, as a - as a standalone company versus a Hollywood company.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The CEO of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , got some criticism certainly from the union -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: He did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: About his comments that this was just sort of the worst possible time to have this kind of negotiation -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because all of the strain on the industry.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, I think he`s right, but they don`t like that message, right? I think he`s probably right. At the same time he earns an enormous salary, so it`s easy to point to it and say, what a greedy man, that kind of thing. And I get that. And I think it`s important for them to talk about financials. But it almost has nothing to do with what`s going on in Hollywood right now, which is the streaming - the shift to streaming, which is necessary and important, is expensive. The economics aren`t worked out. They`ve overspent. There`s enormous competition between and among all the different companies. And nobody`s figured out how to pay for people.\par \par \uc2 Now, the actors are correct, is, they should get a piece of this. And figuring out whose value -- who values and who`s valuable is going to be very hard.\par \par \uc2 And -- but there - there is a real strain on these - on these companies at this moment in time. It doesn`t mean it`s not going to change, but it certainly is one of those sort of \uc2 Rubicon\uc2  moments that Hollywood faces from time to time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I`m - and I`m guessing that the -- giving everyone a 25 percent pay cut is not something that - that proposal from \uc2 Barry Diller\uc2  is not going to go anywhere?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: No. No. No, I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: But they`re right in like, what`s their value? Whose value is what and who contributes to what? And what happens over time, when you could use AI and all kinds of things to replicate things rather easily, including writers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what I want to ask you about is AI because this isn`t just the entertainment industry, this is every industry.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: No, everything.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: People are saying, am I going to be replaced by a robot?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Right. Right. Well, not a robot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you know what I`m saying.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Maybe a robot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Maybe a robot - well, anyway.\par \par \uc2 But on that point, how is the tech industry thinking about this? Because when you hear the calls for legislation, things are not moving swiftly in Congress. They are just now really starting classified briefings on what AI is.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Right. Right. Well, they haven`t legislated the old internet yet.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Not -- not in 25 years, there`s not been one piece of legislation, not a privacy, not antitrust, not algorithmic transparency. So, I imagine they`ll get to this sometime in 2060, 2070. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 You know, they`re more - they find this more interesting and they`re actually moving a little faster because it`s a global issue. It`s a global issue and a competitive issue with China, for example, and so they`re thinking about it because of the implications of AI are much more profound. They`re more like the shift -- farming shift, the manufacturing shift, the internet shift.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: So, this is a big moment, actually. I don`t - I know Barry said it was under sung. No, it`s not. I don`t - I thought crypto --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He said that about Hollywood but then he didn`t when he was talking about publishing.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, publishing. Of course. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 I think he`s right, they`re not going to suddenly make, you know, an AI, I don`t know, Brad Pitt or something like that. He`ll - he`ll be around until his career is over essentially and be fine.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that - that there`s implications on writers and everybody. I mean someone that -- in the - in the green room just said, is AI going to kill us? And I`m like, what do you do? You know, he`s a lawyer. And I`m like, oh, yes, kind of.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Maybe.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Like a lot of what you do. But it`s going to change what you do versus say you`re a lawyer writing a contract. It can write a contract and then you can check it and it can write it faster. If you`re a PR person, it will write a press release better and quicker. If you`re in journalism, headlines faster and better and then you can pick it.\par \par \uc2 It`s a lot like using a spreadsheet.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: It`s like, you don`t use a calculator anymore, you use a spreadsheet. And you don`t think that that`s put all these accountants out of business. It just changed their work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, what does the entry of \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  announcing he has a new firm and also wants China to be -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What did he say, on team humanity to shape artificial intelligence?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Sure, China`s always really good that way. No, it`s a --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s a surveillance state.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: It`s a surveillance state. You know I think that.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think it has to be a global solution for a lot of issues because when you think about things like killer robots, yes, maybe we should all come that AI robots you need to be thinking about that and there should be global decisions made as a - as a - as a group of countries discussing what should and shouldn`t be used.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, every country is competing and Elon`s was early to AI. I`ve interviewed him many times about it a decade ago. But he was late because he - \uc2 OpenAI\uc2  and he parted ways for a variety of reasons. And then they, of course, launched ChatGPT, et cetera, and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  made the investment. So, Elon was ahead but then was behind. And so now he`s trying to be ahead again after calling for a pause.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, I remember that, a six month pause.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Oh. I know. I know. It`s so funny that he calls for a pause and then started a company.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Then - yes, and -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: And then one would imagine that was hypocritical if one was of that mind, but -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, there was no pause, right?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: There was no pause.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What is his entry - Elon Musk`s entry in -- excuse me, he`s already entered the social media space through \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , but what is the entry of Mark Zuckerberg`s new product Threads mean for \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Very bad.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Very bad for Musk?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Very bad.\par \par \uc2 First real - I mean there`s been a lot of really interesting efforts. I like them all. But this is someone who has an immediate social network that you can tap into, which is \uc2 Instagram\uc2  a very smart way to do it.\par \par \uc2 It`s a good product. They`re slowly rolling it out. It seems safer. It seems more civil. It`s a real -- Musk made - gave Zuckerberg an opening. And if they were, say, in a cage match, he would have had the stuffing knocked out of him at this moment.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see if he can recover. But I don`t - I don`t think it can because advertising is way off. He`s created a haven for white supremacists. There`s no safety on that platform. And so \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  can come in and say, oh, look, we have safety. Oh, look, we have a network. And it was really smart of Mark. And he`s - he`s sort of redefining his narrative. And hopefully he can get it right this time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Versus \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and all the criticism there.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Last time, yes. Yes, exactly. Yes, I think it`s not going to be a huge business. It`s, you know, maybe $8 billion to $10 billion for him if he turns on the advertising and - and \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s gone from $5 billion to maybe $2 billion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A 50 percent drop in revenue.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: At least.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: If not more.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Kara Swisher, it`s always great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Any time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Particularly in person -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To help us make sense of these - these shifts that change our economy -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Change our politics, change our society.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re joined now by the chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee, Texas Republican Representative Mike McCaul.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE MCCAUL (R-TX): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have a lot to get to with you, but I want to start on the National Defense Authorization Act, which passed the Republican- controlled House and it was -- it drew a lot of attention this week because of the social policy issues attached to it. So, this is a bill that pays military personnel. It does things that are necessary for national security.\par \par \uc2 Once this goes to the Senate, they`re going to chop out all those things. You know that.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It comes back to you in the House. Are you confident that Republicans can get this necessary piece of legislation through without having to turn to Democrats to help you get it over the finish line?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: You know, we saw this actually when the Democrats had the majority, they passed a very partisan, you know, NDAA bill. It went over to the Senate. You know, I`m a - we did the conference committee. And, traditionally, the more, you know, was - the more partisan minutes get stripped out.\par \par \uc2 At the end of the day this always ends up it is as a bipartisan bill. You know, but there were some -- certain policies, like, for instance, the Hyde amendment since 1980, not to fund taxpayer abortions that, you know, our members felt was very important to put in there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that`s still -\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: I think that`s one that will survive.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, are you -- you`re talking about the provision that would restrict funding to allow service members to travel -\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: And pay expenses, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it doesn`t, in any way, the Pentagon policy, fund abortions? It`s the --\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, you know, I wish they hadn`t --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Or fertility services.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, they actually kind of started this argument. And, look, this is a process. You know we had a lot of amendments. Our members needed that vote. And I do think, at the end of the day, we come together as a conference. And it will be a bipartisan bill. I think there`s nothing more important than our national defense and our military. We give the largest pay raise in 20 years. We upgrade our triad system, our nuclear capabilities, hyper sonics, a lot to counter China, and particularly in Taiwan. So, it`s vitally important we not politicize this bill at the end of the day. And I feel very confident we`ll have a bipartisan bill coming out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Because you did vote to eliminate the diversity offices at the Pentagon, to deny transgender troops coverage for hormone therapy, for this restrictions on funding for people to get reproductive services, including fertility treatments and abortions to travel. None of that, you think, ends up in the final bill --\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, because that goes against the -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because none of those things will get Democratic votes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, it does against -- since 1980 we haven`t funded anything that goes towards taxpayer, you know, abortions. I think some of the policies on culture that the - that the Defense Department has instituted has caused problems within our own military. Recruitment is at an all-time low now. After Afghanistan, and then to watch these videos that these - these trained, you know, say SEALs have to watch, you know, injecting their own social, moral policies, let`s make it about readiness and our ability to fight a war.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Well, yes. And that`s why the defense secretary said, what, one in five troops are now female. And that what he put this policy in to be able to do is for them to travel to get things that aren`t covered in the states they`re living. So, should - shouldn`t all troops, regardless of where they`re stationed, get the same treatment? Like, why penalize them for living in Texas (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, they`re free - they`re free to travel to another state to have an --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But they`d be penalized. They`d have to take time off.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: To have an abortion. Just not at taxpayer expense.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They`d have to take the time off and the like. And so that would impact their ability to do their jobs, arguably, right, if they have to - to go on leave.\par \par \uc2 But, anyway, I want to also ask you about one of the things that was in there -- not in there, but many conservative members of your caucus wanted it to, and those were restrictions on funding for Ukraine. What does that indicate about what Republicans will get over the finish line in the fall in terms of an actual supplemental to help Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: All right. Well, you know my position on Ukraine. We should have, a year ago, been putting in the weapons we`re putting in just now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: For victory, not to - just to survive.\par \par \uc2 There were several Ukraine amendments. They - they all failed. And I would say the majority of Republicans voted to support Ukraine. At the end of the day, you know, the Reagan Institute did a great - a great poll that showed that over 70 percent of Republicans support Ukraine. And I think that was reflected in our vote. Yes, we had about 70 members that voted against it, but I do think, when it comes back, you`re going to see a more bipartisan support for things like our efforts in Ukraine, particularly as we`re in the counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 To me, it`s very dangerous to have these amendments when Ukraine is in the crossfire trying to push the aggression of Russia back in the counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re talking about amendments like what Marjorie Taylor Greene was trying to attach.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Correct. Correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And Republicans did oppose this thing that they --\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: We -- we did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The conservative (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: And it failed. And I think that`s good news.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ve been pushing for longer range missile systems, like ATACMS. Do you have enough funding in this current allotment or what you can put together in the fall to continue the pace of weapons to go to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Sure. It was already appropriated in the supplemental last year. You know, the $90 billion. I mean, it`s a draw down authority.\par \par \uc2 We have ATACMS. So I have great sources on the ground and they`re telling me right - right now, because of the mines and the fortifications, that what they need -- the cluster munitions are going to help with, you know, killing Russians in the field. However, they need the longer range artilleries to hit the depots, the energy, the - the logistical supply lines. They don`t have that. And they don`t have air cover. And that`s -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: That`s really important here because the F-16s were held back so long by the administration, and the pilot training, that they don`t have what they need to win in this counteroffensive. And it`s really sad.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you quickly about Iran. You`ve been vocal in asking the administration for a briefing on the presidential envoy, Rob Malley, and why he is suspended. Do you have any promise that you`ll get it -- that you`ll get information?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Well, we sent -- I sent a letter. We were rebuked. We have been given no answer about his status. Remember, this is a top negotiator to Iran on one of the most, you know, on nuclear weapons programs --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Our highest classified secrets. So, we are giving a deadline of July 25th to have, you know, the diplomatic security and management secretary come in and brief us in a classified space.\par \par \uc2 Margaret, I can`t tell you how important this is because if he somehow, you know, worst case scenario, if he transferred intelligence and secrets to our foreign nation adversaries --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, but there`s no proof of that at this point?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: There is no proof of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: But if he did, that would be treason, in my view.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Unfortunately, I have to leave it there because we have a hard out here. But more to talk to you about, always.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MCCAUL: Thanks, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to President Biden`s national security adviser, Jake Sullivan, who we spoke with earlier about the latest Chinese cyber hack.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN (National Security Adviser): This was an intrusion actually into \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , into \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 `s cloud system. And they went in through that to get into the unclassified e-mail system of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  agencies. It was actually the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  that discovered the intrusion, alerted \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , shut it down. And we`re taking steps to make sure that that`s not a vulnerability going forward.\par \par \uc2 We have seen this kind of thing before many times over many administrations, and we take steps to try to hold the relevant actors responsible. And we`ll do so in this case.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They`re China-based actors according to \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 . Do you have any reason to dispute that?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: No, I have no reason to dispute what \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  is saying.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Treasury secretary told us that China`s decision to cut off ingredients for computer chips starting August 1st may be retaliatory for some of the actions the U.S. has taken to restrict tech sales to China. Do you see this as tit-for-tat moves here? Are we in a period of escalation despite your diplomatic outreach?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: Look, I can`t get inside the heads of the Chinese decision makers, so I`m not sure what was motivating them. What I do know is that I -- I think it`s a self-defeating move because I believe that it will only reinforce the determination of many other countries in the world to de- risk, to find ways to reduce dependencies and increase the resilience of their own supply chains, including for the kinds of critical minerals that are at issue in this particular decision.\par \par \uc2 So, from our perspective, we are being clear and transparent about the steps we`re taking. We`re not looking to end all trade with China. What we`re looking to do is have a small yard of restrictions on technology with national security implications and a high fence around that yard. That`s what we`re going to continue to do. And China, of course, will have to make its own decisions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Staying in Asia, we saw this week that North Korea appears to have taken a significant step towards an intercontinental ballistic missile that could put the U.S. within range of a nuclear weapon, potentially. Are you concerned that they will carry out another nuclear test in the coming weeks?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: I have been concerned for some time that North Korea would conduct what would be its seventh nuclear test going back multiple administrations. And I remain concerned about that. I don`t see any immediate indications that that`s going to happen, but it would not come as a surprise if North Korea moved forward with another nuclear test.\par \par \uc2 With respect to its intercontinental ballistic missile capability, this is a capability they began testing several years ago. They have continued to test it. We watch all of those tests very closely to see how it is developing. And we coordinate extremely closely with our allies, with Japan and Korea, to make sure that we are responding in lockstep to this threat.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Have you made any new diplomatic overtures since this test in order to negotiate with Pyongyang or talk to them at all?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: Not since this test, but over the course of the Biden administration we have indicated to North Korea that we`re prepared to sit down and talk without preconditions about their nuclear program. And we`ve also made clear to China that it is the United States who is ready for diplomacy and North Korea who is not.\par \par \uc2 So, from our perspective, China has a role to play here, too, given its relationship with North Korea, to indicate to the North Koreans that its continued testing is destabilizing and, frankly, is, in fact, only creating circumstances in which the United States, our allies and partners, have to step up our activities and posture to respond to the threat.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are just back from Europe, where the president was at this \uc2 NATO\uc2  summit. On the diplomatic front, though, there are other issues as well, including the expiration of what`s known as this Black Sea initiative to allow for ships carrying food leaving Ukraine to safely pass without Russia attacking them. Is there any sign from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  that he is willing to extend this?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: Look, I can`t predict what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will do. He has been all over the map with respect to this initiative over the course of the past many months. It is possible that Russia pulls out of it. It is possible they continue.\par \par \uc2 If, in fact, they pull out of it, the rest of the world will take a look at that and say that Russia has turned its back on ensuring that the countries of the global south, in Africa and Latin America and Asia can get the food they need at affordable prices. And I think that will come at an enormous diplomatic cost to Russia going forward.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a choice \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is going to have to make. We are prepared for any scenario and we`re working closely with the Ukrainians on that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Because it`s set to expire at midnight tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the promises made at \uc2 NATO\uc2 , there was this general pledge to potentially allow Ukraine to join in the future. Are you concerned that that will shape a negotiation, potentially, with Russia to end the conflict where they are incentivized to just drag this out?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: The Ukrainians are currently, as we speak, bravely and courageously pushing against the Russian lines in the south and in the east. They are inflicting enormous damage on the Russian forces. The west is working to continue to tighten the squeeze of our sanctions, hollowing out Russia`s defense industrial base, weakening its capacity to produce advanced technology. We will continue to put economic pressure on Russia and the Ukrainians will continue to put military pressure on Russia. So, I think, in the end, if Russia chooses to continue fighting in this war it will come at a grave cost to Russia and Ukraine will continue to make progress on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, we are going to make sure that Ukraine has the support it needs for as long as it takes, and that is a message that came out of the NATO summit. And, finally, yes, we said at \uc2 NATO\uc2 , very simply, Ukraine`s future is in \uc2 NATO\uc2 . We meant it. That`s not up for negotiation. That`s something that now all 31 allies have committed to.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Not up for negotiation. OK.\par \par \uc2 On Iran, before I let you go, you`ve said that the administration is trying to put Iran`s nuclear program back in the box, ultimately through some diplomatic effort. Are you close to any type of understanding on that front and any type of understanding that would allow for the four Americans to be released?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: We have tried very hard to secure the release of the four unjustly detained Americans in Iran. We have done so since the day that President Biden took office. We have had indirect contacts with Iran on this in an effort to try to get a deal that could get them released. We have not arrived at an understanding that would get them out at this point. We are continuing to work at it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And by understanding, in terms of the nuclear program, this would not be any kind of written agreement? We are not close to any kind of actual deal?\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: With respect to the nuclear program, we`re not close to any kind of a deal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: May I quickly ask you about Rob Malley, the president`s envoy. Is he coming back to the administration? We understand he`s being suspended as his security clearance is being reviewed.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN: Rob Malley has served multiple administrations faithfully and well. He is a public servant. He is a diplomat. He is engaged in high- level, high stakes diplomacy for a long time. And he`s someone who a lot of us, including myself, have deep respect for.\par \par \uc2 I can`t speak to the current circumstances. I have to refer you to the State Department on that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Jake Sullivan, thank you for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back with a lot more FACE THE NATION. So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230716ej7g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Hollywood on Strike; Dangerous Heat Wave; Brutal Heat Wave; Birth Control Breakthrough; Iowa`s Abortion Ban; Secret Service Closes on White House Cocaine Investigation; Beaches Closed Due to Bacteria Concern; Aspartame Health Questions; Tiffany Chen`s Struggle; What is Bell`s Palsy; R&B Star, Chxrry 22; Simon Pegg`s Blockbuster Return; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Errol Barnett, Danya  Bacchus, Stephanie Abrams, Janet Shamlian, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Elaine  Quijano, Roxana Saberi, Nancy Chen \par \uc2 14,235 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 July 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to \uc2 CBS\uc2  mornings. It`s Friday. You made it. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER (\uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  president): They plead poverty when giving hundreds of millions of dollars to their CEOs. It is disgusting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hollywood shuts down as actors join writers on strike for the first time in more than 60 years.\par \par \uc2 BOB IGER (\uc2 The Walt Disney Company\uc2  CEO): There`s a level of expectation that they have that is just not realistic. it will have a very, very damaging effect on the whole business.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Extreme heat could cause more wildfires in the west and there`s no relief in sight.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Southwest, record heat in the summer is dangerous heat.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And in a historic move, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  approves the nation`s first over-the-counter birth control pill.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: How big of a deal is this?\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS (UT Health Houston professor and OB-GYN): I think it`s a really big deal.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Secret Service has closed its investigation into the discovery of cocaine at the White House. Republicans are crying foul.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Marketa Vondrousova became the first unseated finalist in Wimbledon since Billie Jean King back in 1963.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s one of Steph Curry`s favorite times of the year, the American Century Championship Golf Tournament in Lake Tahoe.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: First try. Does that count as a hole-in-one, guys? I mean, technically right?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Beyonce kicked off the US leg of her world tour in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And the anchors in Philadelphia, they really got into the spirit.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s Fox 29`s Alex Holley, her backup dancers worker fellow co- hosts.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t know who they are, but they look like a fun group to me. I like that. Local anchors.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I applaud any public dancing, all right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because I don`t do much of it myself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, local anchors.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about you? You don`t want to get into your Beyonce bod?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nope, nope, nope.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You don`t want do it for the G hive?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know that it is -- very cute, Nate. I know it seems hard to believe, but I try to keep public humiliation to a minimum.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But that was good. That was good.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday. I don`t know about you, but it seemed like a very long week. So excited to be here today.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a big, big show for you today, including an exclusive conversation with martial artist, her name is Tiffany Chen -- she is Robert De Niro`s partner -- about a health issue she faced after giving birth to their adorable little girl. Now, it is something many women deal with.\par \par \uc2 We`re also going to meet the baby, Gia, that`s her name, and this will be her first appearance on national TV.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ll share our interview with actor, Simon Pegg in our final day of "Mission Impossible" week. This is his fifth movie playing field agent, Benji Dunn. We asked him about the most intense moments he has had on set, and I`ll tell you what, there have been a few of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll bet.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There sure have been, and of course, that`s an interview that happened before what we`re about to talk about, the big news today.\par \par \uc2 Hollywood on strike, a historic shutdown. Just hours ago, around 160,000 performers went on strike, walked off the job over issues that have major implications for the future of entertainment.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time that the writers and the actors have been on strike at the same time since -- in 63 years. \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  was president of the Screen Actors Guild the last time this happened, not president of America, of the Guild, and it comes after a breakdown in negotiations between the actors union, that`s \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2 , and the group representing the studios.\par \par \uc2 Errol Barnett is in New York, where of course a lot of this work gets done and a lot of that work is not happening as of this morning.\par \par \uc2 Errol, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Hey there, Tony. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 You`re right, tens of thousands of television and movie actors are expected to join rioters already on strike. This likely includes some of the favorite people that you watch on a regular basis.\par \par \uc2 We`re outside \uc2 Warner Bros. Discovery\uc2 . That`s just one of the big studios. You can see the fencing already up for the upcoming picketers.\par \par \uc2 The central issue here, the same as it is in other parts of the economy -- compensation, especially when it comes to a rapidly changing technology like artificial intelligence, and streaming platforms.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: With screenwriters and actors now both on strike.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Hollywood is at a standstill.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: We are the victims here.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  president, Fran Drescher says studios are ignoring enormous shifts in the industry and in the economy.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: You cannot change the business model as much as it has changed and not expect the contract to change, too. What are we doing moving around furniture on the Titanic? It`s crazy.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: The union`s main sticking points include limiting how artificial intelligence can be used, along with increased pay and residuals from streaming services, where \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  claims its members are missing out on millions.\par \par \uc2 MATT DAMON (Actor): We have to protect the people who are kind of on the margins.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: But the studios say streaming has affected their bottom line as well.\par \par \uc2 In the first quarter of 2023, \uc2 Disney\uc2  streaming services, which include \uc2 Disney\uc2 +, \uc2 Hulu\uc2 , and ESPN+ lost the company more than $1 billion.\par \par \uc2 BOB IGER: There`s a level of expectation that they have, that is just not realistic, and they are adding to a set of challenges that this business is already facing.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: The \uc2 Alliance of Motion Picture and Television Producers\uc2 , which bargains for Hollywood companies says in a statement that the strike: "Will lead to financial hardship for countless thousands of people who depend on the industry."\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: They plead poverty, that they`re losing money left and right, when giving hundreds of millions of dollars to their CEOs. It is disgusting.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: And one other note about why this fight over compensation is so crucial for so many in these unions, you have to earn a certain baseline of income, Gayle, to qualify for health care coverage. That`s true under the \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  benefits; and for many middle class actors, this is about their livelihoods.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, this affects so many people and so many actors are living paycheck to paycheck. Thank you very much, Errol.\par \par \uc2 Turning now to the relentless heatwave, and relentless is a word here, engulfing much of the nation. Have you ever seen the weather maps look orange and red and yellow and purple I saw the other day, and magenta, there you go, I`ve never seen anything like that.\par \par \uc2 This is a major health concern with more than 111 million people under extreme heat alerts. Parts of Arizona and Texas have had triple digit temperatures every day this month with no end in sight. Another reason this is actually dangerous, it can lead to more wildfires, especially in California.\par \par \uc2 Firefighters there on high alert as Danya Bacchus shows us.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Californians are being warned. This weekend will bring the hottest weather of the year with desert areas expected to reach a scorching 120 degrees.\par \par \uc2 This sweltering forecast also comes with this serious threat of catastrophic wildfires that thrive in these conditions.\par \par \uc2 TANNER RIGGS (California firefighter): This weekend`s heatwave is something that`s on all firemen`s minds right now. We can have brush fires any day just because it is so hot.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Tanner Riggs (sp?) has been fighting fires in California for more than a decade. He says this year, there`s even more fuel for the flames because all the vegetation that grew after and especially wet winter is turning into hot dry brush.\par \par \uc2 TANNER RIGGS: We have so much grass, it dries extremely fast and it carries fire extremely well.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Firefighters say that along with the unprecedented temps and stronger winds mean the fires are more aggressive than ever before.\par \par \uc2 2020 saw the worst wildfires in the state`s modern history. More than 4.2 million acres burned.\par \par \uc2 TANNER RIGGS: Fires are getting larger, quicker and that`s typical for a heatwave like this. I think we`re going to have more acreage burned this year.\par \par \uc2 It`s concerning to the entire county and the entire state.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: And the recent rise in temperature has caused many dry patches like this one throughout the state. Officials do their best for brush clearance, but the amount currently drying out is more than usual and difficult to maintain -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Danya, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking the heat for us.\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Nate, good morning.\par \par \uc2 If you think it`s been hot, just wait. Things really turn up this weekend in the Southwest. Saturday, records start falling in New Mexico and Arizona. Sunday, we will be close to tying our all-time record high in Vegas at 117 degrees. Death Valley is going to approach 130 with our lows around 100 degrees.\par \par \uc2 And Nate, the thing is, this type of heat is going to continue at least through next week.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Steph, quick question.\par \par \uc2 Last week we talked about it being the hottest day ever recorded. Are we still breaking records for this planet?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Yes. Preliminary daily data shows that we passed the hottest average global temperature on July 3rd, and have been above that value every day since setting a new record on July 6th.\par \par \uc2 Now we`ve been seeing this warming for months. Yesterday, \uc2 NOAA\uc2  released their June Global Climate Report and overall, the temperatures across the US this past June, they were near average, but the planet, we experienced our hottest June on record with nearly nine percent of Earth setting record high temperatures for the month.\par \par \uc2 One of the factors influencing all of this heat, the ocean temperatures. June marked the third consecutive month of record ocean waters, and not only are we seeing the warmth in the Pacific with the building of El Nino, but also over the North Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 And get this, the water temperatures near Miami are in the mid-90s, so that is going to keep the humidity up and it also could cause a significant coral bleaching event.\par \par \uc2 Now the temperatures are expected to stay above average for the next several months, and all of this human-induced warming contributes not only to more heat waves, but also more extreme weather events around the globe.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage on climate news and sustainability, you can watch Pattern on \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices. And Nate, 2023 could rank among the warmest on record continuing a trend we`ve seen over the past decade.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know the rising heat is a serious topic, but I just keep thinking about Nelly, "It`s Getting Hot In Here," Steph. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s getting hot in here, but we`re going to leave our clothes on, we promise.\par \par \uc2 Now we turn to an historic decision by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  approving the first over-the-counter sale of a birth control pill. Anyone of reproductive age will be able to get it without a prescription or parental approval. You`ll be able to buy it in pharmacies, grocery stores, and even online.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian has more on this change, this huge change in reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The daily pill called Opill which has been available in this country by prescription for 50 years could be on store shelves without a prescription by early next year.\par \par \uc2 How big of a deal is this?\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS: I think it`s a really big deal. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Young women --\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dr. Pamela Berens is an OB-GYN and professor at UT Health Houston.\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS: I think having access to birth control is really important for women and this is another way to get easier access.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: More than three-quarters of US women under age 50 in a recent survey said they wanted birth control pills available without a prescription if deemed safe and effective. They are already available over-the-counter in more than 100 countries. The move was opposed by the Catholic Medical Association.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The medication should not be over-the-counter. I think a doctor needs to be counseling and discussing this with patients if that`s what the patient is choosing.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The approval comes amid increased focus on birth control since last year`s overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  reports, of the more than six million pregnancies in the US each year, almost half are unintended.\par \par \uc2 Opill contains the hormone progestin and must be taken at the same time every day to be effective. Common side effects are irregular bleeding, headaches, dizziness, and nausea as with many birth control pills.\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS: This is a very safe option. It may not be the perfect option for many people.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: French drugmaker, Perrigo hasn`t announced a price for the pills, which are sold in 28 day packs, but says, it will make them affordable and provide Opill at no cost to those who can`t pay.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Janet Shamlian in Houston.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In Iowa, the governor plans to sign a new abortion law today banning the procedure after six weeks of pregnancy. She will do it at an evangelical forum that several Republican presidential hopefuls plan to attend, a sign that the abortion issue will play an important role in the campaign.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Des Moines.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Hey, Nate. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Good morning from Iowa where six Republican presidential hopefuls will make their case to over