to beaches.\par \par \uc2 What`s the danger if people go swimming in this water?\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBBIE GOLDSTEIN (Appointed Commissioner of the \uc2 Massachusetts Department of Public Health\uc2 ): People could get sick and many could have a mild illness, something like nausea or vomiting. Some people can have skin reactions, can even get an eye infection or an ear infection.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Nationwide, more than 1,400 beaches had at least one swim advisory or closing last year.\par \par \uc2 The head of \uc2 Massachusetts Public Health Department\uc2  says more beach closures could be the new normal.\par \par \uc2 DR. ROBBIE GOLDSTEIN: As we see increasing rainfall and increasing severe weather patterns mostly associated with climate change, we are seeing increases in the number of beach waters that need to be closed down because of contamination.\par \par \uc2 DAN DOHERTY (Kelly`s Roast Beef director of operations): I want to make a sandwich out of that.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Dan Doherty the head of operations for Kelly`s Roast Beef on Revere Beach, says the 10 beach closures so far there this summer have affected their bottom line.\par \par \uc2 DAN DOHERTY: We are off about 40 percent from last year with the closures. People aren`t coming down here to wait in line for you know, a roast beef sandwich.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: As for a long-term solution, experts say separating Massachusetts combined sewage systems have sewage is in one pipe and storm water in another would be a permanent fix -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ************************************************************************ ***** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: The entire business model has been changed by streaming, digital, AI.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment of history that is a moment of truth. If we don`t stand tall right now, we are all going to be in trouble.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We hope you like the news because there`s going to be a lot more news in your media diet because just about everything else is on strike. Those words there from Fran Drescher. She`s an actor, of course, president of the \uc2 SAG-AFTRA\uc2  union and she is talking about why actors are now joining writers on the picket lines forcing much of Hollywood to shut down production.\par \par \uc2 Other stars like Jane Fonda and Wanda Sykes, Lin-Manuel Miranda had already been protesting along with the writers in support of industry colleagues who they say are struggling just to make a living.\par \par \uc2 In response to the actors now striking, the studio representatives that`s the \uc2 AMPTP\uc2 , said in part: ". presented a deal that offered historic pay and residual increases, the union has regrettably chosen a path that will lead to financial hardship for countless thousands of people who depend on the industry."\par \par \uc2 So with the actors disputing that characterization, of course, what is really going on here, we`re joined by "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " media reporter, Nicole Sperling, who has been covering this extensively.\par \par \uc2 Nicole, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING ("The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " media reporter): Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what`s the immediate impact of both the actors and the writers on strike now?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Well, the industry was pretty much 80 percent shut down with just the writers. A lot of productions have stopped, and surprisingly, a lot of writers were able to shut down productions by picketing very aggressively and forcing studios to not want to go forward with productions.\par \par \uc2 But now, we`re done, like everything shut down. Like there is no -- and there is no promotion of projects, too, which is a really big deal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Big deal for you guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are still recovering from COVID. I mean, it`s a big deal for us now, in terms of guests. You know, I, I worry about the day that we`re just sitting here doing jazz hands because there is nobody here to talk to.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But weren`t we already behind the eighth ball because of COVID?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Yes. I mean, theater chains are still coming back from COVID. They`re still 25 percent down what they were in 2019 before the pandemic. You know, studios had ramped up aggressively so with all of their content with the streaming wars that were happening when everyone was fighting for eyeballs and subscribers, and then that shut down -- a lot of production shut down during COVID. I mean, we saw it happen.\par \par \uc2 Those all came back, but the industry is still struggling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is still not -- what do you make of Fran Drescher? You know, because you could certainly feel her passion, her commitment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was an incredible speech.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought so, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So many quotable lines there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is worth looking at everything that she said, and her anger. Was she effective, do you think?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Oh, I think she was so effective. I mean, I think she rallied those flagging writers who`ve been stuck on the picket line for the last 70 days like this may -- she may be fighting her own battle, but she really rallied troops all around the country, and you`ve got 160,000 actors that she really got going and I think she was -- she was incredible.\par \par \uc2 And she was off the cuff. There was no teleprompter she was reading from.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I saw it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And also, the writers were already out, Nicole. There has to be strength and numbers with the actors.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it true?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Yes, absolutely. I mean, now we have 170,000 people, essentially who are going up against the studios. That`s a big number, and you`re going to see a lot of really recognizable faces on the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: And you know, that`s -- that may be a two-edged sword because you have this perception of actors make a lot of money. What are they out fighting for? But you know, there`s a ton of working actors out there who are living paycheck to --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And have second jobs at grocery stores and --\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And taking side jobs,.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There are recognizable faces. And on the other side, there`s recognizable names and figures in the industry responding, \uc2 Bob Iger\uc2 , being one of those, CEO of \uc2 Disney\uc2 , which is one of the biggest producers of film and movies. He said that their demands are not realistic.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What do you make of that take? And then also, do the studios have the money?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Well, they could cut the salaries of the CEOs and I think that`s the big argument and having him on the day he gets his contract extended for two more years going out and saying their objectives are unrealistic, doesn`t really play well, with all of those people fighting for contracts because of how much money he is being paid and how much money all of these executives -- these top CEOs in Hollywood have been paid for years.\par \par \uc2 So the income inequality that this whole country is dealing with is kind of being -- the spotlight is being shined on the entertainment industry as this microcosm for what`s happening everywhere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Fran Drescher is right when she says the whole business model, it has exploded, right?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And while these media and film companies are still making money as a top line, the streaming divisions are very often in almost every case, losing lots and lots of money.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And so they`re not wrong about that either.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: They are not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So how can that -- what is a fair compensation potential model here? What are people even like theorizing could work?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: I mean, it`s still so up in the air. You know, they want gains.\par \par \uc2 What the actors and the writers want is to share in success. That`s how it used to be. You know, Fran Drescher was on "The Nanny," it`s not the world of "The Nanny" anymore. You`re not going to get 30 episodes. It is going to be syndicated and, you know, go on and have these multiple lives.\par \par \uc2 You`re in a streaming show. It runs for eight episodes, and it doesn`t go anywhere else. So it doesn`t sell to another, you know, DVD or sell to anywhere else where they can get residuals based on that.\par \par \uc2 So that model, they kind of killed their own model because you had a linear television model that then went to cable, that then went to DVD, and then you had a cable model -- like there were many bites at the apple, so everybody was sharing in the wealth and that`s not happening anymore.\par \par \uc2 And I think what surprised all of the studios was just how fast cable revenue went down and how fast linear television revenue went down, like people just stopped watching and they cut their cords. They went straight to streaming.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: And that I think has been devastating, so they are not wrong that the business model has definitely changed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which side has the upper hand here before you go?\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Well at this moment, right today, I think the hand is on the side of the actors and writers because you know the energy is there, and I think people can relate to their struggle at the moment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And there`s power in solidarity.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Meanwhile, with the news, fresh episodes daily.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, fresh episodes, yes, live and in color.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Yes. There you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There we go. It`s a great promo.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nicholas Sperling, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SPERLING: Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, why you may want to think twice before reaching for your next diet soda. We sort through New research from the World Health officials on the widely used artificial sweetener in it. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is a new warning about a popular artificial sweetener found in thousands of products from diet drinks to your gum. A group linked to the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  calls it a possible carcinogen. We are talking about aspartame. I know you`ve heard of it. You may have seen it on many ingredient labels.\par \par \uc2 But another study released in the same report says aspartame is okay when taken in limited quantities.\par \par \uc2 The Food and Drug Administration calls it safe if you don`t exceed recommended levels, and that`s why people are so confused.\par \par \uc2 So Roxana Saberi is going to sort through it all.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Two hundred times sweeter than sugar, aspartame is found in roughly 6,000 products worldwide, from diet sodas to sugar free Jell- O to chewing gum. It`s been in wide circulation since the 1980s and regulatory agencies across North America, including the \uc2 FDA\uc2  have repeatedly called it safe.\par \par \uc2 But the WHO`s cancer agency now says aspartame is possibly carcinogenic, citing limited evidence for a type of liver cancer in humans.\par \par \uc2 But according to its own hazard classification, it says the confidence level in the assessment was low, admitting more research is needed.\par \par \uc2 And a second \uc2 WHO\uc2  group, its Food Additive Committee concluded there was not enough data to suggest the current recommended daily intake of aspartame is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN KEANE (\uc2 American Beverage Association\uc2 ): That is a powerful statement and the WHO`s confidence in aspartame safety.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Kevin Keane, the interim head of the \uc2 American Beverage Association\uc2 , a lobbying group says the \uc2 WHO\uc2  report shouldn`t give consumers pause.\par \par \uc2 KEVIN KEANE: It`s unfortunate some are creating confusion about it, but the \uc2 WHO\uc2  was very clear, its Food Safety Agency was very clear that they find aspartame to be safe.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The \uc2 WHO\uc2  still says daily consumption of up to 40 milligrams for just over two pounds of a person`s body weight is acceptable.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALLISON SYLVETSKY (Nutritionist): No matter how much you weigh, you could have 20, 30, 40 diet sodas per day before you hit that amount.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: But nutritionist, Allison Sylvetsky says that doesn`t mean you should.\par \par \uc2 What should people who are trying to limit their sugar intake do?\par \par \uc2 DR. ALLISON SYLVETSKY There`s a lot of context we need to think about and so rather than thinking that choosing that diet soda is going to somehow make you healthier, right, we can make broader dietary changes to have a healthier dietary pattern.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Roxana Saberi.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Up next, the stories that you`ll be talking about today, but first it is 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is time now for "What to Watch," Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay. Nancy is here.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Happy Friday. Good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How are you?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Good to wrap up the week with you guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In for Vlad.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I am.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to start though with some sad news.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The official cause of death for Lisa Marie Presley, an autopsy report released yesterday says Elvis` only child died from complications of a small bowel obstruction. It was caused by scar tissue from bariatric weight loss surgery years ago.\par \par \uc2 The toxicology report found therapeutic levels of oxycodone and other medicine in her blood, but they were not considered contributing factors to her death. Presley was 54 when she died in January, after complaining of severe abdominal pain. She was found unresponsive in her California home.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is a surgery hundreds of thousands of Americans get, but there are risks. It`s gotten a lot safer, but unfortunately you do see situations like this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So take that pain seriously if you have it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right away get some help.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: All right, now turning to news out of the sports world.\par \par \uc2 A trailblazing Olympic gymnast says she is making a comeback.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: In 2012, Gabby Douglas .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay, Gabby.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: . became the first Black woman to win the all-around title. The last time she competed was in the Rio Olympics. That was seven years ago.\par \par \uc2 But she wrote on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  yesterday: "I wanted to find the joy again for the sport that I absolutely love doing. I know I have a huge task ahead of me and I am beyond grateful and excited to get back out on the floor."\par \par \uc2 Douglas will be 28 years old when the Summer Olympics in Paris begin next year. She joins Simone Biles, the 2016, all-around champion and signaling a return to elite competition.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is going to be something to watch.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am excited about this. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She was mesmerizing to watch back in the day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just looking at her legs, the power in the shape of her legs was just gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the grace in which she formed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. I`m excited about it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: It`ll be fun to watch. I mean 28 is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And 28 is still young, I say.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: I was going to say --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, young in life, I mean, young in gymnastics is a different question.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: You know, that being said, a 30-year-old from Italy won Olympic silver back in 2021 in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gabby for the gold.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: So you never know. We are cheering for her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we are.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We like that. All right.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And Super Bowl MVP, Patrick Mahomes is featured in a Netflix docuseries, "Quarterback." It premiered this week and Nate, you got a sit-down with the \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2  superstar. You spoke about the series as well as football, fatherhood, and being the bad guy for other team`s fans. We have a preview of your conversation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s a better feeling playing the hero at home or the villain on the road?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MAHOMES (\uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2 ): Oh man, I like to be the villain a little bit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Me, too.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MAHOMES: No, it`s a -- obviously, it`s cool and arrowhead to be the hero.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MAHOMES: And win the games and be able to hoist the AFC Championship trophy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK MAHOMES: But it`s just as fun, no offense to any my division opponents to go to their places and win those games and so it`s definitely -- both are great, but even though I had the smile, I like being the villain sometimes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: You say that like -- you asked that question like someone who`s also been the villain on the road.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And you enjoyed it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought that was --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. I enjoyed being the villain occasionally. You have to be.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because those fans are harsh, but you know, I love the fact that Patrick Mahomes opened up.\par \par \uc2 It is rare in this business where you have somebody as good as him. Patrick Mahomes is in -- he is a Hall of Famer already. He is only 27 years old.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two-time Super Bowl champion, two-time Super Bowl MVP and he was able to open up like never before, so we kind of got some unprecedented access into the mind of a truly, truly great player.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A champion.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The documentary is out now. It looks so good. Katy and I got into bed last night to watch a different show, and when I opened up Netflix, the preview came on and I press play. She got up out of the bed. I was like wait, wait just a couple more minutes, a couple -- it`s that good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is good, isn`t it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t want to see it because of the way you were talking about it. I had no desire.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Man, it is really good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, I want to see it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Usually, when I watch something about football, I turn into a football player and I`m very judgmental. I watched the entire "Quarterback" series from start to finish, and it took me into the place where I just felt like a fan watching the game and they give us footage that we have never seen before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never seen before. Oh, I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is really good. Really good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Really looking forward to that. It`s also nice to know the man behind the myth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: All of this and get to know what drives him. So I`m looking forward to that. That airs on Monday here on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And also his wife.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And his wife --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: His joined us and sat down. They talked about the ups and downs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And other quarterbacks are in series as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always like to see the wife.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, her cousin is Marcus Mariota.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Always like to see the wife.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Gayle`s exclusive interview with Tiffany Chen about an unexpected medical crisis after she had a baby with Robert De Niro.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, an exclusive conversation with Tiffany Chen. That`s Robert De Niro`s partner. She introduces us to their gorgeous little girl, talks about her struggle with a condition known as Bell`s palsy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Tony Dokoupil. Tiffany Chen is one of thousands of Americans who experienced Bell`s Palsy. We`re going to talk to an expert about what you need to know and why it`s more common among pregnant women.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. We`re also wrapping up "Mission Impossible" week with actor, Simon Pegg aka field agent, Benji Dunn. He has some amazing behind the scenes stories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The whole movie is amazing. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: An historic Hollywood shutdown as both writers and actors strike at the same time.\par \par \uc2 FRAN DRESCHER: We are being victimized by a very greedy entity.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A punishing heatwave could lead to more wildfires in California as millions face temperatures in the triple digits.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: Things really turn up this weekend in the Southwest. Saturday, records start falling in New Mexico and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  approves the first over-the-counter birth control pill.\par \par \uc2 DR. PAMELA BERENS: I think having access to birth control is really important for women.\par \par \uc2 BONNY REYNOLDS (65 years married): We come here every week.\par \par \uc2 LLOYD REYNOLDS (65 years married): Just the two of us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And a couple celebrates their 65th wedding anniversary at their weekly date night spot, \uc2 Chick-fil-A\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BONNY REYNOLDS: We`ve gotten older, but we`ve grown together\par \par \uc2 LLOYD REYNOLDS: I am still in love with her. She is a blessing in my life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the good life right there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. they are living well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that really is what it`s all about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Honesty.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Going to your favorite place.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Whatever your favorite place is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sixty-five years of marriage. That is a success story.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And having a partner that just makes you feel better and you feel better about them. I love that. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with our exclusive interview with martial artists Tiffany Chen, who is also Robert De Niro`s partner.\par \par \uc2 Back in April, the couple welcomed their first child together. Her name is Gia Virginia Chen De Niro. Soon afterward, Tiffany Chen lost all movement in her face.\par \par \uc2 Now an MRI revealed that she has Bell`s palsy. That`s a neurological disorder that causes severe muscle weakness or facial paralysis.\par \par \uc2 Only on CBS MORNINGS, Tiffany Chen told us what it`s been like to lose her smile while under very intense public scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Intern.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before Tiffany Chen and Robert De Niro started dating, Chen, a world champion martial artist played his tai chi instructor in De Niro`s 2015 movie, "The Intern."\par \par \uc2 How did he do?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN (Martial artist): I`ve had better students, but he is okay. He had a lot of other stuff to do in the movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve had better students, but he was okay.\par \par \uc2 So how do you guys -- how do you go from I`m the tai chi instructor teaching on the movie to now we`re a couple.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: It`s just -- that`s how it evolved. When we were working, it was just work, and then a couple years later, we got back in touch. And I said, oh, I guess we get along and I guess we really get along.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On April 6th, the couple welcomed their first child together. A girl named Gia Virginia Chen De Niro.\par \par \uc2 What was your pregnancy like?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: It was really easy. Really, really easy. I mean, I would joke around like during the pregnancy, you just keep getting bigger, bigger and bigger. But I was very active.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. So you`re an athlete, so you`re in very good shape.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: And I was just very in touch with my body until I had this crazy new experience where I didn`t know what was going on, but I knew something was wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She describes the first sign.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Right after delivering, I noticed I was getting a sharp pain behind my left ear like I was in the room, just after having my C- section.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did the doctors tell you it was when you said the pain behind your ear.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: They kind of just looked at me blank. They just kept saying it`s probably because of the fluid buildup after giving birth. And then when I finally could shower I remember looking at myself in the mirror and saying something definitely is looking odder, but it was all in the eyes. And I said well maybe I`m just off because I have so many meds pumped into me.\par \par \uc2 When I got home, it was like, everything was starting to just fall down on itself, like my face was melting on itself and then a week after giving birth, that was when it all hit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Chen called her doctor and was re-admitted immediately.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: I lost all facial function the minute I got into the hospital, and the one thing that threw everybody off was that it was perfectly symmetrical how everything had fallen, and how everything just wasn`t working.\par \par \uc2 One of the doctors, a couple of times, I heard the doctor say, do you think it can be Bell`s? And they said, no, Bell`s is only on one side. She is perfectly symmetrical.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you are drooping on both sides.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes. Everything fell, and the craziest thing is, you have no idea how much your face does without doing anything. I got to the point where my lower lip would fall, and even if I relaxed my face now, my look can`t fall that way. I got to a point where it was just hanging, and I`d have to hold it up. And to talk, I`d have to hold my chin and I`d have to manipulate my lower lip to make certain sounds.\par \par \uc2 I couldn`t say Bob. So it was like having a speech with my partner --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You couldn`t even say his name?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: No, I couldn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was he saying to you during this time about what was happening? Because this was not his first baby, so he has seen pregnancy and the baby --\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What was he saying about what was happening?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: He was very sweet. He tried to say that he didn`t see any difference. He didn`t see any changes, like no, he`s like -- he`s like, you look fine. He`s like, maybe you look a little stern. And I`m like, really? My whole face has melted on itself, but he was very strong, very supportive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Treatment for Bell`s palsy often includes steroids and antiviral medicine. Chen says she tried steroids, but has mostly relied on acupuncture.\par \par \uc2 Are you self-conscious about your face and --\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: The only -- it is -- you know, I am and then I`m not. I`ve never really cared too much about what I look like, but what bothered me is that I couldn`t smile at the baby at the beginning and I couldn`t -- I couldn`t give her kisses. That`s what made me self- conscious.\par \par \uc2 And then we decided, yes, we`re going to live our life and be social.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know how this goes. Bob De Niro, one of the most talented actors of our generation has a baby at 79. That`s news.\par \par \uc2 And so the first time people see you out in public, that generates a lot of conversation. How did you take that? What was your reaction to the headlines?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Some of the things they said, it takes a life -- I have pretty thick skin, so I was kind of I would see a lot of "Oh, they`re so tired," or they`re this or they`re that or they look somber, or they`re not happy, and I mean, they said those things. It was a little frustrating to see sometimes.\par \par \uc2 And then every time we would go to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, criticized you, too tired to smile, like you said that you don`t look happy. It just shows you don`t know what`s going on in people`s lives is my point.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes. Like mind your own business a bit and if you want to say something, don`t always attach what you think in your mind is the story. You don`t know us. You don`t know what happened in somebody`s life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why did you want to sit and tell your story with us today?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Because I think that this is something that is private to us all in a certain way, especially if you`ve experienced it, but it shouldn`t be kept so private, that people lock themselves in their homes and women should not feel hurt by it, ashamed by it or just scarred by it.\par \par \uc2 The thing I did worry about was, what if it didn`t get better? Was my kid going to get made fun of for having the weird-looking mom? That`s the only time like vanity comes into play. It`s how it affects the child.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people were saying all sorts of nasty things about, you know, my appearance and me being angry, and I`m just kind of like, this is like the happiest time of my life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but how did you handle the nastiness? You said it didn`t bother you. I`m surprised.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes, I was just sort of like, well, you just see how many nasty people are out there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. There is a lot.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: And then when you see how nasty people are, you just look at that, and you`re like, well, how could I be better? What can I do to be better to educate people more? And to also help people who would normally be scarred by these comments not be scarred? How can I be an ally to the people who need the ally?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And how has Gia changed your lives?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: She`s made it more fun.\par \par \uc2 It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at her hair, Tiffany.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes, she is a sun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I pick her up?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can I pick her up? Hi. Oh my God. Look over there. You`re making your TV debut on CBS MORNINGS. Yay.\par \par \uc2 She`s looking right at you. Here`s mommy over here. Here she is.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Hi, honey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There she is. Oh, she smiles. That was a smile.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: She is talking to you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a smile. That was not gassy. That`s was a smile.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: It was legit. Yes. That`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gia, what`s your favorite Robert De Niro movie? Which one do you like?\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: The one in the delivery room.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The one in the delivery room.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: Where I`m the star.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You`re the star. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Okay this my last question. You`re the only one in the room who couldn`t answer this at this particular moment The best thing about Bob De Niro is --\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: How much he loves his family.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s nice.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: That might make me a little teary-eyed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, that`s nice.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tiffany says that Bob De Niro has been very supportive of her during this time and she has also been supporting him after the death of his grandson earlier this month. The De Niro family certainly have been going through it.\par \par \uc2 He was at the interview to cheer her off camera, and of course, he was invited to take part in the interview, but he made it clear, I took a picture of him looking down at us -- he made it clear that this was her story to tell. He wanted to be there to support her, but he didn`t want to take anything away from what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 And I have to say bravo to Tiffany Chen because, you know, she is not a mouse burger girl who`s like, oh, woe is me, people are talking about me, this hurts my feelings.\par \par \uc2 She said, I really want to help other women, just to not be ashamed, that you have no control over this, and that you have to go on and live your life despite how nasty people have been to her, and I was very touched by that.\par \par \uc2 She is wearing sunglasses by the way, not because she`s vain because she said, you know, I came deliberately with no makeup. But because of this condition, it hurts her eyes. The lights hurt her eyes, so she wears these -- she wears her sunglasses.\par \par \uc2 But I think it`s -- listen, I never knew that Bell`s palsy had a connection to pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, me neither.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did not know that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she said a lot of women do, but a lot of women don`t know. And you know, I`m very appreciative that she decided to sit down with us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bravo to her, but also bravo to you and the \uc2 CBS\uc2  team. That was a little bit of an emotional roller coaster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re talking about this disorder, so right out the gate, you grab our attention, and then you go from marriage to their relationship and of course you pull out a baby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, you`re really pulling at our heartstrings there but, bravo, that was well done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She is a great piece.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, and she is a martial arts champion. She`s won many medals, gold medals. You know, she`s very accomplished.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And she is also funny. She is also funny/\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is hilarious.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, hilarious.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was your favorite -- how was Bob as a student? Not my best, but he did okay. I think that`s funny.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is really terrific.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of course, we`ve really just scratched the surface here about the condition, Bell`s palsy. There`s a lot more to learn, so what we`re going to do next is talk to a top expert who is the founder of the Facial Paralysis Institute, that is coming up in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Doctor.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:11, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, actor, Simon Pegg was full of great stories about the "Mission Impossible" series when we spoke to him.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`ll tell us what it was like working on the set of the new film and working with legendary, Tom Cruise.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: You don`t realize how much your lips do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: You have absolutely no idea. You just think it`s your teeth.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I feel like that after I have Novocain at the dentist.\par \par \uc2 TIFFANY CHEN: That`s kind of -- that`s exactly what this feeling was like you have. You`re biting, but your lips actually pull the food into your mouth and I couldn`t pull that way I could just bite.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We just heard Tiffany Chen, a martial artist and partner of Robert De Niro discussing her ongoing struggle with Bell`s palsy.\par \par \uc2 Now it started after their daughter was born back in April. Bell`s palsy is a rare condition affecting about 40,000 people in this country every year, but pregnant women appear to be at three times greater risk for Bell`s, especially in their third trimester or right after giving birth, which is what happened to Tiffany.\par \par \uc2 The cause is unknown.\par \par \uc2 With us now is Dr. Babak Azizzadeh. He`s founder and director of the facial paralysis Institute and joins us at the table.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much because I know this is your specialty. You got on a redeye from California and came here this morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH (Facial Paralysis Institute founder and director): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, for Tiffany, this was a complete surprise to her that this could happen after pregnancy. But it`s more common than most people know, isn`t it? I applaud her for speaking out.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: Yes, it happens all the time. And patients, because they recover most of the time, not all the time, you know, they`re treated and they go on, but it`s a devastating process until the recovery takes place and not everyone recovers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not everyone recovers?\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: No --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because she told us that she feels -- she has been told that she has another two to three months.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: Yes, about 80 percent of people go back to exact normal smile function activities, but about 20 percent of individuals will develop something called synkinesis, where their facial movements are disorganized. They get narrowing of the eyes, they get dysfunction of the smile, not exactly like how it was at the beginning, but long- term problems.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow, how is it treated? How do you go about trying to fix the issue?\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: So initially, it`s critical to get treatment right away. Steroids have been shown to really reduce the risk of long-term problems.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The minute you feel or see something, you say?\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: The minute you see your face kind of not moving correctly, you`ve got to go to the emergency room or urgent care.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really, the ER.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So right away.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: Right away, because studies have shown that very early treatment reduces the long-term problems.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The doctors told her that she probably had fluid buildup, so no one thought you better do something about that. She said the thing that was life changing for her was acupuncture.\par \par \uc2 The minute she had her first acupuncture session, she noticed a difference immediately. Do doctors not know, she is told fluid buildup.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: Yes, not many doctors are actually experts in Bell`s palsy, number one; and number two, she had a unique situation, both sides of her face was paralyzed. So as a result, doctors couldn`t see a lot of difference between the two sides. So it was very, very difficult to diagnose her.\par \par \uc2 And as a result, she got delayed treatment until she got her MRI, and so forth.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle mentioned acupuncture and of course, there`s treatment if you`re seen by a doctor. Is there anything else you might recommend?\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: Acupuncture, actually, without electrical stimulation is fantastic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: There aren`t a lot of Western studies looking at it, but it`s been used for a millennia. Long term, if patients do not get recovery, we`ve pioneered surgeries and treatments with Botox, physiotherapy, and surgeries that can actually help patients really recover well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you find most women don`t talk about this, that they`re suffering in silence with it?\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: Huge, because it`s embarrassing, right? And people go -- they stop smiling and they get depressed because smiling, you know, releases endorphins and makes us happy. And when you stop smiling, you go down and go into major psychosocial issues.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Babak Azizzadeh, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re flying right back to Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: No, I have one day in New York.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, enjoy yourself.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. BABAK AZIZZADEH: I can`t come here and leave right away.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, our conversation with actor, Simon Pegg about his role in the new "Mission Impossible" movie, plus a "Talk of the Table" to remember, a textbook Florida story for you, it involves the phrase`s longest ever and python.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you`re a Florida boy, you could say it. You can talk about Florida.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My shirtless and shoeless years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait for this one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . for this in just a moment, but first local news .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stick around.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ************************************************************************ ***** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we are walking on sunshine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sunshine, always.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And do you have something from the Sunshine State?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: State? There is a lot of sunshine in Florida and I`m a Florida boy made good, and I`m very proud to introduce the following video.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Florida boy made good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just wilds video of some Florida boys, Florida men, I should say, snake hunters in the Sunshine State catching the longest Burmese python ever measured, not just in Florida, ever measured, period, full stop.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nineteen feet long, almost two-story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Say that again? Say it again?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nineteen-feet, snake story. One of the veteran hunters called the capture a crazy, insane, very chaotic. These are the words, and this guy has caught a lot of snakes in his life.\par \par \uc2 He said it was the first snake that scared him so much he didn`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So he kept going.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @GladeBoys/\uc2 Instagram\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A couple of Florida details to notice here. Jake Waleri here -- Jake the Python Hunter, he is 22 years old. He`s a college student.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, my. He is 22. Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Note the fact that most of us wear gloves just to do some yard work. He`s catching snakes bare hand.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bare handed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bare handed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Raw dog.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He has just got a rubber boots on. This is in the Big Cypress Natural Wildlife Reserves.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And why are they doing this, Tony? For what?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, there is actually a great cause here. The state of Florida is happy and thanks them for their service because the python is an invasive species. It came into Florida about the same time I did in the late 70s, early 80s and has been multiplying.\par \par \uc2 So not far from where this capture was made in the Everglades, they found -- another group found a nest with 111 python eggs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It gives me the creeps looking at that, Tony. The creeps.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, better call him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll go next.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ve got something a little nicer than a python. My "Talk of the Table" is supermodel, Karlie Kloss, her husband, Josh Kushner welcoming baby number two. Dad shared a picture of the new baby on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  saying: "Welcome to the world." We don`t know there`s their son`s name. Look at -- I love that picture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Isn`t that nice? The former Victoria`s Secret angel revealed she was expecting again on the Met Gala`s red carpet back in May. She joked that, listen, I brought a plus one -- Kloss and Josh are already parents to a two-year-old son, his name is Levi Joseph. He seemed very excited when he found out he was going to be a big brother.\par \par \uc2 Very happy for these two.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes,. No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a great thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Josh and Karlie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So my "Talk of the Table" I`m sharing my "Mixtape Moment" of the week. It is our musical series, where we each take turns to share a song from an emerging artist.\par \par \uc2 This morning, I want to tell you about rising R&B star, Chxrry 22. Love the name.\par \par \uc2 The artist with Ethiopian roots fell in love with music at a young age, but it wasn`t until her videos went viral on social media that her career really began to take off.\par \par \uc2 She went on to become the first woman to sign to XO Records, that`s a label headed by "The Weeknd." We all know him.\par \par \uc2 Her latest single is "Worlds Away." Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Worlds Away."(\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the vibe right there. The song is about long- distance relationships and the roller coaster of emotions that goes with it. Chxrry 22 says her lyrics come from the heart.\par \par \uc2 To hear more from Chxrry 22 and other songs and artists that we feature on our \uc2 Spotify\uc2  playlist, just search \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Mixtape on \uc2 Spotify\uc2  or scan the QR code on your screen. We`d love to dance and vibe with music, so check out our playlist.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. She looks good. She sounds good. She`s got a playfulness in that video that`s -- I see why "The Weeknd" said hey, I want you on my label.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I get it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s great. Quite a "Mixtape Moment."\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up, our interview with "Mission Impossible" star, Simon Pegg about returning to the blockbuster series for the fifth time.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Plus, his favorite memories from working with Tom Cruise. I`ve heard of him, but first time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All week, we`ve been working the release of "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One" with many of the film stars including one of the franchise`s most popular characters.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, Tony and I spoke to Simon Pegg who played Ethan Hunt`s right- hand man, Benji Dunn in five Mission Impossible movies.\par \par \uc2 Here is our interview with him starting with a clip of Benji leading Tom Cruise`s Ethan into one of the most exciting and dangerous action scenes of the entire series.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Mission Impossible" is from \uc2 Paramount Pictures\uc2 , part of the same company as \uc2 CBS\uc2  and the legendary Simon Pegg joins us right now. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG ("Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One" actor): Hi, fellas. I`m really good. I`m very, very happy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good. Thank you for --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You survived your very bad day there you were having, your character, Benji.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: I was having an okay day there with Tom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt it. And that scene comes right before Tom`s big motorcycle jump.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Take us on the set that day. Was it as chaotic as it seemed?\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: It actually it was the opposite of chaos.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Tom is very, very diligent when it comes to preparing for these things. He`s not crazy. Everyone says, oh, you must be crazy he does, you know -- but he trains hard. Everything is in place. It`s a well-oiled machine.\par \par \uc2 We were up there just to see -- I was doing my off lines for the bit when he comes to the edge, and then we stayed up to watch it happen.\par \par \uc2 And it was day one, you know, day one of the shoot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Day one.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: And you could either see that as sort of like starting as we mean to go on, you know, set the standard, or just in case it doesn`t work, we don`t have to shoot a whole load of the film and then stop.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is your friend and your colleague.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Many years now.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was it like seeing him go?\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: It was scary. We were all a bit hysterical, because, I mean, we trust him and we trust the system. We trust everything involved. It`s not like, you know, the sort of uncertainty is minimal.\par \par \uc2 But still, it`s nerve wracking, and he would disappear, and then we`d wait, and there will be a big sort of pause, and then you`d hear "good canopy," which meant his chute had opened at least.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Still had to get down to the bottom. So it was -- yes, it was exhilarating to say the least.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You actually did pull out your phone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To shoot the stunt --\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: I did. Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So what was going through your mind as he was toppling over?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Simon Pegg.)\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: I was -- I was looking at movie history being made.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It was movie history, right?\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: This is the biggest stunt not only in the franchise, but one of the biggest stunts in movie history, you know? And so I thought I better capture this for posterity, and --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And in case the other cameras fail, we have one shot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We got it. We`re good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You got it on the iPhone.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Don`t worry, guys. I got it. I got it on the phone. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is great. Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 So you were -- you came into the "Mission Impossible" franchise at MI4 or --\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: MI3.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: MI3.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s six, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. That`s a long time ago.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes, yes. It was.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible III.")\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: And he was a kind of lab technician at the time, and JJ Abrams had called me and I thought it was a nice little cameo, and I was going to have a little -- you know, I`d go in Hollywood and then I`d go home to my dogs and everything will go back to normal, but here we are five films later.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Now, I know you`re working with a script. But then you have these scenes, like there`s one where you`re dismantling a bomb while like tossing off one liners to your two buddies.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: High stakes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: High stakes.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How much of that is predetermined and you`re just delivering the line? And how much room is there for you to just have fun? Because you know these people at this point.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Oh, yes. Well, Chris McQuarrie is always really keen for us to invest ourselves in the characters and to throw stuff in. And he`s an Oscar-winning writer, so the script is amazing. But there`s always room in the moment to kind of chuck in little asides.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I mean, you never know what you`d be doing, what you`d be saying when you`re diffusing an artificially intelligent nuclear bomb. So in the moment, things come up, you know that you throw in.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I want to ask you about the villain because it`s a little unusual villain in this movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How would you describe?\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Well, when I was told about the idea of this entity, this sort of self-aware kind of computer program that the Esai Morales, our brilliant, sort of like human villain is working at the behest of, I thought that sounds very "Mission Impossible." It`s a bit sci-fi, mission has always been about new technology.\par \par \uc2 But in the time, since we started shooting to now, the notion of AI has become more and more prominent in our sort of social discussions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: And it feels incredibly prescient now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Speaking of existential threats, potential "Mission Impossibles." You`re on strike right now with the writers.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s a possibility that the actors may go on strike.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In not too distant future. These things need to be worked out or there is no more Hollywood, there is no more entertainment.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What are your thoughts in general on the strikes?\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Well, things need to be fair, you know? Things need to be equitable and the infrastructure needs to be adapted to accommodate the new ways we watch film and TV, streaming.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, the tech companies need to understand that for things to continue, everyone needs to be treated fairly. And as soon as that gets sorted out, and it will do hopefully, things will be okay.\par \par \uc2 But we can`t face another content drought next year, you know? We`ve only just got back on our feet where everyone is only just back in the cinema. If everyone next year is ready to go and there`s nothing to watch, that`s going to be a terrible thing.\par \par \uc2 So we need to just talk sensibly and get it all sorted out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m curious to get your opinion on movie making, filmmaking and also artificial intelligence. You know, when we keep it in the back of our mind what`s going on with the writers.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: The only thing AI can do is create mediocrity, because all it can do is aggregate what`s out there. So yes, it can write a script, but it will be rubbish, do you know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 AI has had no childhood trauma. AI has never had a boyfriend or a girlfriend, never got heartbroken.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re spot on.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: It`s never been through anything the impetus to create art. When it has its own personality and its own experiences, it can write a film about what it`s like to have no body, but at the moment, it can`t do that.\par \par \uc2 And so to rely on it would be to just make everything mediocre and we have to fight mediocrity in order to create great art. I know that sounds lofty.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, no, no that might be one of the best answers I`ve ever heard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s the idea of AI. Hey, Simon Pegg, we appreciate you joining us.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Pleasure, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re one of the most versatile talents in the business.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good luck moving forward.\par \par \uc2 SIMON PEGG: Lovely to be here. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One" is in theaters now.\par \par \uc2 Go see it. Run, don`t walk. We`ll be right back with one more surprise. Stay right there.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before we go we always like it when babies stop by the studio especially if there are babies. Ramy Inocencio, introduce your family.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Gayle, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I swear they were quiet two seconds ago. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They were comfortably wrapped up.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: So, this is Ian and Ethan here, in from London.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, come on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Can we help? Come on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here now Uncle Nate and Uncle Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got these --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You didn`t introduce your husband.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come on. It is okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Anyways.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How old are they?\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: A year-and-a-half, twins. We did fertility and surrogacy, December 24, 2021.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, what did you do?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, no. Oh, no.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: So great to see you guys, I think.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ramy, how do you look so sharp? You`re looking dapper.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: You`re going to have papa later on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jon Tower just said, we can go here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can do better. Give me another chance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome, welcome. It is really good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s cut that again, right? This is not live, is it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good to see you in person, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We will see you Monday on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 This was a great idea 30 seconds ago.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s why I got a vasectomy. No more babies for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before we go, let`s take a look at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend. See you Monday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Bye.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You just can`t handle it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The extreme weather all across the country, nearly 90 million Americans under heat advisories with triple digit temperatures all the way from the Pacific to the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: There is not going to be any break for folks here in the Southwest. It is expected to be hotter this weekend.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Millions from Pennsylvania to New Hampshire we`re left in the path of dangerous flooding.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: This is what`s left of Route 218. Behind me, you can still hear the sound of the sound of the water rushing down this cliff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a dream run at Wimbledon for upstart, American tennis star, Christopher Eubanks.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER EUBANKS (Tennis player): The coolest thing about is when I checked my ESPN alerts for the past week, a lot of times my name was on it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: People are still throwing things at concerts. This time, Harry Styles got hit in the eye on Sunday at his show in Vienna, Austria.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What happened to the good old days when people were throwing garments on stage?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I think that still happens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: By garments, you mean panties and bras.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I didn`t say it, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Yes, what happened to underwear --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You threw your panties on stage before? You didn`t answer the question, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I refuse to answer on the grounds of potentially incriminating --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But she did agree to answer some questions because --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, hey.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, hey. I`ve been waiting for it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARVEY ("Family Feud" host): Good or bad, give me any L word you called your man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Lamb chop.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARVEY: Lamb chop.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Well, you will always do my lamb chop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it was great fun. It was great fun.\par \par \uc2 BEN PLATT (Actor): It is a very tiny indie film with 19 days to shoot and children were shooting upstate and there`s goose poop everywhere, so like it`s the fact that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is what? What? Goose --\par \par \uc2 MOLLY GORDON (Actor): Goose poop.\par \par \uc2 BEN PLATT: There was a lot of goose poop.\par \par \uc2 NOAH GALVIN (Actor): There was a lot of goose poop. There was like a lot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it. Got it.\par \par \uc2 MOLLY GORDON: I am sure "Mission Impossible" have someone to clean up the goose poop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Duh-duh-duh-duh --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s back and this is "Mission Impossible" week. Are you a good guy or a bad guy?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How would you describe yourself in this movie?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA KIRBY (Actor): A bit of both.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 VANESSA KIRBY: I mean, I was preferring the bad guy. He presented me this beautiful box of chocolate like out of nowhere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Tom Cruise is doing these stunts with chocolate in his pocket?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where it`s your heart and mind at that point?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER MCQUARRIE ("Mission Impossible - Dead Reckoning Part One" director): It is very much an out of body experience. It`s life and death.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: And Gayle, you also --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he appreciates the fans.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: You do your own stunts here. I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, walking from here to there, it is really tough for me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When it comes to thick, is it okay for guys to call the woman thick, like If a guy says you know she`s thicker than a Snicker, or she is thicker than a pot of cold oatmeal.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE BUTEAU (Actor): What in the Family Feud is going on?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is thicker than a first grade pencil. You know what I am saying like --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE BUTEAU: Because big women are beautiful.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. That`s right. Damn right.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE BUTEAU: You didn`t agree. I am just kidding.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m still in the thicker than a first grade pencil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There it is. There it is. There it is. You`re learning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got to end on a positive note.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hold on. Hold on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come on out here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Get her in here. If we get you off to a good start in the morning, it is only because she gets us off to a good start in the morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we heard you like sweet potato pie.\par \par \uc2 SHAWNA THOMAS: I love sweet potato pie. You know what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Make a wish.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Birthday, Shawna.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may  not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or  broadcast without the prior written permission of VIQ Media  Transcription, Inc. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright  or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230714ej7e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For June 25, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Debora Patta, John Sullivan, David Martin, Ian Lee, Margaret  Brennan \par \uc2 8,341 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: A dangerous drama unfolds in Russia, as a mercenary group fighting the Ukrainians turns on \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his government. In a breathtakingly bizarre twist to the war in Ukraine this weekend, what could have been a disastrous armed conflict inside Russia, the country considered America`s top nuclear adversary, came to a sudden halt late Saturday.\par \par \uc2 The crisis has cooled for now. But what will the impact of the uprising be on Vladimir Putin`s already weakened hold over a country struggling in its war against Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 Here in Washington, intelligence agencies have been quietly monitoring the escalating tensions between the Wagner mercenary group and the Putin government for some time and spent the weekend nervously watching events in Russia unfold.\par \par \uc2 We will hear from Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  and the head of the House Intelligence Committee, Mike Turner.\par \par \uc2 And one year after the end of Roe v. Wade, the march to ban abortion in some states continues.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (R-Presidential Candidate): We will never rest and never relent until we restore the sanctity of life to the center of American law in every state in the land.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: As do efforts to motivate voters to fight for the right to choose an abortion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The court was betting that all of us would remain silent. Well, we`re not. We will not remain silent.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will talk with Biden campaign surrogate and Texas Congresswoman Veronica Escobar.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the new head of the \uc2 United Nations World Food Program\uc2 , Cindy McCain, joins us to talk about where the hunger crisis is worst and why we need to do more.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 There is a lot we don`t know yet about the motivations behind and the consequences following Wagner Group leader Yevgeny Prigozhin`s actions in Russia this weekend. What we do know about what one senior administration official calls a very bizarre episode is that there seems to be a pause, at least for now, due to a truce struck between \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and Prigozhin that was brokered by the president of Belarus, a Putin ally.\par \par \uc2 The terms of that deal are still emerging. But even after Prigozhin abruptly stopped his march to Moscow yesterday, there remains trepidation about the risks ahead on this unpredictable path for Russia, which holds the world`s largest nuclear arsenal.\par \par \uc2 CBS News correspondent Ian Lee reports from Dnipro, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE (voice-over): Rarely do failed mutineers receive a heroes` departure.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Wagner!\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: But residents cheered Wagner soldiers as they left the Russian city of Rostov late last night.\par \par \uc2 "Come back alive and take care of yourself," shouted people in the crowd. The mercenary group`s leader, \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2 , smiled and posed for selfies on his way out.\par \par \uc2 Some greeted the returning Russian authorities less warmly, with cheers and insults. For 24 hours, the world watched Wagner forces inch closer to Moscow. Updates of their advance splashed across social media. The Russian military secured the capital, soldiers deployed on the streets, and heavy equipment ripped up roads leading to the city.\par \par \uc2 Prigozhin launched his mutiny after accusing the Russian military of killing his men in Ukraine with a missile strike, a claim Russian defense officials deny. His men quickly seized Rostov, home of Russia`s southern military headquarters, before advancing toward Moscow.\par \par \uc2 On Russian state TV, President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  accused Prigozhin of treason, though he never mentioned his name, and called for unity.\par \par \uc2 (PRESIDENT VLADIMIR PUTIN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: "We will protect our people and country from any threats, including internal betrayal," said Putin, "and what we`re facing is precisely betrayal."\par \par \uc2 And with Wagner troops just 124 miles from Moscow, the Kremlin spokesman announced a deal. Wagner troops would be pardoned and returned to their bases, criminal charges against Prigozhin would be dropped, and he`d go into exile in Belarus, whose leader brokered the agreement.\par \par \uc2 This morning, an uneasy calm settled on Moscow. Life in Rostov also returned to normal. Putin`s hold on power survived, battered and bruised, but not all may be forgiven. In a 2018 interview, Putin told a state TV reporter there`s one thing he can never forgive, betrayal, he said.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: Russia continued airstrikes on several cities here in Ukraine, while Kyiv took advantage of the chaos to launch major assaults.\par \par \uc2 But it`ll take time to see what effect this mutiny has on the war -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ian Lee in Ukraine, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  is one of the many top Biden administration officials who has been monitoring the events of the last two days.\par \par \uc2 And he joins us from the State Department.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Mr. Secretary.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (U.S. Secretary of State): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you tell us who in the Biden administration has been in touch with Russian leadership?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Well, I instructed my own team, at the president`s behest, to engage with the Russians, first and foremost, to make sure that they understood their responsibilities, in terms of protecting our own personnel, ensuring their safety and well-being, as well as any American citizens in Russia.\par \par \uc2 So, a number of people have engaged to make sure that the Russians got that message.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is the U.S. ready for further unrest in Russia and the scenario that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  does not remain in power?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Margaret, this is an unfolding story.\par \par \uc2 And I think we`re in the midst of a moving picture. We haven`t seen -- we haven`t seen the last act. We`re watching it very closely and carefully.\par \par \uc2 But just step back for a second and put this in -- in context. Sixteen months ago, Russian forces were on the doorstep of Kyiv in Ukraine, thinking they`d take the city in a matter of days, thinking they would erase Ukraine from the map as an independent country.\par \par \uc2 Now, over this weekend, they`ve had to defend Moscow, Russia`s capital, against mercenaries of Putin`s own making. Prigozhin himself in this entire incident has raised profound questions about the very premises for Russia`s aggression against Ukraine in the first place, saying that Ukraine or \uc2 NATO\uc2  did not pose a threat to Russia, which is part of Putin`s narrative.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: And it was a direct challenge to Putin`s authority.\par \par \uc2 So this raises profound questions. It -- it shows real cracks. We can`t speculate or know exactly where that`s going to go. We do know that Putin has a lot more to answer for in the -- in the weeks and months ahead.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But is the U.S. prepared for the potential of the fall of the Putin government? And is their nuclear stockpile, the largest in the world, secure?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: We always prepare for every contingency.\par \par \uc2 In terms of what happens in Russia, it`s an internal matter for the Russians to figure out. Of course, when we`re dealing with a major power, and especially a major power that has nuclear weapons, that`s something that`s of concern, something we`re very focused on.\par \par \uc2 We haven`t seen any change in Russia`s nuclear posture. There hasn`t been any change in ours, but it`s something we`re going to watch very, very carefully.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is appearing on television this morning. But it appears to have been prerecorded.\par \par \uc2 Do you know the whereabouts of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  right now? Is he in Moscow?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: I don`t want to -- I don`t want to speculate on that or what information that we have.\par \par \uc2 Again, we`re watching that -- that carefully. I think one of the things this -- this tells you is that we still don`t -- don`t have finality in terms of what was actually agreed between Prigozhin and Putin. I suspect that we`re going to learn more in the days and weeks ahead about what -- what deal they struck.\par \par \uc2 The president brought together not only the national security Cabinet yesterday. He brought together the leaders of our key allies and partners. He instructed all of us to do the same. We have tremendous unity of purpose and unity of action when it comes to supporting Ukraine. And that`s where our focus is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, as you just said, Prigozhin drew into question the very premise for Vladimir Putin`s war.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, do the Wagner fighters return to the fight in Ukraine? Do we know?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Too soon to tell what`s going to happen to the Wagner forces, whether they go back to the fight.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was extraordinary that they were moving out of Ukraine and into Russia. But it`s too soon to tell whether they`re going to go back into the fight as Wagner, whether they get integrated into regular Russian forces, what this means for Wagner in other parts of the world.\par \par \uc2 I mean, keep in mind...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: ... both Putin and Prigozhin are responsible for committing terrible acts in Ukraine against Ukrainian civilians.\par \par \uc2 But also, in the case of Wagner, in country after country in Africa, wherever Wagner is, death and destruction and exploitation follow.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: But all of this is likely to unroll in the in the coming days, in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 To the extent that it presents a real distraction for -- for Putin, and for Russian authorities, that they have to look at -- sort of mind their rear, even as they`re trying to deal with the counteroffensive in Ukraine, I think that creates even greater openings for the Ukrainians to do well on the ground.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, as you just indicated, \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  has a footprint that goes from Africa to Syria to Ukraine. Do you have any idea where he is right now?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Again, I can`t get into what we know or don`t know through -- through -- through intelligence. It`s something that we`re looking at and that we`re tracking.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: One of the things Prigozhin did was directly undermine the Russian military leadership.\par \par \uc2 Do we know who is in charge of the Russian military right now? And how could \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  agree to any changes in the leadership of his military and still look like he`s in charge?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Now, those are -- those are great questions. And I think we`ll get the answers in the -- in the days and weeks ahead. It`s too soon to say with any -- any certainty what the final chapter in this particular book is going to be.\par \par \uc2 The -- the rising storm of Prigozhin inside of Russia is something that many people have seen over -- over months now, direct challenges to the leadership, to the military leadership, powerful criticism of Russia`s conduct of its aggression against Ukraine, and now questioning the very premises of the -- the war, Prigozhin himself saying that Ukraine and \uc2 NATO\uc2  did not pose a threat to Russia, which has...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: ... as you know, been part of Putin`s narrative.\par \par \uc2 These create more cracks in the Russian facade. And those cracks were already profound economically, militarily, its standing in the world. All of those things have been dramatically diminished by Putin`s aggression against Ukraine. He`s managed to bring Europe together. He`s managed to bring \uc2 NATO\uc2  together. He`s managed to get Europe to move off of Russian energy.\par \par \uc2 He`s managed to alienate Ukrainians and unite Ukraine at the same time. So, across the board, this has been a strategic failure. Now, you introduce into that profound internal divisions, and there are lots of questions he`s going to have to answer in the weeks ahead.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is there a possibility of civil war?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: I don`t want to speculate on that. It -- these are fundamentally internal matters for the Russians to -- to figure out. It`s not our place to do that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Will President Biden reach out directly to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ? Has the CIA director reached out to Russian intelligence?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Margaret, I`m not going to get into any diplomatic contacts that we -- we may have or have had.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you that, on my instruction, on the president`s instruction, we had some engagement with the Russians over the weekend to make sure they understood their responsibilities when it comes to looking out for the safety and security of our personnel in Russia. Very important that we do that, and we did that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about Beijing.\par \par \uc2 I was there with you earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I listened to you pick every single one of your words very carefully. And then, on our way home, President Biden called Xi Jinping a dictator with economic problems who didn`t know what his own military was doing by flying the spy balloon over the United States.\par \par \uc2 How much did that hurt the work you did?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Margaret, one of the things that I think you -- you heard me say during the trip and after the trip is that the main purpose was to bring some greater stability to the relationship.\par \par \uc2 But one of the things that I said to Chinese counterparts during this trip was that we are going to continue to do things and say things that you don`t like, just as you`re no doubt going to continue to do and say things that we don`t like. And if you look at what comes out of the \uc2 Chinese Foreign Ministry\uc2 ...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you saying that was a strategic remark?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: ... on a daily basis, you`ll hear that.\par \par \uc2 The -- the president always speaks candidly. He speaks directly. He speaks clearly, and he speaks for all of us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You also said that Chinese officials assured you they won`t provide legal assistance to Russia, but that Chinese companies are.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2 , Chinese companies have also done business with the Wagner group. Have you reached out to the Chinese about trying to gauge what is happening on the ground inside Russia now?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Again, I can`t get into any diplomatic contacts that we may or may not have had.\par \par \uc2 But you`re exactly right that, when it comes to the visit, the Chinese did reiterate to us, as well as to many other countries, that they have not and will not provide lethal military assistance to Russia for use in Ukraine. I also raised the concerns that you said about Chinese companies providing that kind of support and pressed them to be vigilant about that.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure they`re making their -- their own assessments about what`s happened inside of Russia in recent days.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Secretary Blinken, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ANTONY BLINKEN: Thanks, Margaret. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the chairman of the House Intelligence Committee, Republican Congressman Mike Turner of Ohio. He joins us from Dayton.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER (R-Ohio): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I understand top congressional leaders, yourself included, have been briefed multiple times in recent days by the administration in regard to the risk posed by the Wagner group.\par \par \uc2 Last year, U.S. intelligence had extraordinary detail, those are the words, the head of U.S. intelligence, about Putin`s plans to invade Ukraine. I wonder how you would describe the intelligence the U.S. had about this march on Moscow?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 Well, the intelligence community was very much aware of that the conflict between Prigozhin and Putin was inevitable. And, even from public sources, which you`ve seen Prigozhin for months has put out videos critical of the Russian government, critical of Putin.\par \par \uc2 Putin has allowed this. And, as the secretary said, those videos themselves even included criticizing Putin`s very premise of the war, that it was not started by \uc2 NATO\uc2 , that there were not Nazis in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And then entering into Moscow -- entering into Russia itself and taking their convoy to Moscow, that really shows to the basic issue of whether or not Putin controls his military. For any government to have stability, they have to control their military. Obviously Prigozhin, in order to make it that distance, has to have accomplices.\par \par \uc2 You know, where was the air force? Where was the Russian air force in preventing this? That`s going to be an issue that -- that Putin is going to have to deal with both internationally and domestically, is his government, as an authoritarian government depends, on its assertion of power, force, in order to be able to contain -- to continue to wield power, and that certainly is going to be an issue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Was \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  himself aware of the potential of this uprising?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Well, I can`t go into what our intelligence was.\par \par \uc2 But I can tell you this. These videos that he was allowing Prigozhin to put -- put out were public. They were distributed around the world.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Putin certainly was aware of them because he was allowing them, the content of them, where they not only criticized Putin and the Russian government and called for the removal of the minister of defense.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, as you know, this weekend, Prigozhin`s statement was removal of the president himself. So, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  certainly had lots of public notice that Prigozhin was -- was a -- was a critic and was threatening the -- the government, and now ultimately took this military action into Russia itself.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that raises the question of whether this was a strategic move by Prigozhin or just sort of a gamble and an opportunity he seized.\par \par \uc2 Do we have any insight?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Well, you know, he`s a military guy.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this is like a 12-hour trip from Ukraine to Moscow, and he got within two hours of Moscow. Now, being a military guy, he understands the logistics and really the -- the assistance that he`s going to need to do that. This is not a weekend trip he`s taking, taking his convoy, and his military convoy, up to Moscow.\par \par \uc2 There`s a number of accomplices, including, as we saw, some of the Russian people on the border with Ukraine who clearly support the Wagner group, in contrast to their support for the Russian government. This is something that would have had to been planned for a significant amount of time to be executed in the manner in which it was,\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There was a report last month that Prigozhin had offered in January to help Ukraine attack the Russian military by sharing information on troop positions that he had.\par \par \uc2 Is this in any way helpful, what has just occurred, to ending the war in Ukraine? Like, where is Prigozhin`s interest?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Right.\par \par \uc2 So this really does hurt -- hurt Putin, and not only just politically and in his leadership in Russia and his presidency, but in his efforts to continue the war in Ukraine. You know, I think, obviously, in the beginning, there`s going to be an initial increase of activity from Russia against Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 But because he went -- Putin himself went on a national TV to respond to Prigozhin, and Prigozhin said that your government has lied to you, this is not a war that \uc2 NATO\uc2  started, there are no Nazis in Ukraine, taking down the very premise makes it much more difficult for Putin to continue to turn to the Russian people and say, we should continue to send people to die in this war that -- for which Prigozhin himself has said to the Russian people, the premise is a lie.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There was a lot that Secretary Blinken said he could not answer during our interview. Is that because U.S. intelligence does not know or because it`s classified?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Well, you know, I can`t answer that either. But you can -- you can assume certainly that we have been very focused on Russia and Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And this is an area where Ukraine`s successes has been a result in part because of the successes of the intelligence community. So we`ve been very, very focused on this. I think that as we -- as we go forward, this is going to be even more critical as it face -- as we face the threats both for Ukraine and for the United States as to what`s going to happen to Putin and Russia next.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Chairman, please stay with us.\par \par \uc2 We have to take a break. We`ll be right back in one minute with more of our conversation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re back with House Intelligence Committee Chair Mike Turner.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk a bit about China here as well, sir. But let me just button up, what -- how would you define the relationship between China and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  right now?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Right.\par \par \uc2 So, President Xi has got to be very concerned right now, because, as you know, he went to Moscow, stood next to Putin and said, you know, "We`re together," he and \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , are bringing about change that hasn`t happened for 100 years.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, that is the march of authoritarianism against democracy that we won in World War Two that they`re now rising up against. But now he`s standing next to a guy who can`t even control his -- his own -- his own military.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Putin, in his national address said that these individuals are going to have inevitable punishment. And then, in the end, Prigozhin gets a vacation in -- in Belarus, and his troops are now going to sign contracts. It sounds more like paperwork than a KGB agent, than doing inevitable punishment.\par \par \uc2 Xi, in seeing that with Putin, has got to understand that -- that Putin`s stature in the world has diminished. That diminishes President Xi. And, certainly as Putin looks weakened, certainly not being able to control his -- his military and being a strong nuclear power, President Xi has to be worried about the stability of Russia itself.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be watching that.\par \par \uc2 You know, last Friday, the -- or this past Friday -- sorry. I`m back from Beijing, and my time frame`s all screwed up here.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the director of national intelligence released this declassified summary of the findings in regard to links between the Wuhan Institute of Virology and the origin of the COVID pandemic.\par \par \uc2 It says several researchers at that institute were ill in 2019 with symptoms consistent with, but not diagnostic of COVID. Why is it so inconclusive still?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Well, and this is the problem, really, what -- what the director of national intelligence has done, Director Haines.\par \par \uc2 We passed a law saying, declassify the information that you have about the COVID and Wuhan lab`s activities. What they did is they basically went and -- and did a paper on what they believe about the intelligence they`ve looked at. I will give an example of this.We`ve asked to open the curtain and release the intelligence, and they went behind the curtain, read this stuff, and came out and said, well, this is what we think about it.\par \par \uc2 This is not sufficient. And, certainly, this is going to be -- set up between a battle between Congress and the director of national intelligence to make certain that -- that the law that was passed unanimously, both the Senate and the House and signed by the president, is complied with, but also the American public get the answers they deserve.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So there was a classified annex to this, though, that was not released, I would assume that you have read that.\par \par \uc2 Is that...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: No, actually, we just got this late, late Friday, so I haven`t had access to it in a classified setting.\par \par \uc2 But even releasing a classified annex goes against what the law says. The law says declassify, not give us more classified information. I mean, my committee has already seen a significant amount of this intelligence. Giving my committee more intelligence doesn`t give it to the American public, and that`s what the declassification law was about.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the -- the report says that it details two agencies say it was a lab accident. CIA can`t determine. The National Intelligence Council and four other agencies say most likely caused by natural exposure.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe that there actually is a definitive conclusion that the government`s not releasing?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: So, I -- I have seen, for example, the classified annex to the report that President Biden requested the intelligence community gave.\par \par \uc2 What you just read were more conclusions by the intelligence community.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: In the report that was given to the President, the 90-day-or-so report, they have -- they have information in that report that contradicts, I believe, the impressions that are given in -- in these statements by the Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 We want the intelligence released, not their opinion about the intelligence. If we wanted their opinion, we would have asked for it. We passed a law saying, declassify it. It`s the law of the land. Release this so the American public and see it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Experts out there in the community besides the intelligence community...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: ... need to take a look at this and help us understand what really happened that resulted in millions of people dying.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you miss an episode of Face the Nation or want to watch one of our extended interviews, you can find it all on CBSNews.com, or search Face the Nation on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 Wagner group leader \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  is actually wanted by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  here in the U.S. for his efforts to medal in the 2016 elections.\par \par \uc2 CBS News foreign correspondent Debora Patta has been tracking the Wagner group and she reports this morning from Johannesburg, South Africa.\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Yevgeny Prigozhin`s uprising appears to have been extremely well planned and executed. Who would have thought one of Putin`s closest allies, a former convict and Kremlin caterer, would eventually be pitted against the Russian leader.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA (voice over): It was during Russia`s first invasion of Ukraine in 2014 that Prigozhin made the leap from Putin`s shift (ph), to warlord, running an off the books mercenary group. Wagner soldiers started showing up in Syria, then across Africa. And while his hired guns deal in death, Prigozhin makes his money by plundering natural resources in places like the mineral rich Central African Republic, or CAR. In exchange, Wagner provides the mercenary muscle to prop up the country`s leader, even guarding the president. What Wagner doesn`t say is that they effectively run this nation through violence and a galaxy of shell companies.\par \par \uc2 Now, this model is repeated across Africa, allowing Prigozhin to evade sanctions and rake in billions to fund what the U.S. has called a transnational criminal organization, as well as his private army in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Until recently, Prigozhin vigorously denied any links to Wagner, but stepped out of the shadows last year, recruiting prisoners from Russian penal colonies in exchange for pardon. And at salaries far higher than any regular Kremlin soldiers. And he certainly enjoyed the notoriety, filming himself strutting around the battlefield and delivering Putin his only real victory after months of war, capturing Soledar and Bakhmut at a heavy cost, though, as many of his mercenaries were killed in the fighting.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 DEBORA PATTA: Now, throughout this war, Prigozhin has appeared untouchable, and has survived even after this armed insurrection. And he seems convinced, Margaret, that at the very least he will continue his rein in Africa, the real Wagner money spinner.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Debora Patta, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more on the situation in Russia, we turn to CBS News national security correspondent David Martin and former U.S. ambassador to Russia, now a CBS News contributor, John Sullivan.\par \par \uc2 Good to have both of you here.\par \par \uc2 David, let`s start on just what happened on the ground. 124 miles outside of Moscow. That`s how far the Wagner group says they got. What does this tell us about Russia`s intelligence in military?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, it came as a surprise to U.S. intelligence. They - they had some warning that there was going to be a mutiny. But they were surprised when they -- Russians put up no resistance, allowed Prigozhin to go into their military headquarters in Rostov and then send his army unopposed north toward Moscow.\par \par \uc2 And then they were surprised again by how quickly a deal was made. They had expected a longer, more violent affair. And that`s why people like the national security adviser, chairman of the Joint Chiefs of Staff, canceled their travel plans, because there was the danger that this mutiny could mushroom into a civil war. And that brings up all sorts of concerns about the security of Russia`s nuclear weapons and what you learn is that when a person like Putin is sitting on top of an arsenal of thousands of nuclear weapons, his problems very quickly become your problems.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 And, Ambassador, I mean, it`s -- it sounds strange sometimes, that phrase catastrophic success when foreign policy analysts talk about it, but are we actually in a situation where \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is preferable to \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  in terms of running the Russian state?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SULLIVAN: Well, he`s certainly a known quantity. He`s a hardened adversary of the United States, but the alternative could be worse. So, I think the Biden administration is rightfully concerned, as - as David suggests, with chaos and uncertainty in Russia with their nuclear arsenal is very dangerous, not just for the United States but for the world.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, when we - when you look at the map, Rostov, the city that you mentioned, it`s a major logistics hub on that route to Moscow. Do we have any insight yet, David, into what`s happening within the Russian military right now? Are they remaining loyal to \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: There was no sign that any of the security apparatus around Putin had - had switched sides. They seem to hang - hang tough with Putin.\par \par \uc2 The - the question of why there was no Russian resistance -- I mean one possible explanation is because Putin told them not to resist. We`re going to settle this as quickly and as peacefully as possible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ambassador, it was a surprise to many when it was Belarus that announced that they were the brokers here, that the president of that country. Now that country is pretty much viewed as a vassal state of Russia. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  controls it. There are nuclear weapons that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says he`s putting there. Explain this part of the puzzle? Like, why would \uc2 Yevgeny Prigozhin\uc2  move to Belarus? Why are they suddenly appearing to be power brokers?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SULLIVAN: Well, as - as you point out, Margaret, Lukashenko is in power now as president because of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  came to his rescue in August 2020. It was Lukashenko who was dependent on Putin. But now, think about this, this is, as you note, Belarus is part of a union state with Russia. They are conjoined. How dependent now is Putin on Lukashenko? It`s an -- it`s - it`s evidence of the weakness that this reveals, what`s happened in the last three or four days, the weakness of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . It`s not just an appearance of weakness, it`s actual weakness. A person that he has said is a traitor, who has stabbed him and his nation in the back, he struck a deal with? A deal that he needed to strike to avoid bloodshed and chaos? What strong leader does that?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, exactly. And - and when you look at - I think for so many Americans who are learning about Wagner group for the first time, and they just heard Debora`s great reporting there, the U.S. considers them a transnational criminal organization. Is this like the mafia has its own military? I mean how do we think about this?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SULLIVAN: Well, Prigozhin himself spent most of the 1980s in prison because he`s a career criminal. Wagner operates in states in Africa and elsewhere, not because they are patriots who were executing policy on behalf of the Russian government, they`re there to get access to gold mines, oil resources and so forth. This is a money-making organization, corrupt organization, that the United States correctly treats as a transnational criminal organization.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that`s what -- David, it was interesting to here, from both the secretary and Mike Turner, this concern of what happens next, not just in Ukraine, but in Libya, in Syria, throughout Africa. Do we have any concept yet? I mean does this become a separate company? Does this become part of the Russian military?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, I somehow don`t think that Prigozhin has gone to Belarus to live out his days in idle exile. I don`t think he`s out of the game. And although there`s been this deal with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , who says \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is going to deliver on the deal? I mean if I were Prigozhin, I would keep my bodyguards close and my food taster closer because poison is one of Putin`s favorite instruments of getting revenge.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He has a force of, what, 25,000 under his command, allegedly?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: That`s - that`s what he`s credited for. He was -- at the start of this year, he was credited with 50,000. And I think the drop from 50,000 to 25,000 is a measure of how much they lost in the - in the fighting in - in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This was great to have your analysis and your reporting. Thank you, both.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SULLIVAN: Thanks, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s now been one year since the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade, and the political dissension over abortion rights continues to grow. We`ve recently discussed the topic with Republican presidential candidates on this broadcast, but today we turn to Texas Congresswoman Veronica Escobar, a co-chair of President Biden`s re-election bid. And she joins us from El Paso.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR (D-TX): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s been 50 years since 1973 and that ruling. But in that time, Congress failed to pass any protections for abortion access. Even when Democrats controlled both houses, even when presidents were Democrats.\par \par \uc2 We`re now at this point where our CBS News polling shows 53 percent of Democrats feel as though your party isn`t doing enough on the issue of abortion. Why do Democrats think this is a winning issue for the party when they`ve not been able to deliver on it for so long?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Well, Margaret, the - the House Democrats have passed the Women`s Health Care Protection Act. We did that both sessions of Congress, the last two, when we had a majority. But as you know, and as the American people know, we did not have a wide enough majority in the Senate. In the Senate because of the filibuster, the Senate has not acted on protecting access and women`s freedom to have access to abortion care.\par \par \uc2 But it is really important that we look at what`s happened since Roe v. Wade was overturned by the Republican-controlled Supreme Court. We have seen 23 million women lose access to reproductive health care. We`ve seen 18 states enact harsh abortion bans. And we have also seen every single Republican nominee express support for a federal national abortion ban. We cannot go in that direction, and that`s why these upcoming elections are critically important.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But even when there was unified control, it wasn`t delivered on. When you look at what`s happening now, half of those polled by \uc2 CBS\uc2  say abortion access has become more restricted over the past year, as you`ve just detailed.\par \par \uc2 So, we know President Biden is taking these executive actions and orders. Why isn`t there more grassroots mobilization at the state level if the entire point of the court ruling was that it goes back to the states?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: We have seen grassroots mobilization at the state level. We`ve seen states such as --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re saying you`re losing the argument, though?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: I`m sorry?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you just detailed that state by state, in many places, you`re losing that argument.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Well, states are making every efforts and grassroots organizations and women across the country are working to put in protections at the state constitutional level. But the challenge that we will face, should Republicans maintain control of the House and gain control of the Senate or the White House, is that we would see national restrictions that are harsher and more serious than - than what we see today.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got a very - we`ve got a huge challenge on our hands in the sense that women`s reproductive freedoms continue to be rolled back. And the only way to win that is by winning elections. Both making sure that we flip the House and regain control, and that we elect a wide enough margin, a filibuster proof majority, or senators willing to lift the majority to protect women, and we`ve got to maintain the White House.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley was recently on this program and she said candidates aren`t telling the American people the truth. Republicans and Democrats she puts in that bucket. She said, there`s not -- you know, if there`s neither the consensus nor the votes for either party to either legalize or fully ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what she said.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VC)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R) 2024 PRESIDENTIAL CANDIDATE: So let`s be honest with the American people and say, let`s find national consensus. Let`s agree on, you know, getting rid of late-term abortions. Let`s agree on the fact that we need more adoptions. Let`s agree on the fact that we need accessible contraception. Let`s agree on the fact that mothers shouldn`t be jailed or go to - you know, get the death penalty for abortions.\par \par \uc2 (End VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Doesn`t she have a point? There are smaller issues related to abortion you can find consensus on?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: The national consensus, Margaret, is that 80 percent of Americans do not agree with the overturning of Roe v. Wade. That is --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But in terms of what you could actually get passed in Congress?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Well, we -- again, we -- Democrats passed the Women`s Health Care Protection Act in the House.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In the House. Yes, only in the House.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: (INAUDIBLE). And the challenge in the Senate is that you need a super majority.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: You need 60 votes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: And so, right, which is why we need to win elections this -- next November. And, furthermore, we`ve got to retain the White House because there`s only one person who will be on the ballot next November, and that`s President Biden, who has promised and committed to fighting for women`s reproductive freedom. Make no mistake about it -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: As much as Nikki Haley wants to talk about finding consensus here and there, the bottom line is all these --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, stopping women from being prosecuted, for example, the death penalty. I mean you have to appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Yes, but -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean why not pass a law on that front?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Can - can you imagine that`s --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that not worth it?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: That`s their - that`s where they want to allow consensus?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, she`s saying, let`s pass a law to prevent that on the national level.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Well, I -- my perspective, and I think the vast majority of Americans` perspectives, is, we want the protections under Roe v. Wade restored. Eighty percent. And, in fact, even 65 percent -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, 24 weeks?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: I`m sorry?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Protection up to 24 weeks of pregnancy, that`s your defined position? I know that`s what was in the protection act, but specifically that`s what you are endorsing?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Roe v. Wade essentially protects a women`s right to access abortion. And what we are seeing in states like my own, in Texas, where the rollbacks have happened and the bans are occurring, is that even in cases where women`s health is at risk, politicians don`t really care about the health of the woman.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you for coming on and making that case.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the global hunger crisis and our conversation with the new executive director of the \uc2 United Nations World Food Program\uc2 , Cindy McCain. She spoke to us Thursday from New York, where she addressed the \uc2 U.N. Security Council\uc2  about what she called a spiraling hunger crisis in parts of the world.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CINDY MCCAIN (\uc2 World Food Program\uc2  Executive Director): There are a lot of fires in the world right now. There`s a lot going on. There`s a lot of countries that are in deep distress. Somalia being one of them. And so we`re spread pretty thin right now. And so to be able to continue the work that we do, obviously we need more help, but we need the world to pay attention to us also and make sure that people understand it`s not just a security crisis in Somalia, it`s a humanity crisis as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know you`ve recently lost some employees in Sudan where the United States has pulled out because of the violence there. Are you still able to feed people despite the war?\par \par \uc2 CINDY MCCAIN: Well, we never left Sudan. We stalled for a little bit, we paused for a few days because, you`re correct, we did lose three people there. And we had to evacuate our other citizens, our other nationals, as well as our international people out. But we never left.\par \par \uc2 And so we are now, again, we`re back in, we`re distributing food. Again, reminding everyone, it`s extraordinarily dangerous there right now. So, our methods and how we`re doing it were -- are a little bit different. But we also have asked the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . to please guarantee us a humanitarian corridor for us to be able to work and operate so we can deliver our food.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know the U.S. is the largest financial donor to the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2  and the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., having given $7.2 billion, more than all other donors combined last year. Meanwhile, the world`s second largest economy, China, gave $11 million. How receptive is Beijing to your requests?\par \par \uc2 CINDY MCCAIN: Well, I`d like to encourage Beijing to get involved and be a part of this. We need -- not only do we need their funding, but we need their expertise on many things. Their - their technology, with regards to agriculture, and the technology with regards to - to climate change can be very helpful in these countries that are really struggling with drought and lack of food, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is the issue that the government wants credit and so, therefore, they want to do it under their own flag and not through the international system, which you represent?\par \par \uc2 CINDY MCCAIN: I think, to some degree, you`re correct on that. I think it`s also just a willingness to be a part of - of working together as a team worldwide. In these countries that we`re in, one agency cannot do - cannot do the job. We need partnerships. And so we encourage the Chinese and we encourage many - encourage many other countries around the world to partner with us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, in the room, when you are trying to pitch to the Security Council, you are looking at the United States, you were talking to China. You`re also talking to Russia in addition to some other members. But I want to pick up specifically on the Kremlin because they said this past week there were no grounds to extend the Black Sea grain initiative. That is the deal under which Russia agreed to allow grain to leave the ports of Ukraine, a country it is militarily occupying. That`s arguably weaponizing food.\par \par \uc2 What`s the impact if that deal goes away?\par \par \uc2 CINDY MCCAIN: Well, the impact is, again, we`re short on grain, and what does that mean? It affects a lot -- a large portion of Africa. We`re also short on fertilizer. Fertilizer is the other half of this that`s kind of - that`s supposed to be coming out. And so, without the fertilizer, in many cases, they`re not going to be able to grow crops that are as large or as productive as they could be.\par \par \uc2 For all the things that - that are going on, I truly wish that we could end this war so that we could begin again to feed people around the world and - and so that the Ukrainians can also feed themselves. What`s at stake here is starvation and famine. That`s what we`re looking at.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s been an uptick in migrants to the United States from Haiti, which is now largely controlled by gangs. How do you keep the food you are getting into that island nation out of the hands of criminals and into the mouths of starving children?\par \par \uc2 CINDY MCCAIN: Well, you`re exactly right, the importance is, is that the international community needs to be in there to not only help keep the country safe, but to - but to help us be - enable our organization, other organizations, to be able to move the grains around and move food around in general.\par \par \uc2 The idea in - in Haiti, which is such a lush tropical island, but it is also affected by climate change, it`s also affected by -- by, you know, by land use. I think the world community has taken a step forward and kind of forgotten Haiti a little bit.\par \par \uc2 So -- so my job, having returned from Haiti, is to remind the world that Haiti is still there, is -- still needs our help, it still needs food, it still needs security, and it needs to be able to prosper in a way so that they don`t lose a generation of children.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Broadly speaking, extreme weather is a factor that`s affecting crops and migration. You`ve said climate change is influencing the situations in a number of the examples you just gave. Where are you seeing it impact the most?\par \par \uc2 CINDY MCCAIN: Well, one of the places is the Sahel. I mean the -- if you could see what`s down there and see the impact that the - that climate change has had on it. So what we`re -- what we are doing with regards to the Sahel and other regions, particularly in Africa, is water management, teaching ancient ways, which are very simple to do, and climate change, not just in Africa or the Sahel, climate change is worldwide. And we`re going to be seeing. You know, we`re having to manage crops now that are - they have to be more resilient to drought. Our animal feed and things have to be more resilient so the animals can be more resistant to drought. There`s a lot of things at stake here. And I think when people talk about climate change, and - and those naysayers that think climate change isn`t real, I`d like to take them to the Sahel and show them what`s real.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Cindy McCain, at the United Nations, thank you.\par \par \uc2 CINDY MCCAIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, or FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230625ej6p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 A Coordinated Republican Campaign Aimed At Blocking State Constitutional Amendments To Preserve Abortion Access Is Examined\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 936 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: This week marks one year since the Supreme Court`s landmark decision overturned Roe v. Wade. A new CBS News poll finds most Americans, 57 percent, in fact, say overturning Roe has been bad for the country.\par \par \uc2 Abortion rights advocates are now turning directly to voters to pass state constitutional amendments to preserve access. But a CBS News investigation has uncovered a coordinated campaign aimed at blocking them.\par \par \uc2 In our series After Roe, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns reports on the Republican megadonor who is heavily funding the effort.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from May 10)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): This was the scene at the Ohio Statehouse when the Republican-dominated legislature approved a controversial plan, making it harder to cement abortion access into the state`s Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Republican lawmakers set a special election for August to decide whether changing the state Constitution would now need 60 percent approval from Ohio voters, up from just over 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR (Former Ohio Supreme Court Justice): It`s misleading. It`s deceptive.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And critics like former Ohio Supreme Court Chief Justice Maureen O`Connor say there was a reason. In a poll last year, 59 percent of Ohio voters said they believed abortion should be generally legal. In Kansas and Michigan, voters preserved abortion access with just under 60 percent approval.\par \par \uc2 FORMER CHIEF JUSTICE MAUREEN O`CONNOR: That`s why they chose the 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: So this was strategic?\par \par \uc2 FORMER CHIEF JUSTICE MAUREEN O`CONNOR: Very much strategic. And when you keep changing the rules and moving the goalposts you are intentionally silencing the vote of the people.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Ohio Republican Secretary of State \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2 , who first called for the 60 percent measure, says changing the state Constitution should be rare and require a broader consensus.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2  (R-Ohio Secretary of State): We don`t want to see a really radical abortion amendment put in our state Constitution. This is about protecting our state Constitution from out-of-state special interests.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But a CBS News investigation found a Florida-based nonprofit called the Foundation for Government Accountability has been promoting the 60 percent supermajority requirement since 2021.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN FISCHER (Campaign Finance Expert): They have tried to push this idea in South Dakota, in Arkansas, in Missouri, and now in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Campaign finance expert Brendan Fischer says much of the foundation`s support can be traced to Republican megadonor Richard Uihlein, a staunch opponent of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN FISCHER: A foundation controlled by \uc2 Dick Uihlein\uc2  has given nearly $18 million to FGA.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: As the 60 percent measure moved through the Ohio House...\par \par \uc2 (Clip from political ad)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have less than a week to save Ohio`s Constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: ... Uihlein gave more than a million dollars to a campaign targeting Republican lawmakers on the fence.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from political ad)\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s time for state Representative Al Cutrona to stop the liberal takeover of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The campaign was effective. And, on August 8, voters will get the final say.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Can we count on a no-vote from you in August?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: OK. Awesome. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Caitlin Huey-Burns, CBS News, Columbus, Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The world`s largest online retailer is being sued for allegedly tricking customers into paying for its Prime service. That`s just ahead.\par \par \uc2 And, later, a major change for America`s longest-running game show.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from The Price is Right)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is being accused of tricking millions of people into paying for Amazon Prime.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Federal Trade Commission\uc2  is suing the world`s largest online retailer. It says \uc2 Amazon\uc2  knowingly duped customers into signing up for a renewing of its $139-a-year Prime service and then made them jump through hoops to cancel. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  says the FTC`s claims are false.\par \par \uc2 Well, investigators say they found a solid new DNA link in their investigation into the murders last year of four students at the University of Idaho. According to court documents, a cheek swab taken from accused killer Bryan Kohberger is a statistical match to the DNA on a knife sheath that was found near two of the victims. Investigators say the DNA is five octillion times more likely to be Kohberger`s than from a random person.\par \par \uc2 Well, lab-grown chicken, would you eat it? That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The age of lab-grown meat has arrived in this country.\par \par \uc2 The Agriculture Department just approved two California companies to sell chicken grown in steel tanks using cells that come from an animal egg or a special bank of stored cells. The cell-cultivated chicken will first be available at a restaurant in San Francisco and another one in Washington, D.C., owned by famed chef Jose Andres before hitting stores.\par \par \uc2 One of America`s favorite game shows is saying goodbye to its legendary Hollywood studio. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230622ej6l000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Thwarting The Will Of The People\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 1,396 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Can you believe that this week marks one year since a landmark Supreme Court decision overturned Roe v Wade, sending the fight over abortion rights back to the states.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News poll out just this morning finds a majority of Americans, 57 percent say overturning Roe has been mostly bad for the country. In some places with new restrictions, abortion rights advocates are turning directly to the voters to amend state constitutions to protect access.\par \par \uc2 Now, a CBS News investigation has found that a coordinated campaign heavily funded by a leading Republican megadonor aimed at making it harder to pass those amendments.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns joins us now with that story.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, in the years since Roe v. Wade was overturned, voters in Kansas and Michigan voted to preserve abortion access in their state constitutions with wide support, and we found that that success has kickstarted a nationwide campaign to quash similar amendments and that`s all zeroing in on the state of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting in protest.)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: These were the protests that broke out last month when the Republican-dominated Ohio legislature approved a controversial plan.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers set a special election in August to decide whether amending the state constitution would now need 60 percent approval from Ohio voters, up from just over 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR (Retired Republican Ohio Supreme Court chief justice): It is misleading, it is deceptive, and if it weren`t so serious, it would be laughable.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Maureen O`Connor recently retired as the Republican chief justice of the Ohio Supreme Court. She says Republicans are pushing an election at a time when turnout is historically low to make it easier to secure their agenda.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR: They are holding an August election so that they can have this 60 percent requirement in time for the November election where there will be a ballot initiative to enshrine reproductive rights in our constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In a poll last year, 59 percent of Ohio voters said they believed abortion should be generally legal; and in Kansas and Michigan last year, amendments preserving abortion access passed with just under 60 percent approval. Numbers, O`Connor says caught the eye of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR: That`s why they chose the 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: So this was strategic.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR: Very much strategic.\par \par \uc2 When you keep changing the rules and moving the goalpost, you are intentionally silencing the vote of the people.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2  (Ohio Republican secretary of State): And so yes, it`s about abortion, but it`s about so many other things as well.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Ohio Republican secretary of State, \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2  first proposed raising the threshold to 60 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 : We don`t want to see a really radical abortion amendment put in our state constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But it is changing the rules at this state in the game. Isn`t that stacking the deck?\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 : No, not whatsoever. I mean, again, the game as you called it is an ongoing public conversation about public policy.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: As abortion rights groups in Ohio raise funds nationally for a November vote on the amendment, LaRose says changing the state constitution should be rare and require a broader consensus.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 : I`ve been consistent all along that this is about good government. This is about protecting our state constitution from out-of- state special interests.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But a CBS News investigation discovered, this very proposal was promoted by conservative out-of-state interests.\par \par \uc2 In this 2021 memo, a Florida-based nonprofit called the Foundation for Government Accountability touted the 60 percent supermajority requirement.\par \par \uc2 DR. TRAVIS N. TAYLOR (Foundation for Government Accountability senior market research analyst): Members of the committee --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A lobbyist for the foundation was the only one to testify on behalf of the 60 percent measure. When it was first introduced in the Ohio House.\par \par \uc2 DR. TRAVIS N. TAYLOR: I encourage you to support this resolution increasing the required support threshold for constitutional initiatives to 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN FISCHER (Documented author): The playbook being executed in Ohio was also executed in South Dakota in Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Brendan Fischer is an expert with the political finance watchdog, Documented.\par \par \uc2 He has traced much of the foundation`s support to Illinois billionaire, \uc2 Richard Uihlein\uc2 , a Republican megadonor and staunch opponent of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN FISCHER: A foundation controlled by \uc2 Dick Uihlein\uc2  has given nearly $18 million to FGA.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In April, when the bill appeared to falter.\par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: We have less than a week to save Ohio`s constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Uihlein gave more than a million dollars to a pressure campaign targeting Republican lawmakers on the fence.\par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: It`s time for State Representative Al Cutrona to stop the liberal takeover of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Doesn`t that speak to the very issue that you are talking about having an out of state donor support a measure in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 : Well, listen, there`s massive amounts of money being spent on both sides of that issue and that is free speech.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Sure, during a campaign, but this was during the time that this was being talked about in the legislature and a way to try to persuade Republicans to come on board with it, and it was effective.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 : And those kinds of things are done transparently.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Can we count on a no-vote from you in August?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Awesome. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: On August 8th, Ohio voters will get the final say on what it will take to amend the state`s constitution.\par \par \uc2 In order to change the constitution, shouldn`t it be more difficult.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR: Ohioans have operated since 1912 with this process, and it has worked well, so it is not broken, and there`s no need to fix it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The Foundation for Government Accountability declined to comment and \uc2 Richard Uihlein\uc2  did not respond to our requests. And right now, Ohio has a six-week abortion ban on the books, but it is being held up by the courts, and a constitutional amendment would prohibit that ban -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Caitlin, you just mentioned that turnout for the August elections is typically low. So, what are they expecting this time?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yes, I think that`s a really key point in all of this, is that last year`s election in August was just eight percent turnout. And in fact, Republicans actually voted to eliminate August special elections altogether because of that.\par \par \uc2 But for this, they brought them back and critics are concerned that this means that just a small percentage of voters are going to decide how to amend the state constitution, and this could actually be a roadmap for other areas.\par \par \uc2 With the battle of abortion now put to the states, it is these kinds of constitutional amendments that are considered the last line of defense for abortion access -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Caitlin, thank you. We`ll see how this turns out. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, the effort to rebuild a collapsed section of Interstate 95 and reopen the highway weeks ahead of schedule.\par \par \uc2 But first, Norah O`Donnell has what`s coming up on this week on "CBS Evening News."\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Hey there. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 One year after the reversal of Roe vs. Wade, we take a look at OB-GYN shortages and restricted abortion access.\par \par \uc2 That`s all this week on the "CBS Evening News,."\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230622ej6l000gq\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Noises Heard by Sub Search Team; Hunter Biden`s Plea Deal; Dangerous Southern Heat; Tropical Storm Bret Forecast; Violence Escalates in West Bank; Alito Responds to Lavish Vacation Report; Thwarting the Will of the People; Speedy Bridge Repair; Helping Ukraine`s Wounded Warriors; "Echo in the Distance"; "FBI True" Launches New Episodes; Fashion for Everyone; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Roxana Saberi, Catherine  Herridge, Stephanie Abrams, Scott MacFarlane, Caitlin Huey-Burns; Elaine  Quijano, Vladimir Duthiers, Holly Williams, Shayla Michelle Reaves \par \uc2 14,228 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Republicans vowed to continue investigating Hunter Biden after he strikes a deal with prosecutors on tax and gun charges.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): They`ve made him a very good deal and he sucked it up like a Hoover Deluxe, and I don`t -- I can`t blame him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: New developments overnight in the search for a missing sub near the wreck of the Titanic.\par \par \uc2 MAN: They are going to be at almost freezing temperatures, assuming they lost all their power.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: An attacked by Palestinian gunmen left four Israelis dead near a settlement in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is just the latest in the surge of violence.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Record breaking heat blankets millions in the south including many already recovering from severe storms.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is going to feel like it`s 110 to 120 degrees.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Social media influencer, Andrew Tate and his brother are being charged with rape and human trafficking in Romania.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Minor leaguer, Jo Adell hit an absolute bomb, 514 feet.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The longest home run and the Majors or Minors since 2015.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Come on, Jo, that ball has babies.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This birthday girl is a big fan of Barbie and \uc2 Michael Myers\uc2  from the "Halloween" movie.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: So she had a Barbie party with a cameo from \uc2 Michael Myers\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s seven-two and he might be the tallest pitcher to ever take the mound.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Victor Wembanyama will be the first overall pick in Thursday`s \uc2 NBA\uc2  draft at Yankee Stadium throwing out the first pitch.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Let`s see. Oh no.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Look at what that baseball looks like in his hand. You don`t blame him. It`s like, what is this? A game for ants, you know?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: My what big hands, you have Mr. Wembanyama?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are all staring at our hands.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So baseball is not a sport, but I can`t wait to see him on the basketball court.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Maybe with them long arms, he sort of just wound up and just dropped it off in the glove.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was the problem, he released it. That was the issue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my. I can`t wait to see him in action.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got some big stories for you today including an exclusive CBS News investigation into a crucial abortion fight one year after Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 So we`re thinking, if you really want to understand, I mean you need to really understand how the politics around abortion access works, you really need to stick around for a bit to watch this piece.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, everybody deserves to look good in whatever they want to wear, and Emma Mcilroy`s company Wildfang, produces gender blending clothing to satisfy that promise.\par \par \uc2 She will be here for a special edition of Pride Month that we call "Changing the Game."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we are going to begin with the news, the breaking news this morning: A glimmer of hope in the search for a submersible that disappeared on its way to the wreck of the Titanic. Rescue teams picked up underwater noises in the search zone, but this is hundreds of miles off the coast of Massachusetts and the zone itself about the size of the state of Connecticut. It`s a very small vessel in a very large area of the ocean and time is running out, less than 24 hours of oxygen left on board. That`s the estimate anyway.\par \par \uc2 Five people on board. They are missing including Stockton Rush, the founder of OceanGate, the company that built and deployed this sub.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi is in Boston for us with the latest on this. Boston is where the staging is happening. The rescues are being coordinated if they happen.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Roxana.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The US Coast Guard\uc2  confirms that a Canadian aircraft detected underwater noises in the search area. Crews then refocused their search to try to pinpoint where those noises are coming from. They have not been able to find anything so far, but their search continues.\par \par \uc2 To put in perspective how massive this search area is, it is larger, as you said than the state of Connecticut and roughly twice as deep as the Grand Canyon.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAPT. JAMIE FREDERICK (\uc2 US Coast Guard\uc2 ): We will do everything in our power to effect a rescue.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: With just hours of oxygen left, rescuers say they are doing everything they can to find the missing submersible.\par \par \uc2 What are the biggest challenges you`re facing?\par \par \uc2 CAPT. JAMIE FREDERICK: You`re dealing with a surface search and a subsurface search and frankly, that makes it an incredibly complex operation.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: US-based \uc2 OceanGate Expeditions\uc2  operated the voyage, which takes tourists to the famous Titanic shipwreck in the North Atlantic Ocean, nearly two-and-a-half miles underwater.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from OceanGate.)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Rescuers say the vessel could be there on the ocean floor or on the surface, but even if it is floating, spotting the Titan won`t be easy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`re not expecting to see a large portion of it showing above the surface of the water, maybe a couple of inches, a couple of feet of that at the most.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Do you know if the people aboard the vessel have any way of communicating?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We haven`t received any indication that they have any sort of communication device for us to be listening to.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: OceanGate CEO and founder, Stockton Rush is among those on board.\par \par \uc2 STOCKTON RUSH (\uc2 OceanGate Expeditions\uc2  founder and CEO): We run the whole thing with this game controller.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: In an interview with "CBS Sunday Morning" last year, Rush defended the safety of the submersible.\par \par \uc2 STOCKTON RUSH: The pressure vessel is not MacGyvered at all because that`s where we work with \uc2 Boeing\uc2  and \uc2 NASA\uc2  and \uc2 University of Washington\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Everything else can fail. Your thrusters can go. Your lights can go, you`re still going to be safe.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: According to 2018 court filings, a former OceanGate employee said he was fired after voicing concerns about the Titan, saying it posed potential danger to passengers as it reached extreme depths.\par \par \uc2 But in court documents filed this April, OceanGate said the vessel was capable of diving the depth to the Titanic site with a comfortable safety margin.\par \par \uc2 MIKE REISS (Submersible tourist): On every dive we took, they lost communication. You know going in how very dangerous this is.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Mike Reiss traveled on the Titan almost a year ago to see the Titanic wreckage.\par \par \uc2 MIKE REISS: No one walks into this surprised. Before you even get on, you sign this long, long waiver that mentions possible death three times on the first page, so you know what you`re getting into.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: As for those underwater noises that have been detected, the \uc2 US Coast Guard\uc2  says data from the Canadian aircraft has been shared with \uc2 US Navy\uc2  experts for more analysis and will be used in the search efforts -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, Roxana, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on this search, we`re joined by Rear Admiral John Mauger of the \uc2 US Coast Guard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Admiral Mauger, thank you so much for taking the time this morning.\par \par \uc2 You know, I went to bed thinking about this story, woke up thinking about this story, hoping that there would be some news, and it seems like we have news.\par \par \uc2 How optimistic, how encouraged should we be by this turn of events that they`re heard some kind of noise down there?\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN MAUGER (\uc2 US Coast Guard\uc2 ): Yes, well, thank you for having us on this morning.\par \par \uc2 This continues to be a really complex case with both a surface search and a subsea search, and our thoughts are with both the crew members and the families. I know that this is an incredibly difficult time for the family, and so we`re working very hard as part of an international effort, as part of a unified command with the \uc2 US Navy\uc2  with the Canadian Armed Forces, \uc2 Canadian Coast Guard\uc2  and private enterprise to bring as many assets to bear, as many capabilities to bear at the search site to conduct both a thorough air surface and subsea surface search.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we know you guys have all the best people in the business working on this 24/7. Do you remain optimistic at this point? We know that the hours left of oxygen that they have is getting very, very, very, very close to expiring, if there`s a way to say that.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN MAUGER: Yes, this is an incredibly complex case and so we`re really focused on bringing all those capabilities to bear. As mentioned in the brief, we had a P3 aircraft, Aurora aircraft flying yesterday that had sonar buoys, the sonar buoys detected noise in the water. We don`t know the source of that noise, but we`ve shared that information with Navy experts to classify it.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, what we`ve done is reprioritize the two remote operated vehicles that we have searching in the water, and the one surface vessel that has sonar capability at the surface of the water in the area of those noises to see if we can locate the source of those noises and locate the people in the submersible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, Admiral, how common is it to pick up noises? Is the ocean a noisy place? Because a regular person hears of these noises and thinks well, it`s got to be them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It has got to be them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It has got to be them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How likely is it in your experience that this is a human- caused noise?\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN MAUGER: So this is an incredibly complex site there. You have to remember that it is the wreck site of the Titanic. So there is a lot of metal and different objects in the water around the site. That`s why it`s so important that we`ve engaged experts from the Navy that understand the science behind noise and can classify or give us better information about what the source of that noise may be.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, it is something, it`s a target, it`s a focus for us to look at and so we`ve deployed the remote operated vehicles and the surface vessel, the \uc2 Canadian Coast Guard\uc2  surface vessel that has sonar capability in the vicinity of that to see if we can detect anything in the water in that area.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Admiral, we have to get going, but just quickly, how long will you hold on to hope for before you start planning for the worst?\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN MAUGER: And so as long as there`s an opportunity for survival, we will continue to work with this broad unified command to bring every resource to bear on the search. Over the course of next 24 hours, we`re going to bring additional vessels, additional remote operated vehicles, and we`re going to continue to fly in the air, so we`ll continue to look.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`ll continue to hold out hope as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Admiral Mauger, thank you.\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN MAUGER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning now to Washington and the response to Hunter Biden`s agreement to plead guilty on tax charges, Republicans are angry that the president`s son is likely to avoid prison.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  calls the charges a mere traffic ticket. Biden`s defenders say anyone else in the same spot would never have been charged.\par \par \uc2 Senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge is at the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 With a plea deal waiting for a judge`s approval, there is already disagreement over whether the years` long probe into the president`s son is over and it quickly turned political.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): If you are the president`s son, you get a sweetheart deal.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: As soon as Hunter Biden`s plea agreement was filed, there was quick reaction on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOHN KENNEDY (R-LA): They`ve made him a very good deal, and he sucked it up like a Hoover Deluxe.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): This to me demonstrates the seriousness of law enforcement under the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Three charges were filed in the Delaware court: Two counts of willful failure to pay federal income tax on annual earnings in excess of $1.5 million, both are misdemeanors. A third count, possession of a handgun by a drug user would be dismissed if Biden remains drug free and commits no additional crimes for two years.\par \par \uc2 A source familiar with the terms set, Biden`s legal team expects probation, not jail time, though final approval rests with the judge.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CLARK (Hunter Biden`s attorney): I think the judge is going to do what`s fair and I think what`s fair is, you know, my client gets on with his life.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: When asked about the plea deal, President Biden offered a brief response.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I`m very proud of my son.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: David Weiss, the US attorney in Delaware who led the probe was appointed by then President Trump and left in place by the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Weiss said the investigation is ongoing, but Hunter Biden`s team sees it differently.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS CLARK: My understanding is that we`re done.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Republican Congressman James Comer leads the House Oversight Committee, which is investigating the Biden family`s foreign business dealings, a probe disputed by the White House.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMES COMER (R-KY): We`re just beginning and I want to remind everyone, this is an investigation of Joe Biden. It always has been from day one.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Two years ago, Hunter Biden spoke about the Justice Department investigation on "CBS Sunday Morning."\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN (Joe Biden`s son): I am absolutely certain, 100 percent certain that at the end of the investigation, that I will be cleared.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: This plea deal comes weeks after an \uc2 IRS\uc2  whistleblower came forward to CBS News and Congress alleging the investigation had been slow walked. A congressional source told CBS News, new records from that whistleblower could be public as early as tomorrow -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Catherine, thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nearly 30 million Americans are continuing to swelter in relentless heat in the south.\par \par \uc2 San Angelo, Texas -- get this -- had its warmest day ever yesterday, 114 degrees is the readout. That`s a high number.\par \par \uc2 For more on this brutal heatwave, let`s bring in meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Steph, good morning.\par \par \uc2 First day of summer, first big report on serious heat.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): That`s right. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 And it definitely feels like summer, especially if you live in Texas. We are topping out above 100 degrees, but in the southeast, we`re only going to get into the 70s, and it is going to stay like this for the next few days because we have two systems that aren`t moving and they`re responsible for these above and below average temperatures.\par \par \uc2 Not only will our cut off low keep us cool, it is also going to rotate around and it`s going to be squeezing all the moisture out of the atmosphere and that could potentially cause some flooding as we head through the entire day and as we head into the evening hours.\par \par \uc2 We also have Tropical Storm Bret. It has actually gained some strength with winds now at 60 miles an hour. There is some good news with this. The shear is going to help keep it as a tropical storm as it moves through the Lesser Antilles into our Thursday and into our Friday.\par \par \uc2 For more in-depth coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices.\par \par \uc2 And Tony, the hurricane season is off to an aggressive start. Let`s hope this isn`t a sign of what`s to come.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, indeed. \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 , thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Turning overseas, the latest violence in the Middle East is raising tensions between Israelis and Palestinians.\par \par \uc2 Two Palestinians fired gunshots at a gas station near an Israeli settlement in the West Bank yesterday, killing four Israelis on the scene. The Islamic group, \uc2 Hamas\uc2  has claimed responsibility.\par \par \uc2 Now, this attack follows a violent clash in the city of Jenin on Monday. Israeli troops raided a refugee camp and then helicopter gunships were sent in to help evacuate the soldiers.\par \par \uc2 Six Palestinians were killed including a 15-year-old teenager and 90 people were wounded. The State Department says the US is concerned about the escalating violence and will continue to work with Israeli and Palestinian authorities to calm things down.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead, Supreme Court justice, \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  responds to a report that he accepted a free vacation trip from a billionaire then ruled on cases he had interest in. That`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:15, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead, a CBS News investigation looks at an effort to make it harder to protect abortion rights in Ohio and the Illinois billionaire who has heavily funded this campaign.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Supreme Court justice, \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  is facing scrutiny over a new report claiming he accepted a lavish vacation from a conservative billionaire with frequent business before the court. It is a story written up by \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  and it comes as some are calling for reform to the High Court`s ethics.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is at the Supreme Court for us this morning with more. Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday`s report claims Justice Alito took a luxury fishing trip with Republican megadonor, Paul Singer but didn`t recuse himself years later when cases connected to Singer`s business came here before the High Court. Even before the reporting was published, Alito was mounting a response.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The report says Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  did not report gifts on annual financial disclosure forms, including a private flight to Alaska and other amenities provided by billionaire hedge fund manager, Paul Singer during a fishing trip in 2008.\par \par \uc2 The report details that in July of that year, they stayed at the King Salmon Lodge and were served multicourse meals of Alaskan king crab legs or Kobe fillet. They also enjoyed wine that cost $1,000.00 A bottle.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 , Singer`s Manhattan-based hedge fund was involved in at least 10 cases brought before the Supreme Court, many of them high- stakes business cases. Those cases were heard after that fishing trip in 2008 with one decision in 2014, when the high court voted seven to one in Singer`s favor, and Justice Alito did not recuse himself from many of them.\par \par \uc2 In an op-ed published hours before \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 `s report, Justice Alito denied what he dubbed charges made against him.\par \par \uc2 In reference to the ride on that private plane, Alito said that "Singer allowed me to occupy what would have otherwise been an unoccupied seat," and that he stayed in a modest one-room unit.\par \par \uc2 Alito wrote that he, ". followed what I understood to be standard practice" by excluding the flight from his financial report.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Alito is the latest justice to respond to claims of undisclosed gifts and luxury travel. Earlier this spring, Democrats in Congress called on Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  to step down after \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  reported he had accepted decades of luxury travel from a donor.\par \par \uc2 It is important to note, the Judicial Conference just recently required justices to disclose travel accommodations to social events -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Scott. Got it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, a CBS News investigation into efforts that target abortion access in some states. What we learned about a coordinated campaign focused on state constitutions and who is paying for that.\par \par \uc2 Local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Can you believe that this week marks one year since a landmark Supreme Court decision overturned Roe v Wade, sending the fight over abortion rights back to the states.\par \par \uc2 A CBS News poll out just this morning finds a majority of Americans, 57 percent say overturning Roe has been mostly bad for the country. In some places with new restrictions, abortion rights advocates are turning directly to the voters to amend state constitutions to protect access.\par \par \uc2 Now, a CBS News investigation has found that a coordinated campaign heavily funded by a leading Republican megadonor aimed at making it harder to pass those amendments.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns joins us now with that story.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, in the years since Roe v. Wade was overturned, voters in Kansas and Michigan voted to preserve abortion access in their state constitutions with wide support, and we found that that success has kickstarted a nationwide campaign to quash similar amendments and that`s all zeroing in on the state of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE chanting in protest.)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: These were the protests that broke out last month when the Republican-dominated Ohio legislature approved a controversial plan.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers set a special election in August to decide whether amending the state constitution would now need 60 percent approval from Ohio voters, up from just over 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR (Retired Republican Ohio Supreme Court chief justice): It is misleading, it is deceptive, and if it weren`t so serious, it would be laughable.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Maureen O`Connor recently retired as the Republican chief justice of the Ohio Supreme Court. She says Republicans are pushing an election at a time when turnout is historically low to make it easier to secure their agenda.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR: They are holding an August election so that they can have this 60 percent requirement in time for the November election where there will be a ballot initiative to enshrine reproductive rights in our constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In a poll last year, 59 percent of Ohio voters said they believed abortion should be generally legal; and in Kansas and Michigan last year, amendments preserving abortion access passed with just under 60 percent approval. Numbers, O`Connor says caught the eye of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR: That`s why they chose the 60 percent.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: So this was strategic.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR: Very much strategic.\par \par \uc2 When you keep changing the rules and moving the goalpost, you are intentionally silencing the vote of the people.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2  (Ohio Republican secretary of State): And so yes, it`s about abortion, but it`s about so many other things as well.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Ohio Republican secretary of State, \uc2 Frank LaRose\uc2  first proposed raising the threshold to 60 percent last year.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 : We don`t want to see a really radical abortion amendment put in our state constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But it is changing the rules at this state in the game. Isn`t that stacking the deck?\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 : No, not whatsoever. I mean, again, the game as you called it is an ongoing public conversation about public policy.\par \par \uc2 