ce.\par \par \uc2 VOICE OVER: It`s time for State Representative Al Cutrona to stop the liberal takeover of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Doesn`t that speak to the very issue that you are talking about having an out of state donor support a measure in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 : Well, listen, there`s massive amounts of money being spent on both sides of that issue and that is free speech.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Sure, during a campaign, but this was during the time that this was being talked about in the legislature and a way to try to persuade Republicans to come on board with it, and it was effective.\par \par \uc2 FRANK LAROSE\uc2 : And those kinds of things are done transparently.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Can we count on a no-vote from you in August?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Awesome. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: On August 8th, Ohio voters will get the final say on what it will take to amend the state`s constitution.\par \par \uc2 In order to change the constitution, shouldn`t it be more difficult.\par \par \uc2 MAUREEN O`CONNOR: Ohioans have operated since 1912 with this process, and it has worked well, so it is not broken, and there`s no need to fix it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The Foundation for Government Accountability declined to comment and Richard Uihlein did not respond to our requests. And right now, Ohio has a six-week abortion ban on the books, but it is being held up by the courts, and a constitutional amendment would prohibit that ban -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Caitlin, you just mentioned that turnout for the August elections is typically low. So, what are they expecting this time?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yes, I think that`s a really key point in all of this, is that last year`s election in August was just eight percent turnout. And in fact, Republicans actually voted to eliminate August special elections altogether because of that.