 This he t convinces me that I must go to heaven.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tens of millions of Americans brace for more potentially record-breaking heat and the south.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS: And definitely feels like summer especially if you live in Texas. We are talking out above a hundred degrees.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA): This weekend, our struggling local businesses here will hopefully be filled again.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Pennsylvania`s governor says I-95 will partially reopen this weekend, about two weeks after an overpass collapsed due to a fire.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JIM KENNEY (D) (Philadelphia): This is not easy work, but together we are making great progress.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Do you want cake and ice cream to celebrate?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And a World War Two veteran surprised was a party for his 101st birthday.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think that there are so many people out there with stories still in them that they haven`t had a chance to express and it was a way to say just "thank you."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nate, you were just telling me on your 100th birthday, that`s a 101st, you would go with a meal if you can still handle one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With a big meal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A big old fat meal. Not healthy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Probably stuff I shouldn`t be eating at 100.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re not talking about vegetable tray.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the time to do it though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that thinking.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I will pour Ranch dressing in the vegetables.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that thinking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are going to begin this hour with the news, the latest on the search for a submersible, the one that disappeared on its way to the wreck of the Titanic.\par \par \uc2 Rescue teams have detected -- this is good news, hopeful news anyway -- underwater noises in the search area, but it is unclear if they came from the vessel and it is estimated that there is less than 24 hours of oxygen left for the five people on board.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi is in Boston for us following the search. That`s where they are coordinating the search efforts as well.\par \par \uc2 Roxana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with the Coast Guard official earlier today who told us while they don`t know for sure where those noises came from, it`s still something.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REAR ADMIRAL JOHN MAUGER: . focus for us to look at and so we`ve deployed the remote operated vehicles and the surface vessel, the \uc2 Canadian Coast Guard\uc2  surface vessel that has sonar capability in the vicinity of that to see if we can detect anything in the water in that area.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Finding the submersible is challenging if it`s on the ocean floor. These are just some of the obstacles that crews are dealing with, first darkness. Those who have been to the wreckage say they descended for more than two hours in total darkness before even reaching the ocean floor.\par \par \uc2 Then there is the depth. The wreckage is 12,500 feet underwater, that`s around twice as deep as the Grand Canyon, and the pressure there is crushing, nearly 390 times greater than the pressure on the surface. You also have the deep ocean underwater currents affected by the wind, tides, and water density.\par \par \uc2 Rescuers say, there is also a chance the vessel could be on the ocean`s surface, but even then, it won`t be easy to find. They`ve told us they expect maybe a couple of inches or a feet to be showing at most -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Our thoughts are with the families of those in the sub and we`re all holding on to hope right now.\par \par \uc2 Roxana, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Shifting gears now, Ukrainians have been paying a heavy price as Russia invaded their country last year. Thousands and I mean, thousands have lost arms and legs and that has created an urgent need for artificial limbs.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams visited a clinic in Ukraine providing prosthetics and rehabilitation and met two young wounded warriors who are learning to walk again.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Weslana Danalkina (sp?) signed up for the military two months after Russia invaded her country, a radio operator, she celebrated her 19th birthday on the frontline.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: But then in February, her unit came under mortar fire.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: And it tore off Weslana`s left leg.\par \par \uc2 Did you realize immediately that you had lost your leg?\par \par \uc2 WESLANA DANALKINA (19 years old volunteer): No.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: And what did you feel at that point?\par \par \uc2 (WESLANA DANALKINA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "I grabbed my leg and realized that I was holding it in my hand, just a bone," she told us. "I needed to give myself first-aid. It was scary. It was painful."\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: We met Weslana just four days after she was fitted with a new prosthetic, no longer on the battlefield, but still a fighter, she was already pushing herself to the limits.\par \par \uc2 She told us she plans one day to dance again.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine says more than 10,000 of its people have lost a limb during Russia`s bloody invasion, a generation scarred for life.\par \par \uc2 The Superhumans Center set up far from the frontline is Ukraine`s first state-of-the-art clinic for prosthetics and rehabilitation. They started treating wounded warriors here even while construction workers finished the building.\par \par \uc2 HOWARD BUFFETT\uc2  (Howard Buffett Foundation): If you look at what Russia has done in Ukraine, it is barbaric.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: This Superhumans Center is funded by the Howard Buffett Foundation`s. He`s the son of the billionaire, Warren Buffett.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: And says his charity has given Ukraine nearly $300 million since the invasion began, and Buffett has visited the country seven times during the war.\par \par \uc2 What are your impressions of Ukraine and Ukrainians?\par \par \uc2 HOWARD BUFFETT\uc2 : Well, I think Ukraine is actually teaching the world a lesson. I think they`re teaching us the kind of commitment and resiliency and fight that can be mustered up against somebody like Russia.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Denis Kevinko (sp?) will need every ounce of resilience he can muster. A triple amputee, who told us he lost both legs and his left arm to an anti-tank rocket.\par \par \uc2 But this former infantry soldier is so driven, his doctors believe he`ll one day be able to run again.\par \par \uc2 (DENIS KEVINKO speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "We can`t give up," he told us. "We have a long life ahead of us and we should take fate by its horns."\par \par \uc2 You`re very good friend.\par \par \uc2 (WESLANA DANALKINA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Just like Ukraine, Weslana and Denis never wanted this war, and it has left them shattered, yet like their country, they are unbroken and undefeated.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Holly Williams, Lviv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m so sorry we are even still telling the story about this war that it still continues over a year later. There are still so many layers to this, and you hear about the statistics of the wounded and the dead, and there are real people behind that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Once again, we see that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Live and up close and impersonal. It`s very difficult.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re fighting for their country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re giving up a lot for it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are not giving up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly ends on the note that I think is resonant in that country: They are undefeated.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They continue on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And still very confident.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well said.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Despite all the pain.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, we are going to talk with Shayla Michelle Reaves. She is from our Minneapolis station, that`s WCCO. She is a member of the \uc2 CBS\uc2  family. We like that. There she is.\par \par \uc2 I like the color of your dress today.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is the author of a new children`s book which aims to connect Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr.`s inspiring words to the present day.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:08, I like the color of her dress, see? Get it? 8:08. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up in a moment, Wildfang makes clothes for all genders. The company`s founder is going to be here to talk about empowering everyone to wear whatever it is they want.\par \par \uc2 Plus, in our CBS MORNINGS Deals, something that could help keep your groceries fresh longer, all available at exclusive low, low prices.\par \par \uc2 You are watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That of course is John Legend. It has been nearly 60 years since Dr. Martin Luther King, Jr. gave his famous "I Have a Dream" speech and now, a new children`s book aims to connect his words to a new generation.\par \par \uc2 The book is called "Echo in the Distance." The author is Shayla Michelle Reaves and this is she reading from the book.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Suddenly, thunder splits the skies, ripping hearts persecuting lives, splitting the evil, dismembering the lies, devouring ignorance and passionate cries engulfing injustice entangled in time, swallowing fear and fragile minds, digesting change, disaggregating the times. People are people, not color-coded crimes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that line in the book. Shayla Michelle Reaves wrote those words 20 years ago in a college writing competition that she won by the way. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now, she is an Emmy Award winning anchor and reporter at our \uc2 CBS\uc2  station, that`s WCCO in Minneapolis, St. Paul.\par \par \uc2 Shayla, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Good morning to you. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I`m glad you`re here. WCCO rejected me back in the day. I still have that rejection letter. Dear Gayle, we wish you luck in your career. But unfortunately, blah, blah, blah.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Well, Gayle, you are shining.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So I am glad --\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: You are shining.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about when you wrote that.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You wrote that 20 years ago -- you were a freshman in college.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What were you thinking at the time? Surely you weren`t thinking one day this is going to be a book.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What were your thoughts as you wrote those words?\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: No, at the time, I was actually a freshman entering a writing competition at \uc2 Northwestern University\uc2 . They had a written expression competition for MLK, and the winner of that contest would have a chance to perform in 2003.\par \par \uc2 So I ended up winning that competition.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Performed my piece in 2003 and at the time, I just was writing what I hoped would be an exceptional spoken word piece, and it was an honor to perform that piece for the university.\par \par \uc2 I received invitations to also perform it at \uc2 DePaul University\uc2 , as well as the Safer Foundation in Harvey, Illinois. But like anything that you don`t do more with, it collects dust over time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is true. It`s true.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: But I always had in the back of my mind, this would be such a great illustrated book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: But I just never did more with it and I didn`t have the resources as a college freshman to do more with it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you did more with it. How did you find an illustrator?\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Well, \uc2 Instagram\uc2 . We are in the age of \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Instagram\uc2  you say you got it?\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes, yes. After hearing me talk about it for a long time, my husband said, you know what, for your Christmas gift, a couple of years ago, he said, I`m going to make this your gift. It is like paying for you to bring this into the world because you`ve been talking about it for so long.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s sweet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What a nice husband, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: So I had to do all the research into the illustrator and finding the publisher and all of that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And good old \uc2 Instagram\uc2  come through.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Good old \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I was on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  and someone who had interviewed, her name is Audra Robinson with an awesome product, "Rocky Robinson Self-Care for Girls" standing right next to Kprecia Ambers, the illustrator for my book and I said, I wonder who this person is? Clicked right on this picture, ad all of these beautiful illustrations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now, the two of you have got a wonderful --\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I like -- let`s talk about the imagery in your book.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you make Martin Luther King like a superhero.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is the message you were sending there?\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes. So the image I think that connects with people the most, it goes with the line "People are people, not color-coated crimes."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which means what?\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes, so that line, what I really want people to take away is just because of the color of someone`s skin, it doesn`t mean that they have criminal intent or nefarious motives. It`s because of the color of their skin, it doesn`t mean that there`s something bad or terrible on the inside. It is appealing to people to see the humanity of other people.\par \par \uc2 And so the superhero character, I`m not even someone who was connected to superheroes and really attached to that growing up, but I woke up in the middle of the night and I said, that`s what needs to go with that line.\par \par \uc2 And when you think of superheroes or Superman, he is fair, he is confident, he`s compassionate. He is considerate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: But there`s something about that particular character that connects all kinds of people. No matter what people look like, they connect with that particular character.\par \par \uc2 And there is something about him no matter the race of people, they connect with him, and race is the issue that keeps people divided.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: And so I said what if you take this character that is able to transcend this issue that gets people stuck, give him a little bit more color? And then instead of the S, you put a heart on his chest?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and voila, "Echo in the Distance."\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes. And there you have it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you`ve been connecting with kids at schools.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What`s it been like speaking in front of that audience?\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: It`s been really amazing. Kids have some incredible questions. And I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they are honest.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: They are very honest.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: And I think one of the things that`s been really interesting about that is they`re not only interested in the book, but they`re showing me that they`re interested in how I occupy space.\par \par \uc2 And they are showing me that it`s not only interesting for them to see me as a journalist, as a writer, but how they too, can occupy space in the world, that it`s important for them to see somebody that can be on television, somebody that can write a book, somebody that can illustrate a book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: All the things that they can be in the world, standing in front of them, and then telling them that they can do that, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: I think that`s valuable and important, and so I think that`s important to be in classrooms and showing them who and how they can exist in the world as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, we`ll just say this to you, Shayla Michelle Reaves, now author, congratulation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Something tells me, this is not going to be your last book.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Oh, it`s not. I`ve already got a few more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, all right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is not going to be your last book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We know where to find her.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the name of your first book is called "Echo in the Distance."\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s on sale now wherever you like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 SHAYLA MICHELLE REAVES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. Ahead, "Talk of the Table" and you know we always have fun during that segment.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "FBI True" is back. Ten new episodes explore the real stories behind some of the Bureau`s most famous and infamous missions. It is a docuseries streaming exclusively on Paramount+ and it offers up a behind the scenes look at some of the most shocking cases as well.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "FBI True.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The best part is, real agents are the ones telling the story.\par \par \uc2 One episode looks at the case of four Americans kidnapped by pirates off the coast of Somalia and how the \uc2 FBI\uc2  led that pursuit.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "FBI True.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Season 3 "FBI True" streaming now with our corporate siblings at Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got the low cacti table going for us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. The cacti.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The cacti.\par \par \uc2 Nate, what are you inspired to talk about today?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, I`m inspired to talk a little music, so my "Talk of the Table" is a first-time honor for one of hip-hop`s founding fathers.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Dre will receive the first ever ASCAP Hip-Hop Icon Award tomorrow night. It will be presented in LA during the Rhythm and Soul Music Awards celebration of 50 years of hip-hop.\par \par \uc2 Organizers say the award is given to members whose music has made an indelible impact on art and culture of hip-hop.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He has certainly done that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, he has done that and also just out of the studio to with Dre Beats.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is one hell of a businessman, so shout out to Dr. Dre.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" -- we already talked about what Nate would do on his 100th birthday. We have got another option here for a celebration.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Robert Moore, he lives in the Bay Area and he`s a dog lover. His family knew that. He turned 100 and they put a little notification on the Nextdoor app to say hey, he would love a dog parade outside of his house.\par \par \uc2 They thought maybe 20 dogs would show up, instead some 200 dogs showed up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two hundred?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @CarolSwag247/TikTok.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We have pictures, do we not? All kinds of doggies came by to say "hello." I just think it`s a great --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a great outing for the whole community.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They came from near and far.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Such unconditional love. I love this story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of wagging tails and apparently an endurance test here. He pet every single dog, so all 200 of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but just thinking about how happy that made him. I love it, Tony.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh man, it made him so happy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that. May we all make it to a hundred.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: May we all make that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Knock on cacti table.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is the demise of QR code menus. Aren`t you guys sick of those?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Are they done?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll go first.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is it over?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Well, we`re hoping so.\par \par \uc2 The online menus became very popular, of course, we know this during the pandemic, but now, many restaurants are getting rid of them. They say that the customers and the waiters just don`t like them. Yes, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 The menu management company called Must Have Menu says that from April 1st to May 16th of this year, it saw a 27 percent drop in the number of scans in restaurant QR codes compared to the same period in 2021.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just think they are such a pain. In the beginning, I got it, because we needed that for our safety. But now, you scan it and then when you want dessert, you have to scan it again. They said the world gets more digitized every day.\par \par \uc2 It felt like restaurants were one of the few analogue public spaces that we have left. And they said it ruins the mood because you`re not supposed to be on your phone, but you pick it out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes, that`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When you`re sitting and dining with whoever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. You see notifications. You see news. You see text messages.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I am so hoping that this is over sooner than later.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I don`t mind it. I don`t mind the scanning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You don`t? Oh, God. Why?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well just because sometimes you get that menu and there are fingerprints all over.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am good with that. I love that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re good with that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That doesn`t bother me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Build up that immune system a little bit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like the laminated, greasy spoon kind of thingy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just like to hold it and read it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Tony and I will sit on this side of the room.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, we`re going to take a turn now to a Pride Month event we`re having coming up right here, a celebration of leaders in the LGBTQ community, part of our series "Changing the Game."\par \par \uc2 And what we do with that is we highlight extraordinary women who are making a difference now and for future generations.\par \par \uc2 Emma Mcilroy is founder and CEO of Wildfang. It is a gender fluid clothing company and Mcilroy launched it way back in 2013 after she noticed that certain silhouettes like blazers and tailored pants were not available to women in the same quality and fit as for men.\par \par \uc2 The clothes are available online or in one of the brands to retail stores in Portland and Los Angeles and celebrities like Janelle Monae have stepped out in Wildfang wear and the company has raised more than a million dollars for charity.\par \par \uc2 So we are very glad to welcome Emma Mcilroy to the show.\par \par \uc2 Emma, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY (Wildfang founder and CEO): Yes. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you -- there`s a quote here and I don`t know if it`s the right one here. So I`m going to start with it. You`ve got to explain. Clothes are just clothes, are they?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Look, there`s been a lot of things that have been passed on from generation to generation around gender norms, right? How we get to show up in the world? How we get to talk? What jobs do we get to do?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Emma, you remember the phrase, clothes make the man?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Yes. Correct.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Clothe maketh the man, right? And so, clothes are a really important part of how we express ourselves, Tony, right?\par \par \uc2 So, it is how we show up in the world, how we want others to see ourselves and at Wildfang, we just like to play with that, right? We want to take those norms and we want to sort of throw them out the window and just have a little bit more fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you`re saying kind of both things, like clothes are really important. They are how you express yourself.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Super important.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are a representation of identity. But at the same time, don`t overthink it. Don`t go with what was. You can go in any direction you want.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Correct. However you want to show up in the world, we get you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you started this company 10 years ago, and you started why? Were you out shopping around and saying, I need X, Y, & Z and it`s not here. What happened?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Exactly. So I walked into a department store with my best friend and we both walked through the women`s department. I was looking for a graphic tee, she was looking for a great blazer and all the blazers in the women`s section had like those weird fake pockets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Where you can only get your fingernails in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t understand the fake pocket.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Right. And so we wandered through, and we ended up in the men`s department. And then lo and behold, that is where all the great blazers were hanging out, right? And the great graphic tees that were bold and provocative and strong.\par \par \uc2 And so we were like, well wait? Why can`t women have a great blazer, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Agree.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: So we decided that was where it came from.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, I love that. You see this crossover quite a bit. You see women that might want to bag your fit and like, well, I have to go to the men`s section to find this or a guy, he may see a beanie or some accessories.\par \par \uc2 My wife might be wearing something, I`m like, you know what? If she`s done, I`m taking that to my side of the closet and I`m aware of that beanie because I like the colorway.\par \par \uc2 So you creating this brand, it`s absolutely genius and necessary. It is so well done. It`s been profitable, quickly growing. What do you think, is the reason for this success so soon?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: I think we put our customer at the heart and soul of every decision. I think everything for us starts and ends with the person that walks through our doors, you know? What do they want? What makes them feel great? And that guides every decision?\par \par \uc2 And I think our brand has a heartbeat and I think our customer feels that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, you say we`re here to rewrite the future of gender. What does that mean exactly to you, Emma?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: So like I said, there`s all these rules that being passed on, right, like jobs that at one point, you and I weren`t able to do, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Or ways we weren`t able to show up, ways that we weren`t able to talk and all of those norms, all of those rules connected to your gender. We just want to throw them out the window and we want to allow you to be exactly who you want to be and show up exactly how you want to show up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And challenging these norms, as a queer founder, did you have different challenges when you first started out the business?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How were you received? What did people say?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Yes. When I started, most people were like, oh, it`s a gay brand. It`s for a couple of gay girls in the northwest, you know? And now, as the conversation has progressed, that`s no longer the case. Right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What does that mean even? Gay girls? Yes, what does that mean?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Yes, so if I -- you know, if I`d had coffee, if I`d started a coffee company, it would be gay coffee, right? If I`d had a toothpaste company, would it be gay toothpaste, right? Obviously, that didn`t make sense. And I think now, we understand that.\par \par \uc2 We understand that there`s a lot more fluidity in that conversation and there is a lot more ability to express yourself.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So companies that have not been founded with your vision from the ground up might, in this particular month, have a line or a set of branded items, you know, the ones I`m talking about.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: I think I know who you are --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right? And they are sometimes accused of commercializing their solidarity, right? Making a buck off of their ally ship. Is there a better way for companies to approach it?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: Look, I love folks stepping into this space and uplifting the LGBTQ community, especially at this time of year. I think if you`re going to do that and you are going to make money off the community, you better make sure you`re there to support them, too. Right? And that looks like standing behind them when they need it. That looks like donations.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve given back over a million dollars to charities that we support, right? So making sure you`re putting your money where your mouth is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why is that important to you? To give back? Because a lot of brands say, we do, but we need to check the books and they really don`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or, we`ll get to that later.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let us grow first.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: I think it comes back to the question you asked me before about putting your consumer at the heart of everything you do. It matters to our consumer, right? It`s table stakes for them.\par \par \uc2 And so as a result, it`s for us, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, Emma, we appreciate you joining us. We love the brand. Love the name of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, what does Wildfang mean?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Had us singing one of our favorite songs all morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: "Wild Thing." Are you going to sing it out? Here we go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve never heard that before? But what does Wildfang mean? What does mean to you?\par \par \uc2 EMMA MCILROY: You know, when I was looking for a name for the company, I find this word Wildfang. It has German roots. And to me it sounded like it was either a rock band, or it was a kid`s book. And that`s what I wanted for the brand, right?\par \par \uc2 I wanted the ferociousness and rawness of the rock brand an