\uc2 GAYLE BASS: I love the price range on this. This starts at $22.49 and remember, we have these in bundles. So think about seasonings, salsa, sauces, hot sauce, you get a kit combination. Just go to CBSDeals.com and you can pick what you love.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Gayle, the other thing that drives me crazy especially in the summer is, I buy a lot of fruit and within like three days if you don`t eat it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is gone.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is gone. You`re picking up moldy the strawberries --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Spoiled.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nothing I hate more than moldy strawberries.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes. Deb Meyer GreenBoxes are one of bestsellers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t eat those, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sometimes I just eat around them, Gayle, because I love strawberries.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, these GreenBoxes. Hey, I just cut them out.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Eat around them the mold, yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: But Deb Meyer GreenBoxes are my personal --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wait, wait, wait. Do you really do that? Are you being serious?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I cut -- you cut the little moldy part off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, you can do that. You`ll be fine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Like on cheese --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can make jam out of it, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle was disgusted.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is totally like, wait. Stop this segment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Next you`re going to tell me, you eat around the moldy cheese, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I am French.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, well you chop it off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He buys it moldy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Okay, this is what you do. When you get GreenBoxes, you put your veggies in there, you wash them, you put them in, and they extend the life of your vegetables, and your snacks and your bread rolls.\par \par \uc2 That`s why they are such a good seller because people know that you pay a lot of money for your fruits and vegetables.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You want to extend the life. You go to a farmer market and you`re thinking, well, I don`t want to buy all of that. I`m going to waste it. You`re not.\par \par \uc2 Like the other day, we featured s`mores yesterday, remember, me and boyfriend having a date night with bonfires that you can get on CBSDeals.com. You can put them in there to save them for the next day.\par \par \uc2 This is a great must have and we have the 16-piece set. That`s the 16 set, that`s 32 pieces. Sixteen pieces here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For how much?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: $18.18.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Eighteen dollars, eighteen cents -- under $20.00.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, all right. Lastly, you`ve got something to help --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sell it, Gayle. Sell it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Okay I am going to --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People won`t be slipping in the shower with this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes. You know, after you`ve had all that barbecue, you get dirty and you need to jump in. Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How do you eat at your dinner table, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Messily. Messily. These are wonderful if you want to make sure that the people you care for don`t slip in the shower.\par \par \uc2 You know how surfers stay on their surfboards? Because of the Kahuna grip. These were developed for surfers so they don`t slip and fall. So you put these in your bathroom and use these. These are -- they are less than a millimeter thick, and we have these.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How much?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: $15.99.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Fifteen ninety-nine. You won`t slip in the shower.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. With sticker.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It would be nice and safe.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You see how easy that is. I did it with one hand.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Gayle Bass. We love having you, of course we partner with all these companies to get these deals.\par \par \uc2 To buy anything you saw today, scan that QR code on your screen or head to CBSDeals.com to start shopping now.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, Candi Carter, chief content officer of Knocking will return to \uc2 CBS\uc2  with more, more deals.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re still talking about Vlad`s love of a very good breast. We will leave you on that note.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you tomorrow if we still have jobs on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230621ej6l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 One Reason Why The Country Is Facing A Shortage Of OB-GYNs And The Impact It Could Have On Your Health\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 849 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We turn now to a looming crisis when it comes to women`s health care. The shortage of OB-GYN doctors is expected to get worse in the years ahead. It`s having an effect on medical students who are deciding on what and where to practice based on a state`s abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian takes a closer look at what`s changed since Roe v. Wade was overturned and our series After Roe: One Year.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): Erin Duffey is a third-year med student in Florida. But this is not where the future OB-GYN wants to be a doctor.\par \par \uc2 ERIN DUFFEY (Med Student): Unless things change, it wouldn`t be my first choice to practice here.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: What about other restrictive states, Texas, elsewhere?\par \par \uc2 ERIN DUFFEY: It`s just not an environment I really want to be in.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Students like Duffey are increasingly steering clear of OB-GYN residencies in states with abortion bans. Applicants in those states plummeted more than 10 percent since Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Some are deciding to avoid the specialty altogether, worried about the ability to practice evidence-based medicine.\par \par \uc2 ERIN DUFFEY: There`s also the big concern of the possibility of being legally prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 DR. NICOLE SCOTT (Indiana University School of Medicine): Hugh is coming in Thursday morning.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dr. Nicole Scott is the director of the OB-GYN residency program at Indiana University School of Medicine, says she`s already seen a drop in applications.\par \par \uc2 DR. NICOLE SCOTT: What I`m specifically worried about is the retention of those doctors once they`re finished training and their practice after residency.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And that puts the health of all women at risk, as OB-GYN also screen for cancer, perform well women exams, and prescribe contraception.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMELIA HUNTSBERGER (Obstetrician/Gynecologist): This really isn`t just about abortion access.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dr. Amelia Huntsberger was doing that in Idaho, where most abortions are banned.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMELIA HUNTSBERGER: It`s very clear that Idaho is no longer a safe place to practice medicine.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The OB-GYN is leaving from neighboring Oregon, after her rural hospital closed its maternity unit, citing, in part, staffing and Idaho`s political climate.\par \par \uc2 DR. AMELIA HUNTSBERGER: If I`m an OB-GYN resident coming out of residency, and I`m looking around at different options, why would I look at Idaho and say, oh, I really want to move there, to the state where I could be charged with a felony before providing medical care?\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Current doctors leaving and new ones practicing elsewhere, leading to labor pains in women`s health care.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, CBS News. Orlando.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, Juneteenth celebrations were held today across the country. The federal holiday marks the day in 1865 when the last enslaved people in the United States learned they were free.\par \par \uc2 In Fort Worth, Texas, 96-year-old Opal Lee right there took part in a two- and-a-half-mile freedom walk. She spent decades advocating for this day to be recognized as a national holiday. And she`s known as the grandmother of Juneteenth.\par \par \uc2 Well, some communities out West are a hot spot for some migrating crickets. That`s straight ahead.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: People living in parts of Nevada and Idaho are dealing with an invasion of so-called Mormon crickets.\par \par \uc2 The crickets, which look like large grasshoppers, are migrating through the area after being dormant for four years. They don`t fly. They don`t bite. But when they get squished by cars, they make a mess. Some have been forced to use leaf blowers and even a snowplow to push piles of these little critters out of the way. Nice.\par \par \uc2 Well, pop singer Bebe Rexha is recovering from a frightening incident at a concert in New York.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bebe Rexha`s performance)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: What she`s now telling her fans tonight -- next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Pop star Bebe Rexha is assuring fans that she is OK after being hit in the face with a cell phone during a concert in New York City Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bebe Rexha`s performance)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: In a video today, she showed her bruised and bandaged eye and reassured her fans she is all right. The 27-year-old man who threw the phone is facing an assault charge.\par \par \uc2 Well, how the faces of classical musicians are changing -- that`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230621ej6j0008e\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Breast Cancer Breakthrough?\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 VIQ Solutions, Inc. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We turn now to a possible game-changer when it comes to the fight against breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 CBS ` Janet Shamlian reports on a new study which shows, in the age of A.I., technology is better at predicting a patient`s risk than current methods.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voice-over): It`s cutting-edge research. Artificial intelligence is significantly better at predicting a patient`s cancer risk, according to a new study out today.\par \par \uc2 DR. POLLY NIRAVATH (\uc2 Houston Methodist Hospital\uc2  ): The role of A.I. in predicting breast cancer risk is definitely in our future.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dr. Polly Niravath is a breast oncologist with \uc2 Houston Methodist\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 DR. POLLY NIRAVATH: Assessing a person`s risk, the A.I. can do it probably a lot cheaper and faster as well.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Current clinical models use a patient`s age, family history and information like breast density to determine risk. Artificial intelligence is able to get biomarkers, more information, from the mammogram, helping doctors make personalized recommendations.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  research analyzed the mammograms of more than 18,000 women. A.I. took just seconds to determine their five-year breast cancer risk, and it was up to 25 percent more accurate when combined with personal history. The \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  reports, this year alone, doctors will diagnose almost 300,000 cases of breast cancer. More than 43,000 U.S. women will die of the disease.\par \par \uc2 The rate of breast cancer in women ages 25 to 39 has increased 32 percent since 2009.\par \par \uc2 DR. POLLY NIRAVATH: I think it ends up being a very valuable tool in our arsenal.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dr. Niravath says younger women could benefit from this technology, a predictor of when they should get screened, which could save lives.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And here at \uc2 Houston Methodist\uc2  , they are already using A.I. to help determine breast cancer risk.\par \par \uc2 While it`s not widespread, its use is growing, and the technology could be especially helpful in areas that lack advanced medical resources -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It is such an important story.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, 75 million Americans from New England to the Midwest and as far south as the Carolinas are under air quality alerts. That`s because smoke from wildfires in Eastern Canada is causing dangerous pollution in the U.S. You can actually see how dense that smoke is in the satellite image. In New York City, alerts are expected to remain in place through Friday, meaning you should limit your time outdoors.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230608ej66000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Nikki Haley Live\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,600 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Seven thirty, CBS MORNINGS. Pretty picture there of Charleston, South Carolina because we`re going to begin this half hour with a conversation with Republican presidential candidate, Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from CNN.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, you might have seen last night in the CNN town hall, the former governor and \uc2 UN\uc2  Ambassador called China the number one national security threat, and said the US must support Ukraine`s fight against Russia.\par \par \uc2 She also talked about hot button social issues for GOP voters like gun laws, abortion laws, and transgender rights.\par \par \uc2 So Ambassador Nikki Haley, it is my pleasure to welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. Thank you for getting up with us this morning.\par \par \uc2 You are in Iowa today, where you have been for a very long time. You were the first candidate to announce and you seem committed to visiting every farm in that state.\par \par \uc2 One thing that voters do when they`re hearing your pitch is imagine you in the job right now if you were in charge. So, I want to start there.\par \par \uc2 We had this incident in the Taiwan Strait yesterday where a Chinese warship cut in front of an American Destroyer, how should the US respond?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Well, with strength to start with. I mean, I think it`s not just that. You look at Russia invading Ukraine, you look at, you know, China on the march; you look at Iran building a bomb, you look at North Korea testing ballistic missiles -- none of this would have happened had we not had that debacle in Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the idea that we left Bagram Air Force Base in the middle of the night, without telling our allies who stood shoulder-to-shoulder with us for decades because we asked them to be there. Think about what that told our friends, but more importantly, think about what that told her enemies.\par \par \uc2 So now they smell blood in the water, and we`re seeing -- they sense weakness and they`re trying to move through. What we need to do is show strength. We need to let them know what we expect of them and what we`re going to do about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you think a military conflict with China could come in the next four to eight years?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I think what`s important is that we prevent that, and the way you prevent that is that you have to be strong. And that`s why I think it`s so important that Ukraine has to win this war on Russia, because if Ukraine wins this war, it sends a message to China on Taiwan, it sends a message to Iran wanting to build a bomb and threaten Israel, it sends a message to North Korea, and all the other enemies that want to destroy us.\par \par \uc2 If Ukraine loses, then Russia is going to move in to Poland and the Baltics, and we`re looking at World War Three, and you`re going to see China move into Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 So it doesn`t mean we put money on the ground, it doesn`t mean we put troops on the ground. But what it means is we should continue to work with our allies so that they have the equipment and the ammunition they need to win.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Madam Ambassador, let`s talk about abortion. That is a defining issue for many voters, particularly there in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Last night, you declined to take a position on a federal six-week ban, calling it a hypothetical, it would never pass as you described it. But where would you draw that line? How many weeks, at what point federally?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: What I`ve said is that I am unapologetically pro-life, but it`s a very personal issue. I`m pro-life because my husband was adopted. I`m pro-life because I had trouble having my children.\par \par \uc2 And what I think we need to do is understand everybody has a story. I don`t judge people for being pro-choice any more than I want them to judge me for being pro-life.\par \par \uc2 But if we`re going to have a conversation about a federal ban, let`s have an honest one. In order to do a ban, you`d have to have a majority of the House, 60 Senate votes and a signature by a president. We haven`t had 60 pro-life senators in over a hundred years, we might have 45.\par \par \uc2 So the reality is, whatever we do on the federal level has to be consensus. So a Republican president can`t ban abortions, any more than a Democrat president can ban all these laws that are happening in the states.\par \par \uc2 So where can we agree? We can agree, let`s stop late-term abortions. Let`s make sure that if doctors and nurses don`t believe in abortion, they shouldn`t have to perform them. Let`s encourage more adoptions and make sure our children in foster care feel love.\par \par \uc2 Let`s make sure contraception is accessible, and let`s say that if a woman has an abortion, she shouldn`t go to jail or get the death penalty -- let`s start there.\par \par \uc2 And instead of demonizing the issue, let`s humanize the issue.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Madam Ambassador, another topic driving conversation today is transgender rights. There was a question last night about transgender sports, particularly youth sports. You took a run at it and I don`t know if it came out exactly as you intended.\par \par \uc2 I want to play that sound and then have you respond to it. Let`s go with it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Biological boys playing in girls` sports. It is the women`s issue of our time.\par \par \uc2 My daughter ran track in high school. I don`t even know how I would have that conversation with her. How are we supposed to get our girls used to the fact that biological boys are in their locker rooms? And then we wonder why a third of our teenage girls seriously contemplated suicide last year?\par \par \uc2 We should be growing strong girls, confident girls.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Madam Ambassador, I wanted you to clarify if you could, the connection there to teen suicide?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Well, I mean, first of all, when girls are seeing multiple -- I talked to a senior high school girl in New Hampshire a couple of weeks ago. And she said, in every single classroom, they`re talking about transgenderism. And she said, I feel like I`m walking on eggshells, because we don`t know what we can say and what we can`t.\par \par \uc2 Girls need to feel confident in their own skin. They need to be able to do that. When you see someone like Dylan Mulvaney mocking women, when you see the fact that they`re now having to go and have biological boys in their locker rooms, that`s not good for teenage girls, that`s not something they need to be focused on.\par \par \uc2 It`s not that we don`t understand and appreciate the challenges of someone who is transgender, but why are you putting that on everybody else? Let the girls have their own locker room. Let the girls have their own sports. That was the whole point of Title 9.\par \par \uc2 Don`t go and push, you know, the challenges of a small minority onto the majority of our girls. That`s not -- they don`t deserve that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Madam Ambassador, another question is what care should be on the table when a 12-year-old child in this country assigned female at birth says actually, I feel more comfortable living as a boy. What should the law allow the response to be?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Well, I think the law should stay out of it and I think parents should handle it. This is a job for the parents to handle, and then when that child becomes 18, if they want to make more of a permanent change, they can do that. But I think up until then, we see with our teenage kids, they go through a lot during puberty, they go through a lot of confusion, they go through a lot of anxiety, they go through a lot of pressures. We should support them the whole way through, but we don`t need to go in enforce something in schools.\par \par \uc2 We don`t need school sitting, they are hiding from the parents what gender pronoun they`re using. We don`t need to have those conversations in schools. Those are conversations that should be had at home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Nikki Haley, first female governor of South Carolina hoping to be the first female president of the United States. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Thanks, go to NikkiHaley.com.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And still ahead, important new information about a critical moment in American history, the Iran hostage crisis in 1980.\par \par \uc2 And up next, the concerns over new facial recognition technology at American airports.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230606ej65000p2\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For June 4, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Strassmann, Ed O`Keefe, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Robert Costa,  Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,618 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 June 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: debt crisis averted. Now what else can a divided Washington accomplish for Americans and the economy?\par \par \uc2 President Biden signed the bipartisan deal to cut federal spending and suspend the nation`s debt ceiling just hours before what could have been a catastrophic default.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): No one got everything they wanted, but the American people got what they needed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will ask two dealmakers, West Virginia`s Democratic Senator Joe Manchin and Louisiana Republican \uc2 Garret Graves\uc2 , about what`s next for the country.\par \par \uc2 Plus, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  CEO \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2  on how brinkmanship impacts an ever-changing economy.\par \par \uc2 Then: The field of Republicans running for president is growing, with one high-profile competitor diving in.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida) (Presidential Candidate): Our great American comeback will start by sending \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  back to his basement in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And more expected to cruise into the race this week.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Former Vice President of the United States): Come this Wednesday, I`m announcing in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will check in with our experts CBS News political panel on what voters can expect as the race intensifies.\par \par \uc2 Overseas, Russia steps up its attacks in and around Kyiv. Can Ukraine`s coming counteroffensive put Moscow back on its heels? We will check in with Ukrainian Ambassador \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot to get to this morning.\par \par \uc2 And we begin with Republican Congressman \uc2 Garret Graves\uc2 , who led Republican negotiators during the debate over raising the debt ceiling. He joins us from Baton Rouge, Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 Good to talk to you.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES (R-Louisiana): Hey. Great to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden said you all were respectful, honest and operated in good faith.\par \par \uc2 Do you see any sign on the heels of this that Republican leaders can work with the White House on big issues like immigration reform and border security? Is there momentum?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: Look, the negotiations certainly were in good faith, but they also included a lot of candor.\par \par \uc2 We had some tense moments throughout. But I would love to tell you that we could build upon this. We certainly have a crisis in the energy space. We continue to have a financial crisis. And, as you indicated, immigration is a huge issue where we should be able to work together.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, more Democrats and Republicans actually voted for the deal that you negotiated. And it leaves largely intact most of the President`s economic agenda.\par \par \uc2 How can you claim, as you told my \uc2 CBS\uc2  colleague the other day, that the White House got schooled?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: Because the White House laid down about seven red lines, including that they wouldn`t negotiate, that they wouldn`t allow anything to be done on work requirements, that they wouldn`t allow any changes to environmental laws, that they wouldn`t allow us to take any funds from \uc2 IRS\uc2  agents, and on and on.\par \par \uc2 And yet every single red line that they laid down, we crossed right over in negotiations, resulting in the largest savings in any legislation in the history of the Congress. This was a huge accomplishment, huge legislative wins. And lastly, Margaret, I think when you look at the president`s statements, all he could do is talk about things that didn`t happen versus anything that did that was positive for his agenda.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Well, a trillion is a trillion-and-a-half over 10 years in savings.\par \par \uc2 But you said there was an erosion of trust within your own party between you and some of those harder-line Freedom Caucus members, who said it was a bad deal that you negotiated. And, this morning, Congressman \uc2 Ken Buck\uc2  of Colorado said Speaker McCarthy has credibility issues, and while a motion to vote vacate won`t happen right away, he should be concerned about being removed from office.\par \par \uc2 How big of a problem is this right wing of your party?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: Look, there were erosion of trust issues because folks went out and began attempting to define or interpret a deal while we were still in negotiations. There wasn`t even an agreement struck.\par \par \uc2 And, of course, their interpretation or definition of the agreement, as you can imagine, was flawed. In regard to Speaker McCarthy, let`s be crystal clear. This speaker has been one of the best strategists we`ve ever had, delivering transformative immigration, energy, parents bill of rights and other legislation that, looking back at previous congresses, not even one of these things has been done, much less we`re about 10 huge wins this year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 But 71 members of the House Republican caucus voted against it. I know you had 67 percent. I mean, you had the majority of your caucus, but 71, that`s not nothing there. I mean, can you rule out a government shutdown in the fall? Are these hard-line members going to be emboldened on the back of this?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: Well, Margaret, let`s talk about what their goals are. Their goals are trying to change the trajectory of spending, transform Washington in a way that`s more responsive, long-term thinking about the next generation and the fiscal crisis that we`re approaching.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that should be a partisan issue. We should all be on board with those objectives. In this case, I think that leverage was trying to be exercised that really threatened the economy in a large way. We`ve already seen, under this administration, stock market has lost around $9 trillion in value, hurting retirement accounts and families all across the country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: We can`t continue to see those types of challenges.\par \par \uc2 And so I do think that, in the appropriations process, we are going to be working hard to continue the momentum that we`ve gained through this negotiation, saving trillions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you rule out a government shutdown?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: I`m not ruling out anything. It depends on how reasonable each side is, obviously, in the negotiations. It`s very difficult to predict.\par \par \uc2 But I want to be clear. Republicans are going to demand continuing to build upon the success that we were able to achieve in debt ceiling negotiations, in changing the way that Washington spends.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But I know you`re one of Speaker McCarthy`s lieutenants here, but when you hear a member go out on television on CNN and say that he should be worried about his job, that he doesn`t have the faith of the caucus, that`s got to be concerning.\par \par \uc2 That affects your tactics, at a minimum.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: Look, I have known \uc2 Ken Buck\uc2  for years and certainly respect him.\par \par \uc2 It is not a mainstream position. I will tell you right now, Speaker McCarthy`s position is absolutely safe. He is going to continue delivering wins for the country, changing the direction of Washington and being more representative of the -- of the priorities of Americans across the country.\par \par \uc2 I have no doubt his position is safe, and we`re going to keep marching forward continuing to build upon the historic wins that he`s been able to achieve this year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, to -- to get what you`re calling these historic wins, though, Democrats would say Republicans took the economy hostage. It was a pretty big game of chicken here to threaten default.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Fitch Ratings\uc2  is now saying, even with this deal, they still -- still could downgrade U.S. credit outlook here because of political standoffs around the debt limit and last minute suspensions before the X- date. It lowers confidence in America.\par \par \uc2 Why was it worth possibly damaging America as -- as the benchmark of the financial system and the credibility?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: You know, yeah, Margaret, it`s a great question.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s one that you need to pose to the White House, considering the fact that they refuse to negotiate for 100 days, despite indicating otherwise. They`re the ones who brought us to the brink. If we had begun negotiations back in February, when the president and speaker first had their -- their meeting to discuss this...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, but it was a point of leverage that Republicans are using.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: ... then we`d never -- we wouldn`t be in this situation today. We would have finished negotiation months ago.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So would you take -- some Democrats are talking about take the debt ceiling just off the table entirely. I know you`ve suspended it for two years. But what about a vote to just get rid of it?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: No, I don`t think that`s appropriate...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you need the leverage?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: I think that it`s really important, just like if you had a child mark that hit their credit card limit and was unable to pay their credit card bill that month, you`d probably step in and help them out.\par \par \uc2 I know that I would. But I would also have a candid conversation about reform of spending. The Committee for Responsible Federal Budget has calculated that every child or grandchild born in America today is going to end up paying about $4.5 million because of the irresponsible spending in Washington, $4.5 million over their lifetime.\par \par \uc2 That is absolutely inexcusable. And we cannot continue on this trajectory.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, one of the things that fellow Republicans are criticizing is the fact that you adopted the Biden defense budget and the 3.5 percent boost, which doesn`t keep up with inflation. How do you justify that that doesn`t hurt defense?\par \par \uc2 Because that`s what Republicans like Lindsey -- \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  are saying.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: Yes. Yes, it`s a great question.\par \par \uc2 So -- so let`s keep in mind that we`re actually increasing defense by about $20 billion to $886 billion. When you take every other function of government, it equals about $704 billion. There`s no question in my mind that we can improve efficiency of the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2 , the large- scale acquisition programs, whether it be weapons systems, aircraft and others.\par \par \uc2 We`re all familiar with the toilet seats and hammer stories of the past, where the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  was paying outrageous prices. So we are going to continue...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE GARRET GRAVES: ... ensuring that our war fighters have the upper hand, while we also ensure that the \uc2 Department of Defense\uc2  improves its efficiency.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we`ll have to have you back.\par \par \uc2 Garret Graves\uc2 , we`re going to leave it there for today.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to West Virginia Democratic Senator Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome back. We really came down to the wire here.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN (D-West Virginia): Yes. Well, it`s good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When you were...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Well, it`s a shame that we do that, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know.\par \par \uc2 When you were with us in March, I remember you saying Speaker McCarthy and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , they just have to stop grandstanding and they needed to start negotiating. How much of a miscalculation was it by Democrats to wait?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: I think it`s harmful.\par \par \uc2 It shows you that the extreme left was pushing so hard not to even negotiate, not to even talk about it, you know, just hold your ground, hold your ground, hold your ground. That`s not who we are in America. The process of democracy, you sit down and work.\par \par \uc2 I want to get...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, they -- they said that the Republicans were hostage-takers, right?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: How can you be a hostage-taker if basically you`re talking about shouldn`t we recognize that we have an unmanageable debt?\par \par \uc2 Shouldn`t we figure out how we got here so fast and so high at $31.5 trillion? Trajectory is out of sight if we don`t do something. So I want to give both sides, negotiating teams, credit.\par \par \uc2 When you have \uc2 Garret Graves\uc2  and -- Congressman Graves and his team, on behalf of Kevin McCarthy, did a wonderful job, and when you have basically -- you have Shalanda Young, you have Steve Ricchetti, and Louisa Terrell working on behalf of the president, those were good people that worked together to make this happen.\par \par \uc2 It`s a shame that we didn`t acknowledge it much quicker...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... three months ago, at least, and not come down to brinksmanship, when it kind of throws the uncertainty into the market to where here we are, the reserve currency of the world market.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: The reserve currency of the world has to have stability where people believe that it`s -- it`s safe.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: You can`t play these games.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Agreed.\par \par \uc2 Well, you -- you also got an energy project tucked into this, the \uc2 Mountain Valley Pipeline\uc2 , which cuts through your state. It`s also cutting through Virginia. And Senator Tim Kaine said, this was a slimy way to handle it -- that was his word -- because it bypasses the courts and has the government seizing land from people in a hard-hit part of his state.\par \par \uc2 How do you respond to that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Well, first of all, Tim Kaine is my dear friend and a good person. He and I were governors together, so we understand both of our states very well.\par \par \uc2 And the Southwest Virginia area he`s talking about is entirely -- my entire state is demographics along those lines. First of all, this has been under review for over eight years, eight years. Now, after eight years, basically, a lot of this land has already been purchased and people have been paid fair market values. And they`ve negotiated and paid.\par \par \uc2 Next of all, it`s gone through eight NEPA reviews, eight. It`s been in the court system nine times. And then, on top of that, Margaret, these processes or these projects cannot move forward...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... unless the FERC commission determines there is a need. Now, three times, they have issued a need for this product as far as more gas in the market.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Next of all, \uc2 Jennifer Granholm\uc2  and this administration came out with a statement saying this is the national interest.\par \par \uc2 The people in the Carolinas are paying 10 times more than we are in West Virginia for the gas we produce. That`s ridiculous for them to have that...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... a heck of a stranglehold on them, and also helping the rest of the secured national markets as far as energy.\par \par \uc2 You have to be independent. You have to be secured. This is a great project that`s going to help, not only just West Virginia...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... but the entire energy security system.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, it`s interesting, because, last month, you said you were going to block all of Joe Biden`s -- President Biden`s \uc2 EPA\uc2  nominees until he halts a radical climate agenda. But he just green-lit a whole bunch of fossil fuel projects, including this one.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been very critical of the president in recent months. Does this change your point of view? Are you ready to endorse him?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Well, the bottom line is, is that I`m going to work whenever I think that, basically, we`re moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 What you`ve just seen is basically the extremes trying to run this operation, the \uc2 United States government\uc2 , from the far left and far right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: It was the middle that pulled everybody together. It was basically reacting to the people saying, enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 That`s what bothers...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that wasn`t a yes when I asked you about endorsing him, though.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Well, the bottom line is, I`m not involved in the political process right now, with all the things that we have to do.\par \par \uc2 Everyone thinks about politics first. I don`t. It`s not about the politics. It`s not about my reelection or anyone else. We have an awful lot of work to do. And we still have permitting to get done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: We have geopolitical unrest around the world. We have to support Ukraine. We have to make sure that we get our financial house in order and get inflation down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: But if you throw politics in, I will guarantee you, you won`t get any decisions on any of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. To...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: And I don`t think we would have got this bill done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To get all those permitting issues done, because you only got a fraction of them in this bill, you want things that will help speed up energy infrastructure permitting for both fossil fuels and for clean energy.\par \par \uc2 Do you need a second term?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you need a second term to get those things done?\par \par \uc2 I mean, another term. Excuse me. Are you running for reelection?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Well, we basically continue...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: You have got to continue to keep working.\par \par \uc2 We can get it done now. We`re on the brink of getting it done right now. We could have got it done last year, but politics entered into and we couldn`t. And now here we are. We got part of it done. We got basically some reforms done as far as permitting.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: And now what we can do is basically make sure that we`re able to get the pipelines and the delivery system we need for clean fossil all over this country to be energy-independent, and also be able to invest in the technologies, the cleaner technologies with -- with less carbon.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to need more transmission.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you know why...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: I`m for all-in -- above energy policy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you know why I asked it that way, because...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: I can support this from wherever I`m at.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: From wherever you`re at. You`re leaving your options open.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Yes, I know. I know exactly why.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know why, because, Jim Justice, your governor, Republican, I mean, he is coming for your job.\par \par \uc2 He has declared. And I know you have said you are going to take your time, until the end of the year to decide. But doesn`t he have an at least six- month advantage here? Don`t you need to tell people what your plans are?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Well, here`s the thing, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 You just said I`m -- I`m -- about my job. My job is to do what I can to help the people of West Virginia and support this great country of ours and defend the Constitution. That`s truly the primary cause of my job. It`s not for me to get reelected.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Now, if I run, I`m going to win.\par \par \uc2 And I`m looking at everything I can to basically help my country to be stronger, to be more moderate, more centrist-governed than anything I have ever done. That`s what my focus is, because I know that`s where good decisions -- look what happened here.\par \par \uc2 We got clear to the brink.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: But this is the first time I have ever seen from the House, working with the Democrats, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  and his team...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... working with Kevin McCarthy and his team to make something happen. I read about that when it used to be Tip O`Neill and President Reagan.\par \par \uc2 Now I have seen it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: And I have seen also President Biden and his team come back to the middle...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... getting away from where the left have pushed them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: So, basically, it`s the middle that brought them together.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that`s what -- Joe Manchin, there`s always more to talk to you...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: That`s where we are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, more to talk to you about. And I want you to come back to talk to us when you make a decision about whether you`re running for reelection...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: I will.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... or that other option you said last time you`re leaving open, possibly a presidential run.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Everything`s open. Everything`s on the table and nothing off the table.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: But, hey Margaret, one thing I wanted to mention to you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I got..\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: We need a risk -- we need a risk assessment team, a risk management team.\par \par \uc2 For the United States of America to get to this brinksmanship every time...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... we should be taking on -- this on seriously every day. What`s the risk we`re taking if we don`t do that, rather than wait until the last day?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to talk -- I want to talk about that with one of our upcoming guests, the CEO of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We got some good news about America`s job market this week, but there appear to be economic headwinds on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: The bill is passed.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): Turmoil averted by Washington`s treasured blueprint, governing by crisis.\par \par \uc2 MAYA MACGUINEAS (President, Committee for a Responsible Federal Budget): The great news for Americans right now is that we avoided the catastrophe of hitting the debt ceiling. I think the troubling news is that we came that close.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But, like Washington, the rest of us have bills to pay. Good news first: Spending consumers keep creating more jobs.\par \par \uc2 Employers added another 339,000 jobs in May, gains in health care, construction, leisure and hospitality, but still not enough job seekers for restauranteur Nick Osborne (sp?).\par \par \uc2 NICK OSBORNE (Restauranteur): We are in need of some extra depth, and it has been hard to find that extra depth, for sure.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But there were also layoffs in the tech sector and a sharp rise in black unemployment that pushed up the overall jobless rate. It`s a snapshot of a complicated, wobbly economy, with stubbornly high inflation and the specter of a recession.\par \par \uc2 Pessimism is having a late spring to remember. In a recent Gallup poll, 55 percent of people believe their financial situation is only fair or poor. Half say it`s worsening. Fewer Americans, less than two in three, have $400 squirreled away for an emergency.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: And that`s...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: For house hunters, that struggle hits home, loan applications down roughly 30 percent from a year ago. Why? Rising interest rates, now just under 7 percent, tightening credit, and cities like Orlando have a startling lack of inventory, one-third the sellers this market needs.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: They don`t want to leave because they don`t want to give up their interest rate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: For the first time in more than a year, the Fed is expected to hold rates steady at its regular meeting later this month, at least for now, like everyone, uncertain whether this economy is stabilizing -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more, now we are joined by the CEO of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good to have you back here in person.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2  (CEO, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 ): It`s great to be here, Margaret. Thank you for having us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the -- our last two guests talked about the fact that we came to the edge.\par \par \uc2 What do you think is the financial cost here, since America`s credit rating is still in question?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Well, since I have been CEO, we had the one in `11. We had the one in `13.\par \par \uc2 It`s not good for the United States to go through this. It`s a political process. It`s -- it`s good they came to resolution. It`s good it`s behind us. And I think that has provided momentary momentum in the markets and allows us to face the real economic issues and real debt level issues ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because some of the CEOs of banks have talked about setting up war rooms and doing emergency planning. I mean, how much of an impact is there?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Well, it`s -- because it`s over, there was an impact. We were doing all that. We were prepared in case it didn`t come out right.\par \par \uc2 But the good news that came out, right -- and you heard your past guests talk about the -- coming together in the center and voting it through, which is good. And so now we can move forward, but there`s still issues ahead of us. And we can talk about those.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, do you think that they should get rid of the debt ceiling, as some CEOs have called for?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : It -- that`s a political question. At the end at the end of the day, we need to have a serious discussion about how much debt we can have in this country, how much we could afford and how it`s spent.\par \par \uc2 And that discussion needs to go on. The debt ceiling triggers certain debates around at certain times. It`s -- it`s part of the process of government and, whether you`re Republicans, Democrats, have to get together and do it. And we have to be careful that we keep the financial stability and strength in the United States paramount, because, at the end of the day, if we`re not strong, the rest of the world is not going to be strong.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, let`s talk about that strength, because the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is predicting a mild recession on the horizon.\par \par \uc2 What are you seeing there in terms of how the consumer is behaving and where we might be seeing any kind of slowdown?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : So the last time I was here was -- it was the end of last year, and we predicted a recession this year. We have moved it out. It`s basically third quarter this year, fourth quarter this year, into first quarter, a mild recession.\par \par \uc2 And unemployment gets up in high 4 percent range, still very low by historical norms. And that`s our core prediction. As you think ahead of it, what`s going on now is the impact of the Fed`s tightening has had -- had its effect. So consumer spending, `22 over `21 by \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  consumers, $4 trillion-plus a year, was up 9 percent.\par \par \uc2 Year to date, it`s up about 5 percent. In the month of May, it was up about 4 percent over last May. So it`s slowing down. That level is more consistent with a 2 percent growth economy and a 2 percent inflation economy, not a 4 percent inflation level economy, 4 to 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 So the Fed is getting the consumer spending level in line. And now you`re - - we have the jobs report and other things which sends some confusing...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : ... and ambiguous messages.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is, the -- the activity of consumers more in line with what the Fed wanted, because the rate increases and other aspects have had their effect. Quantitative tightening has had the effect.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You just gestured to this that the prediction of recession keeps getting pushed out. It`s basically like economists keep getting surprised by some of the data out there.\par \par \uc2 Is the prediction that going into this presidential election year ahead of us, that we will be in the midst of a recession?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : You know, I think we`re at the point now, with the Fed, having tightened as much as it has, with the impact of the failures of two or three banks which are different than a lot of the banks, but failed, the impacts of the Treasury funding has to come forward.\par \par \uc2 The impacts of the environment around us. It slowed the economy down. The question is, is inflation under control?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : And, right now, most people are thinking and like, in 1984, when Continental Illinois failed and Volcker, who was fighting inflation heavy, paused then, that maybe the Fed should pause...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : ... and let -- let the effects of all this take hold, and let`s be seen what happens.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : But, at the end of the day, that has a slight recessionary -- it`s a slight recession.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re going finish this thought on the other side of the break.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you miss an interview or an episode, you can find it on our Face the Nation \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will continue our conversation with \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  CEO \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2  when we come back.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We`re back with the CEO of \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I want to pick up on something you indicated earlier when you said the Fed had accomplished something. You said recently the Fed won the war. Is that the war against inflation?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2  (CEO, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 ): Well, the inflation is tipping over. But one of the toughest things for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is the power of the U.S. consumer. And they were - they were - they were employed, they were earning more money and they were spending. And what you`re seeing is that`s tipping down. That`s a key component to actually getting inflation under control because if people - the unemployment`s at all-time lows. People are getting, you know, five, six, seven percent wage increases. And on a multi- year above the rate of inflation and the one year not (ph), that -- that creates a hard job for the Fed to slow that down because the U.S. economy is so consumer led. They - they have won that battle. They`ve won that battle. Inflation is tipping over, but importantly you can see the U.S. consumer activity is slowing down, which then, if you project that out, ought to bring inflation down to match.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: With one of the impacts of the banking turmoil we just went through, for lack of a better term, we`re hearing about credit tightening up. What are you doing to tighten up lending standards? How much harder is it for consumers to come out and borrow money?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : You know, at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  we have responsible growth. We`ve been (INAUDIBLE) for decades. We don`t really change lending standards that much given different types of cyclicals. But just to give you a sense, the consumer credit quality in our industry is very strong. Most of us are reporting numbers now that are below 2019, which is a multidecade low in consumer credit costs. So that`s good news.\par \par \uc2 Lending has tightened largely because of two factors, beginning last year, just the feeling that a recession -- everybody was predicting a recession and going into a recession. People tend to tighten credit standards in tougher (INAUDIBLE) and things like that. And then the second thing is the disruption caused the capacity of lending to come down, which, again, is the -- as they pulled money out of the banking system and onto the -- into the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  as cash or into the overnight repo facility and deposit have come down, part of the monetary tightening, there`s less engine in the engine room to lend. And so that causes banks to make -- be more careful when they lend and who they lend to and how much they lend. And so that`s tightened credit standards pretty dramatically.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it`s more expensive to get a mortgage.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : It`s - it`s more expensive because rates went up.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : When the Fed raises rates, they`re trying to slow down borrowing capacity.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : And in the most -- you know housing and autos and things are most rate sensitive come down the quickest. And then things like mortgage loans, most - you know, 90 percent of the consumers are locked into mortgage loans below 5 percent. So, that slows down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : And so - but the home buying and other things kind of bounce around until everybody gets used to rate cycle and then it moves back up. We were in a very low rate cycle for 15 years, and that`s - that`s a little bit what`s confusing people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you slowing down your hiring right now?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : Yes, we are. Last May we hired 3,000 people. This May we hired 700 people. And that`s due to the attrition rate that has slowed so much and we need to trim head count. So we`ll be down 3,000 FT this quarter. We don`t - we`re not making layoffs. We`re trying to do it by attrition. But even attrition has slowed to half what it was last year.\par \par \uc2 And when I talk to other companies, I get the same input. It depends on the industry. If you`re trying to get welders in very specific areas, that`s - that`s a lot of - that`s a tough work on the employment side.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : But if you`re in a general industry, the employment conditions have leveled back out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : They`re still -- still strong for employment but have leveled out a bit. And that`s good news because, again -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN MOYNIHAN\uc2 : That allows the tightening cycle to pause or slow down and let the economy get back under itself.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Brian Moynihan\uc2 , good to have you here in person.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Campaign 2024 got down to business in Iowa this weekend as most of the declared or potential Republican candidates visited the early nominating state. Notably absent the GOP frontrunner, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who was off the trail this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to discuss are three of our sharpest political reporters, CBS News political correspondent Caitlin Huey-Burns, Ed O`Keefe is senior White House and political correspondent, and Robert Costa is our chief elections and campaign correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to you all.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with you on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because we learned from the Department of Justice that they will not be bringing charges against \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  for his classified documents that he was mishandling. What do we know about the case against Mr. Trump?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Sources with knowledge of the investigation believe that a charging decision in the documents case on the federal level being led by the special counsel is imminent. It could be coming in the next few weeks. In fact, the grand jury might have some activity this week. We hear from sources close to this investigation. We also hear that the Trump lawyers might be meeting at some point with the Justice Department to talk through where things stand.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When will we know? When you say the next few weeks and imminent. There is a Republican debate in August. Are we going to know the answer to this before they all take the stage?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The investigations, Margaret, over former President Trump, they loom over this entire presidential race. We hear that a charging decision could be made in June on the documents case. The January 6th investigation by the special counsel continues, but that could end up going into 2024 when some of these primaries are unfolding next year.\par \par \uc2 But right before the debate, the thing to pay attention to, a charging decision and a possible indictment in Georgia, where Trump is being investigated for pressuring election officials, that could come in early August, just weeks before that first debate in Milwaukee in late August.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This is going to be an incredible primary season with that.\par \par \uc2 I want to turn to you, Caitlin, because when they take this debate stage, in order to even walk on alongside Mr. Trump or whomever else, there are some parameters that are being sent. We know there are at least ten Republicans who may be on the stage. To get up there they have to agree to endorse whoever the nominee is.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yes, they`re being asked to sign this loyalty pledge. And what`s interesting about this field is that when you ask the candidates, will you commit to supporting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  if he`s the nominee, they really won`t say. I`ve interviewed \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and Tim Scott, and they have not been able to answer that question.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, Trump hasn`t been able to answer that question either. He hasn`t said whether he will support the eventual nomination if it`s not him or if he will participate in the debates altogether. That still remains kind of a wild card here.\par \par \uc2 He could be advantage skipping the first debate. He`s ahead of the polls, of course. He`s the frontrunner, at least at this point. But at the same time, if he does skip the debate, that does give his rivals the opportunity to make their case as the alternative without having to stand there and kind of weather the attacks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that assumes there are attacks, right? I mean we`re listening to some of these speeches out in Iowa and he was the candidate who loomed large but his name was not spoken.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And \uc2 Chris Christie\uc2 , who may be jumping in, one of the few who - who does take aim directly at the former president, but he said he`s not going to be a hired assassin. That`s what he told "Politico."\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Right. Look, there`s a - there`s a ceiling that he`s hitting right now about 35 percent to 40 percent. Everyone else in this race knows that and they`re focused on that 60 percent or so that`s still out there to be had. Everything is Donald Trump`s to lose. I think anyone showing up to an event hosted by someone else is clearly curious. And the idea of all the legal matters he`s facing, all the personal issues he`s dragged along with him through the years, is on the minds of these voters, especially in Iowa, where we were this week.\par \par \uc2 A lot of them saying, look, if he`s ultimately the guy, I`ll pull the plug. I`ll be there for him. But all this stuff or this baggage, you know, I`m kind of done with it and I`m hoping somebody else rises. And the names we hear, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Tim Scott, Nikki Haley are the three that came from voters` voices most frequently when talking about somebody else. All of them understand they`ve got to lay out what they`re about, what they would do differently, because they`re obviously not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And DeSantis, notably several times over the course of this past week, was asked about Trump or made inferences to him in his remarks, making it clear that he thinks the party has to move on. He called his attacks juvenile. Said that`s why he lost voters back in 2020. And why he won`t be able to drag them back. I mean if that`s not the argument some other Republican isn`t going to make -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You know, what will work to convince those Republicans otherwise.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But - but then that question, Robert, is always, does a possible, you know, movement in this case, does an indictment for hypothetical purposes matter here, or is that even what we`re talking about at this point?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: To build on what Caitlin and Ed laid out, there`s a real wait and see mentality with so many of these campaigns. They don`t want to attack Trump on the investigations. They want to see all of that run its course.\par \par \uc2 But they are starting to not necessarily attack Trump, but take out the scalpel on policy. And you can expect Pence, when he gets into the race this week, according to people close to him, to start saying Trump is not conservative enough to be the Republican nominee.\par \par \uc2 And you`re also seeing that same type of attack from Governor DeSantis coming at Trump from the right, linking him with Dr. \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 , saying the response to the pandemic was too favorable to corporations, too federal in its nature. So, this is a different kind of position for Trump to be in, not to be facing questions about his conduct or his character, but about his conservatism.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s fascinating.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, you have been looking very closely at the issue of abortion as this galvanizing political force. President Biden had said that he would set a national standard of access up to the point of viability, which is roughly 24 weeks under Roe versus Wade. But the Republican candidates each has a different answer here and they don`t seem to have a unified position on how to handle something that is so politically loaded.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Exactly. Well, Republicans right now are caught between a primary electorate that is cheering the overturning of Roe versus Wade, and a general electorate, a broader electorate that has really rejected that decision. We saw that at the ballot box in 2022 and especially in places like Wisconsin and Pennsylvania. We saw it earlier this year in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 When you talk to Republicans, operatives and also those on the anti- abortion side, they want the candidates to be engaging in this issue because they feel like they ceded ground last time around. And so you do have groups like Susan B. Anthony List pushing candidates to take a stance on federal abortion restrictions. And that has kind of tied these candidates up in knots.\par \par \uc2 I think what changed this week, however, is how the candidates were talking about it in Iowa. Obviously, Iowa is where the evangelical base is. Governor \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  signed a six-week abortion ban there. So, you heard Governor DeSantis talking about that, playing up his six-week abortion ban that he signed into law in Florida. That`s a play for those voters and they`re more open about it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: And we also got a more concrete answer from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who has tried to kind of avoid this altogether, trying to remind voters that he was responsible for overturning it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the federal - but the Republican line on this had been, it`s up to the states. And I thought it was interesting when Nikki Haley was on this program recently she said, candidates are not being honest when they claim it`s possible to get a national law on abortion.\par \par \uc2 CAITLAN HUEY-BURNS: And there is some truth to that, right, the idea of, you know, having 60 senators pass anything is unlikely. But it is trying to kind of thread that needle between the primary electorate that you need to come out and support you in a place like Iowa, and general electorate, especially women. Also notable that they`ll play this issue differently in New Hampshire and Nevada than they do in Iowa and South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ed, I have to ask you about the man running for re- election, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It was a big week for him with this bipartisan deal. But in that speech from the Oval Office it went quickly from very bipartisan to a bit of a campaign speech.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes. Let`s raise taxes on the -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: On - on the wealthy and - and let`s remind ourselves of what Republicans were pushing for in that deal that didn`t get through.\par \par \uc2 The goal through the end of this year is to just keep him being president, looking presidential. He`s got meetings this week with the Danish and British prime ministers. He`s going to have some events on the economy. He`s speaking with the \uc2 Kansas City Chiefs\uc2 . All of that sort of checks the box of, I`m commander in chief, I`m in charge. Look at the big deal I just got.\par \par \uc2 He`ll be raising a lot of money between now and the end of the year. Not a lot of campaigning. That`s not because of his age. That`s not because of his vigor. It`s consistent with what President Obama did back in 2011 going into 2012. And they believe if he can raise all that money now, they`ll be more than happy to spend it next year. And they want to just sit back and watch Republicans squabble over how many weeks of a ban should there be for abortion, who`s conservative enough, knowing that they should be able to go into those elections next year in the six or seven battleground states and - and hope that they can continue to make the compelling argument. They worry that if it`s not Trump there`s a generational argument to be head, which is why you saw \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , alongside his wife and young kids so much in recent days and you`ll continue to. It`s also why they`ve got to get the vice president out there a bit more to talk about, you know, issues of concern, especially to the base.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, this is going to be a very interesting campaign season.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to all of you.\par \par \uc2 COSTA: And it`s just starting.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s just starting. But get ready for a busy summer. There`s no slow summers anymore news wise.\par \par \uc2 O`KEEFE: No there are not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thanks to all of you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 A quick programming note. Tune in next Sunday for an interview with North Dakota Republican Governor \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2 , who`s expected to launch a 2024 presidential bid in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the war in Ukraine. Ambassador \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2  joins us for an update.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you. It`s good to have you back with us.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA (Ukrainian Ambassador to the U.S.): Good morning. Always good to be back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about what`s happening now because Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  said U.S. trained Ukrainian brigades have arrived in Ukraine and they`ve been rehearsing, training and maintaining for weeks. He mentioned the weather has improved. He`s describing the conditions for that counter offensive to potentially begin very soon. What are you expecting in the coming days and weeks?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, you know, for us, counteroffensive never stopped to be honest. Since February 24 that`s all we were focusing on, how to liberate our land.\par \par \uc2 But, of course, with additional trained brigades, with additional equipment, with everything that we are working on now, we are hoping that our commanders, as soon as they will see the openings, will start this -- whether it`s going to be one or several counter offensives. But the faster we can liberate all the territory and all our people, of course the better.\par \par \uc2 So, I will note, of course, as you heard our president and our commanders, we will not announce anything. We will not - we will see it when the results are there. But we are expecting to liberate more soon.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And your president said we are ready. Russia, though, throughout the course of the week, has been launching missile attacks, including on the capital of Kyiv. They appear to be testing your air defenses. And I know President Zelenskyy has said Ukraine needs more patriot missiles. Are you getting help getting that kind of air defense right now? Is the U.S. doing enough?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, we are - we are very grateful for everything we are receiving. So it`s patriots, it`s nasoms (ph), it`s all other capabilities. But we clearly need more. So what Russians have been doing again for 466 days now, but also this week, you know, we have seen how daily, several times a day, there were deadly attacks by rockets, but also Shahid drones, all - all the time. So, it`s the terror, it`s the destruction that it brings, but also it`s the, you know, expending our capabilities that we have now. We definitely need more.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Biden administration released another $300 million in military aid. Some of that included drones. Are these surveillance drones, are these attack drones that the U.S. is giving?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: It`s a range of - of the drones. So -- and we need all of them. We need the surveillance. We need the attack drones. We need the Kamakahi drones. So there will be different capabilities in the packages.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There is an upcoming \uc2 NATO\uc2  meeting in July that the president, President Biden, is expected to attend and Ukraine`s entry -- possible entry into the military alliance is a topic there. Do you know what the U.S. is going to promise?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, we all look forward to this summit. Ukraine has \uc2 NATO\uc2  aspirations, transatlantic aspirations -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For some time now.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: In our hearts, in our constitution, and the majority of people support it. Since 2008, when the first declaration was made that we will be members of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , we believe it`s time to start discussing some specific steps in that regard.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, as President Zelenskyy said, we are realists. We understand the limitations that the ongoing war puts to it. But I think at this moment, after this horrible violation of international law, after Russia attacked us completely not provoked, when we were not part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , after historic decisions on Finland and Sweden, it`s time for everyone to realize that it`s about good versus evil. And we need to be members of \uc2 NATO\uc2  as much as \uc2 NATO\uc2  needs Ukraine also in order -- because we have a lot to add to \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, yes, we look forward to this forum to discuss this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your president said he knows this wouldn`t happen until after the war is concluded. In other words, the U.S. wouldn`t be drawn in directly to this conflict now as a \uc2 NATO\uc2  member. But promise of future entry. What are the security guarantees that you are looking for?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, you know, President Zelenskyy put out this peace formula which clearly outlines how the war should end and how to build -- restore the security. So there is a lot of elements. It`s - it`s the military security, justice, rebuilding -- building Ukraine, ensuring that we can altogether deter Russia from making it again. And it`s all been discussed with - with partners individually but also with the group. It was one of the items discussed at the G-7 summit and others. So, it`s a work in progress. But I think, you know, the real security guarantee, and not for Ukraine, but for transatlantic community and for our part of the world but globally for everyone who believes in \uc2 U.N\uc2 . statehood (ph) is for us, in the future, to become the member of the alliance that is a peaceful defensive alliance of the people with like-minded values.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: $45 billion in U.S. support to Ukraine has been pledged so far but that funding ends in September and there will have to be a request for more. Are you concerned about all the political pressures that may make that more difficult, particularly going into a presidential election year?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, any democracy, and Ukraine and the U.S. are both democracies, have good Democratic elections and processes. And, yes, it`s an additional factor. But I believe American people support us. And we are very grateful for American people for the support we have received. We will always remember it.\par \par \uc2 And I know that the majority of politicians on both sides of the aisle, you know, equally, we have this strong bipartisan support and I really hope that Ukraine will continue uniting people in Ukraine even through this electoral process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ambassador, we`ll be watching closely in the coming days and weeks and thinking of Ukraine. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Turning now to rising tensions between the U.S. and China. A Chinese warship came within 150 yards of hitting an American missile destroyer in the Taiwan Strait. U.S. Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  said it was extremely dangerous. China`s defense minister called the presence of that American destroyer a provocation to China and defended sailing the warship across its path.\par \par \uc2 It`s just the latest flare up in the relationship between the two powers. And it came 34 years since the crackdown in Tiananmen Square when Beijing brutally quashed peaceful pro-democracy protests by unleashing tanks and troops on demonstrators. The death toll is still unknown, but estimates range from several hundred to several thousand.\par \par \uc2 Seven days after the massacre, then FACE THE NATION moderator Lesley Stahl briefed Americans on the fallout ahead of a conversation with the U.S. ambassador to China at the time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL, FACE THE NATION MODERATOR (June 11, 1989): The White House now says the Chinese government has murdered many, many of its own people. Leaders Dong Cho Ping and the other hardliners are consolidating their control over the country, searching out, rounding up the pro-democracy leaders while western businesses pack up and begin pulling out of China.\par \par \uc2 Official Chinese television is denying there was ever any blood bath, any brutality in Tiananmen Square, showing pictures of the arrests of what authorities there are calling thugs and hooligans.\par \par \uc2 Ambassador Lilley, in the morning papers today there are a lot of stories suggesting that the U.S.-China relationship is tearing apart, is disintegrating rapidly. There`s a lot of anti-Americanism in their official press. What`s your assessment of where we stand? President Bush keeps saying he wants to preserve the relationship. How is that possible at this point?\par \par \uc2 JAMES LILLEY, U.S. AMBASSADOR (June 11, 1989): I think we`ve gone through many ups and downs with China. And I think right now we`re going through a down. But I don`t think we should sort of give up on it. I think that would be a terrible mistake.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Those ups and downs continue today. China`s defense minister refuses to speak to Secretary Austin, but two top U.S. officials are arriving in Beijing today in an attempt to thaw relations. The visit comes after CIA Director \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2 ` secret trip to China last month becoming the most senior Biden administration official to visit Beijing since our relationship with China sharply deteriorated following the February shoot down of Chinese surveillance balloons over American territory.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. I`ll be off next Sunday, but you will be in great hands with CBS News primetime anchor \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230604ej6400001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Breast Cancer Surgery Battle\b0\par\par\uc2 Nancy Chen, Anna Werner, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 1,337 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS, and we have an update to a story we told you about in January on how a medical coding change could limit reconstruction options for women who have lost a breast to cancer and want to avoid implants.\par \par \uc2 We partnered with KFF Health News to learn more about a young mother from Central Illinois to see how some patients are fighting back.\par \par \uc2 We should point out, following our earlier report, the government agency that oversees coding set a hearing to review the change. That`s tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Consumer investigative correspondent, Anna Werner has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATE GETZ (Breast cancer survivor): We`ve got some squish.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Kate Getz never thought she`d become an advocate for breast cancer survivors. She`s an accountant and single mom to daughter, Nora.\par \par \uc2 KATE GETZ: And then go wash your hands.\par \par \uc2 She is eight. She is in second grade.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: But this past January, doctors diagnosed her with breast cancer and told her she needed a mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 KATE GETZ: That`s not news that you expect to hear at 30. It`s a scary moment.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Then she had to figure out what kind of breast reconstruction she wanted. She knew she didn`t want implants because the \uc2 FDA\uc2  says they should be replaced every 10 years. Instead, she wanted a natural reconstruction using what`s called a deep flap procedure in which a surgeon removes skin, fat, and blood vessels from a woman`s abdomen. Essentially doing a tummy tuck then uses them to create a new breast with her own tissue.\par \par \uc2 The problem -- she couldn`t find a surgeon in her area to do it.\par \par \uc2 Then she saw our CBS News story from January explaining that a change in the way the government codes the procedure is about to make that deep flap surgery even harder to get. Some insurance companies had already started sending out letters like these saying their reimbursements to doctors would drop drastically.\par \par \uc2 As Texas breast surgeon, Dr. Elisabeth Potter told us then --\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH POTTER (Texas breast surgeon): Make no mistake, women who want the surgery and have money will always have access to it. The only people that this coding change affects and hurts are women who need access through insurance.\par \par \uc2 KATE GETZ: I felt like I was on just a ticking time bomb. There`s only so much time for me to get this procedure before my insurance company won`t pay for it anymore.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Sure enough, when she reached out to her insurer, \uc2 Cigna\uc2 , it was already lowering its reimbursement. The company told her in this March 29th e-mail that it would only pay for the deep flap under the new lower paying code, Getz faced the task of finding the rare surgeon who would accept that lower insurance payment.\par \par \uc2 Her only other option, pay tens of thousands of dollars in cash.\par \par \uc2 KATE GETZ: That`s even an option. I`m a single mom. We get by, right? But I`m not wealthy by any means.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Now , she has become one of many voices pushing for the code change to be reversed.\par \par \uc2 On social media, she called out \uc2 Cigna\uc2  posting this picture of her mastectomy scar.\par \par \uc2 Why did you do that?\par \par \uc2 KATE GETZ: I felt highly motivated that women needed to start sharing their stories. I felt like the only way we were going to get insurance companies to change and to roll back what they were doing was they needed to hear us. I think they need to see us and I think they need to hear us.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: But how did that code change occur? The Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services or CMS, the government agency that oversees coding pointed to the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 , but when we asked the AMA, they told us they never asked CMS to get rid of that higher-paying code.\par \par \uc2 So who did? We found this document from 2021 showing that the lobbying association that represents 34 \uc2 Blue Cross\uc2  and Blue Shield insurance companies nationwide submitted a request to discontinue the higher paying code for deep flap saying it was no longer needed.\par \par \uc2 Medical coding researcher, Dr. Karen Joynt Maddox says that the association`s move --\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN JOYNT MADDOX (Medical coding researcher): The insurers presumably decided that they wanted to pay less, and took the tactical approach of trying to get that procedure rolled back under a code that was a less expensive, less resource intensive lower paying code.\par \par \uc2 The upshot of that is that women who need this procedure are left to foot the bill.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: It sounds like you`re saying that maybe patients were left out of the equation here.\par \par \uc2 DR. KAREN JOYNT MADDOX: I think patients are generally left out of the equation, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: As to the coding change, the BCBS Association now tells us: "We are examining our position and are currently in discussion with our member plans."\par \par \uc2 In April, \uc2 Cigna\uc2  agreed to pay the higher reimbursement under the old code for Getz to have a deep flap reconstruction and told us in a statement they have paused using the lower paying code and are reprocessing deep flap claims.\par \par \uc2 KATE GETZ: It is surgery time.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: On May 15th, Getz had her surgery, but she says she is not done advocating for other survivors.\par \par \uc2 KATE GETZ: I`m fighting for women around the country to have access to the surgery.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: It sounds like you`re going to keep fighting.\par \par \uc2 KATE GETZ: Yes. I`m here for the long haul.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Both \uc2 Cigna\uc2  and \uc2 Blue Cross Blue Shield\uc2  told us and KFF Health News, they are committed to preserving affordable coverage and access to care, including \uc2 Cigna\uc2  said, surgery for breast cancer patients.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Dr. Potter did Kate Getz`s surgery. She had to travel to Texas, mind you, to get it done. Dr. Potter helped her out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I mean, insurance companies are for profit organizations, so they are also committed in addition to their customers to their shareholders, and they want to make a profit. So there is that accusation that they shifted this code in order to make a little bit of more money. They say no, but we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a great point, though. You`re not making an excuse, but people have to realize it is for profit.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is a business.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Well, the question is this case really is, is there a middle between this higher paying code that micro surgeons say they need payment for a more complicated surgery? And it really -- they are saying, you really just can`t jam it under a lower paying code and say, that`s enough, because you`re limiting the availability only to women who have the money to pay cash.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tens of thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: And should that be the case? I think most people would say no.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Agree. Yes. All right, cool.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anna, thank you very much. Appreciate it up.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`re going to go to the site of the Fukushima nuclear disaster in Japan where there`s a plan now to flush treated radioactive water back into the ocean and that is raising new fears.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230601ej5v0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 South Carolina Has Joined Other Republican-Led States Across The South In Passing Tighter Abortion Restrictions\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 955 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: South Carolina is the latest Republican-led state to pass a near-total ban on abortion. The state Senate approved a six-week ban last night, and the governor says he will sign it as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Caitlin Huey-Burns sat down with the only five women in the Senate, who call themselves the sister senators. They stood together to oppose a ban that one Republican said was about controlling women.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): The legislation passed after months of wrangling and has some exceptions, including for rape and incest through 12 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Once signed Into law, Virginia will be the only state left in the South with relatively unrestricted access, up to 26 weeks.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR SANDY SENN (R-South Carolina): I cried this morning, actually, with, I feel like, so much weight was on our shoulders, not just for South Carolina, but Florida, Georgia, the Southeast now.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Republican Sandy Senn is one of just five women in the South Carolina Senate.\par \par \uc2 What is it about this issue that unites you?\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR MIA S. MCLEOD (I-South Carolina): All of us are women, all of us are moms, and that is a pretty strong--\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Bond.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR MIA S. MCLEOD: Bond.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR MARGIE BRIGHT MATTHEWS (D-South Carolina): I think South Carolina can be a beacon for the fact that, yes, you can find common ground with folks on the other side of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR MIA S. MCLEOD: Never before, in the history of our state, have we had five women serve simultaneously. I agree they are stronger than ever, but we need help.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR SANDY SENN: If we had had three more women, in addition to the Democrats, we would have been able to hold this.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR KATRINA FRYE SHEALY (R-South Carolina): So, we have to fight hard to keep our seats and keep the women we have, and we have to work hard to get more women in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR PENRY GUSTAFSON (R-South Carolina): If there`s anything I want to say to the country is, you do not have to be ugly to push your platform, to push your beliefs, to push your legislation. It doesn`t have to be contentious.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: You are hopeful?\par \par \uc2 (Crosstalk)\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR MIA S. MCLEOD: Absolutely. Absolutely. And we are fighters. All of us are here because--\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR KATRINA FRYE SHEALY: Because we are fighters.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR MIA S. MCLEOD: -- to fight.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR KATRINA FRYE SHEALY: Nobody got here easily.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR MIA S. MCLEOD: Right.\par \par \uc2 STATE SENATOR MARGIE BRIGHT MATTHEWS: We live to fight another day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, once this is signed into law, it will likely face legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 Back in January, the state Supreme Court struck down a similar provision, but, since then, the court`s only woman retired and changed the makeup of the court. And Republicans here think this new version will stand -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Seismic change in the South.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Authorities tonight in Texas are still surveying the damage after severe storms blew through several communities north of Houston on Tuesday. Two people were killed and several others injured in Montgomery County after a home that was under construction collapsed due to high winds. Dozens of buildings were impacted across the region, while trees and power lines littered the roadways.\par \par \uc2 As the storms move east, it could prove to be a wet Memorial Day weekend for much of the Southeast.\par \par \uc2 For details, let`s bring meteorologist Alex Wilson from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Alex.\par \par \uc2 ALEX WILSON (Meteorologist, \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ): Well, Norah, headed into the holiday weekend, unfortunately, less-than-ideal weather at a lot of those Southeast beaches, all thanks to a surface load developing over Florida causing some travel troubles to the second half of the week.\par \par \uc2 But, over the weekend, this low begins to strengthen as it moves up the coast, bringing strong, dangerous rip currents, rough surf, and then the wind and the rain, again, keeping a lot of people, I think, off of those beaches.\par \par \uc2 And we will even see those showers inland as well, so you don`t need to have to be at the beach to get on that wet weather. National Hurricane Center given this a small chance of development. As I mentioned, the winds will also be picking up. Get ready for winds gusting up to, even over at times 40 miles an hour for parts of the mid-Atlantic and the Southeast, on top of that, cooler-than-average temperatures across the West.\par \par \uc2 So, Norah, other areas cooling off.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Alex Wilson, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, government leaders are scrambling to prevent an unprecedented debt default, which could happen as soon as next week. Today, while White House and Republican negotiators tried to work out a deal, all 213 Democrats in the House signed a petition to raise the debt ceiling, but they still need to find five Republicans to join them.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230525ej5o0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Debate Over The Abortion Pill Is In Federal Court, As All Eyes Are On The Carolinas, With Lawmakers Making Major Moves On Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 632 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, now to the ongoing fight over abortion rights affecting millions of American women. North Carolina`s Republican- controlled legislature successfully overrode Democratic Governor Roy Cooper`s veto to ban most abortions after 12 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 More than half of the country, 26 states, have now passed some form of abortion restrictions or an outright ban since the Supreme Court`s repeal of Roe versus Wade last summer.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Caitlin Huey-Burns has reaction from North Carolina`s capital of Raleigh.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: The bill becomes law.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): Republican lawmakers moved quickly Tuesday night, turning new abortion restrictions into law.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER (D-North Carolina): The majority of the people of North Carolina don`t want right-wing politicians in the exam room with women and their doctors, and they are mad.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: A party switch by State Representative Tricia Cotham last month gave Republicans a supermajority and the power to override the governor`s veto. Cotham had campaigned as a Democrat, pledging to a protected abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE TRICIA COTHAM (R-North Carolina): I will not be controlled by anyone.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Under the new law, which goes into effect July 1, most abortions will be banned after 12 weeks, instead of 20, with exceptions for rape, incest, certain fatal fetal conditions, and the mother`s life.\par \par \uc2 The law also requires longer waiting periods and more in-person visits to obtain the procedure. Republicans say the legislation includes compromises.\par \par \uc2 STATE REPRESENTATIVE TIM MOORE (R-North Carolina): If you look at some of the bills that are around the country, some that have been just really very -- much more heated than this bill, they were bills that went further in one way or went further the other. This one really took kind of a middle- of-the-road approach.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But it will have an impact beyond North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 What does this mean for abortion care in this region?\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN (CEO, A Woman`s Choice Clinic): It is dangerous, it is scary right now to be -- to be a pregnant person in the South.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Kelly Flynn runs an abortion clinic in Raleigh that has seen an influx of patients from out of state since last year.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: We worry about women that are going to start to self-manage their care, which is going to be very dangerous. Hospitals could be inundated with a lot of tragic situations.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now there are only two states in the South with relatively unrestricted access to abortion, Virginia and South Carolina, where they are considering a six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 And this all comes as a federal appeals court today heard arguments on whether to restrict abortion medication nationwide -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to that quadruple murder case in Idaho. Bryan Kohberger was indicted by a grand jury today. The criminology Ph.D. student was charged with four counts of murder and one count of burglary. He allegedly broke into the off-campus home of University of Idaho students and stabbed four of them to death last November.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230518ej5h0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 With \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  And \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  Accusing Paparazzi Of Being Highly Aggressive And Following Them For Two Hours\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Errol Barnett \par \uc2 1,068 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: a setback for abortion rights, as a 26th state further restricts the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the royal drama in New York City, with \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  accusing paparazzi of being highly aggressive and following them for two hours.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 What we are learning about the pursuit described by the royal couple`s spokesman as a near catastrophic car chase.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): I don`t think there`s many of us who don`t recall how his mom died.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The abortion rights battle. The debate over the abortion pill is in federal court, as all eyes are on the Carolinas, with lawmakers making major moves on bans.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: There are so many migrants showing up in Chicago that volunteers are turning places like this former factory into shelters.\par \par \uc2 BYRON SIGCHO-LOPEZ (Chicago Alderman): The situation is dire. The number keeps growing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There is growing outrage over the death of a man killed while allegedly shoplifting from a \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 . Surveillance video shows Banko Brown struggling with a security guard before he was shot and killed. The DA says no charges will be brought against the guard.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Recruiting struggle. We are at the Pentagon talking to two of the top Air Force leaders ahead of Armed Forces Day.\par \par \uc2 GEN. CHARLES Q. BROWN JR. (Chief of Staff, \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2 ): I am very focused on developing the culture within organizations, where all our airmen can reach their full potential.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Take a look at this, the first full-size 3-D scan of the Titanic, revealing never-before-seen details.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The remarkable story of a dad who managed to adopt eight boys just before they aged out of foster care and the transformation it was for all of them.\par \par \uc2 JOE TOLES (Father): My boys are my boys. They are my kids.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 We are following a number of big stories, but we do want to begin tonight with a stunning allegation by \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and Meghan Markle`s spokesperson, that they were involved in a near-catastrophic car chase at the hands of a ring of highly aggressive paparazzi, allegedly involving half-a-dozen blacked-out vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Well, the couple was in New York City as \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  was given an award by Gloria Steinem during the Women of Vision Awards gala in Midtown Manhattan. The news that Harry and Meghan were relentlessly pursued in a chase that resulted in multiple near-collisions brought back memories of the fatal car crash of Harry`s mother, Princess Diana, in the summer of 1997.\par \par \uc2 New York City Mayor \uc2 Eric Adams\uc2  condemned the paparazzi and called their actions reckless and irresponsible.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Errol Barnett is going to start us off tonight from the theater in New York City where it all started.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Errol.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the \uc2 NYPD\uc2  is investigating what exactly took place across the streets of the country`s biggest city last night. But, for many, hearing that phrase `near-catastrophic car chase` certainly evoked memories of the late Princess Diana`s death nearly 26 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT (voiceover): In Midtown Manhattan last night, \uc2 Prince Harry\uc2  and \uc2 Meghan Markle\uc2  stepped out to cheers and stepped into apparent danger, after a spokesperson says the royal couple were involved in a near- catastrophic car chase.\par \par \uc2 The duke and duchess of Sussex accused highly aggressive paparazzi of relentlessly pursuing them, at times driving on sidewalks in an incident lasting more than two hours.\par \par \uc2 ERIC ADAMS (D-Mayor of New York City): I would find it hard to believe that there was a two-hour high-speed chase. I don`t think there`s many of us who don`t recall how -- how their -- his mom died, and it would be horrific to lose an innocent bystander during a chase like this.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Law enforcement sources tell CBS News, after leaving the event around 10:00 p.m., the couple did not want the paparazzi to know where they were staying, so they circled Midtown for more than an hour, with \uc2 NYPD\uc2  squad cars providing crowd control.\par \par \uc2 Police say, while Harry and Meghan were not in danger, two uniformed officers could have been injured during the incident. At one point, the couple jumped into the back of Sukhcharn Singh`s yellow cab outside the 19th Precinct, but he only drove them around for about 10 to 15 minutes.\par \par \uc2 SUKHCHARN SINGH (Cab Driver): They were following us the whole time. You know, there was nothing reckless or anything like that, but they were following us.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: The couple finally arrived at the police station, where they were able to switch cars and get home safely.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Netflix\uc2  2022)\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN MARKLE\uc2  (Duchess of Sussex): Do we have that pap on the scooter again?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes, ma`am.\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: In their the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  docuseries, they spoke about being hounded by the paparazzi. Harry recalls losing his own mother, Princess Diana, who died in a fatal collision back in 1997 after the car she was being driven in tried to outrun the paparazzi in Paris.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 Netflix\uc2  2022)\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2  (Duke of Sussex): But I knew I had to do everything I could to protect my family. You know, I didn`t want history to repeat itself.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ERROL BARNETT: Now, back on the West Coast, in Montecito, California, where Harry and Meghan live full-time, there was an arrest this week at their home.\par \par \uc2 Sources telling CBS News, an alleged stalker was detained by their private security detail before being arrested by local police -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Errol Barnett, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230518ej5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Overseas Abortion Help\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Haley Ott \par \uc2 1,840 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The issue of abortion pills in America returns to the courts today. A federal appeals court holds a hearing to reconsider a judge`s ruling that the abortion drug, mifepristone was wrongly approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, with abortion now banned in some states, more Americans are seeking abortion pills from Europe.\par \par \uc2 CBS News` Haley Ott spoke to a doctor in the Netherlands who is providing them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Amsterdam`s historic squares and narrow canals are just part of the morning commute for Dr. Rebecca Gomperts.\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA GOMPERTS (Aid Access): I don`t live very far away.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Her organization, Aid Access helps Americans get abortions, even though they are thousands of miles apart.\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA GOMPERTS: In other countries, it`s not controversial and abortion is recognized as a human rights.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Experts say Aid Access is a significant provider of abortion pills for people in states with restrictive abortion laws, meaning many who seek out medication abortions in those states will be getting a prescriptions from a doctor in this European city.\par \par \uc2 Here is how it works: Someone seeking an abortion in the US fills out an online form, which depending on what state they are in is reviewed by a healthcare professional at home or in The Netherlands.\par \par \uc2 They get a prescription for abortion pills that can be filled domestically or at an Aid Access recommended pharmacy in India and the pills are mailed to them.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gomperts says demand has skyrocketed since Roe v. Wade was overturned last year.\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA GOMPERTS: So we have seen an enormous increase in requests and especially of course from the states where there are restrictions and I think, at this moment, we`re seeing between a thousand and fifteen hundred requests per day.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  says that medication abortion which in the US involves taking the drugs mifepristone and misoprostol is safe and it is approved in over 80 other countries.\par \par \uc2 But Aid Access is working in a legal gray area. Professor Greer Donley from the \uc2 University of Pittsburgh\uc2  specializes in abortion law.\par \par \uc2 GREER DONLEY (\uc2 University of Pittsburgh\uc2 ): And if the states or federal government don`t have jurisdiction to really go after those people, then just because it`s illegal doesn`t mean it`s actually going to stop.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: So even though somebody outside the US might be breaking the law by prescribing abortion pills, there`s no way the law can reach them.\par \par \uc2 GREER DONLEY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Donley says an \uc2 FDA\uc2  personal use exemption may apply to Americans ordering abortion pills from overseas.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  sent Aid Access a warning letter, but Gomperts sued in response and the regulatory agency took no enforcement action. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  did not respond to our request for comment.\par \par \uc2 As far as Dr. Gomperts is concerned, she is just doing her job.\par \par \uc2 DR. REBECCA GOMPERTS: I`m acting in accordance with my medical professional oath that I am supposed to make sure that people that need medical care, that they will get it. That`s what I do and that is my laws and my conscience that I abide to.\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: Aid Access has a growing network of American clinicians, shielded by new state laws that are able to prescribe abortion pills at home.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Our bodies, our choice.")\par \par \uc2 HALEY OTT: But while the future of mifepristone hangs in the balance of the courts, Gomperts says her European operation won`t be going anywhere.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Haley Ott, Amsterdam.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that we`ll be talking about today. But first, it is 7:45. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Vlad, try not to dance. We`ve got no time for it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve got no time for it. I am going to do that thing. I am going to do that thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do what you want to do.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m going to do what I`ve got to do.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NBA\uc2  commissioner is weighing in on the latest gun controversy with \uc2 Memphis Grizzlies\uc2  superstar` Ja Morant. The team suspended Morant after that \uc2 Instagram\uc2  video came out on Saturday night. He was also suspended back in March after another video showed him holding a gun in a nightclub.\par \par \uc2 Commissioner \uc2 Adam Silver\uc2  told \uc2 ESPN\uc2  that he told Morant after the first gun incident, not to do it again.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADAM SILVER\uc2  (\uc2 NBA\uc2  commissioner): And we spoke for a long time about not just the consequences that could have on his career, but the safety issues around him, he could have injured and maimed killed himself, someone else.\par \par \uc2 We can`t act like that, honestly, I was shocked when I saw this weekend that video. Now we are in the process of investigating it and we will figure out exactly what happened to the best we can. And again, it`s the video, it is a bit grainy and all of that, but I`m assuming the worst.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Morant put out a statement saying he takes full accountability for his actions. He added: "I know I`ve disappointed a lot of people who have supported me. This is a journey and I recognize there is more work to do." And he also said, "My words may not mean much right now."\par \par \uc2 And I think that that`s a recognition that some people were like, again?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Again?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Show some action.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is very disappointing. Let`s hope that he gets the lesson yet again.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is still very young. I can`t emphasize it enough, because he is especially talented.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But we can`t keep using that as an excuse saying he`s young and he`s dumb.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Excuse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: After a while you have to learn and mature. He`s one of the most recognizable faces in the league. There are a lot of kids that are looking up to him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He has a responsibility. Most guys tried to escape that type of lifestyle. He made it and is starting to adopt a lifestyle. That`s what we he is running from.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 All right, let me lighten it up here a little bit. Art lovers are getting a new impression of a Picasso masterpiece even though he`s not an impressionist, right?\par \par \uc2 Take a look at Le Moulin de la Galette from 1900s.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Say that again?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Le Moulin de la Galette.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What? Tony, did you get that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: From 1900 --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A painting --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So let me just show you. It shows people dancing and sitting at a table ...\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am still on the other one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: ... in Paris. Museum experts discovered something very, very interesting at the table. There is a stowaway underneath that black paint.\par \par \uc2 Picasso originally included a small dog with a red ribbon. The image was uncovered using x ray technology. One expert believes Picasso covered up the dog because it would have distracted from the rest of the painting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it would have.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How cool is that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It would have, but that`s very cool.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Picasso. Right before his blue period. Blue period being in the --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was nice. All right, Vlad, appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He knows almost about everything.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Picasso.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up, I`ll take some swings at one of the best golfers in the world, Justin Thomas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Blue?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, it is so much more than a dance company. It`s like a second home for many young performers. We`ve heard Jennifer Lopez learned some of her moves here. Adriana Diaz takes us inside the cultural treasure, Ballet Hispanico.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Author, Christy Harrison says that wellness is not just about the fads you see online, in fact, some can do more harm than good, and she`ll tell us what it means to be truly healthy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. I hit the range with one of the best golfers in the world, Justin Thomas. We also talked about greatness and how his father helped him get there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You hit the range. Let`s see how that goes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just like you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT JOE BIDEN: Default is not an option. America pays its debts.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: President Biden plans to shorten an overseas trip as debt ceiling negotiations continue with the US just days away from a possible default.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: He plans to speak with congressional leaders by phone regularly and that their staffs would meet here in Washington daily.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: North Carolina state Republicans override a veto to pass a new law banning most abortions after just 12 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Secret Service is investigating how a man broke into the home of national security adviser Jake Sullivan last month, and then got away.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The man slipped past at least three Secret Service agents, and through the robust security system inside.\par \par \uc2 KHALIL WATSON (Was paralyzed in 2016 after being shot): I do this for my family, my friends, myself. I will now and be able to say that all my hard work has finally paid off.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And this was an incredible walk across the stage for a college graduate who was paralyzed when he was shot seven years ago.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Khalil "Amazing" Watson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, what a feeling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Love to see it. Love to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I do, too. Just getting that diploma is one thing, but then to be able to walk when people said you would never be able to do that again.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Double the congratulations, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230518ej5h000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Abortion Restrictions\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 1,226 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 President Biden will cut short a trip overseas as he negotiates a deal to raise the debt ceiling and avoid economic catastrophe.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): The staff is going to continue meeting daily to make sure we do not default.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Republican lawmakers in North Carolina pass new abortion restrictions over a veto by the governor.\par \par \uc2 JULIE VON HAEFEN (D) (North Carolina State Representative: This devastating ban on abortion is deeply unpopular.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tech leaders behind artificial intelligence call on Congress to regulate their industry before it`s too late.\par \par \uc2 SAM ALTMAN (\uc2 OpenAI\uc2  CEO): If this technology goes wrong, it can go quite wrong.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Secret Service announced the drunk intruder made their way into the home of a national security adviser.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Taco Bell filing a petition to cancel the Taco Tuesday trademark apparently owned by a place called Taco John`s.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Yankees\uc2  pitcher, Domingo German busted for using sticky stuff.\par \par \uc2 MAN: German is now facing a 10-game suspension.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And Soler hits one to the left. Soler, so long.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A two run walk off home run for the \uc2 Marlins\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Jokic just gets it off in time. It is up. Oh, it is good. Of course, it goes in. Jokic stares down. Anthony Davis just stares at him.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Announcers running out of adjectives to describe him. Buzzer beater to end the third quarter. Another triple double for the Joker.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 Nuggets\uc2  survived the comeback and win Game 1. Well, I can`t wait for Game 2.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think he had it right. Can`t wait for Game 2. But remember Nate, last time, you said whoever wins the first between the Lakers in the Warriors would win the -- do you still say that?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a tough blow to recover from the Joker. The one that hit that shot right before the fourth quarter, it is one of the best, if not the best player in the game.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what do you say?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So it is going to be tough.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are you saying, Mr. Burleson?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am still going with the Lakers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m not going to bet against LeBron.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Against LeBron.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. And don`t bet against this show because we have a good one.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We are taking some big swings on today`s show with one of the best golfers in the world. Justin Thomas, he is the star of a hit reality show about his life in the game including a special relationship with his dad.\par \par \uc2 We also talk with him about the controversial LIV Golf Tour and his friendship with Tiger Woods.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got a lot to talk about with him.\par \par \uc2 Plus, Adriana Diaz takes us inside the Ballet Hispanico. It`s a cultural treasure right here in New York City for the last 50 years.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Lopez is just one of the dancers who trained there. We know she can dance. We`re going to speak to the young performers who say this is so much more than choreography. Can`t wait for that.\par \par \uc2 But first we`re going to start with this. There has been a dramatic shift in the laws on abortion affecting millions of women in this country.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina abortion rights supporters protested last night`s vote by Republican lawmakers to override the governor`s veto and establish a 12- week limit on abortions.\par \par \uc2 In Montana, where the state constitution protects abortion rights, the governor signed a bill yesterday there to outlaw the most common method of abortion after 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Montana and North Carolina join other Republican-controlled states in reducing abortion access.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin Huey-Burns is in Raleigh, that of course is the capital of North Carolina, with more on the story.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, North Carolina had been one of the few remaining states in the South with access to abortion. Now, it joins 19 other states that have either banned or restricted the procedure and to get it done, Republicans here pushed the measure through and just 42 hours.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: The bill becomes law --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: At North Carolina`s State Capitol in Raleigh, Tuesday night, Republicans overruled the Democratic governor and pushed through new abortion restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Under the new law, which goes into effect July 1st, most abortions will be banned after 12 weeks of pregnancy, a reduction from 20 weeks under the previous law.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ROY COOPER\uc2  (D-NC): The veto stamp has been applied.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Just three days earlier, North Carolina`s Democratic governor, \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2  rejected the bill.\par \par \uc2 GOV. ROY COOPER (D-NC): It will make abortion unavailable to many women --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But his power is limited.\par \par \uc2 Last month, a Democratic state representative from the liberal leaning Charlotte area switched parties just five months after elections.\par \par \uc2 TRICIA COTHAM (North Carolina state representative): I will not be controlled by anyone.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The change gave Republicans as supermajority and the power to override vetoes from Governor Cooper.\par \par \uc2 Is there anything that you can do unilaterally on the issue of abortion?\par \par \uc2 MAN: We will never stop fighting to protect women`s health.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: The White House protested late Tuesday night saying: "Women have been left with no choice, but to travel hundreds of miles for the care they need."\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN (A Woman`s Choice CEO): We just helped a patient from Alabama --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: We went to a clinic in Raleigh last October that was already seeing a surge in out-of-state patients.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: They drove a 10-and-a-half hour trip to make sure they were at the clinic at nine o`clock that morning.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Ten-and-a-half hours.\par \par \uc2 KELLY FLYNN: Each way.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Now, there are only two states in the south that offer unrestricted -- relatively unrestricted access to abortion, that`s Virginia and neighboring South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But Gayle, Republicans in South Carolina are working to push through a six- week abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Caitlin, the conversation continues. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In our next half hour, we`re going to take you overseas to Amsterdam, to find out how a European doctor is helping Americans get access to abortion care.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230518ej5h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For May 14, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,747 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: border politics and an exclusive with Republican presidential candidate Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 Title 42, the pandemic era restriction that allowed the U.S. to quickly expel migrants, has been lifted.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TED CRUZ (R-Texas): The invasion we`re going to see is going to be massive.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): It`s going to be chaotic for a while.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But have those fears been realized? We will have firsthand reports from Republican Congressman Tony Gonzales and the mayors from two big border cities, Laredo and El Paso.\par \par \uc2 And what`s the solution for fixing the overall immigration issue? The 2024 election is still more than 500 days away, but, already, Republican primary contenders are offering their ideas.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida): Don`t tell me foreigners can just decide to come across the border when they want to. We decide who comes into this country as Americans.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): When you say to a family that, if you come, we`re going to break you up, they don`t come.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will speak exclusively with former South Carolina Governor and Trump \uc2 U.N\uc2 . Ambassador Nikki Haley, and get her thoughts on solving the border crisis.\par \par \uc2 Plus: As inflation slows ever so slightly, we will talk with the Biden administration`s new top economic adviser, \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Finally, on this Mother`s Day, we will take a closer look at a disturbing trend, a rise in maternal mortality rates. We will be joined by Democratic Congresswoman Lauren Underwood, who`s trying to find a way for Congress to help.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Customs and Border Patrol says the first days of new border policies did not bring a spike in illegal crossings. But migration is still at historically high levels. Border Patrol is preparing for daily migrant arrivals to increase to between 12,000 to 14,000 per day, and they face major operational challenges, with many detention facilities over capacity.\par \par \uc2 Not surprisingly, the narratives we`re hearing about the situation coming from the two political parties are at odds. We have three Texas guests standing by for firsthand updates, a Republican Congressman, a Democratic mayor and an independent mayor.\par \par \uc2 But we want to begin with former South Carolina Governor and presidential hopeful Nikki Haley.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY (R-Presidential Candidate): Good morning. Happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And happy Mother`s Day to you as well.\par \par \uc2 I want to start with the issue of the day, what`s happening at the border. The Biden administration has done a number of things. They have restricted asylum, barring migrants who appear at a port of entry without first having asked for refuge in a third country. If they try to enter without permission, they face a five-year ban.\par \par \uc2 They have increased deportation flights, and they plan to open processing centers in Latin America. Do you support any of that?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Well, I`m just surprised it took them so long.\par \par \uc2 If you look at the fact that it wasn`t broken to start with, they broke it. Five million illegal immigrants have crossed the border. I went with Congressman Gonzales 400 miles along that border. And what I saw was unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 You have ranchers that get up and get their coffee in the morning and go see if someone died crossing the fence. They pick up any little kids left over and take them to Border Patrol. When you talk to sheriffs, sheriff say, before 7:00 a.m., they have rounded up illegal immigrants, turned them over to Border Patrol, who documents them, and then releases them until their court date three or four years from now.\par \par \uc2 You ask Border Patrol what they do, and they said: We`re glorified baby- sitters.\par \par \uc2 We need to let them do their job. We have got to enforce some things. And we should do what I did when I was governor, which is pass one of the toughest immigration laws in the country, and do a mandatory E-Verify program that says no businesses can hire anyone that`s in this country illegally.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Just for context, there is a global migration crisis, as you know.\par \par \uc2 But, for decades, Congress has failed to enact any kind of significant immigration reform, including during the Trump administration, when there was unified Republican control.\par \par \uc2 How different would it be? Why would it be any different if you were in charge?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Well, first of all, I will tell you, this is a crisis created by Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 This very much should have been dealt with a long time ago, and it wasn`t. What I would do is, first of all, do the mandatory E-Verify. I would defund sanctuary cities. I would go back to remain-in-Mexico, because no one wants to remain in Mexico.\par \par \uc2 I would fire the 87,000 IRS agents that are going after Middle America and put 25,000 Border Patrol and ICE agents on the ground. And instead of catch-and-release, let`s go to catch-and-deport. We have to be serious about the fact that we are a country of laws.\par \par \uc2 And the second we stop being a country of laws, we give up everything this country was founded on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, as you know, deportation is problematic to countries where the U.S. has strained relations, like Venezuela and Cuba. So, even if you catch, you can`t necessarily deport.\par \par \uc2 So, for Cuba and Venezuela, would you keep those Trump era sanctions on those countries, which are already economically distressed? And some would say the sanctions make it even worse, sending migrants here.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: We always want to take care of people who have been persecuted, but we got to take care of Americans first.\par \par \uc2 We got to start looking at the fact that every state`s a border state, that we have had enough fentanyl cross the border that would kill every single American. The number one cause of death for adults 18 to 49 is fentanyl. Why don`t we focus on that first? We can`t take care of anybody else if we can`t take care of ourselves.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you would keep the sanctions on those countries is what I hear?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I think -- I think we need to stop the bleeding at the border and completely do immigration reform...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: ... before we can think of taking anybody else into this country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How would you change America`s asylum policies? Because many of those people coming to these ports of entries are doing it legally. They are asking for protection.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Well, I think there`s legal immigration that we can focus on at the same time, and I think that needs to be focused on. What does our country need?\par \par \uc2 Let`s do it by merit. Let`s do it by talent. Let`s do it based on what the businesses need. But let`s not do it just because people happen across the fence and get away. Let`s not do it because we have crowded facilities and we can`t hold any more. That`s the wrong way to go about it.\par \par \uc2 We have to make sure this is a national security issue. We have to vet them. We have to know exactly who`s coming in here. Iran has said that the easiest way to get into America is through the Southern border. We shouldn`t wait for another 9/11 to realize that Republicans and Democrats have to get in a room and figure out immigration reform...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: ... and start working for the American people, instead of the other way around.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would you revive President Trump`s policy of separating migrant children from their parents as deterrence?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: It should never get to that point.\par \par \uc2 No, we should not be separating families. But we shouldn`t be taking families that we don`t have any control over. That`s the biggest issue, is, no one wants to be inhumane about this. I saw when I was at the United Nations what happens to these people who are trafficked.\par \par \uc2 I mean, if a child loses a shoe, they just leave the child there. If a person gets sick, they leave them to die. So we shouldn`t be saying -- waving the green flag in the first place. This started when Biden took office and said, America`s open. We never should have had that happen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, migration has been spiking for years now.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you about another issue, and that is abortion. It is legal in your state of South Carolina up to 20 weeks post-conception, which is a law that you signed back in 2016 when you were the governor. There are exceptions if a mother`s life`s in danger or the fetus cannot survive. Do you want that to be the national standard?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Well, I don`t want unelected justices to be deciding something that`s personal.\par \par \uc2 I have long said I am pro-life not because the Republican Party tells me to be, but because my husband was adopted. But, having said that, I think what happened when it went back to the states, now there could be consensus in each state. There`s some states that have been pro-life. I welcome that. There are some states that have erred on the side of abortion. I wish that wasn`t the case, but it is.\par \par \uc2 I think that we need to make sure that people`s voices are heard. And I think we need to do this from a humanizing standpoint, and not a demonizing standpoint, which is done in the past.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. So the law you passed in South Carolina, you would not necessarily want as the national standard?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Well, the law in South Carolina was the furthest we could get it at the time. That was before Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 For a national standard, I think we have to tell the American people the truth. In order to do a national standard, you would have to have a majority of the House, 60 Senate votes, and a president. We haven`t had 60 pro-life senators in 100 years.\par \par \uc2 So the idea that a Republican president could ban all abortions is not being honest with the American people, any more than a Democrat president could ban these pro-life laws in the state. So let`s be honest with the American people and say, let`s find national consensus. Let`s agree on getting rid of late-term abortions.\par \par \uc2 Let`s agree on the fact that we need more adoptions. Let`s agree on the fact that we need accessible contraception. Let`s agree on the fact that mothers shouldn`t be jailed or go to -- get the death penalty for abortions.\par \par \uc2 But I will also ask you, let`s go and, if we`re going to talk about weeks, ask Kamala and Biden, are they good for 35 weeks, 36 weeks, 37 weeks? At what point are they OK?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Because, up until now, they were up for abortion up until the time of birth.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Is that -- is that what they think the national standard should be?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, President -- well, President Biden said he would sign Roe into law, which would be up to the point of fetal viability, which was roughly assumed to be about 24 weeks.\par \par \uc2 The majority of abortions are performed...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: Which is six months, which is six months, which is late-term abortion.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The majority of abortions are performed under 13 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Two of your fellow South Carolinians, including one that`s going to run for president, Tim Scott, we think, has said that he would sign into law abortion protections up to the 20th week. So, he is picking a week. Some of your fellow Republicans are. Why do you feel like that`s a misdirection?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I`m not going to lie to the American people. Nothing`s going to happen if we don`t get 60 votes in the Senate. We`re not even close to that on the Republican or the Democrat side.\par \par \uc2 Why try and divide people further? Why not talk about the fact that we should be trying to save as many babies as possible and support as many mothers as possible? I think the media has tried to divide them by saying we have to decide certain weeks. In states, yes. At the federal level, it`s not realistic. It`s not being honest with the American people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you, as a conservative woman, do you think it undermines your party if the Republican front-runner is someone who was just found liable, legally liable, for sexually abusing a woman?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: I have always said that anyone that feels like they have been sexually assaulted in any way should come forward and have their voice heard.\par \par \uc2 I also think anyone that`s been accused should be able to defend themselves. I was not on the jury. I`m not the judge. I think that both of them had their voices heard. There`s been a verdict.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: And there`s been an appeal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Trump was offered the...\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: And that`s for Trump to defend, and that`s Trump to decide that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He gave a deposition, but he didn`t go in to defend himself. And it was a jury that came to this conclusion.\par \par \uc2 Are you drawing into question the legal findings?\par \par \uc2 NIKKI HALEY: No, I said there`s a verdict and I think there`s been an appeal. And I think it stands where it stands. And I think the American people need to make a decision based on that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Nikki Haley, thank you for coming on today, making your case.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to go now to Republican Congressman Tony Gonzales with more on what he is seeing at the border.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES (R-Texas): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know you have shared with us some videos that you filmed Friday inside a Customs and Border Patrol facility in El Paso.\par \par \uc2 I want to show them to our audience now. And I want you to tell us what we`re seeing here. Why do you think this is significant?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Yes, first, I want to thank Nikki Haley for coming to my district a month ago and spending the day with me, seeing it firsthand.\par \par \uc2 This is what I`m hearing on the ground from mayors, from Border Patrol agents, from embedded media. Everyone is saying, it`s not that bad. So, on Friday, I visited El Paso. I went to the central processing center. And you`re seeing these videos. And this is what not that bad looks like.\par \par \uc2 In that -- in the El Paso Sector, there`s over 6,000 people that are in custody. In this particular facility, it`s meant to house 1,000 people. It`s housing over 3,000. In one of these rooms, it`s meant that max capacity is 90 people. There was over 400 in here. That`s a 450 percent capacity.\par \par \uc2 In another room meant to house 120 people, there was over 700 people in there. We can`t allow not that bad to be the normal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Just this week, there was a migrant child that died in \uc2 HHS\uc2  custody. It`s only the second time in history.\par \par \uc2 I`m not looking to point fingers. I`m trying to go that we should not allow not that bad to be the normal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that was the second death that we know of during the Biden administration of a child in custody. There were about six during the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 And it is horrific, I agree with you, in terms of children passing away like that.\par \par \uc2 But to the point you`re making about overcrowding, this is at the very heart of a legal dispute right now. As you know, in a Florida court, a federal judge has blocked the Biden administration from releasing migrants, even though the administration said they would monitor migrants if they had to release them.\par \par \uc2 They`re doing that because of the overcrowding. So what is the alternative, if a court is saying -- a judge is saying this?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Yes, the Biden -- this is where I disagree with the Biden administration. The Biden administration is getting it wrong, because you`re so focused on illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 They`re trying ways to streamline it with the app and other areas, that what needs to happen is, we need to focus on legal immigration. This is what is happening. You have got -- you have got McCarthy on one side, Kevin McCarthy on one side, that is demonizing those that come over illegally. You have got \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  on the other side in the Senate that makes it a morally good thing to help people that are coming over illegally.\par \par \uc2 Who gets hurt and this is people that have legal claims.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: You mentioned it earlier, legal, legitimate asylum claims.\par \par \uc2 And immigration reform gets further and further behind when we only focus on the illegal part. What we have to do is, we you have to enforce the laws that are already on the books. And Congress has to create a route where people can come over illegally.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, but, to that point, if someone is asking -- is waiting on processing and they need to be held, they can`t be deported, or they are waiting for an asylum claim, what is the alternative to releasing them?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: The alternative is bringing -- the president should have surged, should surge immigration judges to the border, and that person should get their case heard in days, not years.\par \par \uc2 Right now in El Paso, if you apply on the One app -- I was at the port of entry -- if you apply on the One app, your court date is 2031.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I mean, that`s eight years from now. The president can surge -- instead of surging 1500 troops, surge immigration judges. This is America. Get your day in court.\par \par \uc2 If you do not qualify for asylum, which nine out of 10 people don`t, you get sent back to your country of origin. The other part is this. Where is Secretary Blinken in this argument? His job is to build relationships with Cuba, with Venezuela, with Nicaragua. He is nowhere to be found.\par \par \uc2 Everything is put on Secretary Mayorkas as to solve. It`s unfair all the way around, in particular to the Americans that live here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you saying lift the sanctions off Cuba and Venezuela?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I think someone should go there and have a conversation...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: ... and work with these places.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these places aren`t easy places to work. There`s a reason why people are fleeing Venezuela, right? So, Instead of having them go down the asylum route, where we know they`re not going to qualify, where they`re going to have to spend their entire lives living in the shadows, why don`t we create a route where people can come over legally?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I think that`s through working visas.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, yes, but you need to get a law passed. You need to work with Democrats to do that.\par \par \uc2 So, I need to ask you because you just said something about Speaker McCarthy and demonizing some migrants. Homeland Security Chair Mark Green said this morning he has a five-point plan to build a case potentially to impeach the homeland security secretary, Alejandro Mayorkas. He wouldn`t say something like that if the speaker hadn`t signed off on it.\par \par \uc2 Is that where you should be focusing, Republicans should be focusing their energy right now, instead of brokering deals on new laws, an impeachment?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Well, what you see is, you see career politicians blame somebody else. It`s always somebody else`s fault, and not them.\par \par \uc2 And if the light can be shined on someone else, the more, the better, the more, the merrier. And, right now, the light is shined on Secretary Mayorkas. The reality, this is all of our problems, starting with the president of the United States, who needs to lead this country, and starting in Congress.\par \par \uc2 And you`re exactly right. I have been working with my colleagues on the other side of the aisle to come up with a piece of legislation that can get passed in the 118th Congress, not what I want, not what they want.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. So...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: What can we pass today to move the ball forward?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I think this country needs leadership, not - - not pointing fingers at one another.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Is Speaker McCarthy leading on migration and border reform?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I think you -- we passed this H.R.2 bill this week.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: And what you`re going to see is, everyone`s saying, we did our part. It`s those Senate Dem -- Senate Democrats that aren`t doing their part.\par \par \uc2 But in a district like mine, which is 42 percent of the Southern border, 60 percent of El Paso County, we`re not looking for symbolic bills that have no chance of becoming law.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: We only get help when a bill becomes a law and the stress is alleviated at all levels.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: What I`m seeing is, this overcrowding is becoming normal. It`s normal to release people. It`s normal to have these folks live in the shadows.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: All of it is wrong.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, I have to ask about one other thing.\par \par \uc2 You`d said in the past, if there were un-Christian anti-immigrant bills brought to the floor by Republican leaders, that you would block a vote to lift the debt ceiling. You used that as leverage to get some things taken out of this border bill that just passed.\par \par \uc2 Have you committed to leadership that you will vote yes on any debt ceiling bill that the speaker is able to get through in terms of his negotiations?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I have not. And, yes, no, that`s what -- that`s what leadership, whether it`s Democrat or Republican, always wants you. They want you to give your vote away to them.\par \par \uc2 I`m not -- my vote is for the people of the 23rd District. And one of the things that I have fought so hard in H.R.2 was to make sure that these anti-immigrant bills, like H.R.29, died on the vine. This was a bill that banned legal -- legal claims.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: That`s what I have been fighting for. And many in Congress, there is a new generation of both Republicans and Democrats that are fighting for legal immigration.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: And we are absolutely against illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I think you`re going to see our voice get louder and louder as we go on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, we will be tracking that work.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation is back in a minute.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to check in with the mayors of two cities on the Texas border with Mexico, starting with Mayor Oscar Leeser of El Paso.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mayor, are you getting the resources from the state and federal government that you need?\par \par \uc2 OSCAR LEESER (D-Mayor of El Paso, Texas): You know, we are.\par \par \uc2 And it`s really important to talk about that, because Secretary Mayorkas, FEMA, they really stepped up to help us make sure that we do the job of the federal government. We all know they`re not coming into El Paso. We know they`re coming into the United States, so it`s -- we`ve been working with them to make sure.\par \par \uc2 And we all know the immigration process is broken. There`s no ifs and buts about it. But we are getting the resources that we need, because our city and the Southern border couldn`t do it without federal aid.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you seeing any kind of health impact in your community?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR OSCAR LEESER: Any what? I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Health impact. There have been reports about health facilities -- facilities being overburdened.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR OSCAR LEESER: You know, and one of the things that`s -- when the judge passed that law that says that they have to have a court date prior to being released into the community, we`re prepared.\par \par \uc2 we are prepared. We have two schools that were -- that were empty, that -- that were not being used. We`ve opened it up to be ready to help the asylum seekers, to help them go, because, once they get their A-number, then they`re prepared to go through to their destination, whether it`s to meet with family, friends, or a job that they`ve arranged.\par \par \uc2 We`re prepared. And we have gotten the help. And we do see that the -- the Border Patrol sector is full. But, this morning, I got a number, and they`re at 5,700. And -- but we are prepared. We`re ready to move forward.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR OSCAR LEESER: And, you know, Secretary Mayorkas and the Biden administration has been a big help to our community, because we couldn`t do it on our own.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, there have been some complaints from nonprofit organizations that one of the problems with the federal funding through FEMA is that the government will only reimburse for documented migrants, not undocumented ones. That puts some strain on those local aid groups. What`s the impact?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR OSCAR LEESER: And that`s -- and that`s correct.\par \par \uc2 So, the -- we -- we had the Red Cross come in about two weeks ago to help the NGOs, help the diocese to provide food, provide help, and continue to work with them. So one of the things that, if you go into a federally aided shelter, that is the -- what ends up happening.\par \par \uc2 But we have had the Red Cross. The Red Cross is here in El Paso. And we`re -- they`ve been prepared to help. They`re continue to help, to help them overburdened, because the -- you know, we need the manpower. There`s no ifs and buts about that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Red Cross? I mean...\par \par \uc2 MAYOR OSCAR LEESER: Yes, the Red Cross.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... when -- when you hear, I -- I think that`s just a -- a statement that Americans hear about the Red Cross going into crises overseas. They don`t think about it in a U.S. city.\par \par \uc2 But that help is what you -- what you need. Are you seeing a concern with the undocumented population in your city?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR OSCAR LEESER: You know, that -- those are the ones that are not able to move through and continue to move through. And those are the ones that are continuing to be -- to work through the immigration process.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that, if you saw on Monday, we had about 3,000 that were out at Sacred Heart Church. The federal government, the Border Patrol went in there and actually helped them all get -- filled out the documents, get moving forward, and then the ones who couldn`t figure that out, they were returned into their -- hopefully into their country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR OSCAR LEESER: But one of the things you see is that we did something that hadn`t been done, and that`s to help them register...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR OSCAR LEESER: ... to help them do that, so they can move forward.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Mr. Mayor, good luck to you.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be back with a lot more "Face the Nation," including Laredo Mayor Victor Trevino.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A programming note: Be sure to tune in to Tuesday`s CBS Mornings.\par \par \uc2 Nate Burleson sat down with former President Barack Obama for a wide- ranging interview, including the work of his organization My Brother`s Keeper.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation, including the new head of the National Economic Council, \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Mayor Victor Trevino for a look at what`s going on in the southwestern border of Texas in Laredo.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 DR. VICTOR TREVINO (Mayor of Laredo, Texas): Good morning. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 A few days ago you said your city was boarding up like a hurricane in preparation for the end of Title 42. Does your city have the resources it needs? Do you feel like the hurricane has passed?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR TREVINO: Well, first - first of all, there`s no doubt that we`re seeing historic challenges in our border. And as a doctor I had already been seeing this. Even before the end of Title 42, our local hospitals were already at or near capacity, and there`s no pediatric intensive care unit.\par \par \uc2 But everything that we have been doing since the declaration of emergency has held up. And we have not been overwhelmed at this point. But yesterday we did receive around 700 migrants. And, however, because we received the overflow from El Paso and Brownsville, we`re still on high alert. And until we see the numbers at the border patrol custody centers go down, this is when we can say the episode has passed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You just said that there is no pediatric health center in your area. You said you were already medically underserved as a community. What`s the health situation for the migrants, particularly the children, that are coming?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR TREVINO: Yes, before they are mainly individuals. Now they`re family units. And family units have children that have traveled miles and miles. And, obviously, they will need some medical care. So, because we don`t have the pediatric intensive care unit in our city, that makes it concerning because as it is we`re at capacity most of the time in our hospitals. And ambulances sometimes have to wait outside the emergency room for one or two hours before we can treat patients.\par \par \uc2 Now, with this surge, this was very concerning. That`s why we activated the declaration of a disaster.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The mayor of El Paso was just with us and he said he was getting what he needed from the state and federal government and thanked Homeland Security.\par \par \uc2 Do you agree? Are you getting what you need?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR TREVINO: Well, we`re getting the buses. And migrants come in. They get processed. They get sent to our NGOs. And the amount of migrants we`re expecting initially, the big flow, is not here yet. A lot of things have to do with Mexico taking some - some migrants and the asylum rules have changed. We have to ask for asylum in different countries before you get here. And also Title 8 changed. You have to -- if you get deported, then you can face being barred for five years.\par \par \uc2 And also with the event of the TRO, that changed things a little bit. You have to get a court date before you get enrolled into a detentions -- into the - in to NGOs.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. Right. You`re talking about all the things the Biden administration has tweaked to make it essentially more difficult for those going through asylum, though still having that legal pathway there. More difficult meaning there are more restrictions around it.\par \par \uc2 When you hear presidential candidates talk about shutting down the border, what do you think about that?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR TREVINO: I think this is not a Democratic or Republican problem, it`s an American problem. And things should have been done a long time ago. Immigration and reform is long overdue.\par \par \uc2 And what we`re seeing now is a result of that. And as Americans, as -- we need to do better as a country. And the leader of the free world, these are things that we are the example of humanitarian efforts. But we have to have the laws also to coincide and do in balance with that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I think a lot of Americans would agree with that.\par \par \uc2 Where do you see the disconnect between the conversations in Washington and what you are seeing on the ground?\par \par \uc2 VICTOR TREVINO: I think that`s one of the reasons we have to have real time information from border patrol and people that work and live here. Border mayors, and cooperation is crucial. We need to have these things set and they need to listen and come down here to see what the actual situation is. And that is the disconnect I see that has been happening. Unless they get the real-time information and the real perception of what things are, then I think they can move on and make adequate rules.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Dr., Mr. Mayor, thank you for your time. Good luck to you.\par \par \uc2 And one quick note. I called Customs and Border Protection border patrol. So, my apologies to those agents. It`s border protection.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the economy and the new chairman of the National Economic Council, \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2 . She was most recently the number two at the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome to FACE THE NATION. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD (Director, \uc2 White House National Economic Council\uc2 ): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: Happy Mother`s Day to you too, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have certainly been busy in this new job. I want to ask you about where we are with the state of negotiations over the debt ceiling. The president has said talks are moving along. The secretary of the Treasury said negotiators have found some areas of agreement. That sounds like movement. How close are we to a deal?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: Yes, so the staff is very engaged. I would characterize the engagement as serious, as constructive. And, you know, I think it is, though, Margaret, helpful to just lift up and talk a little bit about what is at stake here. So, when I talk to CEOs, to business leaders around the country, they tell me things are actually going very well. But their biggest concern is that Congress might fail to prevent default and that that would be catastrophic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: It would lead to higher borrowing costs for cars, for mortgages, for small businesses, even for the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . And so the most important thing is making sure that Congress fulfills its basic responsibility to avert default.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said talks were constructive. I know Leader McConnell said the U.S. is not going to default. And the president, when he spoke, he said there was substantial movement in the sense that most everyone agreed that the -- defaulting the debt is off the table. So, you`re saying it`s not necessarily off the table?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: So, default is something that Congress knows how to avoid. Congress has acted --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s still very much a risk?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: Congress has acted 78 times in a row to avert default, to prevent default, and we know they have done it. They certainly did it three times under the last administration. So, our expectation is that Congress will do what is necessary, even as we continue to have parallel discussions on the budget, which is a normal thing that Congress needs to do every year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that`s what you`re talking about now, the -\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, the president was asked if the only option was a short-term lift of the debt ceiling. And he said he still thought there was time for a whole deal. Is that what you`re saying, it`s budget and debt ceiling and this can get done before June 1st?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: You know, a short term is not a fix. It`s not really addressing that core uncertainty that CEOs are talking about. It`s just really important to take default and address it. And Congress has the tools to do that. They have done it again 78 times previously.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we also are at the staff level seeing productive discussions around the budget, separately, but in parallel. And those discussions are also important and, of course, that`s something that Congress wrestles with every year. And so our hope is that those conversations also continue and arrive at a constructive place.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, the goal is still whole deal by June 1st?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: So, right now, the focus is on the budget and those discussions, but our expectation is that Congress will act to avert default in a timely manner.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you were just talking about your conversations with business leaders. I mean it seems like we`re in this like game of chicken here. \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2 `s CEO, \uc2 Jamie Dimon\uc2 , said that he set up a war room for contingency planning. They meet once a week and he`s going to have them start meeting multiple times potentially starting next Sunday. So, he`s planning for potentially the worst.\par \par \uc2 Was it a mistake to let things get to this point? Were you not the head of the largest bank of America - in America having to do this? Should the White House have started negotiating earlier?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: So, this is really a responsibility of Congress. Let`s just step back again. The U.S. always pays its bills. The discussions over the debt ceiling, Congress` basic obligation, is really just about paying the bills Congress has already passed laws to authorize.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: And so the conversations that are ongoing right now really very much focused on the budget, which is the future of the fiscal commitments that the U.S. can make. But in terms of the responsibility to pay our bills -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: America isn`t a deadbeat nation. We do pay our bills. Congress has always acted. And they know how to do it. And they have the capacity to do it in a timely manner.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure. But the bottom line is, you have to get to a deal by a hard date on the calendar. So, more time, I think, would have been helpful, no?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: So, you know, again, that is Congress` basic responsibility. I think there is a very clear record here where the American people want the U.S. to pay its bills. And I`m sure Congress will see its way clear to making sure that happens.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m going to ask you about banks in America. The Fed is the main regulator for many American banks. That`s where you were before you came to the White House.\par \par \uc2 You said the banking system is sound back in April, but since that time we saw another bank failure. We`re still seeing pressure. Why still, if it`s actually sound?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: So, it`s important to remember, Margaret, that our banking system has nearly 5,000 banks. Banks of all sizes. Small community banks in small communities all over the country, to the largest banks, the money center banks. And so, you know, back in March we did see the failure of three banks that are of midsize banks. And those were dealt with in a way where depositors never had any question but that they would have access to their deposits. So, in that sense, Americans have confidence.\par \par \uc2 The banking regulators took strong actions. We have the tools and they can be used again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But doesn`t -\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: More broadly, though, the banking system is sound, although we are monitoring very carefully.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But doesn`t the fact that we still see pressure here show the government sort of ad hoc decision-making on backstopping depositors hasn`t solved the problem?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: So, look, the environment has changed a lot over the last few years with interest rates going up by a large amount, and banks -- several banks that have now failed and been taken over by healthier banks didn`t manage their risks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: Those were isolated problems. But, of course, we`re all monitoring the situation very carefully. But we have the tools. We`ve taken strong actions. I think people know what the playbook is. And so in that sense --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, no more failures?\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: Well, in that sense, the system overall is sound. I can`t speak to particular institutions, obviously. That sits with the bank regulators. But, overall, you`ve seen that the banking system is sound and - and deserves continued vigilance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, \uc2 Lael Brainard\uc2 , thanks for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 LAEL BRAINARD: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Here in the U.S., maternal mortality is at a 60-year high with minority women facing a disproportionately higher rate. Democratic Congresswoman Lauren Underwood joins us from Naperville, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 REP. LAUREN UNDERWOOD (D-IL): Good morning, Margaret. Happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you very much for saying that. Happy Mother`s Day to all mothers out there.\par \par \uc2 Can you tell me, with - with those mothers in mind, why are so many American women dying?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD: So, we know that the United States leads the industrialized world in maternal deaths. And since 2018, maternal deaths have increased 89 percent. We`ve seen deaths among moms in all racial and ethnic groups. And a lot of that has been due to the Covid pandemic. Eighty percent of these deaths are preventable. We know why moms are dying and we know what policy solutions to take to save moms` lives. And so I`ve introduced a piece of legislation called the Momnibus, which is that evidence-based solution to end this crisis.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You put forward that package actually in 2020, again in 2021, including when Democrats controlled Congress. And I know Vice President Harris has been an ally on many of these issues. But why didn`t Democratic leadership push more of these into, you know, progress? Like, you`re still chipping away at this.\par \par \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD: You`re right. So, in 2021 we were so pleased that the first bill from the Momnibus, the Protecting Moms Who Served Act, to support our veteran moms, was signed into law by President Biden. That piece of legislation passed the House with unanimous bipartisan support. So, you`re absolutely right, the American people are united behind this as a priority, a shared value. And we`ve had broad, bipartisan support from the Congress to solve this problem.\par \par \uc2 We have several bills within the package that are bipartisan at this time. We have another bill to protect veteran moms called the Maternal Health for Veteran`s Act. We have one to address maternal mental health conditions and substance use disorders and another to make sure that the technology tools that are available on the market are available to all moms across the country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ve said many of these -- but none of those things that you are proposing now have a promise to make it to the president`s desk, right? I mean you`ve -- you`ve got to really push this and build a bipartisan coalition to turn this into law. Do you have Republican allies?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD: Absolutely. So, the Black Maternal Health Caucus is one of the largest bipartisan caucuses on Capitol Hill. As I`ve outlined, there are several pieces within the Momnibus that do have bipartisan support. And we`re coming on the heels of passing 80 percent of the Momnibus through the House in the last Congress. And so we have a pathway to getting this piece of legislation signed into law this year, and we are working hard with our colleagues across the aisle in the House and the Senate to do so.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, black and Native American women are disproportionately affected by maternal health issues. There have been books, like "The Pain Gap" by Anushay Hossain, that have characterized this as medical misogyny and institutionalized racism.\par \par \uc2 How much of a factor are those things?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD: Well, we certainly know that black moms are three to four times more likely to die of pregnancy-related complications. We know that Hispanic moms saw the largest increase year over year in the most recent CDC data. And we know that Native American moms have significant disparities as well.\par \par \uc2 But I want to be clear that this is a problem that touches every mom and all of our families in this country. It doesn`t matter if it`s an urban or rural community, it doesn`t matter if you live on the East Coast or West Coast or in the south. This is something that touches all of our lives. And so we need to make sure that we`re passing these solutions that solve the problems that we`re facing in our health care system.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, according to CDC, the deadliest states to give birth, those with the highest maternal mortality rates, are Arkansas, Mississippi, Tennessee, Alabama and Louisiana. These are all states with very large rural areas. Is it simply a lack of access to facilities?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD: So, that`s part of it. We`re seeing a lot of consolidation in our rural health care systems, a lot of hospital closures. Moms aren`t able to get into a facility of their choice to deliver their babies. And we also know that there is some real inequities when we talk about Medicaid and Medicaid expansion. We`re losing half of our moms in that postpartum period, a third in that extended postpartum period, where in a lot of these states Medicaid coverage does cut off after 60 days postpartum. That`s why I`m such a big supporter and the black maternal health caucus is a big supporter of a mandatory year-long postpartum Medicaid expansion that my colleague, \uc2 Robin Kelly\uc2 , has been leading in the Congress.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that matters because these maternal mortality rates, it`s not just dying in the process of giving birth, it`s going up to 40 days after giving birth. So if your health care cuts off at 60 days --\par \par \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD: A full year postpartum.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. So --\par \par \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. So those dates are key to you.\par \par \uc2 I want to also ask you about what we just heard in the recent days, that there were two children, two unaccompanied minors, one four and one 17 years old, who recently died in U.S. custody. I know you were very critical of the Trump administration when this happened on their watch. What do you make of this happening again?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD: In the United States we shouldn`t have children dying in our custody. And we have to continue to make sure that there is robust support along the border for those institutions - those agencies that are caring for migrant children, to making sure that they are getting the needed medical care. And I`m certainly focused on doing a thorough investigation of these deaths.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you think these may have been preventable?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN UNDERWOOD: I don`t know. We don`t know. I`ve not gotten those details yet. But since that time of the Trump administration, we have had success in shepherding resources to bolster the medical contracts and the services that are offered to migrant families along the border while they`re in U.S. custody. And since that time we had not had any deaths of children in custody. Again, in the United States, we should not have children dying while in U.S. custody. That`s unacceptable. And we certainly will be pursuing an investigation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, Congresswoman, we will look for progress on that investigation and with your bill here as you continue pushing for more protections for women giving birth in this country. Thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On this Mother`s Day, we want to take a look at the power of moms.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice over): Ever since I became one, the phrase "mommy" lands differently. My two-year-old is learning to talk, but he can shout "mommy" at the top of his lungs. His four-year-old brother shouts it daily around 3:00 a.m. It rarely fails to make me jump.\par \par \uc2 That five-letter phrase has so much embedded in it. Help me, I`m scared, I`m tired, I`m hungry, I want you to make it better. It`s inevitable that one day they`ll learn I`m not all powerful.\par \par \uc2 But motherhood means you don`t stop wanting to help. Whether it is mothers organizing the push for better schools, or to stop violence in them, or walking across continents to find them a better life.\par \par \uc2 Writer Elizabeth Stone said that having a child is to decide forever to have your heart go walking outside your body.\par \par \uc2 Motherhood is hard. I think of my own mom now with wonderment at how hard it must have been to juggle it all. I`ve learned multi-tasking is a distinctly mom trait.\par \par \uc2 My mother is one of the most sensitive and creative people that I know. She still answers around the clock calls for help from her kids, and my wonderful dad. I`m also grateful to my mother-in-law for raising a great son, and often riding to the rescue of her grandkids.\par \par \uc2 America has asked a lot of its moms in recent years. Today should not be the only day we take the time to say "thank you," we could not do it without you.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Some of my fellow \uc2 CBS\uc2  moms and I did a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  live conversation late last week. If you want to see it, you can find it on our FACE THE NATION \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching. And to all you moms out there, happy Mother`s Day.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230514ej5e00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Trump`s Controversial Town Hall\b0\par\par\uc2 Vladimir Duthiers, Robert Costa, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,384 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We turn now to former President Donald Trump`s return to the primetime TV spotlight and new signs his 2024 re-election campaign will be a lot like the last two.\par \par \uc2 In a CNN townhall last night, Trump repeated the lie that the 2020 election was rigged. He dodged questions on abortion, mocked his sexual abuse accuser, and celebrated his supporters who stormed the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa is in Manchester, New Hampshire with more on the townhall`s potential impact on the presidential race.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s allies are casting this event as a showcase for Republican voters coming amid his mounting legal challenges. But Democrats, meanwhile, are saying that some of Trump`s statements might scare off Independent voters, especially his suggestion that Congress should consider allowing the nation to default on its debt.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I say to the Republicans out there, congressmen, senators, if they don`t give you massive cuts, you`re going to have to do a default.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In a high-stakes townhall, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said Congress should allow the government to default, if it can`t cut a deal with the White House on spending as a June 1st deadline to raise the debt ceiling looms.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We might as well do it now, because you`ll do it later. And it could be very bad. It could be maybe nothing.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And on the Russian invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : If I`m president, I will have that war settled in one day, 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The former president would not commit to continuing US aid to Ukraine and would not say if he wants Ukraine to win the war.\par \par \uc2 KAITLAN COLLINS: Do you want Ukraine to win this war?\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I don`t think in terms of winning and losing, I think in terms of getting it settled.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Trump also repeated his false claims of fraud in the 2020 presidential election, dodged questions on abortion, and defended his actions on January 6th, as well as those who attended his rally the day the Capitol was stormed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : They were there with love in their heart. That was an unbelievable and it was a beautiful day.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Prompting President Biden to post on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : "The January 6 rioters were not good people. End of story."\par \par \uc2 The townhall was the first time Trump faced questions since a New York civil jury found him liable for sexual abuse and defamation of writer E. Jean Carroll, whose claims he repeatedly denied, disparaged, and mocked.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : What`s happening is they`re doing this for election interference, and I swear and I`ve never done that and I swear to -- I have no idea who the hell -- she is a whack job.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And at a New Hampshire diner, the legal issues facing Trump don`t seem to faze Republican voters like Louise Hoyt, who said she`s voting for Trump.\par \par \uc2 All these legal challenges, they don`t faze you?\par \par \uc2 LOUISE HOYT (New Hampshire Trump supporter): No, because they`ve come up with them out of woodwork from day one.\par \par \uc2 ASA HUTCHINSON (R), (Former Arkansas governor): The future of the Republican Party is at stake.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Campaigning to beat Trump for the GOP nomination, former Arkansas governor, \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2  told CBS News, Republicans will suffer if they backed Trump again.\par \par \uc2 What`s your assessment of Trump`s legal challenges?\par \par \uc2 ASA HUTCHINSON: All of his legal challenges are distractions from the issues that impact Americans and that candidates want to talk about.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Several Republican sources tell CBS News that Trump showed rivals and potential rivals exactly who he is in this townhall as this presidential race unfolds and some top Republicans privately tell us that while they would like to see Trump stumble in the coming months, they do not expect him to fade anytime soon -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, he has not changed, Bob. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now for more on this is our chief Washington correspondent, Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Major, I felt like someone said hey Siri, play Donald Trump`s greatest hits, and off he went. What stood out to you and what does it tell us about 2024?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So let`s think about this metaphorically as one hour at a political \uc2 Golden Corral\uc2 , a buffet. Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And what happens at a buffet when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the host? Well, because he says so many things that are factually inaccurate, all the food is mislabeled, but that doesn`t mean customers don`t get what they want.\par \par \uc2 For Trump customers, they loved the jokes. They love the Biden bashing. They loved the January 6 revisionism, and they love the sense that he was in the media`s lion`s den and he pushed back. So Trump supporters got what they wanted.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also at this metaphorical political \uc2 Golden Corral\uc2  got what they wanted. They saw Trump say that he was proud to have paved the way to ending now national protections legally for abortion, saying that the answer to inflation is to drill, drill, drill. For climate change motivated voters, that`s a red line.\par \par \uc2 They also heard him say that default is a real option and not such a bad thing. Democrats got a lot out of this \uc2 Golden Corral\uc2  experience as well, so to probably did Jack Smith, the special counsel here in Washington because he heard a lot about Trump`s, understand, motivations and frame of mind regarding documents he took to Mar-a-Lago and everything leading up to his mentality about January 6th.\par \par \uc2 So whether Trump intended to or not, three different customers at this political \uc2 Golden Corral\uc2 , metaphorically came, got what they wanted, and probably left more satisfied than they thought they would be.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, Major, I guess journalists in this metaphor of yours are kind of like food inspectors. They are there to make sure, everything is sanitary and up to code. What lessons were there last night for how to cover this unusual candidate in the months ahead?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: The facts are real and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will give what he regards and what his supporters regard as contextual truths. What do I mean by that? Trump lied and lied again about the 2020 election, but he also said things that for his supporters have gone beyond the facts of whether or not the 2020 election was stolen. Of course, it wasn`t. But he talks about Hunter Biden`s laptop, he talks about the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and \uc2 Twitter\uc2  Files, all those things contextually rings so much more true to his supporters, they don`t care about those other things.\par \par \uc2 So in that sense, we establish the facts. We remind the voters of that, and Trump`s politics will be Trump`s politics and the country will make its ultimate decision if he is the nominee.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Major. I will say, the \uc2 Golden Corral\uc2  never mislabels its food. It`s terrific. I always find it on the road when I`m out there. We appreciate the metaphor though. It`s a good one.\par \par \uc2 All right, ahead a surprising development in the case of Natalee Holloway, the Alabama teenager who disappeared 18 years ago on a Caribbean island, but first, it is 7:16. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up: A proposed change that could make birth control pills much more accessible.\par \par \uc2 Plus, when office workers stay home, this is what they leave behind. Find out how all of those empty offices could affect the nation`s economy.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230512ej5b0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Breast Cancer Screening Guidelines\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Dr. Celine Gounder \par \uc2 568 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A panel of medical experts recommends a major change to breast cancer screening in draft guidelines released just yesterday.\par \par \uc2 CBS News medical contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder is here with us. She is also by the way editor-at-large for public health at KFF. We`re glad you`re here, Dr. Gounder.\par \par \uc2 So what are the recommendations? This is big.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes. So this is the \uc2 US Preventive Services Task Force\uc2 . This is one of the many organizations that makes recommendations on breast cancer and other kinds of screening, and they are now recommending that all women start breast cancer screening by the age of 40 and that they get --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It used to be 50.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It used to be 50, right, and that they get their mammograms at least every other year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why the change do you think now?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, we`ve seen an increase in breast cancer rates among women under 50 over the last several years, particularly since 2015. And we`ve also had these really sticky health disparities.\par \par \uc2 We haven`t been able to move the needle on the rate of breast cancer mortality, deaths from breast cancer among Black women versus White women, and so this is an effort to try to improve those disparities.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But are the recommendations every other year you get the screening?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Every other year at a minimum.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At a minimum, okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: And insurance will cover mammograms and other breast cancer screening starting from the age of 40 every year. So if you were to want more frequent breast cancer screening, you do have that option.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Will insurance cover these?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Insurance will cover mammograms every year starting from age 40. What may or may not be covered, depending on the state you live in as the other testing like ultrasounds and MRIs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we keep hearing about women that have dense breast cancer tissue. Will this have an impact on that? Is that one of the reasons, too?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: So something like half of women have dense breasts, and your breasts get less dense as you age, but we know that`s a risk factor for breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 The recommendations are the same for now, but more research is needed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you would say this is a good thing. I don`t see a downside to getting it earlier, do you?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I don`t see a downside, particularly given the data suggests that you can save lives by doing this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. I think this is great news, actually.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much, Dr. Celine Gounder.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, our exclusive conversation with Charles Kelley, the hitmaking country trio, Lady A, talking to him right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230511ej5a000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Guidelines Are Announced For Breast Cancer Screenings\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nikki Battiste \par \uc2 831 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to an important story. An increase in breast cancer diagnoses has led to new guidelines for when women should get their first screenings. \uc2 The U.S. Preventive Services Task Force\uc2  now says women should begin regular mammograms starting at the age of 40.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, breast cancer accounts for more than 43,000 deaths every year, and it is the second leading cause of cancer deaths among women.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikki Battiste reports, it`s a -- it`s a disease that one in eight women will develop in her lifetime.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voiceover): The new guidelines, mammograms every other year beginning at 40, are a dramatic shift from the task force`s previous recommendation that women begin biannual screenings at age 50.\par \par \uc2 DR. MAXINE S. JOCHELSON (Memorial \uc2 Sloan Kettering Cancer Center\uc2 ): I`m happy they moved to 40, but every other year is not good enough.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: In fact, the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  recommends screenings every year, but beginning at age 45.\par \par \uc2 It`s also confusing. What should women do?\par \par \uc2 DR. MAXINE S. JOCHELSON: I think it`s very important for women to be proactive and really promote themselves for having what is good screening.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: According to the \uc2 Radiological Society of North America\uc2 , the rate of metastatic breast cancer among women between 25 to 39 is up 32 percent since 2009.\par \par \uc2 SARA GIGLIO (Breast Cancer Survivor): I had a double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Despite having no family history, Sara Giglio chose to get annual mammograms before she was 40. She was just 42 with a newborn baby when she was diagnosed.\par \par \uc2 SARA GIGLIO: I did find out after the surgery that the cancer did spread.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Like nearly half of women 40 and older in the U.S., Giglio has dense breasts, which increase the risk of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jules Cohen is her oncologist.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULES COHEN (Medical Oncologist, Stony Brook Cancer Center): If you`re worried that you`re going to be diagnosed with an incurable disease, and that keeps you away from screening, I would say that`s -- that`s not something you need to worry about.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The new guidelines do not apply to people with a personal history or high risk of breast cancer. They should continue to consult doctors on exam frequency.\par \par \uc2 Women with dense breasts can opt for additional screening, like an ultrasound or MRI, but they`re often not covered by insurance -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikki Battiste with that important information, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, President Biden sat down with congressional leaders in the Oval Office this afternoon in hopes of finding common ground on raising the debt limit and avoiding an economic disaster.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 All right, Nancy, did any agreements come from this, come out of this high- stakes meeting?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Unfortunately, Norah, if Wall Street and Main Street were looking for reassurance coming out of this meeting, they did not get it.\par \par \uc2 Instead, President Biden and House Speaker Kevin McCarthy stuck to their positions and agreed to meet again on Friday. But, at that point, the U.S. could be less than three weeks away from defaulting on its debt, unless Congress acts.\par \par \uc2 And here`s why this is so serious. Even a brief default could cause a downgrade of U.S. credit and delay the distribution of food stamps and Social Security checks and so much more, while a prolonged crisis could drive up interest rates and put more than eight million Americans out of work.\par \par \uc2 Now, here`s the impasse. President Biden says Congress needs to raise the nation`s borrowing limit, no strings attached. But Speaker McCarthy and Republicans say the debt limit hike needs to be paired with major domestic spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Did the president tell you that there were any spending cuts that he would be open to?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): Well, I asked him numerous times, are there some places we could find savings? He wouldn`t give me any.\par \par \uc2 So I`m hopeful that we will be able to find them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Top aides from both sides are going to keep talking, but there`s not much time left to work this out. Congress only has 12 working days between now and June 1, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Quite a stalemate.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230511ej590002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS for May 9, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Manuel Bojorquez, Nicole Sganga, Nancy Cordes,  Nikki Battiste, Omar Villafranca, Major Garrett, Elaine Quijano, Jan  Crawford \par \uc2 3,696 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  becomes the first former president in history to be found liable of sexual abuse and defamation. The breaking news, after a federal jury awards former columnist E. Jean Carroll $5 million in damages.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 A jury in New York City deliberates for less than three hours in the civil trial against the former president.\par \par \uc2 JOE TACOPINA (Attorney For \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ): Part of me was obviously very happy that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not branded a rapist.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news, embattled George Santos charged by the Justice Department. What we are learning.\par \par \uc2 New details about the Texas mall shooter`s disturbing online history and the hero police officer who saved lives.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN HARVEY (Allen, Texas, Police Chief): He immediately addressed an attacker that was injuring and murdering innocent victims.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The debt ceiling standoff shifts to the White House, where President Biden holds a high-level meeting with congressional leaders.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): We have now just two weeks to go.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Today, there are new guidelines on breast cancer screening. How often should we get a mammogram?\par \par \uc2 So you think every other year is not sufficient?\par \par \uc2 DR. MAXINE S. JOCHELSON (Memorial \uc2 Sloan Kettering Cancer Center\uc2 ): Correct.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON WRAY (Principal Interior Designer, Quinn Evans): Every librarian is asking to have one in their space.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Our series, Moms in Focus: What happens when a mother sees a problem? She comes up with an innovative way to help other working parents.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Lonnie Walker gets up the first shot on the court.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: Lonnie Walker, great effort.\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Walker, pull-up. Walker hits another one! It`s the Lonnie Walker game!\par \par \uc2 LEBRON JAMES (American Professional Basketball Player): We don`t win the game without Lonnie Walker tonight. That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 We began with breaking news, verdicts in the civil rape and defamation trial of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . A federal jury in New York found \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  liable for sexually abusing advice columnist E. Jean Carroll nearly 30 years ago. The jury also found the former president liable for defaming Carroll when he denied the allegations and said she was lying to get publicity for her book.\par \par \uc2 The former writer for "Elle" magazine was awarded $5 million in damages. The trial saw Carroll testify in graphic detail about the assault inside a luxury department store dressing room in the 1990s. The jury was also shown a videotaped deposition of Trump being questioned under oath about the allegations.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elaine Quijano has been following the trial and is going to start us off tonight outside the federal courthouse in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 E. Jean Carroll appeared to nod as the jury`s decision was read. It was an emotional nine-day trial. And, in a statement, Carroll called the verdict a victory, not just for her, but for democracy and sexual assault survivors everywhere.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO (voiceover): Writer E. Jean Carroll walked out of court saying very little, but the jury`s verdict spoke volumes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We`ve been here the whole time.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: After just under three hours of deliberations, the jury of six men and three women found Trump liable for one count of battery and one count of defamation.\par \par \uc2 It awarded Carroll about $2 million for abuse and about $3 million for defamation.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: This verdict is the first time that a former president of the United States has actually been found liable for sexual abuse, as well as defamation.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Carroll had accused Trump of raping her in a New York department dressing store dressing room in the mid-`90s. She first went public with her claim in 2019 memoir.\par \par \uc2 Trump denied the accusation, calling it a hoax.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I was falsely accused by this woman. I have no idea who she is. It`s ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: During the trial, Carroll gave graphic testimony. She told jurors: "I`m here because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  raped me, and, when I wrote about it, he said it didn`t happen. He shattered my reputation."\par \par \uc2 Trump waived his right to testify, and the defense called no witnesses. Outside the courthouse, his lawyer, Joe Tacopina, said the deck was stacked against his client.\par \par \uc2 JOE TACOPINA (Trump Defense Lawyer): Part of me was obviously very happy that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was not branded a rapist.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: But, during the trial, jurors heard parts of Trump`s deposition in the case.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: And she`s not my type.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: He misidentified Carroll in a photograph as his second wife, Marla Maples.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: It`s Marla.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: And was asked about that infamous "Access Hollywood" recording.\par \par \uc2 ROBERTA KAPLAN (Attorney For E. Jean Carroll): It`s true with stars that they can grab women by the (EXPLETIVE DELETED)?\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: Well, that`s what -- if you look over the last million years, I guess that`s been largely true, not always, but largely true, unfortunately or fortunately.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: His deposition could be said to be a disaster in this case. The worst witness against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Trump`s lawyers say they plan to appeal. The former president is facing a host of other legal challenges, including criminal probes in New York, Washington and Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 The former president has denied any wrongdoing -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elaine Quijano, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s bring in chief Washington correspondent Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 Good evening.\par \par \uc2 All right, what`s the political impact of this? Because he`s running for president.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And he`s the current Republican front-runner, not just a former president. But this was, in all terms, Norah, a character sketch of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  then and now. And how -- and Senate Republicans are not rallying to his defense.\par \par \uc2 Let`s just talk about the case for a second. The defense for former President Trump was, he`s rude, he`s crude, but he wouldn`t sexually batter this woman and then defame her. Well, in a contested process, where everyone had every opportunity to put evidence and witnesses before a jury, a jury said no, in fact, that`s precisely what the former president did.\par \par \uc2 Now, those who rally to the former president`s defense say, well, there was a character debate in 2016 he passed and was elected or this is all politically timed. But Senate Republican leaders today, Norah, on Capitol Hill, in the aftermath of this verdict, said, there`s too much drama. Voters will evaluate that. And, yes, Trump could get the nomination, but he can`t be elected in a general election.\par \par \uc2 That`s Senate Republicans, their verdict on this verdict.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: That suggests the tide may be starting --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Possibly.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- to turn.\par \par \uc2 Major Garrett, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s turn now to some more breaking news. CBS News has just learned that the Justice Department filed charges against embattled New York Republican Congressman George Santos. He is expected to surrender in New York tomorrow, when the nature of the charges will be revealed.\par \par \uc2 CBS News previously reported that federal prosecutors were looking into the finances and financial disclosures of the freshman congressman after he admitted to lying about parts of his resume and background. A spokesman for Santos has declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Now to the investigation into the mass shooting at an outlet mall in the Dallas suburb of Allen, Texas. Eight people were killed, including three children, and seven others injured.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Omar Villafranca reports, the gunman had been researching the location for weeks. But police say his victims appeared to be chosen at random.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voiceover): Tonight, investigators are running digital forensics on the gunman`s cell phone and computers, trying to learn more about a possible motive.\par \par \uc2 Troopers confirmed the shooter had embraced white supremacist and neo-Nazi ideology in online posts he made on a Russian social media site.\par \par \uc2 HANK SIBLEY (Regional Director, \uc2 Texas Department of Public Safety\uc2 ): We do know that he had neo-Nazi ideation. He had patches. He had tattoos. Even his signature verified that.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Investigators today admitted they still don`t know why the gunman opened fire outside the Allen Premium Outlets.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR HANK SIBLEY: The big question that we`re dealing with right now is, what`s his motive? Why did he do this? Well, the big question is, we don`t know.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: But authorities say the attack didn`t last long, thanks to the quick response of an Allen police officer who stopped the shooter.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR HANK SIBLEY: I think, within three or four minutes, the Allen P.D. officer had neutralized a suspect.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: We understand that he worked some private security gigs before. Did he ever work at the Allen Premium Outlets?\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR HANK SIBLEY: Not to my knowledge.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Investigators also said they believe the shooter targeted the location, but shot people at random.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR HANK SIBLEY: He was very random in the people he killed. It didn`t matter the age, same race or sex. He just shot people, and which is horrific in itself.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Among the eight people killed was 26-year-old engineer Aishwarya Thatikonda. For friends and co-workers, the shock of the shooting is still hard to fathom.\par \par \uc2 SRINIVAS CHALUVADI (President & CEO, Perfect General Contractors LLC): She had big dreams. She wanted to make a living in this country, to become a permanent resident, buy a home, get married, make a family, build a family.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Troopers say the shooter had three guns on him at the time of the attack and had five more in the car, along with dozens of rounds of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 All were legally purchased. And investigators aren`t releasing the name of the Allen police officer who stopped the shooter. But many are calling him a hero. And, through his attorney, he says he`s doing well. He`s just trying to process this tragedy -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: No doubt.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now to an important story. An increase in breast cancer diagnoses has led to new guidelines for when women should get their first screenings. The U.S. Preventive Services Task force now says women should begin regular mammograms starting at the age of 40.\par \par \uc2 In the United States, breast cancer accounts for more than 43,000 deaths every year, and it is the second leading cause of cancer deaths among women.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikki Battiste reports, it`s a -- it`s a disease that one in eight women will develop in her lifetime.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE (voiceover): The new guidelines, mammograms every other year beginning at 40, are a dramatic shift from the task force`s previous recommendation that women begin biannual screenings at age 50.\par \par \uc2 DR. MAXINE S. JOCHELSON (Memorial \uc2 Sloan Kettering Cancer Center\uc2 ): I`m happy they moved to 40, but every other year is not good enough.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: In fact, the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  recommends screenings every year, but beginning at age 45.\par \par \uc2 It`s also confusing. What should women do?\par \par \uc2 DR. MAXINE S. JOCHELSON: I think it`s very important for women to be proactive and really promote themselves for having what is good screening.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: According to the \uc2 Radiological Society of North America\uc2 , the rate of metastatic breast cancer among women between 25 to 39 is up 32 percent since 2009.\par \par \uc2 SARA GIGLIO (Breast Cancer Survivor): I had a double mastectomy.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Despite having no family history, Sara Giglio chose to get annual mammograms before she was 40. She was just 42 with a newborn baby when she was diagnosed.\par \par \uc2 SARA GIGLIO: I did find out after the surgery that the cancer did spread.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Like nearly half of women 40 and older in the U.S., Giglio has dense breasts, which increase the risk of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jules Cohen is her oncologist.\par \par \uc2 DR. JULES COHEN (Medical Oncologist, Stony Brook Cancer Center): If you`re worried that you`re going to be diagnosed with an incurable disease, and that keeps you away from screening, I would say that`s -- that`s not something you need to worry about.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The new guidelines do not apply to people with a personal history or high risk of breast cancer. They should continue to consult doctors on exam frequency.\par \par \uc2 Women with dense breasts can opt for additional screening, like an ultrasound or MRI, but they`re often not covered by insurance -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Nikki Battiste with that important information, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, President Biden sat down with congressional leaders in the Oval Office this afternoon in hopes of finding common ground on raising the debt limit and avoiding an economic disaster.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes joins us now from the White House.\par \par \uc2 All right, Nancy, did any agreements come from this, come out of this high- stakes meeting?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Unfortunately, Norah, if Wall Street and Main Street were looking for reassurance coming out of this meeting, they did not get it.\par \par \uc2 Instead, President Biden and House Speaker Kevin McCarthy stuck to their positions and agreed to meet again on Friday. But, at that point, the U.S. could be less than three weeks away from defaulting on its debt, unless Congress acts.\par \par \uc2 And here`s why this is so serious. Even a brief default could cause a downgrade of U.S. credit and delay the distribution of food stamps and Social Security checks and so much more, while a prolonged crisis could drive up interest rates and put more than eight million Americans out of work.\par \par \uc2 Now, here`s the impasse. President Biden says Congress needs to raise the nation`s borrowing limit, no strings attached. But Speaker McCarthy and Republicans say the debt limit hike needs to be paired with major domestic spending cuts.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Did the president tell you that there were any spending cuts that he would be open to?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): Well, I asked him numerous times, are there some places we could find savings? He wouldn`t give me any.\par \par \uc2 So I`m hopeful that we will be able to find them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Top aides from both sides are going to keep talking, but there`s not much time left to work this out. Congress only has 12 working days between now and June 1, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Quite a stalemate.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, and another deadline facing the Biden administration. Border cities have declared states of emergency, as thousands of migrants await the ending of the pandemic-era rule known as Title 42.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Manuel Bojorquez reports, President Biden spoke with Mexico`s president by phone today to try to work on some possible solutions to this growing crisis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ (voiceover): Hundreds of migrants continue to arrive in El Paso daily, seeking shelter at facilities that are already filled to capacity, among them Johana Yovera, who is from Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 She crossed the border five days ago with her 11- and 13-year-old daughters. They were one of the lucky ones to get a bed.\par \par \uc2 (JOHANA YOVERA speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: She wants a better life for her daughters, she says. The economy and the education in Venezuela are not good.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Today, border officials handed out these flyers, warning migrants to go through proper immigration channels or face arrest or deportation. You can see long lines of migrants waiting to surrender.\par \par \uc2 With days to go before Title 42 expires, city and state officials are bracing for a sudden increase in migrant crossings. Republican Texas Governor Greg Abbott sent nearly 600 more National Guard troops to beef up the border.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GREG ABBOTT (R-Texas): President Biden is laying down a welcome mat to people across the entire world, saying that the United States` border is wide open.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: But the Biden administration announced new regulations. One policy would banish migrants from the U.S. for five years if they go outside the asylum process or are deemed ineligible for protection.\par \par \uc2 Still, in Brownsville, Texas, city officials are also dealing with the migration influx.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nicole Sganga is there.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Here in Brownsville, about 2,000 migrants arriving daily, immigration officials using Fort Brown, a former military outpost, as a pop-up processing center to help expedite asylum claims.\par \par \uc2 They`re hopeful this contingency measure meets the demand. But the question now, for how long?\par \par \uc2 TREY MENDEZ (Mayor of Brownsville, Texas): The city of Brownsville`s process has always been something that could process about 1,000 people a day. We have been doing it about 16 hours a day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Back here in El Paso, authorities said they would limit traffic along the main port of entry as part of the operation to reduce illegal crossings.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, the first of 1,500 military personnel are set to arrive at the border, not to interact with migrants, but support Border Patrol operations -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Manny Bojorquez, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Also in Brownsville, a vigil is planned tonight for the eight victims killed by an SUV driver who slammed into a group of migrants outside a shelter on Sunday. The driver has been charged with eight counts of manslaughter and 10 counts of aggravated assault with a deadly weapon. Investigators are awaiting toxicology results and are trying to determine if the crash was intentional.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas, Russian leader \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  presented his twisted take on his invasion of Ukraine today, saying the West has unleashed a real war against Russia. Putin spoke at a World War II Victory Day parade, which was scaled down to only a single tank because much of Russia`s weaponry is either in Ukraine or has been destroyed in the war. Overnight, Russia attacked Ukraine with dozens of missiles.\par \par \uc2 An investigation is under way after a small plane crash in the suburb of Tacoma, Washington.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Two people were critically injured today when a small sea plane crashed in a suburb of Tacoma, Washington.\par \par \uc2 You can see the mangled wreckage in the yard of a home. No houses were hit and no one else was injured. The cause of the wreck now under investigation.\par \par \uc2 An 8-year-old is lost in the wilderness for two days. We will have his remarkable story. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to a remarkable story of survival from Michigan`s Upper Peninsula.\par \par \uc2 Police say an 8-year-old boy camping with his family got lost while gathering firewood on Saturday. For two days, more than 150 people searched for him. On Monday, he was finally found safe and sound about two miles from his campsite. He had sheltered under a log with nothing to eat or drink, but came through it appearing to be just fine.\par \par \uc2 Very lucky, indeed.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up: A Virginia library system is helping working moms stay connected to their kids, and it`s catching on around the world.\par \par \uc2 That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Finally tonight: While remote work has been a blessing for millions, it comes with its own unique challenges.\par \par \uc2 Nobody understands that more than working moms, who often find themselves doing double duty, both their jobs and childcare.\par \par \uc2 In our continuing series Moms in Focus, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford finds, necessity is truly the mother of invention.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voiceover): For keeping your concentration, it is hard to beat the library.\par \par \uc2 T`KEIAH WOODSON (Parent): Hello.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Especially when you have to keep your eye on your little one.\par \par \uc2 T`KEIAH WOODSON: At home, it`s impossible. Here, I can just get things done.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Here at the Fairfield Area Public Library, near Richmond, Virginia, there is a special place for children and their parents, like kindergarten teacher T`keiah Woodson.\par \par \uc2 T`KEIAH WOODSON: With the creative center, it is much, much easier, and they`re close enough where I can just reach over, but I can still do what I need to do on the computer.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It`s called Work and Play Station, designed and created by two working moms.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA WEEDMAN (Director, Henrico County Public Library): I would see moms and parents and caregivers struggling, and I thought, there has got to be a better way to do this.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: When the library opened four years ago, photos quickly went viral.\par \par \uc2 SHANNON WRAY (Principal Interior Designer, Quinn Evans): All of the public libraries I`m working on right now, every librarian is asking to have one.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And this started with you thinking, there has to be a better way.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA WEEDMAN: It touches me. It really does. It visibly shows people that we care.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And, as working moms, they knew there was a better way.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford, CBS News, Henrico County, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Always a better way.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell, looking forward to Mother`s Day this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230510ej59000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For May 7, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Charlie D`Agata, Nicole Sganga, Omar Villafranca, Margaret  Brennan \par \uc2 8,947 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: another weekend rocked by gun violence. And, at America`s Southern border, preparations are under way for a migration surge that may strain resources and ignite political tension.\par \par \uc2 Gunfire and panic at an outlet mall just outside of Dallas, as another gunman opens fire and, once again, an American community is the target of a mass shooter. We will have the latest.\par \par \uc2 Plus: With pandemic era border restrictions under Title 42 set to expire Thursday, once again, our Southern border braces for an influx of migrants seeking a better life in America. Leaders in border communities are sounding the alarm about the likely humanitarian crisis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. VICTOR TREVINO (Mayor of Laredo, Texas): If all the numbers of migrants are transferred like it`s planned, then it`ll be devastating.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will ask Homeland Security Secretary Alejandro Mayorkas about the security challenges we`re facing within the U.S.\par \par \uc2 And a rare conversation with Arizona`s independent Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 , who says the Biden administration had two years to prepare for the migrant surge and did not do so.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Have you talked to the White House directly about this?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA (I-Arizona): Yes, I have.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What`s the response?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: It has not been adequate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you care to elaborate?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: I do not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And as the U.S. barrels toward potential default on its debt, President Biden will sit down with congressional leaders Tuesday to negotiate lifting the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 We will discuss that and the concerns about stability in America`s banking system with the chairman of \uc2 the House Financial Services Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Patrick McHenry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then: The North Carolina Republican legislature passes new restrictions on abortion. Can the Democratic governor, Roy Cooper, stop it from becoming law? We will ask him.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation. We have a lot to get to this morning, including that migrant surge expected at the border.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin another Sunday with news of gun violence shattering several communities across the country yesterday. The deadliest toll was taken in a suburb about 25 miles north of Dallas.\par \par \uc2 CBS News correspondent Omar Villafranca is on the scene -- Omar.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Margaret, just after 3:30 yesterday afternoon, there was mayhem and carnage at this suburban mall when a gunman who was heavily armed and wearing body armor opened fire, killing eight people and injuring seven others, before police finally shot and killed him.\par \par \uc2 Investigators have not released the names of the victims or the shooter. We want to warn you, some of the video you`re about to see is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA (voice-over): Dashcam video captured the moment the gunman emerged from a gray car in the parking lot and opened fire on people shopping at the Allen Premium Outlets.\par \par \uc2 (GUNSHOTS)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Shoppers ran for cover as the gunfire continued, until an Allen Police Department officer in the area on an unrelated call sprang into action.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN HARVEY (Allen, Texas, Police Chief): He heard gunshots, went to the gunshots, engaged the suspect and neutralized the suspect.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Witnesses described the panic and terror as the shooter fired dozens of rounds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It was at least 50 to 100 rounds. It was nonstop. And there was nothing we could do.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It was just terrible.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All of a sudden, we hear pop, pop, pop, pop. And I go -- I just grabbed my kids and ran.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Seven people were killed at the scene, including the gunman. Two later died from their injuries. Three of the victims are in critical condition. Four are stable.\par \par \uc2 KEN FULK (Mayor of Allen, Texas): We knew you are grieving, we are grieving. Rest assured, the nation and the world are also grieving.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Texas has been here before. Almost a year ago, 19 children and two teachers were killed in an attack at Robb Elementary School in Uvalde, Texas.\par \par \uc2 According to the Gun Violence Archive, this is the 199th mass shooting of 2023. Today is only the 127th day of the year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Omar, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to what`s likely to be a difficult next few weeks at the border, where several communities have already declared states of emergency.\par \par \uc2 Our homeland security and justice reporter, Nicole Sganga, is there.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: Margaret, the clock is ticking down on an end to Title 42, another flash point in the immigration debate.\par \par \uc2 But here on the U.S.-Mexico border, it`s not just a policy change. It`s the front lines of a humanitarian crisis.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SGANGA (voice-over): In South Texas, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  says they have encountered an influx of migrants ahead of Title 42`s end, driven by an uptick in Venezuelan nationals.\par \par \uc2 Still, others wait in Mexico camped along the Rio Grande River. New Biden administration guidelines that kick in after May 11 promise more consequences, like a one-way ticket on ICE Air. We watched as 133 migrants, hands and feet shackled, boarded a plane back to Guatemala Friday.\par \par \uc2 (NICOLE SGANGA SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Venezuela.\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: In Laredo, Texas, this emergency shelter is a welcome reprieve for many, like 29-year-old Margarita Habrew (sp?), who crossed the U.S.-Mexico border pregnant, with her 5- and 3-year-old daughters.\par \par \uc2 Back at home in Venezuela, she says there is nothing.\par \par \uc2 (DR. VICTOR TREVINO SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: The mayor of Laredo has declared a citywide emergency.\par \par \uc2 DR. VICTOR TREVINO (Mayor of Laredo, Texas): The disaster is an imminent disaster. It may not be here right now, But it`s like a hurricane that`s coming. You board up before the hurricane.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NICOLE SGANGA: With this week`s policy change, U.S. officials expect as many as 10,000 migrants a day to traverse the U.S.-Mexico border at crossings like the one behind me -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Nicole, thank you.\par \par \uc2 For more, we`re joined now by the Homeland security secretary, Alejandro Mayorkas.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to have you here in person, sir.\par \par \uc2 ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS (U.S. Secretary of Homeland Security): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Before we get to migration, I want to ask, do you have any information about this latest mass shooting in Texas, which may have been with an AR-15-style weapon?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Margaret, another horrific tragedy in our country.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with the governor last night, as well as the mayor. The matter is still under investigation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No information about the shooter?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: No, Margaret, I think it`s -- it`s under investigation. The state and local authorities are leading that investigation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let`s get to the border.\par \par \uc2 This is the greatest migration surge, you said, in the Western Hemisphere since World War II. And you`ve been preparing for more than a year-and-a- half. How rough will the next few weeks be?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: You know, Margaret, we`ve been preparing for this for more than a year-and-a-half. You are correct.\par \par \uc2 And it is indeed a regional challenge. And it requires a regional response, which is why we are working so closely with many countries to the south. It`s going to take our plan a while to really take hold, for people to understand that they can access lawful, safe, orderly pathways before they reach the border.\par \par \uc2 And, quite frankly, if they come to the border, they will receive a consequence under our enforcement authorities.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you are, as an administration, setting up processing centers in Colombia and Guatemala, so migrants can start the asylum process before they make it to the border. But those aren`t set up yet. When will those be functional?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: So, we are furthest along with Colombia. It should be a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 But we also have additional lawful pathways that already have existed for people to access the...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The phone apps, you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Yes, the parole program for Cubans, Haitians, Nicaraguans and Venezuelans.\par \par \uc2 We are expanding our family reunification programs.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you have announced 1,500 troops that are going to El Paso, Texas. Why not other parts of the border? Is Texas the most porous area?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Well, they will be dispersed as operational needs require.\par \par \uc2 And so the deployment of active-duty troops is not to do enforcement work, not to interact with the migrants, but to provide other support so that our border patrol agents can be out in the field.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The governor of Arizona and the senior senator told us that the federal government is unprepared.\par \par \uc2 Senator Sinema said Homeland Security is not sharing information with her or local law enforcement on numbers of migrants, processing time and available buses to transport them. The governor also said she needs more urgency, and she can`t get specific information on dollars for emergency shelters.\par \par \uc2 Why isn`t that kind of specific detail being shared?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Well, I respectfully disagree with the senator and the governor. Number one, we are prepared.\par \par \uc2 As we noted at the very beginning of our conversation, we`ve been preparing for this for quite some time. We tried to end Title 42 repeatedly and were -- were stopped from doing so by the courts, so we are prepared, number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, we have a migration information center that is specifically set up to communicate with state and local officials. And we have been doing so. We are using our FEMA regional coordinators as our key points of contact.\par \par \uc2 I spoke with Senator Sinema, I think, within the last two weeks, and our personnel are in touch with other officials on a regular basis.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: She said she had been in touch with Homeland Security, also with the White House, but the information was not adequate.\par \par \uc2 So do you think there is a communication problem here?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: I do not. And if there is a question that has been unanswered, we will answer it. I will tell you, though, let`s take a step back because there`s a very important message, not to communicate only to Senator Sinema, but to all senators and all members of the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 We need immigration reform. Everything that the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  is doing, everything that our partners across the federal government are doing, is within a broken immigration system. The president passed to Congress a proposal to fix our broken immigration system on the first day in office.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that`s like a to-do list. That`s different than putting your shoulder behind it, picking up the phone and saying, excuse me, Democrats control the Senate, let`s be out front on immigration.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Margaret, we have been pushing for immigration legislation since day one. And, by the way, it didn`t start on day one. This is a decades-long problem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It is.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: The immigration system hasn`t been fixed since the `90s.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Absolutely. And it has only seemingly gotten worse with the set of circumstances we`re in now.\par \par \uc2 But, to that point, this gets so politicized and you take a lot of the political heat yourself because you run the agency on the security portion of this. But if the politics are so bad, and the security situation is so difficult, and you need more resources, why isn`t the president out there talking more about the need for a border bill? Why isn`t Leader Schumer doing this?\par \par \uc2 It seems like the issue is being conceded to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: I -- I -- Margaret, I just respectfully disagree. I spoke with Leader Schumer also within the last two weeks. We are in constant communication.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is there a timeline for him?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: We -- we are constantly -- there is -- there is not a day that goes by that we are not urging Congress to pass reform.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So does the administration support the bipartisan bill from Senator Sinema and Tillis that would allow for expelling of migrants for two years, similar to Title 42?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Title 42 and the expulsion authority is a public health authority.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: It is not an immigration authority.\par \par \uc2 We will be using our immigration authorities, which call for a consequence regime, which is why we have to correct the lies that smugglers tell vulnerable migrants. They think they`re coming and they`ll be able to stay, and that is just unequivocally false.\par \par \uc2 And what I would say to the senators, Senators Tillis and Sinema, what we need is our system fixed...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: ... not this Band-Aid solution.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , who`s the Chair of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee, a Democrat, said the administration has ignored his proposals.\par \par \uc2 He objected to the sending of troops, and he criticized lack of planning. So, if you have people who are border-state Senators who want to do something, and then you have the chairman of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee saying he wants to do something, why is the administration ignoring that or pressing it -- pushing it aside?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: The administration is not ignoring it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Menendez said that.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: We are -- we are pressing forward.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, Senator Menendez, the Chairman of the Foreign Relations Committee, set forth a really thoughtful document with a number of pillars of action. And we have indeed implemented a number of those.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Henry Cuellar, a Democrat, said he has confidence in you and Homeland Security, but he said the White House is holding you back.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Untrue. One team, one mission, and we are prepared to execute it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mr. Secretary, we`ll be watching. Good luck to you.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY ALEJANDRO MAYORKAS: Thank you so much, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Late last week, we traveled to the border state of Arizona for the McCain Institute`s Sedona Forum honoring the late senator, who was known for his straight talk and ability to forge bipartisan compromise when the need arose.\par \par \uc2 We spoke with the state`s newly independent Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2 . She left the Democratic Party last fall and is very much aligned with that maverick mind-set.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Now we`re in the midst of the hemisphere`s worst refugee crisis.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Partisanship is at such a high level. Is it simply a political reality that you can`t get comprehensive reform done; you have to chip away at this piecemeal?\par \par \uc2 What you introduced was a temporary two year authority to expel migrants, but with an exemption for asylum claims because of the immediate Title 42 expiration that`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: That`s right. That`s a Band-Aid.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration had two years to prepare for this and did not do so. And our state is going to bear the brunt. And migrants will be in crisis as soon as next week. It will be a humanitarian crisis, because we are not prepared.\par \par \uc2 So, the legislation we introduced yesterday is about tiding this over, giving us some time and space for the Biden administration to do their job and for us legislators to actually create a plan that can get through both the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the votes aren`t there for this replacement, essentially, of Title 42, the two year ability to expel migrants without guaranteed asylum hearings.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: We don`t know that yet. We just introduced the bill yesterday.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Republican Senator Lankford was out criticizing it, saying it doesn`t solve the problem. And he has been a partner to you.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: Oh, it doesn`t solve the problem, yeah.\par \par \uc2 No, but I don`t think that`s a criticism. He`s right that it doesn`t solve a problem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You think you can get this passed before next week, before May 11?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: Oh, God, no, Margaret. This is the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s what I was saying.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: I don`t think you can get agreement on a restroom break by next Thursday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  is functioning at a fairly dysfunctional level right now. And that`s due to the partisanship that is driving both parties. As you and I both know, both parties have benefited for decades by not solving this challenge.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: They use it to bash each other in elections. And what bothers me about that, Margaret, is that, look, they don`t live in a border state.\par \par \uc2 So they don`t know that the mayor of Gila Bend has to put migrants in his car and drive them to Phoenix, because they are released in a town that has no bus stop. They don`t know what it`s like for migrants to sleep outside in the farms in Yuma because there is nowhere for them to go.\par \par \uc2 This is a crisis for our border communities and for migrants. And so, unfortunately, the parties are thinking about this from a political perspective...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: ... rather than a human perspective.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said the administration failed to create a workable plan to process migrants after May 11, Title 42.\par \par \uc2 They would say they`ve got the troop deployment, 1,500, processing centers, a phone app, expansion of legal migration, regulations that will bar migrants from asylum if they did not first seek asylum in a third country.\par \par \uc2 Do you support any of what the administration has done?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA\uc2 : Well, these steps, which all have been announced in the past week or so, are helpful.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: These are very helpful.\par \par \uc2 Having 1,500 troops along the U.S.-Mexico border is helpful. It is a border of over 2,000 miles though. So 1,500 troops isn`t going to get the job done. And that`s just a reality.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: We also are very concerned that all this is happening in the week or so before the rollout.\par \par \uc2 Just today, I was on the phone with a sheriff of Cochise County. He has gotten no information from the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  or the federal government about what the flow is going to look like, about what they can expect for processing, in terms of how long it takes to process migrants. He`s got no information -- neither have I -- about how many buses that will be available to transport migrants.\par \par \uc2 Now, he`s gotten the information he does have from me, because I call him every few days, but he`s not gotten that information from the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Margaret, what`s unfortunate is that I`m asking for that information, and I`m not getting it. And so either -- either the administration has that information and they`re choosing not to share it, which is a problem, since we`re the ones who are going to deal with the crisis, or they -- or they don`t have it.\par \par \uc2 And that`s even more concerning, because how do you prepare for the inflow of migrants when you don`t know what you`re going to expect?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that hasn`t been shared with the governor?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: It has not been shared with the governor. I spoke with the governor yesterday.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: It has not been shared. So we do not have this information.\par \par \uc2 There are three nonprofit organizations in Arizona that provide incredible, incredible assistance for our border communities. One is in Yuma. One is in Tucson. One is in Phoenix. They don`t have this information. And they`re the ones who are responsible for accepting the migrants after they`re released from custody.\par \par \uc2 So while it`s wonderful that the administration is announcing things like a 1,500 troop deployment and these new processing centers, which will not be operational by next Friday, those are good things. Those are aspirational. That`s not the same as operational.\par \par \uc2 Rent the buses. Hire the drivers. Build a soft-sided facility so that we can process individuals. We need more holding capacity. I mean, let`s be realistic here. And that`s what`s not -- we`re not prepared for that. And that`s frustrating, Margaret. And the reason why it`s frustrating is because I know that that means that the sheriffs in our southern border are going to be bearing the brunt of it.\par \par \uc2 And our men and women of the Border Patrol will be working even longer shifts.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Have you talked to the White House directly about this?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: Yes, I have.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What`s the response?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: It has not been adequate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you care to elaborate?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: I do not.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because what you`re laying out is a level of crisis concern. So...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: Yes, that`s correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, they returned your call?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: That`s what I have been doing. I have been raising the alarm, because...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do they return your calls?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: Oh, yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And just not share information?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: Just not -- right. That`s correct. This is a problem. We`ve had two years to prepare for this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you think that`s personal?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: No. No, not at all. I don`t think it`s personal.\par \par \uc2 I think that there`s a system in Washington, D.C. that is deeply disconnected from the real lives and experiences of border communities and the migrants who seek to come to this country.\par \par \uc2 But what I would like, Margaret, is for them to learn.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden acknowledged you as the lead co-author of bipartisan infrastructure bill.\par \par \uc2 He called you "the most determined woman I know.` Leader McConnell said you`re "the most effective first-term senator I have seen in my time in the Senate."\par \par \uc2 What would you want to get done in the second term?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: Immigration. It`s been part of my life`s work.\par \par \uc2 Folks who`ve known me for a long time in Arizona know that I started my career as a social worker. And when I started my career as a social worker, I actually worked in the immigrant refugee community. That was many years ago. So this is -- this has been -- it has been really important to me my entire life.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are we that far off from it, that we`re looking past 2024?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KYRSTEN SINEMA: No, I would like to do it before then.\par \par \uc2 Our state has suffered for the last 40 years because of the federal government`s failure to do anything about it. And we`re facing the worst crisis of my life right now with immigration.\par \par \uc2 So I want to do it now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will have more of our conversation with the senator in our next half-hour.\par \par \uc2 And we will be back in one minute.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In preparation for our trip here to Sedona, Arizona, and the conversation with Senator Sinema, we looked back through the Face the Nation archives to see what the late Senator \uc2 John McCain\uc2  had to say about immigration.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what he said in 2007.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FMR. SEN. \uc2 JOHN MCCAIN\uc2  (R-AZ): Whenever there`s been a wave of immigration into this country, whether it`s legal or illegal, there`s been a certain backlash. You can go all the way back to the Irish and "Irish need not apply, you know, to the signs of stores in the 19th century.\par \par \uc2 And, unfortunately, in some ways, it`s gotten, I think, too emotional and too intense. Americans want the border secured, so we have to secure the borders first. They didn`t trust us or believe us that -- when we said we would do it, so we have to do that first.\par \par \uc2 But just let me add -- and, as I pointed out, they are God`s children. There are people who are being abused as we have spoken that are human -- as we are speaking, who are human beings, and people who are bad people are having them work and not paying them. People are being exploited. The coyotes are doing terrible things. There are shoot-outs on our freeways in Arizona. Our emergency rooms are overcrowded.\par \par \uc2 These safe houses, terrible things happen. So there is a humanitarian side to this issue. And I think maybe we ought to also take that in consideration.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it`s still an emotional issue. And those challenges from 12 years ago still haven`t found solutions.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You can find the full version of our conversation with Senator Sinema and other interviews on our Web site, FacetheNation.com, or on our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with more of our conversation with Senator Sinema, who weighs in on the debt ceiling negotiations.\par \par \uc2 And we will also be joined by the chair of the Finance Committee, Congressman \uc2 Patrick McHenry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 Here`s more of our conversation with Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  in Sedona last week.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about a quote that stood out to me that was in a recent "New York Times" profile of you. It quotes you as saying, one of the big problems in negotiations is that often some, not exclusively men, but often men, are so busy talking about what they need, they`re not spending any time hearing what someone else needs. If you give them what they need, you can get what you want.\par \par \uc2 So, on May 9th, five men, including the president of the United States, and four congressional leaders, are going to meet to discuss the standoff over the debt ceiling. You`ve been listening. What does a deal look like?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: I`ve been disappointed with the conversations up to date. Both parties are talking without listening to each other. They`re just talking right past each other, right?\par \par \uc2 So, President Biden says, I want a clean debt limit to meet the full faith and responsibility of the United States of America. To be clear, he`s correct, we must meet the full faith and credit responsibility for the United States of America. That is our duty.\par \par \uc2 However, it`s not correct to assume or to pretend that either party is used to or always is willing to pass a debt limit without conditions. Both parties have played this game for years. And so we`re in a situation where one party is saying they will not negotiate at all with the other party. I think that`s a very dangerous place to be because, one, it`s not realistic. And, two, that is not going -- it`s just not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 So, Kevin McCarthy, as we all saw, took him a long time to become speaker. Barely squeaked by with the votes. Had to make a lot of concessions to get the job. And he has a very, very, very narrow road to walk. So, he has to thread a needle where he can get the votes he needs to pass a debt limit increase and continue to be speaker.\par \par \uc2 Now, there have been sounds coming out from the Republican conference in the House for months, \uc2 Patrick McHenry\uc2 , who`s the chair of \uc2 the House Financial Services Committee\uc2 , Patrick has been talking for months about what a deal could look like. People have not been listening. They should be. I think folks should actually say, let`s hear these options.\par \par \uc2 The reality is, the bill that Kevin and his colleagues passed through the House is not going to be the solution.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: The votes do not exist in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  to pass that. But what the president is offering is not a realistic solution either. There`s not going to be just a simple, clean debt limit. The votes don`t exist for that.\par \par \uc2 So, the sooner these two guys get in the room and listen to what the other one needs, the more likely they are to solve this challenge and protect the full faith and credit of the United States of America. We`re in really shaky ground right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And now we have June 1st as the x date according to the Treasury secretary.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 So, do you think that gives enough time for a broad agreement? If there`s been no talking to date, how do they get it done?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: They could get it done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: By (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well in advance before an actual default.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: They could get it done, but it would be a challenge. I think what it would require is both Speaker McCarthy and the president and their representative party machines to kind of drop the facade, you know, like where they`re at right now and just sit down and talk about brass tacks. What does Kevin need in order to deliver the votes and what does the president need in order to feel comfortable the full faith and credit of the United States of America. Get to that point and then figure out a way to give each man what he needs.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you weighing in on this at all?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: I have conversations with my colleagues every day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That sounds like a yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, back in 2011, it was a senator who helped deliver, you know, coming back from the brink, the last time there was the risk of default -\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When Leader McConnell jumped in. Right now it doesn`t look like he`s jumping in.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: I don`t think he can.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: Well, I don`t think that a solution that was negotiated by Senator McConnell would carry the kind of weight that is needed with House Republican members.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So -\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: I think - I think Senator McConnell knows that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which is why he`s saying talk to the House.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, they`ll all be in the room on May 9th.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR KRYSTEN SINEMA: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, we`ll see what happens.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our full conversation with Senator Sinema is on our website and our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to the chairman of \uc2 the House Financial Services Committee\uc2 , \uc2 Patrick McHenry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Chairman, good morning.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY (R-NC): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The senator previewed some of your proposals here. So, I want to talk to you in depth about that. But just level set for us here, because we have the congressional leaders getting in the same room with the president Tuesday. You said in March you`ve never been more pessimistic about negotiations. Where is your confidence level today?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: Instead of being at the depths of the ocean, I`m merely drowning. I mean if that - that tells you any - so, my level of optimism is from complete and utter pessimism, anything could get done, to some level of modest pessimism now.\par \par \uc2 What`s changed since that interview is that the House acted. We passed a debt ceiling increase with a Republican plan attached to it. It talks about growth, spending restraint --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Just barely.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: But we did. It`s a narrow House. It`s going to be a narrow vote. But we - we dealt with growth, we dealt with immediate spending and long-term savings. So, a balance program here.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve sent this over to the Senate. The president said, show us your plan. We`ve not only shown up with a plan, we`ve passed a plan.\par \par \uc2 The Senate can`t do it now with 43 senators saying we`re not going to go along with the Schumer plan for a clean debt ceiling increase, the Biden plan, and now the Biden -- President Biden has to come to a table for a negotiated solution. He needs to listen to his economic advisors, not his political advisors, and take this very seriously given the late stage that we`re currently in.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You were just referencing a letter that was signed on to by a number of senators, including Minority Leader McConnell, who seems to be throwing his weight behind the speaker of the House. What does a bipartisan deal actually look like?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: It looks a lot like the bill that we passed out of the House. It touches growth. It touches --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s dead on arrival in the Senate. You know that.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: Well, we sent a significant large bill that brings down the cost of government by $4.5 trillion over the next decade. It`s big, yes, but we sent growth, short-term cap steel (ph) on spending so we can fund our government for the next two years without drama, and then long-term savings. So, a pairing of one, two, and three, that`s what a deal looks like.\par \par \uc2 I`ve talked to a lot of senators, a lot of Democrats and Republicans in the House and the Senate, to try to see what a deal would look like. And at this stage of the game, the one key ingredient I don`t have is what the administration would come to terms with. We have to have something that can pass, that addresses our fiscal house at a time where we have record inflation and record federal spending, and we need to have something that can both pass with Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Exactly. It has to be bipartisan. And you acknowledge it`s going to be likely a narrow vote in the House. With the - but the vote you did get through, there were four Republicans against it, two of them have said they will never vote to raise the debt ceiling, Tim Burchett and Andy Biggs. So, compromise is where you have to get here, right? I mean but - but -\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: You`re saying this to a member of the House that actually passed a debt ceiling increase and a president who would not have a second meeting with the speaker of the House. The first meeting was February 1st.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: We`re 100 days past. Everyone knows in divided government you have to negotiate. And the president says, he will not negotiate. So, the absurdity of the position the president has put himself in where he is playing politics with the economy is markedly different than previous debt ceiling increases where Republicans have been viewed as the recalcitrants. We`ve actually done something and the administration says we`re not going to talk.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Treasury secretary said today it`s you all who are putting a gun to the head of the American economy. That is what she said. And she`s talking about the fact that you have a --\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: A hell of a statement on a day like today -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It is, but -\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: Which shows that it`s all about politics for this administration -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: U.S. Treasuries are the bedwalk -\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: Not about economic stability.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Bedrock of the financial system. You know that very well. So, don`t you need to just say, default`s off the table?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: And that`s what we did by passing a plan. The president did not think we could pass a plan out of the House. So, therefore, he said, it`s a clean debt ceiling or nothing. And so debt -- a clean debt ceiling is now off the table with Republicans in the House and Senate saying, time to negotiate between the speaker and the president. That`s all we`re saying.\par \par \uc2 The speaker has not laid down a red line. Those -- that`s been done in previous iterations of the debt ceiling by Democrats and Republicans in the legislative branch.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: He didn`t do that. There are no red lines other than the fact that we must address our fiscal house at a time where federal spending is up 40 percent from pre-Covid levels. I think it`s a reasonable thing for us to do. And, in fact, that`s what the American people say -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So -\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: Three out of four Americans say the president shouldn`t negotiate with the speaker to address our fiscal house.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to get to banking too, but just, very quickly, is a short-term patch off the table, a short term lift of the debt ceiling?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: I think everything`s on the table at this point. The key thing that has to be in this equation is addressing our fiscal house, short term and long term.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 On the banking sector right now, last time you were here you mentioned concern about some of the smaller banks in America, community banks, being endangered. On Monday the government, the \uc2 FDIC\uc2 , sold failed regional bank \uc2 First Republic\uc2  to \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2 . That`s the country`s largest bank. It got even bigger here.\par \par \uc2 Are you going to take action to address some of this? Because there`s concern on both sides of the aisle about big banks getting bigger?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: Yes. And the way we have to do this is, I agree with Michael Barr, the vice chair of the Fed`s review, we have to provision for liquidity more quickly for these smaller banks. We have to make sure that we have a healthy banking arrangement across the whole spectrum. And we have to ensure the banking models can exist in a society where bank runs can happen more quickly than ever before.\par \par \uc2 But let`s get to the fundamentals here. If we look at the reason why these banks, the three of the 30 largest banks in America failed in the last two months, it`s because of interest rate sensitivity of their balance sheet, which means they misjudged inflation. The Fed misjudged inflation. They`ve admitted it. They`re behind the curve. The administration has been asleep at the switch for the supervisors of these institutions. But the root cause of this is inflation. And if we can address inflation -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: It gets to the disease rather than functioning -- addressing the symptoms.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, management choices and hedging their bets could have been a big factor here when it came to the CEOs who ran these institutions.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you going to call them in for testimony?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: And they`re going to be in.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: Two weeks before \uc2 the House Financial Services Committee\uc2 . And this is going to be an important hearing. In the next two months, \uc2 the House Financial Services Committee\uc2  will have the CEOs of these failed institutions. We`re going to have the regulators in, including Secretary Yellen and Chair Powell. At the end of June, we`re going to have our Humphry Hawkins hearing to hear from the chair of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 . Those are important dates in this calendar, especially given the state of banking in America today.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And all the pieces affecting the economy right now.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you as well, though, about the stability of the banking sector because you had Jaime Dimon and \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2 , two of the most important people in the financial space, saying banks are solid. And then we saw all the volatility in the marketplace again this week. California`s PacWest, Arizona`s Western Alliance, those two regional banks under pressure. Are we going to see more government rescues?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: Unfortunately, we`re not out of the woods. But what depositors need to understand is, since 1933, when we enacted and created the Federal Deposit Insurance Commission, insured deposits have never had a penny of loss. We have 99 percent of the accounts in America are under the insured deposit cap level.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE PATRICK MCHENRY: And so 99 percent of the deposits in America are safe and sound. What we have to do is address, over a period of time, the safety and stability of smaller banks, in a time where the market is judging their business model, their interest rate sensitivity and the assumption that regulators are going to require a lot more capital for these banks to exist, they`re making big assumptions. But the stability of the accounts, they`re there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ve got your work cut out for you. Good to have you on the program today. We`ll be watching May 9th. And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Late last week, North Carolina`s Republican-led legislature passed a bill that would limit abortion access in the state.\par \par \uc2 For more we want to go to the state`s Democratic governor, Roy Cooper, who joins us from Raleigh.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER (D-NC): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Just To make it clear, abortion is currently permitted up to 20 weeks of pregnancy in your state. This bill coming out of your legislature would bring it down to 12 weeks, which, according to the CDC, would still allow more than 90 percent of abortions to continue. Republicans say they`re offering a middle ground here. Why do you think this bill is too restrictive?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: They`ve dressed this up as a 12-week ban, but it`s really not. They rammed through a bill in 48 hours with no public input, with no amendments, that drastically reduces access to reproductive freedom for women. It will effectively ban many abortions altogether because of the obstacles that they have created for women, for clinics, and for doctors. They have tried to disguise the disastrous impacts of this bill, but we`re going to expose them.\par \par \uc2 This bill has nothing to do with making women safer and everything to do with banning abortions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well --\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: You only need --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Just to --\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So our viewers can follow along with you here, the bill would cut it off at the end of the first trimester, roughly 12 weeks. There would be an allowance for abortion up to 20 weeks in case of rape or incest, 24 weeks if there are fetal abnormalities. But what you`re talking about are requirements like number of times you have to visit a doctor, information that a woman would have to share publicly.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: For sure. And also, in fact, for medication abortion, the bill specifically limits it to 10 weeks. And with these additional requirements of three in-person visits that doctors have said are medically unnecessary, with more requirements put on clinics that are already strained with four-week backlogs of people, North Carolina has become an access point in the southeast.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: And what this legislation is going to do is going to prevent many women from getting abortions at any time during their pregnancy because of the obstructions that they have put here. Many of these clinics are working very hard to treat women and now they`re going to have many new medically unnecessary requirements that I think many of them are going to have to close.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: North Carolina has become a haven in the south because so many of your surrounding states have severely restricted access. I know that you have -- and we`re showing a map there just to show where you are at the moment -- but you have vowed to veto this bill. But your state legislature has a super majority that could override it. So, what is your plan to stop them?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: Well, first, we only need one Republican to keep a promise, at least four Republican legislators made promise that their constituents during this campaign that they were going to protect women`s reproductive freedom. They only have a super majority by one vote in the Senate and one vote in the House. And we`ve seen Republicans across the country step up. We saw them step up in South Carolina. We saw them step up in Nebraska, because they know that people don`t want abortion bans. And that`s what this bill is. The more people find out about it --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why would they respond - why would they respond to your public calling out? Why do you think that would matter to them?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: Well, they don`t have to answer to me, they have to answer to their constituents. So what I`m doing is trying to educate the public about the disaster that this bill is. And I`m going to go into their districts, I`m going to go into their districts this week, we`re going to have forums with doctors and advocates and women who care deeply about the restrictions in this legislation, and we`re going to educate the public.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: Look, they kept this bill under lock and key. They wouldn`t let their own members take a copy out. The public only saw it for 48 hours.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: It`s 46 pages long.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: And it creates so many problems for women and clinics that it`s going to operate as an effective ban. And we`re not going to - we`re not going to let them disguise this thing as something reasonable when it`s not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I mean, compared to some of your surrounding states, 12 weeks is more permissive but -\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: If it were - if it were 12 weeks, Margaret, but it`s not. It`s not a 12-week ban.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. Well - OK.\par \par \uc2 You don`t have the votes, though, to codify abortion access in the state of North Carolina. Why have Democrats --\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: No, we do not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No. And so, why have Democrats been so out maneuvered on this issue? I mean this just seems to be that even if you get rid of this bill you`re going to have this fight again and again and again. So, where is the compromise?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: So, the problem is, we have super gerrymandered districts and the Democrats were in the super minority. Every single Democrat has signed a bill to enact Roe v. Wade standards. We`re all standing together and fighting. And what we have to do now is defend ourselves from these right wing politicians who want to go into the exam room with women and their doctors.\par \par \uc2 You know, these right-wing politicians make crappy doctors and they`ve gone in and defined medical doctors. Doctors are looking at this legislation and say, what in the world does that mean. So, what we`re going to do is call them out.\par \par \uc2 Look, there are four Republicans -- four Republicans who said they would protect women`s reproductive freedoms during the campaign.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: All we need is one of them. We can block this disastrous legislation and then we can wait for the next battle.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: But what we`re going to do is continue to work to protect women`s reproductive freedom in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you`re just going to be on the defensive there. So, I mean, how does this actually get resolved? Do you -- can you hold a referendum? Can you do anything? If you say the public`s with you, how do you find a compromise?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: Yes. Well, first, we`re not a referendum state.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: We had gerrymandered districts. So, we have a Republican majority. Thank goodness I`m a Democratic governor so I can rally the troops. For four years - for four years I have kept abortion legislation from becoming law that Republicans had passed. But in this election cycle, we - we lost -- they - they gained a super majority by one vote in each chamber. So, now we`ve in a different position. We`ve - we`ve - we`ve held the - held the line for four years.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: But if we can get a Republican to say, look, this is not right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: Like they did in South Carolina. Like they did in Nebraska.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR ROY COOPER: And their constituents, the more they learn, the more they are going to demand that these Republican legislators step up.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right. All right, Governor, we`ll leave it there.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The fighting in Ukraine has intensified as Kyiv launches a series of drones in Crimea ahead of an expected spring counter offensive against Russia. Senior foreign correspondent Charlie D`Agata is in Dnipro with more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice over): The battle for Bakhmut has burst into flames. Ukraine accusing the Wagner mercenary group of using banned incendiary weapons, possibly white phosphorus, posting videos as proof. Deploying them in civilian areas is a war crime.\par \par \uc2 A ferocious parting shot at the head of Wagner, Yevgeny Prigozhin, declares he`s pulling his forces out of Bakhmut by Wednesday following a rant against Russian defense officials in front of dozens of corpses of his men, saying their blood is still warm, blaming their deaths on a severe lack of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 The death toll after ten months of fighting has run into the thousands, unsustainable losses for both sides. A sea of Ukrainian flags fly over the final resting place of the country`s war dead.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (on camera): The Ukrainian government never reveals the true death toll of those soldiers killed in action, but these fresh graves tell their own story, including those they`re preparing to fill.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice over): Ahead of the looming Ukrainian counter offensive, Russia has gone on the offensive. The \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  clocked more than 150 air strikes since the start of May. Ukraine has stepped up its attacks too, striking targets like fuel depots on Russian territory.\par \par \uc2 It`s still not clear who was behind the attempted drone strike of the Kremlin on Wednesday, but U.S. officials and Ukrainian experts tell CBS News it had to be launched from inside Russia. Maybe not the terrorist act to kill Putin the Kremlin says it is, but it`s an embarrassment ahead of a show of power for the president, Moscow`s victory parade, an annual symbol of Russian military might while the bloody battlefields of Ukraine tell a much different story.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Although that Moscow parade is still scheduled to go ahead on Tuesday, more than 20 parades across Russia have been canceled citing security concerns. Although there are questions about whether there are enough troops and equipment to put on display.\par \par \uc2 Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. For FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230507ej5700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Domino Effect\b0\par\par\uc2 Sharyn Alfonsi \par \uc2 2,556 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: 60 Minutes\par \uc2 SXMN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: The United States is in the middle of a maternal health crisis. Today, a woman in the U.S. is twice as likely to die from pregnancy complications than her mother was a generation ago.\par \par \uc2 Statistics from the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  show the United States has one of the highest rates of maternal death in the developed world. Women in the U.S. are 10 or more times likely to die from pregnancy-related causes than mothers in Poland, Spain or Norway.\par \par \uc2 Some of the worst statistics come out of the South in places like Louisiana, where deep pockets of poverty, health care deserts and racial biases have long put mothers at risk.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, you will hear from some of the women trying to improve maternal health care in Louisiana and why they say last summer`s abortion ban set off a `domino effect,` making a bad situation, worse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: The state of maternal health in the United States is abysmal. And Louisiana is the highest maternal mortality in the U.S. So, in the developed world, Louisiana has the worst outcomes for women having babies.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Hello. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): As an OB-GYN, Louisiana`s former state secretary of health, and founder of Nest, a primary health service for families, Dr. \uc2 Rebekah Gee\uc2  spent a career advocating for better maternal care.\par \par \uc2 Renowned for its rich culture and legendary celebrations, Louisiana also holds the distinction of being one of the riskiest places in the country to give birth.\par \par \uc2 Thirty nine out of every 100,000 mothers in Louisiana die during or shortly after childbirth.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: How did we get here? Why is Louisiana in this position?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: The high C-section rates have contributed, the lack of access to well woman care before and after pregnancies. Fifty percent of the time, women don`t get that postpartum care, which means they have untreated hypertension, untreated diabetes, untreated depression. The fact that we have racial bias in health care. And so all of these things are compounded especially worse for low-income women.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): As Louisiana`s secretary of health, Dr. Gee helped expand postpartum Medicaid coverage and pushed the state to track how race impacted care and maternal outcomes.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Was it under-reported?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: Absolutely. In the prior administration, when I was medical director, we were told we were not allowed to show data that showed health disparities. There was a real --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Because why?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: Because the political establishment didn`t want to admit that there were disparities.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): A state board now reviews and reports every maternal death in Louisiana. Dr. Gee worked with that board and found the results especially upsetting. Data showed 80 percent of maternal deaths in the state were potentially preventable.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: You`ve looked at this as a doctor, as a policymaker. What needs to be done?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: We have to prioritize motherhood, right? As a country, we have, and particularly in the Deep South, said that we`re pro-birth. If we`re really going to be pro-birth, we need to be pro-motherhood and pro- family, right? So --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What does that look like?\par \par \uc2 DR. REBEKAH GEE: Making sure that women have time off to get their medical appointments, making sure that we have affordable childcare, making sure that women have access to well woman care.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Access is a big hurdle for many women in Louisiana.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: So what`s it like to raise a family here?\par \par \uc2 THERESA DUBOIS: Uh, a struggle.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Twenty-eight-year-old Theresa Dubois and 32- year-old Brittany Cavalier are both married, and mothers of two.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY CAVALIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): They`re expecting their third babies this summer.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY CAVALIER: Who`s going to win?\par \par \uc2 BOY #1: Me.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Brittany runs the local day care that Theresa`s younger daughter attends.\par \par \uc2 They live in Assumption Parish, a rural county of 21,000, where sugarcane is plentiful -- and doctors are scarce.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: So to give us a sense of kind of where we are, how close is the nearest pediatrician?\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY CAVALIER: Almost 45 minutes.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: OB-GYN?\par \par \uc2 THERESA DUBOIS: An hour and 35 minutes.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: When you have to go to the OB-GYN and it takes you an hour and a half --\par \par \uc2 THERESA DUBOIS: Oh, it`s a nightmare. It`s a lot. It`s a lot emotionally. It`s a lot in the car. It`s a lot on your body, just waiting that long to get help.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): A third of Louisiana`s parishes are maternal health deserts -- meaning they don`t have a single OB-GYN, leaving more than 51,000 women in the state without easy access to care and three times more likely to die of pregnancy-related causes.\par \par \uc2 The only hospital in Assumption isn`t equipped to deliver babies. So Cavalier and Dubois have to travel more than an hour to get to hospitals in Baton Rouge to give birth -- a harrowing journey when there`s an emergency.\par \par \uc2 BRITTANY CAVALIER: I mean, we are supposed to be one of the best countries in the world. And you`re just leaving the women out there to dry.\par \par \uc2 LATONA GIWA: Starting with the most marginalized --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Latona Giwa saw those disparities when she worked as a delivery room nurse. In 2011, she co-founded the New Orleans- based Birthmark Doula Collective.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: Hey, Trinity (sp?). How are you?\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Doulas provide emotional and physical support before, during and after childbirth. Last year, the Collective worked with 2,000 mothers.\par \par \uc2 LATONA GIWA: We work with the most marginalized families who are most at risk for poor birth outcomes. And we prioritize working with black and brown families, with low-income families.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): In Louisiana, black women are up to four times more likely than white women to die during or after childbirth.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What do you see in your line of work that black and brown women are facing when they`re pregnant versus a white woman?\par \par \uc2 LATONA GIWA: We live in a country that does not guarantee insurance coverage and healthcare to everyone. There is different and discriminatory care. Black and brown people are more likely to be on Medicaid. They`re going to practices that are busier, that take more patients. And that`s where the Doula comes in.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: So you have your baby laying --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Studies show better birth outcomes for black women who`ve had Doula care.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: There you go, good job.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Birthmark`s work in Louisiana caught the attention of Every Mother Counts.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURLINGTON: How are you?\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): A maternal advocacy group founded by model Christy Turlington after she suffered complications with the birth of her daughter in 2003.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURLINGTON: I hemorrhaged. There was a lot of blood. There was a whole sort of stream of things that needed to happen.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Really scary.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURLINGTON: Scary and painful. But it was when I got home and really started to think, what about everyone else in the world that this happens to but doesn`t have that team of care working together, understanding what`s happening and actively managing it?\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Those questions led Turlington around the world to document the challenges women face giving birth -- stories of midwives in Haiti and mothers in Baton Rouge.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: How do you compare U.S. maternal health care to maternal health care in the rest of the world?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURLINGTON: Well, the U.S. is one of eight countries that have actually had an increase in maternal mortality. So we`re certainly at the bottom rung.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Last year, Every Mother Counts distributed more than a million dollars to groups focused on strengthening maternal care in the U.S., a mission that became even more difficult last summer.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Whose body?\par \par \uc2 ALL: My body.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #3: Whose choice?\par \par \uc2 ALL: My choice.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): After Roe versus Wade was overturned by the Supreme Court in June, Louisiana implemented a sweeping abortion ban. The ban set off a domino effect across the state, impacting women like Kaitlyn Joshua, a community organizer.\par \par \uc2 Joshua and her husband were thrilled to learn she was pregnant last summer. The couple have a four-year-old daughter and were looking forward to expanding their family.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: We started experiencing cramping. I, you know, lost a lot of blood.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): She told us she went to Woman`s Hospital in Baton Rouge where they did an ultrasound, examined and monitored her. Joshua says, that`s where the treatment ended.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: And so I said, okay. So is this a miscarriage? And the young lady, she said, I can`t really tell you that right now. I don`t know. And I said, well, what do you mean you don`t know? We did the ultrasound. I recall her saying, "We`re just sending you home with prayers. We`re going to hope for the best."\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: So you`re in pain.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: You think you`re miscarrying, and they say, we`re going to send you home with prayers?\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Yes. You know, we`re Christians too, like we pray but it just was very insulting in the moment just because women come there for answers. So, it would have been nice to get a definite response.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Woman`s Hospital told 60 MINUTES, "It`s complex. When diagnosis of early pregnancy loss is unclear, the standard of care is to wait."\par \par \uc2 The next day Kaitlyn Joshua told us her pain became unbearable. So she sought care from a second hospital -- Baton Rouge General -- where a doctor ordered another ultrasound.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: She straight up said, this doesn`t look like a baby at all. Are you sure you were ever pregnant? This just looks like a cyst.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Wait. She --\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: She questioned whether you were pregnant or not?\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Yeah, absolutely. And they discharged me maybe within an hour and a half or so after monitoring me. And on the paperwork, it literally said, "potential miscarriage" or "possible miscarriage," but nothing definite.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Knowing what you know now about what your body was going through, could you have died?\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: When I finally did get care from a midwife on, like, day five, she said that you certainly could have died. She said, "The amount of blood you lost, the amount of fluids that you were passing was a lot for someone in such a short period of time."\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: Why do you think there was such ferocious pushback from not one, but two hospitals?\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: I just have to believe that it is just the vagueness of the abortion ban in this state that`s caused so much fear around physicians doing their job.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): We reached out to Baton Rouge General. They told us, "Every patient is different," and that since the ban, they have "not changed the way they manage miscarriage or the options available" to treat them.\par \par \uc2 The hospital left Joshua with one option -- to take Tylenol and monitor for worsening symptoms.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, some physicians are now afraid to offer methods typically used to treat miscarriages because those same methods are used in abortion and could be seen as illegal, potentially landing health care providers in jail.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: To be clear, you were not seeking abortion.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: You were trying to have a healthy baby. You needed care and nobody would touch you.\par \par \uc2 KAITLYN JOSHUA: Absolutely. I think a lot of times, we fail to realize the intersectionality between maternal health care, reproductive justice and abortion care. Until we understand that all of those things interconnect, we probably will not see change any time soon.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: We take an oath to do no harm, and that`s really our north star as a physician. But when the prospect of doing that might cause you to be brought up on criminal charges, that`s a really difficult place for our physicians to be.\par \par \uc2 What`s drastic is --\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): Dr. Jennifer Avegno has been an ER doctor for 22 years and is the current Director of the New Orleans Health Department.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, Attorney General \uc2 Jeff Landry\uc2  sent a letter to doctors about the new abortion ban that Avegno says paralyzed maternal health care across the state.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: The letter was something that I`ve never seen before as a practicing physician. A non-medical layperson inserting themselves into medical care. And there was a direct line about any physician who violates this will lose their liberty and medical license. And so really it was a threat.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: What kind of criminal penalties does a doctor face?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: At least one year and up to 10 years of imprisonment with hard labor.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: This is about going to jail -\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: This is about going to jail. Doctors don`t want to be in a war with their own state. They want to be able to just practice.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI (voiceover): As the New Orleans Health Department director, Dr. Avegno has seen how doctors are struggling to interpret the language of Louisiana`s new abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: What they`re being told is, "Well, you can consult with the hospital attorney." But I don`t know of any other disease or process where routinely you`re being told, "We`ll get the hospital attorney involved."\par \par \uc2 That also brings up, what if the attorney advises something that the physician really feels is harmful. So really, what our physicians are facing is a terrible choice to make decisions that might not be in the best interest of the patient, or risk going to jail.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: We reached out to three urban hospitals, rural hospitals, providers. And they said, "We`d love to talk to you, but we`re afraid."\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: Yes. That has been the consistent echo from providers. There are several providers I think that would love to speak out but were told by their hospitals that it`s too risky.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: So why are you sitting here today?\par \par \uc2 DR. JENNIFER AVEGNO: I am concerned that we are going to see a worsening of our morbidity and mortality rates. Simply because of access and simply because of fear.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SXMN000020230502ej4u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For April 30, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan, Charles D`Agata \par \uc2 9,111 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: New signs of spring strain on the economy, and Washington politicians prepare for the annual surge of migrants at the border.\par \par \uc2 An 11th-hour rescue is under way in an effort to save \uc2 First Republic Bank\uc2 , as the Fed takes partial responsibility for the March collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We will talk with California Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Former Trump administration economic adviser \uc2 Gary Cohn\uc2  will also be here.\par \par \uc2 Then:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES (R-Texas): In Texas 23, not only are we the epicenter of this border crisis. It feels as if it is hell for us.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Republican Congressman Tony Gonzales weighs in on the Biden administration`s new plans to stem an expected border surge and what his party is proposing as those pandemic border restrictions, known as Title 42, will soon be lifted.\par \par \uc2 And as the state-by-state march to solidify abortion laws continues. South Carolina Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace will join us. She`s warning her party to find a middle ground on abortion access.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the annual White House Correspondents Dinner honors Americans wrongfully detained overseas. The power of the press in a democracy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): A free press is a pillar, maybe the pillar, of a free society, not the enemy.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, of course, some self-deprecating humor, Washington style.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : After all, I believe in the First Amendment, not just because my good friend Jimmy Madison wrote it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 As we look to a new month, we`re keeping a close eye on the economy, which continues to be the number one issue of concern for Americans. Last week`s news that economic growth slowed more than expected in the first quarter of the year will almost certainly factor in to the Federal Reserve`s decision- making this week, as we prepare for yet another interest rate hike to try to curb inflation.\par \par \uc2 Complicating the Fed`s dilemma is the political battle on raising the debt ceiling, fallout from the collapse of \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2 , and the looming prospect of a third government bank rescue, this time for California-based First Republic.\par \par \uc2 We begin with Democratic Congressman \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , whose district was home to \uc2 SVB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2  (D-California): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Last time you were here, there were urgent efforts under way to save a bank, today, again, urgent efforts, this time \uc2 First Republic\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We are hearing it could be seized by the \uc2 FDIC\uc2 , but there are also efforts to find a buyer to absorb it. What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, this is what a modern bank run looks like.\par \par \uc2 Let me tell you what companies in my district are saying. They`re saying that payroll companies are telling them, move your deposits out of \uc2 First Republic\uc2 . You`re not going to be able to have access to the accounts.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I think it is so important that we guarantee all bank depositors. I had said this last time I was on. There`s $8 trillion, Margaret, is uninsured deposits in these banks; $10 trillion is insured.\par \par \uc2 Until we guarantee them and we can charge a fee, we run the risk that there...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We is Congress.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, the Congress, yes, Congress. I have been working with Senator Rubio on this, on a proposal to say, look, pay a fee, and have some guarantee on these deposits, because, otherwise, what`s going to happen?\par \par \uc2 Regional banks, they`re going to be insecure. People are going to be concerned. And they`re going to start to consolidate into the top banks. And you have a lot of these payroll companies telling these companies to move to this -- the bank.\par \par \uc2 So, I believe the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  is doing fine and well now, but we have got to prevent this from happening again. And the best way to do it is to guarantee these deposits.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, in the meantime -- what you`re talking about takes -- takes a long time. We`re talking immediate sense here for \uc2 First Republic\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And some progressive Democrats have objected to the idea that some of the big banks, who can afford to buy little ones and the systemically important ones, that they should be prevented from stepping in to buy up \uc2 First Republic\uc2 . Is that where you fall?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : No, I think that the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  needs to look at the lowest-cost alternative. That`s their mandate.\par \par \uc2 And, right now, they may need to work with banks and private capital to save \uc2 First Republic\uc2 . I mean, that is the state we`re in. But we can move quickly. Look, in the CARES Act, we actually said that all deposits were guaranteed for transaction accounts.\par \par \uc2 I think, if we do something like that quickly, we prevent this going again. We also need reform. I mean, look at what has happened, Margaret. Every time the economy heats up, we somehow say, deregulate, deregulate, and it never works out.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, the deregulation basically sent a signal to the Fed to stop the oversight on these banks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, there was a lot in that report that I want to get into that just came out.\par \par \uc2 But, first off, one of the things blamed was poor management. \uc2 Gregory Becker\uc2 , who you knew...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... the former CEO of \uc2 SVB\uc2 , he had donated to your campaign as well.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : He had.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When he was at the helm, did you think at the time it was a problem that he was also on the board of the chief regulator there, the \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : I did not know at the time, but I do think now that that should not be allowed.\par \par \uc2 I mean, no, I didn`t have a sense back then that \uc2 Silicon Valley Bank\uc2  was going to have a challenge. Obviously, there was mismanagement. One reform we should have is that bank executives should not be on the board of the Fed, regional Fed that`s overseeing them. And I think that`s a lesson.\par \par \uc2 I also think there needs to be a clawback of his bonus. There needs to be a clawback of executive compensation and for tougher rules on bank executives who have failed banks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And yet the momentum doesn`t seem to be there in Congress, either from the Democratic leader in the Senate or in the Republican-led House. Why?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, there should be momentum for three things, one, the clawbacks on these executives. That could be bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 Two, for greater oversight and the rules on these banks. I mean, we`re -- we basically told these banks not to regulate. But the big place -- and I`m working on a bipartisan basis -- protect the regional banks. That`s going to help regional community banks the most and mainstream America. That`s the biggest risk to the economy.\par \par \uc2 Look, you have -- Margaret, you have 5 percent interest rate. People are moving out into money market funds. They`re moving out of banks. If we don`t have a guarantee, we run a real risk across this country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On this, though, in terms of regulation, there were regulations in place. There were 31 supervisory findings, warnings from regulators about \uc2 SVB\uc2  when it failed. There were warnings in August. There were warnings in February.\par \par \uc2 Doesn`t that suggest it wasn`t lack of regulation, but regulators not acting?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, it was both.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They`re raising flags and not doing anything.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : When Congress passed deregulation, there`s not a straight line between that and what happened, but it certainly sends a signal to the Fed that maybe the Congress doesn`t care as much.\par \par \uc2 And I think there was lax oversight. Look, they noticed that there was risk there. But they didn`t do the right liquidity or stress test. I mean, they didn`t do a test to see what happens in a high-inflation environment, when Chair Powell has been saying for over a year he`s going to raise rates. So...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you -- you have confidence in Chair Powell and \uc2 Mary Daly\uc2  at the \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : I don`t think you can blame Chair Powell. I have disagreed, but I don`t think you can blame him for this.\par \par \uc2 I do think we need to know, what are the concrete reforms? Let`s stop having bank executives on these boards. Let`s make sure that you have better liquidity tests and stress tests. Let`s have the regulations we repealed in 2018 back there and have some statement on guaranteeing the bank deposits, so I don`t have to come here every...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : ... every few weeks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: With another crisis.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about a looming one, what to do about the debt ceiling. The Fed is predicting a mild recession is ahead of us. The president has overseen the highest inflation that we have seen in decades in this country. Whether he caused it or not, he`s overseeing it.\par \par \uc2 You have all these looming issues, and now you have the standoff over the debt ceiling. It is not a sustainable position for the White House to say they`re not going to negotiate with Republicans, is it?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, here`s what`s sustainable.\par \par \uc2 They -- the Republicans should do what I did under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and that`s pay your bills. It`s patriotic to pay your bills. Look, if you`re a family, you have credit card debt.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It sounds like you`re agreeing it`s not a sustainable position.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, I think we should pay the bills and then negotiate. And we should negotiate on deficit reduction.\par \par \uc2 The last person to leave a surplus was \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . I will tell you how we lower the debt. Let`s repeal the Trump tax cuts. Let`s repeal some of the Bush tax cuts for the very wealthy. Let`s not have all these overseas wars.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the Democrats have a plan. And let`s raise taxes on the top wealthy. But, before we get there, we pay our bills. If you`re a family and you a credit card debt...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : ... who says, let`s not pay the bill?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the president was saying, Kevin McCarthy, pass something, or at least put out a plan, and I will talk to you.\par \par \uc2 Now he`s passed something, and the president says he still will not engage with him. I understand the back-and-forth. But the political cost and the economic one is very real here. And it will stick to the president.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Well, the president is saying he`s not going to be hostage in having veterans cuts on health care, in having cuts on K-12 education, in having cuts on food stamps, in having cuts on manufacturing to just pay our bills.\par \par \uc2 He`s saying, we can discuss that. We can negotiate, but, first, pay your bills.\par \par \uc2 And I think the -- I think Senator McConnell understands this. And I think the president will sit down with Senator McConnell. He knows that we can`t default.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You think that`s the back channel that`s going to figure this out?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : That`s why I said that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The leader in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 RO KHANNA\uc2 : Or Kevin McCarthy has got to come without 22 percent cuts on veterans` benefits.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we will see.\par \par \uc2 And I do hope to have you on again, when there`s not a looming crisis, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 Last week, two heavily Republican states, Nebraska and South Carolina, failed to pass near-total abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Republican Congresswoman Nancy Mace joins us now.\par \par \uc2 And it`s good to have you here in person. You`re often joining us remote.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (R-South Carolina): Good morning, and thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, I want to get to a lot of what we just talked about there with the debt ceiling. But let`s start off on abortion.\par \par \uc2 You have been talking about the -- this being a very moral issue, but also a political one that will impact voters in 2024. Nikki Haley, the former ambassador, former governor of your state, said no Republican president will have the ability to ban abortion nationwide.\par \par \uc2 And she believes there is a federal role on abortion. But she didn`t say specifically what it is that she believes, no limits exactly, no exceptions. Does she need to?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, I think any presidential candidate, anyone running for office at the federal level, including members of Congress, incumbents and people that are running to flip seats in `24, need to have a position, need to articulate that position.\par \par \uc2 And what I have been trying to do since Roe was overturned is show a road map. I represent a swing district in South Carolina, but show a road map for winning states that are very purple or districts that are purple, and articulating where we stand on the message.\par \par \uc2 And we have to show -- and I`m pro-life, but I`m a conservative who reaches across the aisle and works with the other side. I work with \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 . Great to see him this morning on your program. I work with Democrats all the time on issues where we can agree on.\par \par \uc2 And there`s so much when it comes to protecting life and protecting women that we can agree on, in terms of gestational limits. That`s something that can happen at the state and the federal level. And I talk to and listen to my constituents all the time.\par \par \uc2 And I read a letter from a woman who`s no longer a Republican. She`s an independent voter now. She`s pro-choice. And her gestational limits are 14 weeks. Well, I`m a pro-life legislator. Mine are 15 to 20 weeks and want to make sure there are exceptions.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s so much, I think, in how we talk about the issue, but we have to, as Republicans, show compassion towards women and life. I mean, you can do both and win. I did it overwhelmingly in November, when we won by 14 points, overwhelmingly in a swing district.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the -- just for clarity, the majority of abortions are performed in that window of 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, former President Trump has taken criticism from Republicans, though, for not specifying a position either, has just said it`s up to the states.\par \par \uc2 Both of South Carolina senators have picked that window, 15 weeks, 20 weeks. Tim Scott`s running for president potentially here. He said he`d signed a 20-week limit. Is he closer to what the Republicans need to be doing than Nikki Haley is?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, I think 15 to 20 weeks is the sweet spot here, because Democrats often are at 24 weeks. And that`s too far for a lot of people. No one wants zero. No one wants zero weeks, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you want this, to be clear, at the federal level? You want a law, not kicking it back to the states?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: It can be. I mean, I think there`s a role for the state government and for the federal government. If it`s gestational limits, certainly, we can find some agreement. That`s why I say 15 to 20 weeks.\par \par \uc2 That`s something even pro-life groups like Susan B. Anthony`s List, although they`re against it now, two years ago, they were for 20 weeks with exceptions. They have just moved the goalposts. But, for years, Republicans have been for 15-to-20 week bans, with exceptions, including both my senators from South Carolina at the federal level. And I think most -- 80 percent of America would agree with that.\par \par \uc2 But they want us to protect women and girls who are victims of rape, victims of incest, women whose lives are at risk. We also need to make sure that we`re making -- that -- that birth control -- that women have access to birth control...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: ... particularly in rural areas.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina, we have 14 counties in our state that don`t have a single OB-GYN doctor. And then what do we do with the babies who are born who are unwanted? What about our foster care system, adoption care, birth control?\par \par \uc2 There are so many things that we can work on to show that we`re pro-women.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Rape kits. We have over 100,000 rape kits in this country that have yet to be processed. We can do both. We can walk and chew gum at the same time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you have said Republicans need to have a woman on the ticket.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Nikki Haley endorsed you. Are you ready to endorse her?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, we`re going to see how the race shapes up that -- not everybody is in. I`m watching very carefully.\par \par \uc2 I love Nikki Haley. I have not been quiet about that. She was the only person to endorse me in my election last year. And I do want to see a woman on the ticket, but I also want to see who jumps in. I want to give everybody a shot. But I am cheering her on. She`s a constituent. And I love what she`s doing.\par \par \uc2 I love that she gave a speech last week on women`s issues. That`s very important. Women are watching,\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you want her to go farther and be more specific, is what I understand.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: I would love to see her move on, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, can a Republican candidate who supports or signs into law, as Governor DeSantis did, a six-week limit to abortion...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: In dead of night, by the way, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can he succeed in running for the presidency?\par \par \uc2 I mean, is this really going to be something that -- and people who are in Republican districts have to make this calculus. You say you`re in a purple one, so you have to compromise.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: It`s the -- well, it`s the number two issue in my district. Number one is inflation. The debt ceiling is an important issue.\par \par \uc2 Number two is abortion and finding a middle ground. Signing a six-week ban that puts women who are victims of rape and girls who are victims of incest -- incest in a hard spot isn`t the way to change hearts and minds. It`s not compassionate.\par \par \uc2 The requirements he has for rape victims are too much, not something that I support. It`s a nonstarter. I am a victim of rape. I was raped by a classmate at the age of 16. I am very wary. And the devil is always in the details. But we`ve got to show more care and concern and compassion for women who`ve been raped. I don`t like that this bill was signed in the dead of night.\par \par \uc2 And it puts him in a very difficult position for a general election in my opinion, which is why I have been so vocal on this issue. I would like us to win. I would not only like us to win the Electoral College. I want us to win the popular vote. And if we can show the middle ground, which shouldn`t be controversial -- birth control shouldn`t be controversial.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: It keeps the number of abortions down if women have access to birth control. It shouldn`t be controversial.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, it`s interesting...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... because some candidates also, like \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , have said he would sign a 15-week limit, but he ultimately wants to get to a ban.\par \par \uc2 So there`s some -- some nuance in there too in some of these positions.\par \par \uc2 But I want to ask you about debt ceiling. You opposed and then voted for Speaker McCarthy`s bill that we were just discussing with Congressman Khanna here to lift the debt ceiling in exchange for trillions of cuts. The bill is dead on arrival in the Senate. The White House says they`re not ready to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 Can you actually be confident that we will avoid getting close to default, even if we don`t get over that cliff? It`s very dangerous, the longer this goes on.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: It is. And we don`t want to play chicken with the economy.\par \par \uc2 And when I sat down with Kevin McCarthy, the speaker, on Wednesday, we talked about us leading a balanced budget amendment. I believe we need to do that. As you mentioned, as Ro mentioned earlier, the last time we balanced the budget was under \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2 . Republicans in `94 put together a plan to balance a budget in 10 years.\par \par \uc2 They did it in `98, four years later, with a Democrat president and a Republican majority. It is not out of -- out of the thinking that we can do this together, with a Democrat president, Republicans in charge. We did it again. We had cut, cap and balance in 2010 with Obama as president. So...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are there red lines here for you, though?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, certainly, the fear tactic of default is a red line. That is not going to happen. We get 11 times the revenue that we need to pay the interest on the debt. The president can also prioritize spending.\par \par \uc2 We don`t -- no one wants to cut veterans benefits or Social Security or food stamps. That`s not what this is about. But we have $31 trillion in debt that was started by both sides, under President Trump, $8 trillion added to the debt, under President Biden, $4 trillion. That`s $12 trillion in six years.\par \par \uc2 The president needs to come to the table, take a look at what we have offered and start negotiating. This is a serious problem. And, as Ro mentioned earlier, families are living paycheck to paycheck. They have to balance their checkbooks every week. So too should the federal government. Our tax revenues last year were $4.7 trillion, yet we`re spending over $6 trillion every single year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we are not going to abandon this issue. We know it`s going to be looming for some time. Thank you, both of you, for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to immigration and the ongoing effort to secure America`s southern border.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined by Texas Republican Congressman Tony Gonzales. He`s at our affiliate in San Antonio, KENS 5.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Thank you for having me, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I don`t have to tell you this date of May 11. But, for our viewers, that`s when this health emergency restriction known as Title 42 will expire.\par \par \uc2 Homeland Security is looking at 10,000 people per day potentially crossing the border. Do the agents you represent have what they need to deal with the surge?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: They do not, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 And this is, honestly, the fourth time that we`ve seen this Title 42 is going to end. And every time we come to this situation, before the crisis is this uptick in illegal immigration. And we`re seeing that now, whether it`s Brownsville, whether it`s Eagle Pass, or whether it`s El Paso.\par \par \uc2 Now it`s all three of those areas. And so the numbers are getting worse. The agents are beyond an exhausting point. And it`s not just at the border. Now what you`re seeing is places 100, 150 miles from the border are just as overwhelmed as if they were on the Rio Grande themselves.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you are set to vote in the House on May 11 on a border bill.\par \par \uc2 You`re part of the Congressional Hispanic Conference, which had opposed the Republican bills, on the grounds that you needed provisions in it to help legal migrants in the asylum process.\par \par \uc2 I know you are now on board, dropped that position. What changed for you? And is there anything in here that can get through a Democratic-controlled Senate?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Yes, Margaret, it`s an exciting time for the Congressional Hispanic Conference, which I`m the co-chair with \uc2 Mario Diaz- Balart\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s -- we`ve got 18 different members. It`s the first time we`ve set a marker down and said, our voices will be heard. One of the -- I have been very public. I have essentially negotiated both in public and in private. One of the things that I, in particular, asked for is to not curb legal immigration, not hamper those that are legally doing the things that they need to do to come over.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of that was stripped away in the Judiciary part of it, which was half of the package. The Homeland Security package, which I sit on that -- that committee, I asked for some specific things. One was ensuring that we hold cartels accountable. You want to get to the root of the cause, they`re the issue.\par \par \uc2 So we go down this path of labeling cartels as terrorists. Another is giving resources to the people that are doing the work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What does that do?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: We added $110 million -- yes, $110 million for deputy sheriffs and -- and local law enforcement officers.\par \par \uc2 I did a ride-along with the Medina County Sheriff`s Office on Monday. Once again, this is 120 miles from the border.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Their police cruisers are getting -- are getting trashed and -- and other things.\par \par \uc2 And the last piece is, we got to give a pay raise to the border patrol agents.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: They got a $9,000 bonus in this aspect of it. But we got to go further.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, on that House bill you just voted for to lift the debt ceiling in exchange for cuts, the White House argues it would mean cutting funding for Customs and Border Protection. Do you have any guarantees from Republican leadership that, in a future deal, there won`t be cuts to the agents you represent?\par \par \uc2 Because you just signed a bill that would do that.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Look, I was on the fence. It was 215 to 215 when I ended up voting in favor of the debt ceiling. So I have some very deep concerns with -- with the direction we`re going.\par \par \uc2 Universal cuts don`t -- don`t always solve the issue. In the same breath, just throwing money at a problem doesn`t solve the issue. So what I`m trying to do is, I`m trying to go, where is the money going? How does DHS, instead of getting them a blank check, how do we give them money to the things that are going to help secure the border, like repatriation flights?\par \par \uc2 These are flights where people that do not qualify for asylum, they don`t get flown to New York or D.C. or Chicago. They get flown back to their country of origin. That is how you solve the border crisis, other things like hiring Border Patrol agents. But you can`t just give DHS a blank check and have them go spend it on all these things that is -- only encourages illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have more about -- about this to get into with you, but I have to take a quick commercial break, so please stay with us, Congressman.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be back with all of you and a lot more Face the Nation in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you miss an episode of Face the Nation, you can listen to our podcast. Find us on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Music or wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation, including more with Congressman Gonzales and former head of the National Economic Council in the Trump administration \uc2 Gary Cohn\uc2 , plus a conversation with parents about kids and the impact gun violence is having in their lives.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We return to our conversation with Texas Republican Congressman Tony Gonzales, who joins us from San Antonio this morning.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, I want to pick back up on border security. Migrant families, after May 11th, if they cross into the U.S. illegally will not be detained by the Biden administration. The Trump and Obama administrations did detain families. Should families be detained?\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES (R-TX): There should be repercussions for people that enter the country illegally. And this is where the Biden administration is getting wrong. First off, they`re doing more. And I appreciate the fact that they`re trying different things. It`s taken them a while, but I appreciate that. This is what they`re getting wrong. They`re putting all their time and effort into illegal immigration, finding ways to increase capacity, finding ways where people can come over illegally quicker. The reality is, nine out of ten people that come over into our country illegally do not qualify for asylum. So, stop sending them down that route when you know they`re not going to qualify for asylum.\par \par \uc2 I am of the mindset, we need to encourage those that come over legally. I believe in work visas. And, once again, I`m -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You would have to change the asylum laws though. That`s Congress.\par \par \uc2 TONY GONZALES: You do have to - you do have to change the asylum laws. And it`s -- it`s something that the president should work with Congress on.\par \par \uc2 You haven`t heard the president say one thing about immigration, other than just blame others. Congress has a role to play. I think that us passing this bill in the House on security is important. The next step is immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY GONZALES: I am committed to doing that. You haven`t seen anyone even try immigration reform in the past decade. I think it`s long time that we do something.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY GONZALES: In my opinion that starts with protecting those doing that are doing it legally through work visas.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, just to be clear, do you think it`s humane to keep kids in detention centers with their parents?\par \par \uc2 TONY GONZALES: I - I think there needs to be a process where folks have their asylum claim heard in days, not years. And if they qualify for asylum, welcome to the United States. And if they don`t, you have to send them back to their country of origin.\par \par \uc2 What - what we can`t do is what we`re having now. Right now we have tens of thousands of children that are just being released into this country. What is happening to these children? Regardless of their legal identity, I mean, what is happening to these children-these children?\par \par \uc2 So, we have to enforce the lawsuit that are on the books and we have to encourage those to come over legally.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY GONZALES: And make it easier for folks to obtain work visas.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Well, Health and Human Services takes those kids -- custody of them, but - but point taken on needing to watch what happens next.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you very much for your time today.\par \par \uc2 We want to turn now to \uc2 Gary Cohn\uc2 , who is the vice chairman of \uc2 IBM\uc2 , former \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  president and former Trump administration top economic adviser.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you. Lots of titles, Gary, lots of experience. That`s why we like having you here.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about what`s happening with \uc2 First Republic\uc2 . It`s been under pressure. We know they`ve been looking for a buyer. The \uc2 FDIC\uc2 , the government, is looking to arrange moving it into government control and then maybe selling it. What are you hearing about how this would roll out?\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2  (Vice Chairman, \uc2 IBM\uc2 ): Margaret, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 I think you`re portraying the situation as we find ourselves again on a weekend. As we closed business Friday, the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  was in the process of looking for acquirers or bidders for the assets. Over the course of the weekend, I think the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  has asked potentially three banks for their final bids for the entire bank. The \uc2 FDIC\uc2  would prefer to sell the bank in its entirety than the pieces. What will most likely happen is the \uc2 FDIC\uc2  will seize control and then simultaneously resell the asset to the successful bidder. I think that will happen sometime later this afternoon before the markets open in Asia this evening.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And this will be a faster process than what happened with SVB (INAUDIBLE)?\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : It will be - it will be a much faster process.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`ve been going down this process for the last two weeks or so -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : As First Republic continues to be under pressure and continues to lose deposits. Unfortunately, \uc2 First Republic\uc2  reported this week that they had a massive outflow of deposits over the last quarter.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, if \uc2 First Republic\uc2  is sold, then the acquirer would take on the deposits.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, what do you think about the conversation we had earlier with Congressman Khanna about whether Congress needs to do something here? Because it seems like we`re just going into emergency mode now for three banks.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Does there need to be a broader change to the regulatory system and to the laws?\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : Well, it`s an interesting question. So, look, I don`t agree with Congressman Khanna that we want unlimited \uc2 FDIC\uc2  insurance. I think that, to me, is a bit of a race to the bottom.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You had picked like $2 million, $5 million, $10 million.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : Yes, I mean there`s got to be some limit. At some point you have to limit because you don`t want a total race to the bottom where, you know, the weakest bank with the weakest balance sheet in the world can offer you the highest rate of return on your deposits and therefore you take your deposits there because, guess what, they`re insured by the federal government.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : That`s not what we want to see. We want to see some type of discipline in the system.\par \par \uc2 When you talk about more and more regulation, I smiled because if you look at the report that came out that you referenced with \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  as well, you know, one of the findings in the report is that the regulators did not do a very good job in enforcing the existing rules. So, if you can`t enforce the rules you already have on the books - and it`s hard to enforce the rules because there are so many rules -- do you want to create more and more rules when you can`t enforce the ones you already have. Part of me feels like we need to get a simpler, more coherent set of rules so the bank regulators can actually enforce them. And they know what the important rules are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the bank regulators here are at the Fed. That`s what we`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : They`re at the fed and at the states. Remember, we have state regulated banks and federally regulated banks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. State - that true. Well, then, that`s a big conversation for California since they just had two banks have some big problems.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : It is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But Fed Chairman Powell is going to face questions from the press midweek.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They - he gives a press conference around the decision on interest rates that he is expected to be making.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you think these banking problems are going to interfere with his plan?\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN: I don`t think these problems are going to interfere with his plans. I actually think they`re helpful to his plans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because they`re slowing the economy?\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : So -- exactly. What the - what the chair has been trying to do is slow the economy down. He`s been trying to tamp down inflation. Inflation is too many goods chasing too few products. And part of the chasing has been the easy availability of credit. Now that we`ve seen deposits lose the -- leave the system, and we`ve seen banks entire financial position, they are not offering loans as easily as they were before, and the loans have become more expensive. So, people are borrowing less money. They have less access to credit. So, their ability to purchase is going down. Purchasing power is waning in the United States, which is exactly what the chairman has been trying to do by raising interest rates. So, he`s, in essence, getting an enormous amount of help out of this banking crisis. Not what he wanted to see happen in any way, shape or form -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN: But the unintended consequence is very helpful to slowing down the economy and tamping down inflation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, does it up the odds of a recession being more than mild?\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN: It probably ups the odds, yes. I mean it definitely ups the odds. It takes control out of the Fed. The Fed is no longer in total control of slowing down the economy. They`ve now got the banking industry playing along with them. But as we`ve seen in the economic data recently, the consumer in the United States still is in relatively good shape. They are starting to run out of savings. The money that they got during Covid, we put an enormous amount of stimulus into consumers` bank accounts and that -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Both administrations, Trump and Biden.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : Both administrations. Every - every administration put an enormous amount of stimulus into the bank accounts. We see from the savings data that`s starting to wear down, it`s starting to run off. So as that runs off further and further, the economy would become more credit dependent to keep driving. So, I think we will see a slowdown. And I still think we`re in a relatively decent shape. We may have a recession, but I still think we could muddle through the bottom here without a real deep recession.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The chair of \uc2 the House Financial Services Committee\uc2 , Congressman McHenry, called the Fed`s report a self-serving justification of Democrats` long held priorities. He may be venting. It doesn`t look like Congress is doing anything to change regulation or laws related to banking.\par \par \uc2 There was an \uc2 FDIC\uc2  report on the collapse of \uc2 Signature Bank\uc2 , which blamed bad management, but it also said regulators just didn`t have enough staff in New York. I mean this -- there`s some pretty damaging bits of information in here. If you put aside the politics, the regulators don`t enough staff? They didn`t act? So who are they being held accountable by, unless it`s Chair Powell?\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : Well, I -- it is Chair Powell. And I think - I think when the chairman goes to Congress - and, remember, he testifies in front of both the House and the Senate a couple times a year -- historically all the questions have been on monetary policy. I think we`re going to start seeing a lot more questions on the regulatory policy. How is regulation working? Are they keeping up to what they these to do? Do they have proper staff? Are there issues that are going by that are not being covered? This is a huge finding? I mean this is a bit of a seismic moment because we believe in the United States and I think the U.S. population believes that the banks, where they deposit their hard earned money, are well regulated.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : And we have found out this week, in the Fed`s own report, that these banks are not well regulated and they admitted it themselves.\par \par \uc2 I ran a regulated bank. I know that if we would have ever told our regulator that we did not have enough people to regulate ourselves, they would have shut us down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 GARY COHN\uc2 : So, we cannot be in a position where the regulators themselves say, we do not have enough staff to regulate you properly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You ran one of the biggest banks.\par \par \uc2 Gary, we`ve got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A search is underway in Texas for a gunman who allegedly killed five of his next-door neighbors, including an eight-year-old on Friday. Sheriff`s deputies say the suspect entered the neighbor`s house firing his AR-15 execution style after they asked him to stop firing the gun in his yard while their baby slept.\par \par \uc2 Thursday, we spoke with some parents about the impact of gun violence on their children, and we began with a mother who says her biggest concern about raising her young daughter is her safety.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE (Indiana): We never had things like lockdown drills or anything like that in school. Having her come home when she was even in kindergarten and first grade telling me about this is something that is a little bit concerning to me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What was that conversation like? How did you talk to her about gun violence?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE: Well, I live in a home where my husband does have firearms, and we`ve learned to use them like respectfully. And there`s a lot of rules and regulations around the safe use of guns. She didn`t really seem very scared about it. She said that, you know, the teachers did a very good job of it. What was concerning to me is just like at such a young age that they`re learning that this could happen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know, Scott, you are in Ohio, and, Wayne, you are in Arkansas. I imagine you are familiar with guns yourselves in your communities.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT (Ohio): Yes. And also lockdown drills. My wife is an early childhood intervention specialist and has been for decades. And I`ve been a substitute teacher. Luckily, we have not had any shooters in those, but we`ve had to talk to our kids and we`ve had to -- they`ve had to live through us notifying them that we were locked down in a school and them notifying us that there were lockdown in schools.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Does that cause your children to worry?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: They`ve seen us make it through it. And they have understood what the lockdowns were about. Often they were, in my wife`s neighborhood, somebody was shot a couple blocks away, but that`s enough to lock down the school. I think they understand, you know, that there is a risk, but that risk is spread out across the United States and - and even - even with the awful amount that`s happening, the chances of them happening at their very school on that day, they have bigger worries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Wayne, how much concern does gun violence cause for your children, particularly in school?\par \par \uc2 WAYNE (Arkansas): With me being ex-military, I`m a veteran, they come to me telling me, we want to learn. My biggest concern is, I have an elementary school here that when everybody`s inside, it turns into a prison. All the gates are locked, all the windows are locked. And I`m kind of concerned about that because if it gets past the front door, they can`t get out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Scott, I know in your state there was recently a law passed to make it easier for public school teachers to carry guns. Do you think that makes schools more secure?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: No. I think that it does quite the opposite. Again, a school shooter is not a common experience. You bring in -- guns into classrooms, then you have millions of guns across the nation in classrooms five days a week. You have all sorts of opportunities to create a gun disaster that you wouldn`t have had before they did this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Show of hands, do all of you believe that mental health is an issue right now and primarily to blame for the gun violence that we`ve been seeing? Raise your hand if you think it`s true? All of you think mental health.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE: I do think it`s a contributing factor, though, especially when you see some of the school shootings with some of the younger individuals. Yes, I feel like we`re not doing enough to stop it from happening.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. So, how many of you, show of hands, think gun laws should be more strict in your state?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Scott, you`re the only one who wants your state of Ohio to have stricter gun laws. OK.\par \par \uc2 How many of you think the federal government can do more to make life safer for kids when it comes to gun violence?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Scott, again, you`re the only one.\par \par \uc2 Christine, why don`t you think that government can do more?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE: A lot of people think of restricting, passing more laws. And, like, the truth of it is, is that the criminals don`t listen to the laws. If they did, they wouldn`t be criminals.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, Wayne, when it comes to mental health, you also don`t think there`s something that the state or federal government could do as it relates to the link between mental health and gun violence?\par \par \uc2 WAYNE: I think the laws that are already on the books need a little bit more human involvement. We need to have more -- people that look at what - who`s picking up this gun and what they`re going to do with it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How many of you are optimistic about the country right now? Raise your hand if you`re optimistic.\par \par \uc2 Wayne and Scott. Christine, no?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE: I mean, the economy is not great. Some of the relationships with foreign countries are not as good as they used to be. I mean, it`s just - it - yes, I do think like drugs are a big deal to me, the bullying is a big deal. I don`t know, I just -- I do think there`s a lot of issues.\par \par \uc2 WAYNE: I`m a born again Christian. I have a very tight understanding of what that means. I am seeing this country at this moment. In ten years, it`s going to be about the same. They`re making it seem like there`s something going on that`s pulling our economy down. And if we keep thinking that, we will go down. If we change our mindset, if you can shake it off, the sun comes up, you keep going.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Scott, what are you optimistic about?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I`m -- it also is related to my church experience. I see a lot of people connecting and still connecting. You know, you see on the media how everything is going down and is just on the edge of falling off a cliff. I think we can pull together and move forward - move ahead.\par \par \uc2 WAYNE: We`ve got to pull together. Because if we don`t, we will go down -- we will go down that rabbit hole.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I like - I like your sentiment that we all need to pull together. I think that`s a good note for us to end on today.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Russian forces fired dozens of missiles and drones into Ukraine, the heaviest barrage in weeks, just as Kyiv says preparations are underway for a major spring counteroffensive.\par \par \uc2 Our Charlie D`Agata has the latest from Dnipro.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice over): A fuel storage depot erupted into an inferno on the Russian occupied peninsula of Crimea. Russia blamed a drone strike. A Ukrainian official would only call it God`s punishment.\par \par \uc2 But the heaviest bombardment this country has seen in weeks. Almost two dozen civilians killed in the central city of Uman (ph), including small children. We found residents digging out after a missile strike outside Dnipro that killed 31-year-old Olga (ph) and her two-year-old daughter Veronica (ph).\par \par \uc2 Her uncle, Sirhi (ph), told us, she was very funny, very clever. We had big hopes for her.\par \par \uc2 There are no military targets here and it`s nowhere near the front line. Where trench warfare rages on, especially in an around the contested city of Bakhmut. A front line that might be about to expand dramatically as Ukraine`s defense minister announced that the counteroffensive is about ready for launch. His words, just waiting for God`s will and the weather.\par \par \uc2 NATO\uc2  announcing that 98 percent of the promised combat vehicles have been delivered. Among them, 230 tanks. But there are serious concerns, part of those leaked intelligence documents over Ukraine`s rapidly dwindling air defense systems, a question we put to senior defense official Alexi Danalove.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (on camera): What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice over): I can tell you that we are constantly working on this issue, he said. We are in great need of aircraft. We need a new means of anti-air defense if we want to be successful in this war.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say if Russian fighter jets and bombers are able to operate freely over the skies in Ukraine, it could drastically change the course of the war.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It`s impossible to overestimate the importance of air defense systems in safeguarding cities like here in Dnipro and the capital Kyiv, where defense officials say 11 missiles and two drones were intercepted in the latest wave of attacks.\par \par \uc2 Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 The struggle for democracy and freedom of the press were the main focus of last night`s annual White House Correspondents` Dinner, in a Washington ballroom packed with journalists, politicians and celebrities.\par \par \uc2 The president did get some laughs as he made fun of his age and when he took some swipes at Republicans and conservative leaning media outlets. But his tone was sober when he called for the release of wrongfully detained journalist \uc2 Evan Gershkovich\uc2  of "The Wall Street Journal," currently imprisoned in Russia, and Austin Tice, who`s been held in Syria for 11 years.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President Of The United States): Journalism is not a crime. Evan and Austin should be released immediately, along with every other American held hostage or wrongfully detained abroad. Paul Whelan, unjustly held in Russia for more than four years.\par \par \uc2 They are not forgotten. And I promise you, I am working like hell to get them home.\par \par \uc2 My commitment is to bring them home. Just as I know your commitment is to continue to be a free and fearless press. And that`s what we honor tonight.\par \par \uc2 You make it possible for ordinary citizens to question authority.\par \par \uc2 The free press is a pillar, maybe the pillar, of a free society, not the enemy.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The president`s remarks are usually the highlight of the event, but last night there was one guest in attendance who stole the show.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President Of The United States): I`ll never give up on hope. Things can get better. Things can turn. Things can change.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, unlike last year, Brittney Griner is here with her wife, Cherelle. Brittney, where are you, kid? Stand up. Come on. I love this woman. It`s great to have you home. And, boy (ph), I can hardly wait to see you back on the court, kid.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Griner is now using her public platform to advocate for Americans who are wrongfully detained, including the family of Ahmad Shargy and others held for years in Iran.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This programming note, our CBS News streaming daily political broadcast "Red and Blue" is going to get a new name and a new look. "America Decides" premiers tomorrow and airs starting Monday at 5:00 p.m. Eastern, with second runs at 6:00 p.m. and 9:00 p.m. Eastern. As for us, until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230430ej4u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Debate Over Abortion Heats Up\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Jan Crawford, Gayle King \par \uc2 1,106 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to the debate over abortion rights in this country after the Supreme Court chose to keep a commonly used abortion pill available for now. This case has exposed another political rift between Republicans like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  on an issue that has long divided this country.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The Supreme Court`s ruling leaves the abortion pill, Mifepristone available without restrictions.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "We say prochoice.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The political fight over abortion as a campaign issue is intensifying.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Former Vice President of the United States): I`d like to see this medication off the market.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was weighing a presidential run in 2024 told CBS News` Robert Costa that access to abortion should just be left up to the States. He is pushing for action at the Federal level and says Congress should pass a nationwide ban on abortion after the 15th week of a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 More than 90 percent of abortions occur before 13 weeks.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: This isn`t a State`s only decision. I think the American people would welcome a minimum national standard.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But that puts him at odds with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who says Congress should stay out of it.\par \par \uc2 Other Republicans say the GOP should be careful with this messaging ahead of the 2024 campaign season.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): We`re going to lose huge if we continue down this path of extremities.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The brief unsigned order from the Supreme Court Friday night is the most significant action involving abortion rights since the Justices overturned Roe versus Wade less than a year ago, but the issue is likely to be back before the Supreme Court next year after the Lower Courts weigh in on the merits, whether the \uc2 FDA\uc2  followed the right steps when it approved mifepristone 23 years ago and made it easier for women to get it in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Friday`s emergency action with just two of the nine Justices dissenting suggests the Court is unlikely to block access to the drug the next time around. There are significant questions about whether the challengers even have standing to bring the case.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now Justice \uc2 Sam Alito\uc2  wrote a dissent saying he didn`t think the Court needed to even issue this emergency stay because the pill still would be available, but that doesn`t really tell us anything about how he will see the merits of this case. I would not be surprised if he joins with a majority to say this case never should have been brought in the first place -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Jan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 CBS News confirms that President Biden is expected to launch his reelection campaign as soon as tomorrow. Now, if he wins, America`s oldest President would stay in office until he is 86 years old, and a lot of Americans say frankly they are not comfortable with that.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at the White House with more on that part of the story. Weijia, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Good morning, Gayle. Good morning to everybody.\par \par \uc2 President Biden spent the weekend at Camp David with staff and family members who he has consulted with for several months now about a second term.\par \par \uc2 The President is expected to make the official announcement in a campaign video message and he has some convincing to do according to a new NBC News poll that shows 70 percent of Americans do not think Mr. Biden should run again, including 51 percent of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Now half of those people who say he should not enter the 2024 race cite his age as a major reason why. CBS News has also confirmed that President Biden is expected to choose Julie Chavez Rodriguez to serve as campaign manager.\par \par \uc2 Now, right now, she is the Director of the White House Office of Intergovernmental Affairs. That`s one of the few Latinas who hold a top position in the West Wing.\par \par \uc2 People familiar with ongoing deliberations about Biden 2024 say that many key staffing and operational decisions have yet to be locked in including whether tomorrow is actually the day that the President will jump into the race, but of course we will be tracking it all -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We sure will be. Weijia, thank you very much. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right now to a scary moment in the sky. A video taken by a passenger shows fire coming from the engine of an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  plane. The flight was bound for Phoenix from Columbus, Ohio. Look at that. And the \uc2 FAA\uc2  said the plane was able to return safely to the Columbus airport. That`s good news.\par \par \uc2 Officials believe a bird strike may have caused this fire and firefighters were able to quickly put out the flames once the plane landed. No one was hurt in the incident.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What you never want to see -- fire in the wings when you`re in the air. Yikes.\par \par \uc2 The owners of \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2  say the Big Box retailer is going out of business putting 14,000 jobs in jeopardy. The company had been struggling for years and the pandemic just made it even more challenging to stay afloat.\par \par \uc2 The owners filed for bankruptcy protection yesterday. \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2  has 360 stores nationwide, along with 120 \uc2 buybuy BABY\uc2  stores. They will stay open while the owners in their words implement an orderly wind down and try to sell off their remaining assets.\par \par \uc2 I was so sorry to hear this. I hope they can work it out, because who hasn`t been to a \uc2 Bed Bath & Beyond\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve been to a lot of \uc2 buybuy BABY\uc2 `s as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nobody. Yes, there you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel bad for those workers by the way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, I will go buy something if it goes on sale.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, you and me both.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is happening.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230426ej4o000dy\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For April 23, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Strassmann, Jan Crawford, David Martin, Robert Costa,  Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,595 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 And today on Face the Nation: The Supreme Court preserves access to an abortion pill, for now.\par \par \uc2 And a daring evacuation of \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  personnel out of the embattled country of Sudan. Overnight, the mission to get U.S. diplomats and other personnel out of Sudan was successful. But what about the hundreds more Americans still trapped there?\par \par \uc2 We will talk with Delaware Democrat Chris Coons. He`s on the Africa Subcommittee in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Then: The Supreme Court makes an emergency ruling to keep mifepristone accessible for abortion, but sends the case back to the lower courts. Is the fight over? We will tell you what`s next.\par \par \uc2 And the politics of abortion within the Republican Party. Our Robert Costa sat down with former vice president and potential 2024 candidate \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Will he enter the race and challenge the former president?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Are you leaning in or are you leaning away from running?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Former Vice President of the United States): Well, I`m here in Iowa, Robert.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Plus, a series of deadly shooting errors, firing first and asking questions later, is taking its toll on a nation already anxious about gun violence.\par \par \uc2 We will talk with the mayor of Kansas City, \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2 , about the challenges he`s facing running a blue city in a red state.\par \par \uc2 Finally, an interview with Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 . He`s back in power, but what`s different now?\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We begin with a daring Special Forces rescue operation to get American diplomats out of Sudan, where fighting continues, as two top generals there jockey for power.\par \par \uc2 The State Department says security conditions aren`t expected to improve anytime soon, and has temporarily suspended operations at the U.S. Embassy in Sudan`s capital, Khartoum. Meanwhile, there are still hundreds of American citizens stranded in Sudan, where the airports have been closed for days.\par \par \uc2 For more now, we turn to national security correspondent David Martin.\par \par \uc2 David, good morning.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, this operation was high-risk. You had U.S. forces flying 800 miles from Djibouti to Ethiopia, then launching from Ethiopia to Sudan, and then had to go all the way back to Djibouti at the end, airlifted about 100 people out of the U.S. Embassy.\par \par \uc2 How did all this come together?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, distance was the primary challenge here, 800 miles. Helicopters just can`t go that far. So you had to set up this forward staging base in Ethiopia, where they could top off before the aircraft went into the embassy in Khartoum.\par \par \uc2 The other thing was the uncertainty of whether or not they were going to be shot at. Both generals of these two warring sides had been warned in no uncertain terms, do not interfere. But you couldn`t count at that, because we have -- we have seen all these cease-fires break down.\par \par \uc2 So the aircraft went in at night low level. And they had 100 special operations commandos on board. Those commandos set up a perimeter around this landing zone that was just outside the embassy and guarded that perimeter while the diplomats boarded the helicopters.\par \par \uc2 Overhead, there were two C-130 aircraft, one of which was for communications. The other was a gunship ready to take anybody who tried to approach the embassy under fire. They were on the ground for a little more than half-an-hour.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: No shots were fired. And then they were back on their way.\par \par \uc2 Somebody called it a pretty easy in-and-out, but it was long and grueling night. I mean, from start to finish, it was 17 hours.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it was so high-risk. This went, sounds like, as best as could possibly be expected.\par \par \uc2 But there are still hundreds of American citizens who are on the ground. There is no plan to evacuate them. These were just government personnel pulled out. The State Department and the Pentagon say they will do what they can to help Americans get out. What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Well, it certainly doesn`t mean going in and seizing the airport and doing the normal kind of evacuation aboard airliners out of there.\par \par \uc2 What it means is conducting reconnaissance along this land route that goes from Khartoum all the way over to Port Sudan on the Red Sea, which is a 12- hour drive under the best conditions. So they can conduct reconnaissance over that. And then they can have \uc2 U.S. Navy\uc2  ships waiting to take in any Americans who make that drive.\par \par \uc2 But that, again, will be at the -- at a minimum, a long and grueling drive. And the conditions are just chaotic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. And it is such a high-risk environment.\par \par \uc2 David, thank you very much for all of your reporting.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Sure thing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to Democratic Senator Chris Coons, who joins us from Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 I know you`ve said you feared this violence for the past few weeks. And it was this intense fighting between Sudan`s armed forces and a paramilitary group that led to this dramatic evacuation. Do you think the U.S. should have pulled out sooner?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS (D-Delaware): Well, Margaret, if I would been on this show, just two weeks ago, we wouldn`t have been talking about fighting in Sudan, because there wasn`t any.\par \par \uc2 There were special envoys from the \uc2 U.N\uc2 ., the A.U., the U.S. all negotiating with these two generals, General Hemedti, General Burhan, of the regular army and the paramilitary. I`m still hopeful that they could return to a civilian government. It unwound fast in just the last week. And I`m grateful that our Special Forces have now successfully overnight evacuated the U.S. nationals who work in our embassy in Khartoum.\par \par \uc2 This is a temporary suspension. It`s my hope and theirs that we will be able to return to Khartoum and the situation will stabilize. But, Margaret, this is the same sort of thing that happened in Kyiv in Ukraine, that has happened in other countries, in Yemen and Syria, where, when the fighting gets intense quickly, we rely on our Special Forces to evacuate U.S. nationals who staff an embassy in a country that descends into a war zone.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I know you`ve been saying the country may tip into all-out civil war.\par \par \uc2 Russia and China have really been extending their influence throughout Africa, Russia in Sudan as well, including that paramilitary group Wagner. They -- they have left behind hundreds of American civilians in Sudan, who now don`t necessarily have a way out of the country. Are you concerned about how the United States can use some kind of leverage to help its citizens escape?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS: Well, Margaret, just a reminder that Sudan is a vast country. It`s the third largest country in Africa, a country of 45 million people spread over a huge amount of territory.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I am concerned about the safety and security of U.S. nationals who`ve been serving in humanitarian missions or in other ways across the country. There are quite a few U.S.-Sudanese dual nationals in the country, and the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and the U.S. and a number of other countries will do their best to help return to civilian rule, to end the fighting, to support a stabilization in Sudan.\par \par \uc2 But as for right now, an evacuation through some overland convoy is the most likely path out for folks who work for the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2 , for example, who serve other countries in Khartoum and around the country, and for those remaining U.S. nationals who may wish to leave.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But does the U.S. have any leverage to stop the fighting?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS: Just a reminder, Margaret, this is a country that for 30 years was under the brutal dictatorship of...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS: ... \uc2 Omar al-Bashir\uc2 . It was on the state sponsor of terrorism list. We don`t have deep relationships with the Sudanese military or with the paramilitary force, the RSF.\par \par \uc2 We have some leverage, in that we provide development assistance, humanitarian relief. But, frankly, these two warring factions have started what may well be a fight to the finish. And we may have limited leverage in the next couple of weeks and months, as they carry out a fight to see who will ultimately be in control of the security of Sudan.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that is why there`s so much concern.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you as well about your position on the Judiciary Committee. \uc2 CBS\uc2  interviewed earlier this week an attorney for an \uc2 IRS\uc2  agent who is seeking whistle-blower status from Congress to share information, he says, would contradict sworn testimony to Congress by a senior political appointee regarding the investigation into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Do you think it`s worth looking into the possibility of undue influence here?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS: Look, anyone who comes forward and seeks whistle- blower protection status should be given that status. That`s part of what we`ve put in place over many years, a system that allows career folks who work in different federal agencies the chance to blow the whistle and testify if they see something wrong.\par \par \uc2 I will remind you, nothing`s been presented yet. This person hasn`t come forward in any detail. If and when they do, if there`s any substance to it, I expect that the chairman of the Judiciary Committee, Dick Durbin, and the ranking member will ensure that they are fairly and appropriately treated.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you confident in the conduct of Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  when it comes to this case?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS: I am.\par \par \uc2 Look, President Biden, from the days he was campaigning to his first days as president, made it clear that he thought restoring the independence of the Department of Justice, removing any political influence for potential investigations was a core value that he brought to this service as president, and I`m confident that that`s in no small part why he chose a seasoned circuit court judge, someone with also deep experience at DOJ.\par \par \uc2 I am confident that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has conducted himself appropriately here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 CBS\uc2  reported back in October that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had gathered evidence sufficient enough to charge \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  with tax and gun-related crimes and sent it to the U.S. attorney in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 And we know that, in the coming days, Mr. Biden`s attorneys are set to meet with the U.S. attorney in Delaware. Do you have any sense if this is going to conclude soon? It`s been ongoing since 2018.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS: No, I don`t, Margaret, nor should I. It is an ongoing investigation that, as you say, has been conducted for years. The U.S. attorney here in Delaware is the U.S. attorney who was appointed by the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS: And, look, if there are any charges ever brought, we`ll discuss them at that time. At this point, I think this is a long- going federal investigation, which I hope will reach a conclusion at some point soon.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Senator Coons, thank you for joining us this morning and giving us your perspective.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS COONS: Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the Friday Supreme Court decision, which preserves access to a widely used abortion pill, for now, while the legal process in the lower courts continues.\par \par \uc2 Chief legal correspondent Jan Crawford joins us.\par \par \uc2 Jan, it`s good to have you back here.\par \par \uc2 This was a decision you predicted, 7-2, the dissent coming from Justice Alito, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  also objecting. The Supreme Court is keeping the drug available now. So what happens next?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the bottom line is that this drug will remain available nationwide without any restrictions while these appeals play out. And that could take at least a year, I mean, even though this case is really on a fast track.\par \par \uc2 There`s an argument next month before a panel of judges of the U.S. Court of Appeals for the Fifth Circuit, which will decide at some point whether that lower court judge in Texas was right that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  improperly authorized mifepristone some two decades ago.\par \par \uc2 But regardless of whatever the appeals court decides, whoever loses is going to go right back to the Supreme Court and ask the justices to step in and decide the merits, whether the \uc2 FDA\uc2  properly followed the right steps when it approved mifepristone in 2000, and then when it agreed to make it more widely available, easier for women to get in 2016.\par \par \uc2 That will set the stage for a major Supreme Court case on abortion access possibly as soon as next year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: An election year too.\par \par \uc2 Jan, there`s great irony in the fact that the Supreme Court sent the decision on abortion access back to the states after Dobbs, and now we`re talking about going back to the Supreme Court to decide on it again.\par \par \uc2 Will the justices -- I mean, how involved will they get? I mean, you think this is inevitable it ends up there?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I think it`s going to go right back to the Supreme Court, because whoever loses will appeal it and ask the justices to get involved in decide it.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that they will.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You don`t think they will hear it?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I don`t think they will ever reach the merits.\par \par \uc2 Well, I think they will -- they will have to hear it. But I think they`re going to dismiss it on standing grounds. They`re going to say that these challengers who went after the \uc2 FDA\uc2  authorization weren`t able to show that they had right to be in federal court in the first place.\par \par \uc2 And let me just -- I mean, those are kind of bedrock conservative legal principles that really go to the heart of this case. To get into federal court, you can`t just be upset about some issue. You have got to show you have been harmed, that you have a stake in the case. It can`t be just something speculative in the future.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s a real problem for the challengers here. Now, the lower courts saw it differently. The Trump appointee, federal judge, saw it differently. But these conservative justices take those kinds of standing issues very seriously, because it goes to the point of judicial restraint.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why what they`re doing with this case is entirely consistent with what they did with Dobbs, the ruling that overturned Roe v. Wade and - - last year and sent it back to the states.\par \par \uc2 What they`re doing in this case is really saying, if they follow this rule on standing, that these challenges don`t have business being in federal courts. We`re going to set -- keep that kind of a high bar for getting into the courts. We don`t want federal judges ruling on these social issues. That belongs in the political process.\par \par \uc2 So, the bottom line for this case, I think, next year...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: ... whenever they get back to it, is, I think they`re going to dismiss it on standing. I think these conservative justices will join with the liberals and say, the challengers don`t have a right to sue in this case.\par \par \uc2 It could be 8-1, possibly even unanimous.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Very quickly, a lot of scrutiny of \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . What do you think is going to happen to him?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, as you know, there`s been reports that he failed to disclose a couple of different things on his disclosure forms, vacations that were paid for by a really rich friend of his that he didn`t disclose.\par \par \uc2 The rules on that were not very clear. They were recently amended. So, he said he will report that going forward. I think the more problematic one is some property that he and his family sold to the same friend, that that wasn`t disclosed. His -- his -- people close to Thomas have suggested that`s because he didn`t make a profit on that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: So he thought he didn`t need to disclose it.\par \par \uc2 The bottom line, though, is, Democrats are calling for hearings. Republicans are saying, this is ridiculous, this is politics.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: If it sounds like a mess, it is. And the Supreme Court could do well looking at adopting its own code of conduct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford, thanks so much for your reporting.\par \par \uc2 The issue of abortion will likely play a role in the 2024 elections.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, our Robert Costa went to Iowa and spoke with one of those potential Republican candidates, former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , and asked him about the court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: I`m pro-life. I don`t apologize for it.\par \par \uc2 But I think the fact the Biden administration allowed mifepristone to be made available on a mail-order basis was a -- a fundamental change, even in states that have limited abortion. I would like to see this medication off the market to protect the unborn.\par \par \uc2 But, also, I -- I have deep concerns about the way the \uc2 FDA\uc2  went about approving mifepristone 20 years ago. I`m grateful that action is being taken in the courts to hold the \uc2 FDA\uc2  accountable to what the law requires in reviewing any medication that`s made on the marketplace.\par \par \uc2 So, for the sake of protecting the unborn, but also for the health and safety of women, I -- I`m looking forward to this -- this litigation continuing and holding the manufacturers of mifepristone accountable and, ultimately, and putting the interests of women first.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has disputed claims that it`s unsafe, saying that serious complications are rare, and less than 1 percent of patients need hospitalization. That`s their position.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, I understand that, Robert, but, under the Obama administration, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  actually stopped chronicling nonfatal results of mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that I hope changes in the course of this litigation is, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  gets back to reporting to the American people all of the health impacts. But I do believe this issue bears upon the health and safety of women, and we`ve got to hold the \uc2 FDA\uc2  accountable to the law.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: This issue is also very, very big right now in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 There`s a real debate. Anti-abortion activist Marjorie Dannenfelser has said that anyone who takes former President Trump`s position that states should decide what happens on abortion, she has called that a morally indefensible position. Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: The cause of restoring the sanctity of life to the center of American law is the calling of our time.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court in the Dobbs decision last June gave the American people a new beginning for life. It returned the question of abortion to the states and to the American people. But it didn`t just return it exclusively to the states. And that`s where I disagree with the former president. This isn`t a states-only decision.\par \par \uc2 We have elected representatives in the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2  and we`ll elect a president again in 2024. And I think the American people would welcome a minimum national standard in Washington, D.C., 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Is former President Trump in retreat on the abortion front?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, I would -- I would leave that to others.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: What`s your take?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: You can ask him.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Your top aide Marc Short has said on the record, former President Trump is in retreat.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well -- I -- I -- look, that`s a great question for him.\par \par \uc2 I couldn`t be more proud to have been vice president in the administration that appointed three of the justices to the Supreme Court that sent Roe v. Wade to the ash heap of history. But, Robert, now we`re in a new season.\par \par \uc2 And I think millions of pro-life Americans want to see us seize every opportunity to put the interests of -- of the unborn first and also, in equal measure, to demonstrate the generosity and compassion of the American people toward women who`ve been caught up in abortion in the last 50 years and women who find themselves in a crisis pregnancy today.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Face the Nation will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There has been a string of recent shootings, some deadly, following simple everyday mistakes.\par \par \uc2 Our Mark Strassmann has a look at the impact it`s having on an already anxious nation.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): Time and again lately, innocence has met armed Americans assuming the worst. A stray basketball rolled into a neighbor`s yard in North Carolina. Gunshots.\par \par \uc2 KINSLEY WHITE (6 Years Old): He shot my daddy.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: A Texas cheerleader got into the wrong car. And, in New York, cars pulling into the wrong driveway became a fatal mistake. Each time, gunshots.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHRIS MURPHY (D-Connecticut): My friends, there is a toxic mixture in this country today of hate, of anger, and a population that is increasingly armed to the teeth with deadly weapons.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: More than 70 percent of adults say gun violence is a significant source of stress. About half say guns are a constant threat or a major concern,and 62 percent of black and Hispanic Americans.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 30 percent bought a gun as protection from gun violence. We`re a country up in arms. In just two years of the pandemic, Americans bought 60 million guns. Almost half of us now have a gun at home. But to the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and its supporters, blaming guns for gun violence misses the target.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): This is a mental health problem. This is a social problem. This is a cultural problem. This is a spiritual problem.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In Kansas City, 16-year-old Ralph Yarl rang the doorbell of the wrong house. He`s recovering from a gunshot to the head.\par \par \uc2 CLEO NAGBE (Mother of Ralph Yarl): He was supposed to stay outside, and his brothers were supposed to run outside, get in the car, and they -- and they come home. And that was what was supposed to happen.\par \par \uc2 And while he was standing there, his brothers didn`t run outside, but he got a couple of bullets in his body.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Legal experts say homeowner Andrew Lester may claim self- defense under Missouri`s stand your ground law.\par \par \uc2 About 30 states have stand your ground laws. Florida was first in 2005, a law made famous by the Trayvon Martin shooting in 2012. But those laws do not provide blanket protection for shooting anyone who comes at you.\par \par \uc2 And one study linked stand your ground laws with an up to 11 percent monthly increase in gun homicides. It`s a volatile, violent mix, armed Americans already on edge and, a minor mishap later, gunshots.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our Mark Strassmann reporting.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you miss an episode of Face the Nation, you can listen to our podcast. So, find us on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Music or wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation, including more of Robert Costas` interview with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , plus Kansas City Mayor \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2  and Israeli Prime Minister \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION and more of Robert Costa`s conversation with former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: There has been a spate of gun violence in recent weeks and it`s, at times, legal gun owners shooting people who come up to their door, on a driveway, in a parking lot. What is happening in America, and can anything be done to dial down the fear and the violence?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Former U.S. Vice President): Well, our - our hearts go out to the families of lost loved ones in the incidents in Kansas City and in upstate New York. I just can`t imagine the pain that they`re enduring in that tragedy.\par \par \uc2 But tragedy should not require us to forfeit our liberty. And the right of law-abiding citizens to keep and bear arms is enshrined in the Constitution of the United States.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know the facts of those cases. I`m confident local law enforcement will move forward and apply the law in a proper way. But I can`t help but suspect that this recent spate of tragedies is evidence of the fear that so many Americans are feeling about the crime wave besetting this country.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But I think most people would agree, even if you have fear about crime in your community, there`s no excuse to be just shooting at somebody at your door or in your - in a parking lot.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I can`t imagine the circumstances that I read about in the press in either of those cases. And -- and - and I`m - I`m sure local law enforcement will hold people to a proper accounting. I - but I - but, at the end of the day, I -- I just wonder - I wonder if it isn`t some reflection of the fear the American people feel about the crime wave that`s impacting our country, literally from coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You have agreed to appear before the special counsel`s ongoing grand jury investigating January 6th with some constraints on your testimony. Have you set a date with the special counsel about your appearance?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, our - our attorneys have worked that out with the Justice Department. And I -- but I will say, I`m grateful that the court recognized that there are specific constitutional protections unique to the vice president when you`re serving in your role as president of the Senate. I thought it was important to make that challenge. For the first time in history a federal court acknowledged that that provision of the Constitution applies to the vice president and they`ve -- they`ve limited what they`ll be requesting of me. But we`ll -\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Help us understand that a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Beyond that, I can tell you, Robert, we`ll - we`ll obey the law. We`ll tell the truth. And the story that I`ve been telling the American people all across the country, the story that I wrote in the pages of my memoir, that will - that will be what I tell in that setting as well.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But for a lay person who`s not a lawyer, what are the constraints, in your view, on your upcoming testimony? Will you, for example, be able to testify in your view about the private conversations you have had with President Trump? Is that within the range of what you could do before the grand jury?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I think I`m limited about what I can say about the proceedings of the grand jury or the decision of the judge. But people can be confident that we`ll - we`ll obey the law. We`ll comply with the law.\par \par \uc2 But I`ve got to tell you, Robert, nobody is talking to me about this.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Fox News just settled with Dominion for $787 million over false claims that were on the network. Any reaction?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, I - I would assume that - that Fox News determined what - what the appropriate settlement was and what the exposure was in that case. I can`t - I can`t really speak to it. That was not a time in my life that I was watching a lot of television, Robert. I was focused on the task at hand, focused on doing our duty under the Constitution of the United States. The role that you, your network, and other members of the media play is vital to our democracy. And I`ll always stand for a free and independent press, even when I don`t agree with what you say or do.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It`s almost May. When are you going to decide on whether you are running for the Republican nomination?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, we`re getting awful close, but I don`t have anything to announce today, Robert, but I - I promise --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Will you make a hard decision by late June?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I think anyone that would be serious about seeking the Republican nomination would need to be in this contest by June and -\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: So, you will make a decision by late June?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I -- I think -- if we have an announcement to make --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Whether --\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: It will be well before late June.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But are you leaning in or are you leaning away from running?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, I`m here in Iowa, Robert.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Well, that`s a tell.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Look, I love this country. And I think America is in a lot of trouble. And what I hear people telling me is that the challenges that we`re facing in an increasingly dangerous world, the challenges that we`re facing in this economy with inflation at a 40-year high, a crisis at our border, are going to require someone who has the ability to step in on day one and set our country back on a path towards security and prosperity. And so we`re thinking very deeply about that. And as I said, I - I don`t have anything to announce today, but -\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It sounds like you`re leaning in. It sounds like you`re leaning in.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I, look I -\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Leaning toward it versus away?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I - I would tell you that I`m very humbled by the encouragement that we`re receiving, and I promise when we have something to announce, you`ll be among the first to know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Robert Costa`s full conversation with the former vice president is on our website and our FACE THE NATION \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to the mayor of Kansas City, which is where the shooting of Ralph Yarl happened.\par \par \uc2 Quinton Lucas\uc2  joins us now from our affiliate, KCTV.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Mr. Mayor.\par \par \uc2 This case is --\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS (Mayor of Kansas City, Missouri): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is so tragic. And it has really captured the attention of the country. The shooter was 84-years-old, and he shot an unarmed 16-year- old for ringing his doorbell. He says he thought he was about to be robbed.\par \par \uc2 I know you already have a high rate of gun violence in Kansas City, but what has this particular tragedy meant?\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS\uc2 : Well, to me it says several things. And one of those was mentioned just a moment ago by Vice President Pence in the interview. I think that actually it is this culture of fear and paranoia that`s drummed up by some, including politicians like the former vice president, who mention it almost in a way as if it`s an excuse for this type of action.\par \par \uc2 This was in the safest neighborhood of Kansas City, or one of our safest neighbors, and this was a man, who in his statement to the police said, I was scared of this, in essence, large, black person outside of his door. He thought the child was six feet tall. He`s only 5`8". He thought he was a threat. He was on the other side of two locked doors.\par \par \uc2 This is the sort of thing that happens when you have this culture of paranoia and fear that`s being drummed up by politicians and some in the media and, of course, this fettization (ph), I said before, of guns. More stand your ground laws, more laws that say you should use your gun and have it absolutely everywhere.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, your governor, who is a Republican, condemned the shooting very clearly, but he also said that President Biden was politicizing it by calling the family of that boy, Ralph Yarl. He didn`t call the families of the victims in New York and in Texas. What do you make of that? And does it complicate things when the president gets involved just given how divided our country is right now?\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: It absolutely does not complicate anything when the president gets involved. First of all, this was a - a news story, an incident, a situation that had the attention of the country long before President Biden called and actually did the juts humane thing and gave best wishes to a boy who had just been shot twice. This is a 16-year-old in the Midwest who had been shot twice. He gets a call from the president of the United States, which I think is a nice thing.\par \par \uc2 But, really, if you think about all the conversation, there was not a conversation I had certainly in this city but with any mayors around the country who weren`t noticing this story. The racial dynamic, the fact of these laws that are extreme are, frankly, arming our citizens and having them more scared, I think, than they`ve ever been before. This was an 84- year-old man who went to sleep in one of the safest neighborhoods of Kansas City but still had a loaded gun. And when he heard a disturbance at his door, the first thing he thought to do wasn`t just to brandish it, wasn`t even to say a word or scream at somebody who would be outside, it was to shoot and to shoot twice.\par \par \uc2 The facts of this case were astonishing in and of themselves. And had the president never said a word, there would still be lots of attention. I strongly disagree with the governor, and particularly when his party often has politicized any number of incidents relating to border crossings and beyond in places like Missouri, far away from our southern border, to use any number of political examples. I think that this is a serious situation and the real politization are the people, who after each one of these incidents -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: Say, oh, let`s blame it on mental health, let`s blame it on society. It`s tragic right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You just talked about your Republican governor. And I just want to point out that in Kansas City it`s a little bit unusual because you, as mayor, don`t oversee the police department. There`s a board appointed by the state that oversees them.\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you`re a Democrat, in a red state, where you can`t control the police department in your own city. So, how do you work with the governor to crack down on the gun violence if it is the Republicans in control of it?\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS\uc2 : Well, we beg, we pray and we plead with them. These - these are Missourians who are - are shot, right? This is not some sort of thing where the city is just an evil place far, far away. We are within this state. But you`re seeing not just in Missouri, we`ve had this set up for a while in Kansas City. Right now there`s an effort to take over state control of police. And in St. Louis you saw a lawsuit filed by the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  on Friday night in Jackson, Mississippi, relating to that issue where there`s this state takeover.\par \par \uc2 The cities are now punching bags. They have been for a while, but you`re seeing this new extreme of everything that happens here, even our crime, isn`t something that we need to fix for the state. Instead, it`s an indictment, let`s say, on the city people themselves. I think it has lots to do with racial differences that are present in the city, our different views on gun crimes, and, frankly, it`s something that can scare people a little bit more than perhaps talking about international affairs or deficits.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: Instead, it`s something that seems to be down the street and it is harming and tearing apart our country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A lawyer for Ralph Yarl`s family spoke to my colleague, Gayle King, and said they were pleased with the felony charges that the police did, you know, proceed along with, but they want to know why attempted murder wasn`t part of it and they have questions about if there were civil rights violated. Do you have answers from the police or anyone on - on those points?\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: I know our police department has worked hard to review this situation. And while there are critiques, certainly we welcome those and further study.\par \par \uc2 I think the challenge with a hate crimes charge is just the proof of intent that relates to it. What we do have is that there was a man who said that he was afraid of a black male outside of his door, he shot twice. The felony assault charge carries life in prison, which for an 84-year-old is a substantial, potential sentence. There`s an additional armed criminal action charge. But I believe that our federal investigators and so many others will look into this to see if there are further charges.\par \par \uc2 I think what a lot of people, though, wanted to see over the last week is that this was taken seriously.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: It was astonishing to some that someone who could shoot someone twice was then back in their bed later that night. I know that we have worked hard to try to address that, but we`ll answer more questions as time goes along with this tragic situation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Just to button up what you were saying in terms of culture of fear, can`t both things be true, that there is too much anxiety and manipulation of fear at the same time there is a legitimate concern about rising crime?\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: You know, both can be true, but I don`t think that`s the situation now. I mean think about the fact that - and you hear certain political figures who talk about cities that are fundamentally safer than actual cities in their own state.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: Often this is kind of the Governor DeSantis bashing of New York City, which is much safer than a lot of the largest cities in the state of Florida.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: I think this is, in many ways, fully drummed up and it`s part of getting people more guns, getting them more afraid.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 QUINTON LUCAS: And I don`t think it in any way relates to the data on the ground each day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Mayor, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to Israel, where tens of thousands of protesters marched through Tel Aviv last night in opposition to the government`s plans to overhaul the judicial system. This is just days ahead of Israel`s 75th anniversary celebrating its independence.\par \par \uc2 For more we go now to the country`s prime minister, \uc2 Benjamin Netanyahu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Mr. Prime Minister.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2  (Prime Minister of Israel): Good morning. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re glad you ae here.\par \par \uc2 You know, it has been a month since you hit pause on those judicial reforms. At this moment in time, when you need national unity, why not withdraw them?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Well, I think there is a broad consensus that we have to make corrections in our judicial system. There`s a -- obviously, a dramatic difference between the views of how, to what extent and so on. But I think this should not cloud the fact that we`re celebrating here a modern miracle. Israel`s 75th anniversary is the change that happened to the Jewish people who were decimated in the Holocaust. A third of our people were lost to this independent nation that has become a power in the world. And I think everybody unites around that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You had to cancel a Monday appearance at the largest gathering of North American Jewish leaders in years because of these protest concerns. Again, why not withdraw the proposal to overhaul the judicial system, which would give parliament, which is controlled by your allies, authority to overturn supreme court decisions?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Well, I`ve actually said that I will not accept a blanket ability of the parliament to overcome judicial supreme court decisions, just as we don`t accept that the supreme court can abrogate any decision by the parliament or the government. Both sides - both of these extremes actually hinder the balance between the three branches of government, which is exactly what we`re trying to bring into balance now. There is, I think, a difficult and happy middle.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, but, Sir, you`re making this sound like it`s just a debate.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : It is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re making it sound like this is just a simple debate like any other country, but you, yourself, used the phrase that you were pausing because you wanted to stop the possibility of civil war. That was a phrase you used when you hit pause.\par \par \uc2 I want to - I want to just lay out for you here what it has done here in the United States. Those judicial plans led President Biden to say he won`t be inviting you to Washington any time soon.\par \par \uc2 Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I`m very concerned. And I`m concerned that they get this straight. They cannot continue down this road.\par \par \uc2 Hopefully the prime minister will act in a way that he is going to try to work out some genuine compromise.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Biden told you to walk away.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You seem to be betting that there won`t be consequences to alienating your closest ally.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Well, I value the alliance with the United States, and I value the friendship I`ve had over 40 years with President Biden. I don`t think anything will get in that way.\par \par \uc2 But it`s -- it`s an internal matter that we have to resolve. And we`re doing it. And the way we`re doing it is by seeking a consensus as we speak right now, Margaret, as we speak right now, there are teams of my own party, the Likud, and the coalition, with teams from the opposition, speaking in the president`s house. This is now the fifth or sixth meeting they`ve had, seeking the compromise that I think is the mark of democracies. You don`t walk away from a problem, you try to solve it. But you try to solve it through as broad a consensus as you can.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, walk away from your proposal. Walk away from your proposal, which would allow parliament with a simple majority to override any decision by the supreme court.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Well, we`ve already changed - we - we`ve already changed -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That is your one check and balance on power. Very different from the American system.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about the makeup of your government, because it is impacting U.S. relations. Your finance minister calls himself a homophobe. He said a Palestinian village should be erased. You did say that was inappropriate. Your public security minister was rejected from army service because of past ties to an extremist group designated by the U.S. as a terrorist organization.\par \par \uc2 I know you need to keep your coalition together to prevent a collapse, but are you confident you can rein in people like this?\par \par \uc2 NETANYAHU: I think a lot of them have changed over time and they themselves say that.\par \par \uc2 But the important thing to understand is, they joined me. I didn`t join them. We have by far the largest party in the Knesset, and certainly in the coalition. There`s smaller parties.\par \par \uc2 The mainstream policies are decided by me. And that`s what I`m doing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, this national security minister I just mentioned, Ben=Gvir, who threatened to quit, which would have collapsed your government, you promised him a national guard will be established under his control. The \uc2 IDF\uc2 , and your security forces, are more than strong. He`s already gone out and said he wants police to remove Palestinian flags from public spaces. What exactly do you think he`s going to do with this national guard?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Well, the national guard is not merely his idea. It`s a wide proposal, which is, by the way, recognized -- was actually proposed by the previous government as well because you need -- Israel has a small police force relative to the size of the population. And we face, unlike other police forces around the world, we face the constant threat of terror.\par \par \uc2 There is a national guard. It`s going to be under one of our national security -- under one of our security arms. It`s not going to be any individual persons or minister`s militia. That`s not going to happen in Israel.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Not under me, and I suspect not under anyone else. It`s just not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you clarify this for us as well because it`s making headlines in the U.S. Politician May Golan said that you are considering appointing her to be consul general in New York. She calls herself a proud racist. She`s denounced African refugees as Muslim infiltrators and criminals spreading HIV.\par \par \uc2 Are you nominating her to actually serve in New York, an incredibly important post for Israel?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : It is indeed an incredibly important post and anyone I`ll nominate, and I haven`t done so, will have to abide and will abide by the mainstream positions that I`ve advocated and I welcome the fact that the United States has a multiracial, in pluralistic (ph) society. So does Israel. And as anyone I appoint will have to reflect the value that I attach to that quality and our democracy and in yours.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you are not appointing her?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : I haven`t, but I`m telling you --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you won`t? \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : That anyone that I will appoint will abide stringently by that view that I`ve advocated throughout my lifetime. And it`s not proforma. It`s not lip service. I really believe that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Sounds like you`re saying she`s not coming to New York.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about some of the Americans coming to Israel. Florida`s governor, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , is visiting Israel this week and he`s presumed to be running for president here, as you know. Do you plan to meet with him?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Of course. I`ll meet with everyone. Why not? I meet with Republican governors and Democratic governors. I`m not avoiding the question. And, actually, I`m - I`m rushing right into it. I`d meet with every American representative, governor, senator, members of Congress, and I think it`s - it`s my job and I think it`s important for Israel`s bipartisan support in the United States. I make a point of it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Because for the first time there was a poll from Gallup last month that showed Democrats are likelier to sympathize with Palestinians than with Israelis for the first time. I know it`s easy to dismiss polls, but this seems to be a reflection of public sentiment in the United States that relates directly to Israel`s influence in America. Forty-nine percent of Democrats sympathize with Palestinians, 38 percent with Israelis. Do you think that matters?\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Yes, I do think it matters. And I think we have to work harder to persuade our Democratic colleagues or those of - those Democratic - our Democratic colleagues who forget, perhaps, that Israel is the solitary democracy in the Middle East, that America has no better friend and no better ally than Israel.\par \par \uc2 But I`ll tell you why I think this happens. First of all, it`s happening over time. It happened over time. It`s not related to this or that administration in Israel because it happened, it continued under the previous government as well. I think there is a demonization of Israel in some of the reports -- many of the reports that come out of here. And I think there is a portion of the American public that finds it hard to understand that once you - you enter the - the realm of nations, you have to act to defend yourself. And I think we have to work hard to persuade both sides of the aisle --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Democrats.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : And the independents in between. Yes, both sides of the aisle, and -- in this case Democrats, because we have solid support among independents and solid support among Republicans and considerable support among Democrats. But I`m not going to give up.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mr. Prime Minister, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 BENJAMIN NETANYAHU\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230423ej4n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 With The Supreme Court Maintaining Access To A Widely Used Abortion Pill For Now, Advocates Ready For Future Fights\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Willie James Inman \par \uc2 557 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Abortion rights activists are celebrating a reprieve by the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , blocking for now a Lower Court decision that restricted access to a widely used abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Willie James Inman joins us now for what is next in the legal battle.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Willie.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Good evening to you, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 The Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals is set to hear arguments in less than a month regarding the FDA`s authority over the abortion pill, mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuit has been fast tracked by the court and could end up before the Supreme Court once again.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: The legal battle over the FDA`s approval of mifepristone, the nation`s most widely used abortion drug, now moves to the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals in New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: The Fifth Circuit is a very conservative circuit. I think they tip their hands as to where they`re going with respect to that partial stay.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: Ten days ago, the Fifth Circuit ruled against a sweeping decision by a federal judge in Texas, who issued a complete ban on mifepristone. But the court put up restrictions on the drug`s use.\par \par \uc2 After an emergency appeal, the Supreme Court weighed in Friday night, restoring Mifepristone use without restrictions while the appeals process plays out.\par \par \uc2 A lawyer that pushed for the ban in Texas believes the courts will agree the \uc2 FDA\uc2  should have never approved mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Courts have found our case compelling because the \uc2 FDA\uc2  fails to actually look at the data that they had before it and what our doctors have presented to them, clearly showing that these drugs present dangerous situations to women and girls.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: The Justice Department and the \uc2 FDA\uc2  dispute the claims in the Texas lawsuit and say the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has rigorously reviewed mifepristone`s safety and effectiveness repeatedly since it was first approved 23 years ago.\par \par \uc2 More than five million women in the U.S. have used the medication. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  says a ruling that weakens FDA`s authority would create chaos in the prescription drug market.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noticias Telemundo Interview)\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: Arguably, what they`re doing with mifepristone could happen to any one of those drugs in your medicine cabinet.\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: After more than two weeks with mifepristone in legal limbo, some states are stockpiling the drug, including Oregon.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Stockpiling is never our first choice, it is a backup option for extreme situations, which this is.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIE JAMES INMAN: President Biden says he stands by the FDA`s authority to authorize prescription drugs like mifepristone and the White House is promising to fight against challenges to abortion access -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Willie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020230425ej4m0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Charges Against Alec Baldwin Are Dropped\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jonathan Vigliotti \par \uc2 1,593 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, any moment now, the Supreme Court is set to weigh in on whether a common abortion pill should remain widely available. What the court`s action on abortion rights since overturning Roe v. Wade could mean for American women.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The new legal showdown over abortion.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: This case is far from over. It is in the appeals process, and, at some point, it will be right back up here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: The charges against Alec Baldwin are dropped after that movie set shooting, but why he may not be in the clear.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: Severe weather hit parts of Texas overnight, as people in Oklahoma pick up the pieces from multiple tornadoes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #2: We have got above-average temperatures across the Eastern Seaboard.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Planning to travel this summer? Is your passport up to date? What you need to know about the potential turbulence.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN (U.S. Secretary of State): And the unprecedented demand. We are looking for a regular passport about 10 to 13 weeks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: New warnings about what they`re calling a -- quote -- "emerging threat" from a potentially deadly new drug cocktail.\par \par \uc2 MISSY OWEN (Davis Direction Foundation): Xylazine and fentanyl combined have been described as the deadliest drug in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And On the Road with two old friends who met in an unexpected way.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GINNY SCHRAPPEN (Retired Schoolteacher): He was in prison for murder. I wasn`t worried.\par \par \uc2 LAMAR JOHNSON (Exonerated): When you have people who believe in you and they won`t give up on you, then it makes it harder for you to give up on yourself.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 We are awaiting a Supreme Court order on the use of a common abortion drug.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` chief legal correspondent, Jan Crawford, standing by outside the Supreme Court. When we have those orders, we will bring them to you, just any moment now.\par \par \uc2 But, first, we want to begin with the news that Oscar-nominated actor Alec Baldwin is off the hook for now. Tonight, we are learning that -- new details in the prosecutor`s decision to drop the charges related to the fatal shooting on the side of Baldwin`s film "Rust."\par \par \uc2 Court filings reveal that new facts came to light that require further investigation and forensic analysis. Baldwin has always denied any wrongdoing in the case, saying he never pulled the trigger and that the gun went off accidentally. Involuntary manslaughter charges have not been dropped against the film`s armorer, Hannah Gutierrez Reed, who was in charge of the weapons on the set.\par \par \uc2 Remember, Baldwin was rehearsing a scene when the gun fired, killing cinematographer Halyna Hutchins and injuring director Joel Souza.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jonathan Vigliotti is going to start us off tonight from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Good evening to you, Norah.\par \par \uc2 And the question tonight is, what new facts have led to such a major change in the charges? The original DA stepped down last month, handing this case to a new team that is now responsible for combing through all of this evidence.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI (voice-over): The real-life twists and turns in the "Rust" shooting case are playing out like one of Alec Baldwin`s movies.\par \par \uc2 Last night, the 65-year-old actor posted on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  this photo with his wife, Hilaria, saying: "I owe everything I have to this woman."\par \par \uc2 This after the stunning reversal by prosecutors, who decided to drop the involuntary manslaughter charge, saying: "New facts were revealed that demand further investigation and forensic analysis."\par \par \uc2 ALEC BALDWIN (Actor): Someone is responsible for what happened, and I can`t see who that is, but I know it is not me.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Baldwin has insisted he never pulled the trigger of the weapon that turned out to be loaded with live ammo, killing cinematographer Halyna Hutchins and injuring director Joel Souza.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: I was very surprised when I heard the charges had been dropped. And then I was even more surprised when I read what the reasoning seems to be.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: CBS News legal analyst Rikki Klieman cited new reports which suggest the gun in question may have been modified before it was delivered to the site.\par \par \uc2 RIKKI KLIEMAN: What we have learned is that, if this gun had in fact been modified, and that it could go off without someone pulling the trigger, is that the prosecutors and their first investigation was not thorough.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Prosecutors say the decision does not absolve Baldwin of criminal culpability and charges may be refiled.\par \par \uc2 Charges have not been dropped against Hannah Gutierrez Reed, the armorer in charge of the weapons on set. Her lawyers say they expect her to be exonerated.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And prosecutors say this shooting was preventable and criminal.\par \par \uc2 For now, all blame is focused on Gutierrez Reed, who will appear back in court in August, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jonathan Vigliotti, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Back here in Washington, all eyes on the Supreme Court, which is facing a self-imposed deadline of midnight to decide if women will continue to have access to the widely used abortion drug mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford is outside the Supreme Court as we await this decision.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jan.\par \par \uc2 So what are some of the different scenarios?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, right now, the justices are going to decide whether they`re going to keep a delay, a pause on these lower court rulings in place, as the appeals process plays out.\par \par \uc2 That would mean that the pill would remain available nationwide during the appeals process. So we could get one of three things from the court tonight. We could see the court block the lower court rulings in their entirety. And that would basically be the status quo, nothing would change, the abortion pill would be available, just like it has been for the past 20 years, and then it was revised again in 2016.\par \par \uc2 Or we could see the justices let the lower court rulings take effect. And that would mean major significant changes across the country, because then that would mean restrictions on the use of that pill. A third option could be some kind of mixed bag, where they blocked part of it, let others go into effect. That`s what the appeals court did in this case.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we`re just waiting, expecting to hear from the court before midnight tonight whether they will extend that temporary hold that Justice Alito handed down last Friday. That kind of put everything, froze everything in place. Tonight, we will hear from the court whether it will keep that in place throughout the appeals.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Jan Crawford there, and, as that uncertainty continues, many of these states stockpiling the pill. We will have more with Jan Crawford.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And more severe weather is in the forecast for this weekend after a tornado touched down last night in Tyler, Texas. Authorities say some homes and businesses were damaged, but, thankfully, no serious injuries were reported.\par \par \uc2 And officials in Oklahoma continue to sift through the damage after a dozen twisters plowed through the state, destroying virtually everything in their path. Thousands across the state are still without power.\par \par \uc2 And for more on what`s to come, including a dangerous Saturday, let`s bring in meteorologist \uc2 Mike Bettes\uc2 . He`s with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mike.\par \par \uc2 MIKE BETTES\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Norah, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Another pleasantly warm day in the Eastern U.S. today, our eighth 80-degree day in Washington, D.C., this month alone. Notice tomorrow, temperatures once again into the 70s and 80s, a lot of humidity as well, one factor that will go into forming storms tomorrow ahead of this front.\par \par \uc2 We have got good upper-level support as well. So, a lot of different things come together to produce storms. They race their way from the Tennessee Valley right into the Northeast, especially from, say, Myrtle Beach up toward Washington, D.C., and all points in between. Watching for severe storms that could produce some hail and some high winds.\par \par \uc2 As we go through the afternoon and evening, I-95 getting hit, and some of these storms could produce some very heavy rain, Norah, for many locations. What we will watch for is flooding Saturday into Sunday.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Mike, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Turning overseas to the war in Sudan, we are learning new details about the Biden administration`s potential plan for an evacuation of dozens of government personnel from the embassy in Khartoum. An order has not been given yet, and the military is waiting for an opening during some of this heavy fighting. CBS News has learned not all Americans who would be extracted have reached the embassy.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230425ej4l000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 SCOTUS Delays Abortion Pill Ruling\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Jan Crawford, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 824 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And in Washington, the Supreme Court is taking more time to decide if the abortion pill, Mifepristone should remain available nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The White House is appealing Lower Court rulings including one from a Judge who said the drug had been wrongly approved.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 You know, we had expected it now, this morning whether the Court was going to kind of indefinitely block those Lower Court rulings, but without any explanation, the Justices have pushed that deadline back until tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Just hours before Wednesday`s deadline, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  announced an extension, which keeps the abortion pill, mifepristone available nationwide for at least two more days.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration and the drug manufacturer have asked the Justices to step in or block Lower Court rulings that would ban or greatly restrict the pill, which is used and more than half of abortions in the US.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: All of this is unusual, from top to bottom.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: CBS News legal contributor, Jessica Levinson says it is uncommon for the Justices to push their own self-imposed deadline.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: We don`t know if they are so close to being able to have a unanimous agreement and they want to be able to get there or if there`s just such a huge breakdown that there are really aggressive majority opinion and a really aggressive dissent.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: While the Court is not currently considering the merits of the case, it will determine if women continue to have access to the drug while cases work their way through the Courts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  says recent rulings could undermine \uc2 FDA\uc2  approvals of other drugs.\par \par \uc2 DR. JACK RESNECK, JR. (\uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  President): We just think it`s incredibly reckless to upend our entire drug approval system in this country and create chaos where we`re going to have individual Courts and States around the country, basically undoing those decisions.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Some Republicans in Congress say that concern is overblown.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Now, this isn`t related to regulation of drugs. This is related to a Federal bureaucracy following the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But the White House says it is ready for a battle in the Courts.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE: We are prepared for any outcome the Supreme Court may issue and we are prepared for a long legal fight, if necessary.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And it probably will be a long legal fight because even if the Justices put these rulings on hold, the case is going to kind of continue to work its way through the Lower Courts, and there are other challenges in other States.\par \par \uc2 So either way, the issue of abortion is going to end up right back here before these Justices -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Police in Akron, Ohio broke up a street protest over a grand jury`s decision not to charge eight officers who shot and killed Jayland Walker. Police said they fired pepper spray and chemical irritants after protesters threw rocks and bottles last night.\par \par \uc2 Officials say at least 100 people blocked traffic with their vehicles for as long as 15 minutes. No arrests were made.\par \par \uc2 Walker was shot 46 times after a car and foot chase last year. Officers say he fired at them first from his vehicle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And in Kansas City, the man accused of shooting a teenager who walked into his house has pleaded not guilty to two felony charges. Eighty- four-year-old Andrew Lester appeared in Court yesterday for the first time since the shooting. Sixteen-year-old Ralph Yarl was shot last week.\par \par \uc2 Lester told police, he feared a break-in and was scared to death when the teenager mistakenly came to his door and rang the doorbell. Lester will remain free on bond. The next Court date is set for the beginning of June.\par \par \uc2 The Yarl family shared a new photo of Ralph who is a very talented musician playing his cousin`s toy guitar as he recovers at home. We`re told he recently accepted an invitation to perform at the White House when he is feeling better.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That incident in Kansas City, of course, is just one of multiple shootings that have shocked the country in recent days.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230425ej4k000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Deadly Tornado Breakout; SCOTUS Delays Abortion Pill Ruling; Violence at Jayland Walker Protest; Not Guilty Plea in Mistaken Address Shooting; Shootings Across the Nation; Arrests in Birthday Shooting Rampage; Guns and Violence in America; Whistleblower Comes Forward in Hunter Biden Probe; US Missiles Arrive in Ukraine; Ukraine Prepares for Spring Counteroffensive; Arizona Water Controversy; \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  Live; Beyonce`s BeyGOOD Gives Back; Adding Inspiration to the Classroom; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Janet Shamlian, Jan  Crawford, Mark Strassmann, Jim Axelrod, Holly Williams, Ben Tracy, Vladimir  Duthiers, Scott MacFarlane \par \uc2 14,210 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday. \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  is going to be here today. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. So, let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it is your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Severe storms turn deadly in the Midwest after multiple tornadoes cause widespread damage and leave thousands of people without power.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GIBBONS (McClain County Sheriff`s Deputy): Based on the damage reports that we`re getting back, reasonably there could be more injuries or deaths that we don`t know about at this time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A whistleblower claims the investigation into Hunter Biden`s tax returns is being mishandled.\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE (Attorney): We`re compromised because of political consideration.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: At least 78 people are dead after a crowd surge during a Ramadan charity event in Yemen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Supreme Court chooses to delay its decision on whether a commonly used abortion pill will remain available nationwide.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): The stakes could not be higher and we are going to continue to fight.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In Akron, police break up what they called an unlawful protest.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Demonstrators called for justice in the Jayland Walker case.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Grizz cruise to a 10-point win and Dillon Brooks had these words for LeBron.\par \par \uc2 DILLON BROOKS (\uc2 Memphis Grizzlies\uc2 ): I don`t care, he is old.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: There is new music from the Foo Fighters. It is their first album since the death of drummer, Taylor Hawkins.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Rescued.")\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): A new AI generated song featuring fake vocals from Drake at the weekend has been pulled from streaming services. Here`s a sample.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Heart on My Strings" by AI-generated Drake and The Weekend Vocals.)\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I mean, I dig. I like it. Very good. You know what I say, we`ve still got a week. Let`s get AI in the car, guys.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m with you James Corden. It does sound good, but it is not them.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also it`s terrifying. What if it is a politician.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is terrifying.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . making a speech that they never made.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. Yes. Very scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. We`re very excited because we`ve got a very special guests in the show today. It`s the former First Lady. What`s her name guys?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 . She`s going to be here in the studio. She has been touring the country. She`s been spreading the word about her new best- selling book. It`s called "The Light We Carry: Overcoming in Uncertain Times."\par \par \uc2 And boy, don`t we have some uncertain times right now. I`ll go first. Yes, we do. So, we`ve got plenty to talk to her about today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And her light shines bright.\par \par \uc2 Speaking of women shining a bright light, plus only on CBS MORNINGS, we have a big announcement from Beyonce`s charity foundation, BeyGOOD. We can`t say what it is yet, but it is something that will likely make a big difference for a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But we are going to begin with the news this morning and that is the latest outbreak in a deadly 2023 tornado season.\par \par \uc2 A wave of severe storms hammered Oklahoma, Kansas, and Iowa last night and people in Central Oklahoma reported several tornadoes leading to severe damage. You see it on your screen now. One of them is blamed for killing two people in Cole, Oklahoma, a little town 25 miles south of Oklahoma City.\par \par \uc2 As Janet Shamlian shows us, survivors say the power of that twister in particular was terrifying.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DENNIS NEWBERG (Tornado survivor): It looked like a bomb went off.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE NEWBERG (Tornado survivor): It was a devastation.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Dennis and Michelle Newberg (sp?), in their words say their home was wiped off the face of the map, escaping just five minutes before a tornado destroyed their house in Cole, Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 The tornado that plowed through the community with a population of about 600 decimated houses and property and claimed at least two lives.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GIBBS: It is reasonable to expect possibly more based on the damage that we`ve seen thus far.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The tornado warning started early Wednesday evening.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is definitely violent, and it is getting bigger.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: First, large hailstones pelted the area just outside Oklahoma City, previewing what was to come.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is on the ground.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: About 60 miles east of Cole, another tornado touched down in Shawnee. Our affiliate station, KWTV in Oklahoma City caught it live on air just as the town`s power went out.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, look at that.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: That storm took aim at Oklahoma Baptist University, shredding walls of buildings and leaving a trail of debris on campus. No injuries are reported.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My immediate reaction got me like "holy crap," you know because like there was a huge tree overturned, like there was glass that was like stained glass, beautiful, it was shattered.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Janet Shamlian.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And in Washington, the Supreme Court is taking more time to decide if the abortion pill, mifepristone should remain available nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The White House is appealing Lower Court rulings including one from a Judge who said the drug had been wrongly approved.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 You know, we had expected it now, this morning whether the Court was going to kind of indefinitely block those Lower Court rulings, but without any explanation, the Justices have pushed that deadline back until tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Just hours before Wednesday`s deadline, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  announced an extension, which keeps the abortion pill, mifepristone available nationwide for at least two more days.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration and the drug manufacturer have asked the Justices to step in or block Lower Court rulings that would ban or greatly restrict the pill, which is used and more than half of abortions in the US.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: All of this is unusual, from top to bottom.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: CBS News legal contributor, Jessica Levinson says it is uncommon for the Justices to push their own self-imposed deadline.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: We don`t know if they are so close to being able to have a unanimous agreement and they want to be able to get there or if there`s just such a huge breakdown that there are really aggressive majority opinion and a really aggressive dissent.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: While the Court is not currently considering the merits of the case, it will determine if women continue to have access to the drug while cases work their way through the Courts.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  says recent rulings could undermine \uc2 FDA\uc2  approvals of other drugs.\par \par \uc2 DR. JACK RESNECK, JR. (\uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  President): We just think it`s incredibly reckless to upend our entire drug approval system in this country and create chaos where we`re going to have individual Courts and States around the country, basically undoing those decisions.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Some Republicans in Congress say that concern is overblown.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY (R-LA): Now, this isn`t related to regulation of drugs. This is related to a Federal bureaucracy following the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But the White House says it is ready for a battle in the Courts.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE: We are prepared for any outcome the Supreme Court may issue and we are prepared for a long legal fight, if necessary.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And it probably will be a long legal fight because even if the Justices put these rulings on hold, the case is going to kind of continue to work its way through the Lower Courts, and there are other challenges in other States.\par \par \uc2 So either way, the issue of abortion is going to end up right back here before these Justices -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jan, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Police in Akron, Ohio broke up a street protest over a grand jury`s decision not to charge eight officers who shot and killed Jayland Walker. Police said they fired pepper spray and chemical irritants after protesters threw rocks and bottles last night.\par \par \uc2 Officials say at least 100 people blocked traffic with their vehicles for as long as 15 minutes. No arrests were made.\par \par \uc2 Walker was shot 46 times after a car and foot chase last year. Officers say he fired at them first from his vehicle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And in Kansas City, the man accused of shooting a teenager who walked into his house has pleaded not guilty to two felony charges. Eighty- four-year-old Andrew Lester appeared in Court yesterday for the first time since the shooting. Sixteen-year-old Ralph Yarl was shot last week.\par \par \uc2 Lester told police, he feared a break-in and was scared to death when the teenager mistakenly came to his door and rang the doorbell. Lester will remain free on bond. The next Court date is set for the beginning of June.\par \par \uc2 The Yarl family shared a new photo of Ralph who is a very talented musician playing his cousin`s toy guitar as he recovers at home. We`re told he recently accepted an invitation to perform at the White House when he is feeling better.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That incident in Kansas City, of course, is just one of multiple shootings that have shocked the country in recent days. Three people including two teenagers have now been charged in last weekend`s mass shooting in Alabama; and in Texas, a man fired at a group of cheerleaders after one of them started to open his car door by mistake.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann has the latest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In Texas, 18-year-old Payton Washington remains in the ICU after being shot early Tuesday. Her teammates say she was with a group of cheerleaders when one of them mistakenly tried to get into the wrong car in a grocery store parking lot.\par \par \uc2 Authorities said the person in that car, Pedro Tello Rodriguez Jr. opened fire striking Washington and another teammate.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: She is a mentor and a role model to so many kids in this industry. She`s an amazing athlete, amazing kid.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In Upstate New York, a Judge denied 65-year-old Kevin Monahan`s request to be released on bail in the shooting death of 20-year- old, Kaylin Gillis.\par \par \uc2 Prosecutors say Monahan opened fire after Gillis and a group of friends accidentally drove up Monahan`s driveway, Saturday night. Gillis was struck as they were turning to leave.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GILLIS (Kaylin Gillis` father): My daughter was an honor student. She had hopes and dreams of becoming -- she had hopes and dreams of becoming a marine biologist, and this man took that away from us.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And in Dadeville, Alabama, brothers Travis and Ty Reik McCullough, 16 and 17 years old, along with 20-year-old Wilson LaMar Hill, Jr. have been charged with four counts of reckless murder following Saturday`s shooting that killed four people and injured 32 others at what was supposed to be Alexis Dowdell`s sweet 16 birthday party.\par \par \uc2 MIKE SEGREST (District attorney): There is an uncut cake and unburned 16 candles that never got lit. Alexis` brother was one of the victims on her 16th birthday party. She`d been helped by her brother as he took his last breath.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS DOWDELL (Brother killed in shooting): I`m glad that they found them.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Dowdell told us of her family`s grief over her brother and relief his alleged killers are behind bars.\par \par \uc2 Do you know these guys?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS DOWDELL: I don`t have no clue who they are at all.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Never heard of them before?\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS DOWDELL: No.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: They weren`t invited.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS DOWDELL: No.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Police haven`t commented on the motive behind the killings, or whether there`s a relationship between the suspects and the people they allegedly shot.\par \par \uc2 ALEXIS DOWDELL: Images still seem unreal for any of these. So I`m just still trying to process it, but I just take it day by day, because I know it`s going to be really hard.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: For CBS MORNINGS, Mark Strassmann, Dadeville, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Of course, gun laws are different in every State, but with all of these shootings all across the country and the different circumstances of each, we did want to take a step back and assess some of the numbers.\par \par \uc2 So listen to this, there are nearly 434 million firearms in civilian possession nationwide, these are your friends and neighbors, your fellow Americans, that`s according to a Firearm Industry Trade Association. What it means is that there are roughly a hundred million more guns in the country than there are people in the country.\par \par \uc2 And as far as the three States that Mark Strassmann just mentioned, Alabama and Missouri well, they have the fourth and ninth highest firearm mortality rates in the country respectively; and Texas, it has the heaviest burden of gun deaths in this country, deaths by gun violence, 4,600 a year, and that is only good enough in that State for 27th as a mortality rate middle of the pack. Think about that.\par \par \uc2 In all three of those States, no permit is required to purchase a rifle, to purchase a shotgun, or to purchase a handgun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to a major development in the Federal investigation into Hunter Biden`s tax returns. An attorney for an \uc2 IRS\uc2  supervisor is sending a letter to lawmakers today asking for whistleblower protection for his client. He is alleging that investigation is being improperly influenced by "preferential treatment and politics."\par \par \uc2 Our chief investigative correspondent, \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  is here with more.\par \par \uc2 Jim, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 We are learning more about this potential whistleblower described by his attorney as a nonpartisan career civil servant who is looking for protections to share what he has experienced.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE: My client wants to come forward to Congress, he`s ready to be questioned about what he knows and what he experienced under the proper legal protections.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Attorney, Mark Lytle`s client is a Supervisory Special Agent at the \uc2 IRS\uc2  who is prepared to tell Congress the investigation he`s been working on has been hampered by what he thinks is special treatment.\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE: Typical steps that a law enforcement investigator would take were compromised because of political considerations.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Lytle wouldn`t talk in specifics declining to identify either his client or the target of the investigation his client helped conduct.\par \par \uc2 Can you identify him?\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE: I can`t at this stage, Jim.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : But CBS News has learned the investigation the whistleblower worked on is about Hunter Biden.\par \par \uc2 HUNTER BIDEN (President Joe Biden`s son): What we`re doing is, is being completely cooperative.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: That was Biden two years ago, after the DOJ opened an investigation into his finances.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  collected what it believed was sufficient evidence to charge Biden with tax crimes, and last year sent its findings to the US Attorney in Delaware. Since then, silence.\par \par \uc2 Why can`t your client talk to us directly at this point?\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE: There are laws that provide protection to whistleblowers, and he has to navigate that.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Yesterday, Lytle sent this letter to Congress claiming his client could provide information that would contradict sworn testimony by a senior political appointee.\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (US Attorney General): I promise to ensure that he is able to carry out his investigation.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: CBS News is learned, that was Attorney General, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , who gave testimony about the Trump-appointed US Attorney in Delaware conducting the Hunter Biden investigation.\par \par \uc2 Lytle says neither he nor his client are motivated by politics.\par \par \uc2 Would you find any evidence or allegation of a political agenda?\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE: No. To him, the truth is one truth and he wants to come forward with it.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : Lytle told us, the whistleblower has been with the \uc2 IRS\uc2  for more than a decade, and has extensive documentation to support his allegations.\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE: The things he`s been through are very well-documented in e- mails and other communications with the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: We asked Lytle if there is a chance his client can only see his slice of the investigation and not the big picture. Lytle said that`s possible, but that his client had expressed his concerns to superiors a number of times before seeking whistleblower status.\par \par \uc2 Neither the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , Department of Justice, nor a lawyer for Hunter Biden would comment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To be continued for sure. Thank you, Jim.\par \par \uc2 Turning to the war in Ukraine, the country`s Armed Forces are getting more weapons from the West as they prepare for a counteroffensive that`s expected this spring.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is in Ukraine getting a rare look at the Armed Forces that are getting ready to take on the Russian troops.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, Ukraine says it has taken delivery of a long-awaited Patriot Air Defense System. That`s US technology that can shoot down aircraft and missiles, but we`ve been meeting with some Ukrainian soldiers that are still hoping for new American weapons.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Through dense forest in Eastern Ukraine, down muddy tracks, we were taken to see something hidden amongst the trees, an entire battalion of Ukraine`s Third Tank Brigade, known as the Iron Brigade.\par \par \uc2 Their machines are well-camouflaged, so they can`t be spotted by Russian drones. This one is a 1980s-era tank. It is pretty old technology.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: They are not state-of-the-art. These are all Soviet-made tanks more than three decades old, but they allow the Ukrainians to penetrate deep into territory occupied by Russia.\par \par \uc2 You can hit something 12 kilometers, that`s eight miles away.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Volodymyr (sp?) is a Tank Commander who told us he used to serve in the Soviet Army alongside Russian comrades back when Ukraine was part of the USSR. Now the Russians are his enemy.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s new friends, the US and its European allies are sending more capable modern tanks, including 31 Abrams from America expected later this year.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s government calls tanks the punching fist of democracy.\par \par \uc2 For Volodymyr, the Abrams can`t come soon enough.\par \par \uc2 (VOLODYMYR speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "My parents lived here. My children live here. My grandchildren live here," Volodymyr told us, "This is our land."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: The soldiers manning those tanks asked us to pass on their thanks to the American people for their support, and that is something that we`ve heard over and over again here in Ukraine -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And now the American people are hearing it as well.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams, for us in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 You`re going to want to stay with us because ahead, an extraordinary story developing in the West where there is a growing water crisis. As you may know, in the middle of Arizona in the desert there you can find a huge field of green however all containing a water hungry crop that`s being shipped back to the Middle East. Arizona`s Attorney General is furious.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: So cows in Saudi Arabia are essentially drinking Arizona water.\par \par \uc2 KRIS MAYES (Arizona Attorney General): Correct. There`s nothing to say except that`s insane.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: So, if this is so obviously insane. How did this happen?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Insanity ahead. That`s a Ben Tracy investigation you do not want to miss, but first it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up: Former First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  will join us in the studio. She`s here talking about her new book and podcast, and give us an exclusive look at her new Netflix special with Oprah.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS where we are counting down to Earth Day. It`s going to be Saturday, by the way, and we`ve got our series, "Protecting the Planet."\par \par \uc2 We are investigating a growing controversy in Western Arizona that could lead to a reckoning over scarce water supplies. You may be surprised to learn that farms in that desert region are growing one of the most water intensive crops there is, alfalfa, and they`re doing it in an area where there is a shortage of available water.\par \par \uc2 Some farms are foreign owned and they are shipping the crop overseas to Saudi Arabia. Amid a backlash, the State Legislature is now considering a ban on most foreign-owned farms.\par \par \uc2 Senior national and environmental correspondent, Ben Tracy has our story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Just outside of a Western Arizona town called Hope.\par \par \uc2 BRAD MEAD (Cattle rancher): And as you can see, there`s no water.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Cattle rancher, Brad Mead (sp?) is finding it hard not to lose his.\par \par \uc2 This was your well.\par \par \uc2 BRAD MEAD: This was my well.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And now you`ve got nothing.\par \par \uc2 BRAD MEAD: Nothing but dust, and you can toss a rock in it, it`s gone.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: He then took us here.\par \par \uc2 This is the edge of your land here --\par \par \uc2 BRAD MEAD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: To show us his neighbor`s land. He claims they`ve used so much water that his well went dry.\par \par \uc2 BRAD MEAD: And that`s all \uc2 Fondomonte\uc2 `s --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: All of that green over there.\par \par \uc2 BRAD MEAD: All of that green --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: That green is alfalfa, one of the most water intensive crops. It`s used as cattle feed and being grown on these massive farms in the Arizona desert by \uc2 Fondomonte\uc2 , which is owned by one of the largest dairy companies in Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 So when you look out there and you see all that green, what do you think?\par \par \uc2 BRAD MEAD: I see money leaving America. I see water getting depleted.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY IRWIN (La Paz County Board of Supervisors): It makes me very angry because --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: La Paz County Supervisor, Holly Irwin has been sounding the alarm about these foreign-owned farms since they began operating here in 2015.\par \par \uc2 Why would a company from Saudi Arabia be growing alfalfa in the Arizona desert?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY IRWIN: Because they`ve depleted their natural resource over there. So now they`re here.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: It`s illegal to grow alfalfa in Saudi Arabia because it takes too much water, so \uc2 Fondomonte\uc2  bought vast tracts of desert in Western Arizona on top of a massive groundwater aquifer, in part because there are no regulations on how much water can be pumped out of the ground.\par \par \uc2 So anybody can come out here and buy or lease land.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY IRWIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And if you can put a well in and you can find water, you can take it.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY IRWIN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: As climate change fuels devastating droughts, Arizona and its rapidly growing cities are facing drastic cuts to their surface water supply from the critically low Colorado River system.\par \par \uc2 KRIS MAYES: We cannot afford to give our water away, frankly, to anyone, let alone the Saudis for free.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Kris Mayes is Arizona`s newly elected Attorney General. As we talked to her at one of \uc2 Fondomonte\uc2 `s farms, we saw a series of \uc2 Fondomonte\uc2  trucks hauling dried alfalfa off the property and down this dirt road. They ship it back to the Middle East to feed cattle.\par \par \uc2 So cows in Saudi Arabia are essentially drinking Arizona water.\par \par \uc2 KRIS MAYES: Correct. These pumps are pumping water out of the ground that belongs to the State of Arizona and essentially, it`s being exported to Saudi Arabia.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: \uc2 Fondomonte\uc2  declined our request for an interview or statement, but what it`s doing here is not illegal. In fact, the State of Arizona itself is renting this particular piece of land to \uc2 Fondomonte\uc2  for just $25.00 an acre. It can then pump unlimited amounts of groundwater for essentially no cost.\par \par \uc2 KRIS MAYES: There is nothing to say except that`s insane.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: So if this is so obviously insane, how did this happen?\par \par \uc2 KRIS MAYES: It`s a great question.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: CBS News obtained 