st people in our greenroom. Look who`s here. What your name is?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA (Former First Lady of the United States): \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 . She is here. We are so glad to have you here.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time you`ve been in our Times Square studio. Let me tell you, Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: It is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mrs. Obama, you have put people in a tizzy. Yesterday, for the first time, a technician came to me and said, how does Mrs. Obama like her room temperature?\par \par \uc2 Honest to God. Does she like it hot?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: What? They don`t normally ask that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Does she like it cold? Never.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: They don`t care about your room temperature?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I said -- no. We`re like human popsicles.\par \par \uc2 How does she like her room temperature? I go, I guess, she likes comfortable. So was it comfortable?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: It was very comfortable. To the technician guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then Katie -- come here, Katie. Come here, Katie.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Katie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Katie, her mom said what are you wearing? And she said I`m wearing jeans.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Very cute.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Her mother said, that`s disrespectful to the First Lady.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Oh no. No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell Katie`s mom, is this okay?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: We are in -- it`s okay. We`re in jeans era. For a former jeanser.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are so excited that \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  is here.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Katie`s mom. It`s fine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Talking about her new documentary. Thank you, Katie. Mrs. Smith, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  says your daughter looks good.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  will be here today. She`ll be sitting right there. In fact, we couldn`t be more excited. You should see Klaus` face on Steadicam. The former First Lady is joining us to talk about her book and her podcast.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And I`m Tony Dokoupil. We`re going to introduce you to a 91-year-old who has been teaching at the same school since before The Beatles were a band. He has inspired multiple generations and he has no plans to retire.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Plus, we`ve got a big announcement only on CBS MORNINGS from BeyGOOD, that`s Beyonce charity group and they`re doing a lot of good. Oh, hey.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Oh, hi.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, hi.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, like you didn`t see her standing there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I did not, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Surprise.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Surprise. This is a great surprise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This has never happened before, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , that we`ve had somebody join us in the "Eye Opener," but first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight o`clock."\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is definitely violent, and it is getting bigger.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Deadly tornadoes tear through parts of Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: All of this is unusual, from top to bottom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Supreme Court delays its decision on access to a commonly used abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 CBS News learns an \uc2 IRS\uc2  employee has requested whistleblower protections to talk about the Hunter Biden investigation.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Why can`t your client talk to us directly at this point?\par \par \uc2 MARK LYTLE: There are laws that provide protection for whistleblowers and he has to navigate that.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: I do struggle sometimes with the word "moist."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And John Legend tries to make an unpleasant sounding word more appealing with a new song.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEGEND (Singer-songwriter): Moist, like the morning dew. Let me put some moisture on you. Moist, like the summer rain. Moist like a fresh coat of paint. Moist because I love you, baby. Let me put some moisture on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I happen to like the word "moist."\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin this hour with former First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  who is here in our studio live and in color. She is, listen to these, guys, the number one best-selling author of "Becoming," and her more recent book, it`s called "The Light We Carry: Overcoming in Uncertain Times." By the way that was number one, too.\par \par \uc2 She travelled the country. She talked to big names about her recent book, including there was Tyler Perry, Ellen DeGeneres, and Oprah. There`s only one.\par \par \uc2 You can hear those conversations on "\uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 : The Light Podcast."\par \par \uc2 The Former First lady also recorded a Netflix Special with Oprah during her final stop. Here is a little sneak peek.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: So, we`re coming to stay with you, graciously hosting us, and you said bring your friends. And I was like, okay. And she`s like, how many people? I said, well, it`s like 12.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (Talk show host): Twelve.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And you`re like, what did you say? You were like, you don`t have 12 good friends.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I did.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: I was like, I do. I do. I am not just --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY\uc2 : Who has 12 good friends?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: I am not going to set the date. I do.\par \par \uc2 And Oprah was like, I`ve got Gayle. And when I said was like --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Because like, the only person I know with 12 good friends was Jesus and the disciples.\par \par \uc2 He is the only person I know.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: But then you meant --\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: And then one of them betrayed Him.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Okay. But then you met my kitchen table.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Yes, yes. That was fun.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And you saw that I collect and keep my friends throughout life.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , we welcome you to the studio. I mean, Oprah and I actually did have a conversation about this, because we always say we don`t have a lot of friends.\par \par \uc2 I know a lot of people, but when it comes to friends, and then we met your friends.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they really are your true blue friends.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Yes. Over the span of a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And it is just interesting because you and Oprah have been kind of in the same business, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: But I have been a lawyer, a Law School student and I almost take -- I have a friend from each era of my life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Each chapter of your life.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: You know, it`s like, I tell people, you don`t need a ton of friends.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree with that.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: You leave an experience with one good friend, you`re ahead of the game.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But your friends have gotten you through many things.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know you can trust them and when you`re at the level that you are, it is very, very difficult.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk about the difference between the two books because "Becoming" was a best-selling novel, over 12 million sold -- drop the microphone very much -- so when you started writing the second one, did you feel pressure that I have to top that or I want to make another bestseller?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You didn`t.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Actually, there was no commitment in my contract. I didn`t have to write the book. The book just sort of came over the course of the last few hard years of being in quarantine, dealing with COVID, all the political uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 You know, I had a lot of time on my hands to just sort of stew and to mull over questions that young people in particular would ask about just managing life in uncertain times.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I told you, I took up knitting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And a lot of the ideas came as I was learning to knit and thinking about how did I get here? What are the tools?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you took up knitting after learning it on \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , you said.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: I taught myself how to knit.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That surprised me, too.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But this is the thing about you. You have always been very candid, very authentic, very much uniquely yourself. And so many people, Mrs. Obama at your stature, when people say, how are you doing? Oh, fine, fine, fine. But you have been very open about your insecurities and your anxieties, even your frustrations.\par \par \uc2 Why did you choose to be so vulnerable? And do you regret being so vulnerable and open?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: No, no, I don`t. In fact, you know, it`s the only way I know how to be me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And when you have to operate at a high level, I think it`s easier just to be completely yourself then you don`t have to keep up with the lie or the facade that you`ve kept up.\par \par \uc2 But I also think it is a responsibility to people with a platform. You know, in particular, young people are watching us and a lot of times, they glamorize the lives of the famous, and they don`t understand that we feel anxiety and guilt and remorse and fear.\par \par \uc2 So when they experience those same natural emotions, they feel like something is wrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wrong. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Because we don`t tend to share the hard stuff. I don`t think it helps anyone to put on a brave face and to pretend to be something that you`re not, and I never want to be that for people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve never been that person.\par \par \uc2 So what have you learned about working through your own anxieties? You think it could help others.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: It is a practice. First of all, I`ve learned to embrace the fact that basic anxieties, I`m not talking about dealing with severe mental health issues.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. I am just saying --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: But basic anxiety is a part of life. You know, anxiety has gotten me where I am today, you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Not wanting to mess up at school, being anxious about doing what I`m supposed to do. You know, making sure I`m not living on the streets, so I want to be able to have a job -- that kind of anxiety, you know, propels me forward.\par \par \uc2 But I`ve also learned that sometimes, I`ve got to just step out of the noise and know that I have to turn off the things that cause me upset. So there are times, even in the White House where I just had to not hear anything, you know. I didn`t want to hear about the country through the voice of someone else.\par \par \uc2 A lot of times, I let my experiences with the American people shape my attitude, and nine times out of 10, when I base my feelings on my interactions with people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: I was always in a better place.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Because I say, it`s harder to hate up close.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I so believe that.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And people are a lot better one-on-one than we look, when we see ourselves on the news, or you know on TV.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. I know. I`ve heard you say that, you know, when you`re in the White House, did you feel tremendous pressure? Because all eyes were on you and I heard you say once, we just knew we could not screw up.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you had eight years where there were really relatively no scandals to speak of --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Compared to the --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Compared to what we`re dealing with even now.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, did you feel that type of pressure when you were there?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Absolutely. I talk about the pressures being the first, being an only. There is that added --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Being the other. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Being the other, knowing that your successes and failures define the rest of your community and others like you coming behind you. So yes, Barack knew that he couldn`t afford a scandal. I mean, he wore a tan suit and got criticized, did you know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you know, you were long shorts in a 110-degree weather at the Grand Canyon. People said, look at her.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And I knew. We debated. We had a long debate in the plane. I said, well, if I get off in a dress, I`ll look out of touch because we`re going on a hike.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: But then if I walk off in shorts, some fashion critic is going to say, that`s a no-no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: So there`s -- yes, we were always mindful of the example that we set, but guess what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: That`s what you`re supposed to be when you`re President of the United States and First Lady. You should be mindful of the messages, the things that you say, the example that you set, you know, that`s part of the job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You know, you`re also very candid about relationships and I think you speak for many women, when you so candidly said, I mean, people took off on this. You know, there were years where you just didn`t like, many spouses in this room will say, you know there`s some days, I don`t like him. I don`t like her.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: You know, yes, so what is the controversy? It`s like, why are you --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you said it out loud.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Like we like everybody every day, it is impossible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were there times that you think he didn`t like you? Because I know you said --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Of course not.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t like him for 10 years. What did he say? I was wondering what did he say when you said, you know, I didn`t like him for 10 years. I was mad in that picture.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Right. No, no. I share these things because marriage is hard.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And a lot of young people quit on marriage over things that are just a part of the commitment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: We`ve been married for 30 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: You know, if I fell out with him for 10 and we had great 20 years, I`d take those odds anytime, but I don`t -- we glamorize marriage and in this day and age, marriage is more about the dress, and the dresses.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And the proposal and the honeymoon and all the stuff around it, and young people aren`t ready for the real of marriage.\par \par \uc2 So I think once again, it`s incumbent upon us, people who have had successful marriages, to be really honest about the fact that making a commitment to be with someone means you compromise and compromise ain`t always fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: And don`t add kids into the mix, because they really mess up your life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, let`s talk about kids, because people think, God, I hope she writes a book on parenting, honestly, because you have raised two very confident, not arrogant girls.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: As have you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who are very poised. Thanks. Thanks. Knock on wood.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: I do that all the time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who are very poised. Who are really generous human beings.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you want -- when they could have come out of the White House and been very obnoxious, and they were not. That has to do with the way they are raised. What is it that you want your girls to know how you wanted them to move in the world? A lot of people take lessons from this.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Gratitude, because they are way more blessed than we ever were.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: They had way more and learning to parent with more was the thing I didn`t know. I want them to have compassion and empathy. I want them to be independent. I want them to be able to stand on their own two feet and not depend on us.\par \par \uc2 And the very important thing, I want them to know that their happiness is up to them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: That the world doesn`t owe them anything and neither does anybody else, and control happens when you know how to make yourself happy, where you know how to create your own joy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: So those are some of the things and what comes with that are boundaries, expectations, giving them responsibilities, handing them their lives early, you know, not being afraid of allowing them to fail, standing back and letting them sit in their failure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And also, it`s okay for them to make mistakes.\par \par \uc2 We have bills to pay around here, Mrs. Obama, so we`re going to have to take a break.\par \par \uc2 Please don`t run out like your hair`s on fire.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to continue the conversation, but first time, to check your local weather. It`s 8:12. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back with more from former First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , so stick around.\par \par \uc2 And still ahead, meet a 91-year-old teacher who hits the treadmill to stay strong and keep up with the students. While he says he has no plans to retire. Age ain`t nothing but a number.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to continue our conversation with the former First Lady. Her name, of course is \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I have to say something about the book because it`s called "The Light We Carry: Overcoming in Uncertain Times."\par \par \uc2 I really think that these are uncertain times and I look at the things in the news recently, the three recent shootings, the terrible shooting in Kansas City with the 16-year-old, thank God, he survived.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Yes, .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A young woman in New York was shot turning in the driveway -- just normal things.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that we can ever figure out a way to reconcile gun violence in this country? Because it does keep me up.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: I hope and pray that that at some point, enough becomes enough.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. What is that point?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: You know, we wondered that throughout our entire presidency, thinking that this time -- you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Barack Obama said Newtown was one of the worst days of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we still didn`t get that message pushed forward.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: But we are the only developed country on the planet where its citizens can have unfettered access to firearms. That is not a good thing, and more of us have to feel strongly about it, and particularly our young people.\par \par \uc2 This is where democracy comes in. Voting this -- all of this stuff is decided.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It matters.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: . in the -- in the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then you`ve got the Supreme Court when they overturn Roe v. Wade, you put a very poignant message about you were heartbroken.\par \par \uc2 What`s your reaction today as you see a battle over abortion access continue to play out? Uncertain times, scary times.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: This is -- this was to be predicted. I can`t tell you how many times we talked about the importance of the Supreme Court, the importance of voting, voting in every election.\par \par \uc2 So now we are here. I am not surprised by it. It was coming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Now, the problem is that our young people have to understand that just because an issue doesn`t affect you today, doesn`t mean it doesn`t -- it`s not going to matter in five or 10 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Voting matters. That`s all I can say.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I know and you`ve said it over and over.\par \par \uc2 I emceed this event at Lincoln Center Jazz at Lincoln Center for Wynton Marsalis and just mentioning your name that you were going be on and people were "Yes."\par \par \uc2 I mean, people have such expectations and hopes that will she? Will she? I`m not even going to ask you that. But do you ever wonder or do you ever think when will politics leave you?\par \par \uc2 Because you said I`m not a political person. Politics isn`t something I wanted, but people still hope and want you to do something.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Yes. I am authentic. If I were going to engage in politics, I would let people know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: But I am a public figure. I still care about these issues.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: I will always find ways to use my platform to speak out. That`s why I`m focusing on getting young people to vote.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Because none of it matters. It doesn`t matter who`s in the Oval Office if we can`t give them a Congress that they can work with, if we are not --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, listen --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: You know, managing our State Houses and on and on. I could go on and on about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, and I`m not even pushing for you running. That ship has sailed, I get it.\par \par \uc2 So what gives you joy these days, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Joy --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are you looking forward to?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: So much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: So much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In less than 30 seconds. What is it? Other than being with all of us today.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE OBAMA: Watching my girls become the women that they were meant to be, and watching young people evolve. I mean, that`s still the thing that gives me, not just joy, but hope is when I meet these young people who are really grappling with these issues in a major way. They want to have an impact. They are so full of promise.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I stay connected to young people because it`s the best way 