rants.\par \par \uc2 BeyGOOD will also host the luncheon for entrepreneurs with networking opportunities.\par \par \uc2 BeyGOOD Executive Director, Ivy McGregor has traveled around the world leading relief efforts and programs for the foundation, and she is with us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR (BeyGOOD executive director): Good morning. I`m fantastic. How are you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am fantastic as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is always good to come with good news.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: It is good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is great news.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: To come with great news. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So as we mentioned, the foundation will provide $1 million to support small businesses along Beyonce`s Renaissance World Tour, which I think is amazing.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She`ll be twice as busy, but it`s a great thing. Now explain how this program works and promotes economic equity?\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Absolutely. Well, first off, I just want to say thank you all. It`s such an honor to be with you all this morning. And listen, across every single one of Mrs. Carter`s tours, there`s always been community impact.\par \par \uc2 She has always -- I think about a time that we were in Philadelphia on tour, Mrs. Carter literally would not take stage because there was devastation. Rowhomes had caught fire, and she wanted to make sure that those families were taken care of.\par \par \uc2 So every single tour, there is always community impact. They are always programs that impact the community.\par \par \uc2 Gayle was with us when we were in South Africa in 2018. Again, Mrs. Carter, ahead of her performing, she said, I need to make sure that we are making the country better as a result of her being there, so we went --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she wanted to see it.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: She wanted to see it. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She wanted to see it, yes.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: So we were there. I think I was there a month ahead of her performance in 2018 at the Mandela 100 Global Citizens Festival. Until we partnered with local organizations on the ground, that`s kind of the model of BeyGOOD, right?\par \par \uc2 We don`t, in silos, develop programs, but we meet people and we hear them and we understand what their needs are, and then we develop programs and partnerships and friendships and long-term relationships.\par \par \uc2 So along the Renaissance World Tour, we will be doing two things. When she announced her tour, she said education is important and supporting small businesses is important.\par \par \uc2 And so along the tour, we`ll be making stops in multiple cities.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not just in the US.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Not just in the US.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: We actually start in Stockholm, Sweden and across the tour across Europe, we will be stopping in certain cities, and we`re hosting impact luncheons for small businesses, and then we`re also doing these incredible, absolutely incredible college rallies.\par \par \uc2 I`m telling you, you need to come to one of those.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll be there.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: You need to stop by.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how do small businesses apply? How does it work? So what constitutes a small business?\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Right. So one, we are intentional about supporting minority owned businesses. Right? And so the big announcement today, I think we need a drumroll --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, right. Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: We`ve got to drumroll. We`ve got to drumroll.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We always produce --\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Come on, we need a drumroll. Listen, the big announcement today is that in 2020, Mrs. Carter assisted over 900 small businesses. We know that was a time of uncertainty across the world, right?\par \par \uc2 And so she identified that small businesses are essential, right? These were businesses that intended to stay open, to keep the grocery stores open, to help those communities.\par \par \uc2 So she gave grants to over 900 small businesses of $10,000.00 and then elevated them socially. And the big announcement today is that the Black Parade route, Small Business Impact Grants are back, and people can go to BeyGOOD.org today and apply.\par \par \uc2 And if they`re their application is accepted they will attend one of the incredible luncheons. Our first one is in London on May 28th. It`s going to be incredible.\par \par \uc2 There are so many surprises. It`s more than just the cheque that people will receive.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Because we`ve heard from them, so we`ve listened. We`ve stayed in contact to understand what are your needs? Right? And we know that small businesses support. They are a backbone to our economy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Right? And so we want to make sure that they are aided and assisted as they are assisting their communities.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, Ivy, I love you calling her Mrs. Carter.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Because she is --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is her name.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is certainly her name.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when she said Mrs. Carter, I went okay, I like that. I like that, Ivy.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So will Mrs. Carter be -- is she ready for the tour? That`s what I want to know. I want to know about the tour and are you going to leave us your number?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let`s start with the music.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Are you going to leave us your number so we can reach out to you for tickets?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let the people know how they can get in touch with you.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Gayle, you cannot give my number out. You have my number. But I won`t tell --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how is that going? She is getting ready.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: She is getting ready and the tour is going to be amazing, right?\par \par \uc2 What I like to tell people, Mrs. Carter is so intentional that before she takes stage, she is sending in the good wave. So before she takes stage in every city, BeyGOOD will be in those cities during luncheons, working with students at colleges and universities.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Will she go to some of the luncheons? Will she surprise --\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: We can`t tell it all. We can`t --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle putting her on the spot like that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Okay.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: We can`t tell it all.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like Beyonce going to be there?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because I want to know which luncheon people need to go to.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: When we come to New York, you have to come to the luncheon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, no, no.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Okay?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I will definitely go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the concert?\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: but I will say something about tickets.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: So, something that we do about tickets is charity groups that we visit along the tour are often surprised with tickets as well are the college students, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: So the rallies are incredible, and they are so high energy and these kids dance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Okay, so I will be --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You loving this job, Ivy McGregor? You loving this job?\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: I love it. I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can tell.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what we need do, Nate, Vlad, we`ve got to pose as college kids.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To get those tickets.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: But here is the thing, you have to know how to dance.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hello, fellow youth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh. You`re doing that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is ready.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: You`re ready?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We knew those lessons would pay off.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: Turn up the music, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Paying dividends.\par \par \uc2 Ivy McGregor, thank you very much. You made my day. I appreciate you being there.\par \par \uc2 IVY MCGREGOR: There we go.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up: The high school Math teacher finishing his 65th year in the classroom and he is ready for more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She looked at you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am ready for more, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I only learned in the past year. But first, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The schools of this country are facing a massive wave of retirements with almost a quarter million teachers likely to leave the classroom for good when this school year is over, but there is one Math teacher in Virginia, Lou Kokonis, 91 years old and he is not among them.\par \par \uc2 In fact, retirement is not even on his radar.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane shows us how his dedication has inspired at this point really generations, plural, of students.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Lou Kokonis hits the treadmill most mornings to keep up his endurance and his strength for a career that must feel something like a marathon.\par \par \uc2 Now in his 90s, Kokonis is in his 63rd year teaching Math at Alexandria City High School in Northern Virginia.\par \par \uc2 LOU KOKONIS (91-year-old Math teacher): Divided by two --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And that adds up to thousands of students.\par \par \uc2 Sixty-four hundred students.\par \par \uc2 LOU KOKONIS: Some of them are doctors and lawyers.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: You teach the children of your former students. Do you teach the grandchildren of your former students?\par \par \uc2 LOU KOKONIS: I think I had one.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: He`s also had students like Mark Eisenhour become fellow teachers and then beat him to retirement. And he`d been here nearly 30 years. You graduated. You had a 30-year career.\par \par \uc2 You retired. He`s still here? How is that possible?\par \par \uc2 MARK EISENHOUR (Former Alexandria City High School teacher and administrator): Yes, I didn`t think it was possible. I didn`t think it was -- I thought he was close to retirement when I was a student here.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It`s only possible because Kokonis first entered these halls before The Beatles former.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Beatles.")\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Before space travel.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from NASA.)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And back when there were only 48 States.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from news about additional star on the flag.)\par \par \uc2 LOU KOKONIS: There is the denominator.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Calculus Kokonis teaches hasn`t changed.\par \par \uc2 LOU KOKONIS: How many got this one right?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But his Calculus had to, because Kokonis says today`s students hold new challenges right in their hands.\par \par \uc2 LOU KOKONIS: It`s not easy trying to keep them from using their cell phone. They`re addicted.\par \par \uc2 Okay, y`all want to look over your tests.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But it`s his old school approach of offering free tutoring on Saturdays and being the first to arrive each day which connects with his students.\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED ELTIREKE (Alexandria City High School student): To be working and teaching for this long, and to still like put in all this extra effort on Saturdays, I really admire that about him.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And while the pay is a lot better than the $4,800.00 a year Kokonis first made in 1959, it is still significantly lower than other jobs requiring college degrees.\par \par \uc2 One study showed teacher and principal stress is twice that of other professions.\par \par \uc2 Mentor some of the younger teachers. I mean, they must look to you for advice?\par \par \uc2 LOU KOKONIS: Well, I look to them for computer help.\par \par \uc2 Does anybody know what the answer is?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: With no wife or children, Kokonis devotes himself completely to his students.\par \par \uc2 LOU KOKONIS: If I had a wife, she`d probably leave me within a week. I mean, I`m sitting there working all night trying to grade papers and don`t have time to run around.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And he insists retirement is not one of the three R`s he will consider.\par \par \uc2 LOU KOKONIS: I don`t think that way. I just -- my mind has said that I`m going to do well this year and I`m going to enjoy and I`m going to try to come next year if they will take me.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Scott MacFarlane in Alexandria, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You go, Mr. Kokonis.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talk about dedication.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I like when he said I need them to help me with the computer, and he clearly still knows his stuff.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love watching him at the board and doing those complicated problems and I think that is part, I`ve got to believe, that is part of what keeps you young and active.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It keeps you going.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Your mind is going all the time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because I don`t understand anything back there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, my gosh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I see E equals MC two. That`s all I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: MC squared. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, MC two. Clearly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s call it E equals MC squared.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s all right, Vlad. You`re good on TV.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at that smile.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, up next, our \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: MC two.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle Bass is in our progressive greenroom. She will show us a water pitcher designed to filter water fast, and you can get it at an exclusive discount.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I like that.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is time now for CBS MORNINGS Deals where we show you products that might make your day a little better all at exclusive low, low prices.\par \par \uc2 To start shopping now, scan the QR code that you see on the screen or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 Lifestyle host, Gayle Bass joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, Gayle. What`s up?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS (Lifestyle host): Good morning, Vlad to Dad. It is so good to be here. This is something you really want to invest in, because --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, talking bread.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, you can`t afford to waste food.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: And \uc2 Tupperware\uc2 `s bread saver --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve got a baby at home, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: It`s perfect. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, there will be bread that`ll sit there that if you don`t use this, it might get a little moldy. This is designed to take away the condensation, minimize condensation and add ventilation.\par \par \uc2 You can see there are holes right there. You see in the video. There are holes right there that let the air just kind of surround the bread so it lasts longer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How long can you keep bread in the house?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You can keep bread as long as it lasts in your house, really.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: That`s the thing, because if you`ve got a lot of kids and they`re like getting sandwiches and making stuff on their own, you know, you put the bread in there, they take it in and they take it out.\par \par \uc2 If you`re not on -- if you don`t want to eat a lot of bread, but you`ve got this big loaf and it`s just for you like me, this is perfect, because it keeps the bread longer. It lasts longer for me because it`s just me eating the bread.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: So this is a way where you can`t afford to waste your bread.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No. So what is this going to run me?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes. You`re going to want to go to CBSDeals.com and get this right now, very popular because it is $20.99.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: They have it for 30 percent off. This is a big seller. People like it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I would imagine so.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk about water now. Need a little bread, need a little water.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes. Especially in Arizona where I live. You know this is -- Earth Day is coming up this weekend and this is a great way to purify your water.\par \par \uc2 And what is really cool is that this does not use plastic beads in the filter like other filters. This uses coconut shells.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now, that`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, this is the Hydros water pitcher, and we have it in the 40 ounce and the 60 ounce. What`s really great is that this filter is interchangeable with all -- with both of these. These can switch around.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, that`s interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Use it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s interesting as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And one of the things that`s annoying, I think for a lot of people with the other types of filters is you get that charcoal stain in your craft after a little bit of time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Not this one. This one is beautifully designed. I really love the glass that it`s made with and this is sustainable, and also what I like about it is that it takes about a minute to fill it up. You just put your water on a third of the rate, you fill it up and boom there you go, and you could put it in the refrigerator.\par \par \uc2 This fits in under the door on the fridge.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: So it fits anywhere you want, and here have a sample.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me try.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: You can have it. It is acceptable to drink. This one has my lipstick so, this this mine.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am a water sommelier.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: No tannins.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tastes very good. And how much is it going to cost?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes. We have it for 25 percent off. So we start at $26.25 for this. This is something you definitely want to have.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am in. I am in. All right, let`s talk about something that can help people from slipping and falling in the bathroom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, this is the Kahuna Grip and I love that they call it Kahuna Grip because it`s inspired by surfers. They are on the surfboard. They don`t want to slip and fall.\par \par \uc2 This is designed unlike the Bon Jovi album, this is --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Slippery when Wet."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: This is designed to be not -- this is designed to be not slippery when wet. So, it`s a peel and stick. You put it in the shower and it has multiple varieties. And so when you have somebody visiting who`s a little older or just not stable on their feet, this is something where you can think about the safety when you step on it.\par \par \uc2 And this happens to me. My boyfriend has got old tiles in the shower. Every day I`m thinking --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re slipping and sliding.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Is this going to be the day? Is this going to be the day where I was like, coming in with a brace on my foot or my knee.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You better be, help me, I`ve fallen. I can`t get up. We don`t want that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes. We have a great value on this. We have this for 28 percent off. So it starts at $15.99, and I love that it is peel and stick and like I love the bamboo style, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it.\par \par \uc2 All right, last but not least, this is a magic carpet. Does it fly?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Oh, wow. This is totally fly. And I love this for -- I love this for parents because this is eco-friendly and washable. So if baby is taking a nap, baby wakes up, baby spills Cheerios or whatever it is on the floor, milk or if you spill red wine on the white shag. This is washable.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: So you have that party and there is always somebody who`s going to spill the wine on the expensive carpet. This is what you want.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How much?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: This starts at $54.99. And look, we have a variety of colors to match any room in your house.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like it. I like it. Gayle Bass, always great to have you. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we partner with all of these companies to get these deals. To buy anything that you saw today, scan that QR code on your screen or head over to CBSDeals.com to start shopping now.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow. Hey, we`re all here.\par \par \uc2 Try some of that bread. Try some of that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bye. Bye-bye.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Rub-a-dub-dub.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: It is coconut.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 . Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it smells good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE BASS: Yes, it is very nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, that`s very nice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Gayle Bass.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, and \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you Mrs. Carter and Ivy McGregor.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bread will make you spread.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230420ej4k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Supreme Court Delays Its Decision On Access, Making The Medicine Available Nationwide For Now\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Jan Crawford \par \uc2 889 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, breaking news in the abortion pill showdown. The Supreme Court delays its decision on access, making the medicine available nationwide for now.\par \par \uc2 Here are tonight`s headlines.\par \par \uc2 The last-minute extension for the nation`s highest court to make a decision on the use of a common abortion drug, what could it mean?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: It`s another step in their concerted effort to deny women access to reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 MAN: In Upstate New York, 65-year-old Kevin Monahan is charged with second-degree murder in connection to the death of 20-year-old Kaylin Gillis.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW GILLIS (Father of Kaylin Gillis): Kaylin was an amazing young lady.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Two teen brothers and a 20-year-old face murder charges after the deadly massacre at a sweet 16 birthday party.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SGT. JEREMY BURKETT (Alabama Law Enforcement Agency): When you pull out a gun and you start shooting people, we are going to put you in jail.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: CBS News investigates our exclusive with the lawyer of the potential IRS whistle-blower, and new disclosures about the Hunter Biden probe.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: A judge has agreed to delay today`s detention hearing for the Massachusetts Air National Guardsman accused of leaking classified documents.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Twenty-one-year-old Jack Teixeira is being charged under the Espionage Act.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: UFO sighting, the new Pentagon video. What could the mysterious object to be?\par \par \uc2 And the baseball team bringing a special group of kids some Major League fun.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAMIEN LOPEZ (Teenager): It was really inspiring.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this busy Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, we want to begin right here in Washington, where the Supreme Court has given itself until the end of the week to decide whether the abortion pill that has been used by millions of American women should remain available nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  has pushed a decision until midnight on Friday. At issue, a lower court ruling that rolled back the Food and Drug Administration`s 23-year-old approval of Mifepristone. About half of all abortions in the United States are medication abortions. The drug is also commonly prescribed by doctors to treat miscarriages. It is approved for use through 10 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Friday`s decision will be the first major court action related to abortion since it overturned Roe v. Wade in June of last year.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Jan Crawford will start us off tonight from outside the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Jan.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 The justices were expected to announce by midnight tonight whether they were going to continue blocking these lower court rulings and keep this bill available nationwide. The court gave no more explanation for why it`s decided now to push that back until Friday.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD (voice-over): In two brief orders, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  extended the deadline, keeping the abortion pill mifepristone available nationwide at least two more days.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration and a drug manufacturer have asked the justices to step in and set aside or block lower court rulings that would ban or greatly restrict the pill. The case is an effort by a conservative legal group in Texas to limit abortion nationwide, arguing that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn`t properly approve mifepristone more than 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Federal Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk, a Trump appointee, sided with the challengers and revoked the FDA`s authorization. An appeals court said Kacsmaryk went too far, but it kept some restrictions in place, saying mifepristone could only be used for abortion up to seven weeks, despite \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of 10 weeks, and requiring three visits to a doctor.\par \par \uc2 The administration is arguing that one federal judge cannot set abortion policy nationwide. The \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  also is pointing to the bigger picture, calling the lower court rulings dangerous and saying they could undermine \uc2 FDA\uc2  approvals of many other drugs.\par \par \uc2 DR. JACK RESNECK JR. (President, \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 ): We just think it is incredibly reckless to upend our entire drug approval system in this country and create chaos, where we are going to have individual courts in states around the country basically undoing those decisions.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now, there are conservative legal principles at the heart of this case on standing that suggest the justices are going to continue to block these lower court rulings indefinitely, Norah.\par \par \uc2 The delay, we have no idea why. It could mean there is some dissent being written, or it also could mean they just simply wanted more time to go through the case -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: No doubt everyone watching very closely.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230425ej4j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Protests Over Shooting Of Kansas City Teen\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Adriana Diaz, Gayle King \par \uc2 1,139 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to the latest on a black teenager who was shot after walking up to the wrong house in Kansas City. Hundreds of people rallied yesterday to seek justice for 16-year-old Ralph Yarl. The man who shot him is scheduled to be arraigned on assault charges today.\par \par \uc2 Yarl is now recovering at home.\par \par \uc2 And this morning, we are hearing from a neighbor who helped him after he was wounded. Adriana Diaz has more from Kansas City.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Say his name. Ralph Yarl.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: At a rally in the heart of Kansas City, Tuesday evening, crowds said they`ve had enough.\par \par \uc2 ASTRID MOSS (Protester): I have two young sons, two black sons, and this personally hit home. What if that was my son that ring that doorbell?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Sixteen-year-old Ralph Yarl was shot in the head and then in the arm last Thursday, when his family says he knocked on the wrong door while trying to pick up his twin brothers.\par \par \uc2 Ralph went to an address on this road. This is Northeast 115th Street, when in fact his little brothers were just a block away. You can see that next green street sign, that`s northeast 115th Terrace, just about a hundred yards away.\par \par \uc2 Attorney Lee Merritt represents the family.\par \par \uc2 LEE MERRITT (Ralph Yarl`s family attorney): Our biggest concern is that this man never walks the street again. The charges that he has, if successfully convicted will lead to life imprisonment, and then and that`s enough for us.\par \par \uc2 ZAC DOVEL (Andrew Lester`s neighbor): There was blood from where he was at all the way up, all over the door.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Zac Dovel says Ralph came to his family`s front door for help after the shooting.\par \par \uc2 ZAC DOVEL: All I hear is a bunch of banging at our door. So, then we call 9-1-1. They`re like yes, there was a gunshot in the area, unidentified were like, just don`t answer the door.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The police said don`t answer the door.\par \par \uc2 ZAC DOVEL: The operator --\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The operator.\par \par \uc2 ZAC DOVEL: Told us not to answer.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But after seeing Ralph`s condition, Dovel and his mom went out to him.\par \par \uc2 ZAC DOVEL: No one knows he was hurt. He needs to get help. We needed to get him. He needed to be the priority right now.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Ralph`s mom, Cleo Nagbe told CBS MORNINGS that her son is recovering, but still processing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is Ralph able to communicate, Cleo, with you all with no problem? He can walk and talk and all that stuff?\par \par \uc2 CLEO NAGBE (Ralph Yarl`s mother): He is able to communicate mostly when he feels like it, but mostly he just sits there and stares and the buckets of tears just rolls down his eyes. You can see that he is just replaying the situation over and over again.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Ralph`s aunt is helping care for him.\par \par \uc2 What do you tell him to try to give him strength?\par \par \uc2 DR. FAITH SPOONMORE (Ralph Yarl`s aunt): Look at all of these people that are pouring love into you, buddy. Then he goes, why are they making such a big deal? Because that`s just who he is. He might try to be in the background and help everybody else.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: According to the criminal complaint, Lester told police that he was "scared to death." He says he thought someone was trying to break in and says he saw a black male approximately six feet tall trying to open the storm door. Ralph told police he didn`t try to open the door, and Gayle, Ralph`s aunt told us he is five eight and 140 pounds.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Adriana, I was wondering that exact question. How tall is he? I am so glad you answered that.\par \par \uc2 A lot of people pouring love into Ralph this morning. Thank you very much, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2  has until midnight tonight to step in and keep a commonly used abortion pill on the market. A Federal Judge in Texas ruled that the drug, mifepristone, should be suspended nationwide even though it has been available for decades.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford is at the Supreme Court with more on what`s at stake here.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 You know, this case is an effort by a conservative legal group in Texas to restrict access to abortion nationwide, even in States that would otherwise allow it. They are trying to outlaw that abortion pill saying that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn`t properly approve it more than 20 years ago.\par \par \uc2 Now, a Federal Judge who is a Trump appointee agreed he revoked that authorization that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  had handed down so the pill could no longer be used for abortions across the country.\par \par \uc2 A Federal Appeals Court said that Judge went too far, but it kept some limits in place. So now we have the Biden administration, a drug manufacturer here at the Supreme Court, asking the Justices to block those rulings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jan, what do you expect the Court to do now?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, Gayle, I think that the Justices are going to block those rulings. They are going to keep this pill available nationwide, and that`s because at the heart of this case, it is directly at odds with bedrock conservative principles, especially on the issue of standing and whether these challengers even have a right to be in court in the first place. It`s not clear.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you`ve got to show you`ve suffered some injury or you have a stake in the case to be able to sue and if the court lowers the bar here and just throws open the courthouse doors to anyone who is upset about any social issue, you`re going to see groups coming in and trying to get Judges to weigh in on all kinds of things and that is exactly the opposite of what these Justices have said in previous cases, that the role of the courts should be -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, people on both sides watching this. Thank you very much, Jan.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230425ej4j0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 SCOTUS To Rule On Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nancy Cordes, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 592 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Supreme Court has to make a big decision this week on the subject of abortion access. There were demonstrations over the weekend as the High Court weighs the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of mifepristone, one of two commonly used drugs in medical abortions.\par \par \uc2 A ruling in Texas suspending that approval is on hold, along with restrictions on the drug`s use.\par \par \uc2 So for now, it is still available nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is following this story for us from the White House. Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, think of this as the stay that was imposed by the Supreme Court on Friday as a bid to buy a little more time because it is going to expire just before midnight on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the Court is going to examine this ruling restricting the use of mifepristone and decide whether to put a longer term hold on the ruling.\par \par \uc2 All this comes as new polling suggests that most Americans think the abortion pill should remain available.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN chanting "They say no choice. We say prochoice.")\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Protesters hit the streets across the country this weekend over the future of the abortion drug, mifepristone as the Supreme Court weighs what to do about a Texas ruling that could take the pill off the market.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): When you attack the rights of women in America, you are attacking America.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Mifepristone is one of two medications used together to carry out more than half of all abortions in the US. A new CBS News poll found that 67 percent of adults in the US think the drug should continue to be available in States where abortion is legal. That includes nearly half of all Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has asked the Supreme Court to reverse the new restrictions, arguing they would have sweeping consequences for the pharmaceutical industry, women who need access to the drug and the FDA`s ability to implement its statutory authority.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  opposes abortion and once proposed a nationwide 15-week abortion ban, but even he seemed concerned about the Texas ruling.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I don`t like a District Court Judge issuing a national decree, even if I agree with the Judge.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In the meantime, some Democratic Governors are insisting their residents will have access to the abortion drug, no matter what.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2  (D-NM): We`re going to make sure that medication abortion is available in our State.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Overall, our polling shows that half of all adult women in the US say that access to reproductive healthcare is getting harder. In a statement, the drugmaker that produces mifepristone said it was gratified by the Supreme Court`s decision to put this Texas ruling on hold even for a few days and said the company will look forward to the Court`s ongoing consideration -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230418ej4h000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Deadly Sweet 16 Party Shooting; America`s Gun Crisis; FOX News Defamation Trial Delayed; SCOTUS to Rule on Abortion Rights; Teen Shot after Going to Wrong House; Violence Surges in Sudan; Deadly Missile Strikes Hit Ukraine; Boston Bombing: Ten Years Later; Taiwan Preparing for China Attack; "60 Minutes" Goes to Inside \uc2 Google\uc2 `s AI; Note to Self; Jordan Klepper Live; Shakespeare Gets a New Look; Questlove Returns to His Roots; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Omar Villafranca,  Scott MacFarlane, Nancy Cordes, Ramy Inocencio, Holly Williams, Lilia  Luciano, Nancy Chen; Elizabeth Palmer, Scott Pelley \par \uc2 13,447 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday morning, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is on assignment today, but we`re still here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A mass shooting at a sweet 16 birthday party in Alabama leaves four dead and dozens more injured as the national gun debate intensifies.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): Republicans can no longer be silent on this issue. We don`t say anything, we want to bury our heads in the sand and hope that it goes away.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The defamation trial against FOX News over false election claims delayed by a day as a last minute settlement in the works.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s been a very rough week for \uc2 FOX\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Japanese Prime Minister, Fumio Kishida, had to be evacuated after someone threw an apparent smoke bomb at an event in Western Japan.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ten years after the Boston Marathon bombing, thousands of runners from around the world will take part in the 2023 race today.\par \par \uc2 REP. KATHERINE CLARK (D-MA): Boston Strong, one Boston.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Violent clashes have resumed this morning in the African nation of Sudan following a \uc2 UN\uc2  backed ceasefire Sunday.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A North Carolina man coming face-to-face with a bear.\par \par \uc2 MAN: De-escalation.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, de-escalation.\par \par \uc2 MAN: How should I get out of this quickly?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Swing and a miss. Strike out number 10.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Yankees\uc2  and Twins from the Bronx, Gerrit Cole toss in a complete game shutout.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A brilliant performance on this Sunday afternoon in the Bronx.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Fox blocks into the three -- there is your answer/\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The \uc2 Sacramento Kings\uc2  put down the \uc2 Golden State Warriors\uc2  in Game One for their first playoff win since April 30, 2006.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And the \uc2 Sacramento Kings\uc2  prevail. They have vanquished the reigning champs. The first playoff win in almost 17 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was a good weekend for basketball.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it`s just getting started right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Here we go.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS today, and we`re going to begin with some terrible news. It`s a mass shooting in Alabama at a sweet 16th birthday party.\par \par \uc2 Four people were killed and 28 others hurt, most of them were teenagers. Now, this happened at a dance studio in the City of Dadeville, Alabama.\par \par \uc2 Witnesses report that there were multiple shooters, but so far, police have made no arrests in this case. There have already been 31 mass shootings just this month in America, and today is only the 17th day of April.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca is in Dadeville, Alabama with more on the story. Omar you certainly don`t think of a sweet 16 party as a deadly place, but that is the world we live in today, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 When people say it can happen anywhere, this is what they mean.\par \par \uc2 Dadeville is small town USA, population just over 3,000. The venue where it happened, right behind me, right on the Old Main Street. That dance studio used to be the old bank.\par \par \uc2 And for teenagers, this was probably the place to be this weekend. A birthday party. Everybody was probably talking about it, a sweet 16, and then on Saturday, the unthinkable happened again.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: After Saturday night`s deadly shooting at Mahogany Masterpiece Dance Center, ambulances, police cars, and parents descended on the local hospital.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JIMMY "FRANK" GOODMAN, SR. (Dadeville, Alabama): There were people everywhere running and yelling and crying and going on.\par \par \uc2 TANAYA COX (Shooting survivor): I got shot right here. Right here.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Eighteen-year-old, Tanaya Cox (sp?) was shot three times. She says she saw multiple gunmen inside the dark club.\par \par \uc2 TANAYA COX: I just ducked in the bathroom and that`s where I ran.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Police have not released any details about the suspect or suspects and would not say if anyone is in custody for the attack.\par \par \uc2 BEN HAYS (Pastor): We will never be the same.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Pastor Ben Hays (sp?) led a vigil for the victims Sunday night.\par \par \uc2 PASTOR BEN HAYS: Most of the kids there were from Dadeville. This impacted us a lot.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: He says, one of the deceased is senior star athlete, Phil Dowdell. It was his sister`s birthday party. Their mother was also shot.\par \par \uc2 Dowdell was just weeks from graduation, and recently committed to play football at Jacksonville State next year.\par \par \uc2 DEQUAN DAS (Phil Dowdell`s friend): He was a good person.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: DeQuan Das (sp?) says he was with Dowdell at the party, but the two ran in opposite directions when the shooting started.\par \par \uc2 DEQUAN DAS: I was standing right beside him. And when the shooting went on, I think he went right into it, I went the opposite way. I wish I could have pulled him out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Police have been very tight-lipped about this investigation. There is a lot we do not know. We don`t know how many suspects they`re looking for, or how many suspects are looking for if it`s one, two, based on what witnesses are saying, we don`t know what kind of gun was used.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know the motive, but we`re hoping to learn more at a press conference later on this morning -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. We know the result, and that`s knowing a lot, Omar, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right, for folks at home, if it seems like you`re thinking you can`t get through a day without one of us here reporting on yet another mass shooting, well, you are on to something because that is the reality here in this country.\par \par \uc2 There have been 162 mass shootings in the US so far this year alone, according to the Gun Violence Archive, and our kids are noticing. How could they not?\par \par \uc2 According to the latest CBS News poll, gun violence is a top worry among kids. Six in 10 in fact, say it is a major concern, something on their minds, and it is on the very same list as you find more common teenage concerns like bullying and academic performance.\par \par \uc2 Now how to deal with it? Well, more than 60 percent of Americans are in favor of an outright ban on AR-15 semi-automatic rifles. That`s the type of gun used in school shootings in Nashville and Uvalde and so many other places.\par \par \uc2 The ideological differences, however, remain stark. Most self-identified liberals, for example, think the US would be safer if fewer to even no people own guns, while four in 10 conservatives think the US would be safer if more people, even everyone had guns.\par \par \uc2 And so there we are. Overall, Americans have grown increasingly frustrated over a partisan gun control debate, and many believe that neither political party is doing enough.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Today`s long awaited trial in a $1.6 billion lawsuit against \uc2 FOX Corporation\uc2  and FOX News Channel has been delayed by one day. The Judge`s decision came hours before opening statements in the case brought by \uc2 Dominion Voting Systems\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is at the Courthouse in Wilmington, Delaware.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Vlad, this really cracks the door open for a last minute settlement of his case to help \uc2 FOX\uc2  avoid a trial and avert a possible spectacle.\par \par \uc2 Top \uc2 FOX\uc2  personalities including \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2  and \uc2 FOX\uc2  Chair, \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2 , could testify by midweek this week if the trial proceeds. Dominion has argued \uc2 FOX\uc2  defamed their voting machine company when the hosts and the guests knowingly broadcast bogus claims Dominion was part of a scheme to flip votes and flip the 2020 election against former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 They have already revealed internal messages from \uc2 FOX\uc2  hosts and executives acknowledging the claims are bogus, but some were more concerned about possible ratings collapse.\par \par \uc2 FOX\uc2  has claimed Dominion`s defamation suit is a political crusade and a threat to the First Amendment. In the Court filings, \uc2 FOX\uc2  and Dominion are hundreds of millions of dollars apart on just determining the value of \uc2 Dominion Voting Systems\uc2  and the losses incurred.\par \par \uc2 Jury selection was supposed to begin here this morning followed by the opening arguments pushed back a day, but Gayle, legal analysts emphasize this case could still settle after the trial begins here in Wilmington.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of people watching this, mostly the media. We are all watching this case, big time. Thank you very much, Scott.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has to make a big decision this week on the subject of abortion access. There were demonstrations over the weekend as the High Court weighs the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of mifepristone, one of two commonly used drugs in medical abortions.\par \par \uc2 A ruling in Texas suspending that approval is on hold, along with restrictions on the drug`s use.\par \par \uc2 So for now, it is still available nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is following this story for us from the White House. Nancy, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Well, think of this as the stay that was imposed by the Supreme Court on Friday as a bid to buy a little more time because it is going to expire just before midnight on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 In the meantime, the Court is going to examine this ruling restricting the use of mifepristone and decide whether to put a longer term hold on the ruling.\par \par \uc2 All this comes as new polling suggests that most Americans think the abortion pill should remain available.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN chanting "They say no choice. We say prochoice.")\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Protesters hit the streets across the country this weekend over the future of the abortion drug, mifepristone as the Supreme Court weighs what to do about a Texas ruling that could take the pill off the market.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): When you attack the rights of women in America, you are attacking America.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Mifepristone is one of two medications used together to carry out more than half of all abortions in the US. A new CBS News poll found that 67 percent of adults in the US think the drug should continue to be available in States where abortion is legal. That includes nearly half of all Republicans.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration has asked the Supreme Court to reverse the new restrictions, arguing they would have sweeping consequences for the pharmaceutical industry, women who need access to the drug and the FDA`s ability to implement its statutory authority.\par \par \uc2 South Carolina Republican Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  opposes abortion and once proposed a nationwide 15-week abortion ban, but even he seemed concerned about the Texas ruling.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): I don`t like a District Court Judge issuing a national decree, even if I agree with the Judge.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: In the meantime, some Democratic Governors are insisting their residents will have access to the abortion drug, no matter what.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2  (D-NM): We`re going to make sure that medication abortion is available in our State.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Overall, our polling shows that half of all adult women in the US say that access to reproductive healthcare is getting harder. In a statement, the drugmaker that produces mifepristone said it was gratified by the Supreme Court`s decision to put this Texas ruling on hold even for a few days and said the company will look forward to the Court`s ongoing consideration -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We also want to tell you this morning about a Kansas City teenager fighting for his life after being shot for knocking on the wrong door.\par \par \uc2 The family of 16-year-old Ralph Yarl says he was trying to pick up his younger brothers when he made a mistake, he mistook one address for another and someone shot.\par \par \uc2 Police say the person who shot this teen has not been charged, at least not so far.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano has more on this story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And you should never, ever have to worry that your life will be taken.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: One block difference is what the family of 16-year-old Ralph Yarl say landed him in the hospital Thursday with what`s described as life-threatening injuries.\par \par \uc2 Yarl was supposed to pick up his younger brothers from a friend`s house on 115 Terrace in Kansas City, but she says instead, he went to 115th Street where he rang the bell and that`s when police say a man opened fire shooting him in the head.\par \par \uc2 Yarl`s aunt said the man then shot him again.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There is a thorough investigation underway now. There will be a thorough review on the prosecutor`s office. We will make sure we do all we can to be fair, to make sure that we`re as expeditious as possible.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: The homeowner who allegedly shot the teen was taken into custody on a 24-hour hold and was released. Police cited the need for further investigation and for a formal statement from the victim as some of the reasons.\par \par \uc2 While the identity of the shooter remains unknown, many in the community worry race may be a factor. Kansas City`s Police Chief acknowledged that concern.\par \par \uc2 STACEY GRAVES (Kansas City Missouri`s New Police Chief): I want everyone to know that I`m listening, and I understand the concern that we are receiving from the community.\par \par \uc2 (PROTESTERS chanting "Black lives are under attack. What do you do? Say they`ll fight back.")\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Protesters gathered and demonstrated on Sunday calling for justice, including Yarl`s father.\par \par \uc2 PAUL YARL (Ralph Yarl`s father): I have a message for the prosecutor, we want charges, that`s what we want. If he goes free, the next Black kid that`s going to ring that doorbell could get shot again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was Lilia Luciano reporting.\par \par \uc2 You could see how easily you could make the mistake, 115 Street, 115 Terrace. But let`s talk about Ralph Yarl for just a second. He`s described as a section leader in the marching band, one of the top bass clarinet players in Missouri.\par \par \uc2 His friends and teachers describe him as a kind soul, quiet, friendly, well-mannered, willing to help, no criminal record his family says, super smart and a musical genius.\par \par \uc2 I mean, thank God he is still alive. Let`s just start with that. Thank you so much he is still alive.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there are still so many questions. You knock on the door, they say, he wasn`t threatening according to the family and the attorneys. He was just knocking on the doors to pick up his little brothers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, based on what we know now, unless it is legal to shoot delivery people who are knocking on the door, how is this okay?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know. Police are going to be looking into it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And we do have to question ourselves, as society when we get to the point where someone ringing a doorbell, somebody who is lost or somebody who is looking for help, can find themselves facing down the barrel of a gun and a child.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It happened to police who went to the wrong address in a different State as well, so something is going on here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, more and more questions on the story and we will stay on top of this for you.\par \par \uc2 Americans in Sudan have been told to stay off the streets because of an outbreak of political violence in the East African nation. A power struggle between two military factions is blamed for around 100 killings in the past few days.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio is tracking the violence that threatens to wipe out hopes of creating a democracy there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Sudan sliding into chaos, now in open warfare for a third day. Its Armed Forces against its most powerful paramilitary group, an estimated 100,000 strong.\par \par \uc2 LAKSHMI PARTHASARATHY (American tourist): Woke up to sound of gunshots.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: American, Lakshmi Parthasarathy is trapped in Khartoum, the capital. With her drone, she captured smoke rising from the now closed International Airport.\par \par \uc2 LAKSHMI PARTHASARATHY: I don`t think anyone expected this to happen.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: A passenger plane had been hit with deaths reported. People in the terminal trembled on the floor.\par \par \uc2 In this fog of war, both militaries now claim control of key installations.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: At the heart of this hatred, the breakdown of their leaders` agreement to integrate a power sharing deal in 2021 between the Army Chief, General Abdel Fattah al-Burhan and the head of the Rapid Support Forces, General \uc2 Mohamed Hamdan Dagalo\uc2 , now smashed despite a coup they lead together against a civilian government.\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2  (US Secretary of State): People in Sudan want the military back in the barracks. They want democracy.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Secretary of State, \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  called for the protection of non-combatants.\par \par \uc2 The UN`s Food Assistance Program has been suspended after three staffers were killed with civilians suffering the most.\par \par \uc2 Sudan TV is now off air after gunshots were fired. The feed going blue.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And the human rights activists say people are running out of food and water after the past three days. Fighting continues across this country, Tony, with no end in sight.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ramy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to Ukraine where new Russian attacks hit civilians over the weekend. This, despite reported Intel that suggested Russia`s forces were weakening. Ukraine`s military is also reported to be running short of weapons and ammunition.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams visited this airfield where Ukrainian pilots are fighting on. She joins us now from Ukraine once again.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 Most people in this country are Orthodox Christians, and they celebrated Easter over the weekend, but despite that, Ukraine says Russia continue to strike at cities, including Sloviansk, where Ukrainian officials say the death toll has now reached 10.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: In the small city of Sloviansk, people`s homes and lives have been reduced to dust.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine says Russian missiles rained down on Sloviansk. We don`t know what their target was, but what they hit here was an apartment building full of civilians.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine wants to stop its people being slaughtered like this and documents recently leaked from The Pentagon appear to show that Ukrainian Air Defenses are dangerously depleted but they`ll soon be getting a Patriot Air Defense System from the US with more on the way from Europe.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, there are new signs that Russia`s military is faltering. Some of the leaked documents show Russian Special Forces have been decimated by the invasion according to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Bolstered by new weapons, a Ukrainian counteroffensive against Russian forces is expected to launch in the next few weeks and at this airfield hidden amongst farmland, we were taken to see some of their most valuable assets.\par \par \uc2 Ukrainian helicopter gunships fly 20 feet above the ground to avoid detection targeting Russian positions.\par \par \uc2 MIKOLA (Ukrainian soldier): It is quite dangerous, but these tactics, it works very well.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Mikola (sp?) flies bombing runs on machines that rely heavily on old Soviet era technology. They get the job done, he told us, but they need more.\par \par \uc2 It`s never enough.\par \par \uc2 MIKOLA: Never. It`s never enough. It`s for sure, it`s never enough.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: There have been a lot of speculation that Ukraine`s counteroffensive could be hampered or even delayed by the leak of those \uc2 US government\uc2  documents, but publicly, at least Ukrainian officials have downplayed their impact -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Holly Williams in Ukraine for us. Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, "60 Minutes" gets incredible access to \uc2 Google\uc2 `s Artificial Intelligence labs. We will show you want left Scott Pelley speechless.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up, 10 years after the Boston Marathon bombing, how one survivor is trying to make the future better.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead, musician Questlove will be here. He`s got a new book for kids.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. About 30,000 athletes are taking part in the Boston Marathon today. This year`s race marks a somber milestone. It has been 10 years since two bombs exploded near the finish line in a terror attack. Three people were killed on that horrific day and nearly 300 were wounded.\par \par \uc2 Two heroic officers involved in the four-day manhunt for the suspects also died. Over the weekend, the city honored the victims and survivors in a ceremony attended by hundreds of people, including first responders.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen joins us from near the finish line.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Vlad, good morning --\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: As longstanding tradition, the 2013 Boston Marathon to crowds from all over the world, but in an instant, everything changed when two bombs exploded near the finish line. Eight-year-old Martin Richard, 23 year old Lu Lingzi, and 29-year-old Krystle Campbell were killed.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER ABBOTT (Boston Marathon bombing survivor): I was catapulted through the open doors of the restaurant, and I landed on the ground then realized my foot was in a lot of pain.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Hundreds, including Heather Abbott were injured, the damage stretched for blocks.\par \par \uc2 ED DAVIS (Former Boston Police Commissioner): This building was all caved in.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Ed Davis was the Boston Police Commissioner at the time.\par \par \uc2 ED DAVIS: The fact that it happened at sort of a hometown event just sort of brought this home to the whole nation, that these things can happen anywhere.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The bombings had a lasting impact on security practices nationwide.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that other races around the country changed their ways of policing after this?\par \par \uc2 ED DAVIS: I know they did. There was enormous transfer of Intelligence and information so that our brothers in other cities would be able to prepare better.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Today, the marathon still finishes in the same spot, the embodiment of Boston Strong, a mantra the city adopted after the attack. It`s a reminder Abbott kept in mind after her leg was amputated below the knee.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, we got it.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: She learned to walk again, and has since graduated to snowboarding, running part of the marathon course, and wearing her beloved heels.\par \par \uc2 Why was it important for you to be able to walk in heels?\par \par \uc2 HEATHER ABBOTT: It was just kind of part of who I was, and it was something I didn`t want to have to give up.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Healing has also meant giving back. Abbott has helped more than a hundred people get access to prosthetic devices through a foundation she started.\par \par \uc2 HEATHER ABBOTT: I`ve made an effort to not focus on what I can`t change, and what I can`t change is what happened the day of the Boston Marathon bombing.\par \par \uc2 So, I try to look forward and think about how I can change the future and how I can make it better.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Nancy Chen is joining us live again from Boston. Sorry about that, Nancy, a little technical difficulty there in the studio.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Yes, absolutely. Good to see you all. And I`m sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 We should mention that Heather Abbott who you just heard from right there will be here at the finish line later to be a part of her Foundation`s Annual Watch Party.\par \par \uc2 Also today, there will be tens of thousands of athletes, but none will be wearing the bib number 2013, which was not assigned this year in remembrance of this tragedy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Nancy Chen live for us in Boston. Nancy, thank you. It is remarkable that it`s been 10 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I was thinking, too. I imagine -- I`m wondering how the people of Boston feel today. I imagine every year about this time, it triggers all sorts of feelings for people, but good to see Heather Abbott up and about, but you can`t help but remember the people that lost their lives that day. That was so shocking. We were on the air.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was such a shocking day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And the #BostonStrong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Boston Strong.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She embodies it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really does. Yes. She does.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nancy, thanks again.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: We take you to Taiwan where we`ll show you how people there are preparing for a potential invasion by China.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This morning, we look at the worry in Taiwan that China will attack it someday. It is a tense time for that whole region.\par \par \uc2 Take a look at this: US warship yesterday sailing through the Taiwan Strait separating the island from China just a narrow body of water and it followed days of Chinese military drills around Taiwan. \uc2 US Navy\uc2  called that voyage a routine movement.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer looks now at how the people of Taiwan themselves are preparing for a possible invasion while hoping it never happens.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Taiwan`s steep coast and mountainous terrain make it something of a natural fortress, but China`s People`s Liberation Army could well mountain attack anyway, so Taiwan is beefing up its military, and training with new US weapons.\par \par \uc2 Michael Cole is an analyst for the Republican Institute in Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COLE (Republican Institute in Taiwan analyst): The aim is not to defeat the PLA, but to promise that the PLA would suffer such damage that it would not be worth trying to invade Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Some civilians want to be part of that deterrent. Joyce Chang is one of them.\par \par \uc2 And is at the back of your mind and the back of your teammates` minds that one day this could be for real. You might have to fight to defend Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE CHANG (Civilian shooter): Sure. It`s no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Right now, though, everyone is praying it won`t come to that, but when the Taiwanese people woke up 10 days ago to the news that \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  had ordered military exercises in the sea and the air around Taiwan, they were reminded that when he says he will take control of the island by force if necessary, he means it.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HU (Former Taiwanese Army Major General): I know exactly how terrible a war is.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Richard Hu retired as a Major General in Taiwan`s Army.\par \par \uc2 A lightning Chinese missile attack, he says could knock out critical infrastructure before Taiwan or the US had time to react.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HU: It could wipeout 95 percent of the power in Taiwan if it is a war.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Wow. So you`re saying there is -- the strategic planning overall is not serious.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HU: Not very serious.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Taiwan`s military is getting more serious, but not everyone believes in fighting off China. The main \uc2 Kuomintang\uc2  political opposition wants closer links and some want to outright join it.\par \par \uc2 But young Taiwanese like, Berton Li and Tess Chu (sp?) say absolutely no way. They were proud to show me life in this flourishing democracy that they say would be crushed by Chinese rule.\par \par \uc2 BERTON LI (Taiwanese): We will lose our freedom, democracy, our religion, our tradition.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: This tropical island from its ultra-high tech semiconductor factories to its rural highlands where tea is still picked by hand is now the most dangerous flashpoint in the confrontation between the US and China.\par \par \uc2 Twenty-three million lives, American leadership, and probably the world`s economy all hang in the balance.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Elizabeth Palmer in Taipei.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Up next, the stories you`ll be talking about today, including "60 Minutes" getting a very revealing look at \uc2 Google\uc2 `s new Artificial Intelligence, but first, it is 7:42 exactly. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." One of the many beautiful songs in the canon made by human beings.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. That`s right, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re talking about things that made by a computer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we are glad about that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right, we are talking about --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Diana Ross.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Diana Ross, the great team behind all the great hits at Motown.\par \par \uc2 So let`s talk about art and what it means when we reflect it through the lens of AI. We start with a candid message from \uc2 Google\uc2 `s CEO about Artificial Intelligence. He says it will impact everything.\par \par \uc2 On last night`s "60 Minutes," CEO \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2  gave Scott Pelley an inside look into the company`s new AI chatbot, it is called Bard.\par \par \uc2 Bard spent several months reading everything on the internet to create its own communication model. Pelley asked Bard to do a few simple tasks, then he asked Bard to get creative with the famous short story, which Bard did with mind blowing speed.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: We played with a famous six word short story often attributed to Hemmingway. For sale baby shoes never worn.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: The only prompt we gave was finish this story.\par \par \uc2 In five seconds. Holy cow. The shoes were a gift from my wife, but we never had a baby. They were --\par \par \uc2 From the six word prompt, Bard created a deeply human tale with characters it invented, including a man whose wife could not conceive, and a stranger grieving after a miscarriage and longing for closure.\par \par \uc2 I am rarely speechless. I don`t know what to make of this.\par \par \uc2 Give me the story in verse.\par \par \uc2 We asked for the story in verse. In five seconds, there was a poem written by a machine with breathtaking insight into the mystery of faith. Bard wrote, she knew her baby`s soul would always be alive. The humanity at superhuman speed was a shock.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so we`re going to talk about this. But let me also point out that "60 Minutes" also visited an AI Lab, it is \uc2 Google\uc2 `s AI Lab, it`s called Deep Mind.\par \par \uc2 Deep Mind has made a number of incredible breakthroughs in Artificial Intelligence among them, Scott, watched robots that play soccer. Here`s the thing. They were not programmed to play, they learned the game by themselves.\par \par \uc2 I know, Gayle, I know. It`s wild.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2 `s Bard is also teaching itself new things, which the company can`t even explain, and that you might find a little scary. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Some AI systems are teaching themselves skills that they weren`t expected to have. How this happens is not well understood.\par \par \uc2 For example, one \uc2 Google\uc2  AI program adapted on its own, after it was prompted in the language of Bangladesh, which it was not trained to know.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We discovered that with very few amounts of prompting in Bengali, it can now translate all of Bengali. So now all of a sudden, we now have a research effort, where we now trying to get to a thousand languages.\par \par \uc2 SUNDAR PICHAI (\uc2 Google\uc2  CEO): There is an aspect of this which we call all of us in the field call it as a black box. You know you don`t fully understand and you can`t quite tell why it said this or why it got wrong. We have some ideas and our ability to understand this gets better over time, but that is where the state of the art is.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: You don`t fully understand how it works, and yet you`ve turned it loose on society.\par \par \uc2 SUNDAR PICHAI: Let me put it this way. I don`t think we fully understand how a human mind works either.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, so we`ve been talking about -- I know, it --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I see why Scott was speechless. Honestly. It`s a little scary to me.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s fascinating. It is frightening. I`m of the mind and \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2  mentioned this, that to me, this art that it is creating, I don`t know that I`d call it humanity. You know, to me, it can learn how to play sports, but can it respond to Little Johnny Sylvester in 1926, as Babe Ruth did and promised to hit him a homerun and then go on to hit three home runs in the 1926 World Series for Little Johnny? And then the kid miraculously recovers.\par \par \uc2 You know, can it like a butterfly and sting like a bee and jive when it listened to win the heavyweight champion in 1960.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sundar is a very smart guy, but even he was like, saying, I don`t know all the answers here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To everything that is happening.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The art question --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a little -- listen, I watched the movie "Megan" on the plane over the weekend.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now, I know, people are going to laugh at me and say, Gayle, that`s a movie. But I thought it raised some very interesting questions and I know it`s just a movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sundar is correct in saying that we don`t understand how the human mind works. The difference is, we didn`t put the human mind here.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right? Some other power did and now we are playing that power, and by their own admission, it`s a black box teaching itself stuff. We don`t understand why. Teaching itself how to do things, we don`t understand how.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is frightening, and to the question, is Bard safe, which Scott asked, he said, I think so.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Based on how we`re doing it now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think so.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So much to unbundle there. We are going to keep talking about it.\par \par \uc2 But now we`re going to talk about the \uc2 Netflix\uc2  show, "Love is Blind" which turns out love is blind because no one got to see it yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Fans were disappointed when they logged on to watch the highly anticipated Season 4 live reunion. A technical glitch delayed the special for more than an hour. Cohost, Vanessa Lachey shared this video on social media while they worked out the problem. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 VANESSA LACHEY ("Love is Blind" cohost): We are sitting here apparently y`all, everybody broke the internet to see this reunion.\par \par \uc2 NICK LACHEY ("Love is Blind" cohost): We are ready to roll.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA LACHEY: So we are ready to roll. We`ve just got to figure this out.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know people who were hot, but it didn`t go, as planned.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, at it again. We are live this morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Live every day.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Jordan Klepper, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this eight o`clock hour, a powerful Note to Self from a survivor of the Boston Marathon bombing. It has been 10 years, what Rebekah Gregory would tell her younger self.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. Jordan Klepper is this week`s guest host of "The Daily Show." It`s a program he knows very well, of course. We`re going to talk to him about why he likes to spar with people he disagrees with.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. We`ve also got Questlove in the house. He`s a Grammy winner, an Oscar winner, a legend in hip-hop, and now, he`s an author for kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Put another feather in his cap.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 TANAYA COX: I was wondering what`s going on. I just saw blood coming down my arm.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A mass shooting at a sweet 16 party and Alabama leaves at least four people dead.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There is not a playbook for something like this. So the best thing you`ve got to do is just love all your kids.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: When you attack the rights of women in America, you are attacking America.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The battle over a commonly used abortion pill heats up with the Supreme Court set to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Violence breaks out between Sudan`s military and a paramilitary group leaving dozens dead.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bad Bunny performance at Coachella.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Bad Bunny becomes the first Latino and Spanish language artist to headline Coachella and brings out Post Malone for a surprise collaboration.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Bad Bunny and Post Malone performance at Coachella.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a very good collaboration.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t see that coming, but I like both of them, and so together, I think it would be very nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pretty good crowd there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. This is turning out to be probably going to be one of the coolest Coachella`s ever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s begin this hour with a powerful story about perseverance and courage from a survivor of the Boston Marathon bombing.\par \par \uc2 Ten years ago, Rebekah Gregory and her five-year-old son, Noah attended the race as spectators when a bomb exploded just feet away from them.\par \par \uc2 Gregory`s body shielded Noah, but she was seriously injured, eventually losing her left leg. For our series, "Note to Self," Gregory shares a letter with her younger self, about what happened that day, her determination, and her mission to help others.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REBEKAH GREGORY (Boston Marathon Bombing survivor): Dear 10-year-old Rebekah.\par \par \uc2 Right now, you are sitting on the school sidewalk with a suitcase beside you waiting for your dad to pick you up. You feel embarrassed and abandoned because he didn`t show up, again. His abuse is not your fault, and I wish I could take away your pain, but I can`t.\par \par \uc2 What you will learn many years from now is that the hurt you feel will fade away, but the impact from it will become your legacy.\par \par \uc2 For now, call your mom and she`ll be there. She always will be and for many years to come.\par \par \uc2 While you wait, why not make your sisters laugh like you`re good at. You`re going to need that humor later on when you don`t have a leg to stand on.\par \par \uc2 I can`t promise it`s going to be easy, Rebekah. Some challenges you will bring on yourself and others you will have zero control over. Before you know it, you will be a mom at just 20 years old. You will have no clue what you`re doing, but your son, Noah, will give you a purpose that you`ve never experienced before.\par \par \uc2 You will grow up together and he will teach you, make you a better person, and save you time and time again.\par \par \uc2 By your mid-20s, you will feel stability and happiness. You and Noah will have a beautiful home in Texas, your career will be thriving, and for your 26th birthday, you will travel to Boston to attend the marathon with a guy you`ve been seeing who you will later marry.\par \par \uc2 That day in 2013 will change the trajectory of your entire life.\par \par \uc2 MAN: There`s been a terrorist attack in Boston, two bombs were placed in the crowds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Two explosions killed at least three people, at least 144 were wounded.\par \par \uc2 REBEKAH GREGORY: When Noah is sitting down on your feet, a bomb will explode less than three feet behind you and your body will shield him from the shrapnel. It`s a lot to take in, I know.\par \par \uc2 And as you navigate through the ups and downs of your recovery, it will be a lot. You`ll undergo 76 operations, you`ll lose your left leg below the knee. You`ll lose your grandparents, your fairy tale marriage after a tragedy, any sense of normalcy, but what you will gain will be indescribable.\par \par \uc2 You will learn to adapt to a new normal and do things you never knew you were capable of, like running in the Boston Marathon on a prosthetic leg.\par \par \uc2 This was the hardest thing that I think I`ve ever had to do, and when I crossed the finish line, I took my life back today.\par \par \uc2 And confronting the remaining bomber in Federal Court. You will look him in the eyes and tell him that you are not his victim. And you will promise that you will use your time left to make the world a better place.\par \par \uc2 When you least expect it, you will reconnect with and marry your college sweetheart and he will be your best friends. Together, you will fulfill the promise you made in Court by starting a charity called Rebekah`s Angels, where you will fund trauma therapy for nearly 500 families in the first five years.\par \par \uc2 (RYLEIGH VARNEY singing.)\par \par \uc2 REBEKAH GREGORY: You will have a miracle baby named Ryleigh, a little girl that no one thought you`d have with all your injuries from Boston.\par \par \uc2 But despite her early entrance into the world and her stint in the NICU, she will be perfect. She is a fighter just like her mom.\par \par \uc2 Every day when you wake up, you will reach for a prosthetic leg or a wheelchair, but you will remain so thankful. You will have a deeper understanding that life is fleeting, and you will soak up every single moment. grateful to be alive with the family you fought hard for.\par \par \uc2 Your children are so very loved, Rebekah. Ryleigh is the daddy`s girl that you always wanted to be. You love being their mom.\par \par \uc2 With the wisdom that you have gained, you will travel the world sharing your story and write that book you were dreaming about. You`ll realize every twist and turn is necessary to get you to where you are, and sometimes the most broken parts of life lead us to the most beautiful chapters.\par \par \uc2 Now do you see why I can`t scoop you off that sidewalk? All that pain is part of the recipe for the good stuff, and as long as you wake up with that beat in your chest and air in your lungs, you have so much purpose.\par \par \uc2 So do your best to keep surviving right now so that you can fully live later. Soon, every broken piece of you will heal and you will take your life back because you are a survivor and no one can ever take that away from you.\par \par \uc2 Love always your older, wiser self.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Rebekah was hoping to run the marathon this year, but after her most recent surgery that wasn`t possible. But guess what? Her husband, Chris, stepped up and is running in her place today. I want to wish Chris, good luck in the race today and say hi, of course to Noah and Ryleigh. Noah being a survivor himself, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Beautiful. Yes, beautiful family and Noah, you got so tall.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. That`s what I thought, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s been 10 years, but I`ll bet he`s a great big brother and Chris, her husband, such a beautiful story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like when she said, the most broken part of life sometimes brings us to the most beautiful places, paraphrasing there.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As long as you can be a beat in your chest and air in your lungs, there is future ahead.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Beautifully done. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I always wonder about that. It`s terrible to have to suffer to get to that point of Nirvana and enlightenment, but we all have to sometimes live through things like that to get to that point.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, right now --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good luck, Chris.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, we`re wishing you luck.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, we are.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is 8:09. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m in the progressive greenroom where you meet the nicest people, with comedian and longtime "Daily Show" contributor, what`s your name is --\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER (Comedian): Jordan Klepper.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Guess what? Jordan is getting ready to guest host the show this entire week. I would think you have the inside track. You`ve been a member of the team. You know the staff. You know the people. Do you feel you have an inside track?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Speak to the microphone.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes. But you know, we played a great game. It is a real team effort. Can`t wait till next season.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mr. Klepper, do you want this job?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes, I totally want this job. And if my boss is awake today, please give it to me. I need the health insurance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Has your mom giving you any advice about the job.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: My mom said move back to Kalamazoo. It doesn`t help with the job, but she just loves herself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are watching CBS MORNINGS. A lot of people cheering you on, Jordan pepper.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re very excited about our next guest, it is comedian, Jordan Klepper, the latest guest host of "The Daily Show" that`ll be starting tonight.\par \par \uc2 You know Klepper from two decades of comedy including as a contributor on "The Daily Show," both with Jon Stewart and then with Trevor Noah.\par \par \uc2 His field pieces had been viewed more than 100 million times and that`s what you get with interviews with vaccine skeptics and insurrectionists, they are like a big range of people.\par \par \uc2 And recently, Klepper spoke with supporters of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who were outside the New York Courthouse where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was arraigned. Take a look and a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: While I had fun getting into the scrum, I was surprised to find there were people who wanted to have meaningful conversations about the justice system.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I don`t believe what we`re seeing -- what you`re seeing is justice.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: You don`t believe in the grand jury process?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I don`t believe if this was a civilian, it would --\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: I mean, he is a civilian. He`s a person with a New York address. So he shouldn`t go through the grand jury process.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I believe it should be dismissed.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Because?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Because I`m going by general, gut feeling.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You could see the look on his face, as he realized, I don`t really have a reason.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I just liked it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Other than a feeling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I no longer want to answer this question.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes, I mean we have a legal system in place, but Bill`s guts seem to get his -- feeling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He`s got a feeling here or maybe somewhere else on the body is where that feeling is coming from.\par \par \uc2 Okay, "The Daily Show" -- Jordan Klepper joins us now. I didn`t give you the proper introduction, so I wanted to.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s why I looked into the camera.\par \par \uc2 Jordan Klepper of "The Daily Show."\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: As a host now this week, I can give you notes. Always find in camera. You`ve got to find where your camera is. The words are there. This is nifty job. It is not that hard.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know. I know. And if the prompter goes out, it becomes a state of nature really quickly. We flip the table. We`re making fire. I`m sorry we have you in this chair. And now that you`re an anchor and not at the anchor desk over there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes, frankly, people seeing my legs makes me uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what did you learn, if anything about the opinions on the Trump arraignment that you found outside the courthouse?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And did you see crying people?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  said he saw police officers and people that were crying and apologizing. Did you see any of that?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: I saw very few weeping people outside the Trump indictment, I saw a ton of media. What I learned -- I don`t go to Trump events looking to learn. Rarely, there`s a lot of education being given out.\par \par \uc2 The indictment, what you did see is a media circus just amplified by a few MAGA faithful who were not going to be at all thwarted by any information about this upcoming trial. And then folks like Marjorie Taylor Greene and George Santos coming in to try to get some of that shine.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: George Santos was there. You asked him about where he went to Law School? Stanford? Harvard? That was really great. And then the volleyball legacy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Daily Show.")\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Did he give any answers on that?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: You know, he is still quiet about it. I think America -- this is why you send a comedian into the field. There are questions Americans have about our volleyball history as a nation. It`s not in books. And so if Santos won`t answer these questions, how else are we supposed to?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So Santos has not announced that he is running for President, but other people have. They are going to challenge \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . How do you feel about the Republican field?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: I don`t see anybody who is actually offering a different point of view outside of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . DeSantis seems to run alongside him and I think when I talked to folks in the MAGA universe, there`s interest in DeSantis, but nobody is letting go their big red hat yet, and I think until somebody grows enough of a backbone to say I`m not him, I`m me, he seems to be the choice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well listen, Donald Trump`s core base is still extremely strong, despite the fact that he`s been indicted and other cases are waiting in the wings, and now we`re all looking at the Dominion case this week.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that there`s anything that will come out of that case that could change their minds? How do you think they`re looking at that particular trial?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: I mean, frankly, I think where they`re going to go for their news, they`re not going to hear about this case. \uc2 FOX\uc2  is going to talk about it, I don`t think \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  or OAN is going to make that center stage, and yet they`ve sort of been conditioned to take any information that`s not pleasing to the worldview as something to dismiss it completely.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So your guest hosting this week, you are well known for those field pieces. I wonder what kind of field pieces we can expect this week and what kind of guests? Because what`s been interesting is each guest host has had an interesting perspective on the kind of guests that they want to talk to on the show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And want to put their own spin on it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Yes. I mean, I love going out into the field, and I wanted to bring field elements into the show this week. I went to the world`s largest gun show in Tulsa to talk about American gun culture. So you`re going to see me there, I went and talked to AOC.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Did you talk to any gun CEOs? Tony, and I`ve been talking about this. Were there any people who actually are CEOs or gun manufacturers there at this gun show event?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: They do not want to talk to me for some reason.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I figured.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Gun CEOs don`t return my text messages, but gun owners tend to talk to me in a parking lot, and I got to see the gun show loophole happen in real time.\par \par \uc2 People bring out money. They`re in a parking lot. They show a gun and they exchange hands. So that was sort of mind blowing for me to see that up close.\par \par \uc2 I`m also going to talk to Gretchen Whitmer, AOC. I kind of went out into the world. I want to bring kind of important conversations to this week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why do you want this job? And does your mom want you to get it? Most moms are always pulling for their kids. But does she want you to get it? Does she say to you?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: My mom is the best. We`re from Kalamazoo, Michigan, and she says Droop that Midwest nice and tell them what you want.\par \par \uc2 I would love this job. I`ve been a fan of "The Daily Show" since I was a little kid. Jon Stewart started 47 years ago, and so I`ve been watching ever since and a chance to sit behind the desk, work with such great talented folks. I am looking forward to a great week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Would you change it up? Would you want to change it up?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Would I change the show up?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: I`d totally change it up. I think late night is constantly evolving. I think you have to bring in new voices, whether they`re sitting next to you or you`re talking to them, you also have to go out into the world. The benefit that I have as I talk to real Americans, I see those experiences.\par \par \uc2 And I think we cover news in real time. We`re the only late night show when the indictment happened that had somebody who was there that day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope you can remain friends with all of your former colleagues who are also trying out for the job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: We -- those tensions have been frayed for years.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not coming back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jordan Klepper, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Drop that Midwest nice. That`s what your mom said.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can watch Jordan guest hosting "The Daily Show" tonight on \uc2 Comedy Central\uc2 . Time? What time? 10 PM?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN KLEPPER: Eleven.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: 11:00 PM.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Eleven o`clock, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, we`re going to talk with actor Marcel Spears who is making his Broadway debut. He knocks it out of the park. He will join us after the break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, we talk with Questlove about becoming an author for young readers.\par \par \uc2 Your local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning, but before we get to that, Gayle, you have a very big interview this week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I am really psyched.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tell us. Who is it?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Our former First Lady, some call her their Forever First Lady, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , will be here live in the studio on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She`s got a great best-selling book. She`s a best-selling author, by the way.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Light We Carry" and she did a series of podcasts. There`s a \uc2 Netflix\uc2  special, but she`s coming to talk about the podcast based on conversations when she was touring around the country.\par \par \uc2 It`ll just be good to see her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That would be amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hear from her. Yes. I`m very excited about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`ll be the day --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She will be here at our table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That would be the day a lot of our colleagues decided to stop by the studio.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re right. Exactly. Visiting the studio.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just wanted to come in. show my face.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: See if everything was okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because I work from home every other day, but today, I really want to be here with my colleagues.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s Thursday, live and in color, \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , I can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is a picture I saw of Vlad over the weekend. This was a post to his lovely wife, Marian`s Instagram account. Do we have the picture? There`s Vlad looking like a superhero dad there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony sent this around. I so love this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think this is amazing. You look so at ease with the baby carrier, Baby Celine looks so happy. It`s all so gorgeous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And with the sunglasses, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was reminded of how far you`ve come.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was only about six months ago that we went shopping for baby carriers together.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This was traumatic.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you tried one out with a little --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did he tie it on backwards?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He tried it on with a stuffed animal.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It took forever. Look at this. I couldn`t figure out how to do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now Vlad, you have it down, don`t you?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I have it down. I mean, but thanks to Tony, thanks to "Vlad to Dad." I mean, I learned so much. Believe it or not, yes, it was a series for the show, but it really was helpful.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When we went together and you -- like one thing that you said to me which I had no clue. I was like, we went and we looked at pacifiers, and I just started dumping them into my cart. He was wait, what are you doing? I`m like, I`ll just get this. He goes, no, they are sized. And I`m like what?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Vlad, there is so much for you to discover and learn. It`s going to be fun.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But you`ve come a long way. It`s been great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is going to be fun to watch you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, I mean to commend you. You looked amazing. You`ve got this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is going to be fun.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Brother, thank you very much. We went to see the cherry blossoms.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ve got it big time. You and Marian, they got this.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is "Phantom of the Opera," you know. Final Broadway curtain call last night. It is the longest running show on Broadway. It closed after more than 35 years and nearly 14,000 performances.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Phantom of the Opera" Broadway.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t go. Don`t go. Don`t go.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I said to the Phantom, don`t go, don`t go. I was here to celebrate the occasion rubbing elbows with Broadway stars like Lin-Manuel Miranda, Emilie Kouatchou. She is the first Black actor to play "Phantom`s" lead role of Christine on Broadway and she killed it.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Close, an original "Phantom" cast member was there. Sarah Brightman was there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Whoa. Sarah Brightman was there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I saw her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sarah Brightman was there. The original Christine.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She played Christine. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Brightman stood next to composer, that of course is the great \uc2 Andrew Lloyd Webber\uc2  as he dedicated last night`s performances to his son Nick, who unfortunately died in March after battling cancer. \uc2 Andrew Lloyd Webber\uc2  also gave the audience hopes of a comeback.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW LLOYD WEBBER\uc2  (Composer): It`s just amazing really what has happened. I mean, in the last few months, I don`t think anybody, any of us thought that "The Phantom" would go out quite with the bang it has.\par \par \uc2 And so maybe, it may comeback, we never know.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t say enough about last night`s performance. Emilie in particular. She said she considered going to business school or getting certified as a yoga instructor. Initially decided to do theater because she was shy.\par \par \uc2 She was -- the whole cast was knock out, not just Emilie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Always.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I mean, Lin-Manuel Miranda said something very interesting to me. He said, everybody shows up for opening night, it is interesting to see who shows up for the closing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Those are people who really care about this play, and he is right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean, look, the fact that it could come back, you know how it ends, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It always ends with the spotlight on the mask.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, on the mask. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which is the biggest --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Andrew Lloyd Webber\uc2  left the door open a little. It is closing. Listen, nobody wanted to close, but I hear it`s very costly, very expensive. So we shall see. He gave people hope last night and that was good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: End of an era. It`s one of my favorite Broadway shows of all time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mine, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So we`re still talking about Broadway. My "Talk of the Table" we are talking with an actor who is making his Broadway debut in a highly anticipated show that has already folks getting rave review.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Marcel Spears stars as Juicy in "Fat Ham." It is a modern reimagining of Shakespeare`s "Hamlet."\par \par \uc2 The Pulitzer Prize winning drama adds a few twists of the classic story. "Fat Ham," folks is set at a family barbecue and centers around a Black gay leading character. It is one of only a few plays to move to Broadway from a Black Theatre.\par \par \uc2 (Clip form "Fat Ham.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In this clip, Juicy gives advice to his friend, Opal, a nod to Ophelia. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fat Ham.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I ponder.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I ponder. Yes, I love that line. I ponder.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Marcel Spears is here. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, Marcel Spears.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS ("Fat Ham" actor): Good morning. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is incredible. I`ve seen the play. It`s wonderful. This is your Broadway debut.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: It is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve been an understudy before and I know that you`ve talked about like, even those five minutes that you get to shine on the stage is one that you go home with for a lifetime. What`s it like being the lead role?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: It`s indescribable. It`s kind of like you wake up in a dream of a thing like you hope for it as a storyteller, as an actor. You think like you`ll get an opportunity to do it. And then when it all happens, you almost can`t look at it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fat Ham.")\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: You`ve got to focus on the work, like I`m just like, no, it`s just a rehearsal for a Broadway show.\par \par \uc2 We`re just teching a Broadway show. We are in previews for a Broadway show, and then it`s opening night and everything is like real and you can`t pinch yourself enough.\par \par \uc2 It feels good. It`s really validating, and I`m glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You should feel very validated. Number one, I watched it, not knowing what -- I didn`t read anything about it. So I had no idea.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s interesting, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So I didn`t know what to expect. And the first part, you`re seen going, what the hell is happening here? It`s such a roller coaster of a show. It takes you on so many different emotions. It was a ride I was not expecting.\par \par \uc2 How do you describe this version compared to Shakespeare`s version? How do you describe it?\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: I think like, I`m from New Orleans, so I feel like this is like "Hamlet" with like a little, little hot sauce and like a little extra, like you know, you put "Hamlet," like you`ve got a rue and then you put Hamlet and then you make something different. And so it`s really, really -- I feel like if you love "Hamlet," you will recognize the cannon, you`ll see it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fat Ham.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, it`s definitely there.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: You see it. It`s present, like there are soliloquies that are really like just ripped down to the pages, but some things are repurposed and reimagined and sort of restructured and in a new context, through the lens of blackness, through the lens of queerness, and in a way that is really, really cool and really exciting.\par \par \uc2 And like you said, it is a roller coaster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, love, betrayal, and death.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is you are on stage most of the time.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Most of the time. And the audience is like right, where, Tony is closer on the stage, because it`s like a backyard barbecue in the audience, to me somehow becomes part of the play, too.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s it like when people are right there in your face?\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: I think -- I don`t know how other actors do, but for me, it`s a lot of fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it?\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: And the way that our brilliant playwright --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You talk to us, too.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: James, he wrote the fourth wall out of the play like he got rid of it completely. So the audience is very much a part of what we`re doing, so I can see you. I`m talking to you like a lot of these asides are meant just for you.\par \par \uc2 And for me as an actor, I`m like watching your face in real time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, you are looking at the audience.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: You are having a real conversation about everything that`s going on in this crazy place.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, speaking of things in the studio, during rehearsals, your dog, Gumbo. Do I have this right?\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was there with you.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How did that go?\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Okay. So I have a new puppy. His name is Gumbo.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There he is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, you`ve got one of those French bulldogs. Everybody wants one of those, Marcel.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: There he is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Gumbo is an adorable little dude and he is still young, so I take him to rehearsals with me. I`m one of those guys now. I am one of those actors that shows up with a dog. And it was great because like he plays in between scenes and he`s running around.\par \par \uc2 Our director, Saheem Ali, as brilliant as he is and as generous as he is had to ask Gumbo to leave rehearsal, because he`s a puppy. Gumbo would sleep, like so it`s hard when we`re doing these like really intense emotional scenes and you hear a French bulldog snoring like a grown man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`re a theater nerd. You actually love Shakespeare. You performed Shakespeare. So we just want to play a little game with you. This is Shakespeare soap opera.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A man tries to scam two married women out of money by seducing both of them.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Soap opera.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Shakespeare, "The Merry Wives of Windsor."\par \par \uc2 A man dies, but is brought back to life with a serum that when injected saves life but gives them amnesia.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Soap opera.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Days of Our Lives."\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re correct. A woman goes to Court disguised as a male lawyer to defend her friend.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Shakespeare.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Merchant of Venice."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Merchant of Venice."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And last one, a woman dies from drowning in the same lake that her father drowned ten years prior.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Soap opera?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, "The Young and the Restless."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not bad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the beginning, as a Shakespeare kid, you felt like it was a theater you were not invited to.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did you mean?\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: I mean, I think like just -- it doesn`t feel like I have access to it immediately.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: The way that it`s taught in this country or the way they used to be taught, I think it`s changing now. But the way that it is taught you sort of feel like it`s trying to get you to be everything that you are not.\par \par \uc2 And so it felt like I was sort of on the outside. I was a kid like looking, I couldn`t get in the club. And I was just like, what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 I like this work, I engage with this work. I understand these emotions. And then I got some really good teachers that sort of gave me like the cheat code to get in, and it really is just owning who you are and bringing all of that, all of your raw materials to the work.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You are soaring and "The Neighborhood" is on tonight.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Yes, yes. New episode tonight.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Marcel Spears, thank you. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 You can see Marcel and "Fat Ham" on Broadway right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he is "The Neighborhood" too.\par \par \uc2 MARCEL SPEARS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have the one and only, Questlove. Where is he? In our progressive greenroom. Cue Questlove. There he is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There he is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up next, he`ll share his first book for young readers, and how it was inspired by his own childhood. But first, it`s 8:40, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Seed.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s drummer Questlove and his band, The Roots. The Grammy winning musician and DJ has done a lot during his pioneering career, pioneer is the word. He`s won three Grammys as a member of The Roots. He has been a producer for some of the biggest names in music like Jay-Z, John Legend, to name a few.\par \par \uc2 He`s also won an Oscar for the documentary, "The Summer of Soul," thank you very much. And now he`s the author of a new book for young readers. It is called "The Rhythm of Time." It`s about a seventh grader named, Rahim, who travels back in time in 1997 and accidentally interferes with history.\par \par \uc2 Questlove joins us now live and in color in the studio.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE ("The Rhythm of Time" author): How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations to you, because this is not your first book, but it is your first children`s book.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Yes, it is my first children`s book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your first one.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you said the character, Rahim, was inspired by your own childhood growing up in Philly.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How so? Because in the book, Rahim`s dad didn`t want him to be a rapper, didn`t really want him in music. Was that sort of your life when I was reading it.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Well, you know, my own father. You know, when you`re growing up in this time period, I think for a lot of Black parents, and for Black people in general, like fight or flight and safety is the only thing that you`re concerned with, like, we`re really not given a platform to dream or fantasize.\par \par \uc2 So even doing this book was sort of me not dismissing that fact. But you know, people think that I am joking when I say that my dad didn`t even know about The Roots until our second album.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How is that -- how is that possible?\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: If you look on the front cover of our first album, there`s no photo of us. You know, he just -- my father`s plan was for me to go to either Juilliard or Curtis Music Institute. Like, you know, like Cate Blanchett was entire like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: That`s the world he wanted for me. Because, you know, he`s thinking of safety and respectability, and not, you know what he thought rap music was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, the father plays a very key role here. There are a lot of plot twists and it involves about going back in time, which I also thought was interesting, but you said you wanted characters that normally aren`t seen in children`s books?\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Yes. You know, for a lot of us, especially with -- even with any form of media, I often didn`t see myself in those characters, so you would have to sort of vicariously imagine that you`re Marty McFly, or like when you`re watching "Star Wars" for 10 years, someone as Lando Calrissian, or whatever.\par \par \uc2 So for starters, yes, I wanted to write a book that I wish I got to read when I was 11. I`m a music nerd, not to mention the idea of like, what we call alt-Black kids.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Alt-Black kids.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Alt-black kids. I mean, that`s -- you know, I walk --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Blurred basically.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Blurred.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Yes, I mean, you know, I didn`t -- I got teased for the way that I spoke English. You know, my mom was stylish, but we`re like holes in my jeans and paint on my clothes and I didn`t look like the rest of the kids at my -- my hair always was this big.\par \par \uc2 And so I just wanted to write a book that has kids see themselves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: You know?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You dedicate it to nine-year-old self.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, no. No. Go ahead, Tony. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say no, I have dumb questions. You go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s insane.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Nobody asks dumb questions.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, there is no dumb question, but I love that you love Prince.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And of course, we`re looking back. Prince left us, this is the month. He was such an -- had a profound impact on you, your life. Tell me about that. What was it like? What was your last memory of it?\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: You know what? One of my favorite memories of him was the day that The Roots started "The Tonight Show." People weren`t used to seeing us dressed in suits and he just happened to be in town.\par \par \uc2 And the second he saw me, he was like, shocked. And he reached in his pocket, like Prince has a wallet, and he pulls out a $50.00 bill and he waved. I said, are you tipping me for looking good? He goes, yes, you looks sharp.\par \par \uc2 And I was like, is this $50.00 mine? Yes, and I framed at $50.00 inside my house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So do you remember -- here is my dumb question. You know, when Chris Rock was slapped at the Oscars.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There was an award that was being handed out there. Do you remember who won that award? Who was that?\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: I won -- I won that award. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was you.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: That was my award.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That was you.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For the documentary, "Summer of Love."\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: "Summer of Soul."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: "Summer of Soul."\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Yes, people always say so -- I am just going to do a sequel for "Summer of Love." Yes, that was a surreal night. But the thing is, is that when your name is being read in the nominations, you`re not even concentrating on that. Like no award I`ve ever been up for have I been clear and present for like, okay, where`s my name?\par \par \uc2 Like, literally, it`s like, okay, remember the duh-duh-duh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Remember the duh-duh-duh-duh. And I remember saying to my mom, like, wait, are they allowed to curse like that? Like, I was distracted.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: By -- I wasn`t distracted by what was happening because I wasn`t even focusing and we were so far back in the theater that you couldn`t --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you know what I remember of that night? You won.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You won, and Questlove, you are still winning. You are still winning. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look for another best-selling author title for you. As I said another feather in his cap.\par \par \uc2 QUESTLOVE: I appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Rhythm of Time" goes on sale tomorrow wherever you like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 That will do it for us. Time flies when you`re having fun.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time travel today.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230417ej4h00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Justices Review An Emergency Appeal Over Access To The Pill This Week\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Skyler Henry \par \uc2 561 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Now, to a major week ahead at the Supreme Court and the battle over access to the abortion pill. Use of the medication is now in legal jeopardy 23 years after being approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CBS` Skyler Henry is outside the Supreme Court tonight.\par \par \uc2 Skyler, when will the court weigh in on this Texas ruling?\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Jericka, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 Justice Samuel Alito`s temporary hold preserving access to mifepristone ends Wednesday night, as Justices review the Lower Court`s ruling this week weighing whether to step in.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): When you attack the rights of women in America, you are attacking America.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: This weekend, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  made a surprise appearance at an abortion rights rally in Los Angeles, one of several demonstrations across the country concerned that the courts are turning back the clock on reproductive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: We say pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: On Friday, the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  temporarily preserved access to the widely used abortion pill, mifepristone, after an emergency appeal by the Biden administration. Justices are considering whether to intervene in the court battle over the drug after a Trump-appointed Federal Appeals Court Judge in Texas ruled to block access earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2  (D-New Mexico): If we`re going to use the Federal Courts as a way to bar and ban access, we are looking at a national abortion ban and more. And I think states have to band together to do as much as they can in opposition to that.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: A new CBS News poll out today shows nearly 70 percent of all Americans want to see Mifepristone remain available in states where abortion is legal, including almost half of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Several GOP lawmakers are now challenging their party`s hardline abortion position.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (R-South Carolina): And I have a great prolife voting record. But some of the stances we`ve taken especially when it comes to rape and incest, protecting the life of the mother, it is so extreme, the middle, the Independent voters, right of center, left of center, they cannot support us.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Though the temporary stay will remain until Wednesday night, Justice Alito gave antiabortion medical associations and physicians who brought the challenge of the FDA`s approval of mifepristone until Tuesday at noon to respond -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Skyler Henry at the Supreme Court tonight. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well today, on "Face the Nation," House Intelligence Chairman Mike Turner said the leak online of Pentagon documents, some classified as secret has proven damaging to both the United States and its allies.\par \par \uc2 The National Guardsman charged in connection with the leak will return to a Federal Court in Boston Wednesday. Twenty-one-year-old Jack Teixeira is accused of sharing top secret files, including Ukrainian military positions on a chat app aimed at gamers.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, \uc2 Dominion Voting Systems\uc2  will square off in Court against FOX News. Dominion is suing the network for $1.6 billion. It claims FOX News knowingly spread disinformation including that Dominion rigged the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Observers say the trial could set an important First Amendment precedent.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020230418ej4g00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For April 16, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Jan Crawford, Mark Strassmann, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,869 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Flare-ups on two polarizing issues in America, guns and abortion, reach new levels of concern.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, more gun violence. We will tell you what`s happened, and we will look at the impact it`s having on us and our children.\par \par \uc2 We will talk with Arizona`s Democratic Senator Mark Kelly in a rare Sunday interview, as conservatives wrap up their annual \uc2 NRA\uc2  conference.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Former Vice President of the United States): Stop endangering our lives with gun bans and stop trampling on the God-given rights of the American people.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): The issue is not too many guns. The issue is too many thugs, hoodlums and savage criminals on our street.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Former Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson once headed up the School Shield program for the \uc2 NRA\uc2 . Now he`s running for president. He will join us.\par \par \uc2 And, as the Supreme Court considers whether a decades-old abortion drug should remain on the market, we will talk with New Mexico`s Democratic governor, \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Then: Questions continue, as more jaw-dropping revelations are reported from those classified documents discovered on social media platforms, as a 21-year-old Air National Guardsman is arrested and accused of publishing intelligence secrets.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I have instructed the department to make sure that they get to the root of why he had access in the first place.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And just how damaging are the leaks to our national security? President Biden says he`s not concerned.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Nothing contemporaneous that I`m aware of that is of great consequence.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will get an assessment from the chairman of the House Intelligence Committee, Mike Turner.\par \par \uc2 Finally, the head of the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2 , \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 , joins us to talk about the state of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We are monitoring a developing story in Dadeville, Alabama. Overnight there, there was a shooting at a 16th birthday party at a local dance hall. Information at this point is very limited, but the Alabama Law Enforcement Agency reports there have been four fatalities and multiple injuries.\par \par \uc2 Another big story we are covering today is abortion rights and the increasingly tighter restrictions across the country following the Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade nearly a year ago.\par \par \uc2 We begin with our Mark Strassmann in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): Dadeville, Alabama, population roughly 3,000, the scene of a weekend sweet 16 party that turned to horror.\par \par \uc2 Also overnight, two people were killed when someone fired shots into a crowd in Louisville, Kentucky, still reeling from last week`s bank massacre.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG GREENBERG (D-Mayor of Louisville, Kentucky): This has been an unspeakable week of tragedy for our city.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: New Mexico authorities just released this police bodycam footage, officers responding to a domestic violence call. It`s the wrong house. But the confused homeowner is apparently armed, and police fatally shoot him.\par \par \uc2 It`s another American front line, what to do about gun violence. Our new CBS News poll shows roughly three in four Americans believe mass shootings are preventable. Increasingly worried, parents, 77 percent at least somewhat concerned, up from an already high 72 percent last year. About 60 percent of parents say their kids worry about gun violence.\par \par \uc2 What about fewer guns or no guns? Eighty-three percent of Democrats say America would be safer, but only 25 percent of Republicans. Easy access to guns contrasts with abortion access in America.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Hands off!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Our bodies!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Hands off!\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Our bodies!\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: More precarious than ever.\par \par \uc2 By Wednesday midnight, another potential Supreme Court milestone, deciding the future availability of mifepristone, the most commonly taken abortion drug. More broadly, our CBS News poll shows American women by a roughly 4-1 margin believe access to reproductive health care is getting harder, especially in red states. More than half of voters in those states see more restrictions coming.\par \par \uc2 Take Florida. Last Thursday, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed into law a bill banning abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. With Florida`s new law, abortion access here in the South essentially is limited to two states, North and South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Republican leaders generally approve states deciding the abortion issue, but our poll shows more than half of Americans believe the Republican Party is trying to ban abortion nationally, rather than let states decide.\par \par \uc2 That view is driven by abortion rights supporters, both Democrats and independents.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Mark Strassmann in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to former Arkansas Governor Asa Hutchinson, who is attending a Republican gathering in Nashville. Governor,\par \par \uc2 it`s good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 I know you have said you are running for president. So I want to start there.\par \par \uc2 What is the affirmative reason you want to be chief executive of the United States of America?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON (R-Arkansas) (Presidential Candidate): Because we need leadership that brings out the best of America and doesn`t appeal to our worst instincts.\par \par \uc2 We need to have leadership that understands our responsibility across the globe, and that we`re not an isolationist party or country. And so whenever you look at the challenges we face from the economy, that we could be headed into recession, to our border security and the fentanyl crisis that we face, to the lack of energy supply that`s so critical to our growth in our country, these are all issues that I think need to be solved.\par \par \uc2 And my experience as Congress, as head of the DEA, involved in national security issues, gives me the capability to address those. And I`m excited about the opportunity to run.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I want to ask you about one of the issues on the minds certainly have a lot of American parents. According to our polling, that is most certainly gun violence. Six in 10 parents say their kids express worry to them about gun violence, either a lot or sometimes.\par \par \uc2 You were with us last after Uvalde, when an 18-year-old man took an AR-15 carrying three times the amount of ammunition that a soldier carries into combat and massacred elementary school children. At that period of time, you told me: "The U.S. should look at the type of triggers that can alert law enforcement."\par \par \uc2 What triggers do you want to write into law?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: Well, whenever you look at each of these incidences of mass shootings -- and that`s the challenge that we face in America.\par \par \uc2 We ought to always be looking at what can make a difference. What can we do to save lives? And that`s why I worked very hard on the National School Shield initiative as to what expertise we could bring in the schools to bring more safety.\par \par \uc2 Whenever you look at the Uvalde shooting, I looked at what the solutions were. And thank goodness we had Senator Cornyn and Senator Murphy that stepped up to the plate and said there`s a bipartisan solution that can address that particular instance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: And so these are steps that have been proven to be successful in -- in saving lives.\par \par \uc2 And I think we continue to look at what can be done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that appears to be the limit of what Congress is willing to do, that bipartisan Safer Communities Act you just referenced.\par \par \uc2 And, in it, there was over a billion dollars appropriated for mental health resources at school. Just $188 million has actually been allocated to about 30 states. There was money in it to incentivize red flag laws. But in these states like Kentucky and Tennessee, where these shootings have just happened, they don`t have red flag laws. They don`t appear to want them there.\par \par \uc2 So how do you fix that connection between mental health and mass shootings?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: Well, the investment is important.\par \par \uc2 And, in Arkansas, we made sure that our school counselors can devote them time to actually counseling with students and not doing administrative work. And so putting more money into the school resources and the mental health services across the board are important. Secondly...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: More than a billion dollars already allocated?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: Well, the states have to pick up that responsibility as well.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, you shift into enhancing those mental health services, but also making sure that we have the capacity to identify and respond if someone poses a risk. And this is important, Margaret, that we have to look at actually utilizing the law that`s on the books. And it`s been there since the `70s.\par \par \uc2 But it was used in a different way. And that is, if somebody is a danger to themselves or a risk to others, then they can be committed. It has to go before a judge.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: There has to be a hearing on it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: But we aren`t utilizing that. And that -- we need to change the context of our society to take those steps whenever we identify those mental health problems that pose those kinds of risks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, but that assumes identification of the problem.\par \par \uc2 The shooter in Louisville was 25 years old. His family said he had no history of violence. He had no police record. And he bought an AR-15-style weapon six days before he carried out this massacre.\par \par \uc2 Your solution doesn`t solve for that.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: Well, it doesn`t solve every problem. You have got -- you have got instances of mass shootings that are caused by mental illness and a failure to respond to those instances you can identify.\par \par \uc2 And, here, you cite the case. And I think we`re still learning the facts. But it`s evil. And you have got to be able to enforce the law. And you have got to send the signal that there`s going to be serious consequences and the death penalty when somebody, through a pure act of evil, carries out that kind of shooting.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He was killed.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: And so you have got to go to the heart of that problem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. He was killed on site.\par \par \uc2 The CDC says that more than 50 percent of Americans will be diagnosed with a mental illness or disorder at some point in their lifetime. So, the numbers are with you in terms of mental health crisis in this country. That`s 50 percent of Americans.\par \par \uc2 How are you going to decide who has enough of a problem to institutionalize? Where are you going to draw that line?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: Well, that`s the line that I just recited that`s in the law currently, which is not that you have -- are suffering from depression or not that you have to go into counseling for some reason. We all have those issues in life.\par \par \uc2 But if it reaches the point of paranoia, sociopathic behavior, or that you`re a risk to yourself of suicide or you`re a risk to others in terms of homicide, then we, as a society, if we can identify that, which we can, we have to act on it. And it`s not adjudicated by a police officer. It is by a court where evidence is received.\par \par \uc2 But we have failed in our society to utilize and to act upon that. And so mental illness is there. But whenever it gets to the level of risk and danger to others, we should act as a society. And we have ignored that for the last really 50 years.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to have to change if we`re going to address the issues that we see.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But there are states who are literally turning down money on the table that`s in that act you just praised to put in place red flag laws that would allow for family members to say, hey, my loved one is a danger and shouldn`t be allowed to buy that weapon.\par \par \uc2 Those state governments in Tennessee and in Kentucky didn`t have those laws. They didn`t want them there.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: I think there`s a resist...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, are you talking about some national law you want to create here that would force those states to do things to prevent people with how you define mental illness buying weapons?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: Well, there`s two separate issues here. One is the red flag law that you raised.\par \par \uc2 And then, secondly, there is the adjudication through a court of law for someone who poses a risk to themselves or to others. And that`s on the books. It`s in virtually every state. And that`s dependent upon action that a family member might take when they identify another family member that is a risk.\par \par \uc2 It might be the police that could identify that of somebody that`s on the streets, or it could be a whole host of ways, but it would get it into court. So this is not a federal law that needs to be passed. It is actually a matter of practice, and that the civil libertarians pushed us away from this action 50 years ago, and we have never returned to that kind of action when we see the problem in an individual.\par \par \uc2 On the red flag law, that is a separate issue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: And there`s a resistance because it`s not a -- going into court and fully adjudicating it. It is -- we`re still studying the experience that they had in Florida on this.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure it`s due process, it`s fair, you`re not unnecessarily taking firearms...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR ASA HUTCHINSON: ... away from somebody just because they say they`re having a bad day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor, there`s so much more to get into with you. I got to leave it there for today. Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to Arizona Democratic Senator Mark Kelly, who joins us from Tucson.\par \par \uc2 Senator, welcome to the program.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY (D-Arizona): Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have a lot to get to with you, but I want to start on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Our viewers remember, of course, that your wife, Congresswoman \uc2 Gabby Giffords\uc2 , was critically injured in a shooting many years ago. You`ve made gun safety a real priority issue.\par \par \uc2 Your colleague Senator Murphy of Connecticut said to me recently: "Something is dying inside the soul of this nation."\par \par \uc2 Do you think America is numb to gum -- gun violence?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, I don`t think we`re numb to it.\par \par \uc2 And it`s really heartbreaking to see moms across the country terrified about sending their kids to school. I mean, it`s not the country we should live in.\par \par \uc2 I have a 2-year-old granddaughter, and, in her preschool, she has already gone through one lockdown. Now, she`s 2. She doesn`t know what it was. But, I mean, this -- if we don`t make some serious change, this is going to be her experience growing up.\par \par \uc2 We have some of the most permissive gun laws in the world. And we have the -- some of the highest levels of gun violence. We passed this bipartisan, Safer Communities Act. It is a step in the right direction, but it`s only one step, and there is more we can do.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On that law, it -- as we just talked about with -- with Mr. Hutchinson, it gave a billion dollars for school mental health resources. About $188 million has been awarded to about 30 states so far.\par \par \uc2 Is that money moving fast enough? Is there more that can be done with these resources already allocated?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, I think this issue is so important to address and so tragic.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, Margaret, I`m a gun owner. I`m a supporter of the Second Amendment, but we make it so easy for irresponsible people and criminal -- criminals to get access to firearms. There are three schools in Arizona that have already gained access to this money, but, you know, moving it into the states and into communities faster is going to be -- it`s going to be helpful.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Yes. Well, it only really began moving in February.\par \par \uc2 You just heard \uc2 Asa Hutchinson\uc2 , who is running for president, talk about institutionalizing people with mental health issues to avoid mass shootings. It`s something that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , the former vice president who`s running, also said at the \uc2 NRA\uc2  this weekend. He also called for the death penalty for mass shooters.\par \par \uc2 How do you assess those solutions?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, Governor Hutchinson also said that he didn`t want to compel states to -- to comply with the red flag laws.\par \par \uc2 You know, we provided money. And it`s voluntary for states. We could make that mandatory. Red flag laws work. I mean, we -- we have data that shows that, in states that have red flag laws, you prevent -- you prevent gun violence. So that`s certainly a place to start.\par \par \uc2 And we provided money for mental health services. We`ve got a mental health crisis in our country. There`s more we can do. But listening to the -- the former vice president to say that this isn`t about, you know, firearms, not about guns? I mean, it is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we just make it way too easy. How about more background checks? You know, here in the state of Arizona or Texas or many places, you can go to a gun show and get a gun without a back -- background check. That doesn`t make sense to most Americans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We have to take a break here, Senator.\par \par \uc2 I want to talk to you on the other side of it about your recent trip to Ukraine and other issues. So, please stay with us.\par \par \uc2 More from Senator Kelly in one minute.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re back now with Arizona Senator Mark Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Senator, I know you just returned from Ukraine. So, I want to ask you about this news that we got confirmed, essentially, this week in these leaked Pentagon documents that estimate Ukraine will deplete their stock of anti- aircraft missiles within weeks.\par \par \uc2 It`s been widely reported how quickly they`re going through ammunition. How concerned are you that this is going to give Russia the opportunity to have air superiority?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, Margaret, I spent 25 years in the \uc2 United States Navy\uc2 . I flew in combat. This is my first time I have actually went to a war one, a country that`s been brutally attacked by -- by \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 There`s war crimes committed every day. I mean, the situation is just heartbreaking. You know, one of the things we were looking at was their ammunition supply. I don`t want to comment specifically on the classified intelligence here.\par \par \uc2 But we have to make sure that we continue to give them the weapons and the weapon systems that they need to be successful. We cannot allow Putin to win this thing. I mean, he -- he said what his plan is. I mean, he wants to rebuild the Soviet Empire. And, if we don`t stop him in Ukraine, I mean, there is no telling where he will go next.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But when you say more ammunition, you specifically mean more anti-aircraft missiles now?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: No. I mean, there`s rounds for their howitzers. They need to -- their air defense system is challenged as well. I mean, that`s what you`re specifically talking about.\par \par \uc2 Neither Russia nor Ukraine have air superiority at this time. That`s important in a combat zone. And to get it is -- is challenging. And they`re using a lot of their weapons. I mean, they -- they -- HIMARS is another example.\par \par \uc2 So, the purpose of this trip was to see what they need, see what we can supply. I will go back to DOD and to the administration and give them my assessment of what the situation in Ukraine is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden has said they don`t need F-16s. You disagree.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: Well, I think it`s something we need to look at. And I have communicated that to the Department of Defense and the administration.\par \par \uc2 We recently evaluated here in Arizona, in Tucson, where I live, two Ukrainian F-16 pilots. I spoke to the instructor pilots. It`s still unclear exactly how they want to use the F-16. They`re looking for the next game changer. F-16 is not an artillery piece. It`s not a tank. It`s very complicated and hard to maintain.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY: We -- we -- we`ve also looked at some other options. I mean, there are other countries that have F-16s as well. That might become an option.\par \par \uc2 But it`s going to take some time. I mean, the assessment here is, it`ll take about a year to train 12 Ukrainian -- if we go that route, 12 Ukrainian MiG-29 pilots.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Kelly, it`s good to have you on the program. We hope to have you back.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have to leave it there for today.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to bring in chief legal correspondent Jan Crawford to help us understand what is going on legally when it comes to abortion access and this pill.\par \par \uc2 Jan, it`s good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We know the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade back in June. They sent the decision back to states. Now we`re back at the Supreme Court talking about abortion access again.\par \par \uc2 Will they hear this case on the pill?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, I mean, no, I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 But, first of all, let`s think about what they did last June. They said in that Dobbs decision there`s no constitutional right to abortion. States can decide what they want to do with it. Let it play out in the political process. And that`s exactly what we have seen.\par \par \uc2 We have seen the red states, the more conservative states, restrict or even ban abortion. We have seen the blue states, the more liberal states, say: We`re going to keep it widely available.\par \par \uc2 This case here is an effort by a conservative legal group in Texas to restrict abortion nationwide in every state by trying to outlaw a pill that`s used in more than half of all abortions in this country. They`re saying that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  didn`t properly approve this pill 23 years ago.\par \par \uc2 And a federal judge in Texas, who`s a Trump appointee, by the way, agreed with that. So, now the case is before the Supreme Court, whether they should get involved. And I don`t think the justices are going to go along with this. I think they`re going to block that lower court judge`s order. They`re going to keep this pill -- pill available nationwide.\par \par \uc2 And that`s because there are conservative legal principles that go to the heart of this case. This is not a case about the right to abortion. This is not a case about the Constitution. This is a case about jurisdiction and administrative law. This is a case that says, do these challengers have standing to go into court and attack a law -- a procedure that they just don`t agree with?\par \par \uc2 I don`t think the court is going to go along with that. I think it`s probably going to be at least 7-2 and maybe even 9-0.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... to -- what is your prediction in...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Blocking the lower court`s order and keeping this pill available nationwide.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That would be surprising for those assuming a conservative-leaning court.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But the conservative legal principles...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you`re saying it`s not a...\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: ... at the heart of this case, say you have got to have a good reason to go into federal court and challenge something. You can`t just say: I don`t agree with this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You have got to show that you were harmed by it, that you had a stake in it.\par \par \uc2 And that`s not clear here. And if the court goes along with this, in this case, it will be at odds with what they have said in other cases in the past about the role of federal judges to get involved in social disputes. I think it`ll be very surprising if the court blocks this pill.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let`s take a break, come back.\par \par \uc2 I have more questions for you, Jan.\par \par \uc2 So, we will be right back. And we will speak to New Mexico Democratic Governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 And we are back now with our chief legal correspondent Jan Crawford.\par \par \uc2 Jan, I wanted you to take something head on, which is this Democratic argument that if -- that if this one \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved drug for abortion is blocked -\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That all drugs are somehow at risk. Is that true?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I mean I think that`s a valid argument. If the - if the courts are going to say lower the bar and let people come in to the federal courts to challenge things that were approved 23 years ago, even if they haven`t been harmed by the drug, I mean it`s hard -- that`s certainly a valid argument and it could apply in other cases with other social issues. If the court lowers the bar in this case, you`re going to see conservative groups on other social issues going into the Supreme Court and saying they have a right to sue here, too. You`re going to broil federal judges back into these social issue disputes, which, if we take the Supreme Court at its word, is exactly what they said should not be happening.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Jan Crawford, I have to leave it there. Thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to New Mexico`s Democratic Governor \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s good to have you back here in studio.\par \par \uc2 GOV. MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM (D-NM): Well, thank you, Margaret. I`m happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, your state is part of a 20 state coalition of governors, the Reproductive Freedom Alliance. Some of the states in it have started stockpiling this medication for abortion. When you were here in February you said that`s the wrong focus, the wrong question.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Well --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Has that changed?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: No, it -- for me it hasn`t changed. And we were -- we`re going to make sure, and we already are, that we have access to all of those medications. But if the response is, we`ll stockpile instead of protecting all access, then we`re minimizing the work that we have to do to make sure that women and families are fully protected. Not that in and of itself there`s a disagreement by a state that`s making sure that irrespective of the legal decisions we`re going to make sure that medication abortion is available in our state.\par \par \uc2 But I think that we are moving - And to Jan`s point -- it`s every social issue that you disagree with. Is it stem cell research? Is it fertility drugs? Whatever it is in this context, if we`re going to use the federal courts as a way to bar and ban access, we are looking at a national abortion ban and more. And I think states have to ban together to do as much as they can in opposition to that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the states are on the front line here because there is no federal guarantee.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The court kicked it back to chief executives like yourself back in June.\par \par \uc2 So, currently in New Mexico, abortion is legal, but you don`t actually have a law codifying it. I know you want to write one.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: We do. We do now. So, the last time I was here we didn`t, and you were -- and thank you -- talking about Colorado`s work. We now have a law both codifying right to abortion, abortion care and access, as well as gender affirming care in the state. So that just got signed by me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What - so nail down for me then, how do you define - because up - up until now my understanding is there wasn`t a limit on when in pregnancy a woman could receive an abortion. Have you set any limit on that?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: There are no limits. So, for us, and from --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s very controversial.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: It can be. I mean, look, it`s 1 percent of all abortions and that`s still a sizable number of abortions worldwide.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: One percent over 21 weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Correct. However, you know, look, these are women that have named these soon-to-be born babies. These are horrific medical conditions. And, again, New Mexico`s position and mine is that we should not be interfering with a woman`s right, medical situation, and her decision about that life-threatening potential circumstance. We shouldn`t be doing that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, explain that.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you define fetal viability and - and -- or that line?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: So that`s -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You say it`s very, very uncommon, but.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: It - it is. That is not defined. It is left to two physicians make that decision with the patient. That`s the issue is that the government`s --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Two physicians?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Two physicians. Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And so the fear is that folks could take that to an extreme if someone has an affliction that isn`t life threatening.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Well, of course, and that is -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But they`re picking and choosing which children they want to carry to term or not.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Well, I find that argument not to be nearly as compelling as the arguments that we make that we should be focused on, contraceptives and better maternal health care, which means you have better outcomes. It`s the wrong side of the argument. And it pushes buttons for people`s fears about what`s really happening. Late-term abortions should occur as rarely as humanly possible and they should be only for life- threatening conditions of the - of the fetus or the mother, and that should be analyzed by that physician.\par \par \uc2 If we start making any access points, which we are all around the country, you end up with triggers and six weeks, fewer than six weeks. These are all barriers to women`s health care, comprehensive reproductive health care. And New Mexico`s going to stand with many other states to make sure that`s not the direction we`re headed in.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, your state has become this haven of sorts for the surrounding states that do heavily restrict abortion, like Texas.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: And Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And Oklahoma. So that`s, I`m sure, part of your calculus here in crafting the law you did.\par \par \uc2 But I wanted to come back to something you said, both in February and in other remarks. You talked about using federal lands.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Uh-huh.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You talked about talking to the tribes in your state. You have a large tribal population there. You said, we`re moving towards tribal nations providing access, including abortion. The Hyde Amendment prevents federal dollars being used from abortion. The White House has not endorsed this.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: But sovereign land - yes, but sovereign land is a whole different designation of federalism and federal land.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you doing this?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: We aren`t doing it now, but I think we will, and I think we can.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Well, a couple different ways. A sovereign nation makes its own decisions. Now, the question that I think you`re asking is, would we use Medicaid to actually pay for those services? That is complicated with the Hyde Amendment. So the answer there is no.\par \par \uc2 But we do a ton of state investments and tribes have their own resources. They`re already building behavioral health clinics. They run hospitals. They run primary care clinics. They`re already in the business of delivering health care. And at least one of those tribes, the Pueblo in New Mexico, has certainly indicated that they would be more than ready, willing, able, and interested to make sure that access -- because women of color have limited access for a number of reasons all over the country. And these are Pueblos that want to make sure that the women and families in their Pueblo or sovereign nation have equal access irrespective of distances that they might have to travel.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because last June Vice President Harris was asked about this and said the, no, the White House isn`t looking at it. Are they looking at it or is this you?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Well, I don`t think the White House is looking at it, but they have heard loud and clear from a variety of states, including New York, that every federal tool in the toolbox ought to be used to protect and expand access. New Mexico has an opportunity with 23 independent tribes to do that in a little different way. And so the point was, we won`t leave any access point, right, on the table if it makes sense and we have willing partners.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I want to quickly ask you about the water crisis. There`s this debate over the Colorado River, which appears to be drying up. It`s been drought stricken for like two decades now. Do you need the Biden administration to step in here? Because the states aren`t settling this amongst themselves.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: I think we do. And I think having at least $4 billion, which is an incentive. Look, people aren`t going to give up water rights and automatically lean in to do conservation. It`s hard. And it`s full of risk. The Biden administration, rightly so, got money available to create incentives so that we`re doing better conservation and management. We`ve got six states working pretty well together. California, big water user, going to be tough. But with good snowpacks, money, incentives, and cooperation, we are in the best place ever to do something meaningful about this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the Republican chairman of the Intelligence Committee, Ohio Congressman Mike Turner.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 REP. MIKE TURNER (R-OH): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you have any sense yet of the scale of the damage caused by the leak of this classified material by apparently this 21-year- old airman who has been arrested?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Not completely, but clearly there`s damage that`s done. I mean we have documents classified because we don`t want them to get in the hands of our adversaries, and these have been widely circulated. So, obviously, these are - are - are damaging both to the United States and to our allies.\par \par \uc2 You know, what`s troubling here is when you look at the documents that were circulated, that, you know, without the care of its handling, these relate to actual real people. The marks on maps are - are real people. And they can impact people`s lives. And that`s certainly our concern.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden said when it came to the content of the messages and information, he wasn`t concerned. You seem to disagree with that.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Well, I can tell you, President Zelenskyy certainly would be concerned and so would our other allies. Whenever we`re trusted with information, we`re working in partnership with someone, you know, our intelligence gathering, our intelligence information, if it is released, can represent a vulnerability to them. So, obviously, it`s an issue that`s troubling and that needs to be addressed.\par \par \uc2 In the - the outcome for the Ukraine conflict, though, it`s early enough, and these are static documents, meaning they`re pictures of an exact period of time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Period (ph) of time.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: And mitigation can happen, people can change their strategies, and - and that can change the outcome.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I asked this question to Senator Kelly about the concern of Ukraine running through its ammunition stocks too quickly. Are you concerned about that?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Right. So, some of these documents would be in the form of management documents. When you look at inventories or depleting inventories, they, too, are static. What they show is a to-do list. And what we need to do, and our allies need to do to help Ukraine replenish those. It doesn`t indicate that they have no other sources and, in fact, they`ll - they will - will run out and be completely open and vulnerable to Russia.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. So, not necessarily -- it would be a leap to say Russia will have air dominance on this date because they run out of these things?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On the leak itself, the individual who is accused here, Mr. Teixeira, there`s video that circulated of him saying racist things, shooting guns, anti-Semitic things. He`s apparently posted these things on social media and they were there undetected for a long period of time. What part of this needs to change? Because clearly the protocols failed.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Right. Absolutely. And if you look at the actual complaint and affidavit that was filed when he was arraigned, you - you have the - the also admission from the Department of Defense that they are able to track his movements. So, clearly, he was having access to documents that he should not have had access to and someone should have been paying attention, tapping him on the shoulder and - and ending that access.\par \par \uc2 But in this instance, as you just indicated, you know, through life patterns that were clearly signals that - that he was -- might be a likely leaker of information in the future and then also the access that he was having to this information should have been cut off. He should have never been having access to this level of classified information that could hurt the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But he was working basically in tech support. It wasn`t necessarily analyzing this information. Do --\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Right. He had no reason -- there was no need to know for him of the information that he was accessing. And the Department of Defense admits in the affidavit that they had the ability to track him. That`s going to be the questions my committee is going to be having.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: So, we`re going to be having hearings on this. And what we need to do - and from the 9/11 Commission we learned that we needed to more widely disseminate classified information so that people had actionable intelligence that they could piece together puzzles.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Clearly, we`ve gone too far. And where we have an instance where someone in Massachusetts who`s looking at documents with respect to war plans in Ukraine, and the Department of Defense knows, and that`s what our committee is going to be looking at is, how do we make certain we make changes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to -- so to make those changes I want to ask you to clarify this because there are some conservatives saying things, like Tucker Carlson has, your colleague Marjorie Taylor Greene, in defense of this individual, this 21-year-old man. She called him essentially heroic. White, male, Christian, anti-war, an enemy to the Biden regime. She said he told the truth about troops being on the ground in Ukraine and a lot more.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: First off, let`s be clear, there are - there are no U.S. troops on - on the ground in Ukraine, other than there are troops that are normally at an embassy protecting the embassy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: We do not have the boots on the ground.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They`re not on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: We do not have - have - have troops on the ground. So it`s absolutely incorrect assumption from the documents that - that this individual leaked.\par \par \uc2 The other aspect is, he`s guilty of - of -- if he`s brought through this process and he`s found guilty, it will be of espionage. It`s of being a traitor to your country. That`s not someone who -- to look up to. That is someone who has compromised his country and has certainly compromised our allies. That`s not the oath that he took. That`s not the job that he took.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 You are in the Gang of Eight, that small group of lawmakers that gets access to some of the most classified information, including the documents that were found at the residents of President Biden, President Trump, and former Vice President Pence. Have you looked at the documents and are your questions answered?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Right. No, so the Department of Justice has not been forthcoming in this. And they`ve been somewhat disingenuous. And certainly both the House and the Senate are going to have to address this. One, the documents that were delivered to Congress are not complete. And, secondly, they don`t identify whose documents they were. Whether they came from the trove of Biden`s behind the Corvette, or whether or not they came from Mar- a-Lago. That, obviously, has to be addressed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You can figure that out through the timing.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Timing ought to be able to tell us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: But, still, at the same time, to deliver those documents without even designating whose documents they were clearly shows, you know, a -- an unwillingness to be -- work closely with Congress.\par \par \uc2 And this also it`s incomplete. I can tell you this, in the reviews that we`ve had so far of indexes that do include the documents, there`s no nuclear codes here. There`s no -- no one had anything that - that - that was of an extreme imminent threat to the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Have you seen everything or in the --\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: We`ve seen the - the index of them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: We`ve gotten some of the documents delivered to us. But the Department of Justice really needs to - to come clean. They need to deliver the documents to Congress. They promised them to us. And they - they need to work with us so that we can get an assessment of what happened here.\par \par \uc2 There are laws that need to be changed so that we can more protect our classified documents and those who handle them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: And so we - we need them to work with us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The White House gave access to the classified after action report on Afghanistan about a week ago. Have you seen it yet?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: I have.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And?\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: The -- well, so the -- I`m -- I`m very concerned that the Biden administration is looking more for fault, blame -- and blame than really action items as to what we need to do.\par \par \uc2 What - what clearly happened here in the abrupt departure from Afghanistan is a number of mistakes were made. We can only make certain that we don`t repeat those mistakes if we`re able to -- to really understand them. Congress has put together an Afghan commission that is reviewing our time there and our exit. I think that`s going to be a very helpful avenue also of getting an understanding of what happened and how do we not do this again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman, it`s good to have you here. There was a lot to get through.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we hope to have you back soon.\par \par \uc2 MIKE TURNER: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re joined now by \uc2 Christine Lagarde\uc2 , former head of the \uc2 IMF\uc2 , now the president of the \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2  (President, \uc2 European Central Bank\uc2 ): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : Lovely to be back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And your recovery is going all right?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : Yes. In a couple of days I think I`ll be fine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m glad to hear that.\par \par \uc2 You have a long list of things ahead of you and I want to ask you about the global recovery.\par \par \uc2 You were speaking a few days ago and you said the recovery for the economy is fragile and uncertain. In this country the Fed thinks we`ll see a mild recession later this year. What is it that you predict?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : First of all, there is recovery. That`s, I think, a point that was not really firm only six months ago where we all assumed that there would be a recession, if only a technical one. If you look at all the forecasts at the moment, it`s all positive. It`s been slightly downgrades, but overall we have a recovery. And we are faced with high uncertainty because of multiple factors. You know, from our corners of the world, it`s the war in Ukraine, it`s the financial stability that clearly has been shaken up a bit by the U.S. and Switzerland development. It`s inflation that we are fighting. It`s all that which really create a hollow of uncertainty around a recovery that we want to embed. That`s pretty much where we are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, there were those recent bank failures here in the United States. Also one in Switzerland. Given that, it sounds like you`re saying you don`t see a hard landing. You`re seeing a positive trajectory for the global economy?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : I think we have a narrow path to navigate, which requires that both the governments and the central banks around the world adopt the right policies.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Given the bank failures we just saw, you hear from bank CEOs in this country this idea that they`re getting more cautious about lending money largely.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That there`s some contraction in credit there. How concerned are you and how does that complicate your planning?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : It`s funny you should ask complication, because in a way it facilitates my planning and it complicates the future as far as growth.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because it slows down business activity so you don`t have to raise rates as much or as frequently?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : Yes. Yes. We don`t have to reduce. We -- we`ll see because we need to really measure what will come out of this - this financial events that took place recently. What impact will it have? How will banks react? How will they assess risk and how much credit will they lend?\par \par \uc2 But if they don`t lend too much credit, and if they manage their risk, it might reduce the work that we have to do to reduce inflation. OK. But if they reduce too much credit, then it will weigh on growth excessively.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are predictions that the U.S. could default on its national debt as soon as June, some say September. And we have a political standoff in this country. Virtually no negotiation happening on how to resolve this. Does that undermine your confidence in the United States, and - and what message does that send to the world?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : I have huge confidence in the United States. You know, ever since my year in this country, in this city, in `73-`74, I have had confidence in this country. And I just cannot believe that they would let such a major, major disaster happen of the United States defaulting on its debt. This is not possible. I cannot believe that it would happen. But if it did happen, it would have very, very negative impact, not just for this country, where confidence would be challenged, but around the world. Let`s face it, this is the largest economy. It`s -- it`s a major leader in economic growth around the world. It cannot let that happen.\par \par \uc2 I understand the politics. I`ve been in politics myself. But there is a time when the higher interest of a nation has to prevail. I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you think that will happen? The higher (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : I have huge trust in this country yet again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re bringing a lot of optimism to a show where we don`t have a lot of optimism, Madam Lagarde.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE: Oh, I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, I like it. It`s interesting. It`s a change.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you, though, about what you just said in terms of U.S. leadership. You look to the other side of the globe and \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  has said he wants China to be the world`s leading power by 2049. And Beijing is very interlinked into so many economies, particularly in Europe. Is the U.S. losing global influence?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : There is clearly a competition between these - these large economies. What I hope very much is that they can have a dialog because, you know, all these relationships, whether it`s trade, whether it`s politics, whether it`s economic development, whether it is financial stability, it`s a two-way street. We cannot ignore each other and trade should not be confrontational. It has to be careful, it has to identify the areas that are strategic for one country or the other, or all the others, but it shouldn`t be confrontational. I`m on the same page as Henry Kessinger on that, or Kevin Rudd, the new Australian ambassador. Conflict is not unavoidable.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But there is, it seems, increased political pressure to choose between the United States and China in many ways in some of these political capitals. Is that even practical from an economic point of view?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : It would lead to economic downside. The - the amount of which is uncertain. Is the global economy going to be affected by one or x percent? There are multiple forecasts. All of them are negative.\par \par \uc2 So, the decoupling and the sort of depolarization (ph) of the world would lead to less economic growth, less prosperity in the world, more poverty across the world. So I think that this is something that should be by all means avoided.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Madam Lagarde, it`s always wonderful to have you here.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE LAGARDE\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. Until next, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230416ej4g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Supreme Court Says It Is Temporarily Keeping In Place Federal Rules For The Use Of An Abortion Drug\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Caitlin Huey-Burns \par \uc2 413 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, there is more breaking news tonight on abortion rights. The Supreme Court is allowing a key abortion medication to remain widely available, at least for a few days, as the justices review a lower court ruling.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Caitlin Huey-Burns is following these late departments.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: Are you ready to fight?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS (voiceover): Americans will have full access to the abortion medication mifepristone until at least next Wednesday, as the Supreme Court temporarily halted a court order banning distribution.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  blocked a Texas federal judge`s ruling, which would have suspended the FDA`s approval of the medication, buying the High Court time to decide whether to weigh in.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, the Department of Justice urged the Supreme Court to intervene, warning of sweeping consequences. This week, the Fifth Circuit Court of Appeals partially paused the Texas ruling, but put limits on the drug, halting mail delivery, shortening the time frame for use, and requiring multiple doctor`s visits.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the fate of Mifepristone remains in limbo. The drug has been approved for use for over two decades, and accounts for more than half of the abortions in the United States. It is also used to manage miscarriages. Legal experts say clarity from the High Court is needed, or else access to the drug will depend on which state you live in.\par \par \uc2 GREER DONLEY (\uc2 University of Pittsburgh\uc2 ): We are talking about a truly enormous amount of chaos. The amount of confusion that this really potentially could bring the whole country is fairly profound.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Recent polling shows the majority of Americans say abortion medication should be legal in their states.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, new restrictions are adding to an urgency over the decision. In Florida, the governor signed a six-week abortion ban into law just last night -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Caitlin Huey-Burns, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230418ej4e0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Florida`s DeSantis Signs Six-Week Abortion Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Dana Jacobson, Nancy Cordes, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 741 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Florida is the latest State to approve severe restrictions on abortion access. Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed a bill to establish a six-week limit on abortions if the State`s current 15-week law is upheld in Court.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Biden administration wants the Supreme Court to get involved in a legal challenge to a commonly used abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the Supreme Court with more.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Dana.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Florida is a great example of how quickly and dramatically State laws are changing in the wake of the Supreme Court`s decision last year to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Women in Florida, the nation`s third most populous State will now only be able to get abortions within the first six weeks after conception, a point at which many women still don`t even know they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Amid protests in Tallahassee, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed a six-week abortion ban late Thursday night. It`s called the Heartbeat Protection Act.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have the opportunity to save lives.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are creating a death sentence for women.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Florida now joins the ranks of a dozen Republican led States that have enacted near total bans on abortion in the 10 months since the Supreme Court undid nearly 50 years of Federal protections, and GOP lawmakers aren`t stopping there.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, Republicans are pushing to block websites that provide information about abortions. Just this month, Idaho made it a felony to help a minor get an abortion out of state.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA): Women should have the freedom to make decisions over their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Now several Democrat-led States are stockpiling abortion medication, the latest front in this fight.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Last year, the attacks were on abortion procedures. This year, medication abortion. What`s next? Contraception? Birth control?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: All eyes are now back on the Supreme Court after a conservative Federal Appeals Court panel imposed new restrictions Wednesday on access to the abortion drug, mifepristone, blocking mail delivery of the pill, shrinking the window in which women can take the medication from 10 weeks down to seven and requiring in-person doctor`s visits.\par \par \uc2 ERIK BAPTIST (\uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  senior counsel): It is going to benefit America`s women and girls across this country.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: How is it going to benefit them?\par \par \uc2 ERIK BAPTIST: They`re going to have protections again having to go and meet with a doctor beforehand to determine whether she has life threatening complications before taking these drugs is essential.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Mifepristone is used to carry out more than half of all abortions in this country. It was approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  23 years ago.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Mifepristone is one of the safest medications we have. It went through not just a normal \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval process, but was really held to a higher standard.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Polling finds more than six in 10 Americans think abortion should be legal in all or most cases. Those numbers have a few Republicans like South Carolina Congresswoman, Nancy Mace pushing for a more moderate approach with later gestational limits and exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): I see the pulse of everyday Americans on both sides of the aisle, men and women alike, and they`re not with us. They`re not with the extreme far-right crazy nut jobs that want to go to these extreme places.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Justice Department has appealed these new abortion pill restrictions to the Supreme Court, but if the nine Justices don`t weigh in by the end of the day today, those restrictions will start to go into effect across the country tomorrow -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nancy Cordes, reporting for us. Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230418ej4e00002\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Air National Guardsman Arrested in Leak Case; Florida`s DeSantis Signs Six-Week Abortion Ban; Abortion Pill Fight; Report: Donor Bought Property from Justice Thomas; North Korea Test Fires New Missile; Arrest in Murder of Tech Executive; Flooding Emergency in Florida; Derek Chauvin`s History of Violence; Pence, Trump to speak at NRA Convention; Caitlyn Smith`s High and Low; Christy and Hilda`s Last Dance; Say Hello to Brittney Spencer; Emily Hampshire Live; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Dana Jacobson, Catherine  Herridge, Nancy Cordes, Ramy Inocencio, Jonathan Vigliotti, Manuel  Bojorquez, Lilia Luciano, Robert Costa, Scott Pelley \par \uc2 12,598 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Friday, April 14th. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And I`m Dana Jacobson.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle and Nate are off, but we`re in good hands. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 A 21-year-old Air National Guardsman is under arrest this morning for allegedly leaking classified documents in a video game chatroom.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK S. RYDER (Department of Defense Press Secretary): We do have stringent guidelines in place. This was a deliberate criminal act.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Big developments in the murder investigation of Cash App founder, Bob Lee. A fellow tech executive is arrested.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We can confirm that Mr. Lee and Mr. Momeni knew each other.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed a new law that will highly restrict abortion access. It prohibits abortions after six weeks of pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Scenes of devastation in Florida after a once in a thousand year rain event brings catastrophic flooding.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It just kept raining and raining and the water just kept coming up and up and up.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A new report shows \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  did not disclose he sold three properties to Republican megadonor, Harlan Crow.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A passenger train slammed into a truck carrying cars.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh my, God.\par \par \uc2 MAN: . hit up the left side.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And the Rays have exploded here in the fifth inning.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Tampa Bay Rays\uc2  now tie for the best start in MLB history.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Thirteen streak to begin the season.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): President Biden continued his tour of Ireland today meeting with President Michael D. Higgins.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Higgins cemented their friendship with a tree planting ceremony.\par \par \uc2 It seems like a lot of fanfare for something so small, oh, look at that. My tie is crooked.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. I want that sound to play when I open the fridge.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I was looking for crooked ties on either one of you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve come home.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking good. Dana is here, we appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I want that sound.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Tu-tu-tu-tu.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got people who can make that happen. We`ve got a big show for you to close out the week here at CBS MORNINGS, including a peek into the future, no joke here, "60 Minutes" exclusive access to \uc2 Google\uc2 `s Artificial Intelligence Lab for a look at technology that has never been seen before, technology that creates video out of words. This is something that could truly, no joke, redefine our lives, and it is on a scale that has not -- that we have not seen since the start of the internet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Plus country singer, Caitlyn Smith started out writing songs for others before she eventually found her own voice.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Great Pretender.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Many of us have been there, crying in the bathroom stall. That`s her song "The Great Pretender" off her deeply personal new album. She sat down with our own country superfan, Jan Crawford.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to that, but we are going to begin with the news of dramatic developments in the search for a suspect in the leak of the highly sensitive Pentagon documents.\par \par \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents arrested a 21-year-old Air National Guardsman. His name is Jack Teixeira at his home in Massachusetts. That`s him, hands on his head in the red shorts there.\par \par \uc2 The case is raising serious questions about how something like this could happen and also remain undetected for months. Senior investigative correspondent, Catherine Herridge is at The Pentagon for us.\par \par \uc2 Catherine, as of Monday, they had no idea where these documents were coming from. Now Friday, they have an arrest.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 The suspect has an initial appearance in a Boston Court a short time from now after fast-moving events Thursday culminated in his arrest.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: News crews captured the arrest live showing heavily armed Federal agents taking the suspect into custody Thursday outside his mother`s home south of Boston.\par \par \uc2 The suspect is identified as Massachusetts Air National Guardsman, Jack Teixeira. He is accused of leaking hundreds of pages of classified military documents and CIA updates, including on the war in Ukraine, some revealing how the US gathers Intelligence in both allies and adversaries.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2  (Former US Secretary of Defense): Clearly, there was harm done.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Former Secretary of Defense \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  told CBS News that the security questions are urgent.\par \par \uc2 You can`t think of a good reason for a 21-year-old to have access to this information.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : No, I cannot think of a compelling reason and I assume Secretary Austin and Chairman the Joint Chiefs Milley are probably asking those same questions.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Teixeira installed fiber optic cable and that tech job may have allowed him to monitor networks through which classified intelligence flowed.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. PATRICK S. RYDER: This was a deliberate criminal act.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: The Pentagon first discovered highly classified material posted on social media a week ago, though the leak apparently began late last year when the documents surfaced in a chat group on the gaming platform, Discord.\par \par \uc2 President Biden was briefed Thursday evening about the arrest and commented earlier Thursday during his visit to Ireland --\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I am concerned that it happened. There is nothing contemporaneous that I`m aware of that is of great concern.\par \par \uc2 REP. ABIGAIL SPANBERGER (D-VA): It`s wildly embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , a former CIA officer who now sits on the House Intelligence Committee said the records posted online are revealing.\par \par \uc2 REP. ABIGAIL SPANBERGER (D-VA): We`ll never be able to pull all of that information back from the internet wiped from a place where our adversaries will be able to see it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: After the arrest, Defense Secretary Austin ordered an investigation into how the highly classified materials circulated online for months without The Pentagon detecting it.\par \par \uc2 Teixeira was arrested in connection with the alleged unauthorized removal, retention, and transmission of classified Defense information. His lawyer is not commenting at this time -- Dana.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right, Catherine, still so many questions out there. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Florida is the latest State to approve severe restrictions on abortion access. Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed a bill to establish a six- week limit on abortions if the State`s current 15-week law is upheld in Court.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Biden administration wants the Supreme Court to get involved in a legal challenge to a commonly used abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes is at the Supreme Court with more.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning, Dana.\par \par \uc2 Yes, Florida is a great example of how quickly and dramatically State laws are changing in the wake of the Supreme Court`s decision last year to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Women in Florida, the nation`s third most populous State will now only be able to get abortions within the first six weeks after conception, a point at which many women still don`t even know they`re pregnant.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: My body, my choice.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Amid protests in Tallahassee, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signed a six-week abortion ban late Thursday night. It`s called the Heartbeat Protection Act.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have the opportunity to save lives.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are creating a death sentence for women.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Florida now joins the ranks of a dozen Republican led States that have enacted near total bans on abortion in the 10 months since the Supreme Court undid nearly 50 years of Federal protections, and GOP lawmakers aren`t stopping there.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, Republicans are pushing to block websites that provide information about abortions. Just this month, Idaho made it a felony to help a minor get an abortion out of state.\par \par \uc2 GOV. JOSH SHAPIRO (D-PA): Women should have the freedom to make decisions over their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Now several Democrat-led States are stockpiling abortion medication, the latest front in this fight.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Last year, the attacks were on abortion procedures. This year, medication abortion. What`s next? Contraception? Birth control?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: All eyes are now back on the Supreme Court after a conservative Federal Appeals Court panel imposed new restrictions Wednesday on access to the abortion drug, mifepristone, blocking mail delivery of the pill, shrinking the window in which women can take the medication from 10 weeks down to seven and requiring in-person doctor`s visits.\par \par \uc2 ERIK BAPTIST (\uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2  senior counsel): It is going to benefit America`s women and girls across this country.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: How is it going to benefit them?\par \par \uc2 ERIK BAPTIST: They`re going to have protections again having to go and meet with a doctor beforehand to determine whether she has life threatening complications before taking these drugs is essential.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Mifepristone is used to carry out more than half of all abortions in this country. It was approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  23 years ago.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Mifepristone is one of the safest medications we have. It went through not just a normal \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval process, but was really held to a higher standard.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Polling finds more than six in 10 Americans think abortion should be legal in all or most cases. Those numbers have a few Republicans like South Carolina Congresswoman, Nancy Mace pushing for a more moderate approach with later gestational limits and exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY MACE (R-SC): I see the pulse of everyday Americans on both sides of the aisle, men and women alike, and they`re not with us. They`re not with the extreme far-right crazy nut jobs that want to go to these extreme places.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The Justice Department has appealed these new abortion pill restrictions to the Supreme Court, but if the nine Justices don`t weigh in by the end of the day today, those restrictions will start to go into effect across the country tomorrow -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Nancy Cordes, reporting for us. Nancy, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A new report says a wealthy Republican donor owns the house where Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` mother lives. He bought it from Thomas and his family and the news organization, \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  says the Justice never reported it as required by law.\par \par \uc2 It is the first confirmation that Thomas directly received money from the influential man that he calls a close friend.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Conservative Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is under scrutiny for the second time this month after \uc2 ProPublica\uc2  reported on undisclosed financial ties to Republican megadonor, Harlan Crow.\par \par \uc2 CBS News can confirm \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 `s reporting that Crow`s company purchased properties belonging to Thomas and members of his family in his hometown of Savannah, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 The undisclosed 2014 deal included the purchase of a home and two vacant lots for more than $130,000.00. That made Crow the owner of the home Thomas` mother lived in that later underwent tens of thousands of dollars` worth of renovations.\par \par \uc2 But Thomas didn`t report the sale despite a Federal disclosure law partly designed to help avoid conflicts of interest. That law indicates justices should report most real estate deals over $1,000.00.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA KAPLAN (\uc2 ProPublica\uc2  reporter): The experts told us it appears that Thomas very clearly violated law.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Joshua Kaplan is one of the reporters who wrote the story.\par \par \uc2 JOSHUA KAPLAN: I think one of the issues here is that the Supreme Court has very, very little transparency and very, very little oversight compared to essentially every other part of the Federal government and the Justices have been essentially almost entirely left to police themselves.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Crow said in a statement that he bought it to one day create a public museum at the Thomas home. This revelation comes on the heels of a report last week, Thomas accepted luxury vacations over the past two decades paid for by Crow.\par \par \uc2 In a statement following that report, Thomas said he was advised that this sort of personal hospitality from close personal friends who did not have business before the Court was not reportable.\par \par \uc2 Senate Democrats in support of more transparency from the Court like Rhode Island`s Sheldon Whitehouse are calling on Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  to launch an ethics investigation and refer Thomas to the Attorney General for investigation.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Jan Crawford, Washington.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Turning overseas now to the latest provocation from North Korea. The country`s leadership says it tested a new kind of intercontinental ballistic missile, one using solid fuel instead of liquid which could make it quicker to launch.\par \par \uc2 Overnight, the US and South Korea apparently, in a countermove conducted joint air drills over the Korean Peninsula including F-16 fighters and a B- 52 Bomber. Impressive pictures there.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio looks at what it all means. I want to know as well, Ramy. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Hey, Tony. Yes, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Solid fuel missiles have really been a key goal of North Korea for a very long time, and North Korean state media did broadcast the launch of that new missile set to propaganda music from an undisclosed location.\par \par \uc2 Now, these kinds of missiles can be deployed faster, easier, and also more safely. Solid fuel also doesn`t degrade as fast. That means they can be fueled long before launch. So that means spy satellites might have a tougher time detecting them.\par \par \uc2 Images also appear to show Kim Jong-un watching the launch and even smiling. Other images show him with his daughter, Kim Ju-ae featured prominently. And it`s important to note that anything Pyongyang does here for the public is choreographed.\par \par \uc2 One North Korea analyst says Kim may be trying to portray an aura of stability to the people by showing off his family and that he may be aiming to produce solid fuel missiles that can eventually fly 6,000 miles and Dana, that is just shy of New York and Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: All right, Ramy. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The suspect arrested for the stabbing death of a well-known Silicon Valley executive is due in Court today in San Francisco. Last week`s murder of Bob Lee brought a flood of anguish and accusations that San Francisco has become a lawless and dangerous city.\par \par \uc2 It turns out that Lee, like most murder victims knew the person who is now accused of killing him. Sources tell CBS News, they were seen together on the night of the murder.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti reports from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: More than a week after police discovered Cash App founder, Bob Lee bleeding from multiple stab wounds on a San Francisco sidewalk, investigators now have a suspect in custody.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Come out with your hands up.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Nima Momeni, a tech consultant was picked up by police at his home Thursday morning. He is facing one charge of murder.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We can confirm that Mr. Lee and Mr. Momeni knew each other.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: A source told \uc2 CBS\uc2  there is video that shows the two men leaving a building together and it`s believed that Lee was in a \uc2 BMW\uc2  driven by Momeni prior to the stabbing.\par \par \uc2 An industry leader in mobile payment services at the time of his death, Lee was the Chief Product Officer of the cryptocurrency firm, MobileCoin.\par \par \uc2 Momeni owned a cybersecurity company called Expand IT. Lee`s violent death spurred prominent tech leaders to take to social media to criticize San Francisco leaders approach to crime. One of those critics, \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 San Francisco District Attorney Brooke Jenkins pushed back.\par \par \uc2 BROOKE JENKINS (San Francisco District Attorney): Reckless and irresponsible statements like those contained in Mr. Musk`s tweet that assumed incorrect circumstances about Mr. Lee`s death serve to mislead the world in their perceptions of San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: According to a city survey of 2,500 residents, which was just released yesterday, San Francisco`s safety received the lowest marks in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: And the motive behind this attack remains unclear this morning as investigators continue to piece together exactly what unfolded that night.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the alleged attacker will be arraigned in Court later this morning -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: More to come in the investigation. Jonathan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 This morning, a State of Emergency is still in effect for parts of South Florida after a once in a thousand-year flood event soaked the Fort Lauderdale area shutting down the airport and schools and trapping people in their cars. The storm dropped more than two feet of rain.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez is in Fort Lauderdale.\par \par \uc2 Manny, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Good morning, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 Here we are nearly two days after that record rainfall and there are parts of the city that still have this much standing water and there were downpours again yesterday preventing this water from more quickly receding.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: There are rocks right here.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Rescue teams spent Thursday going door-to-door looking for stranded families in some of Fort Lauderdale`s hardest hit neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I am doing the best we can with what we have.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: First responders received nearly 1,000 calls for help after more than 20 inches of rain Wednesday caused catastrophic flooding in some parts of the city.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I am okay. Just a little scratch, but I am okay.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Volunteers stepped in to help with the rescues, some using swamp buggies to take families to safety. Some residents could be seen wading through the water lugging their belongings in boxes wrapped in plastic to keep them dry.\par \par \uc2 One local fire department had to pull this abandoned car out of a small pond after it got trapped during flash floods. And the roof of this shopping center is thought to have collapsed under the weight of all the excess water. There are no reports of injuries.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The amount of rainfall is unprecedented.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Some of the most intense flooding was at the Fort Lauderdale Airport, which was forced to shut down operations after more than two feet of water flooded runways canceling more than 800 flights.\par \par \uc2 DAN LAGARES (Traveler): We finally get off the exit to the airport and it said "Airport Closed."\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: The Lagares family was among the thousands of stranded passengers Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 You spent the night here?\par \par \uc2 DAN LAGARES: We spent the night here, all night, at the terminal.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: And when do you get to go home?\par \par \uc2 DAN LAGARES: We don`t know.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: I checked in on them last night, they said they were able to get a rental car, which is hard to do right now and drive the two hours to Fort Myers. They are hoping to get on a noon flight today -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We wish them the best and everyone there dealing with all of that water in South Florida.\par \par \uc2 Manny, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in just a moment, newly revealed video shows more violent behavior by the police officer convicted of killing \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 . We`ll tell you how the City of Minneapolis is responding.\par \par \uc2 But first, 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up, "60 Minutes" reveals never before seen AI technology from \uc2 Google\uc2  that can turn text into video. See how \uc2 Google\uc2  is preparing for the potential danger of realistic looking fakes.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dreamin`s Free.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Still ahead, country singer-songwriter, Caitlyn Smith tells our Jan Crawford about some of her most personal music yet.\par \par \uc2 Local news is next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. The City of Minneapolis is paying nearly $9 million to settle two lawsuits alleging more violent behavior from the officer who went on to kill \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Derek Chauvin is currently serving a 22-year sentence for his role in Floyd`s death, and a 21-year sentence for violating Floyd`s rights. Now, bodycam footage has been released from two more violent arrests back in 2017 amid allegations his actions against Floyd were part of a pattern.\par \par \uc2 Lilia Luciano has more. A warning: The footage is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ZOYA CODE (Arrested in Minneapolis in 2017): This is how Black people get treated.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Zoya Code was arrested in Minneapolis in June of 2017, accused of attacking her mother.\par \par \uc2 ZOYA CODE: Take my glasses off.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Newly released video shows Derek Chauvin, the same officer convicted for the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  dragging and carrying her while she is handcuffed, her arms outstretched behind her back.\par \par \uc2 ZOYA CODE: That`s how you`re going to slam me on the ground.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Chauvin slams Code, face first on the ground and kneels on her neck for four minutes and 42 seconds. The same move he used three years later for eight minutes and 46 seconds with \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They were not the only ones. Another plaintiff is John Pope, Jr. In 2017, he was 14 years old.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER WALLS (Former officer): You`re under arrest, so stand up.\par \par \uc2 JOHN POPE (Plaintiff): I`m not under arrest.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: When Chauvin and other officers went into his bedroom, Pope pleaded with officers alleging his mother was drunk when she called law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 JOHN POPE: You can`t touch me in my own house.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Get up now.\par \par \uc2 JOHN POPE: You can`t touch me in my own house.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Former officer Chauvin and Alexander Walls violently wrestled Pope to the ground. Chauvin almost immediately grabbed Pope by the neck and struck him with his flashlight.\par \par \uc2 JOHN POPE: You are hurting me.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Then a chokehold and a knee to the neck, not for four or eight, but for 15 minutes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN POPE: I did fear for my life and my safety throughout the encounter.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Pope says the experience is still with him, even after the $7.5 million settlement. Now, he`s in college studying Criminal Justice in the hopes that he can bring about change.\par \par \uc2 JOHN POPE: The system has a major flaw in it that needs work to be done, and it is not going to get done just talking about it. It is going to get done with solutions.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Two years after the killing of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  in 2022, a report by the State`s Department of Human Rights concluded that the Minneapolis Police Department creates a culture that results in unnecessary escalation and/or excessive force during encounters.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN O`HARA (Minneapolis Police Chief): We are dealing with the ugly consequences stemming from a systemic failure within the Minneapolis Police Department.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: Legal analyst Joe Tamburino says these lawsuits show how those acknowledgments came far too late.\par \par \uc2 JOE TAMBURINO (Legal analyst): If the Minneapolis Police Department would have paid attention to excessive use of force by officers like Mr. Chauvin back in 2017 and years before that, then Mr. Floyd likely would not have been murdered.\par \par \uc2 LILIA LUCIANO: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Lilia Luciana.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The footage speaks for itself. I mean, you can see it, we can all see it with our own eyes, his actions and I think what that gentleman just said if the Minneapolis Police Department had been investigating, or at least been aware, and if they were aware, had done something about this, \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  might be alive.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: You also wonder what can other departments across the country learn from this? And you look at that bodycam footage, is that being looked at in other departments? Whether it`s called for or not to look at video like that and say, we may have a problem here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When police do something on a call and no one says anything about it, it becomes -- there`s a sense that Derek Chauvin thought that was okay, that was standard practice and then it leads to the conclusion we`ve all seen and covered for years now.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now, he is behind bars.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In other news, the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2  begins its Annual Convention today testing the organization`s support at a time when deadly mass shootings remain in the headlines.\par \par \uc2 Delegates are scheduled to hear from top political figures including former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his former Vice President.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa is at the convention site in Indianapolis and joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 This capital city in a Red State is now a crossroads for the national gun debate, as well as for the 2024 Republican presidential race and this event comes just days after a gunman killed five people in downtown Louisville and less than three weeks after a shooter killed three children and three adults at a Nashville school.\par \par \uc2 Here, many \uc2 NRA\uc2  attendees have told us they believe those events are tragic, but say the problem isn`t too many guns and argue the legislative focus should be on mental health and on arming teachers, a view sure to be echoed on stage today, and this weekend.\par \par \uc2 Headliners include former President Trump and former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , both champions of gun rights, but this convention comes as Trump faces mounting legal challenges, including the likely testimony from Pence about Trump`s efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, in Nashville, the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  will be meeting there this weekend for a fundraising event, all as the debate continues in Tennessee over the recent expulsion of two Black Democratic lawmakers who had been calling for more gun control legislation -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bob, I know you`ve a lot of politics to cover there, but you should check out the exhibits as well. I`ve covered the \uc2 NRA\uc2 , 14 acres of guns and gear as it says on the slide behind you. It is a view of America.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, "60 Minutes" gives us an inside look at \uc2 Google\uc2 `s cutting edge AI technology including a way for users to create videos out of words.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This week, "60 Minutes" explores the potential dangers and possibilities created by recent dramatic advances in Artificial Intelligence.\par \par \uc2 The AI market is expected to become a more than $42 billion industry this year, according to the company PitchBook, and AI like ChatGPT can learn or appear to learn based on the stuff that you feed into it.\par \par \uc2 "60 Minutes" correspondent, Scott Pelley was given access to \uc2 Google\uc2 `s campus in California and their AI Lab in London to see their new technology and also meet with the CEO.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a preview.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: How great a risk is the spread of disinformation?\par \par \uc2 SUNDAR PICHAI (\uc2 Google\uc2  CEO): AI will challenge that in a deeper way. The scale of this problem is going to be much bigger.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Bigger problems \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2  says with fake news and fake images.\par \par \uc2 We saw the potential with \uc2 Google\uc2 `s experimental AI that creates pictures out of words.\par \par \uc2 This is text in pictures.\par \par \uc2 ELI COLLINS (\uc2 Google\uc2  Vice President of Product Research): Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Eli Collins is the Vice President at \uc2 Google\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ELI COLLINS: You can generate a picture of pretty much of most things you imagine and I`m happy to put it in a prompt if you`ve got something.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Let`s try pink taxicabs on Fifth Avenue.\par \par \uc2 ELI COLLINS: All right, pink taxi cabs.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: Instantly, we saw a shade unknown on New York cabs. Next, Collins gave us a first look at an AI system \uc2 Google\uc2  has not released yet, experimental text to video, which instantly answered our prompt, golden retriever with wings.\par \par \uc2 Oh, look at that.\par \par \uc2 As with \uc2 Google\uc2 `s chatbot, Bard, this has safety filters. For example, it doesn`t create images of people,\par \par \uc2 SUNDAR PICHAI: It will be possible with AI to create, you know a video easily where it could be Scott saying something or me saying something and we never said that and it could look accurate.\par \par \uc2 But you know, at a societal scale, it can cause a lot of harm. There are deeper risks people worry about, you know, which is at some point that humanity lose control of the technology it is developing? SO those are some of the far out use cases, which we need to think about as early as possible and get it right.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can watch Scott Pelley`s report on Sunday on "60 Minutes" right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 . I will be watching, you bet your butt I will be.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad has got the stories you`ll be talking about. I don`t know why I said it that way. That`s how it came out.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Bottom dollar, but same thing. That`s all right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think -- I hope we could say that on TV, first, it is 7:43, and we went right to commercial break, just like I promised.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Vlad in some rare Friday --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: There you go. Oh, he`s got the moves.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s walk that thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bold dance move.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Dance moves.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: James Brown, it is Friday. Godfather soul, I`m ready to bring some "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 Let`s begin with football. According to sources, the owner of the \uc2 Washington Commanders\uc2  has agreed to sell the team and guess what? It could be the most expensive sale in \uc2 NFL\uc2  history.\par \par \uc2 Owner, Dan Snyder reached a massive $6 billion deal with businessman, \uc2 Josh Harris\uc2 , the owner of the NBA`s \uc2 Philadelphia 76ers\uc2  and the NHL`s \uc2 New Jersey Devils\uc2 . The buyer group also includes basketball legend, Magic Johnson.\par \par \uc2 Three quarters of \uc2 NFL\uc2  owners have to approve the sale, a vote could happen at an owner`s meeting that is scheduled for next month.\par \par \uc2 If approved, it would break the record for the highest price paid for a North American professional sports team. Snyder bought the Commanders in 1999 for $750 million, so he would make a 700 percent profit.\par \par \uc2 Dan Snyder, crazy allegations about the way that he ran the organization. Dana, what`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: There is no surprise that this is happening now, and I think a lot of people feel like the \uc2 NFL\uc2  will be better off if Snyder is no longer there.\par \par \uc2 It is the interesting thing, though, that he makes a lot of money in doing this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Well, I was going to say, 700 percent profit. Did the Commanders gets 700 percent better under his --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: No, but this is what happens. Our economy has certainly changed.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I mean, it will be really nice to see Magic Johnson in that ownership group and seeing more minority ownership in the \uc2 NFL\uc2  circle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So this is a good thing.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s a good thing.\par \par \uc2 All right, baseball`s \uc2 Tampa Bay Rays\uc2  have been unbeatable. This is an incredible winning streak, it looked like it was about to end yesterday and then this happened.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Here is Harold Ramirez, and a ground ball base sent up the left side, down into the corner it goes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Rays scored seven runs in the fifth inning to take the lead. They beat the \uc2 Boston Red Sox\uc2  nine to three finishing a four-game sweep at Tropicana Field.\par \par \uc2 Get this -- the Rays have won their first 13 games of the season. They joined the 1987 \uc2 Milwaukee Brewers\uc2  and the 1982 \uc2 Atlanta Braves\uc2  as the only teams in the modern era to open the year with 13 straight wins. The Rays can break that record tonight in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 By the way, the Brewers finished third in 1987. The Braves won their division in 1982, but did not make the World Series.\par \par \uc2 Like that streak, I`m not a sports like guy like you guys are, but I know my sports history and I was looking into this. If they have 14, like there`s no other team in the history of Major League Baseball. You have to go back to like the 19 Century.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is amazing. Baseball is a crazy game. It`s hard to win that many in a row.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It is. It just doesn`t mean you`re going to win the World Series, which is what matters.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. That`s true.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, I know. Sorry, guys, sorry, but it is what it is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Go Rays. Go Rays.\par \par \uc2 All right, I want to follow up on a story that we told you about last December. You both know as reporters sometimes because we cover so much, it is hard to keep track of all the voices and all the people that we speak to in our careers.\par \par \uc2 But last year, we recorded a Note to Self with Isaiah Marquez-Greene. He lost his sister, Anna in the 2012 Sandy Hook school shooting in Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 In his Note to Self, his younger self, he spoke passionately about being so much more than a Sandy Hook shooting survivor, and he said he was determined to go on to practice law to help families like his.\par \par \uc2 Last night, Isaiah and others were invited down to the ice to meet \uc2 New York Rangers\uc2  players as part of the Madison Square Garden, Garden of Dreams Program.\par \par \uc2 Isaiah, who is a prep school hockey player got a huge surprise when Rangers Captain, Jacob Trouba, asked to talk to him after the ceremony. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA (Rangers Captain): Take a seat.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE (Lost his sister, Anna in the 2012 Sandy Hook school shooting): All right.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: I am going to talk to you.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: All right.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: I know your story.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: I feel for you. You`re an amazing human.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: What do you want to do? Do you know?\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: I want to be a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: This is you, from the Garden of Dreams.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: What`s this?\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: It is scholarship for law school.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Oh, no way.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: So you`re going to graduate from college. Here we go, Law School. You`ve got no debt coming out of school.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: You`ve got a phone?\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: I`m going to give you my number.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: No way?\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: You`re not going to give it to anybody else.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: But I want an invitation to when you graduate Law School.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: I`m going to check in with you when you go to college next year.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: I will text you.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: You`re amazing, buddy. I`m looking forward to what you accomplish in life.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: You`ve got a lot ahead of you.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Thanks. Hey, good game tonight. Good luck on the finals.\par \par \uc2 JACOB TROUBA: You`re an amazing person. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ISAIAH MARQUEZ-GREENE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What an amazing human, both of them.\par \par \uc2 The Rangers` Garden of Dreams Inspire Scholarship, gifted Isaiah $60,000.00 for school. He is going to \uc2 UConn\uc2  in the fall. He`s a top student at Taft High School where he starts as a goalie on the school`s hockey team. And Trouba is basically like, I`m going to be checking in on you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I love that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If you`ve been to Rangers` camp.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . as a player. He played on the hockey team. So hockey is his sport. That`s a very big deal for him, and of course, the money for college is just a parent`s dream as well.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And that number in his cell phone.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. Just don`t give it to anybody else.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, country singer Caitlyn Smith talks with Jan Crawford about her personal new music. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour, country singer-songwriter, Caitlyn Smith, she tells her Jan Crawford about finding her voice in a whole new way during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. This week`s "48 Hours" mystery is about two friends who went out dancing and wound up dead. How social media helped crack the case.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I`m Dana Jacobson. Emily Hampshire is best known for her role on "Schitt`s Creek." Now, she is out with her first graphic novel and she is here to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Love her, love the show. But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 An Air National Guardsmen is arrested and accused of leaking classified information.\par \par \uc2 CATHERINE HERRIDGE: You can`t think of a good reason for a 21-year-old to have access to this information.\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 : No, I cannot think of a compelling reason.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We are creating a death sentence for women.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  signs a bill into law that bans abortions after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We stand for life. We stand with mothers.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Breaking overnight, North Korea says it has tested a new missile.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Solid fuel missiles have really been a key goal of North Korea for a very long time.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is wild out here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fort Lauderdale, Florida cleans up after historic rainfall and flooding.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Gosh, I couldn`t open the door because the water had too much pressure on a door.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : This is for all my Irish ancestors.\par \par \uc2 Fourth one for peace.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And President Biden rings the Peace Bell in Ireland.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: All right, fifth time`s a charm.\par \par \uc2 All right, one more to grow on.\par \par \uc2 Lucky number seven.\par \par \uc2 Crazy Eights.\par \par \uc2 Just come get me when dinner is ready, Jack.\par \par \uc2 Papa Joe`s getting his clang on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope they are not allowing houses near the Peace Bell.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That would drive me crazy.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with a story of vulnerability told through songs.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Lately.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is country singer, Caitlyn Smith`s new song, "Lately" from her album high and low out today.\par \par \uc2 Smith is also one of the industry`s most in-demand songwriters. She has written for artists like Miley Cyrus, Garth Brooks and Dolly Parton.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford recently sat down with Smith to talk about her most vulnerable work yet.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "High.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: For most of her career, it`s been easier for Caitlyn Smith to share life`s highs.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH (Singer-songwriter): The lower side has been harder to be honest about and I challenged myself in this record to step into some of these spaces.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Downtown Baby.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The result of that challenge is Smith`s latest album "High & Low," an autobiographical take on life`s ups and downs.\par \par \uc2 We met recently at the Recording Industry Association of America`s DC headquarters ahead of the album`s release.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: It`s taken me a lot of years to try and shed this thing where I care so much about what other people think. I listened to other people to the ends of the earth instead of look inside and ask, oh, well what do I want? Or what do I think?\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Supernova.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Smith grew up in small town Minnesota. At a young age, she stood out for her powerful singing voice.\par \par \uc2 She eventually moved to Nashville and landed a record deal not for singing, but for writing.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Like I`m Gonna Lose You.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: She quickly became a music hitmaker.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "You Can`t Make Old Friends.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But Smith never lost her dream of performing.\par \par \uc2 After years of writing for others, in 2018, she released her critically acclaimed first album, "Starfire."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from 2018.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: We first met Smith around that album`s debut.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: It`s amazing when you start to say no to more things, how many better yeses come along.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But it hasn`t been easy. Smith was still riding high from her first album`s success when her highly anticipated second album came out, just as the world was shutting down.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: Our release date was March 13, 2020, and so to have that meant --\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Not the best timing.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: Nobody was listening to new music, not the best time.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: During the pandemic Smith, her husband and two young sons moved from Nashville back to Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 Just getting away from Nashville.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: Right.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Writing. Did that change you? I mean, would you have written this record if that hadn`t happened?\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: I don`t think I would have written this specific record had I not been in kind of this isolated space in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 I think going away to Minnesota helped me to get back to who I am, it helped me just step into more of a space of vulnerability in my own life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Great Pretender.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Her album`s new standout song, "The Great Pretender" was a last minute add, written after her record was already done, because of a real-life experience that she had.\par \par \uc2 Do you remember what it was that moment that made you cry?\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: It was at an award show, and someone had said to me, we`re so in your corner, and we are like -- we are your biggest fans, and we`re just waiting for that big thing to pop, and we believe in you.\par \par \uc2 And so, it was an encouraging sentiment, but I think what got me is like, I`m not where I want to be yet.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: It`s like, they were trying to be kind, but I was like, oh, that kind of hurts. Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And how do you then get through that?\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: Where I feel like my former self would then go try and pretend everything`s okay.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: Right? I think living in a more transparent way of, hey, today, I am just feeling sad, and being able to say that out loud.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What`s given you the courage to do that?\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: A lot of therapy, good friends, but also realizing that there is nothing to be afraid of in showing who you really are.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: For Smith, part of showing who she is, is taking control of her own career.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: You know, the first verse of like where, "There are fireworks in the rain, cigarettes and gasoline," like that all that kind of imagery was like swimming around in my head.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Maybe in Another Life.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: With this album, she joins a small group of women who have self-produced their record.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: If one woman takes a look at these album credits and sees, hey, she did this by herself. Maybe I can try that. I feel like I`ve done my job.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dreamin`s Free.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You`ve completely taken control of your creative process, your music, your career.\par \par \uc2 CAITLYN SMITH: I mean, growth happens in such tiny, tiny increments, right? I think in the last couple of years, I`ve finally stepped into this space where instead of looking around letting other people tell me, yes, you do belong, rolling my shoulders back and telling myself, you belong.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Dreamin`s Free.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Jan Crawford in Washington, DC.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What a luminous soul. You know, we were talking about this earlier how the pandemic was so devastating for so many people, but for some who were able to sort of regroup, find your roots, find yourself, center yourself and then produce in this case, incredible art, that`s remarkable.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, and to find a voice, find a different voice and be able to open yourself up which is clearly the growth that she had in that time and space and it`s a different voice and then there`s power in that, too. It is really amazing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Such a great performer and such a great writer and now, clearly she`s found her voice for herself and it`s really working.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I like how she says you know, the old me would have reacted this way, and where she is now is very different even though she points out the change is incremental.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It doesn`t happen -- it is not like a huge change, it is just little steps.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There`s a lesson for all of us though, and that and then no one knows him crying in the bathroom stall and the story behind it where someone said, hey, we`re rooting for you, like that really can undermine people`s feeling, like, wait a minute, am I the underdog? You`re rooting for me?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, that`s really just a great piece and Jan Crawford, always very special.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Delivers.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, actor Emily Hampshire will join us in studio to talk about her debut graphic novel and how the Kardashians helped inspire the story.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:09, it is time to check the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Ahead, "48 Hours" investigates a night of partying that turned into a murder investigation.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: I`m Jonathan Vigliotti, "48 Hours."\par \par \uc2 A night out for two friends ends in tragedy after masked men drops them off at two separate hospitals.\par \par \uc2 That`s coming up on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This week, "48 Hours" is looking into the mysterious deaths of two friends in Los Angeles. Investigators say two young women went out for a night of dancing in 2021 and were last seen at a warehouse party.\par \par \uc2 Later, two masked men were seen leaving them outside two different hospitals.\par \par \uc2 CBS News correspondent, Jonathan Vigliotti on assignment for "48 Hours" shows us what happened that night.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: It started as a girls` night out, two friends in their 20s captured on grainy video dancing at a warehouse party in LA. It ended in a murder investigation.\par \par \uc2 Your world was turned upside down.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Shattered.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Jan Cilliers` wife, Christy Giles and her friend Hilda Marcela Cabrales had been dropped off at different hospitals by two masked men in a car without license plates the day after the party. Both would die from a toxic mix of drugs including cocaine and fentanyl, and in Christy`s case, GHB known as the date rape drug.\par \par \uc2 CBS News consultant and former prosecutor, Mary Fulginiti.\par \par \uc2 MARY FULGINITI: That is a drug that`s usually used by sexual predators that want to take advantage of women.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Jan would learn that hours before Christy was dumped at the hospital, her phone had painted an unfamiliar address. Jan posted it on social media and responses came flooding in.\par \par \uc2 JAN CILLIERS (Christy Giles` husband): That somebody that lives at this location that is a very unsavory person.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: It was the home of David Pearce. According to a police affidavit, the women met him at the warehouse and left with him to go to another party. Somehow they ended up at his apartment.\par \par \uc2 MARY FULGINITI: Something or someone stopped them in their tracks because they never got out and they never left.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: When the case hit the media, multiple women came forward to investigators, all with similar stories about their experiences with Pearce.\par \par \uc2 MARY FULGINITI: He lures women back to his apartment. He provides them with a drink and then they start to feel dizzy, or they blackout and he sexually assaults them.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: David Pearce would ultimately be charged with seven counts of sexual assault involving seven different women. He was also charged with two counts of murder and two felony drug counts for allegedly providing lethal amounts of fentanyl to Christy and Hilda.\par \par \uc2 David Pearce is awaiting trial and has pleaded not guilty to all charges against him.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jonathan Vigliotti joins us now from San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 So how is the District Attorney planning on prosecuting this case?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Yes, Vlad, so the prosecution plans to combine the seven sexual assault charges we mentioned with the murder and drug charges in one trial.\par \par \uc2 At this point, Pearce has not been charged with sexually assaulting Hilda and Christy. So the prosecution presumably will need to establish a deadly pattern of behavior.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, at the heart of this case, at least for me, is this 10-minute window of time where these two young women could have possibly left this night alive, but didn`t. So what happened? We explore that question tomorrow night.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And Jonathan, if he is found guilty on all of those charges, what type of sentence could David Pearce be facing?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: If found guilty and it is a long road ahead, Pearse could face 128 years to life in prison.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Jonathan Vigliotti for us. Thank you very much, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You can see Jonathan`s full report "Christy and Hilda`s Last Dance" on "48 Hours" tomorrow night at 10/9 Central right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  and on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy to say it is time now for our series called Mixtape Moments when each of us take a turn sharing a song from an emerging artist and I am up next and I want to introduce you all to the country artist, Brittney Spencer.\par \par \uc2 She is a trailblazing singer/songwriter who grew up in Baltimore before she started busking. That`s performing on the streets in Nashville when she was in college.\par \par \uc2 Now, here is a bit from her latest EP, it`s called "Better as Friends." Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Better as Friends.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do love that song.\par \par \uc2 Spencer has performed at the \uc2 Grand Ole Opry\uc2  and alongside the country music supergroup, The High Women. She is currently working on her debut album coming out sometime this year, and if she does sound familiar, she is emerging, but we`re now discovering her today for the first time.\par \par \uc2 She sat down with Anthony Mason back in 2021 before her breakthrough single, "Sober and Skinny."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was going to say, "Sober & Skinny." What a great song.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Fantastic song. Fantastic song, in a perfect world, you`d get sober and I`d get skinny and we`d stop living off -- we`ll start living off more than pennies -- it is a beautiful bit of writing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Great lyrics. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She was actually -- Maren Morris was key as a mentor, also an inspiration before she was a mentor and she made her Grand Old Opry debut singing "Sober & Skinny" and it`s just been upward from there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She`s huge.\par \par \uc2 She is also influenced by Ray Charles, which I thought was really interesting. She has talked about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Ray Charles being an influence.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, great things ahead.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Check more of her out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, check out more of Brittney Spencer and other songs and artists that we have featured here on our \uc2 Spotify\uc2  playlist. All you`ve got to do is search \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Mixtape on \uc2 Spotify\uc2  or scan the QR code on your screen right there.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Do it now.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the biggest names from the newest Time 100 list of influential people. Local news coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time now to bring you some of the stories that are the "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 Tony, you are in the lead off.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very good.\par \par \uc2 So my "Talk of the Table" is something that I`ve been thinking about all week, and it is a contrast between our country and the United Kingdom when it comes to e-cigarettes.\par \par \uc2 So in this country, e-cigarettes vaping, there is no e-cigarette product that has been approved as something for smokers to use to quit and no e- cigarette product that has been allowed the claim that it is lower risk, less harmful than traditional smoking. Zero. We`ve approved none of them.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, in the United Kingdom, their public health authorities are not only on board with vaping when it comes to smokers, they are as of this week, for the first time anywhere in the world, they are giving a million smokers in the UK free vaping starter kits to try to step them down off of cigarettes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hmm.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So we`re saying, we don`t know, more research is needed, and they are saying, this is a major plank in our anti-smoking efforts, because here`s a quote, "E-cigarettes are a fraction of the risk of smoking, and using one makes it much more likely you`ll quit successfully." That`s what they say. We say, I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So what do they know that we don`t?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly, and the stakes could not be higher because hundreds of thousands of people die every year in that country, this country because they`re smoking.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right. Because the idea is you want them to quit though it`s not here, take this e-cigarette and smoke that as --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And use that as a crutch or whatever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right. They`re using it as a quitting device. It`s called Swap to Stop. They think it works. We think it doesn`t, or at least not yet. One of us is right and one of us is wrong. And the stakes are literally hundreds of thousands of lives.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s fascinating.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a huge -- that is super interesting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Quit. Let`s say this, quit, quit, quit if you`re smoking.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Anyway that you can.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The best thing for your lungs is pure air.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, my talk on the table is "Time" Magazine revealing this year`s list of 100 most influential people in the world. The actors on the list include our "Talk of the Table" buddy, Drew Barrymore and here`s some others.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got "Creed`s" Michael B. Jordan; actor and comedian Ali Wong. Of course, it`s not just actors, it is icons and titans of industry too like Tesla`s \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , Queen Bey, and newly minted Oscar, Ke Huy Quan.\par \par \uc2 Then there is Angela Bassett, the Oscar-nominated Queen of Wakanda. Bassett, you`ll recall earned her first Oscar nod back in 1984 for playing Tina Turner in "What`s Love Got To Do With It."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "What`s Love Got To Do With It.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, "Time" called on Tina Turner to honor Angela Bassett. And I just want to read to you what Tina Turner had to say.\par \par \uc2 This is a quote from Tina: "Angela, the first time we met, you didn`t look, sound, or move like me, that came later after you worked so hard to make it happen, but even then I could see that the young woman standing before me had strength, determination, and big, big dreams just like me."\par \par \uc2 "She`s perfect. I said, and I was right. You never mimicked me. Instead, you reached deep into your soul and found your inner Tina, and you showed her to the world."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s beautiful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I mean --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s beautiful. And man, did she have the arms. Man, she had the greatest arms.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She had the arms, but then she, and like Tina says, when you watch Angela Bassett in "What`s Love Got To Do With It," you know, you`re not watching Tina Turner, but you can see the inspiration, you can see the luminescence of Tina Turner on the screen.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "What`s Love Got To Do With It.")\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And the inner strength that she developed throughout the movie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It was incredible.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I love how "Time" on the "Time" 100 allows peers to kind of talk about peers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s not just the critics writing about people, it adds a level of depth and interest that is just -- you can`t imagine.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes, okay, I`m not going for -- yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me just say that the Oscar nod was in 1994.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m old.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: We are all getting older every day.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Eighty-four, ninety-four, I know.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I am not going for depth here by the way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I was going to say, Vlad. I definitely knew that one, even I noticed that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s all right.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go for some cute here. My "Talk of the Table" is Bayley, a mini sheepadoodle that`s getting a lot of attention because she looks just like one of the most famous dogs on the planet.\par \par \uc2 Here is Bayley. You`re going to see it. Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Who does that remind you of?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Who is this? This two-year-old is not a beagle, but she sure looks like a very famous one. There it is. Snoopy created by --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s not a beagle. Oh really?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: No. No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Snoopy is not a beagle. I mean, Snoopy is a beagle.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Snoopy is a beagle, so Peanuts cartoonist Charles Schultz created Snoopy in 1950.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes, yes. No, I knew that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: They both have the big white face, the black ears, and the black nose. So, so cute.\par \par \uc2 Bayley`s \uc2 Instagram\uc2  page says she likes to ride in cars and get puppuccino, if you have a dog you know, oh, you`re such a good girl. Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, so cute.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is that an actual order in \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes. The puppuccino, it is whipped cream, basically, so every dog loves it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I want one.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right, exactly. But can Bayley do the happy dance? We do not know this yet.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Snoopy.)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Bayley is a sheepadoodle That`s a sheep dog. And then a doodle, a poodle mix.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s the dance, that`s the move.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And also, you know, Snoopy, he`s just like and then when he cries he goes, ah, wa, wa, wa.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Na, na, na. That`s the cry. Oh, na, na, na.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love it. Oh, Bayley is so adorable.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So cute. Oh my god. Tony has a look on his face. My husband gives me that look.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: What did you just do? I did the cry.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, I think Bayley is -- I keep thinking about Vlad being like, no Snoopy is a beagle. I mean like, Snoopy is a cartoon.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Jon Tower right in my ear said, "Vlad Snoopy is a cartoon."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Shifting gears, if we can. All right, in case you missed our \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals yesterday, we showed you some items that could make your day a little bit better all at exclusive low prices and one of them was reusable food storage containers that might help you save some money by keeping food fresh longer, and by eliminating waste. You can cook your leftovers.\par \par \uc2 Debbie Meyer`s Greenboxes are available at 35 percent off the retail price. To start shopping now, all you`ve got to do is scan the QR code on your screen, upper left there. You don`t even have to get up, just use your camera to focus in or visit CBSDeals.com.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up next, Emily Hampshire is going to share her debut graphic novel about a famous family of witches. There she is in our progressive greenroom enjoying the swing.\par \par \uc2 One of the characters, "Spaghetti the Yeti," but first a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Schitt`s Creek.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is actor, Emily Hampshire in the popular show "Schitt`s Creek," she plays the snarky and hilarious --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Hilarious.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Stevie Budd. Everybody loves Stevie. Now, she is an author. Her debut graphic novel is called "Amelia Aierwood: Basic Witch." I love the title.\par \par \uc2 The story follows Amelia, who is the black sheep of a famous family of witches. Amelia often gets herself in magical trouble, and now her family is about to star in their own reality TV show.\par \par \uc2 The book is distributed by \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , which is part of the same company as \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Emily Hampshire, great to see you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 EMILY HAMPSHIRE ("Amelia Aierwood: Basic Witch" author): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 I still can`t get over this set, the background. This is the most amazing thing that`s ever happened to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All we did is take your beautiful book and put it on the wall.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Our director, our Gabe Almanzar is the graphics guy who does all this, but let`s talk about this book. You say -- so, I probably gave it away in the intro there, but you say this was inspired by the Kardashians?\par \par \uc2 EMILY HAMPSHIRE: Well --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How?\par \par \uc2 EMILY HAMPSHIRE: I mean, well, I was watching "The Kardashians" as one does.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One does.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 EMILY HAMPSHIRE: And I was looking at the show thinking like this is a real family like they`re really sisters. And what if I just happened to be born into this family? I would be the worst Kardashian ever.\par \par \uc2 Like I would get kicked off the show. I would