cool about this is like, speaking of your past, there are, I think, the term is Easter eggs that the illustrators have put, little like things from your acting career that are here.\par \par \uc2 EMILY HAMPSHIRE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Some of your favorites. What are they?\par \par \uc2 EMILY HAMPSHIRE: I mean, there`s a Larry Air in there. There`s also -- so I love musicals. The only -- I just don`t like "The Phantom." And so the artist, Kristen Gudsnuk put a box set of all the musicals except for "The Phantom."\par \par \uc2 There`s also I did a show called "12 Monkeys," and there`s the virus from "12 Monkeys" somewhere amongst the panels. I just found out from one of your producers that there -- she told me about an Easter egg in here that I hadn`t even found. Like, I`m still finding stuff in the panels.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Finding them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So -- go ahead. I know we`re going to ask the same question.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I was going to ask you, no, because Emily, you know, you talk about being the black sheep. You and I`ve communicated about how sometimes it`s difficult when you are talking to your parents about who you are.\par \par \uc2 And you`ve talked about how this book can also serve as a roadmap for younger children to find themselves. What would you say to your younger self as you were trying to navigate your life to the point of where you are now?\par \par \uc2 EMILY HAMPSHIRE: Oh my God, I love that you ask that because I feel like the process of making this book gave me the opportunity to go back and tell my younger self like everything you think that`s wrong with you that is your problem or that you`re trying to fix or hide, that`s your magic, like that`s the thing that makes you special.\par \par \uc2 And when you`re older, that`s going to be the thing that is like your thing so that`s what I feel like I get from the book when I read it. I feel more inspired to be my authentic self and hopefully other people feel that way.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s beautiful. I have to say this real quick I`m sorry, "Schitt`s Creek" -- everybody wants to see a reunion. Something, something. Another season? Come on.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It can happen.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Break it right here. It is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 EMILY HAMPSHIRE: People say this to me or they were really upset when it ended and I`m like, I don`t have that -- I don`t have that job anymore. I lost all my friends. It was harder on me.\par \par \uc2 But I don`t -- I don`t believe that we wouldn`t have something else again. We`re not going to do the series again, but we like each other too much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We love it. We love you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you, Emily.\par \par \uc2 EMILY HAMPSHIRE: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: This is so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Amelia Aierwood: Basic Witch" is on sale right now, pick it up. You won`t be disappointed.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Coming up tomorrow on "CBS Saturday Morning," he is one of the most influential and recognizable voices in sports media. Now, Stephen A. Smith is sharing his own personal story. We will talk to him about his new memoir, "Straight Shooter."\par \par \uc2 Then on "CBS Sunday Morning," Lee Cowan heads to the Bermuda Triangle to dive in search of sunken treasure. How one man`s passion is revealing artifacts from a shipwreck more than 350 years ago.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That looks very exciting. My life is going to be a four- year-old`s birthday party tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So I`m booked. I hope I return Monday.\par \par \uc2 That does it for us. Let`s take a look back at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CRAIG GREENBERG (D) (Louisville, Kentucky): In the darkness of this week, I am so grateful to the people who have brought light and love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Another deadly mass shooting in this country. A man killed five people and wounded eight others inside the \uc2 Old National Bank\uc2  in downtown Louisville.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR CRAIG GREENBERG (D): We need to come together with support and love and prayer for those who are still recovering\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A Federal Appeals Court has preserved access to mifepristone limiting last week`s ruling in Texas to suspend the drug`s \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D) (Tennessee State Representative): I want to welcome the people back to the People`s House.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Two Black lawmakers expelled from the Tennessee State Legislature after protests following the Nashville mass shooting have now both been reinstated.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Rahm wins the Masters marathon.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was a stunning comeback win on Sunday. Jon Rahm dominated the final round and won his first green jacket.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD: Is this reality meeting your dreams? Or is this reality exceeding your dreams?\par \par \uc2 JON RAHM (Professional golf player): Exceeding.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Do you feel like golf is your passion?\par \par \uc2 MADELYN QUINN (Nine-year-old): Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: She`s almost as good as me with a golf club. Did you see that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, she was laughing at you, David.\par \par \uc2 It just make you want to go like this.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? I kind of do that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is how I felt this morning after a vacation. You wake up on a Monday all rested and you`re like, this is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Back to reality.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The Recording Academy honored the Beach Boys -- I`m going to have a little fun, fun, fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Until the daddy takes the T-bird away.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Takes the T-bird away.\par \par \uc2 Some of the biggest names in contemporary music, Gayle brought good, good, good, good vibrations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good vibrations.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And that gave us hits like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: California.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes. Wish we could be California girls, and ba, ba, ba, ba, Barbara Ann.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s roll the professionals now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: In February, it recorded what may be the lowest wind chill in US history.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that Nancy?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I hope that`s not Nancy.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: My nostrils hurt for a good two or three days after that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Couldn`t even tell it was you in a lot of those shots.\par \par \uc2 I will be watching you bet here butt, I will be. Up next, Vlad has got the stories you`ll be talking about. I don`t know why I said it that way.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh my God.\par \par \uc2 When this camera crew walks in, I`m like, who are you people? And then you walk in, Josh, I`ll never forget that. I`ll never forget that.\par \par \uc2 JOSH GROBAN (Singer): And you were singing the song to Oprah on the phone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 When you say you love me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Schmigadoon!")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There was a very fast rat-a-tat-tat. Can you do that or no? It is no problem.\par \par \uc2 JANE KRAKOWSKI (Actor): I think it`s, thank you to the judge and all the members of the jury. I will try to make this brief because I know you`re in a hurry. So my client, the defendant Dr. Joshua de Skinner he was merely at the club and took his darling wife to dinner. He stumbled on the body and was associated to the scene, and it goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re so good. I know. And then it ends in the split, and you do the split.\par \par \uc2 JANE KRAKOWSKI: I certainly hope this won`t be a split decision.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And how`s your favorite son?\par \par \uc2 JANE KRAKOWSKI: When he first watched the number and he saw that fast talking part, he goes, my favorite part was when you rapped like Eminem.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Schmigadoon!")\par \par \uc2 JANE KRAKOWSKI: I`m like, okay, it is primetime.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230414ej4e000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Justice Department Says It Will Ask The Supreme Court To Pause An Appeals Court Decision That Keeps Access To A Commonly Used Abortion Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nancy Cordes \par \uc2 561 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We turn now to some breaking news about abortion rights. Florida lawmakers today voted to ban abortions after six weeks of pregnancy. That is before many women know they are pregnant. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , who is preparing a possible run for president, said he will sign the ban into law.\par \par \uc2 Patients from across the South have been traveling to Florida for abortions since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last June. That ruling triggered abortion bans in 13 states, including states in the South like Tennessee, Louisiana, and Mississippi. Florida will join Georgia now with this six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the battle over access to the abortion pill throughout the U.S. could be headed to the Supreme Court. Today, the Justice Department asked the High Court to intervene.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nancy Cordes on what this means for women`s reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES (voiceover): The new ruling doesn`t block access to the abortion drug mifepristone, but it does make it harder to get, shortening the time frame in which the drug can be used from the first 10 weeks of pregnancy to the first seven, and halting mail delivery of the pill, requiring women to visit a doctor`s office multiple times instead.\par \par \uc2 LEAH LITMAN (University of Michigan Law School): That is likely to be, you know, a significant barrier for individuals who are living in states that have essentially shut down abortion access.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Mifepristone is one of two drugs used together to carry out more than half of all abortions in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The 2 to1 ruling comes five days after a conservative federal judge in Texas sought to reverse \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of the drug altogether. The three- judge Fifth Circuit court of appeals panel cited the risk of serious complications listed on mifepristone`s own label, including sustained fever and sepsis.\par \par \uc2 You see this ruling as a win?\par \par \uc2 ERIK BAPTIST (Senior Counsel, \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 ): We do. We are happy and pleased that the previous safeguards that were there for over 16 years have been restored.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  insists mifepristone is safe and complications rare. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  slammed the ruling, saying it "threatens the rights of Americans across the country."\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): We are going to continue to fight in the courts. We believe that the law is on our side and we will prevail.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: This fight now goes to the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 , but if the justices don`t weigh in by the end of the day tomorrow, these restrictions will go into effect, at least for the time being, Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We will be watching it closely.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Cordes, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230414ej4d000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Life-Threatening Flash Floods; Historic Rainfall Hits South Florida; Indiana Fire Spewing Toxic Smoke; Bank Rampage 911 Calls Released; Vigil Held for Louisville Victims; Second Tennessee Lawmakers Reinstated; Abortion Pill Ruling Blocked; Sen. Feinstein`s Health Concerns; Trump Faces Questioning in Fraud Case; Sen. Tim Scott`s Presidential Ambitions; Dental Device Under Federal Probe; Prince Harry to Attend King`s Coronation; Force of Nature; Last Minute Tax Tips; Steven Yeun Live; \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Vladimir Duthiers, Manuel Bojorquez,  Stephanie Abrams, Cameron Ridle, Roxana Saberi, Mark Strassmann, Scott  MacFarlane, Major Garrett, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Imtiaz Tyab, Ben Tracy, Jill  Schlesinger \par \uc2 14,378 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off, but we`re still here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Historic rainfall hammered parts of Florida bringing life-threatening flooding and shutting down the airport in Fort Lauderdale.\par \par \uc2 MAN: You can`t see anything. The rain is coming out so far, massive. Unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A Federal Appeals Court ruled a major abortion pill will stay available in the US, but with restrictions.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Both Tennessee lawmakers expelled for anti-gun protest have now been reappointed.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON (D) (Tennessee State Representative): They awakened a sleeping giant.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Toxic fumes from a massive recycling plant fire force evacuations. The Mayor blames the company`s owner.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR DAVID SNOW (Richmond, Indiana): Negligent and irresponsible and it`s led to putting a lot of people in danger today.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Juul has agreed to pay $462 million to resolve accusations that it targeted teens with its vaping ads.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Rays improved to 12 and oh start this season, they are now just one win away from tying the all-time record.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Biden showed off his gift of gab regaling Iris firefighters with this story.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN (President of the United States): Came in and saved my wife. Saved my wife. I went there, my cats, and my Corvette.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: You guys are the greatest. You folks saved everything that matters. Wife, life, cat, vet, okay.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That scream is the sound of victory for the \uc2 Chicago Bulls\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Demar DeRozan`s daughter screamed every time the Raptors attempted a free throw, and it worked.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Raptors missed 18 of their 36 free throws.\par \par \uc2 DEMAR DEROZAN (\uc2 Chicago Bulls\uc2 ): I just said, you can miss one day of school when, you know come to a game and I`m glad I did, I owe her some money for sure.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so funny. He said at one point, he thought, is that my daughter?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everything is fair.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It appeared to work.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Everything is fair in plays for basketball.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got a great show for you. A lot to share with you today including a remarkable story of conservation in the wilds of South America.\par \par \uc2 Kris Tompkins, she was the first CEO of the Patagonia outdoor clothing company and decades ago, she actually moved to the Patagonia region. We`re going to hear from Tompkins and also the Oscar-winning filmmakers behind a new documentary about her remarkable life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plus, Oscar-nominated actor, Stephen Yeun is here to talk about his new show. It`s called Beef. It is like nothing you have ever seen before. He and Ali Wong play two people caught up in a road rage incident that takes viewers in some very unexpected directions. I can`t wait to meet him. This thing is so good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It so good. It is so, so good. Probably the best thing on TV right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, we can all agree on that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But let`s begin here in South Florida, where torrential rain caused major flooding overnight. A rare flash flood emergency alert warned of life-threatening conditions after torrential rain made roads impossible to navigate. It trapped people in their cars.\par \par \uc2 It is being called a one in a thousand year flood event.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez is in Fort Lauderdale, which is still flooded this morning.\par \par \uc2 Manny, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 We`re at the airport and behind me, you can see why it remains shut down. There`s water covering the tarmac, but also back there, what resembles a big lake right now, that is the runway, it is submerged, and the airport is not scheduled to reopen until at least noon today.\par \par \uc2 This area got more than 26 inches of rain in just an eight-hour period. To put that into perspective, that is about 40 percent of their annual rainfall in just one day.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Some South Florida streets resembled rivers Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I`ve never seen this much rain.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Leaving cars either stuck, nearly submerged or even abandoned.\par \par \uc2 DAMION ROBERTSON (Tow truck driver): I`ll see like 200 cars stalled out and guys -- I told one guy, don`t drive.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Inundated roads forced people to wade through knee-high waters some even plunging in just across the street.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Now is not the time to be on the roadways.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Authorities are urging residents to seek higher ground and stay off the roads.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Look at these houses, you guys.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Rising water from the relentless rain swamped this hospital parking lot in Fort Lauderdale and flooded many garages like Andra Zakadie`s.\par \par \uc2 ANDRA ZAKADIE (Garage flooded): I`ve lived here for the last four years and as much rain as we get, this is -- this is the worst I`ve ever seen it.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Planes were grounded at Fort Lauderdale International Airport where nearly 200 flights were canceled yesterday. Many passengers were stranded and packed into terminals overnight, sleeping wherever they could.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: And we`re trying to get back home, and they`re telling us that the airport is closed. And so at this point, we don`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Overnight, the airport reopened the departures level so passengers could leave if they wished. Another problem today, Broward County schools will have to close due to the weather -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Manny, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And with all of that rain, you may wonder, is there a link to climate change? Well, there is when it comes to this kind of extreme weather event, and for more on that, we`re going to go to meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, these heavy rain events are increasing across the entire country due to the changing climate.\par \par \uc2 Now, there are several factors that led to the flooding in South Florida. Days of rain, we also had saturated grounds because of that, fronts which are lifting mechanisms, and a juicy atmosphere.\par \par \uc2 In just the last 24 hours, we had over a foot of rain, which is even too much for the sandy soils of Florida. Thunderstorms will continue today and also as we head into tomorrow. We do have some good news, as we head into Saturday, we`ll dry out as the sun will be returning and this flood threat does continue tomorrow morning, all the way from Florida into the Southeast.\par \par \uc2 And Tony, what`s wild is we`re a month away from the rainy season in Florida and don`t forget, hurricane season starts June 1st.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, I`ve heard a lot of adjectives to describe the atmosphere, but never juicy, Stephanie, and we`re not even in the rainy season as you say.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2 : Juicy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2 . Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now to a large industrial fire in Indiana that is expected to continue burning and spewing harmful fumes for days, so that is not good.\par \par \uc2 The fire began on Tuesday. It has already burned an estimated 13 acres at a plastic recycling facility in the eastern part of the state.\par \par \uc2 Cameron Ridle of our Indianapolis station, that`s WTTV has been following this in Richmond for us.\par \par \uc2 Cameron, good morning. I`m glad to see you`re taking precautions this morning.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON RIDLE: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 The air here is the concern. The fire behind us is contained according to the Richmond Fire Chief, but the concern now as it continues to burn is all of the toxic chemicals that are flowing through the air.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE JONES (Indiana State Fire Marshal): This has been a challenging fire just because it is plastics.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON RIDLE: Plumes of smoke have consumed the City of Richmond as the industrial fire enters its third day. Public safety officials say their main concern is the hazardous, potentially carcinogenic particles spreading in the smoke.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE STINSON (Wayne County Health Department executive director): If they`re breathed in, it can cause all kinds of respiratory problems, burning of the eyes, tightening of the chest. It could aggravate asthma.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON RIDLE: Air quality concerns have forced firefighters to battle the flames from a distance and led to evacuation orders for as many as 2,000 people.\par \par \uc2 MAN: This is no joke. You know, chemicals like this and the burning of the plastic.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR DAVID SNOW: The business owner is responsible for this mess, responsible for the disaster, and responsible for putting everyone in danger.\par \par \uc2 CAMERON RIDLE: Richmond`s Mayor placed blame squarely on the recycling facility. Documents from the city`s Unsafe Building Commission revealed the business came under scrutiny in 2019 with one Commissioner detailing boxes stacked all the way up to the ceiling, referring to some materials as a fire hazard, and pointing to no sprinkler system.\par \par \uc2 A Wayne County Court later supported the cleanup order for the facility, calling the buildings in question a hazard to public health and dangerous.\par \par \uc2 Richmond`s Fire Chief on Wednesday appeared to suggest those conditions may have remained as his team worked to evacuate the scene and put out the flames.\par \par \uc2 DEREK BROWN (City of Richmond Fire Department): Our access was very hampered by the rubbish and the piles of plastic that were surrounding the complex.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CAMERON RIDLE: Now in those documents from 2019, the business owner claimed that the building`s fire suppression system was actually broken when the previous owner left.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has reached out to that business owner for comment but so far, we`ve had no luck on a response -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Cameron Ridle masked up for us in Indiana. Cameron, thank you very much. Do stay safe.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now once again to Louisville, Kentucky where police have released 9-1-1 calls for Monday`s mass shooting at a bank there downtown. They continue to fill in what is a harrowing picture of what happened as a gunman shot his co-workers and police officers.\par \par \uc2 One call was from the shooter`s own mother attempting to warn people, of course she was too late.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi is in Louisville for us once again with more, and we do warn you, as you may expect these calls are disturbing to hear.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh my God, there`s an active shooter there.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The first call came from a woman who watched the scene unfold on a video conference meeting.\par \par \uc2 911 DISPATCHER: How do you know you have an active shooter on the site?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I just watched it.\par \par \uc2 911 DISPATCHER: How`d you watch?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I just watched it on a Teams meeting.\par \par \uc2 911 DISPATCHER: You watched on --\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Another woman calling from inside a closet identified the gunman to the dispatcher.\par \par \uc2 911 DISPATCHER: How do you know the person?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: He works with us.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: As more calls poured in, the shooter`s mother called saying he might be headed to the bank with a gun.\par \par \uc2 (GUNSHOTS)\par \par \uc2 SHOOTER`S MOM: We don`t even own guns. I don`t know where he would have gotten a gun.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: But six days earlier, he had bought an AR-15 style rifle and it was already too late.\par \par \uc2 911 DISPATCHER: It`s dangerous there.\par \par \uc2 SHOOTER`S MOM: You`ve had calls from other people, so he`s already there?\par \par \uc2 911 DISPATCHER: Yes, at Old National Bank on East Main Street.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): This person murdered my friend, but still, I can`t imagine how his parents must be feeling right now.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Thomas Elliot was a close friend of Kentucky Governor, Andy Beshear.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): I`m a Governor during this pandemic, I have been governor during tornadoes and floods, and negative 45-degree wind chills and everything else.\par \par \uc2 We`ve lost a lot of people during those, but calling your friend`s wife who is also your friend to tell her that her husband is gone is amongst the hardest things I`ve ever done.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: At a vigil Wednesday night, community members honored the five people killed.\par \par \uc2 (MAN singing "Amazing Grace.")\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Along with the eight injured. One of those is Dana Mitchell who was shot in the back.\par \par \uc2 ROSS MINRATH (Dana Mitchell`s son): I`m just thankful to still have her today.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Mitchell called her son Ross Minrath from the hospital Monday.\par \par \uc2 What`s the toughest part of it for you?\par \par \uc2 ROSS MINRATH: Thinking back and just like you know, like I got that call from her, but I could have got a call from the CEO of the bank, you know, but I got a call from her.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Louisville Mayor, Craig Greenberg, told me yesterday while the 9-1-1 calls were difficult to listen to, he wanted them released so that people could understand what happened here at the bank on Monday and how the police responded. Funerals for at least two of the victims are set for Friday -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, Roxana, so much pain all the way around. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Two Black lawmakers expelled from Tennessee State Legislature after a protest following the Nashville mass shooting have now both been reinstated. State Representatives Justin Pearson and Justin Jones made national headlines for their stand, and Pearson will be sworn back in later today.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann is in Memphis where Pearson was reappointed.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON: Let`s get back to work.\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Celebration in Memphis after seven Shelby County Commissioners voted unanimously to send Justin Pearson back to the Tennessee House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON: They tried to kill democracy.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON: They tried to expel the people`s choice and the people`s vote.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON: And they awaked a sleeping giant.\par \par \uc2 (Cheering and applause)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: This comes almost two weeks after the mostly white Republican majority expelled Pearson and Representative Justin Jones for violating rules of decorum.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON: Power to the people.\par \par \uc2 I`m really grateful to the Shelby County Commission for listening to the power of the people and sending a strong message back that anti-Democratic behavior of the Tennessee State Republican Party is unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In their struggle, Pearson and Jones have found a national platform.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES (D) (Tennessee State Representative): This is a learning opportunity for all Tennesseans and for the Republican leadership.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: We spoke with Jones ahead of Wednesday`s vote.\par \par \uc2 Is the issue being a member of the minority party or is the issue being a Minority State Rep?\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN JONES: Yes. I mean, racism is alive and wellness in the Tennessee Capitol. The Speaker of the House has been a part of that, and we need action to address that culture and that toxic work environment.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: If we don`t get no justice.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Then we don`t get no peace.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Jones and Pearson now say they will take action on the original issue at hand, deadly gun violence, like the Covenant School shooting in Nashville.\par \par \uc2 They took their message to this historic Civil Rights site, the Lorraine Motel where Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2 , Jr. was assassinated.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON: This is a democracy that`s going to lift up the victims of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN J. PEARSON: Instead of supporting the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and the gun lobbyists.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: On Tuesday, Tennessee`s Republican Governor, Bill Lee signed an executive order strengthening background checks for gun buyers.\par \par \uc2 He is also pushing for a Red Flag Law designed to take away guns from dangerous people.\par \par \uc2 GOV. BILL LEE (R-TN): And the truth is that we`re facing evil itself and we can`t stop evil, but we can do something.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Later today, there could be some interesting, if awkward moments in the Tennessee Legislature as those two Democrats, Pearson and Jones, will be back alongside all of those Republican lawmakers who kicked them out a week ago -- Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know you`ll be watching, Mark. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, a new development in a Court fight over a commonly used abortion pill. A Federal Appeals Court has preserved access to mifepristone, at least for now, limiting last week`s ruling in Texas to suspend the drug`s \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval.\par \par \uc2 This new ruling does bring back restrictions on its use for the first time since 2016. The Appeals Court Judges ruled mifepristone can only be used for up to seven weeks of pregnancy instead of 10. Also, it cannot be distributed by mail without a visit to a doctor. The temporary ruling can be appealed to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There are new questions today about the health and future of California Senator Dianne Feinstein, 89 years old. The Democrat has been absent from the Senate business -- or from all Senate -- from all Senate business, rather recovering from illness, and now some of her fellow Democrats claim that she can no longer do her job and she should quit.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill for us.\par \par \uc2 Scott, this is quite a talker. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Tony, in a narrowly divided \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , Dianne Feinstein is a pivotal vote at a pivotal moment. She is the oldest US senator and has been absent now for two months as she recovers from shingles.\par \par \uc2 In a statement last night, she said her return to Washington has been delayed due to continued complications. And also in a rare request, she asked Senate Democratic leaders to temporarily fill her seat on the pivotal \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 . That panel`s work confirming Federal Judges has been partially stalled in her absence.\par \par \uc2 Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  last night said he would do so.\par \par \uc2 But over the past 24 hours, some House Democrats including California`s \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2  have urged her to step down ahead of the 2024 elections, saying Feinstein can no longer fulfill her duties.\par \par \uc2 Her office announced she will not run for re-election next year, but says she will be back here when doctors determine it is safe for her to resume traveling -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Scott, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is back here in New York City for the first time since his historic arrest and arraignment just last week. Trump is giving a deposition in a separate case. This is a lawsuit filed by the New York State Attorney General focused on his business dealings.\par \par \uc2 Chief Washington correspondent Major Garrett is tracking the former President`s legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 You are very busy, Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have a lot to keep track of these days, but when you put - - when you take a look at this one, how concerned should he be about this one compared to the others?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Look this week is not last week. Last week was criminal, this is civil. And former President Trump throughout his business career has done a lot of depositions.\par \par \uc2 We had news chopper coverage last week because it was historic. If there were news chopper coverage of every time that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had done a deposition, there`d be no news choppers left on the Eastern Seaboard, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: That`s happened quite a bit. This is a case brought by the New York Attorney General, not just against the former President, but against all his children and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  alleging that it misstated financial documents as part of a scheme to get tax benefits and preferential loans. Trump denies all of those charges.\par \par \uc2 He had a deposition in August, claimed his Fifth Amendment right to avoid self-incrimination.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Several times.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Four hundred times, which is his right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: But in a civil case, if this goes to trial, it is scheduled for trial in October, jurors can take what is called an adverse inference from not answering questions. They can lean into that a bit more than you can in a criminal case --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So --\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: One of many cases pending.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So politically, with all of these cases .\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . pending, the former President is fundraising off of it, he`s selling t-shirts with a fake mug shot on it. So, is he emboldened by this? Or is he weakened by this politically?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Look, the politics around the events of last week are obvious. For a moment, there was a rallying of the Republican Party, there was fundraising. But there is already polling data that should suggest it`s not wearing well and will not wear well.\par \par \uc2 This is a separate thing. This is about the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 . And on his own social media platform, the former President said I`m eager to prove what a great company, that nothing was done wrong, and this is a ridiculous case that I can win.\par \par \uc2 So on this case, he is asserting that he feels much more confident about it and in the main --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he says he feels confident in all of his cases, though, Major when you listen to him.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Yes, but some of that is bluff and some of that isn`t.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: On this one, they feel, generally speaking, better grounded in the law and what they did, and the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2 , the company itself; and in the politics of it, most people who support Trump, like his business practices, even if they`re a little on the edgy side.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Major Garrett, always great to have you at the table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, unexpected health news about Oscar-winner, Jamie Foxx, but first it is 7:19. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up: The long awaited decision on whether Prince Harry and his wife Meghan will attend \uc2