 Republican presidential nomination, optimism wins over outrage, even as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  leads the field.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I see that America is starving for positive optimistic leadership. If we can unite this country around the solutions, focusing more on those solutions than anything else, it is my only path forward and it is what I`ve chosen.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Is your candidacy, a sign that the Republican Party needs new leadership?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Well, I hope that nation wants new leadership. I happen to be a conservative, because it actually works. It is one of the reasons why I went from a poor kid mired in poverty in a single parent household to a person who was able to start my own business and then run for Congress.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: His announcement stands in stark contrast to the former President`s message.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Our country is going to hell.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But Scott isn`t willing to criticize Trump directly or by name.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I`m not focused on politicians, I`ll be honest with you. What I`ve heard from the American people is that they want us to focus on them, not on each other.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the nominee for the Republican Party, would you support him?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I plan on being the nominee. Obviously, I have an Exploratory Committee moving forward.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But if Trump is the nominee, would you support him?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Obviously, I`m looking forward to being the nominee.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: In that quest for the nomination, Scott would be joining fellow South Carolinian, Nikki Haley, who also spent Wednesday in Iowa.\par \par \uc2 Scott is the Senate`s only black Republican and has put an emphasis on faith and conservative values.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I am certainly a hundred percent prolife, without a question, I`ve been very, very clear about that, very consistent about that. I do think we spend not enough time understanding how far the far left has gone on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: If you were President, would you advocate for Federal limits?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yeah. So once again, I -- once again, I am a hundred percent prolife and I do believe --\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: So, yes?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): That`s not what I said. I do believe that we should have a robust conversation about what`s happening in the -- on a very important topic.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Scott says his personal story sets him apart from the rest of the GOP field. His mother sacrifice is center to a likely campaign.\par \par \uc2 So when you told your mom --\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: That you were going to make this decision.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Yes.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: What did she say?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): Well, she kind of cried, honestly.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yes?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM SCOTT (R-SC): I think it`s -- well, I get emotional myself. It`s amazing to have such a miserable beginning, and then have a chance to continue to represent not only your country, but your family, and her sacrifice, she is my real American hero.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Cedar Rapids, Iowa.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He does have a great personal backstory. There`s no question about that, and I think when he says what this country wants is positive, optimistic leadership, I think people on both sides like that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is not wrong about that, and he has got a compelling story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is not wrong about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is calling it an Exploratory Committee.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But he said, I plan to be the nominee. So, I think the exploration is done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, we will see.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have a couple of other stories to bring you including an update on an exclusive CBS News/KFF health news investigation into a dental device that numerous patients said damaged their mouths.\par \par \uc2 Anna Werner spoke with multiple people who say the device known as the Anterior Growth Guidance Appliance or AGGA was meant to treat conditions like TMJ, a jaw joint disorder, and sleep apnea; instead, they said it mangled their mouths and even -- they even lost teeth.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATHRYN ARUMAE (AGGA patient): My front six teeth also became extremely flared outwards, all six of them instead -- yes, they were doing this.\par \par \uc2 DAVE HORNBLOWER (AGGA patient): I noticed my teeth are starting to get loose.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE PAPPALARDI (AGGA patient): I can`t bite into absolutely anything.\par \par \uc2 LEIGH PETERSON (AGGA patient): I feel like all I have to look forward to now is treatment and pain and fear and debt, and --\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Anna Werner now reports, Federal prosecutors have launched a criminal investigation into the device. According to new filings in Civil Court cases, Federal prosecutors are looking into a possible criminal charge against the device`s inventor, Dr. Steve Galella, his company and the manufacturer of the device.\par \par \uc2 We`ve reached out to the parties involved, but we have received no response. Galella is also being sued in Civil Court and in those cases, he has denied liability.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Actor, Jamie Foxx is recovering this morning after a medical scare, that`s according to his doctor. Corinne Foxx posted about her doubt on \uc2 Instagram\uc2  yesterday. Now, she did not provide many details, but she said Foxx: "Experienced a medical complication." Those are her words on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 She says, he is now on his way to recovery due to what she calls quick action and great care. Foxx has been in Atlanta recently. He is filming a new action comedy with Cameron Diaz.\par \par \uc2 Now, we don`t know what the medical complication. Some have said emergency, but we`re just glad to hear that he`s recovering.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that he is going to be okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everybody loves him and is pulling for Jamie Foxx always.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the GOATS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: One of the GOATS.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: Prince Harry is going -- is going to be going to next month`s Coronation by himself. We will show you why Meghan will be staying home in California, and how Harry has reached his decision.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re heading out the door, please take us with you. We want to go, you don`t need to miss this show. Catch the top interviews and pieces from this morning on our podcast. Just search for \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS On The Go on your favorite podcast app or you can scan the QR code right there on your screen. Do it quick -- to start following us.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We have the answer to the question of whether Prince Harry and Meghan will attend next month`s Coronation of \uc2 King Charles\uc2 . As you know, it has dominated the discussion ever since the date was set in May.\par \par \uc2 Finally, we`ve got an answer today: He is going, she is not.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab is outside Buckingham Palace where the announcement was made yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz, it has been a big should they, will they, could they and now we know.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Gayle, good morning to you, too.\par \par \uc2 Yes, the British press has just been breathless in their speculation of will they or won`t they? Well, we finally have an answer and it came in the form of a very brief two sentence statement from here, Buckingham Palace.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: The statement read: "The Duke of Sussex will attend the coordination service at Westminster Abbey on May 6. The Duchess of Sussex will remain in California with Prince Archie and Princess Lilibet." A compromise it seems for the new King says "Sunday Times" Royal editor, Roya Nikkhah.\par \par \uc2 ROYA NIKKHAH: I think Harry coming on his own was the hope from many inside the Palace, because the focus will be much more on the King and the Queen on Coronation Day as it should be, rather than on what sideshow might be going on with Harry and Meghan in attendance.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Prince Harry hasn`t been in the UK much since he and Meghan stepped back as working Royals in 2020. He attended Prince Philip`s funeral, and the couple returned when Queen Elizabeth died.\par \par \uc2 Prince Harry was also here last month to attend a High Court hearing in his case against the publishers of a British tabloid newspaper. But while promoting his best-selling bombshell memoir, "Spare," he told "60 Minutes" Anderson Cooper in January, he wanted to sit down with members of his family to discuss the issues he has been so open about privately,\par \par \uc2 PRINCE HARRY\uc2  (Duke of Sussex): There needs to be a constructive conversation, one that can happen in private, that doesn`t get leaked.\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: It`s not clear if that conversation happened, nor do we know how or why the decision was made, he would attend the Coronation, alongside 2,000 other guests including the First Lady, and not his wife and children.\par \par \uc2 Whatever the reason, this will be the first time Prince Harry will be seen in public with \uc2 King Charles\uc2 , the Queen Consort, Camilla, and Prince William, since writing so unsparingly about them in his book, including of the physical fight with his brother, and how both of them begged their father not to marry Camilla.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: As for Meghan, while the Coronation happens to fall on Prince Archie`s fourth birthday, but her absence on the King`s crowning moment, well, it`s only going to add fuel to the speculation that this Royal rift is deeper than ever -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imtiaz Tyab reporting from Buckingham Palace. We thank you.\par \par \uc2 It`s like all families have a little bit of drama. I`m glad the decision has been made, all this speculation, I`m glad they`ve decided and made the decision that I think they feel is best for them.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s good that Harry is going.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I think so, too.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is a Prince of the Realm, he should be there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it is his dad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if they haven`t --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is his father.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If they haven`t had that private conversation, that healing conversation, I hope they do while he`s there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, yes. I hope so, too.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, the stories we think you`re going to be talking about later today, but first, it is 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Is that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is that music?\par \