 Grand slam.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Max Muncy of the \uc2 LA Dodgers\uc2  gets hot against the Giants hitting four home runs in a single series.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is gone. Silent killer among Giant killers. They`ll never let him back in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I just -- I missed it. I missed it. What just happened?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, you missed it. He hit four homeruns in a series, which is a big deal, but the really big deal is that Max Muncy had only gotten three hits in his last like 30 at bat, so like imagine being a professional and failing 90 percent of the time at your job. That`s the big deal. He changed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is now succeeding at his job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Go Dodgers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They wanted to make old baseball exciting. It is exciting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Max. Go Max.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is exciting.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It really is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, got it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, let`s begin this hour with our series, "Protecting the Planet," which this morning focuses on one of the most prolific conservationist in the world.\par \par \uc2 A stunning new documentary called "Wild Life" follows the journey of Kris Tompkins, the former CEO of the outdoor clothing company, Patagonia. She`s on an inspiring mission to save wilderness areas in the Patagonia region at the southern end of South America.\par \par \uc2 Senior national and environmental correspondent Ben Tracy sat down with Tompkins and filmmakers to talk about her epic life story.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS (Former Patagonia Company CEO):Come on, Ben.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Keeping up with Kris Tompkins is not easy.\par \par \uc2 Oh, I can tell you spent many an hour in a kayak, because I`m just trying to keep up with you here.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And here on the urban waterways of Austin, Texas, her mind floats to something much bigger and much farther away.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: If you just focused on that bank, that west bank, you`d think, oh, we`re in the middle of a National Park.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Tomkins knows a lot about National Parks. For three decades, she`s helped create or expand 15 of them in South America`s Patagonia region, protecting nearly 15 million acres of land that likely would have been lost to mining and logging.\par \par \uc2 Why is it so important?\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: You have to protect the lungs of the world. We can`t use up every square inch of this planet.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Worldwide, we are losing wilderness at the rate of 27 million acres a year. Just in the tropics, that`s equivalent to 10 soccer fields every minute.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: We`ll have to make space for non-human life, and once you decide that that`s the kind of person you are, your life has changed.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Wild Life.")\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Kris Tompkins` life changed back in 1973 when she began working with her friend, Yvon Chouinard at his little known climbing equipment company.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: And then we started making clothes and that became Patagonia Company.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: She was Patagonia`s first CEO for 14 years until she met and fell in love with Doug Tompkins who also knew a little something about clothes. He was the co-founder of The North Face and later, Esprit.\par \par \uc2 He left the fashion industry in the early 90s and convinced Kris to join him in Chile.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: I`m going to leave my role as CEO.\par \par \uc2 I retired and two days later on Sunday with two little bags, I closed up my beach house and moved to a roadless area on the coast of Chile.\par \par \uc2 God, what a life.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: That journey is the subject of the new documentary film, "Wild Life." It Chronicles how Doug and Kris Tompkins devoted much of their lives and wealth to buying a vast tracts of land in Patagonia.\par \par \uc2 DOUG TOMPKINS (Kris Tompkins` husband): And pretty soon, we had the makings of a park.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: They never intended to keep the land for themselves. Through Tompkins Conservation, they became two of the largest private landowners in the world, protecting what Kris Tompkins calls the Crown Jewels of Patagonia for everyone to enjoy.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: It is the same way we feel about any land we`ve ever conserved. This has never been the Kris and Doug story.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It was all about love.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: But the Kris and Doug`s story is the heart of the documentary by filmmakers, Jimmy Chin, and Chai Vasarhelyi. The husband and wife duo behind the Oscar-winning film "Free Solo" about famed El Capitan climber, Alex Honnold.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Free Solo.")\par \par \uc2 JIMMY CHIN (Filmmaker): We make films about people achieving the impossible.\par \par \uc2 Yellowstone National Park is the biggest National Park in the United States. That`s two million acres. They preserved at this point, 15 million acres.\par \par \uc2 CHAI VASARHELYI (Filmmaker): Doug, he had this audacious vision of these parks and he happened to fall in love with a great CEO who could like execute this stuff really well.\par \par \uc2 DOUG TOMPKINS: How are you doing, Kris?\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: I`m okay.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Chin and Vasarhelyi rally are close friends with most of the people in the film, and Doug Tompkins was Chin`s climbing mentor.\par \par \uc2 JIMMY CHIN: I felt more pressure in making this film than any film I`ve made. When you`re making a film about your heroes, you better get it right.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Wild Life.")\par \par \uc2 CHAI VASARHELYI: I`ve always wanted to make a film about a strong female protagonist, and Kris has that in spades, and I think especially when her third act came, you know, you really see a woman find her voice.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: They took the Zodiac out, and then --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And but that third act was born from tragedy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Douglas Tompkins died Tuesday in an accident in Chile.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Doug Tompkins was killed in a kayaking accident in 2015.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: And then we flew him the next day --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: leaving Kris shattered.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: Thank you for coming.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Is there something you`ve learned about yourself that maybe you might not have had you not lost your husband?\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: Losing Doug was the worst thing that happened to me. In the shadow of that, you realize you have no fear and that makes you absolutely free to take on anything.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: That fearlessness has fueled her work.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: Two point three million. Now, we`re talking.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Securing even more land and helping rewild it with native species including pumas and jaguars.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: Ola.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And in 2018, Kris Tompkins handed over land for five more National Parks to the President of Chile.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: You should be so proud.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: One of the largest private land donations in history.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE cheering.)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: What were you feeling in that moment, as you were saying, okay, here you go.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: Lolo, we made it.\par \par \uc2 It was the purest sense of joy I think I ever have experienced.\par \par \uc2 I love urban parks. They`re the jewels of the cities.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Tompkins saw the documentary for the first time last month at the South by Southwest Festival in Austin. And at age 72, she says she still has a lot more work to do.\par \par \uc2 KRIS TOMPKINS: My intention is to do this work until I drop dead. So we`re just getting warmed up. That`s how I feel.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ben Tracy, in Austin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. Kris Tompkins, my new hero. I can`t wait to see that documentary.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, I mean, there`s an incredible level of spiritual enlightenment when she says --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know, you realize you have no fear and that makes you absolutely free to do anything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s true. You have no fear when you goes through something really horrific to you personally and you survive it. It does make -- it does give you a different kind of fierceness that you probably didn`t even know you had, and they`re not making any more land.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So the fact that she has the land and she is donating it to very good causes, I think she`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And you think of the majesty of Yellowstone Park.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The size of it and what she is talking about is magnitudes larger than Yellowstone Park.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. Fifteen million acres.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is amazing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And when she said giving the land, guys, she said it gave her the purest sense of joy, may we all do that, give something that makes us feel that way.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Amen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, what a feeling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Makes you want to go hiking, doesn`t it, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well done, Ben Tracy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually enjoy hiking. I don`t look good doing it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Go to \uc2 Instagram\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I