par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do they do?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Don`t get in your head about this. We really drive ourselves crazy, but if you`re like most of Americans --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We drive ourselves crazy because we`re scared, you know.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes, well, just think about this. About 90 percent of us are filing with a standard deduction, it should literally take you an hour to do this. You`ve got to use electronic filing. More than anything else, this will really help guide you.\par \par \uc2 And when you electronically file, you don`t have the same propensity to make an error. The \uc2 IRS\uc2  says when you have a paper return, the error rate goes way up because you actually have to check, what did I write down on my Social Security number? What -- did I match this up the correct way?\par \par \uc2 The big mistakes are preventable, and often they are silly, but you know, it`s a pain in the neck to amend it, so it is better to do it the first time out the right way.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So I use an electronic -- I file electronically, and what`s nice is if you use the same software, the information is populated from years past, so it makes it that much easier, but it`s not free. It costs a little money, so for people who are looking for free resources, and they want to file.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : So there is something called \uc2 IRS\uc2  Free File. This is a great resource for people who make not a ton of money, you know, like you can get the software for free, but some of them will put you in touch with software providers walk you right through it.\par \par \uc2 And what`s interesting about this, if you could make up to $73,000.00 and get Free File, that`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 There is another resource I don`t think a lot of people know about. It`s the Volunteer Tax Assistance Program, or VITA. This is great. It is aimed at low to medium earners, and it can help -- you get actually a person who will walk you through, and there`s tax counseling for the elderly.\par \par \uc2 These are folks who are great. They`ll hold your hands if you`re over the age of 60. They`ll hold your hands through a lot of the issues that people have a hard time with, like, well, how do I count Social Security? Is it income, is it not? These are resources that are available.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And those are free?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : All free.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what you`re saying is all well and good and people say, but I still need to file an extension.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I`m busy.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Easy -- easy to do Form 4868. The thing about an extension is you will get an extra six months, great, no problem.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Extra six months.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You get an extra six months.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Without a penalty.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Without a penalty, however, you have to pay what you think you owe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead of time.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Yes. And so that means that right now, if you made a lot more money last year than you did the prior year, you might have to send the \uc2 IRS\uc2  some money. And the thing that people forget about is that maybe you sold a house, maybe you actually sold a stock last year. So this can contribute to extra tax liability.\par \par \uc2 Pay what you think you owe, but filing an extension is easy. It`s really important. An extension is an extension to file, but not to pay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think probably the number one reason why people do not file is because they think they owe and don`t have it in their bank account right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re not going to be able to pay it, so what do you do then?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You know what? You are not the first person to be in that situation, so please don`t worry. The \uc2 IRS\uc2  has a lot of payment programs and they do talk to you. They`re not mean people.\par \par \uc2 These are wonderful civil servants. They will put you on an installment plan. Now, you can get a short term plan. This will give you an extra four months to pay.\par \par \uc2 You can also be on a long term installment plan, where you can pay a certain amount every month, and they`ll work with you.\par \par \uc2 There is one last part that I think people fall in a trap --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Were you screwing up your face? This doesn`t look like --\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Well, I know. Well, people use their credit cards to pay their tax bills and I don`t love that. And I`d rather you`d be on a short- term installment plan or a longer term plan. Credit card companies charge fees for this and we`d rather you not go into credit card debt.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there are penalties and interest from the \uc2 IRS\uc2 , but they`re lower than what a credit card would charge you.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jill, what`s going on with these forms? Form 4868, 1099. Where are they coming up with these numbers?\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : It`s just part of the \uc2 IRS\uc2  Code.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It just sounds complicated. It is intimidating.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : Which is awfully -- it is awfully fun. Well, this is one of the things that I think gets in people`s heads. It becomes complicated.\par \par \uc2 Just know, gang, you can do this. You`re an adult. This is actually in the programs that you`re using. So go get them. Go get your mullah. Twenty-nine hundred bucks.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My morning pep talk. Tony, you can do this. Tony, you`re an adult.\par \par \uc2 JILL SCHLESINGER\uc2 : You`re an adult.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony, you`re and adult.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You are an adult.\pa