BETH WERNER and VLADIMIR DUTHIERS singing "Take me Out to the Ball Game.")\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Go ahead. Keep singing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, how much is this bad boy?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes, so this is $42.99, twenty-eight percent off. Unbelievable. You could change your voice. It`s got sound effects. Ready, listen to this.\par \par \uc2 Take a bow. Take a bow. Take a bow.\par \par \uc2 At home, in the car, every party you have, this is going to be stick of them all.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How much?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Yes. $42.99.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Forty two dollars, ninety nine cents. Elizabeth Werner, thank you very much, friend. Always great to have you.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH WERNER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Always, we partner with all these companies to get these deals. To buy anything you saw today. scan that QR code on your screen or head over to CBSDeals.com to start shopping now.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to grab this mic.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us with Baby Harlow. We thank you for watching. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All mine -- she says.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230413ej4d00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Pill Showdown: The Justice Department Appeals A Texas Judge`s Decision, As Americans Protests Nationwide\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Weijia Jiang \par \uc2 584 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We want to turn now to the fast-moving developments in the battle over a key abortion drug. Today, the Justice Department appealed a federal judge`s ruling that would block access to that drug, asking for an emergency stay.\par \par \uc2 And hundreds of top executives at pharmaceutical firms are also blasting the judge`s decision.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Weijia Jiang is at the White House on what this all means for women throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG (voiceover): Tonight, the White House is promising it will fight to keep the abortion pill Mifepristone available to women.\par \par \uc2 Jen Klein is the Biden administration`s point person on abortion-related issues.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER KLEIN (Director, The Gender Policy Council): It is safe, it is effective, and there is no reason to be removing it from the -- from the market at this point.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: The conservative Texas judge behind the ruling to ban the drug argues that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  improperly approved it 23 years ago, ignoring legitimate safety concerns. But at the time, the \uc2 FDA\uc2  actually spent triple the average amount of time studying the drug.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is one of two approved pills that are used together to carry out more than half of legal abortions in the country.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Abortion on demand!\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Over the weekend, protesters blasted the court decision --\par \par \uc2 PROTESTER: This is going to create utter chaos, utter disruption of women`s lives.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: -- while supporters praised it.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The order is all about righting a wrong now.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Just after the Texas ruling, a Washington state judge did the opposite, blocking any changes to mifepristone`s availability in 17 states and the District of Columbia, where Democrats sued.\par \par \uc2 Today, in Massachusetts, the governor announced her state has started stockpiling mifepristone, buying 15,000 doses.\par \par \uc2 Senator Elizabeth Warren:\par \par \uc2 SENATOR ELIZABETH WARREN (D-Massachusetts): This extremist Texas judge reminds us that Roe will be on the ballot in 2024.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Some Republicans are already leaning into that fight.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES (R-Texas): It may be -- come a point where House Republicans on the appropriation side have to defund \uc2 FDA\uc2  programs that don`t make sense.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: But, today, 400 pharmaceutical leaders issued a scorching condemnation of that Texas ruling, saying, if courts can overturn approved drugs without regard for science, then every medicine is at risk of facing the same legal challenges as mifepristone -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s quite a ruling and a saying, yes.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Well, tonight, the mother of a 6-year-old boy who shot and wounded his teacher in a Virginia classroom is facing criminal charges. A grand jury indicted her on charges, including recklessly leaving a loaded firearm and endangering a child. The teacher still recovering from the January shooting filed a $40 million lawsuit last week, claiming school administrators ignored warnings that the boy had a gun at school and posed an imminent threat.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230411ej4a0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Pill Court Battle\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nancy Cordes, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,520 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday morning. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate is off today, but we`re still here. So let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The future of a common abortion pill is on the line after conflicting rulings from two Federal Judges. What it means for abortion access nationwide.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA (Department of Health and Human Services Secretary): Everything is on the table. The President said that way back when the Dobbs decision came out. Every option is on the table.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Israel responds to rocket attacks with bombings inside Syria as Jews, Muslims, and Christians mark the Holy Days.\par \par \uc2 SEN. BEN CARDIN (D-MD): During the Holiday season, it is always a high tension moment.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The Pentagon says a criminal investigation has been opened surrounding a leak involving dozens of classified documents on the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: China simulates striking Taiwan as a US delegation meets with the Country`s President.\par \par \uc2 REP. MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-TX): If we`re going to have deterrence for peace, we need to get these weapons into Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Educators at \uc2 Rutgers University\uc2  are on strike for the first time in the school`s nearly 216-year history.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Back up.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Odell Beckham, Jr. has signed with the \uc2 Baltimore Ravens\uc2  for a one- year $15 million guaranteed deal.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Klay Thompson, three hundred. Wow. Three hundred threes in a year.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Golden State setting an \uc2 NBA\uc2  record of 55 points in the first quarter as the Warriors blow out the Trailblazers.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Never been done.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That`s wild.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Rahm wins the Masters marathon.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Jon Rahm, his first career green jacket and he becomes the first European player to win a US Open and the Masters.\par \par \uc2 JON RAHM (Professional golf player): Out of all the accomplishments and the many great players that have come before me, to be the first to do something like that, it is a very humbling feeling.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I always love watching people`s dreams come true.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is so happy. His wife and his little boy was there. It was so great to see.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Especially when you put that green jacket on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there is something about the green jacket.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just thinking about it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I means a lot to people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was no contest in the end, and we`re going to have a big piece of that with \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  who is down in Augusta.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve got a big show for you as well. Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got a big update --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And we could say, welcome back to, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and welcome back to Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And welcome back to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did you miss us?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, I missed you guys immensely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tested negative for COVID, I`m ready to go.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`re good. We`re all good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are ready to rock, and we`ve got a big update for you this morning, a story CBS News has been investigating for years now. It`s about major illness, including cancer found among members of the military who served in an overseas base after 9/11.\par \par \uc2 Now some of those servicemembers and their families are suing the Department of Defense looking for answers and they`re speaking to our Catherine Herridge.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Plus, the great Josh Groban is here. Did you catch that? The great Josh Groban. I love this man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: How much do you love him?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love him. I`ve loved him since he was 18. He was already a multiplatinum selling singer when he won a Tony nomination that was back in 2017, and now he is back on Broadway in an iconic role as Sweeney Todd. Speaking of dreams, he said that was a dream for him, too.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with this. There is a new fight over abortion access that could become the biggest battle on this issue since Roe v. Wade was overturned.\par \par \uc2 Protests erupted in multiple cities after a Federal Judge in Texas suspended the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of a well-known commonly used abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 Now that decision affects the entire country. Think about that for just a second. But then another Federal Judge, this one in Washington State ruled that the pills cannot be restricted in more than a dozen States.\par \par \uc2 So Nancy Cordes is following all of this at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, there are a lot of moving parts to this story.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Yes, these are two of the most consequential legal decisions since Roe v. Wade was struck down last year and the legal whiplash leaves millions of women caught in the middle. This is likely to be settled right back at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The President is vowing to fight the Texas ruling and the Department of Justice is seeking a stay.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: End of the abortion pill --\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Protests broke out this weekend over the abortion pill ruling that has the potential to affect not just women in Texas, but women across the country.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This is going to create utter chaos, utter disruption of women`s lives.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A conservative Federal Judge in Texas ruled Friday that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  improperly approved the abortion pill, mifepristone 23 years ago, ignoring legitimate safety concerns about the drug.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The order yesterday was all about righting a wrong.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Mifepristone is one of two approved pills that are used together to carry out more than half of legal abortions in this country. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  insists the drug is safe and that serious complications are rare.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): This ruling is an extreme abuse of power.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Some lawmakers urge the Biden administration to resist enforcing the ruling.\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): We know that the executive branch has an enforcement discretion, especially in light of a contradicting ruling coming out of Washington.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Shortly after the Texas ruling, a Washington State Judge blocked any changes to mifepristone`s availability in 17 states and the District of Columbia as administration officials warn that the legal battle in Texas could have far reaching implications.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA: When you turn upside down the entire \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval process, you`re not talking about just mifepristone, you`re talking about every kind of drug.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Cases like this one ensure that abortion will remain a top issue in next year`s elections.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD cheering.)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That helped to propel liberal Judge Janet Protasiewicz to an 11-point win in a high profile State Supreme Court race in Wisconsin just last week, sparking some debate among Republicans about exactly how far to go.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): We can win this issue at the ballot box if we show up reasonable positions. If we have our head in the sand, we`re going to lose.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: For now, many prescribers say they`re going to dispense mifepristone and patients in States where abortion is restricted can continue to order the medication from abroad.\par \par \uc2 Even if the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is forced to withdraw approval of mifepristone, it is likely the drug would still be available for months or even years as the agency works to redo the regulation, so a very confusing situation right now -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, and the fight will continue.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to the latest on a security leak that is sending shockwaves through the Intel Community and could have a big impact on the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon officials are investigating the reported leak of documents that they say appear to contain sensitive and highly-classified material. One of those documents marked "Top Secret." They suggest that the US is spying on some of its international allies including South Korea and Israel, and the documents also seemed to reveal information about the war effort in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 A top US official told CBS News that some of the documents posted on social media appear to be doctored, although it is unclear what exactly that means and the Department of Justice, looking for answers here has opened a criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230411ej4a000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Battle Lines For Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Skyler Henry \par \uc2 755 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Tonight: New battle lines for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Hands off the abortion pill.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: The Biden administration prepares to defend access to the most widely used drug for abortions. The latest on a bombshell legal ruling in Texas with nationwide implications.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA (\uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  Secretary): We want the courts to overturn this reckless decision.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Also tonight, Easter message. Pope Francis highlights hope as he fights off health issues.\par \par \uc2 (MAN singing)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: While in the Holy Land, three faiths mark a rare overlap.\par \par \uc2 Nashville struggles following a mass shooting, protests, and expulsion of the Tennessee Three.\par \par \uc2 Plus, in the West, record snow for ski resorts like Alta, Utah. It`s the winter that won`t quit.\par \par \uc2 And later, around the world in 80 days, these 81-year-olds did it and they`re not done.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Cheers to both of you.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from New York with Jericka Duncan.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good evening, and thanks for joining us on this Easter Sunday, as well as those of you observing Passover and Ramadan.\par \par \uc2 Well tonight, the Biden administration says it is considering all of its options after a Federal Judge in Texas ignited a new national debate over abortion rights. His decision would suspend approval of a key abortion pill making the procedure harder to access even in states where it is legal.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Skyler Henry is at the White House tonight to help us understand the larger impact.\par \par \uc2 Skyler, good evening.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Jericka, good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, in addition to concerns about what the Texas Judge`s decision could mean for women`s health, the Biden administration also says it undermines the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval process, which could impact other drugs that don`t have anything to do with abortions.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The Biden administration is fighting back. Health and Human Services Secretary, \uc2 Xavier Becerra\uc2  is demanding the courts overturn a Texas judge`s ruling that would block access to the abortion pill, mifepristone, the medication used by women in more than half of all abortions across the country.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA: We intend to do everything to make sure it is available to them, not just in a week, but moving forward. Period.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Becerra didn`t rule out defying the order by the Trump- appointed judge.\par \par \uc2 XAVIER BECERRA: Everything is on the table.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: New York Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  is urging the Biden administration to ignore the Texas decision altogether.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-New York): I do believe that we must start to push back on our system of checks and balances.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Not all Democrats are on board with that idea. Some Republicans are warning of possible repercussions.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES (R-Texas): And it may become a point where House Republicans on the Appropriations side have to defund \uc2 FDA\uc2  programs that don`t make sense.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Some Republicans also remain steadfast in their antiabortion position, despite losses in the Midterms and in a recent Wisconsin Supreme Court race that virtually ensured that state`s abortion ban will be overturned.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-South Carolina): We had an 1840 statute that banned abortion pretty much across the board.\par \par \uc2 I do believe in commonsense restrictions on abortion. That`s where America is at.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: On another front, Skyler, this week, President Biden heads to Ireland to celebrate 25 years since that Good Friday Agreement was signed, which helped to end political conflicts and violence in Northern Ireland, but what more can we expect?\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Yeah. The President is quick to tout his Irish heritage. He will head to Belfast in Northern Ireland on Tuesday, marking the progress since that deal signing while also recognizing the economic potential of the country.\par \par \uc2 Later this week, he will also make a keynote address in Dublin talking about the deep historic ties between the U.S. and Ireland -- Jericka.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Busy week ahead. Skyler Henry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020230411ej4900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Rival Rulings By Federal Judges Put The Future Of A Widely Used Abortion Drug In Uncertainty\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Skyler Henry \par \uc2 761 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 April 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tonight: Abortion access in jeopardy. The rival rulings by Federal Judges put the future of a widely used abortion drug in uncertainty. The latest on the legal standoff and political uproar.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): It is contrary to what makes for good public health policy to allow courts and politicians to tell the \uc2 FDA\uc2  what it should do.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Also tonight, War Games. China sends warships and fighter jets toward Taiwan after the island`s leader`s trip to the United States.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elizabeth Palmer is there.\par \par \uc2 Pontiff in public. \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  is present today at the Vatican`s Easter Vigil after being absent from some Good Friday events.\par \par \uc2 On the CBS Money Watch: The job market cools. Why there is still good news for workers.\par \par \uc2 Plus, treading water. Play suspended again at the soggy Masters.\par \par \uc2 JIM AXELROD\uc2 : I`m \uc2 Jim Axelrod\uc2  in a rainy Augusta where everyone is bundled up, but there`s one jacket the golfers are still chasing.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: And later, it is the best seat in the house for the crowning of Britain`s monarchs, a historic Royal relic.\par \par \uc2 IAN LEE: I am Ian Lee at Westminster Abbey with the historic Royal throne getting spruced up for the coronation of King Charles.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from Chicago with Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good evening.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, nine months after the Supreme Court overturned American`s constitutional right to an abortion, providers are once again scrambling. At issue, use of a key abortion medication after two conflicting Court opinions.\par \par \uc2 Last night, a Federal Judge in Texas blocked the Federal government`s decades` old approval of mifepristone, ruling it was not properly tested. But another Judge in Washington State ruled the pill is safe and should remain available in a swath of States.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Skyler Henry is in Washington, DC with more on the legal fight and reactions to the rulings.\par \par \uc2 Skyler, good evening.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Hey, Adriana. Good evening to you.\par \par \uc2 Well, the Biden administration immediately filed a Notice to Appeal the Texas Judge`s decision late last night. Now, the two competing rulings set up a high-stakes legal showdown that`s likely to end up before the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Federal Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk ordered a hold on Federal approval of mifepristone, the most commonly used abortion drug. The Biden administration says the controversial ruling eliminates basic freedoms for women putting their health at risk.\par \par \uc2 VICE PRESIDENT KAMALA HARRIS: It is contrary to what makes for good public health policy to allow Courts and politicians to tell the \uc2 FDA\uc2  what it should do.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The Judge appointed by President Trump issued a 67-page opinion asserting the FDA`s approval 23 years ago violated a Federal rule because the drug was improperly tested.\par \par \uc2 The ruling could limit abortion access even for women in States where the procedure is legal. Antiabortion rights advocates are pleased with the decision.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN KELLER (California Family Council president): We are encouraged that the Federal Judiciary is finally taking a look at the approval of mifepristone and really holding the \uc2 FDA\uc2  to account.\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: Federal Judge Thomas Rice appointed by President Obama blocked the \uc2 FDA\uc2  from making any changes to mifepristone`s availability in 17 Democrat-led States and Washington, DC that sued to expand access.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is one of two approved pills used in more than half of legal abortions in the country. The outcome in this case may undermine the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval process setting a damaging precedent.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: He is holding mifepristone to a standard higher than any other drug is really being held to.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SKYLER HENRY: The Texas ruling won`t go into effect for at least another seven days. Regardless of what happens in this case and the Appeals or Supreme Court, women can still legally order mifepristone online with a prescription even in States where surgical abortions are restricted -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Skyler Henry, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020230412ej4800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Getting Candid About Menopause\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nikki Battiste, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 2,311 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 March 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to begin this hour with the third part of our series that we call Facing Fertility. This morning, we are focusing on menopause, that`s the end of fertility for women. And now more than ever, women including big names like \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2 , Naomi Watts, and others, they are coming forward to say, we`ve got to talk about this.\par \par \uc2 Most women start menopause around the ages of say fifty-one, fifty-two. Five percent of women experience early menopause, that means between the ages of 40 and 45 and one percent experience it even before the age of 40.\par \par \uc2 So talking about menopause, we know this isn`t always comfortable, but it`s so very, very important. That`s why Drew Barrymore and I said yes, when Nikki Battiste asked us, let`s sit down and have a conversation.\par \par \uc2 We started that conversation with perimenopause. That`s when many women start to notice changes as the body starts transitioning toward the M-word, menopause.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Was there an "aha" moment when you thought, I`m in perimenopause.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I realized that I was in perimenopause when I started having my period every two weeks, and then that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Was it a heavy flow? That`s a sign.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes. Like a teenager.\par \par \uc2 Well, one doctor also just told me like this could last, you know, on the worst case scenario, 10 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ten years.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: And I was like, I will never make it 10 years like this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I did.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Help me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did. And it is true.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: You have to help me through, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is true. It can last 10 years. I mean, the perimenopause didn`t last 10 years, but I still have -- you know, they say by the time you`re 50, definitely there`s something going on.\par \par \uc2 I`m just glad we`re even having this conversation, Nikki, because I didn`t even know the phrase "perimenopause."\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Same.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I had never even heard -- I had heard of menopause, but I`d never even heard the phrase "perimenopause" until I went to the doctor because you know not to get too graphic, but when it first happens for a lot of people, it looked like a crime scene.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Since you`ve been through the whole process --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I still -- I don`t think I`m done because I still have hot flashes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: You still do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still do, not as much as I used to, not as much as I used to. A couple of times during COVID, I`m like, is this COVID or is this menopause?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: What does a hot flash feel like? Because that I`m not getting yet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It feels like -- it starts with -- it feels like you`re burning inside and then --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Like acid reflux?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, it just feels hot.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just, for me, it was just a physical heat and then you can sometimes have dripping, drenching sweats. It just can happen at the most inopportune times. I`ve been on the red carpet where photographers would say "Gayle, are you okay?" I go, "It`s just a hot flash." And they go, "Oh, sorry, sorry, sorry."\par \par \uc2 It`s like people when you say I`m having hot -- oh, sorry, sorry, sorry. Like they`ve said something very bad about you.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: But I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s why I think it is good we`re saying, yes, menopause, hot flashes.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: People know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dry vagina. It is all part of it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Most people just hear menopause and think it`s hot flashes. That`s all it is. But it is so much more.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Why has hot flashes been marketed so well, where people go, oh, sure. But if you say perimenopause, I`m in menopause. You know, I`m experiencing this. Most of it seems very unfamiliar and confusing, not just to men, but women.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Did either of you seek any treatment? Go talk to a doctor?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I did talk to a doctor, but I didn`t take any hormones. You know, I know friends that have creams or they take medication. And I just - - I`ve just sort of weathered through it when I realized you really don`t have to do that.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I was offered hormone therapy and I said, no.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why?\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Because I really didn`t feel like the doctor who was suggesting it understood where I was at. And it seemed preemptive and I am like, until I get so desperate, and this is also obvious, I don`t want to take something preemptively.\par \par \uc2 At some point, there might be a treatment that`s right for you. So don`t do it too fast, but don`t be a hero unnecessarily if you don`t need to be.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Don`t be a hero.\par \par \uc2 The other reason why I think this is hard is because when you think of menopause, you think of old.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You think of old. Her life is over.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is done.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Put her out to pasture.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And what we know is that`s just not true. If there`s any message we could get across, I think that would be the one that would be the most helpful.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: How do we change the conversation about menopause?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To be this --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Humorous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You keep talking, because it is kind of funny.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Crime scene.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Everything is kind of funny.\par \par \uc2 I think the more women also, you know, in their 40s, 50s, and 60s, are looking so attractive, feeling so vibrant, living their best lives, the way menopause has been branded is you`re old, you`re done. That`s not it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You`re old. You`re done. Your vagina is dry.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have no value in society anymore.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: You`re not attractive.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re not attractive. It`s like cobwebs down there.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: You`re not sexy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And all of that is not true. Also, the other thing, Nikki is, you don`t have to have drastic symptoms. Not everybody has these kinds of symptoms.\par \par \uc2 I know some women who they have been through it, and they`ve just sort of sailed through it and have had very minimal things. It wasn`t disruptive to their life. That was not my experience.\par \par \uc2 But my mother also had very bad symptoms. But she never talked about it. Listen, I remember being on vacation with my mom and she had white pants and her period came. One, she was in menopause. Had to be perimenopause and this blood started gushing. I said, what is happening? She goes oh, nothing, nothing. I`m fine. I`m fine. I`m fine. No conversation about it.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Why is that? Why has that been the way for so long?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Even our own moms?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Because they were embarrassed and it was in --\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: A generational thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was generational and it was improper and indelicate to talk about. You`re not raising Frankie in all of that way, I`m sure.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I also think, Nikki --\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And we should also educate our sons.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nikki, I was just going to say that.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: We have to tell the guy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Beau`s mom, I was just going to say that.\par \par \uc2 I think you have to have a conversation with your boys as well. But I do think kids, boys and girls, should be well informed. I don`t want little boys going "ew." I don`t want that.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I agree.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because it is something that we go through and it`s just, you know, male and female. There are differences. And those differences are okay.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: If you were to use one word, one phrase to describe how we should think about menopause. What would it be?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: One word? Reality.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: Mine is natural.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ah, that`s good.\par \par \uc2 DREW BARRYMORE: I think ours are really, as usual hand in hand.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s good. Yes. Reality.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: An OBGYN who specializes in menopause told me that hormone replacement therapy can be safe and effective if it is done at the right age for the right amount of time. What that timeframe is can vary from woman to woman.\par \par \uc2 Another doctor told us that women who reach menopause before 45 especially before 40 should strongly consider hormone replacement therapy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. All the doctors will tell you that it is safe. We did something with Dr. Sharon Malone at Oprah Daily. She says we need to stop - - she is also a doctor who specializes in this -- stop normalizing suffering around this.\par \par \uc2 You know, it seems such a taboo and uncomfortable thing, but I think \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  says it best. She says, you know when you reach menopause, be glad that you`ve reached it. It just shows you`re at another stage of life. She says there`s no shame in my menopause game.\par \par \uc2 And I think we have to figure out a better way to reframe the conversation, so it doesn`t make people uncomfortable, including men.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think that`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Including little boys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s a great point, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Because throughout this conversation that you were having, I think Tony and I were -- we kept asking questions and stunned at my own ignorance.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: When it comes to this particular subject. I`m actually stunned and I consider myself somebody who kind of knows a lot of things about a lot of things and I don`t know anything as much as I should about this and I have a little girl at home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. That`s true. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: And understanding is -- understanding is being able to make us feel more comfortable and I think for me, I always worried, will I be less desirable? Will I be less sexy when I hit menopause? But Gayle King, you are living proof that that is not the case.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. And the answer is no. The answer really is no.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s just so much stuff that happens to a woman`s body. You guys are so lucky in that respect. We have so many moving parts and so much stuff that happens. I don`t feel you guys have half the stuff -- have to go through half the stuff we do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is not a competition.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Gayle, I want to thank you and Drew for your transparency in having this conversation. It helps so many others.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was a good conversation. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: I also want to thank our Facing Fertility series producers, Hilary Cook (sp?) and Rachel Bailey (sp?) and editor, Chris Assan (sp?).\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, shout out.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: They had been wonderful, and a personal things to my fertility doctor, Dr. Eric Forman at Columbia University Fertility Center. He works magic.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Such an important series, Nikki.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am really proud of you and our show for doing this.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Me, too, but Nikki guided us through it, you know, because Drew and I could both be long talkers. Nikki has a -- let`s get back on track, girls. This is what we`re talking about. So, well done.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: I learned so much. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well done.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well done. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Natural and reality, those are the two words. All right, Nikki, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 All right ahead, "Yellowjackets" star Melanie Lynskey talks about landing her big role in the hit Showtime series decades into her acting career.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Yellowjackets.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But first, it is 8:11, I`m being told. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up for our series, talking about Changing the Game. This is a game changer right here. What`s your name, game changer?\par \par \uc2 ALLISON JASLOW (\uc2 Iraq and Afghanistan Veterans of America\uc2  CEO): Allison Jaslow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is Allison Jaslow. She is the first openly gay leader of a major veterans group. She is a knockout. How she is fighting to improve mental healthcare. Something tells me you have something to say this morning.\par \par \uc2 ALLISON JASLOW: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we look forward to talking to you.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. Allison joins us at the table right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230324ej3m000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Showdown Over Abortion Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 Vladimir Duthiers, Christina Ruffini, Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 March 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now to Texas where a conservative Federal Judge may soon issue a ruling that would block access to a long-approved abortion pill nationwide.\par \par \uc2 The pill has been available for two decades and it is the most common method of abortion in the United States.\par \par \uc2 Christina Ruffini is outside the Courthouse in Amarillo, Texas.\par \par \uc2 Christina, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Access to this case was extremely limited. Fewer than 40 seats in the Courtroom and no publicly available live stream or audio feed for a case that could have wide-ranging nationwide implications.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Whose choice?\par \par \uc2 GROUP: Our choice.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: A handful of proabortion rights demonstrators stood outside the Federal Courthouse in Amarillo.\par \par \uc2 HARPER METCALF (Proabortion access protester): Access to medication abortion is one of the last options that we have.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: While inside, an antiabortion rights group called the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine argued the Court should reverse the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval for the drug mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 The medication approved up to the 10th week of pregnancy is part of a two- step medical abortion process that makes up 53 percent of abortions in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The organization made its case in front of Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk, a Trump appointee that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  incorrectly approved the drug through an accelerated process and said it presents a danger to women.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FDA\uc2  says the drug, which has been used in the United States for more than two decades went through a rigorous four-your approval process and says 99 percent of patients are treated without an adverse reaction.\par \par \uc2 Is that the goal to make it harder to get an abortion for women?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (Texas Right to Life President): That`s definitely not the goal.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: John Seago is the President of Texas Right to Life.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO: But when the \uc2 FDA\uc2  looks at what their job is, they need to start over and they need to do the due diligence on this abortion inducing drug.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: The drug is currently available by mail even in States where abortion is illegal, but this decision could impact its availability nationwide.\par \par \uc2 MARY ZIEGLER (University of California Davis Law Professor): It could have ripple effects in every State, not just in States that want to ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And during the hearing, the Judge appeared to issue questions that seemed sympathetic towards the antiabortion rights group, but didn`t say anything to reveal his position.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, he says he hopes to have his decision as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Christina, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to a disturbing new report from the CDC on maternal death rates in this country. In 2021, more than 1,200 women died from pregnancy-related issues in America, up from 861 in 2020. That compares with 658 maternal deaths in 2018.\par \par \uc2 So that means we`re looking at an 83 percent increase over just three years.\par \par \uc2 The report also found significant racial disparities, Black women were 2.6 times more likely to die than White women and nearly 2.5 times more likely to die than Hispanic women.\par \par \uc2 Now, for context, other studies have shown the US has the highest maternal death rate by far among high income nations. And in this country, about 6.9 million women have little to no access to maternal healthcare.\par \par \uc2 And the reasons are, you know, this report didn`t really highlight what led to this change, but we know that there are higher cesarean rates in this country. I had a C-section, Wendy McNeal, our producer I was talking to this morning, she had a C-section. She was okay with me saying that. And then we have issues like obesity and diabetes in this country.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the disparity in terms of race, I mean, that`s an issue across healthcare.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it`s also economic. We`re a high-income country, but we are a high income inequality country as well. And one of the determinants here is economic instability according to the CDC and that cuts across race.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, I`ll take this.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FAA\uc2  held an Emergency Safety Summit yesterday to address a series of aviation close calls all around the country, and the Chair of the \uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  said officials cannot ignore the issue.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOMENDY (\uc2 National Transportation Safety Board\uc2  Chair): These recent incidents must serve as a wake-up call for every single one of us before something more catastrophic occurs, before lives are lost.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There have been at least seven near collisions at airports across the country so far this year alone.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230318ej3g0002u\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Future Of Abortion Pills In America Could Be Hanging In The Balance\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Christina Ruffini \par \uc2 478 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 March 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Now to the biggest challenge to abortion rights since Roe vs. Wade was overturned in June. A federal judge in Amarillo, Texas, heard arguments today in a case that seeks to overturn the FDA`s approval of the widely used abortion pill mifepristone and remove it from the market. More than half of all abortions in the U.S. are medication abortions.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Christina Ruffini is at the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI (voiceover): Members of the public and press lined up before dawn outside the courthouse.\par \par \uc2 Harper Metcalf (sp?) was one of a handful of pro-abortion rights demonstrators.\par \par \uc2 HARPER METCALF (Pro-Abortion Rights Demonstrator): Millions of women have lost access to abortion care, especially here in Texas. So, the access to medication abortion is one of the last options that we have.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Federal Judge Matthew Kacsmaryk, a Trump appointee, heard from an anti-abortion rights organization that argued the \uc2 FDA\uc2  improperly approved mifepristone, posing risks to patients.\par \par \uc2 The judge appeared sympathetic in his questioning, asking the group`s lawyers if there`s precedent for a court to withdraw \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval of a drug. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  argued the drug is safe and effective, approved up to the 10th week of pregnancy, and has been on the market for more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 The drug is available by mail even in states where abortion is illegal.\par \par \uc2 MARY ZIEGLER (\uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 ): In some ways, this could be a bigger deal than the reversal of Roe v. Wade was, because it could have ripple effects in every state, not just in states that want to ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: John Seago, president of the Texas Right to Life, says the goal is to protect Americans from unsafe products.\par \par \uc2 Is there a concern that could expand far beyond this issue and have unforeseen ramifications?\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (President, Texas Right to Life): No. I think this is absolutely appropriate. There has to be some kind of check on the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and on a pro-abortion administration.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: The judge says he hopes to make a decision in this case as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 As for safety, Norah, two major American medical groups filed briefs in support of the \uc2 FDA\uc2 , saying the drug is safe and effective.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Christina Ruffini at the courthouse, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230316ej3f0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Texas Sued Over Abortion Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 1,146 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 March 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the first case of its kind in Texas, five women are suing the State claiming its abortion ban puts them in danger. Texas is one of 12 US States with the most restrictive new laws since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian spoke to one of the plaintiffs who says she was not allowed to claim a medical emergency.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN (Plaintiff): My heart broke into a million pieces.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Anna Zargarian was only 19 weeks pregnant when her water broke. Her doctor told her she had almost no chance of carrying her baby to term and her own life was threatened.\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN: My doctor put it, it was like, it`s a 50/50 shot that you`ll get an infection, but a less than one percent shot that you`re going to leave this hospital with a baby.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Texas doctors told her they couldn`t perform an abortion, so she traveled to Colorado.\par \par \uc2 What was that like to have to leave Texas, your home, to have this done?\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN: Just terrifying. I felt like a shell of a human, but I was just like, you know wrapped up in grief.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Zargarian is now one of five women suing the State of Texas over its abortion law.\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP (Center for Reproductive Rights President): It is now dangerous to be pregnant in Texas.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: After the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade last summer, a State trigger law banned abortion unless a woman`s life is at risk or the fetus has a fatal diagnosis. And doctors who provide the procedure outside those limited circumstances face the possibility of life in prison.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (Texas Right to Life President): We were honored to work on the Texas Heartbeat Act.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: John Seago helped that trigger law get passed. He says doctors need more education on what qualifies as life-saving.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO: Texas law does not require for a woman to be at death`s door to enact and to get involved and actually save that woman.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The lawsuit asks the Court to clarify medical exemptions. Zargarian says she wants that for other women and eventually, a healthy baby for her family.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Do you have hope to try again?\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN: You know, I do. But at this point in my life, it`s just really terrifying to think about getting pregnant in Texas again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: This suit comes as a Federal Judge in Texas is expected to rule soon on a case challenging the FDA`s approval of the common abortion drug, mifepristone. That ruling could impact access even in States where abortion is protected -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, got it, Janet. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, more wet weather on its way to California and those people who are buried in the snow in the Sierra Nevada, they are going to get a break today, but new snow is expected tomorrow. Think about that.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  has more for us now.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 There is no end in sight for the West, which is concerning because this winter has been outrageous. So far this season, the Sierra is well above average from north to south. In just the past week, we have had five feet of snow at higher elevations, but notice the snowpack below 5,000 feet. These areas will experience warmer temperatures, which could lead to dire consequences in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 The reason? The atmospheric rivers over the next week. They will have a tropical connection which means heavy rain and a combo of precip and melting snow really majorly increases the flood threat.\par \par \uc2 Now starting tomorrow, we will get hit hard. Thursday and Friday will be our most intense days, but that rain and snow will continue all the way through the weekend, and by the way, into next week as well.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just the river and street flooding that we have to worry about, snow that is currently sitting on roofs can absorb the downpours increasing the weight, escalating the risk of collapse.\par \par \uc2 Now through Sunday, we will see over five inches of rain and another five feet of snow.\par \par \uc2 Now Tony, with all of that said, California remains in the midst of a mega drought. And one good year is not enough to actually wipe it out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  for us. Stephanie, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Turning now to Washington, Congress is launching a new effort to crack down on TikTok. A dozen Democratic and Republican senators are cosponsoring a bill that would give the government expanded powers to respond to what they call a potential National Security threat.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is at the White House.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning,\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Vlad, Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 The legislation which doesn`t directly named TikTok would give the Commerce Department broader powers to outright ban or at least restrict TikTok and other apps tied to China and five other nations deemed US adversaries.\par \par \uc2 The new bipartisan proposal is the first to get the White House on board. It says the bill would help the Federal government keep apps from exploiting US technology in a way that risks sensitive data or National Security.\par \par \uc2 Now TikTok is used by at least a hundred million Americans each month, and because its parent company is Chinese, the concern among Federal regulators is the Chinese government might one day try to use the data collected by TikTok to track users or use the platform for widespread propaganda.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, TikTok, no fan of this. It says that banning the app in the US is a ban on the export of American culture and values to the billion plus people who use our service worldwide. It says there are other methods for the Biden administration to address National Security concerns.\par \par \uc2 And whether all of this will hold up in Court remains uncertain. Previous attempts by the Trump administration for example, to rein in certain apps have been blocked by Federal Courts out of concern it would violate the First Amendment -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Ed, thank you very much. I got it.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230309ej380002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Five Women Are Suing The State Of Texas Over Its Abortion Ban\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 566 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 March 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, five Texas women are suing the Lone Star State after they claimed they were denied medically necessary abortions because their doctors feared being prosecuted over the state`s ban on the procedure. The women came forward to tell their stories today on the steps of the state capitol in Austin.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Janet Shamlian is there.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN (Plaintiff): And I needed an abortion to preserve my health, but couldn`t get one in Texas.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN (voiceover): Anna Zargarian is one of five women suing the state of Texas over its abortion ban. She was denied the procedure at 19 weeks after her water broke early, she says putting her at risk of a potentially life-threatening infection.\par \par \uc2 Her doctors said they couldn`t perform the abortion. So she had one in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN: I felt like a shell of a human, but I was just, like, you know, wrapped up in grief.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The lawsuit was filed on behalf of the women by the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2 , an abortion rights advocacy group.\par \par \uc2 Texas is among 12 states with the most restrictive abortion rules. The procedure is banned unless a woman`s life is at risk or the fetus has a fatal diagnosis. But physicians say they`re fearful, even when they would typically provide an abortion as the standard of care, because of the law that criminalizes performing the procedure.\par \par \uc2 John Seago helped get Texas` abortion ban passed. He says some doctors are confused about what the law requires.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SEAGO (President, Texas Right to Life): Texas law does not require for a woman to be at death`s door to involve -- get involved and actually save that woman.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Today`s suit asked the court to clarify medical exceptions like Zargarian`s. She hopes to try again.\par \par \uc2 ANNA ZARGARIAN: At this point in my life, it`s just really terrifying to think about getting pregnant in Texas again.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Today`s suit comes as a Texas federal judge is expected to rule soon on a case challenging the FDA`s approval of an abortion judge. That ruling could impact access even in states where the procedure is legal -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Janet Shamlian, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, now to the news on Wall Street. Stocks took a tumble after Federal Reserve Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  warned today that interest rates could be raised more than expected in response to recent positive economic data. Well, that sent investor`s running for the hills, with all three major indexes ending the day in negative territory. Bank stocks were the hardest- hit.\par \par \uc2 Over the past three years, the COVID-19 pandemic has impacted nearly every aspect of our lives, including how and where people work. The ripple effect has been felt in many business districts across the country.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230308ej37001ba\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For March 5, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Bob Schieffer, Robert Costa, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,368 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: the spring to-do list on Capitol Hill gets even longer, and the Republican Party struggle to define itself continues.\par \par \uc2 Congress scrambles to deal with rail safety and environmental concerns. Yet another \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  train derailed yesterday in Ohio.\par \par \uc2 Plus, a key Democrat says he`s had it when it comes to the nation`s debt.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN (D-West Virginia): It can`t be this total political division. It`s not my fault. It`s your fault. It`s their fault.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will talk with West Virginia Senator Joe Manchin.\par \par \uc2 Then: The Republican race for the 2024 nomination is on, and key Republicans are picking sides. But the bigger question is, what exactly does the party stand for?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We had a Republican Party that was ruled by freaks, neocons...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT DONALD TRUMP: ... globalists, open border zealots, and fools.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: As more candidates consider jumping in, one is opting out.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN (R-Maryland): It was a tough decision, but I have decided that I will not be a candidate for the Republican nomination for president.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Former Maryland Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  tells our Robert Costa why he made that decision.\par \par \uc2 And we will talk to Illinois Democrat Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 . He`s being recruited to help President Biden`s expected reelection campaign.\par \par \uc2 And was COVID-19 a result of a lab accident in Wuhan, China? New intelligence reports from the Department of Energy and \uc2 FBI\uc2  conflict with other agency reviews. Ohio Republican Brad Wenstrup is heading up a new committee investigating the origins of the virus. We will talk to him., Then former \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner Dr. \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2  will help us sort through it all.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Saturday`s \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  derailment in Springfield, Ohio, was the fourth train derailment in Ohio in the last five months, including the one in East Palestine that spilled toxic chemicals. \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  tells CBS News that no one was injured and no toxic substances were on board the train involved in yesterday`s incident. The CEO of \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  is expected to testify before a Senate panel on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 We want to begin the show with Democratic Senator Joe Manchin, who joins us from Charleston, West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 I want to start on that...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Good morning, Brennan. Thank -- Margaret, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to start on that derailment.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The president last week praised bipartisan railway safety legislation that would have new rules for trains carrying hazardous materials, increased fines for safety violations, phase in newer cars.\par \par \uc2 Will you vote for it? Is that sufficient?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Yes, I`m going to be supporting that. We need to do it.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2015, in Mount Carbon in West Virginia, we had a derailment, 27 cars, tanker cars carrying Bakken oil. It went off the tracks and derailed and exploded and caused a tremendous problem there. And it was very, very dangerous. It could have been a little -- a lot worse, if it had been a little farther down the tracks, could have torn up a whole town.\par \par \uc2 But with that we were recommended that the electronic pneumatic brakes should be something -- should be considered that might prevent this, routine maintenance checks and auditing and things of this sort. I don`t think any of that has been done.\par \par \uc2 And it`s time for us to get serious about this. We`re moving many, many products, many more products on the rails and on our roads than we ever did before. And we have a lot of people who don`t want any pipelines. Pipelines would help alleviate a lot of this problem with the oil that we need in our country and we will be using for quite some time to do it safer.\par \par \uc2 But out of sight, out of mind. They are thinking, if you don`t have a pipeline, you won`t be using the product. Well, that`s far from the truth. And this is the results of people just not making good decisions. And it`s what`s broken in -- in -- in West Virginia. And -- and...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... broken across the country as far as in Washington, the politics.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: It`s broken. It needs to be fixed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want -- I want to come back to energy in a moment.\par \par \uc2 You`re saying it`s broken. You gave a pretty fiery speech a few days ago in the Senate. You`re at odds with the White House and with many in your own party because you are saying that Democrats need to talk about out-of- control spending and are refusing to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 You did ding Republicans for not offering specific cuts. If you are the dealmaker -- you seem to be positioning yourself there -- where is it that you see room for negotiation?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Well, Margaret, first of all, I encourage Speaker Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 I was hoping that he would, first of all, take things off the table that doesn`t cause a conflict, but most -- most importantly, sit down with the president. And reached out to the White House. They did sit down, had a meeting. I`m encouraging much more of that.\par \par \uc2 But what we can do is, can`t we get together and just talk about how do we have this much debt accumulated this -- in this short of a period of time? Within 10 years, Margaret, we have accumulated the greatest amount of debt in the history of our country in the shortest period of time. Can`t we at least find out what we did and how we expanded?\par \par \uc2 I know that COVID did so much of it. But, you know, we`re past the COVID...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... problems. And what we need to do is to get back to normal.\par \par \uc2 But we`ve gone from $3.5 trillion in spending to over $6.2 trillion in spending every year in the last 10 years. That`s just unacceptable. You`ve got to sit down. Anybody that thinks we don`t have a problem in Washington, anybody that thinks that the politics is not broken in Washington is not living in reality, does not want to face the facts and the truth.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 So -- so, what is the truth here? Because Social Security and Medicare make up nearly 40 percent of spending in 2023. If no one is touching those programs, where are you finding the cuts?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Well, first of all, just do our job on time.\par \par \uc2 We`ve been told that, if we just had a budget done -- we don`t even have a budget anymore. The president is -- is a month late in putting his budget out, which will come out next week. But I don`t see the House or the Senate bringing a budget forward. And, basically, by a piece of legislation was passed back in 1985, we`re supposed to have our budgets...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... from the House and the Senate done by April the 1st.\par \par \uc2 The president basically submits his in February, and, by September the 30th -- I`m told there`s billions and billions of dollars of savings just right there if we just do it on time.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure. But that`s not...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: We`re not even doing that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That -- right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: That`s what I say it`s, not working. That`s...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But that -- but...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: But it gets a downward trajection, basically, and capping...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, but more has to be done...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: How about capping some of the...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... done than that. I mean, you know...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Yes, capping some of the discretionary spending.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You -- OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, there`s a lot to talk about right there. But I want to ask you, what is Joe...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Hey, Mar...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What is Joe Manchin looking for in this deal? Are you looking for your permitting reform, for example, that Democrats didn`t deliver on, though they had told you that you would have an agreement on?\par \par \uc2 Are you looking for that to be tucked into a potential bill in this agreement that you`re saying has to be struck between Republicans and Democrats?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Well, rather -- tucking something in, we`re not trying to hide anything, basically.\par \par \uc2 If we don`t do permitting, and we don`t have permitting reform in America, we`re not going to meet the challenges and be energy-independent energy- secured. If you`re not energy-secured, you`re definitely not going to be a superpower of the world, and depending on other parts of the world to provide what you won`t do for yourself.\par \par \uc2 That has to be done. I don`t care what side of the arena you`re on, if you want transmission, if you want pipelines, if you realize we`re going to have a balanced energy proposal. That`s what the Inflation Reduction Act was for, energy security. The administration, this administration has touted that as strictly an environmental bill.\par \par \uc2 It`s good for the environment, but it`s also very, very necessary for us to have the fossil energy...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: ... using it better and cleaner than anywhere in the world to have the security we need. That`s what we need to do. And that`s what they`ve been avoiding.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: You have to have permitting. If not, all this is going to be voided.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about what appears to be some separation between you and the president.\par \par \uc2 You were quoted a few days ago as saying: "We`re just in different ball games. We`re not even in the same ballpark on many things."\par \par \uc2 Are you going to endorse \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  if he runs for reelection?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Oh, there`s plenty of time for the election. This is the problem with America right now. We start an election every time there`s a cycle coming up.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, he`s the leader of your party.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: I -- I -- no, the bottom line is, let`s see who`s involved. Let`s wait until we see who all the players are. Let`s just wait until it all comes out.\par \par \uc2 My main purpose right now is to work for my country and my -- and my state. That`s my responsibility. I`m not going to make my announcement for anything until the end of the year. I`m not going to make a decision what my political position is going to be or where I`m going to do for my political future.\par \par \uc2 I won`t do it until the end of the year. I got too much work to do now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Your political future, you mean the question of whether you personally are going to run for reelection in the Senate in West Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Your Republican governor, \uc2 Jim Justice\uc2 , says he`s going to run for your seat, or he thinks he`s got a good shot at it. Why haven`t you made up your mind?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: There`s too -- the bottom line, I got plenty of time to make up my mind. The election is not until November 2024. We don`t even file until January of 2024.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: And to be running and basically -- not basically looking at the problems you have, we`ve got a runaway debt. We got inflation that`s killing people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: We`ve got an unsecured energy. We have a border that`s out of control.\par \par \uc2 You`re telling me we`re in the same ball game or the same ballpark? I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said let`s see who all the players are when it comes to running for president.\par \par \uc2 You`ve said you`re not running for president. Is that an open question, though? Who -- who do you think...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: I didn`t say that. I didn`t say anything about that.\par \par \uc2 I -- the bottom line is, I will make my political decision in December, whatever it may be.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To run for president?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: I`m not taking anything off the table, and I`m not put -- and I`m not putting anything on the table.\par \par \uc2 I said I will make a decision in January -- at this -- at the end of this year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`ll make a decision at the end of this year as to...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: Simply that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... as to who you will endorse for president?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: What my political future will be, what...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, but for president.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: What I will be involved with, how I will be involved.\par \par \uc2 I will be making -- any decisions I make politically will not be done until the end of the year. I`m focused on fixing what`s wrong with Washington. And the politics are so toxic, the more you talk about this party, that party, what candidate and this candidate.\par \par \uc2 Look at what you have facing you right now. You`ve got inflation. You`ve got, basically, energy. You`ve got these unsecured borders. You`ve got geopolitical unrest. And we`re talking about everything but that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We are -- we have a lot to talk about.\par \par \uc2 And, Senator Manchin, you`re welcome back.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We got to leave it there, because...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JOE MANCHIN: I`m happy to come back, Margaret, any time.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Manchin was just talking about not wanting to talk about campaign 2024.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to do that. It is up and running, because front-runner former President Trump was in the Washington area yesterday speaking at a conservative political gathering, where he had some pretty tough talk about what would happen if he is not reelected.\par \par \uc2 Our Robert Costa spoke exclusively with former Maryland Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  about why he is not jumping in.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You think by sitting out -- the field, maybe it`s a little tighter?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: I hope so.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It`s a little harder for Trump to get that nomination?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: I sure hope so.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Bob`s here. We`re going to be talking with him next. And you will hear more from that interview with Governor Hogan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So far, there are only three major Republican candidates in the 2024 campaign, but more than a dozen are still undecided.\par \par \uc2 Our Robert Costa spoke to former Maryland Governor \uc2 Larry Hogan\uc2  on Friday.\par \par \uc2 And, Bob, he told you he`s not running. Did he explain why?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He did.\par \par \uc2 The former Maryland governor said he felt conflicted as he made this decision. Let`s hear what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: I was struggling, because my heart was telling me to run. My head was telling me, no, that just does not make sense for a whole host of reasons.\par \par \uc2 And my gut was flipping back and forth. So it really came down to, if I wasn`t 100 percent convinced, then I shouldn`t do it.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You were torn?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: I was torn.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Toughest decision of your political career?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Absolutely, toughest decision I ever made.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Politically, by staying out of the race, it`s a smaller field, may be tougher for Trump to get the nomination?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: I didn`t want to have a pileup of a bunch of people fighting.\par \par \uc2 Right now, you have Trump and DeSantis at the top of the field soaking up all the oxygen, getting all the attention, and then a whole lot of the rest of us in single digits. And the more of them you have, the less chance you have for somebody rising up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Bob, he doesn`t see a path for himself. He worries about too many challengers carving a path, essentially, for the former president.\par \par \uc2 What else did he have to say about the campaign?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Margaret, as you know, there is this lingering divide between traditional conservative Republicans and the Trump wing of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 The former governor said, as he looked at the race, he saw a narrow path for Republicans like himself as they look to 2024.\par \par \uc2 Let`s hear what he had to say about that.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Well, there`s no question it was -- it`s challenging.\par \par \uc2 There`s a big, big fight for the -- I would say the heart and soul of the Republican Party that I have been talking about for years. It`s still going on. We`re making progress, though. I mean, it went from about 90 percent of the Republican primary base was behind Trump, to about 60 percent after January 6, to it`s down to about 30 percent now.\par \par \uc2 There are -- there`s about two-thirds of the people in the Republican Party, while they might have supported Trump and Trump`s policies, they really are ready to consider moving in a different direction.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But he`s still leading the polls.\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: He`s leading in the polls, and there`s no question it`s -- he`s a formidable challenge.\par \par \uc2 But I think a year is an eternity in politics. And the first primaries are about a year away. So I think what it looks like today could be completely different than what it looks like a year from now.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You are a two-term governor, just left office. You`ve long been connected to the legacy of \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , conservative movement politics.\par \par \uc2 Is your decision to not run in 2024 in any way an acknowledgment that that party, that version of the Republican Party is fading or even gone?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: You know, I don`t feel that way at all.\par \par \uc2 I think we certainly went off in the wrong direction. And we`re not back on track. It`s going to take a while. We`re not there yet. I would say the party of Reagan is not dead, and neither is the party of Trump.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: How serious is this moment for your party in terms of beating former President Trump? If he wins the White House again, what would that mean for American democracy?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Well, I`m -- I`m hopeful that it`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But what does it mean if it does?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Then I think it`s -- we`re going to have to do some soul-searching. We`re going to be digging out for a long time.\par \par \uc2 But, look, we`ve lost seven out of the last eight popular votes for -- in the presidential race. And we`ve lost -- we`ve had three horrible election cycles, where we should have done -- had a huge pickup in the last election, and we didn`t. We lost, before that, the Senate, the House, and the White House.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to start getting back to a party that -- that people will vote for, or we don`t get to govern.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: When you look at your effort to try to get the Republican Party to come back to its conservative roots, you`re facing a lot of obstacles.\par \par \uc2 There`s a big battle over what the truth is. And the recent Dominion lawsuit against the FOX News, the \uc2 FOX Corporation\uc2  is one example of how there`s a real battle over whether election fraud happened or not. All these false claims of fraud are out there right now in the political discussion, especially on the right.\par \par \uc2 What`s your response to the Dominion lawsuit against FOX News? And what does it reveal about the Republican Party and perhaps FOX News?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Well, I don`t know all the details of the Dominion lawsuit, but I`m certainly following the controversy.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not -- it doesn`t come as a surprise, because, for years, we`ve had people who, you know, I would hear from, elected officials, and leaders of the party, and people in Congress, or fellow governors, or people in conservative media that would say privately, something -- I was always telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 And I was sometimes criticized for it. But I always stood up and said exactly what I thought. People would say to me: "Oh, I agree with what you`re saying. But I can`t say it."\par \par \uc2 Or they would -- they would be -- you know, they would know the truth, but say something different just to win votes. And I think that`s part of the problem. And I think we`ve got to get back to truth-telling. And we`ve got to stop the conspiracy theories. It`s why we lost all the elections in -- in this last race.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the people that were spouting election lies and that were saying that nothing happened on January 6 or that the -- the pandemic was -- was fake, the virus didn`t happen, they all lost. And the people who actually won were the commonsense conservative Republicans like me who were actually able to win swing votes and that were talking about issues like the economy, like crime and education. They were focused on things people cared about.\par \par \uc2 We can`t keep looking backward and we can`t keep denying facts.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Is Governor DeSantis somebody you could see yourself supporting as the non-Trump candidate in your party?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Well, look, the people of Florida just overwhelmingly elected \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . I have said earlier that I think governors is -- are a good training ground to become president.\par \par \uc2 We have a lot of great governors to consider. I -- maybe \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and I have different styles, but, certainly, he`s -- he`s got every right to get out there and make the case.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Where do you stand on Governor DeSantis`s effort to counter \uc2 Disney\uc2  and its self-governing zone in Florida?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: The controversy, to me, was about telling a business that, if you don`t agree with me, I`m going to put you out of business or take it over.\par \par \uc2 And I`m a -- I`m a traditional kind of small-government conservative Republican who doesn`t believe in heavy-handed local governments telling businesses what to do. So we come at it from a different perspective. It`s not about the -- the -- the issues themselves that they were fighting over, but how -- how it`s being handled, I would disagree with.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He`s talking about his battles with what he calls woke culture.\par \par \uc2 What does the DeSantis approach tell you about his politics?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Well, I think he`s going right after the Trump base, and he wants to be, I think, the younger version of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, you know, he`s trying to fire up the base, which is OK. And it may be a good strategy to win a primary, but my point was, you -- you have to actually focus on winning swing voters as well, or we`ll have \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  as president, and that`s not what we need.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: How rough could it get, DeSantis vs. Trump?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Could get rough. You know, time will tell. It`s going to be very interesting.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is not the guy who should be the next president. And I think most people would agree. But you can`t beat somebody with nobody. We have to come up with the best candidate.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: You`ve been friendly with former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Someone you could support?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: Yes, absolutely. I have a tremendous amount of respect for \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . And I thought he certainly is the kind of guy -- he`s full of integrity and experience.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And if another potential contender is listening to you today, what do you hope they hear from this decision, from your move?\par \par \uc2 FORMER GOVERNOR LARRY HOGAN: I love competition, and I hate coronations.\par \par \uc2 My advice would be, don`t run just because you want to get your name known, or you want to be on TV, or you want to write a book, or get a TV deal, or because you want to be a Cabinet secretary. You should only run if you believe that you`re capable and qualified and that you have a real chance to be the -- not only the nominee, but the next president.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When we come back, we will hear from Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to go now to Democratic Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2  of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Governor.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR J.B. PRITZKER (D-Illinois): Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have a lot to get to with you, but I want to get something out of the way.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " has a big feature on you, calling you the Democrats` SOS candidate, saying you`re keeping your options open for a presidential run in 2024 in case Biden doesn`t run.\par \par \uc2 Is that true?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR J.B. PRITZKER: No, I`m supporting \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . He`s running for reelection, and he`s going to get reelected.\par \par \uc2 I`m just happy that people think of me in that way. That`s certainly very flattering. But I intend to serve out my term as governor of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because you are, according to \uc2 CBS\uc2  reporting, potentially serving on an advisory board to the president if he does run for reelection.\par \par \uc2 You might have heard Joe Manchin, who just told us that it was kind of an open question as to who would be running. He said: "Let`s see who all the players are."\par \par \uc2 He didn`t endorse the president. Does that surprise you?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR J.B. PRITZKER: Well, I`m sorry to hear that.\par \par \uc2 It does surprise me a bit. I will say that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has an awful lot that he gets to run on here. He`s gotten a tremendous amount done for the country, saved literally hundreds of thousands of lives by making sure that vaccines were distributed. He passed legislation in a bipartisan fashion.\par \par \uc2 We got the IRA, which is going to help us fight climate change, infrastructure, which is helping everybody across the country, the CHIPS and Science ACT, which is going to help us bring manufacturing back to the United States, and what is Joe Biden`s superpower, and that is, he demonstrates empathy in everything that he does. He truly cares about the American people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So do you think he needs to make it official and say he`s running, so that there isn`t more speculation or people considering other options?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR J.B. PRITZKER: I don`t think there`s anybody that`s serious that`s actually considering running against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , because he`s done such a great job.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you.\par \par \uc2 On the issue set that Democrats are running on, your office told -- told us you`re very focused on school board races in Illinois to make sure extreme right-wing candidates aren`t dominating them. I`m wondering how strong the Republican ground operation is on things like school boards.\par \par \uc2 Is parents` rights really something you think Democrats need to be concerned about on a national scale?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR J.B. PRITZKER: Well, what Republicans are trying to do is, of course, ban books in libraries. They`re trying to keep our schools from teaching black history.\par \par \uc2 They make up things about CRT in schools that just don`t exist. And so they have got a lot of extreme right-wing candidates, frankly, on the crazy end of things that are running. And we just want to make sure that people know who they are and know not to vote for them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Governor, there`s a lot I want to get to with you on the other side of this commercial break, so please stay with us.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We want to continue our conversation now with Illinois Governor \uc2 J.B. Pritzker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Governor, thank you for staying with us through the break.\par \par \uc2 I want to follow up on something you said right before we took that break. You said you want to make sure people know who they are and not to vote for them. You were talking about Republicans you said trying to do things like ban teaching black history, ban books, and ban CRT. Are you talking about people in Illinois? Who are you talking about? Where is that happening?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2  (D-IL): Well, you asked - you asked me about schoolboards.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2 : And I`m telling you we`ve got people running at the local level who believe that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Got you.\par \par \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2 : But, of course, the Republicans are carrying this as a national message. And, honestly, it`s something that`s offensive to most Americans. This idea of banning black history. It`s important for people to understand the history of slavery in the United States, you know, and our - - our entire U.S. history, warts and all.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, where I think you`re gesturing to, they are not banning black history. It was specifically that AP college course. That`s what you`re referring to there? That version of it?\par \par \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2 : Well, when they`re trying to dive in and take over an AP history exam and edit it and edit out parts that they don`t like, that`s banning history. That`s what they`re doing in Florida. That`s what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is doing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. A potential 2024 candidate.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about the issue of abortion because I know you are one of 20 Democratic governors very much deeply involved in trying to build a firewall here against restrictions.\par \par \uc2 There`s a Texas judge who may soon decide a case that could revoke the approval of the abortion pill, which is the most common kind of abortion in this country. If there is a ruling to restrict it, how will governors respond? What will you do?\par \par \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2 : Well, in Illinois, we protect the other abortion drugs that are available. And we protect women`s right to express their reproductive freedom. And so we`re helping our clinics in Illinois. We`re making sure that all the refugees from the states around us that have banned abortion know that there`s an oasis here in the Midwest, here in the state of Illinois, to protect their health and their reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  is an Illinois-based pharmacy. I know you called in their CEO, Ros Brewer, for a meeting on Friday. They had announced they won`t sell abortion pills in states in which Republican attorneys general have threatened legal action. Can you get them to change the policy and that -- they`re still waiting, I guess, on certification to sell the pill in Illinois itself. Can you get them approval?\par \par \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2 : Well, that`s something that happens at the federal level, but I offered to them to work with the federal government to try to speed up the process of certification. They want to certify it in Illinois and they want to be able to sell it here. So, we`re going to help in any way that they ask us to.\par \par \uc2 But, look, on a broader scale, we should just recognize that these pharmacies need to protect women`s health. That is the business that they should be in. And so in states where it`s legal to have an abortion and legal to sell an abortion pill, they should still be doing it. And I`ve told them that. We need to make sure that the other pharmacies do the same.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we`ll be following that.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you as well about crime as an issue for Democrats. In the city of Chicago, the mayor, \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , just failed to make the runoff in the Democratic primary. She was challenged by a former school CEO backed by a police union and the Cook County commissioner, who was endorsed by the Chicago Teacher`s Union.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m wondering what the takeaway message is here for Democrats? Is it, don`t take on the teacher unions, as she did, or is it to focus more on violent crime?\par \par \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2 : Well, it was a messy primary. There`s no doubt about it. Nine candidates, nobody got 50 percent, or anywhere near 50 percent. So, we have a runoff coming up.\par \par \uc2 I think that these two candidates have to make sure that their messages are clear, what they`re going to do to protect health care, what they`re going to do to address crime. Frankly, they have not been specific on a lot of these issues. And I`m asking them to be.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor, thank you very much for your time today.\par \par \uc2 J.B. PRITZKER\uc2 : Thanks, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to Ohio Congressman Brad Wenstrup, who chairs the select subcommittee on the coronavirus pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, welcome to the program.\par \par \uc2 REP. BRAD WENSTRUP (R-OH): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are 18 different intelligence agencies in this country, no consensus on Covid`s origins. Two intel agencies undecided, four say it was natural transmission and then last week we learned that the Energy Department has joined the \uc2 FBI\uc2  in saying the virus likely spread through a mishap at a Chinese lab.\par \par \uc2 Is all of the evidence circumstantial? Have you seen the intelligence?\par \par \uc2 BRAD WENSTRUP: I`ve seen quite a bit of intelligence, as you might imagine, sitting on the Intelligence Committee. We haven`t seen all that we want to see necessarily. And some of it is very classified that I have seen. And so we have to continue driving forward and getting questions answered because the more we find, the more questions that we may have.\par \par \uc2 So, you do have a variety of opinions. And, really, what we are trying to do is to follow the breadcrumbs, if you will, look at the forensics of what took place. Obviously, this is one of the more serious things that has ever happened to mankind. And so it is important to find the origins of Covid.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you expect the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Energy Department to testify to your committee?\par \par \uc2 BRAD WENSTRUP: That -- there may come a time for that. I would hope that they do it willingly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, if this was indeed the result of a lab leak, what is Congress doing to prevent this from ever happening again?\par \par \uc2 BRAD WENSTRUP: I`m hoping, at the end of the day with the subcommittee, that we have a bipartisan product that can really help us with our readiness going into the future. I keep using a variety of terms that we want to be able to predict a pandemic, we want to prepare for a pandemic, we want to protect ourselves from a pandemic, and hopefully prevent a pandemic. And that should be our goal. And we`re going to have to work with a lot of scientists and specialists to be able to do that. But we have to get to the truth of what actually happened in this pandemic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, in terms of the specifics, I know the White House is said to be considering recommendations from the National Science Advisory Board For Biosecurity to put oversight on gain of function experiments. Those are the things that I guess genetically alter a virus to enhance its functions and maybe make it more deadly. This was allegedly what was happening at this lab in Wuhan.\par \par \uc2 Would you want this kind of regulation, and does that come from the White House or does that come from Congress?\par \par \uc2 BRAD WENSTRUP: Well, it may be a combination of both at the end of the day. And I think it is important that we do that.\par \par \uc2 Look, if we were taking taxpayer dollars to fund research, not only in the United States but in China, concerning this type of methodology, the creation of a chimera, or it`s called gain of function research, where you can take two viruses and put them into one, I don`t see a whole lot of commercial use for that necessarily. So, it`s something that if it`s going to take place, it certainly should have oversight or should have had oversight.\par \par \uc2 In 2015, Ralph Baric of North Carolina, along with Dr. Zhengli Shi in China, published their article about the ability to create these chimeras. And they did that. So, we know that this technology exists.\par \par \uc2 My real question is, why are we doing this with an adversary like China? And we have to look into what the reasoning was for that, and what actually took place, where the money went, and why did it go there?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I know that there has been a lot of focus on Dr. Fauci, who has since retired from \uc2 NIH\uc2 . And I wonder if you think that is misplaced to personalize the scrutiny so much when the intelligence agencies are all so divided. I mean can you reasonably probe this question, in a bipartisan way, without villainizing people?\par \par \uc2 BRAD WENSTRUP: Well, I think that`s the goal. I mean, I just want to get to facts. And when I was asked to chair this select subcommittee, one of the first things I did was call Dr. Raul Ruiz, Democrat from California, emergency physician. As physicians, we have worked on many bills together and we get along very well. We may disagree on a lot of other policies, but we work very well together, especially when it comes to health.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I think a lot of people would welcome just sticking to the facts, which is why I want to ask you about the membership on your committee, because you have \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  on it. She`s shared misleading information about deaths and Covid vaccines. She compared vaccines to Nazis forcing Jews to wear gold stars. Dr. \uc2 Ronny Jackson\uc2 , who said masks never worked. He called the omicron variant the midterm election variant.\par \par \uc2 How do people take your committee work seriously with members like this on it?\par \par \uc2 BRAD WENSTRUP: Well, I think we have a lot of serious members that -- on both sides of the aisle that are just after the truth. I think that they come from a variety of backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 Look, there were things that were said, hey, this is a conspiracy theory, stop this conspiracy theory that it may have come from the lab. Well, now you have agencies that are coming forward and saying that we do think it came from the lab.\par \par \uc2 Look, we have to conduct ourselves in a way that is professional, and I hope that we will. I can`t control everybody.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRAD WENSTRUP: And that goes on both sides of the aisle. Dr. Ruiz can`t either.\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, what I`m seeing from all the members is that they have backgrounds of severe interests, significant interests. They either owned a business, they`re health care providers, they`re concerned about the adverse events that may come from the vaccine. These are legitimate things for Americans to be concerned about.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 BRAD WENSTRUP: And through this process, I think we took doctors out of the equation all too often and left it up to non-physicians to tell America how to treat themselves.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re from Ohio, so I want to make sure I ask you about what was a second train derailment from \uc2 Norfolk Southern\uc2  in your state yesterday on top of this toxic one. President Biden has praised some of the bipartisan legislation in the Senate that would up railway safety. Do you see a need for this kind of legislation right now?\par \par \uc2 BRAD WENSTRUP: Well, certainly. And any after action review or review of something that happened, if we see that there`s some gaps in our safety, then we should take a look at that. Let`s not put things out there that aren`t necessarily facts and say that there was a safety issue if it wasn`t. But, at the same time, you do want to address these issues.\par \par \uc2 Look, we`re always trying to do better. I hope that we can. And the other thing that I would like to see come out of all this, especially with the one where there was such a chemical, toxic reaction with the fires that were started from the derailment is, do we have a standard operating procedure of how we manage a community, what our reaction is from the government, what are we looking for, how do we protect our people? Let`s make sure that we have a good standard operating procedure so although these instances are rare, according to the numbers, we have to be prepared for that 0.1 percent or whatever the case may be.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Dr. Wenstrup, we`ll be watching the hearing this week. Thanks for your time.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we are back now with former \uc2 FDA\uc2  commissioner, and \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  board member, Dr. \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Welcome back. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2  (Former \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Dr. Gottlieb, I wanted you to sort of give us some context here, because I know you`ve said in the past on this program that we will likely never know the origin of Covid-19, short of finding the exact animal that carried the virus or a smoking gun proving that it accidentally leaked out of the lab. You said both theories were plausible.\par \par \uc2 Has anything changed that makes you more certain now?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Well, look, I think that there`s enough information in the public domain to create a presumption that this could have come out of a lab, maybe a strong presumption. We have seen some incremental reporting. There`s classified information that hasn`t been made public. You heard the congressman even refer to classified information that even Congress hasn`t seen in this instance. And I think based on that premise, that there`s, you know, a likelihood that this came out of a lab, we may never be able to prove it with certainty, we should start behaving like it did come out of a lab and start taking the steps to make sure that that couldn`t happen again. There`s a lot of things that happened around the labs in China, particularly the lab in Wuhan, the Wuhan Institute of Virology, that created sloppy conditions. They were doing high-risk research in low level - low security labs. They were doing risky research. You heard the congressman talk about the gain of function research that was going on in that lab. We know the Chinese military was operating in that lab simultaneously. So, we need to look at all those things.\par \par \uc2 I would be focusing on the activities in and around that lab and deriving from that what steps we need to take going toward to make sure that we get better security around high risk research that if this did come out of a lab, it`s not going to happen again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`ve talked on this program to Matt Pottinger who served in the Trump administration, who said intelligence needs to be a more robust part of pandemic protection. And I know you agree with that.\par \par \uc2 You know, there was a piece in "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " by David Wallace Wells, an opinion piece called "We`ve Been Talking about the Lab Leak Theory All Wrong." And the argument is that, lean into the lab theory and just look at how to prevent lab leaks. He`s calling for things like a national registration on research based on risks and benefits, new safety standards, global governance to go with this as well.\par \par \uc2 Why doesn`t that exist, and why isn`t that being created?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Yes, I think it should be. I mean we`re three years into this. There is some recommendations that are on the president`s desk. I think we need to start getting serious and looking at what steps need to be put in place.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re still stuck on the debate about whether it was or wasn`t a lab leak. I don`t think we`re going to prove that. I think we should work on the assumption that there`s a probability that it was a lab leak and start putting in place the kinds of protections that we need.\par \par \uc2 The congressman talked about gain of function research. He made the point that there isn`t a real commercial prerogative of doing that kind of research. I agree with him. We ought to look at whether we outlaw that kind of research. And certainly, if it`s going to take place, conduct it and be a cell-4 (ph) labs, high security labs under very strict conditions where we know what`s going on and don`t outsource it to labs in China. Sometimes the highest risk experiments get outsourced to the worst labs around the world because they`re the ones willing to do those experiments. And so if we`re going to do high risk research because we think it`s important from a national security standpoint, and that`s the only context in which this would make sense. There really isn`t a commercial context in which this would make sense. We need to get better control over it.\par \par \uc2 And to Matt`s point, Matt Pottinger`s point, we need to get the intelligence agencies engaged in this as a national security - as a part of their national security mission and look at public health preparedness through a national security lens. I think we`re doing that now, but we need to be very explicit about that. And that does mean also surveillance around some of the high risk activities that can create these kinds of risks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So - but that`s the White House and that`s the intelligence agencies, rather than a congressional mandate?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Look, Congress certainly has a role here. I think that Congress could start coming up with a list of recommendations to the administration. The administration has recommendations from independent bodies.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, this is going to be federal rules that get imposed on research that gets funded by the federal government, as well as what we try to do through international conventions, working with the \uc2 WHO\uc2  and the World Health Assembly to try to get international agreements in place and what countries are and aren`t going to be willing to do.\par \par \uc2 Now, you can`t prevent rogue regimes from doing this kind of research. That`s where the intelligence agencies come in to do monitoring to see if rogue regimes are doing high-risk research that create conditions for a lab leak, either inadvertently or deliberately.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the Biden administration has called on China to release more information. And they have not delivered it. Dr. Fauci talked about that in November on this program.\par \par \uc2 I also want to ask you about what we just learned about President Biden`s health. He had this skin cancer diagnosis, a basal cell carcinoma lesion removed. Given his age, is there any reason to worry more about it?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : There`s no reason to be concerned about this particular lesion. This is a slow growing cancer, usually confined to the surface of the skin that can be completely excised with a small surgical procedure. It sounds like the president had this fully removed. It shouldn`t reoccur. It typically doesn`t spread. These kinds of lesions typically occur in regions of the body that are exposed to sun. It is related to exposure to UV light. And so typically you see it on the neck or the face. Just for awareness, it`s not like a melanoma where it appears as a very dark, irregular lesion. Typically it will appear as a sort of a clear and waxy kind of lesion, maybe scaly. So, it has a different kind of appearance. But the president should be fully cured of this through a small surgical procedure to remove it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Dr. Gottlieb, always good talk to you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back with a look at the anniversary of a major milestone in the civil rights movement.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden will travel to Selma, Alabama, later today to commemorate the 58th anniversary of bloody Sunday, a landmark event for the civil rights movement and for the late Congressman \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 . In 2015, our Bob Schieffer visited Selma with \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  and crossed the legendary Edmund Pettus Bridge.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: This week marks 50 years since the shooting of Jimmy Lee Jackson, whose death at the hands of a highway patrolman, at a peaceful protest, inspired one of the iconic events of the civil rights movement, the Selma, Alabama, march.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER (voice over): Movement leaders had chosen Selma as the place to dramatize the demand for the right to vote. After Jackson`s death, a 54- mile march from Selma to Montgomery was planned. Fiery young activist \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  was one of the leaders.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : We are marching to our state capital to dramatize to our nation and to the world our determination to win first-class citizenship.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: But the march was not to be. The protesters ran into trouble soon after they started as they tried to cross the Edmund Pettus Bridge.\par \par \uc2 I had never been to Selma, but with the anniversary of that march looming, I went there yesterday and walked across that bridge with now Congressman \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 , and asked him what was going through his mind on that fateful day.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : We were marching in twos in an orderly, peaceful, non-violent fashion, on our way to Montgomery, to dramatize to the nation that people wanted to register to vote. I really thought we would be arrested and jailed that day.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER (on camera): When did you realize when you got to the high point here, that`s when you saw all of the law enforcement people down there?\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : We saw down below the state troopers and behind the state troopers, and the sheriff`s posy on horseback.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You have to disperse.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : We got to the bottom of the bridge.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: See that they turn around and disperse.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : And they came toward us, beating us with night sticks, using tear gas and trammeling us with horses.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: You were right in the front and -\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : I was in the very front.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: So, you were among the first that was hit?\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : I was the first person to be hit. And I still have the scar on my forehead. I was - I was knocked down. My legs just went out from under me. I thought I was going to die on this bridge. I said to myself, this is the last protest for me.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: What happened? Do you remember anything after that?\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : I remember being back at the church. I don`t even recall how I got back to the church. But apparently someone carried me back. And I guess I become conscience. And someone asked me to say something to the audience, and I stood up and said, I don`t understand it, how President Johnson can send troops to Vietnam and cannot send troops to Selma, Alabama, to protect people whose only desire is to register to vote.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: And then, in a matter of weeks, of course, he did send the troops and you were able to make that march to Selma.\par \par \uc2 LYNDON JOHNSON, FORMER U.S. PRESIDENT (March 15, 1965): There is no cause for pride in what has happened in Selma. Their cause must be our cause too.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : And two weeks later, after President Johnson delivered that speech, we did make the march all the way from Selma to Montgomery. It was a sea of humanity marching on this highway.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: And you did have people protecting you then all the way.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : All the way, people inspecting the bridges along the way, guarding the camps at night. It was - it was our military, it was our military at its best.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: You know, Congressman, a lot of people who were not there, who didn`t know how it was, they don`t know how different it is now.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : Well, it is -- it`s a different - it`s a different world. Back in 1965, only 2.1 percent of black of voting age were registered to vote in this county. You had to go down to the county courthouse. It was the only place you could attempt to register on the first and third Mondays of each month. You had to pass a so-called literary test. There were people to asked to count the number of bubbles in a bar of soap, the number of jelly beans in a jar. People stood in unmoving lines.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: So, things are better, but not as good as they ought to be, I guess is the way I would say it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN LEWIS\uc2 : Things are much better, but we`re not there yet. We still have problems and we`ll make it. We will get there. Selma was a turning point.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230305ej3500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Providers Brace For A New Ruling That Could Ban An Abortion Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Meg Oliver \par \uc2 512 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 February 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the most consequential legal ruling over abortion rights since Roe was overturned could impact 40 million women nationwide. A federal judge in Texas is set to rule in a lawsuit that seeks to ban the drug mifepristone, one of two drugs typically used to induce a medicated abortion.\par \par \uc2 CBS` Meg Oliver reports that more than half of all abortions in the U.S. use this medication.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER (voiceover): Dr. Kristyn Brandi, an OB-GYN in New Jersey, is nervous. The Texas federal court ruling could mean drastic changes for her patients.\par \par \uc2 If this lawsuit bans this drug, how would that affect patient care?\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTYN BRANDI (\uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 ): It would be devastating, because a lot of people rely on this medication. It`s something that has been the standard of care for over 20 years.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: But a lawsuit filed by the Alliance for Hippocratic Medicine, an anti-abortion organization, seeks to reverse the FDA`s approval of the drug and remove it from the market, claiming insufficient safety studies.\par \par \uc2 JULIE MARIE BLAKE (Senior Counsel, \uc2 Alliance Defending Freedom\uc2 ): We are confident that, when any court looks at the law and looks at the science, it will realize that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has completely failed its responsibility to protect women and girls.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: The \uc2 FDA\uc2  says it does not comment on pending litigation. But physicians groups, including the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , say the drug is safe and effective. It can be ordered online with a prescription even in states where surgical abortions are restricted. It`s also used to manage miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 The decision is now in the hands of a Trump-appointed federal judge.\par \par \uc2 MEGHAN BOONE (Associate Professional, Wake Forest University School of Law): If there is a nationwide injunction, then the drug used for medication abortions will be off-market in all 50 states, at least at during the period where the injunction is in force.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Dr. Brandi says patients will still be able to get a medication abortion even if mifepristone is banned by using the other drug in the combination.\par \par \uc2 DR. KRISTYN BRANDI: It`s a little bit less effective. There may be higher risk of side effects or people needing additional care.\par \par \uc2 MEG OLIVER: Meghan Boone says the outcome in this case may undermine the \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval process, setting a damaging precedent.\par \par \uc2 Meg Oliver, CBS News, New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Here in Washington tonight, the crackdown on TikTok continues. The White House today gave government agencies 30 days to remove the video-sharing app from all federal devices and computer systems.\par \par \uc2 The Office of Management and Budget says the move is aimed to -- at protecting U.S. data. Officials suspect that China is using the app to spy on Americans. The ban does not impact the more than 100 million Americans and teens who use TikTok on nongovernment devices.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020230307ej2r0005m\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For February 12, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Imtiaz Tyab, David Martin, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,445 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 February 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: breaking overnight, a U.S. F-22 shoots down a third unidentified aerial object in the skies over Northwestern Canada, and fighter jets are scrambled following a curious radar signal over Montana. What are these incursions all about?\par \par \uc2 Just one day after U.S. fighter jets shot down a high-altitude airborne object over Alaska, on Saturday, an unidentified item was taken down over the Yukon territory of Canada. Debris recovery for both objects are under way, as the \uc2 FBI\uc2  continues its analysis of what`s left of that Chinese spy balloon that was shot down a week ago off the coast of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 We will talk with two key lawmakers, House Foreign Affairs Committee Chairman Michael McCaul and Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Saturday night, the military sent fighter aircraft to investigate what it called a radar anomaly over Tester`s home state of Montana. Frustration is mounting on Capitol Hill about what`s going on here and what more we can do to head off these intrusions.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we heard the president`s take on the State of the Union. But as the nation`s governors convene in Washington, we will check in with four of them on the state of their states and the challenges that they`re facing.\par \par \uc2 And, as the death toll grows in Turkey and Syria, we will have the latest on recovery efforts and share our perspective on some of these global threats.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 As we come on the air, the big question in our minds today is, what is going on here with what seem like a deluge of potential incursions? What are these objects? Where are they coming from? What is their purpose? And are we experiencing an increase of the so-called unidentified aerial objects, or are we just looking for them more carefully following the Chinese spy balloon event?\par \par \uc2 We will do our best to try and get some of those questions answered today.\par \par \uc2 We are going to begin with Montana Senator \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER (D-Montana): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, late Saturday, NORAD and NORTHCOM said there was a radar anomaly over your state, which is why airspace was closed.\par \par \uc2 Was it a false alarm, or is there an object over Montana?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Well, I think the investigation is still going on as we speak.\par \par \uc2 The truth is, is that there was an anomaly, and they`ve investigated. I think it got dark last night, so they couldn`t fully check it out. I`m sure, as we speak, it`s being checked out right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So it hasn`t been ruled out? There may still be something up there?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Absolutely. There may still be something out there. It may be a false alarm.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is the policy now to shoot down any unidentified object?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Well, I think that`s a very, very good question, and that`s a better question for General Milley.\par \par \uc2 But the truth of the matter is, is that they need to have a policy. They being the military needs to have a policy to recommend to the president. It`s something that, as -- as chairman of the Defense Committee and Ranking Member Collins, we`ve already talked about this. We`re going to make sure that there is a plan. We`re going to make sure, if that plan needs to be funded, that it gets funded.\par \par \uc2 This is -- what`s gone on the last two weeks or so, 10 days, has been nothing short of craziness. And the military needs to have a plan to not only determine what`s out there, but determine the dangers that go with it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you don`t know what will happen to this object over Montana?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: My guess is, it`ll get shot down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: But the military will make an assessment as to potential collateral damage, just like they did on the Chinese spy balloon.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you have spent time, as I understand it, with General Milley...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: I have.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... the chairman of the Joint Chiefs, and other DOD officials.\par \par \uc2 Could he share with you anything about this mysterious object that was cylindrical and floating over Canada or the car-sized one over Alaska?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Well, I think you`ve got what he shared with me at that moment in time, and that they had done an assessment of it and determined that it was unmanned and determined that it should be shot down because they weren`t absolutely positive that it was of no threat.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, the object over Alaska was near Prudhoe Bay, which is one of the most important energy fields in this country.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Does that sound to you like it`s espionage, state-driven espionage?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Well, look, I don`t think things happen by mistake when it comes to China.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You think this was China?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: I don`t know. I don`t know that it`s China. We will find out later on if, in fact, it was affiliated with the Chinese Communist government or not.\par \par \uc2 But -- but the bottom line is, is that I think we need to take these things seriously. I think the president and I think, more importantly, the military are taking it very, very seriously. And -- and, to back that up, I think through the appropriations process in the Defense Committee, we`re going to make sure that they`re taking it seriously.\par \par \uc2 So, the checks and balances will be there as we move forward. But this is - - like I said, this has been a phenomenon that we haven`t had recently, where we`ve had other countries that have went into our airspace for the purpose of trying to gather information on what we`re doing here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said and you underscored your key role in helping to determine the budget there for the Pentagon...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... that you don`t remember hearing anything that dealt with balloons.\par \par \uc2 How long has the military actually been tracking this?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Well, I mean, that`s a better question for the military. From my perspective...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But they weren`t sharing it with Congress.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: They weren`t sharing it with me.\par \par \uc2 And so I can`t say what their awareness was over the last 10 years. But -- but, obviously, there was some awareness. But whether it was up to where it needed to be, that`s -- that`s a debate that Congress needs to have and questions that need to be answered by our -- our military leadership.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You were very critical -- you made that very clear and plainspoken -- about the fact that administration didn`t shoot down the confirmed Chinese spy balloon over the state of Montana.\par \par \uc2 And you wanted it shot down as soon as it was in U.S. airspace. Has your view changed at all after you`ve been briefed?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, so, initially, I was very much for shooting it down when it was over the Aleutians. I think what transpired was is, is that the military took assessments as to potential collateral damage and the threat of this balloon. And, you know, we pay these folks good money to make sure we keep our nation safe.\par \par \uc2 And I respect their view, and the president followed that. Going on in the future, I think there needs to be a plan that`s right up front, so we know exactly what`s going to happen when these balloons come in and their threat is assessed, what`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 But -- but, look, we -- I got briefed both in open session and a classified session. And, quite honestly, the military and intelligence community`s explanation of what transpired with that balloon, I accept.\par \par \uc2 Is it something that I would have done right out -- right out of the chute? No. I would have probably done it different, but that`s not saying that I`m right or I`m wrong or they`re right or they`re wrong.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: In the end, we ended up with a balloon that they`ve recovered, and they`re going to take and put it back together and reverse- engineer it, and we`ll find out what they`re up to, plus the information that was gathered while it came across the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, I hear you say there`s value in the -- that intelligence.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, in terms of damage, according to what was declassified the Chinese balloon could intercept signals intelligence. It could pick up chatter.\par \par \uc2 It hovered over some pretty key states, locations in your state, including one that hose -- houses -- excuse me -- 150 intercontinental ballistic missiles.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: No doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Was there damage done?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: No doubt about that, and there better not have been damage done, or it makes my case for shooting the doggone thing down over the Aleutian Islands.\par \par \uc2 Look, we`ve got ICBMs in Montana. We have got 150 of them. Malmstrom Air Force Base is an incredible deterrent for this country and has been since the early `60s. They -- the military made an assessment that they wouldn`t be able to gather the information that -- that the military thought was important to China.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: And, if that didn`t happen that way, somebody screwed up.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You, on the issue of China -- according to the federal government, 3 percent of the nation`s farmland is owned by foreign investors.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have recently introduced a bill to try to restrict foreign ownership of farmland.\par \par \uc2 I know this is an issue in a number of farming states. Why do you think that needs to be a federal ban on foreign ownership?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Well, look, I`m a farmer. I have been farming my grandparents` land at the homestead.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s really important for food security. The folks -- this is a ban against China, Russia, North Korea and Iran, folks who don`t want to see us exist anymore as a nation. I don`t think they should have any opportunity to try to dictate our food supply or...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Any Chinese-owned company, period?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: Right, period, done, because they`re all connected with the Communist Chinese government anyway.\par \par \uc2 And so I think it`s a reasonable step to take. Senator Rounds out of South Dakota does too. So it`s bipartisan. And I think we should do it as a matter of course. And I`m all about private property rights. I think people ought to be able to sell who they want to sell to, but not in this particular case, because China wants to do bad things to us.\par \par \uc2 Same thing with North Korea, Russia and Iran. So, let`s -- let`s take that off the table, both in farmland and in agribusinesses.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: I think -- I think it`d be a mistake, a -- really a mistake for national security and for food security.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Tester, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR JON TESTER: You bet. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good to have you here in person.\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is David Martin, our national security correspondent.\par \par \uc2 David, you have been listening this conversation.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When you speak to the Pentagon, are they any more clear on whether it is now established U.S. policy to shoot down any UFO over North America?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: I don`t think they have a policy.\par \par \uc2 I think, if a balloon like -- or an object like the last two Friday and Saturday is impinging on commercial aviation space -- these both were up at about 40,000 feet, which is just the edge of commercial aviation -- and if you don`t know what they`re doing, then you shoot.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what they did in these two cases.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s an expensive habit.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: It`s an expensive habit.\par \par \uc2 And, also, it may be a bad habit, because you don`t want to shoot first and ask questions later. But at least now that they`re down, we`re going to get some answers. The Canadian prime minister says he has a ground search team at the wreckage that went down over Canada. They are not yet at the wreckage that went down just off Northern Alaska, because they`re dealing with something like minus-45 degree temperatures.\par \par \uc2 But, sooner or later, we will get the -- we will get that wreckage and we will know.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do we call these? They`re called -- being called objects because the military doesn`t really have a term they want to share with the public about them.\par \par \uc2 Are they balloons? What are they?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: I would call them balloon-like objects. They do not appear to have any maneuvering capability. They appear to be floating along at the speed of the wind.\par \par \uc2 I just don`t have a better word for than -- than balloon-like.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the prime suspect is China?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: I wouldn`t say that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: I wouldn`t say that, no.\par \par \uc2 The prevailing wind brings everything that way, from west, east across Northern Alaska and Northern Canada. And there is a lot of what officials call sky trash up there. And sky trash includes balloons that are put up by governments, that are put up by corporations, that are put up by research institutes, and probably just by private individuals, and not for nefarious purposes, but just to collect scientific data.\par \par \uc2 In the past, the U.S. just hasn`t paid much attention to those balloons. But this Chinese balloon was a game-changer.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: And now, certainly, the Biden administration does not feel it can simply let these other objects pass through American airspace.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are reports out of China about preparing to shoot down objects in their airspace.\par \par \uc2 I know, when you were here last week, you warned about the risk of miscalculation.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 I was talking about reconnaissance flights. So, we don`t, as far as I know, penetrate Chinese airspace with our flights, but just do it around the periphery. The Chinese have a history of coming out and buzzing those planes. And, sometimes, it gets pretty darn close. And now, in the wake of this balloon incident, you wonder how much closer it`s going to get and whether the U.S. needs to take precautions.\par \par \uc2 I asked the secretary of defense about that in an interview this week. And he just said you can be sure we are going to take all measures necessary to protect our planes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I imagine that`s one of the things he wanted to speak to the defense minister about, although that phone call was not -- not answered by the his Chinese counterpart.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: No.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: David, thank you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Sure thing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to Congressman Michael McCaul.\par \par \uc2 He is the Chairman of the House Foreign Affairs Committee.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL (R-Texas): Morning, Margaret. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to start on this unusual activity, three takedowns in eight days.\par \par \uc2 In the case of the spy balloon, this was Chinese surveillance, according to the administration. On Friday, they put restrictions on six Chinese companies that allegedly helped China`s military build that balloon.\par \par \uc2 Is this the right move, to just try to make it harder for them to get U.S. technology, or does Congress need to do something that`s more broad?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL: Well, it`s certainly the right move.\par \par \uc2 It will be one of my number one priorities, as the chairman of the Foreign Affairs Committee in this Congress, to stop the export of technology to China that then goes into their most advanced weapons systems, in this case, a sophisticated spy balloon that went across three nuclear sites, I think it`s important to say, in plain view of the American people, you know, in Montana, the triad site, air, land, and sea nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 In Omaha, the spy balloon went over our Strategic Command, which is our most sensitive nuclear site. It was so sensitive that President Bush was taken there after 9/11. And then, finally, Missouri, the B-2 bomber, that`s where they are placed. It did a lot of damage.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that what U.S. intelligence told you? They have been saying they mitigated the impact.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL: They say they mitigated it.\par \par \uc2 But my assessment, and -- and I can`t get into the detail of the intelligence document -- is that, if it was still transmitting going over these three very sensitive nuclear sites, I think -- I think, if you look at the flight pattern of the balloon, it tells a story as to what the Chinese were up to as they controlled this aircraft throughout the United States.\par \par \uc2 Going over those sites, in my judgment, would cause great damage. Remember, a balloon could see a lot more on the ground than a satellite.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you said you want to try to stop the export of technology that can be used by China`s military.\par \par \uc2 As a conservative, though, how much -- this has to make you a little uncomfortable to have government try to control private business investment. How do you do that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL: Well, we have what`s called an entities list. The Department of Commerce had jurisdiction over the office within their -- the Department of Defense has one.\par \par \uc2 We need to harmonize those, make it more security-focused. You know, capital flows is one issue, but technology exports into China that they use to turn -- that maybe eventually turn against us, we have to stop doing that.\par \par \uc2 And I think we can do it by sectors. They do it by companies now. Obviously, they identified the six. I think, shockingly, when the balloon was recovered, it had American-made component parts in there with English on that. It was made -- you know, parts made in America that were put on a spy balloon from China. I don`t think the American people accept that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you believe that this was a strategic choice by Xi Jinping`s government in Beijing, or do you believe that it was just the left and right hand not knowing what was going on?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL: When I saw the sites that it was flying over, it was very clear to me this was an intentional act. It was done with provocation to gather intelligence data and collect intelligence on our three major nuclear sites in this country.\par \par \uc2 Why? Because they`re looking at what -- what is our capability in the event of a possible future conflict in Taiwan? They`re really assessing what we have in this country. I find it extraordinary the timing of this flight as well, right before the State of the Union speech, and also right before Secretary Blinken was scheduled to meet with Chairman Xi.\par \par \uc2 I think it was very much an act of belligerence on their part. And perhaps they don`t care what -- what the American people think about that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Before I let you go, I want to ask you.\par \par \uc2 You voted in the last Congress to provide a lot of assistance to Ukraine. But, this past week, at least 10 of your members, Republican members, introduced a bill called the Ukraine Fatigue Resolution to try to cut off aid.\par \par \uc2 How hard is it going to be to have a Republican-led House continue to help Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL: I still believe, Margaret, there are many, on both sides of the aisle, a majority of the majorities in support of this.\par \par \uc2 We have -- we have factions on the left and right that do not support Ukraine...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This WAS a Republican bill.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL: ... assistance, and that will probably continue.\par \par \uc2 Right. And I do think, for me, particularly, it`s -- we have to educate, where has the money gone? You know, the audits that are in place right now, there are four of them on Ukraine funding. And we have to explain, why is Ukraine so important?\par \par \uc2 You know, what happens in Ukraine impacts Taiwan and Chairman Xi, that China`s aligned with Russia, Iran and North Korea against freedom, democracy in the West. And I think that`s a debate we`ll have, but I still feel very confident that we will give them the assistance they need.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL: I would like to see it faster, so they can win this faster.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you -- you think Matt Gaetz, Marjorie Taylor Greene, others who signed this need to be educated?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL: I -- you know, look, we took Marjorie Taylor Greene into a briefing.\par \par \uc2 She was satisfied, I thought, with what -- the controls that have been put in place on the spending. But I don`t think that they will be -- ever be persuaded that this cause is something that they would support.\par \par \uc2 I think they have this false dichotomy that somehow we can`t help Ukraine beat back the Russians, who invaded their country and -- and secure the border. We can do both. We`re a great nation. And the fact of the matter is, unfortunately, this administration has chosen not to secure the border. He can`t even control and secure our airspace now, it looks like.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman McCaul, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MICHAEL MCCAUL: Thanks, Margaret. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Face the Nation will be back in one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The death toll from that massive earthquake in Turkey and Syria continues to climb.\par \par \uc2 There are now more than 28,000 dead. And the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . says they expect that number to double or more.\par \par \uc2 Our Imtiaz Tyab reports from Turkey.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB (voice-over): There are no words for this kind of grief.\par \par \uc2 (SCREAMING)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: But for the tens of thousands whose loved ones were also killed in the quake, it`s a gut-wrenching agony they all share, an agony that has touched every street and every corner of the Southeastern city of Hatay, one of the hardest-hit by Monday`s 7.8-magnitude quake and the powerful aftershocks that followed.\par \par \uc2 These rescue workers remain determined to keep looking for survivors, even as the hope of finding someone alive slips away with every passing minute.\par \par \uc2 It`s been seven days now, and, despite the exhaustion, the cold and the sadness, these men say they`re determined to keep searching. So too are these rescuers, as they carefully sift through the ruins of an apartment building, but then their worst fear, as dislodged chunks of concrete and twisted metal start raining down on top of them. One emergency worker was moderately injured, the rest unharmed.\par \par \uc2 For survivors who have already buried their loved one, the sadness is all- consuming, as more fresh graves are dug up in anticipation of mass burials to come. The scale of the devastation continues to defy comprehension. International aid is now pouring in from around 45 countries, including the U.S.\par \par \uc2 But, for badly devastated Syria, just two convoys have made it into the northwestern Idlib province, the last remaining rebel-held territory in a nation already torn apart by more than a decade of civil war. This week, the \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  announced it would ease sanctions on the Syrian government for 180 days as part of efforts to speed up humanitarian assistance.\par \par \uc2 But President \uc2 Bashar al-Assad\uc2  insists on handling all the aid shipments himself, including to rebel-held territories, a major concern for most international donors, who remain slow in committing support. So, for now, most of what`s crossing the border into Syria from Turkey are the remains of Syrian refugees killed in the earthquake, a country they once had to flee in order to save their lives, now only to return in death.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Imtiaz Tyab reporting from Turkey.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Be sure to join us next Sunday. We will be talking with Bernie Sanders, two-time Democratic presidential contender and Vermont independent senator.\par \par \uc2 That`s next Sunday on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When we come back, we will be talking with four of our nation`s governors.\par \par \uc2 And we will start off with New Hampshire Republican Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , who is also eying a possible presidential run in 2024. We will ask him about that and a lot more.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by the Republican governor of New Hampshire, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it`s good to have you here...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (R-New Hampshire): Thanks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... in person.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Great to be here. Better here than the rest of Washington, because this whole town gives me the -- it gives me the chills sometimes.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you might need to go get over that if you`re going to run for 1600 Pennsylvania Avenue, as, apparently, you are considering doing.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Well, look, a lot of opportunity to change things, right?\par \par \uc2 I think New Hampshire has this awesome model of live free or die, limited government, local control, individual responsibility, really putting the voters first, send them some money, which is nice, but send them the regulatory authority too.\par \par \uc2 So a little decentralizing out of Washington and maybe a little better attitude would be -- would be a good thing for America.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What`s the proactive reason you want to be president, not something that President Biden is doing wrong...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... but something you want to achieve?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Yes, which is the right question you`re asking, by the way, because I -- it drives me crazy when Republicans talk in an echo chamber about how bad the president is, and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 We got the memo, as Republicans. You got to be for something. What I`m trying to do is kind of show that New Hampshire model, show the opportunity to get stuff done. I have had Republicans in my legislature. I have Democrats in my legislature. I always get my conservative agendas done.\par \par \uc2 We always cut taxes. We always balance a budget. And I can explain to folks in Washington what a balanced budget actually means. So, there are paths. And I think America is looking for results. We need results-driven leadership, not just leadership that...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Like what?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Look, whether it`s cutting taxes, being pro- business, the regulatory reform, the immigration stuff that we were told was going to happen in 2017 and 2018 as Republicans, and it didn`t.\par \par \uc2 We were told health care reform would happen. It didn`t. We were told we were going to secure the border, and we didn`t. So, there`s all this great opportunity that has a domino effect. They`re not just things to check off a list, but those things have huge impacts on the American economy and, most importantly, American families, right?\par \par \uc2 They just want flexibility to do what they do. And, frankly, they`re tired of the nonsense in D.C. They`re tired of -- of extreme candidates. They`re tired of gridlock. They want somebody to come to the table. And it could be myself. It could be other governors. It could -- but it has to be leadership with proven results.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: I have been in the private sector as an engineer and a business leader. I have been in the public sector. You got to be able to deliver.\par \par \uc2 And you got to, hopefully, be inspirational and hopeful, as opposed to all this negativity you see.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you still have to get the Congress to work with you to do that very long laundry list of things you just read off to us.\par \par \uc2 So, when you were here in November, you told us that President Biden would not run for president, in your estimation. You just saw him up close for the past few days.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that still what you believe?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Well, I know other people will definitely run. They`re going to get in the race.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Democrats, you believe, will challenge him?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Oh, absolutely, yes, yes, because...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why do you say that? Did someone tell you that in the last few days?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Well, Joe Biden has tried to move the first-in-the- nation primary from New Hampshire, right? But we`re going to -- we`re going first, whether the president likes it or not.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s going to be a huge opportunity for anybody who wants to step up and challenge him. And if you look at the polls across the country, the average Democrat says, yes, thanks for your service on one term, but let`s keep it to one term, President Biden.\par \par \uc2 And I just don`t believe the Democrat left-wing elite is going to sit on the sidelines, knowing you could come to New Hampshire, get all the earned media, all the attention...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: ... without a whole lot of money, all that political momentum. He`s opened up his political flank, so to say, to give someone else a huge opportunity to charge right through and take that nomination from him.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we`ll see if your -- if your projection plays out.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been talking about trying to sort of remind the party that Republicans are about limited government.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said recently: "Republicans are almost trying to outdo Democrats at their own game of being big government and having a solution and a say on everything."\par \par \uc2 Who were you thinking of when you say that?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Oh, there`s a lot -- look, I think there`s a lot of leadership out there that forget -- that forgets.\par \par \uc2 At heart, I`m a principled free market conservative. Let the markets decide. So there`s no individual, per se, but there`s a lot of leadership that says, you know what, when we`re not getting that result out of a private business or locality, we`ll just impose from the top down our conservative will.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re not talking...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... about the Florida governor and \uc2 Disney\uc2 , for example, are you?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Well, that`s a bad example. Yes, that`s -- that`s an example, one of the many examples you see out there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  may be running for president as well.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Sure. Yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes, look, Ron`s a very good governor. He is. But I`m just trying to remind folks what we are at our core. And if we`re trying to beat the Democrats at being big government authoritarians, remember what`s going to happen. Eventually, they`ll have power in a state or in a position, and then they`ll start penalizing conservative businesses and conservative nonprofits and conservative ideas.\par \par \uc2 That is the worst precedent in the world. That`s exactly what the founding fathers tried not to -- tried to avoid. And so I`m trying to remind my conservative friends about federalism, free markets, and being for the voter first, being for the individual.\par \par \uc2 Do I like what every private business says? No, I hate this woke cancel culture. But it`s a cultural...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What does that mean to you then?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Woke cancel culture?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Oh, it`s -- it`s -- look, it`s...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because you`re not a culture warrior, really.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: No, no, no. No, but it`s there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What does woke cult -- what does that mean in your platform?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: It`s the -- it`s the divisiveness -- divisiveness we see not just in our schools, but in our communities, where it is me vs. you, whereas, if you are not adhering to my ideals, then I`m going to cancel you out.\par \par \uc2 It is us vs. them. It is this binary where everything`s a war. That`s a cultural problem we have to fix in America. And it starts with good leadership, good messaging, more hopeful and optimistic. But government never solves a cultural problem.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. Well...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: We can lead on it, but we never solve it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Interesting idea, but you are contradicted by the Republican governor of Arkansas, who gave the response for your party after the State of the Union, who embraced culture war.\par \par \uc2 She says America`s in one.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Yes, we are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: She says it`s been waged by the left wing, "a woke mob that can`t even tell you what a woman is."\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: That`s absolutely right. And that`s...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean, are you going to engage on things like this, like -- like Sanders and DeSantis has in terms of issues on gender and issues of race?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: There should be absolute leadership on that about what that`s about.\par \par \uc2 And this idea that you have to -- you know, we have forced language, that we have forced ideas on our kids, that we`re going to force anything...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you are going to be a culture warrior?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: No, we have to talk about that, but it isn`t the government`s role to solve it.\par \par \uc2 The government is not here to solve your problems. It`s not. The government is here to include as many...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, governors shouldn`t be actually talking and engaging and telling school boards and doing things like this or trying to pass laws like they are?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: I don`t think governors should be trying to pass laws to subvert the will of the voters that know better than us.\par \par \uc2 Voters are -- know more than I do. The voters on that school board know, the voters in those towns know a lot more. And if -- that`s the free market of politics. If they don`t like the school board, they get -- they go to a town meeting, they fire them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are -- you call yourself a pro-choice Republican.\par \par \uc2 You still have to win in a Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is there room for someone who calls himself a pro-choice Republican?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Oh, yes, look, that issue is -- look, that issue is going to change three different ways now that Dobbs has happened, right? States can decide what they want to do, right?\par \par \uc2 So, I think the definition of pro-life and pro-choice and pro-abortion are -- are going to be very different, because if you`re a pro-life Republican, that`s fine. That`s -- as a governor, you can do that. You can ban it in your state, and you can stay -- stand behind those ideals. And maybe that`s exactly what your state wants. No problem.\par \par \uc2 I`m a pro-choice Republican in a very pro-choice state. But, at the end of the day, you`re going to have the pro-lifer over here...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: .. pro-abortion over here, and then the rest of us are, well, we have a 24-week ban, and you have a 22-week...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: ... and an 18-week ban.\par \par \uc2 So, the rest of us are kind of in this spectrum of debating about weeks. So that the whole conversation is going to change.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to talk about some of these issues in-depth with you in a moment, so stay with us, Governor.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to bring in a panel of bipartisan governors with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re back with Governor Sununu.\par \par \uc2 And we`re joined by Democrat Wes Moore, who was just sworn in as Maryland`s first black Governor. \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2  is the Democratic governor of New Mexico. Republican \uc2 Doug Burgum\uc2  is the governor of North Dakota.\par \par \uc2 And it`s good to have you all here at the table together.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2  (D-New Mexico): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR DOUG BURGUM (R-North Dakota): Great to be with you too.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE (D-Maryland): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to talk about some of these issues of common cause.\par \par \uc2 Governor Burgum, I know the fentanyl crisis, the drug crisis was a topic at the White House in recent days. Your state is one of six with the lowest rates of drug overdose deaths, according to the CDC. How is that possible, when this is a national crisis?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR DOUG BURGUM: Well, I think all of our governors, we`re all border states now with the fentanyl that`s coming into this country, perhaps manufactured in China, coming across the Southern border.\par \par \uc2 But, in North Dakota, we`ve really taken an approach of understanding that, if you`re going to have a war on drugs, which is this thing we`ve been doing in our country since the 1970s and `80s, it can become a war on people who have a health issue. They`ve got -- addiction is a disease.\par \par \uc2 And so we want to be very tough on the people that are importing and distributing, but we also have to understand that, if people have the disease of addiction, it`s not a moral choice or a failure. And so we`ve taken an approach on a number of fronts.\par \par \uc2 One of the things that`s been most successful is treat -- treating the disease of addiction is with peer support specialists, because we know now that someone who`s got lived experience, whether that`s in the criminal justice system, or living with the disease of addiction in recovery, that they can help people through it as much as an addiction counselor.\par \par \uc2 So, the whole approach is, we`ve turned it towards one of -- of treating this as a national health crisis, which it is. So we want to be tough on suppliers. But we want to be super supportive of those that are dealing with it.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : I really appreciate that approach. That is probably going to be the nexus of real bipartisan work.\par \par \uc2 In New Mexico, we have a significant issue with substance abuse and overdose deaths. And I wish I could tell you that fentanyl is not a problem. It is. And, in fact, we were part of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  sting with one of the largest fentanyl busts in United States history, a million pills, two million in cash.\par \par \uc2 But we didn`t have any behavioral health system when I became governor. The former administration literally canceled behavioral health. And there were no providers. They were all in litigation. Everybody left to other states.\par \par \uc2 So now you have a crisis on top of a national, building crisis that COVID did none of us any favors to really address. Evidence-based work about making sure that treatment is available is exactly how we`re going to get ahead of this. So all of the up-front, so dealing with poverty and food security and jobs and workers and good education, while we`re treating folks who are currently dealing with this disease.\par \par \uc2 And I think we can start to do that regionally with creative solutions that allow Medicaid to pay for services across states when it`s behavioral health, and we`ve eliminated co-pays for behavioral health services.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And this is something you`re asking the federal government for help to do?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : You know, I didn`t actually do that during the -- we have very -- it`s a limited -- who can ask questions. And that sounds awful.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure that we`re all benefiting by a topic matter that we can all take back with us and get a sense of where the federal government is headed. But the Western Governors Association, which is another really effective example about bipartisan work, we`re interested in taking on behavioral health in a more regional effort.\par \par \uc2 And following some of the best practices of North Dakota absolutely is going to make its way into achievements and better outcomes in New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governor Moore, I mean, you`re new on the job.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what is it that you plan to execute to deal with this problem?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: Well, I think what was said here was -- is a really important point, where we cannot go through the process -- excuse me -- the idea that we`re going to criminalize our way out of this.\par \par \uc2 And I think we`ve learned that throughout this process, that we`re dealing with behavioral health and mental health. When you look at the proposed budget that we laid out, our proposed budget makes historic increases, increases of 39 percent, that`s actually focusing exclusively on substance abuse disorders, on making sure that we`re actually helping people when they`re returning from incarceration, things like how are we dealing with elements of record expungement, job retraining, job rescaling, making sure there`s a better reintegration with the family.\par \par \uc2 But there has to be a larger holistic way in the way that we are dealing with this challenge, because it is true we have spent two decades now dealing with a behavioral health challenge essentially by criminalizing it.\par \par \uc2 And there are long-term consequences, economic consequences, societal consequences that I know, in the state of Maryland, that we are aggressively pushing on within the way our administration is looking at this work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We were looking at some of the research. And, in Maryland, in one county, they`ve had to use Narcan on students 11 times during the past year.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean, you`re actually putting this in the schools because this is so common?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: Because we -- and because we have to.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the thing. As an administration, I always say, as a leader, I am data-driven and heart-led, right? I wear my heart on my sleeve, but I don`t move without data. And the data has been so clear about the damage that this has done to our communities, both urban and rural. I mean...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And children, apparently.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: And children, and to the point where we`ve actually now appointed a special secretary who is a former mayor of Hagerstown who actually got into politics because of the issue of opioid addiction, had her best friend lost to -- to an overdose, who is now serving as our special Secretary on this exact issue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You were nodding at the Narcan in schools.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Oh, yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 Look, one of the biggest issues is -- you have heard...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This is the drug when someone overdoses, to basically help them survive it.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: And Narcan works, for the most part. We can talk about where it doesn`t work.\par \par \uc2 But schools -- you need access points to schools. Kids need to know that -- that there is help there, what those systems are. Rural access to care is absolutely huge. People have to understand it`s not a 28-day problem, right? That`s old-school thinking. Sometimes, recovery is a lifelong journey.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: So, you need -- you need recovery-friendly workplaces. You need wraparound housing and those types of services.\par \par \uc2 Understand that also the fentanyl crisis that`s now being mixed with everything, it`s in vape cartridges. It`s in marijuana. It`s being mixed with xylazine. And let me tell you, if you don`t understand the xylazine- fentanyl crisis that`s coming, it`s horrid. It negates the ability of Narcan to revive you.\par \par \uc2 And so the mixing of everything -- I call it a cartel-driven crisis now. It`s no longer overprescription. That`s always part of it. But the cartels have such access. And they basically are creating their own markets. They`re putting it in Adderall. They`re putting it -- they`re mixing it with black market Adderall. They`re mixing it with Xanax.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How are people buying it?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Black market.\par \par \uc2 As inflation goes up, more people are going to buy their pharmaceuticals offline. And so they`re going to get Adderall for the kids offline. It becomes mixed. Or the kids try to buy vape cartridges offline. And it comes mixed with fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 And the dealers know, look, we might lose a couple, but we`re going to create addicts out of it. And so the crisis is the mixing, where we have so many folks that overdose that had no idea they were even doing fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : We need to be careful, in my view, that we don`t do this paradigm just shifting from one extreme to the other.\par \par \uc2 Absolutely, organized crime and cartels, which are embedded in every state, have to be held accountable. And we all collectively -- that`s a federal and state-by-state decision`\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you have asked the federal government for more \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : I have.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And they denied you that request, why?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : Well, they -- so far.\par \par \uc2 Let`s -- I`m -- I`m tenacious. So, I`m going to -- I`m -- bet on me.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : I`m going to get those agents.\par \par \uc2 But, to your point, if we were all using our National Guard, to some degree -- we need -- we need them for our own state emergencies. But if we were doing drug interdiction work, then we`re dealing with the bad guys and gals in this system. And we need to do that collectively.\par \par \uc2 Instead, we`re doing it. I do a little. You do a little. You do -- we should be doing it collectively. That`s the right kind of leveraging.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR DOUG BURGUM: One thing that we`ve stopped even using the word overdose, because no one is making a choice to kill themselves, as Chris was saying.\par \par \uc2 I mean, these people don`t know what they`re taking. We have an epidemic of just huge proportions, like we`ve never had before. And you talked about \uc2 FBI\uc2 . One of the things that`s happening across the Western U.S. on all of the tribal lands, that -- like the sovereign nations that we share geography with in North Dakota, is, they don`t have enough BIA agents. The federal government...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The federal agents.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR DOUG BURGUM: Forget \uc2 FBI\uc2 . So, the \uc2 Bureau of Indian Affairs\uc2  that would be doing that drug interdiction, their staffing is way down.\par \par \uc2 Organized crime is preying on those tribal communities. And that`s where they`re basing their operations out of.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you`re seeing that in your tribes as well?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : We`re seeing that too, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to -- this is a huge topic, but I want to make sure I get to you on the issue of abortion, because, of course, the Supreme Court threw this back to state capitals in June when they overturned Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Governor Burgum, the very last abortion provider in your state left after that Supreme Court decision. What has been the impact of that...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR DOUG BURGUM: Well...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... of not having that care?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR DOUG BURGUM: Well, in the -- in the case of this case, because the last one was in Fargo, it moved about 400 yards. It`s now in Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 So the effect of people having access to care is really...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They leave.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR DOUG BURGUM: Well, they leave, but they`re going 400 yards further east.\par \par \uc2 And I think this is -- this is what will happen across our nation. It`s now back in the hands of states. North Dakota has proven at the ballot box, the citizens voting. Our legislature very much is a pro-life with exception states. And so this is a -- something that -- that the state of North Dakota has been pursuing for a long time.\par \par \uc2 But, as Chris was talking about in the earlier segment, this is something that I think states can decide. And I think it ought to be decided at the state level.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s a long way for a lot of people to travel, though, if they`re not in Fargo, no?\par \par \uc2 I mean, what has been the impact in your states?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : We`re seeing -- we`re seeing women all across America come to New Mexico. And it is a long way to travel.\par \par \uc2 And what about after-care? And what about your family? And what about the other potential family members, like other small children? It is catastrophic. This is where I think constitutional rights are a federal matter, making sure that wherever I live in this country, one country, that my constitutional rights, my bodily autonomy ought to be protected.\par \par \uc2 And with all due respect for people who have moral -- who morally oppose my position on abortion, they don`t have to seek that care. But women who need that care get limited or reduced or eliminated access, and women die all across this country. And we`re going backward.\par \par \uc2 And I could not disagree more with my good friend Doug about this being a state-to-state matter.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I want to ask you on a very specific issue how you are planning to spin this forward, because abortion is legal in New Hampshire, Maryland, New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 The majority of abortions provided in this country happen via pill these days.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And there`s a Texas Court decision that is pending that might strike down \uc2 FDA\uc2  approval, as I understand it.\par \par \uc2 Are you preparing? Are you stockpiling the drug? Are you planning at all?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: We are. We are preparing, as long as I`m the governor of the state of Maryland, that Maryland will be a safe haven for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 I believe abortion is health care. This is really about how are we, coming up with multiple ways, are preparing for this, and ensuring that all women know that we believe that their health, their safety and their security and of the providers is something that should be guaranteed in the state of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you stockpiling a drug or no?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: We`re not yet stockpiling the drug.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR WES MOORE: But we are in preparation for that.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : But I think that stockpiling might be, if I can push back a little...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, tell me.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR \uc2 MICHELLE LUJAN GRISHAM\uc2 : ... the wrong -- the wrong question. Do we have enough of those drugs available in places -- and each state is going to be a little different.\par \par \uc2 But you can get those in our public health system. We`re moving towards tribal nations providing access in any number of ways, including abortion, direct abortion care inside clinic, in addition.\par \par \uc2 And the chilling effect of not having these medications now routinely manufactured is, they`re used to treat men and women for other -- like bleeding ulcers. We`re going to have deaths unrelated because doctors are saying, in my state, I can`t prescribe this.\par \par \uc2 So this decision could have an even more chilling effect. So we`re trying to figure out ways, can we get a manufacturer? How much do we have? How long does it last? What are we doing about contraceptives and contraceptive care and sex education and school-based health centers? So all-of-the-above approach, we`re in.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Governors, thank you very much for your time today. There`s so much more to talk to you about. I enjoyed this.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We find ourselves in yet another moment where global crises seem to be converging, and yet another reminder for us about how to put it all into perspective.\par \par \uc2 Four times in the past eight days, U.S. fighter jets scrambled to take out perceived threats tens of thousands of feet above North America.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PILOT: That is a T-kill. The balloon is completely destroyed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The spy balloon and aerial objects are a dramatic wakeup call that the U.S. isn`t quite as insulated or as isolated as many like to think.\par \par \uc2 Record-level migration was already an unmistakable sign that failing governments, dramatic climate shifts and economic strain thousands of miles away eventually end up on America`s doorstep.\par \par \uc2 As President Biden pointed out Friday, the two biggest democracies in the Americas both recently withstood insurrections staged by far right mobs.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): In both the United States and Brazil, democracy prevailed.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Democracy also feels under strain in Israel, where tens of thousands took to the streets Saturday to protest against their new right-wing government.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Ukraine`s democracy continues its yearlong battle to survive Vladimir Putin`s brutal onslaught. \uc2 NATO\uc2  warned last week that China is expanding its nuclear arsenal. And so is North Korea. Kim Jong-un paraded a dozen intercontinental ballistic missiles down Pyongyang`s streets Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 And if the food and migration crisis in drought-stricken East Africa wasn`t already a sign of Mother Nature`s power, she sent us another signal Monday. This one has taken the lives of 28,000 and counting, lives in Turkey and war-torn Syria.\par \par \uc2 For all of us, the ground does feel like it is shifting. Global instability may be a constant for the near future. And that may be a needed reminder to put our petty political disagreements in perspective.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This programming note: Caitlin Huey-Burns and our investigative team unravels the mystery of who is George Santos in the new \uc2 CBS\uc2  Reports documentary that`s available through our CBS News app.\par \par \uc2 That`s it for us here at Face the Nation today. Thank you all for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for Face the Nation, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230212ej2c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For January 22, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Robert Costa, Ed O`Keefe, Jeff Pegues, Kris Van Cleave, Margaret  Brennan \par \uc2 9,162 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation. I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 As we come on the air this morning, we are covering two major breaking news stories. Ten people are dead following a mass shooting in Monterey Park, a city just east of Los Angeles. At least 10 more are wounded. And, at this point, the gunman is not in custody. The shooting happened just after a lunar new year celebration.\par \par \uc2 Here in Washington, we have learned of even more classified material uncovered at President Biden`s home in Wilmington, Delaware, following a nearly 12-hour search on Friday facilitated by the Department of Justice, along with White House counsel and the president`s personal attorney. We will have more on that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But we want to begin with the very latest on that shooting in Monterey Park, California.\par \par \uc2 And our Kris Van Cleave is on the scene -- Kris.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Margaret, good morning.\par \par \uc2 A lot of unanswered questions face investigators this morning. The work at the scene here continues, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and local police still here. The suspect has not been identified and at last report was still on the loose. The motive, the why for all of this, that remains a mystery.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 EMERGENCY SERVICES DISPATCH: I got three immediates in here, and I have got approximately 10 deceased.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE (voice-over): This morning,a community celebration of the lunar new year turns to tragedy. Investigators say the deadly shooting came as the event was wrapping up in Monterey Park outside Los Angeles. Authorities believe a lone gunman opened fired on a crowd, killing 10 and injuring at least 10 others, some critically.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. ANDREW MEYER (Los Angeles Sheriff`s Department): When officers arrived on scene, they observed numerous individuals, patrons of the location pouring out of location screaming. The officers made entry to the location and located additional victims.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The mass shooting took place inside a ballroom dance club a little before 10:30 Saturday night.\par \par \uc2 CAPT. ANDREW MEYER: All I can tell you is that it was a firearm that was used.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Monterey Park is a community of nearly 62,000. Almost two-thirds of its population is Asian. The lunar new year celebration is a big attraction here. Witnesses said they initially thought the shots were fireworks.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I did -- heard some sounds going off, and I was like, fireworks? It`s very surprising, shocking to hear that something like this went on in not only a small city, but my city.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: According to some witnesses, the shooter appeared to be firing indiscriminately.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: The lunar new year festivities here in Monterey Park attract about 100,000 people a day. It`s a two-day event.\par \par \uc2 All of the plans for today have been canceled. We understand police are at a second location a few miles from here in a neighboring city. The sheriff`s department says it may be related to the shooting here. We`re still waiting for details on that, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Kris Van Cleave, thank you. And we will come back to you later in the show if there is any new development.\par \par \uc2 But, right now, we go to our chief national affairs and justice correspondent, Jeff Pegues.\par \par \uc2 Jeff, we know both the president and vice president have been briefed on this. What are you hearing from your sources about this investigation?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Well, it`s ongoing in terms of tracking down who this person is.\par \par \uc2 The question is, what kind of weapon was involved here? Is it a hate crime? Some of the initial questions that law enforcement, whether it`s local and federal are asking right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, why are federal investigators involved in it at this point, and how do they determine things like motive?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Well, here`s where I think law enforcement has an advantage.\par \par \uc2 In this phase of an investigation, when you have a suspect still out there, having the help of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , the ATF can -- the ATF, for one, can trace the weapon, the origin of the weapon, also help with the crime scene, as can the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . But, of course, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  can also talk to witnesses who are anywhere across the country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: At this point, all we`re hearing is that it was a firearm.\par \par \uc2 Why is that significant, in terms of the type of weapon?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Well, it looks like, based on the amount of wounded and dead, that this was a high-powered sort of weapon. Whether it was semiautomatic or automatic, it could unleash several rounds a minute.\par \par \uc2 And so investigators are going to look at that as they try to process the scene as it unfolded moment to moment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How -- I mean, when you hear that the shooter is still potentially on the loose here, what happens in a scenario like that?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: We don`t see -- in terms of active shooter situations like this and mass shooting situations like this, you don`t see a lot of suspects who do the act and then take off.\par \par \uc2 In many cases, they will take their own life. And so this is an interesting case in that way, that investigators are still searching for the suspect. They have not at this time released a description of the suspect or what kind of vehicle this person could or may be riding in.\par \par \uc2 And so there are still a lot of questions about the investigation going forward in terms of tracking that suspect down, which is so rare in situations like this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Jeff Pegues, I will let you get back to making calls. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the other big breaking news story we`re covering today. Last night, we learned that, on Friday, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  executed what the White House is calling a comprehensive search of President Biden`s Wilmington, Delaware, home and took possession of six more items with classification markings.\par \par \uc2 The search, which Mr. Biden`s attorneys say was conducted with their full cooperation, began at approximately 9:45 a.m. and wrapped up around 10:30 p.m.\par \par \uc2 President Biden`s personal attorney, Bob Bauer, said in the statement that the Department of Justice had -- quote -- "full access" to all the materials in the Wilmington home, and that the search included personally handwritten notes, files, papers, binders, memorabilia, to-do lists, schedules, and reminders going back decades.\par \par \uc2 Bauer also said that some of the items taken were both from Mr. Biden`s tenure as vice president and from his years in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 CBS News does not know the level of classification for the six new items that were retrieved Friday. But, at this point, we do know that the number of known classified documents that have been recovered since November two is between 25 and 30.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice is considering searches of other locations tied to the president. The one Friday was overseen by U.S. attorney \uc2 John Lausch\uc2 . Special counsel Robert Hur does not take over the case until the end of the month.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden dodged questions about the document investigation most of last week, but, on Thursday, reiterated that he was fully cooperating with the investigation and hoped it would soon be concluded.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have no regrets. I`m following what the lawyers have told me they want me to do. That`s exactly what we`re doing. There`s no "there" there.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to Ohio Congressman \uc2 Mike Turner\uc2 . He is expected to head up the House Intelligence Committee.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER (R-Ohio): Good morning Margaret. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, we have this development in regard to the further materials that were found at President Biden`s Delaware home.\par \par \uc2 What is your reaction? And what does it signify to you that no one realized that this classified material was missing, some of it dating back to his Senate years?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Yes, this is really incredible.\par \par \uc2 And, as you know, congratulations to you. We would not know anything about this if it hadn`t been that \uc2 CBS\uc2  had broken this story. The White House nor the Department of Justice had shared any of the information with the public. And this really is one matter. We wouldn`t have this issue if it hadn`t been for Biden`s attorney general making the decision to raid former President Trump`s house looking for -- for classified documents that were being held there.\par \par \uc2 What`s amazing about all this is, it takes us to the question of, why were these documents here? Well, now that we learn that some of these go back to his Senate time. Clearly, he`s -- he`s become a serial classified document hoarder. Why did he have these? Who did he show them to?\par \par \uc2 I mean, the only reason you can think of as to why anyone would take classified documents out of a classified space at home is to -- is to show them to somebody. Who did he show them to? This is going to be crucial, I think, to the special counsel`s investigation, is, why did the president have these documents? Who did he show them to? And is it connected to the Biden family businesses?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you know the differences of course to -- I want to talk about the Biden situation.\par \par \uc2 But, just to clarify, when you reference President Trump, there were 300 classified documents. There was a warrant. There was refusal to comply in terms of handing things over. And the White House and the president`s lawyer are pointing out that, in the case of Biden, he granted permission, and this was consensual for the DOJ to come in and search.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Well...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Does the fact that the Justice Department conducted the search signify anything more to you? And do you have any insight into the sensitivity of the documents?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Sure. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 I think this looks more like a cover-up than an investigation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you have any facts to back up your -- your allegations that he was hoarding things in terms of intention to take classified material vs. -- it`s been characterized that it was somehow accidental?\par \par \uc2 Do you have any insight into what these materials were?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Well, they didn`t fly to his home without him. They went on a train with him from the -- his Senate offices and then in boxes that he was in charge of.\par \par \uc2 The chain of custody here is going to be important, because we know that these were in Joe Biden`s hands, in Joe Biden`s control, then ended up behind his Corvette in his garage and in his office that he did not control and also throughout his house.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: And so the special counsel is going to have to deal with the issue of, what was the chain of custody? Who had these? Why did he take them to begin with? When did he get them?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: When was he handed these documents? And what did he do with them?\par \par \uc2 And this is a real critical question to all this. Why did he have these documents to begin with?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: And that is why the special counsel`s work is going to be really important, because I can think of no reason why the president should have taken home, as a senator or as vice president, any classified documents that clearly have no protection.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: They`re available and open to anybody.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have also before this development asked for a briefing from the director of national intelligence.\par \par \uc2 You set a deadline of Thursday. Do you have any further reason to believe they will meet that deadline, that you will get any insight into these materials?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Well, we`ll have to see, but what`s critical here...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They haven`t responded?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: And this is very important. This is what`s very important to all of this, Margaret, and that is the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the national archivist were working completely independent of the intelligence community or the Department of Defense.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: They claim this was all an issue of national security, but they did not speak to anyone who`s involved in national security.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, no response yet from the intelligence community?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: I have not received a response, no.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 I also want to ask you what leadership looks like with Republicans in charge. You are also on House Oversight.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are -- of the 26 Republican members on the committee, 19 of them denied the results of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 Your colleagues now include \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , Paul Gosar, Andy Biggs, \uc2 Lauren Boebert\uc2 , Scott Perry. They all played critical roles in -- in the former president`s attempts to overturn the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 Do you have any concerns about working with these lawmakers? I mean, you`re very much a centrist.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Well, even on the Democrat side, there`s been a number of people who objected to President Bush`s reelection and voted against certifying his election.\par \par \uc2 There`s a long history of both sides...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I am asking about you, your party, and your colleagues.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: There`s a long history of both sides having raised issues, including, you recall, the -- \uc2 Al Gore\uc2  taking President Bush`s election all the way to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are not an election denier, by \uc2 CBS\uc2  standards, just to be clear.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: I am -- I am not. And I work with both sides of the aisle, and there are election deniers on both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are comfortable with all those individuals I just rattled off and the fact that the majority of the Republicans on this committee denied the election results? Is that what you are saying?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: What I`m comfortable with is, the electorates are very smart. And these people have been sent to Congress to represent their districts and to be part of the congressional debate...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: ... to lead us to what`s going to be bipartisan, bicameral resolutions.\par \par \uc2 We have a split government right now. Republicans control the House. The Senate is controlled by the Democrats. You have a Democrat president. We`re going to have a lot of debate and discussions. And I think this is going to be a very fruitful period for -- for Congress and for our country, because it`s going to have to be bipartisan, bicameral.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that the president, in opening negotiations with Republicans, is beginning to start that process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What is actually possible in this bipartisan, bicameral situation? What can you actually get legislation through on?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Well, I -- I mean, depending on what the pending -- depending on what the president`s willing to do, I think it`s unlimited, right?\par \par \uc2 We have really tough issues right now. We have out-of-control inflation. We have an open border and record people crossing our border.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What about gun control?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: We have -- we have the issue of Russia and certainly in Ukraine and certainly China. I think we`re going to have a number of issues that we`re going to have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Congressman Turner, we have to leave it there today.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE MIKE TURNER: Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Face the Nation will be back in one minute.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For some political analysis, we`re joined now by chief election and campaign correspondent Robert Costa and Ed O`Keefe.\par \par \uc2 Ed, let me start with you, because it was last night probably around, what, 7:00 or so when all of us got the e-mail from the president`s attorney and then a statement from the White House about this latest discovery of documents.\par \par \uc2 This drip, drip, drip just continues.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It does.\par \par \uc2 And this is by design now. The White House Counsel`s Office, his personal training, making very clear that they withheld this information until after the search was conducted in his home in Delaware. And this is how we`re going to be learning about things, essentially Once steps are taken by the Justice Department, in this case, an \uc2 FBI\uc2  search of a sitting president`s home, a pretty extraordinary development in this case, and something we have not seen before, but a step that they made very clear they took voluntarily.\par \par \uc2 They were there for this, attorneys were, and they took away six more items marked classified for now a total of about 25 to 30 classified documents found in -- at his former office here in Washington and then in his home in Delaware.\par \par \uc2 And it raises questions about, what was that material? What more could be found? Where else might they search?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, and what the classification level is.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the reporting on this is continuing. But the politics, Bob, as you know, were laid bare there with Congressman Turner talking about outrage, frankly, that, in so many different locations, these materials could have been found.\par \par \uc2 And it changes the calculus for Republicans. And it gives them an advantage, frankly, politically, to make this argument.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The Republicans remain confrontational with this White House.\par \par \uc2 They see divided government as an opportunity, especially in the House, where they have the majority, to continue this investigation, even as the Justice Department continues its own investigation. You have someone like the Judiciary Committee chairman, Jim Jordan, now mounting his own investigation of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  and the Justice Department and the intelligence agencies.\par \par \uc2 This also comes politically at a moment of transition for the Biden White House. \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2 , the chief of staff, has signaled that he will be departing his position in the coming months. He has been a key link to progressives, has helped Biden work on signature legislation, like the American Rescue Plan.\par \par \uc2 And he`s lasted quite a long time for a chief of staff, two years.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I`m glad you bring that up, because the other thing that the departure of the chief of staff raises questions about is this looming policy and political conversation about the debt ceiling.\par \par \uc2 Who runs point on that? Obviously, the Treasury secretary has a huge role. But in terms of talking to the Hill and the negotiations, who`s doing that if the chief of staff is leaving?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: What I`m told from people inside the West Wing is that President Biden himself has a relationship with \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Senate Republican leader, of course, with \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , the Senate Democratic leader.\par \par \uc2 They are in some ways going to try to cut out Speaker Kevin McCarthy and the House Republicans. There`s not an appetite among Democrats to put spending cuts on the table at all. They would like to see a clean debt limit extension. And \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , one of the top Democrats in the House, recently told me he could see a scenario where centrist House Republicans band together with House Democrats for a clean debt limit extension.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And that outreach is under way.\par \par \uc2 We have heard from the White House saying that they are trying to reach out to these new members. There`s going to be a meeting this week with newly elected members invited to the White House to come and meet the president and say hello.\par \par \uc2 And you better believe that one of the ways they will try to work this is, are there enough Republicans out there maybe in districts that the president won or that just know they`re going to face a tough reelection who can be at least talked to about the risks and the potential to work together on this issue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But clarify that. When you say cut out the speaker of the House, are you saying it`s 212 Democrats, plus however many, 15 or more, that Republicans, they can pull over, rather than Republicans moving to lift the debt ceiling?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: What I`m hearing is that many more moderate, centrist House Republicans, those who are traditional Republicans, are looking at this, and they don`t want to get in front of McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 They know he has to meet with Biden in the coming weeks and tell the president he wants this in that in terms of a deal. But if a deal falls apart, as Ed said, they`re starting to have back-channel conversations of, could a coalition of 10 to 15. House Republicans get together with 200-plus House Democrats to get a clean extension through the House?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Not the prettiest way to do it, but certainly worth considering and something they have to talk about.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, when you talk about Leader McConnell and President Biden, they have history going back to the last time...\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You`re so right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... we went to the brink with the debt ceiling in 2011.\par \par \uc2 But the politics are even more complicated than they were back then. So how do they carry out this strategy? I feel like you`re laying out on \uc2 ESPN\uc2  the different potential strategies on the playing field here.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Privately, I`m told President Biden and Senator McConnell have chuckled behind the scenes with longtime friends about how at this stage in divided government, it`s these two men who have long been friends who are being counted upon to perhaps cut a deal.\par \par \uc2 I remember, when I first started covering Congress a decade ago, I would remember Vice President Biden was the one...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: ... who came to the Capitol to meet with Senator McConnell to cut a deal on that so-called fiscal cliff way back then.\par \par \uc2 So, they have that history, and they were recently in Kentucky together, showing at least, not political solidarity, but in terms of a personal relationship, there`s a real rapport.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, politically, this still is something that we`re going to be -- it can be very complicated. I mean, you can`t completely cut the House out of the situation, right?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: You can`t. You can`t.\par \par \uc2 But, look, this is also helpful for the White House. It`s a helpful foil, in essence, because one of the things they want to keep doing, other than talking about that potential fight, other than talking about this search of his home over the week on Friday, is about the economy.\par \par \uc2 And so you can go out there as president, as a Cabinet secretary, as the vice president, and say: Look, the economy is on the rebound. We seem to have held off inflation, for the most part. Things are in decent shape. It`s going to get much worse if Republicans allow this brinksmanship to continue, and even worse so if they go after things like carving into Social Security and Medicare as a way to pay for things.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, those are the third rail, but then you add in the potential of cutting defense spending, and that also becomes problematic.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it does seem like Republicans are shifting the conversation in some way towards the need to have a conversation about fiscal spending.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 And keep an eye on Senator Joe Manchin of West Virginia. He is telling his colleagues he is open to having a discussion about spending cuts. So, as many top Democrats say, hey, we want a clean extension, they now have Senator Manchin and a few others saying maybe we should negotiate a little bit.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a tension point in that area.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We have to see if there are other Senate Democrats also willing.\par \par \uc2 And, hopefully, we will talk to one of them that on the show.\par \par \uc2 Thanks to both of you.\par \par \uc2 We do have Virginia Senator Tim Kaine standing by, but we have some technical issues, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Today marks what would have been the 50th anniversary of Roe vs. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And even though it is no longer the law of the land, both pro- and anti- abortion rights supporters are marching on Washington and other cities this weekend. We took a look back through our archives and found a challenge to Roe vs. Wade from 1989 that also prompted marches on Washington.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Welcome to Face the Nation. I`m Lesley Stahl.\par \par \uc2 Sixteen years ago, the Supreme Court said a woman has a right to a legal abortion. The court, far more conservative today, is about to review that decision.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN SMITH (\uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 ): We`re hopeful that we will get to a point in our nation`s history where we restore protection to unborn children.\par \par \uc2 KATE MICHELMAN\uc2  (National Abortion Rights Action League): This -- this right is in danger and it is in serious danger now.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voice-over): So worried are the pro-choice forces, they`re hoping to rally more than a quarter-million people to march in Washington today.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think Washington is literally going to be shut down.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Abortion, it`s become a litmus test for public figures.\par \par \uc2 GIRL: Let`s suppose that I -- I`m 12 and I was sexually molested by my father, and I became -- I was sexually molested by my father and I became pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Would you want me to carry that baby to term and have that baby?\par \par \uc2 DAN QUAYLE (Former Vice President of the United States): It`s a difficult question and one that does not have an easy answer, but my answer would be yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Anti-abortion groups have turned to civil disobedience. Some 10,000 activists have been arrested.\par \par \uc2 BOB NOLTE (Operation Rescue): We are seeing clinics closed down for full days. Babies lives are being saved.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: There are still 1.6 million abortions a year in the U.S. Yet, while polls show over 75 percent support abortions for rape or incest, majorities now believe they should be illegal if sought for financial or emotional reasons.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The issue is proving to be a tricky one for some Republican 2024 hopefuls after Democrats had a better-than-expected showing in the 2022 midterms.\par \par \uc2 One big reason was, the party is more supportive of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re back with Democratic Senator Tim Kaine of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 I know you have dealt with a number of mass shootings as governor and senator in the state of Virginia. I wonder your thoughts on Monterey Park this morning.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TIM KAINE (D-Virginia): Margaret, it`s -- it`s just like scar tissue that keeps get reopened.\par \par \uc2 Every time these things happen, we`re reminded in Virginia of the shooting at \uc2 Virginia Tech\uc2  in April of 2007. My heart goes out to this community. I`m worried that the perpetrator is still at large. So, let`s first make sure that the law enforcement has the support to catch him, and then we need to support those survivors and all who`ve been affected.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, Senator, we have to take a quick break, and I want to talk to you more on the other side of it.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 And we continue our conversation with Senator Tim Kaine of Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Senator, yesterday for the fifth time we learned about this other tranche of classified information being kept at the president`s personal residence.\par \par \uc2 How does a senator accidentally take classified material home?\par \par \uc2 TIM KAINE: Margaret, I don`t really know the answer to that question because I - I review classified material as a senator on the Armed Services and Foreign Relations Committees, but when I do it, it`s always in a classified facility where I don`t have access to the materials other than to sit there and read them.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s why there needs to be this independent investigation and independent prosecutor. How many documents are we talking about? Dozens? A handful or hundreds? How serious are they? Why were they taken? Did anyone have access to them? And then, is the president being cooperative? And I think by all accounts it suggests that, yes, of course he`s being cooperative, as - as anyone should be. But these are the reasons why this independent prosecutor and an investigation is necessary.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re one of the few senate Democrats who have said, you know, that you have concern about this issue. I wonder, how concerned you are and, politically, doesn`t this damage the White House and the president in terms of trust and credibility?\par \par \uc2 TIM KAINE: Well, again, those questions do depend on the answer to the -- we`ll get in this investigation. I mean, Margaret, you - you know this, government has a tendency to overclassify. If they are looking at a document and they have to analyze to decide whether to classify, sometimes they just mark it classified, I`ll read it in the SCIF and say, Margaret was reporting on that a month ago, or "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " has been telling me this for the last six months. So, you do have to see, what`s the scope, how serious, did anyone have access? And those questions have to be answered before, you know, we decide, is this -- how significant an issue is this?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. And that may take some time.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about some of the business that Congress will have to get to soon. As you know, the White House wants a clean lift to the debt limit with no strings attached. Senator Manchin said it`s a mistake for the White House to refuse to negotiate with Republicans. Do you think that position is changing? Is it a mistake?\par \par \uc2 TIM KAINE: Well, first, we should - we should have a clean lift of the debt ceiling because the 14th Amendment to the Constitution says nobody should question the creditworthiness of the United States. This is about whether the U.S. we pays our credit card or not. And I don`t think anyone should flirt with not paying the U.S.` credit card, which is what Republicans are doing.\par \par \uc2 So, the White House position is correct, we should raise the debt ceiling. But if Republicans are saying they won`t do it and they`re threatening our creditworthiness because they want cuts, let them put cuts on the table. Is it the cutting Social Security and Medicare that Rick Scott wanted to, is it cutting aid to Ukraine in the middle of a war between a democracy and an illegally invading dictator? Let them put on the table what they want to cut so that the American public can see what their priorities are.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Speaker McCarthy has said the president has invited him to speak -- have a conversation -\par \par \uc2 TIM KAINE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And discuss a responsible lift to the debt ceiling. So, what does that mean? How does this politically get dressed up so we avoid the cliff?\par \par \uc2 TIM KAINE: Well, first, that`s a good thing. I`m very, very happy that the president and Speaker McCarthy are talking. That`s really positive.\par \par \uc2 How do we fix this? \uc2 Jeff Merkley\uc2  and I have a bill that`s based upon an earlier fix that Senator McConnell led during the Obama administration when there was a similar brinksmanship around a debt ceiling. And Senator McConnell said, look, we will allow the president to raise it subject to a congressional disapproval. And that was done. And we think that should be the norm anyway.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a bill called the Protect Our Credit Act that would basically say the president has got to cover the debts of the United States. And if that includes raising the debt ceiling, the president can do that. But if Congress disapproves, then you can have an expedited up or down vote in Congress.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TIM KAINE: And I think that`s the right solution to this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Before I let you go, you`re on Armed Services. How concerned are you that Secretary of Defense Austin left Germany with no agreement among western allies to provide tanks to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 TIM KAINE: Margaret, there are - there are some differences about exactly what and what`s the timing in terms of providing equipment. But we have assembled a global coalition to support Ukraine. The unity has been very strong and the support, bipartisan and bicameral in Congress has been very strong. And I think that will continue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Kaine, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 TIM KAINE: Absolutely. Thanks, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And some of the nation`s mayors are in town. They`re the first line of defense in every city, as you all know.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re back now with four of our nation`s mayors. \uc2 Francis Suarez\uc2  is the mayor of Miami and the head of U.S. conference of mayors. \uc2 LaToya Cantrell\uc2  is the mayor of New Orleans. Plus we have Atlanta Mayor \uc2 Andre Dickens\uc2  with us, along with John Giles, the mayor of Mesa, Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to all of you and thank you so much for being here in person.\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2  (Republican, Mayor of Miami): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL (Democrat, Mayor of New Orleans): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES (Republican, Mayor of Mesa, Arizona): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 , (Democrat, Mayor of Atlanta): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to talk about just what is developing in terms of the shooting in California. Nationwide, we are seeing this spike in violent crime. We`re seeing reports of an uptick in anti-Semitism and hate crimes. All of you are from states where there are fairly permissive gun laws. And I wonder, Mayor Suarez, how you put those pieces together. What is driving this?\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2 : Yes, it`s - it`s so many different factors that are driving this. But, you know, one of the things that we focused a lot on in this mayoral conference that we just ended was mental health. Mental health is a huge component of what`s driving a lot of these -- when you look at the root cause and you go back and you sort of peel back the layers of the onion.\par \par \uc2 Mayors are very concerned about it. We, obviously, had a panel, obviously, on - on - on urban crime. And certainly a lot of these mayors have talked about, you know, gun control in their cities.\par \par \uc2 In our city, you know, we - we have -- we`re very blessed. We had a great year. Our homicide level went down. We`ve been able -- and I knock on proverbial wood, that we haven`t had any of these mass shootings that we`ve seen across the country, which seem to be escalating in terms of frequency and in terms of, you know, the amount of times that we`re seeing them. It`s almost every day it seems like we`re having one.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s been - it`s been tough. It`s something that mayors are grappling with across the country. And we`re focusing in on it in a very comprehensive way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You specifically this week told your fellow mayors that some of this is driven by no cash bail policies. Why do you say that? Are repeat offenders a problem?\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2 : Yes, what - what I`m focusing on is the no cash bail is creating lawlessness in a lot of our cities. What`s happening is, for example, people get out right away. They`re not even - you know, they don`t even have to post bail, so they`re able to get out right away. And so we`re seeing someone go into like a CVS, for example, and - and take thousands of dollars` worth of merchandise, which is causing CVS` to close, which hurts, you know, the rest of the city. But that - that`s not particularly related to - to the gun violence issue. It`s related more to petty crime, which is creating lawlessness in some of our cities.\par \par \uc2 But what we`re do -- we are seeing in the city of Miami is, you know, we are up-funding our police.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2 : You know, a lot of cities got into the defunding police movement and we`re seeing that as a bipartisan issue. I said it at the White House, you know, just a couple days ago, and the president echoed what I said about up-funding police and not defunding police. So I think that is a bigger issue as we struggle with how we solves these issue in - on our cities.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And President Biden put in what $4 billion in grants that`s available for local law enforcement use in cities around the country.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 : Yes, you know, as you talked about this issue that`s happening right now in California, yet another mass shooting in our country, it continues to happen too frequently. And so, it`s just too many guns in America. It`s too many guns in the hands on our streets and guns plus anger equals bad outcomes, equals violence. And so we have to bring back laws that are sensible, common sense gun laws, to be able to reduce the amount of access that people have to guns.\par \par \uc2 And so you see another mass shooting and lives are lost. And my heart goes out to the people of California experiencing that. And so as Mayor Suarez mentioned, we`re talking about mental health and how to make sure that we have anti-violence in our communities. We`re utilizing a cure violence, you know, to bring down the retaliation and make sure we have healing in our communities to try to use policing and non-policing tactics to bring down violence. Midnight basketball, things that are - you know, summer youth employment program to help our youth. But mental health and just getting people the quality care that they need so they make wise decisions.\par \par \uc2 Because most of the violence that we`re seeing in our community is escalating disputes. People that are unable to resolve a conflict that`s just escalated too much. And people aren`t fighting or arguing any more, they`re taking their hands to their pockets and pulling out a gun. And it gets to intense and someone kills someone. And that`s the violence we`re seeing in America.\par \par \uc2 So, we have to take a whole of government approach to be able to bring down this violence. Things that we can do to help our youth, to help our communities.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 : And that`s some of the stuff that we`re doing in Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mayor Giles, you, actually, I`m surprised when I saw that Mesa has such a big population. I think you`ve -- you`re the biggest city at the table.\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it looked at -- I looked at your police site (ph). It says that you are one of America`s safest large cities.\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How do you qualify that? And how are you doing that if that is a fact?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES: Well, a lot of the things that these fellows have just mentioned. We are doubling down on our investment in our police department, and we are shifting the paradigm. A few years ago we changed the name of our fire department. It`s no longer the Mesa Fire Department. It`s Mesa Fire and Medical to better reflect what we do. We need to do the same when it comes to policing. It needs to be the police and mental health department.\par \par \uc2 Last year we diverted over 3,000 911 calls away from a police response to a mental health response. So, again, it -- the importance of mental health is ubiquitous in all that we do. And it was discussed at the - at the conference. It`s -- it has everything to do with how we address homelessness. It has everything that we do -- how we address policing in our community.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mayor Cantrell, I want to get to you on that, too, because President Biden said it`s not about defunding the police, it`s about restraining the place. I wonder if you agree with that. I know you have had a problem in New Orleans with not having enough police officers.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Less than 1,000 for 3,000 people.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL: Sure. And the thing is, is that, it`s about retention and it is also about recruitment. Because of this second tranche of the American Rescue Plan dollars coming our way with direct allocation, oh, it has really been a lifeline, where we`re putting $80 million in public safety across the board.\par \par \uc2 One of the biggest in terms of a retention and incentive package to retain. We see it slowing down, meaning attrition is declining. Our officers are staying. And so we just have to continue to give the tools and resources that our officers need to respond.\par \par \uc2 Also, in terms of the capacity issue you mentioned, you know, I`ve had to put all commissioned officers that were in special ops, over 75 back on the street, because my officers were saying, hey, we need help out here. So, I have to protect my officers so they can protect my city. And so, we`re seeing a real results in regards to our redeployment strategy on the ground.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: New Orleans has the highest per capita murder rate of any major city. Why?\par \par \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL: Why is because, one, dealing with Covid-19, violence, everyone has guns. The ability or the lack of the ability to resolve a conflict without reaching and pulling a gun.\par \par \uc2 Also, as it relates to accountability. You know, low-lying offenses, you know, when they don`t get bail or they`re not restrained, then we`re just seeing how these crimes escalate. People need to be held accountable across the board. And we`re seeing results, I would say. We`re moving in the right direction. But I tell you, we definitely need to hold people accountable.\par \par \uc2 You can`t fight crime just focusing on police. It`s about a system, a criminal justice system. It`s about the D.A., your judges. And it`s about building in accountability. Everyone needs to be held accountable. And that`s how we`re focusing on it. Wholistic approach in \uc2 the city of New Orleans\uc2 . Definitely seeing a decline. Moving in the right direction.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This issue of crime in your city is causing a lot of political problems. And you are the target of a recall drive that`s underway right now. A number of allegations against you as well in regard to financial improprieties.\par \par \uc2 How much of the responsibility with the crime issue do you - do you personally take?\par \par \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL: Well, first of all, it is the New Orleans Police Department that is absolutely under my authority. And with that, making sure that not only I`m listening to my officers, but getting them the resources that they need to fight crime. And that is exactly what we`re seeing on the ground. The incentive packages, retaining officers, as well as recruitment. And that`s the focus.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you believe you`ll survive this recall effort?\par \par \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL: Well, based on what I see is that the residents of my city definitely appreciate continuity in leadership. And so with that, that speaks to keeping progress moving and alive under my leadership, second elected twice in the city, 61 percent the first time, 65 percent the second time. Continuity in leadership is what I`m seeing by my people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to get to all of you on a number of issues, but I know something very intense has just happened in Atlanta, Mayor Dickens. I watched a press conference you held last night following the death of a Georgia-based activist. It turned into a riot. This stems, as I understand it, from the shooting death of an activist. And the body camera from the policeman who`s believed to have shoot this individual doesn`t exist.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us in terms of who is behind the violence that happened yesterday?\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 : Yes. Earlier this week an individual that was protesting in the woods, a number of folks are in the woods trying to protest against a development of a public safety training center, which is for police and firefighters. A new state-of-the-art training center that`s going to allow us to do 21st century policing, allow us to have an emergency vehicle obstacle course and these things that police and fire will be able to work together to be able to bring about, you know, safety in our community.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re building it, but some folks don`t want to see anything built that supports police. So they call it cop city. And these individuals are in the woods protesting it.\par \par \uc2 And, unfortunately, they were engaged by Georgia State Patrol, asked them to be able to move out of the woods. An individual shot at the Georgia State Patrol. And the Georgia State Patrol Officer shot back.\par \par \uc2 And, unfortunately, that individual was killed and the patrol officer, the state patrol officer, was shot in the abdomen. And so now they had a protest last night. And it was peaceful. But there were some individuals within that crowd that meant violence. They had explosives. They burned down a police car. They broke windows at businesses.\par \par \uc2 And so our police department, along with our state and federal partners, took swift action within two blocks and brought that situation under control. And the violence stopped. And those six individuals were arrested. And it should be noted that these individuals were not Atlanta or Georgia residents. Most of them traveled into our city to wreak havoc. And so we love to support people when they`re doing right. Peaceful protest is a part of the American - our freedoms. But when you are violent, we will make sure that you get -- held accountable.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want - I want to pick you up on that point, when you say people from out of town, they`re carrying explosives.\par \par \uc2 Is this an organized movement here? Your local paper says this is having national reach with reaction from groups ranging from, quote, environmental activists, radical anarchists and black revolutionaries. \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , Georgia congresswoman, I`m sure you know her, blamed Black Lives Matter and Antifa. And that she blamed Democrats.\par \par \uc2 On the facts, seven to 13 people have been charged with domestic terrorism.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is this terrorism? Is this crime? What is this? Who`s behind it?\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 : Yes, I won`t go as far as whatever that representative said. But what I will say is that it is a crime. And that`s why they`ve been charged with a crime. And these crimes --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Domestic terrorism.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 : And the crimes range from violence to domestic terrorism to assault and battery and some other things. But, yes, it is violent when someone turns to burn down a police car or break out windows or have explosives on them. I don`t get into the names. I don`t know all the organizations. I`ll let y`all decide who did it. I just know they`re arrested. And if they come into Atlanta again to wreak havoc, they will be arrested again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But do you believe your city is being targeted by organized groups?\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 : In that regard, yes. Those individuals that are protesting against cop city, as they call it, it`s really a public safety training center, they don`t want to see the very things that they ask for, more police training. We can`t train imaginary. We have to do it in a facility that allows for police, firefighters and the community to train together.\par \par \uc2 And so this is bringing about the change that we wanted to see in 2020. And now while we`re doing it, these individuals don`t want to see any resources go towards that training. And so we`re going to develop this training center and those individuals will have to come to a halt.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mayor Giles, you are on the front line of the migration surge. And I think it`s so interesting that you`re characterizing your city as very safe. You know, these issues of migration surges being uncontrolled, and crime are often conflated. How are you doing that in terms of not having this overwhelm your local officials in law enforcement?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES: Well, I wish I could say it`s not overwhelming us. It is. And it has been for decades. And one of the thing I`ve enjoyed about this conference over the last few days is, you`re starting to see more bipartisan frustration. You`re seeing the mayor of New York City and Chicago and Denver are all top of their agendas now is talking about the problems that are being created in their communities as a result of the migration surge.\par \par \uc2 I, in no way, support or encourage the practice of some of our border state governors in sending migrants to these large northeastern cities, but I do have to admit that it has elevated this issue to a place that it has not been previously. Border states have been complaining for decades about the need to address immigration reform, the need to dedicate additional resources to the border. And we`re doing the best we can.\par \par \uc2 But in our city, the church groups, the nonprofits that are being put upon by the federal government to come in and take up the slack, we are past our resources. So, hopefully this new-found bipartisan frustration and joining of northeastern cities to this issue will help elevate it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you have the Super Bowl coming out to Arizona soon. I know -- I read that you plan to take the Homeland Security secretary around your city?\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES: Absolutely. Mayor Gallego and I met with the Secretary Mayorkas just a few days ago. I`ve extended that invitation. We have very limited resources as far as welcoming centers and facilities to process these migrants as they proceed in their - in their journeys on sometimes to the northeastern cities. We need the band-aids to keep coming from the federal government in terms of facilities.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES: But we also need to address the underlying issue of immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mayor Suarez, I need to get to you on this as well because south Florida has seen this influx by boat.\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2 : Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Cubans in particular. Haitians. Customs and Border Protection report a 400 percent increase in the month of October alone. Are these new tighter regulations from the Biden administration making any difference?\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2 : It`s hard to say. They were just imposed. But certainly I think the failure of having an immigration solution, as Mayor Giles said, is creating a sort of Miami and Florida becoming a border state and border city.\par \par \uc2 As you said, we had the single largest increase in public school enrollment year over year this year. And so that -- just to put that in context, about 14,000 new children. If a big school is 2,000 children, that`s seven new schools that we have to create in the system. It, obviously, as you said, puts a homeless -- strain on the homeless system and trying to take care of the least, the last and the lost in our cities. It puts a strain on our public hospital system. We have one of the largest public hospitals in the country that provides hundreds of millions of dollars of indigent care. Puts a tremendous amount of strain on that system.\par \par \uc2 So, I think cities across America, as Mayor Giles said, are coming together in a bipartisan fashion and we`re asking for a long-term solution. This problem has to be fixed. There has to be an articulated strategy. A lot of the immigration is coming from this hemisphere. And it doesn`t seem like -- you know, either party really has focused on solving the problem as opposed to, you know, demonizing the other side for their position. And I think that`s what we`re - what mayors do is we focus on solving problems, not blaming somebody else for it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right. And you delivered that message here in Washington this week.\par \par \uc2 Mayors, thank you very much for coming to the table.\par \par \uc2 FRANCIS SUAREZ\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA CANTRELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JOHN GILES: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANDRE DICKENS\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230122ej1m00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Nationwide Protests Over The New Politics Of Abortion Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, Robert Costa \par \uc2 962 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  WEEKEND NEWS from Chicago with Adriana Diaz.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good evening. There are several developing stories tonight, including a winter storm heading into the Northeast, but we`ll begin with a renewed battle over abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Today, abortion proponents gathered in San Francisco. This weekend marks 50 years since the Roe vs. Wade decision gave Americans the constitutional right to an abortion, a decision that was overturned last summer.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa is at the Capitol tonight with the latest -- Robert.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good evening, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 Abortion rights does remain at the forefront of our national debate here in Washington, in the States, and ahead of the 2024 presidential race.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Does the push to restrict abortion rights continue?\par \par \uc2 FRANKLIN GRAHAM: I think yes, no question. It now moves more or less to the State capitals.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Evangelical leader, Franklin Graham, son of the famed Billy Graham was one of thousands of anti-abortion activists who turned up in Washington Friday for the March for Life, arguing that the end of Roe is not the end of their fight.\par \par \uc2 ELOISE NATHANIEL (Attendee): Nothing has really changed. Abortions are still happening every day.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But the persistence from conservatives on abortion comes as Republicans are at a crossroads.\par \par \uc2 Some, like former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have blamed last year`s disappointing election results on the abortion issue being poorly handled by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Former Vice President of the United States): I`ll be joining some people at the March for Life next week, and, um, I`ve made it very clear, it may take us as long to restore the sanctity of life to the center of American law as it took us to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But in the early days of the 2024 presidential race, possible Trump rivals are shaking their heads at Trump`s assessment and tilting to the right.\par \par \uc2 And would you nudge Governor DeSantis to take further action?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR KRISTI NOEM (R-SOUTH DAKOTA): I would encourage every governor to do what they can to back up their prolife record. I think that that talking about, um, situations and making statements is incredibly important, but also taking action and governing and bringing policies that protect life are even more important.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And the issue has sparked an informal policy standoff among those who might run for President.\par \par \uc2 But many attending the march acknowledged the politics of this issue. Long, highly charged, remain complicated.\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE MARCINIAK (Attendee): In order for the best ideas to come forward, you need to be able to willing to listen to each other. You need to be able to hear your opponents, understand what they are thinking, have empathy and compassion and come up with ideas together.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Meanwhile, 50 years after Roe v. Wade, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  will be in Tallahassee, Florida on Sunday to deliver a major speech on abortion rights as Democrats continue to push on this issue as well -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Bob, there is also news tonight on another subject at the White House.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 White House Chief-of-Staff, \uc2 Ron Klain\uc2  has signaled to advisers and associates that he will leave his position in the coming months. He has been a longtime confidant of President Biden, a key player on legislation and a link to progressive Democrats -- Adriana.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, Robert Costa, thank you.\par \par \uc2 A winter storm is heading into the Northeast this weekend. It has already left its mark across the Midwest. This is Kansas today.\par \par \uc2 For more, let`s check in with meteorologist, Molly McCollum from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MOLLY MCCOLLUM (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  Meteorologist): Good evening, Adriana.\par \par \uc2 It`ll be a snowy finish to the weekend and the Northeast, but not for everyone. It`s been 318 days since we`ve seen measurable snow in New York City, the all-time record now in jeopardy with 332 days.\par \par \uc2 This system will not bring us snow to some of the major cities along the I- 95 corridor. This is what we call an inside runner, bringing heavy snow to parts of the interior making ski resorts very happy.\par \par \uc2 And despite who sees rain, and he who sees snow, everyone is going to see the wind. Check this out, late Sunday into early Monday, that`s when we see the rain and the snow start to evolve making for a very messy Monday morning commute for you in Boston and New York City all the way over to the Capitol district.\par \par \uc2 And Adriana, looking ahead to next week, there is another winter storm it`s going to be taking aim in the lower 48.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: All right, Molly McCollum from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 , Thank you.\par \par \uc2 The Memphis Police Department has fired five officers following the death of Tyre Nichols this month. An investigation found the officers violated department policies including the use of excessive force and failing to give aid after a traffic stop for reckless driving.\par \par \uc2 Nichols said he had trouble breathing. The 29-year-old was hospitalized and died three days later. His family says he was brutally beaten.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020230209ej1l00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Policy May Limit Breast Cancer Coverage\b0\par\par\uc2 Michelle Miller, Anna Werner, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,518 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 January 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT) MICHELLE MILLER: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at a medical coding change that could limit options for some women fighting breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 Each year, more than 100,000 women in the US need a mastectomy after a breast cancer diagnosis. The Women`s Health and Cancer Rights Act requires insurance companies who pay for mastectomies to cover breast reconstruction as well.\par \par \uc2 Well, today in our series Medical Price Roulette, we`re looking at how policy changes could deny some women a more natural type of breast reconstruction, which they believed can help them feel whole again.\par \par \uc2 Our consumer investigative correspondent, Anna Werner is here.\par \par \uc2 Anna, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Good morning, Michelle.\par \par \uc2 This breast reconstruction method uses a patient`s own tissues instead of artificial implants, something many women prefer. Some surgeons say what sounded like a simple change to the coding of this procedure now could make it unaffordable for all, but the wealthiest patients.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS (Breast cancer patient): I noticed a small nodule like right on my breast.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: When the lump in Rita Davis` breast turned out to be malignant, she and her husband Britt had no idea it would mean more than a year long fight against aggressive cancer.\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: We found out that there was much more spread than we knew.\par \par \uc2 BRITT DAVIS (Rita`s husband): I wasn`t ready for that. And I was like, "Oh, my God."\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Doctors had to remove her right breast and give her chemotherapy and radiation. This teacher and mother of four lost her hair, her nails, and her sense of self.\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: Everything is so uncontrollable to you as the patient.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: You`re not in control.\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: You`re not in control.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Everything is something that happens to you.\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So when doctors asked if she wanted a breast reconstruction, she answered yes.\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: Reconstruction, at least, you get to have some sort of a choice. You get to have some sort of a say of what`s happening to you and your body at that point.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Most women undergoing breast reconstruction get implants made of silicone and filled with either a silicone gel or saline solution. They carry risks of additional surgeries, pain, rupture, even an associated immune system cancer.\par \par \uc2 Yet, this middle school teacher knew from her own research, there was another option, something called a deep flap in which a surgeon removes skin fat and blood vessels from a woman`s stomach, essentially doing a tummy tuck and uses them to create a new breast with the woman`s own tissues.\par \par \uc2 What did you like about what you read about it?\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: Using my own skin. I liked that it would be built naturally. You know what I mean? More naturally than with implants.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: But when the couple went to a local plastic surgeon`s office.\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: They said, oh, these are your implants. I mean, they pulled up two.\par \par \uc2 BRITT DAVIS: And let us feel them.\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: Let us feel them.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So literally, it was okay, implants.\par \par \uc2 BRITT DAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: That was the only direction that you got?\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: The only other surgeon she could find in San Antonio who offered deep flap surgery didn`t take her insurance, their cash price $50,000.00.\par \par \uc2 RITA DAVIS: And I don`t think that`s fair. I mean, I don`t think that, especially after fighting this kind of a journey, this kind of a battle to be told because you don`t have the money, you don`t have the option to do anything different.\par \par \uc2 BRITT DAVIS: Because this doesn`t seem right. Something is not adding up here.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH POTTER (Austin, Texas surgeon): Women should not be asked to pay cash for the surgery.\par \par \uc2 So, I`m going to take all of this tissue.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Austin, Texas surgeon, Dr. Elisabeth Potter, who specializes in deep flap surgeries, says part of the problem is that the operation is longer and requires complex micro surgical skills, meaning many plastic surgeons simply don`t offer it.\par \par \uc2 On top of that, some doctors charge much higher fees and won`t accept insurance.\par \par \uc2 You`re already seeing where surgeons are basically saying to patients, either you pay cash or it`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH POTTER: I`m already seeing that.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Dr. Potter does accept insurance, but now, she says a change in the way the government codes the procedure is about to make the surgery even harder to get. The change has prompted some insurance companies to start sending out letters like these, saying their reimbursements will drop, some she estimates by 80 percent.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH POTTER: Make no mistake, women who want the surgery and have money will always have access to it. The only people that this coding change affects and hurts are women who need access through insurance.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: And if I can`t afford to pay for myself.\par \par \uc2 DR. ELISABETH POTTER: Good luck.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: That says Florida Representative \uc2 Debbie Wasserman Schultz\uc2 , a breast cancer survivor is not a good enough answer.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE WASSERMAN SCHULTZ (D-FL): So much of the aftermath as you go through process of being a survivor turns on your psychological wellbeing and how you feel as a human and as a woman and this obstacle was put in the way of breast cancer survivors.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: So why did that change happen?\par \par \uc2 The Center for Medicare and Medicaid Services, the government agency that oversees the coding says the code changes came about after the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2  and plastic surgeons asked for changes, but the doctors` groups told us the insurance companies are to blame for deciding to lower their reimbursements.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of who is to blame, this representative is pushing for a solution.\par \par \uc2 REP. DEBBIE WASSERMAN SCHULTZ (D-FL): They`ve got to get together and fix this. This is a devastating decision for women who have been through this.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: Well, Representative Wasserman Schultz is part of a bipartisan effort to get this change reversed.\par \par \uc2 We contacted several insurance companies, one told us, it has now postponed lowering its reimbursements because of the concerns raised, others didn`t respond.\par \par \uc2 As for Rita Davis, she was lucky. She found Dr. Potter who agreed to do her surgery for whatever amount Davis` insurance will pay, even though Dr. Potter says it will not cover her costs.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: But there`s not a lot of surgeons out there doing those kinds of almost pro bono efforts.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: How did we get here? I mean, how did this happen?\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: You know, that`s a great question, because the answer to that is a little unclear.\par \par \uc2 You know, there is a coding process with the government. It involves doctors, the AMA, the doctors` groups, the government officials, and they establish the codes and then the reimbursement comes from the insurance companies who look at the codes and decides, okay, it`s Code X that pays this amount of money, right?\par \par \uc2 So essentially, you kind of have it moving from one code that paid more to another code that paid less, and nobody is really clear on why, how was this intended, unintended consequences of a change?\par \par \uc2 So then what you have is you have Representative Wasserman Schultz saying and others in Congress saying all right, you know what, this is not okay. Whatever the intent was, the reason was, it`s not okay for this to wind up this way, and you guys need to come together and fix this.\par \par \uc2 And by the way, Representative Wasserman Schultz says she does not think legislation is the answer because she is like, look, that`s a hard, you know, uphill battle. It`s going to take forever if it even gets in.\par \par \uc2 They need to get to -- come to the table and fix it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Doing stories like this helps, so thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: We hope so. We really hope so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 ANNA WERNER: We really hope so.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Anna, thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up how a mob boss hid in plain sight for 30 years until police finally caught up with him yesterday.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020230202ej1h000m9\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For January 8, 2023, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Mark Strassmann, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 8,989 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 January 2023\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2023 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2023 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: the battle for the head of the House is over. The dust is settling. But how will new Speaker Kevin McCarthy rule with such a bitterly divided group of Republicans?\par \par \uc2 At long last, after 15 roll call votes spread over more than four days and 25 hours of congressional togetherness on the House floor, the ultraconservative never-Kevin caucus won a number of concessions from California`s Kevin McCarthy. The arm-twisting finally paid off.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): I hope one thing is clear after this week. I never give up.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For the nine-term Bakersfield, California, native, it was a moment of triumph. On the cusp of victory, he claimed the prolonged haggling was a plus.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY: This is the great part. Because it took this long, now we learned how to govern.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let`s hope so, because the challenges ahead are daunting.\par \par \uc2 Speaker McCarthy`s Republican Conference has only a tiny five-seat majority, difficult even if his party were united. This conference is not.\par \par \uc2 We will talk with two House Republicans, South Carolina`s Nancy Mace and Tony Gonzales of Texas.\par \par \uc2 Then: President Biden is headed to the U.S.-Mexico border after unveiling tough new immigration rules meant to discourage illegal entry.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Do not just show up at the border. Stay where you are and apply legally from there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Some Democrats don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 We will talk with El Paso`s congresswoman, \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2 , and get her take.\par \par \uc2 Maine Senator Angus King is just back from Ukraine. We will talk with him and Ukrainian Ambassador \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have got a new CBS News poll that looks at how Americans are feeling in the new year.\par \par \uc2 And we will take a closer look at the evolution of a congressional tradition.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 It was early yesterday morning when Kevin McCarthy finally secured enough Republican votes to give him the gavel.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY: That was easy, huh?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY: I never thought we`d get up here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That latter sentiment was felt by many Americans, after watching the proceedings drag on for days.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY: Do you solemnly swear, affirm...\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congress was finally sworn in, and they are officially on the job.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CONGRESS MEMBERS: rMD-IT_I do.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the challenges ahead are enormous, especially for McCarthy, who was forced to make concessions to a small group of holdout conservatives in order to win their support. Will he live to regret what he agreed to?\par \par \uc2 We begin with one of the Republican members of Congress who was with Speaker McCarthy on all votes. That`s Nancy Mace of South Carolina.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Congresswoman. Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE (R-South Carolina): Good morning. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Again and again, 14 times, the hard right faction of your party refused to vote for Kevin McCarthy, even after he was making repeated concessions to them.\par \par \uc2 How can Republicans possibly govern when your party is so unruly?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, first of all, I want to say, number one, Kevin McCarthy is the only member that I know of that can bring all the different groups together within our own party, because we do have different factions, just like Democrats do.\par \par \uc2 And that`s -- that`s the first thing. And then the second thing is that, sometimes, democracy is messy. It looked kind of like an unnecessary and prolonged food fight last week. And I agreed with many Americans who thought that. I came home this weekend and listened to folks of all sides. I represent a very purple district. I have all sides to serve.\par \par \uc2 And there was a lot of was a lot of frustration with the prolonged and unnecessary food fight that we had this week. But you saw democracy on full display. And I think that`s healthy to have that kind of debate. I`m glad that it`s over and we can move forward.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I mean, some would say it wasn`t so much democracy as it was dysfunction.\par \par \uc2 "The Wall Street Journal" editorial page was pretty scathing, said: "Don`t believe the happy talk this was a healthy display of deliberative democracy. It was a power play. A group of backbenchers saw an opportunity to exploit the narrow GOP margin of five seats to put themselves in positions of power that they hadn`t earned through seniority or influence with colleagues."\par \par \uc2 If this rules package passes, with all the concessions that Speaker McCarthy made, this will be leave you beholden, won`t it, to those backbenchers?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Well, a couple of things I want -- I want to say.\par \par \uc2 On the rules package, the rules that are governed the way -- that will govern the way the House operates, there are some very great, good ideas in there, like the 72-hour rule, having three days to read a bill before it comes to the floor for a vote, having a path to balance the budget over the next 10 years, ensuring that there are spending offsets, especially with mandatory spending.\par \par \uc2 If you`re going to increase in one area, then you have to decrease in another. But I will tell you, when I ran for Congress two years ago, I won by one point, and I ran to be a new Nancy in the House. And what I saw last week was a small faction of the 20 who were acting just like the old Nancy, trying to cut backroom deals in private and secret without anyone knowing what else was going on.\par \par \uc2 And when they did the rules package at the end of the day, there was only one point that was changed. That was on the motion to vacate. That was the only difference in the package that we`re going to be voting on tomorrow that was different from the original package that was proposed.\par \par \uc2 So, my question really is today is, what backroom deals were cut, did they try to cut, and did they get those? Because we shouldn`t be operating like \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , this small faction. And they`re the ones that are saying they were -- quote -- "fighting the swamp," but then yet went and tried to act like, you know -- like, they actually are the swamp by trying to do these backroom deals.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: And we don`t know what they got or didn`t get. We haven`t seen it. We don`t have any idea what promises were made or what gentleman`s handshakes were made.\par \par \uc2 We just -- we just have no idea at this point. And it does give me quite a bit of heartburn, because that`s not what we ran.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Speaker...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: It is quite ironic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But -- well, so, you`re saying Speaker McCarthy is not being transparent about all the deals that he brokered in order to win this job?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: I`m saying there`s a small handful of individuals in that 20 who were trying to cut deals in secret, and not letting anybody else know about them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Didn`t they succeed in doing that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: We`re not sure. We don`t know at this point.\par \par \uc2 And that does give me pause and gives me significant heartburn on what direction we`re going to take.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: I represent a purple district. I have to represent Republicans, Democrats and independents.\par \par \uc2 I want to know that the positions that I have are going to have a voice, that it will have weight in the conference. There are a lot of members like me that that have issues with some of the policies that we`re going to be working on. Look at what happened after overturning of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: We didn`t have a plan.\par \par \uc2 And I want -- as a woman and as a victim of rape, want to know that we`re going to be serious, that we`re going to be balanced in protecting the rights of women and protecting the rights of the unborn.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: There`s a way to do both, and not be guided by one extreme or the other.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I just want to clarify there, because it -- I had asked you initially about the rules package, which is published and would be voted on tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you saying that you`re going to withhold your vote on those published agreements until you know what these backroom deals were?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: I am considering that as an option right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: I like the rules package. It is the most, open, fair and fiscally conservative package we have had in 30 years. I support it.\par \par \uc2 But what I don`t support is a small number of people trying to get a deal done or deals done for themselves...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: ... in private, in secret to get a vote or a vote present. I don`t support that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: That is just what \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  does. And that`s not what they should be doing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: And so I am on the fence right now about the rules package vote tomorrow for that reason.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Got it.\par \par \uc2 It reportedly includes a pledge that would likely require a $75 billion cut to national security funding. Do you support that part of it?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: I want to see -- I want to see it in writing. I want to see what promises were made.\par \par \uc2 And what we are being told is that these handshakes, what`s going on, these promises, will go through regular order and go through the regular appropriations process. I don`t want to see defense cuts. I -- again, we don`t -- we don`t know what deals were made. And that`s something that we should be transparent about\par \par \uc2 Sunshine is the best medicine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: That`s what we have always said. So, what -- what are we guaranteeing or what promises were made? We should know.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The speaker has reportedly given the Freedom Caucus, that ultraconservative faction, a third of the seats on the powerful Rules Committee, which controls which bills make it to the floor.\par \par \uc2 You have called Matt Gaetz, one of its members, a political D-lister and a fraud. You have sparred with \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 . I will show our viewers part of that and let them interpret your meaning.\par \par \uc2 How are you going to work with these folks to get anything done for the American people?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: It`s going to be very difficult.\par \par \uc2 Matt Gaetz is a fraud. Every time he voted against Kevin McCarthy last week, he sent out a fund-raising e-mail. What you saw last week was a constitutional process diminished by those kinds of political actions. I don`t support that kind of behavior.\par \par \uc2 I am very concerned, as someone who represents a lot of centrists, a lot of independents -- I have as many independents and Democrats as I have Republicans in my district. I have to represent everybody. I am concerned that commonsense legislation will not get through to get a vote on the floor.\par \par \uc2 And I -- for example, we have 12 bills that we`re supposedly going to be voting on in our first week in office. Three of them are abortion -- abortion bills and pro-life bills. I am pro-life, but I have many exceptions. But they are not legislation, pieces of legislation, that can pass the Senate and get onto the desk for the president to sign into law.\par \par \uc2 And so if we`re going to be serious about protecting life, for example, maybe we should look at more centrist views, like ensuring every woman has access to birth control, because, if you can reduce pregnancies, you can reduce the need or want for women to have abortions...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: ... for example, a very commonsense, pragmatic point of view.\par \par \uc2 But that`s not what we`re going to be voting on this week.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: And I am concerned.\par \par \uc2 I want to see pragmat -- pragmatics at work, common sense, fiscal conservative issues at work that represent all views.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Before I let you go, I have to ask you. You have a new colleague from the state of New York, Representative George Santos. He says he`s embellished his resume. You could say he just flat-out lied about work history, education, family background.\par \par \uc2 How can you work with someone like that? And does he need to be removed from office?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: It`s very difficult to work with anyone who cannot be trusted.\par \par \uc2 And it`s very clear his entire resume in life was -- was manufactured, until a couple of days ago, when he finally changed his Web site. It is a problem. If we say we can`t trust the left when they aren`t telling the truth, how can we trust our own?\par \par \uc2 Americans want transparency. And the one lesson I have learned in D.C., if you want a friend you can trust, get a dog.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: So...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I understand you are a dog fan.\par \par \uc2 All right, Congresswoman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: I am a huge dog fan.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY MACE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Nancy Mace, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 We want to go to another Republican, Tony Gonzales of Texas. He is in San Antonio this morning.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, let`s get right to it.\par \par \uc2 I saw you said on FOX News Friday you`re very worried and the American public should be terrified at what the 118th Congress is going to look like with all this division. Exactly what do we need to be prepared for?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES (R-Texas): Yes, Margaret. Good morning. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 You know, the speaker vote is the easiest vote we`ll take in Congress. And it was pretty chaotic. The rules -- the rules package is the next easiest vote. It`s -- look, the House of Representatives is a rough and rowdy place. Anybody that watched \uc2 C-SPAN\uc2  this week got to see it firsthand.\par \par \uc2 This is only the beginning. And with a such a small minority, Republicans are much different than Democrats. We`re not just going to line up and jump off the cliff. All of us represent our districts and we`re going to fight for that.\par \par \uc2 I spent 20 years in the military.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Beyond my district, we`ve got a nation to save. And -- and part of this division doesn`t help. But at the end of the day, you can`t let you know the insurgency caucus take hold and dictate.\par \par \uc2 You know, Kevin McCarthy ran on this Commitment to America, great leadership. He put this together. Many of us got elected this cycle because of this Commitment to America.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: These are the type of policies that we have to initiate.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you dropped a bit of a bomb when you said that, even though you have supported McCarthy and voted for him, you are going to vote against this rules package tomorrow that includes some of the deals he brokered.\par \par \uc2 Why? And how many Republicans are with you? And then what happens?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Yeah, McCarthy`s a great leader.\par \par \uc2 Like, once again, he put together this Commitment to America agenda that we have all circled behind. The reason why I`m voting against the rules package was really two -- two specific reasons. One, we were supposed to vote on this rules package in the dead of the night right after swearing in, which I think is very, not transparent of what happens.\par \par \uc2 I`m pleased to see that that has now been pushed to Monday. That gives a lot of people an opportunity to -- to -- to vote on it in -- during -- during the daylight hours.\par \par \uc2 But the second reason...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Did it change your mind?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: ... more importantly, was, this is -- this has a proposed billions-of-dollar cut to defense, which I think is a horrible idea, when you have aggressive Russia in Ukraine, you`ve got a growing threat of China in the Pacific.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to visit Taiwan here in a couple of weeks. How am I going to look at our allies in the eye and say, I need you to increase your defense budget, but yet America is going to decrease ours?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, that is -- you are still going to vote against it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 How many Republicans are with you? And does this cause further chaos?\par \par \uc2 REP. TONY GONZALES: You know, I am going to vote against it.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a difference between voting against it and whipping other members to vote against it. I would say, in this case, I`m not whipping other members to vote against it. But what I will give you an example. If - - you know, if these -- this insurgency caucus decides to put anti- immigrant legislation on the floor and masquerade it as border security policy, that`s not going to fly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: And I will do everything in my power to make sure that type of legislation fails on the floor.\par \par \uc2 So, this is -- this is one of many votes, I think, in the 118th Congress that have to be well-thought-out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, I want to get to the border in a quick moment.\par \par \uc2 But let me quickly ask you, how can Republicans possibly govern and do things that are essential, like dealing with the debt ceiling, without causing more chaos and potentially economic catastrophe? How can you get your party in line for important, basic work?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: Yes, it`s going to be ugly.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you look at the White House, the White House is a dumpster fire. You look at the Senate, the Senate is chaotic. You look at the House, and you -- I mean, if you`re an American just sitting down watching TV, you`re going, where has this country gone?\par \par \uc2 What has happened, what I see is, there`s nothing but politicians getting up here and grandstanding. It`s time for leadership. And I think what you`re going to see is, leadership has to be from the ground up. It has to be the rank and file come together and say, enough is enough.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`m a conservative Republican that wants to see the border secure...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: ... that wants to see inflation get put down.\par \par \uc2 So, these type of things is what we need to focus on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Last time you were here, you said you wanted to go with President Biden to the border. He`s going there today. Are you participating at all? Have you had any contact with the White House about migration?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I`m very disappointed in the administration.\par \par \uc2 You know, seven months ago, I hosted the president in Uvalde, and I asked him to visit on the border. He looked me in the eye and he said: "Tony, yes."\par \par \uc2 Seven months later, when I tried to be part of this El Paso visit, which I represent El Paso, I represent 50 -- nearly 50 percent of the southern border -- the White House told me, I wasn`t -- I wasn`t able to be part of it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: What does that mean? That means -- that means that Democrats are using this as a political -- they think this is a political challenge, not a policy challenge.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, you know, you see these images a couple of weeks ago of hundreds of people in a cell. That`s not a political challenge. That is a policy challenge.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why? Why did they tell you can`t come? What did they say?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I don`t know. That`s the frustrating part.\par \par \uc2 You know, on one end, you`re hearing them say they -- you know, that they want to work with Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I`m not this crazy extremist Republican. I`m jumping up and down, pushing against my party when I think it`s right, looking for ways to solve problems.\par \par \uc2 What I don`t want to see is a terrorist action happen and say, I told you so. I have no interest in that. I`m trying to keep America safe. The administration just has turned a blind eye. And it goes to show they think that their policy is correct. And it`s dangerous.\par \par \uc2 If you`re -- it should scare the hell out of every American out there to say this administration is going to going to double down. And it`s going to taint the Biden administration. It`s going to taint Biden`s legacy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, well -- well, actual legislation on the border would require your party to also help out.\par \par \uc2 But, very quickly, George Santos, new New York Congressman. We just talked to Congresswoman Mace. She has a problem with the fact that he is consistently lying. He is also under investigation. Do you think he should be removed from office?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: I think -- look, there`s a lot of frauds in Congress. I think a lot of people got to see that firsthand over the last week.\par \par \uc2 I mean, George Santos is the least of this country`s worries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, he should stay?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TONY GONZALES: We have a lot of things to worry about. Step one is -- is getting this rules package done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 All right, I got to go. Congressman, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 When we come back in one minute, new insights into how Americans are feeling about the economy, democracy and politics in the new year.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our first CBS News poll of 2023 is out.\par \par \uc2 Overall, Americans are feeling better about some things in the new year. But there`s still a lot of angst out there.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann reports from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): American confidence, historically fickle, gaped at this scene, the country`s elected grownups grappling, another flash point in a four-day, 15-ballot debacle.\par \par \uc2 No wonder our new CBS News poll shows 70 percent of Americans generally pessimistic about U.S. politics right now, and 55 percent disapprove of the way Republicans picked their new speaker of the House.\par \par \uc2 About the economy, especially with inflation`s long shadow and recession fears, Friday`s jobs report suggested the economy is cooling, with wage gains slowing. Unemployment is at 3.5 percent, a 50-year low, but expect interest rates to rise again in 2023.\par \par \uc2 Our CBS News poll shows Americans feel better about the condition of the economy than they did six months ago. But when it comes to specific economic measures, 59 percent feel pessimistic about the stock market, 70 percent pessimistic about the cost of goods and services.\par \par \uc2 ALEX PELLE (Economist, Mizuho Securities USA): But we are starting from a position of strength. So, that is something that people can take comfort in.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But what about Americans` strength overall in this post- pandemic phase of recovery and renewal? Our poll shows only 34 percent of Americans, one-third, believe life in this country is going well.\par \par \uc2 The good news? That`s up from one in four last year. Nearly half say they`re scared, while nearly another half feel hopeful about 2023.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Two years after the January 6 riots, our poll also showed roughly three in four Americans agree the results of last year`s midterms were legitimate.\par \par \uc2 But most Americans also believe our democracy remains threatened, another area that showed improvement, but still has a long way to go -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And when we come back, we will take a closer look at some of the very new faces we met in the first days of the 118th Congress.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A lot of us were glued to last week`s coverage of the battle for speaker on the House floor, and particularly taken by the pictures of all of the children and grandchildren in the chamber for those marathon sessions, also awaiting the official swearing-in of House members.\par \par \uc2 This year, we saw a lot more dads in charge, both on the House floor during boats and behind the scenes, especially when some moms had to head home to work. California Democratic Congressman Jimmy Gomez was one of those dads. His 4-month-old son, Hodge, became a social media and national TV star as he and his father bonded on and off the House floor. Baby Hodge had a firsthand view of all of the chaos from his forward-facing baby wrap.\par \par \uc2 The last time children were in the spotlight during the first days of Congress was back in 2007. That`s when \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  became the first woman speaker. She appeared on Face the Nation right after that with our Bob Schieffer.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: Let me ask you about being the first woman speaker.\par \par \uc2 That was quite a scene. When you were sworn in, you called all the kids, your grandkids, the members` kids to come up to the rostrum with you.\par \par \uc2 Did you mean that to be a signal of a different kind of House?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): Well, certainly, a woman speaker, it represents change. And I have said it over and over in the campaign, that I will receive the gavel, the speaker`s gavel, from the hands of the special interests and into the hands of America`s children.\par \par \uc2 I was so thrilled. The children weren`t shy. They all came running up. And I want our members to know that every action that we take in the Congress, we must do with an eye to our children and their future. We owe that to them.\par \par \uc2 BOB SCHIEFFER: Do you believe that, as a woman, the first woman, that you will be held to a higher standard?\par \par \uc2 I have heard some people go so far as to say, if she`s not successful, it will have an impact on candidates in the 2008 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Well, let me remove all doubt. I have absolutely no intention of not being successful.\par \par \uc2 I have -- I`m ready for this job. The Democrats in general are ready to lead, prepared to govern, and determined to make the American people proud.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  has served in the House for more than 35 years, eight of those as speaker. And she`s back to being Congresswoman Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 As for little Hodge Gomez, he`s eligible to serve in another 24-and-a-half years.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you miss an episode of Face the Nation, you can listen to our podcast. Find us on \uc2 Amazon\uc2  Music or wherever you get your podcasts.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 President Biden travels to El Paso today after announcing new border enforcement actions last week. Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Veronica Escobar\uc2  will travel with the president to Texas later today.\par \par \uc2 I`m so glad you could join us.\par \par \uc2 REP. VERONICA ESCOBAR (D-TX): Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, the president is going to visit one of the busiest ports of entry in El Paso and he`s going to meet with officials and other aid organizations. What can he do to make sure that this three-hour visit is more than just a photo op?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Well, we`ve worked with the White House to make sure that all the folks who are actually doing the work on the ground day-to-day are the ones that the president will meet with. He needs to hear about how over time the challenges that we have faced as a country on immigration, on border issues, they have grown exponentially.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, we looked at the latest \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling. Only 38 percent of Americans actually approve of what the president is doing on immigration, 62 percent disapprove. This has consistently been one of his weakest issues. And it was just this last week he gave his very first speech on the border policy. For the first time he`s going today. And it`s taken, what, two years to find some kind of alternative to Title 42. I understand it`s going before the Supreme Court, but why is it taking so long to get just this?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: This challenge that we`re facing is so complex. I`m with you. I would have loved to have seen the administration lean in on immigration from day one because --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why didn`t they?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: You know, you`d have to ask them that.\par \par \uc2 The executive branch is not the only branch of government that needs to do its job, though. And - and I will tell you, I`ve worked very closely with Secretary Mayorkas. He has been phenomenal. Every idea that I have asked him to explore, he has. Every collaboration I`ve asked him to engage in, he has. Where - where it`s been -- where I`ve hit a brick wall is Congress. And, frankly, Democrats and Republicans alike.\par \par \uc2 I`m hoping things change. There`s a bipartisan delegation going to El Paso from the Senate tomorrow and I hope they see what the president will see today -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Which is opportunity, but also long overdue work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let`s talk about what the administration did just announce, because it was pretty big, this policy. They`re expanding the use of that same Title 42 policy, the pandemic-era restrictions that allow for expulsion without guarantee of asylum hearings. And it`s going to allow them to dramatically step up expulsions of Cubans, Nicaraguans, Haitians, Venezuelans. They make up the majority of those crossing.\par \par \uc2 You oppose Title 42. Does that mean you oppose what the Biden administration is doing?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Well, it`s a complicated answer. So, the - the administration, in the absence of any legislation from the - the Congress -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Has very few tools available. I -- I`m a staunch opponent of Title 42. In fact, I think Title 42 is the reason why those apprehension -- or encounter numbers are so high, because people kept trying over and over and over again. When you`re expelled from the border, you`re going to try in different areas.\par \par \uc2 I think it also helped fuel the human trafficking that we`ve seen a significant increase in. And all of this started with the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 When -- in my conversations with the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , because a Louisiana judge essentially created a situation where they have to continue to use Title 42, they can`t go to a different policy, which was used prior to Title 42 called Title 8.\par \par \uc2 Now, Title 8 is - is much more punitive. And I think that is what the administration is ultimately going to go towards once a judge allows the Biden administration to do away with Title 42.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which could come in June when the Supreme Court rules.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Could come in June. And - but even then, Margaret, we will not have had a legislative solution. So, all of these executive branch efforts really are just temporary band-aids. Whether it`s Title 8, whether it`s Title 42, we need to make sure that Congress acts.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. And it sounds like you don`t have a lot of high hopes for that kind of action.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask about the Biden policy. So it`s going to allow 30 - up to 30,000 migrants per month from the four countries we just listed to enter if they have a U.S. sponsor. It will also allow migrants to apply via cell phone app, something called CBP1.\par \par \uc2 Does someone fleeing abject poverty really have a cell phone to apply for access?\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: The vast majority of refugees who are fleeing their home countries do have cell phones. That`s how they communicate with one another. That`s how they communicate with their - the folks at home.\par \par \uc2 But I will tell you, we do need far more robust State Department involvement, especially for those who do not have access to that kind of technology. We need far greater education. Many of the refugees that I have spoken to, especially over the last couple of weeks, are - have no concept of what the asylum process is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Their idea is, I`m going to go to the border, I`m going to get a job and I`m - and I`m going to help my family. Something all of us would do, of course. So, there`s a lot of work that needs to be done, whether it`s assisting those who don`t have a cell phone access these services and legal pathways offered through CBP1, or whether it is helping to educate folks about those legal pathways so that they have a better shot.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I asked you that because I know some have argued it`s discriminatory against those who have the least.\par \par \uc2 Mexico, as part of this Biden agreement, has agreed to take about 30,000 people a month from the four countries, but one of the things that we`ve consistently heard over the past few years, and it was a harsh criticism of the Trump administration, is that when individuals are pushed back to Mexico, and they`re awaiting some kind of action, they face a lot of violence.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, if you criticized it during the Trump administration, you must be a critic during the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Absolutely. And that`s why I`m - I`m grateful that the president`s going to Mexico City to talk to the president. Every step of the journey along the way for these refugees, people who leave with little more than a backpack and their hopes and dreams experience a nightmarish journey along the way. And many migrants have told me, in fact, that the - the most dangerous part of the journey is in Mexico. And so there`s a lot of work that needs to be done.\par \par \uc2 But, Margaret, not just in Mexico, but for the entire hemisphere. We are seeing people flee their home countries, heading to Costa Rica, heading to - to countries all - all south of here. We - we have to make sure that diplomatically we are engaging at the highest levels. And, finally, giving the western hemisphere the kind of attention that it has lacked for a long time by - by the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congresswoman, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 VERONICA ESCOBAR: Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Turning now to the war in Ukraine. We are joined by Maine`s independent senator, Angus King, who just returned from a trip to Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator. I understand you -- you just got back.\par \par \uc2 SEN. ANGUS KING (I-ME): Last night.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Last night. And you met Friday with President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 What was the purpose of this visit?\par \par \uc2 ANGUS KING: I once did a visit overseas and an - and an old man in Pakistan, as a matter of fact, said, I know why you are here. And I said, why is that? And he said, because one of day of seeing is better than 100 days of reading. And I believe you got to see, you`ve got to be there and meet the people in person. And we had a very informative trip. There`s a lot to learn and a lot to be able to communicate back here about what`s going on in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, as you were over there, here, the White House announced it was released roughly $3 billion in security aid to Ukraine. It was the biggest, sort of one-time package. It included, for the first time, tank-busting armored vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Why such a surge now? Is it to get ahead of what may be a pretty serious spring offensive? What are we preparing for?\par \par \uc2 ANGUS KING: Well, there are -- there are - there are two factors. And - and the - the Ukrainians, particularly, the first thing is air defense. I mean they`re just being pummeled -- that`s too mild of a word -- in terms of their energy infrastructure. So, air defense is part of it, patriot missiles.\par \par \uc2 But also what`s going on in the east is essentially trench warfare. It`s almost World War I. It`s horrible. And these -- these armored vehicles are designed to deal with it -- that kind of situation -- to give the Ukrainians a fighting chance against this invasion that`s going on.\par \par \uc2 And to put it in perspective for Americans, it`s as if our East Coast, from Maine to Florida, and then west to Houston, Texas, was being occupied by a foreign power. That`s the entire eastern edge of Ukraine is occupied, and that`s where this fight is going to be. And that`s why these maneuver vehicles are going to be so important.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But this drawdown, which is what they call it technically, presidential authority, allows for an immediate or close to immediate delivery of some of this weaponry. And I am hearing increased concern here in the United States that sending weapons there is depleting U.S. stockpiles in a way that`s concerning to some officials.\par \par \uc2 How concerned are you about that? And how does -- how do you, in Congress, get ahead of that to make sure the U.S. isn`t hurt?\par \par \uc2 ANGUS KING: Well, part of it is replenishing our stockpiles. But you`re right, that`s - and that`s a decision that the - the Pentagon, the Defense Department, has to weigh when they`re doing these drawdowns.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, a lot of this equipment is coming from other countries. It`s not just coming from the United States. So, some of the fighting vehicles are coming from France. Patriot battery from Germany. So, that is an issue but it`s one that I know in the deliberations about what the drawdowns are going to look like, stockpiles is - is part of the issue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, and it also makes me think of the -- two of the conversations we just had in this program in terms of potential slash to defense spending that could potentially happen if this House proposal actually gets approved.\par \par \uc2 ANGUS KING: It - it would be -- it would be catastrophic to cut off aid to Ukraine at this point. I would just ask those people that are making those noises to read a little history. Google Sudetenland in 1938, Rhineland in 1936, when Hitler could have been stopped. When he wasn`t stopped, we ended up with 55 million deaths worldwide.\par \par \uc2 This is a moment where we know that Putin wants to take over Ukraine. He wants to rebuild the Soviet Union. Mia Angelou says, if someone tells you who they are, you should believe them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANGUS KING: And who he is, is a guy that wants to rebuild the Soviet empire. And this is a place where he can be stopped.\par \par \uc2 They -- the Ukrainians are fighting for us, for our values. The aid that we`re providing to them isn`t charity, it`s - it`s self-interest.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you believe there is enough accounting for what is being given? Is it --\par \par \uc2 ANGUS KING: I`m so - I`m so glad you asked that, because that was part of my mission. I spent a lot of time in Kyiv on accountability. And, in fact, sitting across from President Zelenskyy, just as you and I are, I asked him point blank, what`s the status of accountability? If there`s a scandal, it`s going to kill our ability to support you. He understands that.\par \par \uc2 And then later we had a meeting with many of their defense officials and privates, their finance people. They`re developing -- they`re working with \uc2 Deloitte\uc2 , the accounting firm, they`re working with SAP software, they`re accounting for every spare part that`s coming into the country.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we have an inspector general from the Pentagon who`s going over, I think, next month. I was very impressed by the level of accountability. And so this argument that somehow the money is being wasted, I don`t think holds water.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let me ask you about things here at home and the conversation about the U.S. border that we have been having.\par \par \uc2 There are Republicans who want to get something done in this new Congress. You`ve got Republican-held House. You`ve got a 51-49 Senate. Is there any possibility of getting legislation on immigration or anything?\par \par \uc2 ANGUS KING: To me, there`s an obvious deal on immigration, which is, heighten border security, which is very complicated. And I want to talk to the congresswoman that you just had on talking about what -- the ideas that she has. Republicans along the border, the Republican Congressman Gonzales. I want to learn. And - and there are a lot of people working on, how do we figure out the border.\par \par \uc2 But the deal is, increased border security, a path to citizenship for dreamers, and workforce. I`m hearing from businesses in Maine all the time about a shortage of workers. It`s one of our biggest economic problems after inflation. So, we`ve - we`ve got to work on legal immigration for workforce.\par \par \uc2 So, I think, as I say, there`s a package. And border security is where it starts. And I - I believe, I`m delighted the administration -- the president`s going down there today. They should have gone sooner, in my view. But let`s - let`s work on this because we - we can`t have the chaos and the humanitarian crisis that we have. Let`s - let`s find that. But then put together a package involving dreamers and involving workforce. That`s where I think we can move. And we ought to be able to move in a - in a bipartisan way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well -- well, you`re a centrist. You`re an independent. Though your caucus with Democrat. So, you can - you can say you want to work with everyone. But the politics right now are just so divisive. I read something you told "The New Yorker" last year where you said you have never been so worried about the future of the country because the structure of our system is at risk.\par \par \uc2 Do you still feel that way? Is it improving?\par \par \uc2 ANGUS KING: Well, what happened in the House this week wasn`t reassuring because what you had was 20 people -- I did the math, 3.7 percent of the membership of Congress held it hostage for a week. And I don`t know what -- what that foretells for the future. Yes, that is a concern.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, since I had that quote, which I certainly think has some validity, we`ve had one of the most productive congresses in the last 25 or 30 years. Almost all on a bipartisan basis. Not all. But the infrastructure bill, the CHIPS Act, the PACT Act for the veterans. All of those bills were done in -- on a bipartisan basis. And they started, interestingly, from the ground up. They started organically from a few members getting together, working from the middle out. The leadership blessed it. They allowed it to happen. But that`s the path. And I`m hoping -- I think immigration is a great opportunity to do the same thing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right. Well, we`ll end on that optimistic note.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 ANGUS KING: Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be back in a moment with Ukraine`s ambassador to the United States, \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We are joined by the Ukrainian ambassador to the United States, \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to the program. Always good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA (Ukrainian Ambassador to the United States): Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I understand you are just back from visiting your family in Ukraine. Over the holidays we saw Russia bombed hospitals on Christmas. On New Year`s they were also bombing your country. What is life like during the holiday season?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, it was - it was very good to be home. And, you know, when this invasion started on February 24th, until my first trip home in April, and it was right after the -- my hometown was liberated a couple of days, literally, before that, I always woke up thinking, is that a bad dream? Like, I always try to, you know, fight with the reality.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, going back always, on the one hand, gives you a good perspective of how horrible it is. And, on the other hand, inspires, because you see everyone not giving up. And everyone is -- even throughout the holiday seasons when the bombs are still flying everywhere, the destruction is enormous, people are very focused on winning. People are very focused on helping each other.\par \par \uc2 And there was still a festive spirit everywhere. People were singing. You know, people were trying to give gifts to each other. But, of course, the main focus is helping our brave defenders and doing everything possible to win.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We were just talking with Senator King about this massive package President Biden just signed off on, which also had sort of increase in the type of weapons and - and defense items for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Why do you need this so immediately? What are you preparing for?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, first of all, let me take this moment to thank the previous Congress for voting on the legislation and allowing us to have the possibility to have this packages this year for everything that we need from the financial assistance, but most importantly security assistance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That was the $45 billion.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Yes. Yes. And the fact that, you know, we have this support from American people is very valued in Ukraine. So, everyone, you know, president, Congress, big thank you.\par \par \uc2 Why do we need more? Because we see that, a, we need to increase the liberation speed of our - of our land. We need to free more people as soon as possible because we see what happens when they`re under occupation. We also do not intend to stop. We do not intend to take operational pauses, which Russia probably needs because they have problem with their motivation, they have problem with even the rockets. I mean they have so many of them. But they -- we would like to faster get to peace, which means we have to liberate our territories faster.\par \par \uc2 So, whatever we can get from air defense to all the armored vehicles to everything that you saw in the package, we need even more because we are facing much larger enemy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, this package of $45 billion from the last Congress should take you through next year according to some officials, U.S. officials.\par \par \uc2 When President Zelenskyy was here in Washington on that surprise visit, he did meet with Kevin McCarthy, who is now speaker of the House. Did you get assurances that U.S. support will continue with Republicans in control?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: We are fighting for the same values and for the same principles. We are fighting against the evil, which is a threat not only to Ukraine, it`s existential threat for us, but is a threat to everyone who believes in democracy.\par \par \uc2 And I can say that our meetings during the president`s visit here, both with president, administration, but also with all leaders in Senate and House of Representatives on a very strong bipartisan basis have been very constructive and very productive. I believe the American people support us and will support us until we together can win this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When the president was here, President Zelenskyy was here, he said, financial assistance is not charity, it`s an investment. And he said, Ukraine will be responsible with it.\par \par \uc2 But given the concerns about corruption over years past, how do you respond when you were asked that question of how do you guarantee every dollar being given to Ukraine is going to defense and not to, you know, embezzlement or something else?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: I can only quote Senator King, who was just here. First on -- there are three types of assistances, right, budget assistance, security and humanitarian.\par \par \uc2 On security, we`re not getting the money, we`re getting the goods. And we`re very grateful for them. And the accountability for every piece that we are getting is there. We are not only sharing implementation, we are implementing \uc2 NATO\uc2  systems in place and we are ready - and already are providing information. And we know where everything is located.\par \par \uc2 On the budget assistance, we have reported on all assistance that has been provided to us. There is an audit underway. But we have also sent - I mean it`s very clear where we are spending the money on. And our ministry of finance has reported even before the audit is complete, and I have shared that report with every member of the previous Congress. And we intend to do so in the future.\par \par \uc2 The inspector generals are involved already, from Pentagon but from other, from Department of State, from Treasury. So, we are not only open and ready, it`s in our interest as well. In order to prove why we need more and that we`re using it very effectively and efficiently, we would like to be as open as possible.\par \par \uc2 Every U.S. dollar that is given to us, we are putting it to a good use and we`re using it as an investment into our joint fight for democracy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about something we`ve talked about in the past, and that is what`s happening to Ukraine`s children. More than 13,000 were deported to Russia. Are you having any luck in getting your children back?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, first, unfortunately, we know the number is much larger than that. The 13,000 is the official number of the children we accounted for, we found. But we know we already have so many, you know, tens if not hundreds of thousands of people who we don`t know their fate yet. And until we liberate our territories, we will not know.\par \par \uc2 Out of the 13,000, the vice prime minister already informed that 212 have been returned. And 212 is, of course, a small number, even though, unlike Russians, every child and every life is very precious to us. So, we`re so happy that these 212 are home.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: But we will not get tired and we will not stop until all of them are back home.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: That`s why we need more weapons to liberate our territories and people.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ambassador, we`ll continue to follow this story and talk to you.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We have to go.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you can`t watch the full FACE THE NATION, you can set your DVR or we are available on demand. Plus, you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount Plus app and on our streaming network.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXIII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2023 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020230108ej1800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Are New Concerns About The State Of Mental Health Among Servicemen And Women In The Navy\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz, David Martin \par \uc2 494 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 December 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Tonight, there are new concerns about the state of mental health among servicemen and women in the Navy.\par \par \uc2 In the wake of four apparent suicides, CBS` David Martin spoke with one of the affected families.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Another outbreak of Navy suicides, four sailors listed as apparent suicides in less than a month, all assigned to the same command in Norfolk, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DECKER (Father): I am furious right now.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Robert Decker says his son Cody had been depressed because of a toxic leader in his previous command.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT DECKER: The military has got to wake up. They`ve got to change. I just want resources for these sailors and I want leadership.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: After the first two deaths, the Navy ordered a mental health stand down and brought in Kayla Arestivo, President of a nonprofit counseling service, who delivered a grim report to the Navy.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA ARESTIVO (Trails of Purpose): I had definitely made them aware of how inundated our clinical team was with the hopelessness that was happening at that Command and how many other people stepped forward and expressed that they also had suicidal ideation within the past year from being at that Command.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: The Command, which is assigned to repair ships on the East Coast includes 1,200 sailors, about 500 of whom are on limited duty status for various reasons, including pregnancy, injuries, and mental health problems.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA ARESTIVO: We know that these people have higher needs right now, they are in a higher stress situation.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: A third apparent suicide occurred the same day as the mental health stand down and a fourth this past weekend.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this year, after three crew members of the aircraft carrier, George Washington committed suicide in a single week, the Navy vowed a new focus on suicide prevention.\par \par \uc2 KAYLA ARESTIVO: I believe that it is Band-Aids over gaping bullet hole wounds. I think we`re slapping on a quick fix.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Each of these latest deaths is now under investigation by local police. And the Navy has launched a separate investigation to determine if there is any link or a common cause among them.\par \par \uc2 David Martin, CBS News, The Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Help is available for anyone in need 24 hours a day. Call 9- 8-8 to reach the National Suicide and Crisis Lifeline. It`s free and confidential.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020221212eic30002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For November 20, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,011 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Change is coming to Washington, but as both parties try to move past the chaos of campaigns `20 and `22, a certain former president is not on board with that.\par \par \uc2 It was a seismic, but not surprising announcement.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): The hour has come for a new generation to lead the Democratic Caucus.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s a changing of the guard, as Democrats move to the minority. That new generation is younger, more diverse and untested when it comes to facing a potential barrage of Republican-led investigations.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-California): They just give you one gavel with the power and the power of subpoena as well, and we`re going to use it.\par \par \uc2 (APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the Republican Party will have to deal with the fallout from Attorney General Merrick Garland`s decision to name an outside counsel to oversee investigations of former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MERRICK GARLAND\uc2  (U.S. Attorney General): Appointing a special counsel at this time is the right thing to do. The extraordinary circumstances presented here demanded it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will talk with a former Trump deputy attorney general, \uc2 Rod Rosenstein\uc2 , as well as House Judiciary Committee Democrat \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Plus, the former vice president opens up to us about January 6 and the big issue dividing him and the former president.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE (Former Vice President of the United States): The 2020 election was not stolen.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Finally, we will take a look at turmoil in the tech world, as \uc2 Twitter\uc2  teeters and crypto crashes.\par \par \uc2 Tech watchers Kara Swisher and Scott Galloway will join us for analysis.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 There is some breaking news overnight from Colorado, as we have learned that five people are dead and at least 18 others have been injured in a mass shooting at Club Q, an LGBTQ nightclub in Colorado Springs. Now, police say they do have the alleged shooter in custody, and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is assisting with the investigation.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with former Deputy Attorney General \uc2 Rod Rosenstein\uc2 . He appointed \uc2 Robert Mueller\uc2  as special counsel for the investigation into Russian interference in the 2016 election and to determine if there were links between that country and former President Trump`s campaign.\par \par \uc2 And he joins us in studio.\par \par \uc2 It is good to have you here, in an extraordinary week.\par \par \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN (Former U.S. Deputy Attorney General): Good morning. Glad to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to get right to it.\par \par \uc2 Due to the former president launching his campaign -- the current president may also run for president -- the attorney general said it is absolutely necessary to have a special counsel oversee this investigation into the classified documents found at Mar-a-Lago and what happened with trying to change the outcome of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 If you were in that old role you once had, would you have appointed a special counsel?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN\uc2 : You know, it`s easy to second-guess from outside the department.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know exactly what \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  knows, what information was available to him. He didn`t say that he was required to appoint the special counsel. He said that he thought it was the right thing to do. I believed, under the circumstances that I faced, that the appointment of \uc2 Robert Mueller\uc2  was the right thing to do with regard to the Russia investigation.\par \par \uc2 But I think, in this case, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  clearly made a discretionary decision. The department had been handling this itself for two years, could have continued to handle it itself. But he believed that this would help to promote public confidence.\par \par \uc2 I think it remains to be seen whether that`s the case.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you wouldn`t have done this, yourself?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN\uc2 : As I said, it`s it`s easy to second-guess from outside.\par \par \uc2 I think my inclination, given that the investigation had been going on for some time, and given the stage which they`ve reached, is that I probably would not have. But I just can`t tell from the outside.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, from where you sit, does the appointment of a special counsel indicate at least a willingness on Merrick Garland`s part to go ahead with a prosecution, or is that overreading the decision?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL ROD ROSENSTEIN: I think what it indicates is that, despite the fact the department has been at this for some time, almost two years on the January 6 investigation, close to a year on the Mar-a-Lago investigation, that they still believe that they have a viable potential case.\par \par \uc2 It doesn`t mean they made a decision to go forward, but it certainly is an indication they believe it`s a possibility.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Now, one case that`s been going on longer, the investigation into \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2 , which \uc2 CBS\uc2  has learned the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has gathered sufficient evidence to charge him with tax and gun-related crimes, and that is before the U.S. attorney in Delaware, David Weiss.\par \par \uc2 I believe you know him, since he was a Trump appointee. Can he independently oversee this, or do we need another special counsel?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN\uc2 : Well, yes, this investigation, as you said, has been going on for a very long time, which is not good for anybody.\par \par \uc2 You know, it promotes conspiracy theories and suspicions. So my hope is the department will make a decision in the near future about whether to go forward. And, hopefully, that decision will be accepted by the public. I do believe that the U.S. attorney in Delaware, you know, has the right experience to make that decision.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we can be confident that he`ll make the right decision in that case.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK, so not in that case.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you about the content of what is being scrutinized here with the former president. I know, when you were U.S. attorney in Maryland, you dealt with individuals who took classified material, sometimes top secret, SCI-level clearances, and kept it at home.\par \par \uc2 And you prosecuted them to the full extent of the law. Why should the president be any different?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN\uc2 : Well, you`re right. We did have a lot of federal agencies in Maryland. And so we had a number of cases that came up during my 12 years as U.S. attorney, both under President Obama and President Bush.\par \par \uc2 And we prosecuted those cases because we believed the facts justified it. Now, if the facts justify a prosecution of President Trump, I think the department will make that decision. But we just don`t know from the outside.\par \par \uc2 You know, there are extenuating circumstances when it`s the president, when there are a lot of staffers and lawyers involved. And so I think we have to wait to see how that all shakes out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Former Attorney General Barr sat with \uc2 PBS\uc2 , and this was right before Merrick Garland`s announcement. But he said that, to indict, the Justice Department needs to show Mr. Trump was consciously involved.\par \par \uc2 Let`s hear what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BARR\uc2  (Former U.S. Attorney General): I personally think that they probably have the basis for legitimately indicting the president. I don`t know. I`m speculating.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET HOOVER, \uc2 PBS\uc2 : You`re speculating, yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER ATTORNEY GENERAL BARR: But -- but, given what`s gone on, I think they probably have the evidence that would check the box. They have the case.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN\uc2 : Well, I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 I think the -- Attorney General Barr, that is -- mentioned later in that interview that he was speculating. And I think it`s -- you know, there are multiple levels of issues that the department needs to consider, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 Number one is, is the evidence sufficient to obtain and sustain a conviction? Number two is, is it an appropriate use of federal resources to bring that case? And a case against a former president, obviously, would be extraordinary, would raise unique concerns.\par \par \uc2 And so I would hope that \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and his team would be very careful about scrutinizing that evidence, not just checking the box, but making sure that they`re prepared to stand behind the decision that they make.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, when you say sustain a conviction, what do you mean by that? Does that mean looking at the courts that are likely to prosecute? I mean, where would you prosecute this case, Florida or Washington, D.C.?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN\uc2 : Well, it means ensuring that, number one, you will get past a jury, that is, being able to persuade 12 random citizens that your case proves the defendant`s guilt beyond a reasonable doubt, number two, that it will be sustained or upheld on appeal.\par \par \uc2 You know, the department sometimes brings cases in which they use novel theories that prevail in district court, but are overruled on appeal. If they`re to bring a case against a former president, you would want to make sure they had a -- a lock-solid case and they were confident both of conviction and of prevailing on any appeal.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that there wouldn`t be some national security implication, such as political violence?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN\uc2 : Well, you know, that`s -- and that`s a difficult issue, Margaret, as to whether or not the attorney general should consider the -- the potential for public unrest if they were to bring a case against the president.\par \par \uc2 But I...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It has to be considered.\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL ROD ROSENSTEIN: I -- I think it highlights the importance of the department ensuring that they have a solid case, that is, that they`re going to win a conviction and they`re going to be able to sustain an appeal.\par \par \uc2 The circumstances, the stakes are higher than in an ordinary case. You need to make sure, if you bring that case, that you can persuade people that it`s meritorious and that you deserve to win.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that gets at the fundamentals, the distrust of institution and where we are at these days.\par \par \uc2 But the former president has already said he`s not going to comply with any investigations. He said that on Friday. So, what does this mean for the timeline? Are we running right into the 2024 presidential campaign?\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL ROD ROSENSTEIN: I`m concerned about -- about the timing.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the new special counsel, Jack Smith, needs to get up to speed in the case. He`s not even in the U.S., so he needs to come back and get engaged and supervise his team. He may need to bring in additional team members, people he trusts to review the circumstances.\par \par \uc2 And then there are other potential delays as well. You know, one of the downsides of appointing a special counsel is the possibility of litigation over the validity of the appointment of the special counsel. And that has always been upheld by the courts. But litigation can impose additional delays.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there`s a fair chance that this is going to drag in -- well into the campaign season.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And then the question of whether the candidate wins or not.\par \par \uc2 Rod Rosenstein\uc2 , thank you for your insight and for joining us today.\par \par \uc2 FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ATTORNEY GENERAL \uc2 ROD ROSENSTEIN\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Before the appointment of a special counsel was announced last week, we spoke with former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  about his new book, "So Help Me God."\par \par \uc2 In our wide-ranging conversation, Mr. Pence details extensively his story, up to and including the January 6 attack.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: The president`s words and actions in and around January 6 were reckless.\par \par \uc2 The tweet that he issued the day that I was in the loading dock before -- below the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  endangered my family and endangered people that were in the Capitol and was indefensible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why did it take you two years to talk about your anger? Weren`t you incandescent with rage that your family was put at risk like that?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Margaret, I was angry that day and many days since.\par \par \uc2 But, on January 6, I have to tell you that I had to put that aside. The president had decided to be a part of the problem. I was determined to be a part of the solution, to work with leaders in Congress, leaders at the Pentagon, leaders in law enforcement to do our part to finish our work under the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You were calling and trying to get the National Guard to come in and restore order.\par \par \uc2 Did you feel you had to do that because the commander in chief was derelict in his duties?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Margaret, I didn`t know what the president was doing at the time. I wasn`t at the White House. I had no contact with the president or the White House that day.\par \par \uc2 When I spoke to the congressional leaders in our first conference call, they informed me that they were getting mixed messages from security personnel. And I asked them if they wanted me to get involved. And they did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you think President Trump needs to be held responsible in his events -- in the events that led to January 6 and the violence of that day?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, I think everyone that perpetrated the violence at the Capitol needs to be held to the strictest account of the law.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what about those who fed it? What about those who gave it oxygen, the lie oxygen to mislead people?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: I`m confident that the American people will hold all those responsible, at the end of the day, and history will be their judge.\par \par \uc2 I mean, in -- in my book, I -- what I have tried to do is share a candid story about the evolution of that controversy. The president and I have developed a ...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You do. You say the president came to you at least five times. You lay it out in detail leading up to January 6.\par \par \uc2 And it`s almost like you couldn`t believe this, because you kept telling him over and over: This is not legal. This is not constitutional.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: I did. Many people did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you look back and say, I wasn`t forceful enough?\par \par \uc2 I mean, what could you have done differently?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, I did tell the president many times that, after he exhausted every legal challenge that the campaign had every right to pursue, that he should simply accept the results.\par \par \uc2 The president was hearing from a cadre of attorneys who, frankly, should never been let on the White House grounds, let alone in the Oval Office, telling him what, as the Bible says, his itching ears wanted to hear.\par \par \uc2 My hope was that, at the end of the day, he would come around. I remember, on the night of January 4, we had a meeting with the president and part of that legal team in the Oval Office. The president left on the helicopter. There were no harsh words between us, but he was continuing to make his case, and I was continuing to make my position clear.\par \par \uc2 But at his rally in Georgia, which I watched on television, the president actually opened up the rally by speaking about me:\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): I hope that our great vice president, our great vice president comes through for us. He`s a great guy.\par \par \uc2 Of course, if he doesn`t come through, I won`t like him quite as much.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: But then he paused and he said to the crowd, now -- now, one thing you know about \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is, he always plays it straight.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And then he called you the next day.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: But I remember, in that moment, thinking, he might be coming around.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But he wasn`t.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: My continued hope was that, at the end of the day, he would recognize what our duty was on that day, as the presiding officer under the Constitution to oversee the count of the electoral vote of an election that we lost.\par \par \uc2 But -- but it was not to be.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Will you answer questions about that day before Congress?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Congress has no right to my testimony.\par \par \uc2 We have a separation of powers under the Constitution of the United States. And I believe it would establish a terrible precedent for the Congress to summon a vice president of the United States to speak about deliberations that took place at the White House. And...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you`re -- you`re closing the door on that entirely?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: I am closing the door on that, and -- but I must say, again, the partisan nature of the January 6 Committee has been a disappointment to me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The lawyers and the chief of staff to the president at the time, the -- Mark Meadows, who let those lawyers you said had no business on the White House grounds, you think, no consequence, only prosecute the people who actually physically went to the Capitol?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: There are those that are speaking in defense of people that rioted at the Capitol and created the conditions where lives were lost.\par \par \uc2 But I believe everyone that was rioting in the Capitol that day and perpetrating violence needs to be held to the strictest account of the law.\par \par \uc2 But I do believe that, at the end of the day, the American people will hold accountable those that permitted the circumstances around which January 6 was able to flourish into violence that day.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You also think the \uc2 FBI\uc2  executing a search warrant to take classified material from the former president`s home was not the way the Justice Department should have handled it.\par \par \uc2 But, to be clear, were you ever personally concerned about Mr. Trump`s handling of classified information?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: I don`t recall ever being concerned about the president or anyone on our administration`s handling of classified information. I -- at least among the senior staff, of which I had regular contact.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, if he had, he would be prosecuted, you would think.\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, let me say, you know, no one is above the law.\par \par \uc2 But as someone that served on the Judiciary Committee for more than 10 years, having oversight over the Justice Department, I just think there were many better ways to obtain those classified materials from Mar-a-Lago than to execute a search warrant against a former president of the United States of America, something that had never happened in American history, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And never been prosecuted. Do you think that that should happen?\par \par \uc2 FORMER VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: Well, look -- well, I -- my -- my hope is that the Justice Department will think very carefully about next steps.\par \par \uc2 This is a very divided time in the life of our nation. I think our nation needs to heal. But the idea of executing a search warrant against a former president of the United States sent the wrong message to the American people and, frankly, sent the wrong message to the wider world that looks at the United States of America as the standard.\par \par \uc2 And that was my disappointment in the decision to execute a search warrant.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And there will be more of our conversation with the former vice president just ahead.\par \par \uc2 But we turn now to California Congresswoman \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2 . She serves on the Judiciary Committee and the Select Committee investigating January 6.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 I want to get straight to it. Does the refusal of the vice president and the former president to comply with your investigation in any way impede the impact or outcome?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN (D-California): Well, we wish they had come in.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, other presidents have come in when asked by the Congress, including Gerald Ford, Teddy Roosevelt, many others. It is almost Thanksgiving, and the committee turns into a pumpkin at the end of December. So, we don`t have time to litigate this.\par \par \uc2 But I think they`ve cheated history, and they should have done otherwise.\par \par \uc2 We, on the other hand, have received substantial information from other sources. And we`re in the process of, as I`m sure you know, writing our report now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you`re continuing to gather information, as I understand it, speaking to two Secret Service officials recently.\par \par \uc2 What more do you need? And are you still sharing that information with the Justice Department?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: Well, we`re not sharing information with the Justice Department. We`re doing our own investigation.\par \par \uc2 However, we anticipate, when our report is released, to release all of the evidence that we have assembled, so the public can see it, including the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 But you have -- I understand the committee has released documents to the Department of Justice. Is that not the case?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: Well, we`re not -- no, we`re -- we`re -- we`re doing our own investigation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: And within a month, they -- the public will have everything that we`ve found, all the evidence, for good or ill.\par \par \uc2 And I think we`ve, as we`ve shown in our hearings...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: ... made a compelling presentation that the former president was at the center of the effort to overturn a duly elected election, assembled the mob, sent it over to Congress to try and interfere with the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 It`s pretty shocking.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, as we know, the Justice Department has its own investigation. And that`s what led us to the attorney general making news just a few days ago with this special counsel to take up the events surrounding January 6.\par \par \uc2 But what does putting this in the hands of a special counsel accomplish here? Do you think it actually removes politics? Or does it still just keep it there, since the attorney general will still have oversight of the special counsel?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: Well, I think, from what the attorney general said, he sought to depoliticize this investigation.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, career professionals are doing it, and to have a special counsel overseeing it. But, you know, the right wing never fails. Up is down, and down is up. The effort to depoliticize, they are now criticizing as somehow a political measure.\par \par \uc2 So, the effort to segregate the investigation from the attorney general himself is in the eye of the beholder. And, of course, the former president is saying he won`t partake, as if, you know, it`s a -- it`s a slice of pizza. I mean, it`s not up to him. He is being investigated for these offenses. And we`ll see what they find.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You sit on the Judiciary Committee. You just heard \uc2 Rod Rosenstein\uc2  say that he thinks the U.S. attorney in Delaware is sufficient in terms of being able to independently decide on what to do with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  and that case.\par \par \uc2 I wonder if you agree with that, or if you think your Republican colleagues are right to ask for a special counsel to deal with the current president`s son?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: Well, I don`t know anything about that case. Certainly, in the case of the...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you have oversight of the Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Yes, but we don`t -- I served with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  on the Judiciary Committee. We don`t oversee and interfere with individual investigations and cases. That would be improper in terms of oversight.\par \par \uc2 You know, if -- if the president`s son has committed offenses, then there`ll be a judgment on whether to prosecute or not. And that`s the rule of law, just as the rule of law applies to the former president. People in this country have to adhere to the law. And, if you don`t, if you commit an offense, and the facts are there, then there`ll be a prosecution.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what it`s about, living in a country where the rule of law, not just politics, leads us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: That`s about our democratic republic.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, the issue of what to do with \uc2 Hunter Biden\uc2  will come before your committee as the chair...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: No.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... the incoming chair of it has said, along with the head of Oversight. They want to lead investigations.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: Well...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: ... they`re going to find...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. So...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: There`s nothing for us -- there`s no role for the legislative body in a prosecution, not...\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No. Understood.\par \par \uc2 But are you prepared, as Democrats, for this knife fight?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: Well, I mean, we`re going to be there.\par \par \uc2 And the incoming Judiciary Committee chair has a history of playing a little and loose with the truth. We`re aware of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE ZOE LOFGREN: And we will be there as truth-sayers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be watching.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be back in a minute.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you can`t watch the full Face the Nation, you can set your DVR or watch on our CBS News Streaming Network throughout the day on Sundays.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We also spoke with former Vice President Pence about the former president`s entrance into the `24 presidential campaign and the prospects for his own run.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, (Former U.S. Vice President): I look forward to being involved in the process in some way. You know, whether we are in the debate as a candidate or in the debate as simply as an active Republican, I look forward to getting behind the Republican cause and supporting candidates around the country, as well as our nominee to get this country turned back to the policies that will make us strong and prosperous again.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That sounds like you`d prefer he`s not the nominee, but you didn`t say no.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, I - I just think there will be better choices. I think now calls for a different time. I think now - I think the American people want to see us move forward.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You talk about moving forward. The idea of relitigating the 2020 election continues to circulate, as you know that, amongst members of your party. Do you think continuing to push these claims, as the former president does, is a direct threat?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: The 2020 election was not stolen. We have a process in this country where states conduct elections. Questions of irregularities of fraud are then adjudicated in the courts. The states then certify electoral votes. And as we did on January 6th. But I do think there`s been far too much talk questioning the integrity of our elections.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: According to a \uc2 CBS\uc2  tally, there are going to be 156 members of Congress that will be sworn in, in January, who continue to raise questions about the validity of the 2020 election. That`s more than back in 2020. Isn`t that a risk? Doesn`t the party need to stop that?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: There`s a First Amendment in this country that people can hold the opinions that they hold, even if I disagree with them. But I have every confidence that the new Republican leadership in the Congress, the new Republican majority, when \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  hands the gavel to Kevin McCarthy, is going to draw the lessons from the midterm campaign, which for me give evidence of the fact that -- that the American people want the Republican Party, and frankly all of our leaders, to be focused on the future.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Did it surprise you then that Republicans didn`t end up with a larger margin?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I was surprised. I was disappointed at the outcome of the election. As you look around, what happened on Election Day, candidates that were focused on the future, candidates that were focused on the issues that the American people are focused on, did quite well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re very proud of the Supreme Court justices put on the court who just recently struck down Roe versus Wade. Do you think the decision on abortion access should stay in the states or should there be a national law banning it?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: From very early on in my public career, I - I was determined to be a champion for life. I just always purposed to advance the sanctity of life. And I always believed that Roe v. Wade would be sent to the ash heap of history. It`s - you know, it`s interesting that \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  even said - who was one of the great Supreme Court justices of our time --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, she questioned the - the legal basis not being solid enough.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: She questioned the legal foundation of it, and - and she was right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what -\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: But from my perspective --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But is - but doesn`t what you`re talking about, the moral imperative -\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Does that Trump states` rights?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, the Dobbs decision really gave the country a new beginning for life. And it did return the question of abortion to the states and the American people. Many alarmists on the American left in the beginning spoke about it banning abortion and taking away their right. And, actually, I think most Americans figured out pretty quickly that, in fact, this question that bears so deeply on the - the life of the nation has simply been returned to the people and their elected representatives.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So no national (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: For my part, I -- I will tell you that I will always support efforts to strengthen protections for the unborn. I think it`s most likely that it will be resolved at the state level. But the 15-week legislation in the Congress, had I been a member of Congress, I - I would have supported because I think it actually -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s not too liberal for you because it would allow access up to 15 weeks of pregnancy?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s where the majority of abortions are performed.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: I would have supported it as a beginning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Rubio/Graham bill, the 15 weeks you`re talking about, also has exceptions for life of the mother after the 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Right. I supported those exceptions during my public career.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You do?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: But, Margaret, I do believe it`s more likely this is going to be resolved at the state level. It may take as long to restore the sanctity of life to the center of American law in all 50 states as it did to overturn Roe versus Wade. But people that know me and my family know that so long as we live, we`ll seek to be about the business of life in this country and doing our part to support the sanctity of life.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I was - I was interested to read that you and your wife, Karen, underwent IVF therapy.\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: We did.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which is a lot to go through. And there are people who are concerned that if you start with abortion access restrictions, that it will also lead to restrictions on IVF treatment. If you believe life begins at conception, you can make that argument. Should it be protected as a right?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Oh, I -- Karen and I struggled for more than five years with unexplained infertility. And, in fact, I`ll never forget the day that I called home, driving off to a work appointment, and Karen answered the phone and said, happy Father`s Day. And our son would come along, then a daughter, then another daughter, all within three years. And we were busy but joyful.\par \par \uc2 And in the midst of all of that, we - we also received word that we had -- we had made the list for an adoption for a young woman with an unexpected pregnancy. But we wanted to find out if the second family on the list was clinically infertile. And when we did, we stepped aside, not wanting to prevent them from having the joy of a little one in their home.\par \par \uc2 But I fully support fertility treatments and I think they deserve the protection of the law.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What about same-sex marriage? A number of Republicans are getting on board, federal protections for it. Do you believe that you need to consider that when you talk about compassion?\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: Well, as a - as a Bible-believing Christian, I`ll always hold the view that marriage is between one man and one woman. I think it was ordained by God. And that will always be my values. But the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  has ruled on this in the Obergefell case. And, you know, we --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you don`t think a federal law is needed like a number of Republicans are now --\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE: We can - we can disagree with Supreme Court decisions, but we can`t disobey them. I respect the pronouncements of the court. And I actually think it`s just as important, as we go forward as a nation, that we make it clear that we don`t believe in discrimination against anyone because of who they are, who they love or what they believe.\par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, I think we need to make sure that we protect the religious freedom of every American that`s enshrined in the Constitution, the ability to live, to work, to worship in a manner accordingly that dictates to your conscious.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Our full interview can be seen on our website at facethenation.com and on our \uc2 YouTube\uc2  page.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This weekend there is uncertainty about \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s future under its new owner, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For more we are joined by tech journalist Kara Swisher, host of "On with Kara Swisher," and, from London, Scott Galloway, who is her co-host on the podcast "Pivot" and also a professor at NYU.\par \par \uc2 It`s good to have you both here.\par \par \uc2 Big weekend.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER, (Host, "On with Kara Swisher" Podcast and Co-Host, "Pivot" Podcast): Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Last night \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , Kara, put \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the former president, back on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He had been suspended after January 6th.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Permanently.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And he went and he founded his own social media company, Truth Social. And according to filings, he has a six-hour exclusive. He can only post there, not on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So what does lifting the ban on his use of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  actually do?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Nothing. I mean he`s been posting -- people have been posting his - his Truths, I think that`s what they`re called, on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  already and it gets out anyway. So, he has - you know, he`s got these contractual obligations to this company that is not doing great. And I think he can`t resist, and he`ll probably go full \uc2 Twitter\uc2  at some point. But I don`t think it makes any difference. Plus, he`s the former president, so it doesn`t quite -- hit quite as hard. So, I think the juice is a little bit out of his power on that platform. We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, he`s also, Scott, a presidential candidate, as we learned just a few days ago. He was kicked off of \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 YouTube\uc2  after January 6th. Do you think this - this opens the floodgates, or is this just a gimmick by \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  for PR?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GALLOWAY (Professor, \uc2 New York University\uc2  and Co-Host, "Pivot" Podcast): I think it`s mostly the latter. I think if Elon`s out of the news for more than 48 hours, he`ll decide to kick him off again. He said that people had spoken in Latin. I found that this poll - you know, Elon Musk`s polls on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  are more for support than illumination. He ran a similar poll to see whether or not he should sell \uc2 Tesla\uc2  stock and it - it ended up he`d already filed to sell those shares. So, I think these polls are mostly a gimmick and I would argue the people haven`t spoken, the GRU has spoken. These -- \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has become --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Russian intelligence, you mean?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GALLOWAY: One hundred percent. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has become a playground for bad actors and fake bots. This poll is meaningless. This decision is meaningless.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you had predicted earlier that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  could collapse. Just -- we`ve seen thousands of employees either get fired or walk off the job. Do you - do you stand by that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GALLOWAY: Well, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  will survive. And even if it doesn`t, let`s keep in mind, it`s not a national treasure. We would all be just fine if \uc2 Twitter\uc2  went away.\par \par \uc2 But I think when you see this sort of wholesale firing and the way in which he`s gone about firing that creates this type of resentment, I easily see this site going down. I - I think we should do a head of lettuce test versus when the site goes down.\par \par \uc2 What we`re not talking about is the knock-on effects at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . You know, when you buy a \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , you`re buying a pair of Air Jordans. You`re associating directly with an individual. And no individual, maybe with the exception of Trump or Putin, has eroded more of their good will and brand equity than \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . And then you go to \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , where everyone who works for a government, and this is a government contractor, has a responsibility to termination (ph) form they have to fill out. And I think he`s antagonized enough officials that we`re going to start seeing more scrutiny on \uc2 SpaceX\uc2  contracts.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I mean, there was already talk for national security purposes -\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To review that because of foreign ownership.\par \par \uc2 Kara, for - for users at home, what does this actually mean? Like, what should we do to protect ourselves? Is there anything to protect ourselves against?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Well, you know, collapsing -- \uc2 Twitter\uc2  used to go down all the time with the fail whale. It was very common. And they`re - you`re going to see more of that. I`m already seeing issues on the platform because of fewer people. And that`s - like, you can`t download your archive very easily. If you - if you sign off and you have two-factor authentication, good luck getting on and stuff like that. You`re going to see around the edges.\par \par \uc2 There may be -- the problem is, if he starts pushing out big changes, you`re going to see problems. If he starts doing lots of things, you`re going to see problems. If one coder makes a mistake, you could see problems. And that`s what it is.\par \par \uc2 What everyone`s going to do, Margaret, I know it`s hard for us media people and politicians to understand, but it`s not very big anywhere else. It`s a very small service compared to the Facebooks, the Instagrams and the TikToks of the world. And so I think it`s not going to matter to most people. It`s going to matter to the chattering class who enjoys dunking on each other throughout the day, essentially.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I mean, it has become, in some ways, a news wire almost --\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For government announcements, things like that.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Scott, I also want to ask you about this stunning fall we saw this week of - of the founder of FTX, this cryptocurrency exchange. Thirty-year-old billionaire \uc2 Sam Bankman\uc2 -Fried, top Democratic donor. He`s known in this town. Justice Department, SEC, CFTC are all now investigating. What exactly happened here?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GALLOWAY: Well, on a very broad level, we don`t -- as a species, we can`t help but to worship people. And in the U.S. we`ve decided to worship tech billionaires. And they don`t fall under the same scrutiny, including a 30-year-old MIT graduate who we`re all hoping is kind of the next Jesus and doesn`t need a board of directors or doesn`t need any sort of regulatory scrutiny. And as a result, in about 24 hours, you had the immolation of $34 billion in capital. And that is literally take a medium size city and everyone in that city on Tuesday has $10,000 and the next day they have zero.\par \par \uc2 So, this will bring unwarranted scrutiny. It`s more spectacle than significant because the entire crypto market now is $800 billion and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has shed over $1 trillion. When the whole FTX debacle was unwinding, the markets were actually up. So it makes for interesting news.\par \par \uc2 And unlike the great financial recession where we saw huge damage to the economy and no one go to jail, I think this is going to be the opposite. I don`t think it`s going to have a very big impact on the economy, but I think you`re going to see some perp walks here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Some arrests. I mean the Treasury secretary was asked by our Nancy Cordes about this, and she said, the absence of appropriate supervision and regulation of digital assets contributed to the collapse.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean that`s saying essentially, we were asleep at the wheel as a \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Well, this is early and they - they haven`t done much regulation on the regular internet. And that`s 20 years hence (ph) essentially. And so they were just starting to come up with ideas between the SEC and the CTFC of how to regulate all this stuff. And Bankman-Fried was part of it. He was consulted quite a bit in the -- a lot of people were. And he was calling for regulation, if you recall, which is somewhat ironic. But there hadn`t -- they hadn`t figured out how to deal with this very new and sort of explosive new financial instrument. And I think that`s more the problem is Washington moves at a glacial pace around these things. They were moving faster than they have in other areas, but they certainly hadn`t figured out. But, ultimately, this is going to be, you know, some sort of fraud case or something. Listen, I agree with Scott, someone`s going to jail.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Scott, there are also layoffs, not just at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  but we saw some at \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , we saw job cuts at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 . You`ve called this a Patagonia vest recession. What does that mean, exactly?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GALLOWAY: Well, most recessions were either labeled white or blue collar, meaning that they typically impact one cohort more than the other. Either executives, white color or blue class, sort of the working class.\par \par \uc2 This is the Patagonia vest recession. And that is, we`re going to see more people laid off in the growthy part of our economy that has sort of been the gift that keeps on giving for 13 years. But again, Margaret, I think this is more noise than news because 99.99 percent of the people on the planet would pray to be recently laid off person from \uc2 Meta\uc2  or \uc2 Google\uc2 . If you`ve been recently laid off from one of these firms, it means that you probably went to an elite school, you live in a city where the growth prospects or the economy is growing like crazy, and you are -- your biggest problem is not going to be what to do, but what not to do. So sort of a --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what if you`re an H-1B visa recipient and you get sent back home suddenly.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GALLOWAY: Fair point.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Fair point. I mean you saw the picture.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GALLOWAY: That`s a fair point.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: You saw that picture from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . I mean everywhere (ph). Elon put some -- another performative photo op of them doing coding. I`m not sure what they were doing. But there were people there that were clearly from other countries. And so they are stuck there. They are kind of -- they can`t leave. And I think that`s one of the issues you`re going to have for all these people is, where do they go?\par \par \uc2 A lot of companies are taking care of it (ph). I think \uc2 Facebook\uc2  was one of them. Or \uc2 Meta\uc2  was doing that. So they`ll try to figure that out. But Scott`s 100 percent right, these are people who have lots of options.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: And there will be more jobs for them comparatively. And I will note that Scott does himself have a Patagonia vest and he`s doing just fine. So --\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GALLOWAY: Never. Never.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, you do, I`ve seen it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, next time you`ve got to wear it, Scott.\par \par \uc2 Thank you. Good to see you. Scott`s joining us there from London.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, Kara, good to have you here in studio.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back with the mayor-elect of Los Angeles, Karen Bass.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to Democratic Congresswoman Karen Bass, who made history last week when she became the first woman and the first black woman to be elected mayor of Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 REP. KAREN BASS (D-CA and Mayor-elect, Los Angeles): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You are going to be sworn in as mayor of L.A. December the 12th. You`ve got a lot of work cut out for you.\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I know you campaigned hard on the -\par \par \uc2 KAREN: BASS: I certainly do.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You did, you campaigned hard on the issue of crime and homelessness. And I want to get right into that.\par \par \uc2 Homicide`s up 14 percent from two years ago. Robberies up almost 16 percent in L.A. over that same time. Do you plan to keep the current police chief?\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS: Yes. There`s no desire on my part to remove the current police chief. You know, we have a crisis in our city with homelessness as well. Forty thousand people are asleep in tents all throughout our city. And four or five of them pass away every morning. And so we have multiple crises right now. And so it`s my intent on day one to address that issue. And it`s my understanding that the chief of police`s contract is up at the end of next year.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you could revisit it at that time, in other words.\par \par \uc2 But I -\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS: Right, revisit that, along with many other general managers as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 When I ask about your approach to policing, I`m mindful that you`re in a pretty unique position because you worked on this in Congress in putting forward the George Floyd Policing Act. In your public safety plan for L.A., you talk about hiring more officers.\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I know in the past you`ve said that defund the police was one of the worst slogans ever.\par \par \uc2 How do you bring all that to changing policing in L.A.? What specifically do you do that`s different?\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS: Well, first of all, I think what`s most important about police reform, whether we`re talking in Los Angeles or anyplace else, is accountability and transparency. Those are two things that are critical.\par \par \uc2 In terms of hiring police officers, we`ve had several hundred police officers retire or move on for other reasons. And what I am proposing is that we replace the ones that the city has allotted for. In other words, bringing the police department up to its full force that is budgeted.\par \par \uc2 The other thing is, is that in many communities they want to see an increased police presence. And so I am calling for moving officers off of administrative duty and putting them on the streets. That`s the way we can hire them as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 But, in addition to that, I believe in obviously stopping crime when it occurs, but doubling down and tripling down in communities where crime prevention strategies and different approaches are required. And I`ve worked on this for a number of years. And so I want to fully fund programs to prevent crime, to intervene, especially with young people.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that our current chief of police said is that he accounted for a spike in crime, especially post-restrictions of the pandemic, because many of the crime prevention programs shut down due to Covid.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You disclosed back in September that - that you yourself were a victim of burglary at your home. And you had two guns stolen at that time. And I wonder, did you have those guns because you felt unsafe in your own home? And did you go out and purchase new weapons?\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS: I have not purchased new weapons. I had two handguns. I had owned them for a very, very, very long time. And I certainly believe in an individual`s right to legally possess guns and properly store them. Mine were legally purchased and properly stored.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you wouldn`t encourage people to feel that they have to arm themselves to defend their own homes?\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS: I - I certainly hope - and, you know, one of the things that happened in the pandemic, not just in Los Angeles, but all across the country, was an increase in gun purchasing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS: One of the reasons why we`ve had an increase in crime in Los Angeles is because of ghost guns. And ghost guns absolutely need to be cracked down on. And that`s something that will be a focus of mine as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What is the long-term solution to the homelessness and housing crisis? Because housing affordability is a pretty big problem in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 KAREN BASS: Absolutely right. Los Angeles has become unaffordable. You have to have a comprehensive approach. There`s no magic bullet. So, first and foremost, you have to prevent people from falling into homelessness. And clearly affordability is key to that.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, people are on the streets for a variety of issues. And you have to address why they`re there. Is it substance abuse? Is it mental illness? Is it just straight-up affordability? We have people who are in tents who actually work full-time. We have thousands of children who are in tents, some with mothers who fled domestic violence, some who are teenagers who aged out of foster care, some people who were formerly incarcerated because they are not able to find housing are in tents. So, we have to have a comprehensive approach and address the reasons why people are unhoused.\par \par \uc2 But, first and foremost, we have to get people off the streets. People are literally dying on the streets in Los Angeles. And this has got to stop.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we will be watching, Mayor-elect, how you do just that. It`s a - it`s a tall order.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you all for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020221120eibk00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Some Serious Health Battles And Literally Have The Scars To Prove It\b0\par\par\uc2 Jeff Glor, Dana Jacobson \par \uc2 1,528 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JEFF GLOR: Last month at the Military Women`s Memorial in D.C., 49 female veterans all breast cancer survivors had their portraits hung in solidarity, proudly displaying the scars of mastectomy surgeries that would normally be hidden.\par \par \uc2 But these 49 are far from alone. A 2009 Pentagon study found that women in the Armed Forces are 20 percent to 40 percent more likely to be diagnosed with breast cancer than their civilian counterparts. Bringing that issue into focus is one professional photographer who`s made it her mission to put a face on the disease.\par \par \uc2 To further express that point, this story will include portraits showing survivors bare chested, images as raw as the emotions behind them.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS (Photographer): Hold yourself up tall, hold your chin up high.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON (voiceover): Using more than just her lens to draw out her subjects, photographer, Charise Isis--\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: And lift your chin up.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: --captures their spirit.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: Beautiful.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Creating transformational images of breast cancer survivors who`ve lost one or both of their breasts to the disease.\par \par \uc2 It is unbelievable how open they are with you with that vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: I think they see the pictures that I`ve been doing and they see these women standing in their power, and their beauty, and they -- they`re like, I want to portrait of me like that.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: The photographs which total more than 600 to date are part of a collection that`s initial idea can be traced back to one woman who had undergone a mastectomy but still posed topless for Charise in a boudoir photoshoot.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: She said, "I have a confession to make. I`m a 12-year breast cancer survivor. I feel mutilated under my clothing. I`ve never shown anyone my scars." In that moment, I`m behind the camera like I had goosebumps. I`m thinking to myself, wow, I`m witnessing somebody let go of like 12 years of shame.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: What was it like to be a part of that?\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: It felt magical. I mean, it was as cathartic for me as it was for her. And I realized that I needed to put something out there that put a face on breast cancer that showed some beauty and courage and grace.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: In 2009, the aptly named Grace Project officially began. Charise collecting stories and taking pictures of women who`ve undergone mastectomy surgery with a symbolic goal of 800 portraits.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: There are about 800 new diagnoses in the United States every day for breast cancer. And then I thought, what a powerful thing if I could have 800 portraits hanging in one space at one time, so you could truly see the impact of breast cancer in America.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: For creative inspiration, she turned to the Venus de Milo, a Hellenistic goddess who`s broken, but beautiful and strong. As for the title--\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: The word grace stems from the word -- it means gratitude, as the word for things, it means a gift. And it also talks about the grace of the body, so it had sort of this whole multiple meaning to it.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And while no survivor`s story is the same, Charise has put some in their own category, the Athena Division. Launched in 2015, it`s dedicated to military veterans.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: Actually, just a little bit - right about there in the middle, there.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Like Sheila Johnson.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON (Breast Cancer Survivor, Retired \uc2 U.S. Air Force\uc2 ): I joined the \uc2 United States Air Force\uc2  in 1985. I did 25 years, four months and 27 days, I`m counting all of them.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Sheila retired when she was diagnosed with metastatic breast cancer at age 43.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: I want you to think about the strength and the pride.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: She credits her military service with some of the resiliency and fight needed to battle her cancer for the last 12 years.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: I know that Sheila feels very blessed because she`s had a lot of time, but when I started photographing her, like the emotion just started pouring out of her immediately. I mean, she had tears streaming down her face.\par \par \uc2 If you start to feel emotional, just be with it. Just be with it, don`t fight it.\par \par \uc2 SHEILA JOHNSON: It took me back when I was diagnosed. And I cry when they told me I had to get out to military. But then you`re looking at all these flags in a room and I see the \uc2 United States Air Force\uc2  flag and I was proud. I`m proud to have served my country.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: I guess you`re never fully done with breast cancer, right?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA ARNDT (Breast Cancer Survivor, Former Marines): No.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: So as Barbara Arndt, who served for four years in the Marine Corps. Though she also calls her service a confusing time after she says she was sexually assaulted while doing her specialty training at Camp Lejeune.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA ARNDT: I didn`t report it. I didn`t tell anybody. But I knew you don`t talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Come on, we`re going this way. Yes, good girl.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Barbara says she learned to push her feelings aside, one she`d later be forced to face as she was at age 41 when she was diagnosed with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 How did you decide to have the double mastectomy?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA ARNDT: It wasn`t a thought in my mind. I knew that I didn`t want my breasts anymore. I felt like my body betrayed me and I`m sorry, but I had really nice breasts. I really liked them. My breasts, my hair and then they were gone, so, you know, I just shut down.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: For Barbara, the Athena project helped open her back up.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: When I photographed her, I could see emotion welling up in her and I think that that really surprised her.\par \par \uc2 That`s it -- don`t fight that feeling. I can see that you`ve got an emotion that wants to come up.\par \par \uc2 And she talked about you`re a Marine, you have to be tough. You`ve got to just keep pushing through. You know, she did that with her cancer, so she was emotional in her photoshoot and it was a really beautiful moment with her.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA ARNDT: I stood in Central Park topless and I didn`t feel weird at all. I felt really empowered. It was like, wow, like I`m still here and I was really mindful of being able to watch people objectively and nobody was like, oh, gross or put a top on.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: She credits the Athena project with what she called the gift of being comfortable in her own skin.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA ARNDT: For the longest time, I thought that I was never going to be with man again. Like I was just going to become this old maid because you know, one of my favorite attributes about myself was gone, so like what do I have to offer you, I`m never going to take my top off.\par \par \uc2 And the fact of the matter is, is that it doesn`t matter what I look like. It`s not about my looks. It`s about who`s in here.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: You really have done it.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA ARNDT: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: It`s a sentiment Charise Isis never tires of hearing.\par \par \uc2 You seem to take this scar that for some is shame and you turn it into strength, which is an amazing thing.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: Yeah. Well, you know, a scar is -- it`s the sign that you have gotten through something really big and you`re still here.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: That`s a sign of a fighter.\par \par \uc2 CHARISE ISIS: Yeah, absolutely. And these women are -- the military women are definitely fighters.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And obviously, all of these breast cancer survivors are one of the things that everyone we spoke with really hopes that this Athena Division can do is not just raise awareness. We know breast cancer`s out there, but the VA is dealing with a female population that is the largest, the fastest growing population among it and they need to start working towards helping these women battle the disease and find earlier detection.\par \par \uc2 JEFF GLOR: Eight hundred people diagnosed every day.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Everyday approximately.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221123eibj0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For November 13, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Anthony Salvanto,Mark Strassmann, Margaret Brennan, Ashley  Etienne, Joel Payne, Leslie Sanchez \par \uc2 9,048 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Five days after the 2022 election, control of the House of Representatives remains undecided, while Democrats keep their Senate majority. How will this midterm muddle change life for Americans?\par \par \uc2 Democrats braced for defeat last Tuesday, but they breathed a sigh of relief after their better-than-expected showing.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): A victory and a vindication for Democrats, our agenda and for the America -- and for the American people.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I`m not surprised by the turnout. I`m incredibly pleased.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Here`s how Republican`s Senate campaign chief described the outcome.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RICK SCOTT\uc2  (R-Florida): Here`s what happened to us. Election Day, our voters didn`t show up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do the results mean for Washington and the rest of the country for the next few years?\par \par \uc2 We will hear from a senior adviser to President Biden and key members in Congress from both parties.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We begin this morning with a CBS News projection. Democrats will keep their thin Senate majority for two more years, after Senator Catherine Cortez Masto`s narrow win over \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  in Nevada last night. It was Democrats` second victory this weekend, following Senator Mark Kelly`s projected win over Blake Masters out in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 But we are still awaiting results in key House races on the West Coast that will determine the shape of Congress. CBS News estimates that Republicans maintain an edge in clinching control of the House of Representatives. But Democrats still have a shot at the majority. President Biden called it perilously close.\par \par \uc2 At this hour, \uc2 CBS\uc2  estimates the GOP will win at least 214 seats and Democrats 210, but a majority requires 218 seats, and neither party will reach that number until a handful of closely contested districts finish counting.\par \par \uc2 CBS News senior national correspondent Mark Strassmann has more from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice-over): For days to come, many states will still tally ballots. But the biggest midterm winter is already clear: democracy. It held, without mobs in the streets, without violence, despite all the election denialism out there.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-Arizona Gubernatorial Candidate): Are you ready to take this state back?\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Arizona`s gubernatorial race between Republican Kari Lake and Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  could still go either way. Lake, a Trump protege, accuses elections officials of slow-rolling returns.\par \par \uc2 Reality check: No state counts all ballots by midnight Election Day.\par \par \uc2 BILL GATES (R-Maricopa County, Arizona, Supervisor): We`re not doing anything wrong at all. And that someone from here would suggest that we are doing something wrong, that`s frustrating.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: On the same ballot in Arizona, Republican Senate candidate Blake Masters has yet to concede. He tweeted: "Voters decide, not the media. Let`s count the votes."\par \par \uc2 And although nearly 60 percent of GOP election deniers are projected to win their races this year, none were top election officials in critical battleground states.\par \par \uc2 Democratic candidates nationwide defied expectations, despite Republicans beating them up on inflation and the relentless GOP drive to yoke them to an unpopular president.\par \par \uc2 But another worry drove many voters.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Abortion, you know, just the right to have autonomy over one`s body.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: And take John Fetterman`s narrow win as Pennsylvania`s next senator, flipping a key swing state seat to Democrats.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D-Pennsylvania Senator-Elect): I never expected that we were going to turn these red counties blue.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: In rejecting Republican Mehmet Oz, Pennsylvania voters ranked abortion as their number one issue. Oz also had the Trump seal of endorsement.\par \par \uc2 Among disappointed Republicans, Trump`s impact on races has led to a finger-pointing election postmortem, complaints about unelectable candidates with extreme positions.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR PAT TOOMEY (R-Pennsylvania): If fealty to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the primary criteria for selecting candidates, we`re probably not going to do really well.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Focus on the Senate now shifts to Georgia`s run-off next month, Democrat \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  squaring off December 6.\par \par \uc2 Democrats want a win here to build a stronger majority and create leverage for Senate leaders -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mark Strassmann, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to CBS News elections and surveys director Anthony Salvanto for more on the trends that influenced the midterm election results.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, I know you haven`t slept...\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Nope.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... at all.\par \par \uc2 And you`re still tabulating here. But, big picture, Democrats defied history. How did they do it?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, this was a turnout story, first and foremost, in my mind, and it was sort of as we expected.\par \par \uc2 The people -- our viewers may remember, a couple of weeks ago, when we were talking about the effect that turnout could have, and how Republicans were on path to a narrow majority, but that the House could become even if younger people especially turned out in force.\par \par \uc2 And what happened, they did. Their numbers started approaching the 2018 record levels. There was a particular group subset we called the young and restless that we were watching. And not only did they turn out, but they even shifted a little bit more Democratic, almost two-thirds of them voting for Democrats. So that certainly helped the Democrats in a lot of states, a lot of districts.\par \par \uc2 But the other thing it did was it offset, in a relative way, the impact of the MAGA Republicans, we would call them, the Trump true believers. And they certainly turned out and had impact, but not as much in that relative way, again, because the Democrats and the younger people had turned out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: At this hour, Republicans still hold an edge in gaining control of the House. It`s a slimmer majority, if they get it. What broke in their favor?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, let me start with another group that you and I talked about throughout this campaign, and that was the pressure parents.\par \par \uc2 These were folks who said that the COVID pandemic had been stressful, stressful on their kids. Also, their finances were under stress. Well, they actually voted narrowly Republican by three points, really reflective of the way the country overall did.\par \par \uc2 And then the other part of that I should mention are the restoring Roe voters. These were women for whom abortion and abortion rights were a priority in their voting.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that momentum had slowed.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: That momentum did slow during the campaign. And then it seemed to come back, because we saw them move in the end, when we did our Election Day polling, move even more for the Democrats, going from eight in 10 to nine in 10 voting for Democrats, which suggests that the Democrats` messaging there in the closing, that that definitely helped the Democrats as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do we watch for next?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: OK, there`s a handful of congressional districts that are going to be the sort of center of political universe. We`re all going to watch out West in the Central Valley, California, Southern California especially.\par \par \uc2 And we`re probably going to have to go district by district until somebody gets to 218. Going to be a couple of days, maybe a couple of weeks. This is typical. These places, I should say, often count their ballots over days and weeks. But that`s we`re watching.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Anthony Salvanto, get your coffee going.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We go now to Arkansas Republican Senator Tom Cotton, who was a regular on the 2022 campaign trail boosting GOP candidates. He`s out with a new book called "Only the Strong," and he joins us this morning from McLean, Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 And on this Veterans Day weekend, I do want to thank you for your past military service.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON (R-Arkansas): Well, thank you, Margaret. That`s kind of you.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to all our veterans for their service in all of our nation`s wars.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Let me get to the story of the moment.\par \par \uc2 Democrats faced historic headwinds. Eight out of 10 Americans said they felt it was out of control. Republicans had issues like economy and crime really break in their favor. President Biden has a low approval rating.\par \par \uc2 How did Republicans end up with this complete disappointment?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: Well, Margaret, I wouldn`t say it was a complete disappointment.\par \par \uc2 On the one hand, we had strong Republican leaders running on positive records of accomplishments who won very big victories, if you look at governors like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in Georgia, \uc2 Mike DeWine\uc2  in Ohio, \uc2 Kim Reynolds\uc2  in Iowa, Greg Abbott in Texas. We had senators with the same victories, like \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  in Florida and Tim Scott in South Carolina, Ron Johnson in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 But, on the other hand, obviously, we hoped that we would have won more seats. I think the lessons in our victories can be applied to some places where we came up a little bit short. We need to focus on serious substantive accomplishments and issues like crime, like our wide-open border, like addressing runaway inflation.\par \par \uc2 Even in places where we came up a little bit short, like Lee Zeldin`s race for governor in New York, he performed very well, compared to Republicans in recent elections, and he probably helped save the House of Representatives by bringing four new Republican congressmen-elect across the finish line in New York.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But...\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: So, I think we have lessons in the places we had victories...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: ... that we can apply to places where we were disappointed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Sure, but you lost the ball game in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Karl Rove blamed candidate quality, and specifically put the blame on former President Trump. He said: "Mr. Trump turned what should have been a referendum on Mr. Biden`s terrible record into a choice between himself and the current president. As in 2020, lots of voters chose Mr. Biden."\par \par \uc2 Should Mr. Trump remain the leader of the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: Well, Margaret, when the party -- when any party is out of power, as Republicans are now, we don`t have a single leader.\par \par \uc2 The former president is obviously very popular with many of our voters.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He`s the center of gravity.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: But we also have important other leaders as well, like some of those victors I just mentioned earlier, like \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  in Georgia, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in Florida.\par \par \uc2 Last year, you had Glen Youngkin have a great victory in a bluish democratic state like Virginia. I hope to remain a leader in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  as well, in addition to people like some of those I just mentioned who were reelected, like Tim Scott.\par \par \uc2 So, when you`re in opposition, you don`t have a single leader. That won`t be the case until we`re through the `24 -- `24 nominating season, and we have -- and we have a new nominee.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said you`re not going to run for president in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The former president says he intends to announce he`s running on Tuesday. Should he be the automatic nominee, or should he face a primary?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: Well, Margaret, since I opted against being a candidate in 2024, I -- I don`t plan to be a pundit or a strategist in 2024. I will say that...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, you just throw out a whole bunch of names of guys who might be running for president, Senator.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Are you endorsing them?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: And I -- and I know almost -- I know almost all of them personally. And I respect their accomplishments, not just their big victories on Tuesday, or last year in Glenn`s case, but also their accomplishments in office.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, I know everyone already wants to focus on 2024. I just want to remind everyone that we`re still in the middle of the 2022 midterm, because we`re in overtime in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: And the most important thing we can do is elect \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  to make sure that we can keep the pressure on Democrats in the Senate not to veer far to the left, as they have over the last two years.\par \par \uc2 That`s where I think everyone should remain focused for these next three weeks.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Should leadership elections in the Senate be delayed until December, and should \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  remain as Republican leader in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: Well, I don`t see why we would delay the election, since all five or six of our leadership elections are uncontested.\par \par \uc2 You know, the great wrestling champion Ric Flair used to say, to be the man, you got to beat the man. And, so far, no one`s had the nerve to step forward and challenge Senator McConnell. So, I support Senator McConnell. I support the other slate of candidates for our leadership elections. I think it`s better that we move forward with these elections, so we can focus, again, on the Georgia run-off.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you as well about what will be a consequential meeting tomorrow between President Biden and President \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  of China, their first to face -- face-to-face of the Biden presidency.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden says he wants to talk through red lines to understand critical interests. Can the U.S. avoid conflict with China at this point?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: We want to avoid conflict with China most certainly in the military sense. I don`t think we can avoid conflict and tension in terms of diplomatic and economic and political competition. I would urge the president to be very firm in drawing those red lines.\par \par \uc2 We don`t want to see a repeat of what happened last summer in Russia, when \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  walked away from their summit in Europe tempted to go for the jugular in Ukraine, especially a month later after the collapse in Afghanistan, or a replay of something I write about in my new book, "Only the Strong," of the disastrous Vienna summit in 1961 between John Kennedy and Nikita Khrushchev, which encouraged Russian leaders to rampage all around the world for the next year.\par \par \uc2 So I hope the president is very clear in drawing red lines, for instance, saying that the recent rule against China using American semiconductor technology is only the first step. I hope he`s very clear about Taiwan, that we will continue to arm Taiwan so it can defend itself, and, if China goes for the jugular in Taiwan, then we will come to their aid.\par \par \uc2 That`s the simplest way to avoid military conflict, is to be clear and firm up front.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But what would you recommend in terms of China not going for the jugular, but a slow strangulation to take over Taiwan, which I know you know is one of the scenarios national security officials are concerned about?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: Well, the simplest thing we can do for Taiwan is what we should have started doing for Ukraine before Russia invaded Ukraine, is to provide them with the weaponry that they need to stop an invasion.\par \par \uc2 It`s urgent that we do so now, because, unlike Ukraine, Taiwan is an island, which means China could blockade it, and we might not be able to resupply them once China might start military conflict.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s urgent that we accelerate arms sales to Taiwan, the kind of anti- ship, anti-aircraft missiles and sea mines that they need to fend off a potential evasion from the communist Chinese.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 With China, it`s a hard problem set because, as you know, our economies are so interlinked; iPhones are made in China. We learned during the pandemic how dependent we are on the supply chain. They own an enormous amount of us debt.\par \par \uc2 You`re arguing to separate the two economies, but people like \uc2 Hank Paulson\uc2  of the Bush administration have said that`ll end up with a less stable world with really dangerous economic forces here. How do you possibly do that?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: Well, Margaret, as I write in "Only the Strong," it was a bad mistake by leaders in both parties going back 30 years to allow China to get itself so entangled in the United States and to allow us to become so economically dependent in so many ways, whether it`s things like high-tech electronic equipment or low-tech basic pharmaceutical ingredients or medical equipment.\par \par \uc2 What I say in "Only the Strong" is that we should try to decouple in a strategic sense, so we don`t harm our own people, but we`re no longer dependent on those critical sectors that we depend on to keep ourselves safe -- safe, healthy and prosperous.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: It`s one thing if Americans buy, say, children`s toys or plastic Christmas trees from China. It`s another thing if China has the market cornered on things like basic pharmaceutical ingredients or the rare earth elements that are so vital to all modern electronics.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Well, we will watch.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOM COTTON: We have to do so in a smart way, but we have to be aggressive and quick about it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll watch to see if there`s congressional action on any of that.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 And I`m joined now in studio by Maryland Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , who also serves on the House Select Committee investigating the January 6 attack on the U.S. Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Welcome.\par \par \uc2 Democrats still have a shot here, although Republicans have the edge in taking the House. What do Americans need to be prepared for what the next two years looks like?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2  (D-Maryland): Well, first of all, don`t count us out, the way we were counted out from the very beginning in this election.\par \par \uc2 They were saying the Republicans were going to pick up 40, 50, 60 seats But the party of democracy and freedom and progress for the people held. And so we`ve had a very impressive election. And we`re going to continue to fight for social progress. And we`re going to fight for the political rights of the people, which means against gerrymandering, against voter suppression tactics, against manipulative use of the filibuster and the Electoral College to thwart the will of the majority.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to continue to fight for a strong democracy program.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, it`s going to be hard to legislate though, any way we look at it.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about what you`re working on with the January 6 Committee. Former President Trump`s basically going to defy the subpoena you issued. He`s planning to announce he`s running for the presidency on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Is the prime achievement of the January 6 Committee simply going to be this written report? You`re not appearing to stop him from running.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 : Well, in a democracy, the people have the right to the truth.\par \par \uc2 And what we withstood was a systematic assault on democratic institutions and an attempt to overthrow a presidential election. So we have set forth the truth in a series of hearings. And we`re going to set forth the truth in our final report, along with a set of legislative recommendations about what we need to do to fortify American democracy against coups, insurrections, electoral sabotage, and political violence, with domestic violent extremist groups involved.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to put all of that out there. Look, when -- when I was over in the Senate with the impeachment team, I told the Republicans there that this was our opportunity to deal with the problem of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who had committed high crimes and misdemeanors against the people of the United States. And they needed to act on behalf of the country and the Constitution, but, if they didn`t, he would become their problem.\par \par \uc2 And, at this point, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is the problem of the Republican Party, and he may destroy their party.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can you get an Electoral Count Act passed in this lame- duck session?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 : I think we can.\par \par \uc2 That`s the bare minimum of what we need to do. It`s necessary. It`s not remotely sufficient to the task because what we saw in -- in 2021 and earlier, in 2020, was a systematic assault on the right to vote and an attempt to steal a presidential election by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , an attempt to overthrow the constitutional order.\par \par \uc2 So, I think we can reform the Electoral Count Act, passing the legislation that \uc2 Zoe Lofgren\uc2  and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  have proposed. But that`s just one small part of what needs to be done...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 : ... needs to be done to protect American constitutional democracy.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Election deniers didn`t prevail in the key battleground, but \uc2 CBS\uc2  tallied it, and at least 155 House Republicans in the new Congress have raised unfounded doubts about the validity or integrity of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 These are going to be your colleagues that you will be working with. What change is that going to affect in the working?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 : Well, that`s a statement about the political contamination of the GOP by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And, again, Kevin McCarthy and other leaders within the Republican Party are now required to make a decision about whether they`re going to try to rid themselves of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his toxic influence on the party.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But these 155 House Republicans are his constituency. Kevin McCarthy, if he wants to be leader, will need to consider the views of everyone.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, it`s a real problem for Kevin McCarthy now, because there are certain pro-Trumpists within his House caucus who refuse to accept that he`s really with Trump, and they want to get rid of McCarthy. And some of them, they have names very early in the alphabet, like Biggs. And they might just vote for Trump when they, you know, take the roll call for speaker.\par \par \uc2 So we know that the -- the hard-right Freedom Caucus people are in search of another candidate. And one potential candidate whose name has been floated is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself, because the speaker of the House does not have to be a member of the House. And they are talking about putting Trump right there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s not a real option, though.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 : Well, they talk about it repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 And if Trump decided he wanted to do it, it would pose a profound problem for their party, because they refused to do the right thing early on. I mean, today, it seems like the spell has been broken. It`s begun to dissolve. We don`t have Republicans around the country claiming that they really won when it`s been certified that they lost their elections.\par \par \uc2 And yet there is still this big lie dogma, which, as you say, has been embraced by 150 members within their caucus. And so that is going to create profound cognitive and political dissonance within the GOP. Is it really Trump`s party? Or does it stand for something else?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 : And \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  and \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  are going to force that question. They`re going to force the Republicans to choose.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Would you urge President Biden and the White House to comply with all these House Republican investigations that have been promised?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 JAMIE RASKIN\uc2 : Well, the -- obviously, everybody`s got to comply with the law, such as the law is.\par \par \uc2 You know. We would hope that they would feel chastened by the voters of America, who dealt them an historic repudiation. I mean, they were talking about picking up 40 or 50 or 60 seats. We -- the Democrats may indeed win the House, the way, yesterday, we won the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So it is a repudiation of that kind of right-wing big lie, election- denying, character assassination politics that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  brought right to the heart of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman, thank you for being here and joining us today.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you can`t watch the full Face the Nation, you can set your DVR, or we`re available on demand, and you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount+ app.\par \par \uc2 We`re also replayed on our CBS News Streaming Network throughout the day on Sundays.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with President Biden`s senior adviser, Anita Dunn.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to Anita Dunn. She`s a senior adviser to President Biden and she joins us this morning from Chevy Chase, Maryland.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 I assume it was a late night for you given that Democrats have defied expectations. You still will have only a thin majority in the Senate. Republicans still have a shot at gaining the House. What becomes possible now?\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN (Senior Adviser to President Biden): You know, the precedent and the Democrats accomplished a huge amount with a 50-50 Senate and an extremely narrow House majority in the first two years of President Biden`s term. So, you know, the president`s been clear, as he was in his press conference this week, Margaret, which is, he`s going to reach out his hand to work with the Republicans. And the question is whether they will reach out their hand to his. But given these narrow majorities, you know, obviously the Senate, we -- Democrats will control. The House is still up in the air. Voters in this country are going to expect their leaders to work together.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The president has said he wants to take it slow in terms of defining priorities over the next few weeks in this lame duck session. What`s the business you need to get done?\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN: Well, Margaret, as you know, we have to keep the government open and funded. That is obviously priority number one. Then -- and it`s going to take a little while still for lame duck priorities to really be set given the uncertainty about the outcomes of the elections still. I don`t think anybody would have predicted that we would still not know who would control the \uc2 United States House of Representatives\uc2  the following Sunday.\par \par \uc2 But other lame duck priorities will certainly include additional funding for Ukraine, which has been, and the president has said, he hopes will continue to be a bipartisan issue in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 . You know, obviously, emergency funding for the natural disasters that Florida and Puerto Rico suffered earlier this year. And additional priorities for the administration as well. But keep the government open and running is the number one priority.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It looks like Democrats owe a big part of their better than expected performance to young voters who turned out. About 26 million Americans are now stuck in limbo because they`ve been promised student debt relief. Now it`s caught up in the courts. If you lose in court, I know you`re waiting for that, will this just be a broken promise?\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN: So, Margaret, we believe we`re going to prevail in court because the arguments that -- and the law are on the administration`s side. And make no mistake about it, the administration -- you know, President Biden made this commitment to people in America. It`s not just young people. It`s also people of every age. We`ve gotten letters at the White House from people who have retired, who are talking about what a difference loan forgiveness is going to make. And we`re not going to give up and we`re not going to back down.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But is there a plan b if the court strikes it down?\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN: We believe we`re going to prevail in court. And, at that point, we will swiftly move to make sure that the over 26 million people at this point who have -- whose information this administration has, then we`ll move swiftly for loan forgiveness.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Voters in key states, like Pennsylvania, rated abortion access as a top concern for them. But if Republicans take the House, what is it that the president can actually do here? And is there room for compromise on restrictions with some of the Republicans who have said they`re open to securing abortion access?\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN: So, the president has been very clear, he believes Congress needs to codify Roe versus wade so that it is the national law of the land. And he has said, if Democrats control the House and the Senate, that he will send a bill to codify this nationally.\par \par \uc2 He will continue to work, as our administration has, since this ruling came down, to make sure that the travel of women who want to go to states where abortion is legal is not impeded, that people are able to get reproductive health care, that women who have other medical issues aren`t denied care, which is happening, Margaret. And, at the end of the day, you know, the president said when this ruling came down it was going to be a political issue that voters needed to go to the polls and make their voices heard.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN: And there`s a huge amount of evidence that`s exactly what happened. So, the real question is whether the Republicans will listen to those voices.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we`ll see if there`s any compromise there.\par \par \uc2 On -- what happens if the Republicans do get this slim majority? It ups the chances of all of these investigations that Kevin McCarthy has promised are coming. Would you say today that the White House will comply with them? The Trump administration was heavily criticized for defying Congress` request for appearance and documents. Will the Biden White House comply?\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN: You know, Margaret, many of the Republicans who have been talking about these investigations, and even potential impeachment, have made no secret of the fact that they have a political agenda that they are trying to advance through these. Obviously, the White House has, and will continue, to apply with fair and legitimate oversight because we are a White House that respects norms and the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 But I think that, you know, the American people didn`t vote for Congress being used to conduct political vendettas over the next two years they voted for.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know we`re still counting votes from this election. But as you know, former President Trump plans to announce he`s running in 2024 this Tuesday. You`ve already been involved, as you said, just being prudent, in planning for President Biden to also run. Does the Trump announcement change any part of your planning or calculus?\par \par \uc2 ANITA DUNN: You know, the president has made it clear he intends to run. And that he will make a formal decision later this year or early next year. You don`t run for president because of what the other side is doing or what another candidate may be doing. The president will run because he feels that he is the best person to continue the progress that we have made in this country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be watching and listening.\par \par \uc2 Anita Dunn, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For some analysis on the election and its impact, we are joined now by a panel of political pros. Ashley Etienne, a \uc2 CBS\uc2  political contributor and a former aide to Vice President Harris and Speaker Pelosi, Joel Payne is a Democratic strategist and also a \uc2 CBS\uc2  political contributor, Leslie Sanchez is a Republican strategist and also part of the team here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 , and Brendan Buck is a former top aide to Speakers \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It`s good to have you all here. And I know you`re going to explain everything that happened.\par \par \uc2 But, Brendan, I want to start with you.\par \par \uc2 We were just hearing about how slim the majorities could be if the GOP does pull off this win in the House. Conservative Republicans from within the Freedom Caucus made your last two boss` lives very, very difficult.\par \par \uc2 What is it that Kevin McCarthy would face if he steps into leadership of the Republican Party in the House?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK (Republican Strategist): Yes, it`s a really difficult moment. You know, when we had trouble sometimes keeping everybody in line, we had a pretty healthy majority. Sometimes 10, 15, 30 seats. This is a potential situation where you could have two seats to play with. And that`s almost impossible.\par \par \uc2 Being Republican speaker of the house is almost impossible to begin with. With a two-seat majority, that means every member has leverage over the speaker of the house. That`s what they`re trying to assert. If you want to be speaker, you have to do things the way I want things done. And that makes a weak speaker. Each one doesn`t care about leadership. They get a lot of power - a rank and file Republican gets a lot of their power by attacking leadership. And it plays to the base and the grassroots.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re in this -- this cycle where they`re just going to keep attacking each other. They`re going to benefit. All it`s going to do is hurt the speaker. And I -- ultimately, I don`t know that much is going to get done legislatively. But these oversight investigations are still going to be going forward. All you need is one more seat. So, I still think it`s going to be a pretty active, busy House.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Ashley, to that point, you know, one Democratic strategist said to me, because of what Brendan described, it`s going to be a knife fight for two years. And you don`t put someone untested in the leadership role on the Democratic side. That sounded to me like Speaker Pelosi, at 82 years old, is staying in Congress.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Well, I`ll just say this, we -- take a step back. I was just thinking how to describe --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because you don`t want to say if she`s staying or not.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: No, no, no, I do. I`m happy to get into it. But I was just - you know, as a Democrat I just want to sort of absorb this moment that we`re currently in. You know, I was thinking, I`m not a linguist, but, like, there`s got to be something worse than a shellacking that happened on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think that the speaker is in the power position. I mean, I`ve always known the speaker to be in a power position. But after the results, we - we overperformed in the election. There was no wave. This really solidifies her legacy as the most accomplished speaker in American history, in my opinion. And I think there`s no doubt about that.\par \par \uc2 But what, you know, when she -- she recently gave an interview, and she basically said this morning, listen, we`re still in this fight. Let`s not make any assumptions. Let`s hold off on it. I`m always willing to bet on the speaker. But there`s two things I know about her. One, she`s going to make her decision on her own terms and, two, she`s going to keep us all guessing.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And on her own terms after this horrendous attack on her husband recently.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Oh, absolutely, yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which says a lot.\par \par \uc2 Joel, we were talking with Senator Cotton about the other question of leadership over in the Senate, where Democrats have this razor-thin majority. What is going on, from your understanding, within the ranks -- and I want Leslie to jump in, too -- of leadership on the Senate side.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, I think it`s pretty stable on the Senate side. If you`re a Democrat, you`ve got to feel really good about \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  delivering on that Democratic agenda, particularly in the last kind of quarter of the last Congress. You`ve got someone like \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2 , who ran the Democratic campaign machine to a lot of aplomb. You`ve got a really strong, experienced leadership core.\par \par \uc2 So I actually think that, you know, for maybe, gosh, Ashley, we`ve got relative peace, peace and prosperity in the Democratic leadership ranks of the Senate, which I think is --\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: And in the House, by the way.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: And - and in the House, by the way. She knows I`m a Senate guy. But, yes, I actually think it`s a time of a lot of peace. And, by the way, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  probably among the winners when you look at what happened this week, leaning into that kind of Democratic agenda. By the way, Biden Democrats did well. And there`s a lot of Biden Democrats in that Senate caucus.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But the best he can get done, if Republicans take the House, is to confirm some judicial nominees. Is that it for the next two years?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: That`s interesting because I`ve - because I`ve been talking to some folks, some high-ranking Democrats, and they`ll tell you, we`re going to double down on prescription drugs, we`re going to go after insulin pricing, we`re going to try to codify Roe, we`re going to keep pursuing anti-inflationary measures.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They don`t have the votes.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, that`s why that race in Georgia becomes really important because in a era where \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  exist, and also when you have \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  in your caucus, who is a little older, it helps to have that extra insurance of another vote.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: I think the only assurance coming out of that is going to be judicial appointments because everything else is going to be really difficult, especially if you`re talking about an economic agenda and getting Republicans to come on board. And, Margaret, I`m just going to jump in since I started on this part.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Please.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: I want to say about Tuesday, Republicans were expecting a revolution. They got a revelation. And the revelation is a lot about the impact of women voters in this area and how they are activated.\par \par \uc2 We know from 2018 and 2020 that they`re -- with a sense of urgency, you see women run, donate, you know, protest, and get engaged. Come the Dobbs decisions - the Supreme Court Dobbs decision in June, it did have that impact that a lot of Republicans are not talking about in areas that matter, whether it was a ballot initiative or something you had like Michigan or a suburban area like Philadelphia and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And the second part of that, we did see positive sides with women and the Republican Party when it came to strong governance, or when governors who looked like they were really holding the line, whether they were male or female, they were rewarded by those suburban white women and also working- class Latino and white women.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there`s a big takeaway that gets lost in this conversation, and that will be a part of the agenda moving forward.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, let`s talk about that and the governor`s mansions.\par \par \uc2 So, in addition to Florida`s governor, DeSantis, other Republican governors who signed abortion restrictions into law also won re-election. I`m thinking of Ohio`s DeWine, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , Georgia, Greg Abbott in Texas. They all won by significant margins. Then you look at those who won with healthy backing from women, DeSantis, Abbott, Sununu, DeWine. These Republicans won women.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it wasn`t about abortion.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: No.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What was it about?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: The economic issue overall. I mean the economic - so single-issue voters, people that were voting on abortion, in those critical swing areas, they made the difference. But if you back up, if you take an aggregate view of a state like Florida, for example, when it was important to get people back to work, you have a lot of working class families who didn`t have the luxury of working from home, that realized their small businesses need to keep moving and tourism was a big part of Florida`s industry. Getting that going helped -- he was rewarded dramatically. And you have a lot of small business owners that are women who cared about those types of issue.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s a very good model. But if it`s an extreme model, I would say, on one side or the other, is when you get a lot -- mostly white, college educated women who come out against those extremes. And, in many cases, the extreme can be a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He - he activates women to respond. The counterresponse is visceral one way or the other.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve been speaking to some MAGA supporters, some loyalists on his side, and they say, well, they still love the former president. The thrill is gone. They are tired of the big ego and the name calling and I think it`s a caution for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Yes, I was just going to say, Leslie, I think that`s the top takeaway. The question that remains is, what -- when are Republicans going to learn the lesson? The MAGA movement has been a loser since day one. You`ve lost three cycles now in a big way. You know, there`s calls inside and outside the party for Republicans to take back their party. \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  and all his properties are sort of abandoning the president.\par \par \uc2 And so, for me, it`s - it`s - it`s, you know, the - the party, the Republican Party is at a crossroads now. The question is, what are you going to do? And I think the challenge for them and the problem is, you can`t win with a MAGA movement, but you can`t walk away from them. As you said earlier, Margaret, there`s 150 of them that are in the House caucus and then the president -- and former president is suggesting that he`s going to run again. He`s omnipresent in this whole debate.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s really the challenge. And I think in the next week it`s going to be interesting to see if the MAGA movement sort of loosen its grip on the GOP going forward. I mean it`s a big, big week for Republicans coming up in terms of their organizing. But that`s the real question, when are you going to learn the lessons?\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK: Yes, and I don`t know that we will. I mean how many times have we been given the opportunity to learn the lesson after - after January 6th. And we`ve had leaders say it`s time to turn the page. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was on the Senate floor, Kevin McCarthy was on the Senate floor saying it`s time to move on.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Sure. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK: But every time that runs into voters. And voters aren`t ready to move on. And so we could have another moment where all the people in Washington say we don`t -- we want somebody else, but if voters don`t, they`re probably going to backtrack very quickly.\par \par \uc2 I thought your interview with Tom Cotton was fascinating earlier. He was saying, as much as all of us could hear, he wants somebody else to be the nominee. He`s name-dropping other people that he wants to be the nominee, but he wouldn`t say it`s time to move on for Trump.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK: Because they can`t do that. Because you`re going to lose base Republican voters.\par \par \uc2 We have an example of this in the Senate race. Joe O`Dea, who is running in Colorado, was running a really great race, everybody thought, and he was doing it a little different than usual. He was saying, I`m not a Trump Republican. An was trying to say, like, I`m, you know, appealing to a middle. He pissed off \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  attacked him and he lost significantly because Trump voters ran away from him.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A Trump adviser I asked who -- because they are planning this announcement on Tuesday for the former president to announce his candidacy. I asked about, don`t you need to show unity with the party since you have this Senate race in Georgia? And the answer was, it`s unity with the base that we are showing. So, is he forcing a question?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I just think that`s a mistake. And also like -- look, I love all this, you know, deep introspection of the Republican Party but I do think you should think about Democrats here, particularly President Biden.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, talking to folks who are pretty familiar with the president`s thinking --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because you`re saying they didn`t lose, you won.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: That`s what I`m saying. If you talk to people who are familiar with the president`s thinking they will --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re bringing that curve, though, you know that.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I am. They will tell you that the Biden coalition is pretty durable. It showed up, obviously, in 2020. It looks like it showed up mostly here, college educated, young voters, Latinos, particularly in some of those areas where we thought they wouldn`t show up, African Americans, independents being competitive with them. That coalition showed up in `20, showed up in `22 and I think they think it could show up in `24.\par \par \uc2 Also, candidate quality matters. Some of the folks that you referenced, those are some of your better Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: The reason why Republicans struggled, I would say, is because the candidate quality imbalance was way in the favor of Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Most of those candidates endorsed by the former president.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: And it looked -- I was going to say, on the fundamentals, people would say that the Trump coalition very much, to this point, is going to show up, that it is very strong. What I`m saying is different. 2016 it was refreshing to have someone elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and now it is more like fatigue post-pandemic. And these are their words, not mine. That`s why I think the cautionary wind is not (ph) Trump.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But pick up, would you, on what Joel was talking about with Hispanic voters because I know you`ve been looking at this very carefully in Texas and you think Democrats shouldn`t bank on that.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: Absolutely not because what we saw is there is a transformation that`s happening with working class Latino families. They`re not necessarily on the education line but on the income line. If they`re moving out to those exerbs (ph), those suburban areas, those rural areas, in red precincts, they are breaking the way of Republicans increasingly. Married families, very traditional in that sense that feel they`re not -- they may still self-identify as a Democrat but they feel the party has left them. That`s an opportunity and Republicans closed that gap by 50 percent this last cycle.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: I agree with that. Republicans do have an opportunity there. And I think, by the way, both sides would be -- they would be wise to not overcorrect to either of their kind of far bases. I think there is a -- an agenda, the Biden agenda, that works, that`s proven to work. I think you`ll see a lot of Democrats who are maybe late adopters who will be a little bit more eager to jump on that Biden agenda. And I actually think there`s probably a muted Biden coalition out there that doesn`t get represented in the public opinion polling, in a lot of the public discussion that, again, I think it does show up pretty reliably.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: But, Joel, let`s talk about what actually did work for the Republicans. Tom Cotton mentioned Kemp and DeSantis. Both of those two candidates and governors ran away from Trump and ran away from the MAGA movement. So as - so I love that we`re having this debate -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: But it seems divorce of the reality that there was not a wave and that there doesn`t need to be some corrections on the part of the - of the Republicans in that the playbook has been laid out. And I think DeSantis and Kemp have laid it out.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ashley, let me ask you about 2024, the Democrats` playbook.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Yes. It feels good.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Vice President Harris was out there talking a lot about reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Yes, I think -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Health. Did she deserve any credit for this? Because we don`t talk about her much in the realm of 2024.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Yes, she actually - I mean, well, a, I will start by saying, I expect that the president will run in 2024 and I think he should. I mean he`s got an incredible record. He`s defied all expectation, all odds. You know, he`s probably the most underestimated politician in recent history.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of the vice president, she did yeoman`s work in -- I think the number`s like 36 states, talking about abortion and women`s rights and reproductive rights and democracy. So, she had a really big impact on the ground that doesn`t really bubble up to Washington, but really drove people out and drove local -- local stories and, you know, energy on the ground.\par \par \uc2 So, I just expect the president will run, and he should.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: With the vice president on the ticket?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Oh, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Yes, of course.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Just have to ask. We`re talking a lot about 2024 since it starts really right now, frankly.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Definitely.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It gets underway.\par \par \uc2 Just, very quickly, before we go, Joel, closing thought from you on what clinched it in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, what clinched it in Pennsylvania looks like was abortion. If you look at the exit polling, abortion outperformed inflation. Doesn`t mean inflation is not important but it does mean that Democrats, as Anthony referred to earlier in the show, did hit a chord with abortion that really resonated with voters.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, good to have all of you here in person no talk.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In these challenging times, we pause to reflect and thank those in the military for their service and sacrifice as we do each November.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (voice over): At the 11th hour of the 11th day of the 11th month of 1918, fighting in World War I stopped. The war to end all wars did not prevent a global one 20 years later. Again, and again, and again, American service men and women have continued to sacrifice. Defending against all enemies, foreign and domestic.\par \par \uc2 Today, less than 10 percent of American adults are military veterans, according to the VA. Just 1 percent of adults serve on active duty. That makes it hard for most to relate to combat survivors battling traumatic stress.\par \par \uc2 Retired Army Colonel Chris Colenda (ph), who served four combat tours in Afghanistan, told me that he has lost more of his unit to suicide and substance abuse than he did to enemy fire.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN (on camera): How should people ask veterans about their service?\par \par \uc2 CHRIS COLENDA (Retired Army Colonel): Tell me about your service. What was the best experience you ever had? What was the most awesome thing about your service? I mean, those sort of very safe questions, very positive questions, are just easy ones that get people talking.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Simply trying to understand is one way to honor the dead and serve the living.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020221113eibd00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Latest On Tropical Storm Nicole\b0\par\par\uc2 Nate Burleson, Jordan Steele \par \uc2 2,769 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, in other news, we are less than 24 hours away from a hurricane making landfall in Florida just a few weeks after Hurricane Ian hammered the state.\par \par \uc2 Tropical Storm Nicole is over the Bahamas right now headed west. It is expected to become a hurricane before it hits Florida`s Atlantic coast and it could flood some of the same areas Ian did.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, Jordan Steele with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  was watching all of this for us and Nicole`s path.\par \par \uc2 Jordan, good morning. So how much time do people have to prepare?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN STEELE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Well, really, Nate, today is the last day, and if anything, if you could do it this morning, that would be best because we know arrival is going to take place tonight into early morning tomorrow, but I`ve got to say, this is one of three big weather headlines and I want to show you all of those. That`s headline number one, yes.\par \par \uc2 But we also have headline number two, a big winter storm that has created a lot of snowfall for the West. Now, we look at blizzard conditions from Winter Storm Alejandra up in the northern tier; on the warm side of this is our next weather headline. That`s where we`re going to have severe weather breakdown in part of the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 But let`s go back and talk about winter storm, or excuse me, let`s go back and talk about our big weather headline number one, and of course, that`s Nicole. Today is the last day to prepare. Hurricane Warnings from Daytona Beach down to West Palm. We could be experiencing hurricane-like winds as soon as tonight and tropical storm winds have already been felt on the Atlantic side of this thing.\par \par \uc2 It is so wide reaching, the storm surge warning is one of our greatest threats here, we can be looking at storm surge of three to five feet above normally dry ground. And that`s going to be a big problem for our beautiful beaches in Florida, and then it takes a turn to the north after it moves into the Gulf and it goes all the way up maybe even into your neck of the woods. We could be looking at big impacts for the Mid-Atlantic and even all the way up into the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 We know the highest wind threat is going to be down here in Florida, but that rainfall is going to be wide reaching.\par \par \uc2 Now, for the latest on Nicole, watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV streaming devices. Yes, Nate. Like I said even folks up in the Northeast should kind of prep for big rain and potential flooding as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what, your right, my son`s football game on Friday was just moved to Thursday because of the weather. Jordan, I appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next, Vlad has a story that you will be talking about today. But first it is 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wait.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s time now for "What to Watch" with Vlad. What`s up, Vlad?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So what`s going on? First of all, Gayle is singing The Beastie Boys`...\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: ... "Up in Here" this morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle knows how to party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am delirious.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We all are. We got to bed at three o`clock in the morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The drugs are kicking in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I love you`re singing The Beastie Boys. And did you see that Special Edition graphic?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I did see.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That our boy, Gabe in graphics hooked up with. It`s a red and blue "What to Watch" intro.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well done.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love it, as always, Gabe.\par \par \uc2 All right, so let`s begin.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Gabe.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you, Gabe.\par \par \uc2 Let`s begin with a Special Edition of "What to Watch" beginning with some historic winds that you should know about. Did you all hear about 25-year- old Democrat, Maxwell Frost becoming the first Member of Generation Z. Nate, I think those are your -- that`s your kids generation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He just want to seat in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2 . He was easily elected in Florida`s 10th Congressional District. That`s the Orlando area. Frost is also a Black Latino who focused his campaign on stricter gun control laws and abortion rights. Congratulations to him.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, Republican Katie Britt became the first woman elected to the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  from the Great State of Alabama. Britt will succeed longtime Senator Richard Shelby who is retiring.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Massachusetts and Maryland replaced their Republican Governors with Democrats. Maura Healy won the Massachusetts Governor`s race. She will be the first openly lesbian Governor in the United States and the first female Governor of Massachusetts. That is huge.\par \par \uc2 And over in Maryland, once more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is huge.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is also huge, Wes Moore, elected the State`s first Black Governor defeating far right Republican Dan Cox. Moore becomes just the third Black person elected Governor in US history.\par \par \uc2 And finally, former White House Press Secretary, Republican \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  was elected Governor of Arkansas. She is the first woman to lead the State and highest profile former Trump administration official elected to office.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations to all of these historic wins.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: To all of them. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really, really cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We remember Sarah Huckabee, of course, during those press briefings.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We sure do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When she was Donald Trump`s Communications Secretary and she is only 40 years old.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s right. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is a new trend --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But that Gen Z -- I love the story...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that story.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: ... of Maxwell Frost.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Twenty-five.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is 25 years old and just the notion -- I mean, do you remember what you were like at 25?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: My man is going to Congress.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So, yes, enough said about that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was just trying wake up one time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You turned out okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You turned out okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It took many, many years, Gayle. It took many, many tries.\par \par \uc2 All right, voters also decided some really important ballot measures. A handful of States put abortion rights on the ballot after the Supreme Court overturned Roe versus Wade back in June. Voters in Michigan, Vermont, and California approved measures aimed at protecting abortion rights. Kentucky voters were asked to change the State Constitution to say there is no right to abortion. The outcome is too early to call, but the vote count is leaning towards a victory for abortion rights groups.\par \par \uc2 In Montana, where abortion is legal, voters considered a ballot initiative that could mean criminal charges for healthcare workers who do not try and save an infant or fetus. We cannot project a result on that.\par \par \uc2 Marijuana reform was on the ballot in five States. Voters in Maryland and Missouri voted to legalize recreational marijuana, but in Arkansas and North Dakota and South Dakota, voters said, "No, thank you to legalization," you`re not going to be smoking weed there.\par \par \uc2 So there we go. That`s really, you know, those are some of the issues that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Aren`t you going to talk about Powerball?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hold on a second. After three months of no winner, I was...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: ... just doing the political stuff, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, okay, okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Now, I`m transitioning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I thought you were wrapping up.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle is like, get to the money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Show me the money, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, after three months of no winner and a night of technical issues, delaying the Powerball drawing, we finally now have a winner. Was it me? Was it you?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Was it you? Nate is still --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It wasn`t anybody in this room.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Sorry, Nate. Sorry, everybody in the newsroom. A single ticket sold at a gas station in a Los Angeles suburb raked in more than $2 billion before taxes, of course. It was the biggest lottery jackpot in world history.\par \par \uc2 The winner has not come forward.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s my advice, too. Do not come forward.\par \par \uc2 But in California, they have a year to claim the prize. When they do, their name will become public. Oh, wait. So, we`re saying that they haven`t even identified themselves?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, they are going to go public.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. But once they do, it will be made public. I know it`s only one ticket, but I really do hope it`s a group.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Okay, I got you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They`ll split amongst --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I hope it is one...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I hope that it is a big group.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: ... person who is like, "Mine."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. I hope it is a group.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like Scrooge McDuck. I`ve always wanted to do that, like dive into coins and swim in money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We had a guy here --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re going to have a concussion.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We had a guy here, Max who said, if he won, he would give everybody a million dollars.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s not possible.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Max. Holding you to it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Vlad. We appreciate you. All right, well, listen, we`ve got a lot more on the battle to control Congress coming up in just a little bit, and that`s going to be with Minnesota Senator, \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. Coming up in this hour, the latest on the Midterms and who will control Congress. We will speak with Democratic Senator, \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m Vladimir Duthiers. Actor, Terry Crews is here with his new book for kids. It`s about the difficulties of growing up fighting for your dreams.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson. If you like to laugh while you`re rocking out, we`ve got you covered. The great "Weird Al" Yankovic, he puts down his accordion and talks about his new movie based on his life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do like to laugh.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We not only won election, we have rewritten the political map.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : We bet on the people of Pennsylvania and you didn`t let us down.\par \par \uc2 JD VANCE (R) (Ohio Senator-elect): I will never forget the great people of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 JOSH SHAPIRO (Governor-elect of Pennsylvania): Because of you, our democracy endures.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: American democracy is alive and well. We`re following Midterm races all across the country as the results continue to come in.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The Senate still up for grabs after several key races went blue.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The critical Georgia Senate race between Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is still too close to call.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A top official with the Secretary of State`s office tweeted overnight, "We feel it is safe to say that there will be a runoff for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  here in Georgia."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  easily wins reelection in Florida as tensions build with former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP: I got him the nomination. He didn`t get it. I got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Republicans may still regain control of the House, but we did not see the red wave that many predicted.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL FROST (D) (Florida Congressman-elect): Know that your congressman loves you and will fight day in and day out.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it looks like Gen Z is in the House. Florida`s Maxwell Alejandro Frost is projected to be the first member of his generation to win a seat in Congress.\par \par \uc2 MAXWELL FROST: The Constitution says you`ve got to be at least 25 years old to serve in Congress. As a 25 year old, I`d say I`m not too young, I`m just on time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Just on time.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, we like him already.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not too young. I`m just on time.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow. Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is making their presence felt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: They really are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s begin this hour with the latest on the Midterms and where we stand right now.\par \par \uc2 With five Senate races still to call, it is still a tossup over who will control the chamber. One of those races, Georgia, may go to a runoff in December. The House is leaning Republican, but it`s nowhere near the red wave that many analysts were expecting.\par \par \uc2 We know whoever has the majority, it will be a narrow one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So one reason Republicans are not celebrating nationwide is this result in Pennsylvania. Democratic Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  beat Dr. Mehmet Oz to flip that seat blue.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan is in Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 And when you think about the amount of money spent in those ads, you could almost call this race a slugfest of sorts. Last night, we were at Fetterman`s campaign headquarters as he celebrated that victory over Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 But the question really is how did we get here? So we took a look at some of the polls coming in and we want to share that information with you. When you look at the gender gap, for instance, between the voters, women in the Keystone State backed Fetterman over Oz by 15 points, while men supported Oz by 13 points and it looks like concerns over abortion rights played a major role. This is one of the only States where abortion was more important to voters than inflation.\par \par \uc2 Something else worth noting that was interesting that happened here yesterday in Luzerne County made up of about 200,000 registered voters and went to Trump in 2020, well, the county ran out of paper ballots. Precincts were ordered to stay open until 10:00 PM versus closing at 8:00 PM.\par \par \uc2 As of this morning, though, the problem seems to have been remedied with the majority of votes now counted, but lawsuits are still ongoing in the fight over mail-in ballots after the state Supreme Court ruled that undated or incorrectly dated ballots would not be counted.\par \par \uc2 But even if those votes are counted, in the end, they are likely to favor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have not heard from the Oz campaign, but it appears that two big State races were won by Democrats. That would be Governor-elect Josh Shapiro and US Senator-elect, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jericka Duncan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 May I just say I like the Mary J. Blige vibe, you`ve got going on this morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You look very nice.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Listen, listen, listen, I forgot -- I lost my makeup bag, so this is the end result.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You look good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Looking good.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I have the glasses, and I am just out here winging it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have pulled it off beautifully. Pulled it off beautifully. Thank you very much Mary J., I mean, Jericka. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221112eib90008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Control Of Congress Up for Grabs\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Robert Costa, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers,  Anthony Salvanto \par \uc2 2,086 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS, today after Election Day. America has decided, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is still on the road in Florida heading up our coverage there. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Republicans still hope to take control of Congress, but a red wave failed to materialize as Democrats do much better than expected.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Clearly, Republicans over read the data and overshot their expectations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are in Georgia where a contentious race could be headed to a runoff. Why that still could determine which party takes control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): We`re going to have received more votes than my opponent, we know that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wins big. What it could mean for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2024.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Thank you for a historic landslide victory.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  becomes the first woman ever elected Governor of the State of New York.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Maryland just made history. Democrat Wes Moore will become the state`s first Black Governor.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): All year long, we`ve been waiting to find out if Republicans will take control of Congress and stop President Biden`s agenda, or if Democrats will keep control of Congress and somehow stopped Biden`s agenda.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): So we`re primarily focused on control of the House and the Senate. The Republicans owning a net gain of the one seat in the Senate and five seats in the house. Don`t know what`s going to happen, but here`s what we do know, there are two possible outcomes tonight, either the Democrats will get more votes and keep the Congress or the Democrats will get more votes lose the Congress. That`s the way our government works.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Stephen kind of summed it up pretty perfectly if you were watching us last night. That`s where we`re going to begin, too, with that big surprise in the Midterm Election results.\par \par \uc2 So far, we are not seeing that GOP surge that many Republicans have forecasted with five races still to call, it`s unclear who will control the Senate. You`ve got Nevada, Arizona, Wisconsin, Georgia, and Alaska still do not have a winner at this hour. Control of the House is leaning Republican, but only barely. Democrats overall are doing much better than expected.\par \par \uc2 Our chief election and campaign correspondent, that`s Robert Costa is following all of this.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you. Politics never gets old, does it?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It does not, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 This was not the night most Republicans expected. Their hope for a big red wave that never materialized. The GOP may yet win control of the House, but the Senate still up for grabs after several key races went blue.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) (Pennsylvania Senator-elect): I`m so humbled. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Pennsylvania`s Democratic Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  overcame any doubts about his stroke recovery with a narrow victory over television, Dr. Mehmet Oz. This Senate race one of the tightest and most watched.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : this campaign has always been about fighting for everyone who`s ever been got knocked down, that ever got back up.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The Keystone State`s Democratic Attorney General \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  nabbed the governor`s race, handily defeating Trump-backed Republican and 2020 election denier, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  who refuse to concede.\par \par \uc2 DOUG MASTRIANO\uc2  (R) (Pennsylvania Gubernatorial Candidate): We`re going to have faith and have patience. We`re going to wait until every vote counts, right?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): When they`re finished counting the votes from today`s election, that we`re going to have received more votes than my opponent, we know that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Down in Georgia, incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  barely outpolled football star and GOP challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and because neither is above 50 percent, Georgia law mandates a runoff, which is now expected for December 6th.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) (Georgia Senate Candidate): We`re in a fight, are we not? We are in a fight.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "JD.")\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Republican and Trump-backed JD Vance took a key open Senate seat in Ohio triumphing over Democrat, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): I have the privilege to concede this race to JD Vance because the way this country operates is that when you lose an election you concede.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In Wisconsin`s Senate race, it is too close to call. Republican incumbent, Ron Johnson is still battling Mandela Barnes for the win in the razor tight tossup. In battleground Arizona, Democrat and former astronaut Mark Kelly is edging ahead of Republican Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 And in the Governor`s race there, Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  has an edge over Trump loyalist, Kari Lake, but that raise might take days to decide and Lake has wasted little time in stoking electoral doubt about some technical issues with voting machines in Maricopa County, but officials there say every vote has and will be counted.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R) (Arizona Gubernatorial candidate): We had a big day today and don`t let those cheaters and crooks think anything different.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We have some races that are hot and heavy --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Probably more than former President Trump was predicting at his rallies leading up to Election Day. Exit polls show inflation and abortion were top of mind and economic headwinds were not enough for some of his backed candidates to fend off some pretty stiff competition, including in the House were predicted huge gains have faded.\par \par \uc2 But Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  might have moved closer to a possible 2024 presidential bid with a commanding victory over former Governor \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Freedom is here to stay.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Also in the Sunshine State, Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  picked up a double digit win over Representative Val Demings, and in Nevada, it is still a tossup, but currently the results show incumbent Democrat \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  trailing Republican Adam Laxalt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bob, thank you. I appreciate you. I know you`re not going anywhere, so we`ll check back in with you a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 To break this down for us, Vlad journeyed across the studio to speak with our elections and surveys director, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not a long journey, Nate, but Anthony is here so we`re feeling good.\par \par \uc2 All right, so let`s talk about the races that we`re still watching this morning that are too close to call, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: All right. Let`s start in Georgia. What`s left? Not a lot, 96 percent in. Where is it? Okay, we`ve got red areas here. Those are Republican, blue counties over there around Metro Atlanta. I`m going to show you where the remaining vote is. I am going to turn the state on its side. The higher bars mean more outstanding vote. It is around Atlanta that could favor Warnock, when it comes in, but we`ll see if anybody gets over 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 Next, I`m going to go up to Wisconsin, okay, and I`m going to show you Mandela Barnes is narrowly trailing. But here again, where is the outstanding vote environment, turn this on its side, and you`ll see the bar pop up around Milwaukee. That`s the one county where Barnes could do really well. So still too close to say.\par \par \uc2 Next, I`m going to take out, I`m going to go to Arizona. This one, Mark Kelly does have a lead, but still substantial number out, right? It is only 67 percent in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which you have leaning Dems.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: We do have it leaning Dem because he has got that lead, and then finally, Vlad. I am going to go out to Nevada. Here, we`ve got \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  down to Adam Laxalt. I`m going to turn this one on the side, and it is all about Las Vegas. All right, it`s all about Las Vegas. You`re going to see that pop up, so still want to vote out there? I don`t know if it`s early vote, I don`t know if it`s Election Day. Republicans tend to do better on Election Day. So when we find that out, we`ll be able to say more about the race.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. So Anthony, we know all throughout this election cycle, Republicans have been predicting a red wave, but \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  last night said, definitely not a red wave. So, where did Republicans go wrong?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Okay, let`s look at where we are in the House estimate right now. Republicans still heavy lead, It`s still leaning towards the Republicans, they could still get over 218, lot of tossups out there. There still at least 15 tossup races that we haven`t projected yet. So that remains to be seen. I think this is going to take a few days, at least take a few days to sort out. So 203 to 190 is our latest House estimate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you know, I was with the exit poll team all night last night and one of the things that we found that was really interesting is Independent voters. They were breaking for Republicans before October since October, breaking for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Democrats did well with Independents last night and you see abortion was a close second to inflation and the economy, on the minds of voters. We talked all the time about the restoring Roe voters, the women who made abortion and abortion rights a priority. They moved even more towards the Democrats, that helped them, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Anthony Salvanto. Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Nate, To be continued.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Vlad head over here to the table.\par \par \uc2 Georgia helped give Democrats a Senate Majority two years ago but it took a runoff to get there. State officials say it looks like we`re headed for another run off this year as neither Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  have hit the 50 percent threshold to win outright.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is outside Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning. So, what comes next?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Hey, yes, good morning to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Well, if this goes to a runoff, then there will be another Election Day in December. A top official with the Secretary of State`s office tweeted overnight: "We feel it is safe to say that there will be a runoff for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  here in Georgia slated for December 6th." Sources close to Republican, Herschel Walker`s campaign tell CBS News that they are waiting for outstanding votes in several counties north of Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Still, Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  says he is feeling good about the outcome.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a look at why this race is razor thin. This all comes down to the economy. CBS News exit poll showed inflation outpaced abortion in terms of importance to GOP voters, but it was abortion rights that was on the minds of Democrats and that was one of many issues that defined the Governor`s race with Republican incumbent, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  defeating Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  in a rematch to win a second term.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Abrams helped Democrats flip this State in 2020 with President Biden winning by a very narrow margin and of course, again in 2021 when Democrats picked up two Senate seats coincidentally in a runoff -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Nikole. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221112eib900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Control of Congress Up for Grabs; Georgia Senate Runoff; \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  Wins Big in Florida; Battle for Congress; Fetterman Flips Pennsylvania; Sen. \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  (D-MN) Live; Warming Readers` Hearts; Terry Crews Live; "Weird Al" Yankovic`s New Role, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nate Burleson, Vladimir Duthiers, Robert Costa,  Anthony Salvanto, Tony Dokoupil, Leslie Sanchez, Joel Payne, Nikole  Killion, Ed O`Keefe, Nancy Cordes, Scott MacFarlane, Jordan Steele, Jericka  Duncan \par \uc2 13,986 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS, today after Election Day. America has decided, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And I`m Vladimir Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tony is still on the road in Florida heading up our coverage there. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Republicans still hope to take control of Congress, but a red wave failed to materialize as Democrats do much better than expected.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Clearly, Republicans over read the data and overshot their expectations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We are in Georgia where a contentious race could be headed to a runoff. Why that still could determine which party takes control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): We`re going to have received more votes than my opponent, we know that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  wins big. What it could mean for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2024.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Thank you for a historic landslide victory.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  becomes the first woman ever elected Governor of the State of New York.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Maryland just made history. Democrat Wes Moore will become the state`s first Black Governor.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): All year long, we`ve been waiting to find out if Republicans will take control of Congress and stop President Biden`s agenda, or if Democrats will keep control of Congress and somehow stopped Biden`s agenda.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): So we`re primarily focused on control of the House and the Senate. The Republicans owning a net gain of the one seat in the Senate and five seats in the house. Don`t know what`s going to happen, but here`s what we do know, there are two possible outcomes tonight, either the Democrats will get more votes and keep the Congress or the Democrats will get more votes lose the Congress. That`s the way our government works.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Stephen kind of summed it up pretty perfectly if you were watching us last night. That`s where we`re going to begin, too, with that big surprise in the Midterm Election results.\par \par \uc2 So far, we are not seeing that GOP surge that many Republicans have forecasted with five races still to call, it`s unclear who will control the Senate. You`ve got Nevada, Arizona, Wisconsin, Georgia, and Alaska still do not have a winner at this hour. Control of the House is leaning Republican, but only barely. Democrats overall are doing much better than expected.\par \par \uc2 Our chief election and campaign correspondent, that`s Robert Costa is following all of this.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you. Politics never gets old, does it?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: It does not, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 This was not the night most Republicans expected. Their hope for a big red wave that never materialized. The GOP may yet win control of the House, but the Senate still up for grabs after several key races went blue.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) (Pennsylvania Senator-elect): I`m so humbled. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Pennsylvania`s Democratic Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  overcame any doubts about his stroke recovery with a narrow victory over television, Dr. Mehmet Oz. This Senate race one of the tightest and most watched.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : this campaign has always been about fighting for everyone who`s ever been got knocked down, that ever got back up.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The Keystone State`s Democratic Attorney General Josh Shapiro nabbed the governor`s race, handily defeating Trump-backed Republican and 2020 election denier, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  who refuse to concede.\par \par \uc2 DOUG MASTRIANO\uc2  (R) (Pennsylvania Gubernatorial Candidate): We`re going to have faith and have patience. We`re going to wait until every vote counts, right?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-GA): When they`re finished counting the votes from today`s election, that we`re going to have received more votes than my opponent, we know that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Down in Georgia, incumbent Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  barely outpolled football star and GOP challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , and because neither is above 50 percent, Georgia law mandates a runoff, which is now expected for December 6th.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) (Georgia Senate Candidate): We`re in a fight, are we not? We are in a fight.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "JD.")\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Republican and Trump-backed JD Vance took a key open Senate seat in Ohio triumphing over Democrat, \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 REP. TIM RYAN (D-OH): I have the privilege to concede this race to JD Vance because the way this country operates is that when you lose an election you concede.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In Wisconsin`s Senate race, it is too close to call. Republican incumbent, Ron Johnson is still battling Mandela Barnes for the win in the razor tight tossup. In battleground Arizona, Democrat and former astronaut Mark Kelly is edging ahead of Republican Blake Masters.\par \par \uc2 And in the Governor`s race there, Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  has an edge over Trump loyalist, Kari Lake, but that raise might take days to decide and Lake has wasted little time in stoking electoral doubt about some technical issues with voting machines in Maricopa County, but officials there say every vote has and will be counted.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R) (Arizona Gubernatorial candidate): We had a big day today and don`t let those cheaters and crooks think anything different.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): We have some races that are hot and heavy --\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Probably more than former President Trump was predicting at his rallies leading up to Election Day. Exit polls show inflation and abortion were top of mind and economic headwinds were not enough for some of his backed candidates to fend off some pretty stiff competition, including in the House were predicted huge gains have faded.\par \par \uc2 But Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  might have moved closer to a possible 2024 presidential bid with a commanding victory over former Governor \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Freedom is here to stay.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Also in the Sunshine State, Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  picked up a double digit win over Representative Val Demings, and in Nevada, it is still a tossup, but currently the results show incumbent Democrat \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  trailing Republican \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Bob, thank you. I appreciate you. I know you`re not going anywhere, so we`ll check back in with you a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 To break this down for us, Vlad journeyed across the studio to speak with our elections and surveys director, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Not a long journey, Nate, but Anthony is here so we`re feeling good.\par \par \uc2 All right, so let`s talk about the races that we`re still watching this morning that are too close to call, Anthony.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: All right. Let`s start in Georgia. What`s left? Not a lot, 96 percent in. Where is it? Okay, we`ve got red areas here. Those are Republican, blue counties over there around Metro Atlanta. I`m going to show you where the remaining vote is. I am going to turn the state on its side. The higher bars mean more outstanding vote. It is around Atlanta that could favor Warnock, when it comes in, but we`ll see if anybody gets over 50 percent.\par \par \uc2 Next, I`m going to go up to Wisconsin, okay, and I`m going to show you Mandela Barnes is narrowly trailing. But here again, where is the outstanding vote environment, turn this on its side, and you`ll see the bar pop up around Milwaukee. That`s the one county where Barnes could do really well. So still too close to say.\par \par \uc2 Next, I`m going to take out, I`m going to go to Arizona. This one, Mark Kelly does have a lead, but still substantial number out, right? It is only 67 percent in.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Which you have leaning Dems.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: We do have it leaning Dem because he has got that lead, and then finally, Vlad. I am going to go out to Nevada. Here, we`ve got \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  down to \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 . I`m going to turn this one on the side, and it is all about Las Vegas. All right, it`s all about Las Vegas. You`re going to see that pop up, so still want to vote out there? I don`t know if it`s early vote, I don`t know if it`s Election Day. Republicans tend to do better on Election Day. So when we find that out, we`ll be able to say more about the race.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. So Anthony, we know all throughout this election cycle, Republicans have been predicting a red wave, but \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  last night said, definitely not a red wave. So, where did Republicans go wrong?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Okay, let`s look at where we are in the House estimate right now. Republicans still heavy lead, It`s still leaning towards the Republicans, they could still get over 218, lot of tossups out there. There still at least 15 tossup races that we haven`t projected yet. So that remains to be seen. I think this is going to take a few days, at least take a few days to sort out. So 203 to 190 is our latest House estimate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you know, I was with the exit poll team all night last night and one of the things that we found that was really interesting is Independent voters. They were breaking for Republicans before October since October, breaking for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Democrats did well with Independents last night and you see abortion was a close second to inflation and the economy, on the minds of voters. We talked all the time about the restoring Roe voters, the women who made abortion and abortion rights a priority. They moved even more towards the Democrats, that helped them, too.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, Anthony Salvanto. Thank you, my friend.\par \par \uc2 Nate, To be continued.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Vlad head over here to the table.\par \par \uc2 Georgia helped give Democrats a Senate Majority two years ago but it took a runoff to get there. State officials say it looks like we`re headed for another run off this year as neither Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  have hit the 50 percent threshold to win outright.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is outside Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning. So, what comes next?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Hey, yes, good morning to you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Well, if this goes to a runoff, then there will be another Election Day in December. A top official with the Secretary of State`s office tweeted overnight: "We feel it is safe to say that there will be a runoff for the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  here in Georgia slated for December 6th." Sources close to Republican, Herschel Walker`s campaign tell CBS News that they are waiting for outstanding votes in several counties north of Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Still, Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  says he is feeling good about the outcome.\par \par \uc2 Here`s a look at why this race is razor thin. This all comes down to the economy. CBS News exit poll showed inflation outpaced abortion in terms of importance to GOP voters, but it was abortion rights that was on the minds of Democrats and that was one of many issues that defined the Governor`s race with Republican incumbent, \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  defeating Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  in a rematch to win a second term.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Abrams helped Democrats flip this State in 2020 with President Biden winning by a very narrow margin and of course, again in 2021 when Democrats picked up two Senate seats coincidentally in a runoff -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Nikole. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, there is no doubt about the results of a series of big wins for Republicans. Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  sailed to victory and sailed is the word here in his reelection campaign, beating former Governor \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In his victory speech last night, DeSantis thanked a long list of supporters, but he did not mention \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The former President watched the results at his Mar-a-Lago resort and called it a great evening, but he also tried to warn the Governor against running again anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 Our Tony Dokoupil is in Miami where he has been covering the Florida campaign, his home state. Tony, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Based on your "Three Meals" yesterday, you`re not surprised that this was the outcome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. Good morning, my friend.\par \par \uc2 We definitely saw this one coming. And good morning to all of you at home. The Midterms are of course a mile marker for America, but they are also, let`s be real here, a starting gun for candidates hoping to make a White House run in 2024.\par \par \uc2 So here comes former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  already teasing that "big announcement" next week, and he is firing an Election Day warning shot at one of his biggest potential rivals in 2024 and one of last night`s biggest winners.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With a big night in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Freedom is here to stay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In a double digit win over Democrat \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): We not only won election, we have rewritten the political map.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  kept a dizzying four-year rise. Narrowly elected as Governor in 2018, now widely talked up as a potential White House contender in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Among his gains last night, more than 80,000 votes in Miami`s traditionally blue Dade County.\par \par \uc2 And compared to four years ago, he picked up voters statewide in nearly every demographic, including women and Latino voters, a group he lost by 10 points last time.\par \par \uc2 But on maybe the biggest day of Ron DeSantis`s political career, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  held his own Election Night event, tallying what he claimed were his victories of candidates he endorsed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Eighty wins and three losses, is that good?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And in a series of interviews, the former President took credit for DeSantis being Governor at all.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I got him the nomination. He didn`t get it. I got it. I thought that he could have been more gracious, but that`s up to him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In comments to "The Wall Street Journal," he also called a possible DeSantis White House run a mistake. And then a warning: "If he did run, I will tell you things about him that won`t be very flattering. I know more about him than anybody other than perhaps his wife, who is really running his campaign."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And when I read that line, guys, it really did take me back to 2016. It feels like a call back in fact to 2016. That was of course when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  used any means necessary from insults to innuendo to clear a field of GOP contenders including, by the way another Florida Governor, that would be Jeb Bush, and another of last night`s big winners, \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As for what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is thinking this morning, we just don`t know the words \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and White House, they did not appear in his big speech last night, guys.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is interesting Tony that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would accuse him of not being gracious and then say words like that. We thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see a little bit later on.\par \par \uc2 Two longtime political pros from both parties are with us now. They are Republican strategist and CBS News political analyst, Leslie Sanchez, and Democratic strategist Joel Payne, who is a CBS News political contributor.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you both.\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Leslie, I want to start with you -- good morning -- because it`s the night that Democrats had worried about and the night Republicans had hoped for, neither materialized. So let`s say what happened to this big Republican wave we all kept hearing was going to happen.\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: The Republican wave turned into a Republican sprinkle. It certainly did not have the winning -- the degree of wins, Gayle, that many people anticipated.\par \par \uc2 What is fascinating about it is for all of the polls and people`s lead up that Republicans were going to be able to tilt some of those blue, traditionally Democratic seats, Republicans had a hard time kind of holding their own. While we did do well in Florida, Oklahoma, Ohio, North Carolina, it is very frustrating and you`re hearing a lot of conversations already internally and a lot of the blame game about what went wrong.\par \par \uc2 And two things they`re talking about. One is the Trump factor. And two, what does the future of the party look like externally, like on a national level, and everybody is talking about DeSantis. So, there is a lot to unpack this morning.\par \par \uc2 But I will say, the positives are Republican Governors did well. Greg Abbott won in Texas for his third term, and you`re going to be hearing a lot about Latino vote. Republicans did incredibly well on Latino vote in a lot of those swing areas, and they solidified in Texas and Florida as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We can only imagine the jockeying that`s going on behind the scenes in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Joel, Democrats though overall did much better than expected as well. What worked for them?\par \par \uc2 JOEL PAYNE: Well, look outside of Florida, I would say, Democrats, I think figured out one, that abortion could be an issue that could work for them, particularly in a state like Pennsylvania. That looks like that is what`s going to carry \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  to win that race. It`s almost like there`s a silent abortion vote that showed up in the electorate that wasn`t showing up in the public opinion polling, and I think that that`s going to be something that a lot of Democrats are going to look to as, "Hey, is this an issue we can continue to be opportunistic about?"\par \par \uc2 I also think maybe Democrats held their own a little bit more on talking about the economy. You know, I talked to people who put these campaign ads together, run these campaigns. They said that the majority of their messaging was about the economy, was about fighting inflation. And by the way, it helped that President Biden and Democrats had that Inflation Reduction Act back in September to count as a victory.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Leslie, you mentioned former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . Exit polls show that Republicans want Governor DeSantis to run for President in 2024, not \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . What does that mean for 2024?\par \par \uc2 LESLIE SANCHEZ: There`s still a tremendous amount of Trump fatigue, Nate, and even internally, and you`re going to see that division among Republicans and it falls along gender lines.\par \par \uc2 Traditionally, you see a lot more men -- Republican men who are excited about a Trump candidacy, the potential run again. But when you dig a little bit deeper and look at women voters, communities of color that have traditionally -- have started to move into the Republican Party, there`s not so much a warm welcome. They want to see diversity of thought. They want to see different type of leadership and quite honestly, people are tired and exhausted from all of the baggage and kind of the cloud that surrounds any type of Trump campaign.\par \par \uc2 That said, there is tremendous excitement still on the ground. You cannot deny the support that the former President has and his ability to raise money, motivate individuals to take sides, so it is to be determined.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it. He wanted to be a kingmaker during the Midterms, not sure if that happened, but Joel Payne and Leslie Sanchez, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We have much more coverage of the 2022 elections coming up, including some of the issues that were on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, the latest on the race to control Congress. We will show you which way the House of Representatives is likely to go.\par \par \uc2 Plus, there are Hurricane Warnings for Florida as Tropical Storm Nicole moves closer. Oh no. We`ll find out when and where this storm is likely to hit.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s 2022 and "Weird Al" Yankovic is hotter than ever.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC (Singer): I run into artists at parties and award shows and things like that and they almost always say, "When are you going to get around to one of my songs?" I developed a bit of a track record now to the point where it`s kind of considered a rite of passage.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up, the five-time Grammy winner talks with Jamie Wax about being portrayed by actor, Daniel Radcliffe.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . on the Midterm Elections that so far have done very little to shift the balance of power in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Now, we should emphasize, there are still a lot of races to be decided. With that said, let`s bring in our top White House Correspondents, Ed O`Keefe and Nancy Cordes, two political pros that belong in the White House Hall of Fame to break this all down.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, it`s a better night than expected for the Democrats. What are you hearing from the White House?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: A big change of mood from the White House in the past 24 hours. Yesterday, they were trying to pre explain why it wouldn`t be a big deal. If they lost a bunch of seats. Now, they`re saying, wait, maybe we didn`t lose a bunch of seats. Maybe we just lost a handful of seats, they`re saying.\par \par \uc2 They feel excitement mixed with some validation because they managed, they think, to buck a historic trend. They didn`t lose dozens of seats the way previous Presidents have.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And we were calling out the fact that the President didn`t go everywhere. The places he went, seemed a little off given where we thought the battlegrounds were, but all the different places --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People didn`t want him to go.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And the ones who didn`t are the ones struggling, but where he did go, they held on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: All right, and you know, it`s a signal, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , especially. There was somebody who have completely embraced the Biden legacy, the Biden White has said, "Come on down and help me out." He came. Former President Obama came and he eked it out.\par \par \uc2 A bunch of races he went to in New Mexico and California and Illinois and other places like that, but we all went, "Why are you going there?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  here in New York, twice he came here to help her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And she pulled it off. So, the lesson is don`t bet against \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t bet against Biden.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Politically, the guy always finds a way it seems, and that`s what the White House is preparing privately, maybe, he`ll say it publicly at some point. He knows what he is doing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It will be interesting to see what he is going to say. Of course, we`re expecting to hear from him at some point today. But let the jokes begin about the red wave. I`ve heard it called, you know, the red sprinkler. I`ve heard called the red splash, the red puddle. Needless to say, there was not this red wave, not this red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, did you see anything earlier in the evening that might have indicated that this is how it would turn out?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They were iron races in Rhode Island, New Hampshire, Connecticut, Indiana, and Virginia. They said, look, we`re going to win those early. It`s going to be the start of it. None of them materialized. They got one raised in Virginia, where \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had won pretty easily back in 2020. Otherwise, the Democrats prevailed in all those other races and they were parts of the country where they were anticipating being able to take districts that had been Democratic for decades, or for like a hundred years, down in Texas, for example, and it just didn`t materialize.\par \par \uc2 Democrats saying look, Midterm voting patterns in these parts of the country at least stayed the same. Abortion clearly was a factor, you saw them playing that up, and there will probably be some indication that the President`s focus in the closing days on issues like democracy, reminding people what had happened on January 6th may have also helped close the gap with Independents.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So Nancy, former President Trump, as you know, wanted to play kingmaker in these Midterm Elections. I saw a lot of prominent Republicans tweeting yesterday that this is primarily on Trump`s shoulders, the candidates that he endorsed did not do as good as well as expected. So, what does that mean for him looming large over the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Some notable high profile stumbles by candidates that were not favored by the Republican establishment, but were anointed by former President Trump. I`m talking about Dr. Mehmet Oz in Pennsylvania, Blake Masters who is trailing right now in Arizona, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  failing to clear 50 percent in Georgia, that State now goes to a runoff.\par \par \uc2 Republicans argue this is another reason to turn the page from former President Trump, but the reality is he still has so much power with the base. And frankly, he is going to call it a victory no matter what.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And one notable thing with those Republican gubernatorial candidates, especially who never committed to certifying 2020 election results or they say they -- wouldn`t say that they would have, all of them lost. The only one we`re waiting on is Kari Lake, who of course, was one of the most vociferous deniers of the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 Again, a signal --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s saying a lot, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes, it is voters who are saying, "Hold on a second, like you`re not in line with actual factual results."\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: And that should not be a tough State for a Republican to win.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: After last night, you think he`s going to run next week?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The guy says he is running. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, he said he has a big announcement. Didn`t say he is running.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well -- oh, you`re talking about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ? Are we talking about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m talking about Trump.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Trump.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, Trump says he has got a big announcement next week and - -\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Big, big announcement.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: We`ll see when the big, big announcement comes, whether -- because we`ve been hearing for the last several months that he was several weeks away from saying something, so let`s see if he makes good next week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy, Ed, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That White House Hall of Fame will be by the vending machine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, it`s by the microwaves.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: We know exactly where that is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we appreciate you guys.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a new potential hurricane that is threatening Florida. We`ll have the latest on Tropical Storm Nicole and the chance that some parts of the State will be hit again.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The biggest change we will see from the Midterm Elections is likely to be in the House of Representatives. Republicans have gained a small number of seats and that might be enough to take control, but dozens of races are still undecided. So Scott MacFarlane is watching that battle.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning. What do you know?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Vlad, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 It turns out democracy and election denialism did play a role in these elections. Democrats defied some of their own expectations going into yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start off in Virginia 