will be a narrow one.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So one reason Republicans are not celebrating nationwide is this result in Pennsylvania. Democratic Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  beat Dr. Mehmet Oz to flip that seat blue.\par \par \uc2 Jericka Duncan is in Pittsburgh.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 And when you think about the amount of money spent in those ads, you could almost call this race a slugfest of sorts. Last night, we were at Fetterman`s campaign headquarters as he celebrated that victory over Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 But the question really is how did we get here? So we took a look at some of the polls coming in and we want to share that information with you. When you look at the gender gap, for instance, between the voters, women in the Keystone State backed Fetterman over Oz by 15 points, while men supported Oz by 13 points and it looks like concerns over abortion rights played a major role. This is one of the only States where abortion was more important to voters than inflation.\par \par \uc2 Something else worth noting that was interesting that happened here yesterday in Luzerne County made up of about 200,000 registered voters and went to Trump in 2020, well, the county ran out of paper ballots. Precincts were ordered to stay open until 10:00 PM versus closing at 8:00 PM.\par \par \uc2 As of this morning, though, the problem seems to have been remedied with the majority of votes now counted, but lawsuits are still ongoing in the fight over mail-in ballots after the state Supreme Court ruled that undated or incorrectly dated ballots would not be counted.\par \par \uc2 But even if those votes are counted, in the end, they are likely to favor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have not heard from the Oz campaign, but it appears that two big State races were won by Democrats. That would be Governor-elect Josh Shapiro and US Senator-elect, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jericka Duncan, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 May I just say I like the Mary J. Blige vibe, you`ve got going on this morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You look very nice.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Listen, listen, listen, I forgot -- I lost my makeup bag, so this is the end result.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You look good.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Looking good.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: I have the glasses, and I am just out here winging it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have pulled it off beautifully. Pulled it off beautifully. Thank you very much Mary J., I mean, Jericka. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 For more on the Midterm results so far and what they mean, we`re joined now by someone who knows a thing or two about running for office, Senator \uc2 Amy Klobuchar\uc2  of Minnesota joins us.\par \par \uc2 Good to see you, Senator. You know, the Democrats did much better than expected last night.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. We`re very glad you got up early with us because I know -- I`ll bet it was a long night for you, too.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): Yes, three hours sleep. I wanted her sunglasses as well inside. That was what I was thinking during her entire report, but continue on.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. Senator, we can all relate to that, we really can.\par \par \uc2 But listen, the Democrats did much better than expected last night. What if anything surprised you about the showing?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 AMY KLOBUCHAR\uc2  (D-MN): Well, a number of things. So many Republicans and pundits were out there predicting this red tide, and let`s just say those predictions did not age well.\par \par \uc2 First thing is we won a number of Governor`s races, as you have just noted, across the country, including in states like Minnesota and Michigan, including a red, red state like Kansas where Governor Kelly was reelected.\par \par \uc2 Then you go to the House of Representatives. Since 1974, the average levels of House seats in a Midterm has been 23, and so we have literally defied the tides of history.\par \par \uc2 No matter what happens, we know it`s going to be incredibly close in the House with people like \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2  winning our seat. Finally, the Senate, we predicted this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that was a big one.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 AMY KLOBUCHAR\uc2  (D-MN): Our incumbents are doing incredibly well and ahead, and Fetterman winning in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But privately, many Democrats were worried. You weren`t concerned last night going into the races? I know the Republicans were saying they --\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 AMY KLOBUCHAR\uc2  (D-MN): Oh, I was just really relaxed, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were? Okay.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): Everyone was concerned. Everyone was concerned because of all the money that had been spent against our candidates. The angry, angry types of ads that we saw on TV and what I think is amazing about the voters. Number one, they showed up in big numbers, many of the election deniers lost. The voters have understood that democracy was on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 Number two, I think we were pushed at for pushing on the issue of reproductive rights. There are a lot of criticisms. But for a number of voters, that mattered. They showed up at the polls because of the outrageous decision by the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 Third thing is inflation, costs. Our candidates have pragmatic answers of bringing costs down for families, including taking on the pharma companies, and I think the people of this country understood more than the pollsters and the pundits thought that Democrats have been doing a whole lot to help, and that in fact, the Republicans didn`t really have a plan and they were playing politics with it, with a problem that`s a worldwide inflation problem.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Yes, Senator, you mentioned those ads and as you know, messaging does matter. Exit polls show voters trust Republicans more than Democrats on issues like crime and the economy. What can Democrats do to change that moving forward?\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): Great question. I think number one, as the House recently did, putting forward plans to go after crime, and that includes funding the police.\par \par \uc2 Right before we broke the Congress to come back home, there was a major vote in the House on funding the police. I lead the bill on funding community, police officers with \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2  of Alaska. I think that is going to be really important going forward that we continue to make that clear, and that we actually help to solve this problem and there are many solutions including mental health, including recruiting police officers. That fentanyl, many things that we have to deal with.\par \par \uc2 The second thing is of course on inflation. To me, that means continuing this work on workforce training, on the supply chain disruptions, and bringing the shipping costs down, and doing more when it comes to pharmaceutical costs and housing and childcare instead of focusing on enshrining tax cuts for the wealthy, which was basically the Republican plan, as well as Social Security and Medicare. Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Senator, it`s Vlad. Let me just ask you about something that you are critical of throughout this election cycle. You are critical of the party strategy of spending millions of dollars supporting election deniers in Republican primaries, because they thought they would be easier to win in General Elections. It seems to have worked, but I don`t know, do you think it`s a good strategy going forward?\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): Again, that will all be analyzed after we`re not after three hours sleep here of what happened. I think you see this going on both sides. No, I don`t like it.\par \par \uc2 I believe in our democracy, but all of this can continue and will continue to happen on both sides, unless we have some kind of campaign finance reform in place and to me, that means the Freedom to Vote Act, it means a constitutional amendment to overturn \uc2 Citizens United\uc2 , the angry ugly ads coming from untold sources of money that no one can figure out, shadow contributions. That`s got to end. It`s really bad for our democracy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Senator Klobuchar, just like many of us here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 , you make three hours of sleep look good. Thank you for joining us. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 SEN. AMY KLOBUCHAR (D-MN): I am going to remember that. I will get me through my day. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re welcome. All right, ahead, we will talk with -- yes, oh, we will talk to her later -- we have best-selling authors Angie Thomas and Dhonielle Clayton about their new book "Whiteout," but first. It is 8:10, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Coming up: Actor, Terry Crews digs into his childhood in his new kid`s book. He will tell us why he wants to encourage children to dream big, no matter what.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: With temperatures falling and winter on the way, our next guests have written a story that aims to warm readers hearts. Angie Thomas and Dhonielle Clayton are two of the six authors behind "Whiteout." The novel is a celebration of Black joy and young love in snowy Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 It`s a follow up to their "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " bestseller, "Blackout." We spoke with the authors last year about the novel. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS (Author): One thing that "Blackout" does, and it does beautifully is that it affirms to Black kids that you can be the center of the love story, and we`re telling them over and over again that their lives matter, but we are telling them in the context of trauma.\par \par \uc2 And I think that by showing various kinds of love stories, it really brings that message home that your lives matter, your dreams matter, your love matters, your hope matters -- all of it. All of it matters.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON (Author): I think we`re like the Captain Planet of --\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: I love it.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Like all of our forces combined has now created this wonderful experience that as we move through the city and move through the stories and meet the characters, it comes alive.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Angie Thomas and Dhonielle Clayton joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, so, Dhonielle, how did the idea for "Whiteout" come about?\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Well, I was talking to my niece who is 16 and she kept saying, "Why don`t Black girls get to be the center of love stories, and film and TV?" And she was really upset about it. And we thought, "Whoa, that`s right." And so we did "Blackout," which is heat. And then we have a couple of Christmas Queens that are part of our six, you know, what I mean, six, so we thought Black people in the snow. Super cute.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: True.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And super unusual. Black people and the snow.\par \par \uc2 But Angie, I`m fascinated by all of you authors together. How do you decide who`s going to write what? Because there`s six of you.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How do you divide up what?\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Well, you know, we call them leadership skills that Dhonielle here has. She basically came to us and she said, "You know what, we have -- there are these tropes in literature, and they so often use like, you know, best friends to lovers, blah, blah, blah, but we rarely get to see Black kids in these tropes." So she`s like, "I`m going to assign you a trope, and I want you to use that and write that story." And so, it was.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I like that. I like that a lot.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: You know, we`re going to.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell us some of the tropes.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Well, we have enemies to lovers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Best friends to lovers. What`s another one? A triangle.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Triangle. And the big one is the grovel.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What`s the grovel?\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: A second chance. You`ve messed up and you`ve got to --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, you messed up and you`ve got to grovel.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: You`ve got to grovel. You`ve got to fix it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And there`s some big groveling in this.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love that you say it is a love story that feels like a big glorious puzzle, because sometimes falling in love feels like a puzzle. Isn`t that good?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. That`s true.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s brilliant.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: And it`s hard and when you`re a teenager and you have all of these feelings.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Trying to figure out how to talk about those feelings and have the courage to love is really hard. You`re a puzzle yourself. So, we wanted to make the book feel like a puzzle of many different kinds of love stories and one big messy love story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, so you -- it does deal with serious topics like sexual identity, mental health. How do you navigate those storylines, so that they resonate with readers?\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Well I think the main thing is as young adult authors, we respect our readers, we respect what they`re going through, we respect their struggles, we respect their triumphs, and we listen to them. And in writing for them, I think that`s the most important thing you have to do.\par \par \uc2 So we`re also talking about, you know, young people who are dealing with things we`ve all dealt with.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: You know, anxiety, and all of these things. We`ve dealt with these. So we`re going from our own experiences as well and showing that hey, here are characters who look like you, who are going through these same things, and hopefully normalizing it for them a little more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There`s a great scene in the book that I cracked up. It`s a Omari and Jordan and they`re in the car and they`re driving and everybody knows, if you`re driving, you have control of the radio.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Everybody knows that.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Everybody knows that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He kept changing the radio station.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Reckless.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jordan was driving and she was very upset, and then he changed on a Beyonce song.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: You do not do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she gets very angry.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And so is that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s how people get cut, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s how people get killed.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Yes, you lose a life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, I was wondering how much of it you drew from your own personal experiences, but I thought something like that is so relatable and so true, and you`re writing in teenage voices, and no disrespect, neither one of y`all is a teenager.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: We are not.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But you look it.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: We are still spring chickens, but because we love and respect the kids that we`re writing for, the teens that we`re writing for, we want to honor their experiences.\par \par \uc2 I was a teacher for 13 years. I`m writing for my students in East Harlem, and also, I`m remembering myself at 16. I was a mess. I didn`t have the courage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You were a mess?\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: A mess.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Share Dhonielle.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: I was more like hate, hate, hate. Ooh, I like, I like, I like. Oh, no, I`m in love. So that`s like probably what I`m like, as an adult, too. But like you know, it is hard.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Yes. That is her as an adult as well.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Yes, it is hard to tell someone you love them. And so we wanted to really sort of channel our teenage selves, but also the teenagers that we work with that we see across the country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DHONIELLE CLAYTON: Honor those experiences. It`s messy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Angie, according to Pan America, books involving LGBTQ themes are being banned more and more. What`s your message to those that are making it harder for books like these to hit the shelves and reaching readers?\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Yes. Books featuring LGBTQI+ characters, books featuring Black characters. You know, my books are some of the top 10 most banned books in the country, and I think what I wish people would think about is the message that it is sending to the kids who see themselves in these stories.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re writing mirrors for them, and they see themselves in these characters and when you`re saying "Ban this book," what you`re essentially saying is "You make me uncomfortable, I don`t want to hear from you."\par \par \uc2 What I wish more people would do is talk to the kids who are reading these books, who see themselves in these books and ask them how has this impacted you? I have yet to have a kid telling me the hate you give make them feel bad for who they are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: You know, if nothing else, it`s opened their eyes and it expanded the world for them and there is nothing wrong with that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it is empowering.\par \par \uc2 ANGIE THOMAS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Angie Thomas and Dhonielle Clayton, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congrats.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Whiteout," it is on sale now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Love will keep you warm.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Make sure you grab it because it is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Love will keep you warm.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up: A new movie tells the story of "Weird Al" Yankovic.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead: Actor, Terry Crews, he has got a new kid`s book because you say you should never stop --\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS (Actor and author): Dreaming.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Dreaming. Deadlines to dreams --\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey, did they pick that because of what we were talking about? Don`t change Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, and I just said to Nate, that was funny when he said, "You get cut changing a song from Beyonce on the radio."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And Gayle said, "You get killed."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Killed.\par \par \uc2 So welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 Nate, what do you have for us?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, I`ll kick this thing off. So, I wanted to remix something we heard a long time ago when we were kids by Mother Goose.\par \par \uc2 So there once was a couple who lived in a shoe. After purchasing their home, they didn`t know what to do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What to do, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So they came up with the plan to make plenty of bread. They thought about selling it, but they went to \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  instead. So, I want you to check out this shoe house that is obviously built like a shoe. It was built in 1948 to help promote a shoe chain and it has been used as a honeymoon suite, an ice cream shop, a tourist attraction.\par \par \uc2 The house has been given nicknames like The Shoe Lace Space, In Step Suite, and Ankle Abode. Now you can rent it a few days a week and it`s only $269.00 a night.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who is going?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think I might go. It is in Pennsylvania for those who are curious. That`s not too far from where we are.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: At least, you can say you slept in a place that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Live in a shoe.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . is shaped like a shoe.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, so that`s Nate and Vlad and Cole in the control room. Brian, you want to go? Brian, you want to go? Klaus, you want to go?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Anybody else want to join us? Having a shoe party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mike, do you want to go? Tony?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, I bet you`re going to feel like a high heel if you stay in there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m going to pass.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is about a bride in South Carolina. Now, before we show the video I want you to pay attention to the groom`s face. This is what caught my eye. It`s hilarious.\par \par \uc2 So, this bride is letting her groom know exactly how she felt about waiting such a long time to get married.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Ten Oh Eight Films via Storyful.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think that is hilarious. Christie Jefferies got quite a laugh from everybody at her wedding, including her husband-to-be, Byron, when she just blew the dust off of her vows. Why? Because the couple has dated for 15 years before exchanging vows.\par \par \uc2 She says it was all in good fun. Christie and Brian say laughter is the key to their relationship, and I think that that`s great. She says -- this is a statement from the couple, it is all good even though she did kind of ghost him at his own wedding.\par \par \uc2 I love his facial expression.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, his face is so great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The dust symbolize all of the love they have given each other until the point, but also represented a new beginning for them as a husband and wife. She said that`s her way of telling him, they should have been married at least five years ago.\par \par \uc2 Congratulations. Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Congratulations.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The look on his face is like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They`ve got a sense of humor.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like what? What did I do? What did I do wrong?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He knew.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And why are you all laughing?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Why are you all laughing at me? It is the dust on those vows.\par \par \uc2 All right, my "Talk of the Table" is actor Terry Crews.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is of course, best known for his action and comedy roles and that includes "Everybody Hates Chris," "Brooklyn Nine, Nine" and of course, the cult classic film, "White Chicks." He is also the host of the hit competition show "America`s Got Talent."\par \par \uc2 Now, Terry Crews is out with a new kid`s book. It is called "Terry`s Crew." Yes. It tells the story of young Terry who faces challenges navigating the complexities of being a middle schooler with big dreams.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, we love saying that, Terry Crews is here in studio with us. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Good morning. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s too early for you to be popping your pecs, man.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: I`ll tell you. It is in my contract. I have got to do that everywhere.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: People at home are trying to eat their oatmeal. You`ve got your pecs popping.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: That`s how you do it. Pop them up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Continue, Terry. Continue.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let me try. Let me try.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nothing.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Bam, bam, bam.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I can`t. I got nothing, Terry. I got nothing. That`s what I felt like in middle school. I got nothing. I am up against guys like you, like Nate, athletes, studs and me, the opposite of that. This is for me.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Well, this is the thing. You know, I was an artist though. When I was a kid, I was an artist who wanted to be an athlete, who wanted both worlds. When I would go to football practice. They were like, "Man, you`ve got to get that art up, man. You might break your hand."\par \par \uc2 When I go to art class, they would look at me like, "You`re just too big jock. You don`t understand us." And I wanted both. And I never gave up. It was my quest to become a multi hyphenate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: And that`s what this book is, it is really about never ever giving up your dreams because sometimes your dream may -- it may cause you to not fit in. You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 And so my whole thing was to convince kids that always hold on. I was a big dreamer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: And my problem was, as a kid, I was always telling everybody what my dreams were. And they were like, "Hey, man, you`ve got to be realistic."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: But the thing is, is that optimism is realistic. If you just continue; if you always, always go for your goals, and I have to let kids know that it is okay to continue to do your thing and you`re going to face some adversity when you do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But the character in the book has teachers that tell him "Um, no that`s -- that you`re talented, but you`re not that talented. You can`t do that." Or "Maybe you shouldn`t aim so high."\par \par \uc2 Did something like that happen to you?\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: It did. I remember, you know, sometimes people aren`t -- they mean well, because they want to save you the pain of being disappointed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How do you mean well when it is causing somebody`s dreams, Terry?\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Well, this is the thing. They don`t know they`re doing that. They think they`re being realistic.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They are saving you from failure.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: But this is the thing, I had a friend I wanted to play -- you know, again, I wanted to play football. And I said, "Man, you know, I want to be a football player." And I had a teacher who took me to a \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  football game. I was in Flint, Michigan, I went over to Michigan, and I went with him and I fell in love.\par \par \uc2 I was like, "Oh, my God, this is amazing." A hundred and five thousand people in the stadium. And on the way back to Flint, in the car. I said, "I`m going to Michigan." And he looked at me like, "What? You can`t go to Michigan." And I said, "What are you talking about?" I said, "You took me here."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: And he said, "Well, you`re not Michigan mature."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: You should look at a community college.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Aw.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: And the whole thing, and I had never been more angry in my entire life because I saw, he had no intention of letting me have this experience. But he was just like -- he was basically showing me off, and this is one thing, I realized a lot of people, they say they want to help the poor, but in fact, they want to use the poor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: And he was using me like, "Oh, look, I have this little kid."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look what I am doing.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Out of hood.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A little charity case.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: I showed him around and look at him. But he could never ever eat from this table.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: And I said -- this is the thing, I vowed -- I will never forget, I vowed when I left that car, I said, "I`m going to go past that."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: I`m going to pass all that. Forget Michigan. My dreams are going to get bigger and bolder and I`m never ever going to stop. But let me tell you, I`m living way beyond wherever I thought I would.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Have you seen the man since then who told you that?\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: I`m sorry?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Have you seen him? Have you seen Mr. Man since then?\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Oh, no. Again, I`m not in the same circles anymore.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is not going to eat at your table.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: I`m so sorry. Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little bragger right there.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We don`t roll in the same circles. No, but you make a great point because oftentimes, adult`s failures be obstacles in the kid`s lives and they end up poisoning the minds and these kids think they can`t accomplish something because adults are dealing with their own issues.\par \par \uc2 But speaking of adults, this book is dedicated to your art teacher.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you talked about being a multi hyphenate. You`re doing everything. You`re a media mogul, but you also -- you draw. I follow you on IG and you are really good at what you do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Terry Crews Instagram.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So dedicating this to your art teacher and here we are showing some of that, is paying homage.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Because see, on the flip side, I had this art teacher Mr. Eichelberger (sp?), who believed in me when I didn`t believe in myself.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Let me tell you, he took all my art, photographed it, filled out all the applications and got me an art scholarship. I had an art scholarship before I had a football scholarship.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at these photos.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: And this is the thing, he said, "Terry," he said, "Your talent is so incredible, you have to believe in this." You`ve got to understand, I thought art, well, it ain`t going to give me no money. Football will give me some money, but art won`t. But it changed my life forever.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But speaking about believing because the book is coming out and you had your first book signing with kids. What was that like for you? The kids that came up to you.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: First of all, to realize you have this kind of influence on ten, eleven, and twelve-year-olds, it is the most -- I feel so responsible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because what did they say to you?\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: They go, "Oh, my God, it is Terry Crews."\par \par \uc2 "America`s Got Talent" is a big family show. A lot of people, you know, this is another thing, I have never gone past 12 years old in my head. I have kids older than me. I`m still 12.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: And they recognize that and it`s got to like, "Oh, my God." This is the thing -- I have been playing at what I do since I was 12. I`m 54 and I still play all the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is awesome.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: That is what I do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: May you never lose that.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Oh. Thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I can see that. You`re looking young.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: You see this? This is why --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There it goes. The great --\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS: Twelve-year-old right here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Terry Crews, thank you so much. "Terry`s Crew" is on sale now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In a minute, you`re going to have to take off that shirt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh come on now, Gayle. All right, up next --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Don`t do it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t do it, Terry. We are going to talk "Weird Al" Yankovic with Jamie Wax about his new biopic, the role that \uc2 MTV\uc2  created with is success.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Don`t tease me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Killjoy, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And no plans of slowing down after four decades of success.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 Gayle, you`re crazy.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Amish Paradise.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Amish Paradise."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, we`ve got to keep it together here. That of course is "Weird Al" Yankovic`s "Amish Paradise," one of the song parodies that made him famous.\par \par \uc2 Now, the accordion playing, funny man is making movies. That`s right. "Weird: The Al Yankovic Story" is a bogus biopic available on Roku that Yankovic wrote himself.\par \par \uc2 CBS News contributor, Jamie Wax joined Yankovic on his recent US tour to talk about all sorts of weird things.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weird Al" Yankovic performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Let me let you in on a little secret. There is nothing actually weird about "Weird Al" Yankovic.\par \par \uc2 Except maybe the fact that he has been so successful for so long.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weird Al" Yankovic performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Are you surprised at the longevity of the success of your career?\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: Yes. Yes, I am. I mean, comedy and novelty music is the domain of one-hit wonders. You don`t generally see somebody doing the kind of material that I do, and you know, hanging around for 40 years.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: But Yankovic is doing more than hanging around. He`s still performing sold out shows like on his recently wrapped up six-month US tour.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Foil.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: His last original album, 2014`s "Mandatory Fun" hit number one on the Billboard charts and netted him the fourth of his five Grammys.\par \par \uc2 You are doing better than you have ever been doing. You are not a has been. You are not a one trick pony of the 80s.\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: Well, thank you. Yes, it`s an amazing thing that every time I put out an album, it`s perceived as a comeback. You`re like, you know, "Oh, Al is back" like I didn`t think I went away, but okay. Glad to be back.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Like a Surgeon.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Yankovic knows that at least some of this success is due to lucky timing.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Fat.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: He was coming of age, just as \uc2 MTV\uc2  was and he filled the need.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Ricky.")\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: My first video, which was "Ricky," the parody of Tony Basil`s "Mickey."\par \par \uc2 That was the first comedy video that \uc2 MTV\uc2  ever played. But \uc2 MTV\uc2  needed content because they were a brand new 24-hour music video channel. So, I was giving them content and then when "Eat It" came out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Eat It.")\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: you know, you talk about overnight fame, and it`s usually like a myth, but it was kind of that way for me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Eat It.")\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: As soon as that went into heavy rotation, the next day, people were like pointing at me and they`re like, "It is the Eat It guy." You know, like that`s never happened to me before.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Yes, and they still call you that.\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Eat It.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: It only makes sense that a guy who`s made his living on parody songs and videos will take his talent to the movies.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Smells Like Nirvana.")\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weird: The Al Yankovic Story.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: And now he has.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weird: The Al Yankovic Story.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: "Weird: The Al Yankovic Story" was released earlier this month, a biopic exactly as you might expect from the mind of Yankovic.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weird: The Al Yankovic Story.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: When you look at this biopic, I`m not going to call it a biopic.\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: It is a biopic, yes.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: It is as real a biopic as your songs are real.\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: You know, it`s as real as a biopic of many other Hollywood biopics.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Okay. I will give you that. I will give you that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weird: The Al Yankovic Story.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: The film, a parody of recent rock biopics features cameos throughout, including by Yankovic himself.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weird: The Al Yankovic Story.")\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: But the star of the film is Daniel Radcliffe who earnestly plays Yankovic through the madcap depiction of his career.\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: He did the Potter movies. He made his fortune. He made his fame. He literally can do anything he wants to do. And I`m so grateful he wanted to do this.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weird Al" Yankovic performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: As for what the Prince of Parody wants to do? Well, that`s pretty simple.\par \par \uc2 "WEIRD AL" YANKOVIC: I just want to continue doing everything I had been doing. I`d like to keep productive and busy as long as I possibly can until people are just completely sick of me.\par \par \uc2 Every day when I wake up, and I`m still a "Weird Al," and I get to do this for a living, it boggles my mind.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Weird Al" Yankovic performance.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: And you`ve got to love this. I mean, that tour that he`s on now took him to prestigious venues like the Kennedy Center and Carnegie Hall.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: "Weird Al" at Carnegie Hall.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He packs them out.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Absolutely. And the fans love him. And the musicianship is very, very good in these performances.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is very talented. You know, I just realized I`ve never even heard his real voice until just then. And I like that the fact that he`s in on the joke.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that he is having a good time with it.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: You know, we all need something dumb and wonderful and fun right now in this world and he provides it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he is creative though.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s great.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Warm blanket. Very creative.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Describe the movie in one word.\par \par \uc2 JAMIE WAX: Hilarious.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Jamie Wax. We thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was very satisfying. We start with politics.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We end with "Weird Al."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Weird Al" and.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With Terry Crews.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Terry Crews.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gentleman in the middle and "Whiteout" sprinkled in. It was really great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: "Whiteout."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`ll do it for us. Guess what? We get to do it again tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Hope you`ll come back and join us. Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221109eib900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Voters Head to the Polls; Election Day in America; Battleground Pennsylvania; High-Stakes Georgia Senate Race; Wisconsin Senate Race Down to the Wire; Battleground Breakdown; Election Day Forecast; Tropical Storm Set to Hit Florida; Pelosi Speaks Out on Husband`s Attack; Historic Powerball Drawing Delay; People`s Sexiest Man Alive Revealed; Arizona`s Competitive Races; Nevada`s Senate Showdown; Three Meals in Florida; Climate Justice Warriors; Make Your Voice Heard, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Jericka Duncan, Nikole  Killion, Adriana Diaz, Scott MacFarlane, Ed O`Keefe, Jordan Steele, Ashley  Etienne, Mick Mulvaney, Nancy Cordes, Vladimir Duthiers, Kris Van Cleave,  Miguel Bojorquez, Margaret Brennan, Jeff Pegues, Robert Costa, Ben Tracy \par \uc2 13,675 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Election Day 2022. With tens of millions of voters going to the polls in Midterm Elections that could reshape the political landscape in Washington.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson. We are here in New York and Tony Dokoupil is out on the campaign trail in Florida.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you see, we are all here, let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world and 90 seconds\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): Vote Republican in a giant red wave.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Power in America lies where it always does, in your hands, the hands of the people.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Americans head to the polls across the country. What`s at stake with control of Congress on the line.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re in the battleground states that could tip the balance of power.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) (Georgia Senate candidate): And they`re talking about a runoff. You know, I am like "Runoff?" We are winning this. We`re talking about no runoff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And with the future of our country on the ballot, we`ll break down the Midterms by the numbers.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Republicans have to win a Blue State, flip it red to get the majority.\par \par \uc2 MAN: House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  says the recent attack on her husband will impact her political future.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): For me, this is really the hard part because Paul was not the target.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That record breaking $1.9 billion Powerball drawing was delayed due to technical difficulties.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A local journalist in Chile was doing a live report on a spike in robberies when a parrot stole his earpiece.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Three seconds left. Willard for heart and touts. And the Blazers walk off.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The streets of downtown Houston were packed as more than a million fans came out to celebrate their Astros winning the World Series.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Someone threw an alcoholic beverage can at Senator Ted Cruz.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): The last day before the election, we spotted this actual headline in "The Daily Beast." "Pollsters have no [bleep] idea what`s going to happen this election."\par \par \uc2 It looks like a weatherman saying, looking out to the weekend, be sure to bring your umbrella and your sunscreen. Don`t forget those ice skates, folks, because it`s a hot mess out there unless it`s a cold mess. That`s the forecast with a margin of error of one to four tornadoes over to Phil with sports and/or local murders.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does feel like a bit of a jumble depending on your point of view, Nate. You`re going to be saying these polls are correct, but you`re hoping, please let them be wrong.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It can be quite confusing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. But today is the day that really matters. Why?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because America decides.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`re going to help with that confusion, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes, we are. We`re going to sort it out for you.\par \par \uc2 It`s a very, very big day in America. History will be made not by the candidates, but by you, the voters casting your ballots across the country today.\par \par \uc2 Here is what it looks like in Atlanta, Georgia right now where the lines outside polling places before they opened started to form. More than 40 million people voted early nationwide. We`re expecting a high turnout today, because there is so much on the line.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, this is where things stand now.\par \par \uc2 Democrats control the House, but not by much. The Senate is split 50/50, but Democrats have control because Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has the tie breaking vote. Republicans hope to take both chambers.\par \par \uc2 Tony Dokoupil is anchoring our election coverage in Florida. Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 There is a lot going on, especially where you are at. What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Hey, good morning, guys.\par \par \uc2 If you love Election Day, I`ve got good news for it. It`s here, and if you want it all to end, I`ve got good news for you as well. It`ll be over soon. There is a lot going on. The polls are already open here in Miami, the big city behind me.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a little bit of rain moving in. Weather is one of the many variables that could affect turnout and turnout is everything in this election. But by the way, guys, every single voter in America has at least one race to vote on today, everybody hearing my voice right now. And the results, they`re going to shape at least the next two years of all of our lives.\par \par \uc2 What we do with the economy, with inflation, the border, crime, access to abortion, and so many other issues we haven`t even imagined yet, because they haven`t come up.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to tell us something about where we want to go as a country right now. And also, and this is important, how much we still trust our elections to bring us there.\par \par \uc2 So Nate, you`re right. It is a very big day, and we`ve got reports ahead from States that could make all the difference in this election, but we`re going to begin actually with the closing arguments from President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and what is at stake in their own words.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : This election is not a referendum, it`s a choice. It is a choice between two very different visions of America.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  closed out the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . very much like he started them.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We know in our bones that our democracy is at risk, and we know that this is your moment to defend it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And for those less worried about democracy than the value of their dollar, he touted relief.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : We`re shipping American made products overseas instead of shipping American jobs overseas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But two years after Democrats won the White House and Congress promising economic revival and national unity, Republicans say they`ve failed.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : Under the Trump administration, we had the greatest economy in the history of the world with no inflation. Biden and the Democrat Congress created the worst inflation in 50 years.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For days now, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the man many blame for Republican losses last election has once again been leading the call for a red wave on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : And if you want to stop the destruction of our country and save the American Dream, then tomorrow, you must vote Republican in a giant red wave.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And history suggests, he is going to get it, too.\par \par \uc2 In the 22 Midterm Elections from 1934 to 2018, the President`s party has averaged a loss of 28 House seats and four Senate seats and has gained seats in both chambers, only twice.\par \par \uc2 Going into Election Day, the latest \uc2 CBS\uc2  Battleground Tracker estimates that Republicans could indeed retake the House needing to net only five seats for control, they could get more than double that.\par \par \uc2 But the Senate is a very different story, and it could go either way. Pennsylvania, Georgia, Arizona, Wisconsin, and Nevada, all are statistical ties, tossups, in other words, just hours to go before the voting ends.\par \par \uc2 And while voting for this election is not yet over some, including the former President seems to already be looking ahead to 2024.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : I`m going to be making a very big announcement on Tuesday, November 15th.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we will see what that very big announcement is next week. I think you could probably guess what it is, but let`s stick with today while we`re living through today, because it always does come down to turnout, of course, money too, by the way.\par \par \uc2 An estimated $60 billion has already been poured into these races. That is a national record. It is the equivalent if you do your calculations by Powerball jackpot of eight Powerball jackpots, and all of it is just to get your interest America and push you to exercise your right to vote. So think about that for a moment.\par \par \uc2 And remember, there`s only one poll that really matters and that`s the one being taken today at the ballot box. So get ready, mark yours.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s go to our correspondents now. In the 2020 presidential election, it came down to the State of Pennsylvania, always a key State especially so today, it remains a hard State to predict however.\par \par \uc2 Our Jericka Duncan has made her way to Braddock, Pennsylvania, that`s just outside of Pittsburgh. She has been covering the race there.\par \par \uc2 Jericka, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Good morning to you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 I`m watching as some cars are just coming into this polling location. Of course, both candidates making their last ditch efforts and arguments late last night.\par \par \uc2 Democrat Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who lives here in Braddock and was Mayor rallied supporters in Pittsburgh yesterday inside a packed Carpenters Union Hall. He cast GOP opponent, Mehmet Oz as a wealthy outsider and told the crowd after his stroke, "I`ve been knocked down, but I got up," and he called that the essence of this campaign.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the State, Oz was in Pittsburgh near Philadelphia, joined by former South Carolina Governor Nikki Haley. Oz argued Fetterman`s positions are extreme, and said Fetterman is soft on crime and immigration.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the line at Philadelphia City Hall was overflowing with people yesterday trying to make corrections to their ballot as the city estimates 3,800 mail in ballots are at risk of being tossed for minor errors.\par \par \uc2 The Fetterman campaign has joined a lawsuit to overturn the State Supreme Court ruling that those ballots with a missing or incorrect date will not be counted.\par \par \uc2 Both Republicans and Democrat voters that I spoke to since I`ve been here said that democracy is on the line for them, and with nearly nine million registered voters here in the Keystone State, today will be a defining moment as voters will have the power to determine the future of the State, and of course, our government as a whole -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is all in their hands. The voters are the ones that count today, Jericka. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s go to the voters in Georgia. That`s another key race here. The Senate race is so tight in fact many people expect it could go to a run off if neither candidate achieves 50 percent of the vote, and Georgia is where we find our Nikole Killion today.\par \par \uc2 And Nikole, I feel like you`re going to be paying taxes in Georgia this year. You`ve been spending so many weeks there, months, really.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. What do you know?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, what I can tell you Tony is that the polls just opened here in Atlanta and you can see lines are already starting to form. The \uc2 US Senate\uc2 , race one of the marquee races in this State. Both candidates are hoping to avoid a runoff and secure an outright victory.\par \par \uc2 Democratic incumbent Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is spending his day canvassing and previously told me that he believes he can win. He is no stranger to a runoff having won in 2021 with Senator \uc2 Jon Ossoff\uc2  who joined him on the trail Monday.\par \par \uc2 Warnock asked supporters for a full six-year term and argued his GOP opponent, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  isn`t qualified for the job, but Walker and his team believe he has a good shot in this race and say voters want change.\par \par \uc2 The former football star held a rally last night with South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  and former HUD Secretary, Dr. Ben Carson.\par \par \uc2 Walker did not appear with Republican Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  who headlined and Election Eve rally nearby for the GOP ticket. Democratic challenger \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  gave a pep talk to volunteers who held a phone and texting bank.\par \par \uc2 Now, both of these races are among the most expensive races in the country with more than $284 million being spent on ads in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race alone -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just a remarkable sum of money, and Nikole for us, once again in Georgia. Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s move now to Wisconsin, home to controversial Republican Senator Ron Johnson, who is best known nationally at least often for the doubt he has cast on things like the COVID vaccine, also the 2020 election results. And now, Ron Johnson is hoping to keep his job, keep his seat in a contest there in Wisconsin where race has also played a role.\par \par \uc2 It is where we find Adriana Diaz in the City of Appleton, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 Adriana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Wisconsin is the only State that President Biden won where a Republican senator is up for reelection, so Democrats could flip a Senate seat here. But in true Wisconsin fashion, the polling shows it is going to be a photo finish.\par \par \uc2 On Election Eve, Democratic Senate candidate Mandela Barnes, Wisconsin`s Lieutenant Governor talked kitchen table issues from a supporter`s living room. Despite slipping in the polls since late summer, he told us his supporters will turn out to oppose Republican Senator Ron Johnson`s positions on abortion, Medicare, and jobs.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, and another Milwaukee suburb, Johnson rode into a cheering crowd. He focused on the economy and the need for unity and blasted his opponent for believing in institutional racism. Early voting has already surpassed 2018 numbers.\par \par \uc2 Now a source for the Barnes` campaign tells us they are feeling "cautiously optimistic." A source for the GOP Party here says they`re feeling "really good."\par \par \uc2 Guys, it could crack 50 degrees here, that`s practically scorching by Wisconsin standards. My coat is open and that bodes well for turnout -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, Adriana. It always comes down to turnout and weather is a big part of turnout. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And as I send it back to you guys in New York, I`d say, we`ve been doing this, electing people to Congress since the 1780s. It`s an American tradition, and it comes down to us to renew it every few years. Today is the day.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As you say it`s the only day, the only poll that really matters is what happens today.\par \par \uc2 Tony, thank you. We`ll talk to you a little bit later on.\par \par \uc2 CBS News has identified 10 battleground races that we estimate will determine control of the Senate. The States we just visited, you saw Wisconsin, Georgia, and Pennsylvania are certainly on that list.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Scott MacFarlane, he is at the Big Board to look at some of the possible outcomes.\par \par \uc2 Scott, a lot of balls in the air. Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Gayle, Nate, good morning. We`re in this now.\par \par \uc2 Here are the 10 States, Democrats have to win five to retain control of the Senate; Republicans would have to win six. The current lay of the land. Democrats ended the night with New Hampshire, Georgia, Colorado, Arizona and Nevada. Republicans have five of these, too, Florida, Carolina, Pennsylvania, Ohio, Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 That`s why there`s been a tidal wave of spending in Georgia, Republicans hoping to flip it red with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . That`s why there has been more than $300 million spent in Pennsylvania. Democrats hope they can flip this to blue, the Republicans retiring if they do so. They can lose one elsewhere on the map.\par \par \uc2 And you want narrow margins? Let me show you the US House: 218 votes needed for control. Republicans ended the night with 213, they have to net five more. Nate, there are more than five endangered Democratic seats on the East Coast alone tonight.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scott MacFarlane breaking it all down, and as Scott just showed us, the stakes are high this morning for Democrats and Republicans and for the American people as well.\par \par \uc2 Ed O`Keefe is with us to discuss the potential impact of these election results around the country.\par \par \uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Good to see you, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. So, what do voters on both sides believe is at stake?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Look for Republicans, today is about getting a seat at the table. They want the access codes back to the room where it happens. That is what`s driving so much of their hunger and their turnout today. They are upset about the direction of the country, they are showing up to vote.\par \par \uc2 Democrats understand losing full access to that room means big problems, potentially for the White House and certainly for the agenda that they`ve passed over the last two years. Whether the convincing argument has been made by President Biden, by all of these Democratic candidates remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, historically, the party in power normally doesn`t have a good night.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The Republicans, I was watching last night already seem to be making plans.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So what does -- what`s on the line for President Biden here tonight?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Well, you know, he and Speaker Pelosi, I noted yesterday started to concede, at least subtly that they may lose control of the House tonight, but Democrats are desperate to hold on to the Senate, and that would make President Biden`s life a little easier, if only for this, and that only requires a majority to get new Cabinet Secretaries confirmed and of course, Judges, potential for Supreme Court vacancies if one were to come up, it would be a lot easier for Democrats to get somebody through if they still control the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But there are going to be bigger policy debates now if Republicans take control of the House over basic government spending, over the future of immigration, review of what happened in Afghanistan, and consideration of what to do about Ukraine, going forward in the future.\par \par \uc2 And of course, all sorts of other investigations into the President`s son, into how the COVID-19 pandemic was handled, and the future the economy.\par \par \uc2 There has been a big discussion about how do you tackle inflation. Republicans say you`ve got to cut government spending. White House Democrats turn around and say, well, then you`d be undoing everything we just did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And then of course, there is a conversation about things like Social Security and Medicare and the future of abortion rights in the States and across the country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ed, I appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know you`ve got a long day.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Long night ahead.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But aren`t you excited about today? I am.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: I love election.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too, Ed.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It is the day the viewers get to stand up off a couch and go say something about what`s going on and let`s hope they do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right, and they will be right here watching us.\par \par \uc2 CBS News will bring you full coverage of the election results beginning at 8:00 PM Eastern right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now to the Election Day forecast. It`s always nice when you can vote in good weather, but in parts of Florida, they are bracing for a rare November hurricane. Oh no.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, Jordan Steele from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  is tracking all of this.\par \par \uc2 Jordan, this is not what we want to hear today, but good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN STEELE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): No.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you as well.\par \par \uc2 I know it`s Election Day and you know, you talked about Florida. You could maybe see a few showers, but the bigger story on Election Day is going to be the massive system out into the West. You were mentioning battleground States. I`m going to highlight in Nevada for a second because you`ve got rain in Vegas and snow in the Great Basin, that`s a big system.\par \par \uc2 But now let`s get into the big weather story and that is going to be Subtropical Storm Nicole. This thing could be a hurricane by the time it arrives early Thursday morning. It will strengthen throughout the day today into tomorrow. Conditions will deteriorate if you`re in Florida, and I want to make sure everybody in the State is going to be prepping for this, even if you`re on the western side of the State.\par \par \uc2 Remember Ian wasn`t long too far ago, we still have tarps on roofs and people playing cleanup. You guys have a Tropical Storm Watch. But the Hurricane Watch is going to be from the Space Coast all the way down to Broward County. And then the Tropical Storm Warning goes up into South Georgia will be feeling this tropical storm force winds, which means we could be looking at power outages, too.\par \par \uc2 Now to get the latest on Nicole and Election Day forecast where you live and vote, check out \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  streaming app -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Jordan. Got it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up. There was a glitch in the largest lottery jackpot in history. So hold on to your numbers. We don`t have the numbers just yet. Why the Powerball drawing was delayed. Oh no.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Coming up: Our political pros will join us as we look ahead to tonight`s election results and how the Midterms could set up a rematch just two years from now.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead Tony takes us on the road in Florida for our series, we call it "Three Meals" and finds an assignment he can well, really sink his teeth into.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So what do you call this again? Croquets? Is that what he is having?\par \par \uc2 MAN: No. Have this one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. How is the orange juice?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Amazing. You want to try some?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I would love some.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Florida orange juice, fresh squeezed.\par \par \uc2 What Florida voters told him as he sampled local favorites before today`s election.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Candidates have been making their final push with the polls opening this morning in these crucial Midterm Elections.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  delivered their closing messages at rallies last night. And for the first time, we also heard House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  describe the moment she learned about the horrible attack on her husband at their San Francisco home.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi told Anderson Cooper at CNN, it was around five o`clock in the morning when she was awakened by loud repeated knocking on her door in Washington.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): So I run to the door and I first see this \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  and they said, "We have to come in to talk to you," and I`m thinking, "My children, my grandchildren." I never thought it would be Paul.\par \par \uc2 They came in at that time. We didn`t even know where he was or what his condition was. We just knew there was an assault on him in our home.\par \par \uc2 For me, this is really the hard part because Paul was not the target and he is the one who is paying the price.\par \par \uc2 It really -- it is really sad because it is a flame that was fueled by misinformation.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER, (CNN Host): There`s obviously been a lot of discussion about whether you would retire if Democrats lose the House.\par \par \uc2 REP. NANCY PELOSI (D-CA): Well, I have to say my decision will be affected about what happened in the last week or two.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are joined now by CBS News contributor, \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2 ; and CBS News political contributor, that`s Ashley Etienne, along with chief White House correspondent, Nancy Cordes.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to you all.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s start with you, Ashley, if we could because it was heartbreaking. She is clearly very traumatized. She became, you know, somewhat emotional during that interview last night.\par \par \uc2 What did you think when you were watching her as someone who knows her well?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: Well, I couldn`t help but also be emotional. You know, it was hard to watch her. I know her to be very strong and very resolute. So that was hard.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think, the one thing we have to do if we could take a step back, you know, this Congress opened up with a deadly attack on the Capitol, and threats to hang the Vice President of the United States. It was bookend by an attack on the Speaker in her house, her husband, in her house.\par \par \uc2 No matter how the elections turn out today, this is an issue that we`re going to have to grapple with as a nation. We`re going to have to figure out how do we get back to a "we" not an "us versus them," which is perpetuating this violence.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: So that was my top takeaway from it. My heart goes out to the Speaker, I know how much she loves her husband, and how much she loves this country. So, I was heartbroken by it.\par \par \uc2 But as a nation, we have to figure out, we, as American people. It won`t be an elected official that`s going to save us here. We`re going to have to say "Enough is enough."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and how do you speak to that, Mick? About the "us versus we," because it is very concerning.\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 : It is. I think Ashley is right. It is not going to be a politician that saves us, for lack of a better word. One politician didn`t get us into this problem, one politician is not going to get us out. People ask me, "How do you fix Washington?" I am like "Washington doesn`t really lead, Washington reflects the nation."\par \par \uc2 The reason Washington is so divided is that the country is. How do you fix that? It`s a harder question. But don`t look to Washington to fix this, look to us to fix it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when it comes to the election deniers, though, most -- that primarily is the Republican Party, and that I think has led to many problems.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: No.\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 : That`s not true. Look, January 6 is what it is. It was awful. It`s terrible. That`s not -- I`m not here to defend that.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats have denied election results just as much as Republicans if you take January 6th out of the equation.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: That`s just factually not true. I mean, there are hundreds of Republican candidates on the ballot tonight who have denied the outcome of the last election. There`s nothing like that on the Democratic side.\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 : \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  still hasn`t admitted she lost last time she ran in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Look, again, this is the debate. Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY: But why can`t we all agree that everybody`s a little bit at risk and a little bit at fault, and we try and fix it as opposed to everybody saying, "Well, it`s got to be a Republican fault, and until they change the country is not going to fix."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy, I want to get to you in a bit, but Mick, I want to touch on something you said. You said "Politicians, they can`t save us," but they sure can divide us, and we hope that politicians reflect this country, but it seems like instead of a reflection, it`s an injection of divisiveness.\par \par \uc2 So why don`t you believe that politicians are at the crux of why we are so divided, why it is "us versus them" during this time of year, at all times of the year to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 : Politicians derive their power from the people that vote for them. There is no magic sort of black dark hand, an invisible hand in Washington, DC looking to the extremes in both parties and saying, Send me \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and send me AOC. That`s not how it works.\par \par \uc2 The people in those districts look at those people and say, "Those are the folks that best represent me. Why don`t we send them to Washington, DC?" That`s what I see the trouble is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the interview yesterday, last night, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  said that what has happened to her husband will affect her decision about whether she will remain in politics. What do you think? How do you think she is leaning?\par \par \uc2 Because the way she said it, on one hand, you could say she is not going on anywhere.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On the other hand, she seemed to be saying, I`ve had enough. You know, this is deeply affected my family.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CORDES: Well, there have long been questions about whether she would actually stay and serve out this term, if her party does lose control of the House. You know, does she really want to be House Minority Leader again? At the same time, for some years now, there have been younger members of the party who have been pushing for new leadership at the top.\par \par \uc2 They want somebody fresh, they want someone new, at the same time, you know, it`s always been difficult to identify who is the person in the Democratic Party who has her negotiating skills, her relationship, her fundraising ability, her ability to be bring a very rambunctious party together.\par \par \uc2 There have been some names that have been floated -- \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  of New York, Adam Schiff of California -- and there is also a question about what her Deputies would do if she were to step down. James Clyburn of South Carolina and \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  of Maryland, they`re both in their 80s as well.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ashley, as we anticipate the Republicans being favored to win the House, there already is some finger pointing when it comes to messaging among the Democrats. What is something that you wish they would have done differently?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: No, I think it is accurate. I mean, I think there is a lot of frustration within the party. Folks feel like we should have leaned more into the economy and inflation. Others feel like we did a great job of sort of doing a de-strategy in all of the above messaging.\par \par \uc2 I mean, my -- I would have advised the President differently. He had really big wins on a bipartisan -- in a bipartisan way, probably the most productive first term we`ve seen in modern history of any President.\par \par \uc2 I would have advised him to find a way to elevate those bipartisan successes and those accomplishments, wrap his arms around Republicans that he did that with, right, to demonstrate to the American people that democracy can still work, that we can still do big things together.\par \par \uc2 You know, the President ran on a message of healing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: That seems to have been lost.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY ETIENNE: But I think, had he sort of come together with Republicans to say, look, you know, America, democracy still works. Not that it`s under threat, but what is under threat is the progress that we`ve made together, both Democrats and Republicans, I think that would have gone a long way.\par \par \uc2 It would have also helped to neutralize the extremes in both parties and build a bigger, broader coalition for him going into the Midterms in 2024.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re right. There are extremes in both parties, and some people when they think extremes, they think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  who has made it very clear, he has a big, big announcement, everybody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Big, big announcement.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all believe it is huge. We all believe that he is going to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mick, do you know what that announcement is?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all believe he`s going to run. As someone who knows him well, do you think that he should lead the Republican Party again?\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 : No, and.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Should he run for President?\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 : . I`ve said that very, very candidly. I think he is the only Republican who can lose in 2024.\par \par \uc2 My party is ready for generational change. We have really, really good folks on the bench -- \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Tim Scott, Nikki Haley, \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  go down the list.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What should the Republican priorities be at this point right now?\par \par \uc2 MICK MULVANEY\uc2 : Priorities should be generational change. We have -- we can get the policies of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  without having \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the ticket. If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is on the ticket, the election will be about him, either vote for him or against him.\par \par \uc2 In all candor, no one voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in 2020. They either voted for or against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is one of the few people who can beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . If he is on the ticket, the election becomes about him instead about the issues that we want to push.\par \par \uc2 When we had a chance this time around to push the issues we want to talk about, inflation and crime and safety, we`re doing well. We don`t get that chance with Trump --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One thing we know for sure, he still has influence on the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on line two for you, Mick.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Nancy Cordes, Ashley Etienne, and of course, \uc2 Mick Mulvaney\uc2 , thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you`ll be talking about today a little earlier than usual, but that`s okay. It`s like Christmas presents. We love it when he comes early.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, and here is a reminder. You can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Hear today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That sounds like a deal to me.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Is it a party in the USA?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Is it a party? It is time for "What to Watch" with Vlad. It is a party, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A little Miley Cyrus.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s a little Miley Cyrus. I actually found myself humming the song and I mean, I like the song and then, I`m like, "Wait, it`s Miley." I realized that I love Miley Cyrus.\par \par \uc2 Let`s begin with a Powerball cliffhanger.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Last night`s drawing for the record breaking $1.9 billion jackpot was delayed. The Multistate Lottery Association says one of the state lo