he line.\par \par \uc2 We will have reports on close races all around the country.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that includes the State of Florida, where I shared three meals with voters from both parties to find out what`s really on their minds.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, climate issues are also on the ballot today. We`ll talk to a young activist who says it is time to stop talking and start doing something to save the planet.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight o`clock."\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "We want more.")\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "USA.")\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: This election is critical.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I believe our democracy is at stake.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I can`t believe people still believe that an election was stolen, and that undermines our democracy.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s all about inflation, and it is all about the economy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think I`m going to have to go to the bank and get a loan to go buy groceries.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Big thing that`s driving my vote is trying to get a handle on inflation.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Abortion rights are huge for me.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The biggest issue is women being able to have the right to do whatever we want to with our bodies.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Republican ticket is the better ticket. It`s the American ticket.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Inflation, immigration, crime. Those three things are just off the rails.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Personally, to me, crime is the biggest issue as a voter.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We used to be able to disagree on issues and still work through the election.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Overall, human rights are really important.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The stakes are just way too high.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I care way more now about voting that I did a year ago.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Your vote is your voice.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I am so tired of people saying, "Oh, we are mad at the Republicans. We are mad at the Democrats." Get mad and go the polls, November 8.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a good cross section of opinions there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Stakes are high.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your voice is your vote.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right. Your voice is your vote. Stakes are high and a lot on the line and we begin right there with the Midterm Elections.\par \par \uc2 You are looking at voters casting their ballots in Miami this morning, as polls are opening across the country.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go back to Tony in Florida where Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is trying to win a second term and is leading in the polls.\par \par \uc2 Tony, what`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Well, you know, I love talking to people. One of my favorite things to do, almost every story I do involves a conversation with somebody out there in America and I`ve been talking to voters here in Florida. There is a lot of enthusiasm for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  as you point out.\par \par \uc2 But I would also point out that polls are just guesses. They are educated guesses, but they`re only guesses and I`ve been talking to people, campaign volunteers.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been talking to people that rally, some of the most passionate voters that there are in this country. I went to a barber shop. I went to a fairground, a race track.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got to say, all of it is really enough to get you misty about our American experiment. People really do care in this country, and here are the top issues that people are talking about, what`s on voters` minds.\par \par \uc2 According to our CBS News polling, those issues include the economy, it includes inflation, crime, election issues, and here is what`s at stake really, the next two years of Joe Biden`s presidency, whether he will have a Congress that will work with him to help advance his agenda, or a Congress that will block it from moving forward.\par \par \uc2 So let`s get to the States. We`ve got correspondents in key election areas all across this country and we`re going to begin in the State of Arizona where Kris Van Cleave is the Grand Canyon State.\par \par \uc2 Kris, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 There`s a gulf there in the Grand Canyon, but the candidates for Governor, and also Senate very, very close.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Well, good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 And ideologically, the gulf is huge between the Republican and Democratic candidates here. We should tell you, the polls in Arizona opened just about five minutes ago. There was a line of people outside of this polling place in Paradise Valley. Some folks got here about a half an hour before the polls opened at 5:30 in the morning here.\par \par \uc2 Now, the majority of Arizonans actually voted early predominantly by mail. We know more than 1.5 million have essentially already voted. Now that said election officials expect it to be a busy day here at the polls. People voting in person. There`s been a lot of emphasis put on that by the Republican candidates here in Arizona, so they expect a busier than usual day at the actual polls. So we will be watching that.\par \par \uc2 The key races are thought to be very, very close. Kari Lake, the Trump- endorsed Republican candidate for Governor has drawn national attention for among other things, denying the outcome of the 2020 election, an election run by her opponent, Arizona`s Secretary of State, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The race for Senate is one CBS News calls a tossup. The former naval pilot and astronaut, Mark Kelly, the incumbent senator has made protecting seniors and a woman`s right to choose key parts of his campaign.\par \par \uc2 His opponent, Republican, Blake Masters has gotten a last minute bump in the polls while appearing with Kari Lake as they`ve been crisscrossing the State.\par \par \uc2 Election officials caution that if these races are as close as some think or particularly, if they are as close as we saw here in 2020, it could take several days to have enough votes counted to be able to clearly point to a winner.\par \par \uc2 Some of that is due to the way that the mail-in ballots come in. You know, votes that arrived Sunday, Monday, today, they have to go through a verification process, to verify the signatures, that takes time. So, they may not be counted until Wednesday, Thursday, even Friday in some places.\par \par \uc2 Democrats here in Arizona and across the country are saying they don`t know in many races if they`ll be able to determine who won tonight. They say, they are also preparing for some of their opponents to declare victory anyway.\par \par \uc2 So that`s something we`ll be watching here in Arizona tonight -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Kris, that`s a great point. A slow count is still a secure count, and people should be patient. We may not have results particularly nationally for a few days, maybe even longer.\par \par \uc2 Kris, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go now to the nearby State of Nevada, where the economy is a top concern for voters headed to the polls today, and that could be complicated news for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Manuel Bojorquez is in Las Vegas for us.\par \par \uc2 Manny, good morning.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 A few things make Nevada unique. One, that`s the iconic Vegas Strip illustrates just how largely tied to tourism the State`s economy is and that took a big hit during the pandemic shutdowns, but also the current rate of inflation here and gas prices are both higher than the national average.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are trying to capitalize on that with the Trump-backed Senate candidate Adam Laxalt tying those pocketbook concerns to President Biden and the incumbent Democrat, \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Laxalt is especially focused on winning Latina voters who are expected to make up 20 percent of the vote this cycle. The State`s most powerful union, however, the culinary workers, they have thrown their support behind Cortez Masto. And the members of that union have knocked on one million doors in Las Vegas and Reno to try to get out the vote.\par \par \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  was joined by Secretary of Transportation \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  last night, a final push to retain what some have called the most vulnerable Democratic seat in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 More than 600,000 early votes have been cast, according to State officials, with Republicans casting slightly more votes than Democrats. But 17 percent of those votes come from people with no party affiliation, and that could make the difference here -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it is an important note there, Manny. We should remember that most of this country is not Democrats or Republicans, but in fact, folks, they`re in the middle. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And as I send it back to you guys in New York, I want to underscore something that Kris said. This is Election Day. We`ll be going into Election Night, but we may not have calls for every State by the time most people go to bed today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s good information to know, Tony. We will check back in with you a little bit later.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, a young activist is shifting the conversation around climate change. That story is ahead.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:08, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up, Tony gets an earful from Florida voters and share some classic food with them for our "Three Meals" series. What voters say are their biggest worries these days.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In our series, "Three Meals," you know what we do? We sample some local favorites while also sampling the minds of voters ahead of the Midterms.\par \par \uc2 And this morning the land of sun, the land of surf and the land of my one and only childhood -- do we have the picture? Please tell me we do. There it is. Come on, we are talking Florida, of course. That`s a parrot on my head by the way. Long live the parrot jungle.\par \par \uc2 Florida, once the nation`s premier swing State, I think that`s fair to say, also fair to say the State is now trending Republican. It had gone back and forth in presidential elections since \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  was President, but former President Trump expanded his lead here from 2016 to 2020.\par \par \uc2 And registered Republicans -- this is historic -- now outnumber registered Democrats. So what do we do? Well, we swung up from Miami to Jacksonville to find out why Florida may no longer swing for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Miami, Miami, Miami is where we found Enriqueta`s, a Cuban sandwich and coffee spot still making it in Midtown.\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN KAPLAN (Voter): Every morning. I stop here every single morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Justin Kaplan (sp?) starts the day with un cafecito and two of these croquetas.\par \par \uc2 Can I have what he is having?\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN KAPLAN: Have this one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes?\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN KAPLAN: Come on. It will change your life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And for our sake, he added a side of local perspective on Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JUSTIN KAPLAN: I didn`t vote for him the first time, I won`t vote for him again. I still think he`s been unfairly vilified.\par \par \uc2 And I think that can national narrative and certainly the narrative outside of the state of Florida is absolutely not in line with what is really going on now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As we moved inside over a plate of bacon and eggs, we learned something else that Florida outsiders may forget.\par \par \uc2 DAYAMI MARQUEZ (Voter): There is a difference between Cuban voters and Latino voters. Cubans tend to vote Republican.\par \par \uc2 Dayami Marquez (sp?) is one of a million Cuban-Americans around Miami, and when it comes to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DAYAMI MARQUEZ: He is a beast.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why is he a beast?\par \par \uc2 DAYAMI MARQUEZ: I like the way he does everything.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that includes his decision to send migrants from the South to states up north.\par \par \uc2 DAYAMI MARQUEZ: I`m an immigrant, but I came legal. I believe in legal immigration. I don`t believe in illegal immigration.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We chased that thought with a little fresh squeezed Florida OJ. This is really good. I love that.\par \par \uc2 And walked out with a bag of Enriqueta`s famous steak sandwiches.\par \par \uc2 It is really good.\par \par \uc2 Before heading for the highway. Driving north along I-95, we hit Central Florida, home of the Space Coast, and tens of thousands of new residents, many of whom seem to lean Republican as they register to vote.\par \par \uc2 Our next stop, Pub Americana in the heart of downtown Melbourne, where Dave Hildebrand (sp?) was kind enough to offer a taste of his fried gator bites.\par \par \uc2 DAVE HILDEBRAND (Voter): Very good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is chewy.\par \par \uc2 But what really gave us something to chew over came from Dave Hoban (sp?).\par \par \uc2 DAVE HOBAN (Voter): We have our freedoms here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVE HOBAN: You know, a lot more than the other States.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He says the economy is a top concern, but a bigger motivator for him is the culture.\par \par \uc2 DAVE HOBAN: What`s going to push is institutional values. All of these other things and ideas that quite frankly, are coming in are being forced down a lot of people`s throats, so we`re going to stand up and they`re going to get voted down.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: After tucking into a burger and a craft beer, we heard from two women across the room who sounded like they might lean Democrat, especially on the issue of abortion.\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE GRAHAM (Voter): Women should have a choice to do what they want.\par \par \uc2 TOMI FRYER (Voter): It`s not my decision to tell someone else what to do.\par \par \uc2 But Katherine Graham (sp?) and Tommy Fryer (sp?), both registered Independents like what \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is cooking up on just about every other issue.\par \par \uc2 TOMI FRYER: He`s just like on it, especially with how he handled COVID like, yes, I feel like that was a big contributing factor to it.\par \par \uc2 KATHERINE GRAHAM: I just feel like he`s very family oriented. He, you know, cares about people. He doesn`t like you know -- he doesn`t BS around.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With two meals down, we were soon back on the road.\par \par \uc2 Our final stop, Jacksonville, a big political surprise in 2020 when the county flipped blue for the first time in more than four decades. It`s where we found Treemendous BBQ and met with owner Terry Rountree.\par \par \uc2 How are you? Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE (Treemendous BBQ owner): Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Who recently had to make some adjustments.\par \par \uc2 Increasing cost of operation. What`s going on there?\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: Can I say Biden?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can say whatever`s on your mind.\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: It wasn`t like that when Trump was President.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For dinner. I wanted something a little lighter.\par \par \uc2 So I`m kind of hoping you`ve got a salad.\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: We do have a salad.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you really?\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hotdog, all right.\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: We have a steak and a burger only.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It also came with a side of nostalgia from Terry`s brother, Ricky (sp?), and customer Daniel Quinn (sp?) as they discussed DeSantis` fight against the so-called woke left.\par \par \uc2 DANIEL QUINN (Voter): Before, we didn`t have to think about what you said; now, you`ve really got to think about it before you say it.\par \par \uc2 RICKY ROUNTREE (Voter): It is a free country. We have -- it says we have free speech, why can`t you say what you want?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pretty clear, Democrats have their work cut out for them here and not only in 2022. Terry Rountree likes \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  for Governor and beyond.\par \par \uc2 Do you like him for 2024?\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: In the White House?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really.\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Over that other Florida resident?\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Really?\par \par \uc2 TERRY ROUNTREE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Pretty big storyline developing there.\par \par \uc2 By the way, I`ve got to point out that once we started digesting our food and digesting the day, we realized that on that day long trip, we spoke to dozens of people up and down the Coast of Florida all in precincts by the way that voted for President Biden in 2020. But to our surprise, we did not run into a supporter of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` Democratic opponent, that would be \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But we did find some Crist supporters last weekend as we stopped for a Get Out the Vote Rally he held. Take a look at what they had to say.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What is the number one thing that makes you support \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Democracy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Democracy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Democracy in every aspect.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How do you think Charlie Crist`s campaign for Governor is going?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think it could have gone stronger because the Democrats do not toot their own horns.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What is the number one issue that leads you to support \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  for Governor?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Women`s rights.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Women`s rights.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is going to go further to restrict abortion?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Of course. He is already robbing us of our freedom, and that`s -- and freedom is not free until you`re free to make your own decisions.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He says he`s the freedom guy.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: He is not freedom. He has stolen our freedom from the State of Florida.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So there we go. We promised conversations on both sides of the issues, both parties, and there you have it.\par \par \uc2 And guys, as I toss it back to you in New York, I have to clarify something as a true Florida boy. What you saw there on camera, I am eating some alligator and it being pretty chewy, I actually spit it out.\par \par \uc2 That`s not typical and that`s not my first time with alligator in the fine State of Florida. It usually tastes a lot like, you guessed it, chicken.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Chicken.\par \par \uc2 Tony, it was interesting though, about the \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  voters that you go from one end of the State to the other and I know you were looking, you only found Charlie Chris supporters at a \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  rally. It was really that difficult.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it was really that tough. At one point, I stood at a stop sign and flagged down every single car to ask the driver who they were supporting for Governor.\par \par \uc2 At another point, I went into a diner and it wasn`t even on our list. This was meal four of the day, I went to every table and couldn`t find one.\par \par \uc2 That`s anecdotal. That`s not scientific. \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  supporters are out there, but it does speak to the fact that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in the last four years after just barely winning the governorship and 2018, has done a really strong job of consolidating support.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS, as Americans cast our votes on this Election Day. We`re looking at two big issues, the state of our democracy and election security.\par \par \uc2 Now, the White House said yesterday there were no credible threats to security, but US law enforcement has warned about domestic violent extremists. There is also some concern about candidates refusing to accept the results.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by CBS News chief election and campaign correspondent, that`s Robert Costa. "Face the Nation" moderator, Margaret Brennan, and our chief national affairs and justice correspondent, that is the great, Jeff Pegues.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you all to the table.\par \par \uc2 Listen, earlier in the show, we had the CBS News Battleground Tracker.\par \par \uc2 Bob, I`m going to start with you. Top of the list, economy, inflation, crime, followed by election issues, guns, and immigration. The point is, the state of the democracy is not in that list, not even in the top 10. What does it say to you about what voters are thinking or feeling about that issue? Because Democrats have hit it very hard.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Well, polls show the economy and other issues are certainly at the top of mind for many voters. Gayle, I really believe based on my travels and reporting in recent months that voters do care about democracy, but not in the way Washington understands it.\par \par \uc2 When I`m on Capitol Hill and people talk about democracy, that word usually is a shorthand reference to the Capitol attack on January 6th. But when I went out in the south, in Pennsylvania, in Ohio, in the southwest, when I talk about democracy, it doesn`t just mean talking about January 6th. It means talking about redistricting, voting rights, the integrity of the broader American system. That`s what people are concerned about this year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And can`t you care about both?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, that`s the thing. You can --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It doesn`t have to be one or the other.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I`m thinking, Nate. It doesn`t have to be either or.\par \par \uc2 Margaret, what about the effect of this -- what will the impact have on this election if people don`t accept the results? That is a big concern.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s hugely corrosive. I mean, this system only works if we continue to have faith in our institutions and the basic premise is that, we settle our differences at the ballot box and not at the barrel of a gun, and you can`t choose the outcome --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But some are already laying the groundwork, though, Margaret, if it doesn`t go their way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We are. And that`s why I think, we, in our jobs, too, we need to be so clear that it is not a flaw, it is designed in a system, this is going to take time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re not going to have absolutely clear views on some of these very closely fought States, and in some places, we might have run of the mill stuff like recounts and reexamination of ballots. That is entirely different than completely sowing doubt in a way that makes people say, you know, it`s not even worth me voting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because if you say, "I`m staying home," you leave that space to be filled by the loudest most extreme voices, and we have seen that highlighted, the direct line, and I know Jeff knows this, between domestic violent extremists and those who are denying the results of the last election.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. that`s spot on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Intelligence is linking that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well said, and speaking of that system, Jeff, when it comes to officials, what are they most worried about when it comes to election security?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: Violence. You know, and it`s so hard to be saying that. In this democracy, we`ve all covered enough elections, but this one is different.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: And it`s not a Presidential Election, but it`s a Midterm Election with the prospect of potential violence hanging out there. And so you have law enforcement. They are on pins and needles. They say they are ready. They`re monitoring social media, they`re looking out for potential domestic violent extremists, the kind of people who sort of look for an excuse to lash out, to cause problems, and so they`re paying attention to that.\par \par \uc2 But just the fact that we`re talking about this on an Election Day in this democracy, post January 6, you have to, because of what happened that day and law enforcement does not want to be caught flatfooted this time around.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and what do you say to voters that are nervous about going to the polls? We`ve seen pictures of people at polling places looking very menacing, some of them armed, and you can see the arm. You can see the weaponry.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scary stuff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What are we going to do with that?\par \par \uc2 JEFF PEGUES: And they`ve been told to stay away and law enforcement sometimes in plain clothes, they are watching out for that. You have DOJ monitors being dispatched to election districts across the country.\par \par \uc2 So as I said, they are preparing for this because no one wants any type of violence, which could happen because of some of these operations, these influence operations coming from China.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen them active in this space over the last couple of days. You have Russia, Yevgeni, Putin`s chef, okay, that`s what we call him because he has catering contracts with the Kremlin. That`s his day job. But in his spare time, he runs this internet research agency that tries to stir up trouble online here in this country, sowing divisions around issues like abortion. So, they`re watching out for that as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Bob, you know President Trump has been teasing us. He`s got a big, big, big, big, big announcement next week.\par \par \uc2 Do you think that the outcome of this election, these Midterm Elections will affect his decision one way or the other? Or do you think he`s already decided, "I`m running."\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: His ally say he is moving towards running and that is likely what`s going to happen next week. He is going to be watching tonight at Mar-a-Lago, I`m told by his friends and supporters how his candidates do. He endorsed a lot of candidates this year. He wants to claim at least partial credit, should Republicans make major gains tonight.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, his talk of an early announcement this far ahead of 2024, some Republicans telling me privately, it`s not a sign of strength for Trump to try to be jumping in this early, that if he really had command of the party, he could take his time and wait.\par \par \uc2 But he knows, there are so many other ambitious Republicans who are hovering over his own shoulder -- Governor Glenn Youngkin of Virginia, Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, as Tony has just been reporting on. All of these names have really become a galaxy of rising stars in the GOP. Trump knows that, and that`s why he`s talking about getting in and potentially trying to freeze the field.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The world is watching Margaret, what are they -- how are we being perceived by our allies?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, this will also impact whether President Biden runs again in 2024. Our allies are watching that. They`re watching what is happening right now, and I was speaking to a Western European diplomat who said to me, she grew up looking at the United States as a bastion of democracy and she was sad that she doesn`t feel she can, because these divisions are exploited by our adversaries.\par \par \uc2 And really, it hurts America`s ability to project power, but I just want to underscore that Homeland Security officials, Jen Easterly was on "Face the Nation" recently underscoring election infrastructure. They have confidence in the integrity of it.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t want our conversation here to turn people away from going and exercising their vote today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure. No doubt about it.\par \par \uc2 Margaret, Bob, Jeff, thank you. Appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 All right, it`s time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Our series "Earth 365" focuses on issues that affect our planet year round. One of the most anticipated voices at the \uc2 UN\uc2  Climate Summit currently underway in Egypt doesn`t belong to a politician or a scientist it belongs to a 25-year-old Ugandan climate activist named Vanessa Nakate.\par \par \uc2 Senior national and environmental correspondent, Ben Tracy, recently caught up with her as the world prepared to focus attention on her home continent of Africa.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Climate Change has got to go.")\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE (Climate activist):We don`t need any more words, we need real action.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Vanessa Nakate benefits from the clarity of youth. The 25-year- old climate activist is tired of politicians saying a lot about the climate crisis, and then doing too little about it.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: The climate crisis is impacting people right now, and yet, we are still, you know, holding conferences. We are still holding summits, and yet people continue to suffer.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And so your point is stop talking, start doing.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Scientists warn that the impacts of climate change are accelerating, and efforts to curb planet warming pollution are not nearly enough.\par \par \uc2 Nakate who`s influential activism has landed her on the cover of "Time" Magazine now finds herself in some ways, representing a continent of 1.4 billion people that is uniquely vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: We`ve seen some of the worst impacts of the climate crisis, you know, across Africa. It is heartbreaking and frustrating, because you didn`t cause this crisis, but then, you are suffering the impacts.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Africa accounts for the smallest share of planet warming greenhouse gas emissions, a fraction of what China, the US, and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  emit.\par \par \uc2 But climate change is punishing the African continent, all the same. Rising sea levels, record flooding, and extreme heat waves and drought are intensifying famine and displacing entire communities.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: Communities are struggling to find food to eat. They`re struggling to find water because the entire place is dry.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: We talk here a lot about can we afford electric cars? Is there enough wind and solar. But what you`re saying is this is much more acute life and death struggles.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: There are people that are suffering severely, and they don`t need an electric vehicle, they need food, they need water.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Nakate started a Youth Climate Movement in her native Uganda in 2019 by protesting on the street.\par \par \uc2 GRETA THUNBERG (Climate activist): We are unstoppable.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: She says she was inspired by Swedish climate activist, Greta Thunberg who waged her own climate strike when she was just 15.\par \par \uc2 What is it do you think that young people understand about climate change that a lot of adults don`t seem to?\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: I think young people understand the science of climate change and what is really happening. I think they understand the urgency of the problem.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting.)\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: But it is important to know that young people can`t do this alone. People say you know, the future is in your hands, but the people with the money to transform communities they hold, you know the power to effect change.\par \par \uc2 In Africa, we`ve really had crazy floods --\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In 2020, Nakate spoke truth to the powerful at the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  in Davos, Switzerland. She then posed for a photograph with four other young climate activists, all of them white, including Greta Thunberg, but when the Associated Press released the photo that was printed around the world, Nakate was cropped out.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: That was one of the most, you know, heartbreaking moments in my activism.\par \par \uc2 The world is so cruel.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In an emotional 10-minute video she posted to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , Nakate said, it wasn`t just her face that was being erased.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: Does that mean that I have no value as an African activist? Or do people from Africa to have any value at all?\par \par \uc2 One of the key things that are emphasized during the press conference was the need to listen to every voice, so it was heartbreaking for me to see that picture and not see myself there.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: It certainly reinforced the message you were trying to deliver in a really unfortunate way.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: And it really showed how those that are on the frontlines or not on the front pages.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: The AP later apologized and Nakate has used that experience to fuel her fight for climate justice and make sure African faces and voices are seen and heard.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: There are those moments of frustration.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: When we met up at the Brooklyn Botanical Garden, she told us she still remains hopeful the world will make progress on combating climate change.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: I have to find a place of hope, because it is in that hope that I get the strength to continue fighting.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In the meantime, she says the rich nations of the world, the so-called Global North need to make good on their promise to provide $100 billion a year to poor countries like Uganda to help them adapt to climate change.\par \par \uc2 VANESSA NAKATE: We need to move from promises to real action. A promise gives hope for a while, but when it is broken, it brings disappointment. We need real action and real action is possible.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ben Tracy in New York.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, thank you, Ben for introducing many people to Vanessa. I first saw her on the cover of "Time" Magazine, but it is painful and insulting as that deliberate decision was to cut her out of the picture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It now has drawn more attention to her and what she is doing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it fueled her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And it fueled her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It motivated her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. Yes. I liked what Ben said, she benefits from the clarity of youth and she is over at conferences and summits. Now, people, let`s do something.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much, Ben.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, Tony shares some final thoughts about the importance of voting -- we still have time, lots of time -- and making your voice heard.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More than 12,000 people have served in Congress since the first Congress sat in 1789. And today, voters will choose the next slate of American leaders.\par \par \uc2 The good news, all of us are invited to be a part of it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It happens in more places than you can imagine, schools and community centers, but also laundromats and bowling alleys, old farm sheds, and lifeguard stands.\par \par \uc2 Americans vote in tens of thousands of locations all across this country, not even counting vote by mail. And this year, yes, this year, we may yet set a record for participation in a Midterm, but only if we care enough to do it. Not because it`s a perfect system, not because this year we have our choice of perfect candidates, but because voting is the only way we have to change that system to punish or reward those candidates.\par \par \uc2 Not just in Washington, by the way, what in your own backyard.\par \par \uc2 Do you care about which crimes get prioritized? Pay attention to your local race for Attorney General. Do you think a lot about what your kids should be learning? You`ll want to vote for a School Board.\par \par \uc2 And what about your money? If you have an opinion about how much of it should go to the State, take a look at your Governor`s race.\par \par \uc2 But above all America, and for whatever reason, vote -- not just for the sticker, though the stickers are pretty cool -- but because it really does matter for the country and for you.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it really does, guys. And as we conclude here, two hours of coverage here on Election Day, it`s a great reminder that this election, everybody who can hear my voice right now, there is something in it that will affect your life and if you`ve got the time today, vote. If you don`t have the time, make it. It really does have consequences.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I absolutely agree. I remember when I turned 18, I couldn`t wait to vote and that still hasn`t changed.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 We invite you to stay with CBS News for a complete coverage of today`s voting as the results come in this evening.\par \par \uc2 Our Midterm Election coverage begins at eight o`clock Eastern Time. I`m so excited. I`m part of a very big team and may I just say, we are ready, Nate, for whatever this day brings, because it really does belong to the voters.\par \par \uc2 Have you voted yet?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because guess what I`m doing as soon as I get off the air? Guess where I`m going? Bringing in a newspaper in case I have to wait in line. I`m ready.\par \par \uc2 And then "The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" will be live tonight. This is a very special Midterm Election Edition that he does on Election Days. And guess who the special guest is this evening?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Who?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why don`t you share with the class?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You? No. \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2  will be Stephen`s guest.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s always good. That starts at 11:35 Eastern Time right here on \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And of course, we will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. We`ll have all the results that we can share with you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Take it easy. We`ll see you then.\par \par \uc2 Don`t forget to vote.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221108eib8000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For November 6, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Christopher Krebs, Caitlin Huey-Burns, Jan Crawford, Kris Van  Cleave, Nikole Killion, Robert Costa, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,381 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: The midterm elections are just hours away, and candidates are spending the weekend barnstorming their states with some of the biggest names in politics.\par \par \uc2 Democrats argue democracy is on the line. Republicans say the party in power is wrecking the economy. It`s a mad dash to get out the vote. And the stakes are high, as both parties vie for control of Congress for the next two years.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 The most important and probably most competitive midterm election in years is now down to the wire. More than 39 million votes have already been cast. And across the country, candidates are out in full force, hoping to persuade the persuadable, turn out their true believers and, perhaps their most difficult challenge, explain to people why this election is so important and why they need to vote.\par \par \uc2 In their words, here is what it comes down to:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR RON DESANTIS (R-Florida): They came into office, and they created the worst inflation crisis that we have seen in four decades.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): The Republicans like to talk about it, but what`s their answer? What`s their economic policy? They want to gut Social Security. They want to gut Medicare. They want to give rich folks and big corporations more tax cuts.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-Pennsylvania Senatorial Candidate): Young couples want to make that first investment for a starter home. They can`t afford the interest rates. We have families all over the commonwealth worried about crime, so much so, they won`t send their kids outside.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Your right to choose is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Your right to vote is on the ballot. There`s something else on the ballot: character. Character is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-Georgia Senatorial Candidate): But you heard him say the biggest threat to democracy is voting for the Republican. Are they serious? The biggest threat to democracy is having \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in the White House.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The main event is Tuesday, control of the House, which has been shifting in Republicans` favor in these last weeks -- they need five seats to take over -- and control of this Senate, where just one turnover will put them on top.\par \par \uc2 We are tracking 10 key battleground races, five of which are hotly contested toss-ups.\par \par \uc2 Our Kris Van Cleave is in Arizona, our Nikole Killion is in Georgia, and our Robert Costa has been reporting in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And that`s where we begin this morning.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : It`s a choice, a choice between two vastly different visions of America.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voice-over): It`s a choice that`s as competitive as ever. Right now, the Senate race between Republican Mehmet Oz and Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  could not be closer. Three presidents barnstormed the state this weekend.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): If you want to stop the destruction of our country and save the American dream, then, this Tuesday, you must vote Republican in a giant red wave.\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: On the Road from the campus of \uc2 Penn State\uc2  to the suburbs of Philadelphia, voters told us that the economy is front and center.\par \par \uc2 Are you feeling economic pain in your life?\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Yes. I`m a fixed income.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Fetterman, who is recovering from a stroke in May, says that voters who want to blame Democrats for inflation should think again.\par \par \uc2 LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR JOHN FETTERMAN (D-Pennsylvania): What I would say to them is, is that you need a senator that is going to push back against corporate greed and the kind of price gouging as well too. I mean, like that -- this is my point.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Oz has courted frustrated voters. Last night, near Pittsburgh, he urged his supporters to win over neighbors by asking them if they`re happy with the nation`s direction.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ: I want you to contact 10 people. Do it at church. Do it before the Steelers game. Just find the time.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But the \uc2 Pittsburgh Steelers\uc2  don`t play this week, and the remark gives Oz`s critics another chance to claim he is out of touch.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: This is Kris Van Cleave in Arizona, where both the Senate and governor`s races appear to be in dead heats.\par \par \uc2 Battling for his political life, Senator Mark Kelly rallied in Phoenix with first lady Jill Biden.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR MARK KELLY (D-Arizona): Women have lost a constitutional right, and that is taking us in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Republican challenger Blake Masters has closed the gap by hammering Kelly on the economy, crime and the border.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R-Arizona Senatorial Candidate): Mark Kelly, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , they have opened up our Southern border. They have given our sovereignty over to the Mexican drug cartels.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Kari Lake is a rising star in the Republican Party and one of three election deniers at that top of the state`s GOP ticket. Democrats have seized on that.\par \par \uc2 FORMER PRESIDENT BARACK OBAMA: Democracy as we know it may not survive in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: That is quite a statement, that you are a threat to democracy.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-Arizona Gubernatorial Candidate): I wonder what he`d call \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , because she`s denied so many past elections. And she`s already denying the next election in 2024.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: But she`s running for governor.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: We have the right -- we have the right to question our government and our elections. It`s called the First Amendment. And I intend to continue to use that right.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Her opponent, Secretary of State \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D-Arizona Gubernatorial Candidate): Kari Lake is focused on being as extreme as possible, whether it`s the issue of abortion, whether it`s the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 KRIS VAN CLEAVE: Starkly different campaigns on a collision course over the future of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: I`m Nikole Killion in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2: From the great state of Georgia, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 !\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: It was a homecoming for \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  at the University of Georgia, where the former football star was game-ready.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : Just like the Dawgs are going to win today, that`s what`s going to happen on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D-Georgia): The way to have a voice in a democracy is to have a vote.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Democratic Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  and Walker are locked in a heated contest that could determine control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D-Georgia Gubernatorial Candidate): I`m here to tell you, we have got the power, Georgia!\par \par \uc2 (CHEERING AND APPLAUSE)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: In the governor`s race, it`s a rematch between Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and Republican incumbent \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS: Polls tell us that this is a tight race.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR BRIAN KEMP (R-Georgia): We need a big turnout on Election Day.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: More than 2.5 million Georgians have already voted, a state record, but, here in Cobb County, elections officials say more than 1,000 absentee ballots were never mailed, calling it a critical error.\par \par \uc2 They blame overworked staff, but say that`s no excuse, and they`re working to contact impacted voters -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Nikole Killion, Kris Van Cleave, and Robert Costa, thanks to all of you.\par \par \uc2 We turn now to \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2 , the former mayor of Atlanta. She now serves as a senior adviser to President Biden, but the White House tells us that, this morning, she`s appearing only in a personal capacity.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS (D-Former Mayor of Atlanta, Georgia): Good morning. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You said last month you were concerned about a lack of voter enthusiasm in Georgia, but the secretary of state says there is a historic level of turnout.\par \par \uc2 What does that signal to you now?\par \par \uc2 FORMER MAYOR KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS: Well, I`m glad that people are turning out to vote.\par \par \uc2 When I said that last month, I was sounding the alarm. And we have seen a record number of people turn out in the early vote, but we still have an election on Tuesday, and we cannot let up until this election is over.\par \par \uc2 I was on the ballot five years ago, seven points down going into Election Day, and I won by less than 800 votes. It doesn`t matter what the polls say. People still have the ability to show up to vote on Tuesday and to make a difference in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 The thought of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  going to the Senate is frightening.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That is the Republican candidate for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Black voters helped turn Georgia blue back in 2020 and send two Democratic senators here to Washington.\par \par \uc2 How do you explain to Black voters now, who are so key in Georgia, that they should help support the party, given that, when Democrats held the majority for these past two years, top priorities like police reform and voting rights really haven`t been addressed?\par \par \uc2 FORMER MAYOR KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS: Well, we have to remember that there has been a very thin margin, but there have been a number of issues that have been addressed that impact not just African-American voters, but voters across this country, student debt relief, $10,000, $20,000 if you are Pell Grant-eligible.\par \par \uc2 A vast majority of African-American students are Pell Grant-eligible. There`s also been a reduction in prescription drug costs. Insulin will be capped at $35 a month, $2,000 a month annually.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Starting in January.\par \par \uc2 FORMER MAYOR KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS: Absolutely, starting in January.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And the loans are -- the student debt relief is caught up in the courts and you know a part of dispute.\par \par \uc2 FORMER MAYOR KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS: They are caught up in the courts because Republicans are pushing back against relief to American families across this country.\par \par \uc2 African-American voters are key in Georgia; 29 percent so far have shown up in the early vote. That means that there are a lot of people left out there, not just African-American voters, but voters across the state, who can still show up on Tuesday and make a difference in this election.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what Democrats will need in Georgia for us to continue to send \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  to the Senate and also to have \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  elected as governor.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In our polling, we see consistently that it is the economy and inflation that are top of mind for voters.\par \par \uc2 The president said just a few days ago that he has passed so many good things, but people haven`t realized how good they are yet.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  said, the truth is: Democrats have done a poor job of communicating our approach on the economy."\par \par \uc2 DNC adviser \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2 , you know him well. He told \uc2 CBS\uc2 : "The president`s message of what he`s been able to accomplish has not gotten out there."\par \par \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  just said: "The work done by Democrats is impressive, but we got to get that message across more effectively."\par \par \uc2 If the policies are so good, why is communicating them such a problem?\par \par \uc2 FORMER MAYOR KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS: Well, it`s been a very difficult couple of years.\par \par \uc2 We have been in the midst of a pandemic. There`s been a lot of misinformation flooding the airwaves. We see it in waves, not just on television, but we`re seeing it through \uc2 YouTube\uc2 . We`re seeing it on other social media platforms. So, it is more difficult to get the message out.\par \par \uc2 But I`m sitting here today getting the message out. This administration, Democrats in Congress have delivered for the American people. And to turn back the clock and not allow us to keep pushing through will be devastating for people across America.\par \par \uc2 If we want prescription drug costs to remain low, then we need Democrats in Congress. If we want voting rights finally passed in Congress, we need Democrats in Congress. If we don`t want a national ban on abortion and for doctors and health care providers to be sent to prison for offering an abortion to a woman whose life may be in danger, then we need Democrats in Congress.\par \par \uc2 And that`s the message that we will continue to push out, not just through Election Day, but beyond, because we know that elections happen quite frequently, and we can`t have people so discouraged that they think their votes don`t matter.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden said this week at a fund-raiser, if Democrats do lose in the midterms -- so, he`s entertaining it is a real possibility -- he said: "It`ll be a horrible two years. The good news is, I will have a veto pen."\par \par \uc2 Is complete gridlock what America needs to prepare for?\par \par \uc2 FORMER MAYOR KEISHA LANCE BOTTOMS: Well, we know that President Biden ran on being able to work across the aisle.\par \par \uc2 So, of course, the American people want us to be able to get things done. But when you have Republicans say that they will pull back aid to Ukraine, that their -- their entire platform is based on doing what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn`t want done, that`s not what the American people want.\par \par \uc2 We want progress in this country. And we want leaders who believe in democracy. We don`t want leaders who deny elections. I heard Kari Lake say that people have a right to question their government. They absolutely do, but they don`t have a right to overthrow their government, in the way that we saw that on January 6.\par \par \uc2 So, when you have election deniers who have the -- who may go to Congress, who may be elected to statewide office -- we have one in Georgia, Burt Jones, who`s running for lieutenant governor, an election denier -- that`s -- that`s not just a danger to Democrats. That`s a danger to everyone who believes in what this country stands for.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Keisha Lance Bottoms\uc2 , thank you for joining us this morning.\par \par \uc2 And we go now to Newfields, New Hampshire, and that state`s Republican governor, \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2 , who is up for reelection on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Welcome back to the broadcast.\par \par \uc2 Governor, our public polling that we`re looking at indicates that you`re likely to keep the governor`s seat after Tuesday. Back in 2021, you canceled your own inauguration due to security threats.\par \par \uc2 You`ve personally experienced the threat of political violence. I`m wondering, how concerned are you in this moment now? And will you hold an inauguration if you win again?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (R-New Hampshire): Yeah, so a couple of things here.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, with -- with Speaker Pelosi and what happened recently, I think that is kind of re-looking at -- everyone`s kind of looking at what this political violence is. It`s on both sides. It`s everywhere. The heat is too high all across America. Good leadership brings that down.\par \par \uc2 I will be holding my inauguration, fully plan to do so. But, again, we have got to, as leaders, bring the temperature down. It`s OK to disagree, but, at the end of the day, you got to be able to move forward. And we fully plan to do that here in New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. I want to come back to that topic in a moment, but, first, inflation. It`s top of mind.\par \par \uc2 New England is facing its highest energy costs in more than 25 years. Could be a cold winter. Your largest utility in the region is asking the White House to prepare emergency measures to prevent a natural gas shortage this winter. What -- what`s the federal response been so far? And are you, at the state level, prepared for what could be a safety threat?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Yeah, I will say the federal response so far has -- has been very underwhelming.\par \par \uc2 All the governors got on the phone recently, about a month ago, with the secretary of energy and tried to talk about what those opportunities were in terms of increasing natural gas. New England is really at the end of the line for natural gas, right? All of our natural gas comes through Albany.\par \par \uc2 And, in previous years, if there was a high demand or a big cold snap, folks come home, they turn their heat on, the Marcellus Shale would increase production. But no one`s incentivized to do that. There`s no -- no opportunity to do that right now.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s where a lot of the utilities, and rightly so, are telling this administration, you`ve put policies in place. It`s having a very drastic effect on energy and fuel oil prices today, and likely is just going to get worse. So we need to see something across New England.\par \par \uc2 There`s nothing political about energy prices, right? But when you have all the ability in the world to produce your own fuels and refuse to do it, obviously, folks in New England are quite frustrated.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, there`s record production right now, as you know. But this is a very real problem.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, because of the Jones Act, because -- and because of the Jones Act, that is this antiquated 100-year-old union-driven policy that President Biden refuses to get rid of, we have very minimal opportunity to bring natural gas from even parts of our own country and land it right here in -- in New England.\par \par \uc2 So it`s not just New Hampshire. It`s Massachusetts. It`s Maine. It`s all of these states that are that are -- that are feeling record high prices because, again, we`ve shut down natural gas plants. We`ve disincentivized fossil fuels. It`s -- look, we all want to transition into renewables. And, of course, that`s a very smart thing to do, but it must be a transition.\par \par \uc2 This administration went all or nothing. So that`s why you see your -- it takes -- costs twice as much to fill your gas tank, your fuel oil, your energy prices. And, in New England, when it gets cold, it`s going to be -- there`s going to be some real pain for all of us.\par \par \uc2 And, again, we`re just asking the administration to reverse some of these policies, in -- incentivize more production and more natural gas through Albany, New York, to get us what we need.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: President Biden, in one of his closing arguments, is framing a selection is protecting democracy against extreme Republicans. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The extreme MAGA element of the Republican Party, which is a minority of that party, as I said earlier, But it`s its driving force, is trying to succeed where they failed in 2020, to suppress the right of voters and subvert the electoral system itself.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you agree that parts of your party are emboldening violence and posing a threat to democracy?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Look, what is shocking to me about all of this is, you have the Democrat Party, which is now using the president of the United States, not as leader of our country, but leader of their party, as a political tool, right before the election, to drive and effectively tell half of America that they`re too extreme for America.\par \par \uc2 It makes absolutely no sense. And to say...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You think the MAGA element of the Republican Party is half of America? Because I wouldn`t necessarily put you in that half.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: No, well, definitely not.\par \par \uc2 But again, to say that that extremism belongs in one party, and it doesn`t appear in the Democrat Party is -- is nonsensical. Nobody buys that...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re saying the party is all one now...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: ... because we see it on both sides.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... unified, that extreme MAGA is part of the Republican Party`s ideology?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: No, no, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Absolutely not, no.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it seemed like that`s what you were implying.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: It`s an absolute minority.\par \par \uc2 But what I`m saying is, extreme -- no, no, no, no, definitely not. Extremism is on both sides. And for the president of the United States to come up and be more of a political tool, as opposed to a uniter -- remember, he got elected because he said he was going to unite folks, not threaten them.\par \par \uc2 He was going to bring everybody together and get stuff done and not polarize this country, which is exactly what has happened.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: If that was not the reality, then Democrats wouldn`t be in for the rude awakening they`re going to come -- that`s going to come Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 But it is coming, and not because of politics. Because of what is happening in people`s homes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: What`s happening at their kitchen tables, what`s happening with trying to balance a checkbook.\par \par \uc2 And the president has to take a lot of responsibility for that. It`s easy to blame ex -- just blame extremism.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: But most of us are not extreme.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, President -- former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is expected to announce his candidacy for the presidency. \uc2 CBS\uc2  is reporting that. That could be within a matter of days.\par \par \uc2 What does that do to your party? Does anyone have a chance of actually beating him in a Republican primary?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: What does it do to our party? Nothing. Nothing. It`ll have no effect on anything. And I mean that quite sincerely.\par \par \uc2 First off, announcing you`re going to run for office between an election and Christmas is a terrible idea, because one thing I can say for America is, we`re all going to be really happy, one way or the other, that the election is over come Tuesday, and everyone`s going to want to take a breath and reengage with their families and deal with some really serious issues.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well...\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: And then politics really gets back into the mix of things in early `23.\par \par \uc2 Whether the -- President Trump decides to run or not, it`s not going to make any difference in terms of the fact that you`re still going to see eight to maybe even a dozen other candidates jump in the race.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: He doesn`t keep anybody out of the race, right?\par \par \uc2 So it`s still going to be, I think, on both sides. I think -- I don`t think President Biden is going to run again. I think on both sides of the aisle, you are going to have maybe a dozen individuals over the next six to nine months come out and decide to run.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, and your name`s floated as one of them, potentially. I know you`re not going to give me an answer to that question right now. But you did turn down the invitation to run for Senate.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Republican Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has voiced some concern about candidate quality in this midterm election.\par \par \uc2 You told "The Washington Examiner" that the Republican majority would just obstruct President Biden until 2024. You didn`t want to be a roadblock for two years.\par \par \uc2 You`re setting pretty low expectations for what a Republican majority would actually mean. Is that what we should expect, just nothing for two years, total gridlock?\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: No, I -- look, I think -- well, I think both sides both sides of the aisle -- both sides of the aisle in Washington have set a horribly low expectation for Washington.\par \par \uc2 I mean, think about it. They pass -- one way or another, they pass a bill, Republicans or Democrats or both, and we cheer it. It`s supposed to be this great, great success because they got something done?\par \par \uc2 It is just an absolute gridlock mess there on both sides of the aisle. So, as a governor, I can have so much more impact on what is happening on the ground level, redesigning systems, implementing better mental health services, implementing better opioid services, whatever it might be.\par \par \uc2 And this is New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: And if you spent -- anyone who spends more than 10 minutes in New Hampshire knows now not an easy place to leave.\par \par \uc2 So I just want to get stuff done. If I`m going to put my family through the difficulties of public service and all that comes with it, I`m sure as heck going to get something done. And you can do that far more effectively as a governor than you can as a senator or congressman.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: Now, leadership can change that. Leadership in Washington can absolutely change that on both sides of the aisle.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU: And I think that`s what America is looking for.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, I`m -- sounds like we`ll be talking to you again.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thank you for your time today.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by \uc2 Chris Krebs\uc2 , former director of the \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2 . He`s a CBS News expert and analyst.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Chris. Good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 How do you react to this idea of President Biden characterizing the MAGA element of the party as an extreme threat to democracy?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: The area that I`m most focused on right now is the undermining of the legitimacy of American elections.\par \par \uc2 And Governor Sununu mentioned leaders need to stand up and -- and speak truth to power, and -- and particularly the elements of the GOP that continue to repeat countlessly debunked claims.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we even have the GOP candidate for governor, Kari Lake, in Arizona just the other day making a joke about there`s no way that President Biden got 81 million votes. In fact, he got more than 81 million votes.\par \par \uc2 But the point here is that we do need leaders like Governor Sununu to stand up and say that this is not acceptable behavior in American democracy, and that we need the -- those that continue to push these narratives for clout, for political influence, for -- for money, for fund-raising, that they need to let it go, and we need to move on, if this American experiment is going to continue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we`re going to continue this on the other side of that, but, just to your point, there are 308 Republican candidates who have raised doubts about the integrity and validity of the last election.\par \par \uc2 They`re standing for office now. There`s a reason they are using that as a political message. We`re going to talk about that in a moment.\par \par \uc2 So, stay with us on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Tune in Tuesday for our CBS News election coverage. We start at 5:00 p.m. on our streaming network and then go on the air at 8:00 p.m. on our broadcast network.\par \par \uc2 So, join me, Norah O`Donnell, Gayle King, \uc2 John Dickerson\uc2 , and the whole CBS News political team. \uc2 Chris Krebs\uc2  will be there too. Full coverage of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Join me Tuesday, Norah O`Donnell, Natalie Morales, Nikole Killion, Caitlin Huey-Burns, all of us.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, we will also have a Twitter Space conversation about the role of women voters in the midterm elections. That`s Monday at 3:00. And you can listen live on the CBS News and Face the Nation \uc2 Twitter\uc2  handles Tuesday, full election coverage.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We return to our conversation now about election security with CBS News cyber security expert and analyst \uc2 Chris Krebs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chris, I want to talk about something that`s happening right now. Social media has already changed the way we communicate, and certainly our political world. President Biden said a few days ago that he has concerns about billionaire Elon Musk`s purchase of \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . He said the platform spews lies all across the world. There`s no editors anymore in America. There are no editors. How do we expect kids to be able to understand what is at stake.\par \par \uc2 It`s not just kids, right? What concerns do you have about this happening just days before the election, these changes to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, I think - I think the government, for one, has a mechanism by which they can review the acquisition. The Committee for Foreign Investment in the United States can take a look at particularly the second and third ownership positions in \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , including Saudi Arabia. That is something that I`m assuming that the Treasury Department is taking a look at right now, see if they can put in place a national security agreement or even potentially unwind the purchase.\par \par \uc2 But I think more specifically to what`s happening right now with \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , I think there are kind of -- there are two Elons that we`re seeing. There`s the public Elon that`s, you know, trolling and saying $8, please, on all the complaints about some of the shifts in the moderation and other activities. Then there`s what`s happening behind the scenes, the conversations with the civil rights groups, with advertisers, with the teams, which, perhaps, may be a little bit more stable.\par \par \uc2 And I think if you look at the platform itself right now, not a whole lot has changed. That may not be a popular opinion, but I think the reality is that most you haven`t seen too much of a change in the moderation.\par \par \uc2 Now, the concern, though -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Is what happens tomorrow where you can buy the blue check for $8 a month. The Twitter Blue.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And just for our viewers who don`t use \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: A blue check is a sign of credibility.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: It has historically been a marker of trust in that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  has said, we have confirmed and authenticated the identity of this person, which tends to be a politician or a news media personality or a journalist, an academic or someone that may be a popular voice in certain civil rights, civil liberties issues.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Now you can buy it for $8 a month.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: And along with a number of other features of editing and longer form video posting. But, again, to have such a dramatic shift in that marker of trust, now you can buy it, in advance of, as we`ve been talking about, a very contentious and important election, it opens the information space to a broader community of influencers, clout chasers, election denialists and - and is --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Foreign actors?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Absolutely. I mean we`ve seen reports lately of Russia, China and Iran back at their old tricks. And it is going to create a very chaotic environment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To that point, in 2018, during the midterms, cyber command took offensive operations to take out Russian trolls who were spreading misinformation. "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " has a story today saying, Russia`s back at it. What does that say to you about U.S. defenses?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, I think - so, recorded future and graphic of two research firms have released information that Russian bots, trolls associated with the internet research agency, which is a group that targeted the 2016 and the 2018 election, are back at it and are undermining this time Democratic candidates for Senate in some of the - the - the more contentious races.\par \par \uc2 I think what it says is that the - that there`s a broader community of actors. They recognize that political discourse is very divisive here in the U.S. and they have more opportunities probably than ever before to continue to undermine confidence, to create chaos, which is really their primary objective here is not necessarily that a winner wins, but that we`ve all lost confidence and they degrade the American, you know, democracy experiment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There are also a number of sitting senators, and, of note, Ambassador \uc2 Richard Grenell\uc2  of -- former President Trump`s acting director of national intelligence, has been posting some misleading information -- that`s him on the screen -- about the election. He said any state which doesn`t count all the votes and announce the winner Tuesday night is incompetent.\par \par \uc2 Now, votes -\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: So, all 50 states then by that formulation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because what you`re saying is just the fact that votes are never finalized on election night. But why do you think someone who knows better is posting something like that?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, whether he knows better, I can`t assume that. But the point here is that it`s for clout-chasing. It`s for influence. There is a reward system and structure set up right now within the far right of the GOP that provides additional engagement. So, you tweet something like that and you can see your likes, your retweets, your amplification really take off. And if you`re just talking about some other, you know, more mundane domestic issue, nobody cares. But there`s a reward system and incentive structure that`s set up where exactly this sort of messaging is - is rewarded. It`s encouraged.\par \par \uc2 And - and this is, again, going back to Governor Sununu`s comments. We need leaders to lead. We need the presumptive leaders of the Republican Party to stand up and say, this is unacceptable. This is not how it works. We need to be good-faith actors in this process and, unfortunately, leaders aren`t leading right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: \uc2 Chris Krebs\uc2 , thank you, as always for your analysis.\par \par \uc2 And we will see you on election night as part of \uc2 CBS\uc2  coverage at our democracy desk here at CBS News.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This year CBS News has been tracking four groups of election influencers who could prove decisive in this year`s midterm campaign. One group, Pressured Parents. They say they`re anxious about the post-Covid era, particularly when it comes to their finances and their children`s well-being.\par \par \uc2 For a closer look at some of the concerns facing this group, we`re joined now by Laura Meckler, national education writer at "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 , as you know him, the former commissioner of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and a board member at \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , and Emily Oster, an economist at \uc2 Brown University\uc2 , and she joins us from Providence, Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you all.\par \par \uc2 Emily, I want to start with you. You wrote an article about pandemic amnesty. You said, basically, we need to forgive public officials for what they didn`t know during the worst of the pandemic. You had argued early on for opening of schools when the pandemic was still raging. What do you think we need to be focused on now? Because some of the debate still seems to be stuck two years ago.\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER, (Economist, \uc2 Brown University\uc2 ): Yes, so I`m an economist, but I`m also a parent. And I talk to a lot of parents. And what I can say is right now parents are very worried about the next steps for their kids. They see the historic test score declines. They see the declines in routine vaccination rates for kids. And they want us to be looking to solutions. They don`t want to be looking to rehash the debates that we had two years ago. They want to know, what investments are we going to make as a policy group to fix the problems that they`re seeing for their kids.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, amen to talking about solutions, but we aren`t, Laura, talking much about them as a country. To those scores that we just heard mentioned, the Education Department reported appalling historic declines. Twenty-five percent of fourth graders are below the basic level in math, 37 percent were below the basic level in reading. How much of it is linked to the pandemic closures? How much of it is linked to a broader problem? And why aren`t we hearing more about it?\par \par \uc2 LAURA MECKLER, (National Education Writer, "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 "): Well, we`re talking -- some of us are talking a lot about it. I think that it is really important. We have seen historic declines in -- particularly in math, but also in reading. And it is certainly linked to the pandemic. Some of it is linked to the schools that were remote for an extended period of time. But even schools that were not remote, also we`ve seen -- I mean that were not remote for very long, I should say -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAURA MECKLER: Have also seen significant declines in academic achievement. So - and I think that compared with the sort of social, emotional mental health real crisis that we`re seeing among children and teenagers right now is really putting enormous amount of pressure on schools and on families.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: To your point, because we looked at it, New Hampshire, which opened earlier, Georgia, Florida, where schools reopened faster, they may have fared better but they still saw declines.\par \par \uc2 LAURA MECKLER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, there`s still something happening.\par \par \uc2 LAURA MECKLER: Exactly. And I think that what most experts will tell you is that, you know, the pandemic was still happening. Even if kids were back in school. First of all, there were closures. There were quarantines. People were in and out of school.\par \par \uc2 But beyond that, you know, people were experiencing loss. They were seeing stress all around them. They didn`t have their normal lives. So, I think that, you know, the pandemic had a deep effect on families and on children and on schools. And I think those are going to be continuing for really many years to come.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And I know I don`t like it when people say the pandemic`s over because I still feel like I`m living through this haze, Dr. Gottlieb. School closures are still a live issue. I mean we`ve seen upticks in RSV, in flu and other viruses and some schools have closed. In Indiana we just saw one. Virginia. Should communities just take this out of the tool kit and keep schools open no matter what?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 , (Former \uc2 FDA\uc2  Commissioner): Well, look, we have a major epidemic of RSV. It may be peaking right now. We`re going to enter into a major epidemic of flu. And we haven`t been taking the actions that we would normally be taking to try to mitigate these kinds of pandemics because. I think a lot of public health officials and I think a lot of school districts are a little bit gun shy right now given the backlash to what we did during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Closing a school for one incubation cycle for two or three days when you had a major outbreak of flu or RSV in the past was not that uncommon. If you had 40 percent, 50 percent of the kids out of school, you might close for a day or two. Now when a school does in Indiana, it`s national news.\par \par \uc2 We know hand hygiene, for example, is very effective at stopping flu transmission. Do you hear any school district talking about hand hygiene right now? We know wearing a mask if you have the flu or RSV when you go out is effective at preventing forward transmission. Nobody wants to say that. So, I think that there`s a lot of reluctance now, in part because of the failures of public health messaging during the pandemic and the things we got wrong and the backlash to it. So we don`t have a good solution for what we`re entering right now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What do you make of - of Emily`s premise here, that there needs to be amnesty?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Well, look, I think we need to distinguish - and she was at the vanguard, to your point, at arguing for schools to opened. A lot of kids got back in the classroom because of her efforts. So she`s - she`s advocating on behalf of others when she talks about amnesty.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to distinguish between structural failures of institutions and mistakes that were made because we were in the fog of viral war and we didn`t understand the - the virus itself. There were institutions that failed. CDC. There were a lot of systemic failures there. Even people talk about the teachers union not working to get people back in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 And then there were things we got wrong. We didn`t recognize the virus was airborne. We thought it was droplet transmission, so we advocated the use of cloth masks when they weren`t effective. So, there were things we did wrong because we didn`t understand the virus. We need to learn from that.\par \par \uc2 But I think the - the structural features that we got wrong, where institutions failed, that we can`t move on from because we need to reform those institutions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Emily, parental rights has been harnessed effectively by Republicans in certain places on the campaign trail. But our CBS News polling shows this is broad concern, to your point.\par \par \uc2 Seventy-two percent of those polled say they`re concerned about learning declines after Covid. Seventy-two percent said they`re worried about bullying at their kids` school. Sixty-eight percent said they`re worried about gun violence. Fifty-seven percent said discussions of sexuality and gender concern them.\par \par \uc2 There`s so much concern about the classroom. What -- where does the focus need to be and where does it need to come from here?\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: I think that the focus needs to be on what we need to do to move forward for solutions, and, you know, and -- to give you a concrete example, when we look at something like test scores, which many parents are very worried about, we see that over the last school year there`s been some test score recovery, but that`s uneven. Some school districts have recovered to where they were in 2019. Some school districts haven`t recovered at all.\par \par \uc2 At this point, if we want to speak to these concerns of parents, we need to think about, what are the investments we`re making to figure out, why have some school districts been successful, what did they do that was successful? How can we comport those lessons over to districts that haven`t been successful? This kind of solutions-based focus has to be where we go rather than rehashing the discussions we had two years ago. That`s how we`re going to find solutions and get kids back on track because it`s, frankly, exactly as you say, kids are really suffering and we`re losing time on getting them back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Laura, the focus that we are hearing about the classrooms is on like the content of what`s being taught in them. And we saw that in that 57 percent discussion of sexuality and gender. Is that just because it`s easier to access or understand or - you -- why?\par \par \uc2 LAURA MECKLER: I think that the sort of culture wars that are -- have gripped schools in the last couple of years are now coming to the question of gender and sexuality. For a long time we saw them centered on issues of race. Like, so-called critical race theory and there was conversation -- discomfort among conservatives of conversations - of talking about systemic racism, for instance. And - and that has kind of given way now to concerns around gender identity, and transgender, transgender women competing in sports. All of those things, I think, are - are concerns among many conservatives and there are a lot of candidates for office and political figures who are playing on that and talking a lot about that.\par \par \uc2 And I think when they talk about parental rights, that`s what they`re talking about. They don`t want - they - they will assert things like, you know, schools are trying to turn boys into girls and girls into boys.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAURA MECKLER: I don`t think that that`s true. But that`s the kind of fears that -- because I think there`s still a lot of discomfort with the idea of questions around gender identity and there are people really working to tap into that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. I mean what Emily has argued is like, we need data on how to make up for educational deficits.\par \par \uc2 LAURA MECKLER: Well, this has nothing to do with that. I mean --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That - that`s (INAUDIBLE) on a bumper sticker?\par \par \uc2 LAURA MECKLER: Yes. I mean, right. Exactly. I mean, yes, that is the core problem facing education today, right now. Actual academics. Can kids do math? Can kids read at the levels they need to? Those are skills you`re going to need on into your education and into life. These other things are just much more emotional and tapping into sort of these culture war conversations that we`re having in this country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Dr. Gottlieb, I mean when you look at parents, it`s like a formula shortage, completely incoherent messaging from the CDC that you just laid out here, child care shortages in part due to what`s going on with infection rates. It is hard not to have anger and emotion about public health and how it`s communicated.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Yes, look, I think that there`s a pervasive sense, rightly so, that public health institutions have failed the public and that they weren`t equipped to tackle the challenges that people have been facing. We shouldn`t have had this shortage that we had with the formula. We should have responded to it more adequately than we did. CDC didn`t put out practical guidance, didn`t advise families on what to do. They required them for six feet of distancing is what kept most schools shut well into the spring of 2021. So, there`s a pervasive sense that public health institutions didn`t work on behalf of families. They were slow to integrate new information. And there`s not a - there`s not real evidence that they`ve reformed themselves. There hasn`t been an effort by the administration to try to, you know, try to reform - fundamentally reform CDC. CDC s working to reform itself, but that`s not usually the way it works. It`s hard to self-organize around a new mission if you`re an agency.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : So, I think if the administration had been more aggressive at addressing these deficits, they`d probably -- people wouldn`t feel so much angst.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Or you need to hear that from perhaps the new Congress, the pressure to reform.\par \par \uc2 Emily, you also raise, in your article, the question of mandates for vaccines. Are you arguing for flu and Covid vaccine mandates, because that`s very controversial?\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: In the article I discuss routine vaccinations, measles pertussis, where we don`t typically have mandates, but the rates have been very high. They have gone down over time. We haven`t seen schools move to Covid vaccine mandates, and I don`t expect them to.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 EMILY OSTER: And, similarly, we haven`t seen flu mandates, and I don`t expect to see those either.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. And I know Dr. Gottlieb also is against mandates of - of flu.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Yes, I don`t think this vaccine reaches the threshold of being mandated in schools.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right. Thanks for all of you. We could do a whole hour or more on all the parental tensions right now, but we`ve got to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment with more on how the fight over abortion rights is impacting the midterms.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We are joined now by our chief legal correspondent, Jan Crawford, and our political correspondent, Caitlin Huey-Burns.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you both here at the table.\par \par \uc2 Caitlin, I want to start with you.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been covering abortion access in states around the country. And that`s where the decision about access will be made, at the state level.\par \par \uc2 There are five states where abortion access is literally on the ballot November the 8th. What`s the expectation on whether this election will make access looser or tighter?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Yes, and in three of those states, California, Vermont, Michigan, the question is whether to amend the Constitution to enshrine abortion rights into law. And then the other two states there is either a ban or attempts to criminalize it.\par \par \uc2 But the biggest one I`m watching is in Michigan, that ballot measure, because there`s such a competitive governor`s race there with \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , the incumbent, the Democrat, very much campaigning on this issue of abortion access and rights, trying to galvanize her base of support and reach out to those independent suburban women in particular. And it`s interesting because Tudor Dixon, who`s the Republican running against her, is trying to say to voters, look, you can vote for this measure and also vote against \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  and vote for me.\par \par \uc2 The biggest question that we`ve been having this election cycle, when it comes to ballot measures is, you know, does this actually translate to support for the Democratic candidate?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: I was in Kansas covering that ballot measure where we saw overwhelming turnout, where we even saw Republicans vote for it. But that was a direct question to voters. And so when it`s on the ballot with candidates, does that have the same effect? And I think Michigan will be an interesting test of that.\par \par \uc2 We`re also looking at states like Wisconsin and North Carolina, that don`t have ballot measures, but the makeup of the state legislatures and other competitive races there could shape how this comes out because that`s really where this is being decided, it`s at the state legislative level.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which is what the court intended.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Sure. I mean it said that, you know, this is not a federal constitutional issue. This is an issue the state should decide. And leave it to the political process. And that`s what we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 I think what`s interesting, though, is, you know, some of the - the - the debate and the outcry among Democrats back in July has kind of receded a bit. I think once people realize the Supreme Court hadn`t banned abortion when it overturned Roe versus Wade and that many state laws weren`t really changing at all has kind of taken a backseat to issues like crime, inflation, the economy. But it does still have an impact.\par \par \uc2 And then, on the flip side, I think it`s important to remember for Republicans what that decision has done. Had the court refused to overturn Roe versus Wade, it would have really demoralized a key segment of the Republican base. Those voters who care deeply about social issues. The pro- lifers, the evangelicals, people who care about religious liberty. Instead, in order turning Roe, they`re galvanized. Those voters are excited, enthusiastic about voting. And -- and that`s an important segment for Republicans.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re also seeing, at the national level, where there isn`t a law protecting abortion. The conversation from President Biden is, if Democrats win, I will enshrine abortion access, specifically at that 24- week -- the language that was in Roe.\par \par \uc2 But then you have Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  trying to start the conversation and say it`s got to be up until 15 weeks. Is the bottom line that we will just not see any national law, period, that it will stay at the states?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: Well, what we`re seeing is both sides are using it to galvanize their supporters, right? Democrats are threatening that Republicans get control and then there will be a ban. And \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  handed them kind of a gift in saying, look, I would like to implement national restrictions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Restrictions but not a ban?\par \par \uc2 CAITLIN HUEY-BURNS: But not an outright ban at 15 weeks. But I think, you know, and you talk to Republicans and they don`t want to say at this point, yes, I`m supportive of a national ban, because they don`t want to turn voters away. That they need to attract here. And it also kind of flies in the face of their argument that this should be a state`s issue. But there - there is no question that this is a base amplifying issue and -- and Democrats need it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Jan, I want to ask you about a case that is also getting politicized potentially here. Moore versus Harper. Former Secretary of State Hillary Clinton spoke about it to a progressive group and said it`s about right-wing extremists having a plan to literally steal the next presidential election. That`s a big statement. What is this case about and what`s the outcome?\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, she`s talking about a case that the court`s going to hear arguments on next month. And it involves this kind of -- called the independent state legislator theory. And to opponents, that`s a radical theory that would really strip state courts from having any oversight into the election laws and procedures that are adopted by state legislatures for federal elections.\par \par \uc2 Now, supporters of this new theory say, well, the federal constitution gives that power to the state legislatures, not to the state courts. And if you`ve got a problem, take it to federal court and they can deal with it.\par \par \uc2 The question I think is not so much would it directly address things like seating presidential electors. The concern for opponents of this theory, and it is a serious concern that they have, is that if the court adopts this theory, then that sends a message, Katie bar the door, state legislatures can do anything they want. And that, they believe, is dangerous in terms of what they may try to do in some of these election laws and procedures.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you, ladies, for tracking that for us.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. We will see you Tuesday for election coverage. For FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020221106eib600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Planned Parenthood Has Launched A New Way To Expand Abortion Access\b0\par\par\uc2 Adriana Diaz \par \uc2 657 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Saturday\par \uc2 CBSA\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This comes as Planned Parenthood has launched a new way to expand abortion access, and it is controversial.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This is Planned Parenthood of St. Louis` latest facility.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (Planned Parenthood`s Chief Medical Officer for St. Louis Region and Southwest Missouri): Folks will be directed to the to the exam rooms where the rest of their care will be delivered.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But it is propped up on wheels.\par \par \uc2 You can like feel the fact that we`re not on --\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We are not in a building.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: It is a little more bouncy.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The abortion clinic is in an RV, a first of its kind for Planned Parenthood. It`ll be parked in Illinois, where abortion is legal near states like Missouri and Kentucky where it`s not, says Colleen McNicholas, their chief medical officer for St. Louis.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: And we know the mobile unit won`t be able to get all the way to the folks who are losing access, but if we could even reduce that trip by a couple of hundred miles for folks, that makes a huge difference for people in terms of how much this care costs and how quickly they can get back to their families and their lives.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The goal is to provide medical abortions by pill on board by yearend and surgical procedures next year.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Hands off our bodies.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Since Roe was struck down, Planned Parenthood has seen a 370 percent increase at its Southern Illinois clinic from patients from outside Illinois and Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Was the influx what you anticipated?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We saw it a lot more quickly than we expected it to come.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It came fast and furious.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: It came fast and furious.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We are in Illinois where abortion is legal. But is providing abortion to women in Missouri where it is illegal undermining Missouri law?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: You know, my priority is not to think about how we are hurting the feelings of the folks in Missouri who are banning access to basic health care. Our priority is making sure that the constituents who have been left behind in those states have access to basic healthcare.\par \par \uc2 SAM LEE (Antiabortion Rights Lobbyist): We think it`s very irresponsible for Planned Parenthood trying to solicit Missouri women and then when a woman has a complication, where can she go back to, to get help when that abortion van is gone?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Sam Lee is an antiabortion rights lobbyist who wants more resources to go to alternatives for pregnant women in Missouri, like pregnancy centers and housing help.\par \par \uc2 SAM LEE: It always comes down to -- for women who are unsure about their decision, or are being coerced into their decision being given true choices so that she does get the support that she needs.\par \par \uc2 PROTESTERS: Pro-life, pro-woman.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With raw emotion on both sides, the RV will initially have a security guard on board a, calculated risk.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We`re really at a critical moment where we need folks to be thinking differently, to be working together to be acting boldly and to be taking risks.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Still ahead on the CBS WEEKEND NEWS, how California is trying to preserve its most precious water supply.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSA000020221110eib50008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Conversation With Sebastian Maniscalco And Dr. Scott Cohen\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Dana Jacobson \par \uc2 2,688 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That, I am getting, too.\par \par \uc2 We`re back now with comedian Sebastian Maniscalco and his family pediatrician, that would be Dr. Scott Cohen. They`ve got a podcast together. It`s called "Daddy vs. Doctor" where they share parenting advice and talk candidly about their own adventures in fathering.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to both of you.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, in this setup package, we saw an interview, a conversation with two parents who are struggling with a baby who will not sleep. This is torture for parents. Everyone can relate to it.\par \par \uc2 Sleep training is an option, but a controversial one. So Dr. Cohen, what do you tell families about sleep training?\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: So, any time after four months, the idea of sleep training is getting a child to sleep through the night, minimum of 10 hours without feeding, without picking them up. And usually you can do it in a healthy child anywhere four months or after.\par \par \uc2 And you`re right, it is not for everybody. Everybody has to make their own choices, but to your point I think parents are happier when their child is sleeping through the night, relationships are better, and the child is happier.\par \par \uc2 And it actually teaches something really important, you`re not starving your child. All of those feedings are self-soothing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: You`re not psychologically damaging them, you`re actually teaching them how to self-soothe, which is an important thing for them to learn down the road, and they can learn that at four months.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, my pediatrician told us that there are three steps to sleep training. Put the baby in the crib.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: One.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Close the door.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Two.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come back in the morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Three.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That sounds simple.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Don`t watch. Don`t watch the baby monitor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t watch the monitor. They`re going to -- there`s going to be crying, but they are learning --\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: You forgot the noise cancelling headphones.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But Tony, like we saw on the piece, the mom said, I cry when the baby cries, and you say the words "crying it out."\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a bad way to describe it.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: Crying it out got a bad name because it sounds like you`re neglecting your child and you`re really not. But the truth is, all sleep training is some variation of letting the child learn to self-soothe, but it`s not easy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`ve been through this as parents. My wife has heard me talk about sleep training so much that her friends used to call her and she would give the advice.\par \par \uc2 So then when my daughter was four months, she`s like, "We`re doing it." Yes, easier said than done.\par \par \uc2 Put her to sleep. Ten o`clock, she`s waking up screaming. I`m in bed pretending not to listen, but I`m like dying inside.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: My wife is literally just shooting me looks, shooting me looks, and then all of a sudden she`s like, done, aborted that night. The whole next day, we fought. I was the insensitive father.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re the bad guy.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: She was the hypocritical mother who gave advice, but couldn`t follow it. We came to a consensus, we did it later that day. My daughter has been a great sleeper, but it`s not easy and not for everybody.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: And Sebastian, you had a night nurse. Right?\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: Yes. So growing up, we didn`t really have sleep training, it was "Go to bed."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: And "Stop crying."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: There was no sleep training. We were fortunate enough to have this woman come in and give us a lot of help. And my kids have not gotten out into bed ever.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Ever since.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: They don`t even know where our bedroom is. So it was really, really helpful in that situation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: And listen, I`m the one that always wants to go with.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: And you know, tend to the baby. My wife is more like "Just let them be." I`m a little bit more sensitive in that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And now Sebastian, you know, you make light of the daddy`s perspective when talking about family issues, kid issues. But has there been a moment or moments where you just like, you reached your limit, like it`s not funny and you feel like --\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: It`s never really funny in the moment, but in the reflection of it all...\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: ... there`s where the morsels of humor start to kind of pop. So yes, sometimes it takes a little time away from the situation for my sense of humor to kick in. But a lot of times, when it`s happening.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s intense.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: I am like, "What`s going on?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But so, everybody says when they become a parent, they`re not going to lose their temper. They`re not going to yell, they`re going to be a certain kind of Zen and get down on one knee, "What`s going on? Tell me what`s happening." And then reality sets in.\par \par \uc2 How do you keep yourself -- when you feel yourself like ready to blow?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Keep composure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, how do you keep your composure or try to?\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: I don`t know. Nothing wrong with a little volume in the house.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is okay.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: Right. I mean, my wife is always like, "Why are you getting upset? Why are you yelling?" I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Why not?\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: I am angry.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`re not listening.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a mess.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: I cannot suppress a lot of like, sometimes when you get like, emotional about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: It`s not like I`m walking around like an animal, but at times, you know, my voice gets a little loud and I think it`s good for the kid. No? Please back me on this.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes. Sebastian, I will back you up.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: Where is your wife right now?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You give a lot of head nods in here right now.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: It is. It absolutely is. You know, we go to you know -- the kids bring us to a level, we go to the next. It`s sort of a back and forth, right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: And it`s hard, and we`ve all been there. And I think that`s what we found most about this podcast. It`s been giving -- the feedback we`ve gotten is that it`s giving parents confidence, because you just want to know as a parent you`re doing it right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Doing okay.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: And you know, we had that dad last week. I mean, this guy should be the poster child of fathers. He was like --\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN: Radiating.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: Radiating glowing, and honestly, he only called in just to say, "Am I doing okay? Am I doing right by my kids?" And he was just glowing about it. I mean, I felt inadequate after he talked, but it was just really nice to be there to say, "Yes. There`s lots of different right ways of doing it. You`re doing a great job."\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Can I go back to a sleep question, but not for babies. So, I have an 11-year-old and you hear all these things that they need a certain amount of sleep, they need --\par \par \uc2 You can`t necessarily get them to sleep and there are some kids that are like eight hours, there are some that are seven. Isn`t there a right amount? How do you make -- you can`t make an 11-year-old go back to sleep when they`re up at six in the morning and they haven`t got --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gate the bedroom.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: Right? I know.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: Well, we just talked about --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: They are not all Sebastian`s kids who are afraid to get out of the bed.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: So there is. I mean, we look for about 10 hours minimum around four months of age and as they get older, it drops a little more so nine hours and then eight hours. So in that eight to 10 range is great. You`re lucky to get eight.\par \par \uc2 At least, we talk about with older kids is getting into a bedtime routine, getting off the electronics at least an hour before bed, just --\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: But if they just get up, like if they are doing all of that, are there just some kids who don`t --\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: Some kids, you just get up early. Yes, but at least it`s their downtime.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: You know? And if they`re not waking you up, right? Are they waking you up?\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Maybe. Sometimes.\par \par \uc2 DR. SCOTT COHEN: Maybe, sometimes.\par \par \uc2 DANA JACOBSON: Sometimes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Sebastian, just having people talk particularly fathers about parenting. It didn`t happen a generation ago, not like this anyway. There is not a lot of outlets to this day. What are you hoping? What are you hearing actually from other dads who know this podcast exists?\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: Well, a lot of like podcasts that are male dominated are talking about sports or are talking about hunting or whatever.\par \par \uc2 You don`t really see two guys talking about, so when your kid doesn`t eat, what do you do? So we`re just -- you know, we`re trying to open up that conversation. And it`s nice that this is a platform where fathers could call in, fathers could listen maybe while they`re mowing the lawn and go, "Oh, yes. I heard that on the podcast. I`ll try it." So yes, it`s just something different.\par \par \uc2 Parents should have fun and we`re trying to make it fun to raise the kids and --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I might go to you on a teenager question.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There we go. Sebastian Maniscalco and Dr. Scott Cohen, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 New episodes of "Daddy vs. Doctor premiere every Friday.\par \par \uc2 We appreciate you guys.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, up next, a look at all that mattered this week. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back. But we`ve got to go. Sebastian, you`ve got this right.\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO: That`s it. That does it for us. Let`s take a look back at all that mattered this week.\par \par \uc2 Have a great weekend people.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Horrifying details about the attack on Paul Pelosi and the alleged plot against House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: A hammer was used by this suspect to carry out this attack.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How did we get to this point?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DICKERSON: You have a system that structurally incentivizes more and more heated rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Republicans are in a good position to win a majority of the House.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: They`re paying attention to the economy, crime, abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Both parties are drawing out big names.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Turnout for early voting has been at a record pace.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The gun violence in America has taken the life of another well-known musician, the rapper known as Takeoff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is another senseless examples.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is just too many of them, whether it`s a mass shooting or something like this.\par \par \uc2 RYAN COOGLER ("Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" director): With Chadwick`s tragic passing, I know we put our all into it. I think -- I think it`s a movie that he would enjoy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Best movie ever.\par \par \uc2 It`s really just getting started.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s just getting started.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At 36, Ryan Coogler.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The great Ryan Coogler, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We learned that Rihanna was going to contribute to the movie.\par \par \uc2 WINSTON DUKE (Actor): Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And it says that you`re going to be in her fashion show, "The Savage." So, will you be wearing lingerie?\par \par \uc2 WINSTON DUKE: Yes, I will be at "The Savage." The legs will be out. They will be oiled.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ain`t nobody mad at that.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I can see the tease on the show, like we will have to be on commercial, like...\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, they`re going to do something -- of course, they`re going to use it.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN STEELE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Mother Nature is actually giving us quite the treat.\par \par \uc2 Bootifully spooktacular day.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got to say spooky, right?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Oh, that`s fun.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am really digging the Clark Kent costume, man. Well played.\par \par \uc2 JORDAN STEELE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Friendsgiving is back this year. Gen Z and Millennials are leading the way.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t a lot of Gen Z live at home still? I mean, like so when it is Friendsgiving, you just invite your friends over and what?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Tell me. Tell me what`s funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What are Mom and Dad doing?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think that`s what they`re calling their mom and dad. My friends are cooking today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Do you take your kids` Halloween candy?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh yes. For sure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is amazing how oblivious a three-year-old really is. It is easy to take candy from a baby as it turns out.\par \par \uc2 LAKE BELL (Actor): The voice is like in constant state of evolution.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve never thought about your voice? You are a professional broadcaster.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I didn`t --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What about like now versus when you first started?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Think about it right now. Yes. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is such a setup.\par \par \uc2 Bob Steele (sp?) is probably no stranger. On at 5:30. He`s as reliable as clockwork and has been for decades.\par \par \uc2 Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 Okay, so does that girl still have a job?\par \par \uc2 LAKE BELL: First of all, you look incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If I look confused, it is because I am. This looks like a lot of steps to me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But people have --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For people that can cook.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re always confused in the kitchen.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Take a look at what he did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually know Formula One, excuse you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, this is NASCAR, a little different. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually know Formula One.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, I`m sorry. You think the World Series though has a halftime.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221108eib4000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Abortion Providers Go Mobile\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Adriana Diaz, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 815 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at the big issues for voters in the Midterms including access to abortion. A new study by an abortion rights advocacy group found that abortions declined by six percent nationwide in the months after Roe v. Wade was overturned, but the number of procedures has gone up in the States where abortion is legal, especially in places like Illinois, which border States with more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz traveled to the southern part of that State, home to a new mobile Planned Parenthood clinic, complete with two exam rooms, a waiting area and a lab.\par \par \uc2 Her report is a CBS MORNINGS broadcast exclusive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN cheering.)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This may look like your standard long haul RV, but step on board and it`s a fully operational medical facility, or as these Planned Parenthood employees call it, an act of activism.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (Planned Parenthood of St. Louis Chief Medical Officer): It looks very much like a regular brick and mortar Health Center for us.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But you can like feel the fact that we`re not on --\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We`re not in a building.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: It is a little more bouncy.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Dr. Colleen McNicholas is the Chief Medical Officer at Planned Parenthood of St. Louis. She says the mobile clinic will be parked in Illinois near the border with Missouri to make abortions easier to access from out-of-state.\par \par \uc2 They plan to provide medical abortions by pill here in the mobile unit by the end of the year and surgical procedures next year.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Hands Off Our Body.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s part of Planned Parenthood`s answer to the Supreme Court overturning Roe vs. Wade in June. Since then, they`ve seen a 370 percent increase at their Southern Illinois clinic from patients traveling from beyond Illinois and Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Was the influx what you anticipated?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We saw it a lot more quickly than we expected it to come.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It came fast and furious.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: It came fast and furious.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We are in Illinois where abortion is legal, but is providing abortion to women in Missouri where it is illegal, undermining Missouri law?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: You know, our priority is making sure that the constituents who have been left behind in those States have access to basic healthcare.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Pro-Life, Pro-Woman.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Missouri only had one abortion clinic left before Roe fell due in part to lobbyists like Sam Lee of Campaign Life Missouri, an antiabortion rights group.\par \par \uc2 SAM LEE (Campaign Life Missouri): We think it`s very irresponsible for Planned Parenthood trying to solicit Missouri women and then when a woman has a complication, where can she go back to, to get help when that abortion van is gone?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Instead, Lee wants to see more resources go to alternatives for pregnant women here in Missouri, like pregnancy centers and housing help.\par \par \uc2 SAM LEE: It always comes down to for women who are unsure about their decision who are being coerced into their decision, being given true choices.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Roe v. Wade has got to go.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With so many raw emotions being expressed on both sides of this issue --\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We were really thoughtful about the security and safety of the unit.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The RV is equipped with cameras and will initially have a security guard.\par \par \uc2 Still, Dr. McNicholas says this is the time to push boundaries.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We`re really at a critical moment, a public health crisis where we need folks to be thinking differently, to be working together, to be acting boldly and to be taking risks.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Adriana Diaz in Southern Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up, the latest on a shooting that killed a prominent rap artist and why many see this as a part of a wider picture of violence in these United States.\par \par \uc2 Also a reminder for you this morning, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Get the day`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That`s a deal.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221103eib2000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 North Korea Fires 20-plus Missiles Near South Korea; Obama and Biden Make Midterm Push; Close Race in Virginia; New Details in Pelosi Home Attack; Another Interest Rate Hike; CVS Agrees to $5 Billion Opioid Settlement; Iran Sentences Protesters to Death; Abortion Providers Go Mobile; Rapper Takeoff Murdered; Remembering Julie Powell; Lena Horne Makes Broadway History; Living with Tourette Syndrome; Flipping Workplace Norms; "The Good Fight" Final Season; One Smart Cookie, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Elizabeth Palmer, Robert  Costa, Scott MacFarlane, Nancy Chen, Imtiaz Tyab, David Begnaud, Adriana  Diaz, Vladimir Duthiers \par \uc2 14,044 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We are all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): You heard me say it before, we`re at an inflection point in history.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R) (Pennsylvania Senate candidate): Here`s the question: Are you happy with where America is headed?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With the Midterms just six days away, both parties are in a fight to the finish over who will control Congress.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Air raid sirens sounded on a South Korean island one.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: One North Korean missile landed close to the South Korean sea border.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  meets today to talk about interest rates. What it could mean for the US economy.\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER (New Jersey realtor): I would describe the housing market as sort of a tug of war between buyer and seller.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the music world is remembering Migos rapper Takeoff who was tragically gunned down.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN I fell to my knees and I started crying because something like this wasn`t supposed to happen.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The sentencing hearing has begun for the shooter found guilty of killing 17 people at a high school in Parkland, Florida back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 Phillies\uc2  win Game Three the World Series and take it to one lead against the Astros.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Monstrous blast.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : You get one of these.\par \par \uc2 DR. JILL BIDEN (First Lady of the United States): Yes.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): The President and the First Lady greeting trick or treaters at the White House for the first time.\par \par \uc2 They had a great time giving out candy to all the trick or treaters. Knowing Biden, I`m guessing the kids got licorice and \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  schedules.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): Designers have created a very limited edition $134,000.00 iPhone with a diamond studded gold Rolex on the back. Here it is here. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 Yes, so now when you drop your phone, you can crack two expensive screens. An iPhone with a built-in Rolex. Can you imagine that?\par \par \uc2 Imagine a phone -- imagine a phone that also tells the time.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Imagine somebody buying that and they are.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Imagine that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they will.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Too much money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, and they will.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS today.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got some powerful stories to share with you, including a very personal one from our David Begnaud. He is talking to people who have Tourette Syndrome. It`s a disorder that can lead to isolation and stigma as well as physical suffering.\par \par \uc2 Now, this is very personal because as David has discussed with us before, he himself has a mild form of Tourette`s. He will tell us about the myths, the medications, and most importantly the hope available for those really suffering.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And the excellent Paramount+ show "The Good Fight" is coming to an end after six seasons. It`s a good excuse to have the beloved star, Christine Baranski here in studio.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with an alarming sign of rising tensions between North and South Korea. Developments over the past 12 hours, the North has fired nearly two dozen missiles into the sea around the Korean Peninsula.\par \par \uc2 According to officials in South Korea and Japan, one of those missiles splashdown in an area that is supposed to be and was until now a no-fire zone.\par \par \uc2 South Korea responded with its own missile test while it continued joint military exercises with the US.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has more now from Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen an unprecedented number of North Korean missile launches this year, but today`s was unusually aggressive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: South Koreans watch the news that one more time their neighbor was firing weapons in their direction and this time, far too close for comfort. One missile flew south and actually crossed the informal maritime border. It landed in the sea just 37 miles from the South Korean City of Sokcho. The other flew near the island of Ulleungdo, near enough to activate air raid sirens --\par \par \uc2 (MAN and WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: And warn people to seek shelter.\par \par \uc2 All week, US and South Korean war planes have been flying joint drills, the latest in a series of military exercises that have infuriated North Korean leader Kim Jong-un.\par \par \uc2 Today he retaliated with multiple missile launches, and in turn, South Korea fired three air-to-ground missiles of its own northward in a dangerous tit-for-tat.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Analysts and my diplomatic sources say that there is no reason to think tension on the Korean Peninsula is going to diminish. anytime soon, in fact just the opposite. They expect that North Korea may escalate with an underground nuclear weapons test sometime quite soon -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Elizabeth, reporting from Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 Here in the United States, the midterms have you know, are just six days away -- six -- and some big names, they are out on the campaign trail right now for Democrats and Republicans, too.\par \par \uc2 Former President Obama spoke in Nevada and President Biden spoke in Florida, both urgently pressing their message that democracy is on the line next Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa joins us now from Pennsylvania, one of five States with extremely close races for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Big win for the \uc2 Philadelphia Phillies\uc2  last night in the World Series has a lot of people in this region excited, but voters in the Philadelphia suburbs, they`re paying attention to more than sports -- the economy, crime, abortion rights, those issues, also the talk of the town.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): Hello, Las Vegas.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Democrats came out swinging Tuesday night hoping to fend off a potential red wave.\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA: Just about every Republican politician seems obsessed with two things, owning the libs and getting Donald Trump`s approval. They`re not interested in actually solving problems.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  was in Las Vegas where the Senate race in Nevada is razor thin.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Democracy is on the ballot this year.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  in Florida where Democrats are in an uphill battle against GOP incumbents.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The economy works for everybody.\par \par \uc2 Compared to the MAGA -- mega MAGA, I call them, trickle-down economics that the Republicans offer these days.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Control of Congress might come down to five crucial States, all with candidates neck and neck in the polls. Bucks County, Pennsylvania northeast of Philadelphia is where Republican candidate, Mehmet Oz kicked off his final push for the Senate.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ: Are you happy with where America is headed?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: As Democratic opponent, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , Tuesday reiterated that he is ready to serve as he recovers from a stroke.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) (Pennsylvania Senate candidate): This campaign is all about anyone that`s ever been knocked down, that has had to get back up.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: But it`s the economy that has independent, Chelsea Mitchell concerned. She owns this vintage vinyl store where inflation has become a broken record.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MITCHELL (Business owner): Well, I can tell you that a copy of Abbey Road on vinyl used to cost like $24.99 and has jumped $5.00. I`m voting for whoever is -- has the small town in mind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Later today, First Lady Jill Biden will be in Philadelphia. She and President Biden have been busy campaigning in Pennsylvania for Democrats. She will attend Game Four of the World Series -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And it`s a good World Series, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Bob, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Control of the House is also up for grabs on Tuesday. Our latest battleground tracker poll shows Republicans favored to win a majority, but there are a lot of close races including one in Virginia involving a member of the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane spent time traveling in that State and now he is here with us.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: And Nate, good to see you as well.\par \par \uc2 This is a race against Republican candidate, Jen Kiggans and incumbent Democrat, \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 , and Congresswoman Luria has something you need to talk about, what she calls her effort to prevent another attempted insurrection and attack on democracy.\par \par \uc2 It is just unclear if that`s going to sway enough votes.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: At a Farmers Market in Virginia Beach, there`s one thing on voters` minds.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Food prices are bad. I mean, it`s gone skyrocketed.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Prices of everything, you know, gasoline, groceries. It`s unreal.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Like in so many congressional races, inflation and abortion rights are dominating the debate between Democratic incumbent Congresswoman \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  and Republican challenger Jen Kiggans. both \uc2 US Navy\uc2  veterans.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): I think a woman should have a right to choice.\par \par \uc2 JEN KIGGANS (R) (Virginia Congressional candidate): We are suffering this year because of the failed policies of the \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  administration.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Except, this is a race in a campaign season like no other.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  maliciously repeated this nonsense.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Because Luria isn`t a typical incumbent. She is one of nine Members of Congress who became national figures leading the House January 6 Select Committee.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): Our hearings have shown the many ways in which President Trump tried to stop the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Her opponent slams Luria for focusing too much on the investigation.\par \par \uc2 JEN KIGGANS: So, I think it shows that she is out of touch with this district.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Being on the Committee is out of touch?\par \par \uc2 JEN KIGGANS: So focusing on January 6th, making that a priority and using her celebrity on that Committee to do things like fundraise, and I just think it`s out of touch with what the voters of the second district are really caring about right now.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Luria strikes back criticizing Kiggans for questioning Virginia`s 2020 election result, a State President Biden won by 10 points.\par \par \uc2 Is there any political peril to being a face of the January 6 Committee in one of these tossup evenly split districts?\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): Honestly, I don`t care. The truth about it is, is that the work that the Committee is doing is so important. Being on the right side of history, getting to the facts.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The three of the other nine members of this panel are going to be gone at the end of the year. Two are retiring. Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , as you recall, lost her primary in Wyoming. This was a wedge issue, her service on the committee, and last night in Michigan, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , who says she is going to defeat the election deniers endorses a Democrat in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. It is a little jarring to see that picture, but she really is standing up for what she believes in. But it`s interesting when you talk to voters, emotions are so intense on both sides. Everybody feels very strongly about the selection.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: There we are in Virginia Beach where they have strong thoughts as you say, but they bring up inflation. They bring up the grocery prices. They bring up the gas prices.\par \par \uc2 I mentioned the January 6 Committee, they`re all familiar with it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s not top of mind for them.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Not top of mind.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: One thing affects you today, the other thing may affect you down the line.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: It`s immediate versus long term.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Scott, it is good to have you in studio. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And a reminder, the Midterms are less than one week away and we will have full coverage for you here on \uc2 CBS\uc2  starting at 8:00 PM Eastern on November 8th.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: CBS News has confirmed new information about the attack on House Speaker Nancy Pelosi`s husband, Paul. Sources say the \uc2 US Capitol Police\uc2  have cameras outside the Pelosi home, which captured images of the break-in, but officers only noticed a problem after local police had responded. This was originally reported in "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The man accused in the attack, his name is David DePape appeared in court yesterday in San Francisco. He has pled not guilty to multiple charges.\par \par \uc2 According to a Court filing, DePape told police that he was "on a suicide mission." He had a list of politicians he was targeting after allegedly breaking in and asking Paul Pelosi "Where`s Nancy" before attacking him with a hammer.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And we have breaking news this morning on the opioid epidemic: \uc2 CVS Health\uc2  has announced a $5 billion settlement of lawsuits tied to the company`s handling of prescription opioids, which of course are addictive.\par \par \uc2 The settlement, if accepted would be one of the largest payouts from the crisis and the first nationwide agreement with a major drugstore chain.\par \par \uc2 Others including \uc2 Rite-Aid\uc2 , \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  have made deals with individual States. CVS, for its part says it did not admit liability and nonfinancial terms have yet to be resolved.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is expected to raise interest rates by another three-quarters of a percentage point today. Regulators are trying to bring down the worst inflation we`ve seen in 40 years. But that policy change has pushed mortgage rates above seven percent.\par \par \uc2 As Nancy Chen shows us, the rising cost of borrowing is hurting home buyers and sellers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TILL (Homeowner would be home seller): Want one more treat?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: for about a year, Andrew Till tried to sell his New Jersey home in search of more space.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TILL: So we`ve had a lot of activity and a lot of offers on the house when we had it on the market. It is like every weekend, our broker calling and saying, "Can you get out?"\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But even after 10 lucrative offers, he and his family took it off the market. He says there are a few options for them to buy a new house, especially after refinancing their mortgage in March to below three percent.\par \par \uc2 You`ve been looking to sell, why haven`t you?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TILL: Our interest rate right now that we have locked in is very low. So our borrowing costs would more than double.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: With today`s rate, a monthly mortgage payment on a $400,000.00 home would cost you about $750.00 more than the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 How would you describe the housing market right now?\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER: So I would describe the housing market as sort of a tug of war between buyer and seller, and that tug of war is almost creating like a shutdown.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Stacy Esser is a realtor in the New Jersey area.\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER: It is not that we`ve never seen a seven percent interest rate, it is that we`ve never seen interest rates in basically one year go from two and a half percent to seven percent.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Esser says, this kind of volatility is causing deals to fall through.\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER: A buyer might have a pre-approval at a certain rate, get an accepted bid and then the interest rate shift, they didn`t lock in and all of a sudden, they can`t afford that same house.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: About 60,000 deals have been cancelled so far this year, the highest share on record since the start of the pandemic, and new listings are down about 19 percent from a year ago.\par \par \uc2 What is the end game to all this?\par \par \uc2 STACY ESSER: I think we`re going to see way less transactions in 2023. I think people are going to be sitting on the sidelines until things settle down.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Till says, he will wait until then.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW TILL: We just wanted to see if we could strike while the iron was hot, it didn`t happen and we`re happy here.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The housing market is a leading indicator of whether the nation is heading into a recession, but here is the thing, it could also contribute to one.\par \par \uc2 Fewer homes being purchased also means less construction, as well as people buying fewer items like appliances and all of this could have huge ripple effects throughout the economy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I haven`t played tug of war since I was a kid. It`ll be interesting to see who wins in this one, the buyer or the seller?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Or we could all potentially end up losing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that, too. Thank you, Nancy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Turning to an escalating situation in Iran. A Court has ordered seven anti-government protesters to be executed. It`s all part of a crackdown against demonstrations that have spread across the country over the past six weeks after a young woman died in police custody.\par \par \uc2 Imtiaz Tyab reports on this latest Court case.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In a trial lasting just 10 minutes with no defense lawyers present, these men, one only 22 years old were sentenced to death for protesting.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: In a video posted online, the Mother of Govagub Mohammad (sp?) pleads for his life saying, "What kind of Court orders an execution and the first appearance?"\par \par \uc2 Iran`s crackdown on the nationwide protests have been brutal. According to the group, Human Rights Activists in Iran, thousands more face similar hearings.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Demonstrations, which were initially led by young women, outraged at the death of 22-year-old Mahsa Amini, who in September died while in the custody of Iran`s so-called Morality Police for are apparently wearing her mandatory headscarf improperly.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Filling the streets of the capital, Tehran chanting "Women, Life, \uc2 Liberty\uc2 ," and even burning their hijabs.\par \par \uc2 In the deeply conservative coastal city of Bandar Abbas, women at a university tore down a cafeteria wall that separates men and women in defiance of the regime`s other strict gender segregation laws.\par \par \uc2 Now, Iranians of all ages are demanding the fall of the theocratic regime, but some are paying the ultimate price for rising up. This video posted online, which CBS News cannot independently verify apparently shows a group of policemen beating a man on the ground before the sound of a gunshot is heard.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 IMTIAZ TYAB: Now, early reports from inside Iran suggests that police are investigating that case, but make no mistake, these protests are the biggest challenge Iran`s all powerful clerics have ever faced.\par \par \uc2 Now, they are blaming "foreign agents" for the unrest, but Iranians tell us all they want is to be free -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Imtiaz Tyab, thank you. Just disheartening video.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You know, we have problems in this country, we obviously do, but the rights and freedoms we have protected that kind of terrible, terrible stuff from happening. I mean, killing protesters on day one of a trial. Think about that. That`s not justice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up: A look back at the career of a rapper called Takeoff from the group, Migos, who was just 28 years old when he was gunned down yesterday, and how it is part of a bigger story about gun violence across America.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:17, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up: A CBS MORNINGS broadcast exclusive, we`re going to take you to a new mobile abortion clinic in Illinois set up after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Why it will be parked near the border with other States.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So let`s talk about Tourette Syndrome. Do you know anyone who has it? Chances are you do, but you don`t know.\par \par \uc2 There are several famous people diagnosed with Tourette`s like singer, Billie Eilish and US soccer star, Tim Howard, and our own David Begnaud who is here to introduce us to three people who are living with TS.\par \par \uc2 So I was saying, David, if you hadn`t told me I wouldn`t have known you had it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Really? You`ve never seen me tic?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I honestly haven`t.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You thought I had seen it?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes. You just were kind enough not to say, "Hey, what`s that?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I would have asked you.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Yes. Knowing you, you would have.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would have just said, "Are you okay?"\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: This morning, we`re going to let you hear from a cop, a local TV reporter, and a CPA -- they all have Tourette`s, but their cases are really so different.\par \par \uc2 And so this morning, we`re going to raise awareness about having Tourette`s and having this conversation to sort of dispel some myths, talk about some medications, and also just let you see how this man for example, had one of the most severe cases I`ve ever seen in my life and is functioning as close to normal today as possible.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at the big issues for voters in the Midterms including access to abortion. A new study by an abortion rights advocacy group found that abortions declined by six percent nationwide in the months after Roe v. Wade was overturned, but the number of procedures has gone up in the States where abortion is legal, especially in places like Illinois, which border States with more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz traveled to the southern part of that State, home to a new mobile Planned Parenthood clinic, complete with two exam rooms, a waiting area and a lab.\par \par \uc2 Her report is a CBS MORNINGS broadcast exclusive.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (WOMEN cheering.)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: This may look like your standard long haul RV, but step on board and it`s a fully operational medical facility, or as these Planned Parenthood employees call it, an act of activism.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS (Planned Parenthood of St. Louis Chief Medical Officer): It looks very much like a regular brick and mortar Health Center for us.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: But you can like feel the fact that we`re not on --\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We`re not in a building.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: It is a little more bouncy.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Dr. Colleen McNicholas is the Chief Medical Officer at Planned Parenthood of St. Louis. She says the mobile clinic will be parked in Illinois near the border with Missouri to make abortions easier to access from out-of-state.\par \par \uc2 They plan to provide medical abortions by pill here in the mobile unit by the end of the year and surgical procedures next year.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Hands Off Our Body.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It`s part of Planned Parenthood`s answer to the Supreme Court overturning Roe vs. Wade in June. Since then, they`ve seen a 370 percent increase at their Southern Illinois clinic from patients traveling from beyond Illinois and Missouri.\par \par \uc2 Was the influx what you anticipated?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We saw it a lot more quickly than we expected it to come.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: It came fast and furious.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: It came fast and furious.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: We are in Illinois where abortion is legal, but is providing abortion to women in Missouri where it is illegal, undermining Missouri law?\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: You know, our priority is making sure that the constituents who have been left behind in those States have access to basic healthcare.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Pro-Life, Pro-Woman.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Missouri only had one abortion clinic left before Roe fell due in part to lobbyists like Sam Lee of Campaign Life Missouri, an antiabortion rights group.\par \par \uc2 SAM LEE (Campaign Life Missouri): We think it`s very irresponsible for Planned Parenthood trying to solicit Missouri women and then when a woman has a complication, where can she go back to, to get help when that abortion van is gone?\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Instead, Lee wants to see more resources go to alternatives for pregnant women here in Missouri, like pregnancy centers and housing help.\par \par \uc2 SAM LEE: It always comes down to for women who are unsure about their decision who are being coerced into their decision, being given true choices.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Roe v. Wade has got to go.")\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: With so many raw emotions being expressed on both sides of this issue --\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We were really thoughtful about the security and safety of the unit.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: The RV is equipped with cameras and will initially have a security guard.\par \par \uc2 Still, Dr. McNicholas says this is the time to push boundaries.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS: We`re really at a critical moment, a public health crisis where we need folks to be thinking differently, to be working together, to be acting boldly and to be taking risks.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Adriana Diaz in Southern Illinois.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up, the latest on a shooting that killed a prominent rap artist and why many see this as a part of a wider picture of violence in these United States.\par \par \uc2 Also a reminder for you this morning, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Get the day`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That`s a deal.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This morning, the all too familiar scourge of gun violence in America has taken the life of another well-known musician. The rapper known as Takeoff was part of the Grammy-nominated hip-hop trio, Migos.\par \par \uc2 Police say he was shot dead early yesterday outside a private party in Houston. Two others were injured and the gunman got away. Authorities say Takeoff was an innocent bystander. He was just 28 years old.\par \par \uc2 There has been an outpouring of support for Takeoff, along with the anger at gun violence in America that has cut down a young talent.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Video obtained by TMZ appears to show the moments leading up to the shooting that killed Takeoff. In it, you can see fellow Migos` member, Quavo who was Takeoff`s uncle in a verbal dispute.\par \par \uc2 Takeoff is seen standing on the outskirts before shots ring out.\par \par \uc2 (GUNSHOTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Moments later, Takeoff his on the ground. Police say he died on the scene, outside a third flood bowling alley in downtown Houston.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We have no reason to believe that he was involved in anything criminal at the time, just as people described him, a very peaceful, loving, great entertainer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Migos performance.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: At just 28 years old, Takeoff was part of one of hip-hop`s juggernaut, Migos.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Migos performance.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The group has claimed two number one albums on the Billboard 200.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In 2018, they appeared on "The Late, Late Show" with James Corden to showcase their rap skills and their sense of fashion.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Following Takeoff`s death, tributes poured in from across the hip hop community.\par \par \uc2 Chance the Rapper tweeted that he was "brokenhearted and confused," calling Takeoff a "one of a kind friend."\par \par \uc2 In an \uc2 Instagram\uc2  post, Drake reminisced about seeing the world with Takeoff and "bringing light to every city we touch."\par \par \uc2 And the artist known as Desiigner announced he was done with rap, following Takeoff`s death.\par \par \uc2 Over the years, there have been several high profile rappers who were shot and killed, including the Notorious B.I.G. and Tupac in the 90s, Nipsey Hussle in 2019, Pop Smoke in 2020, and PnB Rock in September of this year.\par \par \uc2 But experts say those isolated incidents are part of a much bigger issue.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE RICHARDSON (\uc2 Ohio State University\uc2  Professor of Literacy Studies): It`s not just a Black thing, it is not just a hip-hop thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Elaine Richardson is a Professor of Literacy Studies at \uc2 Ohio State University\uc2  with a focus on hip hop in African-American culture.\par \par \uc2 This is a larger problem that we all need to be trying to figure out how to stop the killing.\par \par \uc2 FAT JOE (American rapper): I mean, it`s a terrible time not just for rappers in America. Like, it is so violent in America right now. It is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is scary.\par \par \uc2 FAT JOE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We spoke to hip hop elder, statesman, Fat Joe last month before Takeoff`s death.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Migos performance.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like Fat Joe, some of Migos` music falls into the gangster rap genre, which can have violent themes. But as Fat Joe told us, not all of the stories artists tell indicate who they are privately.\par \par \uc2 FAT JOE: Fat Joe was primarily a gangster rapper his whole career. I`ve been with my wife for 29 years. I`ve put my kids through college. My kids go to private school.\par \par \uc2 You met Fat Joe off the record. He is the nicest guy in the world. But image and music, tough guy. This is creativity. This is music. This is art.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, the hip-hop community, now, the music-at-large community is mourning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because it is another senseless death.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And there is outrage.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Senseless is a word.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There is outrage, because you know, there is gun violence in America. But listen, I have to be honest here. As a Black community, we find outrage when there`s violence against our race against other people, and I`m not saying these two things have to do with each other. But we have to have the same type of outrage when there`s Black on Black violence.\par \par \uc2 And you know, I just get tired of the thoughts and prayers. There are too many of them, whether it`s a mass shooting or something like this, where a young life is taken from us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I`ve met Takeoff actually a couple of times because I know the Migos group.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He was really one of the nicest guys and you know, people get lumped up and make assumptions that are often incorrect, but this is another senseless example.\par \par \uc2 They`re playing a game, the game gets out of hand, and people pull out a gun and start shooting. It makes -- I think Chance said it best "brokenhearted and confused" because this is so unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 And it does put another stain on rap music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: . and rap artists.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s -- yes, and that`s not right either.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, all right, some rappers recently saying being a rapper is one of the most dangerous jobs in America, which is ridiculous.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you`ll be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time for "What to Watch."\par \par \uc2 A little fun with some sad news off the top.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, we`ve got some sad news. This is really heartbreaking when I saw this pop yesterday, I knew I wanted to share it with everybody.\par \par \uc2 We are remembering Julie Powell, the creator of a popular blog that inspired the movie "Julie and Julia." Powell`s husband says she died last week after a cardiac arrest. She was just 49 years old.\par \par \uc2 In 2002, you will remember Powell who was an untrained cook famously blogged about her attempt to spend the year making every recipe in Julia Child`s "Mastering the Art of French Cooking."\par \par \uc2 Well, her blog was turned into a best-selling book and a movie. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Julie and Julia.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That of course is Amy Adams as Powell, cooking in her tiny kitchen. In 2012, Powell joined our table and explained how her cooking led to wisdom outside of the kitchen, too, take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JULIE POWELL (August 11, 2012): There is a great moment that I think is - - that she writes in the middle of the page that I think is sort of a great life lesson which is, it will look like a total mess, you will not know how you`re ever going to come out the other side. But just keep doing and all will come out as it should and I think that`s a good life lesson.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you know, her story was so fascinating. In 2002, she was working as an administrator. She was an aspiring writer and she was knocking her head up against things.\par \par \uc2 She knew she had her art inside of her, didn`t know how to get it out. She started blogging and then thousands and thousands of people waited on every week on her every word, every week for her blog and you know, the rest is history.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re a movie and Amy Adams is playing you.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Her age was what so shocking to me, that at 49, she lost her life.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Forty-nine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Forty-nine years old.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Charming book, a reminder for women in particular cardiac arrest is real, it`s a risk.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You should checked out, talk to your doctor.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She will be missed. She will be missed.\par \par \uc2 All right now to a moment of history in New York theater district. This happened yesterday. The Brooks Atkinson Theatre was renamed the Lena Horne Theater.\par \par \uc2 Horne is the first Black woman to have a Broadway theater named in honor. You know, Horne who died in 2010 was one of the few Black movie stars during the 1940s.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Lena Thorne performance.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She got her start at the legendary Cotton Club in Harlem when she was only 16 years old. She started movies and on Broadway, and importantly, was very active in the Civil Rights Movement.\par \par \uc2 I asked Horne`s granddaughter, actress and producer, Jenny Lumet, what does this historic moment means.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JENNY LUMET (Actress and producer): This is a celebration of the contributions of Black women to theater history, I think.\par \par \uc2 I think it is a celebration of all people and theater history. But Black women, as we know, tend to get the short end of the stick and this, I feel like, it`s not about Grandma. I say, I feel like it`s about a "thank you."\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Grandma was something else.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I got to meet her once.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I did. When Oprah had her Legends Ball, Lena Horne couldn`t come. So, Oprah wanted to deliver the gift to her personally because she got all the legends diamond earrings, that`s what Oprah does, and went to take it to her in person. I got to come.\par \par \uc2 So we`re sitting in the bedroom with Lena Horne, talking to her, we walked out of there like, "Oh, my God, I can`t believe."\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s Lena Horne.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s Lena Horne.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It was. I`ll never forget it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She`s a legend. When I was stuck at home and COVID, I watched "Stormy Weather." I encourage everybody to go out and watch this movie, "Stormy Weather." It`s one of the most incredible movies ever and she is the main star, but of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And she is so beautiful.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Of course with Cab Callaway and Bojangles.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad, we`re now two days after Halloween, but we`re talking Halloween costumes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are talking Halloween costumes. Supermodel, Heidi Klum, she never disappoints when it comes to her creative Halloween costumes.\par \par \uc2 This year, she was thinking outside of the box, the tackle box. Let me show you what I mean.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Villainous.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That`s Klum dressed up as a worm alongside her husband, who was dressed as a fisherman. She posted this video and several pictures of her just warming around during her annual Heidiween Party.\par \par \uc2 Here she was interviewed, laying on the red carpet. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: What is it like you`ve done so many spectacular costumes, how did this one come about?\par \par \uc2 HEIDI KLUM (Model): I don`t know. I just wanted it to be something random I wanted to think outside of the box, and you know I thought, a rain worm would be kind of fun.\par \par \uc2 We all know worms, but I don`t think I`ve ever seen anyone being a rain worm before.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, no, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There is a reason for that. She`s very clever.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is of course, the Queen of Halloween.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very clever. Very creative, but it`s so crazy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. It`s creepy and this is her thing. She does this every year.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I hope, one year, I can get to go to this party.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never disappoint.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Really awesome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: A personal look at a misunderstood disorder. David Begnaud explores his own diagnosis with Tourette syndrome and talks about some treatments he says can help.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil. The hit show "The Good Fight" now in its final season on Paramount+, star, Christine Baranski is here and don`t worry, folks, no spoilers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. C is for cookie, but it`s also for Christina. Milk Bar`s Christina Tosi is here with her new cookbook devoted entirely to that delicious treat.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She says it`s her safe place. We want to go there.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Tensions increase on the Korean Peninsula after the North fires a record number of missiles in a single day.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: My diplomatic sources say that there is no reason to think tension on the Korean Peninsula is going to diminish anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : Democracy is on the ballot this year.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is all hands on deck for Democrats and Republicans in key Midterm races.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER (R) (Georgia Senate candidate): They`re coming after me with everything right now.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): Character matters.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : This campaign is all about anyone that has ever been knocked down, that has had to get back up.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ: Are you happy with where America is headed?\par \par \uc2 CROWD: No.\par \par \uc2 JEN KIGGANS: I think it shows that she`s out of touch with this district.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A close House race in Virginia hinges on how voters feel about the investigation of the January 6th attack.\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): The truth about it is, is that the work that the Committee is doing is so important.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today, the Fed considers raising interest rates again to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The housing market is a leading indicator of whether the nation is heading into a recession, but here is the thing, it could also contribute to one.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Late, Late Show.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the entertainment world is paying tribute to Migos rapper, Takeoff after he was gunned down in Houston.\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN: They called in Takeoff because he`d record his verses off of just a single take, and he was so proud of what Migos had achieved and for what they achieved.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: James Corden always says it`s such a great time in the car with whoever is with him, but last night, he was talking about the three of them being in the car and they`re all singing "Sweet Caroline." Neil Diamond, that was a very funny video.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s so hard to believe. People are still processing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The loss today.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour though with a look at a nervous system disorder that is personal to us, because it affects one of our own.\par \par \uc2 Tourette Syndrome causes sudden repetitive movements known as tics that are very hard to control sometimes. An estimated 350,000 to 450,000 kids and adults in this country are living with Tourette`s, but many of the cases go undiagnosed.\par \par \uc2 You might be surprised but some of the people who live and we have to say thrive with it in their lives. For instance, actor Dan Ackroyd, and singer Billie Eilish have both talked about growing up with Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 And our own lead national correspondent, David Begnaud is raising awareness by sharing his own experiences and insights in this report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN (Milaca, Minnesota Police officer): It is kind of hard to explain, but --\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: In this New York City exam room, the patient, Craig Elgin is here because he has Tourette Syndrome.\par \par \uc2 Watch what happens when he talks.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: Other than -- having to tell people I have Tourette Syndrome.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Okay.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: His condition is evident by those uncontrollable interruptions called tics.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: I`ve got to toe tics. I`ve got legs stretching tic, hand stretching, wrists, arm stretch. I got the huff, the sniff, the laughing tic, hard blinking, throat clearing, face grimace and the new ones come and go.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Like if you tried right now to stop what you`re doing, could you?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: I really have to focus.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Deuce Bigelow: Male Gigolo.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Tourette syndrome has long been an easy target, disrespected in pop culture. That just leads to misconceptions, says Joohi Jimenez-Shahed, the neurologist who is treating Greg Elgin at Mount Sinai Health System.\par \par \uc2 What are some of the most common misconceptions about Tourette`s?\par \par \uc2 DR. JOOHI JIMENEZ-SHAHED (Mount Sinai Health System neurologist): You know things like why can`t you hold it down? So, it must be something that you`re doing on purpose. The misperception that it`s all about swearing and you know, doing inappropriate behaviors, which may affect some individuals with tics and Tourette`s but not everybody.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Even with persistent symptoms, Craig Elgin at 37 years old functions pretty normally. He is a police officer with the Milaca Minnesota Police Department.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: It doesn`t inhibit anything that I do. I mean there are times where you know like everyone you get a little self-conscious about it and you`re like, "No, I shouldn`t do that just because of my tics."\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It looks tiring.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: You get used to it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Really?\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: Yes, like online that`s like one of the top three questions I get asked the most.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Off duty and online, Elgin has brought together more than 150,000 people on social media. That`s where he`s known as Tourette`s cop. That`s how I found him.\par \par \uc2 I was diagnosed with Tourette`s at the age of six.\par \par \uc2 I feel like we know each other I know.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: I know. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I`ll give you a hug.\par \par \uc2 I introduced Craig to Kelsey Christiansen who also reports on television. She, like me has a mild case of Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 Watching her on the air, you really wouldn`t notice anything.\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CHRISTIANSEN (KSTP 5 Eyewitness News reporter): . are still awaiting their sentencing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Off camera though, that continuous eye blinking you see is the giveaway.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I have nose tics, a tongue tic, neck tics, stress often induces them. So does just talking about Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 People are like "What are your secrets?" I tell everybody my secrets, because what I say is, if you try my secrets, you ain`t going to do me like I do me.\par \par \uc2 Meeting other people who have Tourette syndrome is invaluable to better understanding this complex neurological disorder.\par \par \uc2 CRAIG ELGIN: Like I kind of use social media as like, my therapy. Like I`m able to talk about it. I`m able to open up about it like yes, you know, it`s completely okay to have Tourette syndrome. You can still be normal and make noises.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What causes Tourette`s?\par \par \uc2 DR. JOOHI JIMENEZ-SHAHED: Hard question to answer. We know that there is probably a strong genetic component. There`s some new research coming out that relates to environmental exposures. So, things maybe that happened with the individual`s mother perhaps during pregnancy, other psychosocial features that might influence the manifestation. So it`s a very complex disorder in that sense.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: There are extreme cases and this is how that can look.\par \par \uc2 Alex Brown was 15 years old when doctors at Baylor University Medical Center in Texas took that video.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN (Tourette syndrome patient): I had to take a medical leave from school.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You couldn`t even go to school.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: For about five or six years, we didn`t do anything. We didn`t go out to eat. Didn`t go to church. We were homebound. We were completely shut off.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: At the height of it all, what were your tics?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: Anything you can think of. I was opening car doors. When someone would cook at my house, I would walk by and just touch the hot stove. I would scream really, really loud to the point to where now I can`t sing because my vocal cords have been stretched so much.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He lived with his advanced condition called coprolalia for more than seven years Before Dr. Joohi Jimenez-Shahed recommended that he have stimulators implanted deep into his brain.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: It`s right on this side, right in the middle of my head.\par \par \uc2 DR. JOOHI JIMENEZ-SHAHED : So deep brain stimulation is kind of like a pacemaker. So you have a battery, it is placed in the chest, but rather than the battery being connected to wires that go to your heart, the wires actually go into the deep brain structures that are involved in that circuitry that produce the tics.\par \par \uc2 And for some individuals who have these really kind of otherwise difficult to control tics, this may be an effective way to get some relief.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: The procedure, which is often used in Parkinson`s patients is increasingly becoming successful in controlling severe Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: I woke up from the surgery and it had already started working. They had not even turned the stimulator on and I could already tell a difference and that was so liberating. It was magical.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What could you tell?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: It was very soothing. It felt good not having the urge to do tics.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Five years after he received the implants, doctors at \uc2 Baylor\uc2  made this recording.\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: Graduate from high school. I am at \uc2 Abilene Christian University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What is now possible because of this?\par \par \uc2 ALEX BROWN: I passed the CPA exam, I`m working a job. I can do whatever I want, that any other person can do.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But for many others, like Kelsey Christiansen, just getting a diagnosis and finding the right corrective treatment for them can be difficult.\par \par \uc2 What does the diagnosis do for you?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CHRISTIANSEN: It`s clarifying. It`s comforting, because I know why I`m doing what I`m doing.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: What led you to go in and see the doctor?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CHRISTIANSEN: You know, not to sound cheesy, but your story. You`re the only other reporter/journalist on air that I know that has Tourette`s.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: I don`t think it`s a big deal. Do you?\par \par \uc2 KELSEY CHRISTIANSEN: No, I don`t at all.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Road Within.")\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And the media has helped, too, create in conversations, raising awareness about Tourette`s, like in this moment between \uc2 David Letterman\uc2  and musician, Billie Eilish where she is ticking.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LETTERMAN\uc2  (Talk show host): Then the awards -- what`s going on? A fly?\par \par \uc2 BILLIE EILISH (Singer): No, I`m ticking.\par \par \uc2 DAVID LETTERMAN\uc2 : Really?\par \par \uc2 BILLIE EILISH: Yes. If you film me for long enough, you`re going to see lots of tics.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: But for people like Craig Elgin, even without the stigma, there is still suffering.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the day after we interviewed and while our team was still with him, he left the interview and drove to an emergency room. In the days before we met him, he was sleeping less than an hour a night. It had become too much to bear and so we watched as he walked himself into a hospital asking for help.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How is he?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How is he? Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: He is better. So here`s what happened. I introduced him to Dr. Shahed here in New York City. She saw him, she put him on medication. He hadn`t been on meds before. The meds helped, they reduced the tics. But the meds for Tourette`s often have bad side effects. So they cause really bad nightmares.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And restlessness. But they dialed back the medication. He`s still on it. But you know what he said? He said, "David, I want that deep brain stimulation device," and so he is coming back to New York in two weeks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: And he and Dr. Shahed are going to talk about that as a real option for him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But when you have it that way, and you see the man you were talking to and you see the difference that it makes, as scary as that deep brain stimulation looks and sounds, you think if that`s going to help me, I want to do it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: It was life changing for him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I can see that. I think most people when they think of Tourette`s, they think like the clip we showed with the movie where you just scream and say something wildly inappropriate.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: We`re often the butt of a joke.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is so much more than -- yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You know, we`re often just the butt of a joke. And I thought, did you guys know I had Tourette`s? Did you see me ticking?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve talked about it early on. We chatted about it.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: You saw me?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I knew because you told. If you hadn`t told me -- and now when I see in the piece, you do the thing with your neck, I`ve seen you do that. But I didn`t think it was Tourette`s. I just thought you were stretching your neck.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then also, you know, it seems like there`s -- when you have it sometimes just before you`ll do a tag or just before you do a tease, I`ll see it and then also see your ability to turn it off.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Control it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then this profound serenity comes over you, which is what makes you connect with viewers. There`s an ability for you to be so in the moment that I think is somehow a counter strength that comes out of this condition that I`m jealous of to be honest with you. It`s an amazing thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, but not everyone with Tourette`s, to your point has that type of control.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: No. Look if you ask somebody to control their tics, it`s like me saying, "Hey, don`t sneeze. Hold it." Right? or "Don`t blink." At some point, you just erupt.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you don`t know when you`re going to tic.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you can go days and not have a tic, right?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Listening to that piece, listening to that was making me start to tic. You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Stress induces it, but when the red lights sometimes comes on, on the camera, I do stop. But in the last couple of years, I`ve gotten more tics, you know, and like my eyes dart all the time. It can be really humiliating.\par \par \uc2 But the whole point of this story was I wanted to create a conversation because I want little boys and girls watching to know that if David Begnaud be where he is as the national correspondent of CBS News with Tourette`s, you can do whatever the heck you want.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Mission accomplished on that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mission accomplished. Thank you, David.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BEGNAUD: Thank you all listening.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Great story. Great piece. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right ahead, the author of a new book will tell us how companies can break down barriers in the workplace and improve diversity and inclusion.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:12, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up after more than 10 years, actor, Christine Baranski is stepping away from the iconic character, Diane Lockhart. She`ll tell us about the final season of "The Good Fight" and the role she would like to play now.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We hear a lot these days about the push for diversity and inclusion in the workplace. But what exactly does that look like?\par \par \uc2 Well, there`s new research showing that more diverse, more equitable, and inclusive leadership actually helps organizations make better decisions, in fact, nearly 90 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 And now, there`s a new roadmap to achieving that. Author and executive coach, Patrice Gordon is just out with a new book. It`s called "Reverse Mentoring: Removing Barriers and Building Belonging in the Workplace." She says that everybody benefits when leaders and members of underrepresented groups come together, tell stories, share information, and Patrice joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Patrice, good morning.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON ("Reverse Mentoring" author): Good morning, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much for being here. The title, "Reverse Mentoring." I think it`s almost self-explanatory, but not quite.\par \par \uc2 So tell us what reverse mentoring is.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: So, reverse mentoring actually started with Jack Welch in \uc2 GE Capital\uc2  in the 90s. He recognized that the leaders in the organization was so far removed from what was actually happening with the Internet. So the younger individuals and generations that came in, he got them to mentor the senior leaders so they had a really good understanding of what is this thing coming and how do we use it and how do we make the most of it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So rather than older workers pairing off with younger, newer employees, it`s the opposite.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely opposite. And what I have done in my research, and you know, since you know the social change that`s happened, it`s really use the difference that we have within organizations to make sure that leaders understand what it is like to be an underrepresented individual.\par \par \uc2 So in the work that I`ve done and the research I`ve done, I`ve extended that age, to gender, to ethnicity to neurodiversity, to veteran status, for example. So those underrepresented individuals within the organization are really trying to make sure they have a voice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you speak of what you know, because you did this. Your boss -- you ended up mentoring your boss.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: I did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who is this harder for? Is it harder for the mentor or the mentee?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: It depends. And I was quite a confident mentor. So, I was quite confident in my story. But I definitely think through my research and speaking to lots of leaders, the mentees definitely have a hard time, right?\par \par \uc2 As a senior leader, you are expected to have all of the answers, you`re expected to know the direction of the business, you`re not necessarily expected to be vulnerable and necessarily courageous in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 And being a reverse mentor means that you need to put yourself in that position of a novice and really listen to the underrepresented.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So those in position of leadership, it`s hard for them to understand how to be vulnerable.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because they are told not to and they feel like it`s a sign of weakness in front of their employees. What advice do you give mentors?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Mentors or mentees --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mentees.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: The mentee, the senior leader --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The older ones, okay.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: The older ones, the senior ones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Really understanding why you want to get involved. So you have power, how do you want to use it within the organization? Are you willing to share your power? And if you are, you need to put yourself in that room and put yourself in that vulnerable position to be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you have to have buy in from the top. You know, after the death of George Floyd, many companies said, listen, we need to change, we need to be more diverse, we need to be more inclusive, and that seems to have petered off a bit.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You say there is a difference between diverse -- don`t say diversity, you say inclusion.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely. Absolutely. So Verna Maya, she says, you know, diversity is being invited to the party, but inclusion is being asked to dance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: I`m really in organizations, what we`re trying to do is, yes, we can bring as many diverse people in as possible, but actually, are they going to enjoy working in the environment? Are you going to get value from them because they feel included? And that`s the key that I`m trying to get to here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you`ve got to you got to make sure it`s spread throughout the company. It doesn`t matter if the leader says this is what we want to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: If it is not passed on to middle management. You can have these training sessions and coaching sessions. What difference do they really make if there`s not buy in from everybody?\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Absolutely. One of the key things I`m really invested in encouraging companies to do is making sure that we set some targets. So, as we have financial targets or sustainability targets, you know, everyone`s really clear that we can get to net zero by 2050. But what about diversity, equity and inclusion targets?\par \par \uc2 I think we need to get braver, we need to kind of put a stake in the ground, and we need to hold everyone to account and the way that we do that is through hitting them in the pocket, making sure that their financial package is linked to diversity, equity and inclusion goals.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And another way we do that is getting the book.\par \par \uc2 PATRICE GORDON: Yes, get the book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is getting the book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Patrice Gordon, thank you so much. "Reverse Mentoring."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I could listen to you all day. I love your accent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is on sale now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 It`s time to bring you some of the stories that we like to call around her "Talk of the Table" -- why? Because this is a table and we be talking and Tony is in pole position.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a table, we be talking. I`m Tony.\par \par \uc2 I don`t want to talk about me, myself, and I. I want to talk about Ross Chastain who is a NASCAR driver who did something really remarkable at the Xfinity 500.\par \par \uc2 Now to set it up and for Gayle King to understand in particular. We`ve got to show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: First, what typical turns look like in NASCAR.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You slow down on the turn, because if you don`t slow down, you will spin out.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually know Formula One. Excuse you, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Okay, but this is NASCAR, a little different. I don`t know anything about anything.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually know Formula One.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, I`m sorry. You think the World Series though has a halftime.\par \par \uc2 I`m just --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is not true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am just covering my bases, okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was money talk.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. All right. So anyways --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is not true, Nate. Go ahead, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, we got a slow turn. That`s what they usually do. Ross Chastain needed to make up multiple spots to qualify for the championship series. So coming around the final turn at the Xfinity 500, he opted not to slow down and instead did this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Take a look at what he did.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I have never seen anything.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He dropped it in the fifth gear, accelerated through the turn and hugged the wall. The best part of this is his explanation for why he did it. Do we have the sound?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROSS CHASTAIN (Motorsports racing driver): I played a lot of NASCAR 2005 on the GameCube with Chad growing up, and you could get away with it. And I never knew if it would actually work and I mean, I`ve done -- I did that when I was eight years old.\par \par \uc2 I just made the choice, I grabbed fifth gear down the back and full committed. Once I got against the wall, I basically let go of the wheel and just hoped I didn`t catch the turn for an access gate or something crazy, but I was willing to do it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What did he say in the beginning? What did he --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He pulled a move in a real NASCAR race that he had pulled on a Nintendo GameCube in 2005. He had done a video game move in real life, and it swung him -- and set a track record and qualified for the Championship Series.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And that`s not common.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No one had ever done that before, that moment, and when he was all casually like, I hope it didn`t catch a turn for a gate. Oh yes, I hope I didn`t die in a ball of fire, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was risky, but it worked.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I wonder if other drivers will do that now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I hope not. That looks very dangerous, Tony.\par \par \uc2 My "Talk of the Table" is -- drumroll please. Oprah`s Favorite Things List, just in, just in time for your holiday shopping. It is one of the favorite things that we do over at Oprah Daily.\par \par \uc2 So I was with her when she unveiled some of this year`s favorites. It is a whole process.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Family run, husband-wife duo`s.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is nice. I want to get one of these myself.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Local makers.\par \par \uc2 Truff. Truff. Truff. Truff.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The pineapple is good.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: Brands started by friends and work.\par \par \uc2 Great for first apartments. Love, love, love.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Oh, my God.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: When you shop from these folks, you`re investing in their dream.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Investing in a dream. That`s what Oprah likes to do. We`ve got an exclusive clip.\par \par \uc2 You`re seeing first here on CBS MORNINGS when she surprised one of the small business owners who made the list for the first time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: That`s why I`m choosing it as a favorite thing.\par \par \uc2 JAINABA JENG (Kitchens of Africa founder): Oh, my God. I can`t even believe this. I am going to tell my family. They`re going to go crazy. You`ve been teaching me forever.\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY: I`ve been teaching you forever and now I`m eating your sauces. That`s a full circle moment.\par \par \uc2 JAINABA JENG: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is Jainaba Jeng. She`s the founder of Kitchens of Africa, a company that introduces African taste to your kitchen. It`s based by the way in Raleigh, North Carolina and its roots extend all the wake back to Gambia, a tiny country in the Western part of Africa, where Jainaba Jeng was born and raised.\par \par \uc2 But it`s fun because Adam Glassman who is a creative director and Oprah and I were all on a Zoom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So we`re just talking to them about "What`s special about your thing? Why do you think you should be on?" And then Oprah pops up on the Zoom. So, you saw the moment where Oprah just popped up on the Zoom and people just lose it every single time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Every time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that never gets old.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man. All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Favorite Things List" is out, shopping for everyone on your list.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Appreciate it. Thank you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re welcome.\par \par \uc2 All right, so my "Talk of the Table" is a new list of the best places to retire in the US. Move over Florida. Sorry, Tony. Pennsylvania is number one in this year`s US News and World Report Survey. Lancaster to be exact, just 80 miles west of Philadelphia ranked as the most desirable city for retirees.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania took five of the top 10 spots. Florida took four though you know still a decent place to go. The survey looked at six major factors -- housing affordability, happiness, healthcare, quality, retiree taxes, desirability, and job market rate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t see you shoveling the driveway on that list, but that`s what you`re doing in Pennsylvania in the winter.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony, don`t be a hater, okay?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was surprised though.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was surprised.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was surprised about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is something fishy about that. I love Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There will be an investigation.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, what have you got, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We were very excited for our next guest. What have we got, Nate? Christine Baranski, a legend in the house today.\par \par \uc2 For more than a decade, this Emmy, Tony Award winner has graced your TV screens as Attorney Diane Lockhart, first on the hit CBS series "The Good Wife," and then on its spin off. "The Good Fight." That`s on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 Now, she is saying goodbye to this beloved character in the show`s final season. Here`s a taste.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Good Fight.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, she still got power. Christine Baranski joins us in the studio.\par \par \uc2 We felt that the minute you walked in the room, power coming in, power coming.\par \par \uc2 So Christine, I don`t know if we`re ready. I don`t know if we`re ready to let you go. Are you ready? This is it?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI ("The Good Fight" actor): No, don`t think I am. I`ve been -- I`ve played this character for 13 years.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: And not only the character, but the people with whom I work, the whole atmosphere. It took me through so many times of my life, marriage and losing my husband and getting three grandchildren and it`s just part of the fabric of my life and the character, too, you know, articulate, intelligent, passionate, liberal feminist. I liked walking in her shoes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there is no -- and you walk in her shoes very well. She wears very nice shoes and very nice dresses. But you know, there is no other show like this on the air on any platform, because you guys tell stories in real time.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Sometimes shockingly, so.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so real.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: And it wasn`t even intended that way, but we were shooting the pilot for "The Good Fight," six years ago and it was election night and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  became, you know, was claimed the winner. And suddenly the show took a pivot and became about what it is like -- what it has been like for --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you use the real names. That`s the thing. Sometimes shows will take real headlines, but they don`t use the real names.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You all used real names and tell the people.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: No, we are pretty brave and also, the show is -- you know, address topics that are utterly prevalent. In fact, the final season takes us up practically to Election Night, which is uncanny. Our season finale will just abut that Election Night with all of the anxiety and, you know, the precipitous moment that that we`re living through the show takes us through that.\par \par \uc2 Yes, I`m very proud of the show for that reason, and people will look back and think, wow, that captured this crazy dystopian time in our country.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I`m so proud and happy for you. Last time, you were here. You said I`m working harder than I`ve ever been. I look better than I`ve ever been.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Did I say that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: That`s a little arrogant.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, it wasn`t arrogant. You didn`t say it in an arrogant way. You didn`t. You didn`t.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Well, I feel better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You did not say in an arrogant way. And you said I`m making more money than I ever have.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. I am glad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And now you`re 70 years old and you claim that very proudly. Do you still feel all of those things?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I do. You know, I just finished "The Gilded Age" last Thursday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People love that show by the way.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: And so I was number one on two call sheets and doing you know, finishing one show and a day later, just going right into the next show. And you know what it`s like, getting up at 4:30 in the morning to, you know, get in the van and, and spend 12 to 14-hour day, and I thought, "Damn, I`m still doing this." And I am still having fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And dam good doing it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I would hope that I`m an inspiration because I think life can get better and better especially for women. We raise our children, we hold things together. Why do we think suddenly that it is ending, just when in fact so many aspects of your life can open up?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: You know, travel, learning. I took several courses at Oxford in a summer program. I`ve got grandchildren, I`ve got family, I`ve got friends.\par \par \uc2 Life is you know, where`s the wood -- life is good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is good. So, what is next for you? Speaking of just getting started.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I am going on a little vacation. I`m going --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well deserved.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: I am going -- tomorrow, I am going on a vacation, but --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Broadway?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Oh, I don`t -- you know, I think if we are lucky, "Gilded Age" will come back for a third season because it`s so popular and it`s just a fantastic show and that would take me into yet another cycle, and.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: You know that that`s eight to 10 episodes that takes at least six to nine months.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: But, you know that`s life in a corset and a wig, but you know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: People say it has the best dialogue and that you have the best lines in the show.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: It is up in my contract, the best creators of the lines, otherwise, they don`t show up.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The stuff that you get to say.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But are there roles that you are dying to play that are out there that you would like?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: You know, I have Juilliard training and we trained to do classical plays, and I love language. And I would love honestly, to do Shakespeare or to do a play like Tom Stoppard that really demands, you know, the skills that I attained in drama school. And honestly, "The Gilded Age" makes a lot of use of that because of the formality of the dialogue and the setting, that old New York and she`s such an old New York character.\par \par \uc2 So yes, I would love to just, you know, have well-written monologues and just get to sink my teeth into something with the language.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, Christine, you know people and I think if there is anything you want to do, your life really is yours to design. They don`t call you a legend for nothing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When they come up with a name for whatever age we`re living through, you can be in that show. That would be terrific.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Thank you so much. It`s such a pleasure to be here.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. Congratulations. Congrats. And your grandmother name is what?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Mini.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Mini. I like it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Mini.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Congratulations to you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s yours, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, I`m working on it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the child is how old? Seven now?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s either going to be Gaya or Gan-Gan. Whatever.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, we`ve got figure this out. The time is ticking.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I know. I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is almost in high school.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Luca is writing full sentences.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s going to be Mini. Thank you, Christine.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE BARANSKI: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "The Good Fight" is now streaming now on Paramount+. Oh, Nate got jokes. New episodes out on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, coming up next, we`re going to talk to a Chef and Milk Bar owner, Christina Tosi about her new book all about cookies. It is "All About Cookies." She`ll tell us how she finds inspiration for her new recipes. But first, look at those cookies, time for a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Our next guest, Dessert Empire is getting a little sweeter. Award-winning chef Christina Tosi is the owner and founder of the famous Milk Bar. The national bakery chain is known for treats like the compost cookie and their layered birthday cakes.\par \par \uc2 Her new book is all about cookies, a Milk Bar baking book, shares more than 100 new cookie recipes.\par \par \uc2 Christina Tosi joins us now.\par \par \uc2 How are you doing, Christina? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI (Milk Bar owner and founder): Hi, Nate. Good morning. How are you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s good to have you. My mouth is salivating because we have all these treats in front of us. We`ll get to that in a second. Now, you said your love for baking all started with cookies. How so?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: Cookies were my entry into the kitchen. I was raised by these fierce matriarchs and we baked, that`s what we did in our free time. Everything from like the classic dense fudgy oatmeal cookie to a bar cookie.\par \par \uc2 Cookies were also like our way out to create community and support people and show love and make people feel something, feel seen, to speak to their soul.\par \par \uc2 Cookies is where it all became and cookies is like what Milk Bar is all about. And of course what this cookbook is all about, which is like a homecoming for me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you say cookies are your safe space. They are your secret to living life on your own terms, and a cookie is not just a cookie. So what do you mean when you say a cookie is not just a cookie? It seems like you have life lessons even when it comes to cookies.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: They are so -- I mean, dessert outright has its own life lesson, right? Dessert is a totally opt in course. It`s a way for us to speak to people`s souls. It`s a way to make people sort of feel something to indulge et cetera.\par \par \uc2 But for me, the cookie is the underdog of the pastry world. It has this humility. It is portable, it`s transportable, it`s shareable. It`s this low barriered entry. There is a cookie that exists in nearly every culture in this world. And yet a cookie in my opinion has infinite possibility to break the rules and breed creativity and really speak to someone`s tastebuds.\par \par \uc2 So for me, the cookie -- the cookie needs more love and that`s what this cookbook is all about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love the cookie, Christina. The cookie is my number one dessert. It`s not optional, by the way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Breakfast too, Tony. I see you in the morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is not even kidding.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am not lying.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel if we did pumps right now, I`m getting a little bigger every day, but I love those cookies.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is true.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And my mom used to say life is unpredictable, eat dessert first and I live by those words. So, tell us about the cookies on the table in front of us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That you dropped off. These are great.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: Okay, so first off, I brought you a slew of like the classic Milk Bar cookies dense and fudgy. You can get the better bakeries nationwide. You can get them online at MilkBarScore.com. Those are like the hallmark of Milk Bar.\par \par \uc2 But then I also wanted to bring you some of my favorites. This babe right here is the Jelly Donut Sammie. It`s a dough that`s made using fryer oil. You put your favorite jam or jelly inside and then you can decorate it like the top of your favorite donut.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`ve got to go for it.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: Do it. Go for it. There are these what I call cookies that like to party, those giant cookies. That`s what we call an XXL Cookie or Cookie Cake. You can swing by our New York City flagship and create your own cookie, but it`s a cookie that you can slice like a slice of cake that you can share.\par \par \uc2 There`s a peanut butter chocolate caramel one, which is a love letter to my favorite candy bar the Take5 candy bar. There is a chocolate one with pretzels and potato chips.\par \par \uc2 I mean cookies really are about being seen, bringing your tastebuds to life and just giving you the possibility to like shake you from sleepwalking, see life in color, and so I figured I`d send you a whole array.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we thank you. Your cookbook is beautifully done. I could lick the page. You`ve got a de leche cookie, a peachy shortcake cookie. Do you have a favorite cookie if you had to choose and then we`ll let you go?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA TOSI: I mean, that`s like asking someone to choose their favorite like grandkid on your terms, but I think it has to be the fluffernutter. It is the Sandy Cookie made with peanut butter. You swirl some marshmallow in, it`s actually on the back of the cookbook. It is this baby right here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Christina, we`ve got to get out of here. We love you. Thank you for the cookies.\par \par \uc2 "All About Cookies" on sale now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wherever you buy your books.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221102eib200001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Potentially Life-Saving Information About Breast Cancer Screening\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Charlie Dr. Celine Gounder, Tony Dokoupil, Nate  Burleson \par \uc2 1,392 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: As Breast Cancer Awareness Month comes to a close, it`s the last day, we bring you a reminder that it is never, never the wrong time to get screened. Let`s say that again. Never the wrong time to get screened -- screened, ladies.\par \par \uc2 This year, breast cancer is expecting to kill more than 43,000 women in this country, but early detection is helping more women survive the disease.\par \par \uc2 CBS News contributor and the editor-at-large at KFF`s Kaiser Health News, Dr. Celine Gounder documented her annual examine in "Mammography 101."\par \par \uc2 She hopes to demystify the process and explain again why regular screening is vital for detecting breast cancer early.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: One of the awesome things about being a 45-year-old woman is it`s three times the fun with cancer screening. You`ve got screening for breast cancer, colon cancer, and cervical cancer.\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE: My name is Danielle, I`m going to do your exam today. I`m going to please do breast here on the plate. Just some slight compression, just enough to keep your breasts from moving. We will do this in several different angles.\par \par \uc2 Take in a breath, hold your breath. So you can put the gown back on, on the right side and we`ll do the side view. And take in a breath and hold your breath. Beautiful. Okay. Great, looks good.\par \par \uc2 DR. GADALIN: I`m Dr. Gadalin (sp?). Come on in.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Nice to meet you.\par \par \uc2 DR. GADALIN: The right and the left and we get an image and two views for each fast so that we really can look at the full volume of the tissue. So these are your breasts, your mammogram more zoomed in and enlarged. And be very carefully look at really every inch every area of the breast looking for anything that really doesn`t belong there. And so far, it looks fantastic.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So Celine Gounder joins us here at the table. I thought you`re going to have a tag to that piece, but let`s just get right into it.\par \par \uc2 You know, do you have that moment that I do when you`re sitting there waiting? It`s the waiting for them to walk in the room and I`m trying to read their expression before they say, either yay or nay?\par \par \uc2 Did you have that moment yourself as a doctor when you were waiting to get the news? We`re glad no abnormalities were found by the way. Very glad. But do you have that moment to or no, not really?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: I didn`t. I didn`t. I had done a soft breast exam before going in to the appointment and was pretty sure I was, you know, low risk and wasn`t too worried. I don`t have a family history. But you know, you never know.\par \par \uc2 Many women do not have a family history, something like 85 percent of women do not have a family history when they are diagnosed with breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But there`s still so many women, despite all the statistics that say I`m not going to get it because they`re afraid of the process. Or I`ve heard somebody say I just don`t even want to know, which is not good thinking in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, look, a mammogram does not give you breast cancer, it`s really important to know. A lot of times people will say to me, oh, that test gave me such and such disease. And no, it helps you find out that you have it, it helps you get care more quickly if you have it. And it`s really not a painful process.\par \par \uc2 It`s sort of awkward, and you have your breasts compressed between two plates, but it`s not painful and it doesn`t --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is uncomfortable. I think it depends on the technician. And I swear if men had to have their penis mashed the way we do with our breasts, they`d find another way to do it.\par \par \uc2 But right now, this is all we have.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s exactly what I was thinking actually.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is all that we have. It can be a little uncomfortable depending on who is giving you giving it to you, but that is not enough not to go and get it.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Right. It`s not painful. It doesn`t pinch. It`s just sort of awkward.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So when should women get a mammogram?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It depends on the organization. Their recommendations do vary. Some organizations will say every year, others every two years. Across the board, definitely you want to start at age 50. And then if you have risk factors, so for example, family history, obesity, if you drink heavily, if you smoke, those are risk factors, then you may want to start earlier as early as age 40.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And some women get a sonogram in addition to a mammogram, what`s the difference?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Sonograms are used to tease out, have a better look at abnormalities they might see on the mammogram. It`s more likely that you need that in somebody who has dense breasts. Younger women do tend to have denser breasts and there is sort of a difference in philosophy here.\par \par \uc2 Some doctors will say, let`s go ahead and get the mammogram and ultrasound in the same visit. So you don`t have to come back. That`s what my doctor does. Others will say, well, does it really make a difference?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I do.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: You know, does it really save lives? Maybe we should wait and see if we really need that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I want to get every test possible and that`s exactly what I do. It`s troubling to me, though that Black women are 41 percent more likely to be diagnosed with breast cancer than White women. Why is that? And die from breast cancer?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Yes, they`re more likely to die from breast cancer than White women and this is because there are disparities at every step of the process. So they`re less likely to get their mammogram on time, they`re less likely to get the newer 3D mammograms than a 2D mammogram.\par \par \uc2 So a 2D mammogram, you`re basically looking at all the cross sections of the breast flattened into one image. So 3D allows you to have a better look. Then once they`re diagnosed, they start treatment later and they`re less likely to get standard of care, you know, the recommended treatment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is it because they don`t pursue it or the doctors don`t encourage it? There`s a difference.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Some of both.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A little bit of both.\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Some of both, yes, there there`s certainly barriers to getting the mammogram.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Celine Gounder, Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Vital information, life-saving information.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you`re okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s all clear. That`s best news of all.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On this Halloween day, we hope you have a safe and healthy Halloween that you and your kids have a good time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Eat some candy corn. It`s good.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will see you -- it is good. We`ll see you tomorrow. Take it easy. He hates candy corn.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221101eiav000b5\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For October 30, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed O`Keefe, Scott MacFarlane, Anthony Salvanto, David Becker,  Margaret Brennan, Mark Strassmann \par \uc2 9,619 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Last week was an extremely troubling one in American politics. We now have just eight days left until Election Day, and the country is feeling deeply unsettled.\par \par \uc2 Our CBS News Battleground Tracker poll out this morning shows that a stunning 79 percent -- that`s nearly eight in 10 likely voters -- say that the country is out of control. That`s an ominous sign for the Democrats, who hold power in Washington. Of those 79 percent expected to vote next Tuesday, Republicans have a more than 20-point advantage.\par \par \uc2 Two issues, the health of the economy and the health of American democracy, have taken center stage in this campaign, and it is the latter subject and the political rhetoric that could help fuel violence that is under fresh scrutiny following Friday`s attack on Paul Pelosi, the 82-year-old husband of House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , at their home in San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 CBS News congressional correspondent Scott MacFarlane has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): The day after the horrific attack, House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  expressed gratitude for law enforcement and her husband`s medical team and said she and her family were heartbroken and traumatized by the life-threatening attack.\par \par \uc2 President Biden spoke with the Pelosis on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Don`t know for certain, but it looks like this was intended for Nancy. He kept asking: "Where`s Nancy? Where`s Nancy?"\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Forty-two-year-old David DePape will face a series of felony charges in the coming days, including attempted homicide, accused of striking the 82-year-old Pelosi in the head multiple times with a hammer.\par \par \uc2 DePape`s social media posts are saturated with conspiracy theories, Holocaust denial and claims of election fraud. Democrats argue Republicans must do more to denounce the violence and end the baseless claims that could fuel threats.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : That`s the problem. We can`t just say, I feel badly about the violence and we condemn it. Condemn what produces the violence. And this talk produces the violence.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The attack on Paul Pelosi is the latest in a growing wave of threats and plots against elected officials.\par \par \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  records obtained by CBS News show nearly 10,000 investigations into threats on members of Congress last year.\par \par \uc2 Georgia Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock has spent heavily on security this election cycle.\par \par \uc2 Our Nikole Killion caught up with him on the campaign trail.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-Georgia): I`m not naive about the tough moment we`re in. And there are people who are trying to stir that up for short- term political gain game. It`s dangerous stuff.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Meanwhile, an intelligence bulletin obtained by CBS News warns of a heightened threat from domestic extremist groups on and after Election Day, not just possible targeting of elected officials, but of elections administrators, political rallies and of possible attempts to intimidate voters or use claims of fraud to justify violence after the election, especially in cases where it takes additional time to certify the results.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Despite baseless claims of fraud, the 2020 election is considered the most secure and successful in U.S. history.\par \par \uc2 And voting so far in 2022 has been safe and secure. So far, more than 20 million people have voted early, and elections officials say they`re being vigilant and coordinating with police, emphasizing, everyone should feel safe coming out to vote -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Scott MacFarlane, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We go now to Jen Easterly, the director of the \uc2 Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency\uc2 , also known as \uc2 CISA\uc2 . That`s the Homeland Security agency tasked with securing America`s cybersecurity infrastructure and coordinating with states on election security.\par \par \uc2 And you`re going to be very busy. I`m glad you`re here with us today.\par \par \uc2 JEN EASTERLY (Director, U.S. Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency): Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about this bulletin, first off.\par \par \uc2 It warns, domestic violent extremists may view election-related infrastructure, personnel and voters as attractive targets. Are you aware of immediate and credible threats?\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: No.\par \par \uc2 Let me be very clear at the top. We have no information about specific or credible threats to disrupt or compromise election infrastructure. I want that to be very clear. We are putting out information, like the warnings that you mentioned, to make sure that state and local election officials have the information that they need to protect their voting systems and their election infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 That said, Margaret, it is a very complex threat environment. You have cyber threats. You have insider threats. You have rampant disinformation. And, yes, very worryingly, you have threats of harassment, intimidation and violence against election officials, polling places and voters.\par \par \uc2 Let`s be really clear. That has to stop. It is unacceptable behavior. It`s undemocratic. And we all need to work together to ensure that this is a safe and secure election.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And it is the states that administer the election. You are providing support to them.\par \par \uc2 What is the Election Day plan for security and then communication? What are we going to hear and see?\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: So, on Election Day, actually, we at \uc2 CISA\uc2  are going to be in our own operations center.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have federal government partners, private sector partners there, and then we`re going to be in direct communication with all of the state and local election officials whose job it is to run and administer elections. We`re going to be working to share information, and we`re going to be working to be able to respond to anything that happens.\par \par \uc2 But, remember, at the end of the day, the relationship between local officials and local law enforcement is incredibly important. And I was really encouraged by the opinion piece that came out yesterday with the sheriff in Massachusetts, one in Colorado, talking about the fact that ensuring election security is a nonpartisan issue, and threats to election officials have to stop.\par \par \uc2 So, that connectivity at the local level, the information sharing, the planning and exercising that`s happening is really important to ensuring security at the -- at the polling place and at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We talk a lot about rhetoric and the risk of triggering violence.\par \par \uc2 And social media is a place where false information often spreads. So, I want to ask you about what`s happening now at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . It`s now privately owned by billionaire Elon Musk. This morning, he tweeted a conspiracy theory about Nancy Pelosi`s husband.\par \par \uc2 Given how charged our environment is, are you concerned about how this platform might change and that it`s going to make your job more difficult?\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: Well, first of all, horrific attack on Mr. Pelosi, and thoughts and prayers go to their family.\par \par \uc2 That is a decision that social media companies, that \uc2 Twitter\uc2  will make. They make their own decisions, based on terms of service. I am laser- focused...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This is the owner himself tweeting this out.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: I am laser-focused on the next nine days and the time that comes after elections on doing everything we can to ensure security.\par \par \uc2 I do want to be very clear on this, though, Margaret. These elections, election officials, these are not faceless backroom bureaucrats.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: Right?\par \par \uc2 These are our relatives, our friends, our neighbors. They`re in our community. They are dedicated public servants that are working day in and day out to ensure the security of elections. And they deserve not just our support, but our admiration and respect. And they deserve to be safe. And we all need to be responsible about ensuring that`s a safe and secure environment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which is why I`m asking you about the place where these conspiracy theories spread.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 FBI\uc2  report, when we looked at it, in terms of those direct threats to election workers, highlighted Arizona, Colorado, Georgia, Michigan, Pennsylvania, Nevada, and Wisconsin as places where voter intimidation and threats to election workers have been seen.\par \par \uc2 Are these areas of greatest concern for you?\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: Concerned across the board.\par \par \uc2 We have cyber threats from nation states and cyber criminals. You have insider threats. You have physical threats, as we talked about, and then you have disinformation, so, disinformation, foreign influence that can be used to sow discord, that can undermine confidence in election integrity and that can be used to incite violence.\par \par \uc2 So, what are we doing? We`re doing a couple things. First of all, we`re putting out information about tactics and disinformation and how to build resilience against disinformation. We have a rumor vs.reality site that`s basically election literacy.\par \par \uc2 But, most importantly, we are amplifying the voices of local and state election officials. They are the trusted voices that understand how elections work. If anybody has questions about voting or what -- how it all works, you should go to their local -- local election officials.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s who they should be following on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and social media?\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: Those are the experts.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Find out who your local election official is and follow their account?\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I should point out, the National Association of Secretaries of State, NASS.org, TrustedInfo2022, a great source for information, as well as the National Association of Election Directors, NASED.org, frequently asked questions.\par \par \uc2 That`s the best -- best place to go. They know how elections happen. If you`ve seen one state in an election, you`ve seen one state. It`s actually surprisingly technical and complicated, and that`s why I welcome people asking questions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: You know, the beauty of democracy is that it`s participatory. We can all have a role. So, volunteer, be a poll worker, ask questions, the more transparency, the better.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Before I let you go, I want to ask you about the foreign threat.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, Russia probed voter registration logs. We know there are warnings about what China is doing right now. How effective has this campaign by Beijing been? And are there other state actors or non-state actors you`re concerned about?\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: Yeah, we`ve seen Russia, we`ve seen Iran, we`ve seen China use the playbook for influence operations.\par \par \uc2 That`s why it`s so important that Americans realize...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right now.\par \par \uc2 DIRECTOR JEN EASTERLY: ... that they need to build resilience against that.\par \par \uc2 If you see information that`s on the Internet, you`re not sure whether it`s true, be critical about it, ask questions, look at the source, investigate it, and don`t spread that information any more broadly and give -- basically give foreign adversaries a chance to manipulate Americans and to sow discord and to create lack of confidence in our elections.\par \par \uc2 But I want to be very, very clear. I have confidence in the elections that are going to be run because of the massive amount of work that`s been done across the federal government, at state and local election officials, with election vendors, to put multiple, multiple layers of resilience and security controls in place.\par \par \uc2 I am confident that elections will be safe and secure, and the American people should have confidence in the integrity of elections when they go to the ballot box, when they cast their vote.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, good luck to you. It will be a busy next few days and weeks.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And we turn now to the chairman of the National Republican Congressional Committee, Minnesota Congressman \uc2 Tom Emmer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Good to have you here in person.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER (R-Minnesota): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I have to ask you about what we`ve just been talking about here.\par \par \uc2 How concerned are you about the risk of political violence, and how should it be minimized?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: There`s no place for violence, period, in our society, physical violence or violence against someone`s property.\par \par \uc2 I think you`ve already covered that here this morning. The incident in San Francisco, tragic as it is, I think we need some more information about it, but we should all be feeling for Paul Pelosi and his family. Hopefully, there will be a 100 percent recovery.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And just to be abundantly clear, you denounce any kind of attack on the Pelosi family?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 There should be no attacks, period. There should be no violence in our society, again, whether it`s political or otherwise. There`s no place for it in a civilized society.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I`m glad you said that, because I want to ask you about something in our CBS News poll that shows that, even as Republicans are poised to lead this contest and -- and take control of the House, we see suspicion, specifically among Republicans, about the voting process.\par \par \uc2 A big majority of Republicans support the idea of private citizens challenging election officials as they process and report vote counts on election night. We`re seeing that on the screen now. Two-thirds of Republicans support the idea of private citizens patrolling around ballot drop boxes and polling places.\par \par \uc2 This is just Republicans. We don`t see this with Democrats. We do not see this with independents. Would you urge private citizens not to patrol polling places?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Well, you picked the words, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 I would say that it is Republicans, Democrats, independents. All American citizens are very acutely aware, as your previous guest just mentioned, about our election process. I think this is going to be a very good election, because people are awake. They`re paying attention.\par \par \uc2 They should volunteer as poll watchers. It`s a state-based system. Whatever your state requires and allows, you should definitely be involved. And I think it`s going to help the process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And just to be abundantly clear, poll watching is different than voter intimidation, which is unlawful.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Nobody should be intimidated when they`re exercising their most precious right to vote.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, when you see video, as we have seen in a place like in Arizona, where you see individuals with tactical gear, where you see individuals with weapons outside drop boxes, where -- how do you classify that?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Again, no one should feel intimidated when they`re exercising their right to vote.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That is intimidating?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: You`ve got -- you`ve got stories on both sides of the aisle. You have got stories in many different states about how people have felt as though their right was infringed on.\par \par \uc2 I think cooler heads need to prevail.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: I really think we`re going to have a good election. I think you`ve got 15-million plus that have already participated in early voting across this country.\par \par \uc2 I think you`re going to have a big turnout in nine days from now on the election. And I think, again, Republicans, Democrats and others are well aware of the issues that we had during COVID. People were stepping up and trying to do things to make sure we were protected and safe, but they were adjusting election laws on the fly.\par \par \uc2 I think a lot of that has been resolved. I think you`re going to see a really good experience in nine days.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. So you would urge citizens not to patrol polling places?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Again, I urge everyone, get involved.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Whatever your state allows, get involved. Volunteer.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I need...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Exactly what your...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: ... your previous guest said.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because I want to ask...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Be part of the process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you, because, in this bulletin, it also talks about a linkage in drawing and -- and undermining confidence in elections because of 2020.\par \par \uc2 And you are on this list that we have of 307 Republicans running for office who have raised doubts about the integrity of the election. I mean, they`re drawing a direct line here between sowing distrust then and what we are seeing right now.\par \par \uc2 Do you regret sowing doubts yourself?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: I -- what you`re referring to, I have never sowed doubts about the election. I have definitely raised questions.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you signed onto this Texas amicus brief...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Absolutely. Let`s talk about that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... that went to the Supreme Court to overturn it.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: What it said was -- Margaret, what that -- what that said, that amicus brief, is that a lot of people, governors, attorneys general, secretaries of state, took unilateral action during COVID, changing...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: ... changing the election laws themselves.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you don`t regret these...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Again, if I could finish -- changing election laws themselves to try and make sure that we were safe while we exercised our right to vote.\par \par \uc2 That particular amicus brief, all it said was that we need to reaffirm that state legislatures, and legislatures alone, make their election laws. So, as we go forward, once we`re out of COVID, we have to...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, you...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: ... go back to that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, to be clear, you don`t consider yourself an election denier?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the legitimately elected...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: We -- we...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... president of United States of America?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: He`s sworn. He`s serving. He is the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: He is the president of the United States. But you stand by...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: And we`ve got an election in nine days. We`ve got an election in nine days that we`ve been working on for the last two years.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: You reported in the in -- intro that eight out of ten Americans in that same poll that you`re referring to are concerned with where this country is. They say it`s out of control.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right, but you`re not undermining...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: If you look, eight out of 10...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re not meaning to undermine confidence in the 2020 election with your comments here?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Eight out of ten Americans right now think we`re on the wrong track. That`s what they`re going to be voting on in nine days.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I know.\par \par \uc2 And I would love to talk about something other than people being worried for their lives. But, unfortunately, that`s where we are.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about this when it comes to political violence. On your \uc2 Twitter\uc2  feed, you posted this video we`re going to show just a few days ago where you`re firing a gun, and it says: "Enjoyed exercising my Second Amendment rights. #FirePelosi."\par \par \uc2 Why is there a gun in a political ad at all?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: It wasn`t an ad. I was -- I was tweeting out -- I was tweeting out something that I had just done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Hashtag -- or a tweet -- hashtag #FirePelosi, with a weapon.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Well, now you`re...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Wouldn`t a pink slip be more fitting, if it`s about firing her?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: It`s interesting, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why a gun?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: It`s interesting, Margaret, that we`re talking about this, this morning, when, a couple of years back, when a Bernie Sanders supporter shot Steve Scalise...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Which was horrendous and horrific...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: ... when a Bernie Sanders supporter...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... which is why we should be not...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: ... shot Steve Scalise...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... be putting weapons...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: ... I never heard you or anyone else in the media trying to blame Democrats for what happened.\par \par \uc2 We need to stay focused on what we`re all doing...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We did extensive coverage of what happened to Steve Scalise.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Excuse me?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There was extensive coverage, sir.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: But nobody tried to equate Democrats` rhetoric, people that say...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m not talking about your rhetoric. I`m talking about what you posted.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: It`s the same.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You`re shooting a gun. Our viewers just saw it.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Yeah, right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Hashtag #FirePelosi.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Exercising our Second Amendment rights, having fun...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s not a debate about the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: ... shooting a gun. Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s not a debate about the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Hashtag #FirePelosi.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 I -- I`m running the campaign operation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you not understand that that is suggestive to people who are in a bad state, and, in this current environment, how risky it is? As you`re talking about...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Well, I disagree, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... the importance of lowering the rhetoric...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: I disagree with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... why do you leave that up?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Again, I never saw anyone after Steve Scalise was shot by a...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m talking about right now...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: ... Bernie Sanders supporter...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... what just is happening right now.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: .. trying to equate -- equate Democrat rhetoric with those actions. Please don`t do that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 Looking at your candidates, Republican candidates have spent more than $116 million on ads that mentioned Speaker Pelosi by name in their cycle. If this is about the issues, why shouldn`t make it about the issues? Why not depersonalize it?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: It is absolutely about the issues. It`s about the fact that we have double-digit inflation...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You don`t think...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: ... exploding -- exploding cost of living. We`ve got a crime wave across this country that is a direct result -- is the direct result...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In this moment -- we are eight days out -- don`t you think this needs to change?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Again...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Why not pull some of these ads? Why not just delete your tweet?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Well, I`m sure -- I`m sure people would like to talk about anything but what the Democrats have done to this country, which, quite frankly, is exploding cost of living, a crime wave in our major cities that is the result of this defund the police nonsense and cashless bail.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you look at New York City, where you put someone in jail at 9:00 p.m. for assaulting someone on the street and they`re back out on the street at 9:00 a.m. committing crimes again. You look at my state of Minnesota, Minneapolis, it`s had 6,000 assaults since the beginning of the year, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: Those are the issues that are top of mind for every voter in this country.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: That`s why they`re going to show up in -- on the -- on November 8.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE TOM EMMER: And that`s why Republicans are going to win in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s what our projections are showing.\par \par \uc2 But I would suggest more pink slips, fewer weapons in our ads in this environment.\par \par \uc2 Sir, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will be right back. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we`re joined now by David Becker. He is CBS News election law contributor.\par \par \uc2 David, welcome back here.\par \par \uc2 We just had that conversation about the environment that we are in right now. I know you have been raising concerns for a while about many of your colleagues, people you know who serve as election workers, who are fearful about what`s to come.\par \par \uc2 What are you hearing?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Yes, I mean, they have been suffering for two years now abuse, threats and harassment not only directed at them and their staffs, but their families, their children in some cases. It seems like election deniers do want to create an environment of fear.\par \par \uc2 I think they want election officials to be scared. And they want voters to be scared, to some degree. I think it`s also really remarkable that voters have responded with over 20 million of them voting already. We have 1.6 million of them having already voted in Georgia alone.\par \par \uc2 The environment is really scary right now for a lot. I mean, we see that from the polling. We see that from what Scott was just talking about earlier as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And these are people in your community that you know.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This isn`t big, bad Washington.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: No.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: I talk to election officials regularly. I was talking to an election official in Florida just last night who was dealing with an issue at a polling place.\par \par \uc2 I talk to election officials in Arizona, including those issues that we have seen in Maricopa County, Arizona. Important to note that...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Those men in the tactical gear that we were just talking about.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Right, at a couple of...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: ... that a federal -- federal judge says is their -- is permitted.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Right, yes, at least for now.\par \par \uc2 It`s been appealed to the Ninth Circuit, and it will be heard next week. So, we will see if that is still the case. Under federal law, there are multiple statutes that prohibit voter intimidation or attempted voter intimidation.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you, as a former lawyer who used to serve in the Department of Justice, that videotaping or photographing voters against their will, following them, that has been the kind of activity that the DOJ has viewed as intimidating in the past.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to make a very clear distinction here, because we were talking about with Congressman Emmer the call for people -- and you have heard it from the head of the \uc2 RNC\uc2  -- to come and watch voting occur.\par \par \uc2 That is not illegal.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: No, it`s not only not illegal.\par \par \uc2 Properly trained poll watching by both parties is a really good thing. Extreme transparency is something that`s practiced by election officials all over the country. They want people to come in and see what they`re doing. That is a really good thing. But they serve as flies on the wall. If they see a problem, they`re supposed to report it to the proper authorities.\par \par \uc2 When we see vigilantes -- and this is only happening in a few places -- start to take matters into their own hands, those people are not properly trained. But people who want to volunteer to be poll workers working for the actual election jurisdiction, more poll watchers working for their campaigns or their political parties, as long as they`re well-trained, that`s a really good part of the process.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm. But voter intimidation is unlawful.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You -- we were looking in our research here at that the AP has a report saying the most litigation ever fired -- filed before an election is happening right now, most of those lawsuits by Republicans.\par \par \uc2 What does that signal to you?\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Well, I think people are very attuned to what`s going on with the election process. That`s not necessarily a bad thing.\par \par \uc2 We`re also seeing efforts to change rules in red areas as well, things to install hand counting of ballots, which is a really bad idea, really comes up with inaccurate counts, takes a lot longer, breeds more concern about the process. A county called Nye County in Nevada is doing that.\par \par \uc2 But resolving these kinds of disputes before the election is a good thing. So, if we`re having a lot of litigation, it`s important for everyone to know the rules by election night.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: It`s not OK to question those rules after the election. You mentioned that Texas lawsuit that came in December 2020.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: That was a perfect example of that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: All right, thank you very much, David Becker.\par \par \uc2 DAVID BECKER: Thank you, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 And we will be seeing a lot more of you, I know, in the coming days and weeks.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: If you can`t watch the full Face the Nation, you can set your DVR.\par \par \uc2 Plus, you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount+ app. And we`re replayed on our CBS News Streaming Network throughout the day Sunday.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We go now to the chair of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, that`s New York Congressman \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY) (Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee Chair): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to start where we just left off with our other guests. How concerned are you about the risk of political violence? And can you say, unequivocally, that you and your fellow Democrats will accept the outcome of your elections?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 : Of course, we`ll accept the outcome of the elections. Always have. Always will.\par \par \uc2 And - and we`re very concerned about the integrity of our elections, especially when people are trying to intimidate people with weapons, when they`re engaged in these tactics to try to undermine confidence.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re - we`re going to accept the results of the election. That`s - that`s the American way.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, I`m glad you said that because there`s been a lot of attention drawn to comments by some Democrats, including \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , who just released a taped statement online where she was talking about upcoming elections and she said, right-wing extremists already have a plan to literally steal the next presidential election and they`re not making a secret of it.\par \par \uc2 I understand hyperbole, but would you agree that that`s not helpful in the current environment to talk about plots to steal elections?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 : Yes, no, I don`t understand what that means and I didn`t see the comment.\par \par \uc2 Let me - let me tell you what I think. What I think is that it`s perfectly legitimate for both parties to make sure that voting is fair, that there`s no fraud, that when votes are cast, that the people have a right to cast them or don`t. And that`s normal. And in a close election, you might have a recount, you might have other examination of absentee ballots. That`s all run of the mill stuff.\par \par \uc2 What`s different is people showing up with weapons and sitting in the back of a pickup truck next to a drop box trying to scare the tar out of people who are just exercising their vote.\par \par \uc2 What`s different is when a president, for the first time in our history, says he was cheated out of the result when he knows that`s a lie. So, let`s -- let`s not - let`s not - let`s not pretend for a minute that both sides have the same - same amount of accountability for - for the loss of confidence in our elections. One side has been out there for a couple of years now doing everything they can to pretend \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn`t win fair and square when he did. And that`s the difference.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On that topic, Democrats shelled out about $53 million to support 13 Republican candidates during the primaries. I know you`re part of strategy here and the strategy was to elevate less electable Republicans in order to benefit Democrats. And some of this worked for you, but in at least two races these guys have realistic shots at victory. I`m looking at John Gibbs in Michigan, for example.\par \par \uc2 Do you take responsibility for helping election deniers?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 : Well, at the DCCC, there`s only one race in the country, one, where we - where we engage in the - in the tactic you`re talking about. And what we did was we ran an ad that`s true that said he`s too extreme for western Michigan. And we ran that general election ad about two weeks early. And Hillary Scholten has been beating him consistently since she became our nominee. She`s a strong pro-choice Democrat. He`s a nut.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you stand by the strategy?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 : And she`s going to -- and she`s going to beat him.\par \par \uc2 What I can tell you is that, in a budget of $340 million, we spent about $400,000 running a true general election commercial two weeks early calling John Gibbs an extremist, which he is. And - and that`s all you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 Now, I can`t be -- I - I can`t answer to what governors did or what senators did or what other people did. But the committee I ran, in a budget of $300 million, put $400,000 into speeding up a general election ad that was truthful.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 : And we`re going to win that race.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The top three issues when we talk to voters that they tell us they`re concerned about are economy, inflation and crime. Half of voters, according to our poll, think Democrats would cut police funding. Now, I saw an ad the DCCC just bought in your district, and it opens with you talking to a policeman. You are keenly aware of this. Why hasn`t your party been able to shake that perception of being soft on crime?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 : Well, because a lie gets halfway around the world before the truth gets its shoes on, as the saying goes. In my own case, I brought $7 million for local police departments, supported the Invest to Protect Act, which will fund all the police departments in my district, all under 125 officers. That`s the most important support we`ve done for police in 30 years.\par \par \uc2 And if you care about rising crime, my goodness, we should listen to police who are begging us to do something about the gun violence, which is at the heart of so much rise in crime. And, of course, the MAGA crowd is in the pocket of the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and won`t touch common sense (ph) bipartisan gun safety legislation, which we passed through the House.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a record of results supporting good policing and - and - and going after gun violence. And that`s what people need to know.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In our poll, 46 percent of voters believe Republicans` economic policies will help them. Only 40 percent say the same about Democrats. Fifty-three percent of voters believe gas prices will go up under Democrats. Twenty-one percent say the same of the - of the GOP. I mean, since you`re talking strategy here, in these closing days, how do you change these perceptions and how do you get out younger voters?\par \par \uc2 SEAN PATRICK MALONEY\uc2 : Right. Well, what people need to know is that we have a plan for cheaper gas, cheaper groceries, cheaper housing, cheaper health care. We have a plan for safer streets, supporting good policing and attacking gun violence, which is so much of the problem, and supporting our freedoms, our reproductive freedoms and our voting rights. And that`s a -- those are real plans.\par \par \uc2 Now, you can take a poll and you can - you can take a snapshot at any given time. But, of course, your former guest had no plan for the economy. They have no plan for gun violence. They have no plan to move our country forward, protecting voting rights, protecting reproductive freedoms. So -- so what I would say is, don`t punish the people who are fixing your problems and don`t reward the people who are trying to exploit the problems for their own political power. That`s really the difference right now. We`re - we`re engaged in the hard work of bringing our country forward. The other side`s working on their own power.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Congressman, thank you for your time this morning.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to take a closer look at some of our battleground tracker findings and the issues that are setting the mood in America in these final days of campaign `22.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann reports from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): America`s zeitgeist, vulnerability, like this home invasion hammer attack on a politician`s 82-year-old spouse, reinforcing we`re an anxious, often resentful bunch heading into midterms.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Now with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , there`s a big thumb on top of you where you can`t enjoy yourself.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I just need to see some peace. And I think the only way to do this is voting.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The economy, especially inflation, indisputably top of mind in voter anxiety.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: The cost of groceries are outrageous. Utilities are outrageous. It keeps going up.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (on camera): And gas prices. Look at these in Los Angeles, right at $7 a gallon for regular. That would be nightmarish in most of the country. But here in L.A., prices have actually been coming down in the last couple weeks.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN (voice over): In our latest CBS News poll of registered voters, a majority blame President Biden and the Democrats for the economy and gas prices. The president fighting that perception.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Unemployment is not 6.5 but 3.5 percent, the lowest it`s been in 50 years.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Republicans see inflation numbers and smell blood.\par \par \uc2 SEN. TED CRUZ (R-TX): Not just a red wave, but a red tsunami.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Crime is another voter worry, like last week`s deadly school shooting in St. Louis, the 40th this year involving injuries or deaths. In major cities, murder rates and shootings, both down slightly, but from a 30 percent spike two years ago. Our poll shows Republicans have a double-digit lead on crime policies to make you feel safer.\par \par \uc2 But American voters want this election cycle of leadership to confront other challenges. Immigration, an infectious dies trifecta, Covid, the flu and RSV, gun policy and abortion in a post-Roe America. Our poll says a majority of voters think Republicans will pass a national abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 Another worry, culture wars invading the classroom and performance that`s plummeting. It`s one more challenge calling out for grownup intervention as millions of Americans now vote for their idea of a grownup, knowing that whoever wins, half the country will resent it, again.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mark Strassmann reporting from Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Our new CBS News battleground tracker poll shows Republicans still have an edge in the race for control of the House. CBS News elections and surveys director Anthony Salvanto is here.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, tell us more.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`ve been talking to voters throughout this campaign in all the congressional districts. Right now our latest CBS News estimate is that the Republicans lead in 228 districts. That is enough to take the House majority.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a range around these estimates. Let me show you the political implications here. They really center around turnout. You might ask, do Democrats have any chance to hang on, even to a slim House majority? The answer to that starts with the possibility that they`d get bigger turnout from younger voters. If voters show up the way they did in, say, 2018, that could get the Democrats a little bit closer, make the House effectively even. The trouble for them is, we have not seen strong indications that that is going to happen.\par \par \uc2 The more likely scenario is that Republicans, who do have an express turnout advantage, more motivation, more enthusiasm so far, if their turnout advantage grows, we plug that into the model and that would get the Republicans up to 238 seats, a slightly larger majority.\par \par \uc2 Now, in any case, we`re looking at a seat gain here for the Republicans that`s about or even below average for a party out of power. You might ask why that is, especially given the concerns about the state of the country and the state of the economy.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you a split that I think tells a lot of the tale.\par \par \uc2 I asked people, which concerns you more, whether or not the U.S. is going to have a functioning democracy or whether it`s going to have a strong economy? And the results are somewhat split. But look at the voting differences here. Those who are more concerned about functioning of democracy, voting for Democrats. Those more concerned about the strong economy, voting for Republicans. And I think that defines the way that voters see the very large stakes here and the way the parties are expressing those stakes, which is partly why this race is so locked in.\par \par \uc2 Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Anthony Salvanto, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For more now we turn to our political panel.\par \par \uc2 Amy Walter, publisher and editor-in-chief of "The Cook Political Report," senior White House and political correspondent, Ed O`Keefe is here, and Nick Timiraos, chief economic correspondent for "The Wall Street Journal" is also at the table.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to all of you.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER (Publisher and editor-in chief, "Cook Political Report with Amy Walter"): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NICK TIMIRAOS (Chief economic correspondent, "The Wall Street Journal): Hi.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Amy, we just heard Anthony`s scenario here. This is not a red wave, but it is a Republican majority. Does it match with what you`re seeing?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes. I think the way that Anthony pointed it out is a very smart way of thinking about this.\par \par \uc2 You know, elections -- normally midterm elections, the challenge for the in-party usually is that their own side doesn`t turn out. And that`s where the other side is much more motivated. And when you see those big, big gains, like we saw say in 1994 or 2010 on the part of Republicans. This year we`re seeing that Republicans and Democrats are motivated. Republicans a little bit more. But Democrats feel pretty good that they`re at least getting more of their base turned out. Now, young voters being always a challenge for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 But, look, I think what happened over the course of the summer is that the issues surrounding abortion rights, plus the focus on \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , January 6th, and, quite frankly, a little better of an economic picture gave some boost of enthusiasm to Democrats and got them energized in terms of the voters, more energized to turn out. But it feels as if that has pretty much stalled. That it hit this point and it hasn`t really gone much further. And, instead, where the focus is now, it`s no longer as much about abortion or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and much more about the things that Anthony pointed out, which is the economy, gas prices and people feeling, quite frankly, pretty stuck.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we frame this as, you know, sort of a choice with (INAUDIBLE). And - and, Ed, in some of these recent politic ads, I`m thinking about in Maloney`s district, I was just watching one, they`re changing the focus to be about extremism.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So not even talking about the affirmative case, but fear the other side.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Right. Not - and not only in the realm of democracy and - and those concerns, but in the realm of abortion rights and - and Social Security and Medicare being ripped up or rethought.\par \par \uc2 I watched Maloney this week actually make that exact argument to a bunch of seniors in assisted living facilities. He said, if you don`t think they might come after your Social Security and Medicare, look what they just did to abortion rights after 50 years of threatening to do it. So that`s kind of a way to make both arguments at the same time but not make an exclusive (ph) abortion argument. And that`s the kind of pivoting that they`ve done here, now realizing that abortion isn`t as urgent an issue.\par \par \uc2 Part of the reason one Republican I talked to this week said, look, you go look at mothers across the country who may be concerned about abortion rights, but they`re not necessarily bumping up against the issue of abortion rights every day, they`re bumping up against the fact that beef is costing three times as much as it did before.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And those more urgent economic issues may be what`s drawing people back over to the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 I want to come back to the issue of abortion in a second.\par \par \uc2 But, Nick, can you pick up on this because our poll shows 69 percent of voters describe the condition of the national economy as bad. Only 27 percent say it`s good.\par \par \uc2 You`ve got a piece saying -- out in "The Journal" saying basically American consumers are doing pretty well.\par \par \uc2 NICK TIMIRAOS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, why don`t they feel that way?\par \par \uc2 NICK TIMIRAOS: Yes, that`s a great question, Margaret. You know, the issue here is that if you look at the labor market, 3.5 percent unemployment rate, what`s not to like about that? But if you look at, you know, what`s happening with wages, they are not keeping up with prices. We had another report Friday that shows that Americans` wages are not keeping up with prices. And so those are real income cuts. Nobody likes that. You drive by the gas station, you see how expensive gas is and you look at what`s happening right now with interest rates, they`ve gone up a lot this year. Mortgage rates this week are above 7 percent for the 30-year fixed rate mortgage. We haven`t seen that since 2001.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, on paper, you know, there are things you can point to that look great, but people just don`t feel great about it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we have this Fed meeting in the coming days. What are the conversations inside the Federal Reserve right now about when inflation gets better and what they plan to do?\par \par \uc2 NICK TIMIRAOS: Well, the problem for the Fed is that monetary policy takes time. It acts with a delay on the economy. So, you can`t see your moves right away.\par \par \uc2 The Fed, this year, has raised interest rates as the fastest pace since the 1980s. Normally they raise rates by a quarter point every six weeks or so. This year they`ve been going at three-quarters of a percentage point. And when you don`t have time to see how that influences the economy, it`s like barreling down the highway but using the rearview mirror to guide where you`re going. It raises the risk that you`re going to drive off the road.\par \par \uc2 And the problem here for the Fed is, they can`t take a risk of not getting on top of this inflation because even though the risk of doing too much is a recession, the risk of not doing enough is that inflation just stays high and you have to have a bigger downturn later.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, I mean, those are the details of it. That is monetary policy. What is it that - that Republicans are actually arguing, Ed, they can do because, really, the reality is, their hands are pretty tied here.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They are. I - you know, if you read their websites, if you listen to what they say -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: They want to cut government spending and, in some cases, they want to see implementation of the bipartisan infrastructure bill that either they voted for and they don`t want to admit that will help their districts even if they voted against it. And I -\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Yes, I mean -\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Go ahead.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Well, and that`s the bottom line, when you`re in charge, and things are going well, you get the credit, a lot of which you don`t deserve. When you`re in charge and things aren`t going well, you get the blame, even if you don`t` deserve all of it. And when you`re the out party, you get the benefit of the doubt because voters are saying, well, I don`t know, if Ed can really fix this, but these folks who are in charge, they`re not doing a very good job. Let`s just - let`s just put it -- let`s just give them a shot, which is why Democrats are doing the extremism thing.\par \par \uc2 If people are upset with the status quo, which they clearly are, Democrats` only chance to hold on, especially in some of these Senate races, is to say, the bigger risk is taking a risk on the other side. Staying with the status quo you don`t like is uncomfortable but not as bad.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: And the other ironic thing, a lot - much like the Fed, the - the problem for the Biden administration and Democrats is a lot of their accomplishments are going to take time to be implemented.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The Medicare price cuts don`t kick in until next year.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The bridge isn`t getting rebuilt until early 2024.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: So they can talk about all these things in the abstract, but the voter can`t see it yet.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: The president may know, come my re-election, if I`m running in 2024, people will see what I did -\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: But I -- they won`t be able to see it before November 8th.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s also kind of hard sometimes to message around market forces.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Right. Kind of?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, an - an energy analyst that I - that I speak to was pointing out, you know, Ron Klein, the chief of staff to the president, tweets almost daily about the price of gas. He`s like, why are they trying to own something they cannot control?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That is a question that the White House chief of staff is going to have to answer for. And not only is -- why are they tweeting about something that they can`t control, why is he tweeting so much to begin with every morning about this and other issues when he, perhaps, should be running the White House? I`ve heard this from Democrats. I think we`ll hear it a lot more from Democrats after the elections if it goes south for them.\par \par \uc2 It is an obsession for them because they know it is the psychological and the sort of data barometer that we`ve all used to measure inflation and the discussion about the economy. But, yes, it is something completely out of their control. And perhaps by doing that it`s -- as that energy analyst noted, it may have drawn too much attention to the president and allows him to take more of the blame.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You know, Nick, in "The Journal," a survey of economists putting the probability of recession over the next 12 months as 63 percent. So, that is the Congress that Republicans would be walking in to control.\par \par \uc2 NICK TIMIRAOS: Yes. And the issue here is, the economy is slowing. We knew it was going to slow because we had a boom last year. But if it doesn`t slow enough, if consumers keep spending the savings that they`ve accrued during the pandemic, then that just means the Fed is going to come in and raise interest rates more. You know, this week we`re going to get close to 4 percent on the policy rate that the Fed sets, but we could go closer to 5 percent next year. And, you know, there`s a - there`s a Fed chairman who used to say that it was the Fed`s job to take the punch bowl away as the party was getting started. And that`s what you`re seeing this year.\par \par \uc2 But if consumers keep spending money, we had very solid earnings reports this past week from United, \uc2 Visa\uc2 , \uc2 Coca-Cola\uc2 , if consumers keep spending, then \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  is going to get on the phone and call the cops and say, we`ve got to get the noise volume down here. We`ve got - we`ve got to get these people to go home because this is just not sustainable to have inflation continuing to run higher and higher and higher.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right. And that`s hard to explain sometimes that like things -- some things are going to get more expensive by design. It`s going to get more expensive for you to borrow money because of what`s happening.\par \par \uc2 NICK TIMIRAOS: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Coming back to the issue of abortion, Amy and Ed. I think it`s interesting that the dynamic seems to have changed on this in terms of bringing out voters. You were saying, Ed, that people feel inflation, perhaps, is a more immediate crisis than abortion access.\par \par \uc2 I wonder, Amy, because it`s so different state by state.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Right. That`s what we`re -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Is that it?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: It`s quite interesting actually. Where we`re seeing Democratic -- especially candidates for the House, having the most trouble is actually in blue states, like Oregon, California, places where the issue of abortion is pretty well settled. These are Democratic-run states. They`re - the -- it`s not an existential question --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because the state has already codified it?\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Because the state has either codified or it`s Democratic and the governors` candidates have all pledged to go forward in that way.\par \par \uc2 It`s the red and purple states, Michigan, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, where we know that this issue is not settled and where, in Michigan, of course, they have a ballot initiative, as well as a governor`s race. You have governor races in those other states. So, there`s much more of a - of a friction there, right, about the decision being determined by your vote this November that`s not as apparent in some of these blue states.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Yes. And the fact that they have to run into New York, Oregon, the president`s going to New Mexico this week.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It all speaks to the fact that perhaps they may have miscalculated how abortion would work in those states and how much money Republicans have to spend right now in the closing weeks to really put a bunch of Democrats on the ropes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Good to have all three of you here. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for us here at FACE THE NATION today. Thank you for watching. And tune in next Tuesday, Election Day. I can`t believe that. Wow.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER: I can`t believe you just said that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And night, starting at 5:00 p.m. on our streaming network with a special edition of "Red and Blue." And we`ll be on both the broadcasts and streaming networks starting at 8:00 p.m. Eastern Time through the night and into the morning.\par \par \uc2 For FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020221030eiau00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 New Walker Abortion Allegations\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Nikole Killion, Tony Dokoupil \par \uc2 1,479 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS. It`s Thursday, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Georgia Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  denies new allegations from another woman who says he helped her get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) (Georgia Senate candidate): They`re desperate right now and I`ve said this a lie, and they want me to play these guessing games and all of this, but I`m not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mortgage rates hit a more than 20-year high in this country, what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 DARYL FAIRWEATHER (\uc2 Redfin\uc2  Chief Economist): Interest rates are so high, it is going to do some long-term damage to the housing market.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The man who plowed through a parade in Wisconsin last year is convicted and faces life in prison.\par \par \uc2 RYAN KOHNKE (Jessalyn Torres` uncle): As a community we can just start to heal and move forward from this.\par \par \uc2 MAN: IN Michigan, a jury found three more men are guilty of a 2020 plot to kidnap that State`s Governor.\par \par \uc2 DANA NESSEL (Michigan Attorney General): Individuals that wanted to cause mayhem and murder and chaos.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: No one won the Powerball jackpot last night, meaning the prize now grows to $800 million.\par \par \uc2 WOMEN: Kanye West is escorted out of the Skechers headquarters after showing up unannounced.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The company says it has no intention of working with him.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  planning to finalize his $44 billion deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  tweeted out this video of himself walking into \uc2 Twitter\uc2  headquarters carrying a sink. He wrote: "Let that sink in."\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): Because he realized he`s just thrown $44 billion down the drain.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Earlier today, a woman came forward alleging she was in a relationship with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and that he helped her have an abortion. Wait a second. Didn`t we do this story already? Am I in a rerun right now? No, "Late Show" all-new, not a rerun, okay. So...\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I kind of thought that to, Stephen, when I first heard it yesterday. Didn`t we do that story? But this woman, this time has come with receipts apparently video of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in bed, voice tapes with him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Both women had receipts and it does seem to be a pattern.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. We`re going to be talking about that as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got some great guests in the show today, including the creators of a powerful new film on the life of Emmett Till. Till as you know, was lynched in Mississippi back in 1955. Whoopi Goldberg is one of the stars and she produced it, she will be here with the director, Chinonye Chukwu to tell us why they focused on Till`s mother and her crusade for justice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Katie Couric is here. She has been a storyteller for years, but she is here today to share more about her own story, her fight against breast cancer. She has got an important message for all families, in particular, all women.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, we have a fascinating conversation with "Bridgerton`s" executive producers, Shonda Rhimes, and Betsy Beers takes us behind the scenes of their hit show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the two powerhouses.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are going to be sitting side by side on the couch.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with new allegations against Georgia Republican Senate nominee, that`s \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , less than two weeks before Election Day. A second woman now says that he paid for her abortion and even drove her to the clinic after she initially decided not to go through with it.\par \par \uc2 Now, he was married at the time and the woman says they were having an affair. Walker calls the allegations a lie. He also terms it foolishness. This comes in the midst of a very tight race that could help determine control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is doubling down on this new claim saying it is not true, but the accuser insists that this is not politically motivated, and says she has proof.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I`m not going to stop. This seat is too important.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: GOP Senate nominee, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  hit the airwaves Wednesday night hours after a second accuser claimed he pressured her to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 I`m done with this foolishness.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA ALLRED (Attorney): That`s not a denial.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Attorney Gloria Allred represents the woman who she identified as Jane Doe.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (Second woman to accuser \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  to get an abortion): He encouraged me to have an abortion and gave me the money to do so.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The woman described a six-year relationship with Walker while he was married and playing in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . She says that in April 1993, she found out she was pregnant and told Walker she couldn`t go through with the abortion.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE: He was upset and said that he was going to go back with me to the clinic the next day for me to have the abortion.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: While her lawyer did not present receipts from the clinic, she did provide what she claimed was other evidence of the relationship from a photo to handwritten cards and a recorded message purportedly from Walker.\par \par \uc2 MAN (Allegedly \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  recording): I love you and I was thinking about you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The woman said she decided to come forward due to Walker`s antiabortion rights stance.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I believe in life --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And following other allegations by another woman that he paid for her abortion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is a hypocrite, and he is not fit to be a US senator.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : What I`m saying is this is a lie and I`ve said it once and I`ve moved on my campaign.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): This stuff needs to stop. You`re trying to ruin people`s lives.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: On the campaign trail, many of Walker supporters seemed unfazed, although a new poll found Walker with a 52 percent unfavorable rating among Georgia voters, a number that`s gone up since last month.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think what he stands for is the truth. You know, everybody has done bad things in their life. I mean, even me and I`m pretty darn good.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, that woman told us she has already voted for Walker. In fact, more than a million people have cast their ballots in early voting in Georgia shattering records.\par \par \uc2 Walker`s Democratic opponent, Senator Raphael Warnock called the latest allegations part of a troubling pattern -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then the question is, do voters believe it and how much do voters care if they do believe it.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion for us once again. Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Moving on to Pennsylvania now and the Democratic Senate candidate \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , who is responding to concerns from some about his debate performance on Tuesday night. Fetterman as you will recall, used a closed captioning device because he is recovering from a stroke.\par \par \uc2 At a rally in Pittsburgh, he called it one of the hardest things he has ever done.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) (Pennsylvania Senate candidate): To be honest with you, doing that debate wasn`t exactly easy, you know?\par \par \uc2 I knew it. I knew it wasn`t going to be easy after having a stroke after five months, in fact --\par \par \uc2 (MAN shouting "We still love you.")\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : In fact -- in fact, I don`t think that has ever been done before in American political history before.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, concern in some quarters, also a lot of support. Fetterman says he has raised $2 million in the aftermath of that debate. It is an extremely close race and in most polling, he is essentially tied with his opponent, Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221028eiar000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Katie Couric`s Cancer Journey\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 2,612 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: October is Breast Cancer Awareness Month, we all know that. Our next guest is sharing her own journey with this disease.\par \par \uc2 Katie Couric is an award-winning journalist with more than 40 years of experience. In September, she shared the news that she was diagnosed with stage one breast cancer. She documented the screening process and caught the moment her doctor found something of concern.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC (Journalist): So in addition to getting a mammogram, I also get a breast ultrasound.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: On ultrasound, what I saw was a mass and that mass had irregular margins and I looked at you and I looked at the person who was imaging us and I said I think we really need to turn the camera off. I want to speak frankly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Katie Couric joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Katie, take us to that moment, because it`s one of -- any woman who has been through this, this is the worst process -- this is the worst moment for the doctors to say, "Wait, we need to talk about this."\par \par \uc2 So did you go -- were you documenting it because you felt something or because it was just a regular -- you thought it was going to be a regular screening and you just wanted to take viewers inside?\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: Yes, you know, hi, Gayle, first of all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hi.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: You know, I wanted to remind people, millions of women, I think put off their mammograms because of the pandemic. And as a result, many breast cancers and other cancers for that matter being diagnosed much later when they`re harder to treat.\par \par \uc2 So I really went in to not only remind women to get screened, but I also wanted to talk about dense breasts because in addition to mammography, I always get a breast ultrasound. So when she said, "Turn the camera off," my producer was videotaping it on my iPhone. I wanted to say, "No, let`s keep rolling. It`s okay. I`ve been in this world for many years, I`m fine having you tell me whatever you need to tell me."\par \par \uc2 But she insisted that we turn it off, and the next day told me that I in fact, had stage -- an early breast cancer that she thought could be treated but we needed to come up with a plan.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Katie, I hear you talking about it as a TV person, as a person producing you know, the screening; but as a human being when your doctor, you know, their tone of voice changes, their facial expression changes and they say "Turn the camera off." I mean, my heart would have -- my stomach would have sunk to my knees. How were you dealing with it there emotionally?\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: Well, you know, I think my heart stopped, but I -- you know, for some reason, Tony, I just felt like it was probably going to be early. I don`t know why. But it was scary. And it was scary waiting for the results of the biopsy, which she called me about the next day.\par \par \uc2 I have had so much experience with cancer and when my husband, Jay, was diagnosed with colon cancer, it was stage four, it was metastatic. When my sister Emily two years later was diagnosed with pancreatic cancer, it was stage four\par \par \uc2 So I was hopeful and optimistic that if it was something that we caught it early enough and that in fact was the case, but of course, it is nerve racking. But, you know, it was not my first rodeo. And I think I had enough background and experience in cancer that I wasn`t totally freaked out, to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Katie, you mentioned how the pandemic like many other men and women has caused us to miss scheduled health checks. Have you always prioritized your health? And if so, what is your advice? Because there`s people sitting at home, they really don`t think about it. They say, Oh, I`ve got time. I will wait.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I will get to it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll get around to it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: Well, yes. I`ve always been extremely vigilant about my health, especially after my husband died and I became a single mom and my daughters were six and two. I`ve been Uber on top of it.\par \par \uc2 But for some reason, I think the pandemic sort of got us off schedule, and messed with our sense of time, and I was sent my gynecologist and she said, you`re due for a mammogram and I said, "Are you kidding? I just had one." And she said, "Actually, no, you`re six months late." So my breast radiologist recommends A., scheduling your mammogram and when you leave the office, scheduling the one for next year. So you`re on the book.\par \par \uc2 So, even if you have to change it, you`re not going to forget about following through, but really what I wanted to do by going public with this was to help women understand that if they`re among the 45 percent of women with dense breast from 40 and up, they need to A., find out because it`s only indicated on a mammogram. And then they need to talk to their doctor about follow-up screening.\par \par \uc2 In my case, I get a breast ultrasound. I know Gayle does, too. But a lot of women don`t know this and 38 states require doctors to inform patients that they have dense breasts, but they don`t really give them any information about what to do with that.\par \par \uc2 So the FDA is actually changing their policy guidelines and I think by the end of the year, all healthcare providers will be required to not only inform their patients, but tell them what their options are and what they should do to follow up because unfortunately, even 3D mammograms which are far superior to standard mammograms aren`t really always that great at detecting tumors.\par \par \uc2 If you have dense breasts, my radiologist said it`s like finding a snowball against a backdrop of a field of snow because it all comes out white. So a lot of women, almost half need additional screening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t know. That`s why Katie, it`s so important that you`re talking about it. You`re right. I too, have dense breasts. We`re a member of a very big group and it is important that we do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 I have them send me reminder notices.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Katie Couric. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: Thank you, all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. A reminder, women should start getting regular mammography screening at age 50. Now, the option to start at age 40.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 Miss King, you`re up first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. My "Talk of the Table" is the world premiere of "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever." I don`t know why you can`t just say the words without doing it, but you really can`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can`t do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Wakanda Forever."\par \par \uc2 It was a star-studded red carpet last night in LA as the cast and crew came out to celebrate this new movie. There was excitement about the sequel with the late Chadwick Boseman, who starred in 2018`s original blockbuster -- remember that -- was on everybody`s mind, of course.\par \par \uc2 Angela Bassett, who plays his mom talked about visiting his resting place with the cast and crew before shooting of the new movie began.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANGELA BASSETT ("Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" actor): It was wonderful that that our producer, you know, Nate Moore and our director, Ryan Coogler, they had arranged a flight, you know, a plane to take us to Chadwick`s resting place. So, we were all able to be there.\par \par \uc2 Those of us who were starting to project the first days were able to do that to pay, to give love, and to feel his spirit and to stand there with him before we did one frame of anything.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Angela Bassett, just another example of Black don`t crack. We say it all the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just spill your line, Nate. You said, "She`s fine, fine."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She`s fine, fine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She really is.\par \par \uc2 The other big thing about this premiere last night, they were talking about that it is one of the best red carpets I`ve ever seen, because this cast and crew, take a look at some of these pictures, pulled out all the stops.\par \par \uc2 That`s Danai.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stunning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at Michael B. Jordan. I could see you, Nate, in that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nate, he has handled that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll try.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Winston Duke, who by the way guys, we are doing him on Monday. He`s going to be here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Angela Bassett.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at these outfits, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. That`s what I said.\par \par \uc2 I know, they said it was probably one of the most high fashion red carpet events that LA has seen in a very long time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, I`m just so excited to see the movie. We all went back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I am very excited to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to see it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`re excited because the trailers are awesome. But you know, there`s always a surprise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is something that they haven`t shown us in a trailer that we will see when we go watch the movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Rihanna doing the music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Rihanna.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So my "Talk of the Table" inspired by Halloween. I want to talk about a study and I`m going to put "studies" here, about what to do in the event of a zombie apocalypse. You see it in horror movie, if it comes real, "researchers" again had analyzed 26 different factors to measure zombie preparedness, looked at number of people already in poor health in the community, the number of local supermarkets, hunting supply stores.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they came to the conclusion that in the event of a zombie apocalypse, you`re going to want to go to Orlando.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Florida?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. You want to go to Orlando. It is the best place. In fact, three cities from Florida are in the top 10. Miami and Tampa are --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why though?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because of those factors, Nate. The researchers looked at it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of all of those -- okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you wanted to share this with us why, Tony? Why?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that the researchers are actually a hundred percent wrong on this being a Florida boy, you know, in the event of a zombie apocalypse there`s not going to be air conditioning and you don`t want to be anywhere near Florida without air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, look at how he`s saying this was a straight face. Zombie apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is just saying it. People think about this stuff, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You do?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve thought about it before.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where would you go --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Klaus, have you ever thought about zombie -- Patty?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where would you go? Where would you go?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clark? Clark says he has.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Where would you go --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clark is different.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If zombies took over the world, where would you go, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where would you go? Where would you hide? Oprah`s house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would feel very -- very good. I feel very safe in Oprah`s house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony always thinking the same thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She`s got a big closet with a lot of guns in it like in the movies, you know. We are ready.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She doesn`t have guns in the closet, but it would be very safe, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The security is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good idea, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is a bunker under there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 Oh, man. Okay, so my "Talk of the Table" inspired by Halloween as well is the seemingly normal decorated door, right, that turns into a Halloween nightmare when trick or treaters come around. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Greg Dietzenbach/\uc2 YouTube\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you can see the so called "Monstdoor." It turned into a giant purple creature with fuzzy arms and glowing eyes. This is Greg Dietzenbach.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He says that he started planning the trick door at his house after his children basically said they don`t need his help with costumes. So he said okay, I`m going to take my creativity, grabbed some cardboard, foam, broom handles and a bunch of fur and make this door come to life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Greg.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, shoutout to him, man. He says it took them about 10 days. That`s the type of houses you love to go to as a kid.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because it is like a core memory that will last forever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, for sure. Yes, I hope --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Greg is one of those cool dads. I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope he won his kids respect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For calling him a cool dad on TV.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did. You go, Greg.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to Shonda Rhimes and Betsy Beers. They are the Dream Team behind some of the most iconic shows on TV. What did they do? How about this list?\par \par \uc2 They developed and produced the hit series "Grey`s Anatomy," heard of it? Yes. "Scandal," heard of it? Yes. "How to Get Away with Murder," Heard of that, too.\par \par \uc2 The shows broke barriers for their casting and their great storytelling, and helped actors like Ellen Pompeo and Kerry Washington and Viola Davis break into stardom.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, the regency era drama "Bridgerton" became number one on Netflix. Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bridgerton.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you`re burning because that`s what Simon used to say, "I burn for you." I like that.\par \par \uc2 In the new book "Inside Bridgerton," Shonda Rhimes --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221028eiar000ru\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 New Walker Abortion Allegations; Pennsylvania Debate Fallout; Mortgage Rates Continue to Soar; St. Louis Shooter Red Flags; Parade Attack Suspect Convicted; Synagogue Shooting Anniversary; On the Frontlines of Ukraine; Precious Metal Mining Controversy; Japan`s Shrinking Population; The Story of "Till;" Katie Couric`s Cancer Journey; Inside "Bridgerton," Tiny Homes, Big Impact, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nikole Killion, Nancy  Chen, Roxana Saberi, Elaine Quijano, Ben Tracy, Elizabeth Palmer, Vladimir  Duthiers, Omar Villafranca \par \uc2 13,934 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS. It`s Thursday, I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We`re all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Georgia Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  denies new allegations from another woman who says he helped her get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) (Georgia Senate candidate): They`re desperate right now and I`ve said this a lie, and they want me to play these guessing games and all of this, but I`m not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Mortgage rates hit a more than 20-year high in this country, what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 DARYL FAIRWEATHER (\uc2 Redfin\uc2  Chief Economist): Interest rates are so high, it is going to do some long-term damage to the housing market.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The man who plowed through a parade in Wisconsin last year is convicted and faces life in prison.\par \par \uc2 RYAN KOHNKE (Jessalyn Torres` uncle): As a community we can just start to heal and move forward from this.\par \par \uc2 MAN: IN Michigan, a jury found three more men are guilty of a 2020 plot to kidnap that State`s Governor.\par \par \uc2 DANA NESSEL (Michigan Attorney General): Individuals that wanted to cause mayhem and murder and chaos.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: No one won the Powerball jackpot last night, meaning the prize now grows to $800 million.\par \par \uc2 WOMEN: Kanye West is escorted out of the Skechers headquarters after showing up unannounced.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The company says it has no intention of working with him.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  planning to finalize his $44 billion deal to buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2  tweeted out this video of himself walking into \uc2 Twitter\uc2  headquarters carrying a sink. He wrote: "Let that sink in."\par \par \uc2 JAMES CORDEN ("The Late, Late Show With James Corden" host): Because he realized he`s just thrown $44 billion down the drain.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Earlier today, a woman came forward alleging she was in a relationship with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and that he helped her have an abortion. Wait a second. Didn`t we do this story already? Am I in a rerun right now? No, "Late Show" all-new, not a rerun, okay. So.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I kind of thought that to, Stephen, when I first heard it yesterday. Didn`t we do that story? But this woman, this time has come with receipts apparently video of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  in bed, voice tapes with him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Both women had receipts and it does seem to be a pattern.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, yes. We`re going to be talking about that as we welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got some great guests in the show today, including the creators of a powerful new film on the life of Emmett Till. Till as you know, was lynched in Mississippi back in 1955. Whoopi Goldberg is one of the stars and she produced it, she will be here with the director, Chinonye Chukwu to tell us why they focused on Till`s mother and her crusade for justice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Katie Couric is here. She has been a storyteller for years, but she is here today to share more about her own story, her fight against breast cancer. She has got an important message for all families, in particular, all women.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus, we have a fascinating conversation with "Bridgerton`s" executive producers, Shonda Rhimes, and Betsy Beers takes us behind the scenes of their hit show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s the two powerhouses.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They are going to be sitting side by side on the couch.\par \par \uc2 But we begin with new allegations against Georgia Republican Senate nominee, that`s \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , less than two weeks before Election Day. A second woman now says that he paid for her abortion and even drove her to the clinic after she initially decided not to go through with it.\par \par \uc2 Now, he was married at the time and the woman says they were having an affair. Walker calls the allegations a lie. He also terms it foolishness. This comes in the midst of a very tight race that could help determine control of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion is on Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is doubling down on this new claim saying it is not true, but the accuser insists that this is not politically motivated, and says she has proof.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I`m not going to stop. This seat is too important.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: GOP Senate nominee, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  hit the airwaves Wednesday night hours after a second accuser claimed he pressured her to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 I`m done with this foolishness.\par \par \uc2 GLORIA ALLRED (Attorney): That`s not a denial.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Attorney Gloria Allred represents the woman who she identified as Jane Doe.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (Second woman to accuser \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  to get an abortion): He encouraged me to have an abortion and gave me the money to do so.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The woman described a six-year relationship with Walker while he was married and playing in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . She says that in April 1993, she found out she was pregnant and told Walker she couldn`t go through with the abortion.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE: He was upset and said that he was going to go back with me to the clinic the next day for me to have the abortion.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: While her lawyer did not present receipts from the clinic, she did provide what she claimed was other evidence of the relationship from a photo to handwritten cards and a recorded message purportedly from Walker.\par \par \uc2 MAN (Allegedly \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  recording): I love you and I was thinking about you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The woman said she decided to come forward due to Walker`s antiabortion rights stance.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I believe in life --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: And following other allegations by another woman that he paid for her abortion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is a hypocrite, and he is not fit to be a US senator.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : What I`m saying is this is a lie and I`ve said it once and I`ve moved on my campaign.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): This stuff needs to stop. You`re trying to ruin people`s lives.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: On the campaign trail, many of Walker supporters seemed unfazed, although a new poll found Walker with a 52 percent unfavorable rating among Georgia voters, a number that`s gone up since last month.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I think what he stands for is the truth. You know, everybody has done bad things in their life. I mean, even me and I`m pretty darn good.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, that woman told us she has already voted for Walker. In fact, more than a million people have cast their ballots in early voting in Georgia shattering records.\par \par \uc2 Walker`s Democratic opponent, Senator Raphael Warnock called the latest allegations part of a troubling pattern -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And then the question is, do voters believe it and how much do voters care if they do believe it.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion for us once again. Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Moving on to Pennsylvania now and the Democratic Senate candidate John Fetterman, who is responding to concerns from some about his debate performance on Tuesday night. Fetterman as you will recall, used a closed captioning device because he is recovering from a stroke.\par \par \uc2 At a rally in Pittsburgh, he called it one of the hardest things he has ever done.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN (D) (Pennsylvania Senate candidate): To be honest with you, doing that debate wasn`t exactly easy, you know?\par \par \uc2 I knew it. I knew it wasn`t going to be easy after having a stroke after five months, in fact --\par \par \uc2 (MAN shouting "We still love you.")\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: In fact -- in fact, I don`t think that has ever been done before in American political history before.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, concern in some quarters, also a lot of support. Fetterman says he has raised $2 million in the aftermath of that debate. It is an extremely close race and in most polling, he is essentially tied with his opponent, Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now to the economy and a troubling sign for homebuyers. The average interest rate for 30-year fixed mortgage is higher than it`s been in 21 years, 7.16 percent.\par \par \uc2 Mortgage rates are influenced by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 , which is expected to increase its interest rates again to fight inflation.\par \par \uc2 This morning, Nancy Chen looks at the impact on families.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Nate, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Those high rates are sending both buyers and sellers into mortgage sticker shock, as affordability is still a major challenge for many navigating this market.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Jessica and Jackie Fasolino have been searching for a house to call their own in northern New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 How much has the housing market changed during your search?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA FASOLINO (Home buyer): Immensely. It`s -- you can`t even compare.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: After being repeatedly outbid last year, that Fasolino`s are now being priced out because of soaring interest rates.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE FASOLINO (Home buyer): We want to have that home that we can call home that is ours.\par \par \uc2 Isn`t that the American dream, you know?\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Do you feel like that is being taken away from you?\par \par \uc2 JACKIE FASOLINO: A bit.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Rates have more than doubled since the start of the year, with the most popular home loan hitting an average rate of 7.16 percent this week, for the first time since 2001. That`s the fastest they`ve grown on record cooling the red hot housing market.\par \par \uc2 DARYL FAIRWEATHER: I think that we`ve been going through a correction. Interest rates were low for the whole entire pandemic, and that created a bit of a frenzy in the housing market.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: A mortgage payment on a median-priced home stands at about $2,500.00 a month. That`s about $750.00 more than the start of the year.\par \par \uc2 How high could these rates go?\par \par \uc2 DARYL FAIRWEATHER: The Fed likely isn`t going to reverse course until they see really strong conclusive evidence that inflation is turning around.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: For now, mortgage applications are down 42 percent from this time last year, as both buyers and sellers play the waiting game.\par \par \uc2 The Fasolino`s say they`ll stay in their apartment and start their search again next year.\par \par \uc2 JACKIE FASOLINO: We just aren`t ready for that home that we`ve always kind of seen ourselves having.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA FASOLINO: Which will be someday, just not today.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: While we were told that mortgage interest rates probably won`t drop until late next year, there are opportunities in all of this. Experts tell us, buyers should check with multiple lenders and assemble the right team so you`re ready when the opportunity does come up -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, got it, Nancy. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Police investigating Monday`s deadly school shooting in St. Louis say the suspect had the murder weapon taken away from him nine days before the attack, but somehow he got it back.\par \par \uc2 The gunman killed a student and a teacher at the high school where he graduated from last year. Seven other students were injured here. Police say that Orlando Harris had a history of mental illness. They say that his mother called police earlier this month, asking them to remove the AR-15 style rifle from the home.\par \par \uc2 Police say the weapon was given to someone known to the family and they still don`t know how the suspect got that weapon back. Harris was shot and killed by police after barricading himself in a classroom.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The man who drove his SUV through a Christmas parade in Waukesha, Wisconsin last year now faces life in prison. Darrell Brooks was convicted yesterday of six counts of intentional homicide and 61 counts of reckless endangerment, plus nine other charges.\par \par \uc2 Six people were killed and dozens more were injured in the attack.\par \par \uc2 We warn you the video is disturbing.\par \par \uc2 Roxana Saberi is outside the Courthouse in Waukesha just west of Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 Roxana, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Good morning, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Darrell Brooks represented himself in a three-week trial that saw him clash with the Judge on several occasions. In the end, the jury deliberated for just over three hours before reaching its verdict.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JUDGE JENNIFER R. DOROW (Waukesha County): We, the jury find the defendant, Darrell E. Brooks guilty of first-degree intentional homicide.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Darrell Brooks hung his head as the Judge read that 76 guilty verdicts.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Burn in hell you piece of [bleep].\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: One man was removed from Court after that outburst.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN OPPER (Waukesha County District Attorney): We are satisfied that this defendant has been held accountable for his actions.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The 40-year-old was convicted of driving his \uc2 Ford\uc2  Escape through a Christmas parade in Waukesha, Wisconsin on November 21st. He killed six people and wounded dozens more.\par \par \uc2 Ryan Kohnke`s niece, Jessalyn Torres was badly injured in the parade.\par \par \uc2 What do you think this decision will mean for the healing of families like yours and other families?\par \par \uc2 RYAN KOHNKE: I think it`s a huge step forward. You know, I think this gives everybody an opportunity to have some sort of closure.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Seventy-nine-year-old Virginia Sorenson was performing with her troupe The Dancing Grannies when she was killed.\par \par \uc2 After the verdict, her son Marshall recalled his mom`s favorite saying, "Angels watch over you."\par \par \uc2 MARSHALL SORENSON (Virginia Sorenson`s son): My five-year-old daughter came up to me and handed me this necklace with my mom`s ashes in it and she told me to take my mom with us for the sentencing, so she was with us today.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: The Judge set a date for -- set a hearing for Monday to schedule a date for sentencing. Brooks can appeal his convictions -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Roxana, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a sad anniversary to mark today. Four years since that deadly antisemitic shooting at the Tree of Life Synagogue in Pittsburgh. Eleven people were killed on that day during Shabbat morning services. Six more were injured. It`s the deadliest attack, believe it or not, on Jews in US history in this country, at least.\par \par \uc2 As we remember the victims and reflect on the hate that fueled the massacre, Elaine Quijano spoke with Tree of Life`s Rabbi, that`s Jeffrey Myers. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: How did you begin to sort of process the events that took place and to lead your fellow community members in that process of healing?\par \par \uc2 RABBI JEFFREY MYERS (Tree of Life Synagogue): To make sense of the senseless, one cannot do, but to develop the right set of skills to be able to help my congregation heal, but at the same time, make sure I`m healing. It is complicated.\par \par \uc2 ELAINE QUIJANO: Eleven people lost their lives on that day. How are they being remembered? How should people remember them?\par \par \uc2 RABBI JEFFREY MYERS: The way to do it is to show loving kindness to your neighbor to show that we`re above that. That`s not how civilized human beings behave.\par \par \uc2 We offer deeds of loving kindness to help our neighbors, to improve our society, to make the world a better place. Those are the acts that we perform to remember the 11 victims.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rabbi Myers has also said Tree of Life is planning an expansion to include a Memorial and also a museum with the goal of teaching visitors to combat antisemitism and other forms of hatred or bigotry. We had Rabbi Buchdahl here yesterday. She pointed out that Jews are only six million people in this country, it is 2.4 percent of the population, it is a sliver of the population, but more than 50 percent of the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has officially recorded hate crime victims.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, she was pointing out yesterday that you know, 15 million Jewish people in the world. Kanye West has more \uc2 Twitter\uc2  followers than that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s something that you should think about.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very unfortunate.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To hear more Elaine`s conversation with Rabbi Myers, you can go listen to today`s CBS MORNINGS podcast.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This morning, we have a very revealing look at the war in Ukraine from an American volunteer who fought in it.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s armies preparing to try and recapture the key southern city of Kherson and fighting off Russian attacks in the Northeast.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine`s President says Russian artillery strikes are "driving people to their deaths."\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams is in Kyiv, that`s Ukraine`s capital city with more on the story.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning. We`ve been reporting on this war now for over eight months and it is extremely rare to get an unvarnished account of what it`s like at the tip of the spear as Ukraine battles Russia`s invasion.\par \par \uc2 But that`s exactly what we heard from an American who volunteered to fight with Ukrainian forces and we should warn you that his interview contains some disturbing material.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELVIS (American volunteer in Ukraine): You could hear shooting, but then you could also hear grunts and people like fighting to the death with their bare hands. It`s very unique sound when you hear that many death rattles.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: He is from Tennessee, he told us and came to fight in Ukraine because he was horrified by the Russian invasion. He wants to be known only by his callsign, "Elvis."\par \par \uc2 The carnage you`re describing sounds like something out of World War Two.\par \par \uc2 ELVIS: Yes. This is nothing like any conflict in the past 70 to 80 years. This war is not sustainable for either side.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: On the frontline in Southern Ukraine, he says he repeatedly witnessed Russian forces using white phosphorus munitions.\par \par \uc2 ELVIS: It comes down extremely slow, but there is nothing you can do and everything it touches just incinerates --\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Including this incident.\par \par \uc2 ELVIS: Probably about 20 or 30 guys burning alive, and several gunshots because there is nothing else they do.\par \par \uc2 So, a lot of guys have suicide pistols and you`d hear them scream and then they would say goodbye, and then blow their heads off.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: So you actually heard 20 to 30 soldiers being burned alive by white phosphorus.\par \par \uc2 ELVIS: Yes, ma`am.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: And some of them committing suicide with their own pistols.\par \par \uc2 ELVIS: Yes, ma`am.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Elvis, that must have been horrific.\par \par \uc2 ELVIS: It is horror. At least that`s what I told myself.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: He admits that he is traumatized and fears one of his former comrades could commit suicide because of what they went through together.\par \par \uc2 Do you still think this is a righteous war?\par \par \uc2 ELVIS: Yes, absolutely. We`re fighting pure evil.\par \par \uc2 Anybody in the West ask Ukraine to just do peace talks, they need to go to these villages. They need to see what`s been done to these people. If China invaded the US, hypothetically leveled Los Angeles, leveled Seattle, Portland, massacred thousands, do you think the US would just sue for peace? No.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Russia has previously denied using white phosphorus munitions in Ukraine. Elvis told us that if other Americans are thinking of volunteering with Ukraine`s military, they should know that the odds are stacked against them, and they`ll be fighting for their lives -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Holly Williams, that was a powerful conversation -- eye opening and also heartbreaking. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, how a small Minnesota town could play a big role in the future of electric cars and why some people who live there want nothing to do with it.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up: \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  shows up at \uc2 Twitter\uc2 `s headquarters carrying an unexpected object, something that suggests he might finally at long last be ready to buy the social media giant has till Friday for the deal to close.\par \par \uc2 Also, we visit a town in Japan that is urgently looking for new residents. How it is trying to fill the empty spaces left by a shrinking population.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Still ahead: Our conversation with the dream team behind the hit series, "Bridgerton," showrunner Shonda Rhimes and her producing partner, Betsy Beers talks about creating an inclusive period drama and why the female perspective is so important to their story.\par \par \uc2 Your local news is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Back now at 7:30 here on CBS MORNINGS, and as electric vehicle sales take off, the White House is hoping that the batteries that power them will be made right here in America. The problem is, that is not the case right now generally speaking. Seventy-five percent of battery manufacturing done in China.\par \par \uc2 To change that, the Biden administration is awarding $2.8 billion in grants to a dozen States all in an effort to boost battery production. But all of those EV batteries require critical minerals, minerals like nickel.\par \par \uc2 Senior national and environmental correspondent, Ben Tracy is going to take us now to a Minnesota town that could play a very big role in our electric future and he finds out why some residents there are worried.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: In Tamarack, Minnesota, wuthering winds have made it hard to age gracefully. You`re likely to see more hay bales here than actual people, and the town`s heyday seems to have played out long ago.\par \par \uc2 But soon, the trains that rumble through town could be stopping to pick up loads of much needed metal, thanks to a massive deposit of nickel discovered underneath this unassuming home.\par \par \uc2 How big of a deal is a nickel deposit like this?\par \par \uc2 TODD MALAN\uc2  (\uc2 Talon Metals\uc2 ): This is a world class deposit.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: \uc2 Todd Malan\uc2  works for \uc2 Talon Metals\uc2 . His company hopes to turn 100 acres of farmland into the nation`s largest source of nickel, a mineral critical for electric car batteries.\par \par \uc2 Nickel makes the batteries last longer, so the cars go farther, and it is expected the world will need 20 times more of it by 2040.\par \par \uc2 Currently, the US produces less than one percent of world`s supply, leaving American EV makers reliant on supplies from places like Russia, China, Indonesia, and Australia.\par \par \uc2 TODD MALAN\uc2 : No country wants to be dependent on another country for a source of its energy.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: President Biden has put nickel on a list of minerals essential to national security and wants more of it mined domestically.\par \par \uc2 So this is your newest drill?\par \par \uc2 TODD MALAN\uc2 : Yes. It can drill about 8,000 feet, about a mile and a half underground.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Talon has drilled nearly 500 test holes, mapping them in 3D and sorting the core samples inside this warehouse in Tamarack.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN GOLDNER\uc2  (\uc2 Talon Metals\uc2 ` head geologist): This is about nine percent nickel.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Head geologist, \uc2 Brian Goldner\uc2  says the nickel they found here is some of the best in the world.\par \par \uc2 So when you guys drill down there and got a sample and you saw that, what were you thinking?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN GOLDER: We thought it was fake at first.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Talon hopes to open the Minnesota mine in 2026 when the only other US nickel mine is set to close.\par \par \uc2 It says it will create 300 jobs in the poorest county in the State. The company already has a deal to supply nickel to \uc2 Tesla\uc2 . Now, it`s up to the State of Minnesota to approve the mine and some people who live here have questions.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE BENJAMIN (Mille Lacs Band of Ojibwe chief executive): Where is the scientific data that says this is safe.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: Melanie Benjamin of the Mille Lacs Band of Ojibwe. Its tribal land is less than two miles from the proposed mine. She worries about pollution impacting fragile wetlands, where native tribes fish, hunt and have harvested wild rice in the water for generations.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE BENJAMIN: There is a spiritual connection to the water, to the plants, to the animals, to the land. It is going to devastate the land and the land may never come back from that devastation. That`s pretty scary.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: A group of homeowners worried about sulfuric acid runoff from the mind leaking into pristine waterways have banded together to fight the project.\par \par \uc2 Talon\uc2  says it will process the nickel out of State and says the deep underground mind poses little risks to the environment.\par \par \uc2 Do you believe any of that?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS ANDERSON (Tamarack Water Alliance): Asking Talon or asking any mining company about how they`re going to take care of the community and the environment is kind of like asking the fox how he is taking care of the chicken coop.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: And do you understand why people around here would be worried given the history of the mining industry, which is not particularly great.\par \par \uc2 TODD MALAN\uc2 : Absolutely. We totally understand the context and the history. We understand how precious this environment is.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: The need for mining like this raises an inconvenient truth. There is a dirty side to clean energy.\par \par \uc2 TODD MALAN\uc2 : So if you think about solar power, wind power batteries, all of those are dependent on metallic minerals. Instead of saying yes or no about mining operations, we should be transitioning the conversation about how and where.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: But if the where is where you live, that conversation is not so simple.\par \par \uc2 MELANIE BENJAMIN: When this mining is done, they`re going to pack up and leave and we`re going to be the ones left here.\par \par \uc2 BEN TRACY: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Ben Tracy in Tamarack, Minnesota.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up: Japan has one of the world`s lowest birth rates, but one family, they are trying to reverse that. See how they became famous for trying to stop the decline -- whoa.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whoa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: . in Japan`s population.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I couldn`t count how many children in that picture fast enough.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS. We thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Over the last 11 years, the number of people in Japan has been shrinking instead of growing. 2022 saw the biggest decline on record and according to some estimates, Japan`s population could be cut in half by the end of the century.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer looks at one region`s effort to grow and a family that is trying to reverse that trend, one child at a time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The shoes give it away. This isn`t your average Japanese home. Eleven people live here along with their dog, all origamied into a tiny house in Nagoya.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to the Yueda (sp?) family.\par \par \uc2 Mother of the brood, Ari (sp?) shows me around her kitchen and nope, there is no dishwasher. She tells me it is a huge amount of work. But she and her husband, "We love the big family."\par \par \uc2 (ARI YUEDA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: "Some people can`t or won`t have kids," she says, "But I can, and this country needs children, so I kept having more, nine of them in all."\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: The Yueda`s, one of Japan`s largest families have become reality TV stars. They`re overflowing home, and lively family outings are fascinating to a country that has one of the lowest birth rates in the world.\par \par \uc2 You can see it in rural areas where thousands of houses have simply been abandoned.\par \par \uc2 And this used to be a store --\par \par \uc2 Like the one Iyuta Yamashita (sp?) is showing me in Kamikawa, about 350 miles west of Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 It`s huge.\par \par \uc2 (IYUTA YAMASHITA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Back at the office, he has got dozens more at fire sale prices.\par \par \uc2 Yamashita`s job is enticing new residents to Kamikawa. Its selling points are plenty of space and natural beauty and government incentives.\par \par \uc2 (MAYOR SOGO YAMANA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Sogo Yamana is the Mayor.\par \par \uc2 (MAYOR SOGO YAMANA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: "These last six or seven years," he told me, "We are concentrating on attracting new residents of childbearing age."\par \par \uc2 One package is pitched to women who often need extra support, single moms.\par \par \uc2 Yoko Murata (sp?) moved here with her two boys in 2021.\par \par \uc2 (YOKO MURATA speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: "The town found me a job," she said, "A place to live and childcare, and while I was getting a driver`s license, the town staff even babysat for me."\par \par \uc2 People have become Kamikawa`s most precious resource and it is competing with communities across Japan to offset its graying population with young blood.\par \par \uc2 But rural areas no matter how lovely or generous face another problem besides overall declining population.\par \par \uc2 Mei (sp?) is the Yueda`s third eldest child and she stars on her own \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channel. She loves singing about country roads, but actually live on one? Not a chance. Like most Japanese, she is a city creature no matter how crowded.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Elizabeth Palmer in Kamikawa.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s what I call doing your part.\par \par \uc2 All right, up next, Vlad Duthiers has the stories that you will be talking about today.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:43. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Vlad, do you have any props like \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  walking into \uc2 Twitter\uc2  with a sink for some reason?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Let`s talk about \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . Yes, exactly because that`s where we`re going to begin.\par \par \uc2 After months of back and forth, Musk`s Court order deadline is tomorrow to close a $44 billion deal to take over \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . So, what Tony is talking about.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Musk posted this video of himself carrying a sink into the company`s San Francisco headquarters. He wrote: "Entering Twitter HQ. Let that sink in."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Why not an egg like "I`m the good egg." You know why, not a bowl like, "I am the bull in the China shop." Like why -- it`s such a weird thing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think it is fun, "Let it sink in." I think that`s fun.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, I mean --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But he asked someone on his team to go at \uc2 Home Depot\uc2  or Lowe`s, buy a sink.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did he? Did he?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, I don`t know. Did he just have that?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Or he just ripped it out of its foundation in the bathroom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I thought it was giving you all -- everything I have and the kitchen sink.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the kitchen sink.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Then I would buy \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That would have made sense. The problem is, it is bathroom sink.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, so here is the issue, I think, which is that a lot of people at \uc2 Twitter\uc2  are sort of worried about in the future, because according to Bloomberg, he denied a "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " report that says he plans to lay off 75 percent of the company`s workforce.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is denying that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He is denying that, but that has been reported that that`s what he told investors that he was planning to do.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: If the deal -- according to "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." If the deal is not done by 5:00 PM Eastern tomorrow, the Judge said she would schedule a trial for November.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He also changed his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  bio to call himself chief twit.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. So, there is a lot of --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do you like that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He likes to play with us.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So here are the implications, if those cuts are going to happen which \uc2 Twitter\uc2  was already looking into making cuts at the company.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re talking about a workforce of 7,500 people going down to 2,000 people. So those are people`s lives that are affected.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Down the drain.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tony loves the sink, and the other thing that I`ll point out is that it could mean problems for \uc2 Twitter\uc2  itself, the actual application.\par \par \uc2 How else if you don`t have the employees there to monitor pornography, misinformation, hateful speech? Those are the repercussions.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So five o`clock tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Five o`clock tomorrow, we`ll be here talking about it.\par \par \uc2 All right. Are you guys familiar with "The Rocky Horror Picture Show?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Have you been?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know nothing of it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, no, no. I`ve never been. No, no.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That will be the headline of the morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No. No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle King at a Rocky Horror premier.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I am familiar with it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You are familiar with it.\par \par \uc2 All right, so the longest running theatrical release in movie history is taking its campy fun on the road just in time for Halloween.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Rocky Horror Picture Show.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We all know "The Rocky Horror Picture Show." It still has an enormous cult following nearly 50 years after its release in 1975.\par \par \uc2 So there`s this interactive version of the unconventional rock musical that is now on a national tour with Brad Majors himself. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Rocky Horror Picture Show.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So that is actor Barry Bostwick at a tour stop in Sugarloaf, New York last night. You know how we got that exclusive video to CBS MORNINGS?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How? How?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Jon Tower`s wife Joanna shot that for us.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Always be reporting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Joanna Tower.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You send out family members to report for us to get exclusive.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whatever it takes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Whatever it takes.\par \par \uc2 So we spoke with Barry Bostwick about the phenomena`s generation of devoted fans. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BARRY BOSTWICK (Actor): There`s always new people coming in the door to the party who haven`t seen it, they just need to go out and just blow off steam, you know, let them recline as they say, and that`s what we`re offering them.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So you see Barry Bostwick, there is a younger self along with Susan Sarandon, who plays his fiancee when they arrive at this house of Dr. Frank N. Furter.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should say Jon Tower is was one of our big cheeses here because people at home are going, "Who is Jon Tower?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Who is Jon Tower? He is our \uc2 CBS\uc2  broadcast producer?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s his wife.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He`s the guy in my ear who is like "Wrap it up, Vlad. We`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But Vlad, if I had to guess, I bet, you`ve been to a Rocky Horror --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve been several times.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I knew it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle laughs.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I knew it. I knew it. I knew it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I went -- my first time in high school in Provo, New York. It was amazing and I`ve been back at least two times ever since. It`s really cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are not surprised, are we?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is exciting.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no, we are not.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well, tickets for the tour available online. Go check it out.\par \par \uc2 All right, last thing we want to talk about. We have a first look this morning at the cover of Prince Harry`s memoirs. Did you guys know this before you came over to the desk?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I did see it --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right, there is the cover.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the title.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Highly anticipated, it is called, "Spare."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Spare." I love this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The air in the spare.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There you go.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a great picture of Harry.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: This is promising.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Gayle, what do you know? What have you heard? This book is going to be a bombshell.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m just saying, I think that`s a great picture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You got the inside scoop. Talk to us, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, I do not. They confide me about nothing. But I do like that picture.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The picture is really cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that title.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But you know, it was supposed to be out before Christmas.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But now, it is not coming out until after Christmas.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, as you know, Vlad. It is very hard to write all those words behind the cover.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Oh, zing me, Tony. Tony zinging me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Vlad is working on a book.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`m trying. I`m trying.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad, we appreciate you. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, coming up: The legendary Whoopi Goldberg is here to talk about her new movie "Till" along with the film`s writer director, Chinonye Chukwu.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Send Vlad to Book Love.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil and that`s Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: The women behind the powerful new drama "Till," including Oscar winner Whoopi Goldberg and director Chinonye Chukwu. The film focuses on Emmett Till`s mother, who really did change the world.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Katie Couric revealed a tough diagnosis recently, breast cancer. She is going to be here to talk about her own battle and the message she wants to share.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`ll take you behind the scenes of the smash hit show, "Bridgerton" with the great Shonda Rhimes and her producing partner, Betsey Beers.\par \par \uc2 We begin this hour with a new look at a key moment in the Civil Rights movement.\par \par \uc2 Many Americans know the story of Emmett Till, the 14-year-old Black boy who was brutally murdered in Mississippi in 1955.\par \par \uc2 He was visiting from Chicago and accused of whistling at a White woman. Two White men were acquitted of the crime with an all-White jury of course.\par \par \uc2 They later admitted to the killing.\par \par \uc2 A new film, "Till," it focuses on one of the most important, but often overlooked people in the story, Emmett`s mother, Mamie.\par \par \uc2 The movie follows her efforts to draw attention to what happened to her son who she called Bo.\par \par \uc2 It also features her work as a Civil Rights activist after his death. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Till.")\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re joined by producer, Whoopi Goldberg who plays Mamie`s mother, that`s Emmett`s grandmother, and the film`s co-writer and director, Chinonye Chukwu.\par \par \uc2 How are you doing? Good morning.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG ("Till" actor and producer): Good. Nice to be hear.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU ("Till" director): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Now, Whoopi, we hear that you have worked to get this film on the big screen for more than 20 years.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Well, that`s actually the originator of trying to get it up. His name is Keith Beauchamp.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: And he is the reason that we know as much as we know, because he spent much time with Mamie. And also he created the documentary that got the case reopened.\par \par \uc2 So, this has been -- he has been on this to get this done. He wanted to get it done before Mrs. Till passed, but we weren`t able to get that done. But I`m on it 11 years trying to get it done.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We said in the introduction that many Americans know the story of Emmett Till, but the reality is many Americans do not.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How do you hope that this movie changes their understanding of the case?\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: I think most of us don`t know about Mamie Till-Mobley and who she was and how she was a catalyst to the modern American Civil Rights Movement. Without Mamie Till-Mobley, the world wouldn`t know who Emmett Till was, and so she is the heartbeat of the story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: And so watching this film, there is so much to learn about her and all the people who are part of the story like Dr. T.R.M. Howard and Myrlie Evers, Medgar Evers --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Medgar Evers, yes. I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: You know, the community of Mount Bayou, et cetera, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know what you guys did so beautifully is you humanized Emmett. We saw him as a young teenage boy. It opens with him and his mom singing in the car. It shows that he liked to play. It shows that he was a playful really great kid.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Till.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I think that that`s so important to see the humanity of both of these characters. That said, it was so difficult, Chinonye, to watch. It`s so difficult and so painful.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve heard people say, "I don`t think I can watch it." Why do you think it`s important that people look at this movie?\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Well, firstly, it`s important for people to know that this movie was made with care, you know, and care for the story, care for audiences, you know, that there`s an intention behind not showing the physical violence on screen and --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We never actually saw the violence.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Never actually saw the violence and even when we do see Emmett`s body, the camera, it does not objectify him. It`s not their voyeuristic lens, but it`s to capture the love and connection between Mamie and her child.\par \par \uc2 And so this -- and I will also say, you know, even though there are moments of sadness and deep pain, there is also joy.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: And community.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was what was so beautiful about it.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: And love.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because we got to see that -- she was his only child, number one.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t know that. We got to see the deep love and Whoopi, you play the grandmother. Clearly, you`re acting chops are very much well alive and in living color.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you play such a crucial role because mom didn`t want him to go. Mamie didn`t want him to go and his grandmother said, Oh, let the boy go. It`ll be a good experience.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes. And I don`t think she ever got over that.\par \par \uc2 But you know what this movie is? This is -- you know, how you learn through film. You learn about what happened in the world, you know, and for us, it`s not that you don`t want to see it, you do want to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I think you need to see it.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: You have to see it, but what you have to remember is that unless you come and take part in this, you can`t fix it because this is the -- this is exactly what racism at its absolute worse, the systemic racism looks like.\par \par \uc2 It allows people to kill your children. It allows you to be dehumanized as a person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: I mean, the reason we`re talking about the fact that, you know, Emmett has been humanized is because people don`t think of him. They think of him as a picture in a magazine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In that casket, yes.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: That`s it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Of course.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: But this --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A horrible, grotesque picture.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: But when you realize this was a little kid --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Somebody`s baby.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Somebody`s baby.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Somebody`s baby --\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: You know --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Which she said repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your shot choices, I thought, were exquisite. We see Mamie`s eyes close up. When we first see the body after we know what`s happened, you deliberately don`t -- I wonder, was it deliberate? Because we didn`t see it and I sat there bracing myself thinking, "I don`t want to see it."\par \par \uc2 It was just Daniel`s face, who was brilliant, by the way.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But then later in the funeral home, you did show it? What was your thinking on that? Was that a deliberate choice?\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: It was a deliberate choice. You know, I wanted this film to stay within Mamie`s emotional point of view.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Got it.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: And so -- and her emotional experience of seeing her son`s body. So, that`s why when he --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Touching her son`s body.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Touching her son`s body, connecting with her son`s body. And so that is what the scene was about. And so I wanted the camera to capture that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Chinonye, you talked about not being in the right mental space, or hoping that you could be in the right mental space after being involved in this project. What type of energy are you left with?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Because these projects change you, right? I mean, working this closely on a story like this, it`s got to alter you.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Yes. I mean, I did not know much about the story that I thought I knew a lot about, you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Tony`s point is right. You know, so many people think they know this thing.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: They have no idea. And you`re just learning more about who Mamie, like she already was a she-ro, but then just really understanding her story. Yes, it is transformative.\par \par \uc2 And also, I didn`t know about Dr. T.R.M. Howard --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Before going on this journey.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Me neither.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: I mean -- and I think he`s an unsung hero.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Gay; You know, who was brilliant is Danielle. Tell us about this actor, Danielle, Whoopi. We should go get her Oscar already because she for sure is going to be nominated.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: This woman --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who plays Mamie.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Her performance.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: . is transformative.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: You`ve never seen anything like this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I agree.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: For me, it is just -- watching her was heaven, you know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did you know Chinonye was the right director for this project?\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Because she said the stuff that Barbara Broccoli and Fred Solo (sp?) and Keith Beauchamp.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Barbara Broccoli of "James Bond?"\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes, yes, yes. She is one of our other --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Executive producers.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Executive producers and we looked long and hard. We`ve been looking for a long time for somebody. And Chinonye came in and said exactly the stuff we had been talking about, because we felt that if we were to show stuff that people already believe they see when they see it, it`s not -- it doesn`t convey what we need, and it needed to come from Mamie and so the second thing she said, well, it needs to be told from the mother`s point of view.\par \par \uc2 We were like --\par \par \uc2 And then she said -- and I don`t really want to show the violence --\par \par \uc2 It was like --\par \par \uc2 So you`re going to do it. You remember how fast we said, "you`re it," right? Because it was like that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Because, really this is a way to learn the way you know we`ve learned from other horrific things in history. Anne Frank.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: That is how we learned about the Holocaust was through her, through this kid. We want people to learn what systemic racism, what the outcome of systemic racism --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Through the lens of a woman who lost the most precious thing that she has.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Through a woman who brought as much dignity to an undignified --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Unspeakable tragedy.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Unspeakable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I still think about her decision to allow us to see it where she said, "I want them to see."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How powerful that was.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, Nate.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: They need to know what they did to my child.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How needed it was.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: And she was right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Because people wouldn`t have believed it.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: You know? Just like they don`t believe it now when you say this is what happened.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: People say, "Oh, yes, okay."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the men -- the men eventually were found guilty and they were -- they admitted that -- they were acquitted and then they admitted later in an interview -- yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. They admitted it.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, we did it.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And they`re paid $4,000.00. So egregious.\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All the way around.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Whoopi Goldberg --\par \par \uc2 WHOOPI GOLDBERG: You know, there is more.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Chinonye Chukwu, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CHINONYE CHUKWU: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate your presence. And this film, "Till" will be in theaters everywhere tomorrow. Make sure you check it out. It`s a must.\par \par \uc2 Right now, it is 8:10. Time to check a local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up in just a moment, award-winning journalist, Katie Couric will tell us about her breast cancer diagnosis and share a very important message. There is Katie. There is the fruit for you. Stick around, people.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: October is Breast Cancer Awareness Month, we all know that. Our next guest is sharing her own journey with this disease.\par \par \uc2 Katie Couric is an award-winning journalist with more than 40 years of experience. In September, she shared the news that she was diagnosed with stage one breast cancer. She documented the screening process and caught the moment her doctor found something of concern.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC (Journalist): So in addition to getting a mammogram, I also get a breast ultrasound.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: On ultrasound, what I saw was a mass and that mass had irregular margins and I looked at you and I looked at the person who was imaging us and I said I think we really need to turn the camera off. I want to speak frankly.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Katie Couric joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Katie, take us to that moment, because it`s one of -- any woman who has been through this, this is the worst process -- this is the worst moment for the doctors to say, "Wait, we need to talk about this."\par \par \uc2 So did you go -- were you documenting it because you felt something or because it was just a regular -- you thought it was going to be a regular screening and you just wanted to take viewers inside?\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: Yes, you know, hi, Gayle, first of all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Hi.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: You know, I wanted to remind people, millions of women, I think put off their mammograms because of the pandemic. And as a result, many breast cancers and other cancers for that matter being diagnosed much later when they`re harder to treat.\par \par \uc2 So I really went in to not only remind women to get screened, but I also wanted to talk about dense breasts because in addition to mammography, I always get a breast ultrasound. So when she said, "Turn the camera off," my producer was videotaping it on my iPhone. I wanted to say, "No, let`s keep rolling. It`s okay. I`ve been in this world for many years, I`m fine having you tell me whatever you need to tell me."\par \par \uc2 But she insisted that we turn it off, and the next day told me that I in fact, had stage -- an early breast cancer that she thought could be treated but we needed to come up with a plan.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Katie, I hear you talking about it as a TV person, as a person producing you know, the screening; but as a human being when your doctor, you know, their tone of voice changes, their facial expression changes and they say "Turn the camera off." I mean, my heart would have -- my stomach would have sunk to my knees. How were you dealing with it there emotionally?\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: Well, you know, I think my heart stopped, but I -- you know, for some reason, Tony, I just felt like it was probably going to be early. I don`t know why. But it was scary. And it was scary waiting for the results of the biopsy, which she called me about the next day.\par \par \uc2 I have had so much experience with cancer and when my husband, Jay, was diagnosed with colon cancer, it was stage four, it was metastatic. When my sister Emily two years later was diagnosed with pancreatic cancer, it was stage four\par \par \uc2 So I was hopeful and optimistic that if it was something that we caught it early enough and that in fact was the case, but of course, it is nerve racking. But, you know, it was not my first rodeo. And I think I had enough background and experience in cancer that I wasn`t totally freaked out, to be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Katie, you mentioned how the pandemic like many other men and women has caused us to miss scheduled health checks. Have you always prioritized your health? And if so, what is your advice? Because there`s people sitting at home, they really don`t think about it. They say, Oh, I`ve got time. I will wait.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I will get to it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll get around to it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: Well, yes. I`ve always been extremely vigilant about my health, especially after my husband died and I became a single mom and my daughters were six and two. I`ve been Uber on top of it.\par \par \uc2 But for some reason, I think the pandemic sort of got us off schedule, and messed with our sense of time, and I was sent my gynecologist and she said, you`re due for a mammogram and I said, "Are you kidding? I just had one." And she said, "Actually, no, you`re six months late." So my breast radiologist recommends A., scheduling your mammogram and when you leave the office, scheduling the one for next year. So you`re on the book.\par \par \uc2 So, even if you have to change it, you`re not going to forget about following through, but really what I wanted to do by going public with this was to help women understand that if they`re among the 45 percent of women with dense breast from 40 and up, they need to A., find out because it`s only indicated on a mammogram. And then they need to talk to their doctor about follow-up screening.\par \par \uc2 In my case, I get a breast ultrasound. I know Gayle does, too. But a lot of women don`t know this and 38 states require doctors to inform patients that they have dense breasts, but they don`t really give them any information about what to do with that.\par \par \uc2 So the FDA is actually changing their policy guidelines and I think by the end of the year, all healthcare providers will be required to not only inform their patients, but tell them what their options are and what they should do to follow up because unfortunately, even 3D mammograms which are far superior to standard mammograms aren`t really always that great at detecting tumors.\par \par \uc2 If you have dense breasts, my radiologist said it`s like finding a snowball against a backdrop of a field of snow because it all comes out white. So a lot of women, almost half need additional screening.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t know. That`s why Katie, it`s so important that you`re talking about it. You`re right. I too, have dense breasts. We`re a member of a very big group and it is important that we do the right thing.\par \par \uc2 I have them send me reminder notices.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Katie Couric. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 KATIE COURIC: Thank you, all.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you. A reminder, women should start getting regular mammography screening at age 50. Now, the option to start at age 40.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Time now to bring you some of the stories that are "Talk of the Table" this morning.\par \par \uc2 Miss King, you`re up first.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right. My "Talk of the Table" is the world premiere of "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever." I don`t know why you can`t just say the words without doing it, but you really can`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can`t do it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Wakanda Forever."\par \par \uc2 It was a star-studded red carpet last night in LA as the cast and crew came out to celebrate this new movie. There was excitement about the sequel with the late Chadwick Boseman, who starred in 2018`s original blockbuster -- remember that -- was on everybody`s mind, of course.\par \par \uc2 Angela Bassett, who plays his mom talked about visiting his resting place with the cast and crew before shooting of the new movie began.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ANGELA BASSETT ("Black Panther: Wakanda Forever" actor): It was wonderful that that our producer, you know, Nate Moore and our director, Ryan Coogler, they had arranged a flight, you know, a plane to take us to Chadwick`s resting place. So, we were all able to be there.\par \par \uc2 Those of us who were starting to project the first days were able to do that to pay, to give love, and to feel his spirit and to stand there with him before we did one frame of anything.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Angela Bassett, just another example of Black don`t crack. We say it all the time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just spill your line, Nate. You said, "She`s fine, fine."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She`s fine, fine.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She really is.\par \par \uc2 The other big thing about this premiere last night, they were talking about that it is one of the best red carpets I`ve ever seen, because this cast and crew, take a look at some of these pictures, pulled out all the stops.\par \par \uc2 That`s Danai.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Stunning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at Michael B. Jordan. I could see you, Nate, in that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nate, he has handled that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ll try.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Winston Duke, who by the way guys, we are doing him on Monday. He`s going to be here.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh cool.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Angela Bassett.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Look at these outfits, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. That`s what I said.\par \par \uc2 I know, they said it was probably one of the most high fashion red carpet events that LA has seen in a very long time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, I`m just so excited to see the movie. We all went back in 2018.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I am very excited to see it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait to see it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`re excited because the trailers are awesome. But you know, there`s always a surprise.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, there is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is something that they haven`t shown us in a trailer that we will see when we go watch the movie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Rihanna doing the music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Rihanna.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So my "Talk of the Table" inspired by Halloween. I want to talk about a study and I`m going to put "studies" here, about what to do in the event of a zombie apocalypse. You see it in horror movie, if it comes real, "researchers" again had analyzed 26 different factors to measure zombie preparedness, looked at number of people already in poor health in the community, the number of local supermarkets, hunting supply stores.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they came to the conclusion that in the event of a zombie apocalypse, you`re going to want to go to Orlando.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Florida?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. You want to go to Orlando. It is the best place. In fact, three cities from Florida are in the top 10. Miami and Tampa are --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why though?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because of those factors, Nate. The researchers looked at it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of all of those -- okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And you wanted to share this with us why, Tony? Why?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think that the researchers are actually a hundred percent wrong on this being a Florida boy, you know, in the event of a zombie apocalypse there`s not going to be air conditioning and you don`t want to be anywhere near Florida without air conditioning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nate, look at how he`s saying this was a straight face. Zombie apocalypse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He is just saying it. People think about this stuff, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You do?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve thought about it before.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where would you go --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Klaus, have you ever thought about zombie -- Patty?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where would you go? Where would you go?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clark? Clark says he has.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Where would you go --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Clark is different.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: If zombies took over the world, where would you go, Gayle?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where would you go? Where would you hide? Oprah`s house.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I would feel very -- very good. I feel very safe in Oprah`s house.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony always thinking the same thing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She`s got a big closet with a lot of guns in it like in the movies, you know. We are ready.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She doesn`t have guns in the closet, but it would be very safe, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The security is --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a good idea, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is a bunker under there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 Oh, man. Okay, so my "Talk of the Table" inspired by Halloween as well is the seemingly normal decorated door, right, that turns into a Halloween nightmare when trick or treaters come around. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Greg Dietzenbach/\uc2 YouTube\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So you can see the so called "Monstdoor." It turned into a giant purple creature with fuzzy arms and glowing eyes. This is Greg Dietzenbach.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is so cool.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He says that he started planning the trick door at his house after his children basically said they don`t need his help with costumes. So he said okay, I`m going to take my creativity, grabbed some cardboard, foam, broom handles and a bunch of fur and make this door come to life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Greg.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, shoutout to him, man. He says it took them about 10 days. That`s the type of houses you love to go to as a kid.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because it is like a core memory that will last forever.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, for sure. Yes, I hope --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Greg is one of those cool dads. I like that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I hope he won his kids respect.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: For calling him a cool dad on TV.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he did. You go, Greg.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to Shonda Rhimes and Betsy Beers. They are the Dream Team behind some of the most iconic shows on TV. What did they do? How about this list?\par \par \uc2 They developed and produced the hit series "Grey`s Anatomy," heard of it? Yes. "Scandal," heard of it? Yes. "How to Get Away with Murder," Heard of that, too.\par \par \uc2 The shows broke barriers for their casting and their great storytelling, and helped actors like Ellen Pompeo and Kerry Washington and Viola Davis break into stardom.\par \par \uc2 In 2020, the regency era drama "Bridgerton" became number one on Netflix. Here`s a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bridgerton.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you`re burning because that`s what Simon used to say, "I burn for you." I like that.\par \par \uc2 In the new book "Inside Bridgerton," Shonda Rhimes --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The TV producer behind the hit series -- you know, that`s good. It is published by an imprint of \uc2 Simon & Schuster\uc2 , which like \uc2 CBS\uc2  is part of \uc2 Paramount Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So Nate and I talked to them earlier this week. Tony was on assignment, but Nate and I were still here. So then, we talked to them about creating the phenomenon and how it started with Julia Quinn`s "Bridgerton" novels.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Let`s just go briefly back to the beginning, Shonda, because this series came, you`re sick, you`re in a hotel room, 2017. You pick up this book, and you think what?\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES ("Inside Bridgerton" author): I pick up this book, it`s a romance novel. I just needed something to read because I wasn`t feeling well and I couldn`t put it down, and I was sick. I mean, it was good enough that I got up out of my sick bed in a hotel room and went to buy the second book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I mean, and then she reaches out to you Betsy and you said, "Romance novel?"\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS ("Bridgerton" executive producer): That`s exactly what I said.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You hadn`t read a romance novel since you were what?\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: Probably my teens.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A teenager.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: In my teens -- and I said, "Are you sure you`re feeling okay?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: And I kept saying just read it just read and then everybody would read it. And they got what I got, which was exciting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And what did you get?\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: You know what was interesting, it didn`t -- it felt like it was about me, or about any woman like I could see myself in the characters. Even though we`re talking about Regency England, even though originally all the characters were White. It was a world that I wanted to inhabit, this amazing world of the time and the marriage mart and all of these gowns, and just the romance of it all felt strong.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know what I like? I like how you all handle sex, because during that time period, number one, it seems like sex would be a lot of work just going to all those costumes, all of the -- undo this, take this down.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: The clothes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It just seems like a lot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I see an hour once you get undressed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, see you in an hour. But you`ve locked on to something called the feminine gaze, Betsy, that I really like because it is sexual and steamy with and --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: From a woman`s perspective.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: From a woman`s point of view.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Which is good. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Listen, they had one scene with the spoon with -- I thought, God, I wish I could be a spoon and it is just him just licking on a spoon.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Bridgerton.")\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: I think we actually -- that`s one of the great things we cover in the book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I know.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: Specifically, that is a moment.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was not alone in that thinking.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: You were not. You are absolutely not alone in that thinking. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: And it does feel like women have the right to have that gaze and have that gaze seen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What is a feminine gaze though, Shonda Rhimes?\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: It`s really sort of looking at all of these sexual scenes from the point of view of a woman. Usually, you see scenes from the point of view of a man.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: You see their clothes -- women`s clothes or off, there are a million shots of their bodies. It`s not about what the woman`s experience is, or what the woman is saying. It`s about some male fantasy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: We want it to go the other way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, and you mentioned earlier about the original characters being White and it is rare to see people of color. Why was that important to make sure that that was put in "Bridgerton?"\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: I mean, I`m not going to make a show that doesn`t include me in it, somehow, number one; but two, you know, historically, there`s been so much erasure, we talked about this, of people of color in that time period. There were Black composers, there were Black artists, there were all of these people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: And we wanted to have a chance to tell that story. And so, we went from the idea: What if Queen Charlotte, as has been said in history really is biracial, and told the story from there?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You said you became obsessed with Queen Charlotte.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: I am obsessed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because why?\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: I did -- we just made a show about her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: She is glamorous and glorious, but also, you know, as played by Golda is probably -- I mean, I always call it the Beyonce of the show. There`s nobody like her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nothing bad about Beyonce ever.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: Nothing bad about Beyonce.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Not a bad comparison.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: She also has the best wigs.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: I think in the history of wig-dom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s what you all write about. The thing that I also like about this book, may I mention it again? "Inside Bridgerton." You all, it`s like you took every component of the show, the costumes, the food, the styling --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The writers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The writers, the dancing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You wanted to do a love letter to the crew.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: Yes, and I think so many people don`t really understand what it takes to make a show this big and hopefully people will, you know, read this book and read the behind-the-scenes and see these photos and go back to the show and watch it again and see it in an entirely different light.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s about the "Bridgerton" siblings. This is what I learned in the book, that they`re all in alphabetical order.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Can you give us a quick tick tock. I never realized that.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: Anthony, Benedict, Colin, Daphne, Eloise, Francesca, and Gregory, Hyacinth.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I want to talk about the beginning of something, the beginning of your relationship.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You guys have known each other 20 years, about that?\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: Twenty years.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In the first project you guys worked on together, it didn`t take off.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: We wrote a pilot about war correspondents. That was our first thing and neither one of us had worked in television before.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: No.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: I was obsessed with journalism. I wrote this pilot about our correspondents, and it didn`t get made. So then the next time around, the next season, we thought, well, what should we make? And we both were obsessed with surgeries and medical stuff and we knew that they were looking for a medical show, but we also liked this idea of these incredibly competitive women like in their workplace.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: I never really understood medical shows.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: So, when we were excited about the idea, I said, I love medical shows, but I never understand what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Make it digestible.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: There are these people called interns and they don`t know what they`re doing either.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BETSY BEERS: It was the most amazingly wonderful way in for a medical show. And so Shonda wrote this incredible pilot and --\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: We made it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re talking "Grey`s Anatomy" in 2005.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Which is now the longest running primetime medical drama in TV.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In TV history.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There she goes, showing off again, Nate.\par \par \uc2 Of all your hits, Shonda, I know, it`d be like picking your favorite child. You`ve got three gorgeous daughters.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s always hard to pick a favorite, but is there one that holds a special place because I look at Grace and I look at "Scandal?"\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: I mean, I think I love them all, but my "Scandal" family has such a place in my heart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: I`ve learned so much by then that I could actually enjoy the process as opposed to just having my head down and working. So I got to have a lot of fun on "Scandal."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES: And I really enjoyed that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And guess what? Shonda Rhimes and her partner, they are still having fun. "Inside Bridgerton" is on sale now. And she says in Shondaland, you don`t always have happy endings, but in "Bridgerton" with the eight siblings, they take each sibling, tell their story and she wants them all to have a happy ending.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She can reel off the names like they were her own family.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s nice.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Like they were real people. That`s amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, so your favorite scene is the spoon scene?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You know, I like that`s the spoon scene.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hide the spoons.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re not the only one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`m not going to say nothing else about it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It looked pretty good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up next --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I see what you`re trying to do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How some very small homes are changing lives and helping at risk youth see their future in an entirely new way.\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of the local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . adults who age out of foster care.\par \par \uc2 Omar Villafranca has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAXIANA BARRY (20 years old): Here you go, ma`am.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: For the first time in her life, 20-year-old, Daxiana Barry (sp?) has a steady job and for the last two months, a steady place to call home. Two things that were unthinkable just a few years ago, after spending most of her life in Oklahoma`s foster care system.\par \par \uc2 DAXIANA BARRY: It is very important to me because growing up, I never had anything to myself, you know? I was taken away from my parents when I was like three months.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: While Barry takes orders, she is getting her life in order with the help of Pivot, the Oklahoma City nonprofit provides young people with a little house to live in as they start their journey into adulthood.\par \par \uc2 DAXIANA BARRY: I`m very appreciative of what I have right now, what I was provided with because I didn`t have anything.\par \par \uc2 When I first got here I didn`t have any clothes, I didn`t have any food, I didn`t have, you know anything really.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Many of the residents were homeless like bury or aged out of the foster care system at 18.\par \par \uc2 The Little House Project started four years ago with three cozy cottages. But as the need grew, the program expanded. Now, Pivot has 26 little houses on over 12 acres of land. Built with the help of private donations along with State and Federal grants.\par \par \uc2 Residents initially pay $100.0 a month for a home of around 300 square feet with a living space, a kitchen, and the bathroom.\par \par \uc2 Tenants can`t have overnight guests and have to keep the place clean.\par \par \uc2 Pivot CEO Jennifer Goodrich says the goal is to get residents ready for the real world in two years by helping them find jobs, get counseling and plan for a better tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER GOODRICH (Pivot CEO): It is essential because if you don`t know where you`re going, you can`t get there. Right? We give them voice and choice like what do you want it to be? But a lot of times they`re not aware of what are the steps to get down that path.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Government data shows, nearly 4,000 people sleep on Oklahoma`s streets every day. Almost 300 of them are between 18 and 24 years old.\par \par \uc2 Carter Batcher (sp?) was 16 when he went into foster care, then bounced around shelters when he turned 18. He was the first person to enter Pivot`s Little House Program.\par \par \uc2 CARTER BATCHER (First to enter Pivot`s Little House Program.): It was like the whole world was on my shoulders, and finally somebody just kind of lifted it just a little bit and I can breathe easier and I wouldn`t have to worry about where I was going to sleep that night or if someone was going to steal my stuff and I could just go to sleep peacefully for the first time in what felt like years.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: Barry is learning the basics and building a big future from her little home.\par \par \uc2 DAXIANA BARRY: I think this is an opportunity that I don`t think anybody should pass up.\par \par \uc2 OMAR VILLAFRANCA: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Omar Villafranca in Oklahoma City.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Seems like a pretty good idea.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What a great idea.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, it seems like a pretty good idea. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Great idea. Right. For some people, when you turn 18, you`re excited. I`m an adult.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can go see the world. For other people, it is probably the worst time of their lives.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s like, "Oh no."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Oh no."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes. When you age out of the system, there is nowhere to go crash back to.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It just give them a place to be.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just to feel safe. We put -- we take it for granted that you go home, just that you have a place to go so they have someplace to go and feel safe is huge.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well said.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nicely done, Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, we always like to leave you with something here on CBS MORNINGS. I`m thinking you`ve got good movie recommendations from Whoopi.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You got medical -- great medical advice about breast cancer and dense breasts from Katie Couric.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Very, very important.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, yesterday, I yelled out "Happy birthday" to my wife. She said, "You rushed it" and I said well today, "Happy Anniversary." My other kid, today is her anniversary.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Really?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow. Back to back days.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Daily pace.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey, Gayle --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That does it for us. We will see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS. Put that spoon away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, Nate got --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221027eiar000dx\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Football-Star-Turned-Senate Hopeful \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Now Facing Accusations From A Second Woman\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Nikole Killion \par \uc2 913 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Tonight, the breaking news that could affect control of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , with the midterms now less than two weeks away.\par \par \uc2 Football-star-turned-Senate hopeful \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  now facing accusations from a second woman. She says the anti-abortion rights candidate drove her to a clinic and paid for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 And in Pennsylvania--\par \par \uc2 MAN: Good evening, candidates.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: -- voters react to \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Dr. Oz`s only debate, as Democrats worry about the stroke survivor`s performance.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Mr. Fetterman really struggled. It was painful to watch.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Breaking news: the massive drug bust, hundreds of thousands of fentanyl pills, more than 1,000 pounds of meth, plus cocaine and heroin taken off the streets. Tonight, how many lives may have been saved?\par \par \uc2 Children`s hospitals overwhelmed, pediatric beds at capacity, as the country sees a dramatic rise in RSV. We speak with an emergency room doctor.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: How concerned are you about a long winter of illness?\par \par \uc2 DR. SARAH COMBS (Children`s National Hospital): I`m concerned.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: And Putin watches his troops` nuclear drills. \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Holly Williams gets reaction from inside Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: This is the \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS with Norah O`Donnell, reporting from the nation`s capital.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Good evening, and thank you for joining us on this busy Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, there are major developments that could potentially shake up the midterm elections in two key political background -- battlegrounds.\par \par \uc2 The breaking news from Georgia, Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is denying new accusations that he paid for another woman to have an abortion three decades ago. The accuser says she is afraid of retaliation and wants to remain anonymous. And, in Pennsylvania, there is concern tonight from Democrats after the Senate debate between \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 The race is a toss-up. And Fetterman`s performance last night is raising new questions about Fetterman`s recovery from a stroke. These races are important because they could decide the balance of power in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Our latest Battleground Tracker shows Republicans now have a slight advantage, though at least five races are toss-ups. As America decides, we have team coverage of those crucial races in Georgia and Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And we`re going to begin with \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Nikole Killion.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Nikole.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good evening, Norah.\par \par \uc2 This new claim comes as \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is in a dead heat with Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock and amid record early voting turnout in Georgia. Walker recently told me he was trying to move forward with his campaign. Now he`s defending another allegation that he says are lies.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GLORIA ALLRED (Attorney): And to me.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voiceover): In a virtual news conference, attorney Gloria Allred introduced Jane Doe, concealing the identity of a woman who claims \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  drove her to an abortion clinic in the spring of 1993 after learning she was pregnant.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE (\uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  Accuser): He encouraged me to have an abortion and gave me the money to do so.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The woman described a six-year relationship with Walker while he was still married and playing in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . She said she tried to get the abortion herself, but couldn`t go through with it and says he took her to a Dallas area clinic the next day and waited for her while she got the procedure.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE: I was so traumatized by what Herschel had put me through.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Allred shared evidence from her client, including cards purporting to be from Walker, as well as a photo and recording of him as proof of their relationship.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I wanted to say I love you and I was thinking about you.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The woman called the GOP Senate nominee, who is staunchly anti-abortion, a hypocrite and said she was not coming forward for political reasons.\par \par \uc2 JANE DOE: I do not believe that Herschel is morally fit to be a U.S. senator.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R-Georgia Senatorial Candidate): I`m done with this foolishness.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Walker played down the allegations on the campaign trail, along with South Carolina Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-South Carolina): Kavanaugh didn`t back down. He`s on the court. Herschel ain`t backing down. \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is going to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: It is the latest case to envelop Walker, who recently admitted to writing a check for another woman who claimed he paid for her abortion in 2009.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I say that`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Walker`s opponent, Senator Warnock, has tried to steer clear of commenting on some of these controversies.\par \par \uc2 But, tonight, he issued a statement, saying: "This is the latest example of a troubling pattern" -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: What a story in these final days.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion, thank you.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221028eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Elizabeth Banks Live\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Gayle King \par \uc2 1,969 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are always excited about our guests, but we are particularly excited about our next guest. It is actor, director, producer movie star, Elizabeth Banks. She starred in TV shows and movies you know. We`re talking "Hunger Games," "Pitch Perfect," "Modern Family," and her new movie is called "Call Jane" in which she plays a 1960s housewife named Joy, who faces a life-threatening pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Call Jane.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Joy connects with an underground group of women who work to provide abortions before Roe v. Wade was decided.\par \par \uc2 It is a movie inspired by a real-life group based in Chicago, and in the clip you`re about to see, Joy promises to teach the women everything that she has learned about performing abortions.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Call Jane.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Elizabeth Banks joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("Call Jane" actor): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The movie is a triumph.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It really is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a drama that brings you back to historical reality.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How much of that reality did you know existed? Did you know this group existed? And what drew you to the project?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Honestly, I did not know about The Janes before I read the script. I read the script about three years ago now. You know, we planned to make the film before the pandemic and before obviously, the Dobb`s decision from this summer that overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 So for me, when I first read it, I just thought, oh, this will be such a great way to honor a little piece of history of these very revolutionary women in Chicago, who pre-Roe, you know, provided really caring and safe abortion care at a time when so many women put themselves in very dangerous, desperate situations in order to not be pregnant.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, with Dobbs, in so many places in America are getting back to that moment and there are real Janes everywhere now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everywhere. It`s almost eerie to me and surreal that it`s happening at this particular time while it is so relevant for women today.\par \par \uc2 How do you hope the movie will be received? What do you want people to be the takeaway? Because I had never heard of it either. And I thought, oh, what a nice piece of history. And now, we`re looking at it, it is staring us in the face and it`s scary.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes. It is now -- it is recent history.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know, we really depoliticized abortion healthcare in the film as much as possible, really tried to recenter it as the healthcare that it is.\par \par \uc2 My character, I think is really relatable. Joy is a woman who never thinks she`ll have to seek out abortion healthcare, is pretty judgmental of people who do, you know, and really opens up this experience, it opens up a greater sense of her empathy, I think and her ability to have a better understanding of a path that people walk, whether you`re going to walk it or not. And I think for me, that`s what I want people to take away from the movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, the experience was horrible because the doctor was such a jerk. He was so cold.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You go from someone who is laying on that gurney with him not caring about what you were going through.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then performing the abortion.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes. That was real -- that really happened. The women -- one of the things I love about the film, too, is I think there`s a big mythology around abortion healthcare, it is that it`s really scary and you might die. I mean, they are literally activists that yelled out at women who are going into clinics.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And the fact of the matter is that an abortion is safer than most dental experiences. It`s safer than a colonoscopy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the movie, they describe it as like scooping up pumpkin seeds.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was like, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, that`s a good example --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Well, now, you know, now you take two pills at home. So it can be self-managed at home for most American women who are going to seek it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But that line, it underscores an important point. You depoliticize abortion in the movie, but you don`t shy away from the act and from the repercussions.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a big part of it. Was that a tough decision? I mean, one of the key scenes is your character undergoing the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know, I think it was important, we didn`t want to shy away from it. Our director, Phyllis Nagy, who handled the material so beautifully, really wanted to present, I think, the experience for women who were at a time when they had to rely on men for their care. And you know, that`s kind of new history.\par \par \uc2 For thousands of years, women took care of reproductive health, whether it was pregnancy and childbirth and midwifery and all that. And you know, we`ve really gotten away from that for a long time now. We`ve allowed men into that room.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And even with the abortion bans, it`s like, I don`t want to be in the doctor`s office talking about planning my family, with my husband, my doctor, and like Lindsey Graham, who else is in the room?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You mention the word "conversation" plenty of times, and you`ve had conversations with your sons.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: About sex and very openly. It`s one of the toughest things to do when you have a teenager or even -- I have a 12-year-old girl. Why do you think it`s important to be honest, and not shy away from these topics?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Well, I think it`s interesting that you say it`s difficult because to me, that is the key right there. Why is it hard for us in our generation? What shame are we carrying that it`s so hard for us to say to our kids, to talk about their body`s sexuality, sex, which is like everyone`s going to have some sex at some point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come and get it --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And also a reflection of our upbringing, too, you know.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So we are just passing on what we were learning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And we are living in a society where shame and stigma surround these issues so much, that a lot of lies get told to people, I mean, especially young people, and it presents sex as like scary, and you`re going to get STDs and you`re all going to get pregnant and you should only wait until you`re married.\par \par \uc2 It is not people`s lived reality, right? Like, it`s wasn`t my lived reality, like I hate to tell you, but I had sex before I was married.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know, and I am like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You tramp.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes, well, and that`s the other problem.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is -- yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Right? So shame and stigma. That`s it. Like if you`re doing anything outside -- but that`s just not people`s lived reality. Everyone knows it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And we don`t want that for our kids. I want my kids to grow up, have safe, healthy relationships with their partners.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know, that`s it, and I want them to treat women respectfully, forever.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, of course. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And I want them to enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel you on that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know 99 percent of the sex I have was not to make babies.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: It was just to be fun and be intimate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It just is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is called pleasure.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Pleasure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s like working out, just getting Republicans.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The producers are like. Jon is like, "We`ve got to go."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Elizabeth Banks, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, "Call Jane" is an absolutely great film. It hits theaters on Friday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are the best, and one of the best in the business. I seriously mean that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Nate, exercise is not fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Reps.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it is good.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: It builds the endorphins. I get it. I get it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what I`m trying to say, Tony.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, we`re going to have our conversation with "Bridgerton" executive producers that would be badass, Shonda Rhimes, and her partner Betsy Beers. They are a knockout -- about making the hit \uc2 Netflix\uc2  show and why they wanted to create a more inclusive regency era.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES ("Bridgerton" executive producer): I`m not going to make a show that doesn`t include me in it, somehow, number one. But two, you know, historically, there`s been so much erasure of people of color in that time period.\par \par \uc2 There were Black composers. There were Black artists. There were all of these people and we wanted to have a chance to tell that story. So, we went from the idea what if Queen Charlotte as has been said in history, really is biracial and told the story from there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She says she`s obsessed with Queen Charlotte. Find out where they got the idea to create their first hit series. You might have heard of it. It`s a little thing called "Grey`s Anatomy."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And in case you missed it on this week`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, we showed you a few items that might become essentials in your everyday life. Items like -- at all exclusive low prices by the way -- the Drop Stop, aims to prevent driver distraction and is now available at 40 percent off retail price.\par \par \uc2 To buy the Drop Stop and many other items, scan the QR code on your screen, upper left or head over to CBSDeals.com and started shopping now.\par \par \uc2 Talking about essentials in everyday life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My wife`s birthday is today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Birthday, Katy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow. Happy Birthday, Katy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221028eiaq000dy\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Critical Debate In Pennsylvania\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Robert Costa \par \uc2 1,810 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, also known as hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, he is. We are all here. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2  (D) (Pennsylvania Senate candidate): I had a stroke. He`s never let me forget that, and I might miss some words during this debate, mush two words together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A candidate`s health is a topic at the Pennsylvania Senate debate, what it means for national politics.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY): They`re being stabbed, beaten to death on the street with hammers.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: A tense and testy debate as the candidates for New York Governor face off.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Work on keeping people scared or you can focus on keeping them safe.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Brittney Griner faces nine years in a Russian labor camp after her appeal is rejected. We`ll tell you what`s next.\par \par \uc2 NED PRICE (\uc2 US State Department\uc2  spokesperson): This process to put it simply has been a sham.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ukraine says Russia is performing secret work at Europe`s largest nuclear power plant as President Biden warns Putin against using nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Russia will be making an incredibly serious mistake.\par \par \uc2 MAN: After nearly 30 hours, a search and rescue team saved five people who were stuck in the Grand Canyon Caverns.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: When you`re in an \uc2 NBA\uc2  game, make sure you stay alert or where else you might end up like this.\par \par \uc2 MAN: She did not see that one coming.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Kanye West is learning the cost of his antisemitic comments.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The rapper was dropped from "Forbes" billionaire list after Adidas terminated their lucrative contract.\par \par \uc2 TREVOR NOAH ("The Daily Show" host): you can still get a pair of Yeezys, you just have to take a normal pair of sneakers and microwave them for 30 minutes.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Midterms are typically a referendum on the sitting President, which is why \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is getting out there with a powerful closing message, "I am also aware that I am very old."\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : I could get a disease tomorrow, I could drop dead tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT: I mean, come on. I -- I -- I -- I could die tomorrow. Anything can get me, disease, strong wind, particularly spicy chili.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Never good enough where they put crickets music, you know?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know that was music. It might have been in the actual thread.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It might have an actual cricket.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was funny though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, it was --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was funny.\par \par \uc2 Welcome --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stephen Colbert is always funny.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He knows how to get it done.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got an amazing show for you, amazing stories of resilience today, including from a country music star, best known for his work with the Zac Brown Band.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Zac Brown Band performance.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that`s the band`s massive runaway hit song "Chicken Fried" featuring John Driskell Hopkins, but Hopkins is now facing the battle of his life and he talked with our Jan Crawford, who visited Hopkins and his wife at their Georgia home and talked about fighting for their daughters who are called Race, Hope, and Faith.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, beautiful names. Plus, 14 women recently became one of the largest groups of Black Americans to climb up Africa`s Mount Kilimanjaro. That`s the largest freestanding mountain in the world.\par \par \uc2 All 14 of them will be here in studio to tell us about something that Gayle will never, ever do.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I know. I know. You mean no shape, and that`s true.\par \par \uc2 You know, they say, Nate, "Never Say Never." I can actually say, I ain`t never sure of that, but my hat`s off to them, can`t wait to meet them.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin though in Pennsylvania with a Senate race. It`s got huge implications for the country in which party has power in Washington.\par \par \uc2 Now, this was the first and only debate between Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz, it included fiery exchanges on abortion rights, the economy and a range of other issues, but with early voting already underway, John Fetterman`s health was also a topic.\par \par \uc2 Robert Costa is following this campaign.\par \par \uc2 Bob, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 He started right off the bat saying let`s address the elephant in the room. Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: He did, Gayle, good morning. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 Last night`s debate, it was a crucial crossroads in the battle for the Senate. It was also reflective of the state of politics in Pennsylvania and the nation.\par \par \uc2 Tense exchanges on the issues and on character.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : And let`s also talk about the elephant in the room. I had a stroke. He`s never let me forget that.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Lieutenant Governor \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  started off the hour-long debate assuring voters he is still capable of doing the job of Senator five months after having a stroke.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : I might miss some words during this debate, mush two words together, but it knocked me down, but I`m going to keep coming back up.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: While his GOP opponent, Dr. Mehmet Oz went straight to attacking his opponent as soft on crime.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ (R) (Pennsylvania Senate candidate): \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 , during this crime wave has been trying to get as many murders convicted and sentenced to life in prison out of jail as possible.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : He has no experience. He has never made any attempt to try to address crime during his entire career except showing up for photo ops here in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: The two clashed on abortion.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ: I don`t want the Federal government involved with that at all. I want women, doctors, local political leaders, letting the democracy that`s always allowed our nation to thrive to put the best ideas forward so States can decide for themselves.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : If you believe that the choice for abortion belongs with you and your doctor, that`s what I fight for. Roe v. Wade for me is -- should be the law. He celebrated when Roe v. Wade went down.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ: The abortion decision should be left up to States and specifically when \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  was --\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : You roll with \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ: John, you`ll have turn your John.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: And things got heated when they were asked about families struggling with the high cost of college tuition.\par \par \uc2 DR. MEHMET OZ: Basically what \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  are arguing for is for plumbers who didn`t go to college and couldn`t for a bunch of reasons afford it to pay the bills of lawyers who went to graduate school and haven`t paid their debt back.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : Dr. Oz, you know loves free -- free money when it`s a half a million dollar tax break on one of his, you know, homes down and a ranch in Florida and whether it was a $50.00 tax break, you know about his farm in Montgomery County so...\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Fetterman`s use of a closed captioning device during the debate where he read questions in real time on a screen above the moderators spark debate on social media with some observers seeing a strong performance amid recovery, while others cast him as unsteady.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : I -- I -- I do support fracking, and I don`t -- I don`t -- I support fracking and I stand and I do support fracking.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Fetterman would not say if he would release more of his medical records, but argued that he has been transparent.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN\uc2 : I believe if my doctor believes that I`m fit to serve, and -- and that`s what I believe is appropriate.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Fetterman`s advisers tell CBS News that in the coming days they will make abortion rights a focal point and they claim that in the hours after the debate, Fetterman raised more than $1 million.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Mehmet Oz will campaign today with Nikki Haley, the former Trump administration diplomat -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bob, you know the race for Governor, big story in the news in Pennsylvania. Where do things stand with that one?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Our CBS News Battleground Tracker Poll shows the Democratic nominee, Attorney General Josh Shapiro, ahead of State Senator \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , a Trump ally.\par \par \uc2 I sat down with Shapiro here in Harrisburg, Pennsylvania, and he says he is ahead because he is reaching out to Republicans in the suburbs of Philadelphia and Pittsburgh, who don`t want to side with a Trump ally and an election denier.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Bob, we thank you. We`ll see you later on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And here in New York, the Governor`s race is close, surprisingly close for New York.\par \par \uc2 Democratic incumbent \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  and her challenger Congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  clashed at their only debate last night, including on the topics of abortion and crime.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): You`re the only person standing on this stage whose name right now, not years past, but right now is on a bill called Life Begins at Conception. I don`t trust this. I really don`t trust this.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Okay, candidates...\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY): Can I respond to that --\par \par \uc2 MAN: It is -- real briefly, please.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 LEE ZELDIN\uc2  (R-NY): Yes, I mean, listen, I stated that the first day that I`m in office, I`m going to declare a crime emergency and suspend cashless bail and these other pro-criminal laws, because there is a crime emergency.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And those were the big themes of the night.\par \par \uc2 Governor Hochul wanted to talk about abortion and Congressman Zeldin consistently brought it back to crime. By the way, Congressman Zeldin says he would not in fact seek to make abortion illegal in the State if elected.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content