-\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at that schedule --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And then do it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look at the one that no one could get tickets to and that`s the one.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that`s the one you can go on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bring your brother. All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still ahead, Jan Crawford talks with the guitarist John Driskell Hopkins of the country music group, Zac Brown Band who is fighting the battle of his life and still finding time to help others. Love that band.\par \par \uc2 But before that story, local news coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 We will be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Hip-hop artist and designer, Kanye West is rapidly losing support over business and over a series of antisemitic comments. We told you about this yesterday that Adidas ended its partnership with West who now goes by Ye.\par \par \uc2 Both Footlocker and GAP followed suit revealing plans to pull his shoe and clothing lines from their stores. West`s past rhetoric has also included COVID conspiracy theories, and a ludicrous suggestion that slavery was a choice.\par \par \uc2 But the latest attacks targeting the Jewish community have brought the scourge of antisemitism back into the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Vigliotti has our report.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KANYE WEST (Rapper): I can say antisemitic things and Adidas can`t drop me. Now what?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Adidas can and did drop Kanye West Tuesday, more than a week after he made that brazen claim during a podcast appearance.\par \par \uc2 In a statement Adidas called the Grammy-winning rapper`s comments "unacceptable, hateful and dangerous." The company said it would stop producing West`s Yeezy branded products and end all payments to him. As a result, Adidas expects to lose nearly $250 million in profit from its 2022 net income.\par \par \uc2 It comes after the latest in a recent string of antisemitic rhetoric from West who earlier this month said he would go, "death con three on Jewish people." Platforms like \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  have suspended his accounts.\par \par \uc2 OREN SEGAL (\uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2 ): We know that his comments animated the extremists.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Oren Segal tracks extremism for the \uc2 Anti-Defamation League\uc2 , which reported a 34 percent increase in antisemitic incidents from 2020 to 2021.\par \par \uc2 The total: More than 2,700 is the highest ever since the \uc2 ADL\uc2  began tracking those incidents in 1979.\par \par \uc2 OREN SEGAL: When you think about the following that Ye has on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , for example, it is greater than the number of Jews that exist on the planet. And so when somebody of that position normalizes antisemitic messages, it is a huge concern.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: The consequences appear to play out over the weekend, as a small group of antisemitic demonstrators flew a banner over the 405 Freeway in Los Angeles which read, "Kanye is right about the Jews."\par \par \uc2 You passed by that demonstration?\par \par \uc2 STEVE LEDER (Wilshire Boulevard Temple Senior Rabbi): I drove right past it. What really shocked me was the cowardice of it, and the ignorance of it.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: Steve Leder is the Senior Rabbi at LA`s Wilshire Boulevard Temple. He told us some Wilshire members were among those to receive antisemitic flyers like these in recent days.\par \par \uc2 STEVE LEDER: Any kind of othering, it is a cancer, Jonathan.\par \par \uc2 As much as we have had to mention Kanye, let`s get him out of the spotlight. This isn`t about him.\par \par \uc2 All human beings, Jonathan, all of us, we are all 99.9 percent genetically identical. We are all in this together\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN VIGLIOTTI: For CBS MORNINGS, Jonathan Vigliotti, Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should also add that CBS News tried to contact Kanye West for comment, but we were unable to reach him at this time.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Rabbi Angela Buchdahl. She`s a Senior Rabbi at Central Synagogue right here in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Rabbi Buchdahl, we`re very glad you`re here.\par \par \uc2 You know, I`ve heard you say what he just said at the end of the piece that this is so much bigger than Kanye, that we really should take the spotlight off of Kanye, but he does have a big platform. So, we have to say something, don`t you think?\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL (Central Synagogue Senior Rabbi): Well, it is much bigger than Kanye, but let`s start with the fact that Kanye is big. Just to put a little perspective on it, he has 30 million -- over 30 million \uc2 Twitter\uc2  followers, which is more than double the population of all the Jews of the world. There are fewer than 15 million Jews in the world.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I don`t think people realize that.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: I don`t think people understand what a small community we are globally. And yet -- so one man having a platform that is twice as big as if every Jewish person in the world tried to reach someone, it still is not as big as his platform. So, I think that we have to not underestimate what one person`s platform could be.\par \par \uc2 But it`s so much bigger than that, because it`s not just about one person.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: It`s also not just about antisemitism against Jews. He has essentially activated an army of people who hate and are serial haters, and the evidence of that is just the clip we saw of the White supremacists. These are people who don`t just hate Jews, they actually hate people of color, minorities, difference, LGBTQ --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Looking for any reason.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . to hate somebody, something.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Right. So this is -- when we tolerate that that kind of language, it might start with hatred or conspiracy theories of Jews, but it always extends to anyone who is of difference.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, antisemitism is often called the world`s oldest hatred.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is in recorded history.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Where does it come from?\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Well, there are many roots. It is at least, you know, as long as there have been Jews, there has been hatred of Jews, unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 There has been, you know, the Christian church had a whole doctrine that was very explicitly antisemitic, and throughout all the generations and ages, there have been ways that this has morphed and evolved. It`s very resilient, this hatred.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is very resilient.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: However, there are some like foundational principles that have kind of remained the same and just sort of have different permutations. Issues of, you know, kind of blood thirst of Jewish greed, of conspiracy theory that Jews kind of control and run everything.\par \par \uc2 So back in the day, it was about banks; now, it`s about the media. So, it morphs and changes, but it is -- but some principles of basically, that the world`s problems can be blamed on the Jews.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A population of just 15 million.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: That`s the bottom line.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A population of just 15 million people.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m glad you brought that up, because one of those tropes or narratives is the control of Hollywood or media.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . or banks and money, pulling strings behind the scenes. For those who might not understand that that are sitting at home saying, "Well, if you have power, what`s the big deal about it?" Why is that such a bad thing for those who don`t understand that narrative?\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: First of all, it is very -- it puts Jews in a terrible situation and a Catch 22 as my friend, Yair Rosenblum said that, you know, if we do nothing, we let this go unchecked. If we actually say something, and something happens, like Adidas drops him -- drops Ye, then they say, look, see how Jews --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Told you so.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Look at how powerful the Jews are. They can they can drop and make Adidas lose $250 million, so it is a very horrible situation.\par \par \uc2 I will say though, that I personally experienced this myself when there was a gunman who came from England and held a rabbi and four -- and three others hostage in a synagogue in Colleyville, Texas. I received a phone call from the terrorist saying: "You Jews are influential and powerful. And I need you to get this prisoner out of Federal Prison." And I said, "I don`t have that power."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: He said, "Oh, of course you do. You run a large synagogue in New York."\par \par \uc2 So that just is to tell you that like one man`s craziness, but it`s not just one man, he has been fed this in a system of antisemitic rhetoric. If you want to think about why it`s dangerous to say Jews have power and influence, think about a man who holds four people at gunpoint for 10 hours because he believes Jews have influencing powers.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Who has the power when people look at you --\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: So, it is a life and death issue.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When people look at you, Rabbi Buchdahl, they think, "Well, that`s not what I expect a rabbi to look like."\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: That`s right. I`ve heard that before. So I would just say that I think one of the problems of antisemitism today is people really have no understanding of who the Jewish community is.\par \par \uc2 There is certainly an America misunderstanding that Jews have always just been White people, clearly not, and that`s actually not been who the Jewish community has been historically over, you know, a millennia.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: And so I think that part of this is about educating ourselves about who we are, but part of this is also about just rhetoric that wants to put Jews into a particular box.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did it bother you that companies seem to take a -- they took several weeks to decide that they`re going to disengage from him? Does that send a message to you?\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Yes, I think that people are kind of weighing their bottom line. And I think that it was disappointing that it took so long for people, but I really appreciate that ultimately, people did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 I`m not going to go into whether or not I think their motivations for why they did it will ultimately -- I think the bottom line, if you`re best asset is your brand and you allow your brand to be associated with a bigoted, hateful, you know army of people, I think that that is your bottom line.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how do you shine a light on this without giving it a platform? You know, that`s what I struggle with?\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: I would say, shine a light on the antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it is hard not to.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Shine a light on the antisemitism and don`t shine it on the antisemite, because that actually gives more oxygen.\par \par \uc2 So don`t give Kanye more interviews. He just uses it as a platform to spew more hate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: And it gives him -- it is the oxygen that feeds that fire and all his -- and his army of followers.\par \par \uc2 I think what you do is you actually talk about the antisemitic rhetoric and you point it out, and you condemn it, but actually don`t give so much attention to the person who is saying it. That`s really important.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And offer solutions.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, we`re glad you --\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: Offer solutions. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re glad you`re here to give it the appropriate amount of light.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. And the right --\par \par \uc2 RABBI ANGELA BUCHDAHL: I appreciate you doing it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rabbi Angela Buchdahl, thank you very much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, Vlad Duthiers has got the stories you will be talking about today. There`s Vlad.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time now for "What to Watch." Mr. Duthiers who does not know the song. Now, you know now the song.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I think it is Rihanna.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Rihanna. There we go. Rihanna. That would make sense.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: At first I was like, I like this groove. I like this groove.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now you know why you like it?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I heard. I like it because it is Rihanna. We are going to shed a little bit of light and love in this segment of "What to Watch" because we`re beginning with the latest info from Marvel about the upcoming movie sequel "Black Panther: Wakanda Forever."\par \par \uc2 The company posted this clip to its social media accounts yesterday. So check this out. Look what you`re watching here. See that R?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: R?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It pops out from the word "forever."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What does it mean?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Rihanna?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: RiRi.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: RiRi?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right. So the rumor is that the R is for Rihanna who might be releasing a new single for the film this Friday. It is her first new music in six years.\par \par \uc2 On \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , the artist seemed to confirm those suspicions by commenting, if you look at the -- you see that little comment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The emoji.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Crossed arm, Wakanda salute.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, I got it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s not --some people thought, "What? Does this mean the movie is Rated R?" No, it doesn`t mean that the movie is Rated R. It means that -- I know, but you know, people think they are going to internalize --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, but what a great build up for her.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She got the Super Bowl. You know the song is going to be killer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We know that.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It`s going to be awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, we`re going to fall in love with that and then lead right up to the Super Bowl. I love of all of it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So that means, more new music is coming, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Followed it, you would assume.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: First in six years, the first single in five years. People have been waiting. You should see the comments under that. Everybody is like "Rihanna. RiRi. Coming back. Coming back. Here for it."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She doesn`t need to drop music though. Rihanna is killing the game right now.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She is a mogul.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: But I want a little music, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I know, but she has got her makeup, she`s got that lingerie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I don`t buy makeup. I mean, well, I mean, we shouldn`t say I wear makeup, but I don`t buy any makeup.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`d buy music.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And all of her stuff is good. All of it. The lingerie is great.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, yes. Definitely that`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Her makeup is good and her music is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a fact.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We are excited.\par \par \uc2 All right, let me talk to you about this little Colorado boy who is becoming -- who is about to become the youngest person to climb Yosemite National Park`s El Capitan.\par \par \uc2 It is one of the hardest climbs in the world. And guess what? He`s eight.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The eight-year-old adventurer. His name is Sam "Adventure" Baker.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wait, "Adventure` is his middle name?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The middle name is Adventure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: On his birth certificate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: On his birth certificate?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Seriously? Really.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: His name is Adventure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He had no other choice, but to climb mountains.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. He has been climbing since he was three and Sam`s dad says the two have been training every day to prepare to scale the 3,000 foot tall rock together.\par \par \uc2 They started on Monday and expect the trip to take four days.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: What do you guys think?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Look how beautiful that is, Nate?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is going to do that. Yes, he is going to do that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I need to get outside more often.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nate is not a nature guy yet.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`re not a nature guy?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hiking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am not.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: . not a climbing.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I`ve been in your backyard. It looks pretty nature to me.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s why I set it up that way. I`m going to start hiking and we have a special sit down with 14 amazing women who climbed.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Very cool. I know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: . just like him.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You think they`re going to get you inspired to get on to --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I am already inspired.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, no, no. Vlad, when we were talking about all the women coming, we were very excited about them coming, and Nate goes, "What`s the point?"\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of hiking. Not climbing mountains.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What`s the point of hiking?\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hiking --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he said point with a T, exclamation point.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Like meaning like, I really --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like I don`t get it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I can`t wait for them to meet you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Gayle, what happened when you went hiking in the last month? Don`t worry about it. We`ll pull it out later in the show.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t say I was good at it. I didn`t say I was good at it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, Vlad --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I always, I have wondered though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s good, Nate.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: . but people who climb mountains like Everest, I mean, I applaud them, but I`m always like --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 Vlad, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ahead, Jan Crawford`s emotional conversation with guitarist, John Driskell Hopkins of Zac Brown Band and his wife about a major challenge in his life and what lies ahead.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:47. It`s time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER0\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We are here in the progressive greenroom with 14 women who have done something that I`ll bet you`ve never even thought about doing and will probably never do.\par \par \uc2 J`nelle, what did you do?\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE (Mountain climber): We climbed the summit of Mount Kilimanjaro.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the question, is how many made it to the top? That`s a tease question. And guys you have to say something to Nate because he said, "What`s the point" with a T.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why you selling me out in front of everybody?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And Tony is here because Nate said we need some vanilla in this picture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We welcome you back to CBS MORNINGS, I`m Gayle King. That`s Tony Dokoupil, and that`s Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: Stories of love when times get tough. We need it.\par \par \uc2 John Driskell Hopkins, of the Zac Brown Band shares how family has helped him after a very difficult diagnosis, and now, he is now using every minute he`s got to help others.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ain`t no mountain high enough for 14 Black women who climbed up Mount Kilimanjaro in an epic expedition. They call themselves "Sistahs to the Summit" and they are all here in studio to tell us their story of grit and determination.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Elizabeth Banks is in studio with her new movie. It`s called "Call Jane" set in the past, but the story might remind you of today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, we have a great hour for you and we begin this hour with country music star`s story of determination with the help of his family.\par \par \uc2 You might recognize some of his music. Check it out.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of John Driskell Hopkins singing.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s John Driskell Hopkins, one of the founding members of Zac Brown Band, the superstar country music group who has had 14 number one hits.\par \par \uc2 The life of Hopkins has been filled with awards and accolades, but now, he is facing his most difficult challenge.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford met up with him between shows.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning. It`s really great to be here.\par \par \uc2 I mean, John Driskell Hopkins, like you said, Nate, I mean, his band. I mean, they have reached the pinnacle for success.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But now, he is grappling with a diagnosis that`s changed the course of his life.\par \par \uc2 I met up with him at his home in Georgia to talk about the devastating news and the road ahead.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Nestled in a studio in his attic, John Driskell Hopkins is always creating.\par \par \uc2 Best known as one of the founding members of Zac Brown Band --\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Zac Brown Band performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Hopkins sings and plays guitar for one of the top groups in country music, but while Hopkins has been enjoying center stage during the band`s summer tour, the 51-year-old has been facing a difficult battle behind the scenes.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS (Zac Brown Band guitarist): I think it was somewhere around the middle of 2019, I started noticing I couldn`t double time as quickly as -- I would still do it, but I was starting to get angry at my right hand.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: So, I was just in the movements.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: Just in the speed.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The speed.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: And I remember sitting on the bus and talking to the guys and saying, "Something`s wrong with my hands."\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: After months of testing, just before Christmas last year, Hopkins and his wife got the devastating news. Doctors diagnosed him with ALS.\par \par \uc2 Do you remember how you felt when you heard that?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: We were crying three times a day. I was just like, "I didn`t even figure out what this means."\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOPKINS (John Driskell Hopkins` wife): Something like this never crossed our mind.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOPKINS: It was -- you`re about to turn 50, it was lose some weight. It was everything else.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It`s called the cruelest disease or Lou Gehrig`s disease. It eventually paralyzes all muscle movement, trapping patients inside their own bodies. There is no known cure.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: We had done the ALS Ice Bucket Challenge in 2014.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Zac Brown Band members doing ALS Ice Bucket challenge.)\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: I didn`t even know what it was, you know?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOPKINS: And I don`t think people talk about it much because it is so devastating and hard to talk about.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: Hey, Hope.\par \par \uc2 HOPE HOPKINS (John Driskell Hopkins` daughter): Hey, Daddy.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: This is Hope.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: As the father of three, Hopkins greatest fans, daughters Grace, Hope and Faith, give him purpose.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: Aw.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And his soulmate, she gives him his daily treatments and courage.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: I worry about what it means to her, because I don`t want to ruin her adult life.\par \par \uc2 This is the most beautiful woman in the world. She can find anyone tomorrow, but I don`t want to burden this one.\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOPKINS: And he`s my life.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: He is your life?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER HOPKINS: He is my life, him and my girls. And he`s got so much to give. Truly the stars aligned for us to be together and we`re meant to go through this together and whatever that brings, whatever that means is -- this is -- this is our story.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Together, they created a charity Hop on a Cure, which has already raised more than $100,000.00 for ALS research. His benefit concert in September at Nashville`s renowned Ryman Auditorium quickly sold out.\par \par \uc2 You`re doing this for a greater good.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: I`m not a scientist, I`m not going to be the one with the test tubes and the research. I`m someone who has a platform that can explain, I can`t play the guitar like I used to. I might not be able to sing one day. And if I have an opportunity to spread the word that way, then that`s my responsibility.\par \par \uc2 His bandmates of 20 years are standing by his side.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: God willing, I plan to be rockin` with these amazing people for many years to come.\par \par \uc2 They`re all amazing human beings. I mean.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: You`re like a family.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: And that`s another reason why we`ve found success is we -- we really mean it.\par \par \uc2 January --\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Between shows, Hopkins has been working on a different kind of project.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: April, May --\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It`s called voice banking, recording words and sentences to be used for communicating in the future, like giving advice to his daughters when he can no longer talk.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: Girls time for dinner. Boys are all liars.\par \par \uc2 I, as a father, don`t know what they need yet, and I want to be there, want to impart some wisdom that I think they can probably use later.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I mean, it seems like there`s a song there.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: And I`ve got a story that I`m working on that are all in different ways, directly related to what I`m going through.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What is it that you want to say in those songs?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: I was writing a song, just simply, I love you forever --\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: To Jen?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: And to the babies.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And to the girls.\par \par \uc2 Did you sing it?\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: I haven`t finished it, can`t finish it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of John Driskell Hopkins singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Perhaps that`s because Hopkins doesn`t look at his days as numbered, instead as a chance to appreciate all the good in life.\par \par \uc2 JOHN DRISKELL HOPKINS: I still got it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And with his future unknown, Hopkins` is focused on living, being present with his wife and kids, and touring with the band as long as he can. He is even making some new music. He`s got a Christmas album coming out next month. So, he is living life --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: He is right. He still got it. I love that, despite everything he`s going through, he reaches over to his wife. He is concerned about her and his daughters and the messages that he is leaving for his daughters. What a gift that is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also the agony of not knowing what they`re going to need exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I liked that last line of him just strumming the guitar, "Life is good today."\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, you never know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You never think about yesterday, we can be worried about tomorrow, but if life is good today.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Smile on your face.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I`ll take that.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, we don`t know what tomorrow is. None of us do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jan, that was awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jan, beautifully done. Beautifully done.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was awesome.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ahead, they call themselves "Sistahs to the Summit."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like that. We will talk -- hello. Hello ladies. We`ll talk to these history making women, one of the largest groups of Black Americans to reach the top of one of the world`s most famous mountain. Looking good, too.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:08. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still ahead: A story about friendship and also giving back. Tucker Haas, diagnosed with a rare form of cancer only a toddler at the time, now he is all grown up and he`s paying it forward. We`ll show you how he`s doing it.\par \par \uc2 Back in a moment. This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All the way up. Fourteen Black American women are now one of the largest groups ever to climb Mount Kilimanjaro. It`s the largest freestanding mountain in the world and the tallest in Africa. They call themselves "Sistahs to the Summit."\par \par \uc2 You are watching the moment when they reach Kilimanjaro`s highest point at more than 19,000 feet earlier this month. That`s the length of almost 54 football fields.\par \par \uc2 The women range in age from 31 to 53, but they don`t look a day over 25.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: They took an eight-day journey up and down the so-called Roof of Africa through a variety of climates. Two of the 14 women had to turn back mid-trip because of altitude sickness.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That would have been me if I`d been there, it would had been three of 15 had to turn back because of altitude sickness, but the remaining 12, we are happy to say made it to the two highest summit points.\par \par \uc2 First on CBS MORNINGS, all 14 of these amazing women are here with us.\par \par \uc2 Nate, would you do the introduction.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, yes, I`ve got you. All right, so, it is Christy (sp?), Christina (sp?), Danielle (sp?), Jennifer (sp?), Lisa (sp?), Lu-Shawn, Malaak, Marianne (sp?), Naser (sp?), Naomi (sp?), Tanisha (sp?), Taylor (sp?), J`nelle, and Erika. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That could almost be a song, Nate Burleson. That could almost be a song.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Shoutout to DMX.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, you guys noticed the DMX.\par \par \uc2 WOMEN: Right. Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all got it.\par \par \uc2 But Erica, I`ve got to start with you because we have to either thank you or blame you for this.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES (Sistahs to the Summit): Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you were turning 50. You sent out an e-mail to all of your friends, to a lot of your friends, and you said what?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES (Mountain climber): I said, "Hey, I have an idea. I`d like to climb Mount Kilimanjaro," my dear friend, Malaak and I have been talking about this for five years now. Let`s do it. It`s my year of turning 50. And I had a strategy where I did not want to put any pressure on anyone because I know that climbing Mount Kilimanjaro is not for the faint of heart.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: And 18 people responded.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Eighteen?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Eighteen?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And then you got down to 14.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Malaak, just said you all have wanted to do this for five years. Why did you want to do it? What were you thinking?\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK (Mountain climber): We have similar travel sensibilities, and we both want to see the world. We`re talking about Antarctica, next.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we want to see the world and we want to discover places and take ourselves out of our comfort zones. So, we`ve been talking about it, but I`ll tell you, she told me to plan it and I never did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, you never did.\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: But when I got her e-mail, it was like instantaneous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So J`nelle, how did you all train? Because you all didn`t know each other. The common bond here was Erika. So, it`s not like you all knew each other or you all live in the same neighborhood. So how did you train?\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: Well, over the summer, a lot of the women were on Martha`s Vineyard, so they started doing hikes together.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Martha`s Vineyard, not the same as Mount Kilimanjaro.\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: Not the same. Not the same.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: But for me, I couldn`t travel to Martha`s Vineyard, so I broke my boots in in Brooklyn, going to Trader Joe`s and Target. So, we all had to find our ways to train. So, whether it was on the treadmill, doing small hikes in New Jersey, or going in Harlem, that was really just our training strategies.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, LaChant, you had to turn back because of altitude sickness.\par \par \uc2 LU-SHAWN THOMPSON (Mountain climber): Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Like, how tough was that decision? And what does that feel like for people who don`t know?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LU-SHAWN THOMPSON: Well, it wasn`t really up to me. My O2 -- my oxygenation went down to 56. It was supposed to be at least a hundred.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A hundred, yes.\par \par \uc2 LU-SHAWN THOMPSON: So, it wasn`t really up to me. The porters made the decision to carry me down the mountain.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`re talking life or death situations. Let`s be clear.\par \par \uc2 LU-SHAWN THOMPSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Some of these things are really dangerous, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LU-SHAWN THOMPSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`s not like you wimped out. It`s really very, very serious.\par \par \uc2 LU-SHAWN THOMPSON: Yes. Like it took me -- I took -- the other lady is like seven hours to do it. I did it in 16 hours and I was -- and I kept throwing up, I was really weak. And when I got back, I was like, "I`ll just sleep and tomorrow, I`ll get up and I`ll feel better."\par \par \uc2 But then my watch started beeping. Your heart rate is high. Your heart rate is high.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you made the right decision.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But I look at all of you today, I know Erika, you didn`t hike in those shoes.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Would you all describe yourselves as a high maintenance group? And I say that with great respect and love.\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: Hundred percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For somebody who identifies --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Would you say you`re a high maintenance group?\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: Well listen, before we even started the climb, we were like, "Do we have toilets? Do we have showers," right?\par \par \uc2 We need -- we need at least three of each to do this, though we did abandon them because the showers, the reward was not better than the effort of actually going to a shower in rocks and dirt and being cold.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: But the other thing that I think was a game changer, not just because we`re high maintenance, but because -- but it helped us, the strategy to summit and also we bonded with the porters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tell us about the porters there, Erika.\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: The porters on the mountain.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How did that work? Porters? What does that mean?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: So we decided that we wanted to give ourselves the best chance to summit. And so we decided we weren`t going to carry our daypacks, we were just going to roll with our trekking poles and our water.\par \par \uc2 So our snacks, our first day, everything went with our personal porters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So somebody is carrying it you`re saying.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Someone is carrying it, which is very unique on the mountain because everybody who is climbing the mountain, they`re in it, salt of the earth kind of people, granola, and we were the only group -- we were the only group that had someone walking beside us carrying our backpacks.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How many porters did you have?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Seventy-two.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, wow. We need a bigger studio with the porters.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: But we made it.\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: We made it to the top.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: We made it to the summit.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right, were they carrying the bags or were they carrying you all?\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: Both.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Sometimes both.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Seventy-two. I love it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you guys made it to the top this month or last month?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Two weeks ago.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Two weeks ago? Is anyone still sore?\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. You still have the effects.\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: We can`t feel our toes. Our toes are literally numb.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Wow.\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So how did bathing work, J`nelle? How does that work?\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: So we had a shower there, but it was basically, it looked like a Porta-Potty with a bucket that they would fill with hot water. And so you would go in there, but you really only had a couple of minutes to take your shower once you pulled the lever, and you had to basically take your clothes off, put it outside of a chair and once you finished your shower, you had some fresh clothes and you had to change in the shower because it`s outside. The porters are looking at you, other groups are looking at you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: So, the bathing was something everyone was like, "Day three, we don`t need to do this anymore."\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: Because Erika forgot her towel, the first time.\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: She was screaming, "I don`t have my towel."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But let`s do rapid fire questions because I want to get everybody involved.\par \par \uc2 Yay or Nay? Thumbs up or down?\par \par \uc2 Was the climb the most challenging thing you`ve ever done?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Erika says no. Okay.\par \par \uc2 Did you ever want to give up at any point?\par \par \uc2 MALAAK COMPTON-ROCK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. Did you make lifelong friendships?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Ah.\par \par \uc2 Would you do it again with your spouse or significant other?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Hard no.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Hard no.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because of the climb or because of the significant other?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I think we know the answer to that, Tony.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And when you came, Lu-Shawn, you all had set up a thing. What did you do when you reached the top? What did you do when you reached the very top? Did you dance?\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: We had lunch and then we did a TikTok to Beyonce`s "Cuff It."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh. Did anybody have personal mantras to get them through? What was yours?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There it is.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: My daughters.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Your daughters. So, you would just say your daughters` names over and over and over again? Did you have a personal chant, J`Nelle?\par \par \uc2 J`NELLE AGEE: I did. I was listening to my mom the whole time in my head saying, "You can do it, baby. You can do it." And I didn`t have service on the mountain the whole time. So I just remember her saying that to me before I left, "You can make it."\par \par \uc2 ERIKA LILES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That is awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You can do it and you did.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Last yes or no? Should Nate climb a mountain and get into the wide world of the outdoors?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, it looks like we`ve got our next story.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Uh-uh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thanks for setting that up, Tony. And we appreciate you all. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, guys. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Good luck on your next adventure.\par \par \uc2 WOMEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo, ladies. Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is "Sistahs to the Summit."\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Modern Family.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is the fearless Elizabeth Banks in the hit show, "Modern Family."\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, she is going to tell us about stepping into the role of a 60s housewife facing a life-threatening pregnancy for the new movie called "Call Jane."\par \par \uc2 Local news coming at you next.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Now to a story of friendship, hope, and giving back that as you will see comes full circle in a remarkable way.\par \par \uc2 Tucker Haas was just two years old when he was diagnosed with a rare form of cancer. Fraternity members at nearby \uc2 Penn State University\uc2  took him under their wing.\par \par \uc2 Haas, now a senior at \uc2 Penn State\uc2  himself is joining the fight with another young boy facing a similar battle.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Chen traveled to State College, Pennsylvania to see how Haas is paying it forward.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Nate, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 Life for Tucker Haas has not been easy by any means, but you wouldn`t know that by speaking with him. Instead, he is filled with optimism, and now using his story to give hope to others.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Tucker Haas is on a full circle journey, and the path keeps leading him here to State College, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 What does \uc2 Penn State\uc2  mean to you?\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS (Cancer survivor): \uc2 Penn State\uc2  saved my life.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: At the age of two, Haas was diagnosed with an aggressive form of sarcoma.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: In total, I went through four different stints with cancer. Twice is on my face and twice to my right lung, but I had two thoracotomy surgeries, 77 radiation treatments, hundreds of chemotherapy treatments.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But his family has never seen a medical bill and that`s because of a partnership between Four Diamonds, a childhood cancer research and support organization and THON, the \uc2 Penn State\uc2  student run philanthropic effort, known most notably for its 46-hour dance marathon fundraiser.\par \par \uc2 The money THON collects, goes to children and their families impacted by cancer.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Tucker Haas performance.)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: As a nine-year-old, Haas even participated in the event in this viral moment.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: Put your hands in the air.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: This will be your 20th THON this year.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: You`ve been going since you were really little.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: Yes, it`s just one of those things when you walk in to THON, if you`ve never experienced it, it is very breathtaking seeing 15-plus thousand students packed in Bryce Jordan Center on a snowy, freezing cold weekend in February and you just see all these kids with the same passion, the same love for these children and families that are going through cancer.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: But the support goes far beyond one night through THON, a sorority and fraternity also adopted Haas as one of their own.\par \par \uc2 What would they do for you?\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: I mean, everything. They would come visit me at the hospital and drive two hours on a weekend and spend the entire weekend with me. They would send me stuff to the hospital, toys, and presents.\par \par \uc2 Looking back, it really made an impact on my life.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Haas has been cancer free for almost 16 years. When it came time to apply for college, he says \uc2 Penn State\uc2  was a no brainer.\par \par \uc2 Now a senior, he is a leader in the very same fraternity that looked after him as a kid, Alpha Tau Omega and is giving back to kids just like him.\par \par \uc2 Three-year-old Gus Baumgardner was diagnosed with cancer at just two years old. Four Diamonds is paying for the now three-year-old`s treatment.\par \par \uc2 GRETA BAUMGARDNER (Gus` mother): I don`t know how they do it, because it`s not even like, we get bills and we submit them. We don`t get bills, like at all.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Greta Baumgardner is Gus` mom. She credits the support from \uc2 Penn State\uc2  and Haas himself for helping carry their family through this battle.\par \par \uc2 And when you hear about Tucker`s story that not only did he successfully beat childhood cancer, but that he`s thriving like this, what does that mean to you?\par \par \uc2 GRETA BAUMGARDNER: It gave us some hope, because Tucker had a hard, hard journey to and to see him many years later and he is living a life that has purpose, that he is happy and content, even with everything he`s gone through, it has given us a lot of hope.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Do you see yourself in these kids?\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: Yes. Yes. Especially Gus. If you probably put a picture of the two of us side by side, two, two-year-old little boys with bald heads with so much energy, the love of life.\par \par \uc2 I am here, and now I`m able to kind of give back to the families like -0-\par \par \uc2 GRETA BAUMGARDNER: It`s neat to see it come full circle.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: The full circle moment in this story was almost complete as Haas reflected on the college students who looked after him when he endured his own battle.\par \par \uc2 They made a big difference in your life?\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: Yes, for sure. Still people, still guys that I talk to now. They now have children and their own. So that`s crazy to see.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: They really did help change your life.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: What would you say if I told you they are standing behind you right now.\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: No way.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: We arranged a surprise visit with these now men who are in their late teens and early 20s when their fraternity first adopted Haas.\par \par \uc2 You had seen him grow up?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: What has that been like?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Crazy. Yes. I mean, you think of him -- I think, as a three, four, five year-old. You know, coming back to see this. It`s insane.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: He calls you guys role models, but you guys also think of him as a role model?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Absolutely. Yes. There`s no doubt.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Source of strength for everybody.\par \par \uc2 MAN: When him and his family have done sharing their story with countless different pledge classes who have come to the fraternity to find itself, I mean, he`s an inspiration to all of us.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Tucker was what brought us together.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Haas graduates this spring with a Sports Management degree. He is not sure yet where it will take him, but he hopes to continue to inspire others.\par \par \uc2 What do you want people to learn from you and your story?\par \par \uc2 TUCKER HAAS: There`s always a light at the end of the tunnel. I went through my journey with cancer for a reason. I`m at \uc2 Penn State\uc2  for a reason.\par \par \uc2 It all just ties in itself, like why I`m here and why I had the opportunity to do what I`m doing.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And Tucker Haas will raise money on behalf of his fraternity and a sorority for THON this year. He has a goal of half a million dollars, an organization record.\par \par \uc2 Gus, who we introduced you to is now eight months post treatment. His mom says his latest scans showed no evidence of cancer, but you know, in talking to Tucker, he said all these hundreds of college kids that are strangers essentially have become your biggest support system and your biggest cheerleaders.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a powerful example of the good that can be done from sororities and fraternities. You know, we`ve done stories about the opposite.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But this is a wonderful counterexample.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As soon as I saw those men sneak up behind Tucker, I was like, "Oh, it`s too early to be crying like this, Nancy."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Tearfully o`clock hour for sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: And you know, it was a surprise, but at the same time, they`ve stayed in touch for all these years since.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So many full circle moments in that story. Nicely done, Nancy Chen. Good to have you, as always.\par \par \uc2 NANCY CHEN: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take a look at who is in our progressive greenroom. It is -- there she is. She is an actor. She`s a director. She`s a producer.\par \par \uc2 She is Elizabeth Banks. She will join us next to talk about her starring role. This is a good movie, it`s called "Call Jane." I`ll say it again, "Call Jane."\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:37. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are always excited about our guests, but we are particularly excited about our next guest. It is actor, director, producer movie star, Elizabeth Banks. She starred in TV shows and movies you know. We`re talking "Hunger Games," "Pitch Perfect," "Modern Family," and her new movie is called "Call Jane" in which she plays a 1960s housewife named Joy, who faces a life-threatening pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Call Jane.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Joy connects with an underground group of women who work to provide abortions before Roe v. Wade was decided.\par \par \uc2 It is a movie inspired by a real-life group based in Chicago, and in the clip you`re about to see, Joy promises to teach the women everything that she has learned about performing abortions.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Call Jane.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Elizabeth Banks joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS ("Call Jane" actor): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The movie is a triumph.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It really is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. It is.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It`s a drama that brings you back to historical reality.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How much of that reality did you know existed? Did you know this group existed? And what drew you to the project?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Honestly, I did not know about The Janes before I read the script. I read the script about three years ago now. You know, we planned to make the film before the pandemic and before obviously, the Dobb`s decision from this summer that overturned Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 So for me, when I first read it, I just thought, oh, this will be such a great way to honor a little piece of history of these very revolutionary women in Chicago, who pre-Roe, you know, provided really caring and safe abortion care at a time when so many women put themselves in very dangerous, desperate situations in order to not be pregnant.\par \par \uc2 And obviously, with Dobbs, in so many places in America are getting back to that moment and there are real Janes everywhere now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Everywhere. It`s almost eerie to me and surreal that it`s happening at this particular time while it is so relevant for women today.\par \par \uc2 How do you hope the movie will be received? What do you want people to be the takeaway? Because I had never heard of it either. And I thought, oh, what a nice piece of history. And now, we`re looking at it, it is staring us in the face and it`s scary.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes. It is now -- it is recent history.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know, we really depoliticized abortion healthcare in the film as much as possible, really tried to recenter it as the healthcare that it is.\par \par \uc2 My character, I think is really relatable. Joy is a woman who never thinks she`ll have to seek out abortion healthcare, is pretty judgmental of people who do, you know, and really opens up this experience, it opens up a greater sense of her empathy, I think and her ability to have a better understanding of a path that people walk, whether you`re going to walk it or not. And I think for me, that`s what I want people to take away from the movie.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Well, the experience was horrible because the doctor was such a jerk. He was so cold.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You go from someone who is laying on that gurney with him not caring about what you were going through.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Then performing the abortion.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes. That was real -- that really happened. The women -- one of the things I love about the film, too, is I think there`s a big mythology around abortion healthcare, it is that it`s really scary and you might die. I mean, they are literally activists that yelled out at women who are going into clinics.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And the fact of the matter is that an abortion is safer than most dental experiences. It`s safer than a colonoscopy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In the movie, they describe it as like scooping up pumpkin seeds.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I was like, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, that`s a good example --\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Well, now, you know, now you take two pills at home. So it can be self-managed at home for most American women who are going to seek it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But that line, it underscores an important point. You depoliticize abortion in the movie, but you don`t shy away from the act and from the repercussions.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a big part of it. Was that a tough decision? I mean, one of the key scenes is your character undergoing the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know, I think it was important, we didn`t want to shy away from it. Our director, Phyllis Nagy, who handled the material so beautifully, really wanted to present, I think, the experience for women who were at a time when they had to rely on men for their care. And you know, that`s kind of new history.\par \par \uc2 For thousands of years, women took care of reproductive health, whether it was pregnancy and childbirth and midwifery and all that. And you know, we`ve really gotten away from that for a long time now. We`ve allowed men into that room.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And even with the abortion bans, it`s like, I don`t want to be in the doctor`s office talking about planning my family, with my husband, my doctor, and like Lindsey Graham, who else is in the room?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. That`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You mention the word "conversation" plenty of times, and you`ve had conversations with your sons.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: About sex and very openly. It`s one of the toughest things to do when you have a teenager or even -- I have a 12-year-old girl. Why do you think it`s important to be honest, and not shy away from these topics?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Well, I think it`s interesting that you say it`s difficult because to me, that is the key right there. Why is it hard for us in our generation? What shame are we carrying that it`s so hard for us to say to our kids, to talk about their body`s sexuality, sex, which is like everyone`s going to have some sex at some point.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Come and get it --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And also a reflection of our upbringing, too, you know.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So we are just passing on what we were learning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And we are living in a society where shame and stigma surround these issues so much, that a lot of lies get told to people, I mean, especially young people, and it presents sex as like scary, and you`re going to get STDs and you`re all going to get pregnant and you should only wait until you`re married.\par \par \uc2 It is not people`s lived reality, right? Like, it`s wasn`t my lived reality, like I hate to tell you, but I had sex before I was married.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know, and I am like --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You tramp.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Yes, well, and that`s the other problem.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That is -- yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Right? So shame and stigma. That`s it. Like if you`re doing anything outside -- but that`s just not people`s lived reality. Everyone knows it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And we don`t want that for our kids. I want my kids to grow up, have safe, healthy relationships with their partners.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know, that`s it, and I want them to treat women respectfully, forever.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, of course. No doubt.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: And I want them to enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I feel you on that. Yes.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: You know 99 percent of the sex I have was not to make babies.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: It was just to be fun and be intimate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It just is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is called pleasure.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: Pleasure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s like working out, just getting Republicans.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The producers are like. Jon is like, "We`ve got to go."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Elizabeth Banks, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ve got to go.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now, "Call Jane" is an absolutely great film. It hits theaters on Friday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are the best, and one of the best in the business. I seriously mean that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But Nate, exercise is not fun.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Reps.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, it is good.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH BANKS: It builds the endorphins. I get it. I get it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You know what I`m trying to say, Tony.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, we`re going to have our conversation with "Bridgerton" executive producers that would be badass, Shonda Rhimes, and her partner Betsy Beers. They are a knockout -- about making the hit \uc2 Netflix\uc2  show and why they wanted to create a more inclusive regency era.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SHONDA RHIMES ("Bridgerton" executive producer): I`m not going to make a show that doesn`t include me in it, somehow, number one. But two, you know, historically, there`s been so much erasure of people of color in that time period.\par \par \uc2 There were Black composers. There were Black artists. There were all of these people and we wanted to have a chance to tell that story. So, we went from the idea what if Queen Charlotte as has been said in history, really is biracial and told the story from there.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She says she`s obsessed with Queen Charlotte. Find out where they got the idea to create their first hit series. You might have heard of it. It`s a little thing called "Grey`s Anatomy."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And in case you missed it on this week`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS Deals, we showed you a few items that might become essentials in your everyday life. Items like -- at all exclusive low prices by the way -- the Drop Stop, aims to prevent driver distraction and is now available at 40 percent off retail price.\par \par \uc2 To buy the Drop Stop and many other items, scan the QR code on your screen, upper left or head over to CBSDeals.com and started shopping now.\par \par \uc2 Talking about essentials in everyday life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My wife`s birthday is today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Birthday, Katy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Happy Birthday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll see you tomorrow. Happy Birthday, Katy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Bye.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221026eiaq00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Breast Cancer Awareness Month With A Look At A Glaring Racial Disparity\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Elise Preston \par \uc2 803 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Well, tonight, we continue our coverage of Breast Cancer Awareness Month with a look at a glaring racial disparity. Black women are far more likely to die of breast cancer than white women.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Health Watch, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Elise Preston what can be done to eliminate this gap.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON (voiceover): Spending time with her beloved dog comforts LaToya Williams, who`s had a long journey after finding a lump in her breast.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA WILLIAMS (Strategic Director of Health Equity, \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2 ): I was actually misdiagnosed at the age of 29 and accurately diagnosed at the age of 30, only because I was not taken serious.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Williams, now 45, says doctors ignored her concerns and delayed treatment for six months while the lump in her breast grew.\par \par \uc2 What does it feel like when you don`t trust the doctors who are providing care?\par \par \uc2 LATOYA WILLIAMS: I felt like I was at the mercy of the health system and that I did not have a voice, and I just have to kind of go with this and just hope that I live.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Breast cancer is the leading cause of cancer death for black women, who are 41 percent more likely to die than white women, in part due to higher rates of obesity and other health disparities.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATHIE-ANN JOSEPH (NYU Langone Health): It`s still a public health problem.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Dr. Kathie-Ann Joseph works to tackle health inequities impacting women of color.\par \par \uc2 What barriers do black women face?\par \par \uc2 DR. KATHIE-ANN JOSEPH: There are structural barriers, whether it`s access to care. Not all care is equal. Your zip code is really going to dictate your ultimate outcomes.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Black women are also more likely to be diagnosed at a younger age in comparison to white women, and with triple negative breast cancer, a more aggressive subtype.\par \par \uc2 In a new study, Dr. Joseph argues that black women should undergo a breast cancer assessment risk by age 25.\par \par \uc2 DR. KATHIE-ANN JOSEPH: If you start screening at 50, you`re missing the vote on black woman.\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: As a survivor, LaToya Williams now works with the \uc2 American Cancer Society\uc2  to improve health outcomes for black women.\par \par \uc2 LATOYA WILLIAMS: Don`t be afraid to speak up. And if you don`t have a good rapport with your doctor, it`s OK to shop around for a new one.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELISE PRESTON: Now, Dr. Joseph recommends that women who feel they`re not being heard by their doctors go get a second, third or even fourth opinion with a breast specialist.\par \par \uc2 And because of significant underrepresentation in clinical trials, there is now a push to include more black women. That could improve outcomes -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Elise Preston with such good information, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Well, a group of tourists got stuck for about 30 hours at the bottom of Grand Canyon caverns. We`re going to tell you how it all ended when we return.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: A group of Grand Canyon tourists is sharing a terrifying story tonight after they were stranded about 200 feet underground for about 30 hours.\par \par \uc2 They got stuck inside the caverns on Sunday when the tourist attraction`s only elevator broke down. We can report everyone`s OK.\par \par \uc2 A special honor unveiled for the late Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 . That story still ahead.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We learned today that former Defense Secretary \uc2 Ashton Carter\uc2  died of a heart attack.\par \par \uc2 A Rhodes Scholar and physicist by training, Carter oversaw the opening of combat roles to women during his tenure in the Obama administration. Ash Carter was 68.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We wanted to end tonight with a new honor for the late Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 U.S. Postal Service\uc2  unveiled a Forever postage stamp that will be available next year. The portrait shows Ginsburg in a black robe with her trademark intricate white-collar. The post office calls Ginsburg an icon of American culture. We agree.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell.\par \par \uc2 We will see you right back here tomorrow. Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221027eiap000jh\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Breast Cancer Awareness Month: Why Mammograms Fail To Detect Tumors In Some Women\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Roxana Saberi \par \uc2 930 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: October is Breast Cancer Awareness Month. And there`s usually an increase in mammograms during the month. But mammograms can fail to detect tumors in some women.\par \par \uc2 In tonight`s Health Watch, \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Roxana Saberi on what women should be asking at their next appointment.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI (voiceover): For many, the key to beating breast cancer is early detection; 48-year-old Eve Feinberg gets a mammogram every year.\par \par \uc2 EVE FEINBERG (Patient): My two paternal aunts were diagnosed with breast cancer. I have always lived with this fear that I, too, will die of breast cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Her physician, Dr. Sarah Friedewald, says Feinberg is also at a slightly higher risk because her breasts are dense, meaning they have less fat and more tissue, like the breasts in these images.\par \par \uc2 DR. SARAH FRIEDEWALD (Northwestern Medicine): About 40 percent to 50 percent of the women in the country actually have dense breast tissue. It just makes it a little bit harder for us to find cancer on the mammogram.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: That`s because dense tissue shows up white on mammograms, and so do tumors.\par \par \uc2 DR. SARAH FRIEDEWALD: On a very cloudy day, which would be similar to a woman who have very dense breast tissue, we really can`t see the cancer.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: She showed us how, for this patient, additional scans, an ultrasound and an MRI, revealed a large cancerous mass.\par \par \uc2 DR. SARAH FRIEDEWALD: The area lights up very quickly.\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Thirty-eight states now require patients to be notified about their breast density. And the \uc2 FDA\uc2  is planning to announce regulations on informing women about their tissue type and screening options.\par \par \uc2 Patients should ask their doctor about their tissue type. And if additional tests are needed, make sure to get a medical order and check if their insurance company requires pre-approval and covers the cost, ranging from a few hundred to thousands of dollars.\par \par \uc2 Feinberg told us she pays about $2,000 out of pocket each year for an MRI, after insurance coverage, but, she says, it`s worth it.\par \par \uc2 EVE FEINBERG: I think just to know that, if something is detected, it will be detected at an early stage.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROXANA SABERI: Only 16 states and Washington, D.C., require insurers to cover additional imaging. But if breast cancer is caught early, the five- year survival rate is 99 percent -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Roxana, thank you so much for that great reporting and information. It is such an important topic.\par \par \uc2 I actually went today to get my mammogram and sonogram. It took about an hour-and-a-half. And while not comfortable for a few minutes, we all know this can save lives.\par \par \uc2 So, tomorrow, we`re going to take a look at the racial disparity, the survival rate for women of color, which is much lower than for white women. Why is that, and what can be done? That`s tomorrow right here.\par \par \uc2 There is shock and sadness in Hollywood tonight following the tragic death of beloved Will & Grace actor Leslie Jordan.\par \par \uc2 That story when we return.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Some sad news tonight. Emmy-winning actor Leslie Jordan died after crashing his car in Hollywood. Police are investigating whether Jordan had a medical episode before the crash.\par \par \uc2 With his Southern drawl and his really sharp sense of humor, Jordan was best known for his role in Will & Grace. His hilarious \uc2 Instagram\uc2  videos during the pandemic drew millions of views. Leslie Jordan was 67.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 20 dry shampoo products have been recalled because they may contain a chemical that causes cancer. What you need to know when we return.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: There`s a nationwide voluntary recall on nearly 20 dry shampoo aerosol products.\par \par \uc2 Unilever\uc2 , which makes the products under the brand name Dove, Nexxus, Suave, and TRESemme, say they may contain elevated levels of benzene, which is known to cause cancer. Customers are urged to stop using the products and go to \uc2 Unilever\uc2 `s Web site for reimbursement instructions.\par \par \uc2 This was a big day for the chief groundskeeper at the White House. We will tell you about the honor he received for 50 years on the job. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: We wanted to end tonight on a remarkable milestone.\par \par \uc2 Presidents come and go, but a constant for the White House is Dale Haney. He`s the chief White House groundskeeper, and, today, he celebrated 50 years of service, posing with President Biden and the first lady as they planted a tree in his honor.\par \par \uc2 Besides keeping the grounds in tip-top shape, he`s also walked the first family`s dogs for years, including the Bidens` German shepherd, Commander.\par \par \uc2 When I covered the White House, Dale was always there making sure the grounds were perfect for visitors.\par \par \uc2 Dale, congratulations to you, and thanks for your service to our country.\par \par \uc2 And that`s tonight`s \uc2 CBS\uc2  EVENING NEWS. I`m Norah O`Donnell. We will see you back here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Good night.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221027eiao0008d\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Sigourney Weaver Live\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Gayle King, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 1,646 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Our next guest is very familiar with that not so pretty skyline this morning, a little bit rainy out there, a native New Yorker and three-time Academy Award nominee, Sigourney Weaver is best known for -- well, take your picks. She has pegged her role in "Alien" in 1979. She`s appeared in so many movies that you know and love.\par \par \uc2 We`re talking "Ghostbusters," we`re talking "Avatar," "Gorillas in the Mist," "Working Girl." I could go on. You probably are in your own head.\par \par \uc2 Her new movie it`s called, "Call Jane." Weaver stars alongside Elizabeth Banks and Weaver plays a character named Virginia inspired by the leader of a real-life secret group of women who worked to provide abortions in 1960s Chicago. That would be before the landmark Roe v. Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Call Jane.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In this clip, Virginia works to convince Banks` character Joy to keep working with the group.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Call Jane.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow. Sigourney Weaver joins us now.\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for being here. This movie is uncannily well-timed to the point where when I first watched it, I thought "How did they make it so quickly?"\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that`s such a good point though.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: How did how did the meaning of the film change as the national, political, and legal picture changed?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER ("Call Jane" actor): Well, I remember it was difficult for us to find a State in which to film "Call Jane." I didn`t really think about it at the time, but I think it was a foreshadowing of the overturning of Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When did you actually film that?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: We filmed in Connecticut in the end.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When? When? How long ago?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: So that was 2021.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: 2021.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: States would look at the script or the ideas in the film, "Eh, we don`t want that."\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: "We don`t want that kind of film done here."\par \par \uc2 So we were also making it for about 25 cents that might have had something to do -- but anyway, it`s -- I was alive during pre-Roe versus Wade and what`s interesting to me is that the landscape has changed a lot in terms of women`s access to medication.\par \par \uc2 But you know, I think of this as women`s healthcare --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: Basic women`s healthcare, and so women don`t have to provide abortions for other women now, but it is -- now, they are criminalizing anyone who helps a woman trying to get abortion and criminalizing the woman, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So the polls all show that most people in the country are where you are on this issue. They support a woman`s right to some access and yet, the issue is very divisive.\par \par \uc2 People who are opposed are extremely opposed. Were you concerned in taking on this role and making this movie that that could lead to issues?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: You know, I respect other people`s points of view, completely and, you know -- and so I feel that it certainly is a subject that we`re now all talking about, and I think that that`s very healthy. You know, it`s part of life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The movie got to me because it just struck a chord with the decisions that we have to make and what we women, as women people had to go through. They`re saying, you and Elizabeth Banks are calling your performances standout. We totally agree. Two thumbs up for you.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But what did this role mean to you personally, as you`re doing -- reading the script, and you`re acting it out? Did it make you think -- and I`m not even talking about your own life? But what did it mean to you personally, to play this character?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: Well, you know, I loved Virginia right away, I think she was a lifelong activist in the antiwar protests, but I think women were largely sidelined by the men there.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Call Jane.")\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: So finally, Virginia has found this know, is behind this organization that she really believes in. She runs it well.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: And she is, I think, quite a great leader. You know, she just -- this is like a sisterhood of women taking care of other women.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: And I think it is very touching in that way and Elizabeth plays a housewife who, for health reasons, has to get an abortion and our group is there for her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the callus doctor was not really a doctor at one point, describes it as scooping seeds out of a pumpkin.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And I just thought, just to relegate it to that, Sigourney.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. When you walked in, there were whispers, "Sigourney is here." We`re all fans. But then they were followed by whispers about your pin, and it says, "Bans off our bodies." And it`s powerful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why did you decide to wear that? And why did you decide to use your voice for topics like this?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: Well, we were co-sponsored by Planned Parenthood at our premiere in LA, and they gave us these pins, "Bans off our bodies." I don`t think I don`t think most people want the government involved in their very personal choices about their health and their lives, and I think that was - - I mean, I just feel such a connection with the women we took care of in the movie.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, with what`s going on now, women need our protection and our support to go through what is really a serious crisis.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you think of this idea that if people want to stop abortion, which no one wants abortions to happen, that we`re focusing on the wrong gender. We should be focusing on the men, because men cause nine, they cause 100 percent of all unwanted pregnancies and unwanted pregnancies are --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A hundred percent, yes.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: I think that`s very refreshing to hear you say that because that is true. People don`t -- men should be more involved in this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Absolutely. And those of us who have sons need to make sure that they`re aware of that.\par \par \uc2 Can I just ask you about "Avatar?" Are you as excited about that --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As the rest of the world?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, the rest of the people on the planet are?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: Well, I`m sure I will be in November when we start to promote it around the world. No, it`s a great story, a story about family - -\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re happy with how it turned out?\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: I haven`t seen it, of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you did it.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: It`s all under wraps. I did it. I lived through it. All that underwater diving. So, I am as excited as any of you to see it in fact, you know.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We can`t wait for that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is Sigourney Weaver good in it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am excited.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: So good.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m excited for people to see "Call Jane." It would take people back to a time in this country that feels closer than it should.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, and speaking of that, "Call Jane" hits theaters on Friday. Sigourney Weaver, thank you so much for joining us.\par \par \uc2 SIGOURNEY WEAVER: Well, my pleasure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So guys, apparently there is a new robot out that will fold clothes and I think, why do we --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`re going to get one?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No. Why do we need this robot? So I actually don`t fold any clothes. I don`t fold my t-shirts. I don`t fold my shorts. I don`t think it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You just crumple it up and throw them in a drawer?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I just crumple it up and it take it from the dryer and I throw it in. I can always find it. It doesn`t matter what it looks like. It`s a t-shirt, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But it is messy. You don`t like -- you don`t mind having --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Organized mess.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I make the bed so it looks good. I don`t make the bed so I can find it. It`s just get the shirt when you need it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s a passionate take.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I feel very strongly about this.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So now you know that Tony wears wrinkled underwear. Do with that what you will, America.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Don`t fold your clothes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will do it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It save time.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Tony is like, what underwear?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it -- yes, what underwear?\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221027eiao000b6\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For October 23, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Ed O`Keefe, Christopher Krebs, Margaret Brennan, Holly Williams,  Charlie D`Agata \par \uc2 9,150 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan in Washington.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: With just 15 days to go until midterm Election Day, both sides are making their closing arguments about why they deserve your vote.\par \par \uc2 Campaign `22 is hitting the homestretch now. With Democrats facing economic headwinds, is President Biden a drag on some Democratic tickets? House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will be here.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we will talk with the administration`s senior energy security adviser, \uc2 Amos Hochstein\uc2 , about those stubbornly high gas prices.\par \par \uc2 Then, we will look at the surge of respiratory virus cases affecting the very young with former FDA Commissioner Dr. \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And we will get more on political misinformation and election security with tech journalist Kara Swisher and CBS News cybersecurity expert and analyst Chris Krebs.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 On this last full week in October, polls continue to show some incredibly tight races across the country. Can Democrats revive their stalled momentum and get their supporters to the polls, or will Republicans take control of the House and possibly the Senate?\par \par \uc2 President Biden has been on the campaign trail and Pennsylvania telling reporters, it ain`t over until it`s over, quoting Yogi Berra, of course.\par \par \uc2 Senior White House and political correspondent Ed O`Keefe reports.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 AUDIENCE: Fetterman! Fetterman!\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE (voice-over): From Pennsylvania...\par \par \uc2 LT. GOVERNOR JOHN FETTERMAN (D-Pennsylvania): Dr. Oz celebrated when Roe v. fell.\par \par \uc2 (BOOING)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: ... to Arizona.\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR GLENN YOUNGKIN (R-Virginia): Hello, Arizona!\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: ... Democratic and Republican candidates are making their final arguments.\par \par \uc2 BETO O`ROURKE (D-Texas Gubernatorial Candidate): There`s this attack on our democracy, on the very right to vote.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Republicans are sticking to a message focused on the economy and crime.\par \par \uc2 MEHMET OZ (R-Pennsylvania Senatorial Candidate): This is an economic and health crisis. A doctor can help fix it. I will keep us safe, cut your taxes and protect our jobs.\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R-Arizona Senatorial Candidate): You think you and your family deserve better than open borders and fentanyl and double-digit crime increases?\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: While many Democrats believe a focus on abortion rights will help bring out their base.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): The Republican Party have been very clear that it is their agenda to have a national ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: For his part, President Biden has been touting his work to curb inflation and cut gas prices. Last week, he approved the release of more oil from America`s Strategic Reserve, but denied any connection to the looming election.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): I have been doing this for how long now? It`s not politically motivated at all.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Despite the president`s work, many Democrats in the most competitive races don`t want to be seen with him.\par \par \uc2 QUESTION: Tim Ryan in Ohio said he doesn`t want you there. Warnock said -- wouldn`t say. Do you think they`re making a mistake?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : No, there have been 16 I have already done. And we`re getting a lot more asked.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: One Democrat who is in big demand, former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who`s now on the airwaves for Democrats in key races...\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA (Former President of the United States): You can count on \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2 . Make sure he can count on you.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: ... and plans to campaign in Georgia, Michigan, Nevada, and Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In the coming days, attention will focus on Pennsylvania, where Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  and Republican Mehmet Oz will meet in their one and only debate.\par \par \uc2 And here in Washington, there are looming fights over raising the debt limit and future funding for the war in Ukraine, as Republicans say they will force a rethinking on those issues if they take control of Congress -- Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ed, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we are joined now by Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and it`s great to have you here at the table, Speaker.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): My pleasure. Thank you. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You have said a lot is going to be determined by turnout. But you`ve heard our \uc2 CBS\uc2  estimates have the Republicans taking the House with 224 seats. How do you shift the momentum?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Well, let`s just say, first and foremost, good morning. It`s Sunday morning, 15 days or so before the election vote.\par \par \uc2 Vote -- people are already voting. We`re very pleased with our early vote for our owning-the-ground initiative. I -- it`s interesting to me, because, for a year-and-a-half, the media has been saying, oh, they`ve gone -- it`s gone, the president`s party always loses in the off-year.\par \par \uc2 And now we`re down to the stretch. And we`re down to very close races. And we feel very confident. I have been in over 20 states since Congress adjourned in the last month or so. And I see very clearly that the ownership of the ground is with us.\par \par \uc2 It`s about getting out the vote. Everything else is a conversation compared to that. But, in order to do that, you have to have inspiration. You can`t run on empty.\par \par \uc2 And the fact is, is that, when I hear people talk about inflation, as I heard him there, we have to change that subject. Inflation is a global phenomenon -- phenomenon.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : The E.U., the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , the U.K., the British, have a higher inflation rate than we do here.\par \par \uc2 It`s not -- the fight is not about inflation. It`s about the cost of living. And if you look at what we have done to bring down the cost of prescription drugs, to bring down the cost of -- of energy and the rest in our legislation, you will see that that has been opposed every step of the way by the Republicans, and they have no plan for lowering the cost of living or helping with inflation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Absolutely, inflation is a global problem. And it`s hitting a lot of countries very hard.\par \par \uc2 But there is also that question of fiscal spending. Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , who serves in leadership alongside you, said the following earlier this week:\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE JAMES CLYBURN (D-South Carolina): All of us are concerned about these rising costs. And all of us knew this would be the case when we put in place this recovery program. Any time you put more money into the economy, prices tend to rise.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Did you also realize at that time that the congressional spending would add to inflation? Did you see that risk then?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Well, first -- first of all, government spending does -- we had a pandemic, and that brought down unemployment from 7 percent to 3.5 percent now. It put people back to work, children back in school, inoculations in the arm, and it helped take us through that stage of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But let me just say that, because of people -- more people working and the rest, the national -- the deficit has been cut in half from $2.8 to $1.4 trillion. That is a big change. So, it is -- it`s, yes, we had to take a step forward to solve the pandemic problem, but we did so in a way that would reduce the national deficit. And that is -- cannot be ignored.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But on things like sending, you know, those $1,400 checks, putting cash out there.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Yes, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean, didn`t that end up contributing to inflation? Do you have any regrets about the bills you passed and how you structured them?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : No, absolutely not, because this -- that was necessary for people to survive. Our purpose...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The risk, though, was that it was inflationary.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : But the point is, is that, when you reduce unemployment, it`s inflationary. That is a fact.\par \par \uc2 When I was a new member of Congress, I was told that unemployment was dangerously low in our hearings on inflation and unemployment. Unemployment is dangerously low for what it does to inflation.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is, the point is, is that this is about helping America`s working families meet their needs. And that was essential to them, less inflationary than a $2 trillion tax cut for the high end that the Republicans gave and we`re still paying a price for, $2 trillion, 83 percent of the benefits going to the top 1 percent.\par \par \uc2 So we feel proud of what we`ve done. We feel proud of the president to help America`s working families to lower their cost, and, in doing so, to reduce the deficit.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, on the issues that voters tell CBS News are important to them, abortion ranks number seven, up top, economy, inflation, crime, immigration.\par \par \uc2 Was it a miscalculation to believe that the momentum from striking down Roe vs. Wade was going to help Democrats? Why not talk more about these issues around the economy?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Well, I can just say this.\par \par \uc2 Nobody ever -- elections are about the future. They`re about the economy. Everybody knows that. Nobody said we`re doing abortion, rather than the economy, but it`s -- it`s about both.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you that that issue is very, very provocative and encouraging people to vote across the country...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : ... having just been there, not sitting in Washington, but while going around the country.\par \par \uc2 But I will say this.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Twenty-four percent of likely voters, according to our polling, are motivated by this issue of Roe.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : And that`s a good number.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you need them to win, right?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They need to show up.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : But let me -- yes, let me say this.\par \par \uc2 Here`s what happens. Let`s just talk about what this comes down to in these races and why I`m optimistic about it. I mean, it`s a fight. These are close races. It`s like the Olympics. And a half-a-second, you can be gold, silver, bronze, or honored to be an Olympian. So these are tough fights.\par \par \uc2 But that`s what they are. And it`s a big change from what the media was -- media thread of, oh, they can`t possibly win. No, we can possibly win.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing. In these districts, the district -- the Republicans have said that, if they win, they want to subject Medicare, Social Security, health blackmail, to lifting the debt ceiling. They have said they would like to review Medicare and Social Security every five years.\par \par \uc2 They have said that they would like to make it a discretionary spending, that Congress could decide to do it or not, rather than mandatory. So, Social Security and Medicare are on the line. A woman`s right to choose is on the line. The planet is on the line, issues that relate to prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : For years, we`ve been trying to get the -- the secretary to negotiate for lower prices. We couldn`t get it done, until we had the Democratic president and a strong majority enough in the Congress to get it done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about that, the future and the new Congress potentially.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: You told Andrea Mitchell earlier this week: "We need generational change, but, in some cases, there`s no substitute for experience."\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Will you remain in leadership in the new Congress?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : I`m not talking about that. I`m here to talk about how we win the election.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But to deliver on all these things...\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : I was only paying -- I was only paying Andrea a compliment for all the experience that she has.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I see.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : The -- but the fact is, what we need to...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you may or you may not?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : I`m not here to talk about me. I`m here to talk about the future, America`s working families, for the children. It`s always about the children.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Mm-hmm.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI: And the point is this, that the -- these issues, if you`re a senior, you have a lot at risk.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : If you`re a childbearing age woman, you have a lot at risk, if -- your family does.\par \par \uc2 If you care -- if you care about the planet, you have a lot at risk. They said it`s a hoax.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI: When we had the debate on our funding for addressing the climate crisis, not one Republican vote.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : We talked about -- so, what we need to go forward with is more that we had in our agenda and childcare...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : ... child tax credit, issues like that.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Madam Speaker, I appreciate you coming here today.\par \par \uc2 We did also invite the Republican House leader to join us. He declined the invitation.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And we turn now to former FDA Commissioner, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  board member Dr. \uc2 Scott Gottlieb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gottlieb, it is great to see you.\par \par \uc2 But I have other tough issues to ask you about, including this surge in respiratory infections among children in 33 states now. We`re seeing some pediatric units overwhelmed. Why is this happening, and why is it having such a powerful impact?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2  (Former FDA Commissioner): Well, look, we`re seeing a surge right now in Respiratory Syncytial Virus.\par \par \uc2 This is a virus that we typically see in the late winter. We started to see cases in the summertime. And we`re seeing a peak in cases right now. This is not unlike the season last year, where we also saw an early peak in those cases.\par \par \uc2 Some people ascribe it to the fact that children have been somewhat removed from these circulating pathogens, so you don`t have as much immunity in the population generally. So it`s changed the typical cycle for this virus. And we`re seeing these waves of infection from RSV earlier in the season than we would normally see.\par \par \uc2 For most people, this is a self-limiting infection. It`s an upper respiratory infection. It causes cold-like symptoms. But, for children under the age of 2, it can be a serious infection. And it`s especially concerning for those who are immunocompromised, children who are immunocompromised, or those in a neonatal intensive care unit, as well as older adults, elderly Americans...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : ... or people who have other kinds of conditions that put them at risk.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, this is happening, you have flu season coming, and we have COVID ticking up.\par \par \uc2 The White House says everyone needs to go out and get their COVID booster by Halloween. Do you agree with that timeline? And how do you protect yourself against all of these respiratory risks?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Well, look, I think now`s a prudent time to not just get the COVID booster, the new bivalent vaccine, but also the flu vaccine.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we have flu cases picking up. It looks like this may be a more aggressive flu season. The predominant strain right now is H3N2. And the vaccine appears to be a good match for the strain. So, the vaccine, based on what we see right now based on the genetic sequence of the strain that`s circulating and the antigen that`s in the vaccine, does suggest that the vaccine is going to be quite protective this season.\par \par \uc2 So it`s a good time to get it. I think, in terms of just protecting yourselves, it`s just the normal things that we advise people to do, wash your hands, try to avoid crowded spaces, if you are someone who`s at risk.\par \par \uc2 And if you don`t feel well, stay home. Don`t send a child to school if they`re not feeling well, so you don`t expose others to an infection.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Wear a mask?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : So, all the things that we have learned to do to just good -- well, look, I think, if you`re someone who`s at risk or someone who wants to take extra precaution, one-way masking, we know, does work, if you`re going to wear a high-quality mask.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think people are going to be generally wearing them right now. But I think, for people who are at risk, who want extra -- an extra measure of protection, that`s another step...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : ... that can help afford them that.\par \par \uc2 The one thing -- the final thing I will say is that, for parents who have children who have an upper respiratory infection, many times, they`re testing them, finding out it`s not COVID and feeling relieved. I think they still need to be vigilant that it could be RSV, it could be early flu.\par \par \uc2 So, if you see progressive symptoms, seek out help from a doctor. There are treatments that are available that could help children with RSV.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Noted.\par \par \uc2 It is election season. And I want to ask you about a false claim about COVID vaccines that really hit the campaign trail, and it`s been in conservative circles in particular.\par \par \uc2 Under U.S. law, just to be clear, the CDC does not mandate vaccines, as we have talked about here before. The state governors have the authority to do that. Can you explain what the CDC advisers actually did last week?\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Yes, well, this week, they took the recommendation that already existed for children to get vaccinated with the COVID vaccine, which was their general recommendation they issued last year.\par \par \uc2 And they incorporated it into their formal recommendations, which are their permanent recommendations. Some people refer to it as their vaccine schedule. That doesn`t translate into state mandates. There are a lot of vaccines in that schedule. For example, the flu vaccine is in that schedule. They recommend that for children. No states mandate that. The HPV vaccine is in the schedule. Only two states mandate the HPV vaccine for children to attend school, Virginia and Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 Even the dengue fever vaccine is in the schedule. And, obviously, no states mandate the use of that. So, what got started on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  initially was that the CDC recommendations automatically translate into state mandates, which is not true.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons why the CDC went ahead and made this formal recommendation is that it also allows the vaccines to be incorporated into the Vaccines for Children`s Program, which provides federal funding for indigent kids to get access to vaccines. And so that`s part of the impetus.\par \par \uc2 But there will be no state mandates. Quite frankly, I don`t think there should be state mandates. I don`t think we`re at the point right now where we should be considering mandating this vaccine as a condition to attend school. While I think every child should seek out this vaccine, parents should strongly consider vaccinating their kids, I don`t think it should be mandated by states.\par \par \uc2 And I wouldn`t expect to see any state mandating this vaccine.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes. Thank you for your clarity on that, because the misinformation -- misinformation, you just said, started on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  amplified it. But, as we just showed our viewers, you see a number of candidates and sitting governors spreading this. You have presidential candidate potentially Mike Pompeo. You have the governor of Virginia, the governor of Utah, Senator Tom Cotton of Arkansas, the GOP candidate for governor of Arizona, Kari Lake. They`re all putting out tweets with this false idea.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Right. And...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So there`s clearly a political force in an anti-vaccine sentiment.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : Well, look, I think, from a public health standpoint, the more that this becomes a political matter, and the more people make this a political matter, the campaign against mandates bleeds into a campaign against the vaccine itself.\par \par \uc2 And people generally don`t take away the nuance of those messages, if there is any nuance in those messages in the first place. They hear the skepticism against the vaccine, and then they`re less likely to consider it for themselves, even where it makes sense from a clinical standpoint.\par \par \uc2 I didn`t see a lot of people stepping up and saying, look, my state won`t mandate the vaccine. We believe these are decisions that should be left to pediatricians and parents. But I would strongly encourage parents to make sure that their children are up to date with all the available vaccines, especially in advance of this flu and COVID season.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : That would have been an appropriate message, in my view, for a governor to say who wanted to say, look, we`re not going to mandate it, but we still think it`s important.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : I -- the closest I saw anyone coming to that was Spencer Cox in Utah.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 And, to be clear, the CDC wasn`t as clear as they could have been either.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 SCOTT GOTTLIEB\uc2 : That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, that`s why we wanted you to help translate that for our viewers today.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Gottlieb, I have to leave it there. So good to see you again.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to threats to election security and efforts to combat false information online.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined by CBS News cybersecurity expert and analyst Chris Krebs and Kara Swisher, tech journalist and host of the podcasts On With Kara Swisher and Pivot.\par \par \uc2 Good to have you both here.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER (Host, Pivot): Thanks for having us.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We were just talking about this to Dr. Gottlieb, but on the election front -- and, Kara, I want to start with you -- there have been these recent studies talking about \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , TikTok approving even ads with political misinformation on them.\par \par \uc2 How does this continue to happen with vaccines and with political misinformation? Why can`t these platforms get control?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Because they don`t want to make those decisions.\par \par \uc2 They want to opt themselves out of decision-making on editorial. And you heard \uc2 Nick Clegg\uc2 , who`s running -- is number two at \uc2 Facebook\uc2  right now, essentially, just gave an interview where he talked about that: We shouldn`t be the arbiters of this. We shouldn`t do this. It should be government.\par \par \uc2 But then -- then we have the First Amendment, so government can`t intercede. So, I think they don`t -- there`s just so much of it, and it floods the zone so much, and they make money from it, that they don`t want to -- they don`t want the responsibility for it, even though it`s on their platforms and they should have the responsibility.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And some of it sort of dances or flirts with distortions. And so you have to be in that government business of spotting this.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, how do you even do that?\par \par \uc2 FORMER DIRECTOR CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, it`s -- it`s a challenge, as Kara pointed out with the First Amendment issues.\par \par \uc2 You know, DHS launched their Disinformation Governance Board earlier in the year, and that was not actually met with any kind of fanfare, and they ultimately sort of backed down, even though efforts they were taking through that group were -- have been under way for a decade or more within DHS.\par \par \uc2 So, one of the things that we looked at back in the 2020 election was not so much the specific claims or the specific elements of mis- or dis- information, but it was more the thematics that were emerging, so not pinning it to a single individual, instead looking at, hey, these are some of the claims we were seeing, and here are the security controls or preventions in place that would actually not allow that to happen in an election.\par \par \uc2 And we`re seeing some continuation of that, but, once again, there`s so much of it. And, once it hits, it`s really hard to go bit by bit and pull it back.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to show our viewers some of the video, that our \uc2 CBS\uc2  affiliate out in Arizona filmed multiple incidents of possible voter intimidation.\par \par \uc2 They had armed individuals wearing tactical gear, as you can see there, camped out by a voter drop box. There were other two with handguns that were concealed yesterday.\par \par \uc2 So, some of this is driven towards a very specific conspiracy that emerged, right? So, Chris, how does the \uc2 FBI\uc2  head this off, and not allow it to go into political violence?\par \par \uc2 FORMER DIRECTOR CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, the -- I think the important thing here is that, if any voter or election official sees these sort of things happening in Arizona -- Arizona, they -- they make a report. They tell the -- the relevant officials, and they can investigate, and they can look at these people.\par \par \uc2 They can investigate, ensure -- and then there`s a deterrence measure in place.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it takes the guy with the tactical gear before that happens? I mean...\par \par \uc2 FORMER DIRECTOR CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, and -- and that`s the biggest challenge here, as I see it, is that the continued efforts to delegitimize the 2020 election are resulting in candidates that are incentivized to push these lies.\par \par \uc2 But, also, then you have a radicalization and activation across the voter base that are effectively -- this is performative. These -- this doesn`t happen. You know, the claims of mules and ballot trafficking, it doesn`t happen, particularly in a way that someone like this would -- would be able to detect anything.\par \par \uc2 Moreover, what`s happening is, the people that are being accused of ballot trafficking or being a mule actually report it to the officials and say: Here`s my name. Here`s my contact information. I was accused of this.\par \par \uc2 Would any legitimate fraudster or mule do that and then report on themselves? Of course not. So, it`s all performative. And, unfortunately, I am concerned that it results in violence.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: It`s -- more importantly, chaos is the point...\par \par \uc2 FORMER DIRECTOR CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: ... chaos and discord. It`s actually from the Russian playbook.\par \par \uc2 FORMER DIRECTOR CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Chaos is what they want, discord, problems, making people radicalized, radicalizing people.\par \par \uc2 And it`s -- it`s in the mainstream. Before, it was sort of -- it bubbles up from the bottom, but now it`s pushed down from the top. And so, just last night, you saw Trump say a number of things that were radicalizing. I don`t know what else to say it.\par \par \uc2 And so that goes down, and then it goes back up. And it`s -- it`s hit the mainstream now. They don`t even need these -- these ads or anything else, because it`s now infected some of the public so much.\par \par \uc2 And so this is where people get their news, a lot of news.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FORMER DIRECTOR CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: And, before this, we had sort of information obesity. Not enough people got good news. Now they get a lot of information, but not actual facts.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`re going to take a quick break and come back, talk more about this in a moment.\par \par \uc2 Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will be right back with a lot more Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Don`t go away.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 We continue our conversation now with Chris Krebs and Kara Swisher.\par \par \uc2 Kara, I want to pick up with you. We`ve been talking about social media. \uc2 Twitter\uc2  may find a new buyer, billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, on Friday.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: On -- Friday is the day, I believe.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes, supposedly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Senator Mark Warner, who chairs the Intel Committee, has said that there is no American more dependent upon the largess of the communist party than \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 . He`s the CEO of \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , \uc2 SpaceX\uc2 , the rocket ships. \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  saying that the administration`s going to have to do national security reviews potentially of businesses he owns.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, how much of a risk is it if this transaction goes through?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: It depends on what they find, I guess. You know, I mean I think the issue is - is he -- he already does a lot of defense work. A lot with the rockets and everything else. And it`s considered very innovative and has done amazing things. The International Space Station is - is -- depends on \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  and his rockets. And so he does a lot of - you know, obviously, with transportation, that`s another area that`s important to look at, and who owns it. And \uc2 Twitter\uc2  also. Who -- this is a big communications platform, even though it`s a terrible business, so who owns it matters. And so, who are his investors? Who is putting money into it, et cetera, et cetera, should be looked at as a matter of course.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But, yes, I mean it`s almost kind of amazing, Chris, that - that there isn`t more scrutiny. I mean there is \uc2 FCC\uc2  scrutiny of ownership of news organizations.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When it comes to social media and a company like TikTok, it`s owned by a Chinese firm. An increasing number of Americans are relying on it for actual news, not just tweeting about themselves but actual news. Is this a risk?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Well, I - I think, just to the - to your point, as more people use these platforms, as they get more information, as they`re shifting away from traditional media that we`re all used to, the intelligence services of our adversary are seeing this shift as well. And so it`s not necessarily specifically about TikTok. It`s all platforms. And it`s all avenues. But TikTok represents a specific risk. And - and a colleague of mine wrote a paper, Dakota Carey (ph) wrote a paper for cyber scoop talking about how it`s not necessarily the data security challenges, it`s that the Chinese Communist Party may have the ability through the parent company of TikTok to actually shape narratives, suppress, lift, shape what we see on a daily basis. And it doesn`t have to be everything you see all the time, it can just be enough.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: It can just be enough, a little bit. And I think about what happens if -- if the Chinese invade Taiwan? What happens in the runup to the `24 election if they`re not happy with some of the more - you know, the stronger stance that this administration is taking in China?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: There`s a significant amount of risk exposure we have here.\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: And then who -- who owns \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ? Who - who are his investors? I think that`s a normal question for a government to ask. You know, the CCP doesn`t own \uc2 CBS\uc2 , to my knowledge, so -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Chinese Community Party, no.\par \par \uc2 But, for \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , I know you know him, and last question here on this. Russia watcher Fiona Hill said Putin plays the egos of big men, gives them a sense that they can play a role, but in reality they`re just direct transmitters of messages from \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . She just gutted \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  when he was pushing pro-Russia positions.\par \par \uc2 I mean, is it really that? I mean are -- are adversaries use business icons to further their foreign policy?\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Why, yes, that`s happened before. I don`t know if you`ve heard about President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I mean people have talked about that. So, yes, I think - I think Elon`s doing this on his own. But he`s -- you know, he`s -- he`s -- a lot of people in Silicon Valley are also mouthing the same thing. There`s sort of a certain class of tech bros (ph) that are into this idea.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you still think this transition - transaction --\par \par \uc2 KARA SWISHER: Well, they have no -- foreign policy experience, or they have no business doing this, but that doesn`t stop them, which is about everything. There are excerpts on everything case (ph) because they`re the richest people in the world, in case you didn`t know.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s slightly dangerous. I don`t know, Chris?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: A little bit.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We could keep talking about this.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to keep talking about this but I have to take a break here -\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER KREBS: Next week.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And bring some important information to you from voters themselves, actually.\par \par \uc2 We wanted to better understand how Americans could impact the vote in the year`s elections. And our CBS News campaign 2020, I -- 2022 -- sorry, it`s moving quickly, eye in America is tracking four groups of what we are calling election influencers.\par \par \uc2 And on Friday we spoke to some pressured parents. These are voters who describe themselves as feeling post-pandemic stress over finances and kids.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 LASHAWN (Democrat): I feel like Biden is doing a pretty good job. But my concern is like the crime. I want to know who is - you know, what exactly will they do about this because it`s -- it`s out of control in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What about in Arkansas and Texas?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE (Independent): I live more in the suburbs. So it`s not as prevalent in my neighborhood. But it`s still something that`s concerning.\par \par \uc2 JOHN (Republican): Not so much. Greg Abbott, our governor, does a great job. And the only thing, you know, that we`ve been having trouble with is, you know, the border.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, immigration is a concern for you in these upcoming elections?\par \par \uc2 JOHN: We just left a pandemic. When you have thousands upon thousands every day crossing over, they don`t have to get tested. You know, we don`t know what they`re bringing in.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, some of those pandemic-era restrictions are still in place along the border. What do you need to see that would change your impression of immigration in this country?\par \par \uc2 JOHN: I - I really like how DeSantis and Abbott have been taking them and - and shipping them out to bluer states so they can recognize the problems that we`ve been having in - in Texas and in Arizona. You know, we -- Greg Abbott needs to have a lot more control over the border itself.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But you will still vote Republican in this upcoming --\par \par \uc2 JOHN: Yes. I - I would still support Greg Abbott because his running -- he`s running up against Beto, Beto, and, you know, he -- he`s all about the open border.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Show of hands, will all of you accept the election results from these midterm races in 2022?\par \par \uc2 Yes?\par \par \uc2 All of you trust the elections that -- and the systems to run them?\par \par \uc2 How would all of you describe the current state of the U.S. economy?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE: Oh, I would say scary.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Scary?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Lashawn, how would you describe it?\par \par \uc2 LASHAWN: I would say it`s unstable because it`s just so much going on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: John, in one - in one word, how would you describe the U.S. economy?\par \par \uc2 JOHN: Horrible.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Horrible?\par \par \uc2 JOHN: In regards to -- with the inflation. I - I watched last week a bag of six-ounce broccoli go from $1.99 to this week being $2.49.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Can I see a show of hands, is anyone driving less because of the cost of gasoline?\par \par \uc2 So, you`re still filling up your gas tank and still driving despite prices climbing higher.\par \par \uc2 JOHN: Just to go to work. I got - I drive 11 hours to go to work.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JOHN: I live in east Texas, work in New Mexico. So, I have to buy it regardless.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Have high prices changed the way you live your life?\par \par \uc2 LASHAWN: I still have to do whatever I need to do to get around, but, I don`t know, what can you do? You have to just adjust. It`s not just the, you know, heating, the cost of living, the electricity, everything is just -- it`s just really hard.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, John and Stephanie, have you had to make changes in your life because of inflation?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE: Yes, ma`am.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What have you done?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE: We have cut back on our going out to eat, kind of eliminated the Amazon Prime, \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , limiting that kind of stuff. I have looked for ways to budget better with groceries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you think that President Biden has control or greater influence over the economy?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE: I don`t think it`s just as much Congress and also state and local as it is Biden. I won`t think it`s just one person.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What are your biggest concerns about raising children in America right now? John?\par \par \uc2 JOHN: The whole woke culture affecting our children. All these elementary schools and middle schools having woke culture pushed on them from the LGBT Plus community for sexual identity and gender. We should be pushing the actual school studies, math, social studies, science, not, you know, gender studies or sexual identification.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: What have you seen that makes you concerned?\par \par \uc2 JOHN: In the district that I am at - I - that I live in, there hasn`t been really anything going on. But I`m originally from Iowa. And just recently there was some protests at an Iowa school for bringing in a transgender art -- art show.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So you`re not concerned about your kids in Texas, you`re seeing this on the news, that`s what`s raising your concern?\par \par \uc2 JOHN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Lashawn, I`d like to get you to weigh in on this.\par \par \uc2 LASHAWN: I can also agree with some of his points. I really will say sex education. I feel like some things, you know, are brought to the children`s attention they wouldn`t even think about.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you have eight kids. I imagine you have some pretty specific ideas in your mind when you`re speaking about this?\par \par \uc2 LASHAWN: Yes, I do. Yes.\par \par \uc2 The children aren`t -- you know, they`re really influenced. You can teach them one thing at home, but when they go to school, they`re just as much influenced by their teachers and their surroundings. And we should have more input, the parents, of what we want them to learn.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Stephanie, what -- what`s your biggest concern? And if you want to react to any of that, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE: I - I agree. The other issue has to do with Covid. There`s less teachers in the school district. And, because of that, they have student choice school days. So, the students can choose whether or not they want to go to school that day because there`s not enough teachers or bus drivers to get them there. And so I worry about their education.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: When you said your top issue was crime, what did you have in mind?\par \par \uc2 LASHAWN: Robberies and, you know, murders, you know, stabbings and -- it`s like it -- every time you turn on the news, somebody killed their baby. And, you know, they`re losing their mind. It`s like people all -- everywhere are doing everything and it`s not -- it`s like they`re out of control. Nobody`s stopping them.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: John talked about how concerned he was about the border in his state of Texas and migrants coming into the country.\par \par \uc2 LASHAWN: I mean, there`s nothing wrong with helping, but there -- we have more problems here in our country. I feel like there`s so - so much focus on helping immigrants, and it`s not enough focus on the people here that might need assistance.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Do you blame your mayor and your governor for that or do you blame Washington for that?\par \par \uc2 LASHAWN: It`s everybody. Everyone. Washington, the mayor. I mean, using -- it`s like they - they help who they want. Certain people, certain groups of people, they`ll help them and then they`ll neglect someone else.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Often when we do these focus groups, we have people of -- from different party affiliations disagreeing with each other, but I`m hearing all of you echo a lot of the same concerns and agreeing with each other. None of you are very optimistic about the country right now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE: No.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: For a closer look on that additional release from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve and its impact on prices, we`re joined by \uc2 Amos Hochstein\uc2 , the special presidential coordinator for international energy affairs.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Good to have you back here.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has released more oil from the SPR than all previous presidents combined. Republicans are saying this is depleting the stockpile and it`s a national security risk.\par \par \uc2 When you were last here, you said that this would end towards the end of the year. Did you know then that three weeks before the election there was going to be a release?\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN (Presidential Coordinator On Energy Security): Well, first, it`s good to be here. Thank you for having me again.\par \par \uc2 The president, before I came here last time, the president was in the middle already of releasing 180 million barrels. This is still part of that 180 million barrels. So, the announcement that was made the other day is because it takes some time to - to get those barrels released. And these barrels will be released in December.\par \par \uc2 The president`s been committed to bringing down gasoline prices. When -- you mentioned when I was here last, we talked about then prices were coming down from $5 and we were about $4.50. Today they`re at $3.80 or $3.79. And, in fact, most Americans see prices around $3.40 to $3.50. So, he`s been committed to bringing those prices down. And this release is just part of that process that`s been going on for months.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And he says he may do more, but when we last spoke, you said private industry was expected to pick it up and so that you wouldn`t have to keep doing these emergency releases. Why isn`t that working?\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: Well, I think it is working. The private industry is increasing supply and production.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Then why would he have to do more release.\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: Well, what the president said is that we are going to be very watchful to see what happens around the world. There are a lot of geopolitical events. The Russian invasion. I don`t know what Putin`s going to do in December or in January. So, we have to be ready for that. So what the president said is, we`re going to complete the 180 that we`ve - that he has announced many months ago, as he`s been doing monthly. That will continue on into December. And then, as we are - we are making sure that we are prepared, so if we need to do additional releases, we can do them very, very quickly.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s going to be a European ban on Russian crude imports on December 5th. That`s going to have an impact globally. How much of a shock is that going to have on energy prices ahead of the holidays?\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: Well, I think that`s exactly what we`re talking about, right? So, the president made this release -- announced this release that will happen in December, around the time that the ban comes in. We are also working with Europe and other G7 partners around the world, countries in Asia, to see if we can make sure that we continue to have the Russian barrels on the market, but ensure that the price that -- and the revenues that Russia gets continue to be depressed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: This is the price cap you`re trying to roll out still?\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: This is the price cap. Correct.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So that price cap, though, and the negotiations around this has raised speculation that that was one of the factors in Saudi Arabia`s decision to pull 2 million barrels a day off the - off the market. And the president has said he`s furious that he`s going to retaliate against Saudi Arabia. Is that what happened here?\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: Well, look, clearly the president said it better than I can, that he was extremely disappointed in the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  decision and that he thought it was a mistake. And, clearly, nobody can argue that it was warranted for any economic reason to do it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that`s what \uc2 OPEC\uc2  says.\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: I understand, but nobody else thinks that it was warranted. And even \uc2 OPEC\uc2 , when they announced at 2 million, then they say, well, we`re not actually going to cut 2 million, we`re just going to cut 900. And then you look a little deeper and it`s really just about 500. So the impact on the market is not going to be as significant.\par \par \uc2 So, this was more of a big, political statement that \uc2 OPEC\uc2  has - has made. And we`re - look --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: How so?\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: Look, at the end of the day, the president was disappointed in what \uc2 OPEC\uc2  did, but his commitment is to do things here in the United States, where we can - where we can have an impact on the American economy, bringing down prices as they have. And, look, think about it this way, when I was here last, in June we were at $120 a barrel of oil and $5 of gasoline. Today we`re at $85.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: $85.\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: $85. That`s - that`s 30 percent.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  has said today they expect an average of $100 a barrel next year. That just came out this morning.\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: Yes, but look at - but the -- in June \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  expected the last quarter of this year to be $140, prices to rise to $140. So the banks keep saying that the prices is going to go up. The president keeps taking decisive action here that has led to these lower prices.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But these are short-term fixes that the release is.\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: These are not short-term fixes, Margaret. These are -- we`ve been in this now for eight, nine months. This is almost a year now of higher prices and the president has taken these actions. So, I don`t think these are short-term. I understand that the banks make their own analysis. But they`ve -- they haven`t been accurate over the time and the -- what is really what you can measure is that we`re down 30 percent on oil prices and we`re down over $1.20 in -- in the United States on gasoline.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, we`re now almost every single week for the last several months, we`ve seen a decline in gasoline prices in the United States. And so we`re working with allies. We`re trying to strengthen the American economy -\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: While also making sure that our allies` economies are strong as well.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Before I let you go, real quick, are you going to be able to close this deal between Israel and Lebanon that would allow for drilling off their coast?\par \par \uc2 AMOS HOCHSTEIN: Well, I think that this is a really great development, an historic agreement between two enemy countries. Lebanon doesn`t even recognize the state of Israel. We`re going to have a deal. We`re going to sign it hopefully this Thursday. And I hope that this continues to commitment -- our commitment to stability in the region and prosperity for both countries.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Amos, good to have you here. Hope to have you back again soon.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 First up, our Holly Williams reports from Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voice over): In the Russian-occupied city of Kherson, in southern Ukraine, the authorities, installed by Moscow, have now ordered all residents to leave ahead of an expected Ukrainian counteroffensive. Kherson was seized by the Russians in the early days of the invasion. Now Ukraine claims that Russia`s planning to blow up a hydroelectric dam in the region to slow the Ukrainian military`s advance and has already laid mines, risking a catastrophic flood.\par \par \uc2 Herman Halushchenko is Ukraine`s energy minister, and told us if the Russians destroy the dam, it could even leave a nuclear power plant without water for cooling.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (on camera): So if the Russians blow the dam, one potential consequence is a nuclear accident.\par \par \uc2 HERMAN HALUSHCHENKO (Ukraine Energy Minister): It could be. It could be.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voice over): As Russian forces struggle to hold onto territory they`ve seized, they`ve launched a new wave of attacks on Ukraine`s cities, including with so-called kamikaze drones. The U.S. says Iran is supplying drones and even has troops on the ground helping the Russians operate them. Ukraine says the main target, with over 300 strikes, is energy facilities. As a harsh winter looms, there have already been blackouts in some parts of the country.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (on camera): What`s the worst case scenario here? Could Ukrainians end up this winter with no heating and no electricity?\par \par \uc2 HERMAN HALUSHCHENKO: I think not. But, frankly speaking, it also will depend on how intensive these attacks would be in the future.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS (voice over): Ukraine says what it`s still asking for is more air defense systems from its friends in the west.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ukraine says that Russian strikes on Saturday cut off power to more than 1.4 million households.\par \par \uc2 Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Holly, thank you.\par \par \uc2 There are currently 100,000 U.S. troops supporting \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies in European countries that surround Ukraine. Our Charlie D`Agata spent time with some in Constantia, Romania.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice over): The Screaming Eagles have landed. The 101st Airborne Division deployed on the very edge of \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory. With Colonel Edwin Matthaidess and Brigadier General John Lubas, we board Blackhawk helicopters and head toward the border with Ukraine. Specifically trained to hit the ground fighting, when the call came, the colonel said his soldiers were ready.\par \par \uc2 COL. EDWIN MATTHAIDESS (Commander, 2nd Brigade Combat Team): And, you know, you could see it on their face that-that all that stuff we had talked about, about being ready, about training hard, you know, not leaving anything on the table, you know, they had a chance to put it into use and they`re -- they`re doing that same stuff here today.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (on camera): Right. It just got real.\par \par \uc2 EDWIN MATTHAIDESS: It did.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice over): We traverse the Black Sea coast across the water from Russian-held territory in Ukraine and Crimea, until we reached the forward operating site, where soon U.S. and Romanian NATO forces were conducting a joint air and ground assault. Live fire tank and artillery rounds took aim at targets, simulating the battlefields of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (on camera): This isn`t a routine military exercise against a hypothetical enemy. This is a real-life combat scenario against a very real enemy, one that`s not so far away.\par \par \uc2 BRIG. GEN. JOHN LUBAS, (Deputy Commander 101st Airborne Division): We`re about 250 miles from the front line of Russian troops. And the way we are dispersed right now, we are ready to transition from our current locations where we`re currently at to combat operations on order.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: It`s the first time the 101st has been headquartered in Europe since the D-day landings, seen here with then General \uc2 Dwight Eisenhower\uc2 , prepared to defend European allies again if called upon to fight. Now, almost 80 years later, the 101st is back.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Go, go, go!\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Making a mark in history once more.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Those military exercises come at a time when broader \uc2 NATO\uc2  nuclear exercises are underway, now entering the second week. They involve 60 aircraft from 14 countries, including U.S. B-52 bombers. They were scheduled long before the war in Ukraine began.\par \par \uc2 Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020221023eian00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Election Influencers And The Midterms\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Gayle King, Margaret Brennan, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 1,371 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Back now at 7:30 here on CBS MORNINGS, and the midterms are less than three weeks away. Every vote counts, but there are blocs of voters that really could sway things. We wanted to understand them more. And to help us, we are joined now by "Face the Nation" moderator, Margaret Brennan, and CBS News elections and surveys director, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Okay guys, so there are these groups that you`ve termed election influencers. They include, if I have this right, pressured parents, Trump true believers, restoring Roe voters, and the young and restless -- no relation to the show.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, starting with you, what can you tell us about these four groups you`ve isolated.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: We want to understand the election through the motivations of the people who are participating in it and get away from the usual labels -- demographic labels, party labels -- we have interviewed so many voters through the course of our CBS News polling, this is what emerged from that data as people tell us, this is how they process this campaign. So we want to bring you that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, so tell us, can you just give us a tick-tock on each one of them? Pressured parents means?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Indeed, pressured parents are people who were dealing with COVID and reported that they felt stressed about that and their kids, and now they`re facing financial pressure after inflation on top of that. That is where we start and that group is somewhat closely divided, leaning a little bit more Republican right now, but it is those dual pressures that really define them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trump true believers?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Trump true believers are people who consider themselves MAGA Republicans and they don`t like the direction of the country. They want the party to be loyal to the former President, and really the center of gravity in the Republican Party right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Restoring Roe voters.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Restoring Roe is very simply single issue voters on abortion. These women say that is what is motivating them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And my favorite category, not related to the show, as Tony pointed out, the young and the restless.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Young and the restless, I call it -- this is a turnout story for the ages, which is to say they are not the largest group in the electorate, but they could really matter if they show up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Under 30, no kids.\par \par \uc2 And Margaret, you spent some time with people in the pressured parents group. What did you learn?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, we`ve been digging into a lot of these topics and this was one of those powerful focus groups we did. Getting parents to talk about what they`ve been through and the collective trauma of COVID is with us now, it`s in the mental health crisis.\par \par \uc2 This mother telling us about her 12-year-old running out of the house screaming like a three-year-old having a meltdown. A dad talking to us about depression. We are just starting to kind of measure what it has done to us, mental health.\par \par \uc2 Also you can measure it in the education crisis. Reading level declined for nine-year-olds at -- has declined by the largest margin in more than 30 years according to the Education Department.\par \par \uc2 A hundred thousand caregivers, gone. And then the cost you pay has gone up.\par \par \uc2 There are very real stresses, and we`ve seen this erupt politically in School Board meetings getting violent over what is even taught in the classroom. The lack of control is powerful.\par \par \uc2 We saw Republicans harness it in the State of Virginia when it came to that election last year to elect a Governor where education was put at the centerpiece of it. So, we`re watching how these voters define themselves because COVID redefined us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Now one of the groups that we are most about is the Trump true believers. Why is it important to understand their perspective?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And don`t we kind of know how they are going to vote or the perception of how they will vote?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: We absolutely know how they are going to vote. They are voting for Republicans, but what makes them important -- two things.\par \par \uc2 One is, when you see Republican candidates talking about election denialism, talking about being loyal to the former President, they know that they need the votes of these folks.\par \par \uc2 So that helps you understand the narrative of the campaign --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And who they`re talking to.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: And who they`re talking to, even though for the rest of voters, we know that the former President is a net negative.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s very much about turnout for a lot of these groups. And then you ask yourself, if those candidates moderate their stances at all, do some of these folks stay home and not get tough.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: People get caught up in hearing, you know, certain narratives, and they say, well, they are isolating many of us, but it is not that they`re isolating many of us, they are speaking to their core audience.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Margaret, the Roe voters that we`ve isolated, what kind of an influence could they have? Are they going to turn out in the numbers the Democrats are hoping they will?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Anthony has been tracking that very carefully and on Sunday`s "Face the Nation" talked about the momentum stalling for Democrats on the back of what had been a really engaging momentum for them.\par \par \uc2 But what we`ve seen is that for this midterm election, it`s not just a referendum on the President and his party. This issue is literally on the ballot in at least five States.\par \par \uc2 So when we talk about turnout, that could matter, that could be an influence there, and when you look at what happened back in August, if you remember, in the traditionally conservative State of Kansas, people were surprised when the overwhelming vote was to hold on to abortion access.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So this is a new dynamic and for years, abortion had been -- and you could speak to that as well that a single issue voter platform for the other side.\par \par \uc2 And so now Democrats are hoping and you heard President Biden just yesterday come out and say his first legislative priority in a new Congress, if Democrats win, will be trying to create a law to protect abortion access, something that hadn`t been done in 50 years. They`re trying to motivate people to turn out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We all can see in the future.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Twenty days and counting for the election. It`s going to be a long night for all of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Look forward to it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Long night, long week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And making this information digestible.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much Margaret Brennan and Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the latest White House response to high gas prices, one of the issues hurting Democrats in the run up to next month`s elections.\par \par \uc2 And here`s a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Here is what you get: Today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That`s all you need.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a deal. That`s our personal invitation to you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221021eiaj000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Biden`s Midterm Abortion Pledge\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Weijia Jiang \par \uc2 2,149 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to continue our look at the midterm elections this morning with the state of the economy, the top issue for voters right now it seems.\par \par \uc2 One big reason for this year`s rising inflation: Gas prices.\par \par \uc2 The national average for a gallon of regular is $3.85. That`s down from last week, but it`s up from a month ago. Republicans think the economy is a winning issue for them, while the Biden administration wants to show it is working to bring the prices down.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at the White House. She`s always a winner. Weijia, before you get started, I have to say welcome back. You`ve got a new baby in the house. We are so glad that you`re here. First day back from baby leave.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome, welcome.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: You guys, thank you so much. I missed you. And I feel like it just happened because I just saw you at the \uc2 White House Correspondents\uc2 ` Dinner, the next morning, guess who went into labor? This one, Jack couldn`t wait to have a party. He wanted in on the action.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: There you go.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: So here we are.\par \par \uc2 And you know what we were talking about then? Gas prices.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Gas prices.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: And here we are. Here we are.\par \par \uc2 So today the President is going to announce another 15 million barrels of oil released from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve, but that`s not exactly new.\par \par \uc2 This is the last of the 180 million barrels that he already authorized to sell back in March when gas prices started soaring as a result of the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The move is not expected to make a significant difference at the pumps though, especially since \uc2 OPEC\uc2  just announced this month that it would cut oil production by up to two million barrels a day.\par \par \uc2 The President will also detail plans to repurchase and replenish the Strategic Reserve when the price of oil drops to $67.00 to $72.00 a barrel compared to the $85.00 it is right now.\par \par \uc2 Now, senior administration officials say the President is prepared to authorize even more oil be released from the National Stockpile with just about three weeks left to go before the midterms. They are really hoping that this message resonates with voters, that they are doing everything that they can, but the fact is those prices are climbing.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So is the President, Weijia, should doing anything else to entice voters as we get ready to go to the polls?\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Yes, Gayle. He is vowing to make abortion rights the top priority for him next year if Democrats maintain control of Congress. He is warning people that that is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD protesting.)\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: He is urging voters to factor in the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. The President and other Democrats running for Senate are pushing to change the filibuster to codify abortion rights, but unless Democrats win a 60-seat majority in the Senate, they will not have the votes to do that -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, all right, Weijia, Jack and Frankie`s mom, that first day back is always fun, getting back up on that bicycle, but you certainly know what you`re doing. Really good to see you. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Coming up next, Vlad Duthiers, he`s got the stories you`ll be talking about later today. Pretty soon, we`ll be welcoming Vlad back from baby leave, one of these days.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 7:43, time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, Vlad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You just can`t stop your head.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. I can`t, but I`ve got to get to these stories because we have some really, really good stories this morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Let`s do it.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So I`m going to just blast right into it. First an update on Iranian rock climber, Elnaz Rekabi.\par \par \uc2 We told you there was concern about her safety after she competed in South Korea without a headscarf. She returned to Iran overnight and heard this.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Elnaz Champion.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Large crowds gathered outside of Tehran`s airport to give her a hero`s welcome, chanting "Elnaz Champion." She was also greeted by family members with some flowers. Check that out.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to state TV, Rekabi apologized to the Iranian people for causing tension that followed speculation she was signaling support for protests against Iran`s hijab laws when she did not wear a headscarf during her climbing competition on Sunday. There you see that photo, that video of her.\par \par \uc2 Rekabi told reporters today that she was busy putting on her gear and forgot to put on her hijab. Iran`s Embassy in South Korea denied reports that she disappeared after the competition.\par \par \uc2 Rekabi left the airport in a van. We don`t know where she went, but hopefully --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We still don`t know where she went.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We still don`t know where she went.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That support says a lot.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: People are out there supporting her.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It does say a lot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Good to see her\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Also, the carefully worded explanation for why she wasn`t wearing the hijab says a lot as well.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would still like to know more about what happened from the time she got in the van and got home because the concern wasn`t coming from us, it was coming from people who know her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: The woman who died in police custody in Iran, Mahsa Amini has galvanized a movement in Iran. We will where it goes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. And around the world.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Around the world.\par \par \uc2 All right, another movement that everybody is talking about, WNBA All-Star Brittney Griner. She just spent her 32nd birthday behind bars in Russia.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Heartbreaking.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is heartbreaking. So Griner`s representatives shared this message from her yesterday and this is very moving: "Thank you everyone for fighting so hard to get me home. All the support and love are definitely helping me."\par \par \uc2 You`ll recall that Griner was arrested in a Moscow Airport eight months ago. Authorities said she had vape canisters with cannabis oil in her luggage.\par \par \uc2 Last night, \uc2 NBA\uc2  superstar, Steph Curry took time out from celebrating Golden State`s NBA championship to support Griner. Listen to what he said during the Warriors` ring ceremony.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEPH CURRY: We want to continue to use our platform and the opportunity to shout out a very special member of the basketball community: Brittney Griner`s birthday is today. She is 32 years old.\par \par \uc2 We want to continue to let her name be known, and we pray that -- it has been 243 days since she has been wrongfully incarcerated in Russia. We hope that she comes home soon and everybody is doing their part to get home.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And let me tell you, everybody is doing their part. There`s been a hashtag that`s been trending for Brittney Griner, #WeAreBG.\par \par \uc2 Billie Jean King tweeted support. Dawn Staley tweeted support. It`s a really big deal to have Steph Curry...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: LeBron James has tweeted support.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: ... talking about this. Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But you know, when we interviewed Cherelle Griner, her wife a couple of weeks ago, she said one of Brittney`s biggest concerns, she talked to her in August was that people would forget her and think that her life didn`t matter.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s so important that she knows and they will tell her that we are still talking about her in this country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: That is right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She has not been forgotten.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No. And in fact, she is saying in that message that you`re giving her life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: By keeping her in your thoughts and your memories.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I believe that`s true. I believe that`s true.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I agree as well. Yes.\par \par \uc2 All right, we are going to end with a record-breaking donation -- I love this story -- by billionaire/philanthropist MacKenzie Scott to the \uc2 Girl Scouts of the USA\uc2 . You all have daughters.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You know what it is like when you`ve got to go out there schlepping those cookies.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Right? Five dollar boxes. This is going to buy a lot of cookies because the Girl Scouts say the $84.5 million gift is the largest single donation in the organization`s history. The money will help the Girl Scouts expand diversity in membership and recover from the effects of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Since she is divorced, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  founder \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  in 2019, MacKenzie Scott has donated more than $12 billion...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: With a B.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: ... with a B dollars, yes, benefiting more than 1,200 groups. Here`s just a few of the generous donations she has made just this year alone.\par \par \uc2 Back in August Scott gave two Beverly Hills Mansions worth a combined $55 million to the nonprofit, California Community Foundation. In March, she gave $436 million to Habitat for Humanity. And get this, she gave $275 million to Planned Parenthood, and in May she gave more than $120 million to the Big Brothers and Sisters of America.\par \par \uc2 Scott is still one of the wealthiest people in the world though, folks. According to Forbes, she ranked number 41 on the Bloomberg Billionaires Index with a net worth of more than $34 billion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Even after all the giving, she still --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She still has got money.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: She still has got money.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: She deserves a round of applause for that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She is using it for good. You go, MacKenzie.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Well done, MacKenzie Scott. Many are going to be benefiting from her donations.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Vlad, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bravo.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: Kane and Katelyn Brown, that`s his wife, talks to us exclusively about their hit new duet and he lets us in to his man cave.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Jessica Chasteen is here along with the nurse she plays in a new film based on the hunt for a serial killer. How the movie highlights the dedication of all nurses.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 We continue the star power with actress and author, Amber Tamblyn. She is here with her new book on women`s power of intuition.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That intuition stuff is real.\par \par \uc2 But first, here is today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 Russian attacks knock out nearly a third of Ukraine`s energy supply as the cold winter months approach.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): This has been about bipartisan support in Congress to continue to make sure that Ukraine can defend itself.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just before the midterms, new video shows chaos and confusion when Florida State Police arrest people for alleged voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 NEIL VOLZ: When people get a voter ID card. what else are they going to presume except that they`re eligible to vote.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Former President Trump is due to be deposed today in a defamation suit over allegations of sexual assault in New York.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Radioactive contamination is found at a Missouri Elementary School causing outrage in that community.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BERNAUGH: And it`s my biggest fear is that our kids are going to end up with illnesses later on in life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And one man raises the bar with a rockin` Halloween light display using drones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221021eiaj000rt\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Russia`s New Tactics; New Phase of Russia`s Invasion; Deep Freeze Across the US; Controversial Voter Arrests; Radioactive Material Shuts Down Missouri Schools; Election Influencers and the Midterms; Biden`s Midterm Abortion Pledge; Controlling the Price of Gas; Kane Brown`s Superstar Success; Holiday Air Travel Tips; The Good Nurse; The Power of Intuition; CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Holly Williams, Jim  Cantore, Ed O`Keefe, Adriana Diaz, Margaret Brennan, Anthony Salvanto,  Weijia Jiang, Vladimir Duthiers, Peter Greenberg \par \uc2 14,070 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Wednesday, hump day. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here, so let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener," it`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of thousands of Ukrainians are without power and water after Russia`s latest attacks. We are on the ground with the latest.\par \par \uc2 KARINE JEAN-PIERRE\uc2  (White House Press Secretary): When it looks like, he is trying to make sure that the Ukrainian people suffer.\par \par \uc2 MAN: President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is expected to release 15 million barrels of oil from the US reserves after \uc2 OPEC\uc2  slashed their production.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Millions of Americans face freezing temperatures this morning, possibly even snow in some places, what you need to know.\par \par \uc2 MAN: All these locations could set record lows.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Confusion in Florida as ex-cons who thought they could vote are arrested for voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 TONY PATTERSON (Registered sex offender): What is voter fraud?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Voting when you`re not supposed to.\par \par \uc2 MAN: NASCAR has suspended driver, Bubba Wallace for one race for his role in the on track fight, Sunday.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Brittney Griner marked eighth month in a Russian jail on her 32nd birthday.\par \par \uc2 Steph Curry acknowledged her before the Warriors` season opener.\par \par \uc2 STEPH CURRY (Professional basketball player): We hope that she comes home soon and everybody is doing their part to get her home.\par \par \uc2 MAN: High fly ball, right field and deep. It`s gone.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Fans celebrating after the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  take game five.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "We want music.")\par \par \uc2 MAN: Look at this horrible rap song that a group of \uc2 Padres\uc2  fans put together.\par \par \uc2 MAN: It`s really going down to yellow and brown. That`s woods in. \uc2 Padres\uc2  are the loose. Let`s go goose.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Kyle Korver tees off.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 Padres\uc2  lose two-nothing.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Give Philly no slack and send them back. That`s what`s in, so bat, Philly, bat. Bat, Philly, bat.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That is a missile. That is a missile.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Power shot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love the expression on his face.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jaws dropped.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He knew he got it. Aaron Judge --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: \uc2 Yankees\uc2  are still in it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow, what a season. Not happy on it on my side of the table. But --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Amazing. I got respect for Aaron Judge and the \uc2 Yankees\uc2  this morning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And if he wins, should Aaron Judge get more money?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He`ll get $50 million or more.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Just a little bit more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: About fifty.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You heard it here from Nate Burleson. We shall see.\par \par \uc2 We welcome you to CBS MORNINGS. We`ve got some incredible stories to share with you today, including exclusive interview. My favorite story with singer Kane Brown. He gave us a tour of his lovely Nashville home, introduced us to his beautiful family, his wife and his two adorable daughters. But he also talked very openly about his difficult journey to start, and let`s just say Mr. Kane Brown has come a long way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait for that. Plus, Jessica Chastain is here, and so is the woman she plays in her new film, "The Good Nurse." It`s all about the quest to stop a real-life serial killer in a hospital, of all places. This true crime story has a lot of twists and turns, you won`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is a true story there. Very inspiring.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But we`re going to begin with Russia`s deliberate efforts to inflict pain on the civilians in Ukraine as the war`s second winter approaches.\par \par \uc2 The Russian government is admitting to strikes on infrastructure targets including power plants that are crucial to keeping people warm in the wintertime. Ukraine`s President says the bombardment has knocked out a large part of the country`s energy supply. Holly Williams is in Kyiv, and she has got more on that part of the story.\par \par \uc2 Holly, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Well, this was the scene of a strike here in Kyiv just last week, right beside a children`s playground. As you can see, they have already filled in the crater, but just imagine what life is like for Ukrainians right now, with so-called kamikaze drones hitting their cities and the power grid under attack.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ukraine says 30 percent of its power stations have been destroyed by Russian strikes in just over a week, causing rolling blackouts.\par \par \uc2 With Ukraine`s harsh winter looming, long-term power outages could be devastating for the military, the public, and everyone`s morale and that`s probably the whole point.\par \par \uc2 So-called suicide drones, which detonate on impact have also pummeled Ukraine cities this week, killing civilians.\par \par \uc2 Iran denies that it is selling them to Russia for use in Ukraine. The US says Iran is lying.\par \par \uc2 Moscow`s tactics in Ukraine are straight out of the Russian military`s playbook. In Syria, Russia and its regime allies have carried out years of deadly airstrikes. The \uc2 UN\uc2  accuses Russia of war crimes, deliberately terrorizing civilians.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, we traveled to some of the worst hit towns.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "The kids are all dead," this man told us. "The trauma was palpable."\par \par \uc2 In Ukraine, they`ve put up a stunning resistance to Russia`s invasion, and the latest attacks don`t seem to have broken their spirit.\par \par \uc2 (WOMAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: "I`d rather sit in the cold with no water and electricity," laughs this woman must be in Russia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Iran has reportedly agreed to sell more missiles and suicide drones to Russia. Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  said last night that Iran`s assistance to Russia shows that the Kremlin is politically and morally bankrupt -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Holly Williams for us once again in Ukraine. Holly, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 For more on the war, we are joined now by John Kirby, he is the coordinator for Strategic Communications at the National Security Council at the White House, also a retired Admiral in the \uc2 US Navy\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Admiral Kirby, good morning, thank you for being here.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen strikes on civilians, strikes on women and children, and now we are confronting the possibility of a deep humanitarian crisis, people freezing to death, going without water. There`s a perennial question for White Houses like yours, is there a line here? Or are we just going to let people suffer?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET) (Coordinator for Strategic Communications at the National Security Council): Well, we`re going to continue to make sure we`re supporting Ukraine as best we can. The United States is the largest contributor of financial and economic assistance for humanitarian purposes like this to Ukraine. We`re also the largest contributor of security assistance, now $17 billion worth of weapons and capabilities to allow them to continue to defend their territory and to some -- and to take some of those towns and villages back.\par \par \uc2 And they`re very much in the fight right now, both in the East and the South, and we`re going to do everything we can to make sure that they can stay in that fight.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are the largest contributor, and I want to get to the money question in a moment, but is there a line or a zone or a way of thinking where the US intervenes in a deeper way than it is currently, not just money, not just arms.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): There is -- obviously, the President has been very clear, we`re not going to put US troops on the ground to fight in Ukraine. We are going to do everything we can, as we have now for going on eight months to make sure that the Ukrainian Armed Forces have what they need in the field, and that just isn`t coming from the United States, we are also leading, literally leading and coordinating international efforts to get additional weapons and capabilities into their hands. Sometimes, in many cases, capabilities that they know better, and can use faster than even the ones that we`re providing from our own stock.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to stay at this. You`ve heard the President talk about that, as long as it takes, we`re committed to that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: As long as it takes -- so, let`s talk about the money component on the subject of as long as it takes. By our count, it`s nearly $100 billion in aid to Ukraine. I know, we`re not the only country kicking in, but at a time when there are a lot of taxpayer needs here, is there a line for the White House where you`re not going to spend taxpayer money there?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): We have had great support from both sides of the aisle in Congress over the course of the last eight months, terrific support. I think everybody realizes what`s at stake here, which is more than just, you know, the town of Kherson, or Kharkiv or Sloviansk, it is really about this idea of sovereignty and it is really about what the security environment in Europe is going to look like going forward.\par \par \uc2 It has changed today, and it affects the United States and our national security interest, because so many of our allies and partners are now in a different security footprint than they were before.\par \par \uc2 We have 20,000 more troops on the European continent now than we did, you know, again, nine months ago, eight months ago. So, we`re working this very, very hard. And we`re going to stay at it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Admiral, the House GOP leader, Kevin McCarthy on the subject of support from Congress, he said, if the Republicans take control, they`re not going to write a blank check for Ukraine in the New Year, is he going to get a phone call from you today?\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): This has not been about writing blank checks for Ukraine. This has been about bipartisan support in Congress to continue to make sure that Ukraine can defend itself and it can stand up for its sovereignty, because that`s really what`s at stake here, aside, of course, from the literally hundreds of thousands of Ukrainian lives that have been affected by this war.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve had great support. We`re going to continue to work hand in glove with Congress going forward for additional financial support for Ukraine, and we are confident that we`re going to be able to work with them to that end.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Admiral Kirby, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ADM. JOHN KIRBY (RET): My pleasure.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Here in this country, winter seems to be arriving in a hurry. More than 68 million Americans will be waking up to temperatures below freezing.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist, Jim Cantore from our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  joins us right now with the forecast.\par \par \uc2 Jim, good morning. Is there any relief in sight?\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): So, the other day, I told you Nate, keep the shorts and flip-flops available, all right, that`s what I`m going to talk about, that relief, which is in sight, buddy, so hang in there.\par \par \uc2 This morning, though, it feels like December. I mean, really, a lot of these temperatures that we`re dealing with are like December here in October, some of the coldest readings since March or April in many locations, especially down to the south.\par \par \uc2 So let`s take a look at it. Today, we only get to 60 degrees in places like Atlanta, 69 as we take you to Raleigh, that cold temperature, especially given the fact the air is dry. There`s still a wind out there, and look at this in Florida, if we only get to 70 today in Fort Myers, okay, that 