uc2 Ed, good morning.\par \par \uc2 It is very confusing if you`re sitting at home watching this.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: It could be, yes, Gayle. Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The arrests were made following investigations by new a Florida State Police force established by Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  to investigate voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 According to local reports, most of those arrested were Black and registered Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: Some voter fraud, voter stuff.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In footage obtained by CBS News, on August 18th, police showed up at the Tampa home of Tony Patterson, a registered sex offender who was told he was under arrest for voter fraud.\par \par \uc2 TONY PATTERSON: Voter fraud? What is voter fraud?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Voting when you`re not supposed to, sir.\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Put your hands behind your back, please.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: That`s same day, Ramona Oliver, who served 18 years on a second degree murder charge was arrested for voting illegally in 2020, even though she`d received a voter ID card from the State.\par \par \uc2 RAMONA OLIVER (Served 18 years on second degree murder charge): Voter fraud? I voted, but I ain`t commit no fraud.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Patterson, Oliver, and 18 others were accused of violating a 2018 State law that allows most former felons to vote after they complete their sentences, but that law excludes convicts like Patterson and Oliver, who were convicted of a felony sex offense or murder.\par \par \uc2 Florida Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  touted the arrests that same day.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): They are disqualified from voting because they`ve been convicted of either murder or sexual assault.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: More than 11 million votes were cast in Florida in the 2020 election when some of those arrested allegedly illegally voted.\par \par \uc2 In the videos first obtained by the "Tampa Bay Times," Patterson tells a police officer he was misled.\par \par \uc2 TONY PATTERSON: Voter fraud?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: Let`s walk over to my car, okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY PATTERSON: Why is he doing this now, and this happened years ago?\par \par \uc2 OFFICER: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: voting rights advocates say, it`s a game of "Gotcha."\par \par \uc2 NEIL VOLZ (Florida Rights Restoration Coalition Deputy Director): When people get a voter ID card from their government, what else are they going to present except that they`re eligible to vote?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Good evening --\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: In a debate Tuesday night between Florida`s \uc2 US Senate\uc2  candidates, these specific arrests didn`t come up, but the candidates did spar over voting rights.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARCO RUBIO (R-FL): Florida has very good election laws and the States that don`t have very good election laws are the States that actually have gone in the opposite direction of weakening security for the ballot.\par \par \uc2 REP. VAL DEMINGS (D-FL): Florida has an election law police force and if the laws are so wonderful, what`s the need for that?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ED O`KEEFE: Republican Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  and Democrat Val Demings who you saw there are locked in a close and expensive fight.\par \par \uc2 Aides to Governor DeSantis who is running for reelection this year against Democrat \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2  didn`t return a request for comment on the concerns that some of those felons may have been misled or confused by State law -- Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Ed, thank you.\par \par \uc2 People in a St. Louis suburb are outraged by new evidence that an elementary school has unacceptable levels of radioactive contamination.\par \par \uc2 CBS News first reported on the contamination in and around Florissant, Missouri in 2015. A recent round of testing highlights leaks of radioactive material into nearby Coldwater Creek, dating back to the creation of the first atomic bomb.\par \par \uc2 An area has seen the cancer clusters and other health problems for decades. Now, we`re learning that the contamination could be even more widespread.\par \par \uc2 Adriana Diaz talked to some parents that are demanding answers.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TRISHA BURNS (Community member): When you guys start to see the level of disrespect the Department of Energy had for our community, you`re going to be mad.\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY ANDERSON (Community member): I`ve been out as well calling doctors, having bloodwork done, any of those things that I can do right now to test lead levels.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: There is fury in Florissant, Missouri, after Jana Elementary School tested positive for dangerous radioactive contaminants.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BERNAUGH (Jana Elementary PTA President): We cannot allow our community to be devastated by the poor choices of people in the 40s, the 50s, the 60s and the 70s, the 80s and 90s, and now we`re here past the 2000s.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: That`s PTA President Ashley Bernaugh, who has been fighting for transparency for nearly a decade.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BERNAUGH: My biggest fear is that our kids are going to end up with illnesses later on in life, and that it could have been prevented.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: How this is even possible dates back to the 1940s and 50s when radioactive waste was stored near what is now St. Louis Airport, contaminating the soil and nearby Coldwater Creek, which frequently overflows.\par \par \uc2 In 2012, the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2 , which is responsible for cleanup of the area, began testing near the airport and expanded to test along the creek.\par \par \uc2 Army Corps communication to the School District from earlier this year acknowledged to low level radioactive contamination, but said it was within acceptable parameters.\par \par \uc2 And while the Corps has tested 300 feet outside the school, the building itself is not within its designated testing area.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BERNAUGH: The \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  denied us testing, said it wasn`t reasonable and when testing was done, they don`t like the results.\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: Lawyers bringing a class action lawsuit hired their own investigator who did test inside the school building. They found unacceptable levels of radioactive lead inside the kitchen, gym, and ventilation systems. At the playground, lead levels were 22 times higher than normal.\par \par \uc2 The Army Corps of Engineers told us the report is not consistent with our accepted evaluation techniques and must be thoroughly vetted. The Corps acknowledges there is contamination underground in a nearby wooded area, but says they found no contamination between the creek and the playground.\par \par \uc2 Has your son talked about being worried for his health?\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BERNAUGH: Sure. Absolutely. He understands the real ramifications that come with radioactive nuclear material. This is the stuff is superheroes fight against.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ADRIANA DIAZ: There are already bipartisan calls in Congress to investigate this now. The students will report to school in person today before they go virtual on Monday through Thanksgiving. After that, they will be transferred to different schools in the area until further notice.\par \par \uc2 One parent I spoke to off camera last night was in tears because she didn`t want the school split up -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, Adriana, good point that mom made. This is what his superheroes fight against. Little kids understand that one. Thank you, Adriana. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Brittney Griner just spent her birthday in a Russian prison. She turned 32 yesterday. We`ll show you the message of support that \uc2 NBA\uc2  superstar, Steph Curry, sent to the WNBA star.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Still ahead and just a moment, "Face the Nation`s" Margaret Brennan and our election expert, Anthony Salvanto -- there they are. They`re going to show us four pivotal groups of voters and tell us why they matter. That is coming up in just a moment.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Still ahead, our exclusive conversation in Nashville, Kane Brown and his lovely wife, Katelyn about their first ever duet together. He also talks about fatherhood and his own difficult childhood. He`s been through it.\par \par \uc2 Local news coming right up.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Back now at 7:30 here on CBS MORNINGS, and the midterms are less than three weeks away. Every vote counts, but there are blocs of voters that really could sway things. We wanted to understand them more. And to help us, we are joined now by "Face the Nation" moderator, Margaret Brennan, and CBS News elections and surveys director, Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Okay guys, so there are these groups that you`ve termed election influencers. They include, if I have this right, pressured parents, Trump true believers, restoring Roe voters, and the young and restless -- no relation to the show.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, starting with you, what can you tell us about these four groups you`ve isolated.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: We want to understand the election through the motivations of the people who are participating in it and get away from the usual labels -- demographic labels, party labels -- we have interviewed so many voters through the course of our CBS News polling, this is what emerged from that data as people tell us, this is how they process this campaign. So we want to bring you that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, so tell us, can you just give us a tick-tock on each one of them? Pressured parents means?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Indeed, pressured parents are people who were dealing with COVID and reported that they felt stressed about that and their kids, and now they`re facing financial pressure after inflation on top of that. That is where we start and that group is somewhat closely divided, leaning a little bit more Republican right now, but it is those dual pressures that really define them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Trump true believers?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Trump true believers are people who consider themselves MAGA Republicans and they don`t like the direction of the country. They want the party to be loyal to the former President, and really the center of gravity in the Republican Party right now.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Restoring Roe voters.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Restoring Roe is very simply single issue voters on abortion. These women say that is what is motivating them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And my favorite category, not related to the show, as Tony pointed out, the young and the restless.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Young and the restless, I call it -- this is a turnout story for the ages, which is to say they are not the largest group in the electorate, but they could really matter if they show up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Under 30, no kids.\par \par \uc2 And Margaret, you spent some time with people in the pressured parents group. What did you learn?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes, we`ve been digging into a lot of these topics and this was one of those powerful focus groups we did. Getting parents to talk about what they`ve been through and the collective trauma of COVID is with us now, it`s in the mental health crisis.\par \par \uc2 This mother telling us about her 12-year-old running out of the house screaming like a three-year-old having a meltdown. A dad talking to us about depression. We are just starting to kind of measure what it has done to us, mental health.\par \par \uc2 Also you can measure it in the education crisis. Reading level declined for nine-year-olds at -- has declined by the largest margin in more than 30 years according to the Education Department.\par \par \uc2 A hundred thousand caregivers, gone. And then the cost you pay has gone up.\par \par \uc2 There are very real stresses, and we`ve seen this erupt politically in School Board meetings getting violent over what is even taught in the classroom. The lack of control is powerful.\par \par \uc2 We saw Republicans harness it in the State of Virginia when it came to that election last year to elect a Governor where education was put at the centerpiece of it. So, we`re watching how these voters define themselves because COVID redefined us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Now one of the groups that we are most about is the Trump true believers. Why is it important to understand their perspective?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And don`t we kind of know how they are going to vote or the perception of how they will vote?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: We absolutely know how they are going to vote. They are voting for Republicans, but what makes them important -- two things.\par \par \uc2 One is, when you see Republican candidates talking about election denialism, talking about being loyal to the former President, they know that they need the votes of these folks.\par \par \uc2 So that helps you understand the narrative of the campaign --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And who they`re talking to.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: And who they`re talking to, even though for the rest of voters, we know that the former President is a net negative.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s very much about turnout for a lot of these groups. And then you ask yourself, if those candidates moderate their stances at all, do some of these folks stay home and not get tough.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: People get caught up in hearing, you know, certain narratives, and they say, well, they are isolating many of us, but it is not that they`re isolating many of us, they are speaking to their core audience.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Exactly. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Margaret, the Roe voters that we`ve isolated, what kind of an influence could they have? Are they going to turn out in the numbers the Democrats are hoping they will?\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, Anthony has been tracking that very carefully and on Sunday`s "Face the Nation" talked about the momentum stalling for Democrats on the back of what had been a really engaging momentum for them.\par \par \uc2 But what we`ve seen is that for this midterm election, it`s not just a referendum on the President and his party. This issue is literally on the ballot in at least five States.\par \par \uc2 So when we talk about turnout, that could matter, that could be an influence there, and when you look at what happened back in August, if you remember, in the traditionally conservative State of Kansas, people were surprised when the overwhelming vote was to hold on to abortion access.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So this is a new dynamic and for years, abortion had been -- and you could speak to that as well that a single issue voter platform for the other side.\par \par \uc2 And so now Democrats are hoping and you heard President Biden just yesterday come out and say his first legislative priority in a new Congress, if Democrats win, will be trying to create a law to protect abortion access, something that hadn`t been done in 50 years. They`re trying to motivate people to turn out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We all can see in the future.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Twenty days and counting for the election. It`s going to be a long night for all of us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Look forward to it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Long night, long week.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And making this information digestible.\par \par \uc2 Thank you so much Margaret Brennan and Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the latest White House response to high gas prices, one of the issues hurting Democrats in the run up to next month`s elections.\par \par \uc2 And here`s a reminder, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Here is what you get: Today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That`s all you need.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s a deal. That`s our personal invitation to you.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to continue our look at the midterm elections this morning with the state of the economy, the top issue for voters right now it seems.\par \par \uc2 One big reason for this year`s rising inflation: Gas prices.\par \par \uc2 The national average for a gallon of regular is $3.85. That`s down from last week, but it`s up from a month ago. Republicans think the economy is a winning issue for them, while the Biden administration wants to show it is working to bring the prices down.\par \par \uc2 Weijia Jiang is at the White House. She`s always a winner. Weijia, before you get started, I have to say welcome back. You`ve got a new baby in the house. We are so glad that you`re here. First day back from baby leave.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, welcome back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome, welcome.\par \par \uc2 WEIJIA JIANG: You guys, thank you so much. I missed you. And I feel like it just happened because I just saw you at the \uc2 White House Correspondents\uc2 ` Dinner, the 