r \uc2 GAYLE KING: Radioactive contamination is found at a Missouri Elementary School causing outrage in that community.\par \par \uc2 ASHLEY BERNAUGH: And it`s my biggest fear is that our kids are going to end up with illnesses later on in life.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And one man raises the bar with a rockin` Halloween light display using drones.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to begin this hour with country superstar, that would be Kane Brown who is out with his highly anticipated third album. This is the hit song, "Thank God," from the album, it`s called "Different Man."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Thank God.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Your hand fits perfectly in mine," I love that line. So does Tony.\par \par \uc2 The duet with his wife, Katelyn is Brown`s fastest rising single on country radio ever. It`s been streamed nearly 45 million times worldwide since its release last August.\par \par \uc2 So we traveled to the Brown`s home, it`s in Nashville, for an exclusive wide-ranging interview with the country music`s newest power couple.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: First I want to say thank you for letting us come to your home because as you see we don`t travel small.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN (Singer): I like it.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Grand.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kane Brown doesn`t shy away from the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: When I get my mind set on something, on doing something, I make sure that just stay focused on that and when I started doing my covers, that was me practicing in the mirror of just pretending like I was on stage\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Kane Brown.)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Like I Love Country Music."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Brown says his new album, "Different Man" is personal and hopefully relatable, too.\par \par \uc2 You want to have a mixture of happy sad ballads. Is that what you`re trying to do? Or is it about what`s going on in your life?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: That`s the easiest stuff to write about when you`re actually experiencing it, but I look at it as I want at least one person from wherever to be able to take one song off the album and say, "I like this song."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Just one?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: That`s on me.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Kane Brown performance.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Since 2016, Kane Brown has gone from opening act to headliner. His Blessed and Free Tour included stops at every NBA Arena in the country.\par \par \uc2 Is this your man cave?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Is that what you call this?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: This is -- I guess, it`s a man cave.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We met up with Brown on his home court.\par \par \uc2 KB stands for?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Kane Brown.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: For a little one-on-one.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Money.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was not an edit.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Money. That wasn`t an edit. That was first shot.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay, let`s see what you`re going to do.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Not bad. I thought that was it. One more. One more.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Come on, dear. Let`s go to the garage. Let`s go.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: One more. Hold on. There it is. I was going to walk first. Let`s go, okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Are these cars that you actually drive? They`re not just collectors kind of things.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: No. I drive all of them.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, you drive all of these.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Look at this one. Where are you going in that?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Not far. A little sound system in here though.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Okay. Okay, you can come in and sing your part.\par \par \uc2 (GAYLE KING and KANE BROWN singing.)\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Thank God.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Thank God" is a duet Brown sings with a woman he calls his secret weapon, his wife, Katelyn.\par \par \uc2 So I`ve listened to "Different Man" and this is what I learned about Kane. He likes whiskey. He loves Georgia, and he loves everything about you.\par \par \uc2 He doesn`t want to lose you. He doesn`t want to change a thing about you. And I want to know what that`s like for you to hear your husband sing so lovingly about you.\par \par \uc2 KATELYN BROWN (Kane Brown`s wife): Yes, it`s extremely flattering. It`s almost like a diary of our relationship in his life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Thank God.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: "Thank God," I think it`s so beautifully done and when it was done, and you heard the finished cut, what did you all say to each other?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: She was still nervous about it. I was like, "This is a smash."\par \par \uc2 KATELYN BROWN: We`ve just always talked about over like the last six or so years about, "Would it be really cool to do a song together."\par \par \uc2 And now, it`s so different than before. We didn`t have kids. I was like how cool for our daughters to one day listen to mom and dad.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kane and Katelyn have two daughters. Ten-month-old, Kodi Jane and two-year-old, Kingsley Rose.\par \par \uc2 Did you consciously think the kind of father you wanted to be before you had a baby?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: No. I just always knew that I was going to try and be there for her or him. We didn`t know. But I was just always going to try and be there and I just always wanted to give them the life that I know a lot of people say this, but I did want to give them the life that I never had.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Kane calls Kingsley, his best friend.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: She`s just fun. She makes my days better.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: She does.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: She is -- stop being weird, man.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Kane Brown family video.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Raised near the Tennessee-Georgia border, Brown says when he was a kid, music helped him escape from problems at home.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: I don`t know if I`ve ever told you about it, I did have like a crazy childhood. I had to grow a lot faster than the normal person.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Just moving around a lot, and then no father figure, child abuse, like beat a lot. One of my stepdads, punished me, shaved my head bald and put aftershave on me. I mean, like, physically eat half a bar of soap, not just bite into it. I had to physically eat it and swallow it. All kinds of crazy things.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: At school, Brown dealt with racism.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Now, Middle School is where everything hit me at once.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What do you mean by that? What do you mean?\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Well, so the first -- I was -- I was -- where is Kate at? Hi, babe. I am going to tell the story about a Middle School girlfriend. I love you.\par \par \uc2 It was like my first like actual --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Girlfriend.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Girlfriend, you know. We were on the bus and she was about to get dropped off, and then she was like, "I can`t talk to you anymore." I was like, "What do you mean?" And she was like, "My dad said you`re Black."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh man.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: "And I can`t talk to you anymore." And that was the first time that I`d really felt like, "Wow."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Moments like that have stuck with Brown his entire life.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Tip-Off Concert.)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When it comes to being a country artists, he believes his talent should speak for itself.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: Especially in like the country music genre, to me, I shouldn`t have to say anything. You know, I`m up there in front of everybody as a Black artist, I`m selling out arenas. I`m on the radio. I`m doing all this stuff. Right there should tell you that, you know, country music is getting better and people should be opening up.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Good as You.")\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Today, this family man feels like he has it all.\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN: I feel more confident now than I`ve ever felt in my career. I`ve always kind of doubted myself or always been scared of putting something out and now, I just feel confident, it feels great.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does feel great and he is doing great. He does call Katelyn his secret weapon, I think on and off because you see, he has been through a lot in his childhood.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But he certainly seems to have come out on the other side, and he says he loves country music as much as you know, he do other things. He says he will never lose it. He will never leave the country genre because it means so much to him.\par \par \uc2 And she can really sing. This isn`t just the little woman by her husband`s side. This is here, let me sing a song. She has a degree in music from the \uc2 Berklee College of Music\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They met -- they bonded through music. So, she is -- this isn`t just a freebie. She can really sing, but does she want a career? She says no. She wanted to do this.\par \par \uc2 But it`s so good, I wonder if she`ll maybe do another one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What I love about these profile pieces, one, good job.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you, Nate.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Secondly, you get to pull back the layers of the superstars.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That have this, what we think is an amazing life. I didn`t know all of that about him.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But what it does, it compels you to like support and want to listen to his music and also listen to it differently.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because you know what he is feeling.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I feel that, too. Listen, what we all know is everybody got issues and everybody has to deal with stuff.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, but also the relationship seems so real. I like it he was like, where is Kate. Kate, I love you, baby. I`m going to tell a story about an old girlfriend. It`s Middle School.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is Middle School.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was so relatable.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s just beautiful.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The two of them are very tight, but that man cave, guys, I have to say. He has a bona fide locker room, and in the locker room, there is a urinal. I mean, I was impressed. I took a picture.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Let me give you a high five on that jump shot, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. I was hoping for one from the field.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a lucky --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The \uc2 NBA\uc2  tryout is coming up if you want to --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was a lucky shot.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: As soon as that ankle get right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. You should have seen me.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There it is. Oh, slow motion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Boom.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you very much, Shanta.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Boom. Gayle King of the court.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But did you see, he didn`t want to stop until he made a shot.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was confidence because it was oh for two and if he had been oh for three, you just would have to call it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That was awesome.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, he can play, he can play.\par \par \uc2 We thank them, because listen, as you know, when you go to somebody`s house, you`ve got six cameras. You`ve got a crew of a gazillion, but they were very nice 