t into it. So, what day should you buy your plane ticket to get the best deal?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, you know, a couple of years ago, it was Tuesday at one minute after midnight, then it changed.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: . to Friday. Now, it`s Sunday.\par \par \uc2 And the reason why it changed to Sunday, \uc2 Expedia\uc2  reports that business travel is starting to come back. Fares will stabilize on Sunday. You`ll get the lowest deals then. So you log on, you`ll shave about five percent on domestic flights and an average of about 15 percent on international fares. Go Sunday.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So buy it on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What day though should you try to fly on to get the best deals? So, not the day you buy it, but the day you actually travel?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, that is another interesting story because it changes based on the time of the year. Right?\par \par \uc2 So for holiday travel this year, you don`t want to travel on the Wednesday before Thanksgiving.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. That makes sense.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: They made a movie like that. It`s called "Planes, Trains and Automobiles," right? So what you want to do is go on the Friday before or the day of.\par \par \uc2 For Christmas, you want to go the week before.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The week before?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Absolutely. Because remember, Christmas this year falls on a Sunday, so if you leave on the Friday before Christmas, you`re not going to be in good shape at all for fares.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, it would be a mess.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People are looking at each other right now on the couch being like, your family is not coming a week early.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Don`t even think about it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Or you do what I do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What do you do?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: You fly on the very first day -- the very first flight on Thanksgiving morning. Get there in time to carve the turkey and then while everybody is stuck at the mall on Friday on Black Friday, you go home on Friday, you can go bowling on that plane. There is nobody on that plane.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That makes sense.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Right? And you`ve satisfied your family obligations and Nate, you`ll appreciate this. The weekend belongs to you now and you can watch as much bad football as you can.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Are you talking about \uc2 Detroit Lions\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Let`s not go there.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What about Frequent Flyers? Because I basically get a credit card based on my Frequent Flyers.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Sure.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I use that for everything to build up the miles.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, the problem with the Frequent Flyer Rewards right now is the planes are so full, the airlines are not inclined to give you those free Frequent Flyer award seats right now.\par \par \uc2 Although we are in the fourth quarter and that`s a traditionally lower period of travel --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They don`t have to honor your Frequent Flyer miles?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Good luck redeeming them these days, you can`t get on. They don`t want to displace revenue passengers. But in the fourth quarter and going through next May, you can.\par \par \uc2 So think about 330 days out, be flexible, but book them now because the airlines continue to raise the eligibility levels for the amount of miles you have to redeem.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That always bugs me though, when people say they don`t want to replace revenue passengers because I had to pay to get those miles.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Oh, you had to pay a lot. Fifty-four percent of all mileage earned is earned on the ground with credit cards, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: So, if you`ve got a 25,000 mile reward, you know what that means? You spent $14,000.00 to get it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Unbelievable. All right --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t tell me that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are talking about how to get deals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Don`t tell me that. What about staff shortages?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. How do you avoid the big disruption?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And cancellations.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Exactly.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Well, on the \uc2 Expedia\uc2  report, they figured out if you`ve got a flight that leaves after three o`clock in the afternoon, it`s 50 percent more likely to be canceled, and a whole lot more likely to be delayed. So that --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: After three?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Yes, so get an early morning departure, but that`s advice that we all can figure out the first flight of the day. I`ll go one step beyond that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What have you got?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Pick up flight, that first flight of the day on an airline that`s not based in the city where you`re flying from or where they don`t have a crew base. And the reason for that is --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: That very first flight of the day had come in the night before with that crew.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I see what you`re doing, Peter.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: That should do better with the plane. The crew goes back with the plane and you`re on it, you`re gone.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But have you know the crew schedule though, Peter? How do you do that?\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: Very simple. If you`re flying out of Chicago.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: You know what? It`s not United. It could be Delta.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: If you`re flying out of Atlanta, it is not Delta, it`s United.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Peter Greenberg with the hacks. We appreciate you, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 PETER GREENBERG: You`ve got it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We appreciate it. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, when actor and author Amber Tamblyn couldn`t find a book about women`s intuition, she wrote and edited her own.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, she`ll join us with some simple steps for learning to listen to your inner voice.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The new movie, "The Good Nurse" is a true crime thriller that takes place inside the ICU. This is Oscar-winner Jessica Chastain. This is a real life nurse she portrays. You are?\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN (Nurse): Amy Loughren.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A true hero, the same. They are both in our progressive greenroom to talk about working together on the film. It`s good. "The Good Nurse."\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS where we`re talking about a new film inspired by the true story of a critical care nurse who helped stop a serial killer.\par \par \uc2 That serial killer, Charles Cullen in 2004 pled guilty to killing 29 hospital patients, and the real number may even be higher. He admitted to doing it by injecting deadly doses of medicine in their saline drips, and said there were more victims likely over his 16-year career, more than just the 29. That`s a huge number. He is serving multiple life sentences.\par \par \uc2 His fellow nurse and friend, Amy Loughren, she helped stop him. She worked with police and she got him to confess, this was crucial.\par \par \uc2 The thriller, the movie we are talking about is "The Good Nurse" starring Jessica Chastain, and it is based on Amy`s story.\par \par \uc2 In the scene you`re about to see, Amy suffers a heart episode after growing more suspicious of Cullen and wakes up to find him by her bedside. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "The Good Nurse.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the whole movie is like that. You will watch every minute of it and you will love it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re joined now by Jessica Chastain and Amy Loughren. Wow, Amy. That`s a real scene from your life.\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN It is, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What was it like, and Jessica, welcome back. You won an Oscar since the last time we saw you. We have got a lot to talk about. You do a wonderful job portraying Amy on camera or on screen.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN ("The Good Nurse" actor): It was a huge honor.\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: It was a huge one -- I get to be played by Jessica Chastain. Like, wake me up, wake up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That part`s a dream, obviously, but then the story itself, though. I mean, it`s a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is a nightmare.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is a nightmare. What was it like -- I could see you getting emotional just -- as I was reading the story, the summary and then just looking at that clip. What was it like to delve into all of this?\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: Well, for me, of course, you know, reliving it, it is very triggering. It was 20 years ago, so I have had a lot of time to process it. However, I have only been able to watch it once. It`s pretty triggering.\par \par \uc2 And Eddie Redmayne does such an incredible job, truly channeling my friend, Charlie, that I hear his voice and it`s my friend, Charlie all over again.\par \par \uc2 The detectives, Danny Baldwin and Tim Braun, we were with them last night and they said the same thing. They actually had to sit back a little bit because he sounds exactly like Charles Cullen.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Jessica, I don`t know, Amy, but you -- I thought she did such a wonderful job portraying you and what you must have been going through.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I was watching you, Jessica, just do the IV and the way that you were handling the patients like you did seem to me to be a real nurse.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: Well, we went to nurse school.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You and Eddie did?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: Yes, we spent two weeks, it was -- we shot this at the end of the pandemic so we couldn`t really go to nurse school, so they made one for us.\par \par \uc2 And we learned it all. We learned IVs. I started with like the first day of like, the history of nursing. We learned about Florence Nightingale, but yes, the compressions, that`s the one thing I asked. I said, "What is the most -- what is the thing Hollywood always gets wrong?" And I was told the compression, CPR.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Did she get that right?\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: Yes, and in the screening, that was the one that I was like, "Oh, my God, her form."\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: But you know what it is? You`re supposed to keep your arms straight and in your head, and this is what you think, you sing the tune you go, "Ah, ah, ah, staying alive, staying alive." And that`s the rhythm of the compressions.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We all rely on nurses, nurses rely on each other though. It`s a very close occupation. And Amy, I`m always amazed actually hearing you talk about Charles Cullen, the person, who murdered dozens of people, you still, when you think about it, you say my friend, Charles. I don`t even know if you realize you`re doing it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, that struck me, too.\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: So I didn`t really -- I met the murderer twice. The person I knew was truly a different person and he changes, and I saw him go from this wonderful gentle soft-spoken great teammate to this very angry, smug person that I did not know.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: When did you start to suspect him?\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: So, I wish I could say that I suspected him early on, and I didn`t. I have -- I still have a lot of guilt about that that I didn`t see it sooner.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how could you see it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Honestly.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: He worked at so many hospitals -- you were the one who stopped him.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: I mean, that`s so important to acknowledge and recognize.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Amy, for those who are familiar with the story and those who will watch this. The question we wrestle with is why? And everybody has their own reason. In your gut, knowing him, as a friend and a murderer, why do you think he went down this dark path?\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: He definitely had a very challenging childhood that doesn`t excuse anything that he did.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: I had a challenging childhood. I do believe that he was very flawed. I think he had a lot of mental illness. He tried to commit suicide even as a preteen, and he started his murderous tendencies even before --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Before he became --\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: Before he was a teenager -- before he was a teenager. So, I believe it was an obsessive compulsive disorder gone awry. That`s -- and again, that`s just my theory.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because you know what? He never gave an answer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Even when asked, he said, "I got away with it." There is a very crucial scene at the end of the movie, just where you got him to confess, and in that moment, you were so tender to him. And I was wondering, did that really happen? Because we got to see his -- it was hard to see his humanity.\par \par \uc2 But in that scene, you really played his humanity.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: I think what`s so beautiful about what Tobias Lindholm, our director does, and this incredible story is we spend so much time focusing on violence, stopping violence, right? Aggression, stopping violence.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: Those are all the stories were fed in the media. In this true story, what stops the violence and the cycle of violence is compassion and love, and Amy reminds him that he is a human being and reminds him of his humanity when everyone else starts to treat him as a monster and that`s what stops it.\par \par \uc2 And this is a show that focuses on the hero, and not the violence and the murderer, and it is one of the first times I`ve ever seen something do that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We will certainly focus on nurses. I have a very good friend who was in the hospital recently and he was saying that the difference that the nurses make, everybody gives all the glory to the doctors, Amy, but what do you say about nurses and some nurses come in and you could see that they`re really on top in care and others just seem to be phoning it in. What do you say about --\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: Anyone who has ever been in the hospital and especially in critical care, you`ll see the physicians are only there for about 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 AMY LOUGHREN: Maybe a couple of minutes to come in and get report from the nurse. It`s the nurses that will save your life.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The nurses will save your life. Did it change your perception of nurses, Jessica?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: Oh, it`s a hard job. I mean, we made this film at the end of the pandemic, at the time where everyone is going outside to clap, you know and acknowledge healthcare workers. But I think sometimes we have short-term memories.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: And we need to remember the people who are out there caring for ourselves. And this film is about someone who was caring for others and we weren`t really caring for her.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: And I think we need to look at that and say: Are we taking care of the people that are out there putting everything on the line for us?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The movie ends by saying you`re still a good nurse, Amy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We appreciate our nurses, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The movie ends, you`re still a good nurse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A nurse and a hero.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CHASTAIN: Yes, she is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Jessica Chastain and Amy Loughren, thank you so much. We appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 "The Good Nurse" is out in select theaters today and begin streaming on \uc2 Netflix\uc2  next Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is so good.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we have more on "The Good Nurse" on today`s CBS MORNINGS podcast. The director and screenwriter discusses why they focused on Amy Loughren`s story, and were drawn to her compassion, humanity, and effort to find justice.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did a good job, Amy.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You really did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, we`re still talking.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, next: How to tap into your intuition, because I can tell she was nervous -- actor and author, Amber Tamblyn. Hey Amber Tamblyn. She is in our progressive greenroom with her new book. It`s about intuition. This is also good, "Listening in the Dark," it is called.\par \par \uc2 But first, it`s 8:39. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Amber Tamblyn is an actor. She`s an activist. She`s a poet, and guess what? She`s a writer, too.\par \par \uc2 Her new -- may I hold up your book?\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN ("Listening to the Dark" author): Yes, please.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Her new book aims to help people follow your intuition. It`s called "Listening in the Dark: Women Reclaiming the Power of Intuition."\par \par \uc2 So it features personal essays from 15 women, most of them, many of them you know, including Amy Poehler, Huma Abedin, and Tamblyn`s own mother, Bonnie, I loved her story.\par \par \uc2 Tamblyn edited the essays and wrote her own. She joins us first on CBS MORNINGS to discuss.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Amber.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: This is so great. It`s a very easy read, and I think it`s so relatable. But one of the things you point out is that sometimes we`re taught to ignore our intuition. You`re saying, no, do not do that. Because intuition is what and does what?\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Yes, so one of the things that I wanted to write about what this book was sort of the underpinning of all of these conversations we have as women, when it comes to rage and equality, all of the different feelings that we have, and the idea of nurturing this gut feeling that we have, and what happens when we really think about that as a muscle, as an actual form of intelligence that we can use as much as we use rational thought.\par \par \uc2 And once I started reading all of these essays, I was completely blown away by the science behind it and how important this is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes. I love what Huma says, she says, you can have all the academics, you can have all the rationality, all the education, but sometimes it comes down to just doing what feels right.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And that is so true.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Yes. And I think that there is so much emphasis on intelligence and knowledge as fact-based rationale that we can look at.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: But there is this other part of us. There is this tool, this vital tool that is our physical bodies telling us things, what our gut tells us, what the inside of our voice is and what that has to say about our lives.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You mentioned the word "gut."\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And you write about trusting your body. The body knows first.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Why is it important to listen to our bodies? Because we all get that feeling. Sometimes we listen, sometimes we don`t.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Well, I think especially you know, men`s intuition is really fascinating to me, and really a big important part of this conversation, because I think --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They have intuition? Men have intuition?\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Of course, they do.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, Gayle, we got intuition.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I knew you would say that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: I mean, you do, you know, but I think for women, it`s been much more suppressed and oppressed for many, many, many years.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: And so the important factor here is reconnecting with our bodies and what they tell us. And we`re sort of in an age when women are really told not to listen to their bodies, that they are not the arbiters of their own experience, or their own physical livelihood.\par \par \uc2 And so to go against that and talk about intuitive process, and intuitive intelligence, is like this very vital tool in this moment for us to be able to think and feel and be connected to each other and to our own physical selves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You come to the subject through your own experience, and the tragedy of losing a mentor, and not listening to your body and then going back and finding out why.\par \par \uc2 But then you`ve got all these essays from other people with their experiences. How did you go about including this, not including that, how do you edit somebody else`s intuition?\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Oh, it`s such a great question. I loved editing this, I think, for me, it was really important to have different people, women from across industries, doctors, artists, activists, writers, who were -- who didn`t necessarily know what the answer was, right? Because it`s really hard for us to describe what that feeling is of intuition and our gut to really give it a definitive definition.\par \par \uc2 And so I wanted women to say, you know, like, Huma when I approached her, and she was like, "I would love to do this. I have no idea what I`m going to write about and I don`t even know if I have a connection to my intuition." I said, "Great. Let`s write about that. That`s perfect."\par \par \uc2 Because this is not a book that gives answers necessarily, it is helping you to strengthen the muscle of how to ask questions.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And intuition can work to tell you to do something?\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Also work to tell you not to do something.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how do you know, Amber, when it`s intuition, and maybe I`m just afraid, or maybe I`m just anxious?\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Well, there`s --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We all have those feelings, too.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Absolutely. And I think one of the biggest issues that women face is second guessing themselves, and confusing intuition for anxiety, which is something that we feel often. But I think, again, there`s a great conversation with America Ferreira that she and I have in this, it`s like a conversation between friends.\par \par \uc2 She is one of the most brilliant women I know, and she talks a lot about this as a muscle to be strengthened. So, the more that you lean on it, the more you use it, however scary it is, however, you know, unsure you are, it gets stronger.\par \par \uc2 And so your ability to trust yourself, and to hear yourself strengthens over time.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And Gayle, men do have intuition. I was reading this book and it was speaking to me and then I come to the line --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I didn`t -- Tony, I didn`t say you, guys, didn`t have it? I was just posing the question to Amber.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was speaking to me and then I came to a line that had mentioned women, I`d be like, "Oh, yes, that`s right. It`s supposed to be for women," but it is for everybody.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: But can just say, I hope our young boys and I hope men read this book for real --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I do, too.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Because I think it`s so important for us to see intuitive intelligence and trusting our gut through a feminine lens as well. It`s such an important part of who we are as a collective human race.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you have a book -- a person who deals with animals and says animals always go with their -- they have an instinct and their intuition.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: They don`t second guess themselves.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They are not making 10 charts.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s right. Amber, it is really good. Really good.\par \par \uc2 AMBER TAMBLYN: Thank you. Thanks, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Thank you so much for coming this morning.\par \par \uc2 The name of Amber`s book is "Listening in the Dark." It`s on sale now. It`s on sale now, wherever you`d like to buy your books.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In case you missed it on this week`s CBS MORNINGS Deals, we showed you exclusive offers.\par \par \uc2 One of those, the PowerPro by Limitless. It`s a five-in-one charger that provides a surge protection and it might help you save some space. Our exclusive discount is that nearly 60 percent off the retail price. How about that?\par \par \uc2 To start shopping for the PowerPro and many other products, scan the QR code on your screen right now. You can also visit CBSdeals.com.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That will come in very handy.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, we`re sitting down with Dwayne Johnson known as "The Rock." What has he got cooking?\par \par \uc2 The Hollywood mega star is talking about his new superhero blockbuster movie, "Black Adam." He told us what it was like to put on that costume for the first time.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So, I`m curious, how hard was it for you to wear that muscle suit?\par \par \uc2 DWAYNE "THE ROCK" JOHNSON ("Black Adam" actor): Well, first of all, let me just say they --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: How hard was it to wear that suit with all of those muscles?\par \par \uc2 DWAYNE "THE ROCK" JOHNSON: Well, wait a second. You know, we`ve removed the muscle paddings.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know you did. I know you did. That`s what I think --\par \par \uc2 DWAYNE "THE ROCK" JOHNSON: She is giving me the business now, this is it.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`ll have more of that conversation tomorrow including why this film was 15 years in the making. You would think if Dwayne said, "I like this movie. Let`s get it done," it would be, "Right this way, sir."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It does look like a muscle suit. That man is in shape.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, no doubt.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That was all him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`ll do it for us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You were looking, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Oh, I was looking at. I was drooling.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He pointed out that.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And he is married. Hi Lauren. See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Hey.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Take it easy.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221019eiaj000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Three Weeks Until The Midterms, President Biden Vows To Push An Abortion Rights Bill If Democrats Win\b0\par\par\uc2 Norah O`Donnell, Robert Costa \par \uc2 492 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News\par \uc2 CBSE\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: It`s just three weeks until Election Day, and the battle for control of Congress is coming down to the wire. In Ohio, one of the closest Senate races is also one of the biggest surprises, with Democrat \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  trailing J.D. Vance by just two points.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Robert Costa traveled to Ohio for our continuing series America Decides.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : If you care about the right to choose, then you got to vote.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA (voiceover): With the midterms looming, inflation lingering and Democrats` momentum stalling, according to some polls, President Biden today made a promise on abortion rights, rallying Democrats to vote.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden said, if the party expand their majorities, the first bill he would send to Congress would make abortion rights legal across the country.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : The court got Roe right nearly 50 years ago, and I believe Congress should codify Roe once and for all.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: In the tight Ohio Senate race, Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is counting on voters to hear that message and give him the edge over Republican J.D. Vance.\par \par \uc2 Are you confident abortion rights will galvanize Democratic voters in Ohio?\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2  (D-Ohio Senatorial Candidate): It`s going to gavel galvanize voters. So, even people where abortion isn`t their top issue, they don`t want the government in their bedroom or doctor`s office.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: \uc2 CBS\uc2  polling show abortion now does not have the same importance to some voters as it did just a month ago. Still, younger voters in Ohio told us it is on their minds.\par \par \uc2 Do younger Ohio Democrats feel like abortion rights are on the ballot this fall?\par \par \uc2 JESSICA VALSI (Democratic Voter): Yes, definitely. It`s a -- I think that the majority of Americans believe that abortion care should be a choice left to individuals.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Ryan and Vance had sharp exchanges over the issue in a debate last night.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2 : And J.D. and his extreme crew, they want to have a national abortion ban. He called rape inconvenient. Like, this is not a guy who`s ready to protect the rights of women.\par \par \uc2 J.D. VANCE (R-Ohio Senatorial Candidate): And as much as you call me an extremist, you`re the extremist on this issue. I think it`s totally reasonable to say you cannot abort a baby, especially for elective reasons, after 15 weeks of gestation.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ROBERT COSTA: Tomorrow, top Trump allies will campaign with Vance, illustrating how Vance wants the former president`s supporters to show up.\par \par \uc2 But Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  tell CBS News he doesn`t want President Biden to stump for him, and instead will run his own race -- Norah.\par \par \uc2 NORAH O`DONNELL: Robert Costa, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Document CBSE000020221021eiai000gp\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Oprah`s Book Club Selection\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson \par \uc2 2,330 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We always love when this happens here because Oprah is about to reveal her next Book Club selection, only on CBS MORNINGS. What is she talking about this time?\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 OPRAH WINFREY (Talk show host): Hello, CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 I`m here to share my next Book Club selection.\par \par \uc2 As we enter fall with a slight crisp in the air and the leaves starting to turn, our next Book Club selection is perfect to curl up with, set in the mountains of the Southern Appalachians. From the very first sentence you`re going to be intrigued by this epic story of this young boy, "Demon Copperhead" by Barbara Kingsolver.\par \par \uc2 I`m so glad that she`s here with you today to share more about how she crafted this beautiful, incredible story.\par \par \uc2 So get your copy today and join us online at Oprah`s Book Club to share your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 Happy reading, everybody.\par \par \uc2 Barbara Kingsolver.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Happy Reading is a word there.\par \par \uc2 Barbara Kingsolver is the author of 11 best-selling books.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, look at them all lined up, it looks pretty, doesn`t it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER (Best-selling author): Wow.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Eleven best-selling books and former President Bill Clinton awarded her The National Humanities Medal back in 2000 and we are very proud to say, very happy to welcome her here to CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Hello, Barbara Kingsolver. It is really good to have you in the studio.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Hi. Oh, this is so exciting.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You have done it again. You have done it again. Bravo to you.\par \par \uc2 When I first heard about the book, this is how Oprah said it to me. She said, "I found a book that even you`re going to like." I don`t know if I should be insulted or flattered by that because I don`t always like all of them, but she was so right.\par \par \uc2 From the moment that -- from the very first sentence, you drew me in, but you said you use this book, you compare this books to Dickens to tell this story. Explain what you mean.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Well, this is -- I come from Southern Appalachia.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So you know this area and these people very well.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: I know -- this is my home.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And I love it. And Appalachia also has its challenges, and one of them in recent decades is the opioid epidemic.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And the worst of it is that we have a generation of kids who`ve been orphaned by opioid use disorder. And you know, as sort of the attention of the world moves on, I really wanted to tell the story of these kids because it`s not over for them and they live among us.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: I know, you know, we all know these kids, but it`s a dark story and it`s a hard story to tell.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It is.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: I wasn`t sure readers would want to go follow me into that dark place. And so I spent, actually a couple of years trying to get - - trying to find my way into that house. You know how to tell the story. As it happened --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Because "Demon Copperhead" is a person.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Right.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We should say that.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Yes, Demon --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: His real name is Damon.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: His nickname is Demon.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: His nickname is Demon. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And he is called Copperhead because of his red hair. That`s his nickname. Where I come from, everybody has nicknames, it is a thing.\par \par \uc2 So I was really frustrated, and just by chance, at the end of a UK book tour, I had a weekend free and I booked myself into this, just completely on a whim. This in that is in Broadstairs. It`s the former home of Charles Dickens, and it`s where he wrote "David Copperfield."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And so my husband and I, you know, booked ourselves in and we had -- it was creaky old mansion on the ocean and we had it all to ourselves. And they said, "Sure, sure." You know, "Make use of the place. Sit wherever you want," including Dickens` study.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: And so I sat there, I mean, Steven went to bed, you know, and I sat at Dickens` desk looking out the window at the view that you know, the roiling ocean that was in his eyes, and I just started feeling his anger. Not at me, but sort of anger he channeled in his books because orphans, kids thrown away by society. That`s his whole wheelhouse.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You are inspired.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Well, it was really like, he charged me with this mission. He said, "You`ve got to do this."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: But --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It seems like everything that could happen to Demon does happen to him\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Well, what he told me, which was the key to get into the house, was you let the child tell the story. Nobody doubts the child. It`s just that`s the way you tug on the reader`s heart.\par \par \uc2 And that was "David Copperfield" starting with chapter one. "I am born," I said, "Okay, Charles, thank you." I sat at that desk and I started this book that night. And I decided --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Boy, let me tell you something --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA KINGSOLVER: Well, thank you.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, thank you because you take us on quite a ride. I`m not going to tell you how it ends, but it is something.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, we thank you.\par \par \uc2 "Demon Copperhead" is on sale now wherever you like to buy books.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Congratulations. Second time --\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It`s time to bring you some of the stories that we like to call "Talk of the Table" this morning. And guess what? Tony`s in pole position.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very good. So for my "Talk of the Table." I`ve got a new book. It`s called "Ejaculate Responsibly."\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A brand new way to think about abortion. That is the title, it caught my attention. I think it probably caught yours.\par \par \uc2 The author is a self-described religious mother of six, Gabrielle Blair, there she is. And she says that we should not be focusing on women in this conversation, we should be focusing on men.\par \par \uc2 "Ninety-nine percent of abortions are the result of unwanted pregnancies and men cause all unwanted pregnancy," she writes. Don`t believe her? Well, the book is 28 sharp little arguments that make the case that men are the key ingredient here, the thing that men do, and she points out and she is right about this, it is voluntary, it`s easy to control, it`s cheap to control. It`s all preventable.\par \par \uc2 It`s not asking a lot. She concludes for men to avoid this. So...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I actually liked his point. I actually do. Listen, I think the title is very provocative.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It draws you in, but I think she`s making a very good point. What do you think, Tony?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I found this --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, she is talking about like protection...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I like it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: ... using contraceptives, condoms -- what -- or just like, there`s a method that some guys lean on, which is --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, she actually gets into all of that, but her point is that the key ingredient in all unwanted pregnancies, which are the main cause and driver of abortions, which no one wants, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Whether you think it`s a problem to be solved or right to be fought for, no one is rooting for it.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For sure.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if you shift your focus, I just found the idea of shifting it off of women who have to get, you know, all kinds of -- take all kinds of invasive actions to prevent pregnancy and put it on men, it totally flipped the whole conversation on its head for me, and I did not realize the extent to which culturally, I had been thinking about it one particular way until she took it in this little book and just completely reversed it.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: What her name? What`s her name?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Her name is Gabrielle Blair.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go, Gabrielle Blair.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It makes sense though. It makes sense.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And there`s a lot of "methods" that guys try to use and not all of them are, you know...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It gets -- it`s just that word "ejaculate" on morning TV. You go, "Huh?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I know. I know. But you know what? I will be handing this book out around Christmas time. You know, you can stuff this in your stocking for Santa.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: So people will say, "Thanks, Tony."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I prefer a tie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: She does have a point. This is going to be a hot book.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: This is going to be a hot book.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, I agree.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Because you can talk -- whatever your position on the issue of abortion, you can talk about this, and you will prevent the issue from being one.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes. There are certain guys that use certain methods. You`re not -- Tony, you can`t even pull out of the driveway, so you might as well use methods that works.\par \par \uc2 So my "Talk of the Table" is the \uc2 Boston Celtics\uc2 , and how they`re tipping off the season in the grand fashion, and they`re honoring the life and legacy of the legendary Bill Russell with various celebrations including new uniforms.\par \par \uc2 As you can see here, when we show you the actual uniform, it features 11 Gold diamond icons for the 11 championships Russell won with the Celtics. That`s the most \uc2 NBA\uc2  titles of any player. It also features Russell`s retired number six on the front of the jersey in the belt that they used to wear to buckle up the shorts back in the day.\par \par \uc2 Bill Russell died earlier this year in July. I actually had a chance to sit down with him...\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I remember that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: ... in December with one of his last -- it might have been his last interview. But I`m just glad that they were able to honor the legend in the way that they did.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Agree, agree.\par \par \uc2 My talk of the table is Kane Brown. I love me Kane Brown.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I love this guy. The country music star was recently named the Champion of Youth by the Boys and Girls Club. He is a big, big advocate of the organization and even dedicated one of his songs to raise money for them.\par \par \uc2 So in his speech, Brown spoke about the struggles that he faced as a child.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KANE BROWN (Singer): I went through child abuse, I went through racism. My family -- because I`m biracial -- my wife saw me and accepted me when I was first born. I lived in a car for my home in a little while. I just want to let you know that I am not going to get emotional.\par \par \uc2 Everything you go through in life makes you tougher, it makes you stronger from every career and training that you want to do. Like I said, I used to live in a car, and now look at me.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Go Kane Brown. This was in Los Angeles last week. Other big names to win the champion of Youth Award include LeBron James, Jennifer Lopez, Colin Powell.\par \par \uc2 So tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS, we`ve got a great conversation -- well, I think it`s great -- we had a great time with him, with Kane Brown.\par \par \uc2 We went to Nashville to see Kane, his wife, Katelyn, and their two adorable little girls at their Nashville home. It`s an exclusive interview, the first time that they`ve sat down together, we talked to them about recording their first ever duet. Yes, Mrs. Brown can sing.\par \par \uc2 We also played some basketball and I use that word loosely played some basketball. Who knew?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes, that was not an edit. Then we hopped inside Kane`s truck.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, I missed it. We`ve got to play that back or I`ll get it later.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You`ll get to see it tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You keep going.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re going to put it in slow mode tomorrow -- pickup truck to sing one of his hit songs. That`s all coming up tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, me, too.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We showed up at his house, very strong, and they were very hospitable. Really nice.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Awesome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Very cool.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221021eiai000dy\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Russia Attacks Kyiv with Drones; Deadly Iran Prison Fire; Midterm Momentum; Battleground Georgia; New Access to Hearing Aids; Bitter Blast; The Face of Communist China; Crime Victim DNA Controversy; Season of the Sticks; Chelsea Manning Live; A Lesson on Tact; Black Women Will Save the World; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Tony Dokoupil, Michelle Miller, Nate Burleson, Ramy Inocencio,  Holly Williams, Danya Bacchus, Anthony Salvanto; Nikole Killion, Jim  Cantore, Elizabeth Palmer, Janet Shamlian, Vladimir Duthiers, Jan Crawford,  Steve Hartman, Emmett Hartman, Meryl Hartman \par \uc2 13,592 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Monday, October 17th. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And I`m Michelle Miller.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Gayle is off, but we are in good hands. Let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." Your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 Breaking overnight, another wave of explosions rocked residential areas in Ukraine`s capital. Officials blamed a deadly attack on Russian suicide drones.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA (Ukraine`s Ambassador to the US): We have seen all the war crimes, we have seen all the rocket attacks on civilian residential areas killing families.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Fire erupts at a notorious Iranian prison, as widespread protests against the repressive regime continue to grow.\par \par \uc2 JAKE SULLIVAN (National Security Adviser): We are taking a range of aggressive actions to try to support the protesters in Iran.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Police in California say a suspected serial killer behind a string of murders is finally in custody.\par \par \uc2 TORI VERBER SALAZAR (San Joaquin District Attorney): Because you don`t come to our house and bring this kind of reign of terror.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Early voting begins today in Georgia, where a high stakes race for a Senate seat can tip the balance of power.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A group of fishermen had a shock of a lifetime at the Jersey Shore.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The \uc2 Yankees\uc2  pull off the victory to force a winner take all Game 5.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And this one is gone.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Here comes Allen with the keep and the leap.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Battle of the AFC`s best. Chiefs hosting the Bills.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Bills get their revenge and go to five and one.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Cowboys and Eagles. The Eagles, the only undefeated team left in the league.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Never had that like that. That`s what I see with that key lime.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Bring it that key lime.\par \par \uc2 MAN: From 40, on the way in, knuckleball. He got it. And here they come.\par \par \uc2 MAN: The Tennessee Volunteers with a game-winning field goal as time expired to beat Alabama.\par \par \uc2 MAN: What happens when your team beats the best team in the country? You take off with a goalpost of course.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Randy, how much is that going to cost?\par \par \uc2 MAN: It doesn`t matter. It happens every year.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh, boy.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It actually does matter.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`re asking for now to cover the costs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And they`re going to get it.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It matters.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: They`ve got the cash, but you can`t pay for that exuberance.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s nothing like college football.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming from an \uc2 NFL\uc2  analyst. That`s a big statement.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. Michelle Miller, welcome.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Glad to have you here. You in a good mood?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I`m in a better mood now that I`m with on the table live on TV with you, guys.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about the show started?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s good news.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a big show on this back-to-work Monday.\par \par \uc2 Chelsea Manning will be here. She will be live in studio for a conversation about her new memoir, a memoir about her decision to leak huge amounts of classified information about the wars in Iraq and Afghanistan.\par \par \uc2 Does she still think it was the right thing to do? We`ll ask her.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Plus Steve Hartman and the family are back for another edition of "Kindness 101" including another Hartman child who is participating for the first time. Today`s story is all about how to react honestly, when someone asks you a question like, "Isn`t my baby cute?"\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think we`ll kind of keep that one in your pocket if you think the answer is no.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s okay to lie on that one.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right. Yes, I think so.\par \par \uc2 But we are going to begin with our breaking news we mentioned off the top, from Ukraine, where deadly Russian attacks for civilians to run for their lives this morning in the capital city of Kyiv as multiple blasts ripped apart busy neighborhoods.\par \par \uc2 Photographers captured these images on your screen now of a so-called suicide drone. It`s an unmanned aircraft, which is designed to explode on impact.\par \par \uc2 Holly Williams has the latest now on these terrifying aerial attacks.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: For the second time this month, city streets in the center of Ukraine`s capital have been pummeled. Russia has repeatedly denied targeting civilians, but this neighborhood is jam-packed with apartment buildings, schools, and restaurants. Ukraine says the Russians used so called suicide drones, which explode on impact causing devastation.\par \par \uc2 The US says Iran has agreed to sell several hundred of them to Moscow.\par \par \uc2 Today`s attack comes after Russia claimed over the weekend that Ukraine shelled the city of Belgorod, just over the border with civilians wounded.\par \par \uc2 And in Eastern Ukraine, where the frontline extends for around 1,500 miles, there has been more deadly fighting.\par \par \uc2 (VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2  claimed last night that Russia has deployed 2,000 former prisoners to fight there, calling it state-sponsored terror.\par \par \uc2 In the early weeks of Russia`s brutal invasion, Moscow`s forces moved on Kyiv. The Ukrainian resistance though took the world by surprise forcing the Russians to retreat and redirect their attacks to the east of the country.\par \par \uc2 But after Ukraine blew up a bridge in territory occupied by Russia this month, vitally important for the Russian military, Moscow retaliated by again targeting the Ukrainian capital.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: On Saturday, Moscow says two of its soldiers shot and killed 11 other service members while they were training at a firing range inside Russia. The Russian authorities are calling it a terrorist attack. This comes as Russia continues its push to mobilize 300,000 reservists to beef up its forces in Ukraine-- Michelle.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Holly Williams, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Iran this morning. The death toll is rising from a fire at a prison that holds political prisoners and at least two Americans. Eight people are now confirmed dead from the fire that started on Saturday. It is now out.\par \par \uc2 Iran`s government said that it started during a fight among inmates, but critics dispute that claim. They suspect it is tied to anti-government protests that have gone on for nearly a month.\par \par \uc2 Ramy Inocencio has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Smoke billow over Iran`s infamous Evin Prison from a massive fire that ripped through the facility holding political prisoners and dual nationals, including Americans.\par \par \uc2 Gunshots echoed, projectiles were launched into the air.\par \par \uc2 Just outside, anti-government protesters called for the death of Iran`s Supreme Leader.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: There are conflicting narratives about how this fire started. State media claiming prisoners in this sewing workshop started a fight and a fire broke out spreading towards holding women and political prisoners.\par \par \uc2 MAHMOOD AMIRY-MOGHADDAM (Human rights observer): When you hear about and when you think about torture, forced confession, and political dissidence.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Mahmood Amiry-Moghaddam is a human rights observer. He says officials could have instigated the violence.\par \par \uc2 MAHMOOD AMIRY-MOGHADDAM: What we have heard more from the prisoners and their families is that they have attacked one of the wards and they have thrown teargas inside. There has been shooting inside the prison -- beating.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: Demonstrations have now been reported in more than 100 cities and towns sparked by the death of a 22-year-old who died while in custody of Iran`s Morality Police allegedly for wearing an improper head covering.\par \par \uc2 (PEOPLE protesting.)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: But toppling the regime is now the goal. And this time, it`s different.\par \par \uc2 MAHMOOD AMIRY-MOGHADDAM: People have lost their fear and it is a very bad news for the Iranian regime.\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: The regime has survived more than four decades, and if the clerics were to be toppled --\par \par \uc2 MAHMOOD AMIRY-MOGHADDAM: I would say, the countdown of the Islamic Republic has started.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 RAMY INOCENCIO: And CBS News has now learned that one Iranian-American citizen inside Evin Prison is safe, but he has been moved to solitary confinement. His name is Emad Shargi. He is there for serving espionage charges, but his family says that he is a political pawn. He is a political hostage and they are calling on President Biden to get him out ASAP, but case in point, this violence, these fires, more urgency -- needing more urgency to getting him out as soon as possible -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Possibly world changing events there in Iran. Ramy, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to move on now to major developments in the case of a possible serial killer in California. Police are saying they have arrested the man, you see on your screen now in this surveillance video suspected of killing five men since July.\par \par \uc2 As Danya Bacchus reports now, officials believe they interrupted him as he was about to kill again.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: A two-week manhunt in central California is finally over.\par \par \uc2 STANLEY MCFADDEN (Stockton Police Chief): We are sure we stopped another killing.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Stockton Police arrested Wesley Brownlee. They say he was driving around Stockton with a gun, dressed in black with a mask around his neck.\par \par \uc2 CHIEF STANLEY MCFADDEN: Our surveillance team followed this person while he was driving. We watched his patterns and determined he was on a mission to kill.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Police believe the 43-year-old ambushed and killed five men in Stockton between early July and late September. All but one were Hispanic and some were homeless.\par \par \uc2 The first victim was 35-year-old Paul Yaw. His mother spoke to CBS News about her son`s death earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 GRETA BOGROW (Paul Yaw`s mother): I don`t know if I`ll ever have closure because I`ll never get to see my son again.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: Last week, investigators released this grainy video of a suspect who they believe was also responsible for killing a man last year in Oakland and wounding a homeless woman in Stockton.\par \par \uc2 Kevin Lincoln is the Mayor of Stockton.\par \par \uc2 He says the killings have weighed heavily on the community, particularly the unhoused population and that the investigation is still ongoing.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR KEVIN LINCOLN (R) (Stockton, California): We`ll do everything that we can and will you utilize every resource at our disposal to ultimately bring the person or persons responsible for these senseless acts of violence to justice.\par \par \uc2 DANYA BACCHUS: This suspect is currently being held in County Jail without bail and is expected to be arraigned tomorrow on murder charges.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Danya Bacchus in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Now we turn to politics and the upcoming election. Our updated CBS News Battleground Tracker shows the Republicans are holding their advantage in the race to control the House of Representatives. With three weeks to go, we estimate Republicans have 224 seats to the Democrats 211, enough to take back the majority.\par \par \uc2 CBS News elections and surveys director, Anthony Salvanto joins us right now.\par \par \uc2 How are you doing, Anthony?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: I`m all right. How are you?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m good.\par \par \uc2 So, it seems like we were just talking about the Democrats` chipping away at the Republicans lead in the House. What happened?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, we were talking about that, and then it ran into this: Worsening views of the economy, Nate. Look at this, two-thirds of people say it is getting worse, and let me show you why.\par \par \uc2 One of the ways people interact with the economy is gas prices.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: We are paying more. You`re seeing those big signs up.\par \par \uc2 Back in August, there was some optimism. Majority said, well, okay, they`re going down. But look at this. Now, 63 percent say gas prices are going up in their area. So, I asked people, okay, well, let`s turn to politics here. Who do you blame for this? What`s the reason?\par \par \uc2 Well, look, there are more folks now who say the democratic policies have harmed the economy than helped it. It`s not the only reason they see for this, but they are reasoning out of results, right? They`re feeling the pinch, party is in power, okay. They must be doing something wrong.\par \par \uc2 And then by contrast, they think, well, Republican policies more likely would help. Again, they`re not in power, so that`s how they think.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Okay, so the topic of abortion has been tied to Democrats` gains. Where does that stand now?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, it has helped the Democrats and it is still important, but take a look at this chart. What issues are very important. And look at these big numbers over here, the economy, popping up at 80 percent.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Of course.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: So, that`s really swamping it right now, compared to others, and the even now, abortion doesn`t show up here. Now watch this. If you look at the splits between people who say abortion is very important, Democrats are winning them, still are, but Democrats are losing Republicans winning, those who say the economy is very important.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. So let`s talk about voter turnout.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: When it comes to enthusiasm in this election.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, look, this might be the most important thing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What do you have?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Let me show you how each breaks. People who say they will definitely vote -- look at this -- much smaller numbers among under 30.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: They`re not as engaged with politics. They`re feeling like the economy is not working for them. So, they`re kind of standing back and not getting as involved, but older people are. That`s a big difference because the younger people are part of the Democratic base, if they don`t show up, it hurts Democrats` chances.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Younger people, get out there and vote. It`s important.\par \par \uc2 Anthony Salvanto, we appreciate you.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Thanks, Nate.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: All right, early voting begins today in Georgia for a pivotal \uc2 US Senate\uc2  race. Voters have been responding to the only debate so far between Democratic Senator Raphael Warnock and Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 . This is one of the contests that could determine control of the Senate for the next two years.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion covered Friday`s debate and has been speaking with voters since then.\par \par \uc2 Nikole, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Good morning, Michelle.\par \par \uc2 It is unclear if the debate changed any minds, but voters officially get their say starting today. In fact, we`ve already seen a number of folks show up here early this morning and Georgia`s Secretary of State tells us, he is bracing for record turnout.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (GROUP shouting "Get out and Vote.")\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: It`s Game Day in Georgia. Tailgaters at UGA are divided as early voting kicks off.\par \par \uc2 LIBERTY LEBOS (Georgia voter): Raphael Warnock is who we need in the Senate right now.\par \par \uc2 MAN: \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has a good heart, you know, and we believe in how he thinks.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: We visited the campus where the Heisman Trophy winner made his name to the field that bears it in his hometown of Wrightsville.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And we are proud that he will be representative of small town America.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Near Warnock`s childhood home in Savannah, voters are looking his way. Like Tammy Jenkins, who went to high school with him.\par \par \uc2 TAMMY JENKINS (Georgia voter): He was always smart. I think he did -- doing a pretty good job.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are joined by a live studio audience --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: On the debate stage, both candidates appeal to those still undecided.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): This race is about who is ready to represent Georgia in the \uc2 US Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R) (Georgia Senate candidate): He is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , I am for Georgia.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: That GOP challenger again denied reports he paid for a woman`s abortion, but appeared to soften his stance on a total ban.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : And I say I support the Georgia Heartbeat Bill because that`s the bill of the people from Governor Kemp, and I said that has exemptions in it.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Warnock called out his past statements.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): He says no exceptions, even in the case of rape, incest, or the life of the mother. I think that`s extreme --\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But this exchange went viral as the pair clashed over law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): I`ve never pretended to be a police officer, and I`ve never I`ve never threatened a shootout with the police.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I am with many police officers.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: You have a prop. That is not allowed, sir.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : And we have to get behind our men and women in blue.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Both candidates continue to trade barbs as Walker returned to the campaign trail, but didn`t accept an invitation to a second debate Sunday, leaving an empty podium.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): My opponent, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not here and I think that half of being a Senator is showing up.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: The Walker campaign called last night a one-sided sham and said that badge Walker flashed at Friday`s debate was an honorary one from a local department. In a sign of how critical the stakes are, former President Obama is set to campaign here in Atlanta later this month -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Nikole Killion. Nikole, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We want to tell our viewers about a new policy taking effect today that could be a life changer for millions of Americans who are hearing impaired. For the first time, over-the-counter hearing aids will become widely available at a lower cost, significantly lower. It`s a move announced by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  over the summer targeting adults 18 and older with mild to moderate hearing loss.\par \par \uc2 Major retailers will sell the devices including \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , CVS, \uc2 Best Buy\uc2  and HyVee and here is an example of the potential price difference. \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  will sell hearing aids for $799.00 a pair, still steep but better than what the Biden administration says comparable models sold by specialists range from $2,800.00 a pair. Wow. $2,000.00 to $8,000.00 per pair. So, a big difference there.\par \par \uc2 By the way, it`s recommended that anyone thinking of buying one of these should receive a hearing test first but our medical contributor Dr. David Agus calls this policy a remarkable democratizing of technology.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, if it`s not a family member, we all know someone that is using a hearing aid.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We maybe those someone soon.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re right about that, Tony.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s turn to the weather.\par \par \uc2 For a lot of Americans, it is getting cold out there. Tens of millions are looking at a record cold temperatures this week, almost like it is winter already. If anyone knows why this is happening, it`s our guy, Jim Cantore from our partners at "\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2 ." Hey, Jim, I do not want to visit from Jack Frost just yet.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): Okay, so I`m going to make you a deal. If you get a visit from him, keep the shorts and the flip flops nearby, because we`re going to flip the other way.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 JIM CANTORE: A lot of the nation is going to go through this, all right, Nate, so get ready for it.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about here, guys. As we take a look at these temperatures here. We`re going to see the cooldown commence today. All right, it`s going to continue to reach its way south, across many, many areas here. We are talking about 237 million by the time we roll on into Wednesday, and that`s a lot of people here.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, a good chunk of the nation. We have 329 million people here in this country, they`re going to feel the cold temperatures below average. And then we`re going to get into the Northwest as we get into this weekend.\par \par \uc2 So, everybody gets a chance to certainly deal with this.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about it, all right, the cold air will continue to spread on down to the south and as it does, we are talking about bitterly cold air coming all the way down even into Florida. How do highs in the