speech to the Chinese Communist Party`s National Congress yesterday showed his iron grip on the party.\par \par \uc2 It is set to rubber stamp his continued role as Supreme Leader, making him the most powerful Chinese leader since Chairman Mao.\par \par \uc2 Elizabeth Palmer has the story from Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Liz, good morning.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 Until now, term limits had kept Chinese leaders to no more than 10 years in the job, but \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  has managed to change those rules and that tells you something about just how powerful he`s become.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: Here`s a man ready to command the country and the future, and if any of the 2,300 delegates here don`t like it, they wouldn`t say.\par \par \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  in public anyway is never wrong.\par \par \uc2 In party-propaganda like state TV`s fawning series "The Navigator," he leads the way into a socialist utopia.\par \par \uc2 Xi`s cult of personality recalls an earlier one, Chairman Mao, the revolutionary despot who ran China for almost 30 years. Like Mao, Xi has even put out a book and yes, there`s an app, too, \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  Thought recommended reading for loyal citizens.\par \par \uc2 In it, he analyzes Chinese governance, but what he doesn`t explain is why he thinks heavy-handed control and repression have to be part of it.\par \par \uc2 Like facial recognition technology for tracking people, and a Zero COVID Policy that has locked down millions of Chinese lives.\par \par \uc2 Jeff Kingston lectures in Asian politics at \uc2 Temple University\uc2  in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 What makes \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  tick?\par \par \uc2 JEFF KINGSTON (\uc2 Temple University\uc2 , Tokyo Asian Politics lecturer): Well, nobody really knows what makes him tick.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: There are clues his parents were communist elites. But in the 1960s, his father was denounced during Mao`s Cultural Revolution, and young Xi was exiled to a poor village for reeducation.\par \par \uc2 JEFF KINGSTON: I think he was very much influenced by his childhood experience and seeing the politics of the Cultural Revolution and how it suddenly could, you know, hit you even if you`re a privileged family.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: But Xi came back and climbed the party ranks until he got to the very top.\par \par \uc2 JEFF KINGSTON: What we see as a much more aggressive, assertive, saber rattling hegemonic policy, he sees as a patriotic policy consistent with his vision of a Great China.\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: For Xi, a great China includes Taiwan, and he told delegates he`d take control of the island, preferably peacefully, but he would not renounce force.\par \par \uc2 Xi is facing headwinds, though, a real estate crash, growing US military presence in the region and even internal dissent.\par \par \uc2 There was a rare glimpse of that last week on a Beijing overpass a banner that read, "We want food, not COVID tests." And "Xi is a dictator."\par \par \uc2 It was, of course, taken down in a hurry.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH PALMER: But it was an uncomfortable reminder to \uc2 Xi Jinping\uc2  that repression and control can fuel dissent, as well as snuff it out -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Elizabeth Palmer for us in Hong Kong. Liz, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how DNA collected from a crime victim was later used against her.\par \par \uc2 Janet Shamlian has our exclusive interview in a case that`s raising concerns about the use of DNA evidence.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: This morning, we`re learning about the case of a California woman that is raising concern nationwide about how law enforcement is using DNA evidence collected from crime victims.\par \par \uc2 The woman submitted her DNA as part of a rape kit after she was sexually assaulted, but years later, that unique genetic code was used to charge her with a crime.\par \par \uc2 In a CBS News exclusive, she is speaking to our Janet Shamlian.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: At the time, did you have any concerns about submitting a rape kit?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: No, no.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: No privacy concerns about sharing your DNA?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: No.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: This woman who asked got to be identified gave her DNA to San Francisco Police after being raped in 2016 in hopes of convicting the suspect. Police, by their own admission, added it to their criminal database, then used to arrest and charge her with theft last year.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I was in jail for 14 days.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: If you had known your DNA was going to go into a general database, would you have been more hesitant about submitting it as part of a sexual assault?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Would you have submitted it?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: No, I will not.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The charges dropped after the DA learned how the evidence was obtained. Now attorney, Adante Pointer, filing a Federal Civil Rights lawsuit on her behalf against the \uc2 City and County of San Francisco\uc2  and some employees claiming use of her DNA was unlawful search and seizure.\par \par \uc2 ADANTE POINTER (Attorney): What you have going on here is turning the justice system and our legal principles on their head.\par \par \uc2 By gaining someone`s consent to the most personal thing they have, in their most vulnerable moment, and then storing and cataloging that and weaponizing it against them for some unrelated purpose.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: It`s illegal for crime victim`s DNA to be used in Federal databases, but there is no such law in many States to stop officials from storing and using it for other purposes.\par \par \uc2 Jen King is a technology and privacy expert at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Is this case an indication that we`re losing possession of our private DNA?\par \par \uc2 JEN KING (\uc2 Stanford University\uc2  technology and privacy expert): Yes, it absolutely raises some deep concerns. Here is a point where someone was at their most vulnerable gave up their DNA, you know, for a criminal investigation, for justice, and then headed exploited by the SFPD.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: San Francisco`s Crime Lab stopped the practice after the incident, admitting it had also been storing the DNA of violent crime victims and victimized children.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I`m not an animal. I don`t want no specimen of me collected in any way. I`m no one`s pet, I`m my own person. I belong to one person only, and that`s God.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: The Department says it can`t comment on pending litigation, though the Chief earlier this year said: "We own this error." The City of San Francisco told CBS News: "It is committed to ensuring all victims of crime feel comfortable reporting issues to law enforcement, and has taken steps to safeguard victim information." It didn`t say what those steps are.\par \par \uc2 Is there an expectation of privacy when you submit your DNA to the police, after an assault?\par \par \uc2 ADANTE POINTER: The police, when they come and get the DNA and the permission from you, they don`t tell you, "Hey, by the way, you give me this and I can use it for whatever reason I want good, bad or other, I can sell it, I can let other people access it." When a woman or a person is in that point of vulnerability, and the police say "Hey, this is the key we need in order to help us to help you." That person in that moment is thinking to himself, I`ve given away my rights for a lifetime.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Experts say the incident could have a chilling effect on rape victims willingness to come forward.\par \par \uc2 What do you want to see happen?\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I want to see justice. I want to see women being protected. I want to see respect for humanity, for individuals. I want respect for myself.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: For \uc2 CBS\uc2  MORNINGS, Janet Shamlian, San Francisco.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, a very thought provoking piece. A lot of issues there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: California did pass legislation which Governor Newsom signed that would protect sexual assault victim`s DNA. It goes into effect January 1st. So there has been some change and movement here, but lots of questions getting raise.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Lots of questions.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: At least in one State, it has been answered.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: All right, up next, Vlad Duthiers is back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He is back.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: He is back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Lots of thousand cheadles.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Welcome back. But it is 7:42, it is time to check your weather right now -- your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: He is back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, you`re back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Time for "What to Watch" with Vlad Duthiers featuring Vlad Duthiers.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Hey.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Welcome back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: It is good to be back. Thank you guys so much. Everybody was texting me. Everybody was reaching out. Everybody in the studio. Everybody in the floor.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We missed you, buddy.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Michelle, I was saying -- Michelle will be -- I am like all sick in bed. I hear like bzz-bzz. I go, it`