ay everybody is a victim of something, a victim of the pandemic, a victim of inflation. I`ve heard victim -- the word "victim" used about things that I -- I don`t like that word.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: So stay tuned. Our Paramount+ streaming app will soon have an exclusive special on Ghislaine Maxwell. You don`t want to miss that. So, that`s sort of a clip from what she has been saying to Daphne from behind bars.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Pretty, pretty broad there. The biggest mistake is meeting Epstein.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Meeting, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: There are a couple of choices along the way she`s got to own, right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: A lot of choices. Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Yes, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 And there are actual victims in this.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: And she points that -- yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was a very long answer.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And exactly right and then she has been convicted for being the one who facilitated that victimhood. So, more to come on Paramount+.\par \par \uc2 All right, let`s talk BTS. The song "Dynamite" that we were playing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: All right. Members of the K-pop sensation group, BTS will fulfill their mandatory military service in South Korea starting with the oldest member.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Butter.")\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: No, this is not them signing up for the military, but that is Jin, who you see there in the foreground and the band`s management company says Jin who turns 30 in December will stop delaying his enlistment later this month. The six other group members also plan to serve in the military. Their management says BTS is looking forward to reconvening as a group again, but you`ll have to wait until 2025 following their mandatory service.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is mandatory.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Right.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: And hold on. It is mandatory. We used to have -- obviously, we used to have mandatory service in the United States.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There were some very famous celebrities who served in the military, Clark Gable, the king of Hollywood, big deal serving in United States Army Air Force during World War Two. Look at that.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Clark Gable serving in US military.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Look at the handsome, Clark Gable. Also heavyweight champion of the world, Joe Lewis also served in the military. Is when he was heavyweight champion of the world.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Joe Lewis enlisting in US military.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Retained his title. There you see Joe Lewis enlisting and he held on to the title until 1949. And of course, the most famous rock and roll singer of all time in the United States, Elvis Presley entered the army at the height -- the very height of his career.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Elvis Presley enlisting in US military.)\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Got his hair cut. So, it`s not uncommon.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: On the flip side, Ali --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: You`ve got Ali in the 1960s said, "I`m not going."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I wonder if any of the BTS members considered that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well --\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: There are some provisions in the South Korea`s Constitution that allow for people who have exemptions, but in this case --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay. They have to have an exemption.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: They delayed for 12 years, for the eldest. I mean, he is 30 years old.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I know. He is 30 years old.\par \par \uc2 All right --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We are talking about Tom Brady.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: Tom Brady, you all see Tom Brady on the sidelines?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: No.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: We`ve got a little time to show a little bit of Tom Brady screaming at his teammates. Nate, what`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, listen, Tom got a lot going on.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, he does.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: The Buccaneers aren`t playing well. He was screaming at his guys.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: A little colorful language. But when you`re a winner, you want to win. That`s how -- is that how you -- is that a leader galvanizes a football team?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I mean, I think the answer is no. Do you think he is regretting coming back?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, a little bit.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: He has got more trouble.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: He has got more trouble.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But I am glad you came back.\par \par \uc2 VLADIMIR DUTHIERS: I am back.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s the vision I am looking for.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: CBS MORNINGS will be right back. Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 **********************************(08:00AM, EDT)**********************************\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 Coming up in this hour: Chelsea Manning is here speaking for the first time about her new book. She writes about her decision to leak classified information and all that`s happened since.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I`m Michelle Miller. Noah Kahan is back in Vermont for his new album. He tells Jan Crawford why he had to go backward to understand himself.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`m Nate Burleson. Today`s "Kindness 101" is all about awkward conversations. Is it okay to lie to someone just to be polite?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Not about whether their baby is cute.\par \par \uc2 But first, let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener at Eight."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Breaking overnight, Russia launches new deadly drone attacks against Kyiv, including on people`s homes.\par \par \uc2 HOLLY WILLIAMS: This comes as Russia continues its push to mobilize 300,000 reservists.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Early voting begins in Georgia`s high stakes Senate race following a heated debate between Senator Raphael Warnock and \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : He has empowered criminals to think they are better than the police.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RAPHAEL WARNOCK (D-GA): My opponent has a problem with the truth.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: China`s President moves to cement his grip on power and accelerate his country`s military buildup.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And one couple`s effort to trap a mouse inside their home did not go quite as planned.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @CoryMannew.)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Oh man.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I can`t believe --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: A mouse in the house crawling all over you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: On your back. You feel the little hand pods? Ugh. God.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You guys are too young to remember that movie, "Ben."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I guess so.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You are. Other folks will know what I am talking about.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It sounds like I have homework assignment.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: You do have a homework assignment.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, but we begin this hour with a rising star who is lifting a lot of people with his music.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noah Kahan performance.)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s Noah Kahan, performing his song "Stick Season" which has gotten more than 60 million, that`s right 60 million global streams since it came out in July. It`s a breakup song filled with heartache and it peaked at number one on Spotify`s Viral Songs Chart.\par \par \uc2 "Stick Season" is now the title track on the singer-songwriter`s new album. It was recorded in written in Vermont where Kahan lives.\par \par \uc2 Jan Crawford recently visited and she is here now with this.\par \par \uc2 Jan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Well, good morning.\par \par \uc2 So you know, Noah Kahan really broke into the music scene when he was just 17 years old and he has released several hit songs since then. Now 25, Kahan is coming in to himself, but it took going back home to find out who he really wants to be.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 (NOAH KAHAN singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Singer Noah Kahan is going through a transformation and his hit song "Stick Season" is the first page of his new chapter.\par \par \uc2 (NOAH KAHAN singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: What does it mean to you, that song?\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN (Singer-songwriter): It`s about how things move forward. You know, in Vermont fall is beautiful and the leaves fall off the trees and it stinks and then it`s wintertime and it`s pretty again.\par \par \uc2 I think this song means so much more than me than just the song. I think it represents a new path for me in my career.\par \par \uc2 (NOAH KAHAN singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Life has changed a lot for the 25-year-old since he decided to put off college and right out of high school, signed a record deal.\par \par \uc2 (Clip for Noah Kahan performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: With hits like "Young Blood" and "Mess."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Mess.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Kahan is no stranger to success, but with his latest album, "Stick Season" he decided to take a risk, moving full force into folk music.\par \par \uc2 (NOAH KAHAN singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I was listening to a lot of your old stuff, and I mean it`s definitely different sound.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: I think I grew up listening to folk music and kind of narrative storytelling, songwriting. The songs I got signed on were definitely more pop-leaning and I wanted for this newest album to try kind of paying homage to those artists I grew up listening to who were able to tell stories.\par \par \uc2 And I was living in Vermont during the pandemic and I started to kind of tell a story about being in Vermont.\par \par \uc2 I felt like about coming here, in a lot of ways, I get to feel like a little kid. Vermont has got that freedom, too up here in New England.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Kahan spent some of his childhood here in rural Vermont, a place he returned to during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (NOAH KAHAN singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: He wrote "Come Over," a song on his new album "Here" on top of his grandparents` table in the living room of his mom`s house.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: It`s about like a writing room in Nashville or a studio in Los Angeles and there is like history here and there is something innately familial about writing here, and I love that.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And a song like "Come Over," then that`s really authentic.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: Yes, I feel like it comes through like your environment kind of guides what the music sounds like.\par \par \uc2 (NOAH KAHAN singing.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: His songs on "Stick Season" touch on themes like the difficulties of growing up, heartbreak, feeling misunderstood and isolated. Most of the songs are set in Vermont and New England.\par \par \uc2 (NOAH KAHAN singing.)\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: I think coming back home has kind of realigned me with who I was before, instead of this person I was trying to be out up there.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: So being true to yourself has enabled you to reach people in a way that you may never have been able to if you`ve gone to Nashville or LA.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: Yes. No, it`s funny, like the place that you feel like you were moving backwards is actually the place that sent me forward.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Yes, to go backwards to go forward in a way.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: I think like, you have to take stock of where you come from and who you are, and why that place made you who you are, in order to understand yourself.\par \par \uc2 I wanted to feel like this drive.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: The last song in his new album called "The View Between Villages" is about the complicated feelings that wash over him when he is about to return home.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: I wanted the song to build in the same way that the drive builds, I feel this complete sense of peace in the first part and, there is a line that`s like, "Past Alger Brook Road, I`m over the bridge."\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Kahan took us on the drive through his town that inspired this song.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: Here`s the bridge, "It`s the death of my dog, the stretch of my skin. It is all washing over me, I`m angry again."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noah Kahan performance.)\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: These memories come back.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: It`s once you`re in this place, it is like it`s taking you right back to where you were younger.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: And then the last line is "The car is in reverse."\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noah Kahan performance.)\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: "And everything is still." And as it dies down, it kind of feels like that peace again.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Why do you think your music is really resonating with people?\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: A lot of people have told me is that they feel left behind, they feel scared, they feel burned out and people have said that, you know, the biggest compliment I`ve received is that I`ve able to articulate something that they weren`t able to articulate that they felt themselves.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Noah Kahan performance.)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Do you think that your music is especially resonating with people of your generation because of social media? Because everything is -- it`s fake, a lot of it.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: But like your music is real. Your feelings that you`re expressing are raw, and painful, and universal.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Part of Me.")\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: I believe that people resonate with someone just presenting their truth at a really raw way.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Part of Me.")\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Part of Kahan`s truth is that sometimes he struggles. He has been open about going to therapy to help deal with depression and anxiety.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: I`ve come to learn that like depression and anxiety are going to be parts of my life forever. I do what I can to treat the symptoms, but it`s still in there and having music has been really important for me.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Kahan says right now, he is in a good place and he is excited that the music he feels he was always meant to make is finally out in the world.\par \par \uc2 NOAH KAHAN: When I was growing up and I listened to a person sing about something that I was feeling like I felt like no one in the world had ever felt that before and hearing someone say it was the most gratifying thing in the world.\par \par \uc2 My greatest goal is to be that for other people, to kind of provide that lifeline for somebody else is the ultimate goal of me as a songwriter.\par \par \uc2 (NOAH KAHAN singing.)\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Now the second single of Kahan`s new album, you heard it there at the end is called "Northern Attitude" and it has already hit more than six and a half million streams on \uc2 Spotify\uc2 . His tour just started and most shows are sold out.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I think all of them will be after hearing that.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Oh yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course, and I love what he said. He said it`s not a writing room in Nashville. This isn`t a studio in LA. This is home and you know, the meaning of "Stick," it is layered of course, but it`s like he is going exactly where his roots are.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: And remembering where you came from, and then building on that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: . to find your true self, and I think that`s why his music is resonating with this generation, trying to find your place in a world that seems so uncertain.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes, I feel for this generation because they are really stuck in between. They have to have all the answers.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But we are always told when we are young, "Go chase your dreams. Chase your dreams."\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Then you realize you`re chasing something that isn`t able to be touched. It is like an intangible item that we can`t physically touch and then you realize, you`re just running.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Right. Right. Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Returning home is amazing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Jan, thank you for returning to the table.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: With your sables.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Thank you, guys.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: So good to see you.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: I know.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good to see you in person.\par \par \uc2 JAN CRAWFORD: Great to be here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ahead, we`re going to talk with former Army Intelligence analyst and whistleblower, Chelsea Manning, in her first interview about her revealing new book. There she is. Hi, Chelsea.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 8:10. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Coming up in our series. "Kindness101," Steve Hartman and his kids teach us a lesson in tact.\par \par \uc2 Plus, journalist, April Ryan, who has covered the White House longer than any other Black woman in history will be here. She will tell us about her new book, "Black Women Will Save the World: An Anthem." This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Back in 2010, Chelsea Manning who we will speak to in a moment downloaded nearly 750,000 classified and sensitive military and diplomatic documents during her service as an Intelligence analyst in the \uc2 US Army\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She then sent those documents to \uc2 WikiLeaks\uc2 . It was one of the largest unauthorized disclosures of government documents in American history. She was convicted of 20 charges including espionage and theft and sentenced to 35 years in prison, the longest sentence of its kind, ever.\par \par \uc2 Back in 2017, though, President Obama commuted her sentence and she was released after serving nearly seven years. Now, Manning is the author of the new book, "README.txt," or "README.txt." It`s significant to the story.\par \par \uc2 It chronicles her life growing up in Oklahoma, her time in the military, her incarceration, and eventually her release.\par \par \uc2 Chelsea Manning is here for her first interview about the book.\par \par \uc2 Chelsea, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING (Former US Army Intelligence): Good morning. Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 First, there`s a lot in the book. We`re not going to get to get to everything.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People should read it. It`s a real book. It is sharply written. It`s real. It`s raw. I enjoyed every page of it, really.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I do wonder what you hope people will take away from it. What are their walking away ideas?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: So there -- I haven`t really been able to tell my version of events. I haven`t really been able to tell my story. And so, this is the first -- this is sort of my first draft or, if you will, of history, from my perspective.\par \par \uc2 While there is the -- well, I did testify a little bit during the Court Martial -- my voice has been kind of lost throughout this whole process and I feel like that`s going to be the main takeaway. I think the second takeaway from this book is going to be that this is where I come from. This is what -- this is who I am now and how the events of the last 15 years have transpired to shape me and this is a coming of age story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. So in that story, I mean, your rise to prominence is a disclosure of all these secret and classified documents, and you go through that. There was no one breaking point that led you to do it.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: People should read the book to find out why it happened, but could you talk about what your goal was, and looking back now whether you think you`ve accomplished it?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Right. So, I think that one of the primary goals that I had was to try to -- I always had this sense that, especially whenever I was in -- whenever I was actually on the ground in Iraq, that there was the story that was happening on the ground, and I always considered myself a very, you know, informed citizen prior to this and very well-trained at my job.\par \par \uc2 And there was this just cognitive dissonance between what was being told to the public and what was being shared with the public and what I was actually seeing on the ground. And I think that that was one of the main drivers was this immense cognitive dissonance that I had regarding the story that was being you know, portrayed -- how the wars were being portrayed, especially after the Obama administration come in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You write in the book that you didn`t consider what the cost for your personal freedom would be in this disclosure. No one had gone to prison for something like this.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Right? It ended up being a very steep cost that you paid. Was it worth it? Is a world a better place?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Well, you know, I don`t view it through that. I don`t do it through the lens of whether it was worth it or not. I just, I am very glad that the information is out there. And I think that, you know, people can review it themselves.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, I can`t --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can`t talk about it all.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: I can`t talk about it all and there are some redactions in the book, because we went through the government review process. So you know, it`s very -- it was very difficult to try to tell the story.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But when you tell it -- well, you really do because you`ve got your childhood in there, it`s got the service overseas, and it`s got the aftermath. Even in the aftermath, there are people on both sides of the political aisle who say you`re a traitor, you committed a crime to rise to prominence. Even if you were right, you did it the wrong way. What do you say to those people?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: I don`t really encounter them very often. So, I don`t really -- mostly I`m asked by journalists this question. So it just -- it feels kind of dated, like, I think that there was more of that a few years ago, but I just -- I not really encountering, like those same criticisms, as I would have in say 2010 or 2013.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So you`re in a no regrets state of mind right now?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: I`m not viewing it that way. I just -- I am very forward thinking, and one of the reasons why I wanted to write this book is to get past this period in time, you know, I think that we`re entering a very unstable period of time, obviously, you know, just sort of who I am and the way that I analyze the world, I have a lot of concerns about the future.\par \par \uc2 I`m also optimistic about, you know, how we can overcome some of the challenges that we have ahead of us.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: There is an amazing moment in the book where you`ve uploaded all these documents to \uc2 WikiLeaks\uc2 , and then at the same time, you`re done with secrets in your personal life, and you begin to be more public about your transition.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a big part of the book, your fight to transition while in prison in a military prison.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: What do you want people to take away from that part of the book?\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: I mean, I`m just trying to tell what my experience was, you know. Obviously, I needed access to care and I think that I got that. You know, it took a little bit of fighting, eventually, I got that access to care in the end.\par \par \uc2 And, yes, you know, it hasn`t really even been -- you know, being trans can sort of be, I think, turned into something that more than it is because, you know, once you go through the transition process, you sort of finish like you sort of integrate into society. And so, you know, I`m very excited about that part. But yes, it was definitely a very difficult road to travel on.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, a very different road. Well, we`re glad to see you on the other side of it. You are DJing again, first time in 15 years, recently in Brooklyn.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I read all about it.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The music is I think too hip for me, but big fans out there. You`re going to do it again.\par \par \uc2 CHELSEA MANNING: Yes, I`m doing it actually for the after party for the book launch party.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Am I invited? Chelsea Manning, thank you very much. "README.txt" goes on sale tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, "Talk of the Table" -- we`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (08:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we`re having a good time, too. Welcome back to CBS MORNINGS. It is time to bring you some of the stories that are to "Talk of the Table"\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Just give me a call.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Don`t stop me now.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, I`m not going to stop you. It is your turn.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Take it away.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: My "Talk of the Table" -- all about Queen and a song from the band featuring its late frontman Freddie Mercury, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from "Face it Alone.")\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: A little slower cadence, but the band says the song, "Face it Alone" was recorded in the late 1980s, but never made it onto the album, "The Miracle."\par \par \uc2 Now Queen`s production and archive team found it again while working on an upcoming boxset reissue of the album due for release next month. Of course, you know, \uc2 Mercury\uc2  died from AIDS related pneumonia back in 1991 two years after that album first came out. You also know "Bohemian Rhapsody" was a huge Oscar-winning hit a couple of years ago.\par \par \uc2 Surprising that they didn`t come across this this song back then, so much going on. I love Queen. I love Queen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: 0Who you talking to up there?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Thank you, Lord. Thank you, Freddie.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Talking to Freddie.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I am talking to Freddie.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Talking to Freddie. I mean, what is your favorite Queen song?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Another One Bites the Dust."\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: There it is.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on, now.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That`s my first.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Come on, now.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I would have signed off on that one, too. I know them by ear, but not by name.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Okay.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`re all right, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My "Talk of the Table" is an explanation for why I stopped dead on the street this weekend and ignored my family for a full and solid minute. It was this video on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What is it?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Do we have it?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Okay.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from @MIkeBradleyMKE/\uc2 Twitter\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: This is the frozen pizza section of a Wisconsin supermarket, only the frozen pizza section, only the frozen pizza section.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It goes on for days.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just a whole aisle?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is the whole aisle. It takes a full minute to detail all of it. There is more at the end with special deals. So, then I got interested in like what`s going on in Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: By the way a lot of people did this.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What`s going on in Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It has got like 38,000 likes as of Saturday.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Cheese, cheese.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So I don`t know. Apparently Wisconsinites eat more frozen pizza. It`s still going. They eat more frozen pizza than any State in America.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Really?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, more than anybody else.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It`s the Cheese State. Right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It is partly the cheese. For some reason, they also make a lot of the frozen pizzas, like I grew up on Celeste Pizza.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can see that stuff right there. But out of Wisconsin comes DiGiorno, Tombstone, Jack`s, Roma, Palermo`s. The list goes on here.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, so that`s the answer.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, I crashed my car trying to save some pizza once, so I wanted to get that out there because I know I`m going to get some tweets later. Yes, it happened during my \uc2 NFL\uc2  career.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: To save some pizza?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. Well, I reached over. It was wet outside. I thought I was going to hit another car and I just lost control. So, make sure you buckle up your pizza if you`re picking it up.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You`ve got put the pizza in the trunk?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes, I didn`t know that. I didn`t know that.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I am so glad, you didn`t hurt somebody.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, you know -- well, I hurt myself and the team. Sad story, huh?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is there a police record?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, leaning back up a little bit. All right, so there are some people in Canada that actually might enjoy more frozen pizzas at their store because my "Talk of the Table" is a new service for cannabis users in Canada.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Of course.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: For the first time anywhere, Uber Eats will start delivering marijuana. Yes, you heard me right. It`s only offering this service in Toronto where it is legal to buy, use, and possess recreational marijuana.\par \par \uc2 Anyone ordering cannabis has to be 19 or older. The company says a certified delivery person will check the customer`s age and sobriety upon arrival.\par \par \uc2 Now listen, we`re coming out of the pandemic and quarantine. We`re trying to get people out of the house. You know, if you can order snacks, food, and marijuana. You`re going to have a lot of people stuck on the coach for a long time.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: It`s okay to distribute, I presume, too, because that`s what he`s doing.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, yes. The whole market is legal.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Well, listen, if it is legal and the person is of age. You know, I don`t see anything wrong with it.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, the driver is checking sobriety. What is that? Hard to call.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It is up to his discretion -- his or her discretion.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And the other thing is, 19 and up? It`s not 18 and up? They were like 18 would be crazy.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`ve got to do 19.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Just add another year on that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, that`s --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: So, we`ll see how it works in Canada. Shoutout to Canada.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Hey, I hope it goes well. I really do. Keep an eye on in it, New York City.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 All-righty then.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: See how it goes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am serious.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Turning now to our series "Kindness 101" with Steve Hartman. That`s when we share stories built around kindness and character and the people who have mastered those qualities.\par \par \uc2 Today`s lesson is on tact.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Important lesson.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Definitely need to know more about tact myself.\par \par \uc2 Steve and his kids teach us to think about people`s feelings when you`re delivering bad news, like when you`re telling somebody, their baby actually might not be as cute as they think.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Good morning. This is my daughter, Meryl.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Hi.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And we`re here to discuss something very important for civil conversation.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: It`s called tact. And for our definition, we go over to my brother, Emmett, at the Dictionary Desk -- Emmett.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Good morning, Meryl.\par \par \uc2 Tact is having a keen sense of what to say in a conversation when the subject turns a little dicey, especially in a conversation that could potentially hurt someone`s feelings.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Right. It`s like when we talk about my hair. Now, when I look in the mirror, I see brown, I want to think my hair is brown. But you guys always insist on contradicting me and saying my hair is gray.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: That`s because it is gray. It really couldn`t be more gray.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: It`s like you have some sort of hair dysmorphia.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: Even your wife disagrees with you.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Obviously, tact is something we`re still working on around here, which is why today I`m reaching deep into the archives for story I did back when your brother, George was born, a story that explores the delicate balance between honesty and kindness, known as tact.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: We think she`s actually the cutest baby ever to be born. People have said that to us.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You know, they say that to every baby.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Actually. They were telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: What is it that makes parents so delusional?\par \par \uc2 MAN: I don`t know. We just feel like she actually is that cute.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So unabashedly biased.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: I know what`s cute. I know what`s ugly. That`s cute.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Is it?\par \par \uc2 Not long ago, we asked parents to send in pictures of both cute and ugly babies. Nearly 2,000 people sent in shots of their supposedly adorable offspring. But almost no one copped to having even an average looking child.\par \par \uc2 That`s why after my son George was born, I decided to find out once and for all: Is my son cute or not?\par \par \uc2 MAN: There we go.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: That is a cute baby. Look at those eyes.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: As I expected, everyone said he was cute.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, what a big smile.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: This is the picture I showed people at first. But then, as a control, I started showing this distorted version.\par \par \uc2 MAN: That baby is adorable.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Really?\par \par \uc2 MAN: Right? Oh, come on. Look at that. Look at the smile.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Believe it or not. Everyone`s still gushed over my little monster.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Oh, he`s so cute.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: And so it went. No matter who I asked --\par \par \uc2 FATHER KELLY: Oh, good Lord.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Or how ugly I made the picture.\par \par \uc2 FATHER KELLY: That`s not an insect. No?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: No, that`s a hairy mole.\par \par \uc2 FATHER KELLY: That`s a hairy mole.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: But it is still cute, though.\par \par \uc2 FATHER KELLY: Oh, sure, it sure is.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: At the risk of a lightning strike. I then asked Father Kelly, if it`s a sin to tell parents their baby is cute when you really don`t think it is.\par \par \uc2 FATHER KELLY: That`s not a lie. There`s all the difference between a lie and what they call a mental reservation.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Well, if that`s true then how do I know that you think my child is cute?\par \par \uc2 FATHER KELLY: Oh, I see because he is.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I was about to give up. And that`s when I thought of Marlene Wallach.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE WALLACH (Wilhelmina Models): What are you looking for?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: I am looking for honesty.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE WALLACH: That`s what you`ve got.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Marlene heads up Wilhelmina Kids, a child modeling agency. I showed her pictures of what George really looks like, and although at first she too, couldn`t help but be diplomatic.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE WALLACH: It`s a cute baby.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Eventually, I got it out of her.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE WALLACH: I am just saying --\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: You`re lying, aren`t you? It`s okay. I`ve been looking for somebody to tell me the truth.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE WALLACH: Okay, the truth is, the baby is very average. There you go.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: How come nobody else would tell me my baby is average looking.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE WALLACH: But now you hate me?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: No, I don`t know. No, I don`t.\par \par \uc2 MARLENE WALLACH: I think you might.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: No.\par \par \uc2 Fourteen years have passed. Today, George isn`t into the whole TV thing as much as his younger siblings, but he has graciously agreed to join us now.\par \par \uc2 Hello, George.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: Hey.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: So George, I didn`t show you that story for the longest time because I was kind of worried how you`d take it. And now I`m wondering, how did you take it?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: Pretty well. I mean, I am not average by any stretch of the imagination.\par \par \uc2 EMMETT HARTMAN: Did it hurt your feelings to see that you were never going to be a baby model?\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: When do you think it`s especially important to be tactful? What sort of situations?\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: When someone cavalier, pridefully asks you like do you like this? Or do you think this is cool?\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: If somebody`s saying do you like this dress? I would choose politeness.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: If someone said, do you like this dress? I would say, no, but provide a redeeming statement such as, maybe you could do such and such with it.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Like leave it in the closet.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE HARTMAN: Yes, like sew sequence onto it. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 STEVE HARTMAN: Sew sequence on it.\par \par \uc2 Maybe the only thing we`ve settled here is that cuteness is relative. If the baby is your relative, it`s cute.\par \par \uc2 Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 MERYL HARTMAN: And don`t forget to stay kind.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Oh, I love that.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I`ve got to have dinner with the Hartman`s.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I`m telling you --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: If we honestly raffle off a ticket for dinner with a Hartman`s, it would go for a lot of money.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: No doubt about it. So when he went to the church, what was the response? Good Lord?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Good Lord and then the priest took the glasses off just to gather himself.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: He was saying in his head, "The Lord forgive me."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: He was like, "Please Lord, give me the strength."\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Because you can`t tell somebody that their baby is ugly. I`m just saying that, right? If you do, pack an air conditioner in your casket because it is going to be hot down there. You can`t say that.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I agree completely.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You`ve got just be polite. You know what I mean?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, I think honesty has limits. Right there.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And we are all biased. We all think that our kids and babies look great.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No, I --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Michelle, you`re the one that tells people that babies average and ugly?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I wouldn`t say average and ugly.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: What would you say?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Somebody --\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I know -- oh such big cheeks. Oh such a pronounced chin.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Got you. It kind of make sense.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Oh, he doesn`t have any hair.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Oh, got you. I see what you`re doing.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: I have, but then I wouldn`t do that to my children.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All my kids are like Wallace Shawn when they were babies. It is all wrinkled up and small face and everything.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: That was mine. My daughter had huge cheeks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: All right, coming up next, longtime White House correspondent, April Ryan is in our progressive greenroom to share her new book and why she believes "Black Women Will Save The World."\par \par \uc2 But first, here`s a check of your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: April Ryan is the longest serving Black female White House correspondent ever. She joined the White House Press Corps back 25 years ago and has covered five presidencies.\par \par \uc2 Ryan is the Washington bureau chief for TheGrio. Her new book is called "Black Women Will Save The World: An Anthem." In it, Ryan reflects on her own experiences, shares conversations with Black female leaders, and lays out the obstacles that many face and overcome.\par \par \uc2 April Ryan, good morning.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN ("Black Women Will Save The World" author): Good morning, Michelle. How are you?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: First, the title in and of itself is a pretty tall order. How do Black women do it? How have they done it? And why are you sharing the playbook?\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: I`m sharing the playbook for one because now is the moment. We are seeing a change, a shift, and no one is marking the history. And I believe from the unique perch that I`ve been blessed to sit in for 25 years. I said, "I have to do it."\par \par \uc2 We`re at a moment where we have the first Black woman Vice President, the first Black woman on the \uc2 US Supreme Court\uc2 . Women are everywhere, domestic policy adviser, Susan Rice, Shalanda Young, Office of Management and Budget. I mean, the list goes on and that`s just government and \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  in the White House briefing room, we cannot forget her.\par \par \uc2 But the list goes on.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: In the Church house, the school house, government House, in your house.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: So we have to do this. I feel like I`m in church with you right now.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Vaccine making, in the medical profession.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: Dr. Kizzmekia Corbett, \uc2 Moderna\uc2 , and that was considered the number one a vaccine in COVID.\par \par \uc2 People need to understand we are now in spaces and places that we`ve never been before, and no one is marking the moment and I have to, because we are exceptional. We have been lifting the load from the time we came to this nation to now and beyond. And if you don`t mark the moment, we will forget the moment and the numbers will fall.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Your mother played a big role in this book. Can you talk about that and also talk about what your daughters should hopefully take away from this?\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: Yes, my mother was a pioneering woman herself. She enabled so many young Black students at Morgan State University until her death, 42 and a half years.\par \par \uc2 I don`t care where in the world I go, people come "Oh, I remember your mother, Miss Ryan," and it touches my heart. My mother has been gone for about 15 years now. But she told me, I could be and do anything I wanted to do and I believed her.\par \par \uc2 And now, I`m sitting here is the longest serving Black female White House correspondent in history.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: Because of her, the strength, the uplift, the nurturing, the recipe that they leave for us -- I`m not just talking about cooking.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: I`m talking about the recipe for life.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: We hold up the home, we gird up our children, and we push them forward into a society that doesn`t always love us. But when we come back, we know that we get instruction, love, and foundation from that woman who understands the haunts and the ghost of the past and what lays ahead for the future.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Black women have mastered the art of juggling.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: While wearing the weight of the world on their shoulders.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: There`s a lot of Black women that feel invisible. What is your advice? You mentioned occupying space. Some people feel invisible in these spaces, and then some women need advice on how to occupy space with your elbows out. What is your advice to them?\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: I`m glad you brought that up. I might have a great story in this book from Valerie Jarrett, former adviser to former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who said sometimes when she shows up in these major rooms with world leaders at the table, she`s like, "Wait a minute. Do I belong here?" Yes, she belongs -- right, this woman who has been a world leader herself.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Sure.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: And she comes from a lineage of slavery, of the haunts and the ghosts of slavery and many of us walk into rooms and say okay, "I`m here." \uc2 Shirley Chisholm\uc2  said, "If you don`t have a seat at the table, bring a folding chair." We are now convening those tables, have the folding chair on our back, but we`re also making those solid chairs so we can sit in.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Well, you did it in the White House press briefing room.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: But I stand on the shoulders of so many. I stand on the shoulders of Alice Dunnigan and Ethel Payne, the late great Gwen Ifill. Michelle, there are was so many women who came before me and poured into me, so I could stand in some of the toughest of times and the best of times.\par \par \uc2 But I want to get back to this invisible piece, and this is so poignant. Valerie Jarrett gave me a story a long time ago, and she said when we talk about invisibility, think about Michelle Obama.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: When (she) was President of the United States, when they would go for their walks out in -- beyond the White House, people would not recognize Michelle Obama. How can you not recognize --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How about that?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Wow.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: Michelle Obama, invisibility.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE MILLER: Right.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: April Ryan, we appreciate you. Just like this cover, you deserve your flowers, too as.\par \par \uc2 APRIL RYAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: "Black Women Will Save The World," it goes on sale tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That does it for us. We`ll see you tomorrow on CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBST000020221017eiah00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For October 16, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Anthony Salvanto, Charlie D`Agata, Margaret Brennan \par \uc2 9,108 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation: Has the Democratic momentum heading into the midterm elections stalled, as economic headwinds pick up? Democrats` hopes of a last-minute economic rebound before Election Day were dashed with yet another round of sobering inflation numbers, indicating that the Federal Reserve`s rate hikes so far haven`t helped.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is talking up the party`s economic efforts on the campaign trail and warning about the alternative.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Republicans win, inflation is going to get worse. It`s that simple.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But will voters buy what he`s selling, with prices so high and no relief in sight?\par \par \uc2 Infrastructure is at the heart of the administration`s economic plans. We will talk to Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  and \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  economist \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 . She will tell us about the financial pressures facing Americans, especially parents.\par \par \uc2 The global economy is also struggling. Adding to the pressure, Russia`s attacks in Ukraine intensify, destroying regional supply lines and energy infrastructure. Ukraine`s Ambassador to the U.S. \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2  will join us.\par \par \uc2 Then: Dramatic video from the congressional investigation into the January 6 attack shows lawmakers pleading for help to secure the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE NANCY PELOSI (D-California): It`s just horrendous, and all at the instigation of the president of the United States.\par \par \uc2 SENATOR CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-New York): I`m going to call up the effing Secretary of DOD.\par \par \uc2 We have some senators who are still in their hideaways. They need massive personnel now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We will learn more about the behind-the-scenes that day from the authors of a new book, "Unchecked."\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 We are just over three weeks away from midterm Election Day. Voters are already casting ballots in 18 states, and voting will start in eight more this week. What are they focused on and which party do they think should be in control of the House and Senate, as well as the 36 states where there are ongoing contests for governor?\par \par \uc2 Our new CBS News Battleground Tracker poll shows the Republicans are still favored to capture the House, with an estimated 224-seat majority. That`s six seats more than they need to take control. Our last estimates showed Democrats narrowing the gap, but that momentum has stalled.\par \par \uc2 CBS News elections and surveys director Anthony Salvanto is here to tell us why.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Anthony. What indicates momentum is stalling, and why?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Good morning, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 So this is the first time since we have updated the tracker throughout the summer and into the early fall where Democrats haven`t been cutting into that Republican lead. And it comes at a time when people think the economy is getting worse.\par \par \uc2 And, in fact, two-thirds of people say that. Now, we have gotten bad news this week about inflation. And the way that people interact with that often is gas prices. Well, there`s been this stark turnaround. It was, in August, people thought they saw gas prices going down. Now a majority says they think that they`re going up in their area. So that`s one point.\par \par \uc2 But we wanted to understand, how does this connect politically? So, I asked specifically, who`s responsible for this? And are the Democrats and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  responsible for this? The answer is somewhat. People understand that there are other factors there. They know there`s global factors. They know there`s supply issues. But Democrats are, on balance, seen as having been more harmful than helpful.\par \par \uc2 Now, a president is always somewhat tied to their economy. And \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , when asked, gets some responsibility for this, not necessarily a lot. But two-thirds of people do say they think he and the administration could be doing more.\par \par \uc2 So, all that nets out. Democrats are still losing people for whom the economy is the most important issue. They`re still trailing with people who say that their financial situation isn`t good. And that is part of the reason that has stalled.\par \par \uc2 I should add this. Up until now, we have talked a lot about the abortion issue, because that`s been underpinning a lot of those Democratic gains. It is still critical. Democrats are still winning voters who prioritize the abortion issue. But the thing is, there are not more of them in the electorate now than there were last month.\par \par \uc2 So there`s always been this fight about, what is this election about? To the extent that Democrats cannot make it more about the abortion issue than it has been, that helps keep those balance of -- that balance of power right in place.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 And turnout is always a factor. We know voter turnout is typically higher in that group 65 and older. For people on fixed income, inflation is really painful. What do you see?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: It`s really a concern. I`m glad you raise it, because what happens is, they show concern about inflation, about prices. They also show concern about the stock market, because retirees might have money in the market, of course. So that`s out there as well.\par \par \uc2 You`re right. They do vote. In our models, they are showing up. They are saying they`re going show up. And that`s typical for a midterm. So it`s a really, really important voting bloc, Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, Anthony, what could change here?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Yes, let`s look at the possibilities that we can see in the data.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the Republicans have a turnout advantage. More of them say they`re enthusiastic. More of them say that they`re definitely going to vote. What could get the Democrats in contention to maybe hold the House? It`s young people. If young people were to show up more than they say they`re going to, you plug that into the -- into the model, and the Democratic seat number goes up, where they`re right in contention to maybe get a bear majority.\par \par \uc2 So, if you want to know how the Democrats could hold the House, it`s young people showing up more than they say they will.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, let`s talk about that Republican turnout advantage. There are so many close congressional districts that it wouldn`t take very much for even more of them to flip to the Republicans. So, if their turnout advantage goes up even a little bit more, a lot of those seats are going to fall their way, and the model would estimate they would get into the 230s.\par \par \uc2 And that would be an even larger majority. So your takeaway there is, yes, a lot of this comes down to turnout. But that is specifically how.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Anthony Salvanto, you`re going to be busy.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Indeed.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And races are tightening in the Senate too; 35 states hold contests November the 8th. And CBS News has identified 10 important ones that could determine the majority. There are five that we`re watching very closely, Georgia, Pennsylvania, Wisconsin, Arizona, and Nevada.\par \par \uc2 Later on in the broadcast, we will be taking a closer look at that Georgia race, because it has gotten a lot of attention lately.\par \par \uc2 We want to now turn to the secretary of transportation, \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and thank you for being here in person.\par \par \uc2 PETE BUTTIGIEG (U.S. Transportation Secretary): Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to pick up where we just left off on the polling, because it looks like Democrats have a problem here.\par \par \uc2 As you heard, more than two-thirds of registered voters, 68 percent, think your administration, the Biden administration, could be doing more to combat inflation. This is a top concern for all voters.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: It`s also a top concern for the president. It`s one of the reasons why he`s made clear that his top economic priority is fighting inflation.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a very clear choice right now between the policies that we`re advancing on Capitol Hill and in this administration and the policies that have been put forward legislatively by our Republican friends in Congress.\par \par \uc2 Our focus has been on reducing the pressure of cost of living on families. For example, take the Inflation Reduction Act. Part of what that does, at a time when we have pressure on people because the cost of living is going too high, is to cut the costs of things like prescription drugs.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: In the future.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Of course, Republicans voted against that. And...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s not...\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Right, but they have also made clear right now that they`re proposing legislation to reverse that, to repeal that.\par \par \uc2 So, you have something like letting Medicare negotiate the price of prescription drugs, something Americans have wanted to happen for years. We finally got it done. The president signed it, Congress passed it. They`re already seeking to reverse that.\par \par \uc2 So it`s a very clear choice, a very clear difference in approaches here right now on Capitol Hill and among officeholders, where the focus for Democrats, the focus for the president is to cut that cost of living and to cut the pressure, get people more breathing room at a time where inflation remains a major concern.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But it`s not just drug prices that are the problem. I mean, across the board, inflation is hitting people, the cost of shelter, incredible.\par \par \uc2 The administration`s policy, though, to speak about the affirmative, because you say it`s a better platform, so let`s talk about it. If you have pumped $3.6 trillion in fiscal spending into the economy, and you`re not politically getting credit for it, and inflation is not coming down, how do you argue to the American people that it`s actually the best alternative?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, because, if we hadn`t done that, if we hadn`t rescued the economy through the American Rescue Plan, we would not have had the 10 million jobs that were created under this president. We wouldn`t be seeing some of the lowest unemployment numbers in the history of the republic.\par \par \uc2 We would be faced with the kinds of problems that we were faced with when the president arrived, which was an economy that was facing a very real risk of freefall. We`re in a situation right now where demand has come back. Consumer confidence is up. People have more money in their pockets. And an almost record number of Americans are working.\par \par \uc2 The other side of that is...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: There`s also the record inflation, four-decade high.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: And that`s because the -- right.\par \par \uc2 That`s because the supply side is straining to keep up with the demand, which is why we`re also working...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, that`s one factor.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: I think really the main factor.\par \par \uc2 But imagine if we hadn`t raised demand and supply was even worse, which is where we would have been if we hadn`t had the infrastructure work, which is helping on the supply side, and the rescue of the American economy that has brought back demand.\par \par \uc2 So, if anybody`s asking, was it a good idea to rescue the American economy, the answer is yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Right.\par \par \uc2 Well, it`s not just Republicans who make the argument that fiscal spending contributed to some of the inflation. And, in fact, some of the fiscal spending was under the last administration`s watch, in terms of pouring in the rescue funds you`re talking about.\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 San Francisco Fed\uc2  has put forward estimates that between three- tenths to 3 percent of percentage points in recent inflation is due to fiscal spending.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: In other words, not most of it, right, by those numbers.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It`s not nothing.\par \par \uc2 So, to argue that government spending isn`t a contributor here, it doesn`t fully play out. So, what is the alternative in a new Congress?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Yes. Well, first of all...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I mean, are you saying that we have got to stop spending here, we`re not going to move on taxes, but we`re also not going to pump in more money; the best we have is what we`ve put forward?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, again, part of the challenge -- part of the challenge we have is the productive capacity of our country racing to keep up.\par \par \uc2 So, failing to invest in that wouldn`t make the problem better. It would make it worse. But, again, the numbers you just quoted to me would make clear that a majority of inflation is not attributable to fiscal policy.\par \par \uc2 And according to the numbers that were just in the package just now, the American people understand that. They understand that inflation is a global phenomenon, but we`re fighting it here at home with measures to take that pressure off of families.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: And it`s why we strongly believe that we should continue in the direction of prioritizing not tax loopholes for billionaires, not corporate profits, but allowing Americans to be able to get by with the income that they`re making.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, we`re going to talk to an economist next to go through the numbers, but on what the political messaging is, the president said this week that the economy is both strong as hell, but also, if there is a recession, it`ll be very slight.\par \par \uc2 What exactly is the forecast?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, look, I mean, forecasting is, by its nature, something that is a little bit uncertain.\par \par \uc2 What we know is that that...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: But do you -- that`s political spin.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, look, I don`t think anybody could argue that, for example, our unemployment numbers are anything but strong as hell. They`re under 4 percent. That almost never happens. We`re at or near the definition of full employment.\par \par \uc2 We also don`t have any illusions about the challenges that Americans face with prices.\par \par \uc2 But that`s why it`s mystifying that, as we speak, you have got Republicans in Congress arguing against the things that we have done to give Americans a little more breathing room, voting against measures to make prescription drugs cheaper, voting against the $35 insulin cap that is going to be especially important in the -- in an environment where you have inflation, against the energy credits that are going to help more Americans save on energy.\par \par \uc2 We are squarely focused on making it easier for Americans to get by on their income.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to get to infrastructure in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But, quickly, I want to ask you, the president campaigned on a lot of things. Paid leave was one of them. And Democrats stripped that out of legislation that they were able to get through.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, I wouldn`t describe that as something that Democrats alone did.\par \par \uc2 But, yes, obviously, paid leave wasn`t a part...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: It didn`t get through.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Yes, it didn`t make it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And you have got unified control right now, although a slim majority. I get it.\par \par \uc2 But in this new scenario we are talking about -- and you just saw the numbers -- it looks like a very real threat that Republicans will control at least one house. So, are you acknowledging that, basically, you have gotten through what you can get through, give up on paid leave for the end of the Biden administration?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, we`re not going to give up on anything, because these are good policies that are wildly popular among the American people.\par \par \uc2 Most Republicans think this is a good policy, just not most Republicans in Congress. And so, in the same way that we were able to get that infrastructure bill through, where a number of Republicans crossed over to work with Democrats on the president`s priority, I think any other priority, you have to at least give it a shot.\par \par \uc2 And I would point out, after repeated declarations that the infrastructure bill was dead, the way that succeeded, as well as the other policy wins we have had, aren`t just good news. They specifically validate the president`s theory of change, which is that you -- even in a divided Washington, you can get things done, and, sometimes, you can get them done with bipartisan support.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to get to infrastructure, but you know, with paid leave, I was talking about the dispute between Democrats, between Senator Manchin and the rest of the party, on that.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, hold on.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That was a Democratic disagreement on paid leave.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Even -- but if even one Republican were prepared to support paid lead, we`d be in a different territory.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s not let 50 Republicans off the hook...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: ... because we couldn`t get alignment with one or two Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. So it will be taken up in the new Congress?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: I don`t know what the priorities of the new Congress will be.\par \par \uc2 I know that it`s a good policy, and we will keep fighting to get it done.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: OK. And I will keep asking about it.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 So, you have started doling out some of the sort of allocations here that were part of that more than $1 trillion infrastructure law. You have got $120 billion, that you personally, I guess, at the department have discretion over in awarding some of these projects.\par \par \uc2 Where do you prioritize it? Because we have had a number of mayors on this program talk about their frustration that the money is not getting to them, that they know it`s been promised, they have spoken to the White House, but it`s not getting to Miami, Florida, and Jackson, Mississippi.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: Well, look, we have got a lot of discretionary grant programs to run over the course of the next five years.\par \par \uc2 And a lot of applications come in. We say yes to as many as we can. And I can tell you, we`re supporting infrastructure improvement right now in every state in the union. Now, even now, even with this wonderful funding, we won`t be able to say yes to every single project that every local community wants to do.\par \par \uc2 But we can do more than we have ever done before. And I have been one of those mayors, right? I have been a mayor knocking on the door of the DOT, trying to get funding. And, back then, we only had a sliver to work with of what we do now.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So...\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: It is amazing what we have been able to see as I have traveled the country with good news on everything, from port improvements that will help with our supply chain, to fixing bridges that are so deteriorated, the school buses and ambulances can`t use them because there`s a weight limit, to -- we`re in the Inland Empire, Fontana, California, kids walking, having to compete with traffic basically along a highway just to walk to school.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to fix that. We`re getting so many things done. But it`s not going to be overnight. Of course it`s not.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No. Well...\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY PETE BUTTIGIEG: That`s part of how it works with infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: They have been frustrated that it hasn`t been delivered, though it has been promised.\par \par \uc2 But I know we have to leave it there today. Mr. Secretary, thank you for coming on the program.\par \par \uc2 We will be back in a moment, so stay with us on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We go now to \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2  Professor \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 , who previously served as the Department of Labor`s chief economist under former President Obama.\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you, Betsey. Good to have you on the program.\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON\uc2  (Former Council of Economic Advisers Member): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to get straight to it.\par \par \uc2 Last week, one of the top Fed officials, Loretta Mester, said inflation has not yet peaked. How much worse will it get, in your view?\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON\uc2 : Well, I -- you know, it is hard to know how much worse it will get, because what people really care a lot about is, like, energy prices, where things are really volatile.\par \par \uc2 I think the thing that economists are really worried about is when we strip out those volatile food and energy prices and we look underneath that core inflation. that`s the stuff that`s hard to bring down. And when that hit 6.6 percent, I think that made people very worried about how long that will last.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, some of the things, we`re going to see housing prices, rental prices continue to go up, because a lot of the values of the homes that are already out there are up, and that hasn`t filtered through into CPI.\par \par \uc2 And then there are people out there who, really, their wages are so far below, in real terms, where they were, that they`re clamoring for a raise. And I think that those are the folks who aren`t ready to say, I`m going to keep working at my current nominal wage. So I think that`s the kind of risk that we`re facing right now, is, we`re going to see these increased wage pressure that`s going to keep pushing -- pushing prices up.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think we have to worry, though, that it`s going to go much above where it currently is, which is that sort of 6.6 percent, as the core underlying inflation. But we got to be worried that it`s stuck there .\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, there are plenty of people worried. Based on our CBS News polling, 70 percent of voters believe the national economy is bad; 53 percent say their personal financial situation is good. So there`s that. But a large part of the economy is consumer psychology here and what the consumer actually does.\par \par \uc2 And the perception is not that the economy is strong as hell, as the president said, but that these pricing pressures are hurting.\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON\uc2 : You know, it`s such a weird time because we have a record number of job opportunities out there.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it is a great time to look for a new job, to take a new job. And the people who are doing that are getting very large wage increases and are being made better off. And hiring is just continuing at this really high rate.\par \par \uc2 So, if you`re thinking about finding work, changing jobs, better using your skills, it`s a great economy. If you just want to stay where you`re at...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON\uc2 : ... and keep buying the stuff you were buying, you`re struggling, because you`re seeing food prices are up. You`re seeing medical costs going up.\par \par \uc2 Everything around you is going up a little bit. And if your boss doesn`t want to give you a raise, you can`t -- you know, you can`t make ends meet.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, one of the areas that I know you`ve focused on and we want to talk about is the childcare and caregiver shortage.\par \par \uc2 That`s one area of the economy where the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  says there are 100,000 fewer childcare workers now than there were before the pandemic. Where did they go? What -- what is going on?\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON\uc2 : OK.\par \par \uc2 Well, one thing is that we are missing a lot of foreign-born women. I mean, and so that makes us confront the reality, which is immigrant women provide a lot of childcare in this economy. And they left. So we just have fewer immigrant women. So that`s a direct answer to your question, where did people go?\par \par \uc2 I think what`s making everybody really feel the pinch is, the labor supply of parents, parents -- the share of parents who are in the labor force, both mothers and fathers, has returned to where it was pre-pandemic. But, as you just said, we`re missing childcare workers.\par \par \uc2 So, no wonder parents feel like they`re struggling, because now they`re trying to do it with less formal care. And I think that that is causing a lot of stress. And they`re -- that problem is getting worse, because, look, the median wage of a childcare worker is still $12 an hour.\par \par \uc2 So you can do better going to McDonald`s, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , Target. There`s a lot of jobs out there that`s going to pay you more than $12 an hour. And that`s making it harder to hire childcare workers. It`s why in, the last jobs month`s report, we saw a decline in the number of childcare workers, when other things are continuing to grow.\par \par \uc2 But if we pay childcare workers more...\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON\uc2 : ... well, where`s that going to come from? That`s going to mean an increase in the price of childcare services. And now we get back to inflation.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Well, and...\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON\uc2 : You know, the real -- yes, go ahead Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: No, and we also have teacher shortages around the country.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what are you seeing in terms of female employment and the return to the work force? Isn`t that still a big problem?\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON\uc2 : You know, actually, prime age women are back. So that`s our 25-to-54-year-old. So they`re, you know, not -- we sort of focus on that age because they`re not in school, they`re not retired.\par \par \uc2 That age group is back. The problem we had with teachers, with airline pilots, with a lot of jobs is that people retired early. So we`re missing our sort of most skilled older workers, and that`s causing some problems.\par \par \uc2 And then, on the other end, we didn`t train a lot of people in jobs during the pandemic. We didn`t have those student teachers in the classroom in 2020 and 2021. And so we are not training young ones, the older ones are retiring early, and then all the burden is falling on these sort of middle- aged teachers, pilots, all sorts of jobs like that.\par \par \uc2 And it`s making the job worse, which is putting, again, sort of upward wage pressure, which is leading to inflation. You know, the thing is, people are very upset about inflation, and they feel awful about it, but the reality is that inflation hurts some people more than others.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BETSEY STEVENSON\uc2 : It`s not -- it`s a generalized increase in prices, but not all prices are raising the same amount.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got childcare workers where the price of childcare hasn`t gone up to keep up with inflation. And so that`s hurting people in the childcare industry. And the result is, we have fewer people working in childcare.\par \par \uc2 But we have got parents, on the other hand, who can`t really afford to pay more for childcare, because they couldn`t afford to pay for childcare before we had inflation. And that brings us back to needing a government solution to help ensure that all families can afford high-quality childcare.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And that`s why the next Congress is going to matter so much.\par \par \uc2 And, Betsey, thank you so much for talking to us about what you`re seeing out there.\par \par \uc2 We will take a break and be more -- back with more Face the Nation, so stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And if you can`t watch the full Face the Nation, you can set your DVR, and we are replayed on our CBS News Streaming Network throughout the day on Sundays.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Welcome back to FACE THE NATION.\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of Russian missiles have rained down on Ukrainian territory over the past week, most of them aimed at civilian targets.\par \par \uc2 CBS News senior foreign correspondent Charlie D`Agata is in Dnipro, Ukraine, with more.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice over): Days after unleashing the heaviest bombardment that Ukraine has seen since the invasion began, striking a dozen cities across the country, in a coordinated series of rocket and long-range missile attacks, Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  said there is no need for more massive strikes, for now. At a news conference in Kazakhstan, he said he had no regrets, and that Russia is doing everything right.\par \par \uc2 The merciless battering of civilians in front line cities like Zaporizhzhia tell a different story. Residents told us a missile slammed into this neighborhood at around 2:00 in the morning.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (on camera): The missile punched a hole right through this apartment block, causing it to collapse on those inside. Tearing apart not just homes, but lives. The blast scattered children`s clothes among the trees.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice over): At one point, the heavy machinery clearing the rubble stopped. A drone hovered overhead as rescuers worked by hand. And another body was lifted from beneath the debris. Investigators work to put a name to another victim of Putin`s war.\par \par \uc2 Sirhi Nikanorv (ph) lives in the adjacent apartment and escaped serious injury, but he`s so shell-shocked, he can hardly speak.\par \par \uc2 My very close friends live there on the first floor, he said. I lost friends on the second floor, too.\par \par \uc2 And yet, despite Russia`s onslaught, troops have lost ground to Ukrainian forces on the battlefield, where the counteroffensive has advanced south at such a rate the Kremlin installed authorities in the city of Kherson and urge residents to evacuate to parts of Russia.\par \par \uc2 In his address last night, President Zelenskyy switched from Ukrainian to Russian saying -- as for the citizens of Russia who do not want to participate in this criminal war, who surrender to Ukrainian captivity, we`ll save their lives.\par \par \uc2 We visited some of the dozens of those recently liberated villages. The high school that served as a Russian military HQ bears the scars of the battle to wrestle it back. Down the road, we found Oksana, still reeling.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (on camera): What did you think when you first saw Ukrainian soldiers here?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice over): Happiness. She said, I was just very happy. In tears she described the terror under Russian occupation, saying, we were all suffering. It was so hard and so scary.\par \par \uc2 Now, free from Russian military rule, for the first time in months.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: The next day, following that huge barrage, President Zelenskyy pleaded with leaders of the \uc2 G7\uc2 , specifically the United States, for more air defense systems. And while there has been a pledge by the White House for more advanced weapons, they can`t get here quickly enough.\par \par \uc2 Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Charlie D`Agata, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we are joined now by Ukraine`s ambassador to the United States, \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Madam Ambassador, good to have you back.\par \par \uc2 Let`s pick up where my colleague just left off. Last time President Zelenskyy was on this program at the end of September, he talked specifically about the delivery of air defense systems, thanking the United States, but they still have not been delivered. What is the holdup in the delivery of American weapons?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA (Ukrainian Ambassador to the U.S.): Thank you for having me, and thank you for talking about that.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we still need all the weapons we are talking about with air defense being the priority after this horrible strike that we`ve all seen return, and not only to select cities, but everywhere in Ukraine, and especially in Kyiv, and the infrastructure before the winter.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, this systems difficult to produce and they`re not ready on the shelves waiting, but we are doing everything possible and asking our partners to do everything possible to speed up not only the delivery, but also the ordering of the systems. So, hopefully, you heard your president been very clear on trying to accelerate the delivery of those. And we are counting on more systems to be announced and decision taken to produce because we do need to secure as many places in Ukraine as possible, as many children in Ukraine as possible from Russian rockets.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: The Pentagon has said it`s still about a month before those NASAMS come.\par \par \uc2 Let`s talk about what we just heard on Friday from Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . He said that the mobilization drive to increase the number of soldiers will end in the next two weeks, and that massive strikes are no longer needed.\par \par \uc2 What does your government assess he means by this? What are you preparing for?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, as we said, you know, 235 days, no limits and no moral restrictions from Russian aggressor. So, we should be prepared for everything.\par \par \uc2 And it`s actually irrelevant at this point what he says because this partial mobilization has been a big failure. People in Russia do not want to be mobilized. They are not equipped. They are not prepared. They are not motivated yet. There are a lot of them, but it has been a failure in Russia. They are probably not forcing it because they will have the plant mobilization in the autumn. We don`t know.\par \par \uc2 All we -- all we are focused on in staying the course and defending our country everywhere and being prepared for anything that can come from the Russian Federation. We have seen all the war crimes. We have seen all the rocket attacks. We have seen them using these Iranian Shahid (ph) drones on civilian residential areas, killing families. So, what he says is actually irrelevant because what they do, and everyone see what they do, this is what we have to pay attention to.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to ask you about billionaire Elon Musk. He seemed to have reversed his position yesterday, at least publicly, in saying that Starlink, which is an internet service that he has made available in Ukraine, which has helped your military communicate on the battle, that he will pay for it. This was after some back and forth to the Pentagon about not paying for it.\par \par \uc2 What`s the bottom line? Can your military depend on this for communication or not?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, look, I have disagreed with Elon Musk on some of his views about Crimea, and we were happy to discuss it with him. But regard to the company, we have started cooperation with Starlink. Excellent corporation. Before this phase of the war, we got the Starlinks in Ukraine very quickly. In some areas, for humanitarian support, it`s the only connection that we have. And it`s very important to continue having it. And I`m positive that we will find a solution there. And --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, it`s not resolved yet?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: It`s there, it`s working, we will need to be working for a longer time. And, look, we are proud to be one of the fastest growing Starlink countries globally too. But the payment, who`s going to pay, I`m sure we will find the solution there.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Because the "\uc2 Financial Times\uc2 " had reported that there were outages from the satellite that had a direct impact on the battlefield, particularly in the east of the country. Is this payment dispute hurting your military?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, look, there is no payment dispute, per se. I mean there are discussions on -- that we need more and where we need them and how we will need them. But the actual reason for the outages is Russian aggression. And the fact that they are bombing our infrastructure. And this disruption there, the connectivity of all the cellular operators and trying to interfere also with others. We`re trying to resolve it in many possible ways. And Starlink has been an instrumental part of the solution.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: France`s president drew a lot of scrutiny this week because he said, if Russia carries out a nuclear attack on your country, that his country would not respond with nuclear weapons. What exactly is the level of risk right now from the nuclear threat, whether it`s from tactical weapons on the battlefield or what`s happening at your nuclear power plant?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: We cannot rule out anything. And as you - as you said, you know, the nuclear threat is already there because Russians illegally control one of the largest nuclear plants in Europe. And the situation there deteriorates as it`s not returned to Ukraine because it`s -- the personnel there is not in the - in the quantity that needs to be there. They are threatened by Russians. We don`t know exactly what happens to them. And the - the station has to be returned to Ukraine in order to decrease the risk. So, this is a big risk and we need Russia to get out.\par \par \uc2 With regard to the use of nuclear weapons, I think, again, its issue is much bigger than Ukraine. We, in Ukraine, will resist and we will not give up regardless of what Russian federation uses against us. We have, I think, proven it, from rockets, to atrocities, to anything that they try to do in Ukraine, it doesn`t break our will. So, there is no point for Russian federation to use anything else because it will not stop us in defending our homes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: With regard to the global response, it has to be very harsh, even for the talks of Russian federation and Putin about using the nuclear weapons because this is a clear red line. This is on -- you know, on what the whole security infrastructure of Europe and global is built --\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, when the French president says something like that, does it muddy that line? Does it blur that line?\par \par \uc2 OKSANA MARKAROVA: Well, I just - I just hope that everyone understands that the way it should be understood. In 1994, Ukraine became the country that voluntarily gave up the third largest nuclear arsenal and received assurances of our safety because we gave up our nuclear weapons. And if a nuclear weapon will be used by a nuclear power, an aggressor, against non- nuclear country like Ukraine, then the whole nuclear deterrence system is going to be under risk. So, I think we all together cannot allow Russian federation to use it.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Ambassador, thank you very much for your time. Always good to have you with us.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We turn now to our political panel.\par \par \uc2 Rachael Bade of "Politico" is here with us and Karoun Demirjian of "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ." They`re the authors of "Unchecked: The Untold Story Behind Congress` Botched Impeachments of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Good morning to you ladies.\par \par \uc2 KAROUN DEMIRJIAN, (Author, "Unchecked": Good morning.\par \par \uc2 RACHAEL BADE (Author, "Unchecked"): Good morning.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: So, this is a pretty in-depth autopsy, as you can see by the size here, of what you lay out as strategic missteps, in - in many ways by Democrats themselves in their attempt to hold the former president accountable. The impeachment work Senate didn`t convict.\par \par \uc2 It feels like it`s full of these moments where history could have gone in a different direction. And you lay that all out here. Why do you think it`s important to do that?\par \par \uc2 RACHAEL BADE: Yes, so there`s this prevailing wisdom. There`s sort of two things we`re going to be challenging here in this book in our reporting. The first one is that Trump`s acquittal, both of them, were inevitable. We find, as you mentioned, a whole bunch of pivotal moments where things could have gone the other way. Moderate Democrat - or moderate Republicans who, behind the scenes, were freaking out about Trump`s behavior and came so close to voting to impeach or convict. But for certain things that Democrats had done that alienated them or times when Democrats put pressure on their own investigators not to do a full investigation, to try to make the strongest case possible to the public that Trump was dangerous. And so we challenge that.\par \par \uc2 The second sort of preconceived notion we challenge is that, you know, there`s this sort of sentiment out there that Republicans just sort of turned a blind eye to everything Trump did and that`s why he got away with everything while Democrats were doing the best they can to make the strongest case publicly. But, again, we find a lot of examples where Democrats were putting their political concerns over strategic fact-finding to just try to lay out the case and - and privately they have told us that they sort of -- they didn`t -- they did half-baked impeachment and that they didn`t make the strongest case possible, and some of them have regrets.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, Karoun, I mean, in some ways the January 6th committee is a continuation of this case. Do you think that it addressed some of these mistakes as you were laying out?\par \par \uc2 KAROUN DEMIRJIAN: I think that the work the January 6th committee has done in pulling in Republican witnesses, in making sure they fight their subpoenas all the way through the court is by itself a recognition that they pulled some punches when they were actually going after Trump during the first two impeachments and the first two impeachment trials. We`re seeing that corrective action basically taking place in the way and the procedures in which they`ve gone forward with the January 6th investigation, which is going over a lot of similar ground, as at least the second impeachment trial did.\par \par \uc2 And so that is almost a tacit acknowledgment of, you know, we had the opportunity to take these steps almost two years ago and we chose not to take them. And I think in this book we question -- we look at those intraparty fights, basically, that stopped those steps from happening, these moments where but for a couple more hours sometimes, but for, you know, a person`s sense of confidence versus their fear of being able to, you know, actually use the congressional -- constitutional heft of the power that they had to check a president, things may have been able to go a different way. And so, yes, it`s clearly a case where there were opportunities for them to go after witnesses, to run down subpoenas that they didn`t take in the past that they are trying to correct in the present, but Trump`s not in office anymore and you can`t have the same result.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And there was reluctance from the very top, from Speaker Pelosi.\par \par \uc2 KAROUN DEMIRJIAN: Very top.\par \par \uc2 RACHAEL BADE: That`s right. That`s right. We have reporting in the book that on January 6th Pelosi herself shut down an effort by some of her members to try to impeach that -- Trump that very night. And, you know, there was like -- McCarthy was furious. Republicans were just as upset as Democrats that night. I mean what would have happened if they had just done that, put it on the floor? We`ll never know, of course.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 RACHAEL BADE: Well, that`s a great question. But there was -- it was not just Pelosi, right? It was \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and his staff who we learned through our reporting would put sort of pressure on Jamie Raskin`s impeachment team to do a quick trial to not summon and call and test in court their subpoenas, to go after people like Mike Pence`s aides, which now the January 6th committee is doing that, but back then they just wanted the investigation to move quickly, the trial to be over with, to sort of save Biden`s presidency from this messy business of impeachment.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: I want to -- because what you reference there reminds me of what we just heard of this past week from the committee with this video the public at least had never seen before. I want to play some of it here in a minute.\par \par \uc2 And just for our viewers, this is where congressional leaders had been evacuated to a safe place out in Virginia during the attack. And you`ll hear Speaker Pelosi and the Senate majority leader talking to the attorney general at the time, acting one, Jeffrey Rosen, and you`ll see that Republicans are standing there alongside the Democrats at one point.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VC)\par \par \uc2 REP. SPEAKER PELOSI (D-CA): They`re breaking the law in many different ways. And, quite frankly, much of it at the instigation of the president of the United States. And now if - if he could - could at least somebody --\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Yes, why don`t you get the president to tell them to leave the Capitol, Mr. Attorney General, in your law enforcement responsibility. A public statement they should all leave.\par \par \uc2 This cannot be just we`re waiting for so and so. We need them there now. Whoever you got.\par \par \uc2 How soon in the future can you have the place evacuated? You know, cleaned out?\par \par \uc2 JEFFREY ROSEN (Acting Attorney General): I don`t want to speak for the leadership that`s going to be - that`s responsible for executing the -- the operation. So, I`m not going to say that because they`re the -- on the ground and they`re the experts.\par \par \uc2 PELOSI: Well, just pretend -- just pretend for a moment it was the Pentagon or the White House or some other entity that was under siege. And let me say, you can logistically get people there as you make the plan.\par \par \uc2 (END VC)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And, ultimately, we hear later from the Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  speaking to Speaker Pelosi, authorizes the movement to eventually happen.\par \par \uc2 But, Karoun, you see Republican leaders there gathered with Democrats in this moment. How is it that there was no quick action?\par \par \uc2 KAROUN DEMIRJIAN: Well, as our reporting shows in a piece that actually -- an excerpt that ran in "The Atlantic" this morning, the Republicans were there across the hall to meet with the Democrats. We`re in this moment and this mindset where they were basically saying, we`re done with the White House. We`ve got to work together here to make this happen.\par \par \uc2 You had people working the phones on both sides of the aisle. McConnell was doing the exact same thing as the Democrats, trying to get people to move towards the Capitol. It`s -- it`s - it`s surprising that even with all of that effort it did still take hours for that help to arrive. But it shows you that this was a moment where kind of everybody was working - working together to achieve the same ends, which is striking in itself given how quickly that coalition splintered shortly thereafter.\par \par \uc2 Rachael referenced a moment ago that there was this moment -- our reporting showed this moment the night of January 6th where after they left that terrible crisis hours, they`re back on the floor of the -- in - in the Capitol, trying to conclude the Electoral College results. And rank and file Democrats approached the leaders with an impeachment resolution, saying, let`s do this now. Let`s capitalize on the anger. Let`s capitalize on this galvanized sense that we`re all in this together and the party lines don`t matter. And Pelosi decides not to go for it in that moment.\par \par \uc2 She talks at another point in that video about being ready to throw a physical punch at President Trump, but she pulled the constitutional punch they could have potentially leveled in that moment on that day.\par \par \uc2 RACHAEL BADE: Yes, I think the most striking thing about Fort McNair and what was happening was that it was really the first time in Trump`s, you know, four years of Trump`s presidency that you saw congressional leaders on both sides of the aisle sort of come together to try to bring Trump to heal. I mean, you know, McConnell, before he went over to the Democrats` room, and, by the way, we have this bizarre color in the book about how -- for some reason Republicans and Democrats were escorted to different rooms at Fort McNair despite this emergency. And they`re each trying to get through to the Pentagon. They`re each trying to figure out, why is the National Guard not moving. And McConnell, at one point, you know, he - his - his staff is trying to get him on the phone with top defense leaders. They`re put on hold. And people were like, why are -- Republicans were furious, why - why are - he`s - why`s he putting on hold?\par \par \uc2 So, you know, he crosses the room. And, again, he goes and finds Pelosi and Schumer and says, we`ve got to work together. They -- together they were not able to get answers from the Pentagon about why things were taking so fast. And so they all agree that the only person they can really turn to right now is not Trump, it`s \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And so they call him. And that`s where we have this sort of clear the Capitol order.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RACHAEL BADE: It`s sort of the backstory of how Pence got to that moment. It was because Hill leaders, together, were - were pleading with him to do something.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: And -- and it`s - it`s a powerful read. It`s a powerful moment. And, ultimately, it raises even more questions.\par \par \uc2 RACHAEL BADE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: About the path we`re on. Ladies, congratulations on the book. We`ll leave it there and be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: We want to take a closer look now at the Georgia Senate race. One of those key races that could decide which party controls the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Nikole Killion reports from Athens.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice over): Saturdays in the south are meant for college football. But it was Friday`s debate featuring University of Georgia legend \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  that was the talk of the tailgate.\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WALGREN (Georgia Voter): It kind of reaffirmed what I already thought, that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is not fit for office.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (on camera): In what way?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW WALGREN: Doesn`t know policy. Has clearly fabricated parts of his life.\par \par \uc2 TRAVIS PECK (Georgia Voter): \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  has a good heart, you know. And we believe in, you know, a lot of similar things.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION (voice over): In what might have been their only face-to- face debate Friday night, the GOP challenger and Democratic incumbent Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  made their cases to Georgia voters.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2  (D), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: This race is about who`s ready to represent Georgia.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), GEORGIA SENATE CANDIDATE: He`s for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . I`m for Georgia.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Dogged by recent reports that he allegedly paid for a woman`s abortion, Walker issued another denial.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : That`s a lie.\par \par \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : My opponent has a problem with the truth.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But on abortion rights, he appeared to soften his stance, backing off a total ban.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I support the Georgia heartbeat bill because that`s the bill of the people from Governor Kemp. And I said that has exceptions in it.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Walker repeatedly tied Warnock to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : Can he tell me why he voted with joe Biden 96 percent of the time if he was standing for Georgia?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: When asked about their respective standard bearers in 2041, Walker eagerly endorsed former President Trump, but Warnock was noncommittal?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Would you support President Biden running for a second term in 2024?\par \par \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK\uc2 : I have not spent a minute thinking about what politician should run for what in 2024.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: But it was this exchange that went viral as the clashes clashed over law enforcement and Walker`s claim of being a police officer.\par \par \uc2 RAPHAEL WARNOCK: I`ve never pretended to be a police officer. And -- and - and I`ve never -- I`ve never threatened a shootout with the police.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : I am - work with many police officers.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You have a prop.\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 : Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: That is not allowed, sir.\par \par \uc2 (END VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: A Walker aide tells CBS News that was an honorary badge from a local department. But in a sign of just how critical the stakes are here, former President Obama is set to campaign in Georgia later this month. One of many stops he`ll make before the midterms. No word if President Biden or former President Trump will also come down here.\par \par \uc2 Margaret.\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: Nikole, we will be watching. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MARGARET BRENNAN: That`s it for us today. Thank you for watching. Until next week, for FACE THE NATION, I`m Margaret Brennan.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020221016eiag00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 Alex Jones` Defamation Verdict; Final January 6th Hearing; New Russian Attacks in Ukraine; LA City Council Member Resigns; Navy SEAL Death Investigation; Kids COVID Boosters Approved; Recovering from Hurricane Ian; Dodging Putin`s Draft; Respiratory Virus Concerns in Kids; The Debate over Woke Politics; A Zest for Life; Scary Good Books; Hasan Minhaj Live; \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Gayle King, Tony Dokoupil, Nate Burleson, Nikki Battiste, Scott  MacFarlane, Charlie D`Agata, Mark Strassmann, David Martin, Stephanie  Abrams, Chris Livesay, Celine Gounder, Nancy Chen, Janet Shamlian \par \uc2 14,571 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: CBS This Morning\par \uc2 CBST\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Welcome, welcome to CBS MORNINGS on this Thursday. I`m Gayle King.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I`m Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: And I`m Nate Burleson.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: We`re all here. So, let`s go to today`s "Eye Opener." It`s your world in 90 seconds.\par \par \uc2 The January 6 Select Committee promises new revelations at what could be its final public hearing later today.\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Operatives were out mobilizing and galvanizing domestic violent extremist groups.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Ukraine`s President calls on Russia to demilitarize the country`s largest nuclear plant while Moscow launches new attacks.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (President of the United States): Brutal. It is beyond the pale.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The former head of the Los Angeles City Council has resigned her Council seat over leaked racist remarks.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Alex Jones mocked Sandy Hook families as he is ordered to pay nearly $1 billion in damages.\par \par \uc2 ALEX JONES ("Infowars" host): $20 million, $50, $million, $80 million, $100 million. Blah-blah.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Health officials authorized COVID vaccine booster shots for five to 11-year-olds ahead of an expected winter surge.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Wide receiver Davante Adams has been charged with a misdemeanor assault after pushing a photographer to the ground.\par \par \uc2 MAN: A new friend wants to join our party. Hello, friend.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Dodgers bat off the rally goose.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: The rally goose though doesn`t help the Dodgers. This series is tied.\par \par \uc2 MAN: I know this is an intense playoff game. We`re having a blast calling it, but I cannot help but laugh.\par \par \uc2 JAKE TAPPER (CNN Anchor): What`s your message to Democrats, who like you like you, who like what you`ve done, but are concerned about your age and the demands of the job?\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 : You know, well, you can come work out with me in the morning.\par \par \uc2 STEPHEN COLBERT ("The Late Show with Stephen Colbert" host): Do you even lift brougham? Okay. Meet me at 0400 and toss the old medicine ball round. Okay. Watch me pull a fishing boat down the Potomac with just my chompers and I`ll punch a steak until it is tartar, peel off my singlet jumping to the ice barrel.\par \par \uc2 It`s a workout routine I call P-I`m-almost-90X. Come on Jake Jack. Come on.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Stephen Colbert leaving us with some very interesting imagery.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: It`s a simple costume. Just put on the aviator glasses, right?\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes. Take them out of the pocket, put them on. You are transformed.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Seems to work for him.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Best political impersonation ever.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got a packed show for this morning. Welcome to CBS MORNINGS. A lot to get to, including a look at the growing controversy around the word "woke." The people who use it well, you find a lot of them in Florida starting with the Republican Governor. He says the word a lot.\par \par \uc2 We visit the State to explore what woke means and whether it`s a political weapon or still some kind of badge of honor.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I can`t wait for that.\par \par \uc2 Plus comedian, Hasan Minhaj is here and he has been going through some big changes in his life such as moving to Connecticut, a place that`s very easy to joke about in his comedy special on \uc2 Netflix\uc2 . Tony over here joking --\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That`s a big change.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Choking on his water.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Connecticut. Wow.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: But what does he have lined up next? We`ll find out what could be on his agenda.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right. We`re looking forward to that.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to begin with that massive verdict handed down in a lawsuit against Alex Jones. A jury determined the radio talk show host and conspiracy theorist must pay nearly $1 billion in damages to the families of Sandy Hook shooting victims. Jones repeatedly called the school massacre a hoax on the air.\par \par \uc2 Last year, a Judge found him liable of defamation by default after he failed to produce documents demanded in the lawsuit and Nikki Battiste has been following this case for months.\par \par \uc2 We find her at the Courthouse in Waterbury, Connecticut where the judgment was read yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Nikki, good morning.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Tony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 I sat in the Courtroom near several of the Sandy Hook victims family members as that verdict was read and they were emotional, yet poised. Several told me soon after, they were in shock, but that this is the accountability they want Jones to face after and during years of harassment from him and his supporters.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MAN: We, the jury have reached now a verdict as to damages in this case.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: In less than three days, the six jurors decided that conspiracy theorist Alex Jones must pay more than $965 million in compensatory damages for spreading false claims that the 2012 Sandy Hook School shooting was a hoax.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PARKER (Father of Sandy Hook shooting victim, Emilie Parker): I let my voice be taken away from me.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Robbie Parker lost his daughter Emilie. Parker testified that a video of him speaking at his daughter`s funeral was mocked by Jones.\par \par \uc2 ROBERT PARKER: I`m just proud that what we were able to accomplish was just to simply tell the truth.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Jones and his company, Free Speech Systems were sued by eight victims` families and an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent who responded to the shooting over claims the company made millions spreading misinformation about the tragedy.\par \par \uc2 Jones and his attorney maintained that the trial was a sham and an attack on First Amendment rights. Though he was not in Court, Jones watched live on his webcast. He cheered while the verdict was read mocking the families and saying they won`t receive any payments.\par \par \uc2 How likely is it that this will financially ruin or could financially ruin Alex Jones?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOPHER MATTEI (Plaintiff`s attorney): Well, one of the things that Alex Jones tried to do throughout this case was to hide his assets.\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: Jones company filed for bankruptcy in July, but a forensic economist in a previous case estimated that Jones is personally worth as much as $270 million.\par \par \uc2 Since this case will not have a cap on punitive damages, Judge Barbara Bellis will now consider how much Jones will ultimately have to pay.\par \par \uc2 The fight also continues for Mark Barden who lost his son, Daniel, in the tragedy.\par \par \uc2 MARK BARDEN (Father of Sandy Hook shooting victim, Daniel Barden) : And after each victory, Daniel is still dead and nothing will bring him back, but everything that I do is to protect others from having to live this pain.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NIKKI BATTISTE: The jurors is also determined that Jones should pay the plaintiffs` legal and attorney fees. Jones lawyer said he plans to appeal the decision. The radio host faces a third trial in Texas later this year over a lawsuit filed by the parent of another child killed in the shooting -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Nikki Battiste, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Now to the House Select Committee investigating the January 6th assault on the Capitol, which will hold its last planned public hearing in just a few hours. Committee members say they will share never before seen images and revelations about the attack and new information about former President Trump`s role in a plot to overturn the election results.\par \par \uc2 Scott MacFarlane is on Capitol Hill with a preview for us.\par \par \uc2 Scott, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Gayle, good morning.\par \par \uc2 This hearing will look and sound different. There will be no live witnesses this time and all nine panel members will be part of this closing argument with just weeks remaining for the Committee to wrap up its work.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The House January 6 Select Committee is not only attempting to show some of its newest findings, but also trying to regain America`s attention. The Committee staffers conducted a long sought after interview with conservative activist, Ginni Thomas, wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Committee says she was urging the Trump White House to fight the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (D-MS): We still have significant information that we`ve not shown to the public.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Committee Vice Chair, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , says the Secret Service has provided hundreds of thousands of pages of communication after the agency revealed requested text messages had been deleted.\par \par \uc2 The panel says it will also focus on Trump`s state of mind ahead of and during the attack and will show what they call his centrality in the scheme to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 REP. RO KHANNA (D-CA): It is important for people to know that this wasn`t spontaneous, that this was directed and that the President of the United States whose first responsibility is to keep America safe, refused and did not do that on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Meanwhile, a documentary filmmaker tells CBS News he has provided footage to the Committee ahead of today`s hearing from his film about Trump ally, Roger Stone.\par \par \uc2 You shared some of your recording with the House January 6 Select Committee. Could you tell what part of this they were interested in?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTOFFER GULDBRANDSEN (Filmmaker): Their focus was the connections between Roger Stone, the Oath Keepers and the Proud Boys. Those were the groups that were spearheading the attacks on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The Committee will also issue a final written report. It must do so by the end of December, but all of this is happening against the backdrop of the midterm elections which, Gayle, are now less than four weeks away.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know. We are all counting and waiting for that. But Scott, we hear that there are other stories involving Mr. Trump today that there is new information we`re hearing from the investigation of documents that are marked as classified that were removed from Mar-a-Lago. What can you tell us about that?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: That`s right, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 An individual familiar with the investigation tells CBS News investigators have spoken to a witness who works at Mar-a-Lago and says the former President directed him or her to move boxes containing documents after Trump advisers received a subpoena in May. The handling of the boxes and the documents were part of the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s reasoning to get a warrant.\par \par \uc2 This individual is cooperating, has spoken with the grand jury here in Washington and there is video evidence of boxes being handled -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: All right, Scott, thank you very much. That`s an interesting new development. Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 We`ll bring you live coverage of today`s January 6th Committee hearing in a CBS News Special Report. We`re expected to start that at one o`clock Eastern Time today.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Turning to the war in Ukraine, Russia launched new attacks against the Kyiv region overnight and shelled a key city in the south of the country. They are the latest in a series of strikes that began with the wave of missile and drone attacks on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Charlie D`Agata is in a city near the frontlines in Southern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, good morning.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Good morning.\par \par \uc2 We are in the City of Zaporizhzhia, which has been the target of relentless Russian bombardment. This is the middle of a residential area with apartments around us, you can see some of the destruction from this ferocious barrage that took place. Earlier we watched as they retrieved another victim from beneath the rubble.\par \par \uc2 In the past two weeks, more than 70 people have been killed here, all civilians. There was an attack on a civilian convoy just a couple of weeks ago, apartment blocks right downtown, but it really accelerated on Monday in that barrage that spread across the entire country.\par \par \uc2 There were more attacks overnight. Kamikaze drone strikes outside of Kyiv that set off air raid sirens and sent people to the shelters once again. Mykolaiv, a city that`s also been targeted, was hit once again, dropping a five-story building.\par \par \uc2 These are exactly the kinds of attacks that President Zelenskyy is begging international help for to help stop -- missile defense systems to stop these missiles from raining down in residential areas spreading death and destruction and terror across the nation.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, Charlie D`Agata, Zaporizhzhia, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Now on to California, where the Los Angeles City Councilor who ignited widespread outrage with racist remarks caught on tape has resigned under heavy, heavy pressure.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, Nury Martinez said this in part: "I wanted to see a change in my community and fight for my neighbors. This is what it has been about all along."\par \par \uc2 Two other councilors heard on the same tape are ignoring calls for them to step down.\par \par \uc2 Mark Strassmann is in Los Angeles with more on this story.\par \par \uc2 Mark, good morning to you.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Good morning to you, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 Nury Martinez is gone, but Nury fury, the citywide outrage over this leaked tape may go on especially since those two other Council members on that tape refused to resign.\par \par \uc2 Their conversation was offensive, possibly illegal, too. A backroom meeting about city redistricting and California`s Attorney General is now looking into it.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: The investigation announced Wednesday will look into whether three LA City Council members violated State or Federal Voting rights laws for conversations like the one you`re about to hear.\par \par \uc2 On a secretly recorded audio tape, local labor leader Ron Herrera discusses redistricting with former City Council President, Nury Martinez, both resigned their posts this week.\par \par \uc2 RON HERRERA (Former LA City Council member): I get what we have to do, right? Just massage to create districts that benefit you all, and the future. We`ve got to figure out Mark`s seat, too.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: That year-old recording often course and racist was obtained by the "LA Times." It suggests Martinez gaming up potential ways to divvy up the district of disgraced Councilman, Mark Ridley Thomas.\par \par \uc2 NURY MARTINEZ (Former LA City Council President): If he resigns and the African-Americans look at this as a hostile takeover because he`s gone, we`ll have to figure that [bleep] out, because politically they`re going to come after us.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Ridley Thomas is suspended from his position and facing trial scheduled to begin next month on Federal bribery charges. He has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON (Loyola Law School Professor): There is rot in LA City. There`s just no other way to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Jessica Levinson is an election law expert at Loyola Marymount University in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA LEVINSON: These comments are not just vile, they`re not just racist, they`re also potentially legally problematic.\par \par \uc2 What we have are discussions not just about increasing Latino voting power, but taken in context, potentially also about diluting African-American voting power and that is where you get a potential legal issue.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: What`s striking about Martinez`s resignation statement, she apologized only to her staff. Nothing to anyone else she offended, including another Council member`s family and his adopted Black son, whom she called in Spanish, a little monkey.\par \par \uc2 It`s really unbelievable -- Gayle.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It really is. It really is, Mark. It seems like that apology needed to go a little step further. But we thank you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yeah, Mark, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to turn now to new information about the death of a Navy SEAL recruit earlier this year. Kyle Mullen, you may remember the name. He was a SEAL candidate. He died in February of acute pneumonia just hours after completing the infamous Hell Week known for pushing trainees to their absolute limits.\par \par \uc2 As David Martin reports, three Navy officers have been disciplined now for overseeing the training that resulted in Mullen`s death.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Eight hours after these SEAL recruits struggled out of the water to complete the last agonizing steps of Hell Week, one of them, Kyle Mullen was dead of acute pneumonia. His mother Regina can pick him out in a freeze frame.\par \par \uc2 REGINA MULLEN (Mother of Kyle Mullen): My son was towards the end. He is being carried by another man. He was barely able to walk and breathe.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: An investigation found that after five days of what the Navy calls extreme stress in a controlled environment, Mullen was so short of breath and his legs so swollen, he had to be given oxygen twice and move from one location to another by ambulance, but he was still declared fit to train.\par \par \uc2 REGINA MULLEN: My son wasn`t treated medically by the medical team, by their own investigation. Two times they could have saved his life, two times. That`s denied medical treatment right there.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: According to the investigation, Mullen had to be taken to the barracks in a wheelchair. He appeared to be gasping for air, super swollen, with bluish colored skin and fluids coming from his mouth. Recruits had been assured the duty medical officer will see you at any time, but when they called, they were told to dial 9-1-1.\par \par \uc2 REGINA MULLEN: Oh, you could call 9-1-1. They should have ran there and checked them.\par \par \uc2 DAVID MARTIN: Two officers in charge of SEAL training received career damaging warning letters. One of them was also in charge when this video captured SEAL recruits being blanketed with teargas at point blank range for a full minute, even though the rules clearly say it should last no more than 15 seconds.\par \par \uc2 This video was shot during the final phase of SEAL qualification. Kyle Mullen died before he got that far.\par \par \uc2 For CBS MORNINGS, David Martin, The Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: In the fight against COVID, children five years old and up are now eligible for updated booster shots ahead of a possible winter surge. The CDC and the FDA just gave the go ahead for \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and Moderna`s omicron-specific boosters for elementary school children. The \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  shot will be available for kids five and older and Moderna`s for kids ages six and up. They are only for people who received their last COVID vaccine at least two months ago.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Two weeks after Hurricane Ian hit Florida, remember that, that broken bridge to Sanibel Island has been fixed and power crews are on the island. Now, that`s the good news. The bad news, there is a new report that says Ian was the 15th billion disaster in this country this year. Think about that for just a second.\par \par \uc2 Meteorologist \uc2 Stephanie Abrams\uc2  with our partners at \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  joins us now. Stephanie, good morning to you. These disasters seem to be happening more often. Is that the case?\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE ABRAMS\uc2  (\uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  meteorologist): It is, Gayle, you`re correct.\par \par \uc2 For the record, eighth straight year, the US has experienced 10 or more billion dollar disasters, and we`ve already seen like you said 15 separate events from January through September and there`s $29.3 billion of damage. That does not include Hurricanes Ian and Fiona or the Western wildfires, which actually may push the total closer to $100 billion, a total that we`ve actually reached in four of the last five years.\par \par \uc2 Now, unfortunately, the frequency is increasing. In the last five years, there were just 18 days on average between disasters compared to 82 when you go back to the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 Now severe weather does top the list when we`re talking about events. We`ve had 10 severe weather events that have been billion dollar disasters, and we`re going to see that threat today. Hopefully, we`re not going to be at that billion dollar level. We don`t expect to be at that billion dollar level, but we will see wind and that threat for flooding.\par \par \uc2 Look at that, injecting tons of moisture all the way into New England for our Thursday and into our Friday.\par \par \uc2 For the latest coverage, you can watch \uc2 The Weather Channel\uc2  on cable or live on your favorite TV, streaming devices and Gayle, once that rain moves out, colder air is going to come in bringing a chance for snow into the Great Lakes and interior northeast.\par \par \uc2 So my question for you is