e, I guess.\par \par \uc2 (MAN speaking in foreign language.)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: But at an anti-Putin rally in the capital, Georgians fear the Russian draft dodgers are a Trojan horse.\par \par \uc2 All of these Russians, hundreds of thousands of them virtually overnight, clearly isn`t sitting well with everyone in Georgia. Those here see it as yet another form of Russian invasion and they are calling for the border with Russia to be closed.\par \par \uc2 MAN: And they must know that they are not welcome here.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Georgia is no stranger to Russian invasion. In 2008, Putin attacked by land, air, and sea allegedly to protect Russians living here. To this day, 20 percent of the country remains occupied.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We know how Putin start wars.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: So, you`re afraid that Georgia could be next.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Putin can come and say we need to send our soldiers to save the lives of our citizens in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: But in Putin`s army, morale has never been lower, following staggering defeats in Ukraine. Troops are retreating and surrendering, and to dodge the draft, conscripts are so desperate, they are even breaking their own arms and legs as this video is said to show.\par \par \uc2 SKOBAR: The fear, it is like not something rational, not something logic, you just someday wake up and start to feel it.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: Skobar knows how lucky he is to be in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 SKOBAR: Time and safety. That Putin can`t kill me or go -- or I am not in jail. I`m not arrested.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: How long do you think you`re going to be here?\par \par \uc2 SKOBAR: I don`t know. I want to work on my old job. I want to live with my family and my girlfriend. So, I guess I`ll return back when \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  will be dead, I guess. And I will be just fine with this.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS LIVESAY: For CBS MORNINGS, Chris Livesay, Tbilisi, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A whole another perspective. You know the guy with the guitar singing "Ghostbusters" is not a Trojan Horse, number one. Now, I thought the last man we saw, he said, "I don`t want to die and I don`t want to kill people."\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: The fact that they`re running away from this says a lot.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: When people go to war, they go to war for ideas and ideals that they believe in.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: You can tell that hundreds of thousands of Russians do not believe in Putin`s ideals.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Not when you`re breaking your own leg, Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: My gosh.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: To break your own leg just so you can dodge going to neighboring country, says a lot.\par \par \uc2 All right, hospitals across the US are seeing an increase in children with respiratory illness and we are not talking about the flu or COVID. Next, Dr. Celine Gounder will tell us how to protect your kids and I`ve got something for you, you can always get the morning`s news by subscribing to our CBS MORNINGS on-the-go podcast. Here is what you get, today`s top stories in less than 20 minutes. That is a deal.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: COVID and flu are not the only viruses to look out for in your children. Hospitals across the country are seeing a spike in patients with RSV. That`s an acronym, we`ll get to the meaning. It`s a respiratory illness that hospitalizes nearly 60,000 children every year under the age of five.\par \par \uc2 CBS News contributor, Dr. Celine Gounder is here with what you need to know. She is an epidemiologist and editor-at-large at KFF`s Kaiser Health News. Dr. Gounder, thank you for being here. RSV, what does it stand for? What is it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Respiratory syncytial virus. RSV. So, this is another one of these cold viruses kind of like COVID and the flu, can infect your upper respiratory airway so that would be the nose, the throat, in that case that causes a runny stuffy nose, a cough, sore throat, but when it gets into the lower airway, so the bronchi, the lungs. In little kids, that causes bronchitis, pneumonia that can be a lot more dangerous.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Why does it affect little kids and how do they get it?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, when little kids get it, especially infants, they don`t have a mature immune system. So, they`re not able to fight it off as well and they have much smaller airways.\par \par \uc2 So, when you get inflammation in the airways, they get thicker. When you get mucus you get plugging, so they just run into trouble a lot more easily.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But how did they get it, though? That`s -- it`s just one of those things?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: It`s one of these viral respiratory infections. So, it is through the air, through droplets, just like you might get COVID or the flu.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How do we recognize it? What are the warning signs?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes, what`s so scary about young kids is that they`re very noisy breathers in general.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re doing some work on the house right now, so Eloise is back in our room and she doesn`t have RSV as far as we know, but --\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: You don`t know if it`s a cough or a runny nose.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We don`t know if we have to laugh, you know, it is like grandma snoring in the next room. It`s that loud.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. And sometimes, you ignore it as a parent, because it seems normal.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, when does the normal noisy breathing of an infant become problematic?\par \par \uc2 DR. CELINE GOUNDER: Well, so if the baby is not feeding as well, 