s to cook has become the taste of the internet. We meet the man whose homemade videos are getting tens of millions of views.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Well, this morning, we are talking about what is at stake in the midterm elections and a buzzword you`ve probably heard a lot recently, that word is "woke."\par \par \uc2 What`s that all about?\par \par \uc2 Well, the polls show something here is going on. A recent \uc2 CBS\uc2  poll show that more than two-thirds of Americans -- two-thirds believe their rights and freedoms are what is on the line in November. That`s an even greater share of voters and are worried about their financial wellbeing, and nearly half of those surveyed said that our culture and way of life is at stake, again, in November, in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 The result is an election that`s about different policies, yes, but also very different visions of what America is right now and what America should be in the future and perhaps nowhere is that more evident than in the State of Florida.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In the battle to define America, Governor Ron DeSantis has what amounts to his very own fight song.\par \par \uc2 Plus a campaign ad inspired by "Top Gun."\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): When they fire, you fire back with overwhelming force.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And if there`s one political target, he seems to favor above all others, it is this one.\par \par \uc2 GOV. RON DESANTIS (R-FL): Woke is the new religion of the left.\par \par \uc2 We`re not going to let this state descend into some type of woke dumpster fire.\par \par \uc2 We will never ever surrender to the woke agenda.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: In fact, the whole Florida Republican Party seems to be taking on what it calls the "woke left."\par \par \uc2 How are you? Tony Dokoupil.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): Hi. How are you doing?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Including Senator Rick Scott, who released a 12-point plan for all Republicans in the Senate to rescue America from woke elitists, woke indoctrination, and a new religion of wokeness.\par \par \uc2 But hold on a minute: What does woke even mean?\par \par \uc2 To you, what does it mean to be woke?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): I have -- I mean, I don`t know what the definition of woke so much is. I do know that I think most people don`t want to be lectured by companies. So, I think that`s sort of what people think of woke.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Or let me put it differently, in what way is the woke left in your view, a threat to America itself?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): Well, when our schools don`t teach your kids that we`re the greatest country in the world, when they disrespect our flag, when kids are told that they don`t have a shot because of their skin color, I don`t think that`s the country I grew up in.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Merriam-Webster back in 2017 defined woke as aware of and actively attentive to important facts and issues like race and social justice.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "\uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2 .")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: But use of the word has broadened into complex debates about abortion, history, and identity; not to mention whether corporations should get involved in any of it.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAVISON HUNTER (\uc2 University of Virginia\uc2  Professor of Sociology): The right began to use it as a kind of dog whistle for anyone on the left and anything that smacked of progressive politics.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: James Davison Hunter is a Professor of Sociology at the \uc2 University of Virginia\uc2 . He says that new war over woke isn`t really new at all.\par \par \uc2 NEWT GINGRICH (Former US Speaker of the House): We have been winning the cultural war.\par \par \uc2 CHARLTON HESTON (Actor): A cultural war is raging across our land.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Instead, he says, it is a continuation of the culture wars, a concept he first popularized in a book way back in 1991.\par \par \uc2 Why did you call it a war?\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAVISON HUNTER: Well, from the vantage point of the actors involved, it felt like war and there was this sense that what is at stake is what I cherish most about my life and about the world I live in and the world I hope to leave to my children.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: No small thing.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAVISON HUNTER: And cumulatively, it becomes a struggle to define the meaning of America.\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Abolish abortion.")\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that struggle continues today, worrying Hunter because the fight has gotten meaner, he says, and our ability to talk to one another has broken down.\par \par \uc2 Politics in theory is supposed to be how we work things out.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAVISON HUNTER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So we don`t come to violence.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAVISON HUNTER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Is politics working anymore?\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAVISON HUNTER: That`s part of the problem. Our politics is now so driven by culture. When people no longer see a way out politically, they will choose alternative strategies to pursue their agenda. I don`t think there`s any question that there will be more violence going forward.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: More violence, he predicts, along with more elections in which the results are not accepted by all sides and that is the biggest threat to America, at least according to Democratic Florida State Representative, Ana Eskamani.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean to you?\par \par \uc2 ANA ESKAMANI (D) (Florida State Representative): It just means being aware of your history.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Her Orlando office is filled with pictures and slogans that tell you right away where she stands on the issues and hint at why she is o dug in to fight.\par \par \uc2 Is there any room for compromise?\par \par \uc2 ANA ESKAMANI: I mean, it`s very difficult to compromise on a policy that erases the history of people of color. When I`ve had this conversation with Republican leadership on abortion, specifically, of you know, where is the compromise? And the reality is that, it is very hard to compromise when you`re basically telling me that I am less than, and I do not have the same rights as you.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And that unbending attitude is there on the Republican side as well.\par \par \uc2 SEN. RICK SCOTT (R-FL): They`re trying to take freedom, and they want control. That`s exactly what they do. That`s what they do with socialism. They ultimately take away your freedoms. It sounds really nice, right? But in the end, it`s evil.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yet a lot of Americans believe this is all a big distraction and that includes Charlie Crist, who is running against DeSantis for governor.\par \par \uc2 In a statement to CBS News, he says: "Every election, Republicans invent some new boogeyman instead of trying to solve problems and make people`s lives better."\par \par \uc2 But Professor Hunter warns we shouldn`t underestimate just how important these issues are to so many of our fellow Americans.\par \par \uc2 JAMES DAVISON HUNTER: If we just think that these are side issues, that these things actually don`t really matter, we are missing what is most combustible about the culture war and most combustible about the moment that we`re living in right now.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: So, I think the Professor makes such a good point there. These are sometimes talked about as distractions when what really matters is the economy or healthcare, but these are the issues that are core to who people are, who we are as a country.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: The word "woke" specifically came out of Black American culture.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: It was a way to talk about racial inequality. It has broadened 