ul, this school.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: This was his first time back in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 Did you ever think when you were cleaning the floors here many years ago that you would be the star of cooking on the internet today?\par \par \uc2 PASQUALE SCIARAPPA: No. I don`t think so, but I am so happy today. I am never was happy like today. Why I was not like this when I was young? Nobody would look at me.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN: Now, he is going to cook shrimp. He is cooking now.\par \par \uc2 PASQUALE SCIARAPPA: No, no. This is raw. Raw shrimp.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Now, they can`t get enough.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about that famous video.\par \par \uc2 PASQUALE SCIARAPPA: Oh boy. We`ll come back now to this video.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from \uc2 YouTube\uc2 .)\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: What do you think it is about your dad that appeals to people?\par \par \uc2 JEREMY SCIARAPPA: I think the whole thing that everybody why they love him so much is because it reminds them of somebody, like whether it`s their father or their grandfather or just somebody in the family like an uncle.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: But whose 83-year-old uncle has this much energy? And celebrity status?\par \par \uc2 PASQUALE SCIARAPPA: Yes, every place I go. I go to the store, and the people start, "Oh, I want to take a picture with you." This and that.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: But the simple pleasures are where Pasquale gets his kicks. He is happiest in his garden growing herbs, fruits, and vegetables.\par \par \uc2 PASQUALE SCIARAPPA: One over here. Now, we put another one right over here.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: And in his kitchen.\par \par \uc2 PASQUALE SCIARAPPA: Look at my chicken scarpariello. A beautiful look. And now, it`s the time of the taste.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 PASQUALE SCIARAPPA: I want to know if you like.\par \par \uc2 JANET SHAMLIAN: Pasquale Sciarappa is the very definition of a Second Act.\par \par \uc2 What