par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: \uc2 Nury Martinez\uc2 , a rising star in LA politics potentially faces a career in ruins. The first Latina chosen to lead the Los Angeles City Council caught on tape making racist and offensive comments during a redistricting meeting with three Latino Democrats.\par \par \uc2 NURY MARTINEZ\uc2  (Los Angeles City Council President): It`s not us. It is the White members on this Council that will [bleep] you in a heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: That tape obtained by the "LA Times" captured Martinez talking to local labor leader, Ron Herrera, and fellow Council members Gil Cedillo and Kevin De Leon.\par \par \uc2 At one point, Martinez talks about the Black child of Mike Bonin, a White Council member in Spanish calling the boy a little monkey.\par \par \uc2 NURY MARTINEZ\uc2 : There`s nothing you can do to control him, parece changuito.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Then she adds describing the boy allegedly misbehaving at an MLK parade.\par \par \uc2 NURY MARTINEZ\uc2 : They`re raising him like he`s a little White kid. This kid needs a beatdown. Lert me take him around the corner and I`ll bring him back.\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: Councilman Bonin and his husband said, they are appalled, angry, and disgusted. No child should ever be subjected to such racist, mean, and dehumanizing comments, especially from a public official.\par \par \uc2 Herrera, the labor leader resigned his post overnight.\par \par \uc2 Within hours of the recording surfacing, Martinez said she was sorry saying, "I sincerely apologize to the people I hurt. I ask for forgiveness from my colleagues and from the residents of this city."\par \par \uc2 (CROWD chanting "Resign.")\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: But even after she resigned her powerful post as Council President, protesters outside Martinez`s home and at City Council demanded more.\par \par \uc2 She should also resigned her elected seat on LA`s City Council.\par \par \uc2 MAN: We are appalled that these people claim to represent us. They don`t represent us. We are better.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 MARK STRASSMANN: LA`s Mayor and the two candidates running to succeed him now say all three Council members should resign. All of them have apologized, but none has shown any sign of resigning despite mounting pressure for them to do just that -- Tony.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, Mark Strassmann for us in Los Angeles, which is unusual. Mark, thank you very much. Appreciate that report.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to move on now to Florida where the death toll continues to rise from Hurricane Ian. It is now up to 129 people and many people who survived are struggling to clean up after catastrophic damage.\par \par \uc2 Officials say more than $207 million in claims have already been paid out by insurance companies. Statewide, the damage is estimated at more than $100 billion with a B, but as Manuel Bojorquez reports, it appears much of that cost may not be covered.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: As residents return to Hurricane Ian`s Ground Zero, cleaning up is only the start of what will be a years` long recovery effort.\par \par \uc2 MAN: Jesus.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: A few days after the storm hit, we met Robby Podgorski in Fort Myers. He had lost mostly everything.\par \par \uc2 ROBBY PODGORSKI (Green Cup Cafe owner): That`s our house --\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: His home, his business, all destroyed from flooding.\par \par \uc2 ROBBY PODGORSKI: We never, in our wildest dreams, could we have thought this was an outcome that was possible.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: We checked back in with Podgorski yesterday to see how things were going.\par \par \uc2 ROBBY PODGORSKI: Though for insurance, we already talked to our agent and they basically said nothing is covered. Unless, I can prove to them that wind destroyed it before water touched it, there is nothing that I could do.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Podgorski doesn`t have Federal flood insurance. In fact, only about 18 percent of Florida households do and homeowners` insurance stopped covering flooding in the 1960s.\par \par \uc2 Despite living in a zone where Federal flood insurance is recommended, Podgorski told us it was too expensive.\par \par \uc2 ROBBY PODGORSKI: Since we`re in a historic building in technically a FEMA- flood zone, our insurance would be more than our mortgage or our rents.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Homeowners in Florida already pay the highest premiums, nearly three times the national average for property insurance and State Senator Jeff Brandes says it`s only going to get worse\par \par \uc2 JEFF BRANDES (Florida State Senate): After Ian, you`re going to see 30 percent rate increases, largely 20 to 30 percent rate increases throughout the State of Florida.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: Brandes says a major problem is that despite only eight percent of homeowners` insurance claims coming from Florida nationally, a whopping 80 percent of claim related lawsuits are filed there. That drives insurers out.\par \par \uc2 JEFF BRANDES: In the long term, the legislature will ultimately have to fix this. Property insurance really has become the Achilles heel of the Florida real estate market.\par \par \uc2 MANUEL BOJORQUEZ: For CBS MORNINGS, I`m Manuel Bojorquez.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: In medical news, a new study has started a debate about colonoscopies, long regarded as the gold standard of screening for colorectal cancer. The study published in the "New England Journal of Medicine" found that participants who were offered a colonoscopy had only 18 percent lower rates of colon cancer. Researchers say those patients had about the same risk of death from the disease 10 years down the line.\par \par \uc2 CBS News chief medical correspondent, that`s Dr. Jon LaPook is with us. He`s a gastro --\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Enterologist -- gastroenterologist. I`m a doctor.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: That`s what I just said. You`re my doctor, so I even know all of this. But listen, this is certainly your area of expertise. The headlines can be very jarring. What`s your take on this.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: I think the headlines are misleading.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Do you?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Big study, one group was just followed, the other group was offered a colonoscopy. You heard about the statistics, after 10 years, about an 18 percent decrease in the risk of getting colon cancer, but no difference in mortality. But only 42 percent of the people offered a colonoscopy actually got it.\par \par \uc2 Of the people who got the colonoscopy, there was a 31 percent decreased risk of colon cancer over about 10 years and a 50 percent decrease in the in the risk of mortality, risk of dying from colon cancer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: A lot of numbers there though, Jon.\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Okay, so --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Bottom line it for us. I mean, if somebody is thinking, well, now I don`t really need to do it, or I`m reluctant to get it. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: Get it. This still is the gold standard. You know, it is not enough to be offered a colonoscopy or offered a test, you have to actually do the test.\par \par \uc2 Now, I spoke to the author who was in Norway, lot of statistical issues seem to -- we can go over this fancy-shmancy analysis, okay. Was it long enough? Ten years to see a benefit in terms of mortality, where the colonoscopy is good enough that people who did the colonoscopy good enough at picking up polyps and things like that.\par \par \uc2 But the bottom line is, a colonoscopy is a great way, the best way we know of preventing colon cancer. You know, you go in there, I`ve done many, many thousands of them. You find a polyp, and that polyp has the potential to turn into colon cancer over time.\par \par \uc2 You take away that polyp, you remove it, and instead of potentially getting colon cancer, you don`t get colon cancer.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Quickly, what is the age you should get it?\par \par \uc2 DR. JON LAPOOK: First screening at 45. If you`re at average risk, and if you`re at increased risk, lower than that, talk to your doctor about it, your healthcare professional about exactly the timing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Dr. Jon LaPook, we appreciate you giving us the bottom line. No pun intended. Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 All right, we have an update for you. A story that we brought you last week, a CBS News investigation that found 400 children of elite Green Berets are waiting to get into childcare. Senior investigative correspondent Catherine Herridge spoke to Army families in the Florida Panhandle, who drove up to -- check this out -- three hours a day to drop off and pick up their kids because their training camp has no childcare center. The closest military facility is 20 miles farther away in Eglin Air Force Base.\par \par \uc2 CBS News found Special Forces soldiers who say they had to leave the Army altogether over childcare.\par \par \uc2 Now here`s the good news. The Secretary of the Army says a new child development center will be built for families of the Army`s Seventh Special Forces Group at the facility at Camp Bull Simons, which is due in 2025.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: 2025? The kids are going to be free range before that? You`re going to let them out in the yard?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: I mean, they are going to be in middle school by then. At least there is change.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: But shoutout to Catherine Herridge.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I am happy there is change and when you bring attention to it, things move and things happen.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: We tend to do that here at \uc2 CBS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Exactly right. Very glad about that.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, Prince William and his wife, Princess Kate take over the BBC airwaves to talk very candidly about an issue of great concern to both of them.\par \par \uc2 But first, it is 7:18. Time to check your local weather.\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Coming up: How should the US respond to Russia`s latest attacks on Ukrainian cities? Retired General H.R. McMaster, our foreign policy and national security contributor is in our greenroom with how these strikes could affect the overall war.\par \par \uc2 Plus, another controversial penalty has fans asking if the \uc2 NFL\uc2  is going too far to protect players on the field.\par \par \uc2 This is CBS MORNINGS.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Comedian Sebastian Maniscalco is sharing hilarious stories about his experiences as a dad.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SEBASTIAN MANISCALCO (Comedian): I saw the headbanging and my son was doing that for a little bit. Once I saw that I`m like, "That`s it." You know, I always look at it as a major issue, whatever they have, like you just said, my kid is the only one doing this. Something is wrong. Our DNA is screwed off. What`s going on?\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL NEWS)\par \par \uc2 (LOCAL WEATHER)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 *************************************************************************** ** (07:30 AM, EDT)\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: We`re back now on CBS MORNINGS here at 7:30.\par \par \uc2 As Ukraine surveys the damage from massive Russian attacks all across the country yesterday, officials are saying at least 19 people were killed, many lost access to power, to water, to heat in what was the largest Russian attack in months.\par \par \uc2 We`re joined now by retired Lieutenant General H.R. McMaster, a CBS News foreign policy and national security contributor, also US national security adviser.\par \par \uc2 General, thank you very much for being here.\par \par \uc2 This is a landmass about the size of Texas and you can see the scope of these Russian attacks. What stood out to you?\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Well, Tony, as you mentioned, I mean, it`s just the scale of it, right? Eighty-four cruise missiles launched from various locations, about 24 drones, some of these suicide drones provided to the Russians by the Iranians, and the targets were to terrorize the Ukrainian people, to terrorize them with attacks on apartment complexes and school and busy intersections right at rush hour, but also, as you mentioned, to attack infrastructure, right? To pose Ukrainians with the prospect of being cold in the winter. Right?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: And so, all of this was meant to affect Ukrainian will in a negative way. But of course, I think it`s had the opposite effect, Tony. I mean, I think it has bolstered the will of the Ukrainians to fight for their sovereignty and their freedom.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And they have been fighting. There is an attack on a bridge, which we are going to talk to in a second, but these Russian attacks followed a counteroffensive from Ukraine, which have been having some success. Can we talk about that?\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Oh, yes, we should talk about this. And it`s important, because I think the momentum has shifted with this offense.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: And just the guide that we have, this is Russian controlled territory here in the red, and the yellow is where Ukraine has made a push.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: That`s right. So, you have Russia here. You have the Crimean Peninsula here, which is really important, a lot of Russian bases established there after the illegal annexation of Crimea in 2014, and what you see in the yellow is a vast amount of territory, especially here in the North that has been retaken in recent weeks, by a very successful Ukrainian counteroffensive. A counteroffensive that has integrated some new weapons capabilities, precision fires in particular.\par \par \uc2 But you know, the Russians are in a really tough spot. I mean, this is about an 850-kilometer long active front, and you can`t be strong everywhere.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: So what the Ukrainians did is they determined where the defenses were weak, they penetrated these defenses, and turned the Russians out of those prepared defenses and made tremendous gains, 1,200-kilometer gains here, and now they are consolidating some of those gains while they continue small scale offensives.\par \par \uc2 But there`s a very strategic area down here in Kherson along the Dnipro River, because it is critical to Russia controlling the Black Sea coast, and here you have a very deliberate counteroffensive going on, targeting some of the Russian forces.\par \par \uc2 But what`s happened across the front, Tony, is that the Ukrainians have attacked supply depots and ammunition dumps. And so, what this does is it puts a premium on resupply from Russia and this is really the Kerch Strait bridge context.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes. Yes. The bridge, why that mattered so much.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 So if you take out supply depots here and ammunition depots, then you have to throughput supplies along extended lines of supply coming, in this case out of Russia, across this Kerch Strait Bridge, and into some of the key bases that are on the Crimean Peninsula, where they stockpile supplies and then resupply these forces, which are under increasing pressure from the Ukrainians.\par \par \uc2 And so this is the context of the attack that dropped this bridge here in the Kerch Strait, and then you see this massive, you know, retaliation by the Russians because Putin felt like he had to do something after what he saw was a humiliation of this really great achievement of the Ukrainians to interdict that supply route.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: All right, General, let`s mosey over to the table, because I know Gayle and Nate also have some questions for you. We are about six months into the war here and this is the biggest attack we`ve seen since the very beginning.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I know, and General, you said the whole purpose is to terrorize the Ukrainian people, you know, mission accomplished. What do you make of Putin`s strategy, though? I attack your country, and then he goes on Russian TV yesterday, and calls the Ukrainian people terrorists. What do you make of this strategy? The Russian people aren`t always getting the truth here.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: No, they never get the truth. Right? I mean, whatever Putin says, the opposite is reality. And I think what you`re seeing now are some real cracks within the Russian regime and I think in Russian popular opinion. It is hard to believe any polls, right, that come out from Russia?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: And if they say, hey, what do you think of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 ? What are you going to say? Right?\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Right. Of course.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: You just disappear, right, if you give the wrong answer, and of course, it`s important to note that inside of Russia now, there are more political prisoners than there were during the height of the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 In the Russian Internal Security Services, more people than there are in the military, and of course, but it would have Putin tell people, hey, this is going to be easy. Just leave this to me. This is a special military operation.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: It`ll be done quick.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: It`ll be done quick. Yes. And now, they`ve had tremendous losses, Gayle.\par \par \uc2 I mean conservative estimate is 65,000 casualties, right? And now, he is trying to mobilize 300,000 and you`re seeing that a lot of these -- he has mobilized 300,000, but there`s 300,000 people tried to flee the country. I mean, they`ve gone to prisons, to recruit, you know, 80,000 criminals.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s really I think, you`re seeing the strains and we are talking about these massive attacks that just occurred, how many more times can they do that? I mean, there are reports that they are running low on ammunition. They`re buying ammo from North Korea.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: President Biden had worried that we haven`t been this close to Armageddon since the Cuban Missile Crisis.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: How concerned should we be about a nuclear attack? Because that just sounds scary when you use the word "nuclear."\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Well, of course, it does, Nate, and this is all that Putin has got left, right? It is to rattle the nuclear saber and I think that the President`s message was unfortunate there because it does kind of play into Putin`s hands.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course.\par \par \uc2 H.R. MCMASTER: Putin wants to incite fear, and he wants to constrain our support for the Ukrainians who are defending their children. I mean, half of the children in Ukraine are displaced, right? So, we see this as a humanitarian catastrophe. But it`s also very important for American interests, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is the return of major war to Europe, first time since World War Two, right? And, if Putin gets an off ramp as some people talk about, hey, that`s a chance for him to look for the next on ramp, which has been his pattern of activity since he took over in the year 2000, right?\par \par \uc2 I mean, he has conducted massive cyberattacks and disinformation to undermine our democracies. And of course, you know, he invaded Georgia in 2008, invaded Ukraine in 2014. This war has been going on since 2014.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Yes. The war in Ukraine isn`t an easy thing to break down. We appreciate you. That was a masterclass from General McMaster. We appreciate 