sition in May, made his debut as principal last month in a show that runs through this weekend as a reminder of how far we are into the year.\par \par \uc2 After that, he is going to prepare for "The Nutcracker." This Holiday time is coming up fast.\par \par \uc2 Christina Ruffini met with Chun to learn about his path to the heights of the ballet world.\par \par \uc2 Christina, good morning.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good morning, guys.\par \par \uc2 Well, George Balanchine, one of the founders of the New York City Ballet once said, "I don`t want people who want to dance, I want people who have to dance," and nobody embodies that more than the company`s newest leading man.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Ballet begins in preparation, a routine that starts long before dancers ever take the stage.\par \par \uc2 Have you always wanted to be a dancer?\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN (First Principal Dancer of Chinese Descent at New York City Ballet): I started dancing when I was six years old. My sister was in the dance team and I thought that`s so cool and I wanted to be a dancer, too.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: This season, Chun Wai Chan made his premier as principal at the New York City Ballet. He is the first Chinese dancer to ever hold that position in the legendary company`s 74-year history.\par \par \uc2 Was this like a lifelong goal?\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: I never thought about it because it is too big to think about.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Chun`s journey the Lincoln Center started 20 years ago, and more than 8,000 miles away in Guangdong, China.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: We are coming from both traditional family. They want me to became lawyer, doctor.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Chun was accepted to a prestigious National Dance Academy before reaching his teens, but his parents weren`t sold on the idea.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: I wrote them a letter saying that this is really what I want to do. This is my decision. So even though it`s not going well, I will not blame my parents.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: So at 12 years old, you wrote them a letter justifying why you wanted to go to this dance school.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And did they let you go?\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: They let me go.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Talent and a lot of hard work landed him on stage in an international dance competition in Switzerland, which he lost, but won a life-changing consolation prize, an offer to come to the US and join the Houston Ballet at the age of 18.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: In China, I had been focusing on how to improve my technique, mostly. Once I came to America, I learned all those techniques are served to tell a story and that`s changed my mind of ballet. It is not boring at all. It`s so much fun.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: In Texas, he learned English and the joys of social media. He also discovered he could jump really, really high.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: Feeling there is no gravity is one of the best feeling.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I`ve seen you, you look like you`re like on strings.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: It took me a long time because how we train in China, we cannot jump as high as we could right away.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Why?\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: Because they want us to have a good format, good base before we do something hard.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Now, the limits are off and you can you dance as high as you can.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: I can really fly.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Then came the biggest jump of all, to New York City.\par \par \uc2 What do you like about Central Park? Why do you come here?\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: It is so true, and I love people watching.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And people like watching him from his frequent spontaneous performances around the city.\par \par \uc2 That was the best pirouette in grass I`ve ever seen in my life.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: Yes, it is.\par \par \uc2 (Clip from Chinese reality TV.)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: To a high profile stint on Chinese reality TV.\par \par \uc2 So what caught your eye about Chun Wai Chen?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN STAFFORD (New York City Ballet artistic director): His absolute professionalism and command of not only the technique, but this energy that he brought.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Jonathan Stafford is the artistic director of the New York City Ballet, home to legends like Balanchine and Baryshnikov. He says the company`s distinct style puts less emphasis on the precision and uniformity Chun learned in his youth.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN STAFFORD: We want straight lines, but we also want the dancers to dance through the choreography, and if someone pops out a little bit, it`s fine. They`re allowed to be more open to the audience.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Learning to balance Chinese and American dance styles, as well as cultures, Chun says has made him a bit of an ambassador for both.\par \par \uc2 Do you consider yourself American? You`ve been here for more than 10 years now?\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: Yes, I think I would say this is my second home and I enjoy the life here so much.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: He says he is finally comfortable enough with English to start teaching adult classes.\par \par \uc2 (Clip of Chun Wai Chan and Christina Ruffini dancing.)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Helping a certain former dancer turned TV correspondent live out a lifelong dream.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: That was really good.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: This is New York City Ballet.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: Yes. That was amazing.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Why does diversity help with the company?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN STAFFORD: When you have diversity, you`re better. When you can have the best of everything that anyone has to offer. you`re going to be better.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Because even at the top, there is still room for improvement, both for Chun and the New York City Ballet.\par \par \uc2 CHUN WAI CHAN: One of the reason I love ballet because there`s no limit and you can always make it perfect and it`s so challenging that always you have something to improve on.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And if you`re wondering if Chun`s parents ever came around, they were the first people he called when he was promoted to principal. He said he had tears in his eyes and he was calling them and they said this was great, but you know, you have to keep working hard. This isn`t the end. This is only the beginning.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Awesome.\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: I love that. I love reversal of like, we don`t want you do it and then like, all right, if you`re going to do it, you`ve got to be absolute best.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: You have to be at the top of the top of the top of the top. You know, this is like, if you think about how many kids dance when they`re little, right? How many people have their kids in dance class? How many of those make a career out of it? And this is the -- this is like not getting in the \uc2 NFL\uc2 . This is like being an \uc2 NFL\uc2  quarterback, you know in a playoff.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Of course. Of course.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: I could not take my eyes off of his legs. His legs are. Amazing.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: His thighs.\par \par \uc2 NATE BURLESON: Incredible.\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: And the calves. But let me say, you had some moves. Did you take ballet?\par \par \uc2 TONY DOKOUPIL: That was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: No, you did, Christina. I am serious.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: I did. I was --\par \par \uc2 GAYLE KING: You did it as a kid?\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: As a kid, as a teenager. I was quite serious for a wh