b \uc2 A New CBS News Battleground Tracker Poll Is Looking At The Key Issues That Could Decide Who Gets The Edge In\b0\par\par\uc2 Jericka Duncan, Christina Ruffini \par \uc2 601 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Evening News - Sunday\par \uc2 CBSS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Well, we are just 30 days away from midterm elections. A new CBS News battleground tracker poll is looking at the key issues that could decide who gets the edge in tossup contests.\par \par \uc2 CBS\uc2 ` Christina Ruffini is in Washington with the details.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Christina.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Good evening, Jericka.\par \par \uc2 When you look at this polling nationally, the economy is still at the top of voters` mind. But if you drill down into some of these competitive races, other issues including abortion are impacting the vote.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-Arizona Gubernatorial Candidate): Therefore, abortion right up until birth --\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: In the Arizona Governor`s race, Republican Kari Lake is in a dead heat with Democrat, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D-Arizona Gubernatorial Candidate): That is ridiculous, and she is saying this to distract from her incredibly extreme position.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Sixty percent of likely Arizona voters say abortion should be legal in all or most cases, according to a CBS News/\uc2 YouGov\uc2  poll. And more than half say abortion is an important issue for their vote.\par \par \uc2 The same holds true in Georgia where \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is down against Governor \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 . Fifty-nine percent of voters say it`s an important issue.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D-Georgia Gubernatorial Candidate): I`m going to fight as hard as I can to protect the right to abortion, because that is how we protect the rights of women.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: And at Wisconsin, it is 51 percent, even though the Republican and Democratic candidates for Governor are currently tied.\par \par \uc2 Across the country, abortion is a top driver for Democrats, but not the overall electorate.\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2  (Vice President of the United States): And let`s hold on to the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2 . And let`s expand our majority in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: Republicans who seem likely to flip the House are trying to keep the focus on inflation and the economy.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2  (Former President of the United States): You`re going to elect an incredible slate of true America First Republicans up and down the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINA RUFFINI: That of course was former President Trump who was out west this past week. This next week, President Biden, as well as Speaker Pelosi will be heading to L.A. for a Democratic fundraiser.\par \par \uc2 And Jericka, we should expect to see a lot more of those high-profile appearances on the campaign trail as we come in on November.\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: Absolutely. Christina Ruffini in Washington, thank you.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada`s close Senate race, which could decide who controls that branch of government in 2023, Alabama U.S. Senator Tommy Tuberville said this in reference to the Democratic Party and reparations for African-Americans.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR TOMMY TUBERVILLE (D-Alabama): They want reparation because they think the people that do the crime are owed debt. [Bleep]. They`re not owed debt.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 JERICKA DUNCAN: We reached out to the Senator`s office for comment, but have not heard back.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document CBSS000020221012eia90005l\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 60 MINUTES, October 9, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Lesley Stahl, Bill Whitaker, Anderson Cooper \par \uc2 7,239 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: 60 Minutes\par \uc2 SXMN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): How close is the world to witnessing a war between China and Taiwan? We went to the island of 23 million to see for ourselves. We found the Taiwanese calm. Some say too calm, given China`s aggressiveness militarily and--\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: That`s Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Yeah, that`s Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: So you`re designating where they`re attacking with those circles.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Correct.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): It`s been 11 days since Hurricane Ian cut across Florida causing staggering loss of life and property. We went there and saw it all. And then, we saw something we weren`t expecting.\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: And the only damage were a few downed trees and a few shingles off the roofs.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: That`s it?\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: That`s it. And maybe some one lines (ph) on the back of the house, but that was it.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: In Jesus name we pray, amen.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): Bart Barber may be a small town pastor, but he`s also the new president of the largest and most influential evangelical group in the country. As he pledges to clean up his historic sex abuse scandal at the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 , we had a surprising wide- ranging interview.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Do you believe the 2020 election was stolen?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: No.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: You believe \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the legitimate President of the United States?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I do. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: I`m Lesley Stahl.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: I`m Bill Whitaker.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: I`m Anderson Cooper.\par \par \uc2 SHARYN ALFONSI: I`m Sharyn Alfonsi.\par \par \uc2 JON WERTHEIM: I`m Jon Wertheim.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT PELLEY: I`m Scott Pelley. Those stories and more tonight on 60 MINUTES.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 TAIWAN\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Ever since Mao Tse Tung won China`s civil war in 1949 and the losing anti-communist side fled to a small, nearby island, Beijing has insisted that that island, Taiwan, is an integral part of the mainland. The U.S. has walked a tightrope, respecting that "One China" policy, but maintaining a special relationship with Taiwan, today a progressive, thriving democracy.\par \par \uc2 In September, President Biden vowed on this broadcast that the U.S. will protect Taiwan. This past week, the Taiwanese government said China aims to normalize its military pressure on the island, that escalated after House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  visited in August with its largest military drill ever.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): In a display of frightening military might, China subjected Taiwan to three days of continuous sorties, with over a hundred warplanes, a barrage of ballistic missiles and warships that encircled the island, delivering a loud and clear message that China could choke Taiwan any time it wanted to.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: You think they`re going to invade?\par \par \uc2 LEE HSI-MIN: This is not a matter of if they will invade, it`s just a matter of when they will invade.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): Admiral Lee Hsi-min, who used to head Taiwan`s armed forces, has been ringing alarm bells for years because as China`s military has been growing, Taiwan`s is shrinking. The number of soldiers in uniform has been cut in half over the years, the length of mandatory service has been reduced to just four months. And Admiral Lee complains that the government has been buying the wrong weapons for years: tanks and jets from the United States instead of smaller portable missiles.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: What I gather you think the military needs are these stingers and javelins and drones, exactly what they need in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 LEE HSI-MIN: Yeah. It`s the truth.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: And you`re not getting them now--\par \par \uc2 LEE HSI-MIN: No.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: --because they`re giving them to Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 LEE HSI-MIN: We already ordered it. In my view, not enough. But however, we began to order them. But we have not yet received any. Because other countries also have kind of similar requirements. We are not on the top list. But we need now. We need now.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Yeah. Did the Taiwanese military waste all those years buying those big weapons?\par \par \uc2 LEE HSI-MIN: I believe so, you know, but we don`t have time to waste anymore.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): Taiwan doesn`t get U.S. military aid, it buys the weapons. But the manufacturers can`t keep up with the demand. The Taiwanese have already purchased about $14 billion worth of weapons that they have yet to receive. We were surprised that few here seem to share the admiral`s sense of urgency.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Here in Taiwan, you`d never know that the dragon to the north recently sent warships to surround the island. People told us over and over, "no big deal," China`s been doing versions of that for 70 years. While much of the world thought an invasion was imminent, polls show that a majority of Taiwanese think that`s unlikely any time soon, if ever.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): And that`s reflected in what we saw in the capital, Taipei, where life goes on uninterrupted. Morning traffic flows normally. Shoppers do what they always do, during the day and at night.\par \par \uc2 We saw old people painting outdoors; and teenagers practicing hip-hop routines -- despite the threat from the north.\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: This kind of threat is our daily life.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): Wang Ting-yu, a parliamentarian from southern Taiwan, says a kind of war has already started.\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: China, they try to annex Taiwan for past 50 years. They try all different kind of way. Maybe I can give you very concrete figures: there are 20 million cyberattacks, per day.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Per day?\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: Yes, every day.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): Wang, who sits on Parliament`s Foreign Affairs and National Defense Committee, took us to a high-security lab where engineers track those attacks.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: This is Taiwan here.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Ah, okay.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: A small island. But we are proud of it.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): The map shows the attacks from China in real time as they hit Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: And it`s so close to China.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Yeah. Unfortunately.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: So is China hoping to defeat Taiwan without firing a single shot?\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: They dream like that.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: They dream?\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: They desperately dream like that.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): He says on top of cyber warfare, they`re trying to sabotage Taiwan`s thriving economy and intimidate politically powerful groups, like the farmers and fishermen in Wang`s home district of Tainan who`ve been hit especially hard with a series of export bans.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: When Speaker Pelosi was here --\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: -- China, we`re told, banned 1,000 products. Taiwanese products. A lot from your region down here.\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: Yes. It do hurt, do damage to some individual businesses.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): Like the fish industry.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Is there any grouper here?\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): Ninety percent of grouper exports went to China last year.\par \par \uc2 But suddenly in June, Beijing banned Taiwan`s grouper, devastating the fishermen. Boxes and boxes of fish piled up. China also went after pineapples, crushing farmers like this young couple.\par \par \uc2 MAN #2 (Through Translator): It devastated us. Our pineapples got stuck in Taiwan and we lost 60,000 U.S. dollars.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: And I understand the ban was sudden, like that. No warning.\par \par \uc2 WOMAN #1: No warning.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): The government fought back with a "Freedom Pineapple" campaign to entice everyone to buy and eat a lot of pineapples.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Oh, my God, it`s so sweet.\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: Our housewives, they have a voice, "Let`s eat pineapple on our dining tables."\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Everybody`s eating pineapple?\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: Military, they have lunch, they have dinner -- provide pineapples.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): We found a fairly prosperous country, a leading exporter of bicycles and other sports gear. This tiny island is a tech giant, in agriculture innovation and, above all, in semiconductors. Taiwan is practically the world`s only source of the thinnest microchips, manufactured almost exclusively by one company: \uc2 TSMC\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 China relies on these as does the rest of the world for things like iPhones, advanced computers, and car components. Ninety-one-year-old \uc2 Morris Chang\uc2 , TSMC`s founder, explains why a lot of people here think the chips protect them from Xi Jinping`s attacking.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: I`ve heard this expression, Silicon Shield or Chip Shield, talking about your company.\par \par \uc2 MORRIS CHANG\uc2 : Well, it means that perhaps because our company provides a lot of chips to the world, maybe somebody will refrain from attacking it. If that person`s priority is for economic well-being, I think they will refrain from attacking.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: What if the priority is to come here and nationalize your company within, you know, One China?\par \par \uc2 MORRIS CHANG\uc2 : If there`s a war, I mean, it would be destroyed. Everything will be destroyed.\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: China says -- some of the Chinese Communists say: Let`s invade Taiwan and occupy \uc2 TSMC\uc2 , make it become party-owned company.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: Then we will be a superpower. United States and Japan and Europe -- we don`t supply them chips, they will follow Chinese orders. But that`s naive.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Why is that naive?\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: Not only chip company, even a sausage company -- you need a recipe. You need human capital. You need to know how to manufacture, manufacturing that kind of product.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: If there is reunification, what would happen to you?\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: Die.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Die?\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: Yeah. If they annex Taiwan, people like me, a lot, will be perished.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): Beijing has sanctioned Wang Ting-yu personally for being outspokenly pro-Taiwan`s independence. He passionately defends the country`s progressive democracy. We saw campaign billboards everywhere, validating the island`s commitment to clean elections and freedom of speech. Beijing has promised that if there were re-unification, Taiwan could maintain many of its freedoms.\par \par \uc2 And yet, in 2019, China broke a similar promise to Hong Kong. Protests led to beatings, arrests, and stripping of democratic rights. It hit home in Taiwan and led to President \uc2 Tsai Ing\uc2 -wen, leader of the aggressively anti- reunification party, winning re-election in a landslide.\par \par \uc2 PRESIDENT TSAI ING-WEN: We are firmly resolved to defend our freedom, democracy, and way of life.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): Given what happened in Hong Kong and the recent military escalation, we were curious why the people are so stoic.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Asked if they`re in denial or apathetic, a Taiwanese writer said it`s kind of like global warming: You know it`s there`s and it`s going to get worse, but mostly people go about their lives. What can one individual really do?\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): But then the Taiwanese watched the Ukrainians stand up to the Russians. It so inspired Jack Yao, a young Taipei coffee vendor, that he went there to help the fight.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Did you go because you`re Taiwanese?\par \par \uc2 JACK YAO: Yeah. Because--\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: What`s the connection?\par \par \uc2 JACK YAO: Just, like, in the Ukraine situation and our situation - it`s very alike. They also have big neighbors. And they was the communists. And we have to face the China Communists. And then they want to attack us. They always want to attack us.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Was it in your mind that if you go to fight for Ukraine, other people will come here and fight for Taiwan--\par \par \uc2 JACK YAO: Yes! Yeah.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): What the Ukrainians have done is raising a question here in Taiwan: If that small democracy can stand up to its menacing neighbor, why can`t we? You see civil defense classes sprouting up like this one on how to identify Chinese fake news during an attack.\par \par \uc2 And this night class in the park on how to operate two-way radios in Morse code in case the Internet is knocked out.\par \par \uc2 ENOCH WU: We want our students to be able to apply a tourniquet within 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL (voiceover): Enoch Wu, a former special forces soldier, is running training workshops in how to treat bullet and shrapnel wounds and conduct search and rescue.\par \par \uc2 And Admiral Lee wants to take it a step further, calling on the government to arm Taiwan`s citizens and create a volunteer force like Ukraine`s.\par \par \uc2 LEE HSI-MIN: If Ukraine can do that, why not Taiwan? You know, I`m trying to convince more people that it`s important because this is a symbol of the deterrence. Determination.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: So you`re proposing what, I guess I would call, the Ukraine model.\par \par \uc2 LEE HSI-MIN: Similar. Ukraine people really inspire our people. But do our people change fast enough? I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Do you think that Taiwanese have that same kind of determination?\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: I strongly believe this. Because we cherish how we live. We love peace, we don`t like war. But we won`t cede our democracy, our life for peace. That`s surrender.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Is there a chance you`ll surrender?\par \par \uc2 WANG TING-YU: No, not a chance. Never.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: Taiwan`s digital threat from China.\par \par \uc2 LESLEY STAHL: Is Taiwan already at cyber war with China?\par \par \uc2 MAN #3: Essentially, yeah.\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: At 60minutesovertime.com, sponsored by \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 AFTER IAN\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: It`s been 11 days since Hurricane Ian, a Category 4 monster of a storm, cut across central Florida. Even in a state that`s no stranger to hurricanes, the destruction in the wake of Ian is staggering; more than 100 deaths, most by drowning; communities in tatters; the price tag for recovery estimated at more than $100 billion. We went to ground zero, on Florida`s southwest coast where Ian first roared ashore and where the hurricane`s fury was most severe.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): As Hurricane Ian`s violence bore down on Sanibel Island, the Sprecher Family fled with little more than the clothes on their backs. This past Wednesday, one week after the tempest laid waste to the island, the family went back to see what was left of the place they had called home for almost 20 years.\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: Where are we? I can`t even tell.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Ian severed the causeway that connects Sanibel to the mainland, so the Sprechers returned by boat.\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: You wouldn`t even know this is home.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): We were invited to go home with them to see the damage done when the Category 4 hurricane plowed into Florida. John Sprecher told us, the destruction was overwhelming.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPRECHER: This place looks like it`s been bombed out. And I -- you know, I remember our kids playing in the sand when they were a couple years old.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Milissa Sprecher told us she had trouble getting her bearings.\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: There`s no bridge. There is no ferry right now. There is nowhere to stay. There is no running water. There is no electricity. There is no air conditioning. There is nothing. Everybody on this island at this moment is homeless.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Their house is just a block from the beach, usually a few-minute walk they said, but not this day. The path was covered with thick, tacky mud.\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: Watch the mud, because you`re going to -- it`s very slick.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Sixty-five hundred people live on Sanibel. The place the Sprechers described as a tropical paradise now is a debris field. Cars tossed like toys by the storm surge. It stripped the asphalt off their road.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: This was paved?\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: This was paved.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): As they approached the house, the scene was surreal -- a beautiful Florida day, the kind that drew John and Milissa here from Wisconsin years ago, while the home and life they built lay battered under the sun.\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: I`m shocked.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): They had held pool parties in this yard, celebrated birthdays and graduations in these rooms. Now with the roof gone, exposing the accumulations of a lifetime to the elements, they are salvaging what memories they can take on a small boat.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPRECHER: Grandma and grandpa photos.\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: Yeah, grandma and grandpa photos. To be able to have photos and things that the kids have made and be able to take them, that`s huge.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: I`m floored that we have anything, anything.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Have you been in touch with your insurance?\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: We have, yeah.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPRECHER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Will you be able to build back what you lost?\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: I don`t know that -- I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPRECHER: We don`t even know if we want to--\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 JOHN SPRECHER: --honestly.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Really?\par \par \uc2 MILISSA SPRECHER: I would think a lot of people will be leaving this.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Are the phones ringing off the hook?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHAPMAN: Monday morning, we were getting about 15 -- 15 calls a minute.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Brian Chapman owns Chapman Insurance Group, one of the largest independent insurance agencies in southwest Florida, with about 30,000 customers, many who live on Sanibel Island and in hard-hit Fort Myers. Seven of his employees lost homes; his offices suffered water damage and lost power.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: With this hurricane, you had winds gusting up to 150 miles an hour. You had a massive storm surge. How do those two arms of this hurricane impact the reimbursements that your homeowners are going to get?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHAPMAN: That`s where it gets a little complicated because you have two policies, one for flood and one for wind.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Why is that so complicated?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHAPMAN: Well, did the wind damage happen first or the water rise? And was there wind damage before it flooded? And it`s hard to know the answer to that question.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): And just 18 percent of Florida homeowners have flood insurance.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: So why so few?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHAPMAN: Because it`s expensive. But not as expensive as what just happened.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Chapman says he fears Ian will only exacerbate a persistent problem in Florida`s insurance market. Eighty percent of all homeowners` insurance lawsuits in the country are filed here. Most big insurers have scaled way back. Small insurers are being squeezed. Six went out of business just this year.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: What has all of this done to premiums?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHAPMAN: Double-digit, triple-digit rate increases in the last 24 months.\par \par \uc2 My policy was personally, $3,500, then $7,000, and now $10,000. And that`s not including the flood insurance.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Who can afford that?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHAPMAN: It`s not affordable, it`s not sustainable.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: We were on Sanibel Island yesterday and the destruction is widespread. If you`re out there, what hope do you have to recover from this?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHAPMAN: It`s going to be a long road to recovery. The ones that forgoed insurance, I`m certain that there will be some that will sell their real estate or their land.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): All of this was wrought by a hurricane that rapidly spun itself into an electrified killer. Energized by the warm waters of the Gulf of Mexico, Ian took just two days to grow from a Category 1 to a Category 4 storm, packing 150-mile-per-hour winds and stirring up a storm surge that drowned coastal communities in 12 feet of water.\par \par \uc2 That one-two punch was compounded by catastrophic rains. The storm dumped more than 20 inches over central Florida, swelling rivers and flooding neighborhoods in a swath that stretched across the state.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: You knew this was going to be a monster?\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: I did.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): As Ian approached, Tampa-native Bobby Quinn, a former Air Force weather forecaster, private pilot, and tech company founder, wanted to help, so he drove south into the heart of the hurricane.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: It`s kind of hard to leave this spot.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): And got more than he`d bargained for.\par \par \uc2 He shot this video on his phone.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: There was no place to go. There were trees flying by when I was sitting in the truck and wind that would have flipped my truck if I had moved out from behind that wall. I tried at one point and the wheels came off the ground.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): After 13 harrowing hours, he started to put his talents to work. Quinn runs a Tampa-based tech start-up called Paypixl that crowd-sources drone imagery and organizes it on an app. When Ian hit, he repurposed his site so evacuees could view images of their homes and assess the damage, free of charge.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: You see the debris field, you see the destruction in the back.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Wow.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: Now you can turn off the satellite imagery and see the pre- event. This is what it looked like before the storm.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: How about that?\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: And if we add in our street level imagery, somebody can click in and see what that house looks like --\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Oh, wow.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: -- from the front after the storm.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: So you`ve got the whole event. Before, after, ground level.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): After posting some of his work on social media, he was inundated with requests. More than 700 came from a densely populated, spiraling development 35 miles north of Sanibel called Rotonda West.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: This is Rotonda West. This is the neighborhood. And the pink dots that you see are each individual image that was taken in the neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): To build his database, Quinn incorporated satellite images with ground level pictures he took driving street by street.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: So that huge circle that we saw, you were going down every cul-de-sac, going up and down, getting pictures on both sides of the street?\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: Every road. Every house.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: How long did that take you?\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: It took me nine hours, 117 miles.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: And how many pictures did you end up with?\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: Just over 8,000.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): He filmed inside some houses.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: Oh, man.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Quinn`s efforts didn`t go unnoticed.\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: It`s a foot deep and I`m going to have to turn back.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): He told us he`s been contacted by an insurance company and Florida`s Emergency Operations Center, seeking his data.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Why`d you do this?\par \par \uc2 BOBBY QUINN: If you`ve ever felt hopelessness or despair or the anxiety that comes with the unknown, you know it`s a terrible feeling. We know that we can use technology in a way that really hasn`t been used before to get to the right audience, to get to the loved ones and family members. We want to quell that anxiety for them.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): A prescription for that may lie 12 miles northeast of hard-hit Fort Myers. Babcock Ranch is the brainchild of eco- conscious developer and former professional football player, Syd Kitson.\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: So when you look at this building, this just went through a Cat-4 hurricane.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Kitson and his partners purchased 91,000 acres in 2006, bigger than Manhattan, with a dream to build America`s first environmentally friendly, hurricane-proof, fully sustainable small town.\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: We are the first solar-powered town in America. We have a solar field that`s 150 megawatts. But that`s just part of the story.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: How many people live here now?\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: About 5,000 people. And it`ll eventually be 50--\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: And you`ve got plans to grow to what?\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: About 50,000 people.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Kitson rode out the storm in his lakeside home in town.\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: And I remember sitting here. I had the weather on. And the weather person says, "Well, this Category 4 hurricane is now heading for Babcock Ranch." And not only is it heading for Babcock Ranch, but it`s going to be on the eastern side of the wall which is the worst place to be.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: How long did the hurricane sit over you?\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: It was about eight to 10 hours.\par \par \uc2 Unbelievable, you can barely hear yourself think.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): He took video with his iPhone. At the height of the tempest, there were white caps on the lake.\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: So as soon as the sun came up the next morning, I jumped in my car and I started driving out. And the only damage were a few downed trees and a few shingles off the roofs.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: That`s it?\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: That`s it. And so our recovery was maybe a day?\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Babcock Ranch was designed to accommodate Florida`s ecosystem with indigenous plants and natural waterways for drainage. It was built 25 to 30 feet above sea level to avoid storm surges. All electric and phone lines are buried.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Aren`t you just lucky that you happen to be on a higher level than most of the parts of Florida that got washed away?\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: Yes, I think that`s important, but not when it comes to the wind and flooding and rain. And so if that infrastructure`s not built properly, you will have homes that get flooded. You will have that wind damage.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): No one here lost power. Syd Kitson took us to see this massive solar array.\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: What you see is 440 acres.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): Seven hundred thousand panels, built by \uc2 Florida Power & Light\uc2 . They withstood Ian`s brutal battering.\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: There`s a lot of water, but you don`t see a single panel that`s been dislodged. And there was quite a bit of wind that came through here over the last few days.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER: Gusts of 150 or more.\par \par \uc2 SYD KITSON: Gusts of over 150, and it did not take a single panel out of here, which is really just remarkable.\par \par \uc2 BILL WHITAKER (voiceover): After seeing the devastation on Sanibel Island, it felt strange to see children playing in parks here, people enjoying themselves, eating at waterside restaurants, while neighboring communities struggle with Ian`s aftermath, the deadliest hurricane since Katrina.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 CHURCH AND STATE\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: It`s been a turbulent few months for the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 . The \uc2 SBC\uc2  is the largest evangelical institution in America, representing 47,000 churches and about 14 million members. But in May, it was revealed some of its now former executives had ignored hundreds of allegations of sexual abuse in Southern Baptist churches for decades. When \uc2 SBC\uc2  members elected a new president in June, they turned to a small-town, Texas pastor named Bart Barber to lead them.\par \par \uc2 With a Department of Justice investigation into the abuse scandal underway, and midterm elections looming, we weren`t sure he would want to sit down and discuss weighty matters of church and state, but he did. And as you`re about to hear, Bart Barber has a lot to say about faith, scandal and the political extremism threatening American democracy.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: Blind partisanship destroys everything -- except baseball. I`m a \uc2 St. Louis Cardinals\uc2  fan and I`m sticking with that, no matter what. But so many things in church life and beyond that are areas where we have an opportunity to unite and solve problems, and we pass over that opportunity over and over again to shoot at the other team.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: And you see that filtering into the church?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: And it`s absolutely coming into the way that people in churches, who ought to know better, are speaking to one another about the issues that are outside the church that aren`t really theological. The best characterization is they`re not listening.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): Bart Barber lives with his wife and two children in Farmersville, Texas.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: So what we`re hoping to do is to use kind of regenerative farming method.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): He`s got some land with a dozen or so cows.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: If you want to see people come to know Christ, the way to do that is to share the Gospel and pray.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): And has preached every Sunday for 23 years at the First Baptist Church, which only has about 320 members.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: What made you decide to try to become the -- the head of the \uc2 SBC\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I believe that the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  faces some unique challenges right now. I felt like God was calling me to try to give leadership at this moment to help Southern Baptist move forward.\par \par \uc2 MAN #1: The Chair declares the winner to be Bart Barber the next President of \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (Applause)\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): When Bart Barber was elected \uc2 SBC\uc2  president in June, it was just four weeks after an independent investigation revealed that some former members of the SBC`s Executive Committee -- which oversees budget and organizational issues -- had for decades ignored hundreds of credible accusations of sexual abuse in Southern Baptist churches and seminaries, partly to avoid being held financially liable.\par \par \uc2 RYAN BURGE: They actually kept a list of over 700 names of people who had been credibly accused. What they said, though, is, "We couldn`t give that to the churches because local churches have autonomy in who they hire and fire for pastors. We can`t tell them they can`t hire this person."\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): Ryan Burge is an assistant professor at \uc2 Eastern Illinois University\uc2 , and an American Baptist pastor. He is one of the country`s leading data analysts on religion and politics.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Were they calling law enforcement and letting police know that there was a predator at this church in this state?\par \par \uc2 RYAN BURGE: The Executive Committee had the list, put it in a drawer, and didn`t tell anyone about it for over 10 years.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: That`s the mindset that we`re repudiating and moving against.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: When you read that report and to read accounts of people who were brave enough to call in to the Executive Committee, to report abuse, for them to be ignored --\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: That`s not a strong enough word. We didn`t just ignore them. Sometimes we impugned their motives. Sometimes we attacked them. The reason why I`m president of the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  is because our churches do not agree with that and have taken action to correct those things.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): Bart Barber says he`s cooperating with the Justice Department`s investigation and appointed a new, nine-member sexual abuse task force that`s building a registry for credible reports of abuse to help churches track predators.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I have strong feelings about this. I`m -- it`s not just anger, although I`m angry about it. God called me to be a pastor when I was 11. I believe in this. For people to sully this hurts me. I`m not doing this to try to accomplish some PR objective for us. I`m doing this because I want to serve God well.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): For the new president of the \uc2 SBC\uc2 , that means staying true to his deep conservative values and his beliefs about the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Do you believe the 2020 election was stolen?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: No.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: You believe \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is the legitimate President of the United States?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I do. Absolutely. I pray for him consistently as the President of the United States. I believe he was legitimately elected.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Bart Barber told us that he doesn`t believe the election was rigged. He does believe that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was duly elected the President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 RYAN BURGE: That`s a big deal. Sixty percent of white evangelicals believe the election was stolen in 2020. And many, many Southern Baptists go to church every Sunday believing that. Southern Baptist pastors have been afraid to speak about that from the pulpit, because they know lots of people oppose that in the pews.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: How many people, how many voters is Bart Barber in a position to influence?\par \par \uc2 RYAN BURGE: At least 70 million people identify as evangelical today. He can have a huge impact when it comes to who they vote for and why they vote for that candidate.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): Ryan Burge says in 2016, evangelicals accounted for 33 percent of all votes cast for Donald Trump. But Bart Barber`s vote was not among them.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: In 2016, you said, "I think it hurts the credibility of my testimony for me to be a vocal supporter of a demonstrably evil man whose campaign platform consists mainly of his evilness."\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: Yeah. I did not vote for President Trump in 2016. And that lays out my rationale for that pretty well.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: What was the evilness that you saw?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: The way he treated women that had been documented at that point. I thought that a lot of the rhetoric about immigration was wrongful. A lot of Southern Baptists thought that the rhetoric about immigration was wrongful.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: You`re talking about legal immigration?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: Talking about legal immigration.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: You embrace it?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I embrace it. I`m thankful for people who have immigrated. I live in Texas. I`m surrounded by people who are intermarried into our families. They make our community better.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Correct me if I`m wrong. In 2020, you did vote for Donald Trump.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: Part of what changed is that the president advocated for some legislation on sentencing reform, something that really addressed some injustice that affected minority communities. I was encouraged by the consistent pro-life support that the president gave. I didn`t expect that.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 : We`re going to walk down, and I`ll be there with you.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): Barber did tell us what happened on January 6th --\par \par \uc2 ALL: Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . Hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): -- and Donald Trump`s role in it, has had a big impact on his opinion of the former president now.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I -- and I think a lot of Southern Baptists would be thrilled to have the opportunity to support someone for leadership in our country who`s strong on the values that matter to us, who can do that without putting the vice president`s life in danger.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: You would be hard-pressed to vote for somebody who put his vice president`s life in danger?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Donald Trump did invite and incite and encourage a mob of people to march on the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I`ll just say this -- I want to be driven by the principles of Jesus Christ. And that does not involve mob violence. I don`t -- I don`t support that. Anyone who does support that, I`m less likely to vote for them because of their support for that.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: If \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  ran in a primary, you would vote for him in a primary?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: There is nothing that would prevent me from voting for \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  in a primary.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): We asked Barber what he thinks about the Christian nationalist rhetoric increasingly being used by some elected officials, like Congresswoman Lauren Boebert of Colorado.\par \par \uc2 REPRESENTATIVE LAUREN BOEBERT: The church is supposed to direct the government. The government is not supposed to direct the church.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: It stands contrary to 400 years of Baptist history and everything I believe about religious liberty. I`m opposed to the idea of Christian dominion, churchly dominion over the operations of government.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Why do you object to that?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: Okay. I object to it because Jesus said his kingdom is not of this world. I object to it because historically, every time it`s been adopted, it wound up persecuting people like me. Doesn`t stop at persecuting people who are not Christians. It eventually winds up persecuting people who are Christians for whom the flavor of their Christianity is different from that of the government.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): Support for the separation of church and state was a foundational principle for Baptists who faced religious persecution in England and America in the 1600s. Baptists split in 1845 over slavery, which is when the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  was founded. The \uc2 SBC\uc2  supported slavery and later, segregation.\par \par \uc2 On abortion, the SBC`s opposition has hardened over the years. In 1971, they made exceptions in cases where there was, "The likelihood of damage to the emotional, mental and physical health of the mother."\par \par \uc2 But in 1980, they narrowed that exception only to cases where a pregnancy threatened the life of the mother. Bart Barber says he stands by that today.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: Our interest with abortion is not to police everybody`s sex life. Our interest with abortion is that we believe that`s a human person who deserves to live.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: There was just the case recently, a 10-year-old girl who`s raped, barred from having an abortion in Ohio, was able to obtain one in Indiana. I mean, this is a little girl who -- she has a right to life, too.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: Sure.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Even in that case, you think she should have the child?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I do.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: She should be forced to have the child?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I think -- I don`t want that to sound like I don`t have tremendous compassion for her and her circumstance. I wish we could put an end to 10-year-olds being raped. I`m trying to work against child sexual abuse because I think that`s atrocious.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: But you don`t see forcing a 10-year-old child to go to term with a baby from rape as abuse of a child?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I see it as horrible. I see it as preferable to killing someone else.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): Not surprisingly, Barber and the \uc2 SBC\uc2  oppose same sex marriage.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: We`re committed to the idea of gender is a gift from God. We`re committed to the idea that men and women ought to be united with one another in marriage.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Do you still believe that gay people can be -- should be converted out of being gay?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I believe that sinners should be converted out of being sinners, and that applies to all of us.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Can somebody be a good Christian, a member of the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  and be gay or lesbian and married to a person of the same sex?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: No.\par \par \uc2 In Jesus name we pray, amen.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER (voiceover): Can a good Christian in good conscience vote for Donald Trump in 2024? Before we left, we asked Bart Barber one last time about how he`ll vote.\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: I`m not even going to speculate about that. Who are the other choices?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Ahead of the election in 2016, you said who you were going to vote for. In 2020, you said who you voted for. Now you`re not saying who you`d vote for?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: That`s correct.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Somebody seeing this is going to think, "Okay, well, that`s" --\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: Why are you hedging it now?\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: -- "that`s political."\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: It`s not political calculation. The fact in 2016, I could say something and I was speaking only for myself. And now, you know, 50,000 churches of people I love are represented by me when I speak. And so, do I feel a sense of needing to be more wise and careful about things that I say now? Absolutely, I do.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Have evangelicals sold their soul in order to support Donald Trump?\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: First of all, I think we had to choose from the choices that were given to us. And that`s -- that`s an inescapable reality in our political system --\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: But there`s a lot of evangelical support for Donald Trump that goes beyond just somebody holding their nose and saying, "Well, I have these two choices. So I`m going to vote for this person."\par \par \uc2 BART BARBER: That -- there are -- I`m telling you, there are also a lot of people who articulate what I`ve just said. I just think that under President Trump, they saw less backtracking on the things that were promised to them. I do think that Americans are hungry for strong leadership. I think that there`s opportunity for strong leaders to emerge who give us better choices. I`m praying for that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 (\uc2 CBS\uc2  SPORTS HQ)\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 ANNOUNCER: The Last Minute of 60 MINUTES is sponsored by United Healthcare. Get Medicare with more.\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: Eleven years ago, our late colleague, Bob Simon, described \uc2 St. Louis Cardinals\uc2 ` first baseman Albert Pujols as one of the greatest baseball players who ever lived. Well, this season, at 42, after years playing on the West Coast, Pujols came home to the Cardinals for his final season.\par \par \uc2 He smashed his 700th home run, then numbers 701, 702 and 703, joining Barry Bonds, Hank Aaron and Babe Ruth in that elite circle. An 11-time all-star, legendary first work ethic and admired for his charities, Albert Pujols was amazingly chosen 402nd in the 1999 baseball draft.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 BOB SIMON (voiceover): What drives Albert? He says he`s still seething about how he was snubbed in that `99 draft.\par \par \uc2 BOB SIMON: You`ll never get over it, huh?\par \par \uc2 ALBERT PUJOLS: Never, never.\par \par \uc2 BOB SIMON: People have told me that it`s really a bad idea to get you angry.\par \par \uc2 ALBERT PUJOLS: Yeah, you don`t want to do that.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 ANDERSON COOPER: I`m Anderson Cooper. We`ll be back next week with another edition of 60 MINUTES.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document SXMN000020221010eia900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 FACE THE NATION For October 9, 2022, \uc2 CBS\b0\par\par\uc2 Charlie D`Agata, Nikole Killion, Scott MacFarlane, Major Garrett,  Anthony Salvanto \par \uc2 8,778 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 October 2022\par \uc2 CBS News: Face the Nation\par \uc2 FCNT\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright 2022 CBS Broadcasting Inc. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I`m Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 And this week on Face the Nation, we will dive into one of the most closely watched governor`s races in the country and examine problems facing America at home and abroad.\par \par \uc2 We have new \uc2 CBS\uc2  polls in Michigan, Wisconsin and Arizona. And, in back-to- back appearances, we will hear from Republican Kari Lake and Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , as they are running dead even to be Arizona`s next governor. Our political panel will track this unique Arizona event and the week`s news.\par \par \uc2 Then: Financial markets remain jumpy, and recession anxieties got more fuel after the \uc2 OPEC\uc2  cartel cut oil production. We will check in with top economist Mohamed El-Erian about the road ahead.\par \par \uc2 Finally, a key bridge linking the Crimean Peninsula with Russia is destroyed. Ukrainians celebrate, while Russia fumes. What are Vladimir Putin`s options? Could one be battlefield nuclear weapons? We will have a report from the region.\par \par \uc2 It`s all just ahead on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Good morning. Welcome to Face the Nation. Margaret Brennan is off.\par \par \uc2 We have new \uc2 CBS\uc2  Battleground Tracker polls from several key states. In Michigan, Democratic Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  is leading her Republican challenger, Tudor Dixon, 53 percent to 47 percent.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, we`re tracking two statewide races. The governor`s race between Democratic incumbent \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  and his Republican challenger, businessman \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 , is deadlocked, with both candidates at 50 percent. In the Senate race, incumbent Republican Senator Ron Johnson is polling at 50 percent against his Democratic rival, Mandela Barnes, who is at 49 percent.\par \par \uc2 We also learned last week in Arizona incumbent Democratic Senator Mark Kelly is narrowly ahead of his Republican challenger, \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , 51 percent to 48 percent.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now to discuss all of this is CBS News elections and surveys director Anthony Salvanto.\par \par \uc2 Anthony, good morning.\par \par \uc2 Catch us up on the dynamics of these Senate races.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Good morning, Major.\par \par \uc2 So, Wisconsin and Arizona are two Senate races out of, I think, five that will ultimately determine control the chamber. So, they`re both close for reasons that are across all of these races. And that is, it`s the economy against abortion, which is to say, Democrats are winning voters who are concerned about abortion.\par \par \uc2 That`s happening in Wisconsin. Mandela Barnes is getting almost three- quarters of those who say it`s very important. And then the economy. Republicans are winning voters who say the economy is most important, going to punish the party in power. And that`s the Democrats. He`s getting almost two-thirds of them.\par \par \uc2 Now, having said that, each party is sort of jockeying to try to add another set of issues to that list. And on the Republican side, one of those is crime. So, they`re running attack ads. They`re trying to tie Democrats to this unpopular position -- and it is unpopular in Wisconsin -- of defunding the police.\par \par \uc2 And when voters perceive, if they perceive that Barnes supports defunding the police -- again, that`s their perception -- then they aren`t voting for him. And he`s behind Johnson on the idea of, will your -- will their policies keep you safe?\par \par \uc2 Now, Democrats, for their part, would like to add this issue of election integrity, threats to democracy after the attempts to overturn 2020.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Under that banner of threats to democracy, some Republicans won their primaries by denying or questioning the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 To what degree is that position either being embraced or backed away from or having an effect on the general elections they find themselves in now?\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Well, look, for Republicans, it was almost a litmus test in their primaries. And they`re still supporting their candidates regardless of their stance.\par \par \uc2 There`s a little bit of evidence that the position of trying to overturn 2020 hurts Republicans in this sense, if you look at independents who believe that Ron Johnson -- again, their perception -- was trying to overturn the election, they`re not voting for him.\par \par \uc2 And the way it also connects is on candidate qualities. Take a look at Arizona. A majority of people say that they`d prefer their elected officials say that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won, which he did. Now, when you look at voters who say that they think that the candidates are -- or a Republican candidate is talking about 2020, they`re more likely to label that candidate as extreme, as opposed to mainstream.\par \par \uc2 And that goes towards candidate qualities, where Democrats have tended to have an advantage. Those are the dynamics all at work here.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Anthony Salvanto, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 ANTHONY SALVANTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Now to the race for governor of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 Our latest poll shows Democrat \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2  and Republican Kari Lake tied at 49 percent. The two candidates have not scheduled a debate, but they are here to answer our questions. Each candidate will have about eight-and-a- half minutes.\par \par \uc2 We intend to cover the same issues with each candidate, issues identified by our own polling as of leading concern to Arizona voters. Our ability to cover this ground, of course, will be influenced in no small measure by the length of the candidates` answers, first Republican Kari Lake, who joins us from Phoenix.\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE (R-Arizona Gubernatorial Candidate): Good morning, Major. Thank you so much for having me on.\par \par \uc2 Just to clarify, I have agreed to any and all debates, and I will be taking part in one without my opponent next week, on the 12th. I would love it if she would show up, because I think there`s a lot of important issues that the people of Arizona need to hear about.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We`ll get to that, I promise.\par \par \uc2 Your Web site is detailed and specific on the question of immigration and border security. You call, if elected as governor, for Arizona to join other states to form what you call a compact to carry out border security separate from the federal government, including arrest and detention.\par \par \uc2 For the benefit of Arizona voters, can you explain the legal and practical application of this approach?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: Well, of course, if you know the Constitution, you know that Article 4, Section 4 calls for the federal government to protect us from invasion.\par \par \uc2 And under Joe Biden`s lack of leadership, we just aren`t seeing that. And we have an invasion at our border. The cartels, these narco terrorist groups have operational control. And they`re using Arizona to smuggle people, to traffic children, and to traffic the most dangerous drug we`ve ever seen, fentanyl.\par \par \uc2 And so we`re going to invoke our Article 1, Section 10, basically, authority to take care of our own border and protect our own border. It`s right there in black and white in the Constitution. And we meet all three criteria. We have an invasion, our people are in imminent danger, and time is of the essence. There`s no time for delay.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to have other states offer help. I have already got a couple other governors who are willing to help out. And I know that if you ask people in other states that are not border states, they deem this crisis on the border as one of the top issues facing our country...\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Would Arizona...\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: ... with so many young people dying of fentanyl poisoning right now.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Would Arizona go it alone if it didn`t get this compact agreement with other states?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 But I believe we will get help. I have already talked to some other governors. And they`re -- they`re vowing to help us out wherever they can, because they realize that what comes into Arizona, fentanyl, people coming here illegally, children being trafficked, doesn`t stay in Arizona. It goes to all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 And I just spoke last week to a mother and father who lost their son. And it`s just tragic. We`re losing more people to fentanyl in Arizona since \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  took office than we lost in 9/11 or during COVID.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: If, as there would likely be, federal challenges were presented, would you, as governor, await for those federal challenges to this state compact be resolved? And would you respect them if they were to any way impinge upon or restrict or nullify the state compact approach?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: We will -- we will challenge the federal government, if they`re going to challenge us. I think we have the right to do this.\par \par \uc2 And we will have attorneys file lawsuits as well. But we`re not going to back down and let our people be overrun with drugs, watch our children die. We had a 16-year-old die here in the metro area last week from a fentanyl overdose. We can`t keep having this happen. We`re losing our young generation.\par \par \uc2 So I hope that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  doesn`t fight us, because then it would really look like he is on the side of the cartels. And I don`t think he wants the people to think that.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Your Web site also says that Washington D.C. incentivizes illegal immigration to satisfy -- quote -- "big business lust for cheap labor."\par \par \uc2 Does that big business lust reside in Arizona at all?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: I think it resides everywhere.\par \par \uc2 And we saw it with \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  last week. I mean, it was the most racist thing, I think, I have heard her say, although she`s said quite a few things that are offensive. She said these people coming in illegally should go pick fruit in the South. I mean, I couldn`t believe my ears, Major, when I heard that.\par \par \uc2 We want to make sure that our middle class and our working class are healthy. The Democrats used to care about our working class. And now they appear that they don`t. They want to see five million people come in. These five million people, where are they staying? They`re not staying on the streets.\par \par \uc2 It`s American citizens who are homeless. And they`re taking jobs from hardworking Arizonans. And we`re going to start putting Arizona first and protecting our citizens in this state.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Nominee Lake, our polling shows that abortion is a very important issue to Arizona vote voters. You have been quoted as saying Arizona will be a state "where we will not be taking the lives of our unborn anymore."\par \par \uc2 You`ve also more recently been quoted as saying that, in Arizona abortion, should be rare and legal. Are those statements consistent?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: I -- I was in an interview when I said that, and I was -- I was telling the interviewer that, when abortion was first presented, they said it should be -- it should be rare, safe and legal.\par \par \uc2 And now it`s become anything but rare. In \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 ` world -- and you can ask her about this -- I understand she`s coming up next -- they`re for abortion right up until birth. If you are in the hospital in labor, the abortionists are for giving you an abortion, if you desire one.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: What are you for?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: We need to draw the line. We need to draw the line somewhere.\par \par \uc2 I am going to be the executive of the state, the chief executive officer, and I will follow the law. The law right now as it stands is Governor Ducey`s law at 15 weeks, so we`ll follow the law. Listen, I`m a woman.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Would you seek to restrict abortions beyond -- closer than 15 weeks?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: May I finish?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Sure.\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: I am -- I`m a woman. I`m a mother. I`m all for women`s health care.\par \par \uc2 I come from a large family. Seven sisters, I have. Of course I want women`s health care. This has moved beyond health care. We`re not giving women choices. I`m for giving women true choices. And when they walk into an abortion center, they`re only given one choice.\par \par \uc2 And they`re not told that you have the choice to keep your baby, and we can help, and here`s how, or we can help you find a loving family who will adopt your baby. I want to give women true choices. I will uphold the law, whatever that law is.\par \par \uc2 And I want to see to it that we save more lives. Right now, the Democrats have started pushing so far from that rare but safe to anything goes, up to nine months of pregnancy, after birth. \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , my opponent, actually has voted for -- and you look at her voting record -- allowing a baby who survives an abortion, that the hospital would refuse medical care and allow the baby to die on a cold metal tray.\par \par \uc2 She voted for that.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We will take that up with \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , I guarantee you.\par \par \uc2 This week, nominee Lake, a 64-year-old Iowa man was arrested and charged for threatening to lynch Maricopa County Supervisor Clint Hickman. In reaction, Hickman said: "people in positions of influence and leadership in Arizona are silent. How can you be silent?"\par \par \uc2 Do you have a reply from Mr. Hickman? And would you support and do you support federal and state prosecution of anyone who threatens the lives of an election worker?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: I think that anyone who threatens anyone`s lives should be detained and questioned. I`m not for violence in any way.\par \par \uc2 And I should remind you that, during COVID, when a lot of Americans were faced with not being able to use their free speech to speak out against what was happening, they were losing their jobs, they were losing their businesses, they were being forced to get shots that they didn`t want, people were being bullied and -- and attacked and also threatened during that time.\par \par \uc2 I think we need to get back to where we have free speech, and we shouldn`t be threatening people. And I hope that they arrest that man and detain him.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Earlier this week, \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2 , as you know, Republican nominee for the Senate, said he has not seen evidence of vote-counting problems or election results that would have changed the outcome of the 2020 presidential election.\par \par \uc2 He also said President Biden is the legitimate president. Do you agree?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: I think we have major problems in our election system. And we - - and it goes back to 2000.\par \par \uc2 We had Democrats saying the 2000 election wasn`t fair. They were complaining the 2004 election wasn`t fair. 2016, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  spoke out and said that the electronic voting machines were hacked in front of her eyes. And nobody called them election deniers.\par \par \uc2 And now, all of a sudden, in 2020, Garrett, we don`t have free speech anymore. We can`t speak out against our own elections. All I`m asking for is the ability to speak out. When our government does something wrong, we should be able to speak out against it.\par \par \uc2 I think we have major...\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Do you agree with the statement that \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  made?\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: I -- I`m not going to take on what Blake said. I`m going to take on what I said.\par \par \uc2 And what I say is, we have problems in our election. They haven`t been solved in 2016. They weren`t solved in `18. Just a month or two ago, during our primary election -- and I`m sure your voters -- your viewers don`t even know this -- \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 ` office advised the counties on how many ballots to print. This was two months ago.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Right. Nominee Lake...\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: And they underprinted ballots in our biggest county, and they ran out of Republican-only ballots one hour into voting.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Nominee Lake...\par \par \uc2 KARI LAKE: We have to restore honesty to our elections. We must restore honesty.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: In fairness -- in fairness and in timing for both you and \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , we have to end it there. Thank you very much for being with us on Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 Face the Nation will back in just one minute. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Now to \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 . She is the Democratic nominee for governor, and she`s also in Phoenix this morning.\par \par \uc2 Secretary Hobbs, good morning. Welcome to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 So, your opponent said you don`t want to debate. I would like to ask you, are there any conditions under which you would agree to debate Kari Lake, so the voters of Arizona could hear from the two of you side by side and in real time?\par \par \uc2 KATIE HOBBS (D-Arizona Secretary of State and Gubernatorial Candidate): Look, what I have been focused on is opportunities like this, where they can see us back to back, and hear directly from us about the issues that are important to Arizonans and how we would govern, without a circus like she created during the GOP Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 I have no desire to be a part of -- of the spectacle that she`s looking to create, because that doesn`t do any service to the voters of Arizona to hear from us, where we stand on the issues and how we would govern.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, sometimes, voters learn things from moments of duress or challenge or circus. Don`t you think you`re strong enough to handle any kind of circus Kari Lake might present, if, in fact, she were to present one? Don`t the voters of Arizona deserve to see that real?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE KATIE HOBBS: I think the voters of Arizona have had a chance to see how I work under crisis throughout my leadership during the 2020 election as secretary of state, when we had to combat multiple election challenges from former President Trump and his band of election deniers, including my opponent, Kari Lake.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So I want to ask you about immigration. We had a long conversation with Kari Lake about that.\par \par \uc2 Last night, you tweeted: "Arizona bears the brunt of the failures of US immigration policies."\par \par \uc2 Now, by our count, that is your second tweet in a month about immigration. Who specifically is responsible for this failure? And why only now did you decide to share that sentiment with your \uc2 Twitter\uc2  followers?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 : Oh, this is certainly not the first time that I have spoken out about immigration in this campaign.\par \par \uc2 We are a border state, and immigration as it is a very important issue to Arizona border -- voters. And, as a border state, we have borne the brunt of decades of inaction in Washington from both parties to address both border security and comprehensive immigration reform.\par \par \uc2 And -- and we need the federal government to step up. But what I want to be really clear about is that my opponent`s positions on this issue are nothing but empty rhetoric. She`s not offering real solutions. When she talked about invoking the constitutional authority of the state, she`s talking about declaring an invasion at our Southern border.\par \par \uc2 That would do absolutely nothing to increase border security, but it would bring untold levels of chaos into our state. It`s not a real solution. I have a border security plan that`s been endorsed by two border sheriffs because they know that it`s going to focus real solutions and bring meaningful relief and help them make their communities safer.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: As a practical matter, were Arizonans safer under immigration policies of the present administration or the one just before it?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 : Look, we`ve had a -- a pretty bad immigration policy for decades. We need to get comprehensive immigration reform done. And it`s -- both parties in Congress have been dragging their feet to -- to get this done.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: But, when you say there is a failure of current immigration policy, that`s a Biden administration failure, is it not, by definition?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 : Look, Trump centered his whole immigration policy around finishing the wall. And. it`s not done.\par \par \uc2 And -- but Biden does need to step up immigration and border security, absolutely. Arizona is bearing the brunt of -- of illegal drug trafficking, gun trafficking and smuggling. And we do need more border security. It`s not going to get done by declaring an invasion at the border or dismantling the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , which is another thing that my opponent has called for.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: On the question of abortion, your opponent described you as something of an extremist. Those are her words, not ours.\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE KATIE HOBBS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Do you support the current 15-week ban in Arizona? Or would you seek -- seek a different approach? And is there a week limit different than 15 weeks you are in favor of? And, if so, why?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE KATIE HOBBS: I don`t support the 15-week ban.\par \par \uc2 But let me just say that Kari Lake is -- is entirely misconstruing my position on this issue. You and I both know that late-term abortion is extremely rare. And if it`s being talked about, it`s because something has gone incredibly wrong in a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 A doctor`s not going to perform an abortion late in a pregnancy just because somebody decided they want one. That is ridiculous. And she`s saying this to distract from her incredibly extreme position, which has -- she`s gone on the record saying she supports Arizona`s complete abortion ban that would -- that is in the courts right now being decided if that will be the law of Arizona or not.\par \par \uc2 She`s called it a great law. She`s called people who seek abortion murderers and executioners. And under a Kari Lake administration, we would have government-mandated forced births that risk women`s lives.\par \par \uc2 And her position is the one that -- that`s extreme. It`s out of touch where -- with where the majority of Arizonans are, who support access to safe and legal abortion. And, under her administration, women would not be safe.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: What would the Hobbs administration`s week limit be for abortion access? If it`s not 15 weeks, what is it?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 : Look, abortion is a very personal decision that belongs between a woman and her doctor.\par \par \uc2 The government and politicians don`t belong in that decision. We need to let doctors perform the care that they are trained and take an oath to -- to perform.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: So, if an Arizona voter were to conclude from your previous answer that you do not favor any specific week limit on abortion, would they be correct?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE KATIE HOBBS: I support leaving the decision between a woman and her doctor and leaving politicians entirely out of it.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Our polling also indicates that the economy is very important to Arizona voters. And, on that issue, both the economy generally, inflation specifically, according to our data, you trail your opponent.\par \par \uc2 What is your approach to inflation and the economy in Arizona? And why is yours superior to that of your opponent?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE \uc2 KATIE HOBBS\uc2 : Well, first, let me say, I`m the only candidate in this race who is not a millionaire.\par \par \uc2 My husband and I raised our kids through financial ups and downs. And I know the struggle that a lot of Arizonans are feeling right now, having to stretch to put food on the table, thinking about taking a second job, or not being able to pay the mortgage.\par \par \uc2 And so we have a comprehensive plan to address the rising costs that Arizonans are facing right now that will put money back in their pockets. We cut taxes on all kinds of everyday items like over-the-counter medication, school supplies, diapers, feminine hygiene products. We provide a child -- a state-level child tax credit and tax credits for people who want to go back to work in higher-paying jobs to get career and technical education.\par \par \uc2 Economists have looked at my affordability plan next to Kari Lake`s plan, and they said that my plan does put people back to work and -- and help them fight inflation and that Kari Lake`s plan actually will make inflation worse.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Do you identify yourself with the Biden economic administration -- economic plans and inflation-fighting plans?\par \par \uc2 And, if so, would you advise the president to campaign along your side in Arizona?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE KATIE HOBBS: I`m focused on the race here in Arizona and the needs of Arizonans. It`s a race between myself and Kari Lake and -- and the -- the ideas that we`re bringing to the table.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And one -- one more time before we let you go, because I think it is on the mind of Arizona voters, are you saying this morning that there is no circumstance that you can envision or would even try to negotiate in which you and Kari Lake would appear at a debate together before the election?\par \par \uc2 SECRETARY OF STATE KATIE HOBBS: At this point in the race, with 30 days to go, our schedule in terms of -- of forums is pretty much set. And -- and I`m really happy with where we are in the plans we have to continue talking directly to the voters of Arizona.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , secretary of state of Arizona, Democratic nominee for governor, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back with a lot more Face to Nation. We invite you to please stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Here`s something of a pro tip.\par \par \uc2 If you can`t watch the full Face the Nation, you can set your DVR, or we`re available on demand. Plus, you can watch us through our \uc2 CBS\uc2  or Paramount+ app. And we`re replayed on our CBS News Streaming Network at -- on Sundays at 1:30 p.m., 4:00 p.m., and 10:00 p.m.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We will be right back with some analysis with our expert political panel.\par \par \uc2 We ask you, please stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Welcome back to Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 More than 21 months after the attack on the U.S. Capitol, a trial against defendants charged with seditious conspiracy began here in Washington. New disclosures were presented to jurors in what prosecutors call one of the most important cases seeking accountability.\par \par \uc2 CBS News congressional correspondent Scott MacFarlane has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE (voice-over): A crime unlike any in the nation`s history has led to a criminal trial unlike any in history.\par \par \uc2 Stewart Rhodes, military vet, Yale Law grad and former congressional staffer, is founder of the far right Oath Keepers group. Along with four co-defendants, he`s standing trial for seditious conspiracy, accused of plotting to attack and block the peaceful transfer of power in America, and facing decades in prison if convicted.\par \par \uc2 Former Justice Department attorney Michael Greenberger says, no matter the outcome of the six-week-long trial, history will be made.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GREENBERGER (Former Justice Department Attorney): This case is the most important seditious conspiracy case that was ever brought.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: In the trial`s opening days, prosecutors argued the group was plotting just days after the election. Jurors heard a clip of Rhodes from November 9, 2020, urging his group to be ready to fight to create a pathway to keep Trump in power.\par \par \uc2 STEWART RHODES (Founder and Leader, Oath Keepers): I`m willing to sacrifice myself for that. Let the fight start there, OK? That would give President Trump what he needs, frankly.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Prosecutors showed an open letter written by Rhodes to then-President Trump encouraging Trump to invoke the Insurrection Act, to mobilize military veterans and militias, and order a new election the militia would help administer.\par \par \uc2 They played this interview clip of Rhodes for jurors:\par \par \uc2 STEWART RHODES: We have men already stationed outside D.C. as a nuclear option.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: Prosecutors argued the conspirators shared messages referencing civil war and predicting blood and violence, and they said the group staged guns outside the D.C. limits and helped the execution of a military stack formation to breach the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 For the Justice Department, which has gone to trial against approximately 20 January 6 defendants so far and won convictions in every case before a jury, the stakes here are higher. The Justice Department has limited experience going to trial on the charge of seditious conspiracy, but Greenberger says the trial itself could help avert a future attack.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL GREENBERGER: Bringing the trial shows all these people out there who think, oh, I will go to Washington, I will have a good time, it`ll be fun, we will break into the Capitol, no, you`re going to end up, win or lose, convicted or not convicted, devoting a large part of your life and your fortune to defending yourself.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: The trial resumes Tuesday, and the jurors ought to get comfortable. This could last all the way up to Thanksgiving. Six to seven weeks is the estimate.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Speaking of getting comfortable, we have a nice, big, full table here at Face the Nation.\par \par \uc2 I want to welcome everyone. CBS News congressional correspondent Nikole Killion joins Scott MacFarlane. Also, we have "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " chief White House correspondent Peter Baker and "New Yorker" staff writer Susan Glasser.\par \par \uc2 Susan and Peter, I want to also let you know, are co-authors of a new book on the Trump presidency called "The Divider."\par \par \uc2 It`s great to have you all with us.\par \par \uc2 Susan and Peter, I want to start with you.\par \par \uc2 To what degree did what you just saw with Kari Lake, who many in the Trump world regard as a fast -- fast-rising star, reinforce what you write about in "The Divider"?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN GLASSER (Co-Author, "The Divider: Trump in the White House, 2017- 2021): Well, thank you very much, Major.\par \par \uc2 I was really struck in your interview with her that the Trumpist style in American politics, a la the famous paranoid style in American politics, seems to be with us whether or not Trump himself is literally on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 I think that Kari Lake is an example. First of all, she was rerunning a Trump play from the 2018 midterm elections, which did not work, by the way, in which he falsely claimed that there was an invasion at the Southern border and actually sent real U.S. military troops to defeat this fake invasion.\par \par \uc2 You have her and other candidates now using this language of invasion. I think we should point out, as a matter of fact, that we are not actually experiencing an invasion, number one.\par \par \uc2 Number two, you just -- you have brazenness, I think, as a superpower. And so you have -- for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right, he`s willing to go anywhere, to say anything. And one thing he`s found is that millions will follow him, for example, in the election lies about 2020.\par \par \uc2 Interestingly, in your interview, she did not -- she was not willing to actually come out and say that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was not the legitimate...\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: What \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  said...\par \par \uc2 SUSAN GLASSER: Absolutely, even...\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: ... running for the Senate as a Republican in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 SUSAN GLASSER: Even though they have all sought Trump`s endorsement on the basis that they endorse his false claims. Very interesting.\par \par \uc2 Does that mean that they can`t quite fully imitate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Peter, and yet -- I think it`s important to put the "and yet" -- Kari Lake is running neck and neck in Arizona. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  got millions more votes when he ran for reelection in 2020 than 2016.\par \par \uc2 What does that tell you?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER (Co-Author, "The Divider: Trump in the White House, 2017- 2021"): Yes.\par \par \uc2 Well, we call the book "The Divider" not because Trump created the divisions in our society, but he managed to -- he manifested them. He`s the manifestation of them, and he figured out how to exploit them politically.\par \par \uc2 And what we`re now seeing is whether he can -- he can -- his inheritors, if you will, can then translate that same style, that same technique and approach on the state level in places like Arizona, Michigan, Wisconsin, and so forth.\par \par \uc2 And the answer right now is, they have a real strong support. It`s a very evenly divided country. When you see these battleground states at 50/50, it tells you that the Trump part of the country is not just a minority. It is a significant part of America and what -- we have to confront that one way or the other, and rather -- and it can`t be dismissed.\par \par \uc2 This is something that`s going to outlast Trump himself.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Do you think that voters drawn to it are wrong to be drawn to it, or that that is an acceptable alternative in American politics, and that there is something Democrats are missing about this particular approach?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER: Yes, there`s a politics of grievance, right?\par \par \uc2 What Trump successfully tapped into is a sense out there of resentment among a lot of Americans, who feel like the elites in some fashion or another have let them down, whether the elites be in Washington or in their state capitals.\par \par \uc2 It`s also a dividing, of course, along racial lines, along cultural lines, along ethnic and income lines. And it`s been a successful trademark in this era. And the question is, how do you address those concerns in a way that doesn`t exacerbate the divisions, instead of bringing people together?\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Nikole, you`re on Capitol Hill, but you have also been traveling.\par \par \uc2 What have you been seeing in relation to the things that Peter and Susan have been talking about? I know you were recently in Georgia. There`s a Trump-preferred candidate there, had a very stressful week. Catch us up.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, that`s right.\par \par \uc2 And, in terms of \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , for now, the party seems to be standing with him. And so are voters. They are willing to look past his faults. But I think it`s yet another example of many voters being willing to accept anything.\par \par \uc2 At one point, in our races, the issue of candidate quality was something that meant a lot. But now you have candidates who deny elections, candidates who may have had past transgressions, and yet voters are willing to look past that.\par \par \uc2 So, and...\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: And, in -- and, in the case of the Georgia race, it`s not just a transgression. It`s something that fundamentally disagrees with what his position is, if I understand it correctly, on access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 This is somebody who has been staunchly anti-abortion, doesn`t support abortion with exceptions, but yet these reports have started to come out that, at one point in time, he paid for an abortion for a girlfriend, perhaps encouraged her to get an abortion on a second opportunity.\par \par \uc2 And so that didn`t happen. This woman claims to be the mother of his child. Again, CBS News has not been able to independently verify some of those claims. But it does raise questions about where he comes down on this issue.\par \par \uc2 And this race is critical. I mean, this race could really decide control of the Senate. And so this is kind of a make-or-break moment for his campaign.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Scott, you watch the trials, the prosecutions resulting from January 6, 2021, very closely, but you also keep a very close eye on the atmospherics around the Capitol and the midterm elections itself.\par \par \uc2 What is the sense of risk that members of Congress seeking reelection feel they are under, either at home or in the Capitol itself?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT MACFARLANE: A significantly growing risk.\par \par \uc2 In just a year`s time, the \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  have investigated thousands of potential threats against members of Congress. And then the Justice Department reveals that it has a task force that is also reviewing hundreds, if not thousands, of potential threats.\par \par \uc2 And the political impact of this aside -- and there may be a significant political impact to election denialism and questioning the integrity of elections -- let`s talk about the blocking and tackling of administering elections.\par \par \uc2 It puts that at risk as well. You chronicle this well in your book. But I will add, I talked to the elections administrator in Lansing, Michigan, Ingham County, Michigan, a purple county in Michigan, concerned about threats against her poll workers, concerned about recruiting poll workers, finding people to come in and want a piece of that.\par \par \uc2 And we know that some of the administrators of elections nationwide are trying to recruit law students, \uc2 American Bar Association\uc2 -related volunteers, somebody to come man the polls in this uniquely toxic environment.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Toxic environment, Susan, does that feel like something that will be inevitable, not only in this midterm election?\par \par \uc2 It`s not going to go away. Clearly, we know that. Will this toxicity, should we assume it will continue all the way up to 2024, possibly beyond?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN GLASSER: Yes, I think it`s not just a matter of rhetoric, right?\par \par \uc2 It`s structural changes that are being made in our politics. You have Trump, who`s reoriented the Republican Party and radicalized it. "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reported this week that 299 nominees, Republican nominees for House, Senate and major statewide posts, are election deniers, including in -- some will be in key races where they are determining the counting and the certification of votes going forward.\par \par \uc2 And, by the way, many of those Republican nominees are in safe Republican seats, so they`re now guaranteed to have essentially a wave of Trumpian shock troops who have made election denialism a foundation of the Republican -- Republican Party`s new ideology.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not just a matter of rhetoric. I think we`re changing the structure of American politics in ways that are designed to exacerbate this ongoing crisis in American democracy.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Nikole, it`s pretty clear, if you look at the spending patterns in some of the Senate races, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania, dramatic examples of this.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, more than half of the money spent on TV advertising is about crime. To what degree do you see that playing as a potential -- potentially pivotal issue in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 NIKOLE KILLION: Well, it certainly is a Republican talking point and an issue that they want to put front and center.\par \par \uc2 I was just in Wisconsin over the weekend and talked to Senator Johnson, who is going for his third term. And he did say that he feels that that should be a defining issue in this election.\par \par \uc2 On the flip side, though, in terms of his opponent, he has tried to paint him as someone who is soft on crime. But in terms of Mandela Barnes, his approach has really been making this abortion rights issue, which we have seen Democrats time and again in a lot of the Senate races really tried to bring that to the fore.\par \par \uc2 So, again, kind of, as Anthony pointed out earlier, I think it`s the -- each party has a different narrative, and it`s a matter of which one is going to rise to the top.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Peter, in your book with Susan, do you deduce anything that you think is of lasting and positive value from the Trump year -- years?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER: Well, look, I think it`s reinforced this conversation about what our democracy is and should be, right?\par \par \uc2 And it`s forced us to look at the structure, as Susan said, of our politics and of our system. But I think that what the concern is, we don`t know where it`s going to lead us. Do we reaffirm our commitment to this system that we created 240-some years ago, or are we going to find ourselves lost?\par \par \uc2 And I think that that`s the big question heading into 2024.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: I mean, if the former president were sitting here, he would say: I improved the economy. I made American foreign policy stronger.\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Would you say any of that has a validity, based on all the research that went into the book?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER: There are a lot of voters out there who say, look, I like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because I liked some of the things he accomplished. I did like tax cuts, or a conservative Supreme Court justice, or regulation cuts or what have you.\par \par \uc2 But the question that is raised, at least certainly in our book, goes beyond a specific Republican, Democrat, liberal, conservative ideological - - ideological fight. It goes to the larger questions of what we want to be as a country.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s what makes Trump unique. He`s not like other Republicans or Democrats. It`s not about policy. It`s not about what our health care should be or tax cuts. It`s about whether you believe in the system that we created.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: It is a time for uniqueness and larger questions, to be sure.\par \par \uc2 Peter Baker, Susan Glasser, Nikole Killion, Scott MacFarlane, thank you so very much.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: As Russia`s military suffers more setbacks in its invasion of Ukraine, President Biden warned last week that Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is -- quote -- "not joking" -- unquote -- when he talks about using nuclear weapons, saying we face a prospect of nuclear Armageddon unlike anything we have seen since the height of the Cold War.\par \par \uc2 For more on what`s happening on the ground in Ukraine, CBS News senior foreign correspondent Charlie D`Agata has more.\par \par \uc2 (Begin VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA (voice-over): In a serious escalation to the war here, security cameras captured the moment a massive explosion tore through the Crimean bridge on Saturday morning. Russian officials blamed the explosion on a truck bomb, calling it an act of terror.\par \par \uc2 It comes on the back of a series of setbacks on the battlefields. Ukrainian forces have been clawing back territory on multiple fronts, in the south, toward Kherson, and east in the Donbass region, where we traveled to the liberated city of Lyman.\par \par \uc2 On the way, we spoke with Colonel Serhiy Cherevaty, who took part in the battle.\par \par \uc2 Which weapons were important in this fight?\par \par \uc2 (COLONEL SERHIY CHEREVATY SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: "Artillery was very important," he said, "American weapons and, of course, the HIMARS," the High Mobility Artillery Rocket Systems that can strike targets deep behind enemy lines.\par \par \uc2 (GUNSHOTS)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: But every inch of ground retaken in the very territory President Putin has illegally annexed risks retaliation and deepens the specter of a nuclear response.\par \par \uc2 And in an interview with the BBC, President Zelenskyy has warned, Russia may be getting ready.\par \par \uc2 VOLODYMYR ZELENSKYY (Ukrainian President): They begin to prepare their society. That is very dangerous.\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Once again this week, the Russian military showed a reckless disregard for human life.\par \par \uc2 (MAN SPEAKING IN FOREIGN LANGUAGE)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Overnight, rockets rained down on a residential neighborhood in Zaporizhzhia, leveling apartment blocks, killing more civilians, the latest in a series of strikes.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, on a grand avenue in Zaporizhzhia, we found firefighters still dousing a mountain of twisted metal and concrete.\par \par \uc2 Several hours after the rocket attack, and the building is still smoldering. This is no longer a rescue operation. And this is an upmarket neighborhood in the center of town. The more Russian troops lose against Ukrainian soldiers, the more they retaliate against civilians.\par \par \uc2 (End VT)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE D`AGATA: Renewed shelling at the Zaporizhzhia nuclear power plant has cut the main power lines there.\par \par \uc2 Engineers have had to rely on emergency diesel generators. President Putin has signed a decree declaring that it`s now officially under Russian ownership -- Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Charlie, thank you.\par \par \uc2 We will be back in a moment.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: We turn now to America`s economic challenges.\par \par \uc2 And to help us out, I want to bring in Mohamed El-Erian. He is the chief economic adviser at financial services company \uc2 Allianz\uc2 . He is also president of Queens College in Cambridge. He`s good enough to join us this morning from New York.\par \par \uc2 So, this week was a very volatile week in the U.S. stock markets. Volatility has been ever-present, I would say, during this calendar year, but this week seemed really volatile.\par \par \uc2 For my audience, for our audience, break it down. What`s going on? What is the source, if you can identify it, of that volatility?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN (Chief Economic Adviser, \uc2 Allianz\uc2 ): So, the main source of this volatility is changing perceptions of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We are in this incredible situation, Major, where good news for the economy is bad news for the markets. And that`s because the markets are worried that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  will tip us into recession by overreacting to strong economic news.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Every White House I have ever covered -- and it`s been more than one or two -- has said, you know, the market isn`t the economy. The economy is the economy. And what markets do, markets are going to do.\par \par \uc2 Even so, volatility affects people`s retirement, their planning and their sense of their medium- and long-term futures. How should people be looking at that? And when they hear the president, as he did on Friday, talk about navigating this transition, what does that mean?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: So, first, there`s volatility, and there`s unsettling volatility, volatility, when things go up and down and, on the whole, you`re better off over time.\par \par \uc2 But that`s not what we`ve had this year. We`ve had unsettling volatility, because we`ve had the stock market down by anything from 20 to 30 percent. We`ve had bonds, which are supposed to safeguard your investment, also down by about 15 percent.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s been nowhere to hide. That`s why people feel insecure. That`s why they look at their retirement plans with concern.\par \par \uc2 The president is right. We are currently on what I think of as a bumpy journey to a better destination, and we need to navigate both the journey and get ready for the destination. There is a possibility that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  makes another mistake and that that bumpy journey actually changes the destination. That`s why the markets are on edge.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Is it your perspective that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has already made a series of mistakes, either not acting fast enough or overreacting?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: So it`s made two big mistakes that I think are going to go down in the history books.\par \par \uc2 One is mischaracterizing inflation as transitory. By that, they meant it is temporary, it`s reversible, don`t worry about it. That was mistake number one. And then, mistake number two, when they finally recognized that inflation was persistent and high, they didn`t act. They didn`t act in a meaningful way.\par \par \uc2 And, as a result, we risk mistake number three, which is, by not easing the foot off the accelerator last year, they are slamming on the brakes this year, which will tip us into recession.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, unfortunately, this will go down as a big policy error by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Continuing your metaphor, slamming on the brakes, does that mean it is impossible to achieve the either literal or mythical soft landing?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: Even Chair Powell has gone from looking for a soft landing, to a softish landing, to now talking about pain.\par \par \uc2 And that is the -- the problem. That is the cost of a \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  being late. Not only does it have to overcome inflation, but it has to restore its credibility. So, yes, I fear that we risk a very high probability of a damaging recession that was totally avoidable, Major.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Washington, D.C. is a hyperpolitical town, not a news bulletin. So it tends to absorb information internationally, sometimes in personal ways.\par \par \uc2 So there`s a lot of chatter this week that when OPEC Plus decided and announced it was cutting production, that that was against President Biden specifically. Do you agree with that? Or do you think it`s a broader \uc2 OPEC\uc2  Plus declaration about the direction of the global economy?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: So, first, it does hurt the U.S., and we`ve seen oil prices go up above $90 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 What does that mean? It means that inflation, which has been coming down, now risks going up again. So, that -- that is not good for us. However, that it came as a surprise, it didn`t come as a surprise to me.\par \par \uc2 OPEC\uc2  is looking to protect oil prices in the context of declining global demand. All three major areas in the world, China, Europe, and the U.S., are slowing much faster, which means less demand for oil. So, what does \uc2 OPEC\uc2  do? They cut back supply.\par \par \uc2 So, this shouldn`t have come as a big surprise. That`s what they do. That`s the history. But it`s certainly not good news for the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Tying these things together, do you think higher gasoline prices inevitably mean higher inflation, making all the things we`ve discussed more complicated?\par \par \uc2 And do you have a Consumer Price Index prediction for the near future?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: So, the next measure comes out in a few days. That`s going to be for September. Headline inflation will probably come down to about 8 percent.\par \par \uc2 But core inflation, what measures the drivers of inflation and how broad they are, is still going up. So we still have an inflation issue. Inflation will come down, Major. The question is, does it come down with a slowdown in the economy or a major recession? That is the question that`s being debated right now.\par \par \uc2 It`s not whether we`ll have inflation coming down. We will. But it`s the cost of that inflation coming down.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Is the jobs report this week a silver lining in all of this otherwise gloomy assessment?\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: It certainly is.\par \par \uc2 We created 260,000 -- 263,000 jobs. That`s a lot for this stage. We also reduced the unemployment rate to 3.5 percent. That`s really low. There was one, one concern, which is that labor force participation, how many people are in the labor force, came down. And that`s not good news. And it talks to the importance of focusing on human capital.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: One last thing before I let you go. We have about 30 seconds.\par \par \uc2 About two or three months ago, it was common for people on the Web to see stagflation headlines. We are not in a stagflation situation.\par \par \uc2 MOHAMED EL-ERIAN: We are. Growth is coming down. Inflation is still high. Unfortunately, it`s not time to eliminate that term yet.\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Very good.\par \par \uc2 Mohamed El-Erian, we thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 And we will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (ANNOUNCEMENTS)\par \par \uc2 MAJOR GARRETT: Well, that is it for us today. We want to thank you for watching.\par \par \uc2 Margaret will be back next week.\par \par \uc2 For Face the Nation, I`m Major Garrett.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming Copyright MMXXII CBS Broadcasting  Inc. ALL  RIGHTS  RESERVED. Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All  materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not  be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast  without the prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You  may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from  copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 Document FCNT000020221009eia900001\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\